<h2> Reverse of Differentiation</h2>

<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Integration reverses differentiation.
If dy/dx = f(x), then ∫f(x) dx = original function.
</p>

<h3> EXAMPLES</h3>

<p><b>Example 1:</b> derivative x² → integral gives x³/3</p>
<p><b>Example 2:</b> derivative 2x → integral gives x²</p>
<p><b>Example 3:</b> checking correctness of solutions</p>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (3 EXAM-STYLE)</h3>

<p><b>Example 1</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find ∫2x dx</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Apply power rule in reverse</p>
<p>∫2x dx = x² + C</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Add constant of integration</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> x² + C</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 2</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find ∫3x² dx</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Increase power by 1</p>
<p>3x² → x³</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Divide by new power</p>
<p>∫3x² dx = x³ + C</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> x³ + C</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 3</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> If dy/dx = 4x³, find y</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Integrate both sides</p>
<p>y = ∫4x³ dx</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Apply rule</p>
<p>y = x⁴ + C</p>
<p><b>Final Answer:</b> y = x⁴ + C</p>

---

<h3> DIAGRAM</h3>

<pre>
Differentiation ↓
Integration ↑ (reverse process)
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Recovering position from velocity</li>
<li>Signal reconstruction in electronics</li>
<li>AI model inversion problems</li>
</ul>

---
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Integration as Reverse of Differentiation', 15),
  (355, 355, '
<h2> Applications of Integration</h2>

<h3> DEEP NOTES</h3>
<p>
Integration is used to accumulate small changes into a total result. It is essentially the reverse process of differentiation.
</p>

<pre>
∫ f(x) dx → total accumulation
</pre>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (MATHEMATICAL CALCULATION STYLE)</h3>

<p><b>Example 1</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Show why ∫ v(t) dt gives displacement</p>
<p><b>Hint:</b> velocity = rate of change of displacement</p>
<p><b>Steps:</b></p>
<p>Step 1: Let v(t) = ds/dt</p>
<p>Step 2: Multiply both sides by dt → ds = v(t)dt</p>
<p>Step 3: Integrate both sides → ∫ ds = ∫ v(t)dt</p>
<p>Step 4: Left side becomes displacement s(t)</p>
<p>Step 5: Final result → s(t) = ∫ v(t)dt</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Integration of velocity gives displacement</p>
<p><b>Explanation:</b> Integration reverses differentiation and accumulates total change</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 2</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Find area under curve using integration idea</p>
<p><b>Hint:</b> sum of rectangles</p>
<p><b>Steps:</b></p>
<p>Step 1: Divide area into small width Δx</p>
<p>Step 2: Height of each rectangle = f(x)</p>
<p>Step 3: Area of one strip = f(x)Δx</p>
<p>Step 4: Sum all strips → Σ f(x)Δx</p>
<p>Step 5: Take limit as Δx → 0</p>
<p><b>Final expression:</b> ∫ f(x) dx</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Integration gives total area</p>

<br>

<p><b>Example 3</b></p>
<p><b>Question:</b> Show why ∫ F dx gives work done in physics</p>
<p><b>Hint:</b> force × distance</p>
<p><b>Steps:</b></p>
<p>Step 1: Small work done dW = F dx</p>
<p>Step 2: Add all small work contributions</p>
<p>Step 3: ∫ dW = ∫ F dx</p>
<p>Step 4: Total work W = ∫ F dx</p>
<p>Step 5: Result gives accumulated energy transfer</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> W = ∫ F dx</p>
<p><b>Explanation:</b> Integration sums continuous force over distance</p>

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Engineering design (volume of irregular objects)</li>
<li>Physics motion tracking</li>
<li>Economics cumulative profit analysis</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Applications of Integration', 15),
  (356, 356, '
  <h2>Distance vs Displacement</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
When an object moves, we describe its motion using two important physical quantities:
<b>distance</b> and <b>displacement</b>.  
Although they may seem similar, they measure completely different things.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Distance tells “how much ground is covered”, while displacement tells “how far you are from the start point in a straight line.”</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Distance</b> → Total length of the path travelled by an object.</li>
<li><b>Displacement</b> → Shortest straight-line distance from the initial position to the final position.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY DIFFERENCES (IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Distance is a <b>scalar quantity</b> (no direction).</li>
<li>Displacement is a <b>vector quantity</b> (has direction).</li>
<li>Distance is always <b>positive</b>.</li>
<li>Displacement can be <b>positive, negative, or zero</b> depending on direction.</li>
<li>Distance is always ≥ displacement (in magnitude).</li>
</ul>

<h3> FORMULA INSIGHT</h3>
<div class="formula">
Distance = Total path travelled<br>
Displacement = Final position − Initial position (straight line, with direction)
</div>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Straight Line Motion (Same Direction)</strong><br><br>

A student walks <b>5 m east</b> and then <b>3 m east</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Calculate Distance</b><br>
Distance = 5 + 3 = <b>8 m</b><br><br>

<b>Step 2: Calculate Displacement</b><br>
Since both movements are in the same direction:<br>
Displacement = 5 + 3 = <b>8 m east</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b><br>
Distance = 8 m, Displacement = 8 m east
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Opposite Directions</strong><br><br>

A person walks <b>10 m east</b> then <b>6 m west</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Distance</b><br>
Distance = 10 + 6 = <b>16 m</b><br><br>

<b>Step 2: Displacement</b><br>
Take east as positive:<br>
Displacement = 10 − 6 = <b>4 m east</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b><br>
Distance = 16 m, Displacement = 4 m east
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Closed Path (Return to Start)</strong><br><br>

A runner completes one full lap of a <b>400 m track</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Distance</b><br>
Distance = full path = <b>400 m</b><br><br>

<b>Step 2: Displacement</b><br>
Start position = Final position → no change<br>
Displacement = <b>0 m</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b><br>
Distance = 400 m, Displacement = 0 m
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Back-and-Forth Motion</strong><br><br>

A person moves <b>12 m north</b> and then <b>12 m south</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Distance</b><br>
Distance = 12 + 12 = <b>24 m</b><br><br>

<b>Step 2: Displacement</b><br>
Equal opposite movements cancel out:<br>
Displacement = <b>0 m</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b><br>
Distance = 24 m, Displacement = 0 m
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Distance depends on the path taken.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Displacement depends only on start and end position.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Two different paths can give the same displacement but different distances.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking distance and displacement are always equal.</li>
<li>Ignoring direction when calculating displacement.</li>
<li>Forgetting that displacement can be zero even when distance is large.</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Displacement & Distance', 15),
  (357, 357, '
   <h2>Speed</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Speed tells us how quickly an object is moving from one place to another.
It describes the rate at which <b>distance is covered over time</b>.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Speed measures “how fast”, not “in what direction”.</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
<b>Speed</b> is defined as the distance travelled per unit time.
</p>

<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Speed = distance ÷ time</li>
<li>Speed is a <b>scalar quantity</b> (has magnitude only, no direction)</li>
<li>SI unit: <b>m/s (metres per second)</b></li>
<li>Other common unit: <b>km/h (kilometres per hour)</b></li>
<li>A higher speed means an object covers more distance in less time</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
Speed = Distance / Time
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT REARRANGED FORMULAS</h3>
<div class="formula">
Distance = Speed × Time<br>
Time = Distance ÷ Speed
</div>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Basic Speed Calculation</strong><br><br>

A car travels <b>100 m</b> in <b>10 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Write formula</b><br>
Speed = Distance ÷ Time<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute values</b><br>
Speed = 100 ÷ 10<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Speed = <b>10 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 10 m/s
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Medium Distance Motion</strong><br><br>

A runner covers <b>200 m</b> in <b>20 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
Speed = Distance ÷ Time<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
Speed = 200 ÷ 20<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Speed = <b>10 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 10 m/s<br><br>

<b> Insight:</b> Even different motions can have the same speed.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Real-Life Unit (km/h)</strong><br><br>

A cyclist travels <b>60 km</b> in <b>2 hours</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
Speed = Distance ÷ Time<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
Speed = 60 ÷ 2<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Speed = <b>30 km/h</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 30 km/h<br><br>

<b> Insight:</b> km/h is commonly used for vehicles because it suits long distances.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Finding Distance</strong><br><br>

A train moves at <b>20 m/s</b> for <b>5 seconds</b>. Find distance travelled.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use rearranged formula</b><br>
Distance = Speed × Time<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute values</b><br>
Distance = 20 × 5<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Distance = <b>100 m</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 100 m
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 5: Finding Time</strong><br><br>

A car travels <b>150 m</b> at a speed of <b>10 m/s</b>. Find time taken.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
Time = Distance ÷ Speed<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
Time = 150 ÷ 10<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Time = <b>15 s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 15 seconds
</div>

<h3> KEY INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Speed only describes magnitude, not direction.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Two objects can have the same speed but move differently.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Speed can change if distance or time changes.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing speed with velocity (velocity includes direction).</li>
<li>Mixing units (m/s vs km/h without conversion).</li>
<li>Using total distance incorrectly in multi-step motion problems.</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Speed', 15),
  (358, 358, '
<h2>Velocity</h2> 
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Velocity describes how fast an object’s <b>displacement changes with time</b>.
It tells both <b>how fast</b> something is moving and <b>in which direction</b>.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Velocity = speed + direction (it depends on displacement, not distance).</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
<b>Velocity</b> is the rate of change of displacement with time.
It is a <b>vector quantity</b> (has magnitude and direction).
</p>

<div class="formula">
v = s / t
</div>

<h3> KEY CONCEPTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Velocity depends on <b>displacement</b>, not distance</li>
<li>Direction is essential (east, west, north, south)</li>
<li>SI unit: <b>m/s</b></li>
<li>Can be positive, negative, or zero</li>
<li>Zero velocity means no change in position</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT TYPES OF VELOCITY</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Average velocity</b> = total displacement ÷ total time</li>
<li><b>Instantaneous velocity</b> = velocity at a specific moment</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
Average velocity = Total displacement / Total time
</div>

<h3> MOTION GRAPHS</h3>

<p><b>Displacement–Time Graph</b></p>
<pre>
s
|      /
|     /
|    /
|   /
|  /
+------------ t
Slope = Velocity
</pre>

<p><b>Velocity–Time Graph</b></p>
<pre>
v
|      /
|     /
|    /
|   /
|  /
+------------ t

Slope = Acceleration  
Area = Displacement
</pre>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Basic Velocity</strong><br><br>

A student walks <b>240 m north</b> in <b>12 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Identify values</b><br>
s = 240 m, t = 12 s<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Formula</b><br>
v = s ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Substitute</b><br>
v = 240 ÷ 12<br><br>

<b>Step 4: Calculate</b><br>
v = <b>20 m/s north</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 20 m/s north
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Opposite Directions</strong><br><br>

A person moves <b>150 m east</b> then <b>50 m west</b> in <b>10 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Find displacement</b><br>
Net displacement = 150 − 50 = <b>100 m east</b><br><br>

<b>Step 2: Formula</b><br>
v = s ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Substitute</b><br>
v = 100 ÷ 10<br><br>

<b>Step 4: Calculate</b><br>
v = <b>10 m/s east</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 10 m/s east
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Zero Velocity Concept</strong><br><br>

A runner completes a <b>400 m track</b> and returns to the starting point in <b>50 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Understand displacement</b><br>
Start position = End position → displacement = 0 m<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Formula</b><br>
v = s ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Substitute</b><br>
v = 0 ÷ 50 = <b>0 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 0 m/s<br><br>

<b> Insight:</b> You can move a long distance but still have zero velocity.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Circular Motion Concept</strong><br><br>

A car moves in a circle at constant speed of <b>15 m/s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Understand motion</b><br>
Speed remains constant, but direction keeps changing.<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Effect on velocity</b><br>
Since velocity depends on direction, velocity is changing continuously.<br><br>

<b> Final Conclusion:</b> Velocity changes even if speed is constant.
</div>

<h3> KEY INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Velocity depends on displacement, not distance.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Direction is essential in velocity.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Velocity can be zero even when motion occurs.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Changing direction means changing velocity.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing velocity with speed</li>
<li>Ignoring direction signs (+ / -)</li>
<li>Using distance instead of displacement</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Velocity', 15),
  (359, 359, '
    <h2>Acceleration</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Acceleration describes how quickly an object''s velocity changes.
This means an object is accelerating if it is:
<ul>
<li>Speeding up</li>
<li>Slowing down</li>
<li>Changing direction</li>
</ul>
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Acceleration is about CHANGE in velocity, not just motion.</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
<b>Acceleration</b> is the rate of change of velocity with respect to time.
</p>

<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time taken</li>
<li>It is a <b>vector quantity</b> (has magnitude and direction)</li>
<li>SI unit: <b>m/s² (metres per second squared)</b></li>
<li>Positive acceleration → speeding up</li>
<li>Negative acceleration → slowing down (deceleration / retardation)</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
Acceleration = (Final velocity − Initial velocity) / Time
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT REARRANGED FORMULAS</h3>
<div class="formula">
Final velocity = Initial velocity + (Acceleration × Time)<br>
Initial velocity = Final velocity − (Acceleration × Time)<br>
Time = (Final velocity − Initial velocity) ÷ Acceleration
</div>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Basic Acceleration</strong><br><br>

A car increases its speed from <b>0 m/s</b> to <b>20 m/s</b> in <b>5 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Write formula</b><br>
Acceleration = (Final velocity − Initial velocity) ÷ Time<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute values</b><br>
Acceleration = (20 − 0) ÷ 5<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Acceleration = 20 ÷ 5 = <b>4 m/s²</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 4 m/s²
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Increasing Speed</strong><br><br>

A cyclist speeds up from <b>5 m/s</b> to <b>25 m/s</b> in <b>10 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
Acceleration = (v − u) ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
Acceleration = (25 − 5) ÷ 10<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Acceleration = 20 ÷ 10 = <b>2 m/s²</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 2 m/s²
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Deceleration (Slowing Down)</strong><br><br>

A car slows from <b>30 m/s</b> to <b>10 m/s</b> in <b>5 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
Acceleration = (v − u) ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
Acceleration = (10 − 30) ÷ 5<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
Acceleration = −20 ÷ 5 = <b>−4 m/s²</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> −4 m/s²<br><br>

<b> Insight:</b> Negative sign means the object is slowing down.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Finding Final Velocity</strong><br><br>

A bus starts at <b>10 m/s</b> and accelerates at <b>3 m/s²</b> for <b>6 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use formula</b><br>
v = u + at<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
v = 10 + (3 × 6)<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
v = 10 + 18 = <b>28 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 28 m/s
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 5: Finding Time</strong><br><br>

A car increases speed from <b>15 m/s</b> to <b>35 m/s</b> with an acceleration of <b>5 m/s²</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
t = (v − u) ÷ a<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
t = (35 − 15) ÷ 5<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
t = 20 ÷ 5 = <b>4 s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 4 seconds
</div>

<h3> KEY INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Acceleration depends on change in velocity, not just speed.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Zero acceleration means constant velocity.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Negative acceleration means slowing down.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Even direction change means acceleration.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing acceleration with speed.</li>
<li>Ignoring the negative sign in deceleration.</li>
<li>Using speed instead of velocity (direction matters).</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Acceleration', 15),
  (360, 360, '
    <h2>Equations of Motion</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
The equations of motion describe how objects move when they are accelerating uniformly.
They connect <b>velocity, acceleration, time, and displacement</b>.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: These equations only work when acceleration is constant (uniform acceleration).</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT SYMBOLS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>u</b> = initial velocity (m/s)</li>
<li><b>v</b> = final velocity (m/s)</li>
<li><b>a</b> = acceleration (m/s²)</li>
<li><b>t</b> = time (s)</li>
<li><b>s</b> = displacement (m)</li>
</ul>

<h3> THE 3 EQUATIONS OF MOTION</h3>

<div class="formula">
1) v = u + at
</div>

<div class="formula">
2) s = ut + ½at²
</div>

<div class="formula">
3) v² = u² + 2as
</div>

<h3> WHY WE NEED THESE EQUATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>To find unknown velocity, time, displacement, or acceleration</li>
<li>To solve real-world motion problems (cars, falling objects, rockets)</li>
<li>To analyze motion under constant acceleration</li>
</ul>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Final Velocity</strong><br><br>

A car starts at <b>u = 5 m/s</b> and accelerates at <b>a = 3 m/s²</b> for <b>t = 4 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use equation</b><br>
v = u + at<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute values</b><br>
v = 5 + (3 × 4)<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
v = 5 + 12 = <b>17 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 17 m/s
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Displacement (Acceleration Motion)</strong><br><br>

A body starts from rest (u = 0), accelerates at <b>2 m/s²</b> for <b>6 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use equation</b><br>
s = ut + ½at²<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
s = (0 × 6) + ½(2 × 6²)<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
s = 0 + 1 × 36 = <b>36 m</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 36 m
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Final Velocity (No Time Needed)</strong><br><br>

A car has initial velocity <b>u = 10 m/s</b>, acceleration <b>a = 4 m/s²</b>, and displacement <b>s = 20 m</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use equation</b><br>
v² = u² + 2as<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
v² = 10² + 2(4)(20)<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
v² = 100 + 160 = 260<br>
v = √260 ≈ <b>16.1 m/s</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> ≈ 16.1 m/s
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Finding Acceleration</strong><br><br>

A train increases speed from <b>u = 15 m/s</b> to <b>v = 35 m/s</b> in <b>5 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Use equation</b><br>
v = u + at<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Rearrange</b><br>
a = (v − u) ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Substitute</b><br>
a = (35 − 15) ÷ 5<br><br>

<b>Step 4: Calculate</b><br>
a = 20 ÷ 5 = <b>4 m/s²</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 4 m/s²
</div>

<h3> KEY INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Use the correct equation based on known values.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> All equations assume constant acceleration.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Displacement is NOT the same as distance in these equations.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Always check units before solving.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using equations when acceleration is not constant.</li>
<li>Mixing up u and v.</li>
<li>Forgetting to square root in v² equation.</li>
<li>Using distance instead of displacement.</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
Think: “SUVAT system” <br>
S = displacement<br>
U = initial velocity<br>
V = final velocity<br>
A = acceleration<br>
T = time
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Equations of Motion', 15),
  (361, 361, '
   <h2>Friction</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Friction is a force that opposes the relative motion (or attempted motion) between two surfaces in contact.
It always acts along the surface and in the opposite direction of motion.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Friction is a resisting force — it tries to slow things down or stop motion.</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
<b>Friction</b> is the force that opposes motion between two surfaces that are touching each other.
</p>

<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Friction acts <b>opposite to motion</b></li>
<li>It occurs only when surfaces are in contact</li>
<li>It depends on surface roughness and normal force</li>
<li>It converts kinetic energy into heat</li>
</ul>

<h3> TYPES OF FRICTION</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Static friction</b> → acts when an object is not moving but trying to move</li>
<li><b>Kinetic (sliding) friction</b> → acts when objects are sliding over each other</li>
<li><b>Rolling friction</b> → acts when objects roll (usually weaker than sliding friction)</li>
<li><b>Fluid friction</b> → resistance through air or water</li>
</ul>

<h3> EVERYDAY EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Walking</strong><br><br>
When you walk, your foot pushes backward on the ground.<br>
Friction between shoe and ground pushes you forward.<br><br>

<b> Result:</b> You move forward without slipping
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Sliding a Book</strong><br><br>
A book sliding on a table slows down and eventually stops.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> You apply force to move it forward<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Friction acts backward opposing motion<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Book loses energy and stops<br><br>

<b> Result:</b> Motion reduces due to friction
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Car Braking</strong><br><br>
When a car brakes, tyres grip the road.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Brake force slows wheels<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Friction between tyres and road increases resistance<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Car stops safely<br><br>

<b> Result:</b> Friction is essential for stopping motion
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Ice Surface (Low Friction)</strong><br><br>
A person slips easily on ice.<br><br>

<b>Reason:</b> Ice has very low friction, so there is little resistance to motion.<br><br>

<b> Result:</b> Hard to control movement
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Friction always opposes motion or attempted motion.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Without friction, walking, driving, and holding objects would be impossible.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Friction produces heat energy due to resistance.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Rough surfaces → more friction, smooth surfaces → less friction.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking friction is always useless (it is essential for movement).</li>
<li>Forgetting friction acts opposite to motion.</li>
<li>Assuming smooth surfaces have no friction (they still have some).</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
Friction = “Fight against motion” <br>
It always tries to slow things down 
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Friction', 15),
  (362, 362, '
    <h2>Tension & Weight</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Forces can act in different ways. Some act through contact (like ropes), while others act at a distance (like gravity).
Two very important forces in mechanics are <b>tension</b> and <b>weight</b>.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Weight pulls objects downward due to gravity, while tension pulls along a rope or string.</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Tension</b> → The pulling force transmitted through a string, rope, or cable when it is stretched.</li>
<li><b>Weight</b> → The force exerted on an object due to gravity.</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Tension acts <b>along the rope</b>, away from the object being pulled</li>
<li>Weight always acts <b>vertically downward</b></li>
<li>Weight depends on gravitational field strength</li>
<li>Mass stays constant, but weight can change depending on location</li>
<li>SI unit of both forces: <b>Newton (N)</b></li>
</ul>

<h3> FORMULA</h3>
<div class="formula">
Weight (W) = mg<br>
g ≈ 10 m/s² (on Earth)
</div>

<h3> MASS VS WEIGHT (IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mass (kg) → amount of matter, constant everywhere</li>
<li>Weight (N) → force due to gravity, changes with location</li>
</ul>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Finding Weight</strong><br><br>

A mass of <b>5 kg</b> is on Earth.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Write formula</b><br>
W = mg<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute values</b><br>
W = 5 × 10<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
W = <b>50 N</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 50 N
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Hanging Object (Tension)</strong><br><br>

A <b>10 kg</b> object hangs at rest on a rope.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Find weight</b><br>
W = mg = 10 × 10 = <b>100 N</b><br><br>

<b>Step 2: Understand forces</b><br>
Object is stationary → forces are balanced<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Conclusion</b><br>
Tension = Weight = <b>100 N</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> Tension = 100 N
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Weight on Different Planets</strong><br><br>

A <b>2 kg</b> object is taken to the Moon where gravity is weaker.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: On Earth</b><br>
W = 2 × 10 = 20 N<br><br>

<b>Step 2: On Moon</b><br>
Gravity is smaller → weight decreases<br><br>

<b> Final Conclusion:</b> Mass stays 2 kg, but weight becomes smaller
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Understanding Tension Direction</strong><br><br>

A box is pulled upward by a rope.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Tension acts along the rope upward<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Weight acts downward due to gravity<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> If box is stationary, tension = weight<br><br>

<b> Key Insight:</b> Tension always pulls, it never pushes.
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Weight depends on gravity, not just mass.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Tension only exists in strings/ropes under pulling force.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> If an object is at rest, forces are balanced.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Weight acts downward, tension acts along the rope.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing mass (kg) with weight (N)</li>
<li>Thinking tension pushes instead of pulling</li>
<li>Forgetting that weight changes on different planets</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
Weight = “Earth pulling down” ⬇<br>
Tension = “Rope pulling tight” ⬆
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tension & Weight', 15),
  (363, 363, '
   <h2>Circular Motion</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Circular motion happens when an object moves along a circular path.
Even if speed is constant, the object is still accelerating because its <b>direction is continuously changing</b>.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Circular motion requires a force toward the center called centripetal force.</div>

<h3> KEY CONCEPTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Circular motion = motion in a circle</li>
<li><b>Centripetal force</b> always acts toward the center</li>
<li>Without centripetal force, the object moves in a straight line (Newton’s 1st law)</li>
<li>Centripetal force can be provided by tension, gravity, friction, or normal reaction</li>
</ul>

<h3> CENTRIPETAL FORCE FORMULA</h3>
<div class="formula">
F = mv² / r
</div>

<ul>
<li>F = centripetal force (N)</li>
<li>m = mass (kg)</li>
<li>v = speed (m/s)</li>
<li>r = radius of circular path (m)</li>
</ul>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY INSIGHT: Higher speed → more force needed. Larger radius → less force needed.</div>

<h3> REAL-LIFE APPLICATIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Car Turning on a Flat Road</strong><br><br>

A car turns around a bend.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> The car wants to move straight due to inertia<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Friction between tyres and road pulls it inward<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> This friction acts as centripetal force<br><br>

<b> Result:</b> Without friction, the car would skid outward
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Banked Roads (Very Important)</strong><br><br>

Roads are tilted on curves (banked) to help vehicles turn safely.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Normal reaction force is tilted<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Part of this force provides centripetal force<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Less reliance on friction → safer turning<br><br>

<b> Key Insight:</b> Banking reduces risk of skidding at high speeds
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Stone on a String</strong><br><br>

A stone is tied to a string and whirled in a circle.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> The stone wants to fly outward (inertia)<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Tension in the string pulls it inward<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Tension = centripetal force<br><br>

<b> Result:</b> If string breaks → stone flies in a straight line
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Swinging a Bucket of Water</strong><br><br>

A bucket of water is swung in a vertical circle.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> At the top, gravity + motion keep water inside<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> If speed is high enough, water does not fall out<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Centripetal force keeps water moving in a circle<br><br>

<b> Key Insight:</b> Minimum speed is required to prevent falling
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 5: Satellite Orbit</strong><br><br>

A satellite moves around Earth in orbit.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Gravity pulls satellite toward Earth<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> This gravity provides centripetal force<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Continuous free-fall creates orbit<br><br>

<b> Result:</b> Satellite stays in circular motion
</div>

<h3> SPEED CONDITIONS (IMPORTANT INSIGHT)</h3>
<ul>
<li>If speed is too low → object falls inward/out of circular path</li>
<li>If speed is too high → object flies outward (no centripetal force enough)</li>
<li>Correct speed → stable circular motion</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Centripetal force is not a new force — it is provided by real forces.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Circular motion is accelerated motion even if speed is constant.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Inertia tries to move objects in straight lines.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Faster motion requires stronger centripetal force.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking “centrifugal force pushes outward” (it is not a real force)</li>
<li>Confusing speed and velocity in circular motion</li>
<li>Forgetting that direction is constantly changing</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
Centripetal = “Center-seeking force” <br>
Without it → object flies straight 
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Circular Motion', 15),
  (364, 364, '
  <h2>Gravitation</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Gravitation is a universal force of attraction between any two objects that have mass.
It is one of the fundamental forces of nature and acts everywhere in the universe.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Every object with mass attracts every other object with mass.</div>

<h3> KEY CONCEPTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Gravitational force acts between all masses</li>
<li>It is always attractive (never repulsive)</li>
<li>It acts over long distances (no contact needed)</li>
<li>Stronger when masses are large</li>
<li>Weaker when distance increases</li>
</ul>

<h3> NEWTON’S LAW OF UNIVERSAL GRAVITATION</h3>
<div class="formula">
F = Gm₁m₂ / r²
</div>

<ul>
<li>F = gravitational force (N)</li>
<li>G = gravitational constant</li>
<li>m₁, m₂ = masses</li>
<li>r = distance between centers of masses</li>
</ul>

<h3> NEAR EARTH GRAVITY</h3>
<div class="formula">
g ≈ 10 m/s²
</div>

<ul>
<li>g = gravitational field strength</li>
<li>Used to calculate weight near Earth</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
Weight (W) = mg
</div>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Falling Object</strong><br><br>

An object is dropped from rest.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Identify force</b><br>
Only force acting is gravity<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Acceleration</b><br>
Object accelerates downward at <b>g = 10 m/s²</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> It falls with constant acceleration due to gravity
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Weight Calculation</strong><br><br>

A mass of <b>3 kg</b> is on Earth.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Formula</b><br>
W = mg<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Substitute</b><br>
W = 3 × 10<br><br>

<b>Step 3: Calculate</b><br>
W = <b>30 N</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 30 N
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Distance Effect on Gravity</strong><br><br>

Two objects are moved farther apart.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Gravitational force depends on distance squared (1/r²)<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Increasing distance reduces force rapidly<br><br>

<b> Final Insight:</b> Small increase in distance → big decrease in force
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Universal Attraction</strong><br><br>

A book and a table attract each other.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Both have mass<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Therefore both exert gravitational force<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Force is extremely small, so we don’t notice it<br><br>

<b> Key Insight:</b> Gravity exists between all objects, not just planets
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 5: Orbit Concept</strong><br><br>

The Moon orbits Earth.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Earth pulls Moon via gravity<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> This force provides centripetal force<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Moon keeps falling around Earth instead of straight into it<br><br>

<b> Final Insight:</b> Orbit = continuous free-fall due to gravity
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Gravity acts between all objects with mass in the universe.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> It is always attractive, never repulsive.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Weight depends on gravity, mass does not.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Gravity keeps planets, moons, and satellites in orbit.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking gravity only exists on Earth</li>
<li>Confusing mass (kg) with weight (N)</li>
<li>Forgetting that gravitational force decreases with distance squared</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
Gravity = “Invisible pull between all things with mass” ⬇
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gravitation', 15),
  (365, 365, '
  <h2>Newton’s Laws of Motion</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Newton’s Laws explain how and why objects move or stay at rest.
They describe the relationship between force, mass, and motion.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Motion changes only when a net (resultant) force acts.</div>

<h3> NEWTON’S FIRST LAW (LAW OF INERTIA)</h3>
<p>
An object remains at rest or continues moving in a straight line at constant speed unless acted upon by an external force.
</p>

<ul>
<li>Explains inertia (resistance to change in motion)</li>
<li>No net force → no change in motion</li>
<li>Objects “like” to keep doing what they are doing</li>
</ul>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example:</strong> A book on a table stays still until pushed.
</div>

<h3> NEWTON’S SECOND LAW</h3>
<p>
The acceleration of an object depends on the net force acting on it and its mass.
</p>

<div class="formula">
F = ma
</div>

<ul>
<li>F = force (N)</li>
<li>m = mass (kg)</li>
<li>a = acceleration (m/s²)</li>
</ul>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: More force → more acceleration. More mass → less acceleration.</div>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (2nd LAW)</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1:</strong><br><br>

A mass of 5 kg is pushed with a force of 20 N.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Use formula F = ma<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Rearrange → a = F / m<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Substitute → a = 20 / 5<br>
<b>Step 4:</b> Calculate → a = <b>4 m/s²</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 4 m/s²
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2:</strong><br><br>

A 10 kg object accelerates at 3 m/s².<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> F = ma<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> F = 10 × 3<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> F = <b>30 N</b>
</div>

<h3> NEWTON’S THIRD LAW</h3>
<p>
For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.
</p>

<ul>
<li>Forces come in pairs</li>
<li>They act on different objects</li>
<li>They are equal in size and opposite in direction</li>
</ul>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Forces always exist in pairs — you cannot have a single force alone.</div>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (3rd LAW)</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Walking</strong><br><br>

Your foot pushes the ground backward (action).<br>
The ground pushes you forward (reaction).<br><br>

<b> Result:</b> You move forward
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Rocket Launch</strong><br><br>

Rocket pushes gases downward (action).<br>
Gases push rocket upward (reaction).<br><br>

<b> Result:</b> Rocket moves up
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Newton’s 1st Law → explains inertia (why objects resist change)</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Newton’s 2nd Law → links force, mass, and acceleration</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Newton’s 3rd Law → explains interaction between objects</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking action and reaction forces act on the same object</li>
<li>Forgetting net force is needed for acceleration</li>
<li>Confusing mass with weight in F = ma problems</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
1st Law → “Stay as you are” <br>
2nd Law → “Force changes motion” <br>
3rd Law → “Every action has a reaction” 
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Newton''s Laws of Motion', 15),
  (366, 366, '<h2>Work (Advanced + Machines)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Work is done when a force causes an object to move in the direction of that force.
If there is no movement, then no work is done — even if a large force is applied.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Work links force and motion — without displacement, work is zero.</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
<b>Work</b> is the product of force and displacement in the direction of the force.
It represents energy transfer when a force moves an object.
</p>

<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Work depends on both force and displacement</li>
<li>If displacement = 0 → work = 0</li>
<li>Work is a scalar quantity</li>
<li>SI unit: Joule (J)</li>
<li>1 Joule = 1 Newton moving an object 1 meter</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
Work (W) = Force × Distance<br>
W = F × d
</div>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Basic Work Calculation</strong><br><br>

A force of <b>10 N</b> moves an object <b>5 m</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Write formula<br>
W = F × d<br><br>

<b>Step 2:</b> Substitute values<br>
W = 10 × 5<br><br>

<b>Step 3:</b> Calculate<br>
W = <b>50 J</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 50 J
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: No Work Done</strong><br><br>

A man pushes a wall but it does not move.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Identify displacement<br>
d = 0 m<br><br>

<b>Step 2:</b> Apply formula<br>
W = F × 0<br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 0 J (no work done)
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Higher Force</strong><br><br>

A force of <b>20 N</b> moves an object <b>3 m</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Formula<br>
W = F × d<br><br>

<b>Step 2:</b> Substitute<br>
W = 20 × 3<br><br>

<b>Step 3:</b> Calculate<br>
W = <b>60 J</b>
</div>

<h3> MACHINES AND WORK</h3>

<h3> CONCEPT</h3>
<p>
Machines make work easier by reducing the force needed,
but they increase the distance over which the force is applied.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Machines do NOT reduce work — they only change how it is done.</div>

<div class="formula">
Work Input = Work Output + Energy Lost
</div>

<h3> EFFICIENCY</h3>

<div class="formula">
Efficiency = (Useful Output Work / Input Work) × 100%
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example: Efficiency</strong><br><br>

Input work = 200 J<br>
Output work = 150 J<br><br>

Efficiency = (150 ÷ 200) × 100<br>
Efficiency = <b>75%</b>
</div>

<div class="keyfact"> No machine is 100% efficient due to friction and heat loss.</div>

<h3> MACHINES IN REAL LIFE</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Inclined Plane</strong><br><br>
Lifts objects using a slope.<br>
Reduces force but increases distance.<br><br>

Example: Lifting 10 kg to 2 m<br>
Work = mgh = 10 × 10 × 2 = <b>200 J</b>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Pulley</strong><br><br>
Changes direction of force and reduces effort.<br><br>

Example:<br>
Lift 100 N load by 2 m<br>
Work = 100 × 2 = <b>200 J</b>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Lever</strong><br><br>
Longer effort arm reduces force needed.<br>
Distance increases to balance energy.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Wheel & Axle</strong><br><br>
Large wheel multiplies force at axle for easier movement.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Wedge & Screw</strong><br><br>
Convert force into cutting or tightening action.
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Work is only done when force causes movement.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Machines change force and distance, not total work.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Energy is always conserved (never created or destroyed).</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking effort always equals work</li>
<li>Forgetting displacement is required</li>
<li>Thinking machines reduce total work (they don’t)</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
Work = “Force causes movement” <br>
No movement = No work 
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Work', 15),
  (367, 367, '<h2>Power (Advanced)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Power describes how quickly work is done or how fast energy is transferred.
Two systems can do the same amount of work, but the one that finishes faster has higher power.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Power measures the RATE of doing work or transferring energy.</div>

<h3> CLEAR DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
<b>Power</b> is the rate of doing work or the rate of energy transfer.
</p>

<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Power = work ÷ time</li>
<li>Unit: Watt (W)</li>
<li>1 Watt = 1 Joule per second</li>
<li>Higher power → faster energy transfer</li>
<li>Power is a scalar quantity</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
P = W / t
</div>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Basic Power</strong><br><br>

A machine does <b>200 J</b> of work in <b>10 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Formula<br>
P = W ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 2:</b> Substitute<br>
P = 200 ÷ 10<br><br>

<b>Step 3:</b> Calculate<br>
P = <b>20 W</b>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Faster Energy Transfer</strong><br><br>

A machine does <b>500 J</b> in <b>5 s</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Formula<br>
P = W ÷ t<br><br>

<b>Step 2:</b> Substitute<br>
P = 500 ÷ 5<br><br>

<b>Step 3:</b> Calculate<br>
P = <b>100 W</b>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Key Insight Comparison</strong><br><br>

Machine A: 200 J in 10 s → 20 W<br>
Machine B: 200 J in 5 s → 40 W<br><br>

<b> Conclusion:</b> Same work, but Machine B is more powerful because it is faster.
</div>

<h3> OTHER POWER FORMULAS</h3>

<div class="formula">
Electrical Power: P = IV
</div>

<ul>
<li>I = current (A)</li>
<li>V = voltage (V)</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
Mechanical Power: P = Fv
</div>

<ul>
<li>F = force (N)</li>
<li>v = velocity (m/s)</li>
</ul>

<h3> ELECTRICAL POWER INSIGHT</h3>
<p>
Electrical power shows how fast electrical energy is used or transferred.
</p>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example:</strong><br><br>

A device uses 2 A current and 12 V.<br><br>

P = IV = 2 × 12 = <b>24 W</b>
</div>

<h3> POWER TRANSMISSION (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>

<div class="formula">
Power loss = I²R
</div>

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<p>
Electricity is transmitted over long distances using HIGH VOLTAGE and LOW CURRENT.
</p>

<ul>
<li>High voltage → low current</li>
<li>Low current → less heat loss</li>
<li>Less energy wasted in wires</li>
</ul>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY INSIGHT: Reducing current is the most effective way to reduce energy loss.</div>

<h3> TRANSFORMERS</h3>

<div class="formula">
Vp / Vs = Np / Ns
</div>

<div class="formula">
Power conservation: VpIp = VsIs
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example:</strong><br><br>

Np = 100, Ns = 500<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Ns > Np → voltage increases (step-up transformer)<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Used in power transmission<br><br>

<b> Result:</b> Voltage increases, current decreases
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Power measures how fast energy is used.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Same work done faster = more power.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> High voltage reduces energy loss in cables.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Transformers help control voltage for safe transmission.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing power with energy</li>
<li>Thinking power depends only on force or only on work</li>
<li>Forgetting that time is crucial in power calculations</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
Power = “How fast energy is used” ⏱<br>
Fast work = high power 
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Power', 15),
  (368, 368, '<h2>Energy Conversion</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Energy cannot be created or destroyed. It can only be transformed from one form into another.
This is known as the <b>principle of conservation of energy</b>.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Energy changes form, but total energy in a system always stays constant.</div>

<h3> KEY POINTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Energy is conserved in all processes</li>
<li>It only changes from one form to another</li>
<li>Some energy is often “wasted” as heat or sound</li>
<li>No system is 100% efficient</li>
</ul>

<h3> COMMON ENERGY TRANSFORMATIONS</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Falling object:</b> Gravitational Potential Energy → Kinetic Energy</li>
<li><b>Car engine:</b> Chemical Energy → Kinetic Energy + Heat Energy</li>
<li><b>Electric bulb:</b> Electrical Energy → Light Energy + Heat Energy</li>
<li><b>Battery:</b> Chemical Energy → Electrical Energy</li>
<li><b>Wind turbine:</b> Kinetic Energy (wind) → Electrical Energy</li>
</ul>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP EXPLANATIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Falling Object</strong><br><br>

A stone is dropped from a height.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> At the top, it has gravitational potential energy (stored energy)<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> As it falls, height decreases → GPE reduces<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Speed increases → kinetic energy increases<br><br>

<b> Energy conversion:</b> GPE → KE
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Car Engine</strong><br><br>

A car burns fuel to move.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Fuel contains chemical energy<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Engine burns fuel → releases energy<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Energy turns into motion + heat<br><br>

<b> Energy conversion:</b> Chemical → Kinetic + Heat
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Electric Bulb</strong><br><br>

An electric bulb is switched on.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Electrical energy flows into bulb<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Filament heats up<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Light is produced<br><br>

<b> Energy conversion:</b> Electrical → Light + Heat
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Battery Device</strong><br><br>

A phone battery powers the device.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Battery stores chemical energy<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> Chemical energy converts to electrical energy<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> Electrical energy powers the phone<br><br>

<b> Energy conversion:</b> Chemical → Electrical
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Energy is never lost, only transformed.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Some energy is always transferred as heat (wasted energy).</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Falling objects continuously convert energy between forms.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Real systems are never 100% efficient.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking energy is destroyed (it is only transformed)</li>
<li>Forgetting heat loss in real systems</li>
<li>Confusing energy type changes with energy loss</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
Energy = “Shape-shifter” <br>
It never disappears — only changes form
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy Conversion', 15),
  (369, 369, '<h2>Conservation of Energy</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
The law of conservation of energy states that energy cannot be created or destroyed.
It can only change from one form to another, but the total energy in a closed system remains constant.
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Total energy before = Total energy after (always conserved).</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT PRINCIPLE (MECHANICAL ENERGY)</h3>
<p>
In falling objects and many motion systems:
</p>

<div class="formula">
Potential Energy = Kinetic Energy<br>
mgh = ½mv²
</div>

<ul>
<li>m = mass (kg)</li>
<li>g = gravitational field strength (≈ 10 m/s²)</li>
<li>h = height (m)</li>
<li>v = velocity (m/s)</li>
</ul>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY INSIGHT: As height decreases, speed increases — energy is being transferred.</div>

<h3> STEP-BY-STEP WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 1: Energy at Height</strong><br><br>

A <b>2 kg</b> object is dropped from a height of <b>10 m</b>.<br><br>

<b>Step 1: Calculate potential energy</b><br>
PE = mgh = 2 × 10 × 10<br><br>

<b>Step 2: Solve</b><br>
PE = <b>200 J</b><br><br>

<b> Interpretation:</b> This is the total energy the object has before falling.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 2: Energy at Ground</strong><br><br>

At the bottom of the fall:<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Height becomes zero → PE = 0<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> All energy becomes kinetic energy<br>
<b>Step 3:</b> KE = 200 J<br><br>

<b> Final Conclusion:</b> Energy is transferred, not lost
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 3: Partial Height Fall</strong><br><br>

A 2 kg object falls from 5 m.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Total energy = mgh = 2 × 10 × 5 = <b>100 J</b><br><br>

<b>Step 2:</b> During fall:<br>
PE decreases while KE increases<br><br>

<b> Final Insight:</b> Total energy remains constant at 100 J
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example 4: Real-World Losses</strong><br><br>

When an object hits the ground:<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> KE does not disappear<br>
<b>Step 2:</b> It converts into heat, sound, and deformation energy<br><br>

<b> Final Insight:</b> Energy is conserved, but becomes less useful
</div>

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Energy is always conserved in a closed system.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Potential energy transforms into kinetic energy during سقوط (fall).</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Real systems lose usable energy as heat and sound.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Total energy never decreases — it only changes form.</div>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking energy is lost when motion stops</li>
<li>Forgetting energy converts into heat/sound</li>
<li>Confusing energy loss with energy transformation</li>
</ul>

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
Energy = “Never dies, only transforms” <br>
Total in = Total out 
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conservation of Energy', 15),
  (370, 370, '<h2>Kinetic & Potential Energy</h2>

<h3> FOUNDATION UNDERSTANDING</h3>
<p>
Energy exists in different forms. Two of the most important forms in mechanics are:
<b>kinetic energy</b> (energy of motion) and <b>potential energy</b> (stored energy due to position).
</p>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY IDEA: Energy is either stored (potential) or moving (kinetic).</div>

---

<h3> KINETIC ENERGY (KE)</h3>

<h3> DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
Kinetic energy is the energy an object has due to its motion.
Any moving object has kinetic energy.
</p>

<div class="formula">
KE = ½mv²
</div>

<ul>
<li>m = mass (kg)</li>
<li>v = velocity (m/s)</li>
<li>KE = kinetic energy (Joules)</li>
</ul>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY INSIGHT: If velocity doubles, kinetic energy becomes 4 times bigger (because of v²).</div>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLE (KE)</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example:</strong> A 2 kg object moves at 3 m/s.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Write formula<br>
KE = ½mv²<br><br>

<b>Step 2:</b> Substitute values<br>
KE = ½ × 2 × 3²<br><br>

<b>Step 3:</b> Calculate<br>
KE = 1 × 9 = <b>9 J</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 9 Joules
</div>

---

<h3> POTENTIAL ENERGY (PE)</h3>

<h3> DEFINITION</h3>
<p>
Potential energy is stored energy due to position or height in a gravitational field.
</p>

<div class="formula">
PE = mgh
</div>

<ul>
<li>m = mass (kg)</li>
<li>g = gravitational field strength (≈ 10 m/s²)</li>
<li>h = height (m)</li>
<li>PE = potential energy (Joules)</li>
</ul>

<div class="keyfact"> KEY INSIGHT: The higher the object, the more potential energy it stores.</div>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLE (PE)</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Example:</strong> A 3 kg object is raised to 4 m height.<br><br>

<b>Step 1:</b> Write formula<br>
PE = mgh<br><br>

<b>Step 2:</b> Substitute values<br>
PE = 3 × 10 × 4<br><br>

<b>Step 3:</b> Calculate<br>
PE = <b>120 J</b><br><br>

<b> Final Answer:</b> 120 Joules
</div>

---

<h3> ENERGY TRANSFORMATION (VERY IMPORTANT)</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<strong> Falling Object</strong><br><br>

At the top:<br>
PE is high, KE is low<br><br>

During fall:<br>
PE decreases → KE increases<br><br>

At bottom:<br>
PE = 0, KE = maximum<br><br>

<b> Key Idea:</b> PE transforms into KE
</div>

---

<h3> IMPORTANT INSIGHTS</h3>
<div class="keyfact"> Kinetic energy depends on speed (v² effect makes it very powerful).</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Potential energy depends on height and mass.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Energy constantly switches between KE and PE in motion.</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Total mechanical energy = KE + PE (in ideal systems).</div>

---

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking only moving objects have energy (false — stored energy also counts)</li>
<li>Forgetting velocity is squared in KE formula</li>
<li>Confusing height direction in PE problems</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> MEMORY TRICK</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
KE = “Energy of movement” ‍<br>
PE = “Energy of position” 
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kinetic & Potential Energy', 15),
  (371, 371, '<h2>Wave Properties (Full Explanation)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
A wave is a disturbance that transfers <b>energy</b> from one place to another without transferring matter permanently.
Particles in the medium only oscillate (vibrate) around a fixed position.
</p>

<p>
There are two main types of waves:
</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Transverse waves:</b> particles vibrate perpendicular to direction of travel (e.g. water waves, light)</li>
<li><b>Longitudinal waves:</b> particles vibrate parallel to direction of travel (e.g. sound waves)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Wavelength (λ):</b> Distance between two consecutive crests or troughs (meters)</li>

<li><b>Amplitude (A):</b> Maximum displacement of a particle from its rest position. It determines the energy of the wave.</li>

<li><b>Frequency (f):</b> Number of complete waves passing a point per second (measured in Hertz, Hz)</li>

<li><b>Time Period (T):</b> Time taken for one complete wave cycle (seconds)</li>

<li><b>Wave Speed (v):</b> Distance travelled by the wave per second (m/s)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> KEY FORMULAS</h3>

<div class="formula">
v = fλ<br>
f = 1/T<br>
T = 1/f<br>
λ = v/f<br>
λ = vT
</div>

---

<h3> CONCEPT CLARITY QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q1: What is frequency?</b><br>
Frequency is the number of complete waves passing a point in one second. It is measured in Hertz (Hz).
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q2: What is amplitude?</b><br>
Amplitude is the maximum displacement of particles from the equilibrium position. Higher amplitude = more energy.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q3: What is time period?</b><br>
Time period is the time taken for one complete wave cycle. It is the inverse of frequency.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q4: How do we find frequency?</b><br>
We use the formula: f = 1/T or f = v/λ depending on given data.
</div>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 1:</b><br>
Wave speed = 20 m/s, frequency = 5 Hz<br>
λ = v/f = 20/5 = 4 m
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 2:</b><br>
Speed = 10 m/s, period = 2 s<br>
First find frequency: f = 1/T = 1/2 = 0.5 Hz<br>
Then wavelength: λ = v/f = 10/0.5 = 20 m
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 3:</b><br>
Frequency = 8 Hz, wavelength = 3 m<br>
Wave speed = v = fλ = 8 × 3 = 24 m/s
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 4:</b><br>
Time period = 0.25 s<br>
Frequency = f = 1/T = 1/0.25 = 4 Hz
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 5 (Amplitude concept):</b><br>
Wave A has amplitude 2 cm, Wave B has amplitude 5 cm.<br>
Wave B carries more energy because amplitude is higher.
</div>

---

<h3> CONTINUOUS PROBLEM (EXAM STYLE)</h3>

<div class="example-box">
A wave travels along a string with speed 30 m/s. It has a wavelength of 6 m.<br><br>

(a) Find the frequency<br>
(b) Find the time period<br>
(c) Explain what happens if amplitude increases<br><br>

Solution:<br>
(a) f = v/λ = 30/6 = 5 Hz<br>
(b) T = 1/f = 1/5 = 0.2 s<br>
(c) Increasing amplitude increases energy but does NOT change speed, frequency, or wavelength.
</div>

---

<h3> IMPORTANT SUMMARY</h3>
<div class="keyfact">
 Frequency ↑ → wavelength ↓ (if speed is constant)<br>
 Amplitude affects energy only<br>
 Speed depends on medium, not amplitude<br>
 f and T are inversely related
</div>

---

<h3> PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
1. A wave has speed 15 m/s and frequency 3 Hz. Find wavelength.<br>
2. A wave has wavelength 4 m and frequency 6 Hz. Find speed.<br>
3. Time period is 0.5 s. Find frequency.<br>
4. A wave has amplitude 10 cm. What does this tell us about its energy?<br>
5. A wave travels 50 m in 10 seconds with frequency 5 Hz. Find wavelength.
</div>

', 'Canonical lesson notes for Wave properties', 15),
  (372, 372, '<h2>Sound Waves (Full Explanation)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Sound is a form of energy produced by vibrating objects. It travels through a medium (solid, liquid, or gas) as a <b>longitudinal wave</b>.
</p>

<p>
In sound waves, particles of the medium vibrate back and forth in the same direction as the wave travels, forming:
</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Compressions:</b> regions where particles are close together (high pressure)</li>
<li><b>Rarefactions:</b> regions where particles are spread out (low pressure)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Sound is a longitudinal wave</b></li>
<li>It requires a medium (solid, liquid, or gas) to travel</li>
<li>It cannot travel in a vacuum because there are no particles to vibrate</li>
<li>Speed of sound depends on the medium:
    <ul>
        <li>Fastest in solids</li>
        <li>Slower in liquids</li>
        <li>Slowest in gases</li>
    </ul>
</li>
<li>Sound energy is transferred through vibrations, not matter movement</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> KEY CONCEPTS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Compression:</b> High-pressure region where particles are crowded together
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Rarefaction:</b> Low-pressure region where particles are spread apart
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Frequency:</b> Determines pitch (high frequency = high pitch sound)
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Amplitude:</b> Determines loudness (higher amplitude = louder sound)
</div>

---

<h3> IMPORTANT FORMULA</h3>

<div class="formula">
v = fλ
</div>

---

<h3> CONCEPT CLARITY QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q1: Why is sound a longitudinal wave?</b><br>
Because particles vibrate parallel to the direction of wave travel.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q2: Why can''t sound travel in space?</b><br>
Because space is a vacuum and has no particles to transmit vibrations.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q3: What affects loudness?</b><br>
Amplitude of the wave.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q4: What affects pitch?</b><br>
Frequency of the wave.
</div>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 1:</b><br>
A speaker vibrates → it pushes air particles → compressions and rarefactions form → sound travels through air.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 2:</b><br>
Thunder is heard after lightning because light travels faster than sound.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 3:</b><br>
An echo occurs when sound waves reflect off a hard surface like a wall or cliff.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 4 (Calculation):</b><br>
Frequency = 50 Hz, wavelength = 6 m<br>
Speed = v = fλ = 50 × 6 = 300 m/s
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 5 (Time delay concept):</b><br>
If sound takes 2 seconds to reach a wall and 2 seconds to return, echo delay = 4 seconds total.
</div>

---

<h3> CONTINUOUS EXAM-STYLE QUESTION</h3>

<div class="example-box">
A sound wave travels through air with a frequency of 120 Hz and wavelength of 2.5 m.<br><br>

(a) Find the speed of sound<br>
(b) Explain what happens if the sound enters a solid<br>
(c) State what determines the loudness of the sound<br><br>

Solution:<br>
(a) v = fλ = 120 × 2.5 = 300 m/s<br>
(b) Sound travels faster in solids because particles are closer together<br>
(c) Loudness depends on amplitude
</div>

---

<h3> KEY FACTS</h3>

<div class="keyfact">
 Sound needs a medium to travel<br>
 No medium = no sound (space is silent)<br>
 Frequency → pitch<br>
 Amplitude → loudness<br>
 Speed depends on medium, not frequency
</div>

---

<h3> PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
1. Why is sound faster in solids than gases?<br>
2. What is the difference between compression and rarefaction?<br>
3. A wave has frequency 200 Hz and wavelength 1.5 m. Find speed.<br>
4. What happens to sound in a vacuum?<br>
5. Explain how echo is formed.
</div>

', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sound waves', 15),
  (373, 373, '<h2>Light Waves (Full Explanation)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Light is a form of electromagnetic radiation that travels in waves. It does not require a medium and can travel through a vacuum such as space.
</p>

<p>
Unlike sound, light is a wave of electric and magnetic fields oscillating perpendicular to each other and to the direction of travel.
</p>

---

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Light is a transverse wave</b></li>
<li>It is an electromagnetic wave (no particles required)</li>
<li>It can travel through vacuum (space)</li>
<li>Speed of light in vacuum = <b>3 × 10⁸ m/s</b></li>
<li>Light travels in straight lines in a uniform medium</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> KEY PHENOMENA OF LIGHT</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Reflection:</b> Bouncing back of light from a surface (e.g., mirror)
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Refraction:</b> Bending of light when it moves from one medium to another
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Dispersion:</b> Splitting of white light into different colors (rainbow formation)
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Absorption:</b> When light energy is taken in by a material
</div>

---

<h3> IMPORTANT FACTS</h3>

<ul>
<li>Light does NOT need a medium to travel</li>
<li>Light is the fastest known form of energy transfer</li>
<li>Speed in air ≈ speed in vacuum (very close)</li>
<li>Speed decreases in denser materials like glass or water</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> FORMULA</h3>

<div class="formula">
v = fλ
</div>

---

<h3> CONCEPT CLARITY QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q1: Why can light travel in space?</b><br>
Because it is an electromagnetic wave and does not require a medium.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q2: Why does light bend when entering glass?</b><br>
Because its speed changes when moving from air to a denser medium.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q3: What causes a rainbow?</b><br>
Dispersion of light through water droplets.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q4: Is light faster than sound?</b><br>
Yes, light travels much faster than sound.
</div>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 1:</b><br>
Sunlight reaches Earth through space.<br>
Explanation: Space is a vacuum, but light can travel without a medium.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 2:</b><br>
Light passes from air into water and bends.<br>
This is called refraction due to change in speed.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 3:</b><br>
A rainbow appears after rain.<br>
Water droplets act as prisms that disperse light into colors.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 4 (Calculation):</b><br>
Frequency = 6 × 10¹⁴ Hz, wavelength = 5 × 10⁻⁷ m<br>
Speed = v = fλ = 3 × 10⁸ m/s
</div>

---

<h3> CONTINUOUS EXAM-STYLE QUESTION</h3>

<div class="example-box">
A light wave travels in vacuum with frequency 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz.<br><br>

(a) Find its wavelength<br>
(b) State whether light needs a medium<br>
(c) Explain why light slows down in glass<br><br>

Solution:<br>
(a) λ = v/f = (3 × 10⁸) / (5 × 10¹⁴) = 6 × 10⁻⁷ m<br>
(b) No, light does not need a medium<br>
(c) Light slows down because it interacts with particles in glass
</div>

---

<h3> KEY FACTS</h3>

<div class="keyfact">
 Light is an electromagnetic wave<br>
 It travels fastest in vacuum<br>
 It slows down in denser media<br>
 It carries energy without needing matter<br>
 It is responsible for vision
</div>

---

<h3> PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
1. Why is light called a transverse wave?<br>
2. What happens when light enters glass?<br>
3. Calculate speed if f = 4 × 10¹⁴ Hz and λ = 7.5 × 10⁻⁷ m<br>
4. What causes dispersion of light?<br>
5. Why is space dark despite sunlight?
</div>

', 'Canonical lesson notes for Light waves', 15),
  (374, 374, '<h2>Reflection & Refraction (Full Explanation)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
When waves (light or sound) meet a boundary between two media, they interact in different ways depending on the surface and medium change.
</p>

<p>
They may either bounce back into the same medium or change direction as they enter a new medium due to a change in speed.
</p>

---

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Reflection:</b> The bouncing back of a wave when it hits a surface.</li>
<li><b>Refraction:</b> The bending of a wave when it passes from one medium to another due to change in speed.</li>
<li>Reflection occurs on smooth or hard surfaces like mirrors and walls.</li>
<li>Refraction occurs when waves pass between media such as air, water, and glass.</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> LAWS OF REFLECTION</h3>

<div class="example-box">
1. The angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection (i = r)
</div>

<div class="example-box">
2. The incident ray, reflected ray, and normal all lie in the same plane
</div>

---

<h3> KEY CONCEPT</h3>

<div class="example-box">
Refraction happens because the <b>speed of a wave changes</b> when it enters a different medium.
</div>

---

<h3> REAL-LIFE APPLICATIONS</h3>

<ul>
<li>Mirrors use reflection to form images</li>
<li>Eyeglasses use refraction to correct vision</li>
<li>Swimming pools look shallower due to refraction</li>
<li>Mirages in deserts are caused by refraction of light in hot air layers</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> CONCEPT CLARITY QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q1: Why does light reflect from a mirror?</b><br>
Because the smooth surface causes waves to bounce back regularly.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q2: Why does a straw look bent in water?</b><br>
Because light changes speed when moving from air to water.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q3: What causes refraction?</b><br>
Change in wave speed when entering a different medium.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q4: Does reflection change speed of light?</b><br>
No, reflection occurs in the same medium so speed remains constant.
</div>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 1:</b><br>
Light hits a mirror at 30°.<br>
Angle of reflection = 30° (by law of reflection)
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 2:</b><br>
A spoon in water appears bent.<br>
Explanation: light bends as it moves from water to air.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 3:</b><br>
Swimming pool appears shallow.<br>
Explanation: light rays bend away from normal when leaving water.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 4:</b><br>
Light enters glass from air and slows down.<br>
This causes it to bend towards the normal.
</div>

---

<h3> CONTINUOUS EXAM-STYLE QUESTION</h3>

<div class="example-box">
A ray of light strikes a plane mirror at an angle of 45°.<br><br>

(a) State the angle of reflection<br>
(b) Name the law used<br>
(c) Explain why refraction occurs in water<br><br>

Solution:<br>
(a) 45°<br>
(b) Law of reflection (i = r)<br>
(c) Refraction occurs due to change in speed of light when entering water
</div>

---

<h3> KEY FACTS</h3>

<div class="keyfact">
 Reflection = bouncing back of waves<br>
 Refraction = bending due to speed change<br>
 i = r in reflection<br>
 Refraction depends on medium density<br>
 Light bends towards normal in denser medium
</div>

---

<h3> PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
1. State the laws of reflection<br>
2. Why does light bend when entering glass?<br>
3. A ray hits a mirror at 60°. Find angle of reflection<br>
4. Give two real-life examples of refraction<br>
5. Explain why a stick in water looks bent
</div>

', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reflection & refraction', 15),
  (375, 375, '<h2>Diffraction & Interference (Full Explanation)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Waves do not always travel in straight lines only. They can spread out, bend around obstacles, and interact with other waves when they meet.
</p>

<p>
These behaviors help explain many real-life wave effects such as sound bending around corners and patterns formed by overlapping waves.
</p>

---

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Diffraction:</b> The spreading of waves when they pass through a narrow gap or around an obstacle.</li>
<li>Diffraction is more noticeable when the gap is similar in size to the wavelength.</li>
<li>Smaller gap → greater spreading</li>
<li><b>Interference:</b> The meeting and superposition of two or more waves.</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> TYPES OF INTERFERENCE</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Constructive Interference:</b> When waves meet in phase and combine to form a larger amplitude (louder/stronger wave).
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Destructive Interference:</b> When waves meet out of phase and cancel each other partially or completely.
</div>

---

<h3> KEY CONDITIONS</h3>

<ul>
<li>Diffraction is strongest when gap size ≈ wavelength</li>
<li>Constructive interference occurs when crest meets crest or trough meets trough</li>
<li>Destructive interference occurs when crest meets trough</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> CONCEPT CLARITY QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q1: Why does sound bend around corners?</b><br>
Because sound waves undergo diffraction.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q2: What is interference?</b><br>
It is the combination of two or more waves meeting at the same point.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q3: What causes loud and quiet spots in a room?</b><br>
Interference between sound waves.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q4: What happens in destructive interference?</b><br>
Waves cancel each other reducing amplitude.
</div>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 1:</b><br>
Sound from a radio is heard even behind a wall.<br>
Explanation: Sound diffracts around the obstacle.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 2:</b><br>
Water waves pass through a narrow opening and spread out.<br>
This is diffraction because the gap is small.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 3:</b><br>
Two speakers produce loud and quiet regions in a hall.<br>
This is due to interference of sound waves.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 4:</b><br>
Noise-cancelling headphones produce sound waves that cancel unwanted noise.<br>
This is destructive interference.
</div>

---

<h3> CONTINUOUS EXAM-STYLE QUESTION</h3>

<div class="example-box">
Two sound waves meet at a point in a room.<br>
One wave has a crest and the other has a trough of equal amplitude.<br><br>

(a) What type of interference occurs?<br>
(b) What is the result of this interaction?<br>
(c) Give one real-life application of this effect<br><br>

Solution:<br>
(a) Destructive interference<br>
(b) Waves cancel each other producing reduced or zero sound<br>
(c) Noise-cancelling headphones
</div>

---

<h3> KEY FACTS</h3>

<div class="keyfact">
 Diffraction = bending/spreading of waves<br>
 Interference = overlap of waves<br>
 Constructive = louder/stronger wave<br>
 Destructive = weaker or cancelled wave<br>
 Wave behavior depends on wavelength and obstacles
</div>

---

<h3> PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
1. Define diffraction<br>
2. What condition increases diffraction?<br>
3. Explain constructive interference<br>
4. Why do we hear sound behind walls?<br>
5. Give two examples of interference in real life
</div>

', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diffraction & Interference', 15),
  (376, 376, '<h2>Current & Voltage (Full Explanation)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Electric current and voltage are fundamental concepts in electricity. They explain how electric charges move and what causes them to move in a circuit.
</p>

<p>
Electricity is caused by the movement of tiny charged particles called <b>electrons</b> through a conductor such as a metal wire.
</p>

---

<h3> WELL EXPLAINED NOTES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Electric Current (I):</b> The rate of flow of electric charge through a conductor.</li>
<li><b>Voltage (V):</b> The electrical energy supplied per unit charge to move electrons in a circuit.</li>
<li>Current flows from higher potential to lower potential in a circuit.</li>
<li>Electrons actually flow in the opposite direction (from negative to positive terminal).</li>
<li>Unit of current = Ampere (A)</li>
<li>Unit of voltage = Volt (V)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>

<div class="example-box">
Voltage is the "push" that drives electric charges, while current is the actual flow of charges.
</div>

---

<h3> FORMULA</h3>

<div class="formula">
I = Q / t
</div>

Where:
<ul>
<li>I = current (A)</li>
<li>Q = charge (Coulombs)</li>
<li>t = time (seconds)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> CONCEPT CLARITY QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q1: What is electric current?</b><br>
It is the rate at which electric charge flows in a circuit.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q2: What is voltage?</b><br>
It is the energy supplied per unit charge to move electrons.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q3: What causes current to flow?</b><br>
A difference in voltage (potential difference).
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q4: What is the difference between current and voltage?</b><br>
Current is flow of charge, voltage is energy that causes the flow.
</div>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 1:</b><br>
Charge = 10C, Time = 2s<br>
I = Q/t = 10/2 = 5A
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 2:</b><br>
Charge = 20C, Time = 4s<br>
I = 20/4 = 5A
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 3:</b><br>
Charge = 30C, Time = 6s<br>
I = 30/6 = 5A
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 4 (Conceptual):</b><br>
If voltage increases in a circuit (resistance constant), current increases because more energy is supplied to move electrons.
</div>

---

<h3> CONTINUOUS EXAM-STYLE QUESTION</h3>

<div class="example-box">
A charge of 24C flows through a wire in 6 seconds.<br><br>

(a) Calculate the current<br>
(b) Define voltage<br>
(c) Explain what happens to current if voltage increases<br><br>

Solution:<br>
(a) I = Q/t = 24/6 = 4A<br>
(b) Voltage is energy supplied per unit charge<br>
(c) Current increases because more energy is available to push electrons
</div>

---

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>

<div class="keyfact">
 Confusing current with voltage<br>
 Thinking electrons flow in direction of current<br>
 Assuming high voltage always means high current (depends on resistance)
</div>

---

<h3> KEY FACTS</h3>

<div class="keyfact">
 Current = flow of charge<br>
 Voltage = energy per charge<br>
 Voltage is the cause, current is the effect<br>
 I = Q/t is the main formula<br>
 Higher voltage increases potential for current flow
</div>

---

<h3> PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
1. Define electric current<br>
2. What is the unit of voltage?<br>
3. A charge of 18C flows in 3s. Find current.<br>
4. Explain the role of voltage in a circuit<br>
5. What is the difference between current and voltage?
</div>

', 'Canonical lesson notes for Current & Voltage', 15),
  (377, 377, '<h2>Series & Parallel Circuits (Full Explanation)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Electric circuits can be connected in different ways. The arrangement of components affects how current and voltage behave in the circuit.
</p>

<p>
The two main types of circuits are series circuits and parallel circuits.
</p>

---

<h3> SERIES CIRCUITS</h3>

<ul>
<li>Components are connected end-to-end in a single path</li>
<li>Current is the same at all points in the circuit</li>
<li>Voltage is shared between components</li>
<li>If one component breaks, the whole circuit stops working</li>
</ul>

<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 400 100" width="400" height="100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#12122a;border-radius:8px;">
  <!-- Wire top -->
  <line x1="40" y1="30" x2="380" y2="30" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- Wire bottom -->
  <line x1="40" y1="70" x2="380" y2="70" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- Battery left side -->
  <line x1="40" y1="30" x2="40" y2="70" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="2"/>
  <line x1="30" y1="38" x2="50" y2="38" stroke="#f39c12" stroke-width="3"/>
  <line x1="35" y1="45" x2="45" y2="45" stroke="#f39c12" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="20" y="60" fill="#f39c12" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif">+</text>
  <text x="42" y="60" fill="#f39c12" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif">-</text>
  <text x="25" y="88" fill="#aaa" font-size="9" font-family="sans-serif">Battery</text>
  <!-- R1 -->
  <rect x="90" y="22" width="60" height="16" fill="#1a3a6a" stroke="#3498db" stroke-width="1.5" rx="3"/>
  <text x="120" y="34" fill="#3498db" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">R1</text>
  <!-- R2 -->
  <rect x="180" y="22" width="60" height="16" fill="#1a3a6a" stroke="#3498db" stroke-width="1.5" rx="3"/>
  <text x="210" y="34" fill="#3498db" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">R2</text>
  <!-- R3 -->
  <rect x="270" y="22" width="60" height="16" fill="#1a3a6a" stroke="#3498db" stroke-width="1.5" rx="3"/>
  <text x="300" y="34" fill="#3498db" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">R3</text>
  <!-- Right close -->
  <line x1="380" y1="30" x2="380" y2="70" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- Title -->
  <text x="200" y="90" fill="#aaa" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">SERIES CIRCUIT — One path for current</text>
</svg>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Key Idea:</b> One path for current flow only
</div>

---

<h3> PARALLEL CIRCUITS</h3>

<ul>
<li>Components are connected in separate branches</li>
<li>Voltage across each branch is the same</li>
<li>Current splits between branches</li>
<li>If one component fails, others still work</li>
</ul>

<div style="text-align:center;margin:1rem 0;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 380 160" width="380" height="160" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#12122a;border-radius:8px;">
  <!-- Battery -->
  <line x1="40" y1="20" x2="40" y2="140" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="2"/>
  <line x1="28" y1="60" x2="52" y2="60" stroke="#f39c12" stroke-width="3"/>
  <line x1="33" y1="72" x2="47" y2="72" stroke="#f39c12" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="18" y="68" fill="#f39c12" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif">+</text>
  <text x="44" y="82" fill="#f39c12" font-size="10" font-family="sans-serif">-</text>
  <text x="18" y="150" fill="#aaa" font-size="9" font-family="sans-serif">Battery</text>
  <!-- Top bus wire -->
  <line x1="40" y1="20" x2="340" y2="20" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- Bottom bus wire -->
  <line x1="40" y1="140" x2="340" y2="140" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- Right close -->
  <line x1="340" y1="20" x2="340" y2="140" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="2"/>
  <!-- Branch R1 -->
  <line x1="120" y1="20" x2="120" y2="45" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <rect x="98" y="45" width="44" height="18" fill="#1a3a6a" stroke="#e74c3c" stroke-width="1.5" rx="3"/>
  <text x="120" y="58" fill="#e74c3c" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">R1</text>
  <line x1="120" y1="63" x2="120" y2="140" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <!-- Branch R2 -->
  <line x1="200" y1="20" x2="200" y2="61" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <rect x="178" y="61" width="44" height="18" fill="#1a3a6a" stroke="#9b59b6" stroke-width="1.5" rx="3"/>
  <text x="200" y="74" fill="#9b59b6" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">R2</text>
  <line x1="200" y1="79" x2="200" y2="140" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <!-- Branch R3 -->
  <line x1="280" y1="20" x2="280" y2="77" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <rect x="258" y="77" width="44" height="18" fill="#1a3a6a" stroke="#f39c12" stroke-width="1.5" rx="3"/>
  <text x="280" y="90" fill="#f39c12" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace">R3</text>
  <line x1="280" y1="95" x2="280" y2="140" stroke="#7cfc00" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <!-- Title -->
  <text x="190" y="158" fill="#aaa" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">PARALLEL CIRCUIT — Multiple paths for current</text>
</svg>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Key Idea:</b> Multiple paths for current flow
</div>

---

<h3> COMPARISON SUMMARY</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Series:</b> same current, shared voltage, one path</li>
<li><b>Parallel:</b> same voltage, split current, multiple paths</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> REAL-LIFE APPLICATIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
Series: Old Christmas lights (if one fails, all go off)
</div>

<div class="example-box">
Parallel: Household wiring (each appliance works independently)
</div>

<div class="example-box">
Parallel: Car electrical systems
</div>

---

<h3> CONCEPT CLARITY QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q1: Why is current the same in a series circuit?</b><br>
Because there is only one path for charge flow.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q2: Why do parallel circuits work independently?</b><br>
Because each branch has its own path for current.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q3: What happens if one bulb breaks in series?</b><br>
The entire circuit stops working.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q4: What happens to current in a parallel circuit?</b><br>
It splits between branches.
</div>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 1:</b><br>
Two bulbs in series share a battery.<br>
Each bulb receives less voltage → bulbs are dimmer.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 2:</b><br>
Two bulbs in parallel each receive full voltage.<br>
Both bulbs shine brightly.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 3:</b><br>
House wiring uses parallel circuits so that turning off one device does not affect others.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 4 (Current behavior):</b><br>
In a parallel circuit, total current = sum of currents in each branch.
</div>

---

<h3> CONTINUOUS EXAM-STYLE QUESTION</h3>

<div class="example-box">
A circuit has two bulbs connected in series to a battery.<br><br>

(a) What happens if one bulb breaks?<br>
(b) State one disadvantage of series circuits<br>
(c) Why are household circuits connected in parallel?<br><br>

Solution:<br>
(a) The whole circuit stops working<br>
(b) Voltage is shared making bulbs dimmer<br>
(c) So each appliance works independently
</div>

---

<h3> KEY FACTS</h3>

<div class="keyfact">
 Series = one path, same current, shared voltage<br>
 Parallel = many paths, same voltage, split current<br>
 Homes use parallel circuits<br>
 Failure in series stops everything<br>
 Parallel circuits are more reliable
</div>

---

<h3> PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
1. Define series circuit<br>
2. Define parallel circuit<br>
3. Why do bulbs glow brighter in parallel?<br>
4. What happens when one component fails in series?<br>
5. State two advantages of parallel circuits
</div>

', 'Canonical lesson notes for Series & Parallel Circuits', 15),
  (378, 378, '<h2>Magnetic Fields & Magnetism (Full Explanation)</h2>
<h3> FOUNDATION EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
A magnetic field is the region around a magnet or current-carrying conductor where magnetic forces can be experienced.
It is invisible but can be represented using magnetic field lines.
</p>

---
<h3> MAGNETIC FIELD LINES</h3>

<ul>
<li>Field lines show the direction of magnetic force</li>
<li>They flow from <b>North pole → South pole</b> outside the magnet</li>
<li>Inside the magnet, they flow from South → North</li>
<li>Closer field lines = stronger magnetic field</li>
<li>Field lines never cross each other</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> MAGNETIC DOMAINS (DIPOLES)</h3>

<p>
Magnetic materials contain tiny regions called <b>domains</b>.
Each domain acts like a small magnet (dipole).
</p>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Unmagnetised material:</b> domains are randomly arranged → no overall magnetism
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Magnetised material:</b> domains are aligned in one direction → strong magnet
</div>

---

<h3> HOW TO MAGNETISE OBJECTS (3 METHODS)</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>1. Stroking Method:</b><br>
Rub a magnet repeatedly in one direction along a material (like iron).
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>2. Electrical Method:</b><br>
Pass electric current through a coil (solenoid) around a metal core.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>3. Induction Method:</b><br>
Place a magnetic material near a strong magnet → it becomes temporarily magnetised.
</div>

---

<h3> DEMAGNETISATION (LOSS OF MAGNETISM)</h3>

<ul>
<li>Heating the magnet</li>
<li>Hammering or dropping it</li>
<li>Applying alternating current (AC)</li>
</ul>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Prevention:</b> Avoid heat, shock, and strong opposing magnetic fields
</div>

---

<h3> POLARITY TESTING</h3>

<div class="example-box">
A known pole of a magnet is brought near an unknown pole.
If repulsion occurs → same pole is identified.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Key rule:</b> Like poles repel, unlike poles attract
</div>

---

<h3> WHY ATTRACTION IS NOT A RELIABLE TEST</h3>

<div class="example-box">
Attraction is NOT a valid test for polarity because both poles (N and S) attract an unknown pole.
Only repulsion confirms the same type of pole.
</div>

---

<h3> CONCEPT CLARITY QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q1: What do magnetic field lines show?</b><br>
They show direction and strength of magnetic force.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q2: What are magnetic domains?</b><br>
Tiny regions in a material that behave like small magnets.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q3: Why does stroking magnetise a material?</b><br>
It aligns the domains in one direction.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q4: Why is attraction not enough to test polarity?</b><br>
Because both poles attract, so it is not conclusive.
</div>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 1:</b><br>
Iron filings around a magnet arrange in curved patterns → showing field lines.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 2:</b><br>
A steel bar is stroked with a magnet → domains align → bar becomes magnetised.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 3:</b><br>
A magnet dropped repeatedly loses strength → demagnetisation due to disturbance of domains.
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 4:</b><br>
A compass needle aligns with Earth''s magnetic field → showing Earth acts like a giant magnet.
</div>

---

<h3> CONTINUOUS EXAM-STYLE QUESTION</h3>

<div class="example-box">
A steel rod is magnetised using a bar magnet.<br><br>

(a) Explain how magnetisation occurs<br>
(b) What happens to domains in the process?<br>
(c) Why is repulsion used to test polarity?<br><br>

Solution:<br>
(a) Stroking aligns magnetic domains in one direction<br>
(b) Domains become ordered and point the same way<br>
(c) Because attraction occurs for both poles, but repulsion confirms same pole
</div>

---

<h3> KEY FACTS</h3>

<div class="keyfact">
 Magnetic field is invisible but real<br>
 Field lines go N → S outside magnet<br>
 Magnetism depends on domain alignment<br>
 Repulsion is the ONLY sure test of polarity<br>
 Heat, shock, and AC destroy magnetism
</div>

---

<h3> PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h3>

<div class="example-box">
1. What are magnetic domains?<br>
2. State 3 methods of magnetisation<br>
3. Why does heating destroy magnetism?<br>
4. Why is repulsion a better test than attraction?<br>
5. Describe how stroking produces a magnet
</div>

', 'Canonical lesson notes for Magnetic fields', 15),
  (379, 379, '<h2> Electromagnetism (Full Structured Master Notes)</h2>
<h3> WELL ARRANGED NOTES</h3>

<p>
Electromagnetism is the study of the relationship between electricity and magnetism. 
Whenever electric current flows through a conductor, it produces a magnetic field around it.
</p>

<p>
This discovery shows that electricity and magnetism are connected and form a single force called electromagnetism.
</p>

<h4> MAGNETIC FIELD AROUND A WIRE</h4>
<ul>
<li>A straight wire carrying current produces circular magnetic field lines</li>
<li>The direction is determined by the right-hand grip rule</li>
<li>Reversing current reverses magnetic field direction</li>
</ul>

<h4> SOLENOID (COIL OF WIRE)</h4>
<ul>
<li>A solenoid is a long coil of wire</li>
<li>It produces a strong and uniform magnetic field inside</li>
<li>It behaves like a bar magnet with north and south poles</li>
</ul>

<h4> ELECTROMAGNET</h4>
<ul>
<li>An electromagnet is a temporary magnet created using electric current</li>
<li>It becomes magnetic only when current flows</li>
<li>It uses a soft iron core to increase strength</li>
</ul>

<h4> FACTORS AFFECTING STRENGTH</h4>
<ul>
<li>Current: more current → stronger field</li>
<li>Number of turns: more turns → stronger field</li>
<li>Core material: soft iron increases strength</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES (STEP BY STEP)</h3>

<h4> Example 1: Wire magnetic field</h4>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Current flows in straight wire</li>
<li>Step 2: Magnetic field forms around wire</li>
<li>Step 3: Field lines are circular</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A circular magnetic field is produced around the wire.</p>

<h4> Example 2: Solenoid strength</h4>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Coil has many turns</li>
<li>Step 2: Fields from each turn combine</li>
<li>Step 3: Strong magnetic field forms</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A solenoid produces a stronger magnetic field than a single wire.</p>

<h4> Example 3: Electromagnet in crane</h4>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Current is switched on</li>
<li>Step 2: Magnet attracts iron scrap</li>
<li>Step 3: Current is switched off</li>
<li>Step 4: Scrap is released</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The electromagnet can pick and release metals using current control.</p>

---

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing electromagnets with permanent magnets</li>
<li>Thinking magnetism exists without current in electromagnets</li>
<li>Forgetting that field direction depends on current direction</li>
<li>Assuming one wire is stronger than a coil (it is weaker)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Electric cranes in scrapyards</li>
<li>Electric bells and buzzers</li>
<li>Speakers and headphones</li>
<li>Electric motors</li>
<li>Magnetic relays in circuits</li>
</ul>

<h3> WORKED CALCULATION-STYLE QUESTIONS</h3>

<h4> Example 1</h4>
<p>A coil has 10 turns. If turns are increased to 20, what happens?</p>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Compare turns</li>
<li>Step 2: 20 > 10</li>
<li>Step 3: Magnetic field increases</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Magnetic field becomes stronger.</p>

<h4> Example 2</h4>
<p>Current in a coil is increased from 2A to 4A.</p>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Current doubles</li>
<li>Step 2: Magnetic field depends on current</li>
<li>Step 3: Field strength increases</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Magnetic field increases.</p>

---

<h3> KEY FACTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Electric current produces magnetism</li>
<li>Magnetism disappears when current stops</li>
<li>Coils strengthen magnetic fields</li>
<li>Soft iron increases electromagnet strength</li>
<li>Electromagnets are controllable</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electromagnetism', 15),
  (380, 380, '<h2> Ohm’s Law</h2>
<hr>

<h3> WELL ARRANGED NOTES</h3>

<h4> Definition</h4>
<p>
Ohm’s Law states that the current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across it, provided temperature and physical conditions remain constant.
</p>

<h4> Main Law</h4>
<div class="formula">
V = IR
</div>

<ul>
<li>V = Voltage (V)</li>
<li>I = Current (A)</li>
<li>R = Resistance (Ω)</li>
</ul>

<h4> Key Idea</h4>
<ul>
<li>Increase voltage → current increases</li>
<li>Increase resistance → current decreases</li>
</ul>

<h4> Physical Meaning</h4>
<p>
Voltage pushes current, while resistance opposes it.
</p>

<hr>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 1:</b><br>
V = 12V, I = 3A<br><br>
Step 1: Use V = IR<br>
Step 2: R = V/I<br>
Step 3: R = 12/3<br>
<b>Answer: 4 Ω</b>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 2:</b><br>
V = 10V, R = 5Ω<br><br>
Step 1: Use V = IR<br>
Step 2: I = V/R<br>
Step 3: I = 10/5<br>
<b>Answer: 2 A</b>
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Example 3:</b><br>
I = 4A, R = 2Ω<br><br>
Step 1: Use V = IR<br>
Step 2: V = 4 × 2<br>
<b>Answer: 8 V</b>
</div>

<hr>

<h3> COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing current (I) with voltage (V)</li>
<li>Using wrong formula rearrangement</li>
<li>Forgetting units (Ω, A, V)</li>
<li>Assuming all materials obey Ohm’s Law</li>
<li>Ignoring temperature effect</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> REAL WORLD APPLICATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Electric heaters (control current using resistance)</li>
<li>House wiring safety design</li>
<li>Fuses and circuit breakers</li>
<li>Electronic devices (resistor circuits)</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> QUESTIONS (WITH HINTS, ANSWERS & EXPLANATIONS)</h3>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q1:</b> What is Ohm’s Law?<br>
<b>Hint:</b> relationship between V and I<br>
<b>Answer:</b> Current is directly proportional to voltage at constant temperature<br>
<b>Explanation:</b> Increasing voltage increases current if resistance is constant
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q2:</b> A voltage of 20V produces a current of 5A. Find resistance.<br>
<b>Hint:</b> use V = IR<br>
<b>Step:</b> R = V/I = 20/5<br>
<b>Answer:</b> 4 Ω<br>
<b>Explanation:</b> Resistance is opposition to current flow
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q3:</b> What happens if resistance increases?<br>
<b>Hint:</b> think current<br>
<b>Answer:</b> Current decreases<br>
<b>Explanation:</b> Higher resistance reduces flow of electrons
</div>

<div class="example-box">
<b>Q4:</b> Why is temperature important in Ohm’s Law?<br>
<b>Hint:</b> particle movement<br>
<b>Answer:</b> Because resistance changes with temperature<br>
<b>Explanation:</b> Higher temperature increases vibration of atoms, increasing resistance
</div>

<hr>

<h3> V–I GRAPH INSIGHT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Straight line → Ohmic conductor</li>
<li>Gradient = resistance</li>
<li>Curved line → non-ohmic material</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> KEY FACTS</h3>
<ul>
<li>V = IR is the core formula</li>
<li>Resistance opposes current flow</li>
<li>Current depends on voltage and resistance</li>
<li>Ohmic materials have constant resistance</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ohm’s Law', 15),
  (381, 381, '<h2> Fluids</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>
<p>
Fluids are substances that can flow and take the shape of their container. They include both liquids and gases.
Unlike solids, fluids do not have a fixed shape because their particles are free to move past each other.
</p>

<p>
Fluids are essential in everyday life: air is a gas fluid, water is a liquid fluid, and even blood is considered a fluid in the human body.
</p>

<h3> KEY CONCEPTS OF FLUIDS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Density (ρ):</b> Mass per unit volume (ρ = m/V)</li>
<li><b>Pressure (P):</b> Force per unit area (P = F/A)</li>
<li><b>Upthrust:</b> Upward force exerted by a fluid on a body</li>
<li><b>Buoyancy:</b> Ability of a fluid to support floating objects</li>
<li><b>Viscosity:</b> Resistance of a fluid to flow (honey has high viscosity)</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT FORMULAS</h3>
<pre>
Density: ρ = m / V
Pressure: P = F / A
Fluid pressure: P = ρgh
</pre>

<ul>
<li>ρ = density (kg/m³)</li>
<li>g = gravitational field strength (9.8 m/s²)</li>
<li>h = height of fluid column (m)</li>
</ul>

<h3> FLUID PRESSURE IDEA</h3>
<pre>
More depth (h) → More pressure (P)
More density (ρ) → More pressure (P)
</pre>

<h3> FLOW DIAGRAM</h3>
<pre>
Force → Pressure → Fluid motion → Buoyancy effects
</pre>

<h3> WORKED OUT CALCULATIONS</h3>

<h4>Example 1: Pressure in a fluid</h4>
<p>Calculate pressure at a depth of 5 m in water (ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 9.8 m/s²).</p>

<pre>
Step 1: Use formula P = ρgh

Step 2: Substitute values
P = 1000 × 9.8 × 5

Step 3: Multiply
P = 49000 Pa
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 49,000 Pa</p>

<h4>Example 2: Density calculation</h4>
<p>A substance has mass 2 kg and volume 0.5 m³. Find density.</p>

<pre>
Step 1: Use ρ = m / V

Step 2: Substitute values
ρ = 2 / 0.5

Step 3: Calculate
ρ = 4 kg/m³
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 4 kg/m³</p>

<h4>Example 3: Pressure from force</h4>
<p>A force of 200 N acts on an area of 4 m². Find pressure.</p>

<pre>
Step 1: Use P = F / A

Step 2: Substitute values
P = 200 / 4

Step 3: Calculate
P = 50 Pa
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 50 Pa</p>

<h3> ARCHIMEDES’ PRINCIPLE (BASIC IDEA)</h3>
<ul>
<li>When an object is placed in a fluid, it experiences an upward force</li>
<li>This force is equal to the weight of fluid displaced</li>
</ul>

<h3> REAL-LIFE APPLICATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ships floating in water (buoyancy)</li>
<li>Hydraulic systems (brakes, lifts)</li>
<li>Blood circulation in the human body</li>
<li>Aircraft lift due to air pressure differences</li>
<li>Swimming and diving physics</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fluids', 15),
  (382, 382, '<h2> Modern Physics</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>
<p>
Modern physics is the branch of physics that studies matter and energy at atomic and subatomic levels.
It emerged when classical physics could not explain phenomena such as blackbody radiation, atomic spectra, and radioactivity.
</p>

<p>
It focuses on the internal structure of atoms, the nucleus, and how energy is released or absorbed in very small systems.
</p>

<h3> KEY AREAS OF MODERN PHYSICS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Atomic structure:</b> electrons, nucleus, energy levels</li>
<li><b>Radioactivity:</b> spontaneous emission of radiation from unstable nuclei</li>
<li><b>Nuclear reactions:</b> changes in the nucleus (fission and fusion)</li>
<li><b>Quantum physics:</b> energy is quantized (comes in packets)</li>
</ul>

<h3> ATOMIC MODEL FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Atom
 ↓
Nucleus (protons + neutrons)
 ↓
Unstable nucleus
 ↓
Radiation emitted (α, β, γ)
 ↓
More stable atom
</pre>

<h3> TYPES OF RADIATION</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Alpha (α):</b> helium nucleus, low penetration, stopped by paper</li>
<li><b>Beta (β):</b> fast electrons, medium penetration</li>
<li><b>Gamma (γ):</b> electromagnetic waves, very high penetration</li>
</ul>

<h3> NUCLEAR REACTIONS</h3>

<h4> Nuclear Fission</h4>
<ul>
<li>Heavy nucleus splits into smaller nuclei</li>
<li>Releases large amount of energy</li>
<li>Used in nuclear power plants</li>
</ul>

<pre>
U-235 → Ba + Kr + Energy + Neutrons
</pre>

<h4> Nuclear Fusion</h4>
<ul>
<li>Two light nuclei combine</li>
<li>Produces even more energy than fission</li>
<li>Occurs in the sun and stars</li>
</ul>

<pre>
H + H → He + Energy
</pre>

<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>

<h4>Example 1: Identify radiation type</h4>
<p>A radiation is stopped by paper but strongly ionizing. What is it?</p>

<pre>
Step 1: Check penetration → low
Step 2: Check ionization → high
Step 3: Match properties
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Alpha radiation</p>

<h4>Example 2: Compare fission and fusion</h4>
<pre>
Step 1: Fission = splitting of heavy nucleus
Step 2: Fusion = combining light nuclei
Step 3: Both release energy
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Fission splits atoms, fusion joins atoms</p>

<h3> REAL-LIFE APPLICATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nuclear power plants (electricity generation)</li>
<li>Medical imaging (X-rays, PET scans)</li>
<li>Cancer radiotherapy</li>
<li>Energy production in stars (fusion)</li>
<li>Smoke detectors (radioactive sources)</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Modern Physics', 15),
  (383, 383, '<h2> Cathode Rays & Cathode Ray Tube (CRT)</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>
<p>
Cathode rays are streams of high-speed electrons emitted from the cathode (negative electrode) in a vacuum tube when a high voltage is applied.
They travel in straight lines toward the anode (positive electrode).
</p>

<p>
Because they are made of electrons, cathode rays carry a negative charge and can be influenced by electric and magnetic fields.
</p>

<p>
In a Cathode Ray Tube (CRT), electrons are accelerated through a potential difference, gaining kinetic energy which allows them to produce visible light when they strike a fluorescent screen.
</p>

<h3> PRODUCTION OF CATHODE RAYS</h3>
<ul>
<li>A high voltage (potential difference) is applied across electrodes in a vacuum tube</li>
<li>The cathode emits electrons</li>
<li>Electrons are accelerated towards the anode</li>
<li>A narrow beam of electrons (cathode ray beam) is formed</li>
</ul>

<h3> KEY PHYSICS RELATIONSHIPS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Electric potential energy converts to kinetic energy</li>
<li>Energy gained by electron: E = eV</li>
<li>Kinetic energy: KE = ½mv²</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANT FORMULAS</h3>
<pre>
Energy gained by electron:
E = eV

Kinetic energy:
KE = ½mv²

Therefore:
eV = ½mv²
</pre>

<ul>
<li>e = charge of electron = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C</li>
<li>V = potential difference (volts)</li>
<li>m = mass of electron = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg</li>
</ul>

<h3> CATHODE RAY TUBE STRUCTURE</h3>
<figure class="topic-img-container">
  <img src="/images/cathode_ray_tube.png" alt="Cathode Ray Tube Schematic Diagram" class="topic-img" />
  <figcaption>Schematic of a Cathode Ray Tube showing electron emission from cathode, anode acceleration, deflection plates, and glowing fluorescent screen.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3> WORKED OUT CALCULATIONS</h3>

<h4>Example 1: Energy gained by an electron</h4>
<p>An electron is accelerated through a potential difference of 200 V. Find the energy gained.</p>

<pre>
Step 1: Use formula E = eV

Step 2: Substitute values
E = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) × 200

Step 3: Multiply
E = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁷ J
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁷ J</p>

<h4>Example 2: Speed of electron</h4>
<p>An electron is accelerated through 100 V. Calculate its speed.</p>

<pre>
Step 1: Use eV = ½mv²

Step 2: Substitute values
(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100) = ½ × (9.11 × 10⁻³¹) × v²

Step 3: Solve energy
1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷ = 4.555 × 10⁻³¹ v²

Step 4: Rearrange
v² = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷) / (4.555 × 10⁻³¹)

Step 5: Calculate
v² ≈ 3.51 × 10¹³

Step 6: Square root
v ≈ 5.9 × 10⁶ m/s
</pre>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 5.9 × 10⁶ m/s</p>

<h3> KEY OBSERVATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Beam is visible when it hits fluorescent screen</li>
<li>Beam bends in electric field (towards positive plate)</li>
<li>Beam bends in magnetic field</li>
<li>Higher voltage → faster electrons</li>
</ul>

<h3> EXAMPLE CONCEPT QUESTIONS</h3>

<h4>Why does increasing voltage increase electron speed?</h4>
<ul>
<li>Step 1: Voltage increases energy (E = eV)</li>
<li>Step 2: More energy becomes kinetic energy</li>
<li>Step 3: Higher KE means higher speed</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Because increasing potential difference increases kinetic energy of electrons, making them move faster.</p>

<h3> REAL-LIFE APPLICATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Old CRT televisions</li>
<li>Computer monitors</li>
<li>Oscilloscopes</li>
<li>Electron beam experiments</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cathode Rays and Cathode Ray Tube', 15),
  (384, 384, '<h2> Photoelectric Emission</h2>
<h3> WELL DETAILED NOTES</h3>
<p>
Photoelectric emission is the emission of electrons from a metal surface when electromagnetic radiation (light) of sufficiently high frequency strikes it.
These emitted electrons are called <b>photoelectrons</b>.
</p>
<p>
This phenomenon provided strong evidence that light behaves as particles called <b>photons</b>, each carrying discrete energy.
</p>
<h3> KEY CONCEPTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Photon:</b> A packet of light energy</li>
<li><b>Work function (φ):</b> Minimum energy required to remove an electron from a metal surface</li>
<li><b>Threshold frequency (f₀):</b> Minimum frequency required to cause emission</li>
<li><b>Intensity:</b> Affects number of emitted electrons, not their energy</li>
</ul>
<h3> EINSTEIN’S PHOTOELECTRIC EQUATION</h3>
<pre>
E = hf
KE(max) = hf − φ
</pre>
<ul>
<li>E = photon energy</li>
<li>h = Planck’s constant (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s)</li>
<li>f = frequency of light</li>
<li>φ = work function</li>
<li>KE(max) = maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons</li>
</ul>
<h3> CONDITIONS FOR EMISSION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Light frequency must be ≥ threshold frequency</li>
<li>If f < f₀ → no electrons emitted, no matter how intense the light is</li>
</ul>
<h3> PROCESS DIAGRAM</h3>
<figure class="topic-img-container">
  <img src="/images/photoelectric_effect_diagram.png" alt="Photoelectric Effect Diagram" class="topic-img" />
  <figcaption>Illustration of Photoelectric Emission: Light photons striking a metal surface, work function barrier (φ), and emitted photoelectrons.</figcaption>
</figure>
<h3> WORKED EXAMPLES</h3>
<h4>Example 1: Photon Energy</h4>
<p>Calculate energy of a photon of frequency 5 × 10¹⁴ Hz</p>
<pre>
E = hf
E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 5 × 10¹⁴
E = 3.315 × 10⁻¹⁹ J
</pre>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 3.315 × 10⁻¹⁹ J</p>
<h4>Example 2: Photoelectron Kinetic Energy</h4>
<p>A metal has work function 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Light of energy 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is incident. Find KE(max).</p>
<pre>
KE = hf − φ
KE = 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ − 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁹
KE = 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J
</pre>
<p><b>Answer:</b> 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J</p>
<h3> REAL-LIFE APPLICATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Solar panels (conversion of light to electricity)</li>
<li>Automatic door sensors</li>
<li>Camera light detectors</li>
<li>Night vision devices</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Photoelectric Emission', 15),
  (385, 385, '
  <h2>Meaning of Chemistry</h2>

  <p><strong>Chemistry</strong> is the branch of science that studies matter,
  its composition, structure, properties and the changes it undergoes.</p>

  <h3>What Chemistry Studies</h3>

  <p>Chemistry mainly deals with:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>What substances are made of.</li>
    <li>How particles are arranged.</li>
    <li>The properties of substances.</li>
    <li>How substances react with one another.</li>
    <li>The energy changes involved in chemical reactions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>When magnesium burns in oxygen, a new substance called magnesium oxide
  is formed.</p>

  <p>Therefore chemistry can be used to investigate:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>the substances involved,</li>
    <li>what happens during the reaction,</li>
    <li>the new substance formed, and</li>
    <li>the energy released.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Importance of Chemistry</h3>

  <p>Chemistry is used in areas such as medicine, agriculture, manufacturing,
  food production, environmental protection and energy production.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Chemistry', 15),
  (386, 386, '
  <h2>Laboratory Safety Rules</h2>

  <p>Laboratory safety rules are procedures followed to prevent accidents,
  injuries and damage to equipment during practical work.</p>

  <h3>Important Rules</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Wear appropriate protective equipment such as a laboratory coat and safety goggles.</li>
    <li>Do not eat or drink in the laboratory.</li>
    <li>Do not taste chemicals.</li>
    <li>Do not smell chemicals directly.</li>
    <li>Read labels before using chemicals.</li>
    <li>Keep the working area clean.</li>
    <li>Report accidents and spills immediately.</li>
    <li>Handle glassware carefully.</li>
    <li>Follow the teacher''s or laboratory instructions.</li>
    <li>Wash hands after practical work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Safety Matters</h3>

  <p>Chemicals may be corrosive, poisonous, flammable or harmful if handled
  incorrectly. Laboratory equipment may also cause burns, cuts or other
  injuries.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Laboratory Safety Rules', 15),
  (387, 387, '
  <h2>Chemical Hazard Symbols</h2>

  <p>Hazard symbols are standard symbols placed on chemical containers to
  warn users about dangerous properties of the substance.</p>

  <h3>Common Hazards</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Flammable:</strong> the substance can easily catch fire.</li>
    <li><strong>Corrosive:</strong> the substance can damage living tissue or corrode materials.</li>
    <li><strong>Toxic:</strong> exposure can cause serious harm or death.</li>
    <li><strong>Harmful/Irritant:</strong> the substance may cause harm or irritation.</li>
    <li><strong>Oxidising:</strong> the substance can cause or intensify combustion.</li>
    <li><strong>Explosive:</strong> the substance may explode under certain conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Environmental hazard:</strong> the substance can harm the environment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Using Hazard Information</h3>

  <p>A hazard symbol tells you what danger to expect. The information should
  be considered before handling the chemical.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Hazard Symbols', 15),
  (388, 388, '
  <h2>Safe Handling of Chemicals</h2>

  <p>Chemicals must be handled according to their properties and the
  instructions provided on their labels.</p>

  <h3>Important Practices</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Read the label before use.</li>
    <li>Use the correct chemical for the experiment.</li>
    <li>Use the smallest practical quantity.</li>
    <li>Wear suitable protective equipment.</li>
    <li>Keep chemical containers closed when not in use.</li>
    <li>Never return unused chemicals to the original container unless instructed.</li>
    <li>Use a fume cupboard when instructed for substances that release harmful vapours.</li>
    <li>Wash hands after handling chemicals.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Smelling a Chemical</h3>

  <p>Never place your nose directly over a chemical container. If instructed
  to detect an odour, use the wafting method: gently move vapour towards
  your nose from a distance.</p>

  <h3>Handling Acids</h3>

  <p>Concentrated acids can be corrosive. They must be handled carefully and
  according to laboratory instructions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Safe Handling of Chemicals', 15),
  (389, 389, '
  <h2>Measuring Apparatus</h2>

  <p>Different apparatus are designed to measure different physical
  quantities accurately.</p>

  <h3>Common Measuring Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Measuring cylinder:</strong> measures liquid volume.</li>
    <li><strong>Burette:</strong> delivers accurately measured variable volumes of liquid, especially during titration.</li>
    <li><strong>Pipette:</strong> transfers a fixed, accurately measured volume of liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Balance:</strong> measures mass.</li>
    <li><strong>Thermometer:</strong> measures temperature.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Choosing Apparatus</h3>

  <p>The apparatus selected depends on the quantity being measured and the
  required accuracy.</p>

  <p>For example, a measuring cylinder is suitable for ordinary volume
  measurements, while a burette or pipette is used when greater accuracy is
  required in analytical work.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Apparatus', 15),
  (390, 390, '
  <h2>Heating Apparatus</h2>

  <p>Heating apparatus is used to provide heat during laboratory experiments.</p>

  <h3>Common Heating Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bunsen burner:</strong> provides a flame for heating.</li>
    <li><strong>Tripod:</strong> supports apparatus above a flame.</li>
    <li><strong>Wire gauze:</strong> supports glassware and distributes heat.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaporating dish:</strong> holds a solution during evaporation.</li>
    <li><strong>Crucible:</strong> used for strongly heating small amounts of solid substances.</li>
    <li><strong>Test tube:</strong> can hold small quantities of substances for heating.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Using a Bunsen Burner</h3>

  <p>The air hole affects the flame. A properly adjusted burner produces a
  suitable flame for laboratory heating.</p>

  <p>Glassware should be heated carefully to avoid thermal shock and breakage.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heating Apparatus', 15),
  (391, 391, '
  <h2>Separation Apparatus</h2>

  <p>Different mixtures require different apparatus depending on the
  physical properties of their components.</p>

  <h3>Common Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filter funnel:</strong> supports filter paper during filtration.</li>
    <li><strong>Filter paper:</strong> separates an insoluble solid from a liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaporating dish:</strong> allows a solvent to evaporate from a solution.</li>
    <li><strong>Distillation flask:</strong> holds the mixture being distilled.</li>
    <li><strong>Condenser:</strong> cools vapour so that it changes back into a liquid.</li>
    <li><strong>Separating funnel:</strong> separates immiscible liquids.</li>
    <li><strong>Chromatography paper:</strong> provides a stationary surface for chromatography.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Choosing Apparatus</h3>

  <p>The separation method depends on differences in properties such as
  particle size, boiling point, solubility and density.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Separation Apparatus', 15),
  (392, 392, '
  <h2>Apparatus Used in Titration</h2>

  <p>Titration is a laboratory method used to determine the volume of one
  solution required to react completely with a measured volume of another
  solution.</p>

  <h3>Main Apparatus</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Burette:</strong> delivers and measures a variable volume of solution accurately.</li>
    <li><strong>Pipette:</strong> transfers a fixed measured volume of solution.</li>
    <li><strong>Conical flask:</strong> contains the solution being titrated.</li>
    <li><strong>Funnel:</strong> helps fill the burette, but should normally be removed before taking the final burette reading.</li>
    <li><strong>White tile:</strong> makes the colour change easier to observe.</li>
    <li><strong>Indicator:</strong> shows the end point by a colour change.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Basic Arrangement</h3>

  <p>The burette is supported vertically above the conical flask. A measured
  volume of solution is placed in the flask, usually using a pipette.</p>

  <p>The burette tap controls the solution delivered into the flask.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Apparatus Used in Titration', 15),
  (393, 393, '
  <h2>Measuring Mass</h2>

  <p>Mass is the amount of matter in a substance. In the laboratory, mass is
  measured using a balance.</p>

  <h3>Common Units</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>gram (g)</li>
    <li>kilogram (kg)</li>
    <li>milligram (mg)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Useful Conversions</h3>

  <p>1 kg = 1000 g</p>
  <p>1 g = 1000 mg</p>

  <h3>Measuring a Solid</h3>

  <p>If a container is used, its mass may be measured first. The container
  can then be tared or its mass subtracted from the combined mass.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Mass of container = 25.0 g</p>
  <p>Mass of container + chemical = 43.5 g</p>

  <p>Mass of chemical:</p>

  <p><strong>43.5 − 25.0 = 18.5 g</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Mass', 15),
  (394, 394, '
  <h2>Measuring Volume</h2>

  <p>Volume is the amount of space occupied by a substance.</p>

  <h3>Common Units</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>litre (L)</li>
    <li>millilitre (mL)</li>
    <li>cubic centimetre (cm³)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important Conversions</h3>

  <p>1 L = 1000 mL</p>
  <p>1 mL = 1 cm³</p>
  <p>Therefore, 1 L = 1000 cm³.</p>

  <h3>Reading Liquid Volume</h3>

  <p>For many aqueous liquids in a transparent measuring vessel, the reading
  is taken at the bottom of the meniscus when the liquid is viewed at eye
  level.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>If a measuring cylinder contains 35 mL of water, its volume is:</p>

  <p><strong>35 mL = 35 cm³</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Volume', 15),
  (395, 395, '
  <h2>Measuring Temperature</h2>

  <p>Temperature indicates how hot or cold a substance is. In the laboratory,
  temperature is commonly measured using a thermometer or temperature
  sensor.</p>

  <h3>Common Unit</h3>

  <p>The degree Celsius (°C) is commonly used in school laboratory work.</p>

  <h3>Using a Thermometer</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Place the sensing part in the substance being measured.</li>
    <li>Do not allow the bulb to touch the bottom or sides of the container when this would affect the measurement.</li>
    <li>Allow the reading to become steady.</li>
    <li>Read the scale at eye level.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Temperature Change</h3>

  <p>Temperature change is calculated using:</p>

  <p><strong>Temperature change = final temperature − initial temperature</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Initial temperature = 22°C</p>
  <p>Final temperature = 35°C</p>

  <p>Temperature change:</p>

  <p><strong>35 − 22 = 13°C</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Temperature', 15),
  (396, 396, '
  <h2>Reading Measuring Instruments</h2>

  <p>Accurate measurements depend on reading an instrument correctly.</p>

  <h3>Important Rules</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Identify the scale and units before reading.</li>
    <li>Determine the value of the smallest scale division.</li>
    <li>Place your eye at the correct level where appropriate.</li>
    <li>Read the correct part of the scale.</li>
    <li>Record the measurement with suitable precision.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Finding the Scale Division</h3>

  <p>Suppose a scale increases from 10 to 20 and there are 10 equal divisions.</p>

  <p>The value of one division is:</p>

  <p><strong>(20 − 10) ÷ 10 = 1 unit</strong></p>

  <h3>Meniscus</h3>

  <p>When reading many liquid volumes, the bottom of the meniscus is used.
  The eye should be level with the meniscus to reduce parallax error.</p>

  <h3>Recording Measurements</h3>

  <p>A measurement should include its numerical value and unit, for example
  <strong>25.0 cm³</strong> rather than simply writing <strong>25</strong>.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reading Measuring Instruments', 15),
  (397, 397, '
  <h2>Meaning of Matter</h2>

  <p><strong>Matter</strong> is anything that has mass and occupies space.</p>

  <p>Examples of matter include water, air, wood, iron, oxygen and plastic.</p>

  <h3>Two Important Features of Matter</h3>

  <p><strong>1. Matter has mass</strong></p>

  <p>Mass is the amount of matter in a substance.</p>

  <p><strong>2. Matter occupies space</strong></p>

  <p>This means matter has volume.</p>

  <h3>Particles of Matter</h3>

  <p>Matter is made up of very small particles. These particles may be atoms,
  molecules or ions depending on the substance.</p>

  <p>The particles are too small to be seen directly with the naked eye.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>Water is matter because it has mass and occupies space.</p>

  <p>Air is also matter even though it cannot normally be seen because it has
  mass and occupies space.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Matter', 15),
  (398, 398, '
  <h2>Physical Properties</h2>

  <p>A <strong>physical property</strong> is a characteristic of a substance
  that can be observed or measured without changing the substance into a
  different substance.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Colour</li>
    <li>Density</li>
    <li>Melting point</li>
    <li>Boiling point</li>
    <li>Electrical conductivity</li>
    <li>Solubility</li>
    <li>Hardness</li>
    <li>State of matter</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Water has a boiling point of about 100°C at standard atmospheric
  pressure. Measuring its boiling point does not change water into a new
  chemical substance.</p>

  <p>Therefore boiling point is a physical property.</p>

  <h3>Physical Property vs Chemical Property</h3>

  <p>A physical property can be determined without changing the chemical
  identity of the substance.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Physical Properties', 15),
  (399, 399, '
  <h2>Chemical Properties</h2>

  <p>A <strong>chemical property</strong> describes the ability of a substance
  to undergo a chemical change and form new substances.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Flammability</li>
    <li>Ability to react with oxygen</li>
    <li>Ability to react with acids</li>
    <li>Ability to react with water</li>
    <li>Ability to corrode</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example: Iron</h3>

  <p>Iron can react with oxygen and water to form rust.</p>

  <p>The ability of iron to undergo this reaction is a chemical property.</p>

  <h3>Example: Magnesium</h3>

  <p>Magnesium reacts with oxygen when heated to form magnesium oxide.</p>

  <p>Its ability to react with oxygen is therefore a chemical property.</p>

  <h3>Key Difference</h3>

  <p>A physical property can be observed without forming a new substance.
  A chemical property describes how a substance behaves when it undergoes a
  chemical change.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Properties', 15),
  (400, 400, '
  <h2>Solids</h2>

  <p>A solid is a state of matter in which particles are closely packed and
  held in relatively fixed positions.</p>

  <h3>Particle Arrangement</h3>

  <p>The particles in a solid are arranged very close together. There is
  little empty space between neighbouring particles.</p>

  <h3>Particle Movement</h3>

  <p>Particles in a solid cannot move freely from place to place. They
  vibrate about fixed positions.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Solids</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>They have a fixed shape.</li>
    <li>They have a fixed volume.</li>
    <li>They are difficult to compress.</li>
    <li>They usually have relatively high density compared with gases.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>Iron, copper, salt, ice and wood are examples of solids.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solids', 15),
  (401, 401, '
  <h2>Liquids</h2>

  <p>A liquid is a state of matter whose particles are close together but
  are able to move past one another.</p>

  <h3>Particle Arrangement</h3>

  <p>Liquid particles are relatively close together, but their arrangement
  is less ordered than that of particles in a solid.</p>

  <h3>Particle Movement</h3>

  <p>The particles can move and slide past one another.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Liquids</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>They have a fixed volume.</li>
    <li>They do not have a fixed shape.</li>
    <li>They take the shape of their container.</li>
    <li>They are difficult to compress.</li>
    <li>They can flow.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>Water, ethanol, cooking oil and mercury are liquids at suitable
  temperatures.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Liquids', 15),
  (402, 402, '
  <h2>Gases</h2>

  <p>A gas is a state of matter in which particles are far apart and move
  freely in all directions.</p>

  <h3>Particle Arrangement</h3>

  <p>Gas particles are widely separated with large amounts of empty space
  between them.</p>

  <h3>Particle Movement</h3>

  <p>The particles move rapidly and randomly in all directions.</p>

  <h3>Properties of Gases</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>They have no fixed shape.</li>
    <li>They have no fixed volume.</li>
    <li>They fill their containers.</li>
    <li>They are easily compressed.</li>
    <li>They can flow.</li>
    <li>They exert pressure on the walls of their containers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>Oxygen, nitrogen, carbon dioxide and water vapour are examples of gases
  under suitable conditions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gases', 15),
  (403, 403, '
  <h2>Particle Arrangement</h2>

  <p>The particle model explains the different states of matter by considering
  how closely particles are packed and how they are arranged.</p>

  <h3>Solid</h3>

  <p>Particles are very close together and arranged in relatively fixed
  positions.</p>

  <h3>Liquid</h3>

  <p>Particles remain close together but are less ordered and can move past
  one another.</p>

  <h3>Gas</h3>

  <p>Particles are far apart and arranged randomly.</p>

  <h3>Comparison</h3>

  <p><strong>Solid →</strong> closest and most ordered.</p>
  <p><strong>Liquid →</strong> close but less ordered.</p>
  <p><strong>Gas →</strong> far apart and random.</p>

  <p>The arrangement of particles helps explain properties such as shape,
  volume and compressibility.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Particle Arrangement', 15),
  (404, 404, '
  <h2>Particle Movement</h2>

  <p>Particles in matter are continuously moving. The type and freedom of
  movement depend on the state of matter.</p>

  <h3>Solids</h3>

  <p>Particles vibrate about fixed positions.</p>

  <h3>Liquids</h3>

  <p>Particles move around and slide past one another.</p>

  <h3>Gases</h3>

  <p>Particles move rapidly and randomly in all directions.</p>

  <h3>Diffusion</h3>

  <p><strong>Diffusion</strong> is the net movement of particles from a region
  of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration due to random
  particle motion.</p>

  <p>Diffusion is fastest in gases because gas particles move rapidly and
  have large spaces between them.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>If a small amount of perfume is released in one part of a room, its
  particles gradually spread throughout the room.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Particle Movement', 15),
  (405, 405, '
  <h2>Effect of Temperature on Particles</h2>

  <p>Temperature affects the kinetic energy and movement of particles.</p>

  <h3>Increasing Temperature</h3>

  <p>When a substance is heated, its particles gain kinetic energy.</p>

  <p>As a result:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Particles move faster.</li>
    <li>Collisions may become more frequent and energetic.</li>
    <li>Diffusion generally becomes faster.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Cooling</h3>

  <p>When a substance is cooled, its particles lose kinetic energy and move
  more slowly.</p>

  <h3>Example: Diffusion</h3>

  <p>Food colouring spreads through warm water faster than through cold water
  because the particles have greater kinetic energy at the higher
  temperature.</p>

  <h3>Important Point</h3>

  <p>Increasing temperature does not create new particles. It increases the
  average kinetic energy of the particles already present.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Temperature on Particles', 15),
  (406, 406, '
  <h2>Melting</h2>

  <p><strong>Melting</strong> is the change of state in which a substance
  changes from a <strong>solid to a liquid</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>Solid → Liquid</strong></p>

  <h3>What Happens to the Particles?</h3>

  <p>When a solid is heated, its particles gain kinetic energy and vibrate
  more strongly about their fixed positions.</p>

  <p>At the melting point, the particles have enough energy to overcome
  enough of the forces holding them in their fixed arrangement. They can
  then move past one another as a liquid.</p>

  <h3>Melting Point</h3>

  <p>The <strong>melting point</strong> is the temperature at which a pure
  substance changes from solid to liquid at a stated pressure.</p>

  <p>For example, pure ice melts at approximately <strong>0°C</strong> at
  standard atmospheric pressure.</p>

  <h3>Important Point</h3>

  <p>Melting is a <strong>physical change</strong>. The substance itself
  remains chemically the same.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>Ice → liquid water</strong></p>

  <p>Both are H₂O.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Melting', 15),
  (407, 407, '
  <h2>Freezing</h2>

  <p><strong>Freezing</strong> is the change of state in which a substance
  changes from a <strong>liquid to a solid</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>Liquid → Solid</strong></p>

  <h3>What Happens to the Particles?</h3>

  <p>When a liquid is cooled, its particles lose kinetic energy and move
  more slowly.</p>

  <p>As cooling continues, the particles become increasingly restricted
  until they become arranged in fixed positions in the solid.</p>

  <h3>Freezing Point</h3>

  <p>The <strong>freezing point</strong> is the temperature at which a pure
  substance changes from liquid to solid at a stated pressure.</p>

  <p>Pure water freezes at approximately <strong>0°C</strong> at standard
  atmospheric pressure.</p>

  <h3>Freezing Is a Physical Change</h3>

  <p>Freezing does not produce a new chemical substance.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>Liquid water → ice</strong></p>

  <p>Both are H₂O.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Freezing', 15),
  (408, 408, '
  <h2>Evaporation</h2>

  <p><strong>Evaporation</strong> is the change of state in which particles
  escape from the <strong>surface of a liquid</strong> and enter the gas
  state.</p>

  <p><strong>Liquid → Gas</strong></p>

  <h3>How Evaporation Happens</h3>

  <p>Particles in a liquid have different amounts of kinetic energy.</p>

  <p>Some particles at the surface have enough energy to overcome the
  attractive forces holding them in the liquid. These particles escape into
  the gas state.</p>

  <h3>Important Features</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Evaporation occurs at the surface.</li>
    <li>It can occur below the boiling point.</li>
    <li>It can occur at different temperatures.</li>
    <li>It becomes faster when temperature increases.</li>
    <li>It becomes faster when surface area increases.</li>
    <li>Moving air can increase the rate of evaporation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Cooling by Evaporation</h3>

  <p>The particles that escape tend to be the faster-moving, higher-energy
  particles. The particles remaining in the liquid therefore have a lower
  average kinetic energy, causing cooling.</p>
  
<hr>
<h2>Evaporation</h2>

  <p><strong>Evaporation</strong> is used to separate a dissolved solid from a solution by removing the solvent as vapour.</p>

  <h3>Principle</h3>
  <p>The solvent is volatile and changes from liquid to gas, leaving the dissolved solid behind.</p>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Place the solution in an evaporating basin.</li>
    <li>Heat the solution gently.</li>
    <li>The solvent changes into vapour.</li>
    <li>The dissolved solid remains behind.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Common salt can be obtained from salt solution by evaporating the water.</p>

  <h3>Important limitation</h3>
  <p>Evaporation is suitable when the <strong>solid is required</strong>. If the liquid solvent is also required, distillation is more appropriate.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evaporation', 15),
  (409, 409, '
  <h2>Boiling</h2>

  <p><strong>Boiling</strong> is the rapid change of a liquid to a gas that
  occurs throughout the liquid at its boiling point.</p>

  <p><strong>Liquid → Gas</strong></p>

  <h3>What Happens During Boiling?</h3>

  <p>When a liquid reaches its boiling point, vapour bubbles form throughout
  the liquid and rise to the surface.</p>

  <p>The liquid continues changing into gas as long as energy is supplied
  under the same pressure conditions.</p>

  <h3>Boiling Point</h3>

  <p>The <strong>boiling point</strong> is the temperature at which a liquid
  boils at a stated pressure.</p>

  <p>Pure water boils at approximately <strong>100°C</strong> at standard
  atmospheric pressure.</p>

  <h3>Boiling vs Evaporation</h3>

  <p><strong>Boiling:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Occurs throughout the liquid.</li>
    <li>Occurs at the boiling point for a given pressure.</li>
    <li>Vapour bubbles form throughout the liquid.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Evaporation:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Occurs at the surface.</li>
    <li>Can occur below the boiling point.</li>
    <li>Does not require bubbles throughout the liquid.</li>
  </ul>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Boiling', 15),
  (410, 410, '
  <h2>Condensation</h2>

  <p><strong>Condensation</strong> is the change of state in which a gas
  changes into a liquid.</p>

  <p><strong>Gas → Liquid</strong></p>

  <h3>What Happens to the Particles?</h3>

  <p>When a gas is cooled, its particles lose kinetic energy and move more
  slowly.</p>

  <p>As the particles lose energy, attractive forces between them become
  more effective at bringing them closer together. The gas changes into a
  liquid.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Water vapour forming droplets on a cold surface.</li>
    <li>Steam forming liquid water on a cooler surface.</li>
    <li>Water vapour in the atmosphere forming cloud droplets.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Condensation in Distillation</h3>

  <p>During distillation, vapour passes into a condenser where it is cooled
  and changed back into a liquid.</p>

  <p>Condensation is the reverse of vaporisation.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Condensation', 15),
  (411, 411, '
  <h2>Sublimation</h2>

  <p><strong>Sublimation</strong> is the change of state in which a solid
  changes directly into a gas without passing through the liquid state.</p>

  <p><strong>Solid → Gas</strong></p>

  <h3>What Happens to the Particles?</h3>

  <p>The particles in the solid gain enough energy to escape directly into
  the gas state.</p>

  <p>The liquid state is skipped.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Solid carbon dioxide (dry ice) changes directly into carbon dioxide gas.</li>
    <li>Iodine can form iodine vapour when heated.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important Point</h3>

  <p>Sublimation is a physical change. The chemical identity of the substance
  remains the same.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>Solid CO₂ → CO₂ gas</strong></p>

  <p>The substance remains carbon dioxide.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sublimation', 15),
  (412, 412, '
  <h2>Deposition</h2>

  <p><strong>Deposition</strong> is the change of state in which a gas changes
  directly into a solid without passing through the liquid state.</p>

  <p><strong>Gas → Solid</strong></p>

  <h3>What Happens to the Particles?</h3>

  <p>Gas particles lose kinetic energy as they are cooled.</p>

  <p>They move more slowly and become held in a fixed arrangement, forming
  a solid directly.</p>

  <h3>Example: Frost</h3>

  <p>Water vapour in cold air can change directly into solid ice on a very
  cold surface. This forms frost.</p>

  <h3>Relationship with Sublimation</h3>

  <p>Deposition is the reverse of sublimation.</p>

  <p><strong>Sublimation:</strong> Solid → Gas</p>

  <p><strong>Deposition:</strong> Gas → Solid</p>

  <h3>Important Point</h3>

  <p>The chemical identity of the substance remains unchanged. Therefore
  deposition is a physical change.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Deposition', 15),
  (413, 413, '<h2>Elements</h2>

  <p>An <strong>element</strong> is a pure substance made of only one type of atom.</p>

  <p>Each element is identified by its <strong>atomic number</strong>, which is the number of protons in its atoms.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Iron — Fe</li>
    <li>Copper — Cu</li>
    <li>Oxygen — O₂</li>
    <li>Hydrogen — H₂</li>
    <li>Carbon — C</li>
  </ul>

  <p>O₂ is still an element because every atom present is oxygen. The fact that two oxygen atoms are joined does not make it a compound.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p><strong>One type of atom → element.</strong></p>

  <p>Elements cannot be broken down into simpler substances by ordinary chemical reactions.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Elements', 15),
  (414, 414, '<h2>Compounds</h2>

  <p>A <strong>compound</strong> is a pure substance formed when two or more different elements are chemically combined in a fixed ratio.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Water — H₂O</li>
    <li>Carbon dioxide — CO₂</li>
    <li>Sodium chloride — NaCl</li>
    <li>Magnesium oxide — MgO</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The elements in a compound are chemically bonded. The compound has properties different from those of the elements that formed it.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Sodium is a reactive metal and chlorine is a poisonous gas. When chemically combined, they form sodium chloride, NaCl.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p><strong>Different elements + chemical combination in a fixed ratio → compound.</strong></p>

  <p>Compounds can be broken down into simpler substances by chemical methods, not ordinary physical separation.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Compounds', 15),
  (415, 415, '<h2>Mixtures</h2>

  <p>A <strong>mixture</strong> contains two or more substances that are physically combined but not chemically bonded to form a new substance.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Air</li>
    <li>Salt water</li>
    <li>Sand and water</li>
    <li>Iron filings and sulfur powder</li>
    <li>Brass</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important properties</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The substances in a mixture retain many of their own properties.</li>
    <li>The composition can vary.</li>
    <li>The components can be separated by physical methods.</li>
    <li>No new substance is formed simply by mixing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>If 5 g of salt is mixed with 100 g of water, more salt can be added without changing the identity of the water or salt. This variable composition is a feature of mixtures.</p>

  <p><strong>Mixture = substances physically combined, not chemically combined.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mixtures', 15),
  (416, 416, '<h2>Homogeneous Mixtures</h2>

  <p>A <strong>homogeneous mixture</strong> is a mixture whose composition is uniform throughout.</p>

  <p>Every part of the mixture has essentially the same composition and properties.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Salt solution</li>
    <li>Sugar solution</li>
    <li>Air</li>
    <li>Brass</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Key features</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Uniform composition throughout.</li>
    <li>Components are not normally visibly distinguishable.</li>
    <li>It is still a mixture.</li>
    <li>Its components can be separated by suitable physical methods.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>When salt dissolves completely in water, the salt particles become evenly distributed throughout the water. A sample taken from the top has the same composition as a sample taken from the bottom, assuming the solution is properly mixed.</p>

  <p><strong>Homogeneous = uniform throughout.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homogeneous Mixtures', 15),
  (417, 417, '<h2>Heterogeneous Mixtures</h2>

  <p>A <strong>heterogeneous mixture</strong> is a mixture whose composition is not uniform throughout.</p>

  <p>Different parts of the mixture may contain different amounts or types of components.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Sand and water</li>
    <li>Oil and water</li>
    <li>Iron filings and sulfur</li>
    <li>Sand and gravel</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Key features</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Composition is not uniform.</li>
    <li>Different components may be visibly distinguishable.</li>
    <li>Different portions can have different compositions.</li>
    <li>Components can usually be separated using physical methods.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>In a mixture of sand and water, the sand settles at the bottom while water remains above it. The composition is therefore not uniform throughout the sample.</p>

  <p><strong>Heterogeneous = non-uniform throughout.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heterogeneous Mixtures', 15),
  (418, 418, '<h2>Identifying Pure Substances</h2>

  <p>A <strong>pure substance</strong> contains only one type of substance and has a fixed composition.</p>

  <p>Pure substances are divided into two groups:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Elements</strong> — contain one type of atom.</li>
    <li><strong>Compounds</strong> — contain two or more different elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How to identify a pure substance</h3>

  <p>Ask:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Does it contain only one substance?</li>
    <li>Does it have a fixed composition?</li>
    <li>Does it have characteristic physical properties such as a definite melting point or boiling point?</li>
  </ol>

  <p>If the sample is a pure substance, determine whether it is an <strong>element</strong> or a <strong>compound</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Cu → pure substance → element</li>
    <li>O₂ → pure substance → element</li>
    <li>H₂O → pure substance → compound</li>
    <li>NaCl → pure substance → compound</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Pure substance → element or compound.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Pure Substances', 15),
  (419, 419, '<h2>Identifying Mixtures</h2>

  <p>A mixture contains two or more substances that are physically combined.</p>

  <h3>Classification process</h3>

  <p>When given a sample, use this sequence:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Ask whether more than one substance is present.</li>
    <li>If yes, it is a mixture.</li>
    <li>Ask whether the composition is uniform.</li>
    <li>If uniform → homogeneous mixture.</li>
    <li>If non-uniform → heterogeneous mixture.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Salt water:</strong> mixture → uniform → homogeneous.</p>

  <p><strong>Oil and water:</strong> mixture → non-uniform → heterogeneous.</p>

  <p><strong>Air:</strong> mixture → generally uniform → homogeneous.</p>

  <p><strong>Sand and water:</strong> mixture → non-uniform → heterogeneous.</p>

  <h3>Fast classification rule</h3>

  <p><strong>More than one substance?</strong></p>
  <p>Yes → mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Uniform throughout?</strong></p>
  <p>Yes → homogeneous.</p>

  <p>No → heterogeneous.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Mixtures', 15),
  (420, 420, '<h2>Filtration</h2>

  <p><strong>Filtration</strong> is used to separate an <strong>insoluble solid from a liquid</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Principle</h3>
  <p>The method depends on the difference in particle size and the fact that the solid does not dissolve in the liquid.</p>

  <h3>Apparatus</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Filter funnel</li>
    <li>Filter paper</li>
    <li>Beaker or conical flask</li>
    <li>Glass rod</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Place folded filter paper inside the funnel.</li>
    <li>Place the funnel over a clean container.</li>
    <li>Pour the mixture through the filter paper.</li>
    <li>The liquid passes through the paper.</li>
    <li>The insoluble solid remains on the paper.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Important terms</h3>
  <p><strong>Residue:</strong> solid left on the filter paper.</p>
  <p><strong>Filtrate:</strong> liquid that passes through the filter paper.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Sand and water can be separated by filtration because sand is insoluble in water.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Filtration', 15),
  (421, 421, '<h2>Crystallisation</h2>

  <p><strong>Crystallisation</strong> is used to obtain a solid in the form of crystals from a solution.</p>

  <h3>Principle</h3>
  <p>The solubility of many solids changes with temperature. A hot, concentrated solution can be cooled so that crystals form as the solubility decreases.</p>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Prepare a solution containing the dissolved solid.</li>
    <li>Heat the solution to concentrate it.</li>
    <li>Stop heating before all the solvent evaporates.</li>
    <li>Allow the concentrated solution to cool.</li>
    <li>Crystals form as the solubility decreases.</li>
    <li>Filter the crystals.</li>
    <li>Wash and dry the crystals.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Why crystallisation instead of complete evaporation?</h3>
  <p>Crystallisation can produce purer, well-formed crystals and avoids unnecessary strong heating of the solid.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Crystallisation', 15),
  (422, 422, '<h2>Simple Distillation</h2>

  <p><strong>Simple distillation</strong> separates a liquid from a solution by evaporation followed by condensation.</p>

  <h3>Principle</h3>
  <p>The components have different boiling points. The component with the lower boiling point vaporises more readily.</p>

  <h3>Apparatus</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Distillation flask</li>
    <li>Thermometer</li>
    <li>Condenser</li>
    <li>Receiving flask</li>
    <li>Heat source</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Heat the mixture.</li>
    <li>The more volatile component vaporises.</li>
    <li>The vapour enters the condenser.</li>
    <li>Cold water around the condenser cools the vapour.</li>
    <li>The vapour condenses into liquid.</li>
    <li>The liquid is collected as the distillate.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Pure water can be obtained from salt solution by simple distillation.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Simple Distillation', 15),
  (423, 423, '<h2>Fractional Distillation</h2>

  <p><strong>Fractional distillation</strong> is used to separate two or more <strong>miscible liquids</strong> with different boiling points, especially when their boiling points are relatively close.</p>

  <h3>Principle</h3>
  <p>The liquids have different boiling points. The liquid with the lower boiling point vaporises more readily.</p>

  <h3>Fractionating column</h3>
  <p>A fractionating column provides repeated vaporisation and condensation. This improves separation of liquids with relatively close boiling points.</p>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Heat the mixture of miscible liquids.</li>
    <li>Vapours rise through the fractionating column.</li>
    <li>Repeated condensation and vaporisation occur in the column.</li>
    <li>The component with the lower boiling point reaches the condenser more readily.</li>
    <li>It condenses and is collected.</li>
    <li>The temperature is monitored as different fractions are collected.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Crude oil is separated into useful fractions using fractional distillation because it contains several hydrocarbons with different boiling ranges.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fractional Distillation', 15),
  (424, 424, '<h2>Chromatography</h2>

  <p><strong>Chromatography</strong> is used to separate and identify components of a mixture, especially coloured substances such as dyes and pigments.</p>

  <h3>Principle</h3>
  <p>Different substances have different attractions to the <strong>stationary phase</strong> and the <strong>mobile phase</strong>. They therefore travel at different rates.</p>

  <h3>Paper chromatography</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Draw a pencil baseline near the bottom of chromatography paper.</li>
    <li>Place a small spot of the mixture on the baseline.</li>
    <li>Place the paper in a suitable solvent.</li>
    <li>Keep the solvent level below the sample spot.</li>
    <li>Allow the solvent to move up the paper.</li>
    <li>Different components travel different distances.</li>
    <li>Remove the paper and mark the solvent front.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Important rule</h3>
  <p>The starting line is drawn using <strong>pencil</strong>, not ink, because pencil does not dissolve and interfere with the chromatogram.</p>

  <h3>Interpreting results</h3>
  <p>One spot suggests one component under those conditions. Several spots indicate several components.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chromatography', 15),
  (425, 425, '<h2>Decantation</h2>

  <p><strong>Decantation</strong> separates a liquid from a settled solid or separates immiscible liquids by carefully pouring off one component.</p>

  <h3>Separating a solid from a liquid</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Allow the insoluble solid to settle at the bottom.</li>
    <li>The solid forms a sediment.</li>
    <li>Carefully pour the liquid into another container.</li>
    <li>Leave the sediment behind.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Sand and water can be separated by allowing the sand to settle and carefully pouring off the water.</p>

  <h3>Limitation</h3>
  <p>Decantation is generally less effective than filtration when very fine solid particles remain suspended.</p>

  <p><strong>Key idea: settle → carefully pour off.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Decantation', 15),
  (426, 426, '<h2>Magnetic Separation</h2>

  <p><strong>Magnetic separation</strong> is used when one component of a mixture is attracted to a magnet while the other component is not.</p>

  <h3>Principle</h3>
  <p>The method depends on a difference in <strong>magnetic properties</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Bring a magnet close to the mixture.</li>
    <li>The magnetic component is attracted to the magnet.</li>
    <li>The non-magnetic component remains behind.</li>
    <li>Remove the magnetic material from the magnet.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Iron filings can be separated from sulfur powder using a magnet.</p>

  <h3>Important point</h3>
  <p>The method only works when the components have sufficiently different magnetic properties.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Magnetic Separation', 15),
  (427, 427, '<h2>Structure of the Atom</h2>

  <p>An <strong>atom</strong> is the smallest particle of an element that retains the chemical identity of that element.</p>

  <p>An atom has two main regions:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Nucleus</strong> — the small, dense central region.</li>
    <li><strong>Electron region</strong> — the region around the nucleus where electrons are found.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The three main subatomic particles</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Proton</strong> — positive charge.</li>
    <li><strong>Neutron</strong> — no charge.</li>
    <li><strong>Electron</strong> — negative charge.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Protons and neutrons are found in the nucleus. Electrons occupy energy levels around the nucleus.</p>

  <h3>Electrical neutrality</h3>

  <p>A normal atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons, so the positive and negative charges cancel.</p>

  <p><strong>Atom = nucleus + electrons around the nucleus.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Structure of the Atom', 15),
  (428, 428, '<h2>Protons</h2>

  <p>A <strong>proton</strong> is a positively charged subatomic particle found in the nucleus of an atom.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Relative charge = <strong>+1</strong></li>
    <li>Relative mass ≈ <strong>1</strong></li>
    <li>Location = <strong>nucleus</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <p>The number of protons determines the identity of an element.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Hydrogen has 1 proton.</li>
    <li>Carbon has 6 protons.</li>
    <li>Oxygen has 8 protons.</li>
    <li>Sodium has 11 protons.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Change the number of protons → change the element.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Protons', 15),
  (429, 429, '<h2>Neutrons</h2>

  <p>A <strong>neutron</strong> is a subatomic particle found in the nucleus of an atom.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Relative charge = <strong>0</strong></li>
    <li>Relative mass ≈ <strong>1</strong></li>
    <li>Location = <strong>nucleus</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <p>Neutrons contribute to the mass of an atom but do not affect its electrical charge.</p>

  <h3>Finding the number of neutrons</h3>

  <p>The mass number is the total number of protons and neutrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Mass number = protons + neutrons</strong></p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>Neutrons = mass number − atomic number</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Neutrons', 15),
  (430, 430, '<h2>Electrons</h2>

  <p>An <strong>electron</strong> is a negatively charged subatomic particle found in energy levels around the nucleus.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Relative charge = <strong>−1</strong></li>
    <li>Relative mass ≈ <strong>1/1840</strong></li>
    <li>Location = <strong>energy levels around the nucleus</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <p>In a neutral atom:</p>

  <p><strong>Number of electrons = number of protons</strong></p>

  <p>When an atom gains or loses electrons, it becomes an ion.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrons', 15),
  (431, 431, '<h2>Atomic Number</h2>

  <p>The <strong>atomic number</strong> of an element is the number of protons in the nucleus of its atoms.</p>

  <p>It is represented by <strong>Z</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>Atomic number = number of protons</strong></p>

  <p>For a neutral atom:</p>

  <p><strong>Atomic number = number of protons = number of electrons</strong></p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Hydrogen: Z = 1</li>
    <li>Carbon: Z = 6</li>
    <li>Oxygen: Z = 8</li>
    <li>Sodium: Z = 11</li>
    <li>Chlorine: Z = 17</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The atomic number identifies the element.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Atomic Number', 15),
  (432, 432, '<h2>Mass Number</h2>

  <p>The <strong>mass number</strong> is the total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom.</p>

  <p>It is represented by <strong>A</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>Mass number = protons + neutrons</strong></p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>Neutrons = mass number − protons</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>An atom has 11 protons and 12 neutrons:</p>

  <p>Mass number = 11 + 12 = <strong>23</strong></p>

  <p>Electrons are not included in the mass number because their mass is extremely small compared with that of protons and neutrons.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mass Number', 15),
  (433, 433, '<h2>Electron Arrangement</h2>

  <p>Electrons occupy <strong>energy levels</strong> around the nucleus.</p>

  <p>For the first 20 elements, the electron arrangement can be written using the shell pattern:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>First energy level: maximum 2 electrons</li>
    <li>Second energy level: maximum 8 electrons</li>
    <li>Third energy level: up to 8 electrons for the first 20 elements</li>
    <li>Fourth energy level: begins to fill for elements such as potassium and calcium</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>H = 1</li>
    <li>He = 2</li>
    <li>Li = 2,1</li>
    <li>C = 2,4</li>
    <li>O = 2,6</li>
    <li>Na = 2,8,1</li>
    <li>Cl = 2,8,7</li>
    <li>Ca = 2,8,8,2</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For a neutral atom, the total number of electrons in all energy levels equals the atomic number.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electron Arrangement', 15),
  (434, 434, '<h2>Isotopes</h2>

  <p><strong>Isotopes</strong> are atoms of the same element that have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.</p>

  <h3>What remains the same?</h3>

  <p>Isotopes of an element have the same:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Number of protons</li>
    <li>Atomic number</li>
    <li>Element identity</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What changes?</h3>

  <p>They have different:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Numbers of neutrons</li>
    <li>Mass numbers</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example: carbon</h3>

  <p>Carbon-12:</p>
  <p>6 protons + 6 neutrons = 12</p>

  <p>Carbon-14:</p>
  <p>6 protons + 8 neutrons = 14</p>

  <p>Both are carbon because both contain 6 protons.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Isotopes', 15),
  (435, 435, '<h2>Identifying Isotopes</h2>

  <p>To determine whether two atoms are isotopes, compare their <strong>proton numbers</strong> and <strong>neutron numbers</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Rule</h3>

  <p><strong>Same protons + different neutrons = isotopes.</strong></p>

  <p>If the proton numbers are different, the atoms are different elements and therefore cannot be isotopes of one another.</p>

  <h3>Example 1</h3>

  <p>Atom A: 6 protons, 6 neutrons</p>
  <p>Atom B: 6 protons, 8 neutrons</p>

  <p>Same protons + different neutrons → isotopes.</p>

  <h3>Example 2</h3>

  <p>Atom A: 6 protons</p>
  <p>Atom B: 7 protons</p>

  <p>Different proton numbers → different elements → not isotopes.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Isotopes', 15),
  (436, 436, '<h2>Uses of Isotopes</h2>

  <p>Some isotopes are useful because their nuclei are unstable and emit radiation. These are called <strong>radioisotopes</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Medical uses</h3>

  <p>Radioisotopes can be used in medicine for diagnosis and treatment.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Some are used as tracers to follow processes inside the body.</li>
    <li>Some are used to destroy abnormal cells during treatment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Industrial uses</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Checking the thickness of materials.</li>
    <li>Tracing leaks or movement of substances.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Scientific and environmental uses</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Studying biological and chemical processes.</li>
    <li>Determining the age of some materials using radioactive dating techniques.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Radioisotopes must be handled carefully because ionising radiation can damage living tissue.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uses of Isotopes', 15),
  (437, 437, '<h2>Ion Formation</h2>

  <p>An <strong>ion</strong> is a charged particle formed when an atom gains or loses electrons.</p>

  <h3>Cation formation</h3>

  <p>When an atom <strong>loses electrons</strong>, it becomes positively charged.</p>

  <p>Example:</p>

  <p>Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>

  <p>Sodium loses one electron and forms a +1 ion.</p>

  <h3>Anion formation</h3>

  <p>When an atom <strong>gains electrons</strong>, it becomes negatively charged.</p>

  <p>Example:</p>

  <p>Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <p>Chlorine gains one electron and forms a −1 ion.</p>

  <h3>Key rule</h3>

  <p><strong>Lose electrons → positive ion.</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Gain electrons → negative ion.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ion Formation', 15),
  (438, 438, '<h2>Writing Ion Charges</h2>

  <p>The charge of an ion tells us how many electrons an atom has gained or lost.</p>

  <h3>Positive ions</h3>

  <p>Positive ions form when electrons are lost.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Na → Na⁺</li>
    <li>Mg → Mg²⁺</li>
    <li>Al → Al³⁺</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Negative ions</h3>

  <p>Negative ions form when electrons are gained.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Cl → Cl⁻</li>
    <li>O → O²⁻</li>
    <li>N → N³⁻</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Finding the charge from proton and electron numbers</h3>

  <p><strong>Ion charge = number of protons − number of electrons</strong></p>

  <p>Example:</p>

  <p>12 protons and 10 electrons:</p>

  <p>Charge = 12 − 10 = +2</p>

  <p>Therefore, the ion is 2+.</p>

  <p>Example:</p>

  <p>17 protons and 18 electrons:</p>

  <p>Charge = 17 − 18 = −1</p>

  <p>Therefore, the ion is 1−.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Writing Ion Charges', 15),
  (439, 439, '<h2>Arrangement of the Periodic Table</h2>

  <p>The <strong>Periodic Table</strong> is a systematic arrangement of elements according to their atomic numbers and repeating patterns in their properties.</p>

  <h3>Important features</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Elements are arranged in order of increasing atomic number.</li>
    <li>Horizontal rows are called <strong>periods</strong>.</li>
    <li>Vertical columns are called <strong>groups</strong>.</li>
    <li>Elements in the same group have similar chemical properties because they have related outer-electron arrangements.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>First 20 elements</h3>

  <p>The first 20 elements are:</p>

  <p>
  H, He, Li, Be, B, C, N, O, F, Ne,
  Na, Mg, Al, Si, P, S, Cl, Ar, K, Ca
  </p>

  <h3>Why the table is useful</h3>

  <p>The position of an element helps us predict its electron arrangement, valency and chemical behaviour.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Arrangement of the Periodic Table', 15),
  (440, 440, '<h2>Groups</h2>

  <p>A <strong>group</strong> is a vertical column in the periodic table.</p>

  <p>Elements in the same group generally have similar chemical properties because they have similar numbers of electrons in their outermost energy level.</p>

  <h3>Important groups</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Group 1</strong> — alkali metals</li>
    <li><strong>Group 2</strong> — alkaline earth metals</li>
    <li><strong>Group 7</strong> — halogens</li>
    <li><strong>Group 0</strong> — noble gases</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Sodium has electron arrangement 2,8,1.</p>

  <p>Potassium has electron arrangement 2,8,8,1.</p>

  <p>Both have one electron in the outermost energy level, so they belong to the same group: Group 1.</p>

  <p><strong>Same group → similar outer-electron pattern → similar chemical behaviour.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Groups', 15),
  (441, 441, '<h2>Periods</h2>

  <p>A <strong>period</strong> is a horizontal row in the periodic table.</p>

  <p>For the first 20 elements, the period number corresponds to the number of occupied electron energy levels.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>Hydrogen: 1 → one occupied energy level → Period 1.</p>

  <p>Carbon: 2,4 → two occupied energy levels → Period 2.</p>

  <p>Sodium: 2,8,1 → three occupied energy levels → Period 3.</p>

  <p>Calcium: 2,8,8,2 → four occupied energy levels → Period 4.</p>

  <p><strong>Number of occupied energy levels = period number.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Periods', 15),
  (442, 442, '<h2>Electronic Structure and Periodic Position</h2>

  <p>The electron arrangement of an element can be used to determine its position in the periodic table.</p>

  <h3>Finding the period</h3>

  <p>Count the number of occupied energy levels.</p>

  <p><strong>Occupied energy levels → period</strong></p>

  <h3>Finding the group</h3>

  <p>For the first 20 elements, examine the number of electrons in the outermost energy level.</p>

  <p><strong>Outer electrons → group</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Magnesium = 2,8,2</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Three occupied energy levels → Period 3.</li>
    <li>Two outer electrons → Group 2.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Therefore, magnesium is in <strong>Period 3, Group 2</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electronic Structure and Periodic Position', 15),
  (443, 443, '<h2>Group 1 Elements</h2>

  <p>Group 1 elements are called the <strong>alkali metals</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Lithium — Li</li>
    <li>Sodium — Na</li>
    <li>Potassium — K</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Electron arrangement</h3>

  <p>Group 1 elements have <strong>one electron in their outermost energy level</strong>.</p>

  <p>Examples:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Li = 2,1</li>
    <li>Na = 2,8,1</li>
    <li>K = 2,8,8,1</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Reaction with water</h3>

  <p>Group 1 metals react with water to form a metal hydroxide and hydrogen gas.</p>

  <p><strong>metal + water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen</strong></p>

  <p>The metals generally become more reactive down the group.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Group 1 Elements', 15),
  (444, 444, '<h2>Group 2 Elements</h2>

  <p>Group 2 elements are called the <strong>alkaline earth metals</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Beryllium — Be</li>
    <li>Magnesium — Mg</li>
    <li>Calcium — Ca</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Electron arrangement</h3>

  <p>Group 2 elements have <strong>two electrons in their outermost energy level</strong>.</p>

  <p>Examples:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Be = 2,2</li>
    <li>Mg = 2,8,2</li>
    <li>Ca = 2,8,8,2</li>
  </ul>

  <p>They commonly lose two electrons to form <strong>2+</strong> ions.</p>

  <h3>Reaction with water</h3>

  <p>Some Group 2 metals react with water to form metal hydroxides and hydrogen. Reactivity generally increases down the group.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Group 2 Elements', 15),
  (445, 445, '<h2>Group 7 Elements</h2>

  <p>Group 7 elements are called the <strong>halogens</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Fluorine — F</li>
    <li>Chlorine — Cl</li>
    <li>Bromine — Br</li>
    <li>Iodine — I</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For the first 20 elements, fluorine and chlorine are important examples.</p>

  <h3>Electron arrangement</h3>

  <p>Group 7 elements have <strong>seven outer electrons</strong>.</p>

  <p>Example:</p>

  <p>Chlorine = 2,8,7</p>

  <p>They tend to gain one electron to obtain a stable outer shell, forming <strong>1− ions</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Reactivity</h3>

  <p>Halogen reactivity generally decreases down the group.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Group 7 Elements', 15),
  (446, 446, '<h2>Group 0 Elements</h2>

  <p>Group 0 elements are called the <strong>noble gases</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Helium — He</li>
    <li>Neon — Ne</li>
    <li>Argon — Ar</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Electron arrangement</h3>

  <p>Noble gases have full outer energy levels.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Helium = 2</li>
    <li>Neon = 2,8</li>
    <li>Argon = 2,8,8</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Because their outer energy levels are full, noble gases are generally very unreactive.</p>

  <h3>Physical state</h3>

  <p>At room temperature, noble gases are gases.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Group 0 Elements', 15),
  (447, 447, '<h2>Metals and Non-metals</h2>

  <p>Elements can broadly be classified as <strong>metals</strong> or <strong>non-metals</strong> based on their physical and chemical properties.</p>

  <h3>Typical properties of metals</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Good conductors of heat.</li>
    <li>Good conductors of electricity.</li>
    <li>Generally shiny when freshly cut or polished.</li>
    <li>Malleable.</li>
    <li>Ductile.</li>
    <li>Generally solid at room temperature.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Typical properties of non-metals</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Generally poor conductors of electricity.</li>
    <li>Many are dull in appearance.</li>
    <li>Solid non-metals are often brittle.</li>
    <li>Many have relatively low melting and boiling points.</li>
    <li>They may exist as solids, liquids or gases.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Position in the periodic table</h3>

  <p>Most metals are found on the left and centre of the periodic table, while most non-metals are found toward the right.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Metals and Non-metals', 15),
  (448, 448, '<h2>Periodic Trends</h2>

  <p>A <strong>periodic trend</strong> is a pattern in the properties of elements as their position changes across a period or down a group.</p>

  <h3>Across a period</h3>

  <p>As atomic number increases across a period:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The number of outer electrons generally increases.</li>
    <li>Elements gradually change from metallic to non-metallic behaviour.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Down a group</h3>

  <p>Elements have more occupied energy levels as we move down a group.</p>

  <p>This affects atomic size and the attraction between the nucleus and outer electrons.</p>

  <h3>Reactivity</h3>

  <p>Different groups show different reactivity patterns:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Group 1 metals generally become more reactive down the group.</li>
    <li>Group 7 halogens generally become less reactive down the group.</li>
    <li>Group 0 elements remain very unreactive.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Periodic Trends', 15),
  (449, 449, '<h2>Atomic Size</h2>

  <p><strong>Atomic size</strong> refers to the relative size of an atom.</p>

  <h3>Down a group</h3>

  <p>Atomic size generally <strong>increases down a group</strong>.</p>

  <p>This happens because each step down adds another occupied energy level.</p>

  <p>The outer electrons are therefore farther from the nucleus.</p>

  <h3>Across a period</h3>

  <p>Atomic size generally <strong>decreases from left to right</strong> across a period.</p>

  <p>As proton number increases, the nucleus attracts the electrons more strongly while the electrons are being added to the same main energy level.</p>

  <h3>Simple rule</h3>

  <p><strong>Down a group → atomic size increases.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Across a period → atomic size generally decreases.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Atomic Size', 15),
  (450, 450, '<h2>Why Atoms Bond</h2>

  <p>Atoms form <strong>chemical bonds</strong> because bonding can give them a more stable electron arrangement.</p>

  <h3>Valence electrons</h3>

  <p>The electrons in the outermost energy level are called <strong>valence electrons</strong>.</p>

  <p>These electrons are mainly involved in chemical bonding.</p>

  <h3>Stable electron arrangements</h3>

  <p>Atoms tend to gain, lose or share electrons so that their outer energy level becomes more stable.</p>

  <p>For many elements, stability is associated with a full outer energy level.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Atoms may <strong>lose electrons</strong>.</li>
    <li>Atoms may <strong>gain electrons</strong>.</li>
    <li>Atoms may <strong>share electrons</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These processes lead to different types of chemical bonding.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Atoms Bond', 15),
  (451, 451, '<h2>Valency</h2>

  <p><strong>Valency</strong> is the combining capacity of an atom. It indicates how many electrons an atom may lose, gain or share when forming compounds.</p>

  <h3>Common valencies</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Group 1 elements → valency 1</li>
    <li>Group 2 elements → valency 2</li>
    <li>Group 3 elements → valency 3</li>
    <li>Group 4 elements → valency 4</li>
    <li>Group 5 elements → commonly valency 3</li>
    <li>Group 6 elements → commonly valency 2</li>
    <li>Group 7 elements → commonly valency 1</li>
    <li>Group 0 elements → valency 0</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>Sodium has electron arrangement 2,8,1, so its common valency is 1.</p>

  <p>Magnesium has electron arrangement 2,8,2, so its common valency is 2.</p>

  <p>Chlorine has electron arrangement 2,8,7, so its common valency is 1 because it needs one electron to complete its outer level.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Valency', 15),
  (452, 452, '<h2>Ionic Bonding</h2>

  <p><strong>Ionic bonding</strong> is the electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions.</p>

  <p>Ionic compounds are commonly formed between metals and non-metals.</p>

  <h3>What happens?</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>The metal loses one or more electrons.</li>
    <li>The metal becomes a positive ion.</li>
    <li>The non-metal gains one or more electrons.</li>
    <li>The non-metal becomes a negative ion.</li>
    <li>The oppositely charged ions attract each other.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Metal → loses electrons → positive ion</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Non-metal → gains electrons → negative ion</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ionic Bonding', 15),
  (453, 453, '<h2>Electron Transfer</h2>

  <p><strong>Electron transfer</strong> occurs when one atom loses electrons and another atom gains them.</p>

  <h3>Sodium and chlorine</h3>

  <p>Sodium:</p>
  <p>Na = 2,8,1</p>

  <p>Chlorine:</p>
  <p>Cl = 2,8,7</p>

  <p>Sodium loses one electron:</p>

  <p>Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>

  <p>Chlorine gains that electron:</p>

  <p>Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <p>The resulting Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions attract to form sodium chloride.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electron Transfer', 15),
  (454, 454, '<h2>Formation of Positive Ions</h2>

  <p>A <strong>positive ion</strong>, or <strong>cation</strong>, forms when an atom loses one or more electrons.</p>

  <p>Electrons have negative charges. Therefore, losing negative electrons leaves the atom with an overall positive charge.</p>

  <h3>Sodium</h3>

  <p>Na = 2,8,1</p>

  <p>Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>

  <p>Sodium loses one electron and becomes Na⁺.</p>

  <h3>Magnesium</h3>

  <p>Mg = 2,8,2</p>

  <p>Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p>Magnesium loses two electrons and becomes Mg²⁺.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of Positive Ions', 15),
  (455, 455, '<h2>Formation of Negative Ions</h2>

  <p>A <strong>negative ion</strong>, or <strong>anion</strong>, forms when an atom gains one or more electrons.</p>

  <p>Because electrons have negative charges, gaining electrons gives the atom an overall negative charge.</p>

  <h3>Chlorine</h3>

  <p>Cl = 2,8,7</p>

  <p>Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <p>Chlorine gains one electron and becomes Cl⁻.</p>

  <h3>Oxygen</h3>

  <p>O = 2,6</p>

  <p>O + 2e⁻ → O²⁻</p>

  <p>Oxygen gains two electrons and becomes O²⁻.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of Negative Ions', 15),
  (456, 456, '<h2>Formation of Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <p>Ionic compounds form when positive and negative ions combine in the correct ratio so that the total charge is zero.</p>

  <h3>Sodium chloride</h3>

  <p>Na forms Na⁺.</p>

  <p>Cl forms Cl⁻.</p>

  <p>One Na⁺ balances one Cl⁻.</p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>NaCl</strong></p>

  <h3>Magnesium chloride</h3>

  <p>Mg forms Mg²⁺.</p>

  <p>Cl forms Cl⁻.</p>

  <p>Two Cl⁻ ions are needed to balance one Mg²⁺ ion.</p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>MgCl₂</strong></p>

  <h3>Aluminium oxide</h3>

  <p>Al forms Al³⁺.</p>

  <p>O forms O²⁻.</p>

  <p>The smallest combination with zero total charge is two Al³⁺ and three O²⁻.</p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>Al₂O₃</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of Ionic Compounds', 15),
  (457, 457, '<h2>Properties of Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <p>The structure of an ionic compound explains its physical properties.</p>

  <h3>High melting and boiling points</h3>

  <p>Ionic compounds contain strong electrostatic attractions between oppositely charged ions. A lot of energy is needed to overcome these attractions.</p>

  <h3>Electrical conductivity</h3>

  <p>Solid ionic compounds do not conduct electricity because their ions are fixed in position.</p>

  <p>When molten or dissolved in water, the ions can move and carry electric charge.</p>

  <h3>Solubility</h3>

  <p>Many ionic compounds dissolve in water, although not all ionic compounds are soluble.</p>

  <h3>Structure</h3>

  <p>Ionic compounds form giant repeating lattices of positive and negative ions.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Ionic Compounds', 15),
  (458, 458, '<h2>Covalent Bonding</h2>

  <p><strong>Covalent bonding</strong> occurs when atoms share pairs of electrons.</p>

  <p>Covalent bonding commonly occurs between non-metal atoms.</p>

  <h3>Why electrons are shared</h3>

  <p>Sharing electrons allows the bonded atoms to obtain more stable outer-electron arrangements.</p>

  <h3>Hydrogen molecule</h3>

  <p>Each hydrogen atom has one electron.</p>

  <p>Each hydrogen atom needs one more electron to fill its first energy level.</p>

  <p>The two hydrogen atoms share one pair of electrons.</p>

  <p>H—H</p>

  <p>The shared pair forms a <strong>single covalent bond</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Covalent Bonding', 15),
  (459, 459, '<h2>Single Covalent Bonds</h2>

  <p>A <strong>single covalent bond</strong> consists of one shared pair of electrons.</p>

  <h3>Hydrogen</h3>

  <p>H—H</p>

  <p>The line represents one shared electron pair.</p>

  <h3>Chlorine</h3>

  <p>Cl—Cl</p>

  <p>Each chlorine atom contributes one electron to the shared pair.</p>

  <h3>Hydrogen chloride</h3>

  <p>H—Cl</p>

  <p>Hydrogen and chlorine share one pair of electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>One shared pair = one single covalent bond.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Single Covalent Bonds', 15),
  (460, 460, '<h2>Double Covalent Bonds</h2>

  <p>A <strong>double covalent bond</strong> consists of two shared pairs of electrons.</p>

  <h3>Oxygen molecule</h3>

  <p>Each oxygen atom has six outer electrons.</p>

  <p>Each oxygen atom needs two more electrons for a full outer energy level.</p>

  <p>The two oxygen atoms therefore share two pairs of electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>O=O</strong></p>

  <p>The two lines represent two shared electron pairs.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Double Covalent Bonds', 15),
  (461, 461, '<h2>Triple Covalent Bonds</h2>

  <p>A <strong>triple covalent bond</strong> consists of three shared pairs of electrons.</p>

  <h3>Nitrogen molecule</h3>

  <p>Each nitrogen atom has five outer electrons.</p>

  <p>Each nitrogen atom needs three more electrons for a full outer energy level.</p>

  <p>The two nitrogen atoms share three pairs of electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>N≡N</strong></p>

  <p>The three lines represent three shared electron pairs.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Triple Covalent Bonds', 15),
  (462, 462, '<h2>Dot-and-Cross Diagrams</h2>

  <p>A <strong>dot-and-cross diagram</strong> shows the outer electrons involved in covalent bonding or electron transfer.</p>

  <h3>What the symbols mean</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A <strong>dot</strong> can represent an electron contributed by one atom.</li>
    <li>A <strong>cross</strong> can represent an electron contributed by the other atom.</li>
    <li>A shared pair contains one electron from each atom in a simple covalent bond.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Hydrogen chloride</h3>

  <p>Hydrogen contributes one electron.</p>

  <p>Chlorine contributes one electron to the shared pair.</p>

  <p>The shared pair forms the H—Cl bond.</p>

  <h3>Important rule</h3>

  <p>When drawing a dot-and-cross diagram:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the symbols of the atoms.</li>
    <li>Determine their outer electrons.</li>
    <li>Identify the electrons needed for stable outer levels.</li>
    <li>Show the shared pairs.</li>
    <li>Show remaining non-bonding electrons where required.</li>
  </ol>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Dot-and-Cross Diagrams', 15),
  (463, 463, '<h2>Properties of Simple Covalent Substances</h2>

  <p>Simple covalent substances consist of separate molecules containing covalent bonds within each molecule.</p>

  <h3>Low melting and boiling points</h3>

  <p>Many simple molecular substances have relatively low melting and boiling points because the forces between their molecules are relatively weak.</p>

  <h3>Electrical conductivity</h3>

  <p>Simple covalent substances generally do not conduct electricity because they do not contain mobile ions or freely moving electrons.</p>

  <h3>Physical states</h3>

  <p>Simple covalent substances may exist as solids, liquids or gases depending on the substance.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>The covalent bonds <strong>within</strong> a molecule are strong. The attractions <strong>between</strong> separate molecules are generally much weaker.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Simple Covalent Substances', 15),
  (464, 464, '<h2>Giant Covalent Structures</h2>

  <p>A <strong>giant covalent structure</strong> contains a very large number of atoms joined together by covalent bonds in a continuous structure.</p>

  <p>Unlike simple molecular substances, giant covalent substances do not consist of separate small molecules.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Diamond</li>
    <li>Graphite</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>General properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Strong covalent bonds extend throughout the structure.</li>
    <li>Large amounts of energy are required to break these bonds.</li>
    <li>They generally have very high melting points.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Giant Covalent Structures', 15),
  (465, 465, '<h2>Diamond</h2>

  <p><strong>Diamond</strong> is a giant covalent form of carbon.</p>

  <h3>Structure</h3>

  <p>Each carbon atom is covalently bonded to four other carbon atoms.</p>

  <p>This forms a strong three-dimensional giant structure.</p>

  <h3>Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Very hard.</li>
    <li>Very high melting point.</li>
    <li>Does not conduct electricity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why diamond is hard</h3>

  <p>Strong covalent bonds extend throughout the three-dimensional structure, making it difficult to move one carbon atom past another.</p>

  <h3>Why diamond does not conduct electricity</h3>

  <p>All the outer electrons of carbon are involved in covalent bonds, so there are no freely mobile electrons available to carry electric charge.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diamond', 15),
  (466, 466, '<h2>Graphite</h2>

  <p><strong>Graphite</strong> is another giant covalent form of carbon.</p>

  <h3>Structure</h3>

  <p>Each carbon atom is bonded to three other carbon atoms.</p>

  <p>The carbon atoms form layers of hexagonal arrangements.</p>

  <p>The layers are held together by weaker forces than the strong covalent bonds within each layer.</p>

  <h3>Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Soft compared with diamond.</li>
    <li>Conducts electricity.</li>
    <li>Has a high melting point.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why graphite conducts electricity</h3>

  <p>One outer electron from each carbon atom is not used in the three covalent bonds. These electrons can move through the layers and carry electric charge.</p>

  <h3>Why graphite is soft</h3>

  <p>The layers can slide over one another because the forces between the layers are relatively weak.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Graphite', 15),
  (467, 467, '<h2>Ionic and Covalent Structures</h2>

  <p>Ionic and covalent substances differ in how their particles are held together.</p>

  <h3>Ionic substances</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Contain positive and negative ions.</li>
    <li>Have strong electrostatic attractions between ions.</li>
    <li>Form giant ionic lattices.</li>
    <li>Conduct electricity when molten or dissolved if the ions are mobile.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Simple covalent substances</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Contain separate molecules.</li>
    <li>Atoms within molecules are joined by covalent bonds.</li>
    <li>Forces between molecules are generally weaker.</li>
    <li>Usually do not conduct electricity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Giant covalent substances</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Contain a continuous network of covalently bonded atoms.</li>
    <li>Usually have very high melting points.</li>
    <li>Properties depend strongly on their structure.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ionic and Covalent Structures', 15),
  (468, 468, '<h2>Metallic Bonding</h2>

  <p>Metallic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.</p>

  <h3>How Metallic Bonding Forms</h3>

  <p>Metal atoms have outer electrons that are not held strongly by individual atoms. These electrons become delocalised throughout the metal structure.</p>

  <p>The metal atoms form a regular arrangement of positive ions surrounded by a sea of delocalised electrons.</p>

  <pre>
Positive metal ions
      ↓
  M⁺   M⁺   M⁺
    e⁻ e⁻ e⁻
  M⁺   M⁺   M⁺
    e⁻ e⁻ e⁻
  M⁺   M⁺   M⁺

e⁻ = delocalised electrons
  </pre>

  <h3>The Attraction</h3>

  <p>The positive metal ions are attracted to the negatively charged delocalised electrons.</p>

  <p>This electrostatic attraction holds the metal structure together.</p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Metallic bonding occurs in metals.</li>
    <li>Metal ions are positive.</li>
    <li>The outer electrons are delocalised.</li>
    <li>The attraction between ions and electrons holds the structure together.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Metallic Bonding', 15),
  (469, 469, '<h2>Delocalised Electrons in Metals</h2>

  <p>Delocalised electrons are electrons that are free to move throughout the metal structure rather than belonging to one particular atom.</p>

  <h3>Formation</h3>

  <p>Metal atoms contribute their outer electrons to a common pool of electrons.</p>

  <p>The remaining metal particles behave as positive ions.</p>

  <pre>
Metal ions:       M⁺   M⁺   M⁺
                  ↓    ↓    ↓
Electrons:       e⁻ e⁻ e⁻ e⁻ e⁻
                  ↑    ↑    ↑
                free to move
  </pre>

  <h3>Why Delocalisation Matters</h3>

  <p>The movement of delocalised electrons explains why metals can conduct electricity and transfer thermal energy efficiently.</p>

  <p>When a potential difference is applied, the electrons can move through the metal.</p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>The metal ions remain in fixed positions in the structure, while the delocalised electrons can move through the structure.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Delocalised Electrons in Metals', 15),
  (470, 470, '<h2>Metallic Bonding and Metal Properties</h2>

  <p>The structure produced by metallic bonding explains several characteristic properties of metals.</p>

  <h3>Electrical Conductivity</h3>

  <p>Metals conduct electricity because delocalised electrons can move through the structure.</p>

  <h3>Thermal Conductivity</h3>

  <p>Metals conduct heat effectively because mobile electrons can transfer energy through the structure.</p>

  <h3>Malleability</h3>

  <p>Metals can be hammered into sheets because layers of metal ions can slide past one another while the metallic attraction is maintained by the delocalised electrons.</p>

  <h3>Ductility</h3>

  <p>Metals can be drawn into wires because their layers can shift without completely destroying the metallic bonding.</p>

  <h3>High Melting and Boiling Points</h3>

  <p>The strong attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons requires considerable energy to overcome.</p>

  <h3>Summary</h3>

  <pre>
Metallic structure
       ↓
Positive metal ions
       +
Delocalised electrons
       ↓
Strong metallic attraction
       ↓
Conductivity
Malleability
Ductility
High melting/boiling points
  </pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Metallic Bonding and Metal Properties', 15),
  (471, 471, '<h2>Valency and Chemical Formulae</h2>

  <p>The <strong>valency</strong> of an element helps determine the ratio in which its atoms combine with other elements.</p>

  <h3>Common valencies</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Sodium (Na) → 1</li>
    <li>Magnesium (Mg) → 2</li>
    <li>Aluminium (Al) → 3</li>
    <li>Chlorine (Cl) → 1</li>
    <li>Oxygen (O) → 2</li>
    <li>Nitrogen (N) → 3</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Using valency</h3>

  <p>When writing a formula, the total combining capacity of the positive part must balance the total combining capacity of the negative part.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p>Mg has valency 2 and Cl has valency 1.</p>

  <p>One Mg atom requires two Cl atoms.</p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>MgCl₂</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Valency and Chemical Formulae', 15),
  (472, 472, '<h2>Writing Chemical Formulae</h2>

  <p>A chemical formula shows the elements present in a compound and the ratio in which their atoms or ions occur.</p>

  <h3>Method</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the symbols of the elements.</li>
    <li>Write their valencies.</li>
    <li>Find the simplest ratio that balances the valencies.</li>
    <li>Write the subscripts in the formula.</li>
    <li>Do not write subscript 1.</li>
    <li>Reduce the ratio to the simplest whole numbers if necessary.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Example: Aluminium oxide</h3>

  <p>Aluminium = 3</p>

  <p>Oxygen = 2</p>

  <p>Two Al atoms give:</p>

  <p>2 × 3 = 6</p>

  <p>Three O atoms give:</p>

  <p>3 × 2 = 6</p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>Al₂O₃</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Writing Chemical Formulae', 15),
  (473, 473, '<h2>Formulae of Ionic Compounds</h2>

  <p>The formula of an ionic compound must have an overall charge of <strong>zero</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Using ion charges</h3>

  <p>Consider calcium chloride:</p>

  <p>Ca²⁺ and Cl⁻</p>

  <p>One Ca²⁺ gives +2.</p>

  <p>Two Cl⁻ ions give:</p>

  <p>2 × (−1) = −2</p>

  <p>Total charge:</p>

  <p>+2 + (−2) = 0</p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>CaCl₂</strong></p>

  <h3>Important rule</h3>

  <p>Always check the charges after writing an ionic formula.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formulae of Ionic Compounds', 15),
  (474, 474, '<h2>Formulae of Covalent Compounds</h2>

  <p>Covalent compounds are formed when non-metal atoms share electrons.</p>

  <p>The formula shows the actual number or ratio of atoms in the molecule or substance.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Water</strong> = H₂O</p>

  <p>One oxygen atom is combined with two hydrogen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Carbon dioxide</strong> = CO₂</p>

  <p>One carbon atom is combined with two oxygen atoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Ammonia</strong> = NH₃</p>

  <p>One nitrogen atom is combined with three hydrogen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Subscripts</h3>

  <p>A subscript shows how many atoms of that element are present.</p>

  <p>In CO₂, the subscript 2 applies to oxygen only.</p>

  <p>In H₂O, the subscript 2 applies to hydrogen only.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formulae of Covalent Compounds', 15),
  (475, 475, '<h2>Naming Compounds</h2>

  <p>The name of a compound identifies the elements or ions present in it.</p>

  <h3>Ionic compounds</h3>

  <p>For a simple ionic compound, name the positive ion first, followed by the negative ion.</p>

  <p>Examples:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>NaCl → sodium chloride</li>
    <li>MgO → magnesium oxide</li>
    <li>CaCl₂ → calcium chloride</li>
    <li>Al₂O₃ → aluminium oxide</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Common naming changes</h3>

  <p>Many simple non-metal ions end in <strong>-ide</strong>.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>chlorine → chloride</li>
    <li>oxygen → oxide</li>
    <li>nitrogen → nitride</li>
    <li>sulphur → sulphide</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Covalent compounds</h3>

  <p>Simple covalent compounds can use prefixes to show the number of atoms present.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>mono- → 1</li>
    <li>di- → 2</li>
    <li>tri- → 3</li>
    <li>tetra- → 4</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Examples:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>CO → carbon monoxide</li>
    <li>CO₂ → carbon dioxide</li>
    <li>N₂O₃ → dinitrogen trioxide</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Compounds', 15),
  (476, 476, '<h2>State Symbols</h2>

  <p><strong>State symbols</strong> show the physical state of a substance in a chemical equation.</p>

  <h3>The four state symbols</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>(s)</strong> → solid</li>
    <li><strong>(l)</strong> → liquid</li>
    <li><strong>(g)</strong> → gas</li>
    <li><strong>(aq)</strong> → aqueous, meaning dissolved in water</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>NaCl(s) means solid sodium chloride.</p>

  <p>H₂O(l) means liquid water.</p>

  <p>CO₂(g) means gaseous carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p>NaCl(aq) means sodium chloride dissolved in water.</p>

  <h3>Why state symbols matter</h3>

  <p>State symbols provide information about the physical state of reactants and products during a chemical reaction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for State Symbols', 15),
  (477, 477, '<h2>Meaning of Mole</h2>

  <p>A <strong>mole</strong> is a unit used to measure the amount of a substance.</p>

  <p>One mole contains a fixed number of particles:</p>

  <p><strong>1 mole = 6.02 × 10²³ particles</strong></p>

  <p>The particles may be atoms, molecules, ions or other specified particles.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>1 mole of carbon atoms contains 6.02 × 10²³ carbon atoms.</p>

  <p>1 mole of water molecules contains 6.02 × 10²³ water molecules.</p>

  <p>1 mole of sodium ions contains 6.02 × 10²³ sodium ions.</p>

  <p>The number <strong>6.02 × 10²³</strong> is called the <strong>Avogadro constant</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Mole', 15),
  (478, 478, '<h2>Avogadro Constant</h2>

  <p>The <strong>Avogadro constant</strong> is the number of particles in one mole.</p>

  <p><strong>Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹</strong></p>

  <h3>Particles from moles</h3>

  <p>Use:</p>

  <p><strong>Number of particles = moles × Avogadro constant</strong></p>

  <p>N = nNₐ</p>

  <h3>Moles from particles</h3>

  <p>Rearrange:</p>

  <p><strong>moles = number of particles ÷ Avogadro constant</strong></p>

  <p>n = N ÷ Nₐ</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Avogadro Constant', 15),
  (479, 479, '<h2>Relative Atomic Mass</h2>

  <p><strong>Relative atomic mass (Aᵣ)</strong> is the average mass of an atom of an element compared with a standard reference.</p>

  <p>The relative atomic masses of elements are used when calculating formula masses and molar masses.</p>

  <h3>Common values</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>H = 1</li>
    <li>C = 12</li>
    <li>N = 14</li>
    <li>O = 16</li>
    <li>Na = 23</li>
    <li>Mg = 24</li>
    <li>Cl = 35.5</li>
    <li>Ca = 40</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These values are dimensionless because they are relative values.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relative Atomic Mass', 15),
  (480, 480, '<h2>Relative Formula Mass</h2>

  <p><strong>Relative formula mass (Mᵣ)</strong> is the sum of the relative atomic masses of all atoms shown in a chemical formula.</p>

  <h3>Example: H₂O</h3>

  <p>H = 1</p>

  <p>O = 16</p>

  <p>Mᵣ(H₂O) = (2 × 1) + 16</p>

  <p>Mᵣ(H₂O) = 18</p>

  <h3>Example: CO₂</h3>

  <p>Mᵣ(CO₂) = 12 + (2 × 16)</p>

  <p>Mᵣ(CO₂) = 44</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relative Formula Mass', 15),
  (481, 481, '<h2>Molar Mass</h2>

  <p><strong>Molar mass</strong> is the mass of one mole of a substance.</p>

  <p>It is measured in <strong>g mol⁻¹</strong>.</p>

  <p>The numerical value of molar mass corresponds to the relative atomic mass or relative formula mass.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>H₂O:</p>

  <p>Mᵣ = 18</p>

  <p>Therefore molar mass = <strong>18 g mol⁻¹</strong>.</p>

  <p>CO₂:</p>

  <p>Mᵣ = 44</p>

  <p>Therefore molar mass = <strong>44 g mol⁻¹</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Molar Mass', 15),
  (482, 482, '<h2>Calculating Moles from Mass</h2>

  <p>The number of moles can be calculated from mass using:</p>

  <p><strong>n = m ÷ M</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>n = number of moles</li>
    <li>m = mass in grams</li>
    <li>M = molar mass in g mol⁻¹</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Calculate the number of moles in 36 g of water.</p>

  <p>Molar mass of H₂O = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p>n = 36 ÷ 18</p>

  <p>n = 2 mol</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Moles from Mass', 15),
  (483, 483, '<h2>Calculating Mass from Moles</h2>

  <p>Mass can be calculated from the number of moles using:</p>

  <p><strong>m = n × M</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>m = mass in grams</li>
    <li>n = number of moles</li>
    <li>M = molar mass in g mol⁻¹</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Find the mass of 3 mol of water.</p>

  <p>M = 18 g mol⁻¹.</p>

  <p>m = 3 × 18</p>

  <p>m = 54 g</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Mass from Moles', 15),
  (484, 484, '<h2>Moles and Particles</h2>

  <p>Moles and number of particles are directly related through the Avogadro constant.</p>

  <p><strong>N = nNₐ</strong></p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>particles = moles × 6.02 × 10²³</strong></p>

  <p>And:</p>

  <p><strong>moles = particles ÷ 6.02 × 10²³</strong></p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>Always identify what type of particle is being counted: atoms, molecules or ions.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Moles and Particles', 15),
  (485, 485, '<h2>Mole Ratios</h2>

  <p>A balanced chemical equation gives the ratio in which substances react and are produced.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</p>

  <p>The coefficients give the mole ratio:</p>

  <p><strong>2 mol H₂ : 1 mol O₂ : 2 mol H₂O</strong></p>

  <h3>Using the ratio</h3>

  <p>If 2 mol of H₂ react, 1 mol of O₂ is required and 2 mol of H₂O are produced.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mole Ratios', 15),
  (486, 486, '<h2>Calculating Reactant Amount</h2>

  <p>A balanced equation can be used to calculate the amount of one reactant required when the amount of another reactant is known.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</p>

  <p>Suppose 6 mol H₂ are available.</p>

  <p>Ratio H₂ : O₂ = 2 : 1.</p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p>6 mol H₂ require 3 mol O₂.</p>

  <h3>Method</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the balanced equation.</li>
    <li>Identify the two coefficients.</li>
    <li>Set up the mole ratio.</li>
    <li>Calculate the unknown amount.</li>
  </ol>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Reactant Amount', 15),
  (487, 487, '<h2>Calculating Product Amount</h2>

  <p>The coefficients in a balanced equation can be used to calculate the amount of product formed.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃</p>

  <p>The ratio is:</p>

  <p>1 mol N₂ → 2 mol NH₃</p>

  <p>Therefore, 4 mol N₂ produce:</p>

  <p>4 × 2 = 8 mol NH₃</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calculating Product Amount', 15),
  (488, 488, '<h2>Empirical Formula</h2>

  <p>The <strong>empirical formula</strong> shows the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a compound.</p>

  <h3>Method from masses</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the mass of each element.</li>
    <li>Divide each mass by its relative atomic mass.</li>
    <li>Obtain the mole values.</li>
    <li>Divide all mole values by the smallest value.</li>
    <li>Convert the resulting ratio to the smallest whole numbers.</li>
    <li>Write the empirical formula.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>A compound contains 12 g carbon and 16 g oxygen.</p>

  <p>C: 12 ÷ 12 = 1 mol</p>

  <p>O: 16 ÷ 16 = 1 mol</p>

  <p>Ratio = 1 : 1</p>

  <p>Empirical formula = <strong>CO</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Empirical Formula', 15),
  (489, 489, '<h2>Molecular Formula</h2>

  <p>The <strong>molecular formula</strong> shows the actual number of atoms of each element in one molecule.</p>

  <p>The molecular formula is a whole-number multiple of the empirical formula.</p>

  <h3>Method</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Find the empirical formula.</li>
    <li>Calculate its empirical formula mass.</li>
    <li>Use:</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Multiplier = molecular mass ÷ empirical formula mass</strong></p>

  <p>Multiply every subscript in the empirical formula by the multiplier.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Empirical formula = CH₂.</p>

  <p>Empirical formula mass = 12 + 2 = 14.</p>

  <p>Molecular mass = 28.</p>

  <p>Multiplier = 28 ÷ 14 = 2.</p>

  <p>Molecular formula = C₂H₄.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Molecular Formula', 15),
  (490, 490, '<h2>Percentage Composition</h2>

  <p><strong>Percentage composition</strong> gives the percentage by mass of each element in a compound.</p>

  <p>Use:</p>

  <p><strong>% of element = (mass of element in one mole of compound ÷ molar mass of compound) × 100</strong></p>

  <h3>Example: Water</h3>

  <p>H₂O has molar mass 18.</p>

  <p>Mass of hydrogen = 2.</p>

  <p>%H = (2 ÷ 18) × 100</p>

  <p>%H = 11.1%</p>

  <p>Mass of oxygen = 16.</p>

  <p>%O = (16 ÷ 18) × 100</p>

  <p>%O = 88.9%</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Percentage Composition', 15),
  (491, 491, '<h2>Limiting Reactant</h2>

  <p>The <strong>limiting reactant</strong> is the reactant that is completely used up first in a chemical reaction.</p>

  <p>It determines the maximum amount of product that can be formed.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</p>

  <p>Suppose there are:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>3 mol H₂</li>
    <li>2 mol O₂</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The equation requires 2 mol H₂ for every 1 mol O₂.</p>

  <p>3 mol H₂ requires only 1.5 mol O₂.</p>

  <p>Since 2 mol O₂ are available, hydrogen runs out first.</p>

  <p>Therefore, <strong>H₂ is the limiting reactant</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Limiting Reactant', 15),
  (492, 492, '<h2>Gas Volumes at Room Conditions</h2>

  <p>At room temperature and pressure, one mole of a gas occupies approximately <strong>24 dm³</strong>.</p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>Volume = moles × 24 dm³</strong></p>

  <p>And:</p>

  <p><strong>Moles = volume ÷ 24 dm³</strong></p>

  <h3>Unit conversion</h3>

  <p>1 dm³ = 1000 cm³.</p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>24 dm³ = 24,000 cm³</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gas Volumes at Room Conditions', 15),
  (493, 493, '<h2>Gas Volume Calculations</h2>

  <p>The volume of a gas can be connected to a chemical equation using mole ratios.</p>

  <p>At the same temperature and pressure, gas volumes react in the same ratios as their mole coefficients.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃</p>

  <p>The volume ratio is:</p>

  <p><strong>N₂ : H₂ : NH₃ = 1 : 3 : 2</strong></p>

  <p>If 10 dm³ of nitrogen reacts completely:</p>

  <p>Hydrogen required = 10 × 3 = 30 dm³.</p>

  <p>Ammonia produced = 10 × 2 = 20 dm³.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gas Volume Calculations', 15),
  (494, 494, '<h2>Percentage Yield</h2>

  <p>The <strong>theoretical yield</strong> is the maximum amount of product that should be produced according to calculation.</p>

  <p>The <strong>actual yield</strong> is the amount of product obtained experimentally.</p>

  <p>Percentage yield is calculated using:</p>

  <p><strong>% yield = (actual yield ÷ theoretical yield) × 100</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>The theoretical yield is 10 g and the actual yield is 8 g.</p>

  <p>% yield = (8 ÷ 10) × 100</p>

  <p>% yield = 80%</p>

  <p>The actual yield is usually lower than the theoretical yield because reactions may not go to completion and some product may be lost.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Percentage Yield', 15),
  (495, 495, '<h2>Chemical Reactions</h2>
  <p>A chemical reaction is a process in which one or more substances change to form new substances with different properties.</p>

  <h3>Reactants and products</h3>
  <p>The substances present before a chemical reaction are called <strong>reactants</strong>.</p>
  <p>The new substances formed are called <strong>products</strong>.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>
  <p><strong>Magnesium + Oxygen → Magnesium oxide</strong></p>
  <p>Magnesium and oxygen are the reactants. Magnesium oxide is the product.</p>

  <h3>What happens during a reaction?</h3>
  <p>Atoms are rearranged. The atoms themselves are not destroyed or created.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Reactions', 15),
  (496, 496, '<h2>Reactants</h2>
  <p>Reactants are the substances that take part in a chemical reaction and are changed during the reaction.</p>

  <p>In a chemical equation, reactants are written on the <strong>left-hand side</strong> of the arrow.</p>

  <p>Example:</p>
  <p><strong>2Mg + O<sub>2</sub> → 2MgO</strong></p>

  <p>Magnesium and oxygen are the reactants.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p><strong>Left of the arrow = reactants.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactants', 15),
  (497, 497, '<h2>Products</h2>
  <p>Products are the new substances formed during a chemical reaction.</p>

  <p>In a chemical equation, products are written on the <strong>right-hand side</strong> of the arrow.</p>

  <p>Example:</p>
  <p><strong>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>Water is the product.</p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>
  <p><strong>Right of the arrow = products.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Products', 15),
  (498, 498, '<h2>Signs of a Chemical Reaction</h2>
  <p>A chemical reaction may be identified by observable changes.</p>

  <h3>Common signs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Formation of a new colour</li>
    <li>Production of a gas</li>
    <li>Formation of a precipitate</li>
    <li>Change in temperature</li>
    <li>Production of light</li>
    <li>Production of sound</li>
    <li>A new smell may be produced</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A precipitate is an insoluble solid formed when two solutions react.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Signs of Chemical Reaction', 15),
  (499, 499, '<h2>Conservation of Mass</h2>
  <p>The law of conservation of mass states that mass is neither created nor destroyed during a chemical reaction.</p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>
  <p><strong>Total mass of reactants = Total mass of products</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>If 10 g of substance A reacts with 6 g of substance B:</p>
  <p>Mass of reactants = 10 + 6 = 16 g</p>
  <p>Therefore the total mass of products must be <strong>16 g</strong>.</p>

  <p>If a reaction appears to lose mass, a gas may have escaped from an open system.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conservation of Mass', 15),
  (500, 500, '<h2>Chemical Equations</h2>
  <p>A chemical equation represents a chemical reaction using chemical formulae and symbols.</p>

  <p>Example:</p>
  <p><strong>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</strong></p>

  <p>The plus sign separates different substances.</p>
  <p>The arrow means “reacts to form” or “produces”.</p>

  <h3>Equation structure</h3>
  <p><strong>Reactants → Products</strong></p>

  <p>A chemical equation should represent the substances involved and the correct proportions in which they react.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Equations', 15);
INSERT INTO public.lessons (id, topic_id, content, summary, estimated_minutes) VALUES
  (501, 501, '<h2>Writing Chemical Equations</h2>
  <p>To write a chemical equation from a word equation:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Identify the reactants.</li>
    <li>Identify the products.</li>
    <li>Write the correct chemical formula for each substance.</li>
    <li>Use an arrow between reactants and products.</li>
    <li>Balance the equation.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Hydrogen reacts with oxygen to form water.</p>

  <p>Formulae:</p>
  <p>H₂ + O₂ → H₂O</p>

  <p>This equation is not balanced yet.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Writing Chemical Equations', 15),
  (502, 502, '<h2>State Symbols</h2>
  <p>State symbols show the physical state of each substance in a chemical equation.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>(s)</strong> = solid</li>
    <li><strong>(l)</strong> = liquid</li>
    <li><strong>(g)</strong> = gas</li>
    <li><strong>(aq)</strong> = aqueous, dissolved in water</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p><strong>Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)</strong></p>

  <p>The symbols provide additional information about the physical states of the substances.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for State Symbols', 15),
  (503, 503, '<h2>Balancing Chemical Equations</h2>
  <p>A chemical equation is balanced when the number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides.</p>

  <p>Only the <strong>coefficients</strong> in front of formulae may be changed.</p>

  <p>You must not change the small numbers inside chemical formulae.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>H₂ + O₂ → H₂O</p>

  <p>There are 2 oxygen atoms on the left but only 1 on the right.</p>

  <p>Putting 2 before H₂O gives:</p>

  <p><strong>H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>Now hydrogen is unbalanced, so put 2 before H₂:</p>

  <p><strong>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Balancing Chemical Equations', 15),
  (504, 504, '<h2>Balancing Simple Equations</h2>
  <p>Simple equations can often be balanced by counting atoms and adjusting coefficients one element at a time.</p>

  <h3>Example 1</h3>
  <p>Mg + O₂ → MgO</p>
  <p>Put 2 before MgO:</p>
  <p>Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</p>
  <p>Now put 2 before Mg:</p>
  <p><strong>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</strong></p>

  <h3>Example 2</h3>
  <p>H₂ + Cl₂ → HCl</p>
  <p><strong>H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Balancing Simple Equations', 15),
  (505, 505, '<h2>Balancing More Complex Equations</h2>
  <p>More complex equations contain several different elements or compounds. Balance one element at a time and check the entire equation at the end.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Fe + O₂ → Fe₂O₃</p>

  <p>Use coefficients to obtain equal numbers of iron and oxygen atoms:</p>

  <p><strong>4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃</strong></p>

  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Iron: 4 on each side</li>
    <li>Oxygen: 6 on each side</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Balancing More Complex Equations', 15),
  (506, 506, '<h2>Combination Reactions</h2>
  <p>A combination reaction is a reaction in which two or more substances combine to form a single product.</p>

  <p>General pattern:</p>
  <p><strong>A + B → AB</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p><strong>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</strong></p>

  <p>Magnesium and oxygen combine to form one product, magnesium oxide.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Combination Reactions', 15),
  (507, 507, '<h2>Decomposition Reactions</h2>
  <p>A decomposition reaction occurs when one compound breaks down into two or more simpler substances.</p>

  <p>General pattern:</p>
  <p><strong>AB → A + B</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p><strong>CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>One compound produces two products.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Decomposition Reactions', 15),
  (508, 508, '<h2>Displacement Reactions</h2>
  <p>A displacement reaction occurs when a more reactive element displaces a less reactive element from its compound.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p><strong>Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu</strong></p>

  <p>Zinc is more reactive than copper, so zinc displaces copper from copper(II) sulphate.</p>

  <p>The reaction can occur because zinc is higher than copper in the reactivity series.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Displacement Reactions', 15),
  (509, 509, '<h2>Neutralisation Reactions</h2>
  <p>Neutralisation is a reaction between an acid and a base or alkali to form a salt and water.</p>

  <p>General pattern:</p>
  <p><strong>Acid + Base → Salt + Water</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p><strong>HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>Hydrochloric acid reacts with sodium hydroxide to form sodium chloride and water.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Neutralisation Reactions', 15),
  (510, 510, '<h2>Combustion Reactions</h2>
  <p>Combustion is a chemical reaction in which a substance burns in oxygen and releases energy, usually as heat and often as light.</p>

  <h3>Complete combustion of a hydrocarbon</h3>
  <p>A hydrocarbon contains carbon and hydrogen.</p>

  <p>Complete combustion produces:</p>
  <p><strong>Carbon dioxide + water</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p><strong>CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Combustion Reactions', 15),
  (511, 511, '<h2>Properties of Acids</h2>
  <p>Acids are substances that produce hydrogen ions, H<sup>+</sup>, when dissolved in water.</p>

  <h3>Common properties</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>They have pH values below 7.</li>
    <li>They turn blue litmus paper red.</li>
    <li>They react with some metals to produce hydrogen gas.</li>
    <li>They react with carbonates to produce carbon dioxide.</li>
    <li>They react with bases and alkalis to form salts and water.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Examples include hydrochloric acid (HCl), sulphuric(VI) acid (H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) and ethanoic acid (CH<sub>3</sub>COOH).</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Acids', 15),
  (512, 512, '<h2>Reactions of Acids with Metals</h2>
  <p>Dilute acids react with suitable metals to form a <strong>salt and hydrogen gas</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>Acid + Metal → Salt + Hydrogen</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid:</p>
  <p><strong>Mg + 2HCl → MgCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub></strong></p>

  <p>Fizzing occurs because hydrogen gas is produced.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Acids with Metals', 15),
  (513, 513, '<h2>Reactions of Acids with Carbonates</h2>
  <p>Acids react with carbonates to form a <strong>salt, water and carbon dioxide</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>Acid + Carbonate → Salt + Water + Carbon dioxide</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Hydrochloric acid reacts with calcium carbonate:</p>
  <p><strong>2HCl + CaCO<sub>3</sub> → CaCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub></strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Acids with Carbonates', 15),
  (514, 514, '<h2>Reactions of Acids with Hydrogen Carbonates</h2>
  <p>Acids also react with hydrogen carbonates to produce a <strong>salt, water and carbon dioxide</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>Acid + Hydrogen carbonate → Salt + Water + Carbon dioxide</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Sodium hydrogen carbonate reacts with hydrochloric acid:</p>
  <p><strong>NaHCO<sub>3</sub> + HCl → NaCl + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub></strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Acids with Hydrogen Carbonates', 15),
  (515, 515, '<h2>Reactions of Acids with Metal Oxides</h2>
  <p>Acids react with metal oxides to form a <strong>salt and water</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>Acid + Metal oxide → Salt + Water</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Copper(II) oxide reacts with sulphuric(VI) acid:</p>
  <p><strong>CuO + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> → CuSO<sub>4</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Acids with Metal Oxides', 15),
  (516, 516, '<h2>Bases</h2>
  <p>A base is a substance that reacts with an acid to form a salt and water.</p>

  <p>Many metal oxides and metal hydroxides are bases.</p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>CuO — copper(II) oxide</li>
    <li>MgO — magnesium oxide</li>
    <li>Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub> — copper(II) hydroxide</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Some bases are soluble in water. These soluble bases are called <strong>alkalis</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Bases', 15),
  (517, 517, '<h2>Alkalis</h2>
  <p>An alkali is a base that dissolves in water and produces hydroxide ions, OH<sup>−</sup>.</p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Sodium hydroxide, NaOH</li>
    <li>Potassium hydroxide, KOH</li>
    <li>Calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub></li>
    <li>Aqueous ammonia</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Alkalis have pH values above 7.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alkalis', 15),
  (518, 518, '<h2>Litmus</h2>
  <p>Litmus is an acid-base indicator used to distinguish acidic solutions from alkaline solutions.</p>

  <h3>Colour changes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Acid: blue litmus turns red.</li>
    <li>Alkali: red litmus turns blue.</li>
    <li>Neutral solution: no change to either litmus colour.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Litmus', 15),
  (519, 519, '<h2>Universal Indicator</h2>
  <p>Universal indicator is a mixture of indicators that produces different colours over a wide range of pH values.</p>

  <h3>Approximate colour range</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Strongly acidic: red</li>
    <li>Weakly acidic: orange/yellow</li>
    <li>Neutral: green</li>
    <li>Weakly alkaline: blue</li>
    <li>Strongly alkaline: purple</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The colour is compared with a pH chart to estimate the pH.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Universal Indicator', 15),
  (520, 520, '<h2>pH Scale</h2>
  <p>The pH scale is used to describe how acidic or alkaline an aqueous solution is.</p>

  <p>The commonly used scale runs from about <strong>0 to 14</strong>.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>pH below 7 = acidic</li>
    <li>pH 7 = neutral</li>
    <li>pH above 7 = alkaline</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The lower the pH, the more acidic the solution generally is.</p>
  <p>The higher the pH, the more alkaline the solution generally is.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for pH Scale', 15),
  (521, 521, '<h2>Interpreting pH</h2>
  <p>The pH value allows solutions to be compared according to their acidity or alkalinity.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <p>pH 2 is strongly acidic.</p>
  <p>pH 5 is weakly acidic.</p>
  <p>pH 7 is neutral.</p>
  <p>pH 9 is weakly alkaline.</p>
  <p>pH 13 is strongly alkaline.</p>

  <p>For the same type of aqueous solution, a decrease in pH indicates increasing acidity, while an increase indicates increasing alkalinity.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interpreting pH', 15),
  (522, 522, '<h2>Strong and Weak Acids</h2>
  <p>A strong acid ionises extensively in water, producing a high concentration of hydrogen ions.</p>

  <p>A weak acid ionises only partially in water.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Hydrochloric acid — strong acid</li>
    <li>Sulphuric(VI) acid — strong acid</li>
    <li>Ethanoic acid — weak acid</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Strength is not the same as concentration. A dilute strong acid can have a lower concentration than a concentrated weak acid.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Strong and Weak Acids', 15),
  (523, 523, '<h2>Strong and Weak Bases</h2>
  <p>A strong base ionises extensively in water and produces a high concentration of hydroxide ions.</p>

  <p>A weak base reacts or ionises only partially in water.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Sodium hydroxide — strong base</li>
    <li>Potassium hydroxide — strong base</li>
    <li>Aqueous ammonia — weak base</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Strong and Weak Bases', 15),
  (524, 524, '<h2>Neutralisation</h2>
  <p>Neutralisation occurs when an acid reacts with a base or alkali to form a salt and water.</p>

  <p><strong>Acid + Base → Salt + Water</strong></p>

  <h3>Ionic idea</h3>
  <p>In a simple strong acid–strong alkali reaction:</p>
  <p><strong>H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq) → H<sub>2</sub>O(l)</strong></p>

  <p>The hydrogen ions from the acid combine with hydroxide ions from the alkali to form water.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Neutralisation', 15),
  (525, 525, '<h2>Soluble Salts</h2>
  <p>A soluble salt is a salt that dissolves in water to form an aqueous solution.</p>

  <p>Examples include sodium chloride, potassium nitrate and copper(II) sulphate.</p>

  <p>Soluble salts can be prepared using suitable reactions followed by crystallisation or evaporation of water.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Soluble Salts', 15),
  (526, 526, '<h2>Insoluble Salts</h2>
  <p>An insoluble salt does not dissolve appreciably in water.</p>

  <p>Insoluble salts can often be prepared by mixing two soluble solutions so that an insoluble product forms as a <strong>precipitate</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Lead(II) nitrate reacts with sodium chloride:</p>

  <p><strong>Pb(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>(aq) + 2NaCl(aq) → PbCl<sub>2</sub>(s) + 2NaNO<sub>3</sub>(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>Lead(II) chloride is the insoluble precipitate.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Insoluble Salts', 15),
  (527, 527, '<h2>Preparing Soluble Salts from Acids and Metals</h2>
  <p>A soluble salt can be prepared by reacting a suitable acid with an excess of a reactive metal.</p>

  <h3>General method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Place the dilute acid in a suitable container.</li>
    <li>Add the metal gradually.</li>
    <li>Continue until no more metal reacts.</li>
    <li>Filter off excess metal.</li>
    <li>Concentrate the solution.</li>
    <li>Cool to allow crystals to form.</li>
    <li>Filter and dry the crystals.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Preparing Soluble Salts from Acids and Metals', 15),
  (528, 528, '<h2>Preparing Soluble Salts from Insoluble Bases</h2>
  <p>An insoluble metal oxide or hydroxide can react with an acid to prepare a soluble salt.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>To prepare copper(II) sulphate:</p>
  <p><strong>CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O</strong></p>

  <h3>Method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Warm dilute acid gently.</li>
    <li>Add the insoluble base in small portions.</li>
    <li>Continue until no more base reacts.</li>
    <li>Filter off excess base.</li>
    <li>Concentrate the solution.</li>
    <li>Cool to form crystals.</li>
    <li>Filter and dry the crystals.</li>
  </ol>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Preparing Soluble Salts from Insoluble Bases', 15),
  (529, 529, '<h2>Preparing Soluble Salts from Acids and Carbonates</h2>
  <p>Soluble salts can be prepared by reacting an acid with a suitable carbonate.</p>

  <p><strong>Acid + Carbonate → Salt + Water + Carbon dioxide</strong></p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Calcium carbonate reacts with nitric acid:</p>
  <p><strong>CaCO₃ + 2HNO₃ → Ca(NO₃)₂ + H₂O + CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>An excess of the carbonate is used so that all the acid reacts. The excess solid is then filtered off.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Preparing Soluble Salts from Acids and Carbonates', 15),
  (530, 530, '<h2>Titration</h2>
  <p>Titration is a laboratory method used to determine the volume of one solution required to react completely with a measured volume of another solution.</p>

  <h3>Main apparatus</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Burette</li>
    <li>Pipette</li>
    <li>Conical flask</li>
    <li>Indicator</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Basic procedure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Use a pipette to transfer a known volume of one solution into a conical flask.</li>
    <li>Add a suitable indicator.</li>
    <li>Fill the burette with the other solution.</li>
    <li>Record the initial burette reading.</li>
    <li>Add the solution from the burette while swirling the flask.</li>
    <li>Stop at the end point indicated by the indicator colour change.</li>
    <li>Record the final burette reading.</li>
    <li>Calculate the titre.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Titre = final burette reading − initial burette reading</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Titration', 15),
  (531, 531, '<h2>Exothermic Reactions</h2>
  <p>An exothermic reaction is a chemical reaction that releases energy to the surroundings, usually as heat.</p>

  <p>Because heat is released, the temperature of the surroundings usually increases.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Combustion of fuels</li>
    <li>Neutralisation of an acid by an alkali</li>
    <li>Many oxidation reactions</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For an exothermic reaction:</p>
  <p><strong>Energy released to surroundings &gt; energy absorbed from surroundings</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Exothermic Reactions', 15),
  (532, 532, '<h2>Endothermic Reactions</h2>
  <p>An endothermic reaction is a chemical reaction that absorbs energy from the surroundings.</p>

  <p>The absorbed energy is often transferred as heat, causing the surroundings to become cooler.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Thermal decomposition of calcium carbonate</li>
    <li>Some reactions between salts and water</li>
    <li>Photosynthesis</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For an endothermic reaction, energy is transferred from the surroundings into the reacting system.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Endothermic Reactions', 15),
  (533, 533, '<h2>Energy Profile Diagrams</h2>
  <p>An energy profile diagram shows how the energy of a reaction changes from reactants to products.</p>

  <h3>Important features</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Reactant energy</li>
    <li>Product energy</li>
    <li>Activation energy</li>
    <li>Overall energy change</li>
  </ul>

  <p>In an exothermic reaction, the products are at a lower energy level than the reactants.</p>

  <p>In an endothermic reaction, the products are at a higher energy level than the reactants.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy Profile Diagrams', 15),
  (534, 534, '<h2>Activation Energy</h2>
  <p>Activation energy is the minimum energy that reacting particles must have for a successful chemical reaction to occur.</p>

  <p>Particles must collide with enough energy to overcome the energy barrier before new bonds can form and old bonds can break.</p>

  <h3>Important idea</h3>
  <p>A reaction can release energy overall and still require activation energy to start.</p>

  <p>For example, a fuel releases energy when it burns, but it normally requires a spark or flame to initiate combustion.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Activation Energy', 15),
  (535, 535, '<h2>Specific Heat Capacity</h2>
  <p>Specific heat capacity is the amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1°C.</p>

  <p>The symbol for specific heat capacity is <strong>c</strong>.</p>

  <p>The relationship is:</p>

  <p><strong>Q = mcΔT</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Q = heat energy in joules (J)</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms (kg)</li>
    <li>c = specific heat capacity in J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹</li>
    <li>ΔT = temperature change in °C</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Specific Heat Capacity', 15),
  (536, 536, '<h2>Heat Energy Calculations</h2>
  <p>Heat energy calculations use the relationship:</p>

  <p><strong>Q = mcΔT</strong></p>

  <p>Always check that the mass is in kilograms when using the SI form of the formula.</p>

  <h3>Rearranging the formula</h3>
  <p><strong>Q = mcΔT</strong></p>
  <p><strong>m = Q ÷ (cΔT)</strong></p>
  <p><strong>c = Q ÷ (mΔT)</strong></p>
  <p><strong>ΔT = Q ÷ (mc)</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heat Energy Calculations', 15),
  (537, 537, '<h2>Latent Heat of Fusion</h2>
  <p>Latent heat of fusion is the energy required to change 1 kg of a substance from solid to liquid at its melting point without changing its temperature.</p>

  <p>The relationship is:</p>
  <p><strong>Q = mL<sub>f</sub></strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Q = heat energy in joules</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms</li>
    <li>L<sub>f</sub> = specific latent heat of fusion in J kg⁻¹</li>
  </ul>

  <p>During melting, energy is used to overcome attractive forces between particles rather than increasing temperature.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Latent Heat of Fusion', 15),
  (538, 538, '<h2>Latent Heat of Vaporisation</h2>
  <p>Specific latent heat of vaporisation is the energy required to change 1 kg of a liquid into gas at its boiling point without changing its temperature.</p>

  <p>The relationship is:</p>
  <p><strong>Q = mL<sub>v</sub></strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Q = heat energy in joules</li>
    <li>m = mass in kilograms</li>
    <li>L<sub>v</sub> = specific latent heat of vaporisation in J kg⁻¹</li>
  </ul>

  <p>During boiling, energy is used to separate particles from the liquid.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Latent Heat of Vaporisation', 15),
  (539, 539, '<h2>Rate of Reaction</h2>
  <p>The rate of a chemical reaction describes how quickly reactants are used up or products are formed.</p>

  <p>A reaction that happens quickly has a high rate. A reaction that happens slowly has a low rate.</p>

  <h3>Simple expression</h3>
  <p><strong>Rate = change in amount ÷ time taken</strong></p>

  <p>The amount may be measured using mass, volume, concentration or another suitable quantity.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Rate of Reaction', 15),
  (540, 540, '<h2>Effect of Temperature on Rate of Reaction</h2>
  <p>Increasing temperature generally increases the rate of a chemical reaction.</p>

  <h3>Why?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Particles gain kinetic energy.</li>
    <li>They move faster.</li>
    <li>Collisions occur more frequently.</li>
    <li>A greater proportion of collisions have enough energy to overcome activation energy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Therefore the number of successful collisions per second increases.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Temperature on Rate', 15),
  (541, 541, '<h2>Effect of Concentration on Rate of Reaction</h2>
  <p>For reactions involving solutions, increasing the concentration of a reactant generally increases the rate of reaction.</p>

  <h3>Why?</h3>
  <p>A concentrated solution contains more reacting particles in a given volume.</p>

  <p>Therefore particles collide more frequently, producing more successful collisions per unit time.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Concentration on Rate', 15),
  (542, 542, '<h2>Effect of Surface Area on Rate of Reaction</h2>
  <p>For a reaction involving a solid, increasing the surface area of the solid generally increases the rate of reaction.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>Powdered calcium carbonate reacts faster with acid than large pieces of calcium carbonate of the same total mass.</p>

  <h3>Why?</h3>
  <p>More particles of the solid are exposed to the other reactant, giving more collisions per second.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Surface Area on Rate', 15),
  (543, 543, '<h2>Catalysts</h2>
  <p>A catalyst is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without being permanently used up in the reaction.</p>

  <h3>How catalysts work</h3>
  <p>A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy.</p>

  <p>Because the activation energy is lower, more collisions have enough energy to result in a reaction.</p>

  <h3>Important</h3>
  <p>A catalyst does not change the overall chemical equation or the amount of products formed at equilibrium. It changes how quickly the reaction occurs.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Catalysts', 15),
  (544, 544, '<h2>Physical Properties of Metals</h2>

  <p>Metals have physical properties that distinguish them from many non-metals.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Most metals are solid at room temperature.</li>
    <li>They usually have a shiny surface when freshly cut or polished.</li>
    <li>They are generally good conductors of heat and electricity.</li>
    <li>They are generally strong and hard.</li>
    <li>They can usually be shaped without breaking.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Examples of metals include iron, copper, aluminium, zinc, silver and gold.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> State two physical properties of copper.</p>
  <p>Copper is a good conductor of electricity and is malleable.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong> Why is copper used for electrical wires?</p>
  <p>Because copper conducts electricity well and can be drawn into wires.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong> Why can metals be used to make tools?</p>
  <p>Many metals are strong and hard, allowing them to withstand forces.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Physical Properties of Metals', 15),
  (545, 545, '<h2>Electrical Conductivity of Metals</h2>

  <p>Metals conduct electricity because they contain mobile electrons that can move through the metal.</p>

  <p>This makes metals useful for electrical cables, contacts and electrical components.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> Copper is used for electrical cables because it conducts electricity well.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong> Aluminium is also used in electrical transmission because it conducts electricity and has a relatively low density.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong> A plastic coating is placed around a copper wire because plastic is an electrical insulator.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrical Conductivity', 15),
  (546, 546, '<h2>Malleability of Metals</h2>

  <p><strong>Malleability</strong> is the ability of a material to be hammered or pressed into sheets without breaking.</p>

  <p>Many metals are malleable.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> Aluminium can be rolled into thin sheets.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong> Gold can be beaten into extremely thin sheets.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong> Aluminium foil is possible because aluminium is malleable.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Malleability', 15),
  (547, 547, '<h2>Ductility of Metals</h2>

  <p><strong>Ductility</strong> is the ability of a material to be drawn or stretched into wires without breaking.</p>

  <p>Many metals are ductile.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> Copper can be drawn into electrical wires.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong> Aluminium can be formed into wires.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong> The ability to make wires from copper depends partly on its ductility.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ductility', 15),
  (548, 548, '<h2>Reactivity Series</h2>

  <p>The reactivity series arranges metals from more reactive to less reactive.</p>

  <p>A commonly used order is:</p>

  <p><strong>Potassium → Sodium → Calcium → Magnesium → Aluminium → Carbon → Zinc → Iron → Hydrogen → Copper → Silver → Gold</strong></p>

  <p>Carbon and hydrogen are included as reference points even though they are not metals.</p>

  <p>A metal higher in the series is generally more reactive than a metal below it.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> Magnesium is more reactive than copper because magnesium is higher in the series.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong> Zinc is more reactive than iron because zinc is above iron.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong> Copper is less reactive than iron because copper is below iron.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactivity Series', 15),
  (549, 549, '<h2>Displacement Reactions</h2>

  <p>A more reactive metal can displace a less reactive metal from a compound of that metal.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu</strong></p>

  <p>Iron is more reactive than copper, so iron displaces copper from copper(II) sulphate.</p>

  <p>A less reactive metal cannot displace a more reactive metal from its compound.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong></p>
  <p>Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong></p>
  <p>Mg + CuSO₄ → MgSO₄ + Cu</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong></p>
  <p>Cu + ZnSO₄ → No reaction</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Displacement Reactions', 15),
  (550, 550, '<h2>Ores</h2>

  <p>An <strong>ore</strong> is a naturally occurring mineral or rock containing a metal or metal compound from which the metal can be extracted economically.</p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Hematite — an important ore of iron</li>
    <li>Bauxite — the main ore of aluminium</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The method used to extract a metal depends strongly on its position in the reactivity series.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> Iron can be extracted from hematite.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong> Aluminium is obtained from aluminium oxide derived from bauxite.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong> Highly reactive metals require more powerful extraction methods than less reactive metals.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ores', 15),
  (551, 551, '<h2>Reduction of Metal Compounds</h2>

  <p><strong>Reduction</strong> is the removal of oxygen from a substance, or the gain of electrons.</p>

  <p>During extraction, a metal compound can be reduced to form the metal.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>Iron(III) oxide loses oxygen and forms iron.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> Fe₂O₃ → Fe is a reduction because oxygen is removed from the iron compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong> CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O. Copper(II) oxide is reduced to copper.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong> A metal oxide that loses oxygen has undergone reduction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reduction', 15),
  (552, 552, '<h2>Extraction of Iron</h2>

  <p>Iron is extracted from iron ore such as hematite, Fe₂O₃, in a blast furnace.</p>

  <h3>Main raw materials</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Hematite — source of iron(III) oxide</li>
    <li>Coke — provides carbon and produces carbon monoxide</li>
    <li>Limestone — helps remove impurities</li>
    <li>Hot air — supplies oxygen for combustion</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important reactions</h3>

  <p>Carbon burns:</p>

  <p><strong>C + O₂ → CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>Carbon dioxide reacts with more carbon:</p>

  <p><strong>CO₂ + C → 2CO</strong></p>

  <p>Carbon monoxide reduces iron(III) oxide:</p>

  <p><strong>Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>Molten iron collects at the bottom of the furnace.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Extraction of Iron', 15),
  (553, 553, '<h2>Rusting of Iron</h2>

  <p><strong>Rusting</strong> is the corrosion of iron in the presence of oxygen and water.</p>

  <p>The rust formed is hydrated iron(III) oxide.</p>

  <p>A simplified representation is:</p>

  <p><strong>4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃</strong></p>

  <p>In real rust, water is also involved, so rust is commonly represented as hydrated iron(III) oxide.</p>

  <h3>Effects of rusting</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Iron objects become weakened.</li>
    <li>Structures can lose strength.</li>
    <li>Machines and tools can be damaged.</li>
    <li>Money is spent replacing or repairing corroded materials.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Rusting', 15),
  (554, 554, '<h2>Conditions for Rusting</h2>

  <p>Rusting requires both <strong>water</strong> and <strong>oxygen</strong>.</p>

  <p>This can be demonstrated using iron nails under different conditions.</p>

  <h3>Experiment</h3>

  <p><strong>Tube A:</strong> Nail exposed to air and water → rust forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Tube B:</strong> Nail kept dry with a drying agent → little or no rust.</p>

  <p><strong>Tube C:</strong> Nail placed in boiled water covered with oil → little or no rust because oxygen is excluded.</p>

  <p>The experiment shows that both oxygen and water are needed for ordinary rusting.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conditions for Rusting', 15),
  (555, 555, '<h2>Prevention of Rusting</h2>

  <p>Rusting can be prevented by stopping iron from coming into contact with water or oxygen.</p>

  <h3>Barrier methods</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Painting</li>
    <li>Oiling</li>
    <li>Greasing</li>
    <li>Coating with plastic</li>
    <li>Galvanising with zinc</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These methods form a barrier between the iron and the substances needed for rusting.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> Bicycle chains can be oiled to keep water and oxygen away.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong> Iron gates can be painted.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong> Iron sheets can be galvanised by coating them with zinc.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prevention of Rusting', 15),
  (556, 556, '<h2>Alloys</h2>

  <p>An <strong>alloy</strong> is a mixture containing a metal and one or more other elements.</p>

  <p>Alloys are often made to obtain properties that are different from those of the pure metal.</p>

  <h3>Common alloys</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Steel:</strong> mainly iron with carbon.</li>
    <li><strong>Stainless steel:</strong> iron with carbon and other elements such as chromium.</li>
    <li><strong>Brass:</strong> copper and zinc.</li>
    <li><strong>Bronze:</strong> copper and tin.</li>
    <li><strong>Duralumin:</strong> an aluminium-based alloy containing other elements.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Alloys are commonly harder or stronger than the pure metals from which they are made.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alloys', 15),
  (557, 557, '<h2>Uses of Metals</h2>

  <p>The use of a metal depends on its physical and chemical properties.</p>

  <h3>Common examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Copper:</strong> electrical wires because it conducts electricity well and is ductile.</p>

  <p><strong>Aluminium:</strong> aircraft bodies, cooking utensils and electrical transmission because it is relatively light, conducts electricity and is resistant to corrosion.</p>

  <p><strong>Iron and steel:</strong> buildings, bridges, tools and machines because they are strong.</p>

  <p><strong>Zinc:</strong> galvanising iron and steel because it helps protect them from corrosion.</p>

  <p><strong>Gold:</strong> jewellery because it is attractive and relatively unreactive.</p>

  <p><strong>Silver:</strong> jewellery and electrical contacts because it has very high electrical conductivity.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uses of Metals', 15),
  (558, 558, '<h2>Electrolytes</h2>

  <p>An <strong>electrolyte</strong> is a substance that conducts electricity when molten or dissolved in water because it contains mobile ions.</p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Molten sodium chloride</li>
    <li>Sodium chloride solution</li>
    <li>Copper(II) sulphate solution</li>
    <li>Dilute hydrochloric acid</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Solid ionic compounds generally do not conduct electricity because their ions are fixed in a lattice.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> Solid NaCl does not conduct electricity effectively because its ions cannot move freely.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong> Molten NaCl conducts because its ions are free to move.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong> NaCl solution conducts because Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions move through the solution.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrolytes', 15),
  (559, 559, '<h2>Meaning of Electrolysis</h2>

  <p><strong>Electrolysis</strong> is the chemical decomposition of an ionic compound in the molten state or in aqueous solution by passing an electric current through it.</p>

  <p>The substance being decomposed must contain mobile ions.</p>

  <p>During electrolysis:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Positive ions move towards the negative electrode.</li>
    <li>Negative ions move towards the positive electrode.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Molten sodium chloride can be decomposed into sodium and chlorine:</p>

  <p><strong>2NaCl(l) → 2Na(l) + Cl₂(g)</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning of Electrolysis', 15),
  (560, 560, '<h2>Electrodes</h2>

  <p><strong>Electrodes</strong> are conductors through which electric current enters or leaves an electrolyte.</p>

  <p>There are two electrodes in an electrolytic cell:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cathode:</strong> negative electrode in electrolysis.</li>
    <li><strong>Anode:</strong> positive electrode in electrolysis.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Positive ions move towards the cathode.</p>

  <p>Negative ions move towards the anode.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>During electrolysis of molten NaCl:</p>

  <p>Na⁺ → cathode</p>

  <p>Cl⁻ → anode</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrodes', 15),
  (561, 561, '<h2>Anode</h2>

  <p>The <strong>anode</strong> is the positive electrode during electrolysis.</p>

  <p>Negative ions, called anions, move towards the anode.</p>

  <p>At the anode, anions lose electrons. This is oxidation.</p>

  <p>A useful memory rule is:</p>

  <p><strong>AN OX</strong> → Anode = Oxidation.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>During electrolysis of molten sodium chloride:</p>

  <p><strong>2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻</strong></p>

  <p>Chloride ions lose electrons at the anode.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Anode', 15),
  (562, 562, '<h2>Cathode</h2>

  <p>The <strong>cathode</strong> is the negative electrode during electrolysis.</p>

  <p>Positive ions, called cations, move towards the cathode.</p>

  <p>At the cathode, cations gain electrons. This is reduction.</p>

  <p>A useful memory rule is:</p>

  <p><strong>RED CAT</strong> → Reduction = Cathode.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>During electrolysis of molten sodium chloride:</p>

  <p><strong>Na⁺ + e⁻ → Na</strong></p>

  <p>Sodium ions gain electrons at the cathode.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cathode', 15),
  (563, 563, '<h2>Electrolysis of Molten Compounds</h2>

  <p>When an ionic compound is molten, its ions are free to move and can be discharged at the electrodes.</p>

  <p>For molten sodium chloride:</p>

  <p><strong>At the cathode:</strong></p>
  <p>Na⁺ + e⁻ → Na</p>

  <p><strong>At the anode:</strong></p>
  <p>2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Overall:</strong></p>
  <p>2NaCl(l) → 2Na(l) + Cl₂(g)</p>

  <p>The products are sodium metal and chlorine gas.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrolysis of Molten Compounds', 15),
  (564, 564, '<h2>Electrolysis of Aqueous Solutions</h2>

  <p>An aqueous electrolyte contains ions from the dissolved compound and ions from water.</p>

  <p>Therefore, more than one type of ion may compete for discharge at an electrode.</p>

  <p>For example, aqueous sodium chloride contains:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Na⁺</li>
    <li>Cl⁻</li>
    <li>H⁺ from water</li>
    <li>OH⁻ from water</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The products depend on which ions are preferentially discharged at each electrode.</p>

  <h3>Important idea</h3>

  <p>Do not assume that the ions in the dissolved salt are automatically the products.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrolysis of Aqueous Solutions', 15),
  (565, 565, '<h2>Electrolysis of Copper(II) Sulphate</h2>

  <p>Aqueous copper(II) sulphate contains Cu²⁺ ions, SO₄²⁻ ions and ions from water.</p>

  <p>Using inert electrodes:</p>

  <p><strong>At the cathode:</strong></p>
  <p>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</p>

  <p>Copper is deposited at the cathode.</p>

  <p><strong>At the anode:</strong></p>
  <p>Water is oxidised to produce oxygen:</p>

  <p><strong>4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻</strong></p>

  <p>Therefore copper is deposited at the cathode and oxygen is produced at the anode when inert electrodes are used.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrolysis of Copper(II) Sulphate', 15),
  (566, 566, '<h2>Electroplating</h2>

  <p><strong>Electroplating</strong> is the coating of one metal object with a thin layer of another metal using electrolysis.</p>

  <h3>Arrangement</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>The object to be coated is made the <strong>cathode</strong>.</li>
    <li>The coating metal is usually the <strong>anode</strong>.</li>
    <li>The electrolyte contains ions of the coating metal.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For copper plating, Cu²⁺ ions gain electrons at the object:</p>

  <p><strong>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</strong></p>

  <p>Copper is therefore deposited onto the cathode.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electroplating', 15),
  (567, 567, '<h2>Applications of Electrolysis</h2>

  <p>Electrolysis has several important applications.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Extraction of very reactive metals.</li>
    <li>Electroplating objects.</li>
    <li>Purification of metals.</li>
    <li>Manufacture of useful chemicals.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Extraction:</strong> Aluminium is extracted from molten aluminium oxide by electrolysis because aluminium is too reactive to be extracted by ordinary chemical reduction with carbon.</p>

  <p><strong>Electroplating:</strong> Objects can be coated with metals such as copper, nickel or chromium.</p>

  <p><strong>Purification:</strong> Electrolysis can be used to purify copper.</p>

  <p><strong>Chemical manufacture:</strong> Electrolysis of brine is used industrially to produce chlorine, hydrogen and sodium hydroxide.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Applications of Electrolysis', 15),
  (568, 568, '<h2>Simple Cells</h2>

  <p>A <strong>simple cell</strong> converts chemical energy into electrical energy.</p>

  <p>A simple cell can be made using two different metals placed in an electrolyte and connected through an external circuit.</p>

  <h3>How a Simple Cell Works</h3>

  <p>The two metals have different tendencies to lose electrons. A chemical reaction causes electrons to flow through the external circuit.</p>

  <p>The movement of electrons through the external circuit produces an electric current.</p>

  <h3>Main Components</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Two electrodes made from different materials</li>
    <li>An electrolyte</li>
    <li>An external conducting circuit</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The greater the difference in the tendencies of the two electrodes to lose or gain electrons, the greater the potential difference that may be produced.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Simple Cells', 15),
  (569, 569, '<h2>Chemical Cell Voltage</h2>

  <p>The <strong>voltage</strong> or <strong>potential difference</strong> of a cell is the electrical energy supplied per unit charge.</p>

  <p>It is measured in <strong>volts (V)</strong>.</p>

  <p>The voltage produced by a chemical cell depends on the materials used as the electrodes and the electrolyte.</p>

  <h3>Connecting Cells in Series</h3>

  <p>When cells are connected in series in the same direction, their voltages add.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>1.5 V + 1.5 V = 3.0 V</strong></p>

  <h3>Connecting Cells in Opposition</h3>

  <p>If cells are connected in opposite directions, their voltages oppose each other.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>3.0 V − 1.5 V = 1.5 V</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Cell Voltage', 15),
  (570, 570, '<h2>Batteries</h2>

  <p>A <strong>battery</strong> is an arrangement of one or more electrochemical cells used to provide electrical energy.</p>

  <h3>Primary Batteries</h3>

  <p>Primary batteries are designed for use until the chemical reactants are substantially consumed. They are generally not intended to be recharged.</p>

  <h3>Secondary Batteries</h3>

  <p>Secondary batteries can be recharged by passing an electric current through them in the appropriate direction.</p>

  <p>Charging drives chemical changes that restore the chemical substances needed for the cell to operate again.</p>

  <h3>Series Connection</h3>

  <p>Cells connected in series increase the total voltage.</p>

  <h3>Parallel Connection</h3>

  <p>Cells connected in parallel can provide the same voltage as one cell while allowing the arrangement to supply current for a longer period, depending on the cells and load.</p>

  <p>Batteries should be handled and disposed of safely because some contain hazardous substances.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Batteries', 15),
  (571, 571, '<h2>Strong Electrolytes</h2>

  <p>A <strong>strong electrolyte</strong> is a substance that completely dissociates or ionises into ions when dissolved in water.</p>

  <p>Because many ions are present and are free to move through the solution, a strong electrolyte conducts electricity well.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Hydrochloric acid, HCl</li>
    <li>Nitric acid, HNO₃</li>
    <li>Sodium hydroxide, NaOH</li>
    <li>Sodium chloride, NaCl</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>When sodium chloride dissolves:</p>

  <p><strong>NaCl(aq) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>The sodium chloride produces mobile ions throughout the solution.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Strong Electrolytes', 15),
  (572, 572, '<h2>Weak Electrolytes</h2>

  <p>A <strong>weak electrolyte</strong> is a substance that only partially ionises or dissociates into ions when dissolved in water.</p>

  <p>The solution therefore contains both ions and un-ionised or undissociated particles.</p>

  <p>Because there are fewer mobile ions than in a strong electrolyte solution of comparable concentration, a weak electrolyte conducts electricity less strongly.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Ethanoic acid is a weak electrolyte:</p>

  <p><strong>CH₃COOH(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + CH₃COO⁻(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>The reversible arrow shows that only part of the ethanoic acid ionises.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>Weak does not mean that the solution contains no ions. It means that ionisation is incomplete.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Weak Electrolytes', 15),
  (573, 573, '<h2>Non-electrolytes</h2>

  <p>A <strong>non-electrolyte</strong> is a substance that does not produce ions in solution and therefore does not conduct electricity through its aqueous solution.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Sugar dissolves in water but remains mainly as neutral sugar molecules.</p>

  <p><strong>C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(s) → C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>The sugar molecules do not separate into ions.</p>

  <p>Therefore, there are no mobile ions available to carry electrical charge through the solution.</p>

  <h3>Comparison</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strong electrolyte:</strong> completely ionises or dissociates.</li>
    <li><strong>Weak electrolyte:</strong> partially ionises or dissociates.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-electrolyte:</strong> does not produce ions in solution.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Non-electrolytes', 15),
  (574, 574, '<h2>Introduction to Organic Chemistry</h2>

  <p><strong>Organic chemistry</strong> is the study of carbon compounds, especially compounds containing carbon and hydrogen.</p>

  <p>Examples of organic compounds include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Methane, CH₄</li>
    <li>Ethane, C₂H₆</li>
    <li>Ethanol, C₂H₅OH</li>
    <li>Ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Carbon forms a very large number of compounds because carbon atoms can bond strongly to other carbon atoms and to many other elements.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>Not every carbon-containing substance is normally classified as an organic compound. For example, carbon dioxide, CO₂, carbon monoxide, CO, and carbonates are generally treated as inorganic compounds in school chemistry.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> CH₄ is an organic compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong> C₂H₅OH is an organic compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong> CO₂ contains carbon but is treated as an inorganic compound.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Introduction to Organic Chemistry', 15),
  (575, 575, '<h2>Tetravalency of Carbon</h2>

  <p>Carbon has atomic number 6 and has four electrons in its outer shell.</p>

  <p>Carbon normally forms <strong>four covalent bonds</strong>. This property is called <strong>tetravalency</strong>.</p>

  <p>For example, methane has the formula CH₄.</p>

  <p>The carbon atom forms four single covalent bonds with four hydrogen atoms.</p>

  <h3>Methane</h3>

  <p>CH₄ contains:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>1 carbon atom</li>
    <li>4 hydrogen atoms</li>
    <li>4 C–H covalent bonds</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Carbon can also form bonds with other carbon atoms, allowing chains and rings to develop.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tetravalency of Carbon', 15),
  (576, 576, '<h2>Catenation</h2>

  <p><strong>Catenation</strong> is the ability of an element to form covalent bonds with atoms of the same element, producing chains, branches or rings.</p>

  <p>Carbon shows extensive catenation.</p>

  <p>Carbon atoms can join together to form:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Short chains</li>
    <li>Long chains</li>
    <li>Branched chains</li>
    <li>Rings</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>Methane: CH₄ → one carbon atom.</p>

  <p>Ethane: C₂H₆ → two carbon atoms joined together.</p>

  <p>Propane: C₃H₈ → three carbon atoms joined in a chain.</p>

  <p>Butane: C₄H₁₀ → four carbon atoms joined in a chain.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Catenation', 15),
  (577, 577, '<h2>Hydrocarbons</h2>

  <p>A <strong>hydrocarbon</strong> is an organic compound containing only carbon and hydrogen.</p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Methane, CH₄</li>
    <li>Ethane, C₂H₆</li>
    <li>Propane, C₃H₈</li>
    <li>Ethene, C₂H₄</li>
    <li>Ethyne, C₂H₂</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Hydrocarbons can be classified according to the types of carbon-carbon bonds they contain.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Alkanes:</strong> contain only carbon-carbon single bonds.</li>
    <li><strong>Alkenes:</strong> contain at least one carbon-carbon double bond.</li>
    <li><strong>Alkynes:</strong> contain at least one carbon-carbon triple bond.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hydrocarbons', 15),
  (578, 578, '<h2>Alkanes</h2>

  <p><strong>Alkanes</strong> are saturated hydrocarbons containing only carbon-carbon single bonds.</p>

  <p>The general formula for open-chain alkanes is:</p>

  <p><strong>CₙH₂ₙ₊₂</strong></p>

  <h3>First members</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Methane — CH₄</li>
    <li>Ethane — C₂H₆</li>
    <li>Propane — C₃H₈</li>
    <li>Butane — C₄H₁₀</li>
    <li>Pentane — C₅H₁₂</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>For n = 4:</p>

  <p>H = 2(4) + 2 = 10</p>

  <p>Therefore the formula is C₄H₁₀.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alkanes', 15),
  (579, 579, '<h2>Alkenes</h2>

  <p><strong>Alkenes</strong> are unsaturated hydrocarbons containing at least one carbon-carbon double bond.</p>

  <p>The general formula for open-chain alkenes containing one double bond is:</p>

  <p><strong>CₙH₂ₙ</strong></p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Ethene — C₂H₄</li>
    <li>Propene — C₃H₆</li>
    <li>Butene — C₄H₈</li>
    <li>Pentene — C₅H₁₀</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The carbon-carbon double bond is responsible for many characteristic reactions of alkenes.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alkenes', 15),
  (580, 580, '<h2>Alkynes</h2>

  <p><strong>Alkynes</strong> are unsaturated hydrocarbons containing at least one carbon-carbon triple bond.</p>

  <p>The general formula for open-chain alkynes containing one triple bond is:</p>

  <p><strong>CₙH₂ₙ₋₂</strong></p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Ethyne — C₂H₂</li>
    <li>Propyne — C₃H₄</li>
    <li>Butyne — C₄H₆</li>
    <li>Pentyne — C₅H₈</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The carbon-carbon triple bond is characteristic of alkynes.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alkynes', 15),
  (581, 581, '<h2>Homologous Series</h2>

  <p>A <strong>homologous series</strong> is a family of organic compounds with:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The same functional group.</li>
    <li>The same general formula.</li>
    <li>Similar chemical properties.</li>
    <li>A gradual change in physical properties.</li>
    <li>Successive members differing by a <strong>CH₂</strong> unit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example: Alkanes</h3>

  <p>Methane: CH₄</p>

  <p>Ethane: C₂H₆</p>

  <p>Propane: C₃H₈</p>

  <p>Butane: C₄H₁₀</p>

  <p>Each successive member differs from the previous one by CH₂.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homologous Series', 15),
  (582, 582, '<h2>General Formula of Alkanes</h2>

  <p>The general formula of open-chain alkanes is:</p>

  <p><strong>CₙH₂ₙ₊₂</strong></p>

  <p>This formula allows the molecular formula of an alkane to be calculated when the number of carbon atoms is known.</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: n = 3</strong></p>

  <p>H = 2(3) + 2 = 8</p>

  <p>Formula = C₃H₈</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: n = 7</strong></p>

  <p>H = 2(7) + 2 = 16</p>

  <p>Formula = C₇H₁₆</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: n = 10</strong></p>

  <p>H = 2(10) + 2 = 22</p>

  <p>Formula = C₁₀H₂₂</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for General Formula of Alkanes', 15),
  (583, 583, '<h2>General Formulae of Alkenes and Alkynes</h2>

  <p>For open-chain hydrocarbons containing one multiple bond:</p>

  <p><strong>Alkenes:</strong> CₙH₂ₙ</p>

  <p><strong>Alkynes:</strong> CₙH₂ₙ₋₂</p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Alkene with 6 carbon atoms</strong></p>

  <p>H = 2(6) = 12</p>

  <p>Formula = C₆H₁₂</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Alkyne with 5 carbon atoms</strong></p>

  <p>H = 2(5) − 2 = 8</p>

  <p>Formula = C₅H₈</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Alkene with 9 carbon atoms</strong></p>

  <p>H = 2(9) = 18</p>

  <p>Formula = C₉H₁₈</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for General Formulae of Alkenes and Alkynes', 15),
  (584, 584, '<h2>Structural Formulae</h2>

  <p>A structural formula shows how atoms are connected to one another in an organic molecule.</p>

  <p>For example, ethane has the molecular formula <strong>C₂H₆</strong>.</p>

  <p>Its structural formula shows the carbon-carbon bond and the hydrogen atoms attached to each carbon:</p>

  <p><strong>CH₃–CH₃</strong></p>

  <p>Carbon forms four covalent bonds, so the total number of bonds around each carbon must be four.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: Ethane</h3>
  <p>Molecular formula: C₂H₆</p>
  <p>Structural formula: CH₃–CH₃</p>

  <h3>Example 2: Propane</h3>
  <p>Molecular formula: C₃H₈</p>
  <p>Structural formula: CH₃–CH₂–CH₃</p>

  <h3>Example 3: Propene</h3>
  <p>Molecular formula: C₃H₆</p>
  <p>Structural formula: CH₂=CH–CH₃</p>

  <p>The double bond in propene must be shown because it is part of the structure of the molecule.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Structural Formulae', 15),
  (585, 585, '<h2>Condensed Structural Formulae</h2>

  <p>A condensed structural formula represents an organic molecule by grouping hydrogen atoms with the carbon atom to which they are attached.</p>

  <p>Instead of drawing every individual bond, groups such as <strong>CH₃</strong> and <strong>CH₂</strong> are written together.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p>Butane can be written as:</p>
  <p><strong>CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃</strong></p>

  <p>Or with bond lines:</p>
  <p><strong>CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃</strong></p>

  <h3>Reading a condensed formula</h3>
  <p>CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ contains:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>4 carbon atoms</li>
    <li>10 hydrogen atoms</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The order of the groups shows how the carbon atoms are connected.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Condensed Structural Formulae', 15),
  (586, 586, '<h2>Naming Straight-Chain Alkanes</h2>

  <p>Straight-chain alkanes are named according to the number of carbon atoms in the longest continuous chain.</p>

  <p>The main carbon prefixes are:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>1 carbon → meth-</li>
    <li>2 carbons → eth-</li>
    <li>3 carbons → prop-</li>
    <li>4 carbons → but-</li>
    <li>5 carbons → pent-</li>
    <li>6 carbons → hex-</li>
    <li>7 carbons → hept-</li>
    <li>8 carbons → oct-</li>
    <li>9 carbons → non-</li>
    <li>10 carbons → dec-</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Alkanes end with <strong>-ane</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>
  <p>CH₄ → methane</p>
  <p>CH₃CH₃ → ethane</p>
  <p>CH₃CH₂CH₃ → propane</p>
  <p>CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ → butane</p>

  <p>For a straight-chain alkane, count the carbon atoms and select the corresponding prefix, then add <strong>-ane</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Straight-Chain Alkanes', 15),
  (587, 587, '<h2>Naming Branched-Chain Alkanes</h2>

  <p>A branched-chain alkane contains a carbon chain with one or more carbon groups attached to the main chain.</p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the longest continuous carbon chain</h3>
  <p>This gives the parent alkane name.</p>

  <h3>Step 2: Number the chain</h3>
  <p>Number from the end that gives the substituent the lowest possible number.</p>

  <h3>Step 3: Identify the branch</h3>
  <p>A one-carbon branch is called <strong>methyl</strong>.</p>
  <p>A two-carbon branch is called <strong>ethyl</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Step 4: Give the branch its position</h3>
  <p>The number of the carbon carrying the branch is written before the branch name.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃</p>

  <p>The longest chain has four carbons → butane.</p>
  <p>The methyl branch is on carbon 2.</p>
  <p>Name: <strong>2-methylbutane</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Branched-Chain Alkanes', 15),
  (588, 588, '<h2>Naming Alkenes</h2>

  <p>Alkenes contain at least one carbon-carbon double bond.</p>

  <p>They are named using the carbon chain containing the double bond and the ending <strong>-ene</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Rules</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the longest carbon chain containing the double bond.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number the chain from the end nearest to the double bond.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the position of the double bond.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Name the compound using the appropriate carbon prefix and <strong>-ene</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>CH₂=CH₂ → ethene</p>

  <p>CH₂=CH–CH₃ → propene</p>

  <p>CH₂=CH–CH₂–CH₃ → but-1-ene</p>

  <p>CH₃–CH=CH–CH₃ → but-2-ene</p>

  <p>The position number tells us where the double bond begins.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Alkenes', 15),
  (589, 589, '<h2>Naming Alkynes</h2>

  <p>Alkynes contain at least one carbon-carbon triple bond.</p>

  <p>The suffix used for alkynes is <strong>-yne</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Rules</h3>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the longest carbon chain containing the triple bond.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Number the chain from the end nearest to the triple bond.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the position where the triple bond begins.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Use the carbon prefix and the ending <strong>-yne</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>HC≡CH → ethyne</p>

  <p>CH≡C–CH₃ → propyne</p>

  <p>CH≡C–CH₂–CH₃ → but-1-yne</p>

  <p>CH₃–C≡C–CH₃ → but-2-yne</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Naming Alkynes', 15),
  (590, 590, '<h2>Structural Isomerism</h2>

  <p>Structural isomers are compounds that have the <strong>same molecular formula</strong> but different arrangements of atoms.</p>

  <h3>Example: C₄H₁₀</h3>

  <p>C₄H₁₀ can form two different structures:</p>

  <p><strong>CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃</strong></p>
  <p>Butane</p>

  <p>and</p>

  <p><strong>CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₃</strong></p>
  <p>2-methylpropane</p>

  <p>Both have the molecular formula C₄H₁₀, but their atoms are connected differently.</p>

  <h3>Important rule</h3>

  <p>When looking for structural isomers, keep the molecular formula unchanged but rearrange the carbon skeleton.</p>

  <h3>C₅H₁₂</h3>

  <p>Pentane has three structural isomers:</p>

  <p>1. Pentane</p>
  <p>2. 2-methylbutane</p>
  <p>3. 2,2-dimethylpropane</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Structural Isomerism', 15),
  (591, 591, '<h2>Geometric Isomerism</h2>

  <p>Geometric isomerism occurs when compounds have the same molecular formula and connectivity but different spatial arrangements around a carbon-carbon double bond.</p>

  <p>A carbon-carbon double bond does not allow free rotation in the same way as a single bond.</p>

  <h3>Example: But-2-ene</h3>

  <p>But-2-ene can exist in two arrangements:</p>

  <p><strong>cis-but-2-ene</strong> — similar groups are on the same side of the double bond.</p>

  <p><strong>trans-but-2-ene</strong> — similar groups are on opposite sides of the double bond.</p>

  <h3>Why does this happen?</h3>

  <p>The carbon-carbon double bond restricts rotation. Therefore, the groups attached to the double-bonded carbons can remain in different fixed arrangements.</p>

  <p>Geometric isomerism is therefore different from structural isomerism:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Structural isomers:</strong> atoms are connected differently.</li>
    <li><strong>Geometric isomers:</strong> atoms are connected in the same order but arranged differently in space.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Geometric Isomerism', 15),
  (592, 592, '<h2>Alcohols</h2>

  <p>Alcohols are organic compounds containing the <strong>hydroxyl functional group, –OH</strong>, attached to a carbon atom.</p>

  <p>The –OH group is called the <strong>hydroxyl group</strong>.</p>

  <h3>General structure</h3>

  <p>For a simple alcohol:</p>

  <p><strong>R–OH</strong></p>

  <p>R represents the carbon-containing part of the molecule.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>Methanol: CH₃OH</p>
  <p>Ethanol: CH₃CH₂OH</p>
  <p>Propanol: CH₃CH₂CH₂OH</p>

  <p>The presence of the –OH group distinguishes alcohols from hydrocarbons such as alkanes.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alcohols', 15),
  (593, 593, '<h2>Ethanol</h2>

  <p>Ethanol is a two-carbon alcohol.</p>

  <p>Its molecular formula is:</p>

  <p><strong>C₂H₆O</strong></p>

  <p>It is commonly written as:</p>

  <p><strong>C₂H₅OH</strong></p>

  <p>Its structural formula is:</p>

  <p><strong>CH₃CH₂OH</strong></p>

  <h3>Structure</h3>

  <p>Ethanol contains:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>2 carbon atoms</li>
    <li>6 hydrogen atoms</li>
    <li>1 oxygen atom</li>
    <li>1 hydroxyl group, –OH</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The two-carbon chain gives the prefix <strong>eth-</strong>, while the –OH functional group identifies it as an alcohol.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ethanol', 15),
  (594, 594, '<h2>Properties of Ethanol</h2>

  <p>Ethanol is a colourless, volatile liquid with a characteristic smell.</p>

  <h3>Physical properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless liquid.</li>
    <li>It is volatile.</li>
    <li>It is miscible with water.</li>
    <li>It is flammable.</li>
    <li>It is a good solvent for many substances.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Combustion</h3>

  <p>Ethanol burns in oxygen to produce carbon dioxide and water.</p>

  <p><strong>C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>This is a combustion reaction.</p>

  <h3>Solubility</h3>

  <p>Ethanol mixes readily with water because of interactions involving its hydroxyl group.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Ethanol', 15),
  (595, 595, '<h2>Fermentation</h2>

  <p>Fermentation is the process in which sugars are converted into ethanol and carbon dioxide by microorganisms such as yeast, usually in the absence of oxygen.</p>

  <h3>Word equation</h3>

  <p><strong>Glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide</strong></p>

  <h3>Symbol equation</h3>

  <p><strong>C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂</strong></p>

  <h3>Conditions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Yeast is required.</li>
    <li>A suitable temperature is required.</li>
    <li>Oxygen is excluded or kept very low.</li>
    <li>A sugar such as glucose is required.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Fermentation is different from combustion. Fermentation produces ethanol, while complete combustion of ethanol produces carbon dioxide and water.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fermentation', 15),
  (596, 596, '<h2>Oxidation of Ethanol</h2>

  <p>Ethanol can be oxidised to form <strong>ethanoic acid</strong>.</p>

  <p>This can occur when ethanol is exposed to oxygen in the presence of suitable microorganisms, or when an oxidising agent is used in the laboratory.</p>

  <h3>Overall change</h3>

  <p><strong>Ethanol → ethanoic acid</strong></p>

  <p>In the presence of oxygen:</p>

  <p><strong>C₂H₅OH + O₂ → CH₃COOH + H₂O</strong></p>

  <h3>Observation</h3>

  <p>The reaction changes the alcohol into a carboxylic acid.</p>

  <p>Oxidation therefore provides an important link between alcohols and carboxylic acids.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Oxidation of Ethanol', 15),
  (597, 597, '<h2>Carboxylic Acids</h2>

  <p>Carboxylic acids are organic compounds containing the <strong>carboxyl functional group, –COOH</strong>.</p>

  <p>The carboxyl group contains both:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>a carbonyl component, C=O</li>
    <li>a hydroxyl component, –OH</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>General structure</h3>

  <p><strong>R–COOH</strong></p>

  <p>R represents the carbon-containing part of the molecule.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>Methanoic acid: HCOOH</p>

  <p>Ethanoic acid: CH₃COOH</p>

  <p>Propanoic acid: CH₃CH₂COOH</p>

  <p>Carboxylic acids are acidic because they can release hydrogen ions from the carboxyl group.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carboxylic Acids', 15),
  (598, 598, '<h2>Ethanoic Acid</h2>

  <p>Ethanoic acid is a two-carbon carboxylic acid.</p>

  <p>Its molecular formula is:</p>

  <p><strong>C₂H₄O₂</strong></p>

  <p>Its structural formula is:</p>

  <p><strong>CH₃COOH</strong></p>

  <p>It contains the carboxyl group, –COOH.</p>

  <h3>Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless liquid in pure form.</li>
    <li>It has a characteristic sharp smell.</li>
    <li>It is soluble in water.</li>
    <li>It is an acid.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Dilute ethanoic acid is present in vinegar.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ethanoic Acid', 15),
  (599, 599, '<h2>Reactions of Ethanoic Acid</h2>

  <p>Ethanoic acid shows the typical reactions of an acid.</p>

  <h3>1. Reaction with reactive metals</h3>

  <p>Ethanoic acid reacts with some reactive metals to produce a salt and hydrogen gas.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>2CH₃COOH + Mg → (CH₃COO)₂Mg + H₂</strong></p>

  <h3>2. Reaction with carbonates</h3>

  <p>Ethanoic acid reacts with carbonates to produce a salt, water and carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂</strong></p>

  <h3>3. Reaction with alkalis</h3>

  <p>Ethanoic acid reacts with an alkali in a neutralisation reaction.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>The salt formed with sodium hydroxide is sodium ethanoate.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Ethanoic Acid', 15),
  (600, 600, '<h2>Addition Reactions</h2>

  <p>Alkenes undergo addition reactions because they contain a carbon-carbon double bond.</p>

  <p>In an addition reaction, atoms or groups of atoms are added across the double bond, converting the alkene into a saturated compound.</p>

  <h3>Hydrogenation</h3>

  <p>Hydrogen can add across the double bond of an alkene in the presence of a nickel catalyst and heat.</p>

  <p><strong>Ethene + Hydrogen → Ethane</strong></p>

  <p>C₂H₄ + H₂ → C₂H₆</p>

  <p>The C=C double bond becomes a C-C single bond.</p>

  <h3>Addition of Bromine</h3>

  <p>Bromine also adds across the double bond.</p>

  <p><strong>Ethene + Bromine → 1,2-dibromoethane</strong></p>

  <p>C₂H₄ + Br₂ → C₂H₄Br₂</p>

  <p>This reaction is important because it provides a test for unsaturation.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Addition Reactions', 15),
  (601, 601, '<h2>Ester Formation</h2>

  <p>Esters are organic compounds formed when an alcohol reacts with a carboxylic acid.</p>

  <p>The reaction is called <strong>esterification</strong>.</p>

  <p>A carboxylic acid reacts with an alcohol in the presence of a concentrated acid catalyst, commonly concentrated sulfuric acid, and on heating.</p>

  <p><strong>Ethanoic acid + Ethanol ⇌ Ethyl ethanoate + Water</strong></p>

  <p>CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O</p>

  <p>Esterification is a reversible reaction, so an equilibrium is established between the reactants and products.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ester Formation', 15),
  (602, 602, '<h2>Addition Polymerisation</h2>

  <p>Addition polymerisation is a reaction in which many small alkene molecules join together to form a large molecule called a polymer.</p>

  <p>The small molecules that join together are called <strong>monomers</strong>.</p>

  <p>During addition polymerisation, the carbon-carbon double bond in each alkene monomer opens and the monomers join to form a long chain.</p>

  <h3>Example: Ethene</h3>

  <p>Ethene molecules can join together to form poly(ethene).</p>

  <p><strong>nCH₂=CH₂ → [−CH₂−CH₂−]ₙ</strong></p>

  <p>Ethene is the monomer and poly(ethene) is the polymer.</p>

  <p>No small molecule such as water is eliminated during addition polymerisation.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Addition Polymerisation', 15),
  (603, 603, '<h2>Crude Oil</h2>

  <p>Crude oil is a naturally occurring mixture of hydrocarbons found beneath the Earth''s surface.</p>

  <p>It is formed from the remains of living organisms that were buried and subjected to heat and pressure over millions of years.</p>

  <p>Crude oil contains many different hydrocarbons with different molecular sizes and boiling points.</p>

  <h3>Why Crude Oil Is Separated</h3>

  <p>The different hydrocarbons in crude oil have different uses. They must therefore be separated into groups called <strong>fractions</strong>.</p>

  <p>The separation is carried out mainly by <strong>fractional distillation</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Important Point</h3>

  <p>Crude oil is a mixture, not a single compound. The hydrocarbons are separated physically according to differences in their boiling points.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Crude Oil', 15),
  (604, 604, '<h2>Cracking</h2>

  <p>Cracking is the process of breaking down large, long-chain hydrocarbon molecules into smaller, more useful hydrocarbons.</p>

  <p>Large hydrocarbons from heavy crude-oil fractions can be cracked to produce smaller hydrocarbons.</p>

  <p>Cracking usually produces a mixture containing <strong>alkanes and alkenes</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Why Cracking Is Needed</h3>

  <p>There is often greater demand for smaller hydrocarbons such as petrol-range hydrocarbons and alkenes than for some heavier fractions.</p>

  <p>Cracking therefore converts less useful large hydrocarbons into more useful smaller molecules.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Decane can be cracked to produce octane and ethene:</p>

  <p><strong>C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄</strong></p>

  <p>The equation is balanced because there are 10 carbon atoms and 22 hydrogen atoms on both sides.</p>

  <h3>Conditions</h3>

  <p>Cracking requires high temperatures. A catalyst such as an aluminium oxide or zeolite catalyst may be used in catalytic cracking.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cracking', 15),
  (605, 605, '<h2>Properties of Sulphur</h2>

  <p>Sulphur is a non-metallic element represented by the symbol <strong>S</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Physical properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a yellow solid at room temperature.</li>
    <li>It is brittle.</li>
    <li>It does not conduct electricity well.</li>
    <li>It is insoluble in water.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Chemical property</h3>

  <p>Sulphur burns in oxygen to form sulphur dioxide.</p>

  <p><strong>S + O₂ → SO₂</strong></p>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide is a gas with a pungent smell.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Sulphur', 15),
  (606, 606, '<h2>Sulphur Dioxide</h2>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide is a compound containing sulphur and oxygen.</p>

  <p>Its formula is <strong>SO₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Preparation</h3>

  <p>It can be prepared by burning sulphur in oxygen:</p>

  <p><strong>S + O₂ → SO₂</strong></p>

  <h3>Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless gas.</li>
    <li>It has a pungent smell.</li>
    <li>It is soluble in water.</li>
    <li>It is acidic in nature.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide is also an important air pollutant.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sulphur Dioxide', 15),
  (607, 607, '<h2>Reactions of Sulphur Dioxide</h2>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide reacts with water to form sulphurous acid.</p>

  <p><strong>SO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂SO₃</strong></p>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide can also be oxidised to sulphur trioxide.</p>

  <p><strong>2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃</strong></p>

  <p>These reactions are important because sulphur dioxide is an intermediate in the manufacture of sulphuric acid.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reactions of Sulphur Dioxide', 15),
  (608, 608, '<h2>Sulphur Trioxide</h2>

  <p>Sulphur trioxide is a compound of sulphur and oxygen with the formula <strong>SO₃</strong>.</p>

  <p>It is formed by oxidation of sulphur dioxide:</p>

  <p><strong>2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃</strong></p>

  <p>Sulphur trioxide reacts vigorously with water to form sulphuric acid:</p>

  <p><strong>SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄</strong></p>

  <p>This reaction is important in the manufacture of sulphuric acid.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sulphur Trioxide', 15),
  (609, 609, '<h2>Sulphuric Acid</h2>

  <p>Sulphuric acid has the formula <strong>H₂SO₄</strong>.</p>

  <p>It is a strong mineral acid with many important chemical properties and uses.</p>

  <h3>Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a strong acid.</li>
    <li>Concentrated sulphuric acid is highly corrosive.</li>
    <li>It reacts with bases to form sulphates and water.</li>
    <li>It reacts with carbonates to form sulphates, water and carbon dioxide.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example: reaction with sodium hydroxide</h3>

  <p><strong>H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>The salt formed is sodium sulphate.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sulphuric Acid', 15),
  (610, 610, '<h2>Manufacture of Sulphuric Acid</h2>

  <p>Sulphuric acid is manufactured industrially by the <strong>Contact Process</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Step 1: Formation of sulphur dioxide</h3>

  <p>Sulphur is burned in oxygen:</p>

  <p><strong>S + O₂ → SO₂</strong></p>

  <h3>Step 2: Oxidation of sulphur dioxide</h3>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide is oxidised to sulphur trioxide:</p>

  <p><strong>2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃</strong></p>

  <p>A vanadium(V) oxide catalyst is used.</p>

  <h3>Step 3: Formation of sulphuric acid</h3>

  <p>Sulphur trioxide is processed to produce sulphuric acid.</p>

  <p>The Contact Process is important because sulphuric acid is required in many industrial processes.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Manufacture of Sulphuric Acid', 15),
  (611, 611, '<h2>Uses of Sulphuric Acid</h2>

  <p>Sulphuric acid is one of the most widely used industrial chemicals.</p>

  <h3>Major uses</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Manufacture of fertilisers.</li>
    <li>Manufacture of other chemicals.</li>
    <li>Processing of petroleum.</li>
    <li>Manufacture of detergents.</li>
    <li>Use in lead-acid batteries.</li>
    <li>Preparation of sulphate salts.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Its strong acidic properties make it useful in many chemical manufacturing processes.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uses of Sulphuric Acid', 15),
  (612, 612, '<h2>Properties of Carbon</h2>

  <p>Carbon is a non-metallic element with the symbol <strong>C</strong>.</p>

  <p>Carbon is important because it forms a very large number of compounds.</p>

  <h3>Important properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Carbon has four valence electrons.</li>
    <li>It forms four covalent bonds.</li>
    <li>It can bond to other carbon atoms.</li>
    <li>It forms chains, branches and rings.</li>
    <li>It forms compounds with many other elements.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The ability of carbon atoms to bond to one another is called <strong>catenation</strong>.</p>

  <p>Its ability to form four covalent bonds is called <strong>tetravalency</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Carbon', 15),
  (613, 613, '<h2>Carbon Dioxide</h2>

  <p>Carbon dioxide is a compound containing carbon and oxygen.</p>

  <p>Its formula is <strong>CO₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Preparation</h3>

  <p>Carbon dioxide can be produced by complete combustion of carbon:</p>

  <p><strong>C + O₂ → CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>It is also produced when acids react with carbonates.</p>

  <h3>Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless gas.</li>
    <li>It does not support combustion.</li>
    <li>It is denser than air.</li>
    <li>It dissolves in water to some extent.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Carbon dioxide is also produced during respiration and is used by green plants during photosynthesis.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carbon Dioxide', 15),
  (614, 614, '<h2>Carbon Monoxide</h2>

  <p>Carbon monoxide is a compound of carbon and oxygen with the formula <strong>CO</strong>.</p>

  <p>It is produced mainly during <strong>incomplete combustion</strong> of carbon-containing fuels.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>2C + O₂ → 2CO</strong></p>

  <h3>Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is colourless.</li>
    <li>It is odourless.</li>
    <li>It is highly poisonous.</li>
    <li>It burns in oxygen to form carbon dioxide.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Carbon monoxide is dangerous because it reduces the blood''s ability to transport oxygen.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carbon Monoxide', 15),
  (615, 615, '<h2>Allotropes of Carbon</h2>

  <p>Allotropes are different structural forms of the same element in the same physical state.</p>

  <p>Important allotropes of carbon include <strong>diamond</strong> and <strong>graphite</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Diamond</h3>

  <p>Each carbon atom is strongly bonded to four other carbon atoms in a giant three-dimensional structure.</p>

  <p>This makes diamond:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>very hard</li>
    <li>strong</li>
    <li>a poor conductor of electricity</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Graphite</h3>

  <p>Each carbon atom is bonded to three other carbon atoms in layers.</p>

  <p>The layers can slide over one another.</p>

  <p>Graphite conducts electricity because it has delocalised electrons.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Allotropes of Carbon', 15),
  (616, 616, '<h2>Properties of Nitrogen</h2>

  <p>Nitrogen is a non-metallic element with the symbol <strong>N</strong>.</p>

  <p>Nitrogen gas exists mainly as diatomic molecules, <strong>N₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Physical properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is colourless.</li>
    <li>It is odourless.</li>
    <li>It is a gas at room temperature.</li>
    <li>It is only slightly soluble in water.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Nitrogen makes up most of the Earth''s atmosphere.</p>

  <h3>Chemical property</h3>

  <p>Nitrogen is relatively unreactive under ordinary conditions because the N≡N bond is very strong.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Nitrogen', 15),
  (617, 617, '<h2>Ammonia</h2>

  <p>Ammonia is a compound of nitrogen and hydrogen with the formula <strong>NH₃</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless gas.</li>
    <li>It has a pungent smell.</li>
    <li>It is very soluble in water.</li>
    <li>Its aqueous solution is alkaline.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Preparation</h3>

  <p>Industrially, ammonia is manufactured by the Haber process:</p>

  <p><strong>N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃</strong></p>

  <p>The reaction uses nitrogen and hydrogen and is carried out under suitable industrial conditions.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ammonia', 15),
  (618, 618, '<h2>Nitrogen Compounds</h2>

  <p>Nitrogen forms many important compounds.</p>

  <h3>Ammonia</h3>

  <p><strong>NH₃</strong></p>

  <p>Ammonia is an alkaline gas that dissolves readily in water.</p>

  <h3>Nitric acid</h3>

  <p><strong>HNO₃</strong></p>

  <p>Nitric acid is a strong acid and an important industrial chemical.</p>

  <h3>Nitrates</h3>

  <p>Nitrates contain the nitrate ion:</p>

  <p><strong>NO₃⁻</strong></p>

  <p>Examples include sodium nitrate, NaNO₃, and potassium nitrate, KNO₃.</p>

  <p>Nitrogen compounds are important in fertilisers, industry and biological processes.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nitrogen Compounds', 15),
  (619, 619, '<h2>Nitrogen Cycle</h2>

  <p>The nitrogen cycle describes how nitrogen moves between the atmosphere, soil, plants, animals and microorganisms.</p>

  <h3>Important processes</h3>

  <p><strong>Nitrogen fixation:</strong> atmospheric nitrogen is converted into usable nitrogen compounds.</p>

  <p><strong>Nitrification:</strong> microorganisms convert ammonium compounds into nitrites and then nitrates.</p>

  <p><strong>Assimilation:</strong> plants absorb nitrates and use nitrogen to make compounds such as proteins.</p>

  <p><strong>Ammonification:</strong> decomposers convert nitrogen-containing organic matter into ammonium compounds.</p>

  <p><strong>Denitrification:</strong> bacteria convert nitrates back into nitrogen gas.</p>

  <p>The cycle maintains the movement of nitrogen through ecosystems.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nitrogen Cycle', 15),
  (620, 620, '<h2>Composition of Air</h2>

  <p>Air is a mixture of gases. The major components of dry air are:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Nitrogen — about 78%</li>
    <li>Oxygen — about 21%</li>
    <li>Argon and other noble gases — about 0.9%</li>
    <li>Carbon dioxide — about 0.04%</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Air also contains variable amounts of water vapour and small quantities of other gases.</p>

  <h3>Important point</h3>
  <p>Air is a <strong>mixture</strong>, not a compound, because its components are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical methods.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>In 100 dm³ of dry air, approximately 78 dm³ is nitrogen and 21 dm³ is oxygen.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Composition of Air', 15),
  (621, 621, '<h2>Properties of Oxygen</h2>

  <p>Oxygen is a colourless, odourless and tasteless gas at room temperature.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>It is slightly soluble in water.</li>
    <li>It is slightly denser than air.</li>
    <li>It does not burn itself.</li>
    <li>It supports combustion.</li>
    <li>It is essential for respiration.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Test for oxygen</h3>
  <p>A glowing splint is inserted into the gas. If the splint relights, oxygen is present.</p>

  <h3>Combustion</h3>
  <p>Carbon burns in oxygen:</p>

  <p><strong>C + O₂ → CO₂</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Oxygen', 15),
  (622, 622, '<h2>Uses of Oxygen</h2>

  <p>Oxygen is important because it supports combustion and respiration.</p>

  <h3>Main uses</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Respiration in hospitals.</li>
    <li>Welding and cutting metals.</li>
    <li>Steel manufacture.</li>
    <li>Supporting combustion in industrial processes.</li>
    <li>Life-support systems in aircraft and spacecraft.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Medical oxygen</h3>
  <p>Patients who cannot obtain enough oxygen from normal air may be supplied with oxygen.</p>

  <h3>Welding</h3>
  <p>Oxygen is used with suitable fuel gases to produce a very hot flame for cutting and welding metals.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uses of Oxygen', 15),
  (623, 623, '<h2>Properties of Water</h2>

  <p>Pure water is a colourless, odourless and tasteless liquid at room temperature.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>It freezes at 0°C under standard atmospheric pressure.</li>
    <li>It boils at 100°C under standard atmospheric pressure.</li>
    <li>It is a good solvent for many substances.</li>
    <li>It is essential for living organisms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Water as a compound</h3>
  <p>Water is a compound because hydrogen and oxygen are chemically bonded in a fixed ratio.</p>

  <p><strong>Formula: H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Water', 15),
  (624, 624, '<h2>Water as a Solvent</h2>

  <p>A <strong>solvent</strong> is a substance that dissolves a solute to form a solution.</p>

  <p>Water dissolves many ionic and polar substances and is therefore often called a good solvent.</p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>When sodium chloride dissolves in water:</p>

  <p><strong>NaCl(s) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)</strong></p>

  <p>Water is important in living organisms because many substances are transported in aqueous solution.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Water as a Solvent', 15),
  (625, 625, '<h2>Hard Water</h2>

  <p>Hard water contains dissolved calcium and magnesium ions.</p>

  <p>These ions react with soap to form an insoluble substance called <strong>scum</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Effects of hard water</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Soap does not lather easily.</li>
    <li>Scum is formed.</li>
    <li>More soap is required for washing.</li>
    <li>Some types of hard water can form scale in pipes and boilers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>
  <p>Hard water is not necessarily unsafe to drink. The main problem considered here is its behaviour with soap and heating systems.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hard Water', 15),
  (626, 626, '<h2>Soft Water</h2>

  <p>Soft water contains very small amounts of calcium and magnesium ions compared with hard water.</p>

  <h3>Behaviour with soap</h3>

  <p>Soft water reacts less with soap, so soap produces lather readily.</p>

  <h3>Comparison</h3>

  <p><strong>Soft water → easy lathering → little or no scum.</strong></p>

  <p><strong>Hard water → poor lathering → scum formation.</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Soft Water', 15),
  (627, 627, '<h2>Water Purification</h2>

  <p>Water purification removes unwanted substances and harmful microorganisms so that water becomes suitable for its intended use.</p>

  <h3>Common purification processes</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filtration:</strong> removes insoluble solid particles.</li>
    <li><strong>Boiling:</strong> kills many microorganisms.</li>
    <li><strong>Chlorination:</strong> uses chlorine to disinfect water.</li>
    <li><strong>Distillation:</strong> separates water from many dissolved substances by evaporation followed by condensation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The purification method depends on the type of impurity present.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Water Purification', 15),
  (628, 628, '<h2>Carbon Monoxide as an Air Pollutant</h2>

  <p>Carbon monoxide, CO, is a poisonous gas produced mainly by incomplete combustion of carbon-containing fuels.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>2C + O₂ → 2CO</strong></p>

  <h3>Why CO is dangerous</h3>

  <p>Carbon monoxide combines strongly with haemoglobin in blood. This reduces the ability of blood to transport oxygen around the body.</p>

  <h3>Sources</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Incomplete combustion in vehicle engines.</li>
    <li>Burning charcoal in poorly ventilated rooms.</li>
    <li>Faulty fuel-burning appliances.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carbon Monoxide as an Air Pollutant', 15),
  (629, 629, '<h2>Sulphur Dioxide as an Air Pollutant</h2>

  <p>Sulphur dioxide, SO₂, is produced when sulphur-containing fuels are burned.</p>

  <p>A simplified equation is:</p>

  <p><strong>S + O₂ → SO₂</strong></p>

  <h3>Effects</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Irritates the respiratory system.</li>
    <li>Contributes to acid rain.</li>
    <li>Can damage vegetation.</li>
    <li>Can contribute to corrosion of materials.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>SO₂ can react with water in the atmosphere to form acidic substances.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sulphur Dioxide as an Air Pollutant', 15),
  (630, 630, '<h2>Nitrogen Oxides as Air Pollutants</h2>

  <p>Nitrogen oxides, commonly represented as NOₓ, include gases such as nitrogen monoxide, NO, and nitrogen dioxide, NO₂.</p>

  <p>They can form at high temperatures, especially in vehicle engines and industrial combustion processes.</p>

  <h3>Effects</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Contribute to acid rain.</li>
    <li>Can irritate the respiratory system.</li>
    <li>Contribute to photochemical smog.</li>
    <li>Can damage vegetation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Nitrogen dioxide is a reddish-brown gas with a characteristic irritating smell.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nitrogen Oxides as Air Pollutants', 15),
  (631, 631, '<h2>Particulate Matter</h2>

  <p>Particulate matter consists of very small solid particles or liquid droplets suspended in air.</p>

  <h3>Sources</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Vehicle exhaust.</li>
    <li>Industrial emissions.</li>
    <li>Burning biomass and waste.</li>
    <li>Dust from roads and construction.</li>
    <li>Smoke from incomplete combustion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Effects</h3>

  <p>Very small particles can enter the respiratory system and cause or worsen respiratory problems.</p>

  <p>Reducing smoke, dust and uncontrolled combustion reduces particulate pollution.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Particulate Matter', 15),
  (632, 632, '<h2>Acid Rain</h2>

  <p>Acid rain is precipitation that is more acidic than normal because acidic substances formed in the atmosphere dissolve in rainwater.</p>

  <h3>Main gases involved</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Sulphur dioxide, SO₂</li>
    <li>Nitrogen oxides, NOₓ</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Effects of acid rain</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Damages vegetation.</li>
    <li>Acidifies lakes and rivers.</li>
    <li>Can harm aquatic organisms.</li>
    <li>Corrodes metals.</li>
    <li>Can damage limestone and marble buildings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Control</h3>
  <p>Reducing emissions of sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides reduces acid rain.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Acid Rain', 15),
  (633, 633, '<h2>Greenhouse Effect</h2>

  <p>The greenhouse effect is the warming of Earth''s lower atmosphere and surface caused by gases that absorb and re-emit infrared radiation.</p>

  <h3>Greenhouse gases</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Carbon dioxide, CO₂</li>
    <li>Methane, CH₄</li>
    <li>Nitrous oxide, N₂O</li>
    <li>Water vapour</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How it works</h3>

  <p>Earth receives energy from the Sun. The Earth''s surface emits infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases absorb some of this radiation and re-emit it, reducing the rate at which heat escapes to space.</p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>The natural greenhouse effect is necessary for life. The concern is the increase in greenhouse gases caused by human activities, which enhances warming.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Greenhouse Effect', 15),
  (634, 634, '<h2>Pollution Control</h2>

  <p>Pollution control involves reducing the release of harmful substances into the environment and reducing their effects.</p>

  <h3>Air pollution control</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Use cleaner fuels.</li>
    <li>Maintain vehicle engines properly.</li>
    <li>Use emission-control systems.</li>
    <li>Reduce open burning of waste.</li>
    <li>Use renewable energy where practical.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Water pollution control</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Treat sewage before discharge.</li>
    <li>Treat industrial wastewater.</li>
    <li>Prevent harmful chemicals from entering water bodies.</li>
    <li>Control excessive use of fertilisers and pesticides.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Sustainable use of resources</h3>

  <p>Sustainable use means using natural resources in ways that meet present needs while reducing unnecessary damage and preserving resources for the future.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pollution Control', 15),
  (635, 635, '<h2>Test for Ammonium Ions</h2>

  <p>Ammonium ions are represented by <strong>NH₄⁺</strong>.</p>

  <p>They can be identified by adding a suitable alkali, such as sodium hydroxide solution, and warming the mixture.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</li>
    <li>Warm the mixture gently.</li>
    <li>Test any gas produced with damp red litmus paper.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>
  <p>A pungent-smelling gas is produced which turns damp red litmus paper blue.</p>

  <p>The gas is <strong>ammonia, NH₃</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Ammonium Ions', 15),
  (636, 636, '<h2>Test for Copper(II) Ions</h2>

  <p>Copper(II) ions are represented by <strong>Cu²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test using sodium hydroxide</h3>

  <p>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>blue precipitate</strong> of copper(II) hydroxide forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂(s)</strong></p>

  <p>The precipitate is insoluble in excess sodium hydroxide under the usual school laboratory test.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Copper(II) Ions', 15),
  (637, 637, '<h2>Test for Iron(II) Ions</h2>

  <p>Iron(II) ions are represented by <strong>Fe²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>green precipitate</strong> of iron(II) hydroxide forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂(s)</strong></p>

  <p>The precipitate may change colour on exposure to air because iron(II) compounds can be oxidised.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Iron(II) Ions', 15),
  (638, 638, '<h2>Test for Iron(III) Ions</h2>

  <p>Iron(III) ions are represented by <strong>Fe³⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Add sodium hydroxide solution to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>red-brown precipitate</strong> of iron(III) hydroxide forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Fe³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃(s)</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Iron(III) Ions', 15),
  (639, 639, '<h2>Test for Chloride Ions</h2>

  <p>Chloride ions are represented by <strong>Cl⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Acidify the sample with dilute nitric acid.</li>
    <li>Add aqueous silver nitrate.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>white precipitate</strong> of silver chloride forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s)</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Chloride Ions', 15),
  (640, 640, '<h2>Test for Sulphate Ions</h2>

  <p>Sulphate ions are represented by <strong>SO₄²⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Acidify the sample using dilute hydrochloric acid.</li>
    <li>Add barium nitrate solution or another suitable soluble barium salt.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>A <strong>white precipitate</strong> of barium sulphate forms.</p>

  <p><strong>Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄(s)</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Sulphate Ions', 15),
  (641, 641, '<h2>Test for Carbonate Ions</h2>

  <p>Carbonate ions are represented by <strong>CO₃²⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Add a dilute acid to the sample.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Effervescence occurs because carbon dioxide gas is produced.</p>

  <p>The gas can be passed through limewater.</p>

  <p>Limewater turns <strong>milky</strong> if carbon dioxide is present.</p>

  <p><strong>CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Carbonate Ions', 15),
  (642, 642, '<h2>Test for Nitrate Ions</h2>

  <p>Nitrate ions are represented by <strong>NO₃⁻</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>A common school laboratory test involves adding sodium hydroxide and aluminium foil, followed by gentle warming.</p>

  <p>If nitrate ions are present, ammonia gas may be produced.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>The gas turns damp red litmus paper blue.</p>

  <p>The ammonia produced indicates nitrate ions in the original sample.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Nitrate Ions', 15),
  (643, 643, '<h2>Flame Test for Sodium Ions</h2>

  <p>Sodium ions are represented by <strong>Na⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Clean a suitable wire loop.</li>
    <li>Place a small amount of the sample on the loop.</li>
    <li>Place it in a non-luminous flame.</li>
    <li>Observe the flame colour.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Sodium compounds produce a <strong>yellow flame</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Flame Test for Sodium Ions', 15),
  (644, 644, '<h2>Flame Test for Potassium Ions</h2>

  <p>Potassium ions are represented by <strong>K⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Flame colour</h3>

  <p>Potassium compounds produce a <strong>lilac flame</strong>.</p>

  <p>A sodium impurity can produce a strong yellow colour that may mask the lilac colour, so careful observation is important.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Flame Test for Potassium Ions', 15),
  (645, 645, '<h2>Flame Test for Calcium Ions</h2>

  <p>Calcium ions are represented by <strong>Ca²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Flame colour</h3>

  <p>Calcium compounds produce a <strong>brick-red or orange-red flame</strong>.</p>

  <p>The exact appearance can depend on the conditions and sample purity.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Flame Test for Calcium Ions', 15),
  (646, 646, '<h2>Flame Test for Copper(II) Ions</h2>

  <p>Copper(II) ions are represented by <strong>Cu²⁺</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Flame colour</h3>

  <p>Copper compounds can produce a <strong>blue-green or green flame</strong>.</p>

  <p>This provides another qualitative observation that can support identification of copper compounds.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Flame Test for Copper(II) Ions', 15),
  (647, 647, '<h2>Test for Hydrogen</h2>

  <p>Hydrogen gas is represented by <strong>H₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Collect a sample of the gas and bring a lighted splint close to the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Hydrogen burns with a characteristic <strong>squeaky pop</strong>.</p>

  <p>The reaction is:</p>

  <p><strong>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Hydrogen', 15),
  (648, 648, '<h2>Test for Oxygen</h2>

  <p>Oxygen gas is represented by <strong>O₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Insert a glowing splint into the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>The glowing splint <strong>relights</strong>.</p>

  <p>Oxygen supports combustion but does not itself burn.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Oxygen', 15),
  (649, 649, '<h2>Test for Carbon Dioxide</h2>

  <p>Carbon dioxide is represented by <strong>CO₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Bubble the gas through limewater.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Limewater turns <strong>milky</strong>.</p>

  <p>The reaction is:</p>

  <p><strong>CO₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>The calcium carbonate formed is insoluble and causes the milky appearance.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Carbon Dioxide', 15),
  (650, 650, '<h2>Test for Ammonia</h2>

  <p>Ammonia is represented by <strong>NH₃</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Properties useful for identification</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless gas.</li>
    <li>It has a pungent smell.</li>
    <li>It is alkaline in water.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Expose damp red litmus paper to the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>Damp red litmus paper turns <strong>blue</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Ammonia', 15),
  (651, 651, '<h2>Test for Chlorine</h2>

  <p>Chlorine gas is represented by <strong>Cl₂</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Properties useful for identification</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Chlorine is a greenish-yellow gas.</li>
    <li>It has a choking smell.</li>
    <li>It is toxic.</li>
    <li>It has bleaching properties when moisture is present.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Test</h3>

  <p>Expose damp blue litmus paper to the gas.</p>

  <h3>Positive result</h3>

  <p>The blue litmus paper is first turned red and then <strong>bleached</strong>.</p>

  <p>The bleaching occurs because chlorine reacts with water to form substances that can oxidise coloured materials.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Chlorine', 15),
  (652, 652, '<h2>Reversible Reactions</h2>

  <p>A reversible reaction is a chemical reaction that can proceed in both directions.</p>

  <p>The forward reaction forms products from reactants, while the reverse reaction changes products back into reactants.</p>

  <h3>Representation</h3>

  <pre>
A + B ⇌ C + D
  </pre>

  <p>The symbol <strong>⇌</strong> shows that the reaction is reversible.</p>

  <h3>Forward Reaction</h3>
  <p>The forward reaction changes reactants into products.</p>

  <h3>Reverse Reaction</h3>
  <p>The reverse reaction changes products back into reactants.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <pre>
N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃
  </pre>

  <p>Nitrogen and hydrogen react to form ammonia. Ammonia can also decompose back into nitrogen and hydrogen.</p>

  <h3>Important</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Not all chemical reactions are reversible.</li>
    <li>A reversible reaction can proceed in both directions.</li>
    <li>The two directions are called the forward and reverse reactions.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reversible Reactions', 15),
  (653, 653, '<h2>Forward and Reverse Reactions</h2>

  <p>A reversible reaction consists of two opposing reactions.</p>

  <pre>
A + B ⇌ C + D
  </pre>

  <h3>Forward Reaction</h3>

  <pre>
A + B → C + D
  </pre>

  <p>Reactants are converted into products.</p>

  <h3>Reverse Reaction</h3>

  <pre>
C + D → A + B
  </pre>

  <p>Products are converted back into reactants.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <pre>
N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃
  </pre>

  <p>Forward:</p>

  <pre>
N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃
  </pre>

  <p>Reverse:</p>

  <pre>
2NH₃ → N₂ + 3H₂
  </pre>

  <p>The two reactions occur in opposite directions.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Forward and Reverse Reactions', 15),
  (654, 654, '<h2>Dynamic Equilibrium</h2>

  <p>Dynamic equilibrium is reached in a reversible reaction when the rate of the forward reaction becomes equal to the rate of the reverse reaction.</p>

  <pre>
A + B ⇌ C + D
  </pre>

  <h3>Conditions for Dynamic Equilibrium</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>The reaction must be reversible.</li>
    <li>The system must be closed so that substances are not continuously lost.</li>
    <li>The forward and reverse reactions continue to occur.</li>
    <li>The rates of the forward and reverse reactions become equal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>Equilibrium does <strong>not</strong> mean that the reactions have stopped.</p>

  <p>The reactions continue, but they occur at equal rates.</p>

  <h3>At equilibrium</h3>

  <pre>
Rate of forward reaction
        =
Rate of reverse reaction
  </pre>

  <p>The concentrations of reactants and products remain constant, provided the conditions remain unchanged.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Dynamic Equilibrium', 15),
  (655, 655, '<h2>Establishing Equilibrium</h2>

  <p>When a reversible reaction begins, the forward and reverse reaction rates are usually different.</p>

  <h3>Beginning of the reaction</h3>

  <p>If only reactants are initially present:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The forward reaction starts quickly because many reactant particles are available.</li>
    <li>The reverse reaction is initially slow because few or no products are present.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>As the reaction proceeds</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Reactants are used up.</li>
    <li>Products are formed.</li>
    <li>The forward reaction becomes slower.</li>
    <li>The reverse reaction becomes faster.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>At equilibrium</h3>

  <pre>
Forward rate = Reverse rate
  </pre>

  <p>The concentrations then remain constant if temperature, pressure and concentration conditions are unchanged.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Establishing Equilibrium', 15),
  (656, 656, '<h2>Effect of Concentration on Equilibrium</h2>

  <p>Changing the concentration of a substance in a reversible reaction can shift the equilibrium position.</p>

  <pre>
A + B ⇌ C + D
  </pre>

  <h3>Adding a Reactant</h3>

  <p>If the concentration of A is increased, the system tends to use up some of the added A.</p>

  <p>The equilibrium therefore shifts to the right, producing more C and D.</p>

  <h3>Adding a Product</h3>

  <p>If the concentration of C is increased, the system tends to use up some of the added C.</p>

  <p>The equilibrium shifts to the left.</p>

  <h3>General Rule</h3>

  <p>A system at equilibrium responds to a concentration change by shifting in the direction that tends to oppose that change.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Concentration on Equilibrium', 15),
  (657, 657, '<h2>Effect of Temperature on Equilibrium</h2>

  <p>Changing temperature affects the position of equilibrium because one direction of a reversible reaction is exothermic and the other is endothermic.</p>

  <h3>Exothermic Forward Reaction</h3>

  <pre>
A + B ⇌ C + D + heat
  </pre>

  <p>Increasing temperature adds heat to the system.</p>

  <p>The equilibrium shifts in the endothermic direction, which is toward the reactants in this example.</p>

  <h3>Decreasing Temperature</h3>

  <p>Removing heat favours the exothermic direction.</p>

  <h3>General Rule</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Increase temperature → favours the endothermic direction.</li>
    <li>Decrease temperature → favours the exothermic direction.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Temperature changes can therefore alter the equilibrium composition.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Temperature on Equilibrium', 15),
  (658, 658, '<h2>Effect of Pressure on Equilibrium</h2>

  <p>Pressure changes can affect equilibria involving gases when the two sides contain different numbers of gaseous molecules.</p>

  <h3>Increasing Pressure</h3>

  <p>When pressure is increased, equilibrium tends to shift toward the side with fewer moles of gas.</p>

  <h3>Decreasing Pressure</h3>

  <p>When pressure is decreased, equilibrium tends to shift toward the side with more moles of gas.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <pre>
N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)
  </pre>

  <p>Left side:</p>
  <pre>
1 + 3 = 4 moles of gas
  </pre>

  <p>Right side:</p>
  <pre>
2 moles of gas
  </pre>

  <p>Increasing pressure therefore favours the right-hand side.</p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>If both sides contain the same number of gaseous molecules, changing pressure does not shift the equilibrium position.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Pressure on Equilibrium', 15),
  (659, 659, '<h2>Industrial Equilibrium</h2>

  <p>Reversible reactions are important in industry because many useful substances are produced through equilibrium processes.</p>

  <h3>Haber Process</h3>

  <p>The Haber process produces ammonia.</p>

  <pre>
N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)
  </pre>

  <p>The reaction is reversible and exothermic.</p>

  <h3>Important Conditions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>High pressure favours ammonia because the product side has fewer gas molecules.</li>
    <li>A moderate temperature is used as a compromise between rate and yield.</li>
    <li>Iron is used as a catalyst.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Contact Process</h3>

  <p>The Contact process is used to manufacture sulphuric acid.</p>

  <p>An important equilibrium step is:</p>

  <pre>
2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)
  </pre>

  <p>The reaction is exothermic.</p>

  <p>Vanadium(V) oxide, V₂O₅, is used as a catalyst.</p>

  <h3>Important Principle</h3>

  <p>Industrial conditions are chosen as a compromise between obtaining a good yield and achieving a sufficiently fast reaction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Industrial Equilibrium', 15),
  (660, 660, '<h2>Collision Theory</h2>

  <p>Collision theory explains how chemical reactions occur.</p>

  <p>For particles to react, they must:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>collide with each other,</li>
    <li>have enough energy, and</li>
    <li>collide in a suitable orientation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A collision that produces a reaction is called an <strong>effective collision</strong>.</p>

  <p>Simply colliding is not enough. If the particles do not have sufficient energy or the correct orientation, no reaction occurs.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>When hydrogen and oxygen react to form water, their particles must collide with sufficient energy and suitable orientation for bonds to break and new bonds to form.</p>

  <p><strong>Key idea:</strong> More effective collisions per second produce a faster reaction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Collision Theory', 15),
  (661, 661, '<h2>Activation Energy</h2>

  <p><strong>Activation energy</strong> is the minimum energy that reacting particles must have for a successful reaction to occur.</p>

  <p>It is represented by <strong>E<sub>a</sub></strong>.</p>

  <p>If particles collide with energy below the activation energy, the collision does not produce a reaction.</p>

  <p>If particles collide with energy equal to or greater than the activation energy and have suitable orientation, the collision can be effective.</p>

  <h3>Important relationship</h3>
  <p>Higher activation energy means fewer particles have enough energy to react under the same conditions.</p>

  <p>A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Activation Energy', 15),
  (662, 662, '<h2>Effective Collisions</h2>

  <p>An <strong>effective collision</strong> is a collision between reacting particles that results in a chemical reaction.</p>

  <p>For a collision to be effective:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The particles must collide.</li>
    <li>They must have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy.</li>
    <li>They must have a suitable orientation.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Increasing the number of effective collisions per second increases the rate of reaction.</p>

  <h3>Effect of Temperature</h3>
  <p>When temperature increases, particles move faster and have more kinetic energy. A greater fraction of particles can therefore reach or exceed the activation energy.</p>

  <h3>Effect of a Catalyst</h3>
  <p>A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy. This allows a greater proportion of collisions to become effective.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effective Collisions', 15),
  (663, 663, '<h2>Breaking Bonds</h2>

  <p>When a chemical bond is broken, <strong>energy is absorbed</strong> from the surroundings.</p>

  <p>The energy required to break one mole of a particular bond is called its <strong>bond energy</strong>.</p>

  <p>For example, breaking an H–H bond requires energy:</p>

  <p><strong>H<sub>2</sub> → 2H</strong></p>

  <p>The process requires energy because the attraction holding the atoms together must be overcome.</p>

  <h3>Key rule</h3>
  <p><strong>Breaking bonds always absorbs energy.</strong></p>

  <p>The stronger the bond, the more energy is generally required to break it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Breaking Bonds', 15),
  (664, 664, '<h2>Making Bonds</h2>

  <p>When new chemical bonds are formed, <strong>energy is released</strong> to the surroundings.</p>

  <p>The amount of energy released depends on the bonds being formed.</p>

  <p>For example, when hydrogen atoms form hydrogen molecules:</p>

  <p><strong>2H → H<sub>2</sub></strong></p>

  <p>Energy is released as the new H–H bond forms.</p>

  <h3>Key rule</h3>
  <p><strong>Making bonds always releases energy.</strong></p>

  <p>Strong bonds generally release more energy when they are formed than weaker bonds of the same type.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Making Bonds', 15),
  (665, 665, '<h2>Bond Energy Calculations</h2>

  <p>The energy change of a reaction can be estimated from the bond energies of the bonds broken and the bonds formed.</p>

  <p>The relationship is:</p>

  <p><strong>Energy change = Energy absorbed breaking bonds − Energy released forming bonds</strong></p>

  <p>Therefore:</p>

  <p><strong>ΔH = Σ(bond energies of bonds broken) − Σ(bond energies of bonds formed)</strong></p>

  <h3>Interpreting the answer</h3>

  <p>If ΔH is negative, the reaction is <strong>exothermic</strong>.</p>

  <p>If ΔH is positive, the reaction is <strong>endothermic</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>Suppose:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Total energy used to break bonds = 900 kJ</li>
    <li>Total energy released when bonds form = 1200 kJ</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Then:</p>

  <p><strong>ΔH = 900 − 1200 = −300 kJ</strong></p>

  <p>The reaction is therefore exothermic.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Bond Energy Calculations', 15),
  (666, 666, '<h2>Combustion of Fuels</h2>

  <p>A <strong>fuel</strong> is a substance that releases useful energy when it undergoes combustion.</p>

  <p><strong>Combustion</strong> is the reaction of a substance with oxygen accompanied by the release of energy, usually as heat.</p>

  <h3>Complete Combustion</h3>

  <p>When a hydrocarbon burns in a sufficient supply of oxygen, complete combustion occurs.</p>

  <p>The products are <strong>carbon dioxide and water</strong>.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O</strong></p>

  <h3>Incomplete Combustion</h3>

  <p>When oxygen is insufficient, incomplete combustion may occur.</p>

  <p>It can produce <strong>carbon monoxide</strong> and/or <strong>carbon</strong> together with water.</p>

  <p>Carbon monoxide is poisonous because it reduces the blood''s ability to transport oxygen.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Combustion of Fuels', 15),
  (667, 667, '<h2>Calorific Value</h2>

  <p><strong>Calorific value</strong> is the amount of energy released when a unit mass of a fuel undergoes complete combustion.</p>

  <p>It is commonly expressed in <strong>kJ/g</strong> or <strong>MJ/kg</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Calculating Energy Released</h3>

  <p>When calorific value is given:</p>

  <p><strong>Energy released = mass of fuel × calorific value</strong></p>

  <p>For example, if a fuel has a calorific value of 40 kJ/g and 25 g is completely burned:</p>

  <p><strong>Energy = 25 × 40 = 1000 kJ</strong></p>

  <p>Therefore, 25 g of the fuel releases 1000 kJ of energy.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Calorific Value', 15),
  (668, 668, '<h2>Comparing Fuels</h2>

  <p>Fuels can be compared using properties such as:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>calorific value,</li>
    <li>amount of pollution produced,</li>
    <li>availability,</li>
    <li>cost,</li>
    <li>ease of storage and transport,</li>
    <li>safety, and</li>
    <li>renewability.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Calorific Value</h3>

  <p>A fuel with a higher calorific value releases more energy per unit mass when completely burned.</p>

  <p>For example, if fuel A has a calorific value of 45 kJ/g and fuel B has a calorific value of 30 kJ/g, fuel A releases more energy per gram.</p>

  <h3>Environmental Effects</h3>

  <p>Fuels should also be assessed according to the substances released during combustion.</p>

  <p>Incomplete combustion can produce carbon monoxide. Fuels containing sulphur can produce sulphur dioxide, while burning hydrocarbons produces carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p>A good fuel therefore cannot be judged using calorific value alone.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Fuels', 15),
  (669, 669, '<h2>Bauxite</h2>

  <p><strong>Bauxite</strong> is the main ore from which aluminium is extracted.</p>

  <p>It contains aluminium compounds together with impurities such as iron(III) oxide, silicon compounds and other materials.</p>

  <p>Because aluminium is very reactive, it is not extracted from its ore by simple reduction with carbon.</p>

  <h3>Extraction of Aluminium</h3>

  <p>The extraction of aluminium involves:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Obtaining bauxite.</li>
    <li>Purifying the bauxite to obtain aluminium oxide.</li>
    <li>Electrolysing molten aluminium oxide to produce aluminium.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>The purified aluminium oxide is commonly called <strong>alumina</strong>.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Bauxite', 15),
  (670, 670, '<h2>Purification of Bauxite</h2>

  <p>Bauxite contains aluminium compounds mixed with impurities. The aluminium compound must therefore be separated from the unwanted materials.</p>

  <p>The purified product is <strong>aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃</strong>, also called <strong>alumina</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Why Purification Is Necessary</h3>

  <p>Impurities can interfere with the extraction process and increase the amount of material that must be processed.</p>

  <p>Purification produces aluminium oxide that can be used in the electrolytic extraction stage.</p>

  <h3>Extraction Sequence</h3>

  <p><strong>Bauxite → Purification → Aluminium oxide → Electrolysis → Aluminium</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Purification of Bauxite', 15),
  (671, 671, '<h2>Electrolysis of Aluminium Oxide</h2>

  <p>Aluminium is extracted from aluminium oxide by <strong>electrolysis</strong>.</p>

  <p>Aluminium oxide has a very high melting point. In the industrial process, it is dissolved in molten <strong>cryolite</strong> to lower the operating temperature and improve the conductivity of the electrolyte.</p>

  <h3>At the Cathode</h3>

  <p>Aluminium ions move towards the negative cathode and gain electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al</strong></p>

  <p>Aluminium metal is therefore formed at the cathode.</p>

  <h3>At the Anode</h3>

  <p>Oxide ions move towards the positive anode and lose electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻</strong></p>

  <p>The oxygen produced reacts with the carbon anodes, causing them to be gradually consumed.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Aluminium ions are reduced at the cathode.</li>
    <li>Oxide ions are oxidised at the anode.</li>
    <li>Aluminium forms at the cathode.</li>
    <li>Carbon anodes are gradually consumed.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Electrolysis of Aluminium Oxide', 15),
  (672, 672, '<h2>Chemical Properties of Metals</h2>

  <p>Metals undergo chemical reactions with substances such as oxygen, water and acids. Their reactions depend on the position of the metal in the reactivity series.</p>

  <h3>Reaction with Oxygen</h3>

  <p>Many metals react with oxygen to form metal oxides.</p>

  <p><strong>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</strong></p>

  <p>Magnesium reacts with oxygen to form magnesium oxide.</p>

  <h3>Reaction with Water</h3>

  <p>Some metals react with water to form a metal hydroxide and hydrogen gas.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂</strong></p>

  <p>More reactive metals generally react more readily with water.</p>

  <h3>Reaction with Dilute Acids</h3>

  <p>Metals above hydrogen in the reactivity series can react with dilute acids to produce a salt and hydrogen gas.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂</strong></p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>The ease with which a metal reacts depends on its reactivity. A more reactive metal loses electrons more readily than a less reactive metal.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chemical Properties of Metals', 15),
  (673, 673, '<h2>Concentration of Ores</h2>

  <p>An ore contains a useful mineral together with unwanted materials called <strong>gangue</strong>.</p>

  <p>Before the metal is extracted, the ore is usually concentrated to increase the proportion of the useful mineral.</p>

  <p><strong>Concentration of an ore</strong> is the process of removing unwanted gangue from the ore.</p>

  <h3>Why Ores Are Concentrated</h3>

  <p>Concentrating the ore reduces the amount of unwanted material that must be processed during extraction.</p>

  <p>This makes the subsequent extraction process more efficient.</p>

  <h3>Methods</h3>

  <p>The method used depends on the physical and chemical properties of the ore and the gangue.</p>

  <p>Examples include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>gravity separation;</li>
    <li>magnetic separation;</li>
    <li>froth flotation.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For example, magnetic separation can be used when the desired mineral has magnetic properties that differ from the gangue.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Concentration of Ores', 15),
  (674, 674, '<h2>Extraction by Reduction</h2>

  <p>Some metals are extracted from their ores by reducing their metal oxides.</p>

  <p><strong>Reduction</strong> is the removal of oxygen from a metal oxide, or the gain of electrons by the metal ion.</p>

  <h3>Reduction Using Carbon</h3>

  <p>Metals that are less reactive than carbon can often be extracted by heating their oxides with carbon.</p>

  <p>For example, zinc oxide can be reduced by carbon:</p>

  <p><strong>ZnO + C → Zn + CO</strong></p>

  <p>The carbon removes oxygen from zinc oxide, leaving zinc metal.</p>

  <h3>Reduction Using Carbon Monoxide</h3>

  <p>Carbon monoxide can also act as a reducing agent.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>Iron(III) oxide is reduced to iron while carbon monoxide is oxidised to carbon dioxide.</p>

  <h3>Choosing the Method</h3>

  <p>The extraction method depends on the reactivity of the metal.</p>

  <p>Very reactive metals, such as aluminium, cannot generally be extracted by carbon reduction and require electrolysis.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Extraction by Reduction', 15),
  (675, 675, '<h2>Extraction of Iron</h2>

  <p>Iron is extracted from iron ore in a <strong>blast furnace</strong>. The main ore used is haematite, which contains iron(III) oxide, Fe₂O₃.</p>

  <h3>Raw Materials</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Haematite</strong> — source of iron(III) oxide</li>
    <li><strong>Coke</strong> — provides heat and produces carbon monoxide</li>
    <li><strong>Limestone</strong> — removes impurities</li>
    <li><strong>Hot air</strong> — supplies oxygen for combustion</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Formation of Carbon Monoxide</h3>

  <p>Coke burns in the hot air to produce carbon dioxide:</p>

  <p><strong>C + O₂ → CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>Carbon dioxide then reacts with more hot carbon:</p>

  <p><strong>CO₂ + C → 2CO</strong></p>

  <p>The carbon monoxide acts as the reducing agent.</p>

  <h3>Reduction of Iron(III) Oxide</h3>

  <p>Carbon monoxide removes oxygen from iron(III) oxide:</p>

  <p><strong>Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>Molten iron collects at the bottom of the furnace.</p>

  <h3>Role of Limestone</h3>

  <p>Limestone decomposes when heated:</p>

  <p><strong>CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂</strong></p>

  <p>Calcium oxide reacts with silica impurity to form slag:</p>

  <p><strong>CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃</strong></p>

  <p>The slag is separated from the molten iron.</p>

  <h3>Key Sequence</h3>

  <p><strong>Haematite → reduction → molten iron → separation from slag</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Extraction of Iron', 15),
  (676, 676, '<h2>Rusting</h2>

  <p><strong>Rusting</strong> is the slow chemical reaction in which iron reacts with oxygen and water to form rust.</p>

  <p>Rust is mainly a hydrated form of iron(III) oxide.</p>

  <h3>Conditions Required for Rusting</h3>

  <p>Iron requires both <strong>oxygen</strong> and <strong>water</strong> for rusting to occur.</p>

  <p><strong>Iron + oxygen + water → rust</strong></p>

  <h3>What Happens During Rusting?</h3>

  <p>Iron atoms lose electrons during the process while oxygen is reduced. The resulting iron compounds eventually form hydrated iron(III) oxide, commonly called rust.</p>

  <h3>Effects of Rusting</h3>

  <p>Rusting gradually weakens iron and steel objects. It can cause:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>loss of strength;</li>
    <li>damage to structures;</li>
    <li>failure of machinery and equipment;</li>
    <li>economic losses through replacement and repair.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Rusting is a chemical change because new substances are formed and the process is not easily reversed by physical means.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Rusting</h2>

  <p><strong>Rusting</strong> is the slow chemical reaction in which iron reacts with oxygen and water to form rust.</p>

  <p>Rust is mainly a hydrated form of iron(III) oxide.</p>

  <h3>Conditions Required for Rusting</h3>

  <p>Iron requires both <strong>oxygen</strong> and <strong>water</strong> for rusting to occur.</p>

  <p><strong>Iron + oxygen + water → rust</strong></p>

  <h3>What Happens During Rusting?</h3>

  <p>Iron atoms lose electrons during the process while oxygen is reduced. The resulting iron compounds eventually form hydrated iron(III) oxide, commonly called rust.</p>

  <h3>Effects of Rusting</h3>

  <p>Rusting gradually weakens iron and steel objects. It can cause:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>loss of strength;</li>
    <li>damage to structures;</li>
    <li>failure of machinery and equipment;</li>
    <li>economic losses through replacement and repair.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Rusting is a chemical change because new substances are formed and the process is not easily reversed by physical means.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Rusting', 15),
  (677, 677, '<h2>Alloys</h2>

  <p>An <strong>alloy</strong> is a mixture of a metal with one or more other elements, usually made to improve the properties of the metal.</p>

  <h3>Why Alloys Are Made</h3>

  <p>Pure metals may not always have the properties required for a particular use. Mixing a metal with other elements can produce a material with improved properties such as strength, hardness, resistance to corrosion or lower density.</p>

  <h3>Common Alloys</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Steel</strong> — mainly iron and carbon.</li>
    <li><strong>Stainless steel</strong> — mainly iron with chromium and other elements; it has good resistance to corrosion.</li>
    <li><strong>Brass</strong> — copper and zinc.</li>
    <li><strong>Bronze</strong> — copper and tin.</li>
    <li><strong>Duralumin</strong> — mainly aluminium with copper and other elements; it is strong and relatively light.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Alloys Are Often Stronger</h3>

  <p>In a pure metal, atoms are arranged in regular layers that can slide over one another relatively easily.</p>

  <p>In an alloy, atoms of different sizes disturb the regular arrangement. This makes it more difficult for the layers to slide, so the alloy is often harder or stronger than the pure metal.</p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>The composition of an alloy determines its properties and therefore its suitable uses.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Alloys', 15),
  (678, 678, '<h2>Uses of Metals</h2>

  <p>Metals are used in different applications because of their physical and chemical properties such as strength, electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity, low density and resistance to corrosion.</p>

  <h3>Aluminium</h3>

  <p>Aluminium is light, malleable and resistant to corrosion.</p>

  <p>It is used for:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>aircraft construction;</li>
    <li>cooking utensils;</li>
    <li>food and drink containers;</li>
    <li>electrical transmission cables.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Copper</h3>

  <p>Copper is a good conductor of electricity and heat and is relatively resistant to corrosion.</p>

  <p>It is used for:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>electrical wiring;</li>
    <li>water pipes;</li>
    <li>heat exchangers;</li>
    <li>making alloys such as brass and bronze.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Iron and Steel</h3>

  <p>Iron and steel are strong and are widely used in construction and manufacturing.</p>

  <p>They are used for:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>buildings and bridges;</li>
    <li>machinery;</li>
    <li>vehicles;</li>
    <li>tools and equipment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Zinc</h3>

  <p>Zinc is used mainly for protecting iron and steel from corrosion and for making alloys such as brass.</p>

  <h3>Gold and Silver</h3>

  <p>Gold and silver are relatively unreactive and are valued for their appearance and resistance to corrosion.</p>

  <p>They are used in:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>jewellery;</li>
    <li>electrical and electronic components;</li>
    <li>specialised industrial applications.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>The use of a metal depends on the properties that make it suitable for a particular application.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uses of Metals', 15),
  (679, 679, '<h2>Preparation of Hydrogen</h2>

  <p>Hydrogen gas can be prepared in the laboratory by reacting a suitable metal with a dilute acid.</p>

  <p>A common method uses <strong>zinc</strong> and <strong>dilute hydrochloric acid</strong>.</p>

  <p><strong>Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂</strong></p>

  <h3>What Happens</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Zinc reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid.</li>
    <li>Zinc chloride is formed.</li>
    <li>Hydrogen gas is released.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>The hydrogen can be collected and tested using a suitable test.</p>

  <h3>Important Safety Point</h3>

  <p>Hydrogen is highly flammable. It must be kept away from naked flames except when carrying out the controlled test for the gas.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Preparation of Hydrogen', 15),
  (680, 680, '<h2>Properties of Hydrogen</h2>

  <p>Hydrogen is a non-metal and the lightest element.</p>

  <h3>Physical Properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>It is a colourless gas.</li>
    <li>It is odourless.</li>
    <li>It is very light.</li>
    <li>It is only slightly soluble in water.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Chemical Properties</h3>

  <p>Hydrogen is <strong>flammable</strong> and burns in oxygen to form water.</p>

  <p><strong>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</strong></p>

  <p>Hydrogen can also act as a reducing agent in some reactions because it can remove oxygen from certain metal oxides.</p>

  <p>For example:</p>

  <p><strong>CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O</strong></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Properties of Hydrogen', 15),
  (681, 681, '<h2>Test for Hydrogen</h2>

  <p>Hydrogen can be identified using a <strong>lighted splint test</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Procedure</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Collect a small sample of the gas.</li>
    <li>Bring a lighted splint to the gas.</li>
    <li>Observe the sound and reaction.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Hydrogen burns rapidly and produces a characteristic <strong>squeaky pop</strong>.</p>

  <p>The reaction is:</p>

  <p><strong>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</strong></p>

  <h3>Safety</h3>

  <p>Only a small sample should be tested under controlled laboratory conditions because hydrogen is highly flammable.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test for Hydrogen', 15),
  (682, 682, '<h2>Uses of Hydrogen</h2>

  <p>Hydrogen has several important uses because of its chemical properties and its ability to release energy during combustion.</p>

  <h3>Major Uses</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Manufacture of ammonia:</strong> Hydrogen reacts with nitrogen in the Haber process.</li>
    <li><strong>Hydrogenation:</strong> Hydrogen is used to add hydrogen to unsaturated compounds in some industrial processes.</li>
    <li><strong>Fuel:</strong> Hydrogen can be used as a fuel because it releases energy when it reacts with oxygen.</li>
    <li><strong>Reduction:</strong> Hydrogen can reduce some metal oxides to metals.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Hydrogen in the Haber Process</h3>

  <p>Hydrogen reacts with nitrogen to produce ammonia:</p>

  <p><strong>N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃</strong></p>

  <p>Ammonia is important in the manufacture of fertilisers and other chemicals.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Uses of Hydrogen', 15),
  (683, 683, '<h2>Water Pollution</h2>

  <p><strong>Water pollution</strong> is the contamination of water by substances that make it harmful or unsuitable for humans, animals and other organisms.</p>

  <h3>Sources of Water Pollution</h3>

  <p>Water can be polluted by:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>untreated sewage;</li>
    <li>industrial waste;</li>
    <li>agricultural fertilisers;</li>
    <li>pesticides;</li>
    <li>oil and other chemical spills;</li>
    <li>domestic waste.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Effects of Water Pollution</h3>

  <p>Polluted water can cause disease and harm aquatic organisms.</p>

  <p>Fertilisers containing nitrates and phosphates can cause excessive growth of algae. When the algae die and decompose, microorganisms use up dissolved oxygen. This can cause fish and other aquatic organisms to die.</p>

  <p>Toxic substances can also accumulate in organisms and enter food chains.</p>

  <h3>Control of Water Pollution</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>treat sewage before discharge;</li>
    <li>treat industrial waste appropriately;</li>
    <li>reduce excessive use of fertilisers and pesticides;</li>
    <li>prevent oil and chemical spills;</li>
    <li>dispose of domestic waste properly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Preventing pollutants from entering water sources is more effective than trying to remove pollution after contamination has occurred.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Water Pollution', 15),
  (684, 684, '<h2>Ozone Layer</h2>

  <p>The <strong>ozone layer</strong> is a region of the stratosphere containing a relatively high concentration of ozone, O₃.</p>

  <h3>Formation of Ozone</h3>

  <p>Ultraviolet radiation from the Sun can split oxygen molecules into oxygen atoms:</p>

  <p><strong>O₂ → 2O</strong></p>

  <p>The oxygen atoms then combine with oxygen molecules to form ozone:</p>

  <p><strong>O + O₂ → O₃</strong></p>

  <h3>Importance of the Ozone Layer</h3>

  <p>The ozone layer absorbs much of the Sun''s harmful ultraviolet radiation before it reaches Earth''s surface.</p>

  <p>This protection reduces excessive exposure of living organisms to ultraviolet radiation.</p>

  <h3>Ultraviolet Radiation</h3>

  <p>Excessive exposure to ultraviolet radiation can damage living cells and can cause problems such as skin damage and increased risk of skin cancer.</p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Ozone in the stratosphere is important because it absorbs harmful ultraviolet radiation from the Sun.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ozone Layer', 15),
  (685, 685, '<h2>Ozone Depletion</h2>

  <p><strong>Ozone depletion</strong> is the reduction in the concentration of ozone in the stratosphere.</p>

  <h3>Causes of Ozone Depletion</h3>

  <p>Certain chemicals can release reactive chlorine or bromine atoms in the stratosphere. These atoms can participate in reactions that destroy ozone molecules.</p>

  <p>One important group of ozone-depleting substances is <strong>chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)</strong>, which were formerly used in applications such as refrigeration and aerosol propellants.</p>

  <h3>Effect on Ozone</h3>

  <p>Ultraviolet radiation can break down CFC molecules and release chlorine atoms.</p>

  <p>The chlorine can react with ozone, reducing the amount of O₃ present. A single chlorine atom can participate in repeated reactions, allowing it to destroy many ozone molecules.</p>

  <h3>Effects of Ozone Depletion</h3>

  <p>When the ozone layer becomes thinner, more ultraviolet radiation can reach Earth''s surface.</p>

  <p>Increased ultraviolet exposure can:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>damage living cells;</li>
    <li>increase the risk of skin cancer;</li>
    <li>cause eye damage such as cataracts;</li>
    <li>damage some plants and aquatic organisms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Control of Ozone Depletion</h3>

  <p>Ozone depletion can be reduced by controlling the production and use of ozone-depleting substances and replacing them with safer alternatives.</p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Ozone depletion reduces the protection provided by stratospheric ozone and can increase the amount of harmful ultraviolet radiation reaching Earth''s surface.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ozone Depletion', 15),
  (686, 686, '<h2>Sustainable Chemistry</h2>

  <p><strong>Sustainable chemistry</strong> involves designing and using chemical processes and substances in ways that reduce harm to people and the environment while making efficient use of resources.</p>

  <h3>Reducing Waste</h3>

  <p>Chemical processes should produce as little unwanted waste as possible.</p>

  <p>Reducing waste at the source is generally better than producing waste and treating it afterwards.</p>

  <h3>Efficient Use of Resources</h3>

  <p>Sustainable chemical processes aim to use raw materials, energy and water efficiently.</p>

  <p>For example, a process that requires less energy can reduce fuel consumption and associated emissions.</p>

  <h3>Safer Chemicals</h3>

  <p>Where possible, substances that are less toxic and less harmful to the environment should be used instead of more hazardous alternatives.</p>

  <h3>Renewable Resources</h3>

  <p>Renewable raw materials can be used where appropriate instead of resources that are rapidly depleted.</p>

  <h3>Recycling and Reuse</h3>

  <p>Materials can sometimes be recovered, reused or recycled instead of being discarded.</p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Sustainable chemistry aims to prevent pollution and reduce environmental harm by making chemical processes safer, more efficient and less wasteful.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sustainable Chemistry', 15),
  (687, 687, '<h2>Waste Management</h2>

  <p><strong>Waste management</strong> is the proper handling, treatment, recovery and disposal of waste to reduce harm to people and the environment.</p>

  <h3>Types of Waste</h3>

  <p>Waste can include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>domestic waste;</li>
    <li>industrial waste;</li>
    <li>agricultural waste;</li>
    <li>chemical waste;</li>
    <li>plastic waste;</li>
    <li>electronic waste.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Reduce</h3>

  <p><strong>Reducing</strong> waste means using fewer materials and avoiding unnecessary consumption.</p>

  <p>For example, using only the amount of a chemical required for an experiment reduces chemical waste.</p>

  <h3>Reuse</h3>

  <p><strong>Reusing</strong> means using an item again instead of throwing it away.</p>

  <p>Reuse reduces the demand for new materials and decreases the amount of waste produced.</p>

  <h3>Recycle</h3>

  <p><strong>Recycling</strong> involves collecting and processing waste materials so that they can be used to make new products.</p>

  <h3>Safe Disposal</h3>

  <p>Some wastes cannot be safely reused or recycled and require appropriate treatment and disposal.</p>

  <p>Hazardous chemical waste should not be poured into drains or released directly into the environment.</p>

  <h3>Key Idea</h3>

  <p>Good waste management aims to prevent waste where possible, recover useful materials and safely handle waste that remains.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Waste Management', 15),
  (688, 688, '
  <h2>What Is a Cell?</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>cell</b> is the smallest unit of a living organism that can carry out
    the processes needed for life.
  </p>

  <p>
    This means that a cell is not simply a tiny part of an organism.
    It is a living unit with structures that allow it to obtain substances,
    use energy, remove wastes, grow and respond to its surroundings.
  </p>

  <h3>Think of a Cell as a Living System</h3>

  <p>
    Imagine a cell as a very small, organised workshop.
    Different structures inside it have different jobs, but they work together
    to keep the cell alive.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, the cell needs a boundary to control what enters and leaves,
    a region where many chemical reactions occur, and genetic material that
    contains instructions for the cell.
  </p>

  <h3>Cells Are Extremely Small</h3>

  <p>
    Most cells cannot be seen clearly with the naked eye. They are usually
    measured in <b>micrometres (µm)</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    One micrometre is one millionth of a metre:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>1 µm = 0.000001 m</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why Does the Cell Matter?</h3>

  <p>
    Every part of a living organism is ultimately made from cells.
    The ability of cells to carry out life processes is therefore the
    foundation of life in organisms.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why is a cell described as a unit of life?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> A living organism must carry out processes such as obtaining
    substances, releasing energy and removing waste.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> A cell contains structures that allow these processes to occur.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> A cell is called a unit of life because it is the smallest
    organised unit capable of carrying out the processes necessary for life.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Cell = the basic living unit from which organisms are built.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is a Cell', 15),
  (689, 689, '
  <h2>Why Cells Are the Basic Units of Life</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Saying that cells are the <b>basic units of life</b> means that the
    essential activities of living organisms are carried out by cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    A living organism must obtain substances, release energy, remove wastes,
    respond to changes and maintain conditions that allow life to continue.
    Cells are the units that perform these functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Cells Perform Life Processes</h3>

  <p>
    Depending on the type of cell and organism, cells are involved in processes
    such as:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>obtaining nutrients or raw materials,</li>
    <li>releasing and using energy,</li>
    <li>removing waste products,</li>
    <li>growth and repair,</li>
    <li>responding to changes in the environment, and</li>
    <li>reproduction or cell division.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Cells Do Different Jobs</h3>

  <p>
    Not every cell performs exactly the same task.
    A red blood cell is adapted for transporting oxygen, while a root hair cell
    is adapted for absorbing water and mineral ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their jobs are different, but both are living cells carrying out processes
    that support the organism.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> A student says, "The heart is the basic unit of life."
    Is this correct?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> The heart is an organ.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> An organ is made from different tissues, and tissues are
    made from cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> The cells carry out the biological processes that allow the
    heart to function.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The heart is not the basic unit of life.
    The cell is the basic unit of life.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Cells are the basic units of life because living processes occur within
    cells.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Cells Are the Basic Units of Life', 15),
  (690, 690, '
  <h2>Cell Membrane</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>cell membrane</b> is a thin boundary surrounding the cell.
    It separates the contents of the cell from its surroundings.
  </p>

  <h3>Its Main Function</h3>

  <p>
    The membrane controls the movement of substances <b>into and out of the
    cell</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is important because a cell cannot allow every substance in its
    surroundings to enter freely.
  </p>

  <p>
    The cell needs some substances, such as oxygen and nutrients, but it may
    need to prevent or control the entry of other substances.
  </p>

  <h3>Selective Permeability</h3>

  <p>
    The cell membrane is described as <b>selectively permeable</b> because
    different substances cross it in different ways and at different rates.
  </p>

  <p>
    The membrane therefore helps the cell maintain suitable internal
    conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>Connection to Transport</h3>

  <p>
    Processes such as <b>diffusion, osmosis and active transport</b> involve
    movement across cell membranes.
  </p>

  <p>
    We will study these processes separately because they are different
    mechanisms.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why would a cell be damaged if its membrane could no
    longer control what enters and leaves?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Cells need particular substances in suitable amounts.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> The membrane controls movement of substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> If this control is lost, substances could enter or leave
    in inappropriate amounts.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The cell could lose the conditions needed for normal
    functioning and may eventually be damaged.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>The cell membrane controls movement of substances into and out of the cell.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cell Membrane', 15),
  (691, 691, '
  <h2>Cytoplasm</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>cytoplasm</b> is the living material inside the cell membrane,
    surrounding the cell''s internal structures.
  </p>

  <p>
    It contains a watery substance called the <b>cytosol</b> together with
    dissolved substances and structures found within the cell.
  </p>

  <h3>What Happens in the Cytoplasm?</h3>

  <p>
    Many of the chemical reactions needed by the cell occur in the cytoplasm.
  </p>

  <p>
    These reactions are part of the cell''s metabolism.
    The substances involved in these reactions must be able to move and
    interact in the cell''s internal environment.
  </p>

  <h3>Do Not Confuse Cytoplasm With the Cell Membrane</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>cell membrane</b> is the boundary around the cell.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <b>cytoplasm</b> is the internal living material within that boundary.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why is the cytoplasm important even though it does not
    have the same job as an organ such as the nucleus?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Cells need chemical reactions to remain alive.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Many of these reactions occur in the cytoplasm.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Therefore, the cytoplasm provides an important environment
    for cellular reactions.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>The cytoplasm is the internal living material where many cellular
    chemical reactions occur.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cytoplasm', 15),
  (692, 692, '
  <h2>Nucleus</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>nucleus</b> is a membrane-bound structure found in many eukaryotic
    cells. It contains most of the cell''s genetic material.
  </p>

  <h3>Genetic Material</h3>

  <p>
    Genetic material contains instructions that influence how a cell develops,
    functions and reproduces.
  </p>

  <p>
    The genetic material is organised into structures called
    <b>chromosomes</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Is the Nucleus Important?</h3>

  <p>
    The information in the nucleus helps regulate the activities of the cell.
    It is therefore important in processes such as growth, cell division and
    the production of proteins.
  </p>

  <h3>Important Precision</h3>

  <p>
    Do not think of the nucleus as physically performing every reaction in
    the cell.
    Its major importance is that it contains genetic information used in
    controlling cellular activities.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why can damage to the nucleus seriously affect a cell?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> The nucleus contains genetic information.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Genetic information is needed to regulate many cellular
    activities.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Damage to this information or its control can interfere
    with normal cell functioning.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>The nucleus contains genetic material that helps control cellular
    activities.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nucleus', 15),
  (693, 693, '
  <h2>Mitochondria</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Mitochondria</b> are structures in eukaryotic cells where important
    stages of aerobic respiration take place.
  </p>

  <p>
    Aerobic respiration releases energy from substances such as glucose
    using oxygen.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Do Cells Need Mitochondria?</h3>

  <p>
    Cells need a usable supply of energy for processes such as active
    transport, movement, growth, repair and synthesis of substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mitochondria are therefore particularly numerous in cells that have
    high energy demands.
  </p>

  <h3>Structure and Function</h3>

  <p>
    A mitochondrion has an inner membrane with many folds.
    These folds provide a large surface area for reactions involved in
    aerobic respiration.
  </p>

  <h3>Important Correction</h3>

  <p>
    It is common to hear the phrase
    <b>"mitochondria are the powerhouse of the cell."</b>
    This is only a rough analogy.
  </p>

  <p>
    A better scientific description is:
    <b>mitochondria are sites where important stages of aerobic respiration
    occur and where energy is transferred into usable cellular forms.</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why would a muscle cell contain many mitochondria?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Muscle cells require large amounts of energy for contraction.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Aerobic respiration releases energy that can be used by
    the cell.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Mitochondria are sites of important stages of aerobic
    respiration.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Muscle cells need many mitochondria because of their
    high energy demand.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Mitochondria are sites of important stages of aerobic respiration.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitochondria', 15),
  (694, 694, '
  <h2>Ribosomes</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Ribosomes</b> are structures where proteins are made.
  </p>

  <p>
    Proteins are essential because they perform many different roles in cells.
    They can form structures, act as enzymes, transport substances and perform
    other biological functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Does the Cell Need to Make Proteins?</h3>

  <p>
    Cells constantly need proteins for growth, repair, replacement and
    normal functioning.
  </p>

  <p>
    The information needed to make particular proteins ultimately comes from
    genes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The ribosome uses this information to assemble amino acids into a protein.
  </p>

  <h3>Ribosomes Are Not the Same as the Nucleus</h3>

  <p>
    The nucleus contains genetic information.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ribosomes are the structures where proteins are assembled.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> A growing cell needs to produce many new proteins.
    Which cell structure is directly responsible for assembling those proteins?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Proteins are built from amino acids.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Ribosomes join amino acids together in the correct sequence.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Ribosomes are directly involved in protein synthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Ribosomes are the sites of protein synthesis.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ribosomes', 15),
  (695, 695, '
  <h2>Cell Wall</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>cell wall</b> is a strong structure found outside the cell membrane
    of plant cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    The plant cell wall is mainly made of <b>cellulose</b>.
    It is not the same structure as the cell membrane.
  </p>

  <h3>What Does the Cell Wall Do?</h3>

  <p>
    The cell wall provides <b>strength and support</b> to the plant cell.
  </p>

  <p>
    It also helps the cell maintain its shape.
    When water enters a plant cell, the cell wall resists excessive expansion
    and helps the cell become firm.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell Wall vs Cell Membrane</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Cell wall</th>
      <th>Cell membrane</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Strong outer layer of a plant cell</td>
      <td>Thin boundary around the cell</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Mainly made of cellulose</td>
      <td>Made mainly of lipids and proteins</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Provides strength and support</td>
      <td>Controls movement of substances</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Important Point</h3>

  <p>
    The cell wall does <b>not</b> replace the cell membrane.
    A plant cell has both.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why does a plant cell need a cell wall in addition to
    its cell membrane?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> The membrane controls movement of substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> The cell also needs mechanical strength and support.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> The cell wall provides this strength while the membrane
    performs its transport-control function.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The two structures have different functions and work
    together.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>The cellulose cell wall strengthens and supports plant cells.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cell Wall', 15),
  (696, 696, '
  <h2>Large Permanent Vacuole</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Many mature plant cells contain a large <b>permanent vacuole</b>.
    It is surrounded by a membrane and contains a solution called
    <b>cell sap</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>What Does the Vacuole Do?</h3>

  <p>
    The vacuole stores water and dissolved substances such as sugars and
    mineral ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    It also plays an important role in maintaining the pressure inside
    plant cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Turgor</h3>

  <p>
    When a plant cell contains plenty of water, water pushes the cell contents
    against the cell wall.
    This creates <b>turgor pressure</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Turgor helps plant tissues remain firm.
  </p>

  <p>
    If plant cells lose too much water, turgor decreases and the plant may
    become <b>wilted</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Is This Different From an Animal Cell?</h3>

  <p>
    Animal cells do not normally have one large permanent vacuole occupying
    most of the cell.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why can a plant wilt when it loses too much water?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Water is stored in the plant cell''s vacuole.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Water helps maintain turgor pressure.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> When too much water is lost, turgor pressure falls.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Cells become less firm, causing the plant tissues
    to lose their rigidity and wilt.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>The large permanent vacuole stores cell sap and helps maintain
    turgor in plant cells.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Large Permanent Vacuole', 15),
  (697, 697, '
  <h2>Structures Shared by Plant and Animal Cells</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Plant and animal cells are different, but they also share several
    important structures.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both types of cells are eukaryotic cells and commonly contain:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>cell membrane</b></li>
    <li><b>cytoplasm</b></li>
    <li><b>nucleus</b></li>
    <li><b>mitochondria</b></li>
    <li><b>ribosomes</b></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Do They Share These Structures?</h3>

  <p>
    Plant and animal cells are both living cells.
    They therefore need structures that perform fundamental cellular
    functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Cell membrane</b>
    → controls movement of substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Cytoplasm</b>
    → provides an environment for many chemical reactions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Nucleus</b>
    → contains genetic material.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Mitochondria</b>
    → carry out important stages of aerobic respiration.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Ribosomes</b>
    → make proteins.
  </p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Saying that plant and animal cells share these structures does not mean
    that the cells are identical.
  </p>

  <p>
    Plant cells also have structures such as the cell wall, chloroplasts and
    a large permanent vacuole that are not characteristic of typical animal
    cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why do both plant and animal cells need mitochondria?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Both plant and animal cells require usable energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Mitochondria are sites of important stages of aerobic
    respiration.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Both types of cells can contain mitochondria because
    both require energy for cellular processes.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Plant and animal cells share structures required for fundamental
    cellular functions.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Structures Shared by Plant and Animal Cells', 15),
  (698, 698, '
  <h2>Plant Cell vs Animal Cell</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Plant and animal cells share many structures, but some structures differ
    because the cells perform different functions.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Structure</th>
      <th>Typical plant cell</th>
      <th>Typical animal cell</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Cell membrane</td>
      <td>Present</td>
      <td>Present</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Cytoplasm</td>
      <td>Present</td>
      <td>Present</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Nucleus</td>
      <td>Present in most cells</td>
      <td>Present in most cells</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Mitochondria</td>
      <td>Present</td>
      <td>Present</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Ribosomes</td>
      <td>Present</td>
      <td>Present</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Cell wall</td>
      <td>Present</td>
      <td>Absent</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Chloroplasts</td>
      <td>Present in photosynthetic cells</td>
      <td>Absent</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Large permanent vacuole</td>
      <td>Common in mature plant cells</td>
      <td>Absent as a typical large permanent vacuole</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Do Not Memorise the Table Without Understanding It</h3>

  <p>
    The differences exist because plant cells and animal cells have different
    requirements.
  </p>

  <p>
    A photosynthetic plant cell needs chloroplasts to capture light energy.
    A plant cell also benefits from a rigid cell wall and a large vacuole that
    helps maintain turgor.
  </p>

  <p>
    Animal cells do not need these structures for the same reasons.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> A cell has a cell wall, chloroplasts and a large permanent
    vacuole. Is it likely to be a typical plant cell or animal cell?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Animal cells do not have a cellulose cell wall.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Chloroplasts are characteristic of photosynthetic plant cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> A large permanent vacuole is also characteristic of many
    mature plant cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The cell is a plant cell.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Plant and animal cells share basic structures but differ in structures
    adapted to their different functions.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Plant Cell vs Animal Cell', 15),
  (699, 699, '
  <h2>Explaining Cell Differences</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Knowing that plant and animal cells are different is only the first step.
    A stronger biological explanation connects a structure to its function.
  </p>

  <h3>Example 1 — Cell Wall</h3>

  <p>
    Plant cells have a cellulose cell wall.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Structure:</b> strong cellulose wall.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Function:</b> provides mechanical support and helps the cell resist
    excessive expansion.
  </p>

  <h3>Example 2 — Chloroplast</h3>

  <p>
    Photosynthetic plant cells contain chloroplasts.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Structure:</b> chloroplast containing chlorophyll.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Function:</b> captures light energy used in photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>Example 3 — Large Permanent Vacuole</h3>

  <p>
    Many mature plant cells have a large permanent vacuole.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Structure:</b> large compartment containing cell sap.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Function:</b> stores water and dissolved substances and helps maintain
    turgor pressure.
  </p>

  <h3>The Key Biological Skill</h3>

  <p>
    When explaining a biological structure, do not stop at naming it.
    Explain:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>STRUCTURE → FUNCTION → ADVANTAGE</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    This is how we explain why a cell has a particular adaptation.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Explain why a photosynthetic leaf cell contains
    chloroplasts.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Chlorophyll absorbs light energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Light energy is required for photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The chloroplast enables the cell to capture light
    energy needed for photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Biology explanations should connect a structure to what it does and
    why that function matters.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Explaining Cell Differences', 15),
  (700, 700, '
<h2>Growth by Increase in Cell Number</h2>

<h3>What does increase in cell number mean?</h3>

<p>
When cells divide repeatedly, the total number of cells in a multicellular organism
or tissue can increase.
</p>

<p>
This is one important way multicellular organisms grow.
</p>

<h3>Following one cell division</h3>

<p>
1 cell → 2 cells
</p>

<p>
If both daughter cells divide again:
</p>

<p>
2 cells → 4 cells
</p>

<p>
If all four divide:
</p>

<p>
4 cells → 8 cells
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> One cell undergoes three rounds of mitotic division. Assume every
daughter cell divides during each round. How many cells are produced?</p>

<p><b>Round 1:</b> 1 × 2 = 2 cells.</p>
<p><b>Round 2:</b> 2 × 2 = 4 cells.</p>
<p><b>Round 3:</b> 4 × 2 = 8 cells.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 8 cells.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>
<p>
Repeated mitosis can cause cell number to increase rapidly.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Growth by Increase in Cell Number', 15),
  (701, 701, '
  <h2>Unicellular and Multicellular Organisms</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Organisms differ in the number of cells that make up their bodies.
    Some organisms consist of only <b>one cell</b>, while others consist of
    <b>many cells</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Unicellular Organisms</h3>

  <p>
    An organism made of a single cell is called <b>unicellular</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The single cell must carry out all the processes necessary for the
    organism to survive.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include organisms such as <b>Amoeba</b> and many bacteria.
  </p>

  <h3>Multicellular Organisms</h3>

  <p>
    An organism made of many cells is called <b>multicellular</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The cells can become specialised. Different groups of cells perform
    different functions, allowing the organism to perform complex activities.
  </p>

  <h3>The Important Difference</h3>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Unicellular</th>
      <th>Multicellular</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>One cell</td>
      <td>Many cells</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>One cell performs all essential functions</td>
      <td>Different cells can have specialised functions</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Usually simpler organisation</td>
      <td>More complex organisation is possible</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why can a multicellular organism have different types of
    specialised cells?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> A multicellular organism contains many cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> These cells can develop structures suited to different jobs.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Different specialised cells can therefore perform different
    functions for the same organism.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Multicellularity allows division of labour between cells.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Unicellular = one cell. Multicellular = many cells.</b>
  </p>
  
<hr>

  <h2>Unicellular and Multicellular Organisms</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Organisms can also be classified according to the number of cells that make
  up their bodies.
  </p>

  <h3>Unicellular organisms</h3>

  <p>
  A <b>unicellular organism</b> consists of one cell.
  </p>

  <p>
  That single cell performs all the functions necessary for survival,
  including obtaining nutrients, respiration, removing wastes and
  reproduction.
  </p>

  <p>
  Examples include many bacteria and some protists.
  </p>

  <h3>Multicellular organisms</h3>

  <p>
  A <b>multicellular organism</b> consists of many cells.
  </p>

  <p>
  Different cells can become specialised for different functions.
  These cells can form tissues, organs and organ systems.
  </p>

  <p>
  Humans and flowering plants are examples of multicellular organisms.
  </p>

  <h3>Key difference</h3>

  <p>
  In a unicellular organism, one cell carries out all essential life
  processes. In a multicellular organism, specialised cells share different
  functions.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Unicellular and Multicellular Organisms', 15),
  (702, 702, '
  <h2>How Cells Are Organised in Multicellular Organisms</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    A multicellular organism contains many cells, but those cells are not
    arranged randomly. They are organised into levels.
  </p>

  <p>
    The basic sequence is:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Cells → Tissues → Organs → Organ Systems → Organism</b>
  </p>

  <h3>1. Cells</h3>

  <p>
    Cells are the smallest living units.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Tissues</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>tissue</b> is a group of similar cells working together to perform
    a particular function.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Organs</h3>

  <p>
    An <b>organ</b> contains different tissues working together to perform
    a particular function.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Organ Systems</h3>

  <p>
    An <b>organ system</b> consists of organs working together to perform
    major functions in an organism.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Organism</h3>

  <p>
    The complete living individual contains many interacting organ systems.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    Consider the organisation of the human circulatory system.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Heart muscle cells</b>
    → form <b>muscle tissue</b>
    → tissues form the <b>heart</b>
    → the heart works with blood vessels as part of the
    <b>circulatory system</b>
    → which functions within the <b>human organism</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Organisation Matters</h3>

  <p>
    Specialisation allows different parts of a multicellular organism to
    perform different tasks efficiently.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Cells are organised into increasingly complex levels that work together.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for How Cells Are Organised in Multicellular Organisms', 15),
  (703, 703, '
  <h2>How Organelles Work Together</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    A cell does not survive because one organelle performs everything.
    Different structures perform different functions and depend on one another.
  </p>

  <h3>Consider a Cell Making a Protein</h3>

  <p>
    A simplified sequence is:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>DNA in the nucleus</b>
    → provides genetic information
    → <b>ribosome</b> uses the information
    → amino acids are assembled
    → <b>protein</b> is produced
  </p>

  <p>
    The cell also requires energy for many processes.
    <b>Mitochondria</b> carry out important stages of aerobic respiration that
    release usable energy for cellular activities.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <b>cell membrane</b> controls movement of substances needed by the cell
    and removal of substances from it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <b>cytoplasm</b> provides the internal environment where many chemical
    reactions occur.
  </p>

  <h3>Think of the Cell as a Team</h3>

  <p>
    It is better to think of organelles as members of a team than as isolated
    parts.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Structure</th>
      <th>Important role</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Cell membrane</td>
      <td>Controls movement into and out of the cell</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Cytoplasm</td>
      <td>Site of many chemical reactions</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Nucleus</td>
      <td>Contains genetic material</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Mitochondria</td>
      <td>Site of important stages of aerobic respiration</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Ribosomes</td>
      <td>Sites of protein synthesis</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> A cell cannot make enough proteins. Which two structures
    would you investigate first, and why?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Ribosomes are directly involved in protein synthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> The nucleus contains genetic information needed to specify
    proteins.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The ribosomes and nucleus would be important structures
    to investigate.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Cell structures have different jobs, but they work together as one
    coordinated system.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for How Organelles Work Together', 15),
  (704, 704, '
  <h2>What Is Cell Specialisation?</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Cell specialisation</b> is the process by which cells develop
    structures and characteristics that make them suited to particular
    functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a multicellular organism, cells do not all need to perform the same job.
    Different cells become adapted for different roles.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Is Specialisation Necessary?</h3>

  <p>
    A large multicellular organism has many different requirements.
    It needs cells for transport, movement, communication, absorption,
    protection, reproduction and many other functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Specialised cells allow the organism to divide these jobs among different
    groups of cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>red blood cell</b> is specialised for transporting oxygen.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <b>root hair cell</b> is specialised for absorbing water and mineral ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <b>nerve cell</b> is specialised for transmitting electrical signals
    through the nervous system.
  </p>

  <h3>Specialisation Means Adaptation for a Job</h3>

  <p>
    The important question is not simply:
    <b>"What does this cell look like?"</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Ask:
    <b>"How does its structure help it perform its function?"</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why is cell specialisation useful in a multicellular
    organism?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> A multicellular organism needs many different functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Different cells can develop different structures.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> These structures make the cells effective at particular jobs.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Specialisation allows division of labour between cells.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Specialised cells have structures adapted to perform particular functions.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Cell Specialisation?', 15),
  (705, 705, '
  <h2>Why Cells Specialise</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Multicellular organisms contain many cells that must work together.
    If every cell performed exactly the same function, the organism could
    not efficiently carry out all the tasks needed for survival.
  </p>

  <p>
    Specialisation creates a <b>division of labour</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Division of Labour</h3>

  <p>
    Division of labour means that different cells become responsible for
    different jobs.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>red blood cells transport oxygen,</li>
    <li>root hair cells absorb water and mineral ions,</li>
    <li>nerve cells transmit signals,</li>
    <li>muscle cells contract to produce movement.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Structure Must Match Function</h3>

  <p>
    A cell becomes effective at its job when its structures are suited to
    that job.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a cell transporting oxygen needs a structure that allows
    efficient gas transport.
  </p>

  <p>
    A cell absorbing substances from the soil needs structures that provide
    effective contact with its surroundings.
  </p>

  <h3>From Cells to Organisms</h3>

  <p>
    Specialised cells work together in tissues.
    Tissues form organs, and organs work together in organ systems.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, specialisation is one of the foundations of organisation in
    multicellular organisms.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why would a multicellular organism benefit from having
    nerve cells instead of making every cell responsible for transmitting
    signals?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Communication is a specialised function.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Nerve cells have structures adapted for transmitting
    signals.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Other cells can then perform different specialised jobs.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Specialisation allows the organism to perform many
    functions efficiently through division of labour.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Cell specialisation allows division of labour, making complex
    multicellular life possible.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Cells Specialise', 15),
  (706, 706, '
  <h2>Root Hair Cell</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>root hair cell</b> is a specialised plant cell found on the surface
    of young roots.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its main functions are to absorb <b>water</b> and <b>mineral ions</b>
    from the soil.
  </p>

  <h3>How Is It Adapted?</h3>

  <h4>1. Long Hair-like Extension</h4>

  <p>
    The cell has a long extension that increases its surface area.
  </p>

  <p>
    A larger surface area gives the root more contact with the soil and
    provides more area through which substances can be absorbed.
  </p>

  <h4>2. Thin Cell Wall</h4>

  <p>
    The root hair has a relatively thin wall, giving water a short distance
    to travel before reaching the cell interior.
  </p>

  <h4>3. Large Vacuole</h4>

  <p>
    The vacuole helps maintain a low water potential inside the cell,
    supporting water uptake by osmosis.
  </p>

  <h4>4. Active Transport of Mineral Ions</h4>

  <p>
    Mineral ions may be absorbed by active transport when their concentration
    in the soil is lower than their concentration inside the root cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    Active transport requires energy.
  </p>

  <h3>Structure → Function</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Long extension</b>
    → increased surface area
    → more contact with soil
    → efficient absorption.
  </p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Water and mineral ions do not necessarily enter the root hair cell by
    the same mechanism.
  </p>

  <p>
    Water enters mainly by <b>osmosis</b>, while mineral ions can enter by
    <b>active transport</b> when movement against a concentration gradient
    is required.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why does a root hair cell have a long extension?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> The extension increases the cell''s surface area.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> A larger surface area provides more contact with soil.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> More contact provides greater opportunity for absorption
    of water and mineral ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The long extension makes the cell effective at
    absorbing substances from the soil.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>A root hair cell is adapted for absorption by having a long extension
    that provides a large surface area for contact with the soil.</b>
  </p>
  
<hr>

<h2>Root Hair Cell</h2>

<h3>What is a root hair cell?</h3>

<p>
A <b>root hair cell</b> is a specialised plant cell found on the surface
of young roots.
</p>

<p>
Its main functions are to absorb <b>water and mineral ions</b> from
the soil.
</p>

<h3>How is it specialised?</h3>

<p>
The cell has a long, narrow extension called a <b>root hair</b>.
This greatly increases the surface area in contact with the soil.
</p>

<h3>Why is a large surface area useful?</h3>

<p>
Water and mineral ions need to enter the root efficiently.
A larger surface area provides more membrane through which substances
can be absorbed.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why does a root hair cell have a long extension?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The cell absorbs substances from the soil.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> A long extension increases the surface area of the cell.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> A larger surface area allows more contact with soil
particles and water.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The extension increases surface area, improving the
cell''s ability to absorb water and mineral ions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Root Hair Cell', 15),
  (707, 707, '
<h2>Guard Cells</h2>

<h3>What are guard cells?</h3>

<p>
<b>Guard cells</b> are specialised plant cells that surround a
<b>stoma</b> on the surface of a leaf.
</p>

<p>
Their main function is to control whether the stoma is <b>open or closed</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why is this important?</h3>

<p>
The stomata allow gases such as carbon dioxide and oxygen to move into
and out of the leaf.
</p>

<p>
They can also allow water vapour to escape from the leaf.
</p>

<h3>How do guard cells control the stoma?</h3>

<p>
When the guard cells become more swollen with water, they change shape
and the stoma opens.
</p>

<p>
When the guard cells lose water, they become less swollen and the stoma
closes.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> What happens to a stoma when its guard cells become
turgid?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Turgid means the guard cells have taken in water and
become swollen.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The swollen guard cells change their shape.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> This causes the opening between them to become wider.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The stoma opens.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Guard Cells', 15),
  (708, 708, '
<h2>Ciliated Cell</h2>

<h3>What is a ciliated cell?</h3>

<p>
A <b>ciliated cell</b> is a specialised animal cell found lining parts
of the respiratory tract.
</p>

<h3>What are cilia?</h3>

<p>
Cilia are tiny hair-like structures on the surface of the cell.
They can beat in coordinated movements.
</p>

<h3>What do ciliated cells do?</h3>

<p>
The respiratory tract can trap dust and microorganisms in mucus.
Cilia move this mucus towards the throat, where it can be removed.
</p>

<h3>How is the cell adapted?</h3>

<p>
The cell has many cilia on its surface. Their coordinated movement
allows mucus to be moved along the respiratory passage.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why are cilia important in the respiratory tract?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Mucus traps particles and microorganisms.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The mucus needs to be moved away from deeper parts
of the respiratory system.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Cilia beat and move the mucus towards the throat.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Cilia move mucus containing trapped particles and
microorganisms towards the throat.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ciliated Cell', 15),
  (709, 709, '
<h2>Goblet Cell</h2>

<h3>What is a goblet cell?</h3>

<p>
A <b>goblet cell</b> is a specialised cell found in the lining of
parts of the respiratory and digestive systems.
</p>

<h3>What does it produce?</h3>

<p>
Goblet cells produce and release <b>mucus</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why is mucus important?</h3>

<p>
In the respiratory tract, mucus helps trap dust particles and
microorganisms before they can travel deeper into the lungs.
</p>

<h3>How is the cell adapted?</h3>

<p>
The cell is specialised for producing and releasing large amounts
of mucus. Its shape provides space for mucus to accumulate before
it is released.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A person''s respiratory tract contains goblet cells.
What is the advantage of these cells?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Goblet cells produce mucus.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Mucus traps dust and microorganisms.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Trapping these particles helps protect deeper parts
of the respiratory system.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Goblet cells produce mucus that traps particles and
microorganisms, helping protect the respiratory tract.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Goblet Cell', 15),
  (710, 710, '
<h2>Sperm Cell</h2>

<h3>What is a sperm cell?</h3>

<p>
A <b>sperm cell</b> is a male reproductive cell. Its role is to
reach and fertilise the female reproductive cell.
</p>

<h3>How is it specialised?</h3>

<p>
A sperm cell has a <b>flagellum</b>, commonly called a tail, which
helps it move.
</p>

<p>
It contains many mitochondria near the base of the flagellum.
These release energy needed for movement.
</p>

<p>
The head contains the nucleus, which carries the genetic material.
The head also contains an <b>acrosome</b>, which contains enzymes
involved in fertilisation.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why does a sperm cell contain many mitochondria?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The sperm must move towards the egg.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Movement requires energy.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Mitochondria release usable energy during respiration.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The mitochondria provide energy needed for movement
of the sperm cell.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sperm Cell', 15),
  (711, 711, '
<h2>Egg Cell</h2>

<h3>What is an egg cell?</h3>

<p>
An <b>egg cell</b> is a female reproductive cell. It contains genetic
material and can fuse with a sperm cell during fertilisation.
</p>

<h3>How is it specialised?</h3>

<p>
The egg cell is relatively large and contains a substantial amount
of cytoplasm.
</p>

<p>
Its cytoplasm contains substances that support the early stages after
fertilisation before the embryo can obtain nutrients from its developing
environment.
</p>

<h3>Why is the nucleus important?</h3>

<p>
The nucleus contains the egg cell''s genetic material.
During fertilisation, genetic material from the sperm and egg combine.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is the egg cell larger than a sperm cell?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The egg needs to contain enough cytoplasm and stored
resources to support the early stages after fertilisation.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> A sperm cell is specialised mainly for movement and
delivery of its genetic material.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The egg cell is larger because it contains substantial
cytoplasm and resources needed during the early stages following
fertilisation.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Egg Cell', 15),
  (712, 712, '
<h2>Nerve Cell</h2>

<h3>What is a nerve cell?</h3>

<p>
A <b>nerve cell</b>, or neurone, is a specialised cell that carries
electrical impulses through the nervous system.
</p>

<h3>How is it adapted?</h3>

<p>
Many neurones have a long fibre called an <b>axon</b>. The axon allows
an electrical impulse to travel from one region of the cell towards
another cell or an effector.
</p>

<p>
Neurones also have branched structures that allow them to form
connections with other cells.
</p>

<h3>Why is a long axon useful?</h3>

<p>
A long axon allows impulses to travel over relatively long distances
without requiring a separate cell for every short section of the pathway.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is a long axon useful in a nerve cell?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The nervous system must transmit information between
different parts of the body.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Some of these parts are far apart.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> A long axon provides a pathway for the impulse over
a long distance.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> A long axon allows nerve impulses to travel between
distant parts of the nervous system.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nerve Cell', 15),
  (713, 713, '
<h2>Muscle Cell</h2>

<h3>What is a muscle cell?</h3>

<p>
A <b>muscle cell</b> is a specialised animal cell that can contract.
Muscle contraction produces force and movement.
</p>

<h3>Why does a muscle cell need many mitochondria?</h3>

<p>
Muscle contraction requires energy.
Mitochondria release usable energy through respiration.
</p>

<p>
Muscle cells that carry out frequent or powerful contraction therefore
have a high demand for energy.
</p>

<h3>How is the cell specialised?</h3>

<p>
Muscle cells contain specialised contractile structures that allow
them to shorten and generate force.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why would a muscle cell need many mitochondria?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Muscle contraction requires energy.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Mitochondria are sites where energy is released during
aerobic respiration.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> A high energy demand requires a large supply of energy.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Muscle cells need many mitochondria to provide the
energy required for repeated contraction.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Muscle Cell', 15),
  (714, 714, '
<h2>Root Hair Cell Adaptation</h2>

<h3>Why is the root hair cell specialised?</h3>

<p>
A root hair cell is specialised for absorbing water and mineral ions
from the soil.
</p>

<h3>Surface area</h3>

<p>
The long root hair provides a large surface area for contact with
water films surrounding soil particles.
</p>

<h3>Thin cell boundary</h3>

<p>
The cell has a thin surface through which water can move into the cell.
This reduces the distance over which substances need to move.
</p>

<h3>Mineral ion absorption</h3>

<p>
Mineral ions may be present in the soil at lower concentrations than
inside root cells. Plants can therefore use <b>active transport</b>
to absorb some mineral ions against a concentration gradient.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why can a plant still absorb some mineral ions when
their concentration in the soil is lower than inside the root?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Movement from low concentration to high concentration
cannot occur by diffusion alone.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Root cells can use active transport.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Active transport requires energy.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Root cells can use active transport to move mineral ions
against their concentration gradient using energy.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Root Hair Cell Adaptation', 15),
  (715, 715, '
<h2>Ciliated Cell Adaptation</h2>

<h3>How is a ciliated cell specialised?</h3>

<p>
Ciliated cells line parts of the respiratory tract.
Their surface contains many cilia that beat in coordinated movements.
</p>

<h3>What happens when cilia beat?</h3>

<p>
The cilia push mucus along the surface of the respiratory tract.
The mucus can contain trapped dust and microorganisms.
</p>

<h3>Why must the movement be coordinated?</h3>

<p>
If cilia beat in a coordinated direction, mucus can be moved along
the airway instead of simply being moved back and forth.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A smoker has damaged cilia in the respiratory tract.
Explain why this can increase the risk of infection.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Cilia normally move mucus along the airway.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Mucus traps dust and microorganisms.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Damaged cilia are less effective at moving the mucus.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Damaged cilia can reduce mucus clearance, allowing
trapped microorganisms to remain in the respiratory tract for longer,
which can increase infection risk.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ciliated Cell Adaptation', 15),
  (716, 716, '
<h2>Sperm Cell Adaptation</h2>

<h3>Why is the sperm cell specialised?</h3>

<p>
The sperm cell must travel towards the female reproductive cell and
deliver its genetic material.
</p>

<h3>Adaptation 1: Flagellum</h3>

<p>
The flagellum provides movement, allowing the sperm cell to swim.
</p>

<h3>Adaptation 2: Mitochondria</h3>

<p>
Mitochondria release energy required for movement of the flagellum.
</p>

<h3>Adaptation 3: Acrosome</h3>

<p>
The acrosome contains enzymes involved in the process of fertilisation.
</p>

<h3>Adaptation 4: Haploid nucleus</h3>

<p>
The nucleus contains one set of chromosomes. When the sperm fuses with
the egg, the genetic material from the two cells combines.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why would a sperm cell with a damaged flagellum have
difficulty reaching the egg?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The flagellum provides the main means of movement.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> A damaged flagellum reduces the sperm''s ability to swim.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Reduced movement makes it harder for the sperm to reach
the egg.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The sperm would have reduced ability to move towards
the egg because the flagellum is needed for swimming.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sperm Cell Adaptation', 15),
  (717, 717, '
<h2>Egg Cell Adaptation</h2>

<h3>How is an egg cell specialised?</h3>

<p>
An egg cell is specialised for fertilisation and for supporting the
very early stages after fertilisation.
</p>

<h3>Large cytoplasm</h3>

<p>
The egg cell contains a relatively large amount of cytoplasm.
The cytoplasm contains substances that support early development.
</p>

<h3>Genetic material</h3>

<p>
The nucleus contains one set of chromosomes. During fertilisation,
the genetic material from the sperm and egg combines.
</p>

<h3>Changes after fertilisation</h3>

<p>
After fertilisation, changes occur around the egg that help prevent
additional sperm cells from fertilising the same egg.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is the cytoplasm of the egg cell important?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Fertilisation produces a cell that begins development.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Early development requires materials and energy.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The egg cytoplasm contains resources that support
the early stages.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The egg cytoplasm contains resources needed to support
the early stages following fertilisation.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Egg Cell Adaptation', 15),
  (718, 718, '
<h2>Muscle Cell Adaptation</h2>

<h3>What makes a muscle cell specialised?</h3>

<p>
Muscle cells are specialised to contract and generate force.
This allows muscles to produce movement.
</p>

<h3>Contractile structures</h3>

<p>
Muscle cells contain specialised structures that can slide relative
to one another, causing the cell to shorten.
</p>

<h3>Energy supply</h3>

<p>
Contraction requires energy. Muscle cells therefore contain many
mitochondria to support their high energy demand.
</p>

<h3>Why does muscle need a continuous energy supply?</h3>

<p>
Muscles may contract repeatedly or remain contracted for some time.
Continuous contraction requires a continuous supply of usable energy.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A muscle is repeatedly contracting during exercise.
Why does its muscle tissue require a high rate of respiration?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Muscle contraction requires energy.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Repeated contraction increases the energy demand.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Respiration releases usable energy from respiratory
substrates.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The muscle requires a high rate of respiration to
provide enough usable energy for repeated contraction.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Muscle Cell Adaptation', 15),
  (719, 719, '
<h2>Cell Differentiation</h2>

<h3>What is cell differentiation?</h3>

<p>
<b>Cell differentiation</b> is the process by which an unspecialised
cell develops features that allow it to perform a particular function.
</p>

<h3>Why does differentiation happen?</h3>

<p>
Cells in a multicellular organism may contain the same genetic
information, but different genes are switched on or expressed in
different cells.
</p>

<p>
This causes cells to develop different structures and functions.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A cell that develops into a muscle cell acquires structures that allow
it to contract, while a cell developing into a nerve cell becomes
adapted to transmit impulses.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why can two cells in the same organism have different
structures even though they contain the same genetic information?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Cells contain genetic information in their DNA.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Different cells express different sets of genes.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> This causes different proteins and structures to be
produced.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Different genes are expressed in different cells,
causing the cells to develop different structures and functions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cell Differentiation', 15),
  (720, 720, '
<h2>Tissue</h2>

<h3>What is a tissue?</h3>

<p>
A <b>tissue</b> is a group of similar cells working together to carry
out a particular function.
</p>

<h3>Why do tissues exist?</h3>

<p>
Multicellular organisms contain many specialised cells.
Cells with related structures and functions can work together as a tissue.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<p>
In animals, <b>muscle tissue</b> contains muscle cells specialised for
contraction.
</p>

<p>
In plants, <b>xylem tissue</b> contains specialised structures adapted
for transporting water and mineral salts.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A group of specialised muscle cells contracts
together. What level of organisation do these cells form?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The cells are specialised for contraction.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> They work together to perform the same general function.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> They form muscle tissue.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tissue', 15),
  (721, 721, '
<h2>Organ</h2>

<h3>What is an organ?</h3>

<p>
An <b>organ</b> is a structure made from different tissues working
together to perform one or more specific functions.
</p>

<h3>Why does an organ contain different tissues?</h3>

<p>
Different tissues perform different tasks. Working together allows
the organ to carry out a more complex function.
</p>

<h3>Example: the heart</h3>

<p>
The heart contains several types of tissue. Muscle tissue contracts,
while other tissues provide support and allow the heart to connect
with blood vessels and nervous control systems.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is the heart classified as an organ rather
than a tissue?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> A tissue is mainly a group of similar cells performing
a related function.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The heart contains different tissues.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> These tissues work together to pump blood.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The heart is an organ because it contains different
tissues working together to perform a specific function.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Organ', 15),
  (722, 722, '
<h2>Organ System</h2>

<h3>What is an organ system?</h3>

<p>
An <b>organ system</b> is a group of organs that work together to
perform a major function in an organism.
</p>

<h3>Example: digestive system</h3>

<p>
The digestive system contains several organs that work together to
process food and absorb nutrients.
</p>

<p>
The organs have different jobs, but their functions are coordinated
to achieve the overall function of the system.
</p>

<h3>Why are organ systems useful?</h3>

<p>
Complex organisms need many different processes to happen at the same
time. Organ systems allow specialised organs to cooperate.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is the digestive system called an organ system?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> It contains multiple organs.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> These organs perform different parts of digestion
and nutrient processing.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Their activities work together to achieve a common
overall function.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> It is an organ system because several organs work
together to process food and absorb nutrients.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Organ System', 15),
  (723, 723, '
<h2>Levels of Organisation</h2>

<h3>How is a multicellular organism organised?</h3>

<p>
Multicellular organisms are organised into levels. Specialised cells
work together to form tissues, tissues form organs, and organs can
work together as organ systems.
</p>

<p>
The sequence is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Cell → Tissue → Organ → Organ System → Organism</b>
</p>

<h3>Why does this organisation matter?</h3>

<p>
Each level builds on the one before it. This allows a large organism
to divide complex tasks among specialised structures.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Arrange these structures from simplest to most
complex: heart, muscle cell, circulatory system, muscle tissue.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> A muscle cell is one specialised cell.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Similar specialised cells form muscle tissue.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Different tissues form the heart.</p>

<p><b>Step 4:</b> The heart works with other organs in the circulatory
system.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
Muscle cell → Muscle tissue → Heart → Circulatory system
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Levels of Organisation', 15),
  (724, 724, '
<h2>Plant Tissue</h2>

<h3>What is plant tissue?</h3>

<p>
Plant tissue is a group of plant cells that work together to perform
a particular function.
</p>

<h3>Why do plants need different tissues?</h3>

<p>
Plants perform many different functions, including transporting water,
transporting sugars, carrying out photosynthesis and protecting
internal tissues.
</p>

<p>
Different tissues are specialised for these different functions.
</p>

<h3>Example: xylem tissue</h3>

<p>
Xylem tissue is specialised for transporting water and mineral salts
through the plant.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is xylem considered a tissue rather than a
single cell?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Xylem is made from many specialised cells and vessel
elements.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> These structures work together in water transport.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Xylem is a tissue because multiple specialised
structures work together to perform transport.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Plant Tissue', 15),
  (725, 725, '
<h2>Animal Tissue</h2>

<h3>What is animal tissue?</h3>

<p>
Animal tissue is a group of specialised animal cells working together
to perform a particular function.
</p>

<h3>Example: muscle tissue</h3>

<p>
Muscle tissue contains cells specialised for contraction.
The contraction of many muscle cells can produce force and movement.
</p>

<h3>Why are specialised tissues important?</h3>

<p>
A multicellular animal cannot rely on one type of cell to perform
every function. Different tissues allow different tasks to be carried
out efficiently.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is muscle tissue useful to an animal?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Muscle cells can contract.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Many muscle cells working together can generate force.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The force can produce movement.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Muscle tissue allows the animal to generate force
and produce movement.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Animal Tissue', 15),
  (726, 726, '
<h2>Tissue Specialisation</h2>

<h3>What does tissue specialisation mean?</h3>

<p>
Tissue specialisation means that a tissue has a particular structure
and organisation that allows it to perform a particular function
efficiently.
</p>

<h3>Example: xylem</h3>

<p>
Xylem contains specialised conducting structures that form continuous
pathways for water transport.
</p>

<h3>Example: muscle tissue</h3>

<p>
Muscle tissue contains cells specialised for contraction, allowing
the tissue to generate force.
</p>

<h3>Why is specialisation important?</h3>

<p>
Specialisation allows different parts of a multicellular organism
to perform different tasks efficiently.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why would a plant struggle if all of its cells were
unspecialised?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The plant needs to transport water and minerals.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> It also needs to transport sugars and carry out
photosynthesis.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Different specialised tissues perform these functions.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Without specialised tissues, the plant would be much
less efficient at carrying out the different functions needed for survival.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tissue Specialisation', 15),
  (727, 727, '
<h2>Organ Specialisation</h2>

<h3>What does organ specialisation mean?</h3>

<p>
An organ is specialised to perform a particular major function.
Different tissues within the organ contribute to that function.
</p>

<h3>Example: the leaf</h3>

<p>
A leaf is a plant organ specialised mainly for photosynthesis.
It contains several tissues that contribute to this function.
</p>

<p>
For example, palisade tissue contains many chloroplasts, while
vascular tissue transports substances through the leaf.
</p>

<h3>Why do organs contain different tissues?</h3>

<p>
A complex function usually requires several different tasks.
Different tissues can perform these tasks and work together.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is a leaf classified as an organ?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> A leaf contains different types of tissue.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> These tissues perform different roles.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Together they allow the leaf to carry out its main
functions, including photosynthesis and transport.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> A leaf is an organ because different tissues work
together within it to perform its functions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Organ Specialisation', 15),
  (728, 728, '
<h2>Plant Organ Systems</h2>

<h3>Do plants have organ systems?</h3>

<p>
Yes. Plants contain groups of organs that work together to perform
major functions.
</p>

<h3>Shoot system</h3>

<p>
The <b>shoot system</b> includes structures such as stems, leaves,
flowers and fruits.
</p>

<h3>Root system</h3>

<p>
The <b>root system</b> consists mainly of the roots. It anchors the
plant and absorbs water and mineral ions from the soil.
</p>

<h3>Why are these systems important?</h3>

<p>
Different plant organs depend on one another. For example, roots
absorb water and minerals while leaves use water during photosynthesis.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A plant has healthy leaves but severely damaged
roots. Why can this eventually affect the leaves?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Roots absorb water and mineral ions.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Leaves require water and mineral nutrients for normal
function.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Damaged roots may reduce the supply reaching the shoots.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Damaged roots can reduce water and mineral absorption,
which can eventually interfere with normal leaf function.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Plant Organ Systems', 15),
  (729, 729, '
<h2>Sperm Cell Structure</h2>

<h3>What is a sperm cell?</h3>

<p>
A sperm cell is a male gamete specialised for reaching and fertilising an egg.
Its structure is closely related to this function.
</p>

<h3>Main structures</h3>

<p><b>Head:</b> Contains the haploid nucleus.</p>

<p><b>Acrosome:</b> A cap at the front of the head containing enzymes involved
in interaction with the egg.</p>

<p><b>Middle piece:</b> Contains many mitochondria that provide energy for movement.</p>

<p><b>Flagellum:</b> Produces movement that helps the sperm swim.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why does a sperm cell have many mitochondria in its middle piece?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The sperm must move.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Movement requires energy.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Mitochondria release usable energy through respiration.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The mitochondria provide energy needed for movement.</p>

<h3>Structure → function</h3>

<p>
Flagellum → movement.</p>
<p>
Mitochondria → energy supply.</p>
<p>
Haploid nucleus → carries one set of chromosomes.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sperm Cell Structure', 15),
  (730, 730, '
<h2>Sperm Cell Adaptations</h2>

<h3>Why is the sperm cell specialised?</h3>

<p>
A sperm cell must reach an egg and deliver its genetic material. Several
features help it perform this function.
</p>

<p><b>Long flagellum:</b> helps the sperm move.</p>

<p><b>Many mitochondria:</b> provide energy for movement.</p>

<p><b>Streamlined shape:</b> reduces resistance as the sperm moves.</p>

<p><b>Acrosome:</b> contains enzymes involved in interaction with the egg.</p>

<p><b>Haploid nucleus:</b> contains one set of chromosomes.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A sperm cell has a damaged flagellum. How would this affect
its function?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The flagellum is responsible for movement.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> A damaged flagellum reduces the sperm''s ability to move.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Reaching the egg would therefore become more difficult.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The sperm would have reduced ability to swim towards the egg.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Sperm structure is specialised mainly for movement and successful delivery of
the male genetic material.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sperm Cell Adaptations', 15),
  (731, 731, '
<h2>Egg Cell Structure</h2>

<h3>What is an egg cell?</h3>

<p>
An egg cell is the female gamete. It is specialised to support the early stages
following fertilisation.
</p>

<h3>Main features</h3>

<p><b>Large cytoplasm:</b> provides space for stored materials that support the
cell after fertilisation.</p>

<p><b>Haploid nucleus:</b> contains one set of chromosomes.</p>

<p><b>Cell membrane:</b> surrounds and controls movement of substances into and
out of the cell.</p>

<h3>Why is the egg larger than the sperm?</h3>

<p>
The egg contains much more cytoplasm. The sperm is specialised primarily for
movement and delivery of its nucleus.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why does an egg cell contain a large amount of cytoplasm?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> After fertilisation, the early developing embryo needs materials.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The egg provides cytoplasm containing stored resources and
cellular machinery.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Its large cytoplasm provides resources and cellular machinery
for the early stages after fertilisation.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Egg Cell Structure', 15),
  (732, 732, '
<h2>Egg Cell Adaptations</h2>

<h3>How is the egg specialised?</h3>

<p>
The egg has features that support fertilisation and the early development of
the new organism.
</p>

<p><b>Large cytoplasm:</b> provides stored materials and cellular machinery.</p>

<p><b>Haploid nucleus:</b> provides one set of chromosomes.</p>

<p><b>Cell surface:</b> participates in interaction with sperm during fertilisation.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is having a haploid nucleus important in the egg?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The egg contributes one chromosome set.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The sperm contributes another chromosome set.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Their combination restores the diploid number.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> A haploid egg allows fertilisation to restore the normal
diploid chromosome number.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
The egg is specialised not for movement, but for receiving sperm and supporting
the early stages after fertilisation.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Egg Cell Adaptations', 15),
  (733, 733, '
<h2>Sperm and Egg Comparison</h2>

<h3>Both are gametes</h3>

<p>
Sperm and egg cells are both haploid reproductive cells. Each contains one set
of chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>But their structures differ</h3>

<p><b>Sperm:</b> small, streamlined and adapted for movement.</p>

<p><b>Egg:</b> large and contains substantial cytoplasm.</p>

<p>
These differences reflect their different roles in fertilisation.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why would it be incorrect to say that sperm and egg cells
have the same structure?</p>

<p>
They perform different roles. Sperm must travel to the egg and deliver its
genetic material, while the egg receives the sperm and provides cytoplasm and
resources for early development.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Their structures are adapted to different functions.</p>

<h3>Quick comparison</h3>

<p>
<b>Sperm → movement + delivery of male genetic material.</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Egg → reception + resources for early development.</b>
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sperm and Egg Comparison', 15),
  (734, 734, '
  <h2>Animal Cell Specialisation</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Multicellular animals contain many cells that become specialised for
  particular functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: red blood cell</h3>

  <p>
  A red blood cell is specialised for transporting oxygen.
  Its shape and haemoglobin content help it perform this function.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: nerve cell</h3>

  <p>
  A nerve cell is specialised for transmitting electrical impulses.
  Its long extension allows it to communicate over relatively large
  distances in the body.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: muscle cell</h3>

  <p>
  Muscle cells are specialised to contract and generate movement.
  </p>

  <h3>Why specialisation matters</h3>

  <p>
  Instead of every cell performing every function, specialised cells divide
  the work among different tissues and organs.
  </p>

  <p>
  This allows multicellular organisms to develop complex organ systems.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Animal Cell Specialisation', 15),
  (735, 735, '
<h2>Why Cells Divide</h2>

<h3>Why can''t a multicellular organism simply keep growing by making every cell larger?</h3>

<p>
Cells need substances such as oxygen, water and nutrients to enter and
waste products to leave. These substances move across the cell surface.
</p>

<p>
As a cell becomes larger, its volume increases faster than its surface
area. This can make exchange between the cell and its surroundings
less efficient.
</p>

<h3>How does cell division help?</h3>

<p>
Instead of one very large cell, organisms can produce more cells.
The new cells can remain relatively small and carry out exchange
more efficiently.
</p>

<h3>Cells also divide for other reasons</h3>

<p>
Cell division allows multicellular organisms to:
</p>

<ul>
<li>grow</li>
<li>replace old or damaged cells</li>
<li>repair tissues</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is producing more cells useful when an organism
grows?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Growth requires an increase in the amount of living
material.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Cell division increases the number of cells.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The new cells can specialise and become part of tissues.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Cell division increases cell number, allowing the
organism to grow and develop.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Cells Divide', 15),
  (736, 736, '
<h2>The Cell Cycle</h2>

<h3>What is the cell cycle?</h3>

<p>
The <b>cell cycle</b> is the sequence of events through which a cell
grows, copies its genetic material and divides to produce new cells.
</p>

<h3>Main events</h3>

<p>
Before division, the cell:
</p>

<ol>
<li>grows and carries out its normal functions;</li>
<li>copies its DNA;</li>
<li>divides its genetic material;</li>
<li>divides its cytoplasm to form new cells.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Why must DNA be copied first?</h3>

<p>
Each new cell needs a complete set of genetic information.
DNA replication before division makes this possible.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why must DNA be copied before a cell divides?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> DNA contains the genetic instructions needed by the cell.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Cell division produces new cells.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Each new cell needs genetic information.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> DNA is copied so that each new cell can receive a
complete set of genetic information.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Cell Cycle', 15),
  (737, 737, '
<h2>Chromosomes</h2>

<h3>What is a chromosome?</h3>

<p>
A <b>chromosome</b> is a long, organised structure made mainly from
DNA and associated proteins. It carries genetic information.
</p>

<h3>Where are chromosomes found?</h3>

<p>
In eukaryotic cells, chromosomes are found in the nucleus.
</p>

<h3>Why are chromosomes important during cell division?</h3>

<p>
Before a cell divides, its DNA is copied. The copied genetic material
must then be distributed accurately so that the resulting cells receive
the appropriate genetic information.
</p>

<h3>Chromosome number</h3>

<p>
Different species have characteristic chromosome numbers.
For example, human body cells normally contain <b>46 chromosomes</b>,
arranged as 23 pairs.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Where would you expect to find chromosomes in a
human body cell?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Human body cells are eukaryotic cells.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Their chromosomes are contained in the nucleus.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The chromosomes are found in the nucleus.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chromosomes', 15),
  (738, 738, '
<h2>DNA</h2>

<h3>What is DNA?</h3>

<p>
<b>DNA</b>, or deoxyribonucleic acid, is the molecule that carries
genetic information in cells.
</p>

<h3>Why is DNA important?</h3>

<p>
DNA contains instructions that influence how cells develop, function
and produce proteins.
</p>

<h3>Where is DNA found?</h3>

<p>
In eukaryotic cells, most DNA is found in the nucleus, organised into
chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>DNA and cell division</h3>

<p>
Before a cell divides, its DNA must be copied. This allows genetic
information to be passed to the new cells.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is DNA important when a cell divides?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> DNA carries genetic information.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> New cells need genetic information to function.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Copying DNA before division allows the information to
be passed to the new cells.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> DNA must be copied so that the new cells receive the
genetic information required for their functions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for DNA', 15),
  (739, 739, '
<h2>DNA Replication</h2>

<h3>What is DNA replication?</h3>

<p>
<b>DNA replication</b> is the process by which a DNA molecule is copied
before cell division.
</p>

<h3>Why must DNA replicate?</h3>

<p>
When a cell divides, each resulting cell needs genetic information.
Replication creates a copy of the DNA before the genetic material is
distributed.
</p>

<h3>Basic idea</h3>

<p>
The two strands of the DNA molecule separate. Each original strand
acts as a template for building a complementary strand.
</p>

<p>
This produces two DNA molecules containing the same genetic information
as the original DNA molecule, provided replication occurs accurately.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> What is the purpose of DNA replication before mitosis?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Mitosis produces new nuclei.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Each new nucleus requires a complete set of genetic
information.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> DNA replication produces copies of the genetic material
before it is separated.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> DNA replication ensures that the genetic material can
be distributed to the new nuclei during cell division.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for DNA Replication', 15),
  (740, 740, '
<h2>Mitosis</h2>

<h3>What is mitosis?</h3>

<p>
<b>Mitosis</b> is a type of nuclear division in which duplicated
chromosomes are separated so that each new nucleus receives an
equivalent set of chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>What does mitosis produce?</h3>

<p>
Mitosis normally produces two nuclei with the same chromosome number
as the original nucleus.
</p>

<p>
In a typical body cell, the daughter cells therefore retain the
diploid chromosome number.
</p>

<h3>Why is accurate chromosome separation important?</h3>

<p>
Each new cell needs the correct genetic information. Unequal distribution
of chromosomes can affect cell function.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A human body cell undergoes mitosis. How many
chromosomes should each new nucleus normally receive?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> A normal human body cell has 46 chromosomes.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Mitosis maintains the chromosome number.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The chromosomes are distributed between the two new nuclei.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Each new nucleus should normally receive 46 chromosomes.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitosis', 15),
  (741, 741, '
<h2>Prophase</h2>

<h3>What happens during prophase?</h3>

<p>
<b>Prophase</b> is the first major stage of mitosis.
During prophase, the genetic material condenses into visible
chromosomes.
</p>

<p>
The nuclear envelope begins to break down, and structures involved
in chromosome movement begin to form.
</p>

<h3>Why must chromosomes condense?</h3>

<p>
Condensation makes the duplicated genetic material more compact and
easier to move during later stages of cell division.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why does DNA condense into chromosomes during
prophase?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> DNA is normally spread out as chromatin in the nucleus.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> During division, the genetic material must be moved
and separated accurately.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Condensation makes the chromosomes compact and easier
to handle during movement.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Condensation makes the genetic material compact so
that it can be moved and separated more effectively.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prophase', 15),
  (742, 742, '
<h2>Metaphase</h2>

<h3>What happens during metaphase?</h3>

<p>
During <b>metaphase</b>, duplicated chromosomes line up near the
middle of the cell.
</p>

<p>
Spindle fibres attach to structures associated with the chromosomes
and prepare them for separation.
</p>

<h3>Why is positioning important?</h3>

<p>
The chromosomes must be positioned so that their copies can be
separated towards opposite ends of the cell.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> How can you identify metaphase in a diagram of
mitosis?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Look at the chromosomes.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Check whether they are arranged across the middle
of the cell.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> If the duplicated chromosomes are lined up at the
cell''s equatorial region, the cell is in metaphase.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Metaphase is identified by duplicated chromosomes
lined up near the middle of the cell.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Metaphase', 15),
  (743, 743, '
<h2>Anaphase</h2>

<h3>What happens during anaphase?</h3>

<p>
During <b>anaphase</b>, the duplicated chromosome copies separate and
move towards opposite ends of the cell.
</p>

<h3>Why is this important?</h3>

<p>
The separation ensures that the genetic material can be distributed
between the two future nuclei.
</p>

<h3>What happens to chromosome movement?</h3>

<p>
Spindle fibres shorten or change their arrangement, pulling the
separated chromosome copies towards opposite poles of the cell.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A cell has reached anaphase. What is happening to
the duplicated chromosome copies?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The chromosomes had previously lined up in the middle
of the cell.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Their duplicated copies now separate.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> The copies move towards opposite ends of the cell.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The duplicated chromosome copies separate and move
towards opposite poles of the cell.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Anaphase', 15),
  (744, 744, '
<h2>Telophase</h2>

<h3>What happens during telophase?</h3>

<p>
During <b>telophase</b>, the separated chromosomes reach opposite
ends of the cell.
</p>

<p>
New nuclear envelopes form around the chromosome sets, producing
two new nuclei.
</p>

<p>
The chromosomes also begin to become less condensed.
</p>

<h3>Why is telophase important?</h3>

<p>
Telophase re-establishes the nuclei that will contain the genetic
material of the new cells.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> How does telophase differ from anaphase?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> During anaphase, chromosome copies are being separated
and moved apart.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> During telophase, the separated chromosomes have reached
opposite ends.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> New nuclear envelopes form around them.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Anaphase separates the chromosome copies, while
telophase forms new nuclei around the separated chromosome sets.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Telophase', 15),
  (745, 745, '
<h2>Cytokinesis</h2>

<h3>What is cytokinesis?</h3>
<p>
Cytokinesis is the division of the cytoplasm of a cell after the nucleus has divided.
It separates the cell into two daughter cells.
</p>

<h3>How does it happen?</h3>
<p>
During mitosis, the nucleus divides first. Cytokinesis then divides the cytoplasm
and distributes cell contents between the two new cells.
</p>

<h3>In animal cells</h3>
<p>
The cell membrane forms a groove around the middle of the cell. The groove becomes
deeper until the cell separates into two cells.
</p>

<h3>In plant cells</h3>
<p>
A cell plate forms across the middle of the cell. The cell plate develops into a
new cell wall and separates the two daughter cells.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> What is the main difference between cytokinesis in an animal cell and a plant cell?</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Animal cells divide by forming a cleavage furrow, while plant cells form a cell plate because their rigid cell wall prevents the membrane from simply pinching inward.</p>

<h3>Check your understanding</h3>
<p><b>1.</b> What does cytokinesis divide?</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> The cytoplasm.</p>

<p><b>2.</b> What structure forms in the middle of a dividing plant cell?</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> A cell plate.</p>

<p><b>3.</b> What happens to an animal cell membrane during cytokinesis?</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> It forms a cleavage furrow that deepens until the cell separates.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cytokinesis', 15),
  (746, 746, '
<h2>Cytokinesis in Animal Cells</h2>

<h3>What happens?</h3>
<p>
After the chromosomes have been separated and the nuclei begin to reform, the
animal cell membrane starts to constrict around the middle.
</p>

<p>
This inward movement produces a <b>cleavage furrow</b>. The furrow becomes deeper
until the cytoplasm is separated into two parts.
</p>

<h3>Why can the membrane pinch inward?</h3>
<p>
An animal cell does not have a rigid cell wall. Its flexible cell membrane can
change shape and constrict around the middle.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A cell has completed telophase. What visible change would you
expect next as cytokinesis occurs?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The cell membrane begins to constrict.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> A cleavage furrow develops.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The furrow deepens.</p>
<p><b>Step 4:</b> Two separate daughter cells form.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>
<p>
The cleavage furrow is the physical process that helps separate the cytoplasm
of an animal cell.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cytokinesis in Animal Cells', 15),
  (747, 747, '
<h2>Cytokinesis in Plant Cells</h2>

<h3>Why is plant cytokinesis different?</h3>
<p>
Plant cells have a rigid cell wall. Therefore, the cell cannot simply pinch inward
in the same way as an animal cell.
</p>

<h3>What happens instead?</h3>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Vesicles collect at the centre of the dividing cell.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> These vesicles fuse to form a cell plate.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The cell plate grows outward towards the existing cell wall.</p>
<p><b>Step 4:</b> New cell-wall material develops between the daughter cells.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why does a plant cell form a cell plate rather than a cleavage furrow?</p>

<p>
A plant cell has a rigid cell wall. The wall prevents the cell membrane from
constricting inward enough to divide the cell in the same way as an animal cell.
A cell plate therefore develops between the two daughter cells.
</p>

<h3>Key distinction</h3>
<p>
<b>Animal:</b> cleavage furrow.</p>
<p>
<b>Plant:</b> cell plate.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cytokinesis in Plant Cells', 15),
  (748, 748, '
<h2>Mitosis as a Complete Sequence</h2>

<h3>What is the sequence?</h3>

<p>
Mitosis is easier to understand when the stages are followed in order.
</p>

<p><b>Prophase:</b> Chromosomes condense and become visible. Spindle fibres develop.</p>

<p><b>Metaphase:</b> Chromosomes line up at the equator of the cell.</p>

<p><b>Anaphase:</b> Sister chromatids separate and move towards opposite poles.</p>

<p><b>Telophase:</b> Chromosomes reach the poles and new nuclear envelopes form.</p>

<p><b>Cytokinesis:</b> The cytoplasm divides, producing two daughter cells.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A cell is observed with chromosomes arranged across the middle of
the cell. Which stage is it in?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Look at the chromosome position.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> They are arranged at the equator.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Chromosomes at the equator identify metaphase.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Metaphase.</p>

<h3>Order to remember</h3>
<p>
<b>P → M → A → T → C</b>
</p>
<p>
Prophase → Metaphase → Anaphase → Telophase → Cytokinesis
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitosis as a Complete Sequence', 15),
  (749, 749, '
<h2>Mitosis and Chromosome Number</h2>

<h3>What happens to chromosome number?</h3>

<p>
Mitosis normally produces daughter cells with the same chromosome number as the
parent cell.
</p>

<p>
This is important because growth and repair require cells to retain the normal
chromosome number.
</p>

<h3>Example in humans</h3>

<p>
Most human body cells contain <b>46 chromosomes</b>, arranged as 23 pairs.
</p>

<p>
When one of these cells divides by mitosis, each daughter cell normally receives
46 chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A human body cell containing 46 chromosomes divides by mitosis.
How many chromosomes should each daughter cell contain?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the type of division: mitosis.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Mitosis maintains chromosome number.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The parent cell has 46 chromosomes.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Each daughter cell should normally have 46 chromosomes.</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>
<p>
Mitosis is different from the cell division that produces gametes, where chromosome
number is reduced.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitosis and Chromosome Number', 15),
  (750, 750, '
<h2>Mitosis and Growth</h2>

<h3>How does mitosis contribute to growth?</h3>

<p>
An organism grows partly because the number of cells in its body increases.
Mitosis produces new cells that can contribute to this increase.
</p>

<p>
For example, when a young plant grows, cells in regions of active growth divide
by mitosis. The new cells can then increase in size and become specialised.
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
Growth is not simply one cell becoming infinitely large. In multicellular organisms,
growth involves changes such as an increase in cell number and, depending on the
tissue, an increase in cell size.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> A tissue contains 100 cells. If every cell divides once by
mitosis and all daughter cells survive, how many cells will there be?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Each parent cell produces two daughter cells.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> There are 100 parent cells.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> 100 × 2 = 200.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 200 cells.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>
<p>
Repeated mitotic divisions can increase the number of cells available for growth.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitosis and Growth', 15);
INSERT INTO public.lessons (id, topic_id, content, summary, estimated_minutes) VALUES
  (751, 751, '
<h2>Mitosis and Tissue Repair</h2>

<h3>Why is mitosis important in repair?</h3>

<p>
Cells in some tissues become damaged or are lost. New cells can replace them.
Mitosis provides a mechanism for producing genetically similar new body cells.
</p>

<h3>Example: a cut in the skin</h3>

<p>
When the skin is damaged, cells around the damaged area can divide and produce
new cells. These cells contribute to replacing cells that were lost.
</p>

<h3>The process</h3>

<p><b>1.</b> Cells near the damaged region remain or become able to divide.</p>
<p><b>2.</b> The cells undergo mitosis.</p>
<p><b>3.</b> New daughter cells are produced.</p>
<p><b>4.</b> The new cells contribute to rebuilding the tissue.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><b>Question:</b> Why would stopping mitosis completely make tissue repair difficult?</p>

<p>
Without cell division, damaged tissues would have a much more limited supply of
new cells to replace cells that have been lost.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>
<p>
Mitosis supplies new cells needed for the maintenance and repair of many tissues.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitosis and Tissue Repair', 15),
  (752, 752, '
<h2>Mitosis and Replacement of Worn-Out Cells</h2>

<h3>Why must cells be replaced?</h3>

<p>
Cells do not all remain functional forever. Some become damaged, worn out or
reach the end of their normal lifespan.
</p>

<p>
In tissues where cells are continually renewed, cell division produces new cells
that replace cells that have been lost.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Cells in the lining of the digestive tract are continually exposed to mechanical
and chemical conditions that can damage cells. New cells are produced to maintain
the tissue.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is continual cell division useful in tissues where cells
are frequently lost?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Some cells are lost or become unable to function properly.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The tissue needs replacement cells.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Mitosis produces new cells.</p>
<p><b>Answer:</b> Mitosis helps maintain the required number of functioning cells.</p>

<h3>Key distinction</h3>
<p>
Mitosis does not simply happen because a cell is “old.” Cell division is controlled
by the needs and signals of the tissue.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitosis and Replacement of Worn-Out Cells', 15),
  (753, 753, '
<h2>Mitosis in Growing Plant Regions</h2>

<h3>Where does much plant growth occur?</h3>

<p>
Many plants have regions where cells divide actively. These regions contain
meristematic tissue.
</p>

<p>
Cells in these regions can undergo mitosis to produce new cells.
</p>

<h3>What happens after division?</h3>

<p>
The newly produced cells can grow and later become specialised for different
functions.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, plant growth involves more than cell division alone. New cells must
also increase in size and may differentiate.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A plant root becomes longer as cells are produced near its
growing region. What process produces new cells?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The plant requires additional cells.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Cells in actively dividing tissue undergo cell division.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Mitosis produces new daughter cells.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Mitosis produces the new cells.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>
<p>
Mitosis provides new cells for plant growth, after which the cells may enlarge
and specialise.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitosis in Growing Plant Regions', 15),
  (754, 754, '
<h2>Homologous Chromosomes</h2>

<h3>What are homologous chromosomes?</h3>

<p>
Homologous chromosomes are a pair of chromosomes that carry the same types of
genes at corresponding positions.
</p>

<p>
One chromosome of the pair is inherited from the mother and the other from the
father.
</p>

<h3>Are they identical?</h3>

<p>
They are similar in structure and carry genes for the same characteristics, but
they may carry different versions of those genes.
</p>

<p>
These different versions are called <b>alleles</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A human cell has 23 pairs of chromosomes. What does each pair
represent?</p>

<p>
Each pair consists of homologous chromosomes. One chromosome was inherited from
the mother and the corresponding chromosome from the father.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The 23 pairs are homologous chromosome pairs.</p>

<h3>Why does this matter in meiosis?</h3>

<p>
Homologous chromosomes behave as pairs during the first meiotic division. Their
separation contributes to reducing the chromosome number.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homologous Chromosomes', 15),
  (755, 755, '
<h2>Prophase I</h2>

<h3>What happens in Prophase I?</h3>

<p>
During Prophase I, chromosomes condense and become visible. Homologous chromosomes
come together as pairs.
</p>

<p>
The spindle apparatus develops and the chromosomes become prepared for their
movement during the later stages of meiosis.
</p>

<h3>What makes Prophase I important?</h3>

<p>
The pairing of homologous chromosomes is a key feature that distinguishes
Prophase I from ordinary prophase in mitosis.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A student observes homologous chromosomes paired together.
Which stage is most likely being observed?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Look for chromosome pairing.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Pairing of homologous chromosomes occurs during Prophase I.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Prophase I.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Prophase I prepares homologous chromosomes for their separation in Meiosis I.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prophase I', 15),
  (756, 756, '
<h2>Metaphase I</h2>

<h3>What happens in Metaphase I?</h3>

<p>
During Metaphase I, homologous chromosome pairs become arranged at the equator
of the cell.
</p>

<p>
Spindle fibres attach to the chromosomes and help position the homologous pairs
for separation.
</p>

<h3>What should you look for?</h3>

<p>
The important feature is that <b>homologous pairs</b> are positioned at the
equator, rather than individual chromosomes being arranged independently.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A diagram shows homologous chromosome pairs arranged across
the equator. Which stage is represented?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the position of the chromosomes.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> They are at the equator.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> They are arranged as homologous pairs.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Metaphase I.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Metaphase I positions homologous pairs so that they can be separated during
Anaphase I.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Metaphase I', 15),
  (757, 757, '
<h2>Anaphase I</h2>

<h3>What happens?</h3>

<p>
During Anaphase I, homologous chromosomes separate and move towards opposite
poles of the cell.
</p>

<p>
The two chromosomes of each homologous pair move apart.
</p>

<h3>What does NOT happen yet?</h3>

<p>
The sister chromatids of each chromosome remain together during Anaphase I.
They are separated later during Meiosis II.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A cell is observed with homologous chromosomes moving towards
opposite poles while sister chromatids remain attached. Which stage is this?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify what is separating.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Homologous chromosomes are separating.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> This occurs during Anaphase I.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Anaphase I.</p>

<h3>Key distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>Anaphase I:</b> homologous chromosomes separate.</p>

<p>
<b>Anaphase II:</b> sister chromatids separate.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Anaphase I', 15),
  (758, 758, '
<h2>Telophase I</h2>

<h3>What happens in Telophase I?</h3>

<p>
By Telophase I, homologous chromosomes have reached opposite poles of the cell.
The cell then proceeds towards the second meiotic division.
</p>

<p>
Depending on the organism and cell type, nuclear envelopes may reform before
the cell divides.
</p>

<h3>What has changed?</h3>

<p>
The homologous chromosomes have been separated into different regions of the
cell. The chromosome number in each resulting nucleus is now reduced to one
set.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> What major event has already occurred by the time a cell reaches
Telophase I?</p>

<p>
Homologous chromosomes have separated and moved to opposite poles.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Homologous chromosome separation has occurred.</p>

<h3>What comes next?</h3>

<p>
The cell proceeds to <b>Meiosis II</b>, where sister chromatids will eventually
be separated.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Telophase I', 15),
  (759, 759, '
<h2>Prophase II</h2>

<h3>What happens in Prophase II?</h3>

<p>
After Meiosis I, the cells enter the second meiotic division. During Prophase II,
chromosomes condense and the spindle apparatus develops.
</p>

<p>
The chromosomes are already present as one set because homologous chromosomes
were separated during Meiosis I.
</p>

<h3>What is different from Prophase I?</h3>

<p>
Homologous chromosomes do not pair again during Prophase II.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A cell has already completed Meiosis I and is beginning its
second division. Which stage comes first?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The first meiotic division is complete.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The second division begins with Prophase II.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Prophase II.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Prophase II prepares the chromosomes for sister-chromatid separation.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prophase II', 15),
  (760, 760, '
<h2>Metaphase II</h2>

<h3>What happens?</h3>

<p>
During Metaphase II, chromosomes line up at the equator of each cell.
</p>

<p>
Spindle fibres attach to the chromosomes and position them so that sister
chromatids can be separated during Anaphase II.
</p>

<h3>How is this different from Metaphase I?</h3>

<p>
In Metaphase I, homologous chromosome pairs line up at the equator.
</p>

<p>
In Metaphase II, individual chromosomes line up at the equator.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A chromosome is positioned at the equator of a cell during
the second meiotic division. Which stage is being observed?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> It is the second meiotic division.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The chromosome is positioned at the equator.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Chromosome alignment at the equator occurs during metaphase.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Metaphase II.</p>

<h3>Key distinction</h3>

<p>
Metaphase I = homologous pairs at the equator.</p>
<p>
Metaphase II = chromosomes at the equator before sister chromatids separate.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Metaphase II', 15),
  (761, 761, '
<h2>Anaphase II</h2>

<h3>What happens?</h3>

<p>
During Anaphase II, the sister chromatids of each chromosome separate and move
towards opposite poles of the cell.
</p>

<p>
Once sister chromatids separate, each chromatid is regarded as an individual
chromosome.
</p>

<h3>Why is this important?</h3>

<p>
The separation allows each resulting nucleus to receive one copy of each
chromosome.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A diagram shows sister chromatids moving towards opposite
poles during the second meiotic division. Which stage is shown?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify what is separating.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Sister chromatids are separating.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Sister-chromatid separation during meiosis occurs in Anaphase II.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Anaphase II.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>

<p>
Anaphase I separates homologous chromosomes.</p>
<p>
Anaphase II separates sister chromatids.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Anaphase II', 15),
  (762, 762, '
<h2>Telophase II</h2>

<h3>What happens?</h3>

<p>
During Telophase II, the chromosomes reach opposite poles of each cell.
Nuclear envelopes can form around the chromosome sets.
</p>

<p>
The chromosomes may then become less condensed as the cells move towards their
final state.
</p>

<h3>What has been achieved?</h3>

<p>
The sister chromatids have been separated. Each new nucleus contains one set
of chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> What major event has already occurred by Telophase II?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Meiosis II has separated sister chromatids.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The chromosomes have moved towards opposite poles.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> New nuclei can form around the chromosome sets.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Sister chromatids have been separated and the chromosome sets
are at opposite poles.</p>

<h3>What happens next?</h3>

<p>
Cytokinesis separates the cytoplasm, leading to the formation of four cells.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Telophase II', 15),
  (763, 763, '
  <h2>Random Orientation of Homologous Chromosome Pairs</h2>

  <h3>What happens before homologous chromosomes separate?</h3>

  <p>
    During metaphase I of meiosis, homologous chromosome pairs line up at
    the equator of the cell.
  </p>

  <p>
    The orientation of each homologous pair can be different.
  </p>

  <h3>Why is this important?</h3>

  <p>
    The orientation determines which chromosome from each pair moves toward
    a particular pole when the homologous chromosomes separate.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because the orientation can differ, the resulting cells can receive
    different combinations of chromosomes.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Consider two chromosome pairs:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A / a</b><br>
    <b>B / b</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    One possible arrangement can send <b>A</b> and <b>B</b> toward the same
    pole.
  </p>

  <p>
    Another possible arrangement can send <b>A</b> and <b>b</b> toward the
    same pole.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the chromosome combinations entering gametes can differ.
  </p>

  <h3>Do the chromosomes change?</h3>

  <p>
    No.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chromosomes are not being created or destroyed during this process.
    They are simply being distributed into different cells in different
    combinations.
  </p>

  <h3>Key distinction</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Random orientation changes which chromosomes are grouped together in
    gametes; it does not change the identity of the chromosomes themselves.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Random Orientation of Homologous Chromosome Pairs', 15),
  (764, 764, '
<h2>What Is Meiosis?</h2>

<h3>What is meiosis?</h3>

<p>
Meiosis is a specialised type of cell division that produces cells with half the
chromosome number of the original cell.
</p>

<p>
In humans, meiosis is involved in producing the cells that develop into sperm
and eggs.
</p>

<h3>Why is chromosome number reduced?</h3>

<p>
When a sperm and an egg fuse during fertilisation, their chromosomes combine.
Each contributes one set of chromosomes.
</p>

<p>
Reducing the chromosome number before fertilisation prevents the chromosome
number from doubling every generation.
</p>

<h3>Human example</h3>

<p>
Most human body cells have 46 chromosomes. Cells produced through meiosis have
23 chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A human cell containing 46 chromosomes undergoes meiosis.
How many chromosomes should each resulting gamete normally contain?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Meiosis reduces chromosome number by half.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Half of 46 is 23.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 23 chromosomes.</p>

<h3>Key distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>Mitosis:</b> maintains chromosome number.</p>
<p>
<b>Meiosis:</b> reduces chromosome number by half.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Meiosis?', 15),
  (765, 765, '
<h2>Diploid and Haploid Cells</h2>

<h3>What does diploid mean?</h3>

<p>
A <b>diploid</b> cell has two sets of chromosomes. In humans, a diploid cell
normally contains 46 chromosomes arranged as 23 pairs.
</p>

<h3>What does haploid mean?</h3>

<p>
A <b>haploid</b> cell has one set of chromosomes. In humans, a haploid cell
contains 23 chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Why are sperm and eggs haploid?</h3>

<p>
During fertilisation, one haploid sperm and one haploid egg combine. Their
chromosome sets join to restore the diploid number.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A human sperm contains 23 chromosomes and a human egg contains
23 chromosomes. How many chromosomes are present after their nuclei combine?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Sperm contributes 23.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Egg contributes 23.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> 23 + 23 = 46.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 46 chromosomes.</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>

<p>
<b>Diploid = two sets.</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Haploid = one set.</b>
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diploid and Haploid Cells', 15),
  (766, 766, '
<h2>Meiosis I</h2>

<h3>What happens in Meiosis I?</h3>

<p>
Meiosis consists of two divisions. The first division is called <b>Meiosis I</b>.
</p>

<p>
A major event in Meiosis I is the separation of homologous chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Why is this important?</h3>

<p>
Separating homologous chromosomes reduces the chromosome number from two sets
to one set in the resulting cells.
</p>

<h3>Basic sequence</h3>

<p>
Meiosis I has stages corresponding to prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase:
</p>

<p>
<b>Prophase I → Metaphase I → Anaphase I → Telophase I</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> What separates during Anaphase I?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify which meiotic division is occurring.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Meiosis I separates homologous chromosome pairs.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Homologous chromosomes separate and move towards opposite poles.</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
Meiosis I separates <b>homologous chromosomes</b>, while Meiosis II later separates
<b>sister chromatids</b>.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meiosis I', 15),
  (767, 767, '
<h2>Meiosis I and Chromosome Number</h2>

<h3>Why does Meiosis I reduce chromosome number?</h3>

<p>
A diploid cell contains pairs of homologous chromosomes. During Meiosis I, the
homologous chromosomes are separated.
</p>

<p>
Each resulting cell receives one chromosome from each homologous pair.
Therefore, each cell has one set of chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Human example</h3>

<p>
A human starting cell has 46 chromosomes arranged in 23 homologous pairs.
</p>

<p>
After homologous chromosomes separate during Meiosis I, each resulting cell
has one chromosome from each pair, giving 23 chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A cell has 12 chromosomes arranged in 6 homologous pairs.
How many chromosomes should each cell have after Meiosis I?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> There are 6 homologous pairs.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> One chromosome from each pair goes into each resulting cell.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Each cell therefore receives 6 chromosomes.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> 6 chromosomes.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Meiosis I reduces chromosome number because homologous pairs are separated.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meiosis I and Chromosome Number', 15),
  (768, 768, '
<h2>Mitosis Compared with Meiosis I</h2>

<h3>Why compare them?</h3>

<p>
Mitosis and Meiosis I both involve chromosome movement, but they have different
purposes and separate different chromosome structures.
</p>

<h3>Mitosis</h3>

<p>
During mitosis, sister chromatids separate. The chromosome number is maintained
in the resulting daughter cells.
</p>

<h3>Meiosis I</h3>

<p>
During Meiosis I, homologous chromosomes separate. The chromosome number is
reduced in the resulting cells.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A diagram shows homologous chromosomes separating towards
opposite poles. Is this mitosis or Meiosis I?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify what is separating.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Homologous chromosomes are separating.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Homologous chromosome separation occurs in Meiosis I.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Meiosis I.</p>

<h3>Core distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>Mitosis:</b> sister chromatids separate.</p>

<p>
<b>Meiosis I:</b> homologous chromosomes separate.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitosis Compared with Meiosis I', 15),
  (769, 769, '
<h2>What Is Meiosis II?</h2>

<h3>What happens in Meiosis II?</h3>

<p>
Meiosis II is the second division of meiosis. It occurs after Meiosis I and
separates sister chromatids.
</p>

<p>
Unlike Meiosis I, Meiosis II does not reduce the chromosome number again.
</p>

<h3>Why is another division needed?</h3>

<p>
After Meiosis I, each cell has one chromosome from each homologous pair, but
each chromosome still consists of two sister chromatids.
</p>

<p>
Meiosis II separates those sister chromatids.
</p>

<h3>Basic sequence</h3>

<p>
<b>Prophase II → Metaphase II → Anaphase II → Telophase II</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> What separates during Meiosis II?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Meiosis I separated homologous chromosomes.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Sister chromatids are still joined.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Meiosis II separates the sister chromatids.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Sister chromatids separate during Meiosis II.</p>

<h3>Key distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>Meiosis I:</b> homologous chromosomes separate.</p>
<p>
<b>Meiosis II:</b> sister chromatids separate.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Meiosis II?', 15),
  (770, 770, '
<h2>Cytokinesis After Meiosis II</h2>

<h3>What happens?</h3>

<p>
After the nuclear events of Meiosis II, cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm of
the cells.
</p>

<p>
The result is normally four separate haploid cells from one original diploid
cell.
</p>

<h3>Follow the divisions</h3>

<p>
<b>Starting cell:</b> 1 diploid cell.</p>

<p>
<b>After Meiosis I:</b> 2 cells.</p>

<p>
<b>After Meiosis II:</b> 4 cells.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> If one cell begins meiosis, how many cells can result after
both meiotic divisions?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Meiosis I produces two cells.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Each of those cells undergoes Meiosis II.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Each produces two cells.</p>
<p><b>Step 4:</b> 2 × 2 = 4.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Four cells.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
The two successive divisions of meiosis allow one starting cell to produce
four cells.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cytokinesis After Meiosis II', 15),
  (771, 771, '
<h2>Four Haploid Cells</h2>

<h3>What is the final result of meiosis?</h3>

<p>
A typical meiotic division produces four cells, each containing one set of
chromosomes.
</p>

<p>
These cells are therefore <b>haploid</b>.
</p>

<h3>Human example</h3>

<p>
The original human cell is diploid and contains 46 chromosomes.
</p>

<p>
After meiosis, each resulting cell has 23 chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Why does this matter?</h3>

<p>
The haploid cells can participate in sexual reproduction. When two haploid
cells combine during fertilisation, the diploid chromosome number can be
restored.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A human diploid cell containing 46 chromosomes undergoes
meiosis. What is the chromosome number of each of the four resulting cells?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Meiosis reduces chromosome number by half.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> 46 ÷ 2 = 23.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Each resulting haploid cell has 23 chromosomes.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
One diploid starting cell → four haploid cells.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Four Haploid Cells', 15),
  (772, 772, '
<h2>Meiosis as a Complete Sequence</h2>

<h3>The complete process</h3>

<p>
Meiosis consists of two successive divisions.
</p>

<h3>Meiosis I</h3>

<p>
<b>Prophase I:</b> Homologous chromosomes pair.</p>

<p>
<b>Metaphase I:</b> Homologous pairs line up at the equator.</p>

<p>
<b>Anaphase I:</b> Homologous chromosomes separate.</p>

<p>
<b>Telophase I:</b> Chromosomes reach opposite poles.</p>

<h3>Meiosis II</h3>

<p>
<b>Prophase II:</b> Chromosomes prepare for the second division.</p>

<p>
<b>Metaphase II:</b> Chromosomes line up at the equator.</p>

<p>
<b>Anaphase II:</b> Sister chromatids separate.</p>

<p>
<b>Telophase II:</b> Chromosomes reach opposite poles and nuclei may reform.</p>

<p>
Cytokinesis then separates the cells.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Put these events in the correct order:
sister chromatids separate, homologous chromosomes separate,
homologous pairs line up.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Homologous pairs line up during Metaphase I.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Homologous chromosomes separate during Anaphase I.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Sister chromatids separate during Anaphase II.</p>

<p><b>Correct order:</b> Homologous pairs line up → homologous chromosomes separate
→ sister chromatids separate.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meiosis as a Complete Sequence', 15),
  (773, 773, '
<h2>Mitosis and Meiosis: Final Comparison</h2>

<h3>Purpose</h3>

<p>
<b>Mitosis</b> produces new body cells for processes such as growth, repair and
replacement.
</p>

<p>
<b>Meiosis</b> produces haploid cells involved in sexual reproduction.
</p>

<h3>Number of divisions</h3>

<p>
Mitosis involves one nuclear division.
</p>

<p>
Meiosis involves two successive nuclear divisions.
</p>

<h3>Number of resulting cells</h3>

<p>
Mitosis normally produces two daughter cells.
</p>

<p>
Meiosis normally produces four cells.
</p>

<h3>Chromosome number</h3>

<p>
Mitosis normally maintains chromosome number.
</p>

<p>
Meiosis reduces chromosome number by half.
</p>

<h3>What separates?</h3>

<p>
In mitosis, sister chromatids separate during anaphase.
</p>

<p>
In Meiosis I, homologous chromosomes separate.
</p>

<p>
In Meiosis II, sister chromatids separate.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A process starts with one diploid cell and produces four
haploid cells. Is it mitosis or meiosis?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Four cells are produced.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The cells are haploid.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The chromosome number has therefore been reduced.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Meiosis.</p>

<h3>Core distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>Mitosis:</b> one division → two cells → chromosome number maintained.</p>

<p>
<b>Meiosis:</b> two divisions → four haploid cells → chromosome number reduced.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitosis and Meiosis: Final Comparison', 15),
  (774, 774, '
<h2>Why Mitosis Produces Genetically Similar Cells</h2>

<h3>What happens to genetic material?</h3>

<p>
Before mitosis, the cell copies its DNA. This creates two copies of each chromosome
so that genetic material can be distributed to the daughter nuclei.
</p>

<p>
During mitosis, sister chromatids are separated so that each new nucleus receives
one copy of each chromosome.
</p>

<h3>Why is this important?</h3>

<p>
The daughter cells therefore normally receive the same chromosome number and
equivalent genetic information as the original cell.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why must chromosomes be copied before mitosis?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Each daughter cell needs a complete set of genetic information.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The original DNA must therefore be copied.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The copied chromosomes can then be separated during mitosis.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> DNA is copied so each daughter cell can receive a complete set of
genetic information.</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
“Genetically similar” does not mean that every daughter cell will always remain
identical in appearance or function. Cells can later become specialised through
differential gene expression.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Mitosis Produces Genetically Similar Cells', 15),
  (775, 775, '
<h2>What Are Gametes?</h2>

<h3>What is a gamete?</h3>

<p>
A <b>gamete</b> is a reproductive cell that can take part in fertilisation.
In humans, the two main gametes are sperm cells and egg cells.
</p>

<h3>Why are gametes haploid?</h3>

<p>
Gametes contain one set of chromosomes. In humans, this means 23 chromosomes.
</p>

<p>
When a sperm and an egg combine, their chromosome sets join to form a diploid
cell containing 46 chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why must human gametes contain 23 chromosomes rather than 46?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> A sperm contributes one chromosome set.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> An egg contributes one chromosome set.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> 23 + 23 = 46.</p>

<p>
<b>Answer:</b> Gametes are haploid so that fertilisation restores the normal
diploid chromosome number.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Gametes carry one chromosome set into sexual reproduction.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Are Gametes?', 15),
  (776, 776, '
<h2>What Is Fertilisation?</h2>

<h3>What is fertilisation?</h3>

<p>
Fertilisation is the fusion of the nuclei of two compatible gametes to form a
diploid cell called a <b>zygote</b>.
</p>

<p>
In humans, the sperm nucleus contributes 23 chromosomes and the egg nucleus
contributes 23 chromosomes.
</p>

<p>
23 + 23 = 46 chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>Why is fertilisation important?</h3>

<p>
It combines genetic material from two parents and restores the diploid
chromosome number.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A sperm nucleus containing 23 chromosomes fuses with an egg
nucleus containing 23 chromosomes. What is the chromosome number of the zygote?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Sperm contributes 23.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Egg contributes 23.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> 23 + 23 = 46.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The zygote normally has 46 chromosomes.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Haploid + haploid → diploid.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Fertilisation?', 15),
  (777, 777, '
<h2>The Zygote</h2>

<h3>What is a zygote?</h3>

<p>
A zygote is the diploid cell formed when the nuclei of two gametes fuse during
fertilisation.
</p>

<h3>What happens to its chromosome number?</h3>

<p>
The zygote receives one chromosome set from each parent.
In humans, this gives it 46 chromosomes.
</p>

<h3>What happens next?</h3>

<p>
The zygote begins repeated mitotic divisions. These divisions produce more cells
as the early organism develops.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why does the zygote undergo mitosis during early development?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The zygote begins as a single cell.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> A multicellular organism requires many cells.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Mitosis produces new cells while maintaining chromosome number.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Mitosis increases the number of cells needed for development.</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Fertilisation produces the zygote; repeated mitosis then increases the number
of cells during development.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Zygote', 15),
  (778, 778, '
<h2>Fertilisation Restores Diploid Chromosome Number</h2>

<h3>The problem fertilisation solves</h3>

<p>
Meiosis reduces chromosome number so that gametes contain one chromosome set.
Fertilisation then brings two chromosome sets together.
</p>

<h3>Human example</h3>

<p>
Sperm = 23 chromosomes.</p>
<p>
Egg = 23 chromosomes.</p>
<p>
Zygote = 23 + 23 = 46 chromosomes.</p>

<h3>Why does this matter?</h3>

<p>
Without chromosome-number reduction during gamete formation, combining two
diploid cells would double the chromosome number in every generation.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Suppose human gametes incorrectly contained 46 chromosomes.
What would happen if two such gametes fused?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Sperm would contribute 46.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Egg would contribute 46.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> 46 + 46 = 92.</p>

<p>
<b>Answer:</b> The resulting cell would contain 92 chromosomes instead of the
normal 46.
</p>

<p>
This demonstrates why meiosis must reduce chromosome number before
fertilisation.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fertilisation Restores Diploid Chromosome Number', 15),
  (779, 779, '
<h2>From Meiosis to a New Organism</h2>

<h3>The complete connection</h3>

<p>
Meiosis and fertilisation are parts of one continuous reproductive cycle.
</p>

<p>
<b>Step 1:</b> Meiosis produces haploid reproductive cells.</p>

<p>
<b>Step 2:</b> A sperm and egg are capable of participating in fertilisation.</p>

<p>
<b>Step 3:</b> Fertilisation combines their chromosome sets.</p>

<p>
<b>Step 4:</b> A diploid zygote is formed.</p>

<p>
<b>Step 5:</b> The zygote divides repeatedly by mitosis as development begins.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Put these events in the correct order:
mitosis of the zygote, meiosis, fertilisation, formation of a zygote.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Meiosis produces haploid cells.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Fertilisation occurs.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The zygote is formed.</p>
<p><b>Step 4:</b> The zygote undergoes mitosis.</p>

<p>
<b>Correct order:</b>
Meiosis → fertilisation → zygote formation → mitosis.
</p>

<h3>Core idea</h3>

<p>
Meiosis reduces chromosome number; fertilisation restores it; mitosis increases
cell number during growth and development.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for From Meiosis to a New Organism', 15),
  (780, 780, '
  <h2>Why Offspring Are Not Genetically Identical</h2>

  <h3>The key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Children receive genetic information from both parents. Because the
    genetic information contributed by each parent is not exactly the same,
    offspring usually have combinations of characteristics that differ from
    their parents and from their brothers and sisters.
  </p>

  <h3>Where does the difference begin?</h3>

  <p>
    The process begins when gametes are produced by meiosis.
    Meiosis produces haploid cells, such as sperm and egg cells, that contain
    one set of chromosomes.
  </p>

  <p>
    During sexual reproduction, one sperm combines with one egg during
    fertilisation. The offspring therefore receives genetic information from
    two parents.
  </p>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>

  <p>
    If every child received exactly the same combination of genetic
    information, siblings would be genetically identical in the same way
    that genetically identical twins are.
  </p>

  <p>
    Instead, sexual reproduction produces different combinations of genetic
    information. This creates <b>genetic variation</b> among offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a father can produce several genetically different sperm cells
    and a mother can produce several genetically different egg cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    If sperm A fertilises egg A, one genetic combination is produced.
    If sperm B fertilises egg A, a different genetic combination is produced.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, even with the same parents, different fertilisation events can
    produce offspring with different genetic combinations.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Meiosis creates different gametes, while fertilisation combines genetic
    information from two gametes.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Offspring Are Not Genetically Identical', 15),
  (781, 781, '
  <h2>Different Gametes Carry Different Genetic Combinations</h2>

  <h3>The problem with thinking of gametes as identical</h3>

  <p>
    It is incorrect to think that every sperm produced by a male contains
    exactly the same genetic combination.
  </p>

  <p>
    Likewise, the egg cells produced by a female can contain different
    combinations of genetic information.
  </p>

  <h3>How does this happen?</h3>

  <p>
    During meiosis, chromosome pairs are separated into different daughter
    cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chromosomes that enter one gamete are not necessarily the same
    combination as those entering another gamete.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, meiosis can produce gametes that differ genetically from
    one another.
  </p>

  <h3>Simple example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a cell has two pairs of homologous chromosomes.
  </p>

  <p>
    For simplicity, represent the chromosome pairs as:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A / a</b> and <b>B / b</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    A gamete receives one chromosome from each pair.
  </p>

  <p>
    Possible combinations include:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AB</b>, <b>Ab</b>, <b>aB</b>, and <b>ab</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The important point is not the letters themselves. The important idea is
    that different gametes can receive different combinations of chromosomes.
  </p>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>

  <p>
    If different gametes carry different genetic combinations, fertilisation
    can combine these different gametes in many ways.
  </p>

  <p>
    This contributes to genetic variation among offspring.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Different Gametes Carry Different Genetic Combinations', 15),
  (782, 782, '
  <h2>Independent Assortment of Chromosomes</h2>

  <h3>What is independent assortment?</h3>

  <p>
    During meiosis, homologous chromosome pairs can be arranged in different
    orientations before they separate.
  </p>

  <p>
    This means that the chromosomes inherited from the two parents can be
    distributed into gametes in different combinations.
  </p>

  <h3>Why does orientation matter?</h3>

  <p>
    Consider two homologous chromosome pairs.
  </p>

  <p>
    One possible arrangement can result in a gamete receiving chromosomes
    <b>A and B</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Another arrangement can result in a gamete receiving <b>A and b</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Other combinations such as <b>aB</b> and <b>ab</b> can also occur.
  </p>

  <h3>The key idea</h3>

  <p>
    The chromosome pairs do not all have to orient in one fixed way.
    Their different orientations contribute to different combinations of
    chromosomes entering gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose there are two chromosome pairs:
  </p>

  <p>
    Pair 1: <b>A / a</b><br>
    Pair 2: <b>B / b</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Possible gamete combinations include:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AB</b><br>
    <b>Ab</b><br>
    <b>aB</b><br>
    <b>ab</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The different combinations arise because the chromosome pairs can be
    arranged in different orientations during meiosis.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Independent assortment contributes to genetic variation by producing
    different combinations of chromosomes in gametes.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Independent Assortment of Chromosomes', 15),
  (783, 783, '
  <h2>Why Siblings Can Differ Genetically</h2>

  <h3>The question</h3>

  <p>
    If two children have the same biological parents, why are they usually
    not genetically identical?
  </p>

  <p>
    The answer is that each child results from a different combination of
    gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>What happens for the first child?</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>The father produces many sperm cells.</li>
    <li>The mother produces egg cells.</li>
    <li>One sperm and one egg take part in fertilisation.</li>
    <li>Their genetic information combines in the zygote.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>What happens for another child?</h3>

  <p>
    The same parents can produce different sperm and egg cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    A different sperm may fertilise a different egg.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second child therefore receives a different combination of genetic
    information.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Imagine the first child results from:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Sperm A + Egg X</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The second child might result from:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Sperm C + Egg Y</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The two children have the same parents, but they did not receive exactly
    the same combination of gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Important exception</h3>

  <p>
    Identical twins can be genetically very similar because they develop from
    the same fertilised egg that later separates into two embryos.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Same parents does not mean the same combination of gametes.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Siblings Can Differ Genetically', 15),
  (784, 784, '
  <h2>What Is Inheritance?</h2>

  <h3>What does inheritance mean?</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Inheritance</b> is the passing of genetic information from parents to
    their offspring.
  </p>

  <p>
    The genetic information passed from parents influences characteristics
    of the offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Where is the information found?</h3>

  <p>
    Genetic information is stored in <b>DNA</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    DNA is organised into structures called <b>chromosomes</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sections of DNA that carry information for particular characteristics are
    called <b>genes</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>How is information passed to offspring?</h3>

  <p>
    During sexual reproduction, each parent contributes a gamete.
  </p>

  <p>
    The sperm contributes one set of chromosomes and the egg contributes one
    set of chromosomes.
  </p>

  <p>
    When they fuse during fertilisation, the zygote receives genetic
    information from both parents.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    A child receives genetic information from both the mother and father.
    This information contributes to characteristics such as blood group and
    other inherited features.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Inheritance is the transmission of genetic information from parents
    to offspring.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Inheritance', 15),
  (785, 785, '
  <h2>What Is a Gene?</h2>

  <h3>The basic idea</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>gene</b> is a section of DNA that contains information used by a
    cell to produce a functional product, often a protein, that contributes
    to a characteristic or biological function.
  </p>

  <h3>Where is a gene found?</h3>

  <p>
    Genes are sections of DNA located on chromosomes.
  </p>

  <p>
    A chromosome therefore contains many genes.
  </p>

  <h3>Think of the relationship</h3>

  <p>
    <b>DNA → chromosomes → genes</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    More precisely, chromosomes are structures made largely of DNA and
    associated proteins, and genes are particular regions of that DNA.
  </p>

  <h3>Why are genes important?</h3>

  <p>
    Genes carry biological information.
  </p>

  <p>
    The information in genes can influence characteristics by controlling
    the production or regulation of proteins and other functional products.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Blood group is influenced by inherited genetic information.
    The genes involved are located on chromosomes and are passed from parents
    to offspring through their gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not confuse a gene with a chromosome</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>gene</b> is a region of DNA.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <b>chromosome</b> is a much larger DNA-containing structure that carries
    many genes.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is a Gene', 15),
  (786, 786, '
  <h2>What Is an Allele?</h2>

  <h3>Start with the gene</h3>

  <p>
    A gene is a region of DNA associated with a particular biological
    characteristic or function.
  </p>

  <p>
    A gene can exist in different forms. These different forms are called
    <b>alleles</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Simple example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a gene is represented by the letter <b>A</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Different forms of that gene can be represented as:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> and <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    These symbols represent different alleles of the same gene.
  </p>

  <h3>Why are alleles important?</h3>

  <p>
    Alleles can contribute to differences in characteristics between
    individuals.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, different alleles of a gene can result in different forms
    of a characteristic.
  </p>

  <h3>Gene versus allele</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>gene</b> refers to the genetic unit or DNA region associated with a
    particular characteristic or function.
  </p>

  <p>
    An <b>allele</b> is a particular version of that gene.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Do not say that A and a are two completely different genes.
    In a simple inheritance model, they represent two different alleles of
    the same gene.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is an Allele', 15),
  (787, 787, '
  <h2>Dominant Alleles</h2>

  <h3>What does dominant mean?</h3>

  <p>
    In a simple inheritance model, a <b>dominant allele</b> is an allele
    whose effect is expressed in the phenotype when at least one copy of the
    allele is present.
  </p>

  <p>
    A dominant allele is commonly represented using a capital letter.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> = dominant allele
  </p>

  <h3>Consider two combinations</h3>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    There are two copies of the dominant allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    There is one dominant allele and one different allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a simple dominant-recessive model, the dominant allele determines the
    phenotype in both cases.
  </p>

  <h3>Important correction</h3>

  <p>
    Dominant does <b>not</b> mean stronger, better, healthier, or more common.
  </p>

  <p>
    It describes how an allele is expressed in a particular genetic
    combination.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    If <b>A</b> is dominant over <b>a</b>:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b> → dominant phenotype
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b> → dominant phenotype
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Dominant Alleles', 15),
  (788, 788, '
  <h2>Recessive Alleles</h2>

  <h3>What does recessive mean?</h3>

  <p>
    In a simple dominant-recessive inheritance model, a <b>recessive
    allele</b> is expressed in the phenotype when two copies of the recessive
    allele are present.
  </p>

  <p>
    A recessive allele is commonly represented using a lowercase letter.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b> = recessive allele
  </p>

  <h3>Compare two genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The dominant allele A is present, so the recessive allele a is not
    expressed in the simple model.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    There is no dominant allele present. Therefore, the recessive phenotype
    is expressed.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    A recessive allele can be present in an individual without appearing in
    the phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why an individual can carry a recessive allele without showing
    the recessive characteristic.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    If A is dominant and a is recessive:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b> → dominant phenotype
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b> → dominant phenotype
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b> → recessive phenotype
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recessive Alleles', 15),
  (789, 789, '
  <h2>Genotype</h2>

  <h3>What is a genotype?</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>genotype</b> is the genetic constitution of an individual for a
    particular gene or characteristic.
  </p>

  <p>
    In simple inheritance questions, genotype is often represented using
    letters that show the alleles an individual possesses.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> = dominant allele
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b> = recessive allele
  </p>

  <p>
    Possible genotypes include:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why are two letters used?</h3>

  <p>
    In a diploid organism, an individual normally has two copies of each
    autosomal gene, with one inherited from each parent.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, a simple genotype is represented using two alleles.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked identification</h3>

  <p>
    If an organism has genotype <b>Aa</b>:
  </p>

  <p>
    First allele = <b>A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Second allele = <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the genotype is <b>Aa</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Genotype describes the genetic combination; phenotype describes the
    observable characteristic.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Genotype', 15),
  (790, 790, '
  <h2>Phenotype</h2>

  <h3>What is a phenotype?</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>phenotype</b> is an observable characteristic or measurable feature
    of an organism.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include characteristics such as blood group, height, or a
    particular inherited form of a characteristic.
  </p>

  <h3>Genotype versus phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    The <b>genotype</b> describes the genetic combination.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <b>phenotype</b> describes the characteristic that is observed or
    measured.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> = dominant allele<br>
    <b>a</b> = recessive allele
  </p>

  <p>
    An organism with genotype <b>Aa</b> may show the dominant phenotype in a
    simple dominant-recessive model.
  </p>

  <p>
    The letters <b>Aa</b> describe the genotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    The observable characteristic describes the phenotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Phenotype is not determined by genes in every case alone.
  </p>

  <p>
    Environmental factors can also influence some observable characteristics.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Genotype = genetic combination.</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Phenotype = observable or measurable characteristic.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Phenotype', 15),
  (791, 791, '
  <h2>Homozygous Genotype</h2>

  <h3>What does homozygous mean?</h3>

  <p>
    An individual is <b>homozygous</b> for a particular gene when the two
    alleles are the same.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Both alleles are A.
    Therefore, AA is homozygous.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Both alleles are a.
    Therefore, aa is also homozygous.
  </p>

  <h3>How to identify it</h3>

  <p>
    Look at the two alleles.
  </p>

  <p>
    If they are identical, the genotype is homozygous.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked examples</h3>

  <p>
    <b>AA:</b> A = A → homozygous.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa:</b> a = a → homozygous.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Bb:</b> B ≠ b → not homozygous.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Homozygous does not automatically mean dominant.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b> is homozygous dominant, while <b>aa</b> is homozygous
    recessive.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homozygous Genotype', 15),
  (792, 792, '
  <h2>Heterozygous Genotype</h2>

  <h3>What does heterozygous mean?</h3>

  <p>
    An individual is <b>heterozygous</b> for a particular gene when the two
    alleles are different.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The two alleles are A and a.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because they are different, Aa is heterozygous.
  </p>

  <h3>Compare</h3>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b> → same alleles → homozygous
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b> → same alleles → homozygous
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b> → different alleles → heterozygous
  </p>

  <h3>Why is heterozygous important?</h3>

  <p>
    A heterozygous individual can carry a recessive allele without showing
    the recessive phenotype in a simple dominant-recessive inheritance model.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if A is dominant and a is recessive:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b> has a dominant phenotype but carries the recessive allele.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Homozygous = same alleles.</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Heterozygous = different alleles.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heterozygous Genotype', 15),
  (793, 793, '
  <h2>Homozygous Dominant and Homozygous Recessive</h2>

  <h3>Homozygous dominant</h3>

  <p>
    A genotype is <b>homozygous dominant</b> when both alleles are dominant.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Both alleles are A, and A is dominant.
  </p>

  <h3>Homozygous recessive</h3>

  <p>
    A genotype is <b>homozygous recessive</b> when both alleles are recessive.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Both alleles are a, and a is recessive.
  </p>

  <h3>Step-by-step identification</h3>

  <p>
    Consider <b>AA</b>.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>The two alleles are the same → homozygous.</li>
    <li>Both alleles are dominant → dominant.</li>
    <li>Therefore → homozygous dominant.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Consider <b>aa</b>.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>The two alleles are the same → homozygous.</li>
    <li>Both alleles are recessive → recessive.</li>
    <li>Therefore → homozygous recessive.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Quick comparison</h3>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b> → homozygous dominant
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b> → heterozygous
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b> → homozygous recessive
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homozygous Dominant and Homozygous Recessive', 15),
  (794, 794, '
  <h2>Carrier of a Recessive Allele</h2>

  <h3>What is a carrier?</h3>

  <p>
    In a simple dominant-recessive inheritance model, a <b>carrier</b> is an
    individual who has a recessive allele but does not show the recessive
    phenotype because a dominant allele is also present.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> = dominant allele<br>
    <b>a</b> = recessive allele
  </p>

  <p>
    A person with genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    has one dominant allele and one recessive allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a simple dominant-recessive model, the dominant phenotype is expressed.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, the individual still possesses the recessive allele and can pass
    it to an offspring through a gamete.
  </p>

  <h3>Why can the allele be hidden?</h3>

  <p>
    The recessive phenotype is masked when the dominant allele is present in
    this simple model.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Being a carrier does not mean that the recessive allele has disappeared.
    It is still present in the person''s genotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Aa → dominant phenotype, but recessive allele is carried.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carrier of a Recessive Allele', 15),
  (795, 795, '
  <h2>What Is a Monohybrid Cross?</h2>

  <h3>What does "monohybrid" mean?</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>monohybrid cross</b> is a genetic cross used to study the inheritance
    of one gene or one characteristic at a time.
  </p>

  <p>
    The word <b>mono</b> means one.
  </p>

  <h3>Why study one characteristic?</h3>

  <p>
    Studying one characteristic allows us to follow exactly how the alleles
    from two parents can combine in their offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a characteristic is controlled by two alleles:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> = dominant allele
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b> = recessive allele
  </p>

  <p>
    We could cross:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    This is a monohybrid cross because we are following one gene with two
    alleles.
  </p>

  <h3>What are we trying to find?</h3>

  <p>
    A monohybrid cross can help us determine the possible:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>genotypes of offspring</li>
    <li>phenotypes of offspring</li>
    <li>genotypic ratios</li>
    <li>phenotypic ratios</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Monohybrid cross = inheritance of one characteristic at a time.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is a Monohybrid Cross', 15),
  (796, 796, '
  <h2>Determining the Genotypes of Parents</h2>

  <h3>Why do we need parental genotypes?</h3>

  <p>
    Before predicting offspring, we must know the alleles carried by the
    parents.
  </p>

  <p>
    A phenotype alone does not always tell us the exact genotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> = dominant allele<br>
    <b>a</b> = recessive allele
  </p>

  <p>
    The possible genotypes are:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>, <b>Aa</b>, and <b>aa</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>What if the phenotype is recessive?</h3>

  <p>
    In a simple dominant-recessive model, an individual showing the recessive
    phenotype must have two recessive alleles.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    Recessive phenotype → <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>What if the phenotype is dominant?</h3>

  <p>
    A dominant phenotype can result from either:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    or
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, a dominant phenotype alone does not always reveal whether the
    individual is AA or Aa.
  </p>

  <h3>Key lesson</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Recessive phenotype → known genotype in the simple model.</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Dominant phenotype → genotype may be AA or Aa.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Determining the Genotypes of Parents', 15),
  (797, 797, '
  <h2>Finding the Gametes of a Parent</h2>

  <h3>The key rule</h3>

  <p>
    A gamete receives only <b>one allele</b> of a particular gene.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, when solving a simple inheritance problem, we must determine
    which allele each parent can place into a gamete.
  </p>

  <h3>Example 1: AA</h3>

  <p>
    Consider a parent with genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Both alleles are A.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, every gamete receives:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Example 2: aa</h3>

  <p>
    A parent with genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    can produce gametes carrying:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Example 3: Aa</h3>

  <p>
    A parent with genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    can produce gametes carrying either:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> or <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why?</h3>

  <p>
    The two alleles separate when homologous chromosomes separate during
    meiosis.
  </p>

  <h3>Quick rule</h3>

  <p>
    <b>AA → A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa → a</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa → A or a</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finding the Gametes of a Parent', 15),
  (798, 798, '
  <h2>Why Alleles Separate During Meiosis</h2>

  <h3>Start with a diploid cell</h3>

  <p>
    A diploid individual has two copies of an autosomal gene, with one
    inherited from each parent.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a simple example, consider genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The two alleles are located at corresponding positions on a pair of
    homologous chromosomes.
  </p>

  <h3>What happens in meiosis?</h3>

  <p>
    During meiosis I, homologous chromosomes separate.
  </p>

  <p>
    One homologous chromosome carries <b>A</b> and the other carries <b>a</b>
    in this simplified example.
  </p>

  <p>
    As the homologous chromosomes separate, the alleles are separated into
    different cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Result</h3>

  <p>
    The gametes receive one allele:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    or
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>

  <p>
    This is why a parent with genotype Aa can pass either A or a to an
    offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    The alleles do not randomly disappear.
  </p>

  <p>
    The homologous chromosomes carrying them are separated during meiosis.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Meiosis reduces the chromosome number and separates homologous
    chromosomes, causing the two alleles of a gene to enter different
    gametes.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Alleles Separate During Meiosis', 15),
  (799, 799, '
  <h2>Building a Punnett Square</h2>

  <h3>What is a Punnett square?</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>Punnett square</b> is a diagram used to show possible combinations
    of alleles in offspring from a genetic cross.
  </p>

  <h3>Example cross</h3>

  <p>
    Consider:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Each parent can produce gametes carrying A or a.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Write one parent''s gametes</h3>

  <p>
    Put:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> and <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    across the top.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Write the other parent''s gametes</h3>

  <p>
    Put:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> and <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    down the side.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine the alleles</h3>

  <p>
    Each box receives one allele from the top and one from the side.
  </p>

  <p>
    The four combinations are:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why does this work?</h3>

  <p>
    Each box represents one possible combination of a gamete from one parent
    with a gamete from the other parent.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Do not put parental genotypes directly into the boxes.
    Put <b>gametes</b> along the top and side first.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Building a Punnett Square', 15),
  (800, 800, '
  <h2>Completing a Punnett Square</h2>

  <h3>Cross</h3>

  <p>
    We will solve:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    First parent:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> or <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Second parent:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> or <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Combine them</h3>

  <p>
    Combine the top allele with the side allele in each box.
  </p>

  <p>
    A + A = <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    A + a = <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    a + A = <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    a + a = <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Completed combinations</h3>

  <p>
    The four possible offspring genotypes are:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA, Aa, Aa, aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Do not collapse repeated results too early</h3>

  <p>
    The two Aa boxes are separate possible outcomes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Keeping all four boxes allows us to count how frequently each genotype
    appears in the cross.
  </p>

  <h3>Key rule</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Combine one allele from the sperm-producing parent with one allele from
    the egg-producing parent.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Completing a Punnett Square', 15),
  (801, 801, '
  <h2>Genotypic Ratio</h2>

  <h3>What is a genotypic ratio?</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>genotypic ratio</b> compares the numbers of different genotypes
    produced in a genetic cross.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Consider:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The Punnett square gives:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA, Aa, Aa, aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Count each genotype</h3>

  <p>
    AA appears <b>1</b> time.
  </p>

  <p>
    Aa appears <b>2</b> times.
  </p>

  <p>
    aa appears <b>1</b> time.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Write the ratio</h3>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA : Aa : aa = 1 : 2 : 1</b>
  </p>

  <h3>What does this mean?</h3>

  <p>
    In the idealised probability represented by the square, there is one
    equally sized outcome category for AA, two for Aa and one for aa.
  </p>

  <p>
    This does not mean that every family of four children must contain exactly
    one AA, two Aa and one aa.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    The ratio describes the expected probabilities of the possible outcomes,
    not a guaranteed result for every small family.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Genotypic Ratio', 15),
  (802, 802, '
  <h2>Probability in Genetic Crosses</h2>

  <h3>What is probability?</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Probability</b> describes how likely an outcome is to occur.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a Punnett square, the number of boxes representing an outcome can be
    used to calculate its probability.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Consider:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The four possible genotype combinations are:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA, Aa, Aa, aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Probability of AA</h3>

  <p>
    AA occurs in 1 of the 4 boxes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>P(AA) = 1/4 = 25%</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Probability of Aa</h3>

  <p>
    Aa occurs in 2 of the 4 boxes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>P(Aa) = 2/4 = 1/2 = 50%</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Probability of aa</h3>

  <p>
    aa occurs in 1 of the 4 boxes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>P(aa) = 1/4 = 25%</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    These are probabilities for each conception/offspring, not guarantees
    about a fixed number of children.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Probability in Genetic Crosses', 15),
  (803, 803, '
  <h2>A Complete Monohybrid Cross</h2>

  <h3>Question</h3>

  <p>
    In a simple inheritance model, A is dominant over a.
    Two heterozygous parents are crossed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Find the possible offspring genotypes and phenotypic ratio.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Write the parental genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    Heterozygous means two different alleles.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    Each Aa parent can produce:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> or <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    Possible combinations:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA, Aa, Aa, aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Find the genotypic ratio</h3>

  <p>
    AA = 1
  </p>

  <p>
    Aa = 2
  </p>

  <p>
    aa = 1
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 5: Find the phenotypic ratio</h3>

  <p>
    AA and Aa show the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    aa shows the recessive phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>3 dominant : 1 recessive</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Final answer</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Genotypic ratio = 1 : 2 : 1</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Phenotypic ratio = 3 : 1</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for A Complete Monohybrid Cross', 15),
  (804, 804, '
  <h2>Cross Between Two Homozygous Dominant Parents</h2>

  <h3>Question</h3>

  <p>
    Let <b>A</b> be the dominant allele and <b>a</b> the recessive allele.
    Two homozygous dominant parents are crossed.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Write the parental genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    Homozygous dominant means two dominant alleles.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA × AA</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    An AA parent can only produce gametes carrying:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore both parents produce A gametes only.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    A + A = <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Every possible offspring therefore has genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Determine the phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    Since A is dominant, AA produces the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Conclusion</h3>

  <p>
    <b>All offspring are expected to be AA and show the dominant phenotype.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cross Between Two Homozygous Dominant Parents', 15),
  (805, 805, '
  <h2>Cross Between Two Homozygous Recessive Parents</h2>

  <h3>Question</h3>

  <p>
    Let <b>A</b> be the dominant allele and <b>a</b> the recessive allele.
    Two homozygous recessive parents are crossed.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Write the genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    Homozygous recessive means two recessive alleles.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa × aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    An aa parent can only produce gametes carrying:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    a + a = <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore every possible offspring genotype is:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Determine the phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    Because aa contains two recessive alleles, the recessive phenotype is
    expressed in the simple dominant-recessive model.
  </p>

  <h3>Conclusion</h3>

  <p>
    <b>All offspring are expected to be homozygous recessive and show the
    recessive phenotype.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cross Between Two Homozygous Recessive Parents', 15),
  (806, 806, '
  <h2>Cross Between Homozygous Dominant and Homozygous Recessive Parents</h2>

  <h3>Question</h3>

  <p>
    Let <b>A</b> be dominant and <b>a</b> be recessive.
    Cross a homozygous dominant parent with a homozygous recessive parent.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Write the parental genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    Homozygous dominant = <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Homozygous recessive = <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA × aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    AA produces only:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    aa produces only:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine them</h3>

  <p>
    Every offspring receives A from the first parent and a from the second.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A + a = Aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Determine the phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    Aa is heterozygous.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since A is dominant, the dominant phenotype is expressed.
  </p>

  <h3>Final result</h3>

  <p>
    <b>100% Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% dominant phenotype</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Notice that every offspring is heterozygous even though every offspring
    shows the dominant phenotype.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cross Between Homozygous Dominant and Homozygous Recessive Parents', 15),
  (807, 807, '
  <h2>Cross Between Homozygous Dominant and Heterozygous Parents</h2>

  <h3>Question</h3>

  <p>
    Let <b>A</b> be dominant and <b>a</b> be recessive.
    Cross:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    The AA parent can produce only:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The Aa parent can produce:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> or <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Combine the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    A from the first parent can combine with A:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A + A = AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    A from the first parent can combine with a:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A + a = Aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Count the genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    The expected possibilities are:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>50% AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>50% Aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Determine phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    Both AA and Aa express the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% dominant phenotype</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Key lesson</h3>

  <p>
    An offspring can have different genotypes while still showing the same
    phenotype.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cross Between Homozygous Dominant and Heterozygous Parents', 15),
  (808, 808, '
  <h2>Cross Between Homozygous Recessive and Heterozygous Parents</h2>

  <h3>Question</h3>

  <p>
    Let <b>A</b> be dominant and <b>a</b> be recessive.
    Cross:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    The aa parent can produce only:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The Aa parent can produce:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> or <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Combine the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    a + A = <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    a + a = <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Determine the expected proportions</h3>

  <p>
    <b>50% Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>50% aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Determine the phenotypes</h3>

  <p>
    Aa → dominant phenotype
  </p>

  <p>
    aa → recessive phenotype
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>50% dominant phenotype</b>
  </p>

