  <h3>Ratio</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Phenotypic ratio = 1 : 1</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cross Between Homozygous Recessive and Heterozygous Parents', 15),
  (809, 809, '
  <h2>Probability in AA × Aa</h2>

  <h3>The cross</h3>

  <p>
    Let A be dominant over a.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cross:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Gametes</h3>

  <p>
    AA → <b>A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Aa → <b>A or a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Possible offspring</h3>

  <p>
    A + A = <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    A + a = <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>P(AA) = 1/2 = 50%</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>P(Aa) = 1/2 = 50%</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Probability of aa</h3>

  <p>
    There is no way to produce aa.
  </p>

  <p>
    The first parent can only provide A.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>P(aa) = 0%</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Phenotype probability</h3>

  <p>
    Both AA and Aa have the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>P(dominant phenotype) = 100%</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>P(recessive phenotype) = 0%</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cross Between Homozygous Dominant and Heterozygous Parents: Probability', 15),
  (810, 810, '
  <h2>Comparing Crosses Between Homozygous Parents</h2>

  <h3>Case 1: AA × AA</h3>

  <p>
    Both parents can produce only A gametes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% dominant phenotype</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Case 2: aa × aa</h3>

  <p>
    Both parents can produce only a gametes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% recessive phenotype</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Case 3: AA × aa</h3>

  <p>
    The first parent provides A.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second parent provides a.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% dominant phenotype</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why are the outcomes so predictable?</h3>

  <p>
    A homozygous parent can produce only one allele type for the gene being
    studied.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, there are fewer possible combinations of gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Quick comparison</h3>

  <p>
    AA × AA → all AA
  </p>

  <p>
    aa × aa → all aa
  </p>

  <p>
    AA × aa → all Aa
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cross Between Two Homozygous Parents: Comparing Outcomes', 15),
  (811, 811, '
  <h2>Test Cross</h2>

  <h3>What problem does a test cross solve?</h3>

  <p>
    An individual showing a dominant phenotype can have either:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    or
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Looking at the phenotype alone cannot distinguish between these two
    genotypes in a simple dominant-recessive model.
  </p>

  <h3>The solution</h3>

  <p>
    The individual with the unknown genotype is crossed with a
    <b>homozygous recessive</b> individual.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is called a <b>test cross</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Why use aa?</h3>

  <p>
    The aa parent can contribute only the recessive allele <b>a</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the offspring results reveal which allele the unknown parent
    contributed.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose the unknown parent has a dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cross it with:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    If the unknown parent is <b>AA</b>:
  </p>

  <p>
    AA × aa → all <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    If the unknown parent is <b>Aa</b>:
  </p>

  <p>
    Aa × aa → <b>Aa</b> and <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    <b>A test cross uses a homozygous recessive individual to help determine
    the genotype of an individual showing a dominant phenotype.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Test Cross', 15),
  (812, 812, '
  <h2>Interpreting a Test Cross</h2>

  <h3>The unknown parent</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose an organism shows the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its genotype could be:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b> or <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    We cross it with:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Possible result 1: all dominant phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose all observed offspring have the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the unknown parent were AA:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA × aa → all Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Every offspring would show the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Possible result 2: recessive offspring appear</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose some offspring show the recessive phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    The unknown parent must have contributed a recessive allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the unknown parent must be:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Because:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × aa → Aa or aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Key inference</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Recessive offspring in a test cross prove that the dominant-phenotype
    parent carries the recessive allele.</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Important caution</h3>

  <p>
    In a real experiment, observing only a small number of offspring does not
    make probability an absolute guarantee. Larger samples give stronger
    evidence.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interpreting a Test Cross', 15),
  (813, 813, '
  <h2>Solving a Monohybrid Cross from a Written Question</h2>

  <h3>Question</h3>

  <p>
    In a simple inheritance model, allele B is dominant over allele b.
    A heterozygous individual is crossed with a homozygous recessive
    individual.
  </p>

  <p>
    Determine the possible genotypes and phenotypes of the offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Translate the words</h3>

  <p>
    Heterozygous means:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Bb</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Homozygous recessive means:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>bb</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Bb × bb</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    Bb → <b>B</b> or <b>b</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    bb → <b>b</b> only
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine them</h3>

  <p>
    B + b = <b>Bb</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    b + b = <b>bb</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Find the probabilities</h3>

  <p>
    <b>P(Bb) = 1/2 = 50%</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>P(bb) = 1/2 = 50%</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 5: Find the phenotypes</h3>

  <p>
    Bb → dominant phenotype
  </p>

  <p>
    bb → recessive phenotype
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>50% dominant phenotype and 50% recessive phenotype.</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Final answer</h3>

  <p>
    Genotypic ratio:
    <b>1 Bb : 1 bb</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Phenotypic ratio:
    <b>1 dominant : 1 recessive</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solving a Monohybrid Cross from a Written Question', 15),
  (814, 814, '
  <h2>Why Some Inheritance Patterns Differ from Complete Dominance</h2>

  <p>
    In a simple dominant-recessive inheritance pattern, one allele can completely
    mask the effect of another allele in a heterozygous individual.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if <b>A</b> is completely dominant over <b>a</b>, both
    <b>AA</b> and <b>Aa</b> have the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <h3>The important point</h3>

  <p>
    Not every gene follows this exact pattern. In some inheritance patterns,
    the heterozygous genotype produces a phenotype that is different from either
    homozygous genotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two important examples are <b>incomplete dominance</b> and
    <b>codominance</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not confuse genotype with phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    The genotype tells us the allele combination. The phenotype is the observable
    characteristic produced by that genotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, when solving an inheritance problem, first determine the genotype
    and then determine what phenotype that genotype produces under the particular
    inheritance pattern.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose <b>R</b> and <b>W</b> are two alleles and neither completely dominates
    the other.
  </p>

  <p>
    The heterozygous genotype is <b>RW</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike complete dominance, we cannot automatically say that RW has the same
    phenotype as RR or WW. We must know how the alleles interact.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    The inheritance rule determines how genotype is translated into phenotype.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Some Inheritance Patterns Differ from Complete Dominance', 15),
  (815, 815, '
  <h2>Incomplete Dominance</h2>

  <p>
    <b>Incomplete dominance</b> occurs when neither allele completely masks the
    other allele in a heterozygous individual.
  </p>

  <p>
    As a result, the heterozygote has a phenotype that is intermediate between
    the two homozygous phenotypes.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a flower has two alleles:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>R</b> = allele for red flowers</li>
    <li><b>W</b> = allele for white flowers</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Let:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>RR</b> = red</li>
    <li><b>WW</b> = white</li>
    <li><b>RW</b> = pink</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The important feature is that <b>RW is not red</b> and <b>RW is not white</b>.
    It has an intermediate phenotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>

  <p>
    In complete dominance, two different genotypes can produce the same
    phenotype. For example, AA and Aa can both be dominant.
  </p>

  <p>
    In this incomplete-dominance example, RR, RW and WW each produce different
    phenotypes.
  </p>

  <h3>Key distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Incomplete dominance does not mean that the two alleles physically blend
    together or disappear. The individual still carries two distinct alleles.
    The phenotype results from how those alleles are expressed in the
    heterozygote.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Incomplete Dominance', 15),
  (816, 816, '
  <h2>Solving an Incomplete Dominance Cross</h2>

  <p>
    The method for solving an incomplete-dominance cross is similar to a
    monohybrid cross. The important difference is how the genotypes are
    translated into phenotypes.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    In flowers:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>RR</b> = red</li>
    <li><b>RW</b> = pink</li>
    <li><b>WW</b> = white</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Cross a red flower with a white flower.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Write the parental genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    Red = <b>RR</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    White = <b>WW</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>RR × WW</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    RR can produce only <b>R</b> gametes.
  </p>

  <p>
    WW can produce only <b>W</b> gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine the alleles</h3>

  <p>
    Every offspring receives R from one parent and W from the other.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore every offspring is:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>RW</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Determine the phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    RW = pink.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% of the offspring are pink.</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Notice that the cross is solved using the same inheritance logic of
    separating parental alleles, but the phenotype rule is different.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solving an Incomplete Dominance Cross', 15),
  (817, 817, '
  <h2>Incomplete Dominance Ratios</h2>

  <p>
    In incomplete dominance, crossing two heterozygotes can produce three
    different phenotypes.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>RR</b> = red</li>
    <li><b>RW</b> = pink</li>
    <li><b>WW</b> = white</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Cross:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>RW × RW</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    Each RW parent can produce:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>R</b> or <b>W</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Complete the cross</h3>

  <p>
    The possible offspring are:
  </p>

  <p>
    RR, RW, RW, WW
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Count the genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    RR = 1
  </p>

  <p>
    RW = 2
  </p>

  <p>
    WW = 1
  </p>

  <p>
    Genotypic ratio:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>1 RR : 2 RW : 1 WW</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Convert to phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    RR = red
  </p>

  <p>
    RW = pink
  </p>

  <p>
    WW = white
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore the phenotypic ratio is also:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>1 red : 2 pink : 1 white</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    This differs from the 3:1 phenotypic ratio expected from a simple
    complete-dominance cross.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Incomplete Dominance Ratios', 15),
  (818, 818, '
  <h2>Codominance</h2>

  <p>
    <b>Codominance</b> occurs when two different alleles are both expressed
    clearly in a heterozygous individual.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is different from incomplete dominance.
  </p>

  <h3>Incomplete dominance</h3>

  <p>
    The heterozygote has an intermediate phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example:
    <b>RW = pink</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Codominance</h3>

  <p>
    Both alleles contribute to the phenotype at the same time.
  </p>

  <p>
    A classic human example is the ABO blood group system.
  </p>

  <p>
    The alleles <b>I<sup>A</sup></b> and <b>I<sup>B</sup></b> are codominant.
    When a person has both alleles, both are expressed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup></b> produces blood group AB.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not call AB a blend</h3>

  <p>
    Group AB is not an intermediate between A and B. Both A and B antigens are
    expressed on the red blood cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    In codominance, both alleles are expressed in the heterozygote.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Codominance', 15),
  (819, 819, '
  <h2>Solving a Codominance Cross</h2>

  <p>
    Codominance crosses are solved by separating the alleles into gametes,
    combining them, and then interpreting the resulting genotype.
  </p>

  <h3>ABO blood group example</h3>

  <p>
    The three main alleles are:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>I<sup>A</sup></b></li>
    <li><b>I<sup>B</sup></b></li>
    <li><b>i</b></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup></b> and <b>I<sup>B</sup></b> are codominant.
    Both are dominant over <b>i</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Cross</h3>

  <p>
    Consider:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>i × I<sup>B</sup>i</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    The first parent produces:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup></b> or <b>i</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The second parent produces:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>B</sup></b> or <b>i</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Combine them</h3>

  <p>
    Possible offspring:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup></li>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>i</li>
    <li>I<sup>B</sup>i</li>
    <li>ii</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 3: Determine the blood groups</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup> = AB</li>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>i = A</li>
    <li>I<sup>B</sup>i = B</li>
    <li>ii = O</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Therefore all four blood groups are possible in this particular cross.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solving a Codominance Cross', 15),
  (820, 820, '
  <h2>Incomplete Dominance Compared with Codominance</h2>

  <p>
    Incomplete dominance and codominance are often confused because both differ
    from simple complete dominance.
  </p>

  <h3>Incomplete dominance</h3>

  <p>
    The heterozygote has an intermediate phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example:
  </p>

  <p>
    RR = red
  </p>

  <p>
    WW = white
  </p>

  <p>
    RW = pink
  </p>

  <p>
    The heterozygote does not show either parental phenotype exactly.
  </p>

  <h3>Codominance</h3>

  <p>
    Both alleles are expressed in the heterozygote.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example:
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>A</sup> = A
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>B</sup>I<sup>B</sup> = B
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup> = AB
  </p>

  <p>
    In AB blood, both A and B antigens are expressed.
  </p>

  <h3>Quick comparison</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Incomplete dominance:</b> heterozygote shows an intermediate phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Codominance:</b> heterozygote expresses both allele effects.
  </p>

  <p>
    Neither pattern means that the DNA alleles physically merge into a single
    allele.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Incomplete Dominance Compared with Codominance', 15),
  (821, 821, '
  <h2>Multiple Alleles</h2>

  <p>
    A gene can sometimes exist in more than two alternative forms in a
    population. These alternative forms are called <b>alleles</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    When a gene has more than two possible alleles in the population, it is
    described as having <b>multiple alleles</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    An individual still receives only two alleles for a particular gene:
    one allele from each parent.
  </p>

  <p>
    Multiple alleles means that the population has more than two possible
    versions of the gene.
  </p>

  <h3>ABO blood group example</h3>

  <p>
    The ABO blood group system has three alleles:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup></li>
    <li>I<sup>B</sup></li>
    <li>i</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A person receives two of these alleles.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>A</sup>i
  </p>

  <p>
    The person has two alleles, not three.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Multiple alleles describe the possible alleles in the population, not the
    number of alleles carried by one individual.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Multiple Alleles', 15),
  (822, 822, '
  <h2>The Rhesus Factor</h2>

  <p>
    The <b>Rhesus factor</b>, commonly called the <b>Rh factor</b>, is another
    characteristic of human blood.
  </p>

  <p>
    A person is described as <b>Rhesus positive (Rh+)</b> if the relevant
    Rhesus antigen is present on the surface of their red blood cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    A person is described as <b>Rhesus negative (Rh−)</b> if that antigen is
    absent.
  </p>

  <h3>Simple genetic model</h3>

  <p>
    For basic inheritance problems, we can represent the Rh-positive allele as
    <b>R</b> and the Rh-negative allele as <b>r</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    In this simplified model:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>RR</b> = Rh+</li>
    <li><b>Rr</b> = Rh+</li>
    <li><b>rr</b> = Rh−</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Therefore, Rh+ is dominant over Rh− in this simplified model.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    The ABO blood group and the Rhesus factor are separate blood characteristics.
    A person''s complete blood type can therefore include both.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A positive</b> means ABO group A and Rh positive.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>O negative</b> means ABO group O and Rh negative.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Rhesus Factor', 15),
  (823, 823, '
  <h2>Rhesus Factor Genotypes</h2>

  <p>
    In the simplified inheritance model, the Rh-positive allele is represented
    by <b>R</b> and the Rh-negative allele by <b>r</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Rh positive</h3>

  <p>
    A person can be Rh positive with either:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>RR</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    or
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Rr</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Both genotypes contain at least one dominant R allele.
  </p>

  <h3>Rh negative</h3>

  <p>
    A person is Rh negative when the genotype is:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>rr</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Both alleles are recessive, so no dominant R allele is present.
  </p>

  <h3>Working backwards</h3>

  <p>
    If a person is known to be Rh negative, their genotype is certain in this
    model: <b>rr</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If a person is Rh positive, their genotype could be <b>RR</b> or <b>Rr</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, knowing the phenotype does not always tell us the exact genotype.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Rhesus Factor Genotypes', 15),
  (824, 824, '
  <h2>Rhesus Factor Cross: RR × rr</h2>

  <p>
    Consider a cross between an Rh-positive parent with genotype <b>RR</b>
    and an Rh-negative parent with genotype <b>rr</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Write the parental genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    <b>RR × rr</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    The RR parent can produce only <b>R</b> gametes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rr parent can produce only <b>r</b> gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine the alleles</h3>

  <p>
    Every offspring receives R from the first parent and r from the second.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore every offspring has:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Rr</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Determine the phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    Rr is Rh positive in the simplified model.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% of the offspring are predicted to be Rh positive.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Rhesus Factor Cross: RR × rr', 15),
  (825, 825, '
  <h2>Determining an Unknown Rhesus Genotype</h2>

  <p>
    An Rh-positive person can have genotype <b>RR</b> or <b>Rr</b>.
    Therefore, knowing that someone is Rh positive does not reveal their exact
    genotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Use offspring evidence</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose an Rh-positive parent has an Rh-negative child.
  </p>

  <p>
    An Rh-negative child must have genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>rr</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The child must therefore have received an <b>r</b> allele from each parent.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the Rh-positive parent contributed r, that parent must carry r.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore the Rh-positive parent must be:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Rr</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Reasoning chain</h3>

  <p>
    Rh-negative child → rr → receives r from both parents → Rh-positive parent
    supplied r → parent is Rr.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is an example of using offspring genotype to infer a parent''s genotype.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Determining an Unknown Rhesus Genotype', 15),
  (826, 826, '
  <h2>ABO Red Cell Compatibility</h2>

  <p>
    For red-cell transfusion, compatibility depends on the antigens carried by
    the donor''s red blood cells and the antibodies present in the recipient''s
    plasma.
  </p>

  <h3>Group O red cells</h3>

  <p>
    Group O red cells have neither A nor B antigen. Therefore they do not
    introduce A or B antigen to the recipient.
  </p>

  <h3>Group AB red cells</h3>

  <p>
    Group AB red cells carry both A and B antigens.
  </p>

  <p>
    A recipient whose plasma contains antibodies against A or B can react to
    those donor red-cell antigens.
  </p>

  <h3>Basic ABO red-cell rule</h3>

  <p>
    A recipient should not receive donor red cells carrying an ABO antigen
    against which the recipient has the corresponding antibody.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    A group B recipient has anti-A antibodies.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, giving group A red cells would introduce A antigen that can react
    with those anti-A antibodies.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why blood compatibility must be checked before transfusion.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for ABO Red Cell Compatibility', 15),
  (827, 827, '
  <h2>How Advantageous Alleles Become More Common</h2>

  <p>
    Natural selection can change the frequency of alleles in a population over
    generations.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a population contains two alleles for a characteristic:
    <b>A</b> and <b>a</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Suppose individuals with allele A have an inherited characteristic that
    increases survival in a particular environment.
  </p>

  <p>
    These individuals may survive and reproduce more successfully.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their offspring can inherit allele A.
  </p>

  <p>
    Over many generations, allele A may therefore become more common in the
    population.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    The allele does not become more common because individual organisms
    intentionally increase it.
  </p>

  <p>
    It becomes more common because individuals carrying it leave more
    surviving offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Population change</h3>

  <p>
    Natural selection acts on individuals, but its long-term result is a change
    in the genetic composition of the population.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for How Advantageous Alleles Become More Common', 15),
  (828, 828, '
  <h2>What Fossils Can Reveal</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Fossils can reveal information about organisms that lived in the past and
  allow scientists to compare organisms from different periods.
  </p>

  <h3>Comparing fossils</h3>

  <p>
  Suppose fossils from an older rock layer have a particular bone structure,
  while fossils from a younger layer have a modified version of the same
  structure.
  </p>

  <p>
  If the organisms are related and the layers have been correctly placed in
  chronological order, the differences can provide evidence of change over
  time.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  Fossil A has a small molar with a broad surface.
  </p>

  <p>
  Fossil B, found in a younger layer, has a larger molar with a different
  surface structure.
  </p>

  <p>
  Fossil C, from an even younger layer, has a still more modified molar.
  </p>

  <p>
  The sequence does not mean that Fossil A suddenly transformed into Fossil B.
  Instead, it provides evidence that characteristics in the lineage changed
  across generations.
  </p>

  <h3>What fossils cannot automatically tell us</h3>

  <p>
  A fossil usually gives only partial information. It may not preserve every
  structure of the organism or every stage between two forms.
  </p>

  <p>
  Therefore, fossils are interpreted together with other evidence.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Fossils Can Reveal', 15),
  (829, 829, '
  <h2>Limitations of the Fossil Record</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  The fossil record is valuable evidence for evolutionary change, but it is
  incomplete.
  </p>

  <h3>Why fossils are incomplete</h3>

  <p>
  Most organisms do not become fossils. Soft tissues usually decay, and
  organisms may be destroyed before fossilisation occurs.
  </p>

  <p>
  Fossil formation usually requires particular environmental conditions.
  Hard structures such as bones and shells are more likely to leave fossil
  evidence than soft tissues.
  </p>

  <h3>Missing stages</h3>

  <p>
  Because fossilisation is rare, there may be long gaps between fossils.
  Scientists may therefore not have fossils representing every generation
  in an evolutionary lineage.
  </p>

  <h3>Correct interpretation</h3>

  <p>
  The absence of a particular fossil does <b>not</b> automatically prove that
  the organism or intermediate form never existed.
  </p>

  <p>
  It may simply mean that suitable fossilisation conditions did not occur or
  that the fossil has not yet been discovered.
  </p>

  <h3>Why fossils remain useful</h3>

  <p>
  Even though incomplete, fossil evidence can still reveal major changes in
  organisms and the order in which those changes occurred.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Limitations of the Fossil Record', 15),
  (830, 830, '
  <h2>Homologous Structures</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Homologous structures</b> are structures found in different organisms
  that have a similar underlying structural plan because they were inherited
  from a common ancestor, even though they may perform different functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  The forelimbs of humans, bats and whales contain corresponding bones.
  </p>

  <p>
  A human forelimb is adapted for handling objects.
  A bat forelimb is adapted for flight.
  A whale forelimb is adapted for movement through water.
  </p>

  <p>
  Their functions differ, but their underlying arrangement of bones shows
  important similarities.
  </p>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>

  <p>
  If organisms have structures with similar underlying plans but different
  functions, one explanation is that the structures were inherited from a
  common ancestor and modified for different environments or functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
  Homologous structures are identified mainly by their <b>underlying
  structural similarity</b>, not simply because they perform the same
  function.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homologous Structures', 15),
  (831, 831, '
  <h2>Vestigial Structures</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  A <b>vestigial structure</b> is a reduced structure that has little or no
  significant function in its present form compared with the corresponding
  structure in ancestral organisms or related organisms.
  </p>

  <h3>How vestigial structures arise</h3>

  <p>
  An inherited structure may become less useful when the environment or way
  of life changes.
  </p>

  <p>
  If individuals with reduced versions of the structure have no major
  disadvantage, natural selection may not maintain the structure in its
  original form.
  </p>

  <p>
  Over many generations, the structure may become reduced.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  The human appendix is commonly discussed as a reduced structure. It should
  not be described simply as completely useless; it has biological functions,
  but it is greatly reduced compared with the large caecum used in digestion
  by some herbivorous mammals.
  </p>

  <h3>Evolutionary significance</h3>

  <p>
  Vestigial structures can provide evidence of evolutionary history because
  they may represent modified remnants of structures that were more important
  in ancestors.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Vestigial Structures', 15),
  (832, 832, '
  <h2>Comparing Homologous and Vestigial Structures</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Homologous and vestigial structures can both provide evidence about
  evolutionary relationships, but they are not the same concept.
  </p>

  <h3>Homologous structures</h3>

  <p>
  These have a similar underlying structural plan in different organisms.
  Their functions may differ.
  </p>

  <p>
  Example: the forelimbs of humans, bats and whales.
  </p>

  <h3>Vestigial structures</h3>

  <p>
  These are reduced structures that have little or no significant function
  in their present form compared with the corresponding ancestral structure.
  </p>

  <p>
  Example: the human appendix compared with the much larger caecum of some
  herbivorous mammals.
  </p>

  <h3>The key difference</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Homologous</b> focuses on a shared underlying structural plan among
  different organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Vestigial</b> focuses on reduction of a structure compared with its
  ancestral or corresponding form.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not confuse them</h3>

  <p>
  A structure does not become homologous simply because it is small.
  Likewise, a structure is not vestigial simply because different organisms
  use it for different purposes.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Homologous and Vestigial Structures', 15),
  (833, 833, '
<h2>Genetic Diversity</h2>

<p>
<b>Genetic diversity</b> is the variation in inherited characteristics among individuals of the same species.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider a population of maize plants.
</p>

<p>
Some plants may produce larger cobs, while others produce smaller cobs. Some may have greater resistance to a particular disease, while others may be more susceptible.
</p>

<p>
Some of these differences can result from differences in their genes.
</p>

<h3>Why genetic diversity matters</h3>

<p>
A population with genetic variation is more likely to contain individuals with characteristics that may help them survive when environmental conditions change.
</p>

<p>
For example, if a disease affects a crop, individuals with inherited resistance may survive and reproduce more successfully.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
Genetic diversity is different from environmental variation.
</p>

<p>
A difference caused only by the environment is not necessarily an inherited genetic difference.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, when identifying genetic diversity, focus on <b>inherited differences</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Genetic Diversity', 15),
  (834, 834, '
  <h2>Variation in Sexual Reproduction</h2>

  <h3>What is variation?</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Variation</b> means differences in characteristics between individuals
    of the same species.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, people can differ in height, blood group, natural eye colour,
    hair characteristics and many other features.
  </p>

  <h3>Why does sexual reproduction produce variation?</h3>

  <p>
    Sexual reproduction involves two parents.
    Each parent contributes a gamete to the offspring.
  </p>

  <p>
    The sperm contributes one set of chromosomes and the egg contributes
    another set.
  </p>

  <p>
    The combination formed at fertilisation is therefore different from the
    combination found in either parent.
  </p>

  <h3>Step-by-step</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Meiosis produces haploid gametes.</li>
    <li>Different gametes can contain different combinations of genetic information.</li>
    <li>A sperm and an egg meet during fertilisation.</li>
    <li>Their genetic information is combined.</li>
    <li>A genetically unique combination is formed in the zygote.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>

  <p>
    Imagine a mother produces egg A and egg B, while a father produces
    sperm X and sperm Y.
  </p>

  <p>
    Possible fertilisations include:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A + X</b><br>
    <b>A + Y</b><br>
    <b>B + X</b><br>
    <b>B + Y</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    These combinations are not necessarily genetically identical.
    Therefore, different fertilisation events can produce offspring with
    different genetic combinations.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Variation does not mean that every individual has completely different
    genes. Individuals of the same species share many genes, but they can
    possess different versions of genes and different combinations of genetic
    information.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Variation in Sexual Reproduction', 15),
  (835, 835, '
  <h2>Genetic Variation from Fertilisation</h2>

  <h3>What happens during fertilisation?</h3>

  <p>
    Fertilisation occurs when a male gamete and a female gamete fuse.
  </p>

  <p>
    In humans, this means a haploid sperm cell combines with a haploid egg
    cell to form a diploid zygote.
  </p>

  <h3>Why does this create variation?</h3>

  <p>
    A large number of sperm cells can be produced, and they do not all carry
    exactly the same genetic combination.
  </p>

  <p>
    Egg cells can also differ genetically.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the particular sperm that fertilises the particular egg affects
    the genetic combination of the resulting zygote.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose three sperm cells have combinations:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Sperm 1 = AB</b><br>
    <b>Sperm 2 = Ab</b><br>
    <b>Sperm 3 = aB</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Suppose an egg carries <b>ab</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If Sperm 1 fertilises the egg, the combination is different from the
    combination produced if Sperm 2 fertilises the same egg.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the identity of the sperm involved in fertilisation affects
    the genetic combination of the offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Fertilisation is random with respect to which sperm and egg meet, so it
    creates another source of genetic variation.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Genetic Variation from Fertilisation', 15),
  (836, 836, '
  <h2>Meiosis and Genetic Variation</h2>

  <h3>How are meiosis and variation connected?</h3>

  <p>
    Meiosis is not simply a process for reducing chromosome number.
    It also contributes to genetic variation.
  </p>

  <p>
    During meiosis, homologous chromosomes are separated into different cells.
    Their different orientations can result in different combinations of
    chromosomes entering gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Step-by-step connection</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Homologous chromosomes pair during meiosis I.</li>
    <li>The chromosome pairs line up at the equator.</li>
    <li>The orientation of the pairs can differ.</li>
    <li>Homologous chromosomes separate.</li>
    <li>Different chromosome combinations enter the resulting cells.</li>
    <li>Meiosis ultimately produces haploid gametes.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Then fertilisation adds another source</h3>

  <p>
    The genetic variation produced through meiosis is not the end of the
    process.
  </p>

  <p>
    During fertilisation, a sperm combines with an egg.
  </p>

  <p>
    Different sperm and eggs can therefore combine in different ways.
  </p>

  <h3>The complete idea</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Meiosis creates different combinations in gametes.</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Fertilisation combines genetic information from two gametes.</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Together, these processes contribute strongly to genetic variation in
    sexually reproducing organisms.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meiosis and Genetic Variation', 15),
  (837, 837, '
  <h2>Genetic Variation and Environmental Variation</h2>

  <h3>Not every difference is caused by genes</h3>

  <p>
    Individuals can differ because of their genes, because of environmental
    conditions, or because of a combination of both.
  </p>

  <h3>Genetic variation</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Genetic variation</b> refers to differences in genetic information
    between individuals.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, differences in blood group are genetically determined.
  </p>

  <h3>Environmental variation</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Environmental variation</b> results from differences in the conditions
    in which organisms develop or live.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, two plants with similar genetic potential can grow to
    different sizes if one receives enough water and mineral nutrients while
    the other does not.
  </p>

  <h3>Characteristics affected by both</h3>

  <p>
    Some characteristics are influenced by both genes and the environment.
  </p>

  <p>
    Human height, for example, is influenced by inherited genetic information
    as well as factors such as nutrition and health during development.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked classification</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Blood group:</b> mainly genetic.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Plant height in different nutrient conditions:</b> environmental
    conditions can affect the observed height.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Human height:</b> influenced by both genetic and environmental factors.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>A visible difference between two individuals does not automatically
    mean that their genes are different.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Genetic Variation and Environmental Variation', 15),
  (838, 838, '
  <h2>Inherited Variation and Acquired Differences</h2>

  <h3>What is inherited variation?</h3>

  <p>
    An inherited characteristic is one whose genetic information is passed
    from parents to offspring.
  </p>

  <p>
    Such characteristics can therefore be transmitted through reproduction.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Blood group is an inherited characteristic.
  </p>

  <p>
    A child receives genetic information associated with blood group from
    their parents.
  </p>

  <h3>What is an acquired difference?</h3>

  <p>
    An acquired difference develops during an individual''s lifetime because
    of experiences, environmental conditions, training, injury, diet or
    other factors.
  </p>

  <p>
    An acquired difference is not automatically passed genetically to the
    individual''s offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    If a person develops strong muscles through exercise, the increased muscle
    size is an acquired change.
  </p>

  <p>
    The person does not pass the increased muscle size directly to a child
    through their gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Inherited:</b> associated with genetic information passed from parents
    to offspring.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Acquired:</b> develops during an individual''s lifetime and is not
    automatically inherited genetically.
  </p>

  <h3>Exam trap</h3>

  <p>
    Do not assume that every characteristic seen in a parent will be inherited
    by a child.
  </p>

  <p>
    A parent may acquire a characteristic during life without changing the
    genetic information passed to the offspring.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Inherited Variation and Acquired Differences', 15),
  (839, 839, '
  <h2>Sources of Genetic Variation</h2>

  <h3>There is more than one source</h3>

  <p>
    Genetic variation in sexually reproducing organisms can arise from several
    processes.
  </p>

  <p>
    At this stage, focus on the two major processes already established:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Different combinations of chromosomes can arise during meiosis.</li>
    <li>Different sperm and egg cells can combine during fertilisation.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Source 1: Meiosis</h3>

  <p>
    During meiosis, homologous chromosome pairs can be arranged in different
    orientations.
  </p>

  <p>
    This contributes to different combinations of chromosomes entering
    gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Source 2: Fertilisation</h3>

  <p>
    Fertilisation involves the union of one sperm and one egg.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because many different sperm and egg cells can be involved, different
    combinations can be produced in different offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Putting them together</h3>

  <p>
    Imagine:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Meiosis → different gametes</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    followed by:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Fertilisation → combination of two gametes</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The result is an offspring with a particular genetic combination.
  </p>

  <h3>Exam-ready conclusion</h3>

  <p>
    Genetic variation in sexual reproduction is contributed to by the
    production of genetically different gametes during meiosis and by the
    combination of gametes during fertilisation.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sources of Genetic Variation', 15),
  (840, 840, '
  <h2>Phenotypic Ratio</h2>

  <h3>What is a phenotypic ratio?</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>phenotypic ratio</b> compares the numbers of different observable
    phenotypes expected from a genetic cross.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> = dominant allele<br>
    <b>a</b> = recessive allele
  </p>

  <p>
    Cross:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    The Punnett square gives:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA, Aa, Aa, aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Convert genotype to phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    In the simple dominant-recessive model:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b> → dominant phenotype
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b> → dominant phenotype
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b> → recessive phenotype
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Count the phenotypes</h3>

  <p>
    Dominant phenotype appears 3 times.
  </p>

  <p>
    Recessive phenotype appears 1 time.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Phenotypic ratio = 3 : 1</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Do not confuse:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Genotypic ratio = 1 : 2 : 1</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    with:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Phenotypic ratio = 3 : 1</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Phenotypic Ratio', 15),
  (841, 841, '
  <h2>What Is Variation?</h2>

  <p>
    <b>Variation</b> means differences in characteristics between individuals
    of the same species.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, people of the same species can differ in height, blood group,
    skin pigmentation, fingerprints and many other characteristics.
  </p>

  <h3>Variation within a species</h3>

  <p>
    Members of a species have many characteristics in common, but they are not
    necessarily identical.
  </p>

  <p>
    The differences between individuals are described as variation.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Imagine a class of 40 students.
  </p>

  <p>
    The students belong to the same species, but they may have different heights.
  </p>

  <p>
    Those differences in height represent variation.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Variation does not mean that every individual is completely different.
    Individuals share many characteristics because they belong to the same
    species.
  </p>

  <p>
    Variation simply describes differences in characteristics among individuals.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Variation?', 15),
  (842, 842, '
  <h2>Genetic Causes of Variation</h2>

  <p>
    Some variation is caused by differences in the genetic information inherited
    by individuals.
  </p>

  <p>
    Genes contain information that influences characteristics.
  </p>

  <p>
    Different versions of a gene are called <b>alleles</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>How inheritance creates differences</h3>

  <p>
    A child receives genetic material from both parents.
  </p>

  <p>
    The particular combination of alleles received by the child may differ from
    the combinations received by their brothers or sisters.
  </p>

  <p>
    This can produce genetic differences between individuals.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a gene has two alleles, A and a.
  </p>

  <p>
    One child might inherit:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Another child might inherit:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The two children therefore have different genotypes.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Genetic variation is variation caused by differences in genetic information.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Genetic Causes of Variation', 15),
  (843, 843, '
  <h2>Environmental Causes of Variation</h2>

  <p>
    Some differences between individuals are caused by the environment rather
    than differences in their genes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <b>environment</b> includes external conditions that affect an
    organism.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>
    The amount of sunlight a plant receives can affect its growth.
  </p>

  <p>
    The amount of water and mineral nutrients available can also affect plant
    growth.
  </p>

  <p>
    In humans, diet and physical activity can influence characteristics such as
    body mass.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    A characteristic influenced by the environment is not automatically caused
    by a change in DNA.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if two genetically similar plants receive different amounts of
    water, the plants may grow to different sizes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The difference in size can therefore be partly environmental.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Environmental variation results from differences in the conditions
    individuals experience.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Environmental Causes of Variation', 15),
  (844, 844, '
  <h2>Genetic and Environmental Variation Together</h2>

  <p>
    Many characteristics are influenced by both <b>genes</b> and the
    <b>environment</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: height</h3>

  <p>
    Genes influence a person''s potential for growth.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, environmental factors such as nutrition and health can also affect
    how much a person grows.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the final characteristic can result from an interaction between
    inherited genetic information and environmental conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>Think of it this way</h3>

  <p>
    Genes provide biological instructions that influence development.
  </p>

  <p>
    The environment affects the conditions under which development occurs.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, two individuals with similar genetic potential can still show
    differences if their environments differ.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Variation can have more than one cause.
  </p>

  <p>
    A characteristic should not automatically be classified as either
    completely genetic or completely environmental.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Genetic and Environmental Variation Together', 15),
  (845, 845, '
  <h2>Continuous Variation</h2>

  <p>
    <b>Continuous variation</b> occurs when a characteristic can take a range
    of values between two extremes, with many possible intermediate values.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Human height shows continuous variation.
  </p>

  <p>
    People are not limited to only a few possible heights. There can be many
    values between shorter and taller individuals.
  </p>

  <h3>Measuring continuous variation</h3>

  <p>
    A continuous characteristic can usually be measured using a numerical scale.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>150 cm</li>
    <li>151 cm</li>
    <li>152 cm</li>
    <li>153 cm</li>
    <li>and many other values</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The values form a continuous range rather than a small number of separate
    categories.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Continuous variation produces a range of measurements with many possible
    intermediate values.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Continuous Variation', 15),
  (846, 846, '
  <h2>Discontinuous Variation</h2>

  <p>
    <b>Discontinuous variation</b> occurs when a characteristic falls into
    distinct categories rather than a continuous range.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    The ABO blood group system has distinct categories:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>A</li>
    <li>B</li>
    <li>AB</li>
    <li>O</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A person has one of these blood groups rather than a blood group somewhere
    between A and B.
  </p>

  <h3>Another example</h3>

  <p>
    Rhesus status can be classified as:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Rh positive</li>
    <li>Rh negative</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These are distinct categories.
  </p>

  <h3>Key distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Continuous variation gives a range of values.
  </p>

  <p>
    Discontinuous variation gives separate categories.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Discontinuous Variation', 15),
  (847, 847, '
  <h2>Comparing Continuous and Discontinuous Variation</h2>

  <p>
    The easiest way to distinguish continuous and discontinuous variation is
    to ask whether the characteristic forms a range or separate categories.
  </p>

  <h3>Continuous variation</h3>

  <p>
    There are many possible intermediate values.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example: human height.
  </p>

  <h3>Discontinuous variation</h3>

  <p>
    Individuals fall into distinct categories.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example: ABO blood group.
  </p>

  <h3>Comparison</h3>

  <p>
    Height:
  </p>

  <p>
    150 cm → 151 cm → 152 cm → 153 cm → many possible values.
  </p>

  <p>
    Blood group:
  </p>

  <p>
    A | B | AB | O
  </p>

  <p>
    There is no ordinary ABO category halfway between A and B.
  </p>

  <h3>Exam clue</h3>

  <p>
    If a question gives a measurement with many possible intermediate values,
    think <b>continuous variation</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If it gives clearly separated categories, think <b>discontinuous variation</b>.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Continuous and Discontinuous Variation', 15),
  (848, 848, '
<h2>Mean, Range and Variation in Ecological Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ecological investigations often produce several measurements.
Simple statistical measures help researchers summarise the data.
</p>

<h3>MEAN</h3>

<p>
The <strong>mean</strong> is calculated by adding all values and dividing by
the number of values.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Mean = total of values ÷ number of values</strong>
</p>

<h3>RANGE</h3>

<p>
The <strong>range</strong> shows the difference between the largest and
smallest values.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Range = largest value − smallest value</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Plant counts from five quadrats are:
</p>

<p>
6, 8, 10, 7, 9
</p>

<p>
<strong>Mean:</strong></p>

<p>
(6 + 8 + 10 + 7 + 9) ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= 40 ÷ 5
</p>

<p>
= <strong>8</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>Range:</strong></p>

<p>
10 − 6 = <strong>4</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHY THESE VALUES HELP</h3>

<p>
The mean gives a measure of the typical value.
The range gives an indication of how spread out the observations are.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mean, Range and Variation in Ecological Data', 15),
  (849, 849, '
  <h2>What Is a Mutation?</h2>

  <p>
    A <b>mutation</b> is a change in the DNA sequence.
  </p>

  <p>
    DNA contains genetic information. A change in its sequence can therefore
    alter genetic information.
  </p>

  <h3>DNA sequence</h3>

  <p>
    DNA is made from bases arranged in a particular sequence.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, consider a simplified sequence:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A C G T A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    If one base changes, for example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A C A T A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    the DNA sequence has changed.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    A mutation does not automatically mean that an organism becomes unhealthy
    or that the mutation produces a visible characteristic.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mutations can have different effects depending on where the DNA change
    occurs and how it affects the resulting gene product.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Mutation = change in DNA sequence.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is a Mutation?', 15),
  (850, 850, '
  <h2>Causes of Mutation</h2>

  <p>
    Mutations can arise when DNA is copied incorrectly or when DNA is damaged
    by certain environmental agents.
  </p>

  <h3>Spontaneous changes</h3>

  <p>
    DNA replication is highly accurate, but errors can occasionally occur.
    Some errors may remain if they are not corrected.
  </p>

  <h3>Mutagens</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>mutagen</b> is an agent that increases the likelihood of mutations.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include certain forms of radiation and some chemicals.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Radiation does not mean that every exposed cell will definitely develop a
    mutation. It can increase the probability of DNA damage and mutation.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Mutations can arise naturally through occasional DNA-copying errors and can
    also be promoted by mutagens.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Causes of Mutation', 15),
  (851, 851, '
  <h2>Effects of Mutations</h2>

  <p>
    A mutation can have different effects depending on the DNA sequence
    affected and how that change influences the organism.
  </p>

  <h3>Possible effects</h3>

  <p>
    A mutation may have little or no noticeable effect.
  </p>

  <p>
    A mutation may alter the function of a gene product and affect a
    characteristic.
  </p>

  <p>
    In some cases, a mutation can contribute to disease.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some mutations can also produce characteristics that may be advantageous
    under particular environmental conditions.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not assume every mutation is harmful</h3>

  <p>
    The word mutation simply describes a change in DNA sequence.
  </p>

  <p>
    It does not by itself tell us whether the effect will be harmful,
    beneficial or neutral.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Imagine a mutation changes a DNA sequence but does not alter the final
    protein''s function.
  </p>

  <p>
    The mutation may have no obvious effect on the organism.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    The effect of a mutation depends on the change and its biological
    consequences.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effects of Mutations', 15),
  (852, 852, '
  <h2>Mutations and Genetic Variation</h2>

  <p>
    Mutations can introduce new versions of genes into a population.
  </p>

  <p>
    A mutation can change the DNA sequence of a gene and potentially produce a
    new allele.
  </p>

  <h3>Step-by-step connection</h3>

  <p>
    <b>1.</b> A mutation changes a DNA sequence.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>2.</b> The changed sequence can represent a different version of a gene.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>3.</b> That different version is an allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>4.</b> If the allele is inherited and persists in a population, it can
    contribute to genetic variation.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Not every mutation contributes to variation between future generations.
    For a mutation to be passed to offspring, it must occur in genetic material
    that contributes to the offspring.
  </p>

  <p>
    A mutation in an ordinary body cell is generally not inherited by the
    organism''s children.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Mutation is one source of new genetic variation.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mutations and Genetic Variation', 15),
  (853, 853, '
  <h2>What Is Natural Selection?</h2>

  <p>
    <b>Natural selection</b> is the process in which individuals with
    characteristics that help them survive and reproduce in a particular
    environment are more likely to pass those characteristics to the next
    generation.
  </p>

  <h3>Start with variation</h3>

  <p>
    Individuals in a population are not genetically identical. They can have
    different characteristics.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some of these characteristics may give an individual an advantage in a
    particular environment.
  </p>

  <h3>Selection</h3>

  <p>
    Individuals with advantageous inherited characteristics are more likely to
    survive and reproduce.
  </p>

  <p>
    Individuals with less advantageous characteristics may be less likely to
    survive or reproduce successfully.
  </p>

  <h3>Important point</h3>

  <p>
    The environment does not deliberately choose which individual survives.
    Instead, environmental conditions create different chances of survival and
    reproduction.
  </p>

  <h3>Basic sequence</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Variation → different survival/reproductive success → inheritance →
    change in the population over generations.</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Natural selection therefore acts on existing variation within populations.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Natural Selection?', 15),
  (854, 854, '
  <h2>Selection Pressure</h2>

  <p>
    A <b>selection pressure</b> is a factor in the environment that affects
    which individuals are more likely to survive and reproduce.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Predators</li>
    <li>Disease</li>
    <li>Competition for food</li>
    <li>Competition for mates</li>
    <li>Limited water</li>
    <li>Extreme temperature</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Imagine a population of insects living on plants.
  </p>

  <p>
    Birds feed on the insects.
  </p>

  <p>
    Being easily seen by birds creates a disadvantage, while being difficult to
    detect may increase the chance of survival.
  </p>

  <p>
    In this example, predation is a selection pressure.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    A selection pressure does not create a useful characteristic because an
    organism needs it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Instead, it affects which individuals with existing variation are more
    likely to survive and reproduce.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Selection Pressure', 15),
  (855, 855, '
  <h2>Variation and Survival</h2>

  <p>
    Natural selection requires variation between individuals.
  </p>

  <p>
    If individuals have different inherited characteristics, environmental
    conditions may affect them differently.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a population of beetles contains green and brown individuals.
  </p>

  <p>
    Birds can easily see the green beetles on brown soil but have more difficulty
    seeing the brown beetles.
  </p>

  <p>
    The brown beetles are therefore more likely to escape predation in this
    environment.
  </p>

  <p>
    If their brown coloration is inherited, the surviving beetles are more
    likely to pass the allele associated with that characteristic to their
    offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Important reasoning</h3>

  <p>
    The beetles did not change their colour because they wanted to escape the
    birds.
  </p>

  <p>
    The variation already existed, and the environment affected which beetles
    were more likely to survive.
  </p>

  <h3>Core chain</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Existing variation → different survival → different reproduction →
    inherited differences passed on.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Variation and Survival', 15),
  (856, 856, '
  <h2>Survival of Better-Adapted Individuals</h2>

  <p>
    An individual is <b>better adapted</b> to an environment when its inherited
    characteristics increase its ability to survive and reproduce in that
    environment.
  </p>

  <h3>Adaptation is relative to the environment</h3>

  <p>
    A characteristic that is advantageous in one environment may not provide the
    same advantage in another environment.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Thick fur can help an animal reduce heat loss in a cold environment.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same thick fur may be disadvantageous in a very hot environment because
    it can make heat loss more difficult.
  </p>

  <h3>Fitness</h3>

  <p>
    In evolution, <b>fitness</b> refers to reproductive success: how effectively
    an individual survives and contributes offspring to the next generation.
  </p>

  <p>
    It does not simply mean being physically strong.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Natural selection favours characteristics that increase reproductive success
    under the conditions of that environment.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Survival of Better-Adapted Individuals', 15),
  (857, 857, '
  <h2>Reproduction and Natural Selection</h2>

  <p>
    Survival alone is not enough for a characteristic to become more common in
    future generations.
  </p>

  <p>
    An individual must also reproduce and pass inherited genetic information to
    offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Variation</h3>

  <p>
    Individuals have different characteristics.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Selection pressure</h3>

  <p>
    Environmental conditions affect which individuals are more likely to survive.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Reproduction</h3>

  <p>
    Individuals that survive and reproduce contribute their genetic information
    to the next generation.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Inheritance</h3>

  <p>
    If the advantageous characteristic is inherited, offspring can receive the
    associated allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, reproduction connects survival of individuals with changes in
    populations over generations.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Natural selection changes populations because differences in survival and
    reproduction affect which inherited characteristics are passed to future
    generations.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reproduction and Natural Selection', 15),
  (858, 858, '
  <h2>Natural Selection in Bacteria</h2>

  <p>
    Bacterial populations can contain genetic variation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some bacteria may carry a mutation that gives resistance to a particular
    antibiotic.
  </p>

  <h3>Before antibiotic treatment</h3>

  <p>
    The population may contain both susceptible bacteria and bacteria carrying
    resistance.
  </p>

  <h3>During treatment</h3>

  <p>
    The antibiotic creates a selection pressure.
  </p>

  <p>
    Susceptible bacteria are more likely to die.
  </p>

  <p>
    Resistant bacteria are more likely to survive.
  </p>

  <h3>After treatment</h3>

  <p>
    The surviving resistant bacteria reproduce.
  </p>

  <p>
    As generations continue, the proportion of resistant bacteria can increase.
  </p>

  <h3>Critical misconception</h3>

  <p>
    The antibiotic does not normally cause each bacterium to deliberately
    develop resistance because it needs to survive.
  </p>

  <p>
    Instead, bacteria with resistance already present are more likely to survive
    the selection pressure and reproduce.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Natural Selection in Bacteria', 15),
  (859, 859, '
  <h2>Adaptation Through Natural Selection</h2>

  <p>
    An <b>adaptation</b> is an inherited characteristic that increases an
    organism''s ability to survive and reproduce in a particular environment.
  </p>

  <h3>How adaptation develops in a population</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Individuals show variation.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Some inherited characteristics provide an advantage under
    particular environmental conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> Individuals with those characteristics are more likely to
    survive and reproduce.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 4:</b> Their offspring can inherit the advantageous characteristics.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 5:</b> Over many generations, the advantageous characteristic can
    become more common in the population.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose some insects have an inherited colour that makes them difficult for
    predators to see.
  </p>

  <p>
    If these insects survive and reproduce more successfully, the allele
    associated with the colour may become more common over generations.
  </p>

  <p>
    The population can therefore become better adapted to that environment.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Individual organisms do not evolve because they need to.
    <b>Populations change over generations.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adaptation Through Natural Selection', 15),
  (860, 860, '
  <h2>Natural Selection: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

  <p>
    Consider a population of insects containing two inherited colour
    characteristics.
  </p>

  <p>
    Birds can easily detect one colour but have difficulty detecting the other
    colour against the background.
  </p>

  <p>
    The birds eat more of the easily detected insects.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Identify the variation</h3>

  <p>
    The insects have different inherited colours.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Identify the selection pressure</h3>

  <p>
    Predation by birds is the selection pressure.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Identify the advantageous characteristic</h3>

  <p>
    The less visible colour gives an advantage because predators have greater
    difficulty detecting those insects.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Explain survival</h3>

  <p>
    Less visible insects are more likely to survive predation.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 5: Explain reproduction</h3>

  <p>
    The surviving insects reproduce and pass their inherited alleles to
    offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 6: Explain population change</h3>

  <p>
    Over many generations, the allele associated with the less visible colour
    can become more common in the population.
  </p>

  <h3>Complete answer</h3>

  <p>
    Variation exists in the population. Predation creates a selection pressure.
    Less visible insects are more likely to survive and reproduce. If the colour
    is inherited, their offspring can inherit the associated allele. Over many
    generations, that allele can become more common.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Natural Selection: Complete Reasoning Problem', 15),
  (861, 861, '
  <h2>What Is Evolution?</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Evolution</b> is the change in the inherited characteristics of a population
  over many generations.
  </p>

  <p>
  The important word is <b>population</b>. An individual organism does not evolve
  during its lifetime. Evolution occurs when the characteristics or allele
  frequencies of a population change across generations.
  </p>

  <h3>How evolution happens</h3>

  <p>
  A population contains variation. Some of this variation is inherited.
  Environmental conditions can cause some inherited characteristics to give
  their carriers an advantage in survival or reproduction.
  </p>

  <p>
  If those organisms reproduce more successfully, the alleles responsible for
  the advantageous characteristics can become more common in later generations.
  </p>

  <p>
  Therefore, natural selection can cause evolutionary change.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  Imagine a population of beetles containing green and brown individuals.
  Birds can easily see the green beetles on brown soil.
  </p>

  <p>
  If colour is inherited, brown beetles may survive more often and reproduce
  more successfully. Their offspring are more likely to inherit the brown
  characteristic.
  </p>

  <p>
  After many generations, brown beetles may make up a larger proportion of
  the population.
  </p>

  <p>
  The population has evolved.
  </p>

  <h3>Key chain</h3>

  <p>
  Variation → selection pressure → different survival and reproduction →
  inheritance → population changes over generations.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Evolution?', 15),
  (862, 862, '
  <h2>Evolution Occurs Over Generations</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Evolution is usually a gradual population-level process. It becomes visible
  when inherited characteristics change in frequency across generations.
  </p>

  <h3>Generation 1</h3>

  <p>
  Suppose a population contains 80 insects:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>40 are green.</li>
    <li>40 are brown.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
  Birds can see green insects more easily on brown tree bark.
  </p>

  <h3>Selection</h3>

  <p>
  More green insects are eaten. More brown insects survive and reproduce.
  </p>

  <h3>Generation 2</h3>

  <p>
  The surviving brown insects produce many offspring. If the colour
  characteristic is inherited, more offspring are likely to be brown.
  </p>

  <p>
  The proportion of brown insects therefore increases.
  </p>

  <h3>Generation 3 and beyond</h3>

  <p>
  If the same selection pressure continues, the inherited characteristic
  associated with better survival can become increasingly common.
  </p>

  <p>
  No single insect changed from green to brown because it needed to survive.
  The population changed because different individuals survived and reproduced
  at different rates.
  </p>

  <h3>The critical distinction</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Incorrect:</b> The insects changed their colour because they needed to
  survive.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Correct:</b> Variation already existed, and the environment favoured
  individuals with one inherited characteristic.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evolution Occurs Over Generations', 15),
  (863, 863, '
  <h2>Fossils as Evidence for Evolution</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  A <b>fossil</b> is preserved evidence of an organism or its activity from
  the past.
  </p>

  <p>
  Fossils can include preserved bones, shells, teeth, impressions, tracks and
  other remains or traces.
  </p>

  <h3>Why fossils matter</h3>

  <p>
  Fossils provide evidence about organisms that lived in the past.
  Scientists can compare fossils from different periods and observe how
  populations and groups of organisms changed over time.
  </p>

  <p>
  For example, fossils may show that an older form of an organism had
  characteristics that differ from those of later forms.
  </p>

  <h3>Using fossils to investigate change</h3>

  <p>
  Imagine fossils from three different geological periods:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Period A: organism has a short jaw.</li>
    <li>Period B: organism has a longer jaw.</li>
    <li>Period C: organism has an even longer jaw.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
  If the fossils are correctly dated and belong to related populations,
  the sequence provides evidence that the characteristics of the group
  changed over time.
  </p>

  <p>
  Fossils therefore provide evidence that life has changed throughout Earth''s
  history.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fossils as Evidence for Evolution', 15),
  (864, 864, '
  <h2>Homologous Structures as Evidence for Evolution</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Homologous structures can provide evidence that different organisms share
  a common ancestor.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the structural similarity</h3>

  <p>
  Scientists compare structures in different organisms and identify
  corresponding parts.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Compare their functions</h3>

  <p>
  The structures may perform different functions.
  </p>

  <p>
  For example, the forelimbs of humans, bats and whales have corresponding
  bones but are adapted for different uses.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Consider inheritance</h3>

  <p>
  A similar underlying structure suggests that the organisms may have
  inherited that structural plan from a common ancestor.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Modification</h3>

  <p>
  Over many generations, natural selection can favour different modifications
  of the inherited structure in different environments.
  </p>

  <p>
  The result can be related structures with different functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Reasoning chain</h3>

  <p>
  Similar underlying structure → possible common ancestry → inherited
  structure modified over generations → different functions.
  </p>

  <p>
  Homologous structures therefore provide one line of evidence for evolution.
  They should be considered alongside other evidence rather than treated as
  the only evidence.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homologous Structures as Evidence for Evolution', 15),
  (865, 865, '
  <h2>Evidence for Evolution Must Be Interpreted</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Scientific evidence does not mean simply finding a similarity and declaring
  that evolution has occurred.
  </p>

  <p>
  Scientists examine evidence, compare explanations and look for patterns
  that are consistent with evolutionary relationships.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: fossils</h3>

  <p>
  A fossil by itself shows that an organism or trace existed in the past.
  A sequence of correctly dated fossils can provide evidence of changes in
  characteristics over time.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: homologous structures</h3>

  <p>
  Similar underlying structures in different organisms can support the
  explanation that they inherited the structures from a common ancestor.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: vestigial structures</h3>

  <p>
  Reduced structures can support the idea that present organisms retain
  modified remnants of ancestral structures.
  </p>

  <h3>Strong scientific reasoning</h3>

  <p>
  Evidence → observation → comparison → explanation.
  </p>

  <p>
  The strongest evolutionary explanations are supported by multiple
  independent lines of evidence rather than one isolated observation.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evidence for Evolution Must Be Interpreted', 15),
  (866, 866, '
  <h2>Evolution: Putting the Evidence Together</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Evolutionary explanations become stronger when different types of evidence
  point toward the same conclusion.
  </p>

  <h3>Evidence 1: Fossils</h3>

  <p>
  Fossils provide evidence about organisms that lived in the past and can show
  changes in characteristics through geological time.
  </p>

  <h3>Evidence 2: Homologous structures</h3>

  <p>
  Similar underlying structures in different organisms can support the idea
  of common ancestry.
  </p>

  <h3>Evidence 3: Vestigial structures</h3>

  <p>
  Reduced structures can provide evidence that present organisms retain
  modified remnants of ancestral structures.
  </p>

  <h3>Connecting the evidence</h3>

  <p>
  Suppose fossils show that a group changed over time.
  </p>

  <p>
  Suppose living members of the group also possess corresponding structures
  that share a common underlying plan.
  </p>

  <p>
  Suppose some structures are reduced compared with ancestral forms.
  </p>

  <p>
  These observations can be interpreted together as evidence for evolutionary
  relationships and change over time.
  </p>

  <h3>Important principle</h3>

  <p>
  Evidence supports an explanation; scientists still have to interpret the
  evidence carefully.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evolution: Putting the Evidence Together', 15),
  (867, 867, '
  <h2>What Is Biodiversity?</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Biodiversity</b> refers to the variety of living organisms in a particular
  area or on Earth as a whole.
  </p>

  <p>
  Biodiversity includes differences among organisms and the variety of species
  and ecosystems.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  A forest may contain trees, grasses, insects, birds, mammals, fungi and
  microorganisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  The presence of many different forms of life means the forest has high
  biological diversity.
  </p>

  <h3>Three levels commonly considered</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Genetic diversity</b> refers to differences in genetic characteristics
  among individuals of the same species.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Species diversity</b> refers to the variety of species in an area.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Ecosystem diversity</b> refers to the variety of ecosystems or habitats
  in a region.
  </p>

  <p>
  Biodiversity therefore describes variety at more than one biological level.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Biodiversity?', 15),
  (868, 868, '
  <h2>Fungal Hyphae and Mycelium</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Many multicellular fungi are made of thread-like structures called
  <b>hyphae</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  A mass or network of hyphae is called a <b>mycelium</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Why the structure is useful</h3>

  <p>
  Hyphae can spread through or over the material from which the fungus is
  obtaining nutrients.
  </p>

  <p>
  This gives the fungus a large area over which digestive enzymes can be
  released and soluble nutrients can be absorbed.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  When mould grows on bread, the visible fuzzy growth is associated with a
  network of fungal hyphae.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
  A <b>hypha</b> is an individual thread-like fungal structure.
  A <b>mycelium</b> is a network or mass of hyphae.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fungal Hyphae and Mycelium', 15),
  (869, 869, '
  <h2>Yeast as a Fungus</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Yeast</b> is a fungus. Unlike many familiar fungi such as mushrooms,
  yeast is mainly <b>unicellular</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell structure</h3>

  <p>
  A yeast cell is eukaryotic and therefore contains a membrane-bound nucleus.
  It also has a cell wall.
  </p>

  <h3>Nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Yeast obtains organic nutrients from its surroundings.
  It can respire aerobically when oxygen is available.
  </p>

  <p>
  When oxygen is limited, yeast can carry out anaerobic respiration,
  producing ethanol and carbon dioxide.
  </p>

  <h3>Reproduction</h3>

  <p>
  Yeast commonly reproduces asexually by <b>budding</b>.
  A small outgrowth forms on the parent cell, grows and eventually separates.
  </p>

  <h3>Why yeast belongs to Fungi</h3>

  <p>
  Yeast has the fundamental characteristics of fungi even though it does not
  form the large multicellular structures seen in mushrooms.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Yeast as a Fungus', 15),
  (870, 870, '
  <h2>Reading a Scientific Name</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  A scientific name contains information about the classification of an
  organism.
  </p>

  <p>
  Consider:
  </p>

  <p>
  <i>Panthera leo</i>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Identify the genus</h3>

  <p>
  The first word, <i>Panthera</i>, is the genus.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Identify the specific name</h3>

  <p>
  The second word, <i>leo</i>, is the specific name.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Understand the relationship</h3>

  <p>
  The scientific name identifies the species as belonging to the genus
  <i>Panthera</i>.
  </p>

  <p>
  The same genus contains other closely related species, such as
  <i>Panthera tigris</i>.
  </p>

  <h3>What the name does NOT mean</h3>

  <p>
  The second word by itself is not the complete scientific name.
  Both words are needed to identify the species.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reading a Scientific Name', 15),
  (871, 871, '
<h2>Choosing Between Two Characteristics</h2>

<p>
When using a dichotomous key, the most important step is to compare the organism carefully with the two statements provided.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has a backbone → go to 2<br>
<b>1b.</b> Does not have a backbone → go to 3
</p>

<p>
Suppose the organism being identified is a frog.
</p>

<p>
A frog has a backbone, so the correct choice is:
</p>

<p>
<b>1a → go to 2</b>
</p>

<h3>Do not use unrelated characteristics</h3>

<p>
The frog''s colour does not matter at this step because the key asks about the backbone.
</p>

<p>
You must answer the question the key gives you, not a different question.
</p>

<h3>Another example</h3>

<p>
Suppose a key asks:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a.</b> Has feathers<br>
<b>2b.</b> Does not have feathers
</p>

<p>
A bird has feathers, so choose <b>2a</b>.
</p>

<h3>Key principle</h3>

<p>
At each step:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Read both statements.</li>
<li>Observe the organism.</li>
<li>Find which statement matches.</li>
<li>Follow the instruction attached to that statement.</li>
</ol>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Choosing Between Two Characteristics', 15),
  (872, 872, '
<h2>What Is Biodiversity?</h2>

<p>
<b>Biodiversity</b> is the variety of living organisms found in a particular area or on Earth as a whole.
</p>

<p>
It includes the variety of different organisms such as plants, animals, fungi and microorganisms.
</p>

<h3>Three levels of biodiversity</h3>

<p>
Biodiversity can be considered at three related levels:
</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Genetic diversity</b> — differences in genetic characteristics among individuals of the same species.</li>
<li><b>Species diversity</b> — the variety of species in an area.</li>
<li><b>Ecosystem diversity</b> — the variety of ecosystems in an area.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A forest containing many species of trees, insects, birds and fungi has high species diversity.
</p>

<p>
If individuals of the same species also have different inherited characteristics, the area has genetic diversity as well.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Biodiversity is not simply the number of animals in an area. It describes the <b>variety of living organisms and their biological differences</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Biodiversity', 15),
  (873, 873, '
<h2>Why Biodiversity Is Important</h2>

<p>
Biodiversity is important because living organisms are connected through ecosystems and provide many biological resources and functions.
</p>

<h3>Food</h3>

<p>
Humans obtain food from many organisms, including crops, livestock, fish and other organisms.
</p>

<h3>Medicines</h3>

<p>
Plants, microorganisms and other organisms can provide substances that are useful in medicine.
</p>

<h3>Ecosystem processes</h3>

<p>
Organisms contribute to processes such as decomposition, pollination, nutrient cycling and food-web interactions.
</p>

<h3>Genetic resources</h3>

<p>
Genetic diversity can provide characteristics that may be useful in agriculture, such as disease resistance or tolerance to environmental conditions.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
The importance of biodiversity is not limited to individual species. Organisms interact within ecosystems, so losing species can affect other organisms and ecosystem processes.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Biodiversity Is Important', 15),
  (874, 874, '
  <h2>What Is Classification?</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Classification</b> is the process of grouping organisms according to
  their similarities and differences.
  </p>

  <p>
  Scientists classify organisms because there are millions of different
  organisms. Without an organised system, identifying and studying them
  would be extremely difficult.
  </p>

  <h3>Why classification is necessary</h3>

  <p>
  Imagine trying to organise a library containing thousands of books without
  grouping them in any way. Finding one particular book would be difficult.
  </p>

  <p>
  Classification performs a similar job for living organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  Organisms with important similarities can be placed into the same groups.
  Organisms with major differences are placed into different groups.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  A lion and a domestic cat have many similarities. Both have hair, produce
  milk for their young and possess similar basic body organisation.
  </p>

  <p>
  A lion and a maize plant have much less in common structurally and
  physiologically.
  </p>

  <p>
  Classification therefore helps scientists organise organisms according to
  biological relationships and characteristics.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Classification?', 15),
  (875, 875, '
  <h2>Why Organisms Are Classified</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Classification allows scientists to organise biodiversity into meaningful
  groups.
  </p>

  <h3>Identification</h3>

  <p>
  Classification makes it easier to identify an unknown organism by comparing
  its characteristics with organisms that have already been classified.
  </p>

  <h3>Comparison</h3>

  <p>
  Once organisms are placed into groups, scientists can compare their
  structures, functions and other characteristics more easily.
  </p>

  <h3>Communication</h3>

  <p>
  Scientists in different places need a consistent way of referring to the
  same organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  A standard classification and naming system reduces confusion caused by
  different local names.
  </p>

  <h3>Relationships</h3>

  <p>
  Classification can also help scientists communicate information about
  similarities and evolutionary relationships among organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  Therefore, classification is not simply memorising names. It is a system
  for organising and communicating biological information.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Organisms Are Classified', 15),
  (876, 876, '
  <h2>Characteristics Used in Classification</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Scientists do not classify organisms by appearance alone. They examine
  biological characteristics that can be compared systematically.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell structure</h3>

  <p>
  Scientists can consider whether an organism is made of prokaryotic or
  eukaryotic cells and whether the cells possess particular structures.
  </p>

  <h3>Number of cells</h3>

  <p>
  An organism may be unicellular or multicellular.
  </p>

  <h3>Mode of nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Organisms differ in how they obtain nutrients. Some produce organic food
  through photosynthesis while others obtain nutrients from other organisms
  or organic material.
  </p>

  <h3>Body organisation</h3>

  <p>
  Scientists can compare structures such as tissues, organs and specialised
  systems.
  </p>

  <h3>Reproduction</h3>

  <p>
  Reproductive characteristics can also help distinguish groups of organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  Good classification therefore uses multiple biological characteristics
  rather than relying on one superficial feature.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Characteristics Used in Classification', 15),
  (877, 877, '
  <h2>Prokaryotic and Eukaryotic Cells in Classification</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  One important characteristic used when comparing organisms is their cell
  type.
  </p>

  <h3>Prokaryotic cells</h3>

  <p>
  Prokaryotic cells do not have a membrane-bound nucleus.
  Their genetic material is located in a region of the cytoplasm rather than
  inside a nucleus.
  </p>

  <p>
  Bacteria are examples of organisms made of prokaryotic cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Eukaryotic cells</h3>

  <p>
  Eukaryotic cells contain a nucleus surrounded by a nuclear membrane.
  They also contain membrane-bound organelles such as mitochondria.
  </p>

  <p>
  Plants, animals, fungi and many protists are made of eukaryotic cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Why this matters in classification</h3>

  <p>
  The difference between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cell organisation is a
  major biological distinction.
  </p>

  <p>
  It provides a much stronger classification characteristic than simply
  comparing an organism''s colour or size.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prokaryotic and Eukaryotic Cells in Classification', 15),
  (878, 878, '
  <h2>Five-Kingdom Classification</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  A traditional biological classification system groups organisms into five
  kingdoms:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Monera</li>
    <li>Protoctista</li>
    <li>Fungi</li>
    <li>Plantae</li>
    <li>Animalia</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Why these kingdoms are separated</h3>

  <p>
  The groups differ in important characteristics such as cell organisation,
  number of cells, mode of nutrition and body organisation.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Monera</b> contains prokaryotic organisms such as bacteria.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Protoctista</b> traditionally includes diverse mostly unicellular
  eukaryotic organisms, although this group is not considered a natural
  evolutionary group in modern classification.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Fungi</b> includes organisms such as mushrooms, moulds and yeasts.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Plantae</b> includes plants.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Animalia</b> includes animals.
  </p>

  <h3>Important note</h3>

  <p>
  Classification systems have changed as scientific evidence has improved.
  Therefore, students should distinguish between a traditional school
  classification framework and modern evolutionary classification.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Five-Kingdom Classification', 15),
  (879, 879, '
  <h2>Kingdom Monera</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  In the traditional five-kingdom system, <b>Monera</b> contains organisms
  whose cells are prokaryotic.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell organisation</h3>

  <p>
  Members of Monera are generally unicellular and do not possess a
  membrane-bound nucleus.
  </p>

  <p>
  Their genetic material is located in the cell region rather than inside
  a membrane-bound nucleus.
  </p>

  <h3>Nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Different bacteria obtain nutrients in different ways. Some are
  heterotrophic while others can make organic substances using particular
  forms of autotrophic nutrition.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>
  Bacteria are the main examples studied in this group.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Monera is distinct</h3>

  <p>
  The most important distinguishing feature is the <b>prokaryotic cell
  organisation</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  This separates bacteria from organisms made of eukaryotic cells such as
  animals, plants and fungi.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kingdom Monera', 15),
  (880, 880, '
  <h2>Kingdom Protoctista</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  In the traditional five-kingdom system, <b>Protoctista</b> contains a
  diverse collection of mostly unicellular eukaryotic organisms.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell organisation</h3>

  <p>
  Protoctists are eukaryotic, so their cells contain a membrane-bound nucleus.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>
  Examples traditionally placed in this kingdom include <b>Amoeba</b> and
  <b>Paramecium</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  Some algae have also traditionally been included in this broad group.
  </p>

  <h3>Why the group is unusual</h3>

  <p>
  Protoctista contains organisms with very different lifestyles and
  nutritional methods.
  </p>

  <p>
  This reflects the fact that the traditional kingdom system was developed
  before modern molecular evidence transformed evolutionary classification.
  </p>

  <p>
  For school classification, the important distinction is that these
  organisms are eukaryotic and are not classified as plants, animals or
  fungi in the traditional five-kingdom arrangement.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kingdom Protoctista', 15),
  (881, 881, '
  <h2>Kingdom Fungi</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  The <b>Fungi</b> are eukaryotic organisms that include mushrooms, moulds
  and yeasts.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell organisation</h3>

  <p>
  Fungal cells have a membrane-bound nucleus and a cell wall.
  Their cell walls contain <b>chitin</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Fungi are heterotrophic. They do not make their own organic food by
  photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
  Many fungi obtain nutrients by secreting digestive enzymes onto organic
  material. The enzymes break large substances into smaller soluble
  substances, which are then absorbed.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>
  Mushrooms, moulds and yeast are examples of fungi.
  </p>

  <p>
  Although fungi were traditionally grouped with plants in some older
  systems, fungi differ from plants in important ways, especially in their
  mode of nutrition and cell-wall composition.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kingdom Fungi', 15),
  (882, 882, '
  <h2>Kingdom Plantae</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  The kingdom <b>Plantae</b> contains plants such as mosses, ferns,
  flowering plants and many other multicellular plant groups.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell organisation</h3>

  <p>
  Plants are multicellular eukaryotic organisms.
  Their cells contain nuclei and membrane-bound organelles.
  </p>

  <p>
  Plant cells also have cellulose cell walls and, in photosynthetic tissues,
  chloroplasts.
  </p>

  <h3>Nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Most green plants are autotrophic. They manufacture organic food by
  photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
  Photosynthesis uses light energy to produce organic substances from
  carbon dioxide and water.
  </p>

  <h3>Organisation</h3>

  <p>
  Many plants have specialised tissues and organs such as roots, stems and
  leaves.
  </p>

  <p>
  These characteristics distinguish plants from groups such as animals and
  fungi.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kingdom Plantae', 15),
  (883, 883, '
  <h2>Kingdom Animalia</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  The kingdom <b>Animalia</b> contains animals such as insects, fish,
  birds, reptiles, amphibians and mammals.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell organisation</h3>

  <p>
  Animals are multicellular eukaryotic organisms.
  Their cells contain nuclei and membrane-bound organelles.
  </p>

  <p>
  Animal cells do not have cellulose cell walls or chloroplasts.
  </p>

  <h3>Nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Animals are heterotrophic. They obtain organic nutrients by consuming
  other organisms or organic material.
  </p>

  <h3>Organisation</h3>

  <p>
  Animal cells can become specialised and form tissues, organs and organ
  systems.
  </p>

  <p>
  Most animals can respond rapidly to stimuli using coordinated nervous and
  hormonal systems.
  </p>

  <h3>Movement</h3>

  <p>
  Many animals possess specialised structures that allow movement, although
  the method of movement differs greatly among animal groups.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kingdom Animalia', 15),
  (884, 884, '
  <h2>Kingdom Animalia Compared with Kingdom Plantae</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Both plants and animals are multicellular eukaryotic organisms, but they
  differ in important characteristics.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell walls</h3>

  <p>
  Typical plant cells have cellulose cell walls.
  Animal cells do not have cellulose cell walls.
  </p>

  <h3>Chloroplasts</h3>

  <p>
  Photosynthetic plant cells may contain chloroplasts.
  Animal cells do not contain chloroplasts.
  </p>

  <h3>Nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Most green plants are autotrophic and manufacture organic food by
  photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
  Animals are heterotrophic and obtain organic nutrients from other
  organisms or organic material.
  </p>

  <h3>Movement</h3>

  <p>
  Animals commonly possess specialised systems for movement.
  Plants generally remain fixed in one place but show growth movements and
  responses to stimuli.
  </p>

  <h3>Important point</h3>

  <p>
  Both kingdoms contain multicellular eukaryotic organisms. Their differences
  come from several characteristics, not simply from whether they move.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kingdom Animalia Compared with Kingdom Plantae', 15),
  (885, 885, '
  <h2>Kingdom Fungi Compared with Kingdom Plantae</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Fungi and plants are both eukaryotic organisms and many members of both
  groups possess cell walls. However, their cell walls and nutritional
  methods differ.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell wall</h3>

  <p>
  Typical fungal cell walls contain <b>chitin</b>.
  Typical plant cell walls contain <b>cellulose</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Most green plants manufacture organic food through photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
  Fungi are heterotrophic and commonly obtain nutrients by extracellular
  digestion followed by absorption.
  </p>

  <h3>Chloroplasts</h3>

  <p>
  Green plant cells can contain chloroplasts.
  Fungi do not have chloroplasts and therefore do not photosynthesise.
  </p>

  <h3>Why the distinction matters</h3>

  <p>
  Fungi may grow attached to soil or other surfaces like plants, but
  appearance alone does not make an organism a plant.
  </p>

  <p>
  Cell structure and nutrition provide much stronger classification evidence.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kingdom Fungi Compared with Kingdom Plantae', 15),
  (886, 886, '
  <h2>Comparing the Major Five Kingdoms</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  The traditional five-kingdom system groups organisms using several
  characteristics.
  </p>

  <h3>Monera</h3>

  <p>
  Mainly unicellular and prokaryotic.
  </p>

  <h3>Protoctista</h3>

  <p>
  Traditionally contains diverse mostly unicellular eukaryotic organisms.
  </p>

  <h3>Fungi</h3>

  <p>
  Eukaryotic organisms that obtain organic nutrients heterotrophically;
  many multicellular fungi contain hyphae and have chitinous cell walls.
  </p>

  <h3>Plantae</h3>

  <p>
  Multicellular eukaryotic organisms; most green plants are autotrophic and
  photosynthesise.
  </p>

  <h3>Animalia</h3>

  <p>
  Multicellular eukaryotic organisms that obtain organic nutrients
  heterotrophically.
  </p>

  <h3>How to classify an unfamiliar organism</h3>

  <p>
  Do not begin by guessing from appearance.
  </p>

  <p>
  Instead ask:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Is the cell prokaryotic or eukaryotic?</li>
    <li>Is the organism unicellular or multicellular?</li>
    <li>How does it obtain nutrients?</li>
    <li>What important cell structures does it possess?</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
  These observations provide a more reliable basis for classification.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing the Major Five Kingdoms', 15),
  (887, 887, '
  <h2>What Is a Species?</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  A <b>species</b> is a group of organisms that can interbreed naturally
  and produce fertile offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  Domestic dogs belong to the same species even though different breeds can
  look very different.
  </p>

  <p>
  Members of the same species share a common gene pool and have characteristics
  that allow them to reproduce with one another.
  </p>

  <h3>Why fertility matters</h3>

  <p>
  Two organisms may mate but fail to produce fertile offspring. This means
  they are not necessarily members of the same species.
  </p>

  <p>
  For example, a horse and a donkey can produce a mule, but the mule is
  generally sterile. Therefore, horses and donkeys are classified as
  different species.
  </p>

  <h3>Important idea</h3>

  <p>
  Species classification is based on biological relationships, not simply
  physical appearance.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is a Species?', 15),
  (888, 888, '
  <h2>Why Species Are the Basic Classification Unit</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  In the traditional taxonomic hierarchy, the <b>species</b> is the most
  specific major classification unit.
  </p>

  <p>
  A species represents organisms that are closely related and can normally
  reproduce with one another to produce fertile offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>From specific to broad</h3>

  <p>
  Several closely related species can be grouped into a <b>genus</b>.
  Several related genera can be grouped into a <b>family</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  Classification continues into increasingly broader groups.
  </p>

  <h3>Why this is useful</h3>

  <p>
  A species gives scientists a precise biological unit with which to identify
  and study organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  For example, saying "cat" could refer to many related cats. A scientific
  species name identifies the organism much more precisely.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Species Are the Basic Classification Unit', 15),
  (889, 889, '
  <h2>Taxonomic Hierarchy</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Taxonomic hierarchy</b> is the arrangement of organisms into groups
  from broad categories to increasingly specific categories.
  </p>

  <h3>The major ranks</h3>

  <p>
  The traditional hierarchy is:
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species</b>
  </p>

  <h3>How the hierarchy works</h3>

  <p>
  A kingdom contains several phyla.
  </p>

  <p>
  A phylum contains several classes.
  </p>

  <p>
  A class contains several orders.
  </p>

  <p>
  An order contains several families.
  </p>

  <p>
  A family contains several genera.
  </p>

  <p>
  A genus contains one or more closely related species.
  </p>

  <h3>The key pattern</h3>

  <p>
  Moving downward means the group becomes <b>smaller and more specific</b>.
  Moving upward means the group becomes <b>broader</b>.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Taxonomic Hierarchy', 15),
  (890, 890, '
  <h2>Kingdom to Phylum</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Kingdom</b> is one of the broadest traditional taxonomic ranks used
  in school classification.
  </p>

  <p>
  A kingdom contains several <b>phyla</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Why phyla exist</h3>

  <p>
  A kingdom can contain an enormous number of organisms. Dividing it into
  phyla creates smaller groups based on important shared characteristics.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: Animalia</h3>

  <p>
  Animals can be divided into different phyla according to major features
  of their body organisation.
  </p>

  <p>
  For example, humans belong to the phylum <b>Chordata</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  Chordates share important characteristics associated with the chordate
  body plan.
  </p>

  <h3>Hierarchy principle</h3>

  <p>
  Kingdom is broader than phylum.
  </p>

  <p>
  Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Kingdom → Phylum</b>
  </p>

  <p>
  Every member of a particular phylum belongs to the kingdom containing that
  phylum, but a kingdom contains many different phyla.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Kingdom to Phylum', 15),
  (891, 891, '
  <h2>Phylum to Class</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  A <b>class</b> is a taxonomic rank below phylum and above order.
  </p>

  <p>
  A phylum can contain several classes. Each class contains organisms that
  share more specific characteristics than all members of the wider phylum.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: Chordata</h3>

  <p>
  Humans belong to:
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Kingdom Animalia → Phylum Chordata</b>
  </p>

  <p>
  Humans are then placed in the class <b>Mammalia</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  Mammals share characteristics such as mammary glands and hair at some
  stage of their development.
  </p>

  <h3>Hierarchy</h3>

  <p>
  The sequence becomes:
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Kingdom → Phylum → Class</b>
  </p>

  <p>
  Moving from phylum to class makes the group more specific.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Phylum to Class', 15),
  (892, 892, '
  <h2>Class to Order</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  An <b>order</b> is a taxonomic rank below class and above family.
  </p>

  <p>
  A class contains several orders. Organisms within an order share more
  specific characteristics than all organisms in the wider class.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  Humans belong to the class <b>Mammalia</b>.
  Within Mammalia, humans belong to the order <b>Primates</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  Primates include humans, apes, monkeys and other related animals.
  </p>

  <h3>The hierarchy</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order</b>
  </p>

  <p>
  Each step downward reduces the number of organisms in the group and
  increases specificity.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Class to Order', 15),
  (893, 893, '
  <h2>Order to Family</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  A <b>family</b> is a taxonomic rank below order and above genus.
  </p>

  <p>
  A family contains one or more related genera.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  Humans belong to the family <b>Hominidae</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  Hominidae includes humans and other great apes.
  </p>

  <p>
  This means humans can be traced through the hierarchy:
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>
  Animalia → Chordata → Mammalia → Primates → Hominidae
  </b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why family is useful</h3>

  <p>
  Family groups organisms that are more closely related than organisms merely
  belonging to the same order.
  </p>

  <p>
  Therefore, family is more specific than order.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Order to Family', 15),
  (894, 894, '
  <h2>Family to Genus</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  A <b>genus</b> is a taxonomic rank below family and above species.
  </p>

  <p>
  A genus contains one or more closely related species.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  Humans belong to the genus <b>Homo</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  Our species is <b>Homo sapiens</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Family Hominidae → Genus Homo → Species Homo sapiens</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why genus is important</h3>

  <p>
  Members of the same genus are more closely related than organisms that
  are grouped only within the same family.
  </p>

  <p>
  The genus is also the first part of a scientific name in binomial
  nomenclature.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Family to Genus', 15),
  (895, 895, '
  <h2>Binomial Nomenclature</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Binomial nomenclature</b> is the system of giving an organism a
  scientific name made of two parts.
  </p>

  <h3>The two parts</h3>

  <p>
  The first part is the <b>genus</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  The second part identifies the <b>species</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
  For humans, the scientific name is:
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Homo sapiens</b>
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Homo</b> is the genus and <b>sapiens</b> is the specific name.
  </p>

  <h3>Writing scientific names</h3>

  <p>
  When typed, the scientific name is normally written in <i>italics</i>.
  When handwritten, the two words are normally underlined separately.
  </p>

  <p>
  The genus begins with a capital letter. The second word begins with a
  lowercase letter.
  </p>

  <p>
  Example:
  </p>

  <p>
  <i>Homo sapiens</i>
  </p>

  <h3>Why use scientific names?</h3>

  <p>
  Common names can vary between regions and languages. A scientific name
  provides a standard name that can be used internationally.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Binomial Nomenclature', 15),
  (896, 896, '
<h2>Species Diversity</h2>

<p>
<b>Species diversity</b> refers to the variety of different species present in a particular area.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Imagine two areas of equal size.
</p>

<p>
Area A contains:
</p>

<ul>
<li>1 species of tree</li>
<li>1 species of bird</li>
<li>1 species of insect</li>
</ul>

<p>
Area B contains:
</p>

<ul>
<li>8 species of trees</li>
<li>12 species of birds</li>
<li>20 species of insects</li>
</ul>

<p>
Area B has greater species diversity because it contains a wider variety of species.
</p>

<h3>Species diversity is not just population size</h3>

<p>
If a forest contains 1,000 individuals belonging to one species, it does not automatically have greater species diversity than a forest containing 500 individuals belonging to many different species.
</p>

<p>
The important question is:
</p>

<p>
<b>How many different species are represented?</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Species Diversity', 15),
  (897, 897, '
<h2>Competition Within a Species</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Competition</b> occurs when organisms require the same limited resource.
</p>

<p>
When individuals of the <b>same species</b> compete, this is called <b>intraspecific competition</b>.
</p>

<h3>What Can Individuals Compete For?</h3>

<ul>
<li>food</li>
<li>water</li>
<li>space</li>
<li>shelter</li>
<li>mates</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A population of maize plants is growing closely together.
</p>

<p>
The plants require water and mineral ions from the same soil.
</p>

<p>
If resources are limited, the plants compete.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A population of male deer requires mates.
</p>

<p>
If there are fewer females than males, males may compete with one another for access to mates.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose a population becomes very large.
</p>

<p>
More individuals require the same limited food supply.
</p>

<p>
Competition becomes stronger.
</p>

<p>
Some individuals may obtain less food and have lower survival or reproductive success.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Competition becomes particularly important when a resource is <b>limited</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Competition Within a Species', 15),
  (898, 898, '
<h2>Competition Between Species</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Organisms of different species can also compete when they require the same limited resource.
</p>

<p>
This is called <b>interspecific competition</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Two different species of herbivore may feed on the same grass.
</p>

<p>
If grass becomes limited, the two species compete for food.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Species A and Species B both feed on the same plant.
</p>

<p>
During a drought, the plants become scarce.
</p>

<p>
Both species now have less food available.
</p>

<p>
Competition between the species increases.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Two bird species use the same nesting sites.
</p>

<p>
If suitable nesting sites are limited, individuals of the two species may compete for them.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Two species may have overlapping resource requirements but not necessarily compete strongly if the resource is abundant.
</p>

<p>
The important condition is that the resource is <b>limited</b>.
</p>

<h3>Compare</h3>

<p>
<b>Intraspecific:</b> competition within one species.
</p>

<p>
<b>Interspecific:</b> competition between different species.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Competition Between Species', 15),
  (899, 899, '
<h2>Species Richness</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Species richness</strong> is the number of different species present
in a particular area or sample.
</p>

<p>
It is a simple way of describing one aspect of biodiversity.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A quadrat contains:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Grass species A</li>
  <li>Grass species B</li>
  <li>Clover</li>
  <li>Moss</li>
</ul>

<p>
There are four different species.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Species richness = 4</strong>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Species richness counts the number of different species.
It does not consider how many individuals belong to each species.
</p>

<p>
Consider two communities:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Community A:</strong> 10 species with similar numbers of individuals.</p>

<p>
<strong>Community B:</strong> 10 species, but one species makes up almost all
the individuals.</p>

<p>
Both have the same species richness, even though their species abundances
are very different.
</p>

<h3>WHY IT IS USEFUL</h3>

<p>
Species richness can be compared between habitats to identify areas containing
more or fewer different species.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Species Richness', 15),
  (900, 900, '
<h2>Species Distribution Patterns</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The individuals of a species are not always spread evenly across a habitat.
Their positions can form different distribution patterns.
</p>

<h3>THREE COMMON PATTERNS</h3>

<p>
<strong>Clumped:</strong> individuals occur in groups or patches.</p>

<p>
<strong>Uniform:</strong> individuals are spaced at relatively similar
distances from one another.</p>

<p>
<strong>Random:</strong> there is no obvious regular spacing or grouping pattern.
</p>

<h3>CLUMPED DISTRIBUTION</h3>

<p>
This is common when resources occur in patches.
For example, plants may grow in groups where water or nutrients are available.
Animals may also cluster around food or shelter.
</p>

<h3>UNIFORM DISTRIBUTION</h3>

<p>
Uniform spacing can result from strong competition or territorial behaviour.
Individuals may maintain a distance from one another.
</p>

<h3>RANDOM DISTRIBUTION</h3>

<p>
Random distribution occurs when the position of one individual does not strongly
determine where another individual occurs.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Distribution pattern describes <strong>where individuals occur</strong>.
It is different from density, which describes <strong>how many individuals
occur per unit area</strong>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Species Distribution Patterns', 15),
  (901, 901, '
<h2>What Is a Taxonomic Key?</h2>

<p>
A <b>taxonomic key</b> is a tool used to identify an unknown organism by comparing its observable characteristics with a series of choices.
</p>

<p>
The key does not require you to know the organism''s name at the beginning. Instead, you observe the organism and use its characteristics to narrow down the possible identities.
</p>

<h3>How the idea works</h3>

<p>
Suppose you find an unknown organism. You might first ask:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Does it have wings?</li>
<li>Does it have six legs?</li>
<li>Does it have feathers?</li>
<li>Does it have fur?</li>
</ul>

<p>
Each answer removes organisms that do not match the observed characteristic.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Imagine a key begins with:
</p>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has wings → go to 2<br>
<b>1b.</b> Does not have wings → go to 3
</p>

<p>
If the organism has wings, you follow option <b>1a</b> and continue to step 2.
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
A taxonomic key identifies an organism by using <b>observable characteristics in a logical sequence</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is a Taxonomic Key', 15),
  (902, 902, '
<h2>How a Dichotomous Key Works</h2>

<p>
A <b>dichotomous key</b> is a type of taxonomic key in which each stage gives <b>two contrasting choices</b>.
</p>

<p>
The word <b>dichotomous</b> means divided into two parts.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A simple key for identifying four animals could begin like this:
</p>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has feathers → go to 2<br>
<b>1b.</b> Does not have feathers → go to 3
</p>

<p>
The two choices are opposites:
</p>

<ul>
<li>has feathers</li>
<li>does not have feathers</li>
</ul>

<p>
If the organism has feathers, choose <b>1a</b>.
If it does not have feathers, choose <b>1b</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why two choices?</h3>

<p>
Two choices make the identification process controlled and easy to follow. Each choice eliminates some possibilities.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose an organism has no feathers. From the first pair, you choose:
</p>

<p>
<b>1b → does not have feathers</b>
</p>

<p>
You then follow the instruction to the next numbered step.
</p>

<p>
The process continues until the organism''s identity is reached.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for How a Dichotomous Key Works', 15),
  (903, 903, '
<h2>Paired Statements in a Dichotomous Key</h2>

<p>
A dichotomous key normally presents identification choices as a pair of statements.
</p>

<p>
The two statements describe contrasting characteristics.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has six legs → go to 2<br>
<b>1b.</b> Does not have six legs → go to 3
</p>

<p>
The two statements form one decision point.
</p>

<p>
If you observe six legs, choose <b>1a</b>.
If you observe that the organism does not have six legs, choose <b>1b</b>.
</p>

<h3>Another example</h3>

<p>
<b>2a.</b> Has wings → identify as X<br>
<b>2b.</b> Does not have wings → identify as Y
</p>

<p>
You should choose only the statement that matches your observation.
</p>

<h3>Important rule</h3>

<p>
Do not choose a statement because it sounds familiar. Compare the organism with the characteristic actually described.
</p>

<p>
For example, if the key asks whether an organism has six legs, count the legs rather than guessing from the organism''s appearance.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Paired Statements in a Dichotomous Key', 15),
  (904, 904, '
<h2>Following a Key Step by Step</h2>

<p>
A taxonomic key must be followed in the order given. Skipping a step can lead to an incorrect identification.
</p>

<h3>Example key</h3>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has wings → go to 2<br>
<b>1b.</b> No wings → go to 3
</p>

<p>
<b>2a.</b> Has feathers → Bird<br>
<b>2b.</b> No feathers → Insect
</p>

<p>
<b>3a.</b> Has fur → Mammal<br>
<b>3b.</b> No fur → Reptile
</p>

<h3>Identify an organism</h3>

<p>
Suppose the organism:
</p>

<ul>
<li>has wings</li>
<li>has feathers</li>
</ul>

<p>
Start at step 1.
</p>

<p>
It has wings, so choose <b>1a</b>.
</p>

<p>
The instruction says to go to step 2.
</p>

<p>
At step 2, it has feathers, so choose <b>2a</b>.
</p>

<p>
The organism is therefore identified as a <b>bird</b>.
</p>

<h3>The sequence matters</h3>

<p>
You should not jump directly to a conclusion. Every decision must be supported by the characteristics given in the key.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Following a Key Step by Step', 15),
  (905, 905, '
<h2>Constructing a Simple Dichotomous Key</h2>

<p>
A dichotomous key can be constructed by arranging observable characteristics into a sequence of two-choice decisions.
</p>

<h3>Example organisms</h3>

<p>
Suppose we want to identify:
</p>

<ul>
<li>fish</li>
<li>bird</li>
<li>cat</li>
<li>butterfly</li>
</ul>

<p>
We first look for a characteristic that separates some organisms clearly.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has six legs → go to 2<br>
<b>1b.</b> Does not have six legs → go to 3
</p>

<p>
The butterfly has six legs, while the fish, bird and cat do not.
</p>

<p>
We can then separate the remaining organisms:
</p>

<p>
<b>3a.</b> Has feathers → Bird<br>
<b>3b.</b> Does not have feathers → go to 4
</p>

<p>
Then:
</p>

<p>
<b>4a.</b> Has fur → Cat<br>
<b>4b.</b> Does not have fur → Fish
</p>

<h3>Good characteristics</h3>

<p>
A useful characteristic should be:
</p>

<ul>
<li>easy to observe</li>
<li>clear</li>
<li>consistent</li>
<li>able to separate organisms into different groups</li>
</ul>

<p>
The key should eventually lead each organism to one identification.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Constructing a Simple Dichotomous Key', 15),
  (906, 906, '
<h2>Testing a Dichotomous Key</h2>

<p>
After constructing a dichotomous key, it should be tested to make sure that it actually identifies organisms correctly.
</p>

<h3>What should be checked?</h3>

<p>
For every organism in the key, ask:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Can I start at the first step?</li>
<li>Does one of the two choices clearly match?</li>
<li>Does the instruction lead to another appropriate step?</li>
<li>Does the final path lead to the correct identification?</li>
</ol>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose a key is intended to identify a bird.
</p>

<p>
The bird has feathers.
</p>

<p>
If the key gives:
</p>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has feathers → Bird<br>
<b>1b.</b> Does not have feathers → Fish
</p>

<p>
The bird follows 1a and is correctly identified.
</p>

<h3>Finding an error</h3>

<p>
Suppose both choices accidentally say:
</p>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has feathers<br>
<b>1b.</b> Has feathers
</p>

<p>
The choices do not separate the organisms. A bird and a non-bird could not be distinguished using this step.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the key must be corrected before it is used.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Testing a Dichotomous Key', 15),
  (907, 907, '
<h2>Identifying an Unknown Organism Using a Key</h2>

<p>
A taxonomic key is especially useful when an organism has been found but its identity is unknown.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider this key:
</p>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has six legs → go to 2<br>
<b>1b.</b> Does not have six legs → go to 3
</p>

<p>
<b>2a.</b> Has wings and scales on wings → Butterfly<br>
<b>2b.</b> Does not have wings with scales → Other insect
</p>

<p>
<b>3a.</b> Has feathers → Bird<br>
<b>3b.</b> Does not have feathers → Other animal
</p>

<h3>Unknown organism</h3>

<p>
Suppose you observe:
</p>

<ul>
<li>six legs</li>
<li>wings</li>
<li>scales on the wings</li>
</ul>

<p>
Start at step 1.
</p>

<p>
It has six legs → choose <b>1a</b>.
</p>

<p>
Go to step 2.
</p>

<p>
It has wings with scales → choose <b>2a</b>.
</p>

<p>
The organism is identified as a <b>butterfly</b>.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
The identification is based on the characteristics observed, not simply on what the organism looks generally like.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying an Unknown Organism Using a Key', 15),
  (908, 908, '
<h2>Common Errors When Using a Taxonomic Key</h2>

<p>
A key works only when its instructions are followed carefully. Several mistakes can lead to an incorrect identification.
</p>

<h3>Error 1: Choosing without observing</h3>

<p>
A student may guess which statement is correct instead of examining the organism.
</p>

<p>
<b>Better approach:</b> observe the characteristic being tested.
</p>

<h3>Error 2: Ignoring the exact wording</h3>

<p>
If a key asks whether an organism has six legs, having four legs does not mean that the organism should be ignored. It means the correct choice is the statement saying it does not have six legs.
</p>

<h3>Error 3: Skipping a step</h3>

<p>
The key may contain information that distinguishes similar organisms. Jumping ahead can cause an incorrect identification.
</p>

<h3>Error 4: Following the wrong instruction</h3>

<p>
A statement may say <b>go to 4</b>. You must go to step 4, not step 3 or the next line simply because it appears nearby.
</p>

<h3>Error 5: Using a characteristic that is not asked for</h3>

<p>
If the key asks about feathers, colour should not determine your choice unless colour is specifically part of that step.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Common Errors When Using a Key', 15),
  (909, 909, '
<h2>Complete Identification Problem</h2>

<p>
Let''s use a complete dichotomous key from beginning to end.
</p>

<h3>Key</h3>

<p>
<b>1a.</b> Has six legs → go to 2<br>
<b>1b.</b> Does not have six legs → go to 3
</p>

<p>
<b>2a.</b> Has wings → Insect A<br>
<b>2b.</b> Does not have wings → Insect B
</p>

<p>
<b>3a.</b> Has feathers → Bird<br>
<b>3b.</b> Does not have feathers → go to 4
</p>

<p>
<b>4a.</b> Has fur → Mammal<br>
<b>4b.</b> Does not have fur → Reptile
</p>

<h3>Problem</h3>

<p>
An unknown organism is observed to have:
</p>

<ul>
<li>no feathers</li>
<li>no fur</li>
<li>does not have six legs</li>
</ul>

<h3>Step 1</h3>

<p>
It does not have six legs.
</p>

<p>
Therefore choose:
</p>

<p>
<b>1b → go to 3</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 2</h3>

<p>
At step 3, it does not have feathers.
</p>

<p>
Therefore choose:
</p>

<p>
<b>3b → go to 4</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 3</h3>

<p>
At step 4, it does not have fur.
</p>

<p>
Therefore choose:
</p>

<p>
<b>4b → Reptile</b>
</p>

<h3>Final identification</h3>

<p>
The organism is identified as a <b>reptile</b>.
</p>

<p>
The important part is not memorising the answer. The identification comes from following the evidence at every step.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Complete Identification Problem', 15),
  (910, 910, '
<h2>Threats to Biodiversity</h2>

<p>
Biodiversity can decrease when human activities or environmental changes reduce the number of species, populations or habitats.
</p>

<h3>Habitat destruction</h3>

<p>
When natural habitats are cleared, drained or converted to other uses, organisms may lose places where they feed, reproduce and shelter.
</p>

<h3>Pollution</h3>

<p>
Pollutants can damage organisms or make habitats unsuitable for particular species.
</p>

<h3>Overexploitation</h3>

<p>
Taking organisms faster than populations can replace themselves can cause population decline.
</p>

<p>
Examples include excessive fishing, hunting and harvesting of wild plants.
</p>

<h3>Invasive species</h3>

<p>
A species introduced into an area where it is not naturally found can sometimes compete with native species, prey on them or alter their habitat.
</p>

<h3>Climate change</h3>

<p>
Changes in temperature and rainfall patterns can alter habitats and affect the survival and distribution of species.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Threats to Biodiversity', 15),
  (911, 911, '
<h2>Conservation of Biodiversity</h2>

<p>
<b>Conservation</b> means protecting and managing organisms, habitats and natural resources so that biological diversity is maintained.
</p>

<h3>Why conservation is needed</h3>

<p>
Human activities can reduce populations and destroy habitats. Conservation aims to reduce these losses and maintain biodiversity.
</p>

<h3>Protecting habitats</h3>

<p>
One important approach is protecting natural habitats such as forests, wetlands, grasslands and marine environments.
</p>

<h3>Controlling exploitation</h3>

<p>
Harvesting organisms can be managed so that populations are not removed faster than they can recover.
</p>

<h3>Reducing pollution</h3>

<p>
Reducing pollution can help maintain suitable conditions for organisms.
</p>

<h3>Restoring damaged habitats</h3>

<p>
Damaged habitats can sometimes be restored by actions such as replanting vegetation and protecting recovering areas.
</p>

<p>
The central principle is to maintain the organisms and ecological processes that make up biodiversity.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conservation of Biodiversity', 15),
  (912, 912, '
<h2>In Situ Conservation</h2>

<p>
<b>In situ conservation</b> means conserving organisms within their natural habitats.
</p>

<p>
The organism remains in the environment where it naturally occurs while the habitat is protected or managed.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<ul>
<li>protecting forests</li>
<li>establishing national parks</li>
<li>protecting wildlife habitats</li>
<li>creating protected marine areas</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why it works</h3>

<p>
An organism does not exist in isolation. It interacts with other organisms and with its physical environment.
</p>

<p>
Protecting the habitat therefore helps protect the conditions and interactions that support the species.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
If a population of elephants is protected within its natural habitat, the elephants continue to live and reproduce in that environment.
</p>

<p>
This is in situ conservation because the species is being conserved <b>where it naturally occurs</b>.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for In Situ Conservation', 15),
  (913, 913, '
<h2>Ex Situ Conservation</h2>

<p>
<b>Ex situ conservation</b> means conserving organisms or their genetic material outside their natural habitats.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<ul>
<li>zoos</li>
<li>botanical gardens</li>
<li>seed banks</li>
<li>gene banks</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example: seed bank</h3>

<p>
A seed bank stores seeds under controlled conditions so that plant genetic material can be preserved for future use.
</p>

<h3>Example: zoo</h3>

<p>
A threatened animal species may be kept and bred under human care as part of a conservation programme.
</p>

<h3>In situ versus ex situ</h3>

<p>
The key difference is the location of conservation.
</p>

<p>
<b>In situ:</b> the organism is conserved in its natural habitat.
</p>

<p>
<b>Ex situ:</b> the organism or its genetic material is conserved outside its natural habitat.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ex Situ Conservation', 15),
  (914, 914, '
<h2>Comparing In Situ and Ex Situ Conservation</h2>

<p>
Both <b>in situ</b> and <b>ex situ</b> conservation are used to protect biodiversity, but they conserve organisms in different settings.
</p>

<h3>In situ</h3>

<p>
The organism remains in its natural habitat.
</p>

<p>
Examples include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>national parks</li>
<li>nature reserves</li>
<li>protected forests</li>
<li>protected marine areas</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ex situ</h3>

<p>
The organism or its genetic material is maintained outside its natural habitat.
</p>

<p>
Examples include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>zoos</li>
<li>botanical gardens</li>
<li>seed banks</li>
<li>gene banks</li>
</ul>

<h3>How to distinguish them</h3>

<p>
Ask one question:
</p>

<p>
<b>Is the organism being conserved in its natural habitat?</b>
</p>

<p>
If yes → <b>in situ</b>.
</p>

<p>
If no → <b>ex situ</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A giraffe protected in a national park is an example of <b>in situ conservation</b>.
</p>

<p>
A rare plant whose seeds are stored in a seed bank is an example of <b>ex situ conservation</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing In Situ and Ex Situ Conservation', 15),
  (915, 915, '
<h2>Overexploitation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Overexploitation</b> occurs when organisms or natural resources are used at a rate that is greater than their ability to recover.
</p>

<p>
Examples include excessive:
</p>

<ul>
<li>fishing;</li>
<li>hunting;</li>
<li>logging;</li>
<li>harvesting of wild plants.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose a fish population naturally replaces about 1,000 fish through reproduction each year.
</p>

<p>
If 1,500 fish are removed every year, removal exceeds replacement.
</p>

<p>
The population is likely to decline if this continues.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
If too many mature trees are removed before younger trees can replace them, the forest population can decline.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If harvesting is reduced to a sustainable level, the population may have an opportunity to recover.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
The important issue is the relationship between the rate of removal and the rate at which the population or resource can recover.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Overexploitation', 15),
  (916, 916, '
<h2>Sustainable Resource Use</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A resource is used <b>sustainably</b> when it is used at a rate that allows it to remain available for the future.
</p>

<p>
The central idea is to balance present use with the ability of the resource and environment to recover.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<ul>
<li>replanting trees after harvesting;</li>
<li>controlling fishing levels;</li>
<li>reducing unnecessary water use;</li>
<li>protecting breeding populations;</li>
<li>reusing and recycling materials.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A forest is harvested.
</p>

<p>
If trees are removed faster than new trees grow, forest resources decline.
</p>

<p>
If harvesting is controlled and new trees are allowed to grow, the forest can continue supplying timber.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A fishery catches 1,000 fish per year while the population naturally replaces 1,200.
</p>

<p>
If other conditions remain suitable, the population has a better chance of being maintained than if 1,500 fish were removed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Using less water per person can reduce pressure on water supplies without requiring population size to decrease.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Sustainability requires considering both <b>how much is removed</b> and <b>how quickly the resource can recover</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sustainable Resource Use', 15),
  (917, 917, '
<h2>Conservation of Natural Resources</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Conservation</b> means protecting and managing organisms, habitats and natural resources so that they are maintained for the future.
</p>

<p>
Conservation may involve:
</p>

<ul>
<li>protecting habitats;</li>
<li>controlling hunting and fishing;</li>
<li>restoring damaged habitats;</li>
<li>planting trees;</li>
<li>protecting endangered species;</li>
<li>reducing pollution;</li>
<li>using resources sustainably.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A protected area can prevent uncontrolled hunting of wildlife.
</p>

<p>
This gives animal populations greater opportunity to survive and reproduce.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Reforestation can restore vegetation after forest loss.
</p>

<p>
As vegetation returns, habitats for other organisms can also be restored.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Controlling pollution protects organisms without necessarily removing them from their natural habitats.
</p>

<h3>Why Conservation Matters</h3>

<p>
Conservation helps maintain:
</p>

<ul>
<li>biodiversity;</li>
<li>ecosystem functions;</li>
<li>natural resources;</li>
<li>genetic diversity within species.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Conservation is not simply about protecting individual animals. It also involves protecting the habitats and ecological processes that support populations.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conservation of Natural Resources', 15),
  (918, 918, '
<h2>Protected Areas</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <b>protected area</b> is an area managed to conserve biodiversity, habitats, ecosystems or natural resources.
</p>

<p>
Different types of protected areas can have different rules about human activities.
</p>

<h3>Why Protect Areas?</h3>

<p>
Protected areas can:
</p>

<ul>
<li>preserve habitats;</li>
<li>protect threatened species;</li>
<li>reduce uncontrolled exploitation;</li>
<li>maintain ecosystems;</li>
<li>support conservation research and monitoring.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose a population of an endangered animal is declining because of habitat destruction and hunting.
</p>

<p>
Protecting its habitat can reduce habitat loss.
</p>

<p>
Controlling hunting can reduce mortality.
</p>

<p>
Together, these actions can improve the population''s chance of recovery.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A protected forest can conserve many species simultaneously because several organisms share the same habitat.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Protected areas do not automatically solve every conservation problem.
</p>

<p>
They require effective management, monitoring and enforcement.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Protecting a habitat can conserve many interacting species and ecological processes at the same time.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Protected Areas', 15),
  (919, 919, '
  <h2>Why Antibiotics Do Not Cure Viral Infections</h2>

  <p>
    Antibiotics are medicines that act against bacteria.
  </p>

  <p>
    Viruses are fundamentally different from bacteria and do not have the same
    cellular structures and processes that antibiotics target.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, antibiotics do not treat viral infections such as influenza.
  </p>

  <h3>Connection with resistance</h3>

  <p>
    Using antibiotics when they are not needed can expose bacteria in the body
    to the antibiotic.
  </p>

  <p>
    If resistant bacteria are present, they may survive and reproduce.
  </p>

  <p>
    This can contribute to the spread of antibiotic resistance.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Antibiotic resistance is a bacterial population problem. It does not mean
    that the human body becomes resistant to the antibiotic.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Antibiotics Do Not Cure Viral Infections', 15),
  (920, 920, '
<h2>Ecosystem Diversity</h2>

<p>
<b>Ecosystem diversity</b> is the variety of different ecosystems found in an area.
</p>

<h3>What is an ecosystem?</h3>

<p>
An ecosystem consists of living organisms interacting with one another and with the non-living components of their environment.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<p>
A region may contain several different ecosystems, such as:
</p>

<ul>
<li>forest</li>
<li>grassland</li>
<li>wetland</li>
<li>river</li>
</ul>

<p>
These ecosystems differ in their organisms and physical conditions.
</p>

<h3>Why this is biodiversity</h3>

<p>
A region containing several different ecosystem types has greater ecosystem diversity than a region containing only one ecosystem type.
</p>

<p>
For example, an area containing forests, wetlands and grasslands has greater ecosystem diversity than an area containing only grassland.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ecosystem Diversity', 15),
  (921, 921, '
<h2>What Is Ecology?</h2>

<p>
<b>Ecology</b> is the study of how organisms interact with one another and with their physical environment.
</p>

<p>
An organism does not live independently from its surroundings. It interacts with other organisms and depends on factors such as water, temperature, light and soil.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider a maize plant growing in a field.
</p>

<p>
It interacts with:
</p>

<ul>
<li>soil from which it obtains water and mineral ions</li>
<li>sunlight used in photosynthesis</li>
<li>carbon dioxide in the air</li>
<li>insects that may feed on it</li>
<li>other plants competing for resources</li>
</ul>

<p>
Studying these interactions is part of ecology.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Ecology is about <b>relationships between organisms and their environment</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Ecology', 15),
  (922, 922, '
<h2>Biotic and Abiotic Factors</h2>

<p>
Environmental factors affecting organisms can be divided into <b>biotic</b> and <b>abiotic</b> factors.
</p>

<h3>Biotic factors</h3>

<p>
<b>Biotic factors</b> are living components of an environment.
</p>

<p>
Examples include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>predators</li>
<li>prey</li>
<li>competitors</li>
<li>parasites</li>
<li>pathogens</li>
<li>food organisms</li>
</ul>

<h3>Abiotic factors</h3>

<p>
<b>Abiotic factors</b> are non-living physical or chemical conditions of the environment.
</p>

<p>
Examples include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>temperature</li>
<li>light intensity</li>
<li>water availability</li>
<li>pH</li>
<li>soil mineral content</li>
<li>humidity</li>
</ul>

<h3>How to distinguish them</h3>

<p>
Ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>Is it living?</b>
</p>

<p>
If yes, it is a biotic factor.
</p>

<p>
If it is non-living, it is an abiotic factor.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Biotic and Abiotic Factors', 15),
  (923, 923, '
<h2>Habitat</h2>

<p>
A <b>habitat</b> is the place where an organism normally lives and obtains the conditions and resources it needs.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<p>
Different organisms have different habitats.
</p>

<ul>
<li>A fish may live in a lake.</li>
<li>A camel may live in a dry region.</li>
<li>A frog may live in or around freshwater environments.</li>
<li>A tree may grow in a forest.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What makes a habitat suitable?</h3>

<p>
A suitable habitat must provide the organism with the conditions and resources required for survival and reproduction.
</p>

<p>
These may include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>food</li>
<li>water</li>
<li>suitable temperature</li>
<li>shelter</li>
<li>space</li>
<li>breeding conditions</li>
</ul>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
A habitat is the <b>place</b> where an organism lives.
</p>

<p>
It should not be confused with a niche, which describes the organism''s role and way of using resources within its environment.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Habitat', 15),
  (924, 924, '
<h2>Community</h2>

<p>
A <b>community</b> is made up of populations of different species living and interacting in the same area.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A forest may contain populations of:
</p>

<ul>
<li>trees</li>
<li>birds</li>
<li>insects</li>
<li>mammals</li>
<li>fungi</li>
</ul>

<p>
These different populations interact with one another.
</p>

<p>
Together, the populations form a <b>community</b>.
</p>

<h3>Population versus community</h3>

<p>
A population:
</p>

<p>
<b>One species → same area → same time.</b>
</p>

<p>
A community:
</p>

<p>
<b>Different populations → same area → interacting.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
All the zebras of one species in a grassland form a population.
</p>

<p>
The zebras together with grasses, lions, insects and other populations form a community.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Community', 15),
  (925, 925, '
<h2>Ecosystem</h2>

<p>
An <b>ecosystem</b> consists of a community of organisms interacting with one another and with the non-living components of their environment.
</p>

<h3>Two major components</h3>

<p>
An ecosystem contains:
</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Biotic components</b> — living organisms.</li>
<li><b>Abiotic components</b> — non-living physical and chemical conditions.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A pond ecosystem may contain:
</p>

<ul>
<li>fish</li>
<li>algae</li>
<li>insects</li>
<li>bacteria</li>
<li>water</li>
<li>oxygen</li>
<li>light</li>
<li>temperature</li>
</ul>

<p>
The organisms interact with one another and with the physical environment.
</p>

<h3>Building the idea</h3>

<p>
<b>Organisms of one species → population</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Different populations → community</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Community + abiotic environment → ecosystem</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ecosystem', 15),
  (926, 926, '
<h2>Niche</h2>

<p>
A <b>niche</b> describes the role of an organism in its ecosystem and how it uses the available resources and conditions.
</p>

<p>
It includes how an organism obtains food, where it lives, when it is active and how it interacts with other organisms.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Two birds may live in the same forest but feed on different resources.
</p>

<p>
One may feed mainly on insects found on tree trunks, while another feeds on seeds on the ground.
</p>

<p>
They occupy different niches because they use resources differently.
</p>

<h3>Habitat versus niche</h3>

<p>
A <b>habitat</b> is the place where an organism lives.
</p>

<p>
A <b>niche</b> describes the organism''s role and way of using resources in that environment.
</p>

<h3>Why this matters</h3>

<p>
Two species that use exactly the same limited resources in exactly the same way may compete strongly.
</p>

<p>
Differences in resource use can reduce direct competition.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Niche', 15),
  (927, 927, '
<h2>Parasitism</h2>

<p>
<b>Parasitism</b> is an interaction in which one organism, the <b>parasite</b>, obtains resources from another organism, the <b>host</b>, causing harm to the host.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<p>
Examples include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>tapeworm living in the intestine of a human</li>
<li>ticks feeding on the blood of mammals</li>
<li>certain parasites living inside animal tissues</li>
</ul>

<h3>Parasite</h3>

<p>
The parasite benefits because it obtains resources from the host.
</p>

<h3>Host</h3>

<p>
The host is harmed because resources are taken from it or its tissues are damaged.
</p>

<h3>Parasitism versus predation</h3>

<p>
In predation, a predator normally captures and consumes prey.
</p>

<p>
In parasitism, the parasite usually lives on or in the host and obtains resources from it over a period of time.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Parasitism', 15),
  (928, 928, '
<h2>Omnivores</h2>

<p>
An <b>omnivore</b> is a consumer that feeds on both plant material and animal material.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<p>
Humans are omnivores because humans can obtain food from both plants and animals.
</p>

<p>
Some other animals also have diets containing both plant and animal material.
</p>

<h3>Why classification depends on diet</h3>

<p>
The classification describes what the organism normally eats.
</p>

<p>
An animal does not have to eat equal amounts of plants and animals to be an omnivore.
</p>

<h3>Compare</h3>

<p>
<b>Herbivore:</b> mainly plant material.
</p>

<p>
<b>Carnivore:</b> mainly animal material.
</p>

<p>
<b>Omnivore:</b> both plant and animal material.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Omnivores', 15),
  (929, 929, '
<h2>Immigration and Emigration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Population size can change even when no organisms are born or die.
</p>

<p>
This happens when organisms move into or out of the area.
</p>

<p>
<b>Immigration</b> is the movement of individuals <b>into</b> a population.
</p>

<p>
<b>Emigration</b> is the movement of individuals <b>out of</b> a population.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A forest population contains 500 deer.
</p>

<p>
20 deer enter the forest from a neighbouring area.
</p>

<p>
There are no deaths or births.
</p>

<p>
New population:
</p>

<p>
500 + 20 = <b>520 deer</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A population contains 700 birds.
</p>

<p>
50 birds leave the area.
</p>

<p>
New population:
</p>

<p>
700 − 50 = <b>650 birds</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A population starts with 1,000 animals.
</p>

<p>
100 immigrate and 60 emigrate.
</p>

<p>
Net migration:
</p>

<p>
100 − 60 = <b>40 individuals</b>
</p>

<p>
New population:
</p>

<p>
1,000 + 40 = <b>1,040 animals</b>
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>

<p>
<b>Immigration = in.</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Emigration = out.</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Immigration and Emigration', 15),
  (930, 930, '
<h2>Limiting Factors</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <b>limiting factor</b> is a factor that restricts the growth, distribution or size of a population.
</p>

<p>
A limiting factor can be <b>biotic</b> or <b>abiotic</b>.
</p>

<h3>Biotic Limiting Factors</h3>

<ul>
<li>competition</li>
<li>predation</li>
<li>disease</li>
<li>availability of food</li>
</ul>

<h3>Abiotic Limiting Factors</h3>

<ul>
<li>temperature</li>
<li>light intensity</li>
<li>water availability</li>
<li>pH</li>
<li>mineral availability</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A plant population grows in soil containing very little nitrate.
</p>

<p>
Nitrate is needed to make amino acids and proteins.
</p>

<p>
If nitrate is insufficient, plant growth may be restricted.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, nitrate availability can act as a <b>limiting factor</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A rabbit population has abundant grass but very little water during a drought.
</p>

<p>
Water availability may become the factor restricting population growth.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A fish population increases until food becomes scarce.
</p>

<p>
Competition for food becomes stronger.
</p>

<p>
Food availability is therefore acting as a limiting factor.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
The importance of a limiting factor can change when environmental conditions change.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Limiting Factors', 15),
  (931, 931, '
<h2>Distribution of Organisms</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <b>distribution</b> of a species describes where individuals of that species occur within an area.
</p>

<p>
Organisms are rarely distributed evenly.
</p>

<p>
Their distribution is affected by both biotic and abiotic factors.
</p>

<h3>Three Common Distribution Patterns</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Clumped</b> — individuals occur in groups.</li>
<li><b>Uniform</b> — individuals are spaced relatively evenly.</li>
<li><b>Random</b> — there is no obvious regular pattern.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1 — Clumped</h3>

<p>
A plant may occur mainly around patches where soil moisture is high.
</p>

<p>
Individuals form groups rather than being evenly distributed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2 — Uniform</h3>

<p>
Animals may maintain territories and keep other individuals away.
</p>

<p>
This can produce a relatively even spacing between individuals.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3 — Random</h3>

<p>
If resources are distributed fairly evenly and individuals do not strongly attract or repel one another, their positions may appear random.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Distribution pattern is different from population size.
</p>

<p>
Population size asks:
</p>

<p>
<b>How many individuals are there?</b>
</p>

<p>
Distribution asks:
</p>

<p>
<b>Where are those individuals located?</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Distribution of Organisms', 15),
  (932, 932, '
<h2>Ecological Succession</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Ecological succession</b> is the gradual change in the species composition of a community over time.
</p>

<p>
As conditions change, some species become established while others become less successful or disappear.
</p>

<h3>Why Does Succession Occur?</h3>

<p>
Organisms can change their environment.
</p>

<p>
For example, plants may:
</p>

<ul>
<li>add organic matter to soil;</li>
<li>provide shade;</li>
<li>retain water;</li>
<li>create habitats for other organisms.</li>
</ul>

<p>
These changes can make the environment suitable for different species.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Bare ground may initially support only a small number of organisms.
</p>

<p>
Early colonisers establish themselves.
</p>

<p>
As organic matter accumulates and conditions change, other plants can establish.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
As vegetation becomes denser, more animals can find food and shelter.
</p>

<p>
The community therefore changes as new species become established.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A mature community can be very different from the community that first colonised the area.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Succession is a <b>change in community composition over time</b>, not simply an increase in the number of organisms.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ecological Succession', 15),
  (933, 933, '
<h2>Primary Succession</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Primary succession</b> begins in an area where there is no established soil and where organisms have not previously formed a developed community.
</p>

<p>
The process begins with organisms capable of colonising harsh conditions.
</p>

<h3>General Sequence</h3>

<ol>
<li>Initial colonisers become established.</li>
<li>Organic material accumulates.</li>
<li>Soil gradually develops.</li>
<li>More plants become established.</li>
<li>More animals and other organisms can colonise.</li>
<li>A more complex community develops.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Imagine newly exposed bare rock.
</p>

<p>
There is little or no developed soil.
</p>

<p>
Only organisms capable of surviving the harsh conditions can initially colonise it.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
As organisms die and their remains decompose, organic material accumulates.
</p>

<p>
This contributes to soil development.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Once enough soil develops, larger plants can establish.
</p>

<p>
Their roots can further affect the environment, allowing additional species to colonise.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Primary succession is generally slow because soil must develop before many larger plants can become established.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Primary Succession', 15),
  (934, 934, '
<h2>Secondary Succession</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Secondary succession</b> occurs when a community is disturbed but soil remains.
</p>

<p>
Because soil and sometimes seeds, roots or other organisms remain, recovery can occur more quickly than primary succession.
</p>

<h3>Examples of Disturbance</h3>

<ul>
<li>fire;</li>
<li>farming followed by abandonment;</li>
<li>clearing of vegetation;</li>
<li>storms;</li>
<li>other disturbances that leave soil behind.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A grassland is cleared for cultivation.
</p>

<p>
Later, farming stops.
</p>

<p>
The soil remains.
</p>

<p>
Plants can recolonise the area and the community can gradually change.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A fire destroys much of the vegetation in an area, but soil remains.
</p>

<p>
Seeds and roots that survive, together with new seeds arriving from surrounding areas, can help vegetation return.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Compare:
</p>

<p>
<b>Primary succession:</b> little or no developed soil initially.
</p>

<p>
<b>Secondary succession:</b> soil remains after disturbance.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Secondary succession can occur faster because important resources and biological material are already present.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Secondary Succession', 15),
  (935, 935, '
<h2>Biomagnification</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Biomagnification</b> is the increase in concentration of certain persistent pollutants in organisms at higher trophic levels of a food chain.
</p>

<p>
It is especially important for pollutants that:
</p>

<ul>
<li>are difficult to break down;</li>
<li>remain in organisms for long periods;</li>
<li>can accumulate in body tissues.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How It Happens</h3>

<p>
A pollutant enters an ecosystem.
</p>

<p>
Small organisms take it up.
</p>

<p>
Larger organisms eat many smaller organisms.
</p>

<p>
The pollutant can therefore become concentrated in organisms higher in the food chain.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>water → small aquatic organisms → fish → fish-eating bird</b>
</p>

<p>
If a persistent pollutant enters the water, small organisms may contain small concentrations.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A fish eats many contaminated organisms.
</p>

<p>
The pollutant can accumulate in the fish.
</p>

<p>
A fish-eating bird may consume many contaminated fish.
</p>

<p>
The pollutant concentration in the bird can therefore become higher.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Organisms at higher trophic levels can therefore be exposed to greater concentrations than organisms lower in the food chain.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Biomagnification is different from simple exposure to a pollutant. The key idea is that concentration increases at higher trophic levels.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Biomagnification', 15),
  (936, 936, '
<h2>Nitrification</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Nitrification</strong> is the bacterial conversion of ammonia or
ammonium compounds into nitrite and then nitrate.
</p>

<h3>THE TWO MAIN STAGES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Stage 1:</strong> Ammonia/ammonium compounds are converted into nitrites.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Stage 2:</strong> Nitrites are converted into nitrates.
</p>

<p>
Both stages are carried out by specialised soil bacteria.
</p>

<h3>WHY NITRATE MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Nitrate ions are an important source of nitrogen for plants.
Plants absorb nitrate ions from the soil through their roots.
</p>

<p>
The nitrogen can then be incorporated into amino acids, proteins and other
nitrogen-containing compounds.
</p>

<h3>FOLLOWING THE NITROGEN</h3>

<p>
Consider nitrogen-containing waste in the soil.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Organic nitrogen compounds are broken down and nitrogen
can become available as ammonia/ammonium.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Nitrifying bacteria convert ammonia/ammonium into nitrite.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Other nitrifying bacteria convert nitrite into nitrate.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Plant roots absorb nitrate.</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Nitrification is not the same as nitrogen fixation.
Nitrogen fixation introduces atmospheric nitrogen into usable nitrogen compounds,
while nitrification converts one soil nitrogen form into another.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nitrification', 15),
  (937, 937, '
<h2>Denitrification</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Denitrification</strong> is the bacterial conversion of nitrates in the
soil into nitrogen-containing gases, eventually returning nitrogen to the
atmosphere.
</p>

<p>
This process is an important part of completing the nitrogen cycle.
</p>

<h3>WHERE IT OCCURS</h3>

<p>
Denitrification is particularly associated with oxygen-poor or waterlogged
conditions where certain bacteria use nitrate during their metabolism.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Nitrogen exists in nitrate compounds in the soil.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Denitrifying bacteria use nitrate under suitable
low-oxygen conditions.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Nitrogen-containing gases are produced.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Nitrogen returns to the atmosphere.</p>

<h3>WHY IT MATTERS</h3>

<p>
If nitrogen only moved from the atmosphere into soil and organisms, atmospheric
nitrogen would continually decrease.
</p>

<p>
Denitrification provides a pathway that returns nitrogen to the atmosphere.
</p>

<h3>COMPLETE CONNECTION</h3>

<p>
Atmospheric nitrogen → fixation → soil nitrogen compounds → nitrification →
nitrate → plant uptake → animals → decomposition → soil nitrogen → 
denitrification → atmospheric nitrogen.
</p>

<p>
The exact chemical forms change during the cycle, but nitrogen itself is being
recycled through the environment.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Denitrification', 15),
  (938, 938, '
<h2>Fertiliser Runoff</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Fertilisers can improve crop growth when used appropriately.
However, excess fertiliser can be carried from farmland into rivers and lakes
by rainwater.
</p>

<p>
This movement of dissolved nutrients from land into water is called
<strong>nutrient runoff</strong>.
</p>

<h3>WHY RUNOFF IS A PROBLEM</h3>

<p>
Excess nitrates and phosphates entering water bodies can provide unusually
large amounts of nutrients for algae and aquatic plants.
</p>

<p>
This can contribute to <strong>eutrophication</strong>.
</p>

<h3>THE CHAIN OF EVENTS</h3>

<p>
<strong>Excess fertiliser</strong>
→ nutrients enter water
→ rapid algal growth
→ algae die
→ decomposers break them down
→ decomposer respiration uses oxygen
→ dissolved oxygen decreases
→ aquatic organisms may die.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT CONNECTION</h3>

<p>
The fertiliser itself does not directly remove oxygen from the water.
The major oxygen depletion occurs because microorganisms decompose the
increased amount of dead organic material.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain how excessive fertiliser use can cause fish deaths.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Excess nutrients enter a water body.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Algae grow rapidly.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Large amounts of algae die.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Decomposers break down the dead algae.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Their respiration uses dissolved oxygen.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> Oxygen concentration falls.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 7:</strong> Fish and other aquatic organisms may die because there is
not enough oxygen for aerobic respiration.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fertiliser Runoff', 15),
  (939, 939, '
<h2>Water Availability and Plant Distribution</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Water is an essential resource for plants.
It acts as a reactant in photosynthesis, is needed for transport and helps
maintain cell turgidity.
</p>

<p>
The amount of available water therefore influences where different plants
can survive and grow.
</p>

<h3>WATER AVAILABILITY AS AN ABIOTIC FACTOR</h3>

<p>
Water availability is an <strong>abiotic factor</strong> because it is a
non-living part of the environment.
</p>

<p>
Areas with high and reliable water availability can support plant species
that require abundant water.
Areas with little available water favour plants with adaptations that reduce
water loss or improve water uptake.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE: DRY ENVIRONMENTS</h3>

<p>
A plant living in a dry environment may have features such as:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>reduced leaf surface area</li>
  <li>a thick waxy cuticle</li>
  <li>stomata positioned in protected locations</li>
  <li>deep or widespread roots</li>
</ul>

<p>
These features can reduce water loss or improve access to water.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Water availability is not simply a description of rainfall.
It refers to how much water is actually accessible to organisms.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why might a plant adapted to a dry environment survive better than a plant without these adaptations?</p>

<p>
The adapted plant may lose water more slowly or obtain water more effectively.
It is therefore more likely to maintain sufficient water for cell function
during dry conditions.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Water Availability and Plant Distribution', 15),
  (940, 940, '
<h2>Photosynthetic Productivity</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Productivity</strong> in an ecosystem refers to the rate at which
producers build organic material from inorganic substances, mainly through
photosynthesis.
</p>

<p>
Plants use light energy to convert carbon dioxide and water into organic
compounds. The chemical energy stored in these compounds becomes available
to organisms that feed directly or indirectly on the producers.
</p>

<h3>WHY PRODUCTIVITY MATTERS</h3>

<p>
A food chain begins with producers because they introduce energy-rich organic
material into the biological part of an ecosystem.
</p>

<p>
If producers photosynthesise rapidly, they can potentially make more organic
material available to the rest of the ecosystem.
</p>

<h3>FACTORS AFFECTING PRODUCTIVITY</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Light intensity</li>
  <li>Carbon dioxide concentration</li>
  <li>Temperature</li>
  <li>Water availability</li>
  <li>Availability of mineral nutrients</li>
</ul>

<p>
The effect of each factor depends on the conditions under which the plant is
growing.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can low light intensity reduce productivity?</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Photosynthesis requires light energy.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Low light provides less energy for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> The rate of photosynthesis may therefore decrease.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Less organic material may be produced per unit time.</p>

<p>
Therefore, low light intensity can limit productivity.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Photosynthetic Productivity', 15),
  (941, 941, '
<h2>Ecological Balance</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
An ecosystem is constantly changing.
The populations within it interact with each other and with abiotic factors.
</p>

<p>
The term <strong>ecological balance</strong> describes a situation in which
these interactions allow ecosystem components to persist without one process
permanently eliminating all others.
</p>

<h3>WHY BALANCE IS DYNAMIC</h3>

<p>
Population sizes can rise and fall.
Resource availability changes.
Predator and prey numbers change.
Weather conditions change.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, ecological balance does not mean that every population stays at
exactly the same size.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
If rainfall increases, plant growth may increase.
More plants may support more herbivores.
More herbivores may support more predators.
</p>

<p>
Later, increased grazing may reduce plant abundance, which can reduce
herbivore numbers.
</p>

<p>
These changes show that populations are connected.
</p>

<h3>HUMAN DISTURBANCE</h3>

<p>
Human activities can alter ecological relationships through:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>habitat destruction</li>
  <li>pollution</li>
  <li>overexploitation</li>
  <li>introduction of invasive species</li>
  <li>major changes to resource availability</li>
</ul>

<h3>WORKED REASONING</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can removing a predator affect organisms other
than its prey?</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Removing the predator reduces predation.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Its prey may increase.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> More prey may consume more of their food resources.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> This can reduce plant or smaller-animal populations.</p>

<p>
Therefore, changing one population can produce effects throughout a food web.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ecological Balance', 15),
  (942, 942, '
<h2>Percentage Cover</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Counting individual plants is not always practical, especially when organisms
such as grasses grow very close together.
</p>

<p>
In such situations, ecologists can estimate <strong>percentage cover</strong>:
the proportion of the sampled area occupied by a species.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Imagine a quadrat containing grass covering approximately 60% of its surface.
The estimated percentage cover of grass is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>60%</strong>
</p>

<p>
Another quadrat might contain 20% grass cover.
The two locations can then be compared.
</p>

<h3>WHY USE PERCENTAGE COVER?</h3>

<p>
If plants form dense mats, counting each individual may be difficult because
individual boundaries cannot easily be identified.
Percentage cover provides a practical estimate of abundance.
</p>

<h3>HOW TO IMPROVE RELIABILITY</h3>

<p>
Use several quadrats and calculate a mean percentage cover.
This reduces the effect of unusually high or low individual samples.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Three quadrats give grass cover values of:
</p>

<p>
40%, 60%, 50%
</p>

<p>
Mean percentage cover:
</p>

<p>
<strong>(40 + 60 + 50) ÷ 3 = 150 ÷ 3 = 50%</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Percentage Cover', 15),
  (943, 943, '
<h2>Ecological Investigation Variables</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
When investigating how an environmental factor affects organisms, variables
must be identified clearly.
</p>

<h3>INDEPENDENT VARIABLE</h3>

<p>
The <strong>independent variable</strong> is the factor deliberately changed
or selected by the researcher.
</p>

<h3>DEPENDENT VARIABLE</h3>

<p>
The <strong>dependent variable</strong> is what is measured in response to
the independent variable.
</p>

<h3>CONTROLLED VARIABLES</h3>

<p>
Controlled variables are factors that should be kept as constant as practical
so that they do not provide alternative explanations for the results.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A student investigates how distance from a river affects the number of
grass species found in quadrats.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Independent variable:</strong> distance from the river.</p>

<p>
<strong>Dependent variable:</strong> number of grass species.</p>

<p>
Possible controlled variables include quadrat size and the sampling method.
</p>

<h3>WHY THIS MATTERS</h3>

<p>
If several factors change at the same time, it becomes difficult to determine
which factor caused the observed difference.
</p>

<p>
A clear investigation changes or compares the intended factor while keeping
other important conditions controlled where possible.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ecological Investigation Variables', 15),
  (944, 944, '
<h2>Abiotic Factors and Species Distribution</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The distribution of organisms is affected by both biotic and abiotic factors.
</p>

<p>
Important abiotic factors include:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>temperature</li>
  <li>light intensity</li>
  <li>water availability</li>
  <li>soil pH</li>
  <li>soil mineral content</li>
  <li>salinity</li>
  <li>oxygen concentration</li>
</ul>

<h3>HOW AN ABIOTIC FACTOR AFFECTS DISTRIBUTION</h3>

<p>
Each species has conditions under which it can survive, grow and reproduce
successfully.
</p>

<p>
If a particular environmental condition becomes unsuitable, the species may
become less abundant or disappear from that part of the habitat.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE: WATER</h3>

<p>
A plant that requires abundant water may be more common close to a river than
on dry ground farther away.
</p>

<p>
However, other factors may also differ between the locations.
A proper investigation is therefore needed to determine which factor is
associated with the distribution.
</p>

<h3>WORKED REASONING</h3>

<p><strong>Observation:</strong> A plant occurs mainly in areas with moist soil.</p>

<p>
<strong>Reasoning:</strong> Water availability may influence its distribution
because the plant requires water for photosynthesis, transport and maintenance
of cell turgidity.
</p>

<p>
However, this observation alone does not prove that water is the only factor
responsible.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Abiotic Factors and Species Distribution', 15),
  (945, 945, '
<h2>Biotic Factors and Species Distribution</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Biotic factors</strong> are effects caused by living organisms.
They can influence where a species occurs and how abundant it is.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT BIOTIC FACTORS</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Competition</li>
  <li>Predation</li>
  <li>Parasitism</li>
  <li>Disease</li>
  <li>Availability of food</li>
  <li>Availability of mates</li>
</ul>

<h3>COMPETITION</h3>

<p>
A species may be less abundant in an area where another species competes
strongly for the same limited resources.
</p>

<h3>PREDATION</h3>

<p>
Predators can reduce prey population size.
Areas with higher predation pressure may therefore contain fewer prey
individuals.
</p>

<h3>DISEASE</h3>

<p>
Disease can increase mortality and reduce reproduction.
Its effects can be particularly strong when individuals are concentrated
closely together.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Suppose a plant species is common in one area but rare in another.
The two areas have similar temperature and rainfall, but the second area
contains a competing plant species in high abundance.
</p>

<p>
Competition could contribute to the difference because both species may require
some of the same limited resources.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Species distribution is usually the result of several interacting factors,
not one factor acting alone.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Biotic Factors and Species Distribution', 15),
  (946, 946, '
<h2>Correlation in Ecological Investigations</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>correlation</strong> describes a relationship between two variables
in which changes in one variable are associated with changes in another.
</p>

<h3>POSITIVE CORRELATION</h3>

<p>
As one variable increases, the other also tends to increase.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
As plant density increases, the number of herbivores supported by the area
may also increase, within suitable conditions.
</p>

<h3>NEGATIVE CORRELATION</h3>

<p>
As one variable increases, the other tends to decrease.
</p>

<p>
Example:
</p>

<p>
Plant abundance might decrease as distance from a water source increases.
</p>

<h3>NO CLEAR CORRELATION</h3>

<p>
The data may show no consistent relationship between the variables.
</p>

<h3>CORRELATION IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY CAUSATION</h3>

<p>
If two variables change together, this does not prove that one directly caused
the other.
</p>

<p>
A third factor may influence both.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A study finds that plant abundance is higher in areas with higher soil
moisture.
</p>

<p>
This is evidence of a positive relationship between soil moisture and plant
abundance.
</p>

<p>
However, the researcher should consider whether other factors, such as soil
nutrients, temperature or light, also differ between the areas.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Correlation in Ecological Investigations', 15),
  (947, 947, '
<h2>Evaluating an Ecological Conclusion</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A scientific conclusion should match the evidence collected.
A researcher should avoid making a claim that is stronger than the data allow.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A student measures plant abundance at different distances from a river and
finds fewer plants farther from the river.
</p>

<p>
A reasonable conclusion is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Plant abundance decreases with increasing distance from the river in the
area studied.
</strong>
</p>

<p>
A much stronger statement would be:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Distance from the river causes plants to decrease.
</strong>
</p>

<p>
The second statement may not be justified because other factors could also
change with distance.
</p>

<h3>WHAT MAKES A CONCLUSION STRONG?</h3>

<ul>
  <li>It refers directly to the data.</li>
  <li>It identifies the observed pattern.</li>
  <li>It avoids unsupported explanations.</li>
  <li>It considers limitations.</li>
  <li>It is based on sufficient evidence.</li>
</ul>

<h3>RELIABILITY</h3>

<p>
If repeated samples produce similar results, confidence in the observed
pattern increases.
</p>

<h3>VALIDITY</h3>

<p>
The investigation should actually measure what it was designed to investigate.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher samples only one quadrat at each location and finds that plants
are more abundant near a stream.
</p>

<p>
The observation may be useful, but the conclusion is limited because one
quadrat at each location may not adequately represent the whole area.
</p>

<p>
More appropriately distributed repeated samples would provide stronger evidence.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evaluating an Ecological Conclusion', 15),
  (948, 948, '
<h2>Improving an Ecological Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
When an ecological investigation produces weak or uncertain evidence,
the method can often be improved.
</p>

<h3>USE MORE SAMPLES</h3>

<p>
Increasing the number of quadrats or sampling points can make the sample
more representative and reduce the influence of unusual measurements.
</p>

<h3>USE APPROPRIATE SAMPLING</h3>

<p>
Random sampling can reduce selection bias when estimating general abundance.
Systematic sampling can be more appropriate when investigating changes along
an environmental gradient.
</p>

<h3>STANDARDISE THE METHOD</h3>

<p>
Use the same quadrat size, counting method and measurement procedure at each
location.
</p>

<h3>REPEAT MEASUREMENTS</h3>

<p>
Repeated measurements can identify unusual results and allow averages to be
calculated.
</p>

<h3>CONTROL OTHER VARIABLES</h3>

<p>
Where possible, important factors other than the one being investigated
should be kept similar or measured so their effects can be considered.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A student investigates whether distance from a river affects plant abundance.
They use one quadrat at each distance.
</p>

<p>
A stronger investigation could:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Use several quadrats at each distance.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Keep quadrat size constant.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Use the same counting method.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Calculate mean abundance at each distance.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Record other relevant environmental factors where possible.</p>

<p>
This provides stronger evidence for any relationship observed.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Improving an Ecological Investigation', 15),
  (949, 949, '
<h2>Field Investigation of Plant Distribution</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A field investigation can be used to determine whether the distribution of a
plant species is associated with an environmental factor.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE QUESTION</h3>

<p>
<strong>
How does distance from a river affect the abundance of a particular plant?
</strong>
</p>

<h3>METHOD</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the study area.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Place a transect from the river toward the surrounding
land.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Place quadrats at predetermined distances.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Count the target plant in each quadrat.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Repeat the sampling at each distance.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> Calculate the mean abundance at each distance.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 7:</strong> Measure or record relevant environmental conditions,
such as soil moisture.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 8:</strong> Plot the results on a graph.</p>

<h3>INTERPRETING THE RESULTS</h3>

<p>
If abundance decreases as distance increases, the data show a negative
relationship between distance and plant abundance.
</p>

<p>
If soil moisture also decreases with distance and plant abundance decreases
at the same time, water availability may be associated with the distribution.
</p>

<p>
However, additional evidence is needed before claiming that water alone
caused the pattern.
</p>

<h3>WHY REPLICATION MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Repeating the investigation in different sections of the habitat or at
different times can show whether the pattern is consistent.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Field Investigation of Plant Distribution', 15),
  (950, 950, '
<h2>Mean of Ecological Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <strong>mean</strong> is the average of a set of measurements.
It is calculated by adding all the values and dividing by the number of values.
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Mean = sum of all values ÷ number of values
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A student counts the number of plants in five quadrats:
</p>

<p>
6, 8, 7, 9, 5
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Add the values.</p>

<p>
6 + 8 + 7 + 9 + 5 = 35
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Count the measurements.</p>

<p>
There are 5 measurements.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Divide.</p>

<p>
Mean = 35 ÷ 5 = <strong>7 plants per quadrat</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHY USE THE MEAN?</h3>

<p>
Several quadrats may give different counts.
The mean provides one value that represents the overall sample.
</p>

<p>
The mean should be calculated from measurements collected using the same
method and units.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
An unusually high or low value can affect the mean.
This is why ecological data should also be examined for unusual results
rather than relying on the mean alone.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mean of Ecological Data', 15),
  (951, 951, '
<h2>Range of Ecological Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <strong>range</strong> describes the spread of a set of measurements.
</p>

<p>
It is calculated by subtracting the smallest value from the largest value.
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Range = highest value − lowest value
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
The numbers of plants in five quadrats are:
</p>

<p>
4, 7, 5, 11, 8
</p>

<p>
Highest value = 11
</p>

<p>
Lowest value = 4
</p>

<p>
Range = 11 − 4
</p>

<p>
= <strong>7</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHAT DOES THE RANGE TELL US?</h3>

<p>
A small range means the measurements are relatively close to one another.
A large range means the measurements are more spread out.
</p>

<h3>WORKED COMPARISON</h3>

<p>
Sample A:
</p>

<p>
8, 9, 10, 9, 8
</p>

<p>
Range = 10 − 8 = 2
</p>

<p>
Sample B:
</p>

<p>
3, 12, 7, 15, 5
</p>

<p>
Range = 15 − 3 = 12
</p>

<p>
Sample B has the greater spread.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
The range does not tell us the average.
It tells us how far apart the highest and lowest measurements are.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Range of Ecological Data', 15),
  (952, 952, '
<h2>Anomalous Results in Ecological Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
An <strong>anomalous result</strong> is a measurement that differs substantially
from the general pattern of the other results.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher records plant numbers in six similar quadrats:
</p>

<p>
12, 14, 13, 15, 14, 35
</p>

<p>
Most measurements are between 12 and 15.
The value 35 is very different from the others and may be anomalous.
</p>

<h3>DO NOT AUTOMATICALLY DELETE IT</h3>

<p>
An unusual result should not simply be removed because it does not fit the
expected pattern.
</p>

<p>
The researcher should investigate possible explanations.
</p>

<h3>POSSIBLE CAUSES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>recording error</li>
  <li>counting error</li>
  <li>equipment or measurement problem</li>
  <li>incorrect sampling procedure</li>
  <li>a genuinely unusual part of the habitat</li>
</ul>

<h3>WHAT SHOULD THE RESEARCHER DO?</h3>

<p>
Check the original measurement and sampling conditions.
If an error is confirmed, the result can be corrected or excluded with a
clear justification.
</p>

<p>
If no error is found, the result may represent genuine biological variation
and should not automatically be discarded.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Anomalous Results in Ecological Data', 15),
  (953, 953, '
<h2>Drawing a Bar Chart from Ecological Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>bar chart</strong> is useful for comparing separate categories.
In ecology, it can be used to compare quantities such as the abundance of
different species in the same sampling area.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE DATA</h3>

<p>
A quadrat contains:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Grass = 20</li>
  <li>Clover = 8</li>
  <li>Moss = 5</li>
  <li>Daisy = 12</li>
</ul>

<h3>HOW TO DRAW IT</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Put the categories on the horizontal axis.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Put abundance on the vertical axis.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Choose a suitable scale.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Draw one separate bar for each species.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Make the height of each bar equal to its measured value.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> Label both axes and include units where appropriate.</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Bars should be separated because the categories are discrete.
</p>

<p>
The scale must be even and clearly labelled.
</p>

<h3>INTERPRETATION</h3>

<p>
In the example, grass has the greatest abundance while moss has the lowest
abundance.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Drawing a Bar Chart from Ecological Data', 15),
  (954, 954, '
<h2>Drawing a Line Graph from Ecological Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>line graph</strong> is useful when investigating how one measured
variable changes with another continuous variable.
</p>

<h3>ECOLOGICAL EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher measures plant abundance at different distances from a river.
</p>

<p>
Distance (m): 0, 5, 10, 15, 20
</p>

<p>
Plants per quadrat: 18, 15, 11, 7, 4
</p>

<h3>HOW TO DRAW IT</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Put the independent variable on the horizontal axis.</p>

<p>
Here, this is distance from the river.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Put the dependent variable on the vertical axis.</p>

<p>
Here, this is plant abundance.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Choose suitable scales.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Plot each pair of values accurately.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Draw an appropriate line or trend through the data.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> Label both axes and include units.</p>

<h3>INTERPRETING THE GRAPH</h3>

<p>
The data show that plant abundance decreases as distance from the river
increases.
</p>

<p>
This is a negative relationship.
</p>

<h3>KEY RULE</h3>

<p>
The variable deliberately changed or selected by the researcher is normally
placed on the horizontal axis.
The variable measured in response is placed on the vertical axis.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Drawing a Line Graph from Ecological Data', 15),
  (955, 955, '
<h2>Identifying Trends in Ecological Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>trend</strong> is the general direction in which data change.
</p>

<p>
A trend may be increasing, decreasing, constant or more complex.
</p>

<h3>INCREASING TREND</h3>

<p>
As the independent variable increases, the measured quantity generally
increases.
</p>

<h3>DECREASING TREND</h3>

<p>
As the independent variable increases, the measured quantity generally
decreases.
</p>

<h3>NO CLEAR TREND</h3>

<p>
The values fluctuate without showing a consistent direction.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Distance from a water source (m):
</p>

<p>
0, 5, 10, 15, 20
</p>

<p>
Plant abundance:
</p>

<p>
20, 18, 15, 10, 6
</p>

<p>
The values generally decrease as distance increases.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Plant abundance shows a decreasing trend with increasing distance from the
water source.
</strong>
</p>

<h3>USE DATA IN YOUR CONCLUSION</h3>

<p>
A strong conclusion describes the pattern rather than simply saying
“the graph goes down.”
</p>

<p>
Where possible, refer to actual values or ranges from the data.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Trends in Ecological Data', 15),
  (956, 956, '
<h2>Comparing Ecological Data Sets</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ecological studies often compare two habitats, sites or groups of organisms.
The comparison should use measurements collected in a consistent way.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Two grasslands are sampled using the same quadrat size.
</p>

<p>
Grassland A has a mean of 14 plants per quadrat.
</p>

<p>
Grassland B has a mean of 8 plants per quadrat.
</p>

<p>
The mean abundance of the measured plant is therefore greater in Grassland A.
</p>

<h3>COMPARE MORE THAN THE MEAN</h3>

<p>
A useful comparison may also consider:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>range</li>
  <li>sample size</li>
  <li>sampling method</li>
  <li>variation between samples</li>
  <li>environmental conditions</li>
</ul>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Site A:
</p>

<p>
10, 11, 12, 11, 11
</p>

<p>
Site B:
</p>

<p>
4, 8, 12, 9, 7
</p>

<p>
Mean of Site A = 55 ÷ 5 = 11.
</p>

<p>
Mean of Site B = 40 ÷ 5 = 8.
</p>

<p>
Site A has the higher mean.
</p>

<p>
But Site B has greater variation because its values are more widely spread.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Two data sets can have different averages and different levels of variation.
A good ecological comparison considers both.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Ecological Data Sets', 15),
  (957, 957, '
<h2>Reliability of Ecological Results</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A result is more <strong>reliable</strong> when repeated measurements or
repeated investigations give similar results.
</p>

<h3>HOW TO IMPROVE RELIABILITY</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Increase the number of samples.</li>
  <li>Repeat measurements.</li>
  <li>Use consistent methods.</li>
  <li>Use suitable equipment.</li>
  <li>Repeat the investigation where appropriate.</li>
</ul>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A student investigates plant abundance using only one quadrat at each location.
The results vary greatly.
</p>

<p>
It is difficult to know whether the differences represent the real distribution
or simply unusual individual quadrats.
</p>

<p>
The student can improve reliability by using several quadrats at each location
and calculating a mean.
</p>

<h3>RELIABILITY IS NOT THE SAME AS VALIDITY</h3>

<p>
<strong>Reliability</strong> concerns consistency of results.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Validity</strong> concerns whether the investigation measures what it
is intended to measure.
</p>

<p>
A method can produce very consistent results but still be invalid if it
systematically measures the wrong thing.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reliability of Ecological Results', 15),
  (958, 958, '
<h2>Validity of an Ecological Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
An investigation is <strong>valid</strong> when its design allows it to
answer the question being investigated.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Research question:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Does distance from a river affect the abundance of a particular plant?
</strong>
</p>

<p>
The investigation should measure:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>distance from the river</li>
  <li>plant abundance</li>
</ul>

<p>
The sampling should cover different distances and use a consistent method.
</p>

<h3>A VALID DESIGN</h3>

<p>
A transect can be placed from the river outward.
Quadrats can then be placed at several distances.
The plant can be counted in each quadrat.
</p>

<p>
This directly measures the variables in the research question.
</p>

<h3>INVALID OR WEAK DESIGN</h3>

<p>
If the researcher samples only one location beside the river, the investigation
cannot properly determine how abundance changes with distance.
</p>

<h3>KEY QUESTION</h3>

<p>
When evaluating validity, ask:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
“Does this method actually allow me to answer the question I asked?”
</strong>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Increasing sample size can improve reliability, but a large sample does not
automatically make a poorly designed investigation valid.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Validity of an Ecological Investigation', 15),
  (959, 959, '
<h2>Drawing a Scientific Conclusion from Ecological Evidence</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A scientific conclusion should answer the research question using the evidence
collected.
</p>

<h3>THREE STEPS</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1: State the pattern.</strong></p>

<p>
Describe how the variables changed.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2: Support the pattern with data.</strong></p>

<p>
Use relevant values or comparisons from the results.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3: State the appropriate level of certainty.</strong></p>

<p>
Do not claim more than the evidence supports.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher records the following plant abundance:
</p>

<p>
0 m: 20 plants
</p>

<p>
5 m: 16 plants
</p>

<p>
10 m: 12 plants
</p>

<p>
15 m: 8 plants
</p>

<p>
20 m: 5 plants
</p>

<p>
A strong conclusion is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Plant abundance decreased as distance from the river increased. Abundance
fell from 20 plants per quadrat at the river to 5 plants per quadrat at 20 m.
</strong>
</p>

<p>
This conclusion is supported directly by the data.
</p>

<h3>AVOID OVERCLAIMING</h3>

<p>
The data show a relationship between distance and abundance.
They do not by themselves prove exactly which environmental factor caused
the change.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Drawing a Scientific Conclusion from Ecological Evidence', 15),
  (960, 960, '
<h2>Evaluating Evidence in Ecology</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ecological conclusions are stronger when they are supported by sufficient,
representative and consistently collected evidence.
</p>

<h3>QUESTIONS TO ASK</h3>

<p>
<strong>1. Was enough data collected?</strong></p>

<p>
A very small sample may not represent the whole habitat.
</p>

<p>
<strong>2. Was the sampling representative?</strong></p>

<p>
The sample should reflect the area being studied rather than deliberately
favouring particular locations.
</p>

<p>
<strong>3. Was the method consistent?</strong></p>

<p>
The same measurement procedure should be used where comparisons are being made.
</p>

<p>
<strong>4. Were unusual results investigated?</strong></p>

<p>
Anomalous values should be checked rather than automatically removed.
</p>

<p>
<strong>5. Does the conclusion match the data?</strong></p>

<p>
The conclusion should not be stronger than the evidence allows.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EVALUATION</h3>

<p>
A researcher concludes:
</p>

<p>
“River water causes this plant to grow better everywhere.”
</p>

<p>
The study sampled only one small area and measured plant abundance at different
distances from the river.
</p>

<p>
This conclusion is too broad.
The evidence may show an association between distance from the river and plant
abundance in the study area, but it does not establish that river water is the
cause or that the same relationship occurs everywhere.
</p>

<h3>STRONGER CONCLUSION</h3>

<p>
“The plant was more abundant closer to the river in the area studied.”
</p>

<p>
This statement stays within the evidence.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evaluating Evidence in Ecology', 15),
  (961, 961, '
  <h2>Autotrophic and Heterotrophic Nutrition</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  The way an organism obtains organic nutrients is another important
  characteristic used when comparing organisms.
  </p>

  <h3>Autotrophic nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  An organism with autotrophic nutrition makes organic food from simpler
  inorganic substances.
  </p>

  <p>
  Green plants can manufacture glucose through photosynthesis using carbon
  dioxide and water, with light energy absorbed by chlorophyll.
  </p>

  <h3>Heterotrophic nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Organisms with heterotrophic nutrition obtain organic nutrients from other
  organisms or organic material.
  </p>

  <p>
  Animals obtain food by consuming other organisms or their products.
  Fungi obtain nutrients by absorbing soluble substances from organic
  material.
  </p>

  <h3>Why nutrition matters in classification</h3>

  <p>
  Different groups of organisms obtain nutrients in fundamentally different
  ways. This makes nutritional mode a useful characteristic when comparing
  groups.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Autotrophic and Heterotrophic Nutrition', 15),
  (962, 962, '
<h2>Predation</h2>

<p>
<b>Predation</b> is an interaction in which one organism, the <b>predator</b>, captures and feeds on another organism, the <b>prey</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A lion hunting and eating a zebra is an example of predation.
</p>

<p>
The:
</p>

<ul>
<li>lion is the predator</li>
<li>zebra is the prey</li>
</ul>

<h3>Predation affects populations</h3>

<p>
If the number of predators increases, predation pressure on prey populations may increase.
</p>

<p>
If prey numbers decrease greatly, predators may have less food available.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, predator and prey populations can influence one another.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
Predation is not the same as competition.
</p>

<p>
In competition, organisms require the same limited resource.
</p>

<p>
In predation, one organism captures and feeds on another organism.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Predation', 15),
  (963, 963, '
<h2>Producers</h2>

<p>
A <b>producer</b> is an organism that makes its own organic food from simpler substances.
</p>

<p>
Most producers in ecosystems are green plants and algae. They use <b>photosynthesis</b> to make glucose.
</p>

<h3>What does a plant need?</h3>

<p>
For photosynthesis, a green plant needs:
</p>

<ul>
<li>light energy</li>
<li>carbon dioxide</li>
<li>water</li>
<li>chlorophyll</li>
</ul>

<p>
The glucose produced can be used in respiration or converted into other substances such as starch.
</p>

<h3>Why are producers important?</h3>

<p>
Producers introduce energy from sunlight into many ecosystems.
</p>

<p>
Animals cannot obtain this energy directly from sunlight. They obtain chemical energy by feeding on organisms or organic material.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
In a grassland:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → zebra → lion</b>
</p>

<p>
The grass is the producer because it makes its own organic food.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Producers', 15),
  (964, 964, '
<h2>Consumers</h2>

<p>
A <b>consumer</b> is an organism that obtains organic nutrients and energy by feeding on other organisms or organic material.
</p>

<p>
Unlike producers, consumers do not make their own organic food from carbon dioxide and water using photosynthesis.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<ul>
<li>A cow feeds on grass.</li>
<li>A lion feeds on other animals.</li>
<li>A human may feed on plants and animals.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Types of consumers</h3>

<p>
Consumers can be classified according to what they feed on.
</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Herbivores</b> feed mainly on plants.</li>
<li><b>Carnivores</b> feed mainly on animals.</li>
<li><b>Omnivores</b> feed on both plant and animal material.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
In:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → grasshopper → frog</b>
</p>

<p>
The grasshopper and frog are consumers because they obtain organic nutrients by feeding on other organisms.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Consumers', 15),
  (965, 965, '
<h2>Herbivores</h2>

<p>
A <b>herbivore</b> is a consumer that feeds mainly on plants or plant material.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<ul>
<li>cow</li>
<li>goat</li>
<li>zebra</li>
<li>giraffe</li>
<li>grasshopper</li>
</ul>

<h3>Position in a food chain</h3>

<p>
Herbivores commonly feed directly on producers.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → zebra</b>
</p>

<p>
The grass is the producer and the zebra is the herbivore.
</p>

<h3>Why herbivores are important</h3>

<p>
Herbivores transfer chemical energy and organic matter from producers to higher trophic levels when they are eaten.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, herbivores form an important link between producers and many predators.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Herbivores', 15),
  (966, 966, '
<h2>Carnivores</h2>

<p>
A <b>carnivore</b> is a consumer that feeds mainly on other animals.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<ul>
<li>lion</li>
<li>cheetah</li>
<li>hawk</li>
<li>snake</li>
</ul>

<h3>Position in a food chain</h3>

<p>
Carnivores may feed on herbivores or on other consumers.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → zebra → lion</b>
</p>

<p>
The lion is a carnivore because it feeds on the zebra.
</p>

<h3>Predator and carnivore are not identical terms</h3>

<p>
A predator is an organism that captures and feeds on prey.
</p>

<p>
Carnivore describes an organism''s feeding type: it mainly eats animals.
</p>

<p>
A carnivore may therefore act as a predator, but the two words describe different ideas.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carnivores', 15),
  (967, 967, '
<h2>Food Chain</h2>

<p>
A <b>food chain</b> shows the feeding relationship between organisms and the direction in which food and chemical energy are transferred.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
<b>Grass → grasshopper → frog → snake</b>
</p>

<p>
The grass is eaten by the grasshopper.
</p>

<p>
The grasshopper is eaten by the frog.
</p>

<p>
The frog is eaten by the snake.
</p>

<h3>What does the arrow mean?</h3>

<p>
The arrow shows the direction of transfer of food and chemical energy.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → grasshopper</b>
</p>

<p>
means that the grasshopper obtains food and chemical energy by feeding on the grass.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
The arrow does <b>not</b> mean that the grass moves into the grasshopper.
</p>

<p>
It represents the direction of transfer through feeding.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Food Chain', 15),
  (968, 968, '
<h2>Trophic Levels</h2>

<p>
A <b>trophic level</b> is a feeding position occupied by an organism in a food chain.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → grasshopper → frog → snake</b>
</p>

<p>
The trophic levels are:
</p>

<ul>
<li><b>First trophic level:</b> grass — producer</li>
<li><b>Second trophic level:</b> grasshopper — primary consumer</li>
<li><b>Third trophic level:</b> frog — secondary consumer</li>
<li><b>Fourth trophic level:</b> snake — tertiary consumer</li>
</ul>

<h3>Primary consumer</h3>

<p>
A primary consumer feeds directly on a producer.
</p>

<h3>Secondary consumer</h3>

<p>
A secondary consumer feeds on a primary consumer.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Trophic level depends on the organism''s position in a particular food chain.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Trophic Levels', 15),
  (969, 969, '
<h2>Energy Transfer in Food Chains</h2>

<p>
Energy enters most ecosystems through producers capturing light energy during photosynthesis.
</p>

<p>
When a consumer feeds on a producer, chemical energy stored in the producer''s organic substances is transferred to the consumer.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
<b>Sunlight → grass → zebra → lion</b>
</p>

<p>
The grass captures light energy.
</p>

<p>
The zebra obtains chemical energy by feeding on the grass.
</p>

<p>
The lion obtains chemical energy by feeding on the zebra.
</p>

<h3>Energy is lost</h3>

<p>
Energy is not transferred completely from one trophic level to the next.
</p>

<p>
Energy is used by organisms for processes such as:
</p>

<ul>
<li>respiration</li>
<li>movement</li>
<li>growth</li>
<li>active transport</li>
<li>maintaining body processes</li>
</ul>

<p>
Some energy is also lost in waste and uneaten material.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, less chemical energy is generally available at higher trophic levels.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy Transfer in Food Chains', 15),
  (970, 970, '
<h2>Food Web</h2>

<p>
A <b>food web</b> shows several interconnected food chains within an ecosystem.
</p>

<p>
In real ecosystems, organisms often eat more than one type of food and may be eaten by more than one type of organism.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A grassland might contain:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → grasshopper → frog → snake</b>
</p>

<p>
and also:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → rabbit → hawk</b>
</p>

<p>
If the snake can also eat the frog and the hawk can eat the snake, several chains become connected.
</p>

<p>
The resulting network is a <b>food web</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why food webs are useful</h3>

<p>
They give a more realistic representation of feeding relationships than a single food chain.
</p>

<p>
A change in one population can affect several other populations because organisms are connected through multiple feeding relationships.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Food Web', 15),
  (971, 971, '
<h2>Interpreting a Food Web</h2>

<p>
To interpret a food web correctly, first determine what each arrow represents.
</p>

<p>
In a food web, the arrow points from the <b>food organism toward the organism that feeds on it</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → grasshopper → frog</b>
</p>

<p>
The first arrow means:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass is eaten by the grasshopper.</b>
</p>

<p>
The second arrow means:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grasshopper is eaten by the frog.</b>
</p>

<h3>Finding a predator</h3>

<p>
If several arrows point toward a frog, the organisms at the starting ends of those arrows are possible food sources for the frog.
</p>

<h3>Finding prey</h3>

<p>
If an arrow begins at a grasshopper and points toward a frog, the grasshopper is prey of the frog.
</p>

<h3>Important rule</h3>

<p>
Never interpret the arrow as simply meaning "moves toward." In a food web, it represents the transfer of food and chemical energy through feeding.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interpreting a Food Web', 15),
  (972, 972, '
<h2>Effects of Removing an Organism from a Food Chain</h2>

<p>
Organisms in an ecosystem are connected through feeding relationships. Therefore, changing one population can affect other populations.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → grasshopper → frog → snake</b>
</p>

<p>
Suppose the number of frogs decreases greatly.
</p>

<p>
Fewer frogs are available to eat grasshoppers.
</p>

<p>
The grasshopper population may therefore increase if other factors do not prevent this increase.
</p>

<p>
More grasshoppers may feed on the grass, potentially reducing the amount of grass.
</p>

<h3>Why this happens</h3>

<p>
The frog is connected to both the grasshopper and the snake through feeding relationships.
</p>

<p>
Changing the frog population can therefore affect organisms at different trophic levels.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Food-web effects are not always simple because several predators, prey and environmental factors may act at the same time.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, predictions should be based on the specific relationships shown in the food chain or food web.
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effects of Removing an Organism from a Food Chain', 15),
  (973, 973, '
<h2>Population</h2>

<p>
A <b>population</b> is a group of organisms of the <b>same species</b> living in the same area at the same time.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose a grassland contains 200 zebras.
</p>

<p>
If all 200 belong to the same species and live in that area at the same time, they form a population of that species.
</p>

<h3>What makes a population?</h3>

<p>
Three ideas are important:
</p>

<ol>
<li>The organisms belong to the same species.</li>
<li>They occupy the same area.</li>
<li>They exist there at the same time.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Population size</h3>

<p>
The number of individuals in a population is its <b>population size</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example, if a pond contains 80 frogs of one species, the population size is 80.
</p>

<h3>Do not confuse population with community</h3>

<p>
A population contains individuals of <b>one species</b>.
</p>

<p>
A community contains populations of <b>different species</b> living and interacting in the same area.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Population', 15),
  (974, 974, '
<h2>Competition</h2>

<p>
<b>Competition</b> occurs when organisms require the same limited resource.
</p>

<p>
The resource may include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>food</li>
<li>water</li>
<li>light</li>
<li>mineral ions</li>
<li>space</li>
<li>mates</li>
</ul>

<h3>Competition within a species</h3>

<p>
Individuals of the same species may compete for resources.
</p>

<p>
For example, maize plants growing very close together may compete for light, water and mineral ions.
</p>

<h3>Competition between species</h3>

<p>
Different species may also compete when they require the same limited resource.
</p>

<p>
For example, two plant species growing in the same area may compete for light and water.
</p>

<h3>Why resources must be limited</h3>

<p>
If a resource is unlimited, organisms do not need to compete for access to it.
</p>

<p>
Competition becomes important when demand for a resource is greater than the amount available.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Competition', 15),
  (975, 975, '
<h2>Population Size</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <b>population</b> is a group of organisms of the same species living in the same area at the same time.
</p>

<p>
<b>Population size</b> is the total number of individuals of that species in the area.
</p>

<p>
For example, if a pond contains 240 frogs, the frog population size is <b>240 frogs</b>.
</p>

<h3>How Population Size Changes</h3>

<p>
The number of organisms in a population can increase or decrease.
</p>

<p>
The four processes that change population size are:
</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Births</b> add individuals.</li>
<li><b>Deaths</b> remove individuals.</li>
<li><b>Immigration</b> adds individuals arriving from another area.</li>
<li><b>Emigration</b> removes individuals leaving the area.</li>
</ul>

<p>
A useful way to think about the change is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Population change = births + immigration − deaths − emigration</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A population contains 500 rabbits. During one year, 80 are born and 30 die. No rabbits enter or leave.
</p>

<p>
New population:
</p>

<p>
500 + 80 − 30 = <b>550 rabbits</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A population has 300 birds. During a season, 50 are born, 20 die, 15 immigrate and 10 emigrate.
</p>

<p>
Population change:
</p>

<p>
50 + 15 − 20 − 10 = <b>35</b>
</p>

<p>
New population:
</p>

<p>
300 + 35 = <b>335 birds</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A population decreases from 800 to 720 individuals.
</p>

<p>
Decrease:
</p>

<p>
800 − 720 = <b>80 individuals</b>
</p>

<p>
Percentage decrease:
</p>

<p>
(80 ÷ 800) × 100 = <b>10%</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Population Size', 15),
  (976, 976, '
<h2>Measuring Population Size</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Sometimes it is possible to count every organism in a population.
</p>

<p>
For example, a researcher may count every tree in a small garden.
</p>

<p>
However, counting every organism is often impossible when the population is very large or spread over a large area.
</p>

<p>
Scientists therefore use <b>sampling</b>.
</p>

<h3>What Is Sampling?</h3>

<p>
Sampling means studying a smaller part of a population and using the results to estimate or compare the whole population.
</p>

<p>
The sample should be chosen carefully so that it represents the area being studied.
</p>

<h3>Two Important Sampling Methods</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>Quadrats</b> are commonly used for plants and organisms that do not move much.</li>
<li><b>Capture-mark-recapture</b> can be used to estimate populations of mobile animals.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why Sampling Is Useful</h3>

<p>
Sampling:
</p>

<ul>
<li>saves time;</li>
<li>reduces the amount of counting required;</li>
<li>allows large areas to be studied;</li>
<li>can provide an estimate of population size or density.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A researcher wants to estimate the number of grass plants in a large field.
</p>

<p>
Counting every plant would take a very long time.
</p>

<p>
The researcher places quadrats at selected locations, counts the plants inside them and uses the results to estimate plant density.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A pond contains many fish.
</p>

<p>
It would be difficult to count every fish directly.
</p>

<p>
A researcher can capture, mark and release some fish and later use recaptured fish to estimate the population.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If a sample is poorly chosen, the estimate may be misleading.
</p>

<p>
For example, counting plants only beside a stream may give a poor estimate of the whole field if most of the field is much drier.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Population Size', 15),
  (977, 977, '
<h2>Estimating Population Size</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ecologists often estimate population size rather than count every individual.
</p>

<p>
For plants, this may involve quadrat sampling.
</p>

<p>
For mobile animals, capture-mark-recapture may be used.
</p>

<h3>Estimating From Quadrat Density</h3>

<p>
If the average density is known, estimated population size can be calculated using:
</p>

<p>
<b>Estimated population = mean density × total area</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A grassland has an average density of 5 grass plants/m².
</p>

<p>
The grassland covers 400 m².
</p>

<p>
Estimated population:
</p>

<p>
5 × 400 = <b>2,000 plants</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A plant has a mean density of 3 plants/m² in an area of 600 m².
</p>

<p>
Estimated population:
</p>

<p>
3 × 600 = <b>1,800 plants</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose 1 m² quadrats give a mean of 7 plants per m² and the field covers 250 m².
</p>

<p>
Estimated population:
</p>

<p>
7 × 250 = <b>1,750 plants</b>
</p>

<p>
The answer is an <b>estimate</b>, not necessarily an exact count.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Estimating Population Size', 15),
  (978, 978, '
<h2>Birth Rate and Death Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The size of a population is affected by the rates at which individuals are born and die.
</p>

<p>
<b>Birth rate</b> describes the number of births in a population over a given period.
</p>

<p>
<b>Death rate</b> describes the number of deaths in a population over a given period.
</p>

<p>
Rates allow populations of different sizes to be compared more fairly.
</p>

<h3>Basic Calculation</h3>

<p>
A simple birth rate can be expressed as:
</p>

<p>
<b>Birth rate = number of births ÷ population size × 100</b>
</p>

<p>
This gives births as a percentage of the population over the stated period.
</p>

<p>
Similarly:
</p>

<p>
<b>Death rate = number of deaths ÷ population size × 100</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A population contains 500 animals.
</p>

<p>
25 are born during one year.
</p>

<p>
Birth rate:
</p>

<p>
(25 ÷ 500) × 100 = <b>5%</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A population contains 800 organisms and 16 die in one year.
</p>

<p>
Death rate:
</p>

<p>
(16 ÷ 800) × 100 = <b>2%</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If the birth rate is greater than the death rate, and migration has little effect, the population is likely to increase.
</p>

<p>
If the death rate is greater than the birth rate, the population is likely to decrease.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Birth Rate and Death Rate', 15),
  (979, 979, '
<h2>Factors Affecting Population Size</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A population does not grow indefinitely. Its size is affected by conditions in the environment.
</p>

<p>
Important factors include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>availability of food;</li>
<li>availability of water;</li>
<li>availability of space;</li>
<li>predation;</li>
<li>disease;</li>
<li>competition;</li>
<li>temperature and other abiotic conditions;</li>
<li>birth rate and death rate;</li>
<li>immigration and emigration.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Food Availability</h3>

<p>
If food becomes scarce, organisms may compete more strongly. Some may die or reproduce less successfully.
</p>

<h3>Predation</h3>

<p>
An increase in predator numbers can increase the death rate of prey and reduce prey population size.
</p>

<h3>Disease</h3>

<p>
Disease can increase death rates and may also reduce reproduction if infected organisms become weaker.
</p>

<h3>Competition</h3>

<p>
When many organisms require the same limited resource, competition becomes stronger.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose rainfall decreases greatly.
</p>

<p>
Less water may cause plants to grow poorly.
</p>

<p>
Herbivores then have less food available.
</p>

<p>
Their population may decrease.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose a disease spreads through a rabbit population.
</p>

<p>
More rabbits die.
</p>

<p>
The death rate increases, causing population size to decrease if births do not compensate.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose a population of prey increases greatly.
</p>

<p>
Predators may have more food available and their population may later increase.
</p>

<p>
The larger predator population can then increase predation on the prey.
</p>

<p>
This shows that population size can be affected by interactions between species.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Factors Affecting Population Size', 15),
  (980, 980, '
<h2>Population Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Population growth means an <b>increase in the number of individuals</b> in a population over time.
</p>

<p>
A population grows when individuals are added faster than they are removed.
</p>

<p>
The four processes that determine population change are:
</p>

<ul>
<li>births</li>
<li>deaths</li>
<li>immigration</li>
<li>emigration</li>
</ul>

<p>
A useful relationship is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Population change = births + immigration − deaths − emigration</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A population contains 800 organisms.
</p>

<p>
During one year:
</p>

<ul>
<li>120 are born</li>
<li>50 die</li>
<li>30 immigrate</li>
<li>20 emigrate</li>
</ul>

<p>
Population change:
</p>

<p>
120 + 30 − 50 − 20 = <b>80</b>
</p>

<p>
New population:
</p>

<p>
800 + 80 = <b>880 organisms</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A population starts with 500 organisms.
</p>

<p>
There are 40 births, 70 deaths, 10 immigrants and 5 emigrants.
</p>

<p>
Change:
</p>

<p>
40 + 10 − 70 − 5 = <b>−25</b>
</p>

<p>
New population:
</p>

<p>
500 − 25 = <b>475 organisms</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If births and immigration together are greater than deaths and emigration, the population increases.
</p>

<p>
If deaths and emigration are greater, the population decreases.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Population Growth', 15),
  (981, 981, '
<h2>Exponential Population Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
When resources are abundant and limiting factors have little effect, a population can increase rapidly.
</p>

<p>
If the rate of increase becomes larger as the population becomes larger, the population can show <b>exponential growth</b>.
</p>

<p>
An exponential growth curve is typically <b>J-shaped</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why Does the Curve Become Steeper?</h3>

<p>
Imagine one organism produces several offspring.
</p>

<p>
Those offspring later reproduce too.
</p>

<p>
The number of reproducing individuals can therefore increase rapidly.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose a population doubles every generation:
</p>

<p>
Generation 0 = 10
</p>

<p>
Generation 1 = 20
</p>

<p>
Generation 2 = 40
</p>

<p>
Generation 3 = 80
</p>

<p>
Generation 4 = <b>160</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A population begins with 50 organisms and doubles twice.
</p>

<p>
After the first doubling:
</p>

<p>
50 × 2 = 100
</p>

<p>
After the second:
</p>

<p>
100 × 2 = <b>200</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Exponential growth cannot continue indefinitely in a real ecosystem because resources such as food, water, space and shelter are limited.
</p>

<p>
As limiting factors become stronger, population growth slows.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A J-shaped curve represents rapid population increase under conditions where limiting factors are initially weak. It does <b>not</b> mean that real populations can grow exponentially forever.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Exponential Population Growth', 15),
  (982, 982, '
<h2>Carrying Capacity</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <b>carrying capacity</b> of an environment is the largest population size that the environment can support sustainably under particular conditions.
</p>

<p>
It depends on available resources and environmental conditions.
</p>

<h3>What Determines Carrying Capacity?</h3>

<ul>
<li>food</li>
<li>water</li>
<li>space</li>
<li>shelter</li>
<li>competition</li>
<li>predation</li>
<li>disease</li>
<li>other environmental conditions</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose a grassland can sustainably support about 500 zebras under its current conditions.
</p>

<p>
The carrying capacity is approximately:
</p>

<p>
<b>500 zebras</b>
</p>

<p>
If rainfall decreases and less grass grows, the carrying capacity may decrease.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A lake supports 2,000 fish when food and oxygen are sufficient.
</p>

<p>
Pollution reduces oxygen availability.
</p>

<p>
The lake may now support fewer fish.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, its carrying capacity for fish has decreased.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If a population temporarily rises above the carrying capacity, resources may become insufficient.
</p>

<p>
Competition, starvation or disease may increase.
</p>

<p>
Population size can then fall.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Carrying capacity is not necessarily a permanent fixed number. It can change when environmental conditions change.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carrying Capacity', 15),
  (983, 983, '
<h2>Logistic Population Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
In a natural environment, population growth is usually limited by resources and other environmental factors.
</p>

<p>
A population may initially grow rapidly, but growth slows as the population approaches the environment''s carrying capacity.
</p>

<p>
This pattern is called <b>logistic growth</b>.
</p>

<p>
A logistic growth curve is typically <b>S-shaped</b>.
</p>

<h3>Stages of the Curve</h3>

<ol>
<li><b>Slow initial growth</b> — the population is small.</li>
<li><b>Rapid growth</b> — resources are initially sufficient and many individuals reproduce.</li>
<li><b>Slowing growth</b> — competition and other limiting factors become stronger.</li>
<li><b>Stable region</b> — population fluctuates around carrying capacity.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A population starts at 20 individuals.
</p>

<p>
Food is abundant, so it increases rapidly.
</p>

<p>
As the population approaches 1,000, food and space become limiting.
</p>

<p>
Growth slows.
</p>

<p>
The population eventually fluctuates around approximately 1,000.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
If carrying capacity falls from 1,000 to 600 because of drought, the population may eventually fall toward the new carrying capacity.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If environmental conditions improve and more food becomes available, carrying capacity may increase.
</p>

<p>
The population may then increase toward the new carrying capacity.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Logistic Population Growth', 15),
  (984, 984, '
<h2>Population Fluctuations</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A population does not always remain exactly at one size.
</p>

<p>
Its size may <b>increase and decrease over time</b>.
</p>

<p>
These changes are called <b>population fluctuations</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why Do Populations Fluctuate?</h3>

<p>
Population size can change because of:
</p>

<ul>
<li>changes in food availability;</li>
<li>changes in weather;</li>
<li>disease;</li>
<li>predation;</li>
<li>competition;</li>
<li>changes in birth and death rates.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose a grass population increases after heavy rainfall.
</p>

<p>
More grass provides more food for herbivores.
</p>

<p>
The herbivore population may later increase.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
If the herbivore population becomes very large, food may become scarce.
</p>

<p>
Competition increases.
</p>

<p>
The herbivore population may then decrease.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If the herbivore population decreases, predators may have less food.
</p>

<p>
Predator numbers may later decrease.
</p>

<p>
This can reduce predation pressure on herbivores, allowing their population to increase again.
</p>

<p>
Population changes can therefore be connected to changes in other populations.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Population Fluctuations</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Population size does not always remain constant.
It can rise and fall as environmental conditions and interactions between
organisms change.
</p>

<h3>WHY POPULATIONS FLUCTUATE</h3>

<p>
Population size changes when the numbers of births, deaths, immigration and
emigration change.
</p>

<p>
Environmental factors such as food availability, disease, predation,
competition and weather can alter these rates.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE: PREDATOR AND PREY</h3>

<p>
Consider a population of rabbits and foxes.
</p>

<p>
<strong>More rabbits</strong> → more food available for foxes
</p>

<p>
<strong>More foxes</strong> → greater predation on rabbits
</p>

<p>
<strong>Fewer rabbits</strong> → less food for foxes
</p>

<p>
<strong>Fewer foxes</strong> → reduced predation on rabbits
</p>

<p>
Rabbit numbers can then increase again.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
This does not mean every predator-prey population follows exactly the same
pattern.
Real ecosystems are affected by many factors simultaneously.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why a prey population can decrease after
a period of rapid increase.</p>

<p>
As prey numbers increase, predators may obtain more food and their population
may increase or predation pressure may rise.
More prey are then removed, causing the prey population to decline.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Population size is the result of interacting biological and environmental
processes, not a permanently fixed number.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Population Fluctuations', 15),
  (985, 985, '
<h2>Predator and Prey Populations</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <b>predator</b> is an organism that captures and feeds on another organism called its <b>prey</b>.
</p>

<p>
Predator and prey populations can affect one another.
</p>

<h3>How the Relationship Works</h3>

<p>
If prey become abundant, predators may have more food.
</p>

<p>
Predator survival and reproduction may increase.
</p>

<p>
The predator population can therefore increase.
</p>

<p>
More predators may then consume more prey.
</p>

<p>
The prey population may decrease.
</p>

<p>
With fewer prey available, predators may later have less food, causing their population to decrease.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose rabbits increase because food is abundant.
</p>

<p>
Foxes have more rabbits available as prey.
</p>

<p>
The fox population may increase after a delay.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
More foxes increase predation on rabbits.
</p>

<p>
Rabbit numbers may then fall.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
With fewer rabbits available, some foxes may die or produce fewer offspring.
</p>

<p>
Fox numbers may fall.
</p>

<p>
Reduced predation can then allow rabbit numbers to recover.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
The predator population often responds <b>after</b> changes in prey availability because reproduction and population change take time.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Predator and Prey Populations', 15),
  (986, 986, '
<h2>Competition and Population Size</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Competition becomes stronger when organisms require the same resource and that resource is limited.
</p>

<p>
Strong competition can affect population size because some individuals may obtain insufficient resources for growth, survival or reproduction.
</p>

<h3>What Happens When Population Density Increases?</h3>

<p>
As more individuals occupy the same area:
</p>

<ul>
<li>food may become harder to obtain;</li>
<li>water may become limited;</li>
<li>space may become limited;</li>
<li>shelter may become limited;</li>
<li>competition may increase.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A grassland supports 100 grazing animals while food is abundant.
</p>

<p>
The population increases to 400.
</p>

<p>
More animals now depend on the same grass.
</p>

<p>
Competition for food increases.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
If competition causes some individuals to obtain less food, they may grow more slowly or reproduce less successfully.
</p>

<p>
The population may therefore stop increasing rapidly.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If rainfall increases and more grass becomes available, competition for food may decrease.
</p>

<p>
The population may then have greater opportunity to increase.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Increasing population density can increase competition when resources are limited.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Competition and Population Size', 15),
  (987, 987, '
<h2>Disease and Population Size</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Disease can affect population size by reducing survival and sometimes reducing reproduction.
</p>

<p>
Disease-causing organisms include certain bacteria, viruses, fungi and parasites.
</p>

<h3>Why Can Disease Spread Quickly?</h3>

<p>
When many individuals live close together, an infectious disease may have more opportunities to spread from one host to another.
</p>

<p>
High population density can therefore increase the risk of transmission for some infectious diseases.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A population of 1,000 animals experiences an outbreak of disease.
</p>

<p>
If many animals become infected and die, the death rate increases.
</p>

<p>
The population size decreases.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose a disease reduces the ability of infected individuals to reproduce.
</p>

<p>
Fewer offspring are produced.
</p>

<p>
The birth rate decreases, which can also slow population growth.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If population density is high, infected individuals may be close to many susceptible individuals.
</p>

<p>
This can make transmission easier for diseases that spread through close contact.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Disease does not affect every population in exactly the same way. Transmission depends on the disease, the host organisms and environmental conditions.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Disease and Population Size', 15),
  (988, 988, '
<h2>Abiotic Factors and Population Distribution</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Abiotic factors are the non-living parts of an environment.
</p>

<p>
Examples include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>temperature;</li>
<li>light intensity;</li>
<li>water availability;</li>
<li>pH;</li>
<li>soil mineral content;</li>
<li>oxygen concentration.</li>
</ul>

<p>
These factors can affect where organisms are able to survive and therefore affect their <b>distribution</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A plant species requires moist soil.
</p>

<p>
It may be more common near a stream than on a dry hillside.
</p>

<p>
Water availability therefore affects its distribution.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A plant requires sufficient light for photosynthesis.
</p>

<p>
It may grow poorly in very deep shade.
</p>

<p>
Light intensity can therefore affect where the plant is found.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A species may be absent from an area because one environmental condition is outside the range it can tolerate.
</p>

<p>
For example, if a species cannot tolerate very low temperatures, very cold areas may have few or no individuals of that species.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Abiotic conditions help determine <b>where</b> organisms can survive and how common they are in different places.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Abiotic Factors and Population Distribution', 15),
  (989, 989, '
<h2>Population Growth and Resource Demand</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
As a population increases, the total demand for resources can also increase.
</p>

<p>
People require resources such as:
</p>

<ul>
<li>food;</li>
<li>water;</li>
<li>energy;</li>
<li>land;</li>
<li>raw materials.</li>
</ul>

<p>
The effect of population growth depends not only on the number of people but also on how resources are used.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A community contains 10,000 people.
</p>

<p>
If each person requires an average of 50 litres of water per day, total daily demand is:
</p>

<p>
10,000 × 50 = <b>500,000 litres per day</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
If the population increases to 12,000 while average use remains 50 litres per person per day:
</p>

<p>
12,000 × 50 = <b>600,000 litres per day</b>
</p>

<p>
Daily demand has increased by:
</p>

<p>
600,000 − 500,000 = <b>100,000 litres</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If people reduce their average water use, total demand may increase more slowly even when population size increases.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Population size and resource consumption are related, but resource demand also depends on how much each individual consumes.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Population Growth and Resource Demand', 15),
  (990, 990, '
<h2>Population Carrying Capacity</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A habitat can support only a certain number of individuals of a species
under particular environmental conditions.
</p>

<p>
The maximum population size that the environment can sustain over time under
given conditions is called its <strong>carrying capacity</strong>.
</p>

<h3>WHY CARRYING CAPACITY EXISTS</h3>

<p>
Resources are limited.
Examples include:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>food</li>
  <li>water</li>
  <li>space</li>
  <li>nesting sites</li>
  <li>light</li>
  <li>mineral nutrients</li>
</ul>

<p>
As population density increases, competition for limited resources can become
stronger.
</p>

<h3>POPULATION ABOVE CARRYING CAPACITY</h3>

<p>
If a population temporarily becomes larger than the habitat can support,
resources may become depleted.
</p>

<p>
This can increase competition, starvation or disease and reduce reproduction.
The population may then decline.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Suppose a grassland can normally support about 500 grazing animals.
If the population rises to 800, food may become insufficient.
</p>

<p>
<strong>800 animals → greater competition → less food per animal →
lower survival/reproduction → population decreases.</strong>
</p>

<p>
The carrying capacity is not necessarily a permanent fixed number.
It can change when environmental conditions change.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Population Carrying Capacity', 15),
  (991, 991, '
<h2>Population Growth and Carrying Capacity</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
When resources are abundant and limiting factors are weak, a population can
increase rapidly.
</p>

<p>
As population size increases, competition and other limiting factors can
become stronger.
</p>

<h3>LOGISTIC PATTERN</h3>

<p>
A population may initially increase rapidly.
Growth then slows as the population approaches the carrying capacity.
</p>

<p>
The resulting population pattern is often described as an
<strong>S-shaped growth curve</strong>.
</p>

<h3>THREE STAGES</h3>

<p><strong>1. Early growth:</strong> Population size is relatively small and
resources are abundant.</p>

<p><strong>2. Rapid growth:</strong> Births greatly exceed deaths under favourable conditions.</p>

<p><strong>3. Slowing growth:</strong> Competition and other limiting factors
increase as resources become less available.</p>

<p>
Eventually the population may fluctuate around the carrying capacity.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
A population does not automatically stop growing because organisms somehow
"know" the carrying capacity.
The population changes because environmental factors alter birth and death rates.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why does population growth slow as carrying capacity is approached?</p>

<p>
As population density rises, competition for limited resources increases.
Food and space may become less available per individual.
This can reduce reproduction and increase mortality, causing population growth
to slow.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Population Growth and Carrying Capacity', 15),
  (992, 992, '
<h2>Interpreting a Population Distribution Graph</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ecological data can be displayed on graphs to show how the abundance or
density of an organism changes with distance or another environmental factor.
</p>

<h3>READING THE AXES</h3>

<p>
Always identify:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>what is being measured on the horizontal axis</li>
  <li>what is being measured on the vertical axis</li>
  <li>the units used</li>
</ul>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Suppose a transect measures the number of a plant species at different
distances from a river.
</p>

<p>
The horizontal axis could show:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Distance from river (m)</strong>
</p>

<p>
The vertical axis could show:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Number of plants per quadrat</strong>
</p>

<h3>INTERPRETING A TREND</h3>

<p>
If plant abundance decreases as distance from the river increases, the data
suggest that the species is more abundant closer to the river.
</p>

<p>
A possible explanation could be greater water availability near the river,
but the data alone show the pattern rather than proving the cause.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT SCIENTIFIC DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
A correlation or pattern does not automatically prove causation.
Other environmental factors may also change with distance.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
If the number of plants per quadrat changes from 18 near the river to 4
far from the river, the correct conclusion is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Plant abundance is greater near the river and decreases with distance.
</strong>
</p>

<p>
It would be too strong to conclude that distance itself directly caused
the decrease.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interpreting a Population Distribution Graph', 15),
  (993, 993, '
<h2>Population Density</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Population density</b> is the number of individuals of a species living in a given area.
</p>

<p>
It tells us how closely packed the organisms are.
</p>

<p>
The basic formula is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Population density = number of individuals ÷ area</b>
</p>

<p>
The unit depends on the area being measured. For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>individuals per m²</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A grassland covers 200 m² and contains 100 grass plants.
</p>

<p>
Density = 100 ÷ 200
</p>

<p>
= <b>0.5 plants/m²</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A field of 500 m² contains 750 maize plants.
</p>

<p>
Density = 750 ÷ 500
</p>

<p>
= <b>1.5 plants/m²</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Area = 50 m².
</p>

<p>
Number of organisms = 400.
</p>

<p>
Density = 400 ÷ 50
</p>

<p>
= <b>8 organisms/m²</b>
</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>
Population size tells you <b>how many organisms there are</b>.
</p>

<p>
Population density tells you <b>how many organisms occur per unit area</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Population Density', 15),
  (994, 994, '
<h2>Quadrat Sampling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <b>quadrat</b> is a frame enclosing a known area used to sample organisms, especially plants.
</p>

<p>
For example, a quadrat may cover an area of 1 m².
</p>

<p>
The organisms inside the quadrat are counted and the measurements are repeated at several locations.
</p>

<h3>Basic Procedure</h3>

<ol>
<li>Choose the area to investigate.</li>
<li>Place the quadrat in the study area.</li>
<li>Count the organisms inside it.</li>
<li>Repeat the sampling at several locations.</li>
<li>Calculate the mean number of organisms per quadrat.</li>
<li>Use the mean to estimate density or population size.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A 1 m² quadrat is placed three times.
</p>

<p>
Counts are:
</p>

<p>
6 plants, 10 plants and 8 plants.
</p>

<p>
Mean:
</p>

<p>
(6 + 10 + 8) ÷ 3
</p>

<p>
= 24 ÷ 3
</p>

<p>
= <b>8 plants per m²</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Five 2 m² quadrats contain:
</p>

<p>
12, 16, 10, 14 and 18 plants.
</p>

<p>
Total = 70 plants.
</p>

<p>
Mean per quadrat = 70 ÷ 5 = <b>14 plants</b>.
</p>

<p>
Because each quadrat covers 2 m²:
</p>

<p>
Density = 14 ÷ 2 = <b>7 plants/m²</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If a 1 m² quadrat contains 20 plants, this does not mean the entire field contains exactly 20 plants.
</p>

<p>
The quadrat gives a <b>sample</b>. Several samples are needed to make a more reliable estimate.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Quadrat Sampling', 15),
  (995, 995, '
<h2>Using Quadrats Correctly</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The usefulness of quadrat sampling depends on how the quadrats are placed.
</p>

<p>
If the researcher deliberately chooses only areas containing many plants, the sample may be biased.
</p>

<h3>Random Sampling</h3>

<p>
In <b>random sampling</b>, locations are selected without deliberately choosing areas because they contain more or fewer organisms.
</p>

<p>
A common method is to divide the study area into a grid and use random coordinates to decide where to place quadrats.
</p>

<h3>Systematic Sampling</h3>

<p>
In <b>systematic sampling</b>, quadrats are placed at regular intervals along a line or across an area.
</p>

<p>
This can be useful for investigating how organisms change along an environmental gradient.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A field is divided into a grid.
</p>

<p>
Random numbers are used to select grid coordinates.
</p>

<p>
A quadrat is placed at each selected coordinate.
</p>

<p>
This reduces the risk of the researcher choosing only locations that look interesting.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A researcher places a quadrat every 5 metres along a transect.
</p>

<p>
This is systematic sampling because the locations are selected at regular intervals.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose a field has areas with 2, 10 and 20 plants per m².
</p>

<p>
If the researcher samples only the areas containing 20 plants, the calculated density will probably be much higher than the true average.
</p>

<p>
This is <b>sampling bias</b>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Using Quadrats Correctly', 15),
  (996, 996, '
<h2>Capture-Mark-Recapture</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Capture-mark-recapture</b> is a sampling method used to estimate the population size of mobile animals.
</p>

<h3>Basic Procedure</h3>

<ol>
<li>Capture a sample of animals.</li>
<li>Count and harmlessly mark them.</li>
<li>Release them back into the population.</li>
<li>Allow enough time for them to mix with the population.</li>
<li>Capture a second sample.</li>
<li>Count how many of the second sample are marked.</li>
</ol>

<p>
If many animals in the second sample are marked, the original marked group probably represented a large fraction of the population.
</p>

<p>
If very few are marked, the total population is likely to be much larger than the first sample.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
First capture:
</p>

<p>
50 fish are captured, marked and released.
</p>

<p>
Second capture:
</p>

<p>
100 fish are captured.
</p>

<p>
20 of them are marked.
</p>

<p>
The estimate is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Population ≈ (number marked first × number caught second) ÷ number marked second</b>
</p>

<p>
= (50 × 100) ÷ 20
</p>

<p>
= <b>250 fish</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
20 insects are initially marked.
</p>

<p>
A second sample contains 40 insects, of which 10 are marked.
</p>

<p>
Estimated population:
</p>

<p>
(20 × 40) ÷ 10 = <b>80 insects</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If the second sample contains a high proportion of marked animals, the estimated population tends to be smaller.
</p>

<p>
If the second sample contains a low proportion of marked animals, the estimated population tends to be larger.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Capture-Mark-Recapture</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Capture-mark-recapture</strong> is a method used to estimate the size
of a population of mobile animals.
</p>

<p>
It is useful when animals move around an area and cannot easily be counted
directly.
</p>

<h3>THE BASIC METHOD</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Capture a number of animals.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Mark them harmlessly.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Release them back into the population.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Allow enough time for them to mix with the population.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Capture another sample.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> Count how many animals in the second sample are marked.</p>

<h3>THE ASSUMPTION</h3>

<p>
The method assumes that marked animals mix back into the population and have
a reasonable chance of being captured again.
</p>

<h3>ESTIMATION FORMULA</h3>

<p>
A commonly used estimate is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Population estimate =
(first sample × second sample) ÷ number of marked animals recaptured
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
100 animals are captured and marked.
Later, 80 animals are captured.
20 of the 80 are marked.
</p>

<p>
Estimated population:
</p>

<p>
<strong>(100 × 80) ÷ 20 = 400 animals</strong>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The result is an estimate and depends on whether the assumptions of the method
are reasonably satisfied.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Capture-Mark-Recapture', 15),
  (997, 997, '
<h2>Density-Dependent Factors</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>density-dependent factor</strong> has a stronger effect on a
population as population density increases.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Competition</li>
  <li>Spread of infectious disease</li>
  <li>Predation</li>
  <li>Food shortage</li>
</ul>

<h3>COMPETITION</h3>

<p>
When many individuals occupy the same area, they may compete more strongly
for food, water, space or mates.
</p>

<h3>DISEASE</h3>

<p>
High population density can increase the frequency of contact between
individuals.
This can make transmission of some infectious diseases easier.
</p>

<h3>PREDATION</h3>

<p>
Changes in population density can affect interactions between predators and
prey.
Predator numbers may increase when prey are abundant, while increased
predation can then reduce prey numbers.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can disease spread more rapidly in a dense population?</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> More individuals occupy the same area.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Individuals are more likely to come into contact.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> A pathogen can therefore have more opportunities to
move between hosts.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Disease transmission can increase.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Density-dependent factors help prevent populations from increasing
indefinitely.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Density-Dependent Factors', 15),
  (998, 998, '
<h2>Density-Independent Factors</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>density-independent factor</strong> can affect a population regardless
of how densely populated the area is.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Drought</li>
  <li>Flooding</li>
  <li>Extreme temperatures</li>
  <li>Wildfires</li>
  <li>Major storms</li>
</ul>

<h3>WHY DENSITY DOES NOT DETERMINE THE EFFECT</h3>

<p>
A severe drought can reduce water availability whether a population contains
100 individuals or 1,000 individuals.
</p>

<p>
The factor itself is not caused by the number of organisms present.
</p>

<h3>COMPARISON</h3>

<p>
<strong>Density-dependent:</strong>
effect becomes stronger as population density increases.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Density-independent:</strong>
effect does not depend primarily on population density.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why drought can be density-independent.</p>

<p>
A drought is caused by a lack of rainfall and water availability.
It can affect organisms whether the population is small or large.
Therefore, its occurrence is not dependent on population density.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
A factor can affect populations in different ways.
The key question is whether its effect depends on population density.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Density-Independent Factors', 15),
  (999, 999, '
<h2>Sampling in Ecology</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ecologists often study organisms over areas that are too large to examine
completely.
Instead of counting every organism, they select a smaller part of the habitat
and use it as a <strong>sample</strong>.
</p>

<p>
The sample should provide information that represents the larger population
or community being studied.
</p>

<h3>WHY SAMPLING IS USED</h3>

<p>
Suppose a grassland contains thousands of plants.
Counting every plant would be slow and difficult.
An ecologist can sample several smaller areas and use the results to estimate
the abundance and distribution of plants across the grassland.
</p>

<h3>REPRESENTATIVE SAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A good sample should reflect the conditions of the area being investigated.
If the researcher deliberately samples only areas with unusually many plants,
the estimate may be biased.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT FEATURES OF GOOD SAMPLING</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Use an appropriate sampling method.</li>
  <li>Take enough samples.</li>
  <li>Select sampling locations systematically or randomly where appropriate.</li>
  <li>Keep sampling conditions consistent.</li>
  <li>Record results accurately.</li>
</ul>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why might an ecologist use samples instead of counting
every plant in a large field?</p>

<p>
The field may contain too many plants to count individually.
Sampling allows the ecologist to collect data from smaller areas and use the
results to estimate the population across the larger habitat.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sampling in Ecology', 15),
  (1000, 1000, '
<h2>Random Sampling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Random sampling</strong> means selecting sampling locations so that
each suitable location has an equal or known chance of being selected.
</p>

<p>
It helps reduce researcher bias when estimating the distribution or abundance
of organisms.
</p>

<h3>WHY RANDOM SAMPLING MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Imagine a field containing areas with many plants and areas with few plants.
If a researcher chooses only locations where plants are abundant, the estimate
will probably be too high.
</p>

<p>
Random selection reduces the temptation to choose locations based on what the
researcher expects to find.
</p>

<h3>HOW TO RANDOMLY SAMPLE AN AREA</h3>

<p>
A grid can be placed over the study area.
Each grid square can be given a number.
Random numbers can then be generated to select the squares to investigate.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Divide the study area into equal sections.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Number the sections.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Generate random numbers.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Place the sampling equipment at the selected locations.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Record the organisms found.</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Random sampling helps reduce selection bias.
It does not guarantee that every sample will contain the same number of
organisms.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Random Sampling', 15);
INSERT INTO public.lessons (id, topic_id, content, summary, estimated_minutes) VALUES
  (1001, 1001, '
<h2>Systematic Sampling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Systematic sampling</strong> involves taking samples at regular
intervals along a predetermined line or across an area.
</p>

<p>
It is particularly useful when investigating how the distribution of an
organism changes across an environmental gradient.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
An ecologist wants to investigate how plant abundance changes from the edge
of a forest into an open field.
</p>

<p>
A line is placed from the forest edge toward the field.
Quadrats are placed at regular distances along the line.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
0 m → 2 m → 4 m → 6 m → 8 m → 10 m
</p>

<p>
The number of organisms in each quadrat is recorded.
</p>

<h3>WHY THIS IS USEFUL</h3>

<p>
The researcher can identify changes in distribution along the environmental
gradient.
</p>

<h3>RANDOM VS SYSTEMATIC</h3>

<p>
<strong>Random:</strong> sampling positions are selected randomly.</p>

<p>
<strong>Systematic:</strong> sampling positions follow a regular pattern or
interval.</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why would systematic sampling be useful when
studying vegetation from a river bank toward a dry area?</p>

<p>
Conditions such as soil moisture may change gradually with distance from the
river.
Sampling at regular distances allows the researcher to detect how plant
distribution changes along this gradient.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Systematic Sampling', 15),
  (1002, 1002, '
<h2>Using a Quadrat</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>quadrat</strong> is a frame of known area used to sample organisms
within a habitat.
</p>

<p>
Quadrats are particularly useful for studying organisms that do not move much,
such as grasses, mosses and small plants.
</p>

<h3>HOW TO USE ONE</h3>

<p>
The quadrat is placed on the ground at a selected sampling location.
The organisms inside the frame are identified and counted or their abundance
is estimated.
</p>

<h3>WHAT CAN BE RECORDED?</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Number of individual plants</li>
  <li>Presence or absence of a species</li>
  <li>Percentage cover</li>
  <li>Frequency of occurrence</li>
</ul>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The same quadrat size and counting method should be used consistently if
different samples are going to be compared.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A 1 m² quadrat is placed in a grassland.
Eight grass plants are recorded inside it.
</p>

<p>
The measured density for that quadrat is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>8 plants ÷ 1 m² = 8 plants/m²</strong>
</p>

<p>
Several quadrats should normally be sampled before making an estimate for
the entire habitat.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Using a Quadrat', 15),
  (1003, 1003, '
<h2>Estimating Population Size from Quadrat Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Quadrat samples can be used to estimate the total number of organisms in a
larger habitat.
</p>

<h3>THE BASIC METHOD</h3>

<p>
First calculate the average number of organisms per unit area.
Then multiply this density by the total area of the habitat.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Estimated population =
mean number per unit area × total habitat area</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Suppose five 1 m² quadrats contain:
</p>

<p>
8, 10, 12, 9 and 11 plants.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1: Add the counts.</strong></p>

<p>
8 + 10 + 12 + 9 + 11 = 50
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2: Find the mean.</strong></p>

<p>
50 ÷ 5 = 10 plants/m²
</p>

<p>
Suppose the entire habitat has an area of 2,000 m².
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3: Estimate total population.</strong></p>

<p>
10 × 2,000 = <strong>20,000 plants</strong>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
This is an estimate, not an exact count.
Its reliability depends on how representative the sampled quadrats are.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Estimating Population Size from Quadrat Data', 15),
  (1004, 1004, '
<h2>Reliability of Ecological Sampling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ecological measurements are often estimates.
Scientists therefore need to consider how reliable their results are.
</p>

<h3>REPEATED SAMPLES</h3>

<p>
Taking several samples is generally more reliable than relying on one sample.
One quadrat may happen to contain unusually many or unusually few organisms.
</p>

<p>
Repeated sampling reduces the influence of such unusual results.
</p>

<h3>SAMPLE SIZE</h3>

<p>
A larger sample size usually provides a better representation of the habitat,
provided the samples are collected appropriately.
</p>

<h3>CONSISTENT METHOD</h3>

<p>
The same equipment, area and counting method should be used when comparing
different sites or treatments.
</p>

<h3>BIAS</h3>

<p>
Bias occurs when the method systematically favours certain results.
For example, deliberately selecting only areas with many plants can produce
an overestimate of plant abundance.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why is taking 20 quadrats generally better than taking
only one quadrat?</p>

<p>
A single quadrat may not represent the whole habitat.
Twenty appropriately distributed quadrats provide more information and reduce
the effect of unusual individual samples.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
More data does not automatically make an investigation valid.
The sampling method must also be appropriate and unbiased.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reliability of Ecological Sampling', 15),
  (1005, 1005, '
<h2>Transect Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>transect</strong> is a line or strip across a habitat used to
investigate how organisms are distributed along an environmental gradient.
</p>

<p>
A transect is particularly useful when conditions change gradually across
an area.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Imagine a rocky shore where conditions change from the high-water mark toward
the sea.
</p>

<p>
Different organisms may occur at different positions because factors such as
water availability, exposure and salinity change with location.
</p>

<h3>LINE TRANSECT</h3>

<p>
A line is placed across the area.
The organisms touching or occurring directly along the line are recorded at
different positions.
</p>

<h3>BELT TRANSECT</h3>

<p>
A strip of known width is sampled along the transect.
Quadrats can be placed at regular intervals within the strip.
</p>

<h3>WHY TRANSECTS ARE USEFUL</h3>

<p>
They show how the distribution or abundance of organisms changes with
distance from one part of a habitat to another.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why would a transect be useful for studying vegetation
from a river bank toward a dry grassland?</p>

<p>
Water availability is likely to change with distance from the river.
A transect allows the researcher to record plant distribution at different
positions along this environmental gradient.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transect Investigation', 15),
  (1006, 1006, '
<h2>Frequency of an Organism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
In ecology, <strong>frequency</strong> describes how often a particular species
occurs within a set of sampling areas.
</p>

<p>
For example, if 10 quadrats are examined and a plant occurs in 7 of them,
its frequency is 7 out of 10 quadrats.
</p>

<h3>CALCULATING FREQUENCY</h3>

<p>
Frequency can be expressed as a percentage:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Frequency (%) = number of quadrats containing the species ÷ total number of
quadrats × 100
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher examines 20 quadrats.
A particular grass species occurs in 12 quadrats.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Number of quadrats containing the species = 12.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Total quadrats = 20.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong>
</p>

<p>
Frequency = (12 ÷ 20) × 100
</p>

<p>
= 0.6 × 100
</p>

<p>
= <strong>60%</strong>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Frequency tells us how widely a species occurs across the sampled locations.
It does not tell us how many individuals occur in each quadrat.
</p>

<p>
A species can have high frequency but low abundance if it occurs in many
quadrats but only a few individuals occur in each one.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Frequency of an Organism', 15),
  (1007, 1007, '
<h2>Abundance and Density</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Abundance</strong> refers to the amount or number of individuals of
a species found in a particular area or sample.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Density</strong> expresses the number of individuals per unit area
or volume.
</p>

<h3>DENSITY</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Density = number of individuals ÷ area
</strong>
</p>

<p>
The unit might be individuals per square metre, such as plants/m².
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher counts 45 grass plants in an area of 15 m².
</p>

<p>
Density = 45 ÷ 15
</p>

<p>
= <strong>3 plants/m²</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHY DENSITY IS USEFUL</h3>

<p>
Simply saying that one field contains 100 plants and another contains
50 plants is not enough if the fields have different sizes.
</p>

<p>
Density allows the populations to be compared fairly.
</p>

<h3>COMPARISON</h3>

<p>
Field A: 100 plants in 100 m²
</p>

<p>
Density = 1 plant/m²
</p>

<p>
Field B: 50 plants in 10 m²
</p>

<p>
Density = 5 plants/m²
</p>

<p>
Although Field A contains more plants in total, Field B has the greater
population density.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Abundance and Density', 15),
  (1008, 1008, '
<h2>Sampling Bias</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Sampling bias</strong> occurs when the sampling method systematically
favours some parts of a population or habitat over others.
</p>

<p>
Bias can cause an estimate to be consistently too high or too low.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher wants to estimate the abundance of grass across a large field.
They place all quadrats near a water source because the plants are easy to
see there.
</p>

<p>
If grass is more abundant near the water, the resulting estimate may be too high
for the entire field.
</p>

<h3>ANOTHER EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher chooses sampling locations based on where they expect to find
the target species.
</p>

<p>
This is not genuinely random sampling because the researcher''s judgement has
influenced selection.
</p>

<h3>REDUCING BIAS</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Use random selection where appropriate.</li>
  <li>Use systematic sampling when studying gradients.</li>
  <li>Sample across the whole study area.</li>
  <li>Use enough sampling points.</li>
  <li>Use a consistent method.</li>
</ul>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can choosing only areas with many plants produce
an inaccurate population estimate?</p>

<p>
The selected areas are not representative of the whole habitat.
Because high-abundance areas are deliberately favoured, the calculated estimate
is likely to overestimate the true average abundance.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
A large number of biased samples can still produce a biased answer.
Good sampling requires both sufficient data and appropriate selection.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sampling Bias', 15),
  (1009, 1009, '
<h2>Decomposers</h2>

<p>
<b>Decomposers</b> are organisms that obtain nutrients by breaking down dead organic material and waste.
</p>

<p>
Important decomposers include many:
</p>

<ul>
<li>bacteria</li>
<li>fungi</li>
</ul>

<h3>What happens during decomposition?</h3>

<p>
Dead organisms contain organic substances and mineral nutrients.
</p>

<p>
Decomposers break down the organic material and absorb nutrients for their own use.
</p>

<p>
This contributes to the return of nutrients to the environment.
</p>

<h3>Why decomposers are important</h3>

<p>
Without decomposition, dead organic material would accumulate and nutrients would remain locked in dead material for longer.
</p>

<p>
Decomposition therefore contributes to <b>nutrient cycling</b> in ecosystems.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
When a dead leaf falls onto soil, fungi and bacteria can break down its organic material.
</p>

<p>
The nutrients can eventually become available again in the environment.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Decomposers', 15),
  (1010, 1010, '
<h2>Predator-Prey Cycles</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Predator and prey populations can show repeated rises and falls known as <b>predator-prey cycles</b>.
</p>

<p>
The changes are linked because each population affects the other.
</p>

<h3>The Sequence</h3>

<ol>
<li>Prey numbers increase.</li>
<li>More food becomes available to predators.</li>
<li>Predator numbers increase.</li>
<li>More prey are eaten.</li>
<li>Prey numbers decrease.</li>
<li>Less food is available to predators.</li>
<li>Predator numbers decrease.</li>
<li>Reduced predation allows prey numbers to recover.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose a rabbit population rises from 200 to 500.
</p>

<p>
Foxes now have more food.
</p>

<p>
After some time, fox numbers increase.
</p>

<p>
The increased fox population causes rabbit numbers to fall.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
If rabbits fall sharply, foxes may experience food shortage.
</p>

<p>
Fox numbers may then fall.
</p>

<p>
Fewer foxes means less predation.
</p>

<p>
Rabbit numbers may begin increasing again.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
The two populations do not necessarily reach their maximum and minimum values at exactly the same time.
</p>

<p>
A predator population often peaks <b>after</b> the prey population has increased because predators need time to reproduce and increase in number.
</p>

<h3>Exam Reasoning</h3>

<p>
If asked why predator numbers rise after prey numbers rise, link the steps:
</p>

<p>
<b>More prey → more food → greater predator survival/reproduction → predator population increases.</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Predator-Prey Cycles', 15),
  (1011, 1011, '
<h2>Carbon Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <strong>carbon cycle</strong> describes how carbon moves between the atmosphere,
living organisms, dead organic matter, soil, oceans and fossil fuels.
</p>

<p>
Carbon is important because it forms part of carbohydrates, lipids, proteins,
nucleic acids and many other compounds found in living organisms.
</p>

<h3>THE MAIN CARBON RESERVOIRS</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Atmosphere:</strong> mainly carbon dioxide (CO₂).</li>
  <li><strong>Living organisms:</strong> carbon-containing organic compounds.</li>
  <li><strong>Dead organic matter and soil:</strong> carbon in dead organisms and organic material.</li>
  <li><strong>Fossil fuels:</strong> carbon stored for very long periods.</li>
  <li><strong>Oceans:</strong> dissolved carbon dioxide and other dissolved carbon compounds.</li>
</ul>

<h3>HOW CARBON MOVES</h3>

<p>
Plants remove carbon dioxide from the atmosphere during photosynthesis.
The carbon becomes part of organic compounds such as glucose.
</p>

<p>
Animals obtain this carbon by feeding on plants or other animals.
Carbon then moves through food chains.
</p>

<p>
Respiration returns some carbon to the atmosphere as carbon dioxide.
When organisms die, decomposers break down their remains and release carbon
compounds, including carbon dioxide through respiration.
</p>

<p>
Combustion of wood and fossil fuels also releases carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT IDEA</h3>

<p>
Carbon is not created or destroyed during the cycle. It is transferred between
different forms and reservoirs.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain how carbon in atmospheric carbon dioxide can become part of an animal.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> A plant absorbs carbon dioxide during photosynthesis.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The carbon becomes incorporated into organic compounds in the plant.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> An animal eats the plant.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The carbon-containing compounds are transferred into the animal''s body.</p>

<p>
Therefore, carbon can move from the atmosphere → plant → animal through
photosynthesis and feeding.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carbon Cycle', 15),
  (1012, 1012, '
<h2>Decomposition and the Carbon Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
When plants and animals die, their bodies contain carbon in organic compounds.
That carbon must be returned to the environment.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Decomposers</strong>, especially bacteria and fungi, break down dead
organic material and waste.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS TO THE CARBON?</h3>

<p>
Decomposer organisms obtain nutrients and energy from organic material.
During their respiration, some of the carbon is released as carbon dioxide.
</p>

<p>
Some carbon also remains in the soil as organic matter.
Over long periods, some carbon-containing material can become buried and,
under suitable conditions, contribute to fossil fuel formation.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Imagine a tree leaf falls onto the soil.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> The leaf contains carbon-containing organic compounds.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Fungi and bacteria colonise the dead leaf.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Decomposers break down the organic material.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Decomposer respiration releases some carbon as carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Some remaining material contributes to soil organic matter.</p>

<h3>WHY DECOMPOSITION MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Without decomposition, dead organic material would accumulate and nutrients
would remain locked in dead material for much longer.
</p>

<p>
Decomposition therefore connects dead organisms back to nutrient cycling.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Decomposition and the Carbon Cycle', 15),
  (1013, 1013, '
<h2>Combustion and the Carbon Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Combustion</strong> is burning.
When carbon-containing fuels burn in oxygen, carbon is commonly released as
carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>BIOMASS COMBUSTION</h3>

<p>
Wood contains carbon in organic compounds.
When wood burns, the carbon reacts during combustion and much of it is released
into the atmosphere as carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>FOSSIL FUEL COMBUSTION</h3>

<p>
Coal, petroleum and natural gas contain carbon that was stored over very long
periods.
</p>

<p>
When fossil fuels are burned, this stored carbon is transferred into the
atmosphere mainly as carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP</h3>

<p><strong>Example:</strong> Burning coal to produce electricity.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Coal contains stored carbon.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Coal is burned in oxygen.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Carbon-containing compounds react during combustion.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Carbon dioxide is produced.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Carbon dioxide enters the atmosphere.</p>

<h3>WHY FOSSIL FUELS MATTER</h3>

<p>
Fossil fuels contain carbon that has been stored for extremely long periods.
Burning them transfers some of that stored carbon into the atmosphere much
faster than the natural processes that originally stored it.
</p>

<h3>KEY DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Combustion does not create carbon from nothing.
It transfers carbon already present in the fuel into other compounds,
particularly carbon dioxide.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Combustion and the Carbon Cycle', 15),
  (1014, 1014, '
<h2>Nitrogen Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <strong>nitrogen cycle</strong> describes how nitrogen moves between the
atmosphere, soil and living organisms.
</p>

<p>
Nitrogen is essential because it is part of amino acids, proteins and nucleic
acids.
</p>

<h3>THE PROBLEM WITH ATMOSPHERIC NITROGEN</h3>

<p>
Atmospheric air contains a large proportion of nitrogen gas, N₂.
However, most plants cannot use atmospheric N₂ directly to make proteins.
</p>

<p>
Nitrogen must therefore be converted into usable nitrogen-containing compounds.
</p>

<h3>MAIN PROCESSES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Nitrogen fixation</li>
  <li>Nitrification</li>
  <li>Assimilation by plants</li>
  <li>Feeding and transfer through food chains</li>
  <li>Decomposition</li>
  <li>Denitrification</li>
</ul>

<h3>FOLLOWING NITROGEN</h3>

<p>
A nitrogen atom may move from atmospheric nitrogen into soil compounds,
then into a plant, then into an animal, and eventually back toward the atmosphere.
</p>

<p>
The nitrogen atom is being transferred between different chemical forms and
organisms.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Nitrogen is continuously recycled rather than being permanently used up by
living organisms.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nitrogen Cycle', 15),
  (1015, 1015, '
<h2>Nitrogen Fixation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Nitrogen fixation</strong> is the conversion of atmospheric nitrogen gas
into nitrogen-containing compounds that can enter the nitrogen cycle.
</p>

<h3>BIOLOGICAL NITROGEN FIXATION</h3>

<p>
Certain bacteria can convert atmospheric nitrogen into compounds that become
available in the soil.
</p>

<p>
Some nitrogen-fixing bacteria live freely in soil, while others live in
association with plant roots.
</p>

<h3>ROOT NODULES</h3>

<p>
Leguminous plants such as beans and peas can form root nodules containing
nitrogen-fixing bacteria.
</p>

<p>
The bacteria obtain suitable conditions and resources from the plant, while
the association contributes to nitrogen availability in the soil system.
</p>

<h3>WHY FIXATION IS NECESSARY</h3>

<p>
Atmospheric N₂ contains two nitrogen atoms joined by a strong triple bond.
Most plants cannot directly use this nitrogen gas to manufacture proteins.
</p>

<p>
Nitrogen fixation converts nitrogen into forms that can participate in later
steps of the nitrogen cycle.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why are nitrogen-fixing bacteria important to plants?</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> The atmosphere contains abundant nitrogen gas.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Most plants cannot use atmospheric N₂ directly.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert atmospheric nitrogen
into usable nitrogen-containing compounds.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Plants can then obtain nitrogen from the soil and use it
to make nitrogen-containing organic compounds.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nitrogen Fixation', 15),
  (1016, 1016, '
<h2>Decomposition and Nitrogen Recycling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Nitrogen does not disappear when an organism dies.
Nitrogen-containing compounds remain in the dead organism and its waste.
</p>

<p>
Decomposers such as bacteria and fungi break down this organic material.
This allows nitrogen to return to the soil in simpler forms.
</p>

<h3>FOLLOWING THE NITROGEN</h3>

<p>
Imagine a plant containing proteins dies.
</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The dead plant contains nitrogen in proteins and other compounds.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Decomposers break down the dead material.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Nitrogen-containing organic compounds are converted into simpler nitrogen compounds.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> These compounds become part of the soil nitrogen pool.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Other bacteria can convert the nitrogen into forms such as nitrate.</p>

<p><strong>Step 6:</strong> Plants can absorb the nitrate through their roots.</p>

<h3>WHY THIS IS RECYCLING</h3>

<p>
The nitrogen originally in a living organism is returned to the environment
and can later be taken up by another organism.
</p>

<p>
This is why decomposers are essential to nutrient cycles.
</p>

<h3>KEY DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Decomposition breaks down dead organic material.
It is not the same process as nitrification.
Nitrification specifically refers to bacterial conversion of ammonia/ammonium
into nitrite and then nitrate.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Decomposition and Nitrogen Recycling', 15),
  (1017, 1017, '
<h2>Nitrogen in Food Chains</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Nitrogen moves through food chains because organisms contain nitrogen-containing
organic compounds.
</p>

<p>
Plants absorb usable nitrogen compounds from the soil and use the nitrogen to
make amino acids, proteins and other compounds.
</p>

<p>
Animals obtain nitrogen by feeding on plants or other animals.
</p>

<h3>FOLLOWING NITROGEN</h3>

<p>
Consider this food chain:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Grass → Grasshopper → Frog → Snake</strong>
</p>

<p>
The grass contains nitrogen in proteins and other nitrogen-containing compounds.
</p>

<p>
When the grasshopper feeds on the grass, nitrogen-containing compounds enter
the grasshopper''s body.
</p>

<p>
The frog obtains nitrogen when it feeds on the grasshopper.
The snake obtains nitrogen when it feeds on the frog.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS AFTER DEATH?</h3>

<p>
When any organism dies, decomposers break down its remains.
Nitrogen can therefore return to the soil and eventually become available
to plants again.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Energy and nitrogen behave differently in ecosystems.
Energy flows through food chains and is eventually dissipated as heat.
Nitrogen is an element and is continuously recycled between organisms and
the environment.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> How can nitrogen move from soil to a carnivorous animal?</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Plant roots absorb usable nitrogen compounds from the soil.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> The plant uses nitrogen to make proteins.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> A herbivore eats the plant.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> A carnivore eats the herbivore.</p>

<p>
Therefore, nitrogen has moved from the soil → plant → herbivore → carnivore.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nitrogen in Food Chains', 15),
  (1018, 1018, '
<h2>Fertiliser and Nitrogen Availability</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Plants require mineral ions from the soil for healthy growth.
Nitrogen is one of the important mineral nutrients.
</p>

<p>
Farmers can add fertilisers to soil to increase the availability of mineral
ions such as nitrate.
</p>

<h3>WHY FERTILISER CAN INCREASE GROWTH</h3>

<p>
If nitrogen is limiting plant growth, adding a suitable nitrogen fertiliser
can increase the supply of nitrogen available to the roots.
</p>

<p>
The plant can then make more nitrogen-containing compounds needed for growth.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Plants absorb nitrate from the soil.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Nitrogen is used to make amino acids and proteins.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Protein production supports growth and cell functions.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> If nitrogen is limiting, adding fertiliser can increase nitrogen availability.</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT LIMIT</h3>

<p>
Adding more fertiliser does not mean a plant will continue growing indefinitely.
Growth can become limited by other factors such as light, water, temperature
or another mineral nutrient.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why might adding nitrogen fertiliser increase crop growth?</p>

<p>
If nitrogen is deficient, the plant cannot produce enough nitrogen-containing
compounds for normal growth. Adding an appropriate nitrogen fertiliser
increases nitrogen availability, allowing the plant to make the compounds
it needs.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fertiliser and Nitrogen Availability', 15),
  (1019, 1019, '
<h2>Water Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <strong>water cycle</strong> describes the continuous movement of water
between the atmosphere, land, organisms and bodies of water.
</p>

<h3>MAJOR PROCESSES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Evaporation</li>
  <li>Transpiration</li>
  <li>Condensation</li>
  <li>Precipitation</li>
  <li>Collection and runoff</li>
  <li>Infiltration</li>
</ul>

<h3>EVAPORATION</h3>

<p>
Liquid water at surfaces gains energy and changes into water vapour.
</p>

<h3>TRANSPIRATION</h3>

<p>
Plants lose water vapour from their aerial parts, mainly through stomata.
This process is called transpiration.
</p>

<h3>CONDENSATION</h3>

<p>
Water vapour cools and changes into tiny liquid water droplets.
</p>

<h3>PRECIPITATION</h3>

<p>
Water falls from clouds to Earth''s surface as rain, hail, sleet or snow,
depending on atmospheric conditions.
</p>

<h3>FOLLOWING ONE WATER MOLECULE</h3>

<p>
A water molecule in a lake can evaporate into the atmosphere.
It may later condense into a cloud droplet, fall as rain, enter the soil,
be absorbed by a plant and eventually leave the plant through transpiration.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Water Cycle', 15),
  (1020, 1020, '
<h2>Nutrient Cycling and Ecosystem Stability</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Nutrients such as carbon and nitrogen are continuously transferred between
organisms and the physical environment.
</p>

<p>
This recycling is essential because organisms require these elements to build
new cells and carry out biological processes.
</p>

<h3>THE ROLE OF PRODUCERS</h3>

<p>
Plants obtain carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and mineral nutrients from
the soil.
They incorporate these materials into organic compounds.
</p>

<h3>THE ROLE OF CONSUMERS</h3>

<p>
Animals obtain nutrients by feeding on plants or other animals.
Nutrients therefore move through food chains.
</p>

<h3>THE ROLE OF DECOMPOSERS</h3>

<p>
When organisms die or produce waste, decomposers break down organic material.
This returns nutrients to the environment in forms that can re-enter cycles.
</p>

<h3>WHY RECYCLING MATTERS</h3>

<p>
If nutrients were permanently locked inside dead organisms, the availability
of essential elements to living organisms would eventually become limited.
</p>

<p>
Decomposition and other processes return these elements to environmental
reservoirs, allowing them to be reused.
</p>

<h3>COMPLETE REASONING</h3>

<p>
<strong>Environment → producers → consumers → decomposers → environment</strong>
</p>

<p>
The exact chemical form may change at each stage, but the elements themselves
are continually recycled.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Nutrients cycle through ecosystems.
Energy does not cycle in the same way. Energy enters ecosystems mainly through
sunlight, passes through organisms and is eventually dissipated as heat.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nutrient Cycling and Ecosystem Stability', 15),
  (1021, 1021, '
<h2>Habitat Destruction and Biodiversity</h2>

<p>
A <b>habitat</b> is the place where an organism normally lives and obtains the conditions and resources it needs.
</p>

<p>
When a habitat is destroyed or severely changed, organisms living there may lose food, shelter, breeding sites or suitable environmental conditions.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose a forest is cleared.
</p>

<p>
Before clearing, the forest may provide:
</p>

<ul>
<li>nesting sites for birds</li>
<li>food for insects</li>
<li>shelter for mammals</li>
<li>growing conditions for plants</li>
</ul>

<p>
After clearing, many of these resources may disappear.
</p>

<p>
Some organisms may move elsewhere, while others may fail to survive or reproduce successfully.
</p>

<h3>Effect on biodiversity</h3>

<p>
If species are lost from the habitat, the biodiversity of that area can decrease.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, protecting habitats is an important part of conserving biodiversity.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Habitat Destruction and Biodiversity', 15),
  (1022, 1022, '
<h2>Human Population Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Human population size changes through the same basic processes that affect other populations:
</p>

<ul>
<li>births;</li>
<li>deaths;</li>
<li>immigration;</li>
<li>emigration.</li>
</ul>

<p>
When births and immigration are greater than deaths and emigration, population size increases.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A town has 20,000 people.
</p>

<p>
During one year:
</p>

<ul>
<li>600 births occur;</li>
<li>200 deaths occur;</li>
<li>300 people immigrate;</li>
<li>100 people emigrate.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Population change:
</p>

<p>
600 + 300 − 200 − 100 = <b>600</b>
</p>

<p>
New population:
</p>

<p>
20,000 + 600 = <b>20,600</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
If the number of births remains high while the death rate falls, population growth can increase.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If deaths and emigration exceed births and immigration, population size decreases.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Population growth does not mean that every individual has more children. It refers to a change in the total number of people in a population.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Human Population Growth', 15),
  (1023, 1023, '
<h2>Population Density and Human Settlements</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Human population density describes the number of people living per unit area.
</p>

<p>
A common unit is:
</p>

<p>
<b>people per km²</b>
</p>

<p>
Population density can vary greatly between different areas.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A town has 50,000 people and covers 100 km².
</p>

<p>
Population density:
</p>

<p>
50,000 ÷ 100 = <b>500 people/km²</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Area A contains 20,000 people in 200 km².
</p>

<p>
Density:
</p>

<p>
20,000 ÷ 200 = <b>100 people/km²</b>
</p>

<p>
Area B contains 15,000 people in 50 km².
</p>

<p>
Density:
</p>

<p>
15,000 ÷ 50 = <b>300 people/km²</b>
</p>

<p>
Area B has the higher population density even though it has fewer people overall.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A high population density means many people occupy each unit of area.
</p>

<p>
It does not necessarily mean the total population is larger.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Population Density and Human Settlements', 15),
  (1024, 1024, '
<h2>Factors Affecting Human Population Size</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Human population size is influenced by biological, social, economic and environmental factors.
</p>

<p>
Important factors can include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>birth rate;</li>
<li>death rate;</li>
<li>access to healthcare;</li>
<li>food availability;</li>
<li>water supply;</li>
<li>migration;</li>
<li>education;</li>
<li>economic conditions;</li>
<li>sanitation and disease control.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Improved access to healthcare can reduce deaths from some diseases.
</p>

<p>
If the death rate falls while the birth rate remains high, population growth may increase.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Improved sanitation and access to clean water can reduce transmission of some waterborne diseases.
</p>

<p>
This can reduce deaths from those diseases.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Migration can change the population of a particular region even when the total population of a country does not change by the same amount.
</p>

<p>
People moving into a region increase its population.
</p>

<p>
People leaving reduce it.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
These factors interact. Population change usually cannot be explained by one factor alone.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Human Population Factors', 15),
  (1025, 1025, '
<h2>Deforestation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Deforestation</b> is the removal of forests, often involving clearing trees so that the land can be used for other purposes.
</p>

<p>
Forests may be cleared for agriculture, settlement, roads, timber and other activities.
</p>

<h3>Effects on Ecosystems</h3>

<p>
Deforestation can:
</p>

<ul>
<li>destroy habitats;</li>
<li>reduce biodiversity;</li>
<li>increase soil erosion;</li>
<li>alter the water cycle;</li>
<li>reduce carbon storage in vegetation;</li>
<li>increase carbon dioxide in the atmosphere when vegetation is burned or decomposes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A forest is cleared for agriculture.
</p>

<p>
Plants are removed.
</p>

<p>
Animals that depend on the forest for food and shelter may lose their habitat.
</p>

<p>
Their populations may decrease or move to other areas.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Tree roots help hold soil together.
</p>

<p>
After trees are removed, heavy rainfall can remove exposed soil more easily.
</p>

<p>
This increases the risk of soil erosion.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Trees remove carbon dioxide from the atmosphere during photosynthesis and store carbon in their biomass.
</p>

<p>
Large-scale forest removal therefore reduces this carbon storage.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Deforestation can affect several parts of an ecosystem at the same time because organisms and environmental processes are interconnected.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Deforestation', 15),
  (1026, 1026, '
<h2>Habitat Destruction</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <b>habitat</b> is the place where an organism normally lives and obtains the conditions and resources it needs.
</p>

<p>
<b>Habitat destruction</b> occurs when an organism''s habitat is damaged or removed.
</p>

<h3>Causes</h3>

<ul>
<li>deforestation;</li>
<li>urban expansion;</li>
<li>agriculture;</li>
<li>road construction;</li>
<li>mining;</li>
<li>drainage of wetlands;</li>
<li>pollution.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A wetland is drained to create farmland.
</p>

<p>
Species that depend on the wetland lose feeding, breeding or shelter areas.
</p>

<p>
Their populations may decrease.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A forest is divided by roads into smaller sections.
</p>

<p>
Animals that require large continuous habitats may have difficulty moving between suitable areas.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If a habitat supports several species and the habitat is destroyed, biodiversity can decline because several populations may be affected simultaneously.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Habitat destruction does not necessarily kill every individual immediately. It can also reduce access to food, shelter and breeding sites, making long-term survival more difficult.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Habitat Destruction', 15),
  (1027, 1027, '
<h2>Pollution</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Pollution</b> occurs when harmful substances or forms of energy are introduced into the environment at levels that can cause harm.
</p>

<p>
Pollutants can affect organisms, habitats and ecosystem processes.
</p>

<h3>Examples of Pollution</h3>

<ul>
<li>water pollution;</li>
<li>air pollution;</li>
<li>soil pollution;</li>
<li>noise pollution.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Untreated sewage entering a river can introduce organic matter and microorganisms.
</p>

<p>
This can change water quality and affect aquatic organisms.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Smoke from burning fuels can release gases and particles into the atmosphere.
</p>

<p>
Some air pollutants can harm respiratory systems and contribute to environmental problems.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Pesticides entering soil or water can affect organisms that were not intended to be controlled.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Pollution can affect organisms directly or indirectly by changing the conditions of their habitat.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pollution', 15),
  (1028, 1028, '
<h2>Water Pollution</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Water pollution</b> occurs when harmful substances enter water bodies and alter water quality.
</p>

<p>
Sources can include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>untreated sewage;</li>
<li>fertilisers;</li>
<li>pesticides;</li>
<li>industrial waste;</li>
<li>oil and other pollutants.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Effects</h3>

<p>
Water pollution can:
</p>

<ul>
<li>reduce water quality;</li>
<li>harm aquatic organisms;</li>
<li>reduce dissolved oxygen;</li>
<li>introduce toxic substances;</li>
<li>promote excessive plant and algal growth.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Fertilisers containing nitrate and phosphate enter a lake.
</p>

<p>
These nutrients can stimulate excessive growth of algae.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
If the algae later die, decomposers break down the dead organic material.
</p>

<p>
Respiration by decomposers uses oxygen.
</p>

<p>
Dissolved oxygen concentration can therefore decrease.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
If oxygen becomes too low, fish and other aquatic organisms may die or leave the affected area.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Water pollution can cause a chain of effects rather than affecting organisms only at the moment the pollutant enters the water.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Water Pollution', 15),
  (1029, 1029, '
<h2>Eutrophication</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Eutrophication</b> is the enrichment of a water body with mineral nutrients, especially nitrates and phosphates, which can cause excessive growth of algae and plants.
</p>

<h3>Sequence of Events</h3>

<ol>
<li>Nitrates or phosphates enter the water.</li>
<li>Algae grow rapidly.</li>
<li>An algal bloom reduces light penetration.</li>
<li>Some plants and algae die.</li>
<li>Decomposers break down the dead material.</li>
<li>Decomposer respiration uses dissolved oxygen.</li>
<li>Oxygen concentration falls.</li>
<li>Aquatic animals may die because of oxygen shortage.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Fertiliser runs off agricultural land into a lake.
</p>

<p>
The fertiliser contains nitrate ions.
</p>

<p>
Nitrate concentration in the lake increases.
</p>

<p>
Algae grow rapidly.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
The algae form a dense layer near the surface.
</p>

<p>
Less light reaches deeper water.
</p>

<p>
Some submerged plants die because their photosynthesis is reduced.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Dead algae and plants are decomposed by microorganisms.
</p>

<p>
Their respiration uses dissolved oxygen.
</p>

<p>
If oxygen concentration becomes very low, fish may die.
</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p>
<b>Fertiliser → nitrate/phosphate increase → algal bloom → death of algae/plants → decomposition → oxygen depletion → aquatic animal deaths.</b>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Eutrophication', 15),
  (1030, 1030, '
<h2>Carbon Balance and Human Activities</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The carbon cycle contains processes that transfer carbon into the atmosphere
and processes that remove carbon from the atmosphere.
</p>

<h3>PROCESSES THAT RELEASE CARBON DIOXIDE</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Respiration</li>
  <li>Decomposition</li>
  <li>Combustion</li>
</ul>

<h3>PROCESSES THAT REMOVE ATMOSPHERIC CARBON DIOXIDE</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Photosynthesis</li>
  <li>Absorption by oceans and other water bodies</li>
</ul>

<h3>HUMAN IMPACT</h3>

<p>
Human activities can alter the balance of carbon transfers.
</p>

<p>
Burning fossil fuels transfers large amounts of stored carbon into the
atmosphere.
Deforestation can also reduce the amount of carbon dioxide removed by
photosynthesis because fewer plants are available to photosynthesise.
</p>

<h3>WORKED REASONING</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can deforestation contribute to increased atmospheric carbon dioxide?</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Living plants remove carbon dioxide through photosynthesis.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Removing many plants reduces the amount of photosynthesis
taking place.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> The carbon dioxide removal rate can therefore decrease.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> If carbon dioxide continues to be released by processes
such as combustion and respiration, atmospheric carbon dioxide can increase.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The carbon cycle itself is natural.
The concern is that human activities can alter the rates of particular carbon
transfers and therefore disturb the balance.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carbon Balance and Human Activities', 15),
  (1031, 1031, '
<h2>Respiration and the Carbon Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Respiration transfers carbon from organic compounds in living cells back into
carbon dioxide.
</p>

<p>
During aerobic respiration, glucose is broken down using oxygen and carbon
dioxide is produced.
</p>

<h3>AEROBIC RESPIRATION</h3>

<p>
<strong>Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + energy</strong>
</p>

<p>
The carbon in glucose becomes carbon dioxide.
The carbon dioxide can then return to the atmosphere or dissolve in water.
</p>

<h3>WHO RESPİRES?</h3>

<p>
Plants respire.
Animals respire.
Fungi respire.
Bacteria and other organisms also carry out cellular respiration.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, respiration is not something performed only by animals.
</p>

<h3>FOLLOWING ONE CARBON ATOM</h3>

<p>
Imagine a carbon atom inside a glucose molecule in a plant.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> The glucose enters the respiration pathway.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> The carbon-containing molecules are broken down.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Carbon ends up in carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> The carbon dioxide can diffuse out of the organism.</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> How does respiration return carbon to the atmosphere?</p>

<p>
Glucose contains carbon. During aerobic respiration, glucose is broken down
and carbon dioxide is formed. The carbon dioxide can then leave the organism
and enter the atmosphere.
</p>

<p>
Thus:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Organic carbon → respiration → carbon dioxide</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Respiration and the Carbon Cycle', 15),
  (1032, 1032, '
<h2>What Is Respiration?</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Respiration</strong> is the series of chemical reactions in cells that
release energy from food molecules such as glucose.
</p>

<p>
The energy released during respiration is used to power cellular processes
such as active transport, growth, movement, synthesis of substances and
maintenance of body functions.
</p>

<h3>RESPIRATION IS NOT THE SAME AS BREATHING</h3>

<p>
<strong>Breathing</strong> is the physical movement of air into and out of the
lungs.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Respiration</strong> is a chemical process that occurs inside cells.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, breathing supplies oxygen for aerobic respiration and removes carbon
dioxide, but breathing itself is not respiration.
</p>

<h3>WHERE DOES RESPIRATION OCCUR?</h3>

<p>
Respiration occurs in cells.
Different stages occur in different parts of the cell.
</p>

<p>
For example, <strong>glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm</strong>, while later
stages of aerobic respiration occur in mitochondria.
</p>

<h3>WHY DO CELLS NEED RESPIRATION?</h3>

<p>
Cells need a continuous supply of usable energy to carry out life processes.
Without energy released by respiration, many cellular reactions could not
continue.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Respiration is a <strong>chemical energy-releasing process</strong>, whereas
breathing is a <strong>physical gas-exchange process</strong>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for What Is Respiration?', 15),
  (1033, 1033, '
<h2>Energy Released During Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Food molecules such as glucose contain chemical energy.
During respiration, glucose is broken down through a series of controlled
chemical reactions.
</p>

<p>
The energy released is used by cells for essential processes.
</p>

<h3>USES OF ENERGY FROM RESPIRATION</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Active transport</li>
  <li>Muscle contraction</li>
  <li>Growth</li>
  <li>Synthesis of proteins and other substances</li>
  <li>Cell division</li>
  <li>Maintaining body temperature in mammals</li>
</ul>

<h3>WHY RESPIRATION OCCURS IN MANY STEPS</h3>

<p>
Glucose contains a large amount of chemical energy.
Cells do not release all of this energy in one uncontrolled reaction.
Instead, respiration involves a sequence of enzyme-controlled reactions.
</p>

<p>
This allows energy to be released and transferred in a controlled way that
the cell can use.
</p>

<h3>ENERGY IS NOT CREATED</h3>

<p>
Respiration does not create energy.
It releases energy that was stored in chemical bonds in food molecules.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A muscle cell contracts repeatedly during exercise.
The contraction requires energy.
</p>

<p>
The energy required by the muscle cell is supplied through cellular processes
involving respiration.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Food → respiration → energy made available to the cell → muscle contraction
</strong>
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Respiration makes chemical energy from food available for cellular work.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Energy Released During Respiration', 15),
  (1034, 1034, '
<h2>Aerobic Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Aerobic respiration</strong> is respiration in which glucose is broken
down using oxygen, releasing energy.
</p>

<p>
The overall word equation is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + energy
</strong>
</p>

<p>
The balanced chemical equation is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS TO GLUCOSE?</h3>

<p>
Glucose is gradually broken down.
Carbon atoms from glucose eventually form carbon dioxide, while hydrogen and
oxygen contribute to the formation of water.
</p>

<p>
The overall process releases energy that cells can use.
</p>

<h3>WHERE DOES IT OCCUR?</h3>

<p>
The first stage, glycolysis, occurs in the cytoplasm.
Later stages of aerobic respiration occur in mitochondria.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS OXYGEN IMPORTANT?</h3>

<p>
Oxygen is required for the later stages of aerobic respiration and allows
glucose to be broken down more completely than in anaerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The equation describes the overall reaction.
It does not mean that glucose and oxygen react in one single step.
Respiration is a series of enzyme-controlled reactions.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Aerobic Respiration', 15),
  (1035, 1035, '
<h2>Glycolysis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Glycolysis</strong> is the first stage in the breakdown of glucose
during respiration.
</p>

<p>
It occurs in the <strong>cytoplasm</strong>.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS?</h3>

<p>
A glucose molecule contains six carbon atoms.
During glycolysis, the six-carbon glucose molecule is split into smaller
three-carbon molecules.
</p>

<p>
These products can then enter later stages of respiration.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN IS NOT DIRECTLY REQUIRED FOR GLYCOLYSIS</h3>

<p>
Glycolysis itself does not require oxygen.
This is important because it means the first stage of glucose breakdown can
occur even when oxygen availability is limited.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS NEXT?</h3>

<p>
When oxygen is available, the products of glycolysis can be processed through
the later stages of aerobic respiration.
</p>

<p>
When oxygen is insufficient, cells may use anaerobic pathways instead.
</p>

<h3>WHY GLYCOLYSIS MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Glycolysis begins the breakdown of glucose and produces molecules that can
be used in later energy-releasing reactions.
</p>

<h3>KEY DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Glycolysis = first stage of glucose breakdown in the cytoplasm.
</strong>
</p>

<p>
It should not be described as a process that occurs only inside mitochondria.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Glycolysis', 15),
  (1036, 1036, '
<h2>Mitochondria and Aerobic Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Mitochondria</strong> are organelles in which important later stages
of aerobic respiration occur.
</p>

<p>
Cells that require large amounts of energy often contain many mitochondria.
</p>

<h3>STRUCTURE AND FUNCTION</h3>

<p>
A mitochondrion has an outer membrane and an inner membrane.
The inner membrane is highly folded, forming structures called
<strong>cristae</strong>.
</p>

<p>
The folds provide a large surface area for reactions associated with later
stages of aerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>DOES ALL RESPIRATION OCCUR IN MITOCHONDRIA?</h3>

<p>
No.
</p>

<p>
The first stage of glucose breakdown, glycolysis, occurs in the cytoplasm.
Later aerobic stages occur in mitochondria.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A muscle cell has a high energy demand during repeated contraction.
It therefore contains many mitochondria to support the large amount of aerobic
respiration required.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
It is more accurate to say that mitochondria are major sites of aerobic
respiration rather than saying that all respiration happens there.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mitochondria and Aerobic Respiration', 15),
  (1037, 1037, '
<h2>Aerobic Respiration and Oxygen Supply</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Aerobic respiration requires oxygen.
Cells therefore need a continuous supply of oxygen when their energy demands
are being met mainly by aerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>PATHWAY OF OXYGEN</h3>

<p>
In humans:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Air → lungs → blood → tissues → cells
</strong>
</p>

<p>
Oxygen diffuses from the alveoli into the blood and is transported to body
tissues.
</p>

<p>
At the tissues, oxygen moves from the blood into cells and can then be used
in aerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES EXERCISE INCREASE OXYGEN DEMAND?</h3>

<p>
During exercise, muscles contract more frequently and require more energy.
The rate of respiration therefore increases.
</p>

<p>
More oxygen may be required to support the increased rate of aerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>IF OXYGEN SUPPLY IS NOT ENOUGH</h3>

<p>
If the oxygen supply cannot meet the demand of actively respiring muscle cells,
anaerobic respiration can contribute to energy release.
</p>

<h3>WORKED REASONING</h3>

<p>
A person begins running.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Muscles contract more rapidly.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Their energy demand increases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Respiration rate increases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Oxygen demand increases for aerobic respiration.</p>

<p>
Therefore breathing and heart rate increase to help deliver oxygen to the
working muscles.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Aerobic Respiration and Oxygen Supply', 15),
  (1038, 1038, '
<h2>Carbon Dioxide as a Product of Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide is a product of aerobic respiration.
</p>

<p>
The overall equation is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHERE DOES THE CARBON DIOXIDE COME FROM?</h3>

<p>
The carbon atoms in glucose are progressively removed during the reactions of
aerobic respiration.
Some of these carbon atoms eventually form carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS TO THE CARBON DIOXIDE?</h3>

<p>
In humans, carbon dioxide produced by respiring cells diffuses into the blood.
It is transported to the lungs and removed from the body during exhalation.
</p>

<p>
The pathway can be summarised as:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Respiring cells → blood → lungs → exhaled air
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHY MUST CARBON DIOXIDE BE REMOVED?</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide is continuously produced by metabolism.
The body must transport it away from tissues and remove it to prevent excessive
accumulation.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide is a product of respiration.
It is not a reactant in aerobic respiration.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carbon Dioxide as a Product of Respiration', 15),
  (1039, 1039, '
<h2>Water as a Product of Aerobic Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Water is one of the products formed during the overall process of aerobic
respiration.
</p>

<p>
The balanced overall equation is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHERE DOES THE WATER COME FROM?</h3>

<p>
During the later reactions of aerobic respiration, oxygen participates in
reactions that ultimately result in the formation of water.
</p>

<p>
The overall equation represents the combined result of many individual
chemical reactions.
</p>

<h3>DOES THE BODY ONLY GET WATER FROM RESPIRATION?</h3>

<p>
No.
</p>

<p>
Most body water comes from drinking fluids and water contained in food.
Water is also produced in metabolic reactions, including aerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS THIS IMPORTANT?</h3>

<p>
The water produced by metabolism contributes to the body''s total water supply,
although it is not the main source of water for most people.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Aerobic respiration produces:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Carbon dioxide + water + released energy
</strong>
</p>

<p>
The water is a product of the chemical reactions involved in respiration.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Water as a Product of Aerobic Respiration', 15),
  (1040, 1040, '
<h2>Respiration Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <strong>rate of respiration</strong> describes how quickly respiration is
occurring.
</p>

<p>
Respiration rate can be investigated by measuring a variable related to
respiration over a known period of time.
</p>

<h3>MEASURING RESPIRATION</h3>

<p>
Possible measurements include:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>oxygen consumption</li>
  <li>carbon dioxide production</li>
  <li>change in a suitable indicator linked to carbon dioxide</li>
</ul>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
An organism consumes 30 cm³ of oxygen in 10 minutes.
</p>

<p>
Respiration rate based on oxygen consumption is:
</p>

<p>
30 ÷ 10 = <strong>3 cm³ of oxygen per minute</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHY TIME MATTERS</h3>

<p>
A measurement of oxygen consumption alone does not give a rate.
The amount must be related to the time over which it was measured.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
An organism uses 48 cm³ of oxygen in 12 minutes.
</p>

<p>
Rate = 48 ÷ 12
</p>

<p>
= <strong>4 cm³/min</strong>
</p>

<h3>COMPARING RATES</h3>

<p>
If another organism uses 24 cm³ in 12 minutes:
</p>

<p>
Rate = 24 ÷ 12 = 2 cm³/min.
</p>

<p>
The first organism has the higher measured respiration rate.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Respiration Rate', 15),
  (1041, 1041, '
<h2>Factors Affecting Respiration Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The rate of respiration can change when environmental or physiological
conditions change.
</p>

<h3>TEMPERATURE</h3>

<p>
Respiration involves enzyme-controlled reactions.
As temperature increases within a suitable range, enzyme activity can increase,
so respiration rate may increase.
</p>

<p>
At excessively high temperatures, enzymes can become denatured and respiration
rate can decrease.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN AVAILABILITY</h3>

<p>
Aerobic respiration requires oxygen.
If oxygen availability becomes insufficient, the contribution of aerobic
respiration can be limited and anaerobic pathways may become more important.
</p>

<h3>GLUCOSE AVAILABILITY</h3>

<p>
Glucose is a respiratory substrate.
Its availability can affect the rate at which respiration can occur, depending
on the cells and conditions being studied.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Yeast cells are supplied with glucose at different temperatures.
The amount of carbon dioxide produced per minute can be measured.
</p>

<p>
A higher carbon dioxide production rate indicates a higher respiration rate,
provided the investigation is designed so that carbon dioxide production is a
valid measure of the respiration being studied.
</p>

<h3>FAIR TEST</h3>

<p>
If temperature is being investigated, other important conditions should be
kept constant, such as the amount of yeast, glucose concentration and volume.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Factors Affecting Respiration Rate', 15),
  (1042, 1042, '
<h2>Respiration During Exercise</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Exercise increases the energy demand of muscle cells.
The rate of respiration therefore increases to supply more energy.
</p>

<h3>CHANGES DURING EXERCISE</h3>

<p>
When exercise begins:
</p>

<p>
<strong>1.</strong> Muscles contract more frequently.</p>

<p>
<strong>2.</strong> Energy demand increases.</p>

<p>
<strong>3.</strong> Respiration rate increases.</p>

<p>
<strong>4.</strong> Oxygen demand increases for aerobic respiration.</p>

<p>
<strong>5.</strong> Heart rate increases to deliver blood more rapidly.</p>

<p>
<strong>6.</strong> Breathing rate and depth increase to improve oxygen uptake
and carbon dioxide removal.</p>

<h3>INTENSE EXERCISE</h3>

<p>
During very intense activity, oxygen delivery may not immediately meet the
energy demand of the muscles.
</p>

<p>
Anaerobic respiration can then contribute to energy release.
</p>

<h3>AFTER EXERCISE</h3>

<p>
Respiration and ventilation do not immediately return to resting levels.
The body continues recovering, including processing products of anaerobic
metabolism and restoring normal conditions.
</p>

<h3>KEY CHAIN</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Exercise → greater muscle activity → greater energy demand → increased
respiration → increased oxygen demand → increased breathing and heart rate
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Respiration During Exercise', 15),
  (1043, 1043, '
<h2>Features of an Efficient Respiratory Surface</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A respiratory surface must allow gases to move rapidly between the environment
and the organism.
</p>

<p>
Efficient respiratory surfaces commonly have several important features.
</p>

<h3>1. LARGE SURFACE AREA</h3>

<p>
A large surface area provides more space through which gases can diffuse at
the same time.
</p>

<h3>2. THIN SURFACE</h3>

<p>
A short diffusion distance allows gases to move across the surface quickly.
</p>

<h3>3. MOIST SURFACE</h3>

<p>
Respiratory gases can dissolve in a thin layer of moisture before diffusing
across cell membranes.
</p>

<h3>4. GOOD BLOOD SUPPLY</h3>

<p>
A strong blood supply helps maintain concentration gradients between the
respiratory surface and blood.
</p>

<h3>5. MAINTENANCE OF A CONCENTRATION GRADIENT</h3>

<p>
Continuous ventilation and blood flow help prevent oxygen and carbon dioxide
concentrations on the two sides of the respiratory surface from becoming equal.
</p>

<h3>WORKED REASONING</h3>

<p>
If a respiratory surface were thick, gases would have a longer distance to
travel.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Thicker surface → longer diffusion distance → slower gaseous exchange.
</strong>
</p>

<p>
This is why thin respiratory surfaces are advantageous.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Features of an Efficient Respiratory Surface', 15),
  (1044, 1044, '
<h2>Moist Respiratory Surfaces</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The surface of the alveoli is moist.
This is important because respiratory gases must dissolve in the moisture before
they can diffuse across the respiratory surface.
</p>

<h3>WHY MUST GASES DISSOLVE?</h3>

<p>
Oxygen in inhaled air comes into contact with the moist alveolar surface.
Some oxygen dissolves in the thin fluid layer before crossing the cells.
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide also dissolves in the moisture as it moves from the blood toward
the alveolar air.
</p>

<h3>CAUSE AND EFFECT</h3>

<p>
Moist respiratory surface
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Gases can dissolve
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Gases can diffuse across the surface
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Efficient gaseous exchange
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
A moist surface is useful for gas exchange, but the lungs must also prevent
excessive loss of water.
The respiratory system therefore balances efficient gas exchange with
maintenance of the body''s water balance.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Moist Respiratory Surfaces', 15),
  (1045, 1045, '
<h2>Exercise and Oxygen Demand</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The harder muscles work, the greater their energy requirement.
This can increase their demand for oxygen.
</p>

<h3>LIGHT EXERCISE</h3>

<p>
During light exercise, aerobic respiration can usually provide most of the
energy required by working muscles.
</p>

<h3>INTENSE EXERCISE</h3>

<p>
During very intense exercise, energy demand can rise faster than oxygen
delivery can immediately increase.
The muscles may therefore increase their contribution from anaerobic
respiration.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES THIS MATTER?</h3>

<p>
Aerobic respiration produces much more energy per glucose molecule than
anaerobic respiration.
Therefore, the body attempts to deliver sufficient oxygen while exercise
continues.
</p>

<h3>BODY RESPONSE</h3>

<p>
As exercise intensity increases:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>breathing rate generally increases</li>
  <li>breathing depth generally increases</li>
  <li>heart rate increases</li>
  <li>blood flow to active muscles increases</li>
  <li>oxygen delivery increases</li>
</ul>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Anaerobic respiration is not automatically present during every form of
exercise.
Its contribution becomes more important when oxygen supply cannot meet the
energy demand of the working muscles.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Exercise and Oxygen Demand', 15),
  (1046, 1046, '
<h2>Goblet Cells in the Respiratory Tract</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Goblet cells</strong> are specialised epithelial cells that produce
mucus.
</p>

<h3>WHAT DOES THE MUCUS DO?</h3>

<p>
The mucus forms a sticky layer over parts of the respiratory tract.
It traps dust, microorganisms and other particles carried in inhaled air.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS NEXT?</h3>

<p>
Ciliated cells move the mucus toward the throat.
The trapped material can then be swallowed.
</p>

<h3>STRUCTURE-FUNCTION LINK</h3>

<p>
Goblet cell
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Produces mucus
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Mucus traps particles
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Cilia move mucus toward throat
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Goblet cells and ciliated cells have different roles.
Goblet cells produce mucus; ciliated cells move it.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Goblet Cells in the Respiratory Tract', 15),
  (1047, 1047, '
<h2>Anaerobic Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Anaerobic respiration</strong> is the release of energy from glucose
without using oxygen.
</p>

<p>
It releases less energy per glucose molecule than aerobic respiration because
glucose is not completely broken down.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS IT IMPORTANT?</h3>

<p>
Anaerobic respiration allows cells to continue releasing some energy when the
oxygen supply is insufficient for their energy demand.
</p>

<p>
For example, during very intense exercise, muscle cells may not receive enough
oxygen to meet their energy requirements entirely through aerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>COMPARISON</h3>

<p>
<strong>Aerobic:</strong> uses oxygen and releases more energy from each
glucose molecule.</p>

<p>
<strong>Anaerobic:</strong> does not use oxygen and releases less energy from
each glucose molecule.</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Anaerobic respiration is not simply “respiration that happens slowly.”
It is a different metabolic pathway that operates without oxygen.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<strong>
No oxygen → anaerobic pathway → some energy released from glucose
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Anaerobic Respiration', 15),
  (1048, 1048, '
<h2>Anaerobic Respiration in Muscle Cells</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
During very vigorous exercise, muscles may require energy faster than oxygen
can be delivered to them.
</p>

<p>
Muscle cells can then increase their use of anaerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>REACTION IN MUSCLES</h3>

<p>
The simplified word equation is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Glucose → lactic acid + energy
</strong>
</p>

<p>
No oxygen is used in this anaerobic pathway.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS TO GLUCOSE?</h3>

<p>
Glucose is only partially broken down.
Therefore less energy is released per glucose molecule than during aerobic
respiration.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES ANAEROBIC RESPIRATION HELP?</h3>

<p>
It allows ATP production associated with glycolysis to continue when oxygen
availability cannot meet the immediate demand.
</p>

<p>
This provides a temporary energy supply during intense activity.
</p>

<h3>LIMITATION</h3>

<p>
Anaerobic respiration cannot support prolonged intense activity indefinitely.
The body must eventually deal with the products of anaerobic metabolism and
restore normal conditions.
</p>

<h3>WORKED REASONING</h3>

<p>
A sprinter starts a race.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Muscles need energy very rapidly.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Oxygen delivery cannot immediately supply all the
required aerobic respiration.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Anaerobic respiration contributes to energy release.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Lactic acid is produced.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Anaerobic Respiration in Muscle Cells', 15),
  (1049, 1049, '
<h2>Anaerobic Respiration in Yeast</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Yeast can respire anaerobically when oxygen is unavailable or very limited.
</p>

<p>
The word equation is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide + energy
</strong>
</p>

<p>
This process is also called <strong>alcoholic fermentation</strong>.
</p>

<h3>PRODUCTS</h3>

<p>
The two important products are:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>ethanol</li>
  <li>carbon dioxide</li>
</ul>

<p>
Only a relatively small amount of energy is released compared with aerobic
respiration.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS CARBON DIOXIDE IMPORTANT?</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide is a gas.
Its production can therefore be detected or measured in experiments involving
yeast.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Yeast is placed in a glucose solution without oxygen.
Gas bubbles are produced.
</p>

<p>
The gas is carbon dioxide produced during anaerobic respiration.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide + energy
</strong>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Yeast does not produce lactic acid as its main anaerobic product.
That pathway occurs in human muscle cells.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Anaerobic Respiration in Yeast', 15),
  (1050, 1050, '
<h2>Fermentation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Fermentation</strong> refers to anaerobic metabolic processes in which
organic molecules such as glucose are broken down without oxygen.
</p>

<p>
In yeast, alcoholic fermentation produces ethanol and carbon dioxide.
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide + energy
</strong>
</p>

<h3>FERMENTATION IN BREAD MAKING</h3>

<p>
Yeast is mixed with dough containing sugars.
The yeast respires anaerobically when oxygen becomes limited.
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide produced by the yeast becomes trapped in the dough as bubbles.
These bubbles cause the dough to expand.
</p>

<p>
Ethanol is also produced.
Much of the ethanol evaporates during baking.
</p>

<h3>FERMENTATION IN BREWING</h3>

<p>
Yeast can convert sugars into ethanol and carbon dioxide.
The ethanol is retained in the liquid during alcoholic fermentation.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Fermentation does not mean that oxygen is being converted into another
substance.
It specifically involves metabolic pathways that allow cells to obtain some
energy without oxygen.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fermentation', 15),
  (1051, 1051, '
<h2>Aerobic and Anaerobic Respiration Compared</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Aerobic and anaerobic respiration both release energy from glucose, but they
differ in oxygen requirement, products and energy yield.
</p>

<h3>AEROBIC RESPIRATION</h3>

<p>
<strong>Oxygen:</strong> required</p>

<p>
<strong>Products:</strong> carbon dioxide and water</p>

<p>
<strong>Energy:</strong> much more energy released per glucose molecule</p>

<p>
<strong>Breakdown:</strong> glucose is more completely broken down</p>

<h3>ANAEROBIC RESPIRATION IN MUSCLE</h3>

<p>
<strong>Oxygen:</strong> not required</p>

<p>
<strong>Product:</strong> lactic acid</p>

<p>
<strong>Energy:</strong> less energy released per glucose molecule</p>

<h3>ANAEROBIC RESPIRATION IN YEAST</h3>

<p>
<strong>Oxygen:</strong> not required</p>

<p>
<strong>Products:</strong> ethanol and carbon dioxide</p>

<p>
<strong>Energy:</strong> less energy released per glucose molecule than aerobic
respiration</p>

<h3>KEY COMPARISON</h3>

<p>
Both pathways begin with glucose breakdown.
The presence or absence of oxygen determines which downstream pathway can
operate.
</p>

<h3>EXAM REASONING</h3>

<p>
If a question says a cell is respiring without oxygen, do not automatically
answer “lactic acid.”
First identify the organism or cell.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Human muscle → lactic acid.</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>Yeast → ethanol + carbon dioxide.</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Aerobic and Anaerobic Respiration Compared', 15),
  (1052, 1052, '
<h2>Oxygen Debt</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
During intense exercise, the muscles may use anaerobic respiration because
oxygen supply cannot immediately meet energy demand.
</p>

<p>
This results in the production and accumulation of lactic acid in the body.
</p>

<p>
After exercise, additional oxygen is required to help restore the body to
its normal condition.
This additional oxygen requirement is commonly described as
<strong>oxygen debt</strong>.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS EXTRA OXYGEN NEEDED?</h3>

<p>
After intense exercise, oxygen is needed for processes involved in recovery,
including the aerobic processing of lactate and restoration of normal
physiological conditions.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS AFTER EXERCISE?</h3>

<p>
Breathing remains faster and deeper for some time.
This increases oxygen uptake.
</p>

<p>
Heart rate also remains elevated to transport oxygen efficiently around
the body.
</p>

<h3>WORKED SEQUENCE</h3>

<p>
<strong>Intense exercise</strong>
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
High energy demand
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Oxygen supply cannot fully meet demand
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Greater contribution from anaerobic respiration
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Lactic acid accumulates
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Exercise stops
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Extra oxygen is taken in during recovery
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Oxygen Debt', 15),
  (1053, 1053, '
<h2>Lactic Acid During Exercise</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
During intense exercise, muscle cells may increase their use of anaerobic
respiration.
</p>

<p>
The simplified reaction is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Glucose → lactic acid + energy
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES LACTIC ACID INCREASE?</h3>

<p>
The rate of energy demand can rise faster than oxygen delivery can support
aerobic respiration.
Anaerobic metabolism therefore contributes more to ATP supply.
</p>

<p>
Lactic acid is produced as part of this pathway.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS AFTER EXERCISE?</h3>

<p>
The body continues to take in oxygen at an elevated rate.
Lactate can then be metabolised through pathways that help restore normal
conditions.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Do not describe lactic acid as the reason the muscles “run out of oxygen.”
The sequence is the opposite:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
High energy demand → oxygen supply insufficient for full aerobic demand →
greater anaerobic contribution → lactic acid production.
</strong>
</p>

<h3>EXAM APPLICATION</h3>

<p>
If a runner suddenly increases speed, the energy demand of the muscles rises.
If oxygen delivery cannot immediately meet this demand, anaerobic respiration
can contribute and lactic acid production increases.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Lactic Acid During Exercise', 15),
  (1054, 1054, '
<h2>Investigating Respiration in Germinating Seeds</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Germinating seeds are useful for investigating respiration because their cells
are metabolically active as the embryo develops.
</p>

<h3>WHY DO GERMINATING SEEDS RESPire?</h3>

<p>
The growing embryo requires energy for processes such as cell division,
growth and synthesis of new materials.
</p>

<p>
Respiration releases energy from stored food reserves.
</p>

<h3>MEASURING OXYGEN UPTAKE</h3>

<p>
A respirometer can be used to investigate oxygen consumption.
</p>

<p>
If germinating seeds consume oxygen in a sealed system, the volume or pressure
of gas may change.
</p>

<p>
A coloured liquid indicator can be used to detect movement caused by changes
in gas volume or pressure.
</p>

<h3>ROLE OF CARBON DIOXIDE ABSORPTION</h3>

<p>
A substance such as soda lime can absorb carbon dioxide.
If carbon dioxide produced by respiration is removed while oxygen is consumed,
the change in gas volume can be used to estimate oxygen uptake.
</p>

<h3>CONTROL</h3>

<p>
A control containing non-respiring material, such as boiled seeds, can help
show whether changes are due to respiration rather than temperature or
pressure changes alone.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Investigating Respiration in Germinating Seeds', 15),
  (1055, 1055, '
<h2>Investigating Carbon Dioxide Production by Respiring Organisms</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Respiring organisms produce carbon dioxide.
This can be used as evidence that respiration is occurring.
</p>

<h3>YEAST EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Yeast can be mixed with a glucose solution.
When oxygen is limited, yeast carries out anaerobic respiration and releases
carbon dioxide.
</p>

<p>
The carbon dioxide can be detected by measuring the gas produced or by passing
the gas into limewater.
</p>

<h3>LIMEWATER TEST</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide turns limewater <strong>milky</strong>.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, if gas from a respiration experiment is bubbled through limewater
and the limewater becomes milky, carbon dioxide is present.
</p>

<h3>FAIR TEST</h3>

<p>
If temperature is being investigated, the amount of yeast, glucose
concentration and volume should be controlled.
</p>

<h3>WORKED REASONING</h3>

<p>
A student investigates whether warmer conditions increase yeast respiration.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Prepare equal yeast samples.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Give them equal glucose concentrations.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Place samples at different temperatures.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Measure carbon dioxide production over the same time.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Compare the rates of carbon dioxide production.</p>

<p>
A higher rate of carbon dioxide production can indicate a higher respiration
rate, provided the experimental conditions make this a valid measure.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Investigating Carbon Dioxide Production by Respiring Organisms', 15),
  (1056, 1056, '
<h2>Temperature and Yeast Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Yeast respiration can be used to investigate the effect of temperature on
the rate of respiration.
</p>

<h3>WHY TEMPERATURE MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Respiration involves enzymes.
As temperature increases within a suitable range, enzyme molecules generally
collide more frequently with their substrates and reaction rates can increase.
</p>

<p>
At temperatures above the optimum, enzyme structure can be disrupted and
respiration rate can decrease.
</p>

<h3>EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN</h3>

<p>
A student could place equal amounts of yeast and glucose solution at different
temperatures.
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide production can be measured over a fixed time.
</p>

<h3>VARIABLES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Independent variable:</strong> temperature.</p>

<p>
<strong>Dependent variable:</strong> respiration rate, estimated from carbon
dioxide production per unit time.</p>

<p>
<strong>Control variables:</strong> amount of yeast, glucose concentration,
volume of solution and measurement time.</p>

<h3>EXPECTED PATTERN</h3>

<p>
At low temperatures, respiration may be slow.
</p>

<p>
As temperature rises toward the optimum, respiration rate may increase.
</p>

<p>
Above the optimum, respiration rate may decrease because enzymes are damaged
or denatured.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The exact optimum temperature depends on the organism and experimental
conditions.
Do not assume one temperature is the optimum for every organism.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Temperature and Yeast Respiration', 15),
  (1057, 1057, '
<h2>Respiration During Seed Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germinating seeds have a high demand for energy because cells are actively growing and dividing.</p>

<p>Respiration releases energy from stored organic compounds.</p>

<h3>Why Respiration Increases</h3>

<p>During germination, cells carry out processes such as:</p>

<p>cell division, cell elongation, active transport, synthesis of new substances and growth.</p>

<p>These processes require energy.</p>

<h3>Oxygen</h3>

<p>For aerobic respiration, oxygen must be available.</p>

<p>This is why suitable aeration is important when germinating seeds.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two groups of seeds are supplied with water.</p>

<p>Group A receives sufficient oxygen.</p>

<p>Group B is kept in conditions where oxygen availability is very low.</p>

<p>Group A is expected to germinate more successfully if oxygen is required for the energy-demanding processes of germination.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Respiration is not the same as germination.</p>

<p>Respiration provides energy that supports the processes involved in germination.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for respiration_during_germination', 15),
  (1058, 1058, '
<h2>Using Respiration Evidence to Explain Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germinating seeds show increased metabolic activity compared with a dry, dormant seed.</p>

<p>One way to investigate this is to measure evidence of respiration.</p>

<h3>Example Investigation</h3>

<p>Place germinating seeds in a sealed apparatus connected to a carbon dioxide indicator or a suitable carbon dioxide detection method.</p>

<p>Use a control containing non-respiring material under otherwise similar conditions.</p>

<h3>Expected Evidence</h3>

<p>If the germinating seeds are respiring, carbon dioxide production should be detectable.</p>

<h3>Biological Explanation</h3>

<p>Stored organic substances are used as substrates in respiration.</p>

<p>Respiration releases energy required for growth and other cellular processes.</p>

<h3>Why a Control Is Important</h3>

<p>The control helps show whether the observed change is associated with the germinating seeds rather than the apparatus or environment.</p>

<h3>Exam Reasoning</h3>

<p>If carbon dioxide increases in the apparatus containing germinating seeds, this is evidence that respiration is occurring.</p>

<p>It does not mean that carbon dioxide is being used as the seed''s food.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_and_respiration_evidence', 15),
  (1059, 1059, '
<h2>Gaseous Exchange</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Gaseous exchange</strong> is the movement of respiratory gases between
an organism and its surroundings.
</p>

<p>
In humans, the two important gases are:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>oxygen</strong>, which moves into the body and is supplied to cells</li>
  <li><strong>carbon dioxide</strong>, which is produced by cells and removed from the body</li>
</ul>

<h3>WHY DO CELLS NEED GAS EXCHANGE?</h3>

<p>
Cells require oxygen for aerobic respiration.
Aerobic respiration releases energy from glucose.
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide is produced during aerobic respiration and must be transported
away from cells.
</p>

<h3>WHERE DOES HUMAN GASEOUS EXCHANGE OCCUR?</h3>

<p>
In humans, gaseous exchange occurs mainly between the air in the
<strong>alveoli</strong> and the blood in surrounding capillaries.
</p>

<h3>DIRECTION OF MOVEMENT</h3>

<p>
Oxygen:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Alveolar air → blood
</strong>
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Blood → alveolar air
</strong>
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Gaseous exchange is not the same as breathing.
Breathing moves air into and out of the lungs, while gaseous exchange involves
movement of gases across a respiratory surface.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gaseous Exchange', 15),
  (1060, 1060, '
<h2>The Human Gas Exchange System</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The human respiratory system allows air to reach the lungs and provides a
surface for gaseous exchange.
</p>

<h3>MAIN STRUCTURES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Nose and nasal cavity</li>
  <li>Pharynx</li>
  <li>Larynx</li>
  <li>Trachea</li>
  <li>Bronchi</li>
  <li>Bronchioles</li>
  <li>Alveoli</li>
  <li>Lungs</li>
</ul>

<h3>AIR PATHWAY</h3>

<p>
Air travels approximately through this sequence:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Nose/mouth → pharynx → larynx → trachea → bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli
</strong>
</p>

<h3>THE TRACHEA</h3>

<p>
The trachea carries air toward the bronchi.
Its walls contain cartilage that helps prevent collapse.
</p>

<h3>THE BRONCHI</h3>

<p>
The trachea divides into two main bronchi, one leading toward each lung.
</p>

<h3>BRONCHIOLES</h3>

<p>
The bronchi divide into smaller bronchioles that distribute air deeper into
the lungs.
</p>

<h3>ALVEOLI</h3>

<p>
The bronchioles end in groups of tiny air sacs called alveoli.
These provide the main surface for gaseous exchange.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Human Gas Exchange System', 15),
  (1061, 1061, '
<h2>Ventilation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Ventilation</strong> is the movement of air into and out of the lungs.
It is commonly described as breathing.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS VENTILATION NECESSARY?</h3>

<p>
Ventilation brings fresh air containing oxygen into the lungs and removes air
containing carbon dioxide.
</p>

<p>
This helps maintain concentration gradients for gaseous exchange at the
alveoli.
</p>

<h3>INHALATION</h3>

<p>
During inhalation:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>the diaphragm contracts and flattens</li>
  <li>the external intercostal muscles contract</li>
  <li>the rib cage moves upward and outward</li>
  <li>thoracic volume increases</li>
  <li>pressure inside the lungs decreases</li>
  <li>air moves into the lungs</li>
</ul>

<h3>EXHALATION</h3>

<p>
During quiet exhalation:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>the diaphragm relaxes and becomes more domed</li>
  <li>external intercostal muscles relax</li>
  <li>rib cage moves downward and inward</li>
  <li>thoracic volume decreases</li>
  <li>pressure inside the lungs increases</li>
  <li>air moves out</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Air moves because pressure changes are produced by changes in the volume of
the thoracic cavity.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ventilation', 15),
  (1062, 1062, '
<h2>Inhalation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Inhalation</strong> is the movement of air into the lungs.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP MECHANISM</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> The diaphragm contracts.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> The diaphragm moves downward and becomes flatter.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> The external intercostal muscles contract.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> The ribs move upward and outward.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> The volume of the thoracic cavity increases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> The pressure inside the lungs decreases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 7:</strong> Air moves into the lungs because atmospheric pressure
is now greater than pressure inside the lungs.</p>

<h3>WHY DOES VOLUME AFFECT PRESSURE?</h3>

<p>
When the enclosed space containing air becomes larger, the pressure of the air
inside decreases.
</p>

<p>
This pressure difference causes air to move into the lungs.
</p>

<h3>EXAM CHAIN</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Diaphragm contracts
→ thoracic volume increases
→ lung pressure decreases
→ air enters.
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Inhalation', 15),
  (1063, 1063, '
<h2>Exhalation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Exhalation</strong> is the movement of air out of the lungs.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP MECHANISM</h3>

<p>
During quiet exhalation:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> The diaphragm relaxes.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> It moves upward and becomes more dome-shaped.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> The external intercostal muscles relax.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> The ribs move downward and inward.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Thoracic volume decreases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> Pressure inside the lungs increases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 7:</strong> Air moves out because pressure inside the lungs becomes
greater than atmospheric pressure.</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Quiet exhalation does not normally require active contraction of the abdominal
muscles.
It is largely produced by relaxation of the inspiratory muscles and elastic
recoil of the lungs and chest wall.
</p>

<h3>EXAM CHAIN</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Diaphragm relaxes
→ thoracic volume decreases
→ lung pressure increases
→ air leaves.
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Exhalation', 15),
  (1064, 1064, '
<h2>Intercostal Muscles</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Intercostal muscles</strong> are muscles located between the ribs.
They help move the rib cage during breathing.
</p>

<h3>DURING INHALATION</h3>

<p>
The <strong>external intercostal muscles contract</strong>.
</p>

<p>
This moves the ribs upward and outward, increasing the volume of the thoracic
cavity.
</p>

<h3>DURING QUIET EXHALATION</h3>

<p>
The external intercostal muscles relax.
The rib cage moves downward and inward as the respiratory system returns
toward its resting position.
</p>

<h3>FORCED EXHALATION</h3>

<p>
During forceful breathing out, additional muscles can be recruited, including
the internal intercostal muscles and abdominal muscles.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES RIB MOVEMENT MATTER?</h3>

<p>
The ribs form part of the thoracic cavity.
Changing their position changes the volume of the cavity and therefore helps
change lung pressure.
</p>

<h3>WORKED SEQUENCE</h3>

<p>
External intercostal contraction
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Ribs move upward and outward
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Thoracic volume increases
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Lung pressure decreases
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Air enters
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Intercostal Muscles', 15),
  (1065, 1065, '
<h2>Diaphragm and Breathing</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <strong>diaphragm</strong> is a sheet of muscle separating the thoracic
cavity from the abdominal cavity.
</p>

<p>
It plays a major role in changing the volume of the thoracic cavity during
breathing.
</p>

<h3>INHALATION</h3>

<p>
During inhalation, the diaphragm contracts.
</p>

<p>
It becomes flatter and moves downward.
</p>

<p>
This increases thoracic volume.
</p>

<p>
The increased volume lowers lung pressure, causing air to enter.
</p>

<h3>QUIET EXHALATION</h3>

<p>
During quiet exhalation, the diaphragm relaxes.
</p>

<p>
It moves upward and becomes dome-shaped.
</p>

<p>
Thoracic volume decreases.
</p>

<p>
Lung pressure rises and air moves out.
</p>

<h3>COMMON ERROR</h3>

<p>
Do not say that the diaphragm “pushes air into the lungs.”
</p>

<p>
Instead, its contraction changes thoracic volume, which changes lung pressure.
The resulting pressure difference causes air movement.
</p>

<h3>EXAM CHAIN</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Diaphragm contracts
→ moves downward
→ thoracic volume increases
→ lung pressure decreases
→ inhalation.
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diaphragm and Breathing', 15),
  (1066, 1066, '
<h2>Airways and Their Functions</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The airways transport air between the external environment and the alveoli.
Different structures have different roles.
</p>

<h3>NOSE AND NASAL CAVITY</h3>

<p>
The nose and nasal cavity help warm, moisten and filter incoming air.
</p>

<h3>TRACHEA</h3>

<p>
The trachea carries air toward the bronchi.
Cartilage supports its walls and helps keep the airway open.
</p>

<h3>BRONCHI</h3>

<p>
The trachea divides into two main bronchi.
Each bronchus carries air toward one lung.
</p>

<h3>BRONCHIOLES</h3>

<p>
Bronchi branch into progressively smaller bronchioles.
They distribute air throughout the lungs.
</p>

<h3>ALVEOLI</h3>

<p>
The smallest airways lead to alveoli, where gaseous exchange occurs.
</p>

<h3>MUCOUS MEMBRANE</h3>

<p>
Mucus traps dust particles and microorganisms entering with inhaled air.
</p>

<h3>CILIATED CELLS</h3>

<p>
Cilia beat to move mucus toward the throat, where it can be swallowed.
This helps protect the lungs from trapped particles and microorganisms.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Airways and Their Functions', 15),
  (1067, 1067, '
<h2>Composition of Inspired and Expired Air</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The air entering the lungs is called <strong>inspired air</strong>.
The air leaving the lungs is called <strong>expired air</strong>.
Their composition is different because gaseous exchange occurs in the lungs.
</p>

<h3>INSPIRED AIR</h3>

<p>
Inspired air contains a relatively high proportion of oxygen and a very small
proportion of carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>EXPIRED AIR</h3>

<p>
Expired air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than inspired air.
It also contains more water vapour.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES OXYGEN DECREASE?</h3>

<p>
Oxygen diffuses from the alveoli into the blood.
Some of this oxygen is then transported to respiring cells.
Therefore, less oxygen remains in the air that is exhaled.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES CARBON DIOXIDE INCREASE?</h3>

<p>
Respiring cells produce carbon dioxide.
The blood transports carbon dioxide to the lungs.
It then diffuses into the alveoli and is exhaled.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The change in gas composition is evidence that gases are exchanged between
the air in the lungs and the blood.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Composition of Inspired and Expired Air', 15),
  (1068, 1068, '
<h2>Testing Expired Air for Carbon Dioxide</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide in expired air can be demonstrated using
<strong>limewater</strong>.
</p>

<h3>THE TEST</h3>

<p>
Bubble expired air through clear limewater.
</p>

<p>
If carbon dioxide is present, the limewater turns
<strong>milky or cloudy</strong>.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES IT HAPPEN?</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide reacts with calcium hydroxide in limewater to form insoluble
calcium carbonate.
The suspended calcium carbonate makes the solution appear milky.
</p>

<h3>COMPARISON</h3>

<p>
If ordinary atmospheric air is passed through the same setup, the change may
be much less obvious because atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration is much
lower than in expired air.
</p>

<h3>CONTROL</h3>

<p>
A useful investigation compares expired air with ordinary atmospheric air.
The two samples should be tested using the same volume of limewater and the
same procedure.
</p>

<h3>CONCLUSION</h3>

<p>
If expired air turns limewater milky more quickly, this provides evidence that
expired air contains more carbon dioxide than ordinary atmospheric air.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Testing Expired Air for Carbon Dioxide', 15),
  (1069, 1069, '
<h2>Testing Expired Air for Oxygen</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Expired air contains oxygen, but less oxygen than inspired atmospheric air.
This can be demonstrated experimentally by comparing the oxygen concentration
of the two air samples.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT PRINCIPLE</h3>

<p>
A gas test must be interpreted carefully.
A test showing that oxygen is present does not tell us that the oxygen
concentration is unchanged.
</p>

<p>
Expired air still contains oxygen because not all oxygen entering the lungs
is removed from the air during one passage through the respiratory system.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS?</h3>

<p>
Some oxygen:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
alveoli → blood → body tissues
</strong>
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the proportion of oxygen remaining in expired air is lower than in
inspired air.
</p>

<h3>EXAM POINT</h3>

<p>
Do not write:
<strong>“There is no oxygen in expired air.”</strong>
</p>

<p>
The correct idea is:
<strong>“Expired air contains less oxygen than inspired air.”</strong>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Testing Expired Air for Oxygen', 15),
  (1070, 1070, '
<h2>Why Breathing Rate Increases During Exercise</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
During exercise, muscles contract more frequently and require more energy.
Aerobic respiration increases to meet this energy demand.
</p>

<h3>STEP 1: MUSCLE ACTIVITY INCREASES</h3>

<p>
Working muscles require more ATP.
They therefore increase their rate of respiration.
</p>

<h3>STEP 2: MORE OXYGEN IS REQUIRED</h3>

<p>
Aerobic respiration requires oxygen.
Therefore, active muscles need an increased supply of oxygen.
</p>

<h3>STEP 3: MORE CARBON DIOXIDE IS PRODUCED</h3>

<p>
An increased rate of aerobic respiration produces more carbon dioxide.
The body must remove this carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>STEP 4: BREATHING RATE INCREASES</h3>

<p>
The breathing rate increases so that ventilation can increase.
More fresh air reaches the alveoli and more carbon dioxide can be removed.
</p>

<h3>STEP 5: HEART RATE ALSO INCREASES</h3>

<p>
The circulatory system increases blood flow to active tissues.
This helps deliver oxygen and transport carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>COMPLETE REASONING</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Exercise
→ increased muscle activity
→ increased respiration
→ greater oxygen demand and carbon dioxide production
→ increased ventilation and blood flow.
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Breathing Rate Increases During Exercise', 15),
  (1071, 1071, '
<h2>Practical: Investigating Breathing Rate Before and After Exercise</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Breathing rate can be investigated by measuring the number of breaths taken
per minute before and after controlled exercise.
</p>

<h3>VARIABLES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Independent variable:</strong> exercise condition or intensity.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Dependent variable:</strong> breathing rate, measured in breaths per
minute.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Control variables:</strong> exercise duration, exercise type,
measurement method and recovery conditions where appropriate.
</p>

<h3>METHOD</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Allow the participant to rest.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Count breaths for a fixed period, such as 60 seconds.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Record the resting breathing rate.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Perform a controlled exercise for a fixed duration.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Immediately measure breathing rate again.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> Continue measuring during recovery if investigating
recovery rate.</p>

<h3>EXPECTED RESULT</h3>

<p>
Breathing rate normally increases after exercise because active muscles have
increased their demand for oxygen and production of carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>IMPROVING RELIABILITY</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Repeat measurements.</li>
  <li>Calculate a mean.</li>
  <li>Use the same exercise duration.</li>
  <li>Use the same measurement method.</li>
  <li>Allow adequate rest between trials.</li>
</ul>

<h3>SAFETY</h3>

<p>
Exercise intensity should be appropriate for the participant.
The investigation should be stopped if the participant becomes unwell.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Practical: Investigating Breathing Rate Before and After Exercise', 15),
  (1072, 1072, '
<h2>Pleural Membranes and the Lungs</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The lungs are enclosed within the thoracic cavity and are covered by thin
membranes called the <strong>pleural membranes</strong>.
</p>

<p>
A thin layer of pleural fluid lies between the membranes.
</p>

<h3>FUNCTION OF PLEURAL FLUID</h3>

<p>
Pleural fluid reduces friction between the surfaces as the lungs move during
breathing.
</p>

<p>
This allows the lungs to expand and recoil smoothly as the volume of the
thoracic cavity changes.
</p>

<h3>LINK TO VENTILATION</h3>

<p>
During inhalation, the thoracic cavity expands.
The lungs expand with it, allowing their volume to increase.
</p>

<p>
During exhalation, the thoracic volume decreases and the lungs recoil toward
their resting volume.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
The pleural membranes are associated with the mechanics of breathing.
They are not the main surface where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Gaseous exchange occurs mainly at the alveoli.</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pleural Membranes and the Lungs', 15),
  (1073, 1073, '
<h2>Mucus and Cilia in the Airways</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The airways contain cells that produce mucus and cells with microscopic
structures called <strong>cilia</strong>.
</p>

<h3>MUCUS</h3>

<p>
Mucus is sticky.
It traps dust particles, microorganisms and other particles entering with
inhaled air.
</p>

<h3>CILIA</h3>

<p>
Cilia are tiny hair-like structures on the surface of certain airway cells.
They beat in coordinated waves.
</p>

<h3>HOW THE TWO WORK TOGETHER</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Dust and microorganisms enter with inhaled air.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Mucus traps some of these particles.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Cilia beat and move the mucus toward the throat.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> The mucus can then be swallowed and removed from the
airways.</p>

<h3>WHY IS THIS IMPORTANT?</h3>

<p>
The mechanism helps prevent particles and microorganisms from reaching the
deeper regions of the lungs.
</p>

<h3>COMMON ERROR</h3>

<p>
Mucus does not “kill everything” that enters the airway.
Its primary physical role is to trap particles and microorganisms.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mucus and Cilia in the Airways', 15),
  (1074, 1074, '
<h2>Effect of Exercise on Gas Exchange</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
During exercise, working muscles increase their rate of respiration.
This changes the demand placed on the respiratory and circulatory systems.
</p>

<h3>INCREASED OXYGEN DEMAND</h3>

<p>
Active muscles require more oxygen for aerobic respiration.
The respiratory system responds by increasing ventilation.
</p>

<h3>INCREASED CARBON DIOXIDE PRODUCTION</h3>

<p>
Faster respiration produces carbon dioxide at a greater rate.
This carbon dioxide is transported by the blood to the lungs.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS AT THE ALVEOLI?</h3>

<p>
More oxygen must enter the blood and more carbon dioxide must leave it per
unit time.
</p>

<p>
Increased ventilation and increased blood flow help maintain the concentration
gradients required for continued diffusion.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Exercise does not cause the alveoli to suddenly become a different structure.
The body''s transport and ventilation systems increase their activity to meet
the increased demand.
</p>

<h3>REASONING CHAIN</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Exercise
→ increased muscle respiration
→ increased oxygen demand + carbon dioxide production
→ increased ventilation + circulation
→ increased gas exchange.
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Exercise on Gas Exchange', 15),
  (1075, 1075, '
<h2>Effect of Smoking on Gas Exchange</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Smoke contains substances that can damage the respiratory system.
Long-term exposure can reduce the efficiency of gaseous exchange.
</p>

<h3>DAMAGE TO CILIA</h3>

<p>
Smoke can damage ciliated cells.
When cilia do not function effectively, mucus and trapped particles are less
efficiently removed from the airways.
</p>

<h3>MUCUS ACCUMULATION</h3>

<p>
Damage to the airway lining and increased mucus production can contribute to
airway obstruction.
</p>

<p>
Narrowed or obstructed airways can make ventilation more difficult.
</p>

<h3>DAMAGE TO ALVEOLI</h3>

<p>
Long-term smoking can damage alveolar walls.
Destruction of alveolar structures can reduce the available surface area for
gaseous exchange.
</p>

<h3>CONSEQUENCE</h3>

<p>
Reduced functional surface area and impaired ventilation can reduce the
ability of the lungs to exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide effectively.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The exact effects depend on the type, duration and amount of exposure.
The key biological principle is that damage to respiratory structures can
reduce ventilation or gas-exchange efficiency.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Effect of Smoking on Gas Exchange', 15),
  (1076, 1076, '
<h2>Gas Exchange and Carbon Monoxide</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Carbon monoxide is a poisonous gas that can be produced when carbon-containing
fuels burn incompletely.
It is also present in tobacco smoke.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS CARBON MONOXIDE DANGEROUS?</h3>

<p>
Carbon monoxide binds strongly to haemoglobin.
This reduces the ability of haemoglobin to transport and deliver oxygen.
</p>

<h3>WHAT HAPPENS TO OXYGEN DELIVERY?</h3>

<p>
If haemoglobin is occupied by carbon monoxide, less haemoglobin is available
to transport oxygen.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, less oxygen may be delivered to body tissues.
</p>

<h3>LINK TO RESPIRATION</h3>

<p>
Cells need oxygen for aerobic respiration.
Reduced oxygen delivery can therefore limit aerobic respiration in tissues.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Carbon monoxide does not primarily harm the body by “using up” oxygen in the
lungs.
Its major problem is that it binds strongly to haemoglobin and interferes with
oxygen transport.
</p>

<h3>CAUSE AND EFFECT</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Carbon monoxide exposure
→ carbon monoxide binds haemoglobin
→ reduced oxygen-carrying capacity
→ reduced oxygen delivery to tissues.
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gas Exchange and Carbon Monoxide', 15),
  (1077, 1077, '
<h2>Practical: Comparing Inspired and Expired Air</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A simple investigation can compare inspired atmospheric air with expired air
to demonstrate changes in carbon dioxide concentration.
</p>

<h3>QUESTION</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Does expired air contain more carbon dioxide than atmospheric air?
</strong>
</p>

<h3>APPARATUS</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Two containers or gas-washing arrangements</li>
  <li>Equal volumes of limewater</li>
  <li>Delivery tubes</li>
  <li>Source of atmospheric air</li>
  <li>Source of expired air</li>
</ul>

<h3>METHOD</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Place equal volumes of limewater into two identical
containers.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Pass atmospheric air through one sample.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Pass expired air through the other sample for the same
period.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Compare the appearance of the limewater.</p>

<h3>EXPECTED OBSERVATION</h3>

<p>
The limewater exposed to expired air should turn milky more quickly or more
strongly.
</p>

<h3>CONCLUSION</h3>

<p>
Expired air contains more carbon dioxide than atmospheric air.
</p>

<h3>FAIR TEST</h3>

<p>
The volume of limewater, amount of air passed through, duration and apparatus
should be kept the same when comparing the samples.
</p>

<h3>LIMITATION</h3>

<p>
The investigation demonstrates the presence and relative difference of carbon
dioxide but does not by itself measure the exact percentage of carbon dioxide
in the air.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Practical: Comparing Inspired and Expired Air', 15),
  (1078, 1078, '
<h2>Evaluating a Gas Exchange Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A good biological experiment should produce evidence that supports a
conclusion while controlling variables that could affect the result.
</p>

<h3>REPEAT MEASUREMENTS</h3>

<p>
Repeating the experiment helps determine whether the result is consistent.
A mean can then be calculated from repeated measurements.
</p>

<h3>CONTROL VARIABLES</h3>

<p>
Variables such as the volume of limewater, volume of air, duration of
exposure and temperature should be controlled when appropriate.
</p>

<h3>CONTROL SAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Atmospheric air can act as a comparison sample when investigating whether
expired air contains more carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>VALIDITY</h3>

<p>
The experiment should actually test the question being investigated.
For example, if the question concerns carbon dioxide concentration, the
method must provide evidence about carbon dioxide rather than another gas.
</p>

<h3>RELIABILITY</h3>

<p>
Repeated consistent results increase confidence that the observed pattern is
reliable.
</p>

<h3>CONCLUSION</h3>

<p>
A conclusion should be based directly on the observed evidence.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
“Expired air caused limewater to become milky more rapidly than atmospheric
air; therefore, expired air contains more carbon dioxide.”
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evaluating a Gas Exchange Experiment', 15),
  (1079, 1079, '
<h2>Why Large Organisms Need Transport Systems</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A small organism can often obtain substances by diffusion because its cells are close to the environment.
A large multicellular organism cannot rely on diffusion alone.
</p>

<h3>What must be transported?</h3>

<p>Cells need useful substances such as:</p>

<ul>
  <li>oxygen for aerobic respiration</li>
  <li>glucose and other nutrients for metabolism</li>
  <li>water</li>
  <li>mineral ions</li>
</ul>

<p>
Cells also produce substances that must be removed or transported away, such as carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>Why does size create a problem?</h3>

<p>
As an organism becomes larger, many cells become farther away from the external environment.
The distance over which substances would have to diffuse increases.
</p>

<p>
At the same time, a large organism contains many cells that are continuously using oxygen and nutrients.
Diffusion alone cannot deliver substances quickly enough to all of these cells.
</p>

<h3>Transport systems solve this problem</h3>

<p>
A transport system moves substances through the organism so that materials can reach cells that are far from the exchange surfaces.
</p>

<p>
In humans, the circulatory system transports substances in the blood.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
<strong>Large multicellular organisms need transport systems because diffusion alone is too slow to supply all cells and remove wastes efficiently.</strong>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why a human cannot depend on diffusion alone to supply oxygen to all body cells.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Oxygen enters the body through the lungs.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Many body cells are far from the lungs.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Diffusion over a long distance is too slow to meet the continuous oxygen demand of all cells.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Blood therefore transports oxygen from the lungs to tissues.</p>

<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Humans are large multicellular organisms, so many cells are far from the respiratory surface. Diffusion alone would be too slow to supply these cells with enough oxygen, so the circulatory system transports oxygen in the blood.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Large Organisms Need Transport Systems', 15),
  (1080, 1080, '
<h2>Diffusion in Gaseous Exchange</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Diffusion</strong> is the net movement of particles from a region of
higher concentration to a region of lower concentration, down a concentration
gradient.
</p>

<p>
Gaseous exchange across respiratory surfaces occurs mainly by diffusion.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN IN THE ALVEOLI</h3>

<p>
Freshly inhaled air in the alveoli has a higher oxygen concentration than the
blood arriving at the alveolar capillaries.
</p>

<p>
Oxygen therefore diffuses:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Alveoli → blood
</strong>
</p>

<h3>CARBON DIOXIDE</h3>

<p>
Blood arriving at the lungs contains more carbon dioxide than the air in the
alveoli.
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide therefore diffuses:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Blood → alveoli
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES DIFFUSION CONTINUE?</h3>

<p>
Ventilation replaces alveolar air, while blood flow continually carries gases
away from the respiratory surface.
These processes help maintain concentration gradients.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Diffusion does not require the cell to use ATP to move the gas down its
concentration gradient.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diffusion in Gaseous Exchange', 15),
  (1081, 1081, '
<h2>The Alveoli</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Alveoli</strong> are tiny air sacs in the lungs where gaseous exchange
occurs between the air and the blood.
</p>

<h3>STRUCTURAL FEATURES</h3>

<p>
Each alveolus has a very thin wall.
It is surrounded by a network of blood capillaries.
</p>

<p>
The lungs contain a very large number of alveoli, providing a large total
surface area for gaseous exchange.
</p>

<h3>HOW OXYGEN MOVES</h3>

<p>
Oxygen concentration is higher in the alveolar air than in the blood arriving
at the lungs.
</p>

<p>
Oxygen therefore diffuses across the alveolar wall and capillary wall into
the blood.
</p>

<h3>HOW CARBON DIOXIDE MOVES</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide concentration is higher in the blood arriving at the lungs
than in the alveolar air.
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide therefore diffuses into the alveoli and is removed during
exhalation.
</p>

<h3>WHY ARE ALVEOLI EFFICIENT?</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Very large total surface area</li>
  <li>Very thin walls</li>
  <li>Moist surface</li>
  <li>Dense capillary network</li>
  <li>Continuous ventilation</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Alveoli', 15),
  (1082, 1082, '
<h2>Large Surface Area of Alveoli</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The lungs contain millions of tiny air sacs called <strong>alveoli</strong>.
Although each alveolus is very small, together they provide a very large
surface area for gaseous exchange.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES SURFACE AREA MATTER?</h3>

<p>
Diffusion can occur across the respiratory surface at many points at the same
time.
A larger surface therefore allows more oxygen and carbon dioxide molecules to
cross simultaneously.
</p>

<h3>CAUSE AND EFFECT</h3>

<p>
Large number of alveoli
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Large total exchange surface
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
More area available for diffusion
</p>

<p>
↓
</p>

<p>
Rapid gaseous exchange
</p>

<h3>EXAM APPLICATION</h3>

<p>
If the available alveolar surface area is greatly reduced, the capacity for
gaseous exchange is also reduced because there is less surface through which
oxygen and carbon dioxide can diffuse.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
It is the <strong>total surface area</strong> of the alveoli that matters,
not the size of one individual alveolus.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Large Surface Area of Alveoli', 15),
  (1083, 1083, '
<h2>Thin Alveolar Walls</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The walls of alveoli are extremely thin. The alveolar epithelium is generally
one cell thick.
</p>

<p>
The capillary wall is also very thin.
Therefore, oxygen and carbon dioxide have only a short distance to travel
between alveolar air and blood.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN PATHWAY</h3>

<p>
Oxygen moves:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Alveolar air → alveolar wall → capillary wall → blood
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES A SHORT DISTANCE HELP?</h3>

<p>
Diffusion is faster when the diffusion distance is short.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Thin walls → short diffusion distance → rapid gas exchange.
</strong>
</p>

<h3>APPLICATION</h3>

<p>
If the respiratory surface becomes thicker, the diffusion distance increases.
This can slow the movement of oxygen and carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>EXAM ANSWER</h3>

<p>
If asked why alveoli have thin walls, do not simply write
“for faster respiration.”
</p>

<p>
A stronger answer is:
<strong>“They provide a short diffusion distance, allowing oxygen and carbon
dioxide to diffuse rapidly.”</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Thin Alveolar Walls', 15),
  (1084, 1084, '
<h2>Ventilation Maintains Gas Gradients</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ventilation continuously replaces the air inside the alveoli.
This is important for maintaining concentration gradients during gaseous
exchange.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN</h3>

<p>
Freshly inhaled air contains a relatively high concentration of oxygen.
As oxygen diffuses into the blood, its concentration in the alveoli would fall
if the air were not replaced.
</p>

<p>
Inhalation brings in fresh air and helps maintain a higher oxygen concentration
in the alveoli than in incoming blood.
</p>

<h3>CARBON DIOXIDE</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide diffuses from the blood into the alveoli.
Exhalation removes this carbon dioxide.
</p>

<p>
This prevents carbon dioxide concentration in the alveoli from rising too
much.
</p>

<h3>COMPLETE CHAIN</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Ventilation
→ fresh oxygen enters
→ carbon dioxide is removed
→ concentration gradients are maintained
→ diffusion continues.
</strong>
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Ventilation does not directly cause oxygen to diffuse into the blood.
It maintains the conditions that allow diffusion to continue efficiently.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ventilation Maintains Gas Gradients', 15),
  (1085, 1085, '
<h2>Gaseous Exchange and Diffusion Distance</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The distance a gas must travel across a respiratory surface affects how
quickly diffusion can occur.
</p>

<h3>SHORT DISTANCE</h3>

<p>
When the diffusion distance is short, gas molecules can cross the surface
more rapidly.
</p>

<p>
This is why alveolar walls, capillary walls and fish gill exchange surfaces
are very thin.
</p>

<h3>LONG DISTANCE</h3>

<p>
If the exchange surface becomes thicker, gases have farther to travel.
This slows diffusion.
</p>

<h3>WORKED APPLICATION</h3>

<p>
Suppose two respiratory surfaces have the same surface area and concentration
gradient.
</p>

<p>
Surface A is very thin.
</p>

<p>
Surface B is much thicker.
</p>

<p>
Gas exchange will generally be faster across Surface A because the diffusion
distance is shorter.
</p>

<h3>EXAM REASONING</h3>

<p>
If a question asks why a respiratory surface is thin, link the structure to
the process:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Thin surface → short diffusion distance → faster diffusion → efficient gas
exchange.
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gaseous Exchange and Diffusion Distance', 15),
  (1086, 1086, '
<h2>Gas Exchange and Surface Area to Volume Ratio</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The efficiency of gaseous exchange is related to the amount of exchange
surface available compared with the volume of tissue that requires gases.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS A LARGE SURFACE AREA USEFUL?</h3>

<p>
A large exchange surface provides many sites through which gases can diffuse.
</p>

<p>
For example, the many alveoli in human lungs produce a very large total
surface area.
</p>

<h3>SMALL STRUCTURES</h3>

<p>
Small structures can have a relatively large surface area compared with their
volume.
This makes diffusion between their surfaces and surroundings more effective.
</p>

<h3>ALVEOLI</h3>

<p>
Each alveolus is small, but millions of alveoli together create a very large
surface area for gaseous exchange.
</p>

<h3>EXAM REASONING</h3>

<p>
If the total alveolar surface area is reduced:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Less surface
→ fewer sites for diffusion
→ reduced gas-exchange capacity.
</strong>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Surface area alone is not enough.
An efficient respiratory system also requires a short diffusion distance,
moist surfaces, blood flow and maintained concentration gradients.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gas Exchange and Surface Area to Volume Ratio', 15),
  (1087, 1087, '
<h2>Concentration Gradients at the Alveoli</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A <strong>concentration gradient</strong> is a difference in concentration
between two regions.
Diffusion occurs down this gradient.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN GRADIENT</h3>

<p>
Oxygen concentration is higher in the alveolar air than in the blood arriving
at the lungs.
</p>

<p>
Therefore oxygen diffuses:
</p>

<p>
<strong>alveoli → blood</strong>
</p>

<h3>CARBON DIOXIDE GRADIENT</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide concentration is higher in the blood arriving at the lungs
than in the alveolar air.
</p>

<p>
Therefore carbon dioxide diffuses:
</p>

<p>
<strong>blood → alveoli</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHY MUST THE GRADIENT BE MAINTAINED?</h3>

<p>
If the concentrations on both sides became equal, there would be no net
diffusion in one preferred direction.
</p>

<p>
Ventilation and blood flow help prevent this from happening.
</p>

<h3>COMPLETE CHAIN</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Ventilation + blood flow
→ maintain concentration differences
→ maintain diffusion gradients
→ continuous gas exchange.
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Concentration Gradients at the Alveoli', 15),
  (1088, 1088, '
<h2>Why Diffusion Alone Is Not Enough</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Diffusion is the net movement of particles from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration.
It does not require energy from ATP.
</p>

<p>
Diffusion works well across short distances, such as the movement of oxygen across an alveolus into a nearby capillary.
</p>

<h3>The problem in a large organism</h3>

<p>
Imagine an oxygen molecule entering the lungs.
It cannot simply diffuse all the way from the lungs to a muscle in the leg quickly enough to meet the muscle''s needs.
</p>

<p>
The oxygen must first enter the blood and then be transported close to the muscle cells.
It can then diffuse across the short distance from the capillary to the cells.
</p>

<h3>The transport system and diffusion work together</h3>

<p>
The circulatory system does not replace diffusion.
Instead, it reduces the distance over which diffusion must occur.
</p>

<p>
The sequence is:</p>

<p>
<strong>Lungs → blood → capillaries → tissue fluid → cells</strong>
</p>

<h3>Why capillaries are important</h3>

<p>
Capillaries form networks throughout tissues.
This brings blood close to individual cells, allowing diffusion to occur over a short distance.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why does blood need to carry oxygen close to muscle cells instead of allowing oxygen to diffuse directly from the lungs?</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Muscle cells may be far from the lungs.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Diffusion over this large distance would be too slow.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Blood transports oxygen rapidly through the body.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Oxygen then diffuses a short distance from nearby capillaries into muscle cells.</p>

<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Blood transports oxygen close to the muscle cells, reducing the diffusion distance and allowing oxygen to reach the cells quickly.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Diffusion Alone Is Not Enough', 15),
  (1089, 1089, '
<h2>Gas Exchange in Fish Gills</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Fish obtain oxygen from water using their <strong>gills</strong>.
Gills provide a specialised surface for gaseous exchange.
</p>

<h3>GILL STRUCTURE</h3>

<p>
A gill contains structures called <strong>gill filaments</strong>.
Each filament has many tiny structures called <strong>lamellae</strong>.
</p>

<p>
The lamellae greatly increase the surface area available for gaseous exchange.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN MOVEMENT</h3>

<p>
Water flowing over the lamellae contains oxygen.
Oxygen diffuses from the water into the blood.
</p>

<h3>CARBON DIOXIDE MOVEMENT</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide produced by respiration in fish cells is carried by the blood
to the gills.
It diffuses from the blood into the surrounding water.
</p>

<h3>ADAPTATIONS OF GILLS</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Large surface area from numerous filaments and lamellae</li>
  <li>Thin exchange surfaces</li>
  <li>Good blood supply</li>
  <li>Continuous water flow across the exchange surface</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY DIFFERENCE</h3>

<p>
Humans exchange gases with air in alveoli.
Fish exchange gases with water across their gills.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gas Exchange in Fish Gills', 15),
  (1090, 1090, '
<h2>Comparing Human, Fish and Insect Gas Exchange</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Different organisms have different respiratory structures, but their gas
exchange surfaces share the same basic challenge: gases must move rapidly
between the organism and its environment.
</p>

<h3>HUMANS</h3>

<p>
Humans use <strong>alveoli</strong>.
</p>

<p>
Oxygen diffuses from alveolar air into the blood, while carbon dioxide
diffuses from blood into the alveoli.
</p>

<h3>FISH</h3>

<p>
Fish use <strong>gills</strong>.
</p>

<p>
Oxygen dissolved in water diffuses into the blood across the gill surfaces.
Carbon dioxide diffuses from the blood into the water.
</p>

<h3>INSECTS</h3>

<p>
Insects use a <strong>tracheal system</strong>.
</p>

<p>
Air enters through spiracles and travels through tracheae and tracheoles.
Oxygen diffuses close to the body cells.
</p>

<h3>COMMON FEATURES</h3>

<p>
All three systems provide specialised surfaces that allow gases to move by
diffusion.
</p>

<p>
Efficient exchange requires suitable surface area, short diffusion distance
and maintained concentration gradients.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT COMPARISON</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Organism</th>
<th>Main Exchange Structure</th>
<th>Environment</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Human</td>
<td>Alveoli</td>
<td>Air</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Fish</td>
<td>Gills</td>
<td>Water</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Insect</td>
<td>Tracheal system</td>
<td>Air</td>
</tr>
</table>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Human, Fish and Insect Gas Exchange', 15),
  (1091, 1091, '
<h2>Gas Exchange in Insects</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Insects have a specialised gas-exchange system called the
<strong>tracheal system</strong>.
</p>

<h3>SPIRACLES</h3>

<p>
Air enters and leaves the insect through openings called
<strong>spiracles</strong>, usually found along the body surface.
</p>

<h3>TRACHEAE</h3>

<p>
Spiracles lead into larger tubes called <strong>tracheae</strong>.
</p>

<h3>TRACHEOLES</h3>

<p>
The tracheae branch into very fine tubes called <strong>tracheoles</strong>.
These extend close to body cells.
</p>

<h3>HOW GAS EXCHANGE OCCURS</h3>

<p>
Oxygen enters through the spiracles and travels through the tracheae and
tracheoles.
It then diffuses directly to body tissues.
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide produced by respiring cells diffuses in the opposite direction
and eventually leaves through the spiracles.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE FROM HUMANS</h3>

<p>
In insects, the tracheal system delivers oxygen directly to tissues.
The blood does not play the same central role in oxygen transport that it does
in humans.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gas Exchange in Insects', 15),
  (1092, 1092, '
<h2>Cartilage in the Trachea</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The trachea is an airway that carries air toward the bronchi.
Its walls contain rings of <strong>cartilage</strong>.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS CARTILAGE IMPORTANT?</h3>

<p>
Cartilage provides structural support and helps prevent the trachea from
collapsing when pressure inside the airway changes during breathing.
</p>

<h3>WHY MUST THE AIRWAY REMAIN OPEN?</h3>

<p>
Air must travel freely toward the bronchioles and alveoli.
If the trachea collapsed significantly, less air would reach the lungs.
</p>

<h3>STRUCTURE-FUNCTION LINK</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Cartilage
→ supports airway
→ reduces risk of collapse
→ allows air to pass toward the lungs.
</strong>
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The cartilage does not perform gaseous exchange.
Its main role is mechanical support of the airway.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cartilage in the Trachea', 15),
  (1093, 1093, '
<h2>Gas Exchange in Plants</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Plants also need gaseous exchange.
They require carbon dioxide for photosynthesis and oxygen for aerobic
respiration.
</p>

<h3>STOMATA</h3>

<p>
Gas exchange in leaves occurs mainly through tiny pores called
<strong>stomata</strong>.
</p>

<p>
Each stoma is controlled by a pair of <strong>guard cells</strong>.
</p>

<h3>CARBON DIOXIDE</h3>

<p>
During photosynthesis, carbon dioxide enters the leaf through the stomata and
diffuses through internal air spaces to photosynthesising cells.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN</h3>

<p>
Oxygen produced during photosynthesis can diffuse out of the leaf through the
stomata.
</p>

<h3>RESPIRATION</h3>

<p>
Plant cells also carry out aerobic respiration.
Oxygen can diffuse into cells while carbon dioxide produced by respiration can
diffuse out.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
The direction of gas movement depends on the concentration gradients created
by photosynthesis, respiration and environmental conditions.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gas Exchange in Plants', 15),
  (1094, 1094, '
  <h2>Red Blood Cell</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>red blood cell</b> is specialised for transporting oxygen around
    the body.
  </p>

  <h3>How Is It Adapted?</h3>

  <h4>1. Biconcave Shape</h4>

  <p>
    A mature human red blood cell has a <b>biconcave disc shape</b>.
    This gives it a large surface area relative to its volume.
  </p>

  <p>
    A large surface area helps oxygen move efficiently between the cell
    and its surroundings.
  </p>

  <h4>2. Haemoglobin</h4>

  <p>
    Red blood cells contain <b>haemoglobin</b>.
    Haemoglobin binds oxygen and allows the blood to transport it.
  </p>

  <h4>3. No Nucleus in Mature Human Red Blood Cells</h4>

  <p>
    Mature human red blood cells lack a nucleus.
    This leaves more internal space for haemoglobin and contributes to their
    specialised transport function.
  </p>

  <h4>4. Flexible Shape</h4>

  <p>
    Red blood cells are flexible enough to pass through narrow blood vessels
    and capillaries.
  </p>

  <h3>Structure → Function</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Biconcave shape</b>
    → large surface area
    → efficient gas exchange.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Haemoglobin</b>
    → binds oxygen
    → oxygen transport.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>No nucleus in mature cells</b>
    → more internal space for haemoglobin
    → supports oxygen transport.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Explain how the biconcave shape of a red blood cell
    helps it transport oxygen.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> The biconcave shape provides a large surface area relative
    to the cell''s volume.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> A larger surface area allows oxygen to move efficiently
    between the cell and its surroundings.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> The shape helps the cell exchange oxygen efficiently.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Red blood cells are specialised for oxygen transport through
    adaptations such as haemoglobin, a biconcave shape and flexibility.</b>
  </p>
  
<hr>

<h2>Red Blood Cell</h2>

<h3>What is a red blood cell?</h3>

<p>
A <b>red blood cell</b> is a specialised blood cell whose main role
is to transport oxygen around the body.
</p>

<h3>Haemoglobin</h3>

<p>
Red blood cells contain <b>haemoglobin</b>.
Haemoglobin binds with oxygen in the lungs and allows oxygen to be
transported to body tissues.
</p>

<h3>How is its shape useful?</h3>

<p>
A red blood cell has a <b>biconcave shape</b>. This gives it a large
surface area relative to its volume, helping gases move efficiently
between the cell and its surroundings.
</p>

<h3>Why does it have no nucleus when mature?</h3>

<p>
A mature mammalian red blood cell lacks a nucleus. This leaves more
internal space for haemoglobin.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Explain how the shape of a red blood cell helps it
transport oxygen.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Oxygen must move into and out of the cell efficiently.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> A biconcave shape gives the cell a large surface area
relative to its volume.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> A large surface area provides more area across which
oxygen can diffuse.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The biconcave shape provides a large surface area,
allowing efficient movement of oxygen into and out of the cell.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Red Blood Cell', 15),
  (1095, 1095, '
  <h2>ABO Blood Group Genotypes</h2>

  <p>
    The ABO blood group system has three alleles:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>, I<sup>B</sup>, and i</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>A</sup> and I<sup>B</sup> are codominant with each other.
    Both are dominant over i.
  </p>

  <h3>Blood group A</h3>

  <p>
    A person with blood group A can have:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>A</sup></b>
  </p>

  <p>
    or
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>i</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Blood group B</h3>

  <p>
    A person with blood group B can have:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>B</sup>I<sup>B</sup></b>
  </p>

  <p>
    or
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>B</sup>i</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Blood group AB</h3>

  <p>
    The only genotype is:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup></b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Both alleles are expressed.
  </p>

  <h3>Blood group O</h3>

  <p>
    The only genotype is:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>ii</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Pattern to remember</h3>

  <p>
    A and B each have two possible genotypes, while AB and O each have one.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for ABO Blood Group Genotypes', 15),
  (1096, 1096, '
  <h2>Solving an ABO Blood Group Inheritance Problem</h2>

  <p>
    ABO inheritance problems are solved by first converting the blood groups of
    the parents into possible genotypes.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    A mother has blood group A and genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>i</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The father has blood group B and genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>B</sup>i</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Cross:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>i × I<sup>B</sup>i</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the mother''s gametes</h3>

  <p>
    The mother can produce:
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>A</sup> or i
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Find the father''s gametes</h3>

  <p>
    The father can produce:
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>B</sup> or i
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine the alleles</h3>

  <p>
    The possible genotypes are:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup></li>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>i</li>
    <li>I<sup>B</sup>i</li>
    <li>ii</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 4: Convert genotype to blood group</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup> → AB</li>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>i → A</li>
    <li>I<sup>B</sup>i → B</li>
    <li>ii → O</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Therefore, in this cross, offspring can have blood group A, B, AB or O.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    The calculation gives possible outcomes and their probabilities. It does
    not mean that every family must produce one child of every blood group.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Solving an ABO Blood Group Inheritance Problem', 15),
  (1097, 1097, '
  <h2>ABO Blood Group and Rhesus Factor Are Separate</h2>

  <p>
    The ABO blood group and the Rhesus factor describe different characteristics
    of red blood cells.
  </p>

  <h3>ABO system</h3>

  <p>
    The ABO system determines whether the blood group is:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A, B, AB or O.</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Rhesus system</h3>

  <p>
    The Rhesus system determines whether the person is:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Rh positive or Rh negative.</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Combining them</h3>

  <p>
    These two classifications can be combined.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>A positive</li>
    <li>A negative</li>
    <li>B positive</li>
    <li>B negative</li>
    <li>AB positive</li>
    <li>AB negative</li>
    <li>O positive</li>
    <li>O negative</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Therefore, <b>AB positive</b> does not mean that AB and positive are two
    different ABO groups. AB is the ABO group, while positive describes the
    Rhesus status.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for ABO Blood Group and Rhesus Factor Are Separate', 15),
  (1098, 1098, '
  <h2>Why Blood Groups Matter in Transfusion</h2>

  <p>
    A blood transfusion involves giving blood or blood components from a donor
    to a recipient.
  </p>

  <p>
    The donor''s red blood cells carry molecules called <b>antigens</b> on their
    surfaces. The recipient''s plasma contains <b>antibodies</b> that can
    recognise particular antigens.
  </p>

  <p>
    If incompatible red blood cells are given, antibodies can bind to the donor
    cells and cause them to <b>agglutinate</b> (clump).
  </p>

  <p>
    This can be dangerous because the clumped cells can interfere with normal
    blood circulation and may be destroyed.
  </p>

  <h3>Why matching matters</h3>

  <p>
    Before a transfusion, blood is tested to help ensure compatibility between
    donor and recipient.
  </p>

  <p>
    The ABO group and Rhesus status are important parts of this compatibility
    testing.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Blood transfusion is not simply a matter of matching the letters. Actual
    transfusion decisions require proper blood grouping, crossmatching and
    clinical procedures.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Blood Groups Matter in Transfusion', 15),
  (1099, 1099, '
  <h2>Reading a Blood Transfusion Compatibility Problem</h2>

  <p>
    When given a blood-transfusion question, do not guess from the blood-group
    letters. Follow the antigen-antibody relationship.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Identify the recipient</h3>

  <p>
    Determine the recipient''s ABO group.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Identify the recipient''s antibodies</h3>

  <p>
    For example, a group A recipient has anti-B antibodies in the plasma.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Identify the donor red-cell antigen</h3>

  <p>
    Group B red cells carry B antigen.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Check for a matching antibody-antigen reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The group A recipient has anti-B antibodies and group B donor cells carry
    B antigen.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, those donor red cells are incompatible with the recipient in the
    basic ABO red-cell model.
  </p>

  <h3>Reasoning chain</h3>

  <p>
    Recipient A → anti-B present → donor B cells carry B antigen →
    antibody-antigen reaction → incompatible.
  </p>

  <p>
    This reasoning method is more reliable than trying to memorise isolated
    donor-recipient pairs.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reading a Blood Transfusion Compatibility Problem', 15),
  (1100, 1100, '
<h2>Blood Supply to the Alveoli</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Each alveolus is surrounded by a dense network of tiny blood vessels called
<strong>capillaries</strong>.
</p>

<h3>WHY ARE CAPILLARIES IMPORTANT?</h3>

<p>
Blood continuously flows through the capillaries surrounding the alveoli.
This allows oxygen to enter the blood and carbon dioxide to leave it.
</p>

<h3>MAINTAINING THE OXYGEN GRADIENT</h3>

<p>
Blood arriving at the lungs has relatively low oxygen concentration compared
with the air in the alveoli.
As oxygen diffuses into the blood, the blood carries it away from the alveolar
surface.
</p>

<p>
New blood then arrives.
</p>

<p>
This helps maintain the concentration gradient required for continued
diffusion.
</p>

<h3>MAINTAINING THE CARBON DIOXIDE GRADIENT</h3>

<p>
Blood arriving at the lungs contains carbon dioxide produced by respiring
cells.
Blood flow brings this carbon dioxide to the alveoli, where it can diffuse
into the alveolar air.
</p>

<h3>EXAM CHAIN</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Continuous blood flow
→ gases are continually carried away or delivered
→ concentration gradients are maintained
→ diffusion continues efficiently.
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Blood Supply to the Alveoli', 15),
  (1101, 1101, '
<h2>Transport of Oxygen in the Blood</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Oxygen that diffuses into the blood at the alveoli must be transported to
respiring cells throughout the body.
</p>

<h3>RED BLOOD CELLS</h3>

<p>
Most oxygen is transported by <strong>red blood cells</strong>.
They contain the protein <strong>haemoglobin</strong>.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN BINDS TO HAEMOGLOBIN</h3>

<p>
In the lungs, oxygen binds reversibly to haemoglobin.
This forms <strong>oxyhaemoglobin</strong>.
</p>

<p>
The oxygenated blood is then transported around the body.
</p>

<h3>AT RESPIRING TISSUES</h3>

<p>
Where oxygen concentration is lower, oxygen is released from haemoglobin.
It then diffuses into the tissues.
</p>

<h3>COMPLETE PATHWAY</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Alveoli
→ blood plasma
→ red blood cells
→ haemoglobin
→ body tissues
</strong>
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Haemoglobin allows the blood to carry much more oxygen than could be carried
dissolved in plasma alone.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transport of Oxygen in the Blood', 15),
  (1102, 1102, '
<h2>Haemoglobin and Oxygen Loading</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Haemoglobin is a protein found inside red blood cells.
It can bind oxygen reversibly.
</p>

<h3>IN THE LUNGS</h3>

<p>
The oxygen concentration around the alveoli is relatively high.
Oxygen diffuses into the blood and binds to haemoglobin.
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Haemoglobin + oxygen ⇌ oxyhaemoglobin
</strong>
</p>

<h3>IN BODY TISSUES</h3>

<p>
Respiring cells continually use oxygen.
This contributes to a lower oxygen concentration around active tissues.
</p>

<p>
Haemoglobin releases oxygen.
The oxygen then diffuses into the cells where it can be used in aerobic
respiration.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS REVERSIBILITY IMPORTANT?</h3>

<p>
Haemoglobin must be able to load oxygen in the lungs and unload it at tissues.
If oxygen binding were permanent, haemoglobin would not effectively deliver
oxygen to cells.
</p>

<h3>EXAM CHAIN</h3>

<p>
<strong>
High oxygen concentration in lungs
→ haemoglobin loads oxygen
→ blood transports oxygen
→ lower oxygen concentration at tissues
→ haemoglobin releases oxygen.
</strong>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Haemoglobin and Oxygen Loading', 15),
  (1103, 1103, '
<h2>Transport of Carbon Dioxide</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide is produced by cells during aerobic respiration.
It must be transported to the lungs so that it can be removed from the body.
</p>

<h3>FROM CELLS TO BLOOD</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide concentration is higher in respiring cells than in the
surrounding blood.
Carbon dioxide therefore diffuses from the cells into the blood.
</p>

<h3>TRANSPORT IN BLOOD</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide is transported in the blood in several forms.
A large proportion is transported in the form of
<strong>hydrogencarbonate ions</strong>.
Some is dissolved in plasma and some is associated with haemoglobin.
</p>

<h3>AT THE LUNGS</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide is transported to the pulmonary capillaries.
It then moves toward the alveoli and is exhaled.
</p>

<h3>COMPLETE PATHWAY</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Respiring cells
→ blood
→ pulmonary circulation
→ alveoli
→ outside air.
</strong>
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide is a waste product of aerobic respiration that must be removed
continuously because its accumulation can disturb normal cell function.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transport of Carbon Dioxide', 15),
  (1104, 1104, '
<h2>The Role of Red Blood Cells in Gas Transport</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Red blood cells are specialised for transporting respiratory gases, especially
oxygen.
</p>

<h3>HAEMOGLOBIN</h3>

<p>
Their most important gas-transporting feature is the presence of
<strong>haemoglobin</strong>.
Haemoglobin binds oxygen reversibly.
</p>

<h3>BIKONCAVE SHAPE</h3>

<p>
Mature mammalian red blood cells have a biconcave shape.
This gives them a large surface-area-to-volume ratio and contributes to
efficient exchange of gases with their surroundings.
</p>

<h3>NO NUCLEUS</h3>

<p>
Mature mammalian red blood cells lack a nucleus.
This leaves more internal space for haemoglobin.
</p>

<h3>FLEXIBILITY</h3>

<p>
Red blood cells are flexible and can pass through narrow capillaries.
This allows them to reach tissues throughout the body.
</p>

<h3>LINK STRUCTURE TO FUNCTION</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Haemoglobin
→ oxygen transport
</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Biconcave shape
→ large surface-area-to-volume ratio
→ efficient gas exchange
</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>
No nucleus
→ more space available for haemoglobin
</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Role of Red Blood Cells in Gas Transport', 15),
  (1105, 1105, '
<h2>Components of Blood</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Blood is a transport tissue. It carries substances around the body and also helps protect the body.
</p>

<h3>The four main components</h3>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Plasma</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Red blood cells</strong></li>
  <li><strong>White blood cells</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Platelets</strong></li>
</ol>

<h3>1. Plasma</h3>

<p>
Plasma is the liquid part of blood.
It carries dissolved substances such as nutrients, hormones, carbon dioxide and urea.
</p>

<h3>2. Red blood cells</h3>

<p>
Red blood cells transport oxygen using haemoglobin.
Their biconcave shape gives them a large surface area for gas exchange.
</p>

<h3>3. White blood cells</h3>

<p>
White blood cells are involved in defence against pathogens.
Different types have different roles.
</p>

<h3>4. Platelets</h3>

<p>
Platelets are small cell fragments involved in blood clotting.
They help prevent excessive blood loss when a blood vessel is damaged.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
Blood is not just a liquid.
It contains a liquid component, cells and cell fragments, each performing different functions.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> A blood sample contains plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets. Give one major function of each.</p>

<p><strong>Plasma:</strong> transports dissolved substances.</p>
<p><strong>Red blood cells:</strong> transport oxygen.</p>
<p><strong>White blood cells:</strong> defend against pathogens.</p>
<p><strong>Platelets:</strong> help blood clot.</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Components of Blood', 15),
  (1106, 1106, '
<h2>Blood Plasma</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Plasma is the pale yellow liquid component of blood.
Blood cells and platelets are suspended in it.
</p>

<h3>What does plasma transport?</h3>

<p>Plasma carries many dissolved substances, including:</p>

<ul>
  <li>glucose and other soluble nutrients</li>
  <li>urea</li>
  <li>hormones</li>
  <li>carbon dioxide, mainly in the form of hydrogencarbonate ions</li>
  <li>mineral ions</li>
  <li>heat</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why is plasma important?</h3>

<p>
Cells throughout the body are constantly gaining substances and producing waste.
Plasma provides a liquid medium through which many of these substances can be transported.
</p>

<h3>Example: glucose</h3>

<p>
Glucose enters the blood after digestion and absorption in the small intestine.
It dissolves in plasma and is transported to cells.
</p>

<p>
Cells can then use glucose in respiration to release energy.
</p>

<h3>Example: urea</h3>

<p>
Urea is produced when excess amino acids are broken down in the liver.
It is transported in plasma to the kidneys, where it is removed from the blood.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why is plasma suitable for transporting glucose?</p>

<p>
Glucose is soluble in water. Plasma provides a liquid medium in which glucose can dissolve and be carried through the blood to body cells.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Blood Plasma', 15),
  (1107, 1107, '
<h2>Platelets</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Platelets are small cell fragments found in blood.
They are important in preventing excessive blood loss after a blood vessel is damaged.
</p>

<h3>What happens when a blood vessel is damaged?</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Damage exposes structures beneath the normal vessel lining.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Platelets become activated and collect at the damaged area.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Platelets help form a temporary platelet plug.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Clotting reactions produce fibrin, which strengthens the developing clot.</p>

<h3>Why is clotting important?</h3>

<p>
Without clotting, damaged blood vessels could continue to lose blood.
Clotting also helps reduce the entry of pathogens through damaged skin.
</p>

<h3>Platelets are not complete cells</h3>

<p>
In humans, platelets are fragments produced from large cells in the bone marrow.
They do not have the same structure as ordinary complete blood cells.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> What would be the immediate problem if a person had very few platelets?</p>

<p>
Blood clotting would be impaired, so bleeding from damaged blood vessels could continue for longer than normal.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Platelets', 15),
  (1108, 1108, '
<h2>Blood Clotting</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Blood clotting is a protective process that reduces blood loss after a blood vessel is damaged.
</p>

<h3>The basic sequence</h3>

<p><strong>1. Blood vessel damage occurs.</strong></p>

<p>
Platelets become activated at the damaged area.
</p>

<p><strong>2. Clotting reactions begin.</strong></p>

<p>
A series of reactions leads to the conversion of soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin.
</p>

<p><strong>3. Fibrin forms a network.</strong></p>

<p>
Fibrin strands form a mesh that traps blood cells and helps produce a stable clot.
</p>

<p><strong>4. Bleeding is reduced.</strong></p>

<p>
The clot seals the damaged region and reduces further blood loss.
</p>

<h3>Why must fibrinogen become fibrin?</h3>

<p>
Fibrinogen is soluble in blood.
Fibrin forms insoluble fibres that create the mesh needed to strengthen the clot.
</p>

<h3>Clotting is different from antibody defence</h3>

<p>
Clotting prevents blood loss and helps close damaged tissue.
Antibodies are part of specific immune defence.
They are different processes even though both contribute to protection.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain how fibrin helps stop bleeding.</p>

<p>
Fibrin forms a network of insoluble fibres at the damaged area. The network traps blood cells and strengthens the clot, helping seal the damaged blood vessel and reduce blood loss.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Blood Clotting', 15),
  (1109, 1109, '
<h2>The Septum and Separation of Blood</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The septum is a muscular wall that separates the right and left sides of the heart.
</p>

<h3>Why is separation important?</h3>

<p>
The right side carries mainly deoxygenated blood, while the left side carries mainly oxygenated blood.
The septum prevents these two blood supplies from mixing directly inside the heart.
</p>

<h3>Why does this matter?</h3>

<p>
Body cells need a reliable supply of oxygen for aerobic respiration.
Keeping oxygenated and deoxygenated blood separate allows blood leaving the left side of the heart to have a high oxygen concentration.
</p>

<h3>What would happen if there were an abnormal opening?</h3>

<p>
An abnormal opening between the chambers could allow blood to move between the right and left sides.
This can reduce the efficiency of oxygen delivery.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
The septum is not a valve.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Septum:</strong> separates the two sides of the heart.</p>
<p>
<strong>Valves:</strong> prevent backflow and maintain one-way movement.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why is the septum important?</p>

<p>
It separates the right and left sides of the heart, helping prevent mixing of mainly deoxygenated blood with mainly oxygenated blood.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Septum and Separation of Blood', 15),
  (1110, 1110, '
<h2>Atrial Systole</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Atrial systole is the contraction of the atria.
It helps move blood from the atria into the ventricles.
</p>

<h3>What happens?</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Blood has entered the atria.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The atrial walls contract.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Pressure inside the atria increases.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Blood is pushed through the atrioventricular valves into the ventricles.</p>

<h3>Why are the atrioventricular valves open?</h3>

<p>
The pressure in the atria becomes greater than the pressure in the ventricles.
This pressure difference drives blood into the ventricles.
</p>

<h3>What happens next?</h3>

<p>
The ventricles then contract.
As ventricular pressure rises, the atrioventricular valves close.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> What is the main effect of atrial systole?</p>

<p>
It increases pressure in the atria and pushes blood into the ventricles.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Atrial Systole', 15),
  (1111, 1111, '
<h2>Ventricular Systole</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Ventricular systole is the contraction of the ventricles.
It forces blood out of the heart.
</p>

<h3>What happens?</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The ventricular muscles contract.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Pressure inside the ventricles increases.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The atrioventricular valves close.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> When ventricular pressure becomes greater than the pressure in the arteries, the pulmonary and aortic valves open.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Blood is pumped into the pulmonary artery and aorta.</p>

<h3>Why do the atrioventricular valves close?</h3>

<p>
The rising ventricular pressure would otherwise force blood backwards into the atria.
The valves close to prevent this.
</p>

<h3>Where does the blood go?</h3>

<ul>
  <li>right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs</li>
  <li>left ventricle → aorta → body</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why the atrioventricular valves close during ventricular systole.</p>

<p>
Ventricular contraction raises the pressure inside the ventricles. The valves close to prevent blood from flowing backwards into the atria.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ventricular Systole', 15),
  (1112, 1112, '
<h2>Diastole and Ventricular Filling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Diastole is the relaxation phase of the heart.
During this phase, the chambers can fill with blood.
</p>

<h3>What happens during ventricular filling?</h3>

<p>
Blood returning from the body enters the right atrium.
Blood returning from the lungs enters the left atrium.
</p>

<p>
As the ventricles relax, blood flows from the atria into the ventricles through the atrioventricular valves.
</p>

<h3>Why does blood flow without the ventricles contracting?</h3>

<p>
Blood moves because of pressure differences.
When the ventricles relax, ventricular pressure falls.
If atrial pressure is higher, blood flows from the atria into the ventricles.
</p>

<h3>Why is filling important?</h3>

<p>
The ventricles must contain blood before they contract.
The amount of blood entering the ventricles contributes to the amount available to be pumped during the next contraction.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why does blood enter the ventricles during diastole?</p>

<p>
The ventricles relax, causing their pressure to fall. Blood therefore flows from the atria into the ventricles because of the pressure difference.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Diastole and Ventricular Filling', 15),
  (1113, 1113, '
<h2>The Pulse</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The pulse is the rhythmic expansion of an artery caused by pressure changes produced when the heart pumps blood.
</p>

<h3>Why can a pulse be felt?</h3>

<p>
During ventricular systole, blood is forced into the arteries.
This creates a pressure wave that travels along the artery wall.
</p>

<p>
The expansion can be detected at certain points where an artery lies close to the skin.
</p>

<h3>Common pulse points</h3>

<p>
A pulse can commonly be felt at the wrist or side of the neck.
</p>

<h3>Pulse rate</h3>

<p>
Pulse rate is normally closely related to heart rate because each ventricular contraction produces a pulse wave in the arterial system.
</p>

<h3>Pulse during exercise</h3>

<p>
Exercise increases the demand for oxygen in active muscles.
The heart usually beats faster, so the pulse rate also increases.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can the pulse be felt at the wrist?</p>

<p>
An artery lies close to the skin at the wrist. Each heartbeat produces a pressure wave that causes the artery to expand, which can be detected by touch.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Pulse', 15),
  (1114, 1114, '
<h2>Blood Pressure</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Blood pressure is the force exerted by circulating blood against the walls of blood vessels.
</p>

<h3>Why does blood pressure change?</h3>

<p>
The pressure is not constant.
It rises when the ventricles contract and falls as the heart relaxes.
</p>

<h3>Systolic pressure</h3>

<p>
<strong>Systolic pressure</strong> is the higher pressure produced when the ventricles contract and eject blood into the arteries.
</p>

<h3>Diastolic pressure</h3>

<p>
<strong>Diastolic pressure</strong> is the lower pressure when the heart is relaxed between contractions.
</p>

<h3>How is blood pressure written?</h3>

<p>
Blood pressure is commonly recorded as:
</p>

<p>
<strong>systolic pressure / diastolic pressure</strong>
</p>

<p>
For example, a reading of 120/80 mmHg contains:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>120 — systolic pressure</li>
  <li>80 — diastolic pressure</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why are arteries exposed to higher pressure?</h3>

<p>
Arteries receive blood directly from the ventricles, so blood pressure is highest in the arterial system compared with veins.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> In a blood pressure reading of 120/80 mmHg, which value represents systolic pressure?</p>

<p>
The first number, 120 mmHg, is the systolic pressure.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Blood Pressure', 15),
  (1115, 1115, '
<h2>Measuring Pulse Rate Before and After Exercise</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Pulse rate can be used as a simple measure of how the cardiovascular system responds to exercise.
</p>

<h3>Basic investigation</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Allow the person to rest quietly.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Measure the pulse for a fixed time, such as 30 seconds.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Multiply the result by 2 to estimate beats per minute.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Perform a standard exercise for a fixed period.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Measure the pulse immediately after exercise.</p>

<p><strong>Step 6:</strong> Continue measuring during recovery if required.</p>

<h3>Example calculation</h3>

<p>
A student records 38 pulse beats in 30 seconds.
</p>

<p>
38 × 2 = 76 beats per minute.
</p>

<p>
The student''s estimated pulse rate is therefore <strong>76 bpm</strong>.
</p>

<h3>Making the investigation fair</h3>

<p>
If several students are being compared, the exercise duration and intensity should be kept as similar as possible.
Measurements should also be taken at the same time point after exercise.
</p>

<h3>Repeats</h3>

<p>
Repeating measurements reduces the effect of random variation.
A mean can then be calculated from repeated results.
</p>

<h3>Expected result</h3>

<p>
Pulse rate should generally increase after exercise and then decrease during recovery.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> A student has a pulse of 36 beats in 30 seconds before exercise and 60 beats in 30 seconds immediately after exercise. Calculate both rates.</p>

<p>
Before exercise:
</p>

<p>
36 × 2 = <strong>72 bpm</strong>
</p>

<p>
After exercise:
</p>

<p>
60 × 2 = <strong>120 bpm</strong>
</p>

<p>
The pulse rate increased by:
</p>

<p>
120 − 72 = <strong>48 bpm</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Measuring Pulse Rate Before and After Exercise', 15),
  (1116, 1116, '
<h2>Blood Flow and Resistance</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Blood flow is affected by the pressure generated by the heart and by resistance within blood vessels.
</p>

<h3>What is resistance?</h3>

<p>
Resistance is opposition to the movement of blood through a vessel.
</p>

<p>
The diameter of a blood vessel strongly affects resistance.
A narrower vessel generally offers greater resistance to flow than a wider vessel.
</p>

<h3>Why does vessel diameter matter?</h3>

<p>
When the space available for blood flow becomes smaller, blood encounters greater resistance.
The heart must therefore generate enough pressure to maintain circulation.
</p>

<h3>Why is this important in the body?</h3>

<p>
The body can alter the diameter of some blood vessels.
This can change how much blood reaches particular tissues.
</p>

<h3>Do not confuse pressure and flow</h3>

<p>
<strong>Pressure</strong> is the force exerted by blood on vessel walls.
<strong>Flow</strong> describes movement of blood through the vessels.
They are related but are not the same quantity.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which would offer greater resistance to blood flow: a wide vessel or a very narrow vessel?</p>

<p>
A very narrow vessel would generally offer greater resistance to blood flow.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Blood Flow and Resistance', 15),
  (1117, 1117, '
<h2>Stroke Volume</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Stroke volume is the volume of blood pumped out by one ventricle during one heartbeat.
</p>

<h3>Why does stroke volume matter?</h3>

<p>
Heart rate tells us how many times the heart beats per minute.
Stroke volume tells us how much blood is pumped with each beat.
</p>

<p>
Both therefore affect cardiac output.
</p>

<h3>Calculating stroke volume</h3>

<p>
If cardiac output and heart rate are known:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Stroke volume = cardiac output ÷ heart rate</strong>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>
A person''s cardiac output is 5,600 mL/min and their heart rate is 70 beats/min.
</p>

<p>
Stroke volume:
</p>

<p>
5600 ÷ 70 = 80 mL/beat
</p>

<p>
<strong>Stroke volume = 80 mL per beat.</strong>
</p>

<h3>Why can stroke volume change?</h3>

<p>
The amount of blood pumped during each contraction can change depending on how much blood fills the ventricle and how strongly the heart contracts.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A student''s heart rate is 100 beats/min and stroke volume is 50 mL/beat.
</p>

<p>
Cardiac output:
</p>

<p>
100 × 50 = 5000 mL/min
</p>

<p>
<strong>Cardiac output = 5.0 L/min.</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Stroke Volume', 15),
  (1118, 1118, '
<h2>Memory Cells</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>After an immune response, some lymphocytes remain in the body as <strong>memory cells</strong>.</p>

<p>Memory cells allow the immune system to respond more rapidly if the same antigen enters the body again.</p>

<h3>First Exposure</h3>

<p>When a pathogen enters the body for the first time:</p>

<p><strong>Pathogen → antigen recognised → lymphocytes activated → antibodies produced → pathogen controlled</strong></p>

<p>This first response takes time because the immune system must recognise the antigen and build an appropriate response.</p>

<h3>What Happens to Memory Cells?</h3>

<p>Some activated lymphocytes remain after the pathogen has been controlled.</p>

<p>These cells can remember the specific antigen.</p>

<h3>Second Exposure</h3>

<p>If the same antigen enters the body later:</p>

<p><strong>Memory cells recognise it quickly → rapid immune response → antibodies produced quickly.</strong></p>

<p>The second response can therefore be faster and stronger than the first response.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A person encounters pathogen X for the first time. Later, the same pathogen enters the body again.</p>

<p><strong>Why can the second response be faster?</strong></p>

<p>Because memory cells from the first exposure can recognise the antigen and rapidly activate an immune response.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>Memory cells provide the basis for long-term immunological memory.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for memory_cells', 15),
  (1119, 1119, '
<h2>Why Heart Rate Increases During Exercise</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
During exercise, muscles need more oxygen and produce more carbon dioxide.
The circulatory system responds by increasing blood flow.
</p>

<h3>HEART RATE</h3>

<p>
The heart beats faster during exercise.
This increases the rate at which blood is pumped through the circulation.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN DELIVERY</h3>

<p>
Blood carries oxygen from the lungs to active muscles.
A faster circulation helps deliver oxygen to tissues at a greater rate.
</p>

<h3>CARBON DIOXIDE REMOVAL</h3>

<p>
Blood also carries carbon dioxide away from respiring tissues toward the lungs.
Increasing blood flow helps transport this carbon dioxide to the lungs for
removal.
</p>

<h3>COMBINING THE SYSTEMS</h3>

<p>
The respiratory and circulatory systems work together.
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Lungs → oxygen enters blood
</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Heart → pumps oxygenated blood
</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Muscles → use oxygen for respiration
</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Blood → carries carbon dioxide back to lungs
</strong>
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
An increased heart rate does not itself create oxygen.
It increases the rate at which blood transports oxygen and carbon dioxide.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Heart Rate Increases During Exercise', 15),
  (1120, 1120, '
<h2>The Heart as a Pump</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The heart is a muscular organ that pumps blood around the body.
Its continuous contractions keep blood moving through the circulatory system.
</p>

<h3>Why does blood need to be pumped?</h3>

<p>
Blood must travel between the lungs, heart and body tissues.
The heart provides the pressure needed to move blood through the blood vessels.
</p>

<h3>Two destinations</h3>

<p>
The heart pumps blood in two main directions:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>to the lungs for gaseous exchange</li>
  <li>to the rest of the body to deliver substances and collect wastes</li>
</ul>

<h3>The heart is muscular</h3>

<p>
The heart contains cardiac muscle.
When cardiac muscle contracts, it produces pressure that pushes blood out of the chambers.
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
The heart does not simply contain blood.
It actively pumps blood by coordinated contraction and relaxation of its chambers.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why the heart needs to be muscular.</p>

<p>
The heart must generate pressure to move blood through the blood vessels. Cardiac muscle contracts and produces this pressure, so a muscular heart is required for effective pumping.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Heart as a Pump', 15),
  (1121, 1121, '
<h2>The Four Chambers of the Heart</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The human heart has four chambers:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>right atrium</li>
  <li>right ventricle</li>
  <li>left atrium</li>
  <li>left ventricle</li>
</ul>

<h3>Atria</h3>

<p>
The two atria are the upper chambers.
They receive blood returning to the heart.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>right atrium receives blood from the body</li>
  <li>left atrium receives blood from the lungs</li>
</ul>

<h3>Ventricles</h3>

<p>
The two ventricles are the lower chambers.
They pump blood out of the heart.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>right ventricle pumps blood to the lungs</li>
  <li>left ventricle pumps blood to the body</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why are there four chambers?</h3>

<p>
The four-chambered structure keeps oxygenated and deoxygenated blood largely separate and allows blood to be pumped through the lungs and body at appropriate pressures.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Which chamber receives oxygenated blood from the lungs?</p>

<p>
The pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium.
Therefore, the <strong>left atrium</strong> receives oxygenated blood from the lungs.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Four Chambers of the Heart', 15),
  (1122, 1122, '
<h2>The Right Side of the Heart</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The right side of the heart handles mainly deoxygenated blood returning from the body.
</p>

<h3>Pathway</h3>

<p>
<strong>Body → vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs</strong>
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Blood returns from the body</h3>

<p>
After body tissues have removed oxygen from the blood and released carbon dioxide into it, the blood returns towards the heart.
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Blood enters the right atrium</h3>

<p>
The venae cavae carry blood from the body into the right atrium.
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Blood enters the right ventricle</h3>

<p>
The right atrium passes blood into the right ventricle.
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Blood is pumped to the lungs</h3>

<p>
The right ventricle contracts and pumps blood through the pulmonary artery to the lungs.
</p>

<h3>Why does the blood go to the lungs?</h3>

<p>
The blood needs to exchange gases.
Carbon dioxide moves out of the blood and oxygen enters it at the lungs.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Trace the pathway of deoxygenated blood from the body to the lungs.</p>

<p>
Body → vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Right Side of the Heart', 15),
  (1123, 1123, '
<h2>The Left Side of the Heart</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The left side of the heart handles mainly oxygenated blood returning from the lungs.
</p>

<h3>Pathway</h3>

<p>
<strong>Lungs → pulmonary veins → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body</strong>
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Blood returns from the lungs</h3>

<p>
After gaseous exchange in the lungs, blood has gained oxygen and lost carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Blood enters the left atrium</h3>

<p>
The pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium.
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Blood enters the left ventricle</h3>

<p>
The left atrium passes blood into the left ventricle.
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Blood is pumped to the body</h3>

<p>
The left ventricle contracts and pumps blood into the aorta.
The aorta distributes blood to the body.
</p>

<h3>Why must the left ventricle generate high pressure?</h3>

<p>
The blood must travel throughout the entire body.
The left ventricle therefore needs to produce enough pressure to drive systemic circulation.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Trace oxygenated blood from the lungs to the body.</p>

<p>
Lungs → pulmonary veins → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Left Side of the Heart', 15),
  (1124, 1124, '
<h2>Heart Valves</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Heart valves ensure that blood flows through the heart in the correct direction.
</p>

<h3>Why are valves necessary?</h3>

<p>
When a chamber contracts, pressure inside it increases.
Without valves, some blood could move backwards into the chamber it came from.
</p>

<h3>How valves work</h3>

<p>
Valves open when pressure pushes blood in the correct direction.
They close when pressure would cause blood to flow backwards.
</p>

<h3>Valves between atria and ventricles</h3>

<p>
Valves between the atria and ventricles prevent blood from flowing back into the atria when the ventricles contract.
</p>

<h3>Valves at the exits of the ventricles</h3>

<p>
Valves at the bases of the pulmonary artery and aorta prevent blood from flowing back into the ventricles after the ventricles relax.
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
Valves do not pump blood.
The cardiac muscle produces the pressure; valves control the direction of flow.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> What would happen if a heart valve failed to close properly?</p>

<p>
Some blood could flow backwards when the pressure changes. This would reduce the efficiency of the heart''s pumping action.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heart Valves', 15),
  (1125, 1125, '
<h2>Why the Left Ventricle Has a Thick Wall</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The left ventricle has a much thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle.
</p>

<h3>What does the left ventricle do?</h3>

<p>
It pumps blood through the aorta and around the entire systemic circulation.
</p>

<h3>Why does this require a thick wall?</h3>

<p>
Blood must travel through a large network of blood vessels throughout the body.
The left ventricle therefore needs to generate high pressure.
</p>

<p>
A thicker cardiac muscle wall can contract strongly and produce the pressure required for systemic circulation.
</p>

<h3>Compare the right ventricle</h3>

<p>
The right ventricle pumps blood only to the lungs.
The pulmonary circulation is much shorter than the systemic circulation, so the right ventricle does not need to generate the same pressure.
</p>

<h3>Do not confuse thickness with oxygen content</h3>

<p>
The left ventricle has a thicker wall because of the pressure it must generate, not because its blood contains more oxygen.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why the left ventricle has a thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle.</p>

<p>
The left ventricle pumps blood around the entire body and therefore must generate higher pressure. The right ventricle only pumps blood to the nearby lungs, so it requires less pressure.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why the Left Ventricle Has a Thick Wall', 15),
  (1126, 1126, '
<h2>The Cardiac Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The cardiac cycle is one complete sequence of contraction and relaxation of the heart.
It allows blood to enter the chambers and then be pumped out.
</p>

<h3>The two main phases</h3>

<p>
The heart alternates between:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>diastole</strong> — the heart muscle relaxes</li>
  <li><strong>systole</strong> — the heart muscle contracts</li>
</ul>

<h3>During diastole</h3>

<p>
The heart chambers relax.
Blood enters the atria and flows into the ventricles.
</p>

<h3>During ventricular systole</h3>

<p>
The ventricles contract.
The atrioventricular valves close to prevent blood flowing back into the atria.
The pressure inside the ventricles rises and blood is forced into the pulmonary artery and aorta.
</p>

<h3>Why must the sequence be coordinated?</h3>

<p>
The chambers must contract in the correct order.
The atria receive blood first, then the ventricles contract to pump blood out of the heart.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> What happens to the ventricles during diastole?</p>

<p>
The ventricles relax and fill with blood.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Cardiac Cycle', 15),
  (1127, 1127, '
<h2>The Complete Cardiac Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The cardiac cycle is a repeating sequence rather than separate unrelated events.
The stages work together to maintain continuous blood flow.
</p>

<h3>Stage 1: Ventricular filling</h3>

<p>
The heart is relaxed.
Blood enters the atria and flows into the ventricles.
</p>

<h3>Stage 2: Atrial systole</h3>

<p>
The atria contract and push additional blood into the ventricles.
</p>

<h3>Stage 3: Ventricular systole</h3>

<p>
The ventricles contract.
The atrioventricular valves close.
The ventricles eject blood into the pulmonary artery and aorta.
</p>

<h3>Stage 4: Ventricular relaxation</h3>

<p>
The ventricles relax.
The pressure inside them falls.
The heart begins filling again.
</p>

<h3>Why does the cycle repeat?</h3>

<p>
The body continuously needs oxygen and nutrients and continuously produces waste.
The heart therefore has to maintain continuous circulation rather than pump only occasionally.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Arrange these events in the correct order: ventricular systole, ventricular filling, atrial systole.</p>

<p>
<strong>Answer:</strong> Ventricular filling → atrial systole → ventricular systole.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Complete Cardiac Cycle', 15),
  (1128, 1128, '
<h2>The Heartbeat and Heart Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A heartbeat is one complete cycle of contraction and relaxation of the heart.
</p>

<p>
Heart rate is the number of heartbeats occurring in one minute.
It is commonly measured in beats per minute, abbreviated as bpm.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
If a person''s heart beats 72 times in one minute:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Heart rate = 72 beats per minute.</strong>
</p>

<h3>Why does heart rate matter?</h3>

<p>
The heart must pump enough blood to meet the changing needs of the body.
During exercise, muscles require more oxygen and nutrients and produce more carbon dioxide.
The heart therefore usually beats faster.
</p>

<h3>Resting and exercise</h3>

<p>
At rest, the body''s demand for oxygen is relatively lower.
During exercise, demand increases.
The heart rate changes to help increase blood flow to active tissues.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> A student counts 18 heartbeats in 15 seconds. Estimate the heart rate per minute.</p>

<p>
There are four 15-second intervals in one minute.
</p>

<p>
18 × 4 = 72
</p>

<p>
<strong>Heart rate = 72 beats per minute.</strong>
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Heartbeat and Heart Rate', 15),
  (1129, 1129, '
<h2>Heart Rate During Exercise</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
During exercise, muscle cells contract more frequently and require more energy.
Aerobic respiration therefore increases.
</p>

<h3>What happens to oxygen demand?</h3>

<p>
More aerobic respiration requires a greater supply of oxygen.
Muscles also produce more carbon dioxide that must be removed.
</p>

<h3>How does the circulatory system respond?</h3>

<p>
The heart rate increases.
This allows blood to be pumped through the body more frequently.
</p>

<p>
Blood flow to active muscles can therefore increase, helping supply oxygen and nutrients and remove carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>Step-by-step chain</h3>

<p>
<strong>Exercise → increased muscle activity → increased respiration → greater oxygen demand → increased heart rate → increased blood flow.</strong>
</p>

<h3>Heart rate does not increase randomly</h3>

<p>
The change in heart rate is part of the body''s coordinated response to increased metabolic demand.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why a person''s heart rate increases during vigorous exercise.</p>

<p>
Vigorous exercise increases muscle activity. Muscle cells respire more rapidly and require more oxygen while producing more carbon dioxide. The heart rate increases to increase blood flow and help meet these demands.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Heart Rate During Exercise', 15),
  (1130, 1130, '
<h2>Recovery of Heart Rate After Exercise</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Heart rate does not usually return to its resting value immediately after exercise stops.
It gradually decreases during recovery.
</p>

<h3>Why does heart rate remain elevated?</h3>

<p>
After exercise, the muscles may still have an increased demand for oxygen and removal of carbon dioxide.
The body is also restoring normal conditions after the increased activity.
</p>

<p>
The heart therefore continues beating faster for some time before gradually returning towards the resting rate.
</p>

<h3>How can recovery be investigated?</h3>

<p>
Pulse rate can be measured:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>before exercise</li>
  <li>immediately after exercise</li>
  <li>at regular intervals during recovery</li>
</ul>

<p>
The results can be plotted against time.
</p>

<h3>Interpreting a recovery graph</h3>

<p>
A typical recovery graph shows:
</p>

<p>
<strong>high pulse rate immediately after exercise → falling pulse rate → approach towards resting rate.</strong>
</p>

<h3>Why use several measurements?</h3>

<p>
A single measurement only shows one point in time.
Several measurements reveal the pattern of recovery.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> A student''s pulse is 72 bpm at rest, 132 bpm immediately after exercise, 108 bpm after 2 minutes and 84 bpm after 5 minutes. Describe the trend.</p>

<p>
The pulse rate increases substantially during exercise, reaches 132 bpm immediately afterwards, and then decreases during recovery towards the resting rate of 72 bpm.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recovery of Heart Rate After Exercise', 15),
  (1131, 1131, '
<h2>Cardiac Output</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Cardiac output is the volume of blood pumped by one ventricle in one minute.
</p>

<h3>The relationship</h3>

<p>
Cardiac output depends on:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>heart rate</li>
  <li>stroke volume</li>
</ul>

<p>
The relationship is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume</strong>
</p>

<h3>What is stroke volume?</h3>

<p>
Stroke volume is the volume of blood pumped out by one ventricle during one heartbeat.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A person''s heart rate is 70 beats per minute and stroke volume is 70 mL per beat.
</p>

<p>
Cardiac output:
</p>

<p>
70 × 70 = 4900 mL/min
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Cardiac output = 4.9 L/min.</strong>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A person''s heart rate increases from 70 to 100 beats per minute while stroke volume remains 70 mL per beat.
</p>

<p>
New cardiac output:
</p>

<p>
100 × 70 = 7000 mL/min
</p>

<p>
<strong>Cardiac output = 7.0 L/min.</strong>
</p>

<h3>Why does cardiac output increase during exercise?</h3>

<p>
Active muscles need more oxygen and nutrients.
Increasing cardiac output increases the amount of blood pumped through the circulation each minute.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cardiac Output', 15),
  (1132, 1132, '
<h2>How Exercise Changes Cardiac Output</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
During exercise, active muscles require more oxygen and nutrients and produce more carbon dioxide and heat.
The cardiovascular system responds by increasing blood delivery.
</p>

<h3>Heart rate increases</h3>

<p>
The heart beats more frequently during exercise.
This increases the number of times blood is pumped each minute.
</p>

<h3>Stroke volume can increase</h3>

<p>
During exercise, the heart can pump a greater volume of blood with each beat.
This increases stroke volume.
</p>

<h3>Effect on cardiac output</h3>

<p>
Because cardiac output depends on both heart rate and stroke volume, increasing either can increase cardiac output.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume</strong>
</p>

<h3>Complete reasoning chain</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Exercise
→ increased muscle activity
→ increased respiration
→ greater oxygen demand
→ increased heart rate and stroke volume
→ increased cardiac output
→ greater blood supply to active tissues
</strong>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> A student''s heart rate rises from 70 to 100 bpm during exercise while stroke volume rises from 70 to 80 mL/beat. Calculate cardiac output before and during exercise.</p>

<p><strong>Before exercise:</strong></p>

<p>
70 × 70 = 4900 mL/min
</p>

<p><strong>During exercise:</strong></p>

<p>
100 × 80 = 8000 mL/min
</p>

<p>
Cardiac output therefore increases from <strong>4.9 L/min to 8.0 L/min</strong>.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for How Exercise Changes Cardiac Output', 15),
  (1133, 1133, '
<h2>Arteries</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Arteries are blood vessels that carry blood <strong>away from the heart</strong>.
</p>

<h3>Important definition</h3>

<p>
The direction of blood flow determines whether a vessel is an artery or a vein.
Arteries do not mean "oxygenated blood" in every case.
</p>

<p>
For example, the pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood from the heart to the lungs.
</p>

<h3>Adaptations of arteries</h3>

<ul>
  <li>thick muscular walls</li>
  <li>many elastic fibres</li>
  <li>relatively narrow lumen compared with veins</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why are the walls thick?</h3>

<p>
Blood leaving the heart is under relatively high pressure.
Thick muscular and elastic walls help arteries withstand and maintain this pressure.
</p>

<h3>Why are arteries elastic?</h3>

<p>
The heart pumps blood in pulses.
Elastic fibres allow the artery wall to stretch when pressure rises and recoil as pressure falls.
This helps maintain blood flow between heartbeats.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why an artery has a thick wall.</p>

<p>
Blood is pumped from the heart at high pressure. A thick muscular and elastic wall helps the artery withstand this pressure and maintain the movement of blood.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Arteries', 15),
  (1134, 1134, '
<h2>Veins</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Veins are blood vessels that carry blood <strong>towards the heart</strong>.
</p>

<h3>Important definition</h3>

<p>
The direction of blood flow determines whether a vessel is a vein.
Veins do not necessarily carry deoxygenated blood.
</p>

<p>
For example, the pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart.
</p>

<h3>Adaptations of veins</h3>

<ul>
  <li>thinner walls than arteries</li>
  <li>larger lumen</li>
  <li>valves</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why are the walls thinner?</h3>

<p>
Blood in veins is generally at lower pressure than blood in arteries, so veins do not require the same thick muscular and elastic walls.
</p>

<h3>Why do veins have valves?</h3>

<p>
Blood returning to the heart can be moving against gravity, especially from the legs.
Valves prevent blood from flowing backwards.
</p>

<h3>How does skeletal muscle help?</h3>

<p>
When muscles around veins contract, they can compress the veins and help push blood towards the heart.
The valves ensure that the blood is pushed in the correct direction.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why are valves important in veins?</p>

<p>
Blood pressure in veins is relatively low. Valves prevent blood from flowing backwards and help maintain one-way movement towards the heart.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Veins', 15),
  (1135, 1135, '
<h2>The Pulmonary Circulation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Pulmonary circulation is the movement of blood between the heart and the lungs.
</p>

<h3>The pathway</h3>

<p>
<strong>Right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs → pulmonary veins → left atrium</strong>
</p>

<h3>What happens in the lungs?</h3>

<p>
At the lungs, gaseous exchange occurs.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>oxygen diffuses from alveoli into the blood</li>
  <li>carbon dioxide diffuses from the blood into the alveoli</li>
</ul>

<p>
The blood therefore becomes more oxygenated as it passes through the lungs.
</p>

<h3>Why is this called pulmonary?</h3>

<p>
The word pulmonary refers to the lungs.
The pulmonary circulation is therefore the part of the circulation involving the lungs.
</p>

<h3>Important vessel exception</h3>

<p>
The pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood.
The pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood.
</p>

<p>
This demonstrates why arteries and veins are defined by direction of blood flow, not simply oxygen content.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why the pulmonary artery contains deoxygenated blood.</p>

<p>
The pulmonary artery carries blood away from the heart. It carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs, where the blood undergoes gaseous exchange and gains oxygen.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Pulmonary Circulation', 15),
  (1136, 1136, '
<h2>The Systemic Circulation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Systemic circulation is the movement of blood between the heart and the rest of the body, excluding the pulmonary circulation.
</p>

<h3>The pathway</h3>

<p>
<strong>Left ventricle → aorta → body tissues → venae cavae → right atrium</strong>
</p>

<h3>What happens in body tissues?</h3>

<p>
Blood delivers substances needed by cells, including oxygen and nutrients.
Cells use these substances in their activities.
</p>

<p>
Waste products such as carbon dioxide enter the blood and are transported away.
</p>

<h3>Why is the systemic circulation important?</h3>

<p>
It connects the heart with tissues throughout the body.
This allows the transport system to supply cells and remove wastes continuously.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Trace blood from the left ventricle until it returns to the right atrium.</p>

<p>
Left ventricle → aorta → systemic arteries → capillaries in body tissues → systemic veins → venae cavae → right atrium.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Systemic Circulation', 15),
  (1137, 1137, '
<h2>Double Circulation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Humans have a double circulatory system because blood passes through the heart twice during one complete journey around the body.
</p>

<h3>The two circuits</h3>

<p>
The two circuits are:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>pulmonary circulation</strong> — heart ↔ lungs</li>
  <li><strong>systemic circulation</strong> — heart ↔ body</li>
</ul>

<h3>Complete pathway</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Body
→ venae cavae
→ right atrium
→ right ventricle
→ pulmonary artery
→ lungs
→ pulmonary veins
→ left atrium
→ left ventricle
→ aorta
→ body
</strong>
</p>

<h3>Why is it called double circulation?</h3>

<p>
Blood returns to the heart after passing through the lungs and is pumped again before travelling through the systemic circulation.
</p>

<h3>Advantage of double circulation</h3>

<p>
The system allows the heart to maintain appropriate pressures in the pulmonary and systemic circuits.
The left ventricle can generate high pressure for the body without exposing the delicate lung capillaries to the same pressure.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why is human circulation described as double circulation?</p>

<p>
Because blood passes through the heart twice during one complete circuit: once between the heart and lungs and once between the heart and the rest of the body.
</p>

<h3>Complete reasoning problem</h3>

<p>
A red blood cell leaves a muscle cell and eventually returns to the same muscle.
</p>

<p>
It travels through veins to the vena cava, enters the right side of the heart, is pumped to the lungs, returns through the pulmonary veins to the left side of the heart, and is then pumped through the aorta and arteries back to the muscle.
</p>

<p>
The cell therefore passes through the heart twice before completing the full circuit.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Double Circulation', 15),
  (1138, 1138, '
<h2>Why Blood Pressure Is Highest in Arteries</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Blood pressure is highest in the arteries because they receive blood directly from the contracting ventricles.
</p>

<h3>Step-by-step reasoning</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The ventricles contract.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Blood is forced into the arteries.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> This produces a high pressure in the arterial system.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> As blood travels through smaller vessels, pressure progressively falls.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Blood eventually reaches the veins at much lower pressure.</p>

<h3>Why can arteries withstand this pressure?</h3>

<p>
Their thick walls contain muscle and elastic fibres.
These properties help them withstand and accommodate the pressure generated by the heart.
</p>

<h3>Why is low pressure in veins a problem?</h3>

<p>
Low pressure makes it harder for blood to return to the heart, particularly from the legs.
Valves and skeletal muscle contraction help maintain one-way movement towards the heart.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why an artery needs a stronger wall than a vein.</p>

<p>
Blood pressure is much higher in arteries because they receive blood directly from the ventricles. Their thick muscular and elastic walls help them withstand this high pressure.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Blood Pressure Is Highest in Arteries', 15),
  (1139, 1139, '
<h2>Capillaries</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Capillaries are very small blood vessels that connect the smallest arteries, called arterioles, to the smallest veins, called venules.
They form networks through body tissues.
</p>

<h3>Main function</h3>

<p>
Capillaries are the main sites where substances move between the blood and surrounding tissues.
</p>

<h3>Adaptations of capillaries</h3>

<ul>
  <li>walls are only about one cell thick</li>
  <li>very narrow lumen</li>
  <li>form extensive networks</li>
  <li>blood flows relatively slowly through individual capillaries</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why are capillary walls one cell thick?</h3>

<p>
A thin wall gives substances a short distance to cross.
Oxygen, glucose and other substances can therefore move between blood and tissues efficiently.
</p>

<h3>Why is the capillary network extensive?</h3>

<p>
A large network brings blood close to many cells.
This reduces the distance substances must diffuse through tissue fluid.
</p>

<h3>Direction of exchange</h3>

<p>
Oxygen and nutrients can move from blood into tissues.
Carbon dioxide and other waste substances can move from tissues into blood.
The direction depends on concentration gradients and the substance involved.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain how the thin wall of a capillary helps exchange substances.</p>

<p>
The wall is only about one cell thick, so substances have a short diffusion distance when moving between the blood and surrounding tissues. This allows exchange to occur efficiently.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Capillaries', 15),
  (1140, 1140, '
<h2>Exchange Between Capillaries and Body Cells</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Capillaries connect the transport system to individual body tissues.
Their walls are very thin, allowing substances to move between the blood and surrounding tissues.
</p>

<h3>What leaves the blood?</h3>

<p>
Substances needed by cells can move out of the capillaries, including:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>oxygen</li>
  <li>glucose</li>
  <li>amino acids</li>
  <li>water</li>
  <li>some mineral ions</li>
</ul>

<h3>What enters the blood?</h3>

<p>
Waste substances produced by cells can move into the blood, including carbon dioxide and other metabolic wastes.
</p>

<h3>Why is this exchange necessary?</h3>

<p>
Blood cannot directly enter most body cells.
Instead, substances cross the capillary wall and move through the fluid surrounding the cells.
</p>

<h3>The complete pathway</h3>

<p>
<strong>Blood → capillary wall → tissue fluid → cell</strong>
</p>

<p>
For wastes, the direction is reversed:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Cell → tissue fluid → capillary → blood</strong>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain how oxygen reaches a muscle cell from the blood.</p>

<p>
Oxygen leaves the blood in a capillary, crosses the thin capillary wall, passes through tissue fluid and then diffuses into the muscle cell.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Exchange Between Capillaries and Body Cells', 15),
  (1141, 1141, '
<h2>Tissue Fluid</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Tissue fluid is the fluid surrounding body cells.
It provides a medium through which substances can move between blood and cells.
</p>

<h3>Where does tissue fluid come from?</h3>

<p>
Some fluid and small dissolved substances leave blood capillaries and enter the spaces surrounding cells.
This forms tissue fluid.
</p>

<h3>Why is tissue fluid important?</h3>

<p>
Cells are not usually in direct contact with blood.
Tissue fluid allows substances to move between capillaries and cells.
</p>

<h3>Substances entering cells</h3>

<p>
Oxygen and nutrients can move from tissue fluid into cells when concentration gradients favour movement.
</p>

<h3>Substances leaving cells</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide and other waste products can move from cells into tissue fluid and then into the blood.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
<strong>Blood</strong> is contained inside blood vessels.
<strong>Tissue fluid</strong> surrounds cells outside the blood vessels.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why do body cells need tissue fluid?</p>

<p>
Tissue fluid surrounds the cells and provides the medium through which oxygen, nutrients and waste substances can move between cells and nearby capillaries.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tissue Fluid', 15),
  (1142, 1142, '
<h2>Formation of Tissue Fluid</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Tissue fluid forms when some fluid leaves blood capillaries and enters the spaces around body cells.
</p>

<h3>Why does fluid leave the capillary?</h3>

<p>
Blood pressure inside the capillary is relatively high, especially near the arterial end.
This pressure pushes some water and small dissolved substances through gaps in the capillary wall.
</p>

<p>
Large plasma proteins and blood cells normally remain inside the blood because they are too large to pass easily through the capillary wall.
</p>

<h3>What enters the tissue fluid?</h3>

<ul>
  <li>water</li>
  <li>oxygen</li>
  <li>glucose</li>
  <li>ions</li>
  <li>other small dissolved substances</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why does this help cells?</h3>

<p>
The substances in tissue fluid can diffuse to nearby cells.
This allows cells to obtain materials needed for metabolism.
</p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
The capillary wall acts as a selective barrier.
It allows many small substances to leave while retaining most blood cells and large plasma proteins.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why do red blood cells normally remain inside capillaries?</p>

<p>
Red blood cells are much larger than the small molecules that can pass through the capillary wall, so they normally remain inside the blood vessel.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of Tissue Fluid', 15),
  (1143, 1143, '
<h2>Removal of Tissue Fluid</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Not all of the fluid that leaves blood capillaries returns directly to the blood at the venous end.
Some excess tissue fluid enters lymphatic vessels.
</p>

<h3>Why is excess tissue fluid a problem?</h3>

<p>
If fluid continuously accumulated around cells, tissues would swell.
The body therefore needs a system for collecting excess tissue fluid.
</p>

<h3>The lymphatic system</h3>

<p>
Small lymphatic vessels collect excess tissue fluid.
Once this fluid enters the lymphatic system, it is called <strong>lymph</strong>.
</p>

<h3>Where does lymph eventually go?</h3>

<p>
Lymph is eventually returned to the bloodstream through larger lymphatic vessels.
This helps maintain the correct balance of fluid in tissues.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why must excess tissue fluid be removed?</p>

<p>
If excess fluid accumulated around cells, tissues would swell. The lymphatic system collects excess fluid and eventually returns it to the bloodstream.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Removal of Tissue Fluid', 15),
  (1144, 1144, '
<h2>Comparing Arteries, Veins and Capillaries</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Arteries, veins and capillaries are all blood vessels, but their structures are adapted to different functions.
</p>

<h3>Arteries</h3>

<ul>
  <li>carry blood away from the heart</li>
  <li>high blood pressure</li>
  <li>thick muscular and elastic walls</li>
  <li>relatively narrow lumen</li>
  <li>no valves along most of their length</li>
</ul>

<h3>Veins</h3>

<ul>
  <li>carry blood towards the heart</li>
  <li>lower blood pressure</li>
  <li>thinner walls</li>
  <li>larger lumen</li>
  <li>valves prevent backflow</li>
</ul>

<h3>Capillaries</h3>

<ul>
  <li>connect small arteries and veins</li>
  <li>very small diameter</li>
  <li>walls about one cell thick</li>
  <li>allow exchange between blood and tissues</li>
</ul>

<h3>The key comparison</h3>

<p>
The structures make sense when linked to the function:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Artery → withstand high pressure.</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>Vein → return blood at low pressure.</strong>
</p>

<p>
<strong>Capillary → allow efficient exchange.</strong>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> A blood vessel has a very thin wall and a very narrow lumen. What type of vessel is it likely to be?</p>

<p>
It is most likely a capillary because capillaries have walls about one cell thick and are adapted for exchange between blood and tissues.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Arteries, Veins and Capillaries', 15),
  (1145, 1145, '
<h2>Why Capillaries Have a Narrow Lumen</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The lumen is the internal space through which blood flows inside a blood vessel.
Capillaries have very narrow lumens.
</p>

<h3>Why is the narrow lumen useful?</h3>

<p>
A narrow lumen means red blood cells pass close to the capillary wall.
This helps reduce the distance oxygen must travel from the blood to the surrounding tissue.
</p>

<h3>Slower blood flow</h3>

<p>
The extensive capillary network provides a large total cross-sectional area.
Blood moves more slowly through individual capillaries than through the larger arteries.
This gives more opportunity for exchange between blood and tissues.
</p>

<h3>Connection with red blood cells</h3>

<p>
Red blood cells are flexible and can pass through very narrow capillaries.
Their close contact with the capillary wall helps efficient oxygen exchange.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> How does a narrow capillary lumen help oxygen reach body cells?</p>

<p>
It keeps red blood cells close to the capillary wall, reducing the distance oxygen must diffuse before reaching surrounding tissues.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Capillaries Have a Narrow Lumen', 15),
  (1146, 1146, '
<h2>White Blood Cell</h2>

<h3>What is a white blood cell?</h3>

<p>
A <b>white blood cell</b> is a type of blood cell involved in protecting
the body against pathogens and other foreign substances.
</p>

<h3>How is it specialised?</h3>

<p>
White blood cells have structures and behaviours that allow them to
respond to pathogens.
</p>

<p>
Some white blood cells can change shape and move out of blood vessels
towards sites of infection.
</p>

<h3>Why is this important?</h3>

<p>
Pathogens can enter body tissues and cause disease. White blood cells
help the body detect and destroy these harmful organisms.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is it useful for some white blood cells to change
shape?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Infection may occur inside body tissues.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The white blood cell may need to leave a blood vessel.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Changing shape helps the cell move through small spaces
towards the infected area.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Changing shape allows the white blood cell to move
towards sites of infection and respond to pathogens.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for White Blood Cell', 15),
  (1147, 1147, '
  <h2>ABO Antigens and Antibodies</h2>

  <p>
    To understand ABO transfusion compatibility, distinguish between
    <b>antigens</b> and <b>antibodies</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Antigens</h3>

  <p>
    ABO antigens are found on the surface of red blood cells.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Group A red blood cells have A antigen.</li>
    <li>Group B red blood cells have B antigen.</li>
    <li>Group AB red blood cells have both A and B antigens.</li>
    <li>Group O red blood cells have neither A nor B antigen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Antibodies</h3>

  <p>
    ABO antibodies are found in the plasma.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Group A plasma contains anti-B antibodies.</li>
    <li>Group B plasma contains anti-A antibodies.</li>
    <li>Group AB plasma contains neither anti-A nor anti-B antibodies.</li>
    <li>Group O plasma contains both anti-A and anti-B antibodies.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>

  <p>
    An antibody can react with the corresponding antigen.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, anti-A antibodies can react with A antigens.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why the ABO group of donor red blood cells matters during
    transfusion.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for ABO Antigens and Antibodies', 15),
  (1148, 1148, '
<h2>White Blood Cells</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
White blood cells are involved in protecting the body against pathogens such as bacteria, viruses and other disease-causing organisms.
</p>

<p>
Unlike mature mammalian red blood cells, white blood cells contain nuclei.
They also have different shapes and functions depending on their type.
</p>

<h3>Two important types</h3>

<p>
Two major groups studied at secondary level are:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>phagocytes</strong> — engulf and digest pathogens</li>
  <li><strong>lymphocytes</strong> — produce specific antibodies and are involved in immune responses</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why can white blood cells leave blood vessels?</h3>

<p>
Some white blood cells can change shape and move through gaps in capillary walls.
This allows them to reach infected or damaged tissues.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
Red blood cells mainly transport oxygen.
White blood cells mainly defend the body.
They therefore perform very different roles despite both being cells found in blood.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> A person has a bacterial infection. Why might the number of white blood cells increase?</p>

<p>
The body needs to respond to the invading bacteria.
Producing or mobilising more white blood cells can increase the body''s ability to identify, attack and remove pathogens.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for White Blood Cells', 15),
  (1149, 1149, '
<h2>Phagocytes</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Phagocytes are white blood cells that protect the body by engulfing and digesting pathogens.
This process is called <strong>phagocytosis</strong>.
</p>

<h3>How phagocytosis happens</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The phagocyte moves towards the pathogen.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The cell membrane surrounds the pathogen.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The pathogen is enclosed inside the phagocyte.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Digestive enzymes break down the pathogen.</p>

<h3>Why is digestion necessary?</h3>

<p>
Simply engulfing a bacterium does not remove the harmful material.
The pathogen must be broken down so that its components can be destroyed or processed.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose bacteria enter a cut in the skin.
Phagocytes can move towards the infected area, engulf bacteria and digest them.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
<strong>Phagocytosis is a non-specific defence mechanism because the phagocyte can engulf and digest many different types of pathogen.</strong>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Describe how a phagocyte destroys a bacterium.</p>

<p>
The phagocyte surrounds and engulfs the bacterium, enclosing it inside the cell. Digestive enzymes then break down the bacterium.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Phagocytes', 15),
  (1150, 1150, '
<h2>Lymphocytes and Antibodies</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Lymphocytes are white blood cells involved in specific immune responses.
One important function is the production of antibodies.
</p>

<h3>What is an antigen?</h3>

<p>
An antigen is a molecule or structure recognised by the immune system as foreign.
Pathogens can carry antigens on their surfaces.
</p>

<h3>What are antibodies?</h3>

<p>
Antibodies are proteins produced by certain lymphocytes.
They bind specifically to particular antigens.
</p>

<h3>Why is specificity important?</h3>

<p>
An antibody has a shape that allows it to bind to a matching antigen.
This means different antibodies can target different antigens.
</p>

<h3>What happens after an antibody binds?</h3>

<p>
Antibody binding can help the immune system identify pathogens and can interfere with their activity or help other immune cells destroy them.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
If a bacterium carries a particular antigen, lymphocytes can produce antibodies that recognise that antigen.
The antibodies bind to the matching antigen rather than randomly binding to every substance in the body.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
<strong>Antibodies are not the same thing as white blood cells.</strong>
Lymphocytes are cells. Antibodies are proteins produced by certain lymphocytes.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can an antibody recognise one pathogen but not necessarily another?</p>

<p>
Antibodies have specific shapes. An antibody binds to an antigen with a complementary shape, so antibodies target particular antigens.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Lymphocytes and Antibodies', 15),
  (1151, 1151, '
<h2>The Lymphatic System</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The lymphatic system is a network of vessels and organs involved in returning excess tissue fluid to the bloodstream and supporting immune defence.
</p>

<h3>Its transport role</h3>

<p>
Lymphatic vessels collect excess tissue fluid from tissues.
The collected fluid, called lymph, is eventually returned to the blood.
</p>

<h3>Its defence role</h3>

<p>
Lymph nodes contain immune cells and can help detect and respond to pathogens carried in lymph.
</p>

<h3>Why does the lymphatic system matter?</h3>

<p>
It therefore has two important connections to transport and defence:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>maintaining tissue-fluid balance</li>
  <li>supporting immune responses</li>
</ul>

<h3>Blood system vs lymphatic system</h3>

<p>
The blood circulatory system is a closed network driven by the pumping action of the heart.
The lymphatic system collects excess tissue fluid and returns it to the blood.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Give two functions of the lymphatic system.</p>

<p>
It returns excess tissue fluid to the bloodstream and supports immune defence.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for The Lymphatic System', 15),
  (1152, 1152, '
<h2>Antigens</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An <strong>antigen</strong> is a substance that is recognised by the immune system as foreign or abnormal and can trigger an immune response.</p>

<p>Antigens are commonly found on the surfaces of pathogens such as bacteria and viruses.</p>

<h3>1. Where Are Antigens Found?</h3>

<p>Different pathogens carry different molecules on their surfaces. These molecules can act as antigens.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<ul>
<li>A bacterium may have specific proteins on its cell surface.</li>
<li>A virus may have proteins on the surface of its viral particle.</li>
</ul>

<p>The immune system can recognise these molecules as different from the body''s own molecules.</p>

<h3>2. Why Are Antigens Important?</h3>

<p>Antigens help the immune system identify what should be attacked.</p>

<p>The immune response can therefore be thought of as:</p>

<p><strong>Foreign antigen → recognition → immune response</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A bacterium enters the bloodstream carrying a surface protein that is not normally found on human cells.</p>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> What role can this surface protein play?</p>

<p><strong>Answer:</strong> It can act as an antigen because it can be recognised by the immune system as foreign.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>An antigen is not the same thing as an antibody. An antigen is the substance recognised by the immune system, while an antibody is a protein produced by certain lymphocytes in response to specific antigens.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for antigen', 15),
  (1153, 1153, '
<h2>Antibodies</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An <strong>antibody</strong> is a specific protein produced by certain lymphocytes in response to a particular antigen.</p>

<p>Antibodies help the immune system recognise and deal with foreign substances.</p>

<h3>1. Specificity of Antibodies</h3>

<p>An antibody has a particular shape that allows it to bind to a specific antigen.</p>

<p>This can be compared with a lock and key:</p>

<p><strong>Antibody shape → matches a particular antigen</strong></p>

<p>Therefore, an antibody made against one antigen does not normally bind effectively to every different antigen.</p>

<h3>2. What Happens When an Antibody Binds?</h3>

<p>Binding can help the immune system deal with the pathogen.</p>

<p>For example, antibodies can:</p>

<ul>
<li>bind to antigens on pathogens;</li>
<li>neutralise some toxins or viruses;</li>
<li>help mark pathogens for destruction by other immune cells.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A pathogen has antigen X on its surface. A lymphocyte produces antibody X that is complementary to antigen X.</p>

<p><strong>What happens?</strong></p>

<p>The antibody binds to the matching antigen, helping the immune system identify and deal with the pathogen.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Antigen:</strong> recognised foreign molecule.</p>

<p><strong>Antibody:</strong> specific protein produced by the immune system that binds to an antigen.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for antibody', 15),
  (1154, 1154, '
<h2>How Antibodies Help Destroy Pathogens</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Antibodies do not simply float around randomly attacking everything. They recognise specific antigens.</p>

<h3>Step 1: A Pathogen Enters</h3>

<p>Suppose a bacterium enters the body carrying a particular antigen.</p>

<h3>Step 2: The Antigen Is Recognised</h3>

<p>A lymphocyte capable of responding to that antigen is activated.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Antibodies Are Produced</h3>

<p>The lymphocyte produces antibodies that are complementary to the antigen.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Antibodies Bind</h3>

<p>The antibodies attach to antigens on the pathogen.</p>

<h3>Step 5: The Pathogen Is Targeted</h3>

<p>The bound antibodies can help other parts of the immune system identify and destroy the pathogen.</p>

<p>Some antibodies can also block pathogens or toxins from attaching to cells.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Pathogen → antigen recognised → lymphocyte response → specific antibodies → antibody binds antigen → pathogen is neutralised or targeted for destruction.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A bacterium enters the body. Its surface contains antigen A. A suitable lymphocyte produces antibody A.</p>

<p><strong>Why does antibody A bind to the bacterium?</strong></p>

<p>Because the antibody has a shape complementary to antigen A.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>The specificity of antibodies allows the immune system to distinguish between different foreign substances.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for antibody_action', 15),
  (1155, 1155, '
<h2>Primary and Secondary Immune Responses</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The immune response to the first exposure to an antigen differs from the response to a later exposure to the same antigen.</p>

<h3>Primary Immune Response</h3>

<p>The <strong>primary response</strong> occurs when the antigen enters the body for the first time.</p>

<p>There is a delay because the immune system must recognise the antigen and activate suitable lymphocytes.</p>

<p>Antibody concentration then increases as antibodies are produced.</p>

<h3>Secondary Immune Response</h3>

<p>The <strong>secondary response</strong> occurs when the same antigen is encountered again.</p>

<p>Memory cells recognise the antigen rapidly.</p>

<p>As a result, antibody production can begin more quickly and reach a higher level.</p>

<h3>Graph Reasoning</h3>

<p>If a graph shows antibody concentration against time:</p>

<ul>
<li>The first exposure usually shows a slower increase.</li>
<li>The second exposure usually shows a shorter delay.</li>
<li>The second response usually reaches a greater antibody concentration.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A person encounters antigen X at day 0. The antibody concentration rises slowly and reaches a modest peak.</p>

<p>Several months later, antigen X enters the body again.</p>

<p><strong>What would you expect?</strong></p>

<p>The antibody concentration should increase more rapidly and reach a higher level because memory cells are already present.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The secondary response is faster because the immune system has memory of the antigen. It is not because the pathogen automatically becomes weaker.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for primary_secondary_immune_response', 15),
  (1156, 1156, '
<h2>Vaccination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Vaccination</strong> exposes the immune system to an antigen from a pathogen in a form designed to stimulate an immune response without causing the full disease.</p>

<p>The vaccine stimulates the formation of immune memory.</p>

<h3>How Vaccination Works</h3>

<p>The basic sequence is:</p>

<p><strong>Vaccine antigen → immune response → antibodies produced → memory cells formed.</strong></p>

<p>If the vaccinated person later encounters the actual pathogen carrying the same antigen, memory cells can produce a much faster immune response.</p>

<h3>Why This Helps</h3>

<p>The pathogen may be controlled before it can cause serious disease.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A vaccine contains material that exposes the immune system to an antigen associated with a particular pathogen.</p>

<p><strong>What is the purpose of this exposure?</strong></p>

<p>To stimulate an immune response and form memory cells without requiring the person to experience the full disease.</p>

<h3>Important Point</h3>

<p>A vaccine does not normally work by directly killing every pathogen before it enters the body.</p>

<p>Instead, it prepares the immune system to respond more effectively if the relevant antigen is encountered later.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for vaccination', 15),
  (1157, 1157, '
<h2>Vaccination and Population Protection</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Vaccination can protect individuals and can also reduce the spread of some infectious diseases through a population.</p>

<h3>1. Vaccinated Individuals</h3>

<p>A vaccinated person has immune memory against the relevant antigen.</p>

<p>If exposed later, the immune response can be faster and stronger.</p>

<h3>2. Reduced Transmission</h3>

<p>If many people in a population are immune, an infectious pathogen may have fewer susceptible people through whom it can spread.</p>

<p>This can reduce transmission.</p>

<h3>3. Protection of Vulnerable People</h3>

<p>High vaccination coverage can therefore indirectly reduce exposure of people who cannot receive particular vaccines or who may respond poorly to infection.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Suppose an infectious disease spreads easily when many susceptible people are present.</p>

<p>If vaccination greatly increases the proportion of people with immunity, what can happen to transmission?</p>

<p><strong>Answer:</strong> There may be fewer susceptible hosts available, reducing opportunities for transmission.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Population protection does not mean that every vaccinated person can never become infected. Its effect depends on the vaccine, pathogen, coverage and transmission conditions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for vaccination_population_immunity', 15),
  (1158, 1158, '
<h2>Active and Passive Immunity</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Immunity can be described as <strong>active</strong> or <strong>passive</strong> depending on whether the person''s own immune system produces the antibodies.</p>

<h3>Active Immunity</h3>

<p>In active immunity, the person''s immune system is stimulated to produce its own immune response.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<ul>
<li>immunity following infection;</li>
<li>immunity produced following vaccination.</li>
</ul>

<p>Active immunity can produce memory cells, allowing longer-lasting protection.</p>

<h3>Passive Immunity</h3>

<p>In passive immunity, antibodies are received from another source rather than being produced by the person''s own immune system.</p>

<p>Because the recipient does not need to produce the antibodies first, protection can be available quickly.</p>

<h3>Key Difference</h3>

<p><strong>Active:</strong> the person''s immune system produces the antibodies.</p>

<p><strong>Passive:</strong> antibodies are received from another source.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A person receives antibodies produced elsewhere to provide immediate protection.</p>

<p><strong>Is this active or passive immunity?</strong></p>

<p>It is <strong>passive immunity</strong> because the person received antibodies rather than producing them as the primary response.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for active_passive_immunity', 15),
  (1159, 1159, '
<h2>Phagocytosis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Phagocytosis</strong> is the process by which a phagocyte surrounds, engulfs and digests a pathogen or other foreign particle.</p>

<p>Phagocytes are a type of white blood cell.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Detection</h3>

<p>The phagocyte detects a foreign particle or pathogen.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Movement Towards the Pathogen</h3>

<p>The phagocyte moves towards the pathogen.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Engulfment</h3>

<p>The cell membrane extends around the pathogen and encloses it inside the phagocyte.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Digestion</h3>

<p>Digestive enzymes break down the engulfed material.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Removal of Remains</h3>

<p>The digested material can be removed from the cell.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Detect → move towards → engulf → digest → remove remains.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A bacterium enters a wound and is encountered by a phagocyte.</p>

<p><strong>What happens?</strong></p>

<p>The phagocyte surrounds and engulfs the bacterium, enclosing it inside the cell. Digestive enzymes then break it down.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>Phagocytosis is different from antibody production. Phagocytes directly engulf and digest foreign material, while antibodies are specific proteins that bind to antigens.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phagocytosis', 15),
  (1160, 1160, '
<h2>Phagocytes and Lymphocytes</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Phagocytes and lymphocytes are both types of white blood cells, but they perform different roles in the immune response.</p>

<h3>Phagocytes</h3>

<p>Phagocytes defend the body by engulfing and digesting pathogens and foreign particles.</p>

<p>The main process is:</p>

<p><strong>Recognise → engulf → digest.</strong></p>

<h3>Lymphocytes</h3>

<p>Lymphocytes are involved in specific immune responses.</p>

<p>Some lymphocytes produce antibodies that bind to specific antigens.</p>

<p>Other lymphocytes contribute to immune regulation and destruction of infected or abnormal cells.</p>

<h3>Why Both Are Important</h3>

<p>The immune system uses several coordinated mechanisms rather than relying on one type of white blood cell.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p><strong>Pathogen → antigen recognised → antibodies bind → pathogen is targeted → phagocytes can engulf and digest it.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A bacterium enters the bloodstream.</p>

<p>A lymphocyte produces antibodies that bind to antigens on the bacterium.</p>

<p>A phagocyte then encounters the marked bacterium.</p>

<p><strong>What can the phagocyte do?</strong></p>

<p>It can engulf and digest the bacterium.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Phagocytes</th>
<th>Lymphocytes</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Engulf and digest foreign material</td>
<td>Specific immune responses</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Non-specific engulfment</td>
<td>Recognise specific antigens</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Digest pathogens</td>
<td>Some produce antibodies</td>
</tr>
</table>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phagocytes_and_lymphocytes', 15),
  (1161, 1161, '
<h2>Complete Immune Response</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An immune response involves several connected stages. Understanding the sequence is more important than memorising isolated definitions.</p>

<h3>Stage 1: Pathogen Enters</h3>

<p>A pathogen such as a bacterium enters the body.</p>

<h3>Stage 2: Antigens Are Recognised</h3>

<p>Molecules on the pathogen act as antigens and are recognised as foreign.</p>

<h3>Stage 3: Lymphocytes Respond</h3>

<p>Specific lymphocytes become activated.</p>

<h3>Stage 4: Antibodies Are Produced</h3>

<p>Suitable lymphocytes produce antibodies that are complementary to the pathogen''s antigens.</p>

<h3>Stage 5: Antibodies Bind</h3>

<p>The antibodies attach to the matching antigens.</p>

<h3>Stage 6: Pathogen Is Controlled</h3>

<p>The antibodies can neutralise or mark the pathogen, helping other immune mechanisms remove it.</p>

<h3>Stage 7: Phagocytes Remove Pathogens</h3>

<p>Phagocytes can engulf and digest pathogens.</p>

<h3>Stage 8: Memory Cells Remain</h3>

<p>Some lymphocytes remain as memory cells.</p>

<p>If the same antigen appears again, these memory cells allow a faster response.</p>

<h3>Complete Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Pathogen → antigen → lymphocyte activation → antibodies → antibody-antigen binding → pathogen removal → memory cells.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Exam Problem</h3>

<p>A student says:</p>

<p><em>"Antibodies kill every pathogen immediately."</em></p>

<p><strong>Is this statement correct?</strong></p>

<p>No. Antibodies are specific proteins that bind to particular antigens. Their binding helps neutralise pathogens or mark them for removal by other immune mechanisms.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>When explaining an immune response, always connect the stages rather than listing unrelated definitions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for immune_response_sequence', 15),
  (1162, 1162, '
<h2>Xylem Vessel</h2>

<h3>What is a xylem vessel?</h3>

<p>
A <b>xylem vessel</b> is a specialised plant structure that transports
water and dissolved mineral salts from the roots towards the rest of
the plant.
</p>

<h3>What makes xylem suitable for transport?</h3>

<p>
Xylem vessels form long continuous tubes through the plant.
Their walls are strengthened with <b>lignin</b>.
</p>

<p>
The strengthening helps prevent the vessels from collapsing as water
moves through them.
</p>

<h3>Why are mature xylem vessel elements unusual?</h3>

<p>
Mature xylem vessel elements are generally <b>dead</b>. Their contents
are lost, leaving a hollow space through which water can move.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is lignin important in xylem vessels?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Water is transported through the xylem.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The walls need to remain strong while water is being
transported.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Lignin strengthens the walls.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Lignin strengthens xylem walls and helps prevent the
vessels from collapsing.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Xylem Vessel', 15),
  (1163, 1163, '
<h2>Why Plants Need Transport Systems</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants need substances such as water, mineral ions and sugars in different parts of the plant.</p>

<p>Diffusion alone is not sufficient for transporting these substances over long distances in a large plant.</p>

<h3>1. Water</h3>

<p>Water is absorbed mainly by roots but is needed throughout the plant.</p>

<p>It is required for:</p>

<ul>
<li>photosynthesis;</li>
<li>maintaining cell turgidity;</li>
<li>transporting dissolved mineral ions;</li>
<li>cooling the plant through transpiration.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Mineral Ions</h3>

<p>Mineral ions are absorbed from the soil and transported to regions where they are needed.</p>

<p>For example, nitrate ions are needed to make amino acids and proteins.</p>

<h3>3. Sugars</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis produces sugars in leaves.</p>

<p>These products must be transported to other parts of the plant where they are used or stored.</p>

<h3>4. The Two Main Transport Tissues</h3>

<p><strong>Xylem</strong> transports water and mineral ions mainly from roots towards the shoots.</p>

<p><strong>Phloem</strong> transports organic assimilates such as sucrose between sources and sinks.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has its roots in the soil and its leaves high above the ground.</p>

<p><strong>Why is a transport system necessary?</strong></p>

<p>Because substances must move over relatively long distances between different parts of the plant, and diffusion alone would be too slow.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p><strong>Xylem → water + mineral ions.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Phloem → assimilates such as sucrose.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for need_for_transport_in_plants', 15),
  (1164, 1164, '
<h2>Structure of Xylem Vessels</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Xylem vessels are specialised structures adapted for transporting water and mineral ions through the plant.</p>

<h3>1. Dead at Maturity</h3>

<p>Mature xylem vessel elements are dead.</p>

<p>This means there is no cytoplasm inside the conducting vessel, leaving a hollow space through which water can move.</p>

<h3>2. Hollow Lumen</h3>

<p>The inside of the vessel forms a continuous pathway for water movement.</p>

<h3>3. No End Walls Between Vessel Elements</h3>

<p>In xylem vessels, end walls between vessel elements are largely broken down, forming a continuous column through which water can move.</p>

<h3>4. Lignified Walls</h3>

<p>The walls contain <strong>lignin</strong>.</p>

<p>Lignin strengthens the vessel and helps prevent it from collapsing when water is being transported under tension.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student observes a long, hollow plant vessel with thick, lignified walls.</p>

<p><strong>Which tissue is most likely being observed?</strong></p>

<p><strong>Xylem.</strong></p>

<p>The hollow conducting structure and lignified walls are characteristic adaptations of xylem vessels.</p>

<h3>Key Structure → Function Links</h3>

<p><strong>Hollow lumen → pathway for water.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Dead cells → little resistance from cell contents.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Lignified walls → strength and resistance to collapse.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for xylem_structure', 15),
  (1165, 1165, '
<h2>Function of Xylem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The main function of xylem is to transport <strong>water and dissolved mineral ions</strong> through the plant.</p>

<h3>Direction of Movement</h3>

<p>In a typical plant, water absorbed by the roots moves through the xylem towards the stem and leaves.</p>

<p>The movement is therefore mainly:</p>

<p><strong>Roots → stem → leaves.</strong></p>

<h3>Why Do Leaves Need Water?</h3>

<p>Leaves need water for several processes.</p>

<p>Water is a raw material for photosynthesis and also helps maintain cell turgidity.</p>

<h3>Mineral Ions</h3>

<p>Water entering the roots can contain dissolved mineral ions.</p>

<p>These ions are transported through the xylem to regions where they are required.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A mineral ion is absorbed from the soil by a root and later detected in a leaf.</p>

<p><strong>Which tissue transported it through the plant?</strong></p>

<p><strong>Xylem.</strong></p>

<p>The mineral ion was transported in the water moving through the xylem.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not write that xylem transports only water.</p>

<p>The correct statement is:</p>

<p><strong>Xylem transports water and dissolved mineral ions.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for xylem_function', 15),
  (1166, 1166, '
<h2>Root Hair Cells and Water Absorption</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Root hair cells are specialised cells that increase the surface area of roots for absorbing water from the soil.</p>

<h3>1. Large Surface Area</h3>

<p>Each root hair is a long extension of a root epidermal cell.</p>

<p>The extension greatly increases the surface area in contact with the soil.</p>

<h3>2. Water Entry</h3>

<p>Water enters root hair cells by <strong>osmosis</strong>.</p>

<p>Water moves from a region of higher water potential in the soil towards a region of lower water potential inside the root hair cell, across the partially permeable cell membrane.</p>

<h3>3. Movement Through the Root</h3>

<p>After entering root hair cells, water moves through the root towards the xylem.</p>

<p>It can pass from cell to cell before reaching the xylem.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is placed in moist soil. Water enters its root hair cells.</p>

<p><strong>Why are root hairs useful?</strong></p>

<p>They provide a large surface area for contact with the soil, increasing the area available for water absorption.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Water enters root hair cells by osmosis.</p>

<p>Mineral ions may enter by diffusion or active transport depending on their concentration gradients and the ion involved.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_hair_cell_absorption', 15),
  (1167, 1167, '
<h2>Mineral Ion Absorption by Roots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants require mineral ions from the soil for growth and metabolism.</p>

<p>Some mineral ions may be present in the soil at a lower concentration outside the root than inside the root cells.</p>

<h3>Active Transport</h3>

<p>When ions need to move from a lower concentration in the soil to a higher concentration inside root cells, they cannot move into the cells by diffusion.</p>

<p>Energy from respiration can be used for <strong>active transport</strong>.</p>

<h3>Example: Nitrate Ions</h3>

<p>Nitrate ions are important because plants use nitrogen to make compounds such as amino acids and proteins.</p>

<p>If nitrate concentration is lower in the soil than inside root cells, nitrate ions can be absorbed using active transport.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root cell contains a higher concentration of nitrate ions than the surrounding soil.</p>

<p><strong>Which process can move nitrate ions from the soil into the root cell?</strong></p>

<p><strong>Active transport.</strong></p>

<p>The ions are moving against their concentration gradient, so energy is required.</p>

<h3>Key Comparison</h3>

<p><strong>Diffusion:</strong> movement down a concentration gradient; does not require metabolic energy.</p>

<p><strong>Active transport:</strong> movement against a concentration gradient; requires energy.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_ion_absorption', 15),
  (1168, 1168, '
<h2>Movement of Water from Root Hair to Xylem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Water absorbed by root hair cells must move across the root until it reaches the xylem.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Root Hair Cell</h3>

<p>Water enters the root hair cell by osmosis.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Movement Across Root Cells</h3>

<p>Water moves through neighbouring root cells towards the centre of the root.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Endodermis</h3>

<p>The endodermis forms a selective boundary between the outer tissues of the root and the vascular tissues.</p>

<p>The <strong>Casparian strip</strong> contains waterproofing material and helps control the movement of substances into the vascular tissue.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Xylem</h3>

<p>Water eventually enters the xylem.</p>

<p>The xylem then transports the water towards the stem and leaves.</p>

<h3>Complete Pathway</h3>

<p><strong>Soil → root hair cell → root tissues → endodermis → xylem → stem → leaves.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A water molecule has just entered a root hair cell.</p>

<p><strong>What is its next major destination in the root?</strong></p>

<p>The xylem, after moving through the root tissues.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>The root does not simply absorb water and leave it in the root hair cells. The absorbed water must reach the xylem so that it can be transported through the plant.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_to_xylem_water_movement', 15),
  (1169, 1169, '
<h2>Cohesion and Adhesion in Water Transport</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Water has properties that help maintain a continuous column of water inside xylem vessels.</p>

<h3>Cohesion</h3>

<p><strong>Cohesion</strong> is the attraction between water molecules.</p>

<p>Because water molecules attract one another, they tend to remain connected as a continuous column.</p>

<h3>Adhesion</h3>

<p><strong>Adhesion</strong> is the attraction between water molecules and the walls of the xylem vessel.</p>

<p>Adhesion helps water interact with the vessel walls.</p>

<h3>Why This Matters</h3>

<p>Water in a xylem vessel can form a continuous column extending from the roots towards the leaves.</p>

<p>As water is lost from the leaves through transpiration, the movement of water through the xylem can be maintained.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student asks why water does not simply separate into isolated droplets inside the xylem.</p>

<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Cohesion between water molecules helps maintain a continuous water column.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Cohesion → water molecules attract each other.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Adhesion → water molecules attract xylem walls.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for cohesion_adhesion_xylem', 15),
  (1170, 1170, '
<h2>Guard Cells</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Guard cells</strong> are specialised cells surrounding each stoma.</p>

<p>Their main function is to control whether the stomatal pore is open or closed.</p>

<h3>Why Is Control Necessary?</h3>

<p>Plants need carbon dioxide for photosynthesis, but opening stomata also allows water vapour to escape.</p>

<p>The plant therefore needs to balance:</p>

<p><strong>Carbon dioxide entry ↔ water loss.</strong></p>

<h3>Turgor and Stomata</h3>

<p>When guard cells gain water and become more turgid, their shape changes and the stomatal pore generally opens.</p>

<p>When guard cells lose water and become less turgid, the pore generally closes.</p>

<h3>Complete Relationship</h3>

<p><strong>Guard cells gain water → turgor increases → stoma opens.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Guard cells lose water → turgor decreases → stoma closes.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Guard cells become less turgid.</p>

<p><strong>What is likely to happen to the stoma?</strong></p>

<p>The stoma closes, reducing gas exchange and water vapour loss.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>Guard cells do not transport water through the plant. They control the stomatal pore.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for guard_cells', 15),
  (1171, 1171, '
<h2>Leaf Adaptations That Reduce Water Loss</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants living in dry environments face a high risk of water loss.</p>

<p>Some plants have structural adaptations that reduce transpiration.</p>

<h3>1. Thick Waxy Cuticle</h3>

<p>A thick waxy cuticle reduces evaporation of water from the leaf surface.</p>

<h3>2. Reduced Leaf Area</h3>

<p>Small leaves or needle-like leaves have less surface area from which water can be lost.</p>

<h3>3. Sunken Stomata</h3>

<p>Stomata positioned in depressions can be sheltered from moving air.</p>

<p>This can reduce the water vapour gradient immediately around the stomatal opening.</p>

<h3>4. Leaf Hairs</h3>

<p>Leaf hairs can trap a layer of still, humid air close to the leaf surface.</p>

<p>This can reduce the diffusion gradient for water vapour.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant growing in a very dry environment has a thick waxy cuticle and small leaves.</p>

<p><strong>How do these adaptations help?</strong></p>

<p>The thick cuticle reduces water loss across the surface, while the small leaves reduce the total surface area available for water loss.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Always connect an adaptation to the problem it solves.</p>

<p><strong>Adaptation → effect on water loss → survival advantage.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for xerophyte_adaptations', 15),
  (1172, 1172, '
<h2>Interpreting a Ringing Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The value of a ringing experiment depends on correctly connecting the observation to the transport process.</p>

<h3>Observation</h3>

<p>A swelling or increased accumulation of assimilates occurs above the removed ring.</p>

<h3>Immediate Explanation</h3>

<p>The phloem pathway has been interrupted.</p>

<h3>Transport Explanation</h3>

<p>Assimilates produced in leaves cannot move normally through the damaged region to sinks below it.</p>

<h3>Conclusion</h3>

<p>The result supports the idea that phloem transports assimilates through the stem.</p>

<h3>Do Not Jump Too Far</h3>

<p>The observation does not mean that xylem has stopped transporting water.</p>

<p>If the xylem remains intact, water transport can continue.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student observes:</p>

<p><em>"The stem above the ring becomes swollen, so water must be accumulating there."</em></p>

<p><strong>Is this a good conclusion?</strong></p>

<p>No.</p>

<p>The more appropriate interpretation is that assimilates transported through phloem are accumulating because their normal pathway has been interrupted.</p>

<h3>Exam Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Ring bark → phloem interrupted → assimilate transport disrupted → assimilates accumulate above ring.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for interpreting_ringing_results', 15),
  (1173, 1173, '
<h2>Water and Mineral Ion Transport in Xylem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Xylem transports <strong>water and dissolved mineral ions</strong> from the roots towards the shoots.</p>

<h3>Water</h3>

<p>Water enters root hair cells from the soil by osmosis when conditions allow a suitable water potential gradient.</p>

<p>It then moves through root tissues and enters the xylem.</p>

<h3>Mineral Ions</h3>

<p>Plants require mineral ions such as nitrate and magnesium for growth and metabolism.</p>

<p>Mineral ions may enter roots by active transport when they must be moved against a concentration gradient.</p>

<h3>Movement Through the Xylem</h3>

<p>Water and dissolved mineral ions are carried together in the xylem sap.</p>

<p>The water is eventually delivered to leaves and other aerial tissues.</p>

<h3>Why This Transport Matters</h3>

<p>Leaves require water for photosynthesis and other cellular processes.</p>

<p>Mineral ions are needed to make important substances such as proteins and chlorophyll.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant develops a magnesium deficiency.</p>

<p><strong>Why can this affect photosynthesis?</strong></p>

<p>Magnesium is required for chlorophyll production, so a shortage can reduce chlorophyll formation and impair photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Transport Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Soil → root hair → root tissues → xylem → stem → leaves.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for water_and_mineral_transport_in_xylem', 15),
  (1174, 1174, '
<h2>Mineral Ion Transport in Plants</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants need mineral ions from the soil for healthy growth and metabolism.</p>

<h3>Nitrate Ions</h3>

<p>Nitrate ions provide nitrogen needed to make amino acids and proteins.</p>

<p>A shortage of nitrate can reduce plant growth.</p>

<h3>Magnesium Ions</h3>

<p>Magnesium is needed for chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>Magnesium deficiency can cause chlorosis, in which leaves become yellow because chlorophyll formation is affected.</p>

<h3>How Mineral Ions Enter Roots</h3>

<p>Mineral ions may enter root cells by active transport when their concentration in the soil is lower than the concentration inside the root cells.</p>

<p>Active transport requires energy from respiration.</p>

<h3>Why Water and Mineral Ions Are Different</h3>

<p>Water commonly enters root hair cells by osmosis.</p>

<p>Mineral ions may require active transport.</p>

<p>Therefore, water and mineral ions do not necessarily enter roots by the same mechanism.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root cell contains a higher concentration of nitrate ions than the surrounding soil.</p>

<p><strong>How can nitrate ions still enter the root cell?</strong></p>

<p>They can be taken up by active transport using energy from respiration.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Mineral ion needed → concentration gradient may oppose uptake → active transport → energy required.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_ion_transport', 15),
  (1175, 1175, '
<h2>Root Pressure</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Root pressure</strong> is a positive pressure that can develop in the xylem of some plants as water enters the roots.</p>

<h3>How It Can Develop</h3>

<p>Mineral ions can be actively transported into root tissues and the xylem.</p>

<p>This increases the concentration of solutes in the xylem.</p>

<p>Water can then enter the xylem by osmosis.</p>

<p>The resulting increase in pressure is called root pressure.</p>

<h3>Evidence</h3>

<p>Root pressure can contribute to phenomena such as guttation, where liquid water droplets are forced out of leaf margins or other structures.</p>

<h3>Important Limitation</h3>

<p>Root pressure is not sufficient to explain the transport of water to the tops of all tall plants.</p>

<p>The transpiration stream and transpiration pull are major explanations for long-distance water movement in terrestrial plants.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant growing in moist soil produces droplets of liquid water at the edges of leaves during conditions of low transpiration.</p>

<p><strong>Which process may contribute to this?</strong></p>

<p>Root pressure can contribute to the movement of water into and through the xylem.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Root pressure = pressure generated in roots.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Transpiration pull = tension associated with water loss from leaves.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_pressure', 15),
  (1176, 1176, '
<h2>Transport in Plants: Complete Comparison</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants use two major vascular tissues to transport materials: <strong>xylem</strong> and <strong>phloem</strong>.</p>

<h3>Xylem</h3>

<p>Xylem transports water and dissolved mineral ions from roots towards the shoots.</p>

<p>The upward movement is closely associated with transpiration and the transpiration pull.</p>

<h3>Phloem</h3>

<p>Phloem transports organic assimilates, especially sucrose, from sources to sinks.</p>

<p>The direction depends on the positions of sources and sinks and therefore is not simply always upward.</p>

<h3>Water Absorption</h3>

<p>Water enters root hair cells mainly by osmosis.</p>

<h3>Mineral Ion Uptake</h3>

<p>Mineral ions may enter root cells by active transport when necessary.</p>

<h3>Water Movement</h3>

<p><strong>Soil → root hair cells → root tissues → xylem → stem → leaves.</strong></p>

<h3>Assimilate Movement</h3>

<p><strong>Source → phloem → sink.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A mature leaf produces sucrose while a developing root requires it. At the same time, water is being absorbed from the soil and transported to the leaf.</p>

<p><strong>Which tissue carries sucrose?</strong></p>

<p>Phloem.</p>

<p><strong>Which tissue carries water?</strong></p>

<p>Xylem.</p>

<p><strong>Why can the two substances move in different directions?</strong></p>

<p>They are transported by different vascular tissues and follow different transport requirements.</p>

<h3>Final Memory Structure</h3>

<p><strong>ROOTS → absorb water and mineral ions.</strong></p>

<p><strong>XYLEM → water + mineral ions.</strong></p>

<p><strong>LEAVES → major photosynthetic source.</strong></p>

<p><strong>PHLOEM → sucrose/assimilates.</strong></p>

<p><strong>SINKS → use or store assimilates.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for transport_in_plants_complete_comparison', 15),
  (1177, 1177, '
<h2>Transpiration and the Water Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Transpiration</strong> is the loss of water vapour from the aerial parts
of a plant, mainly through stomata in the leaves.
</p>

<p>
It connects the movement of water through plants with the wider water cycle.
</p>

<h3>HOW WATER REACHES THE LEAF</h3>

<p>
Water is absorbed from the soil by roots.
It moves through the plant mainly in xylem vessels.
Water eventually reaches the leaves.
</p>

<h3>LOSS FROM THE LEAF</h3>

<p>
Water evaporates from moist surfaces inside the leaf and the water vapour
diffuses out through open stomata.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Roots absorb water from the soil.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Water moves through the xylem.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Water reaches leaf tissues.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Water evaporates from moist internal surfaces.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Water vapour diffuses out through stomata.</p>

<p>
The water has therefore moved from the soil through the plant and into the
atmosphere.
</p>

<h3>WHY TRANSPIRATION MATTERS TO THE WATER CYCLE</h3>

<p>
Plants transfer substantial amounts of water from soil to atmosphere.
This means vegetation is an active part of the movement of water through
terrestrial ecosystems.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transpiration and the Water Cycle', 15),
  (1178, 1178, '
<h2>Transpiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Transpiration</strong> is the loss of water vapour from the aerial parts of a plant, mainly through stomata in the leaves.</p>

<h3>How It Happens</h3>

<p>Water is transported from the roots to the leaves through the xylem.</p>

<p>Some of this water reaches the leaf cells.</p>

<p>Water evaporates from moist cell surfaces inside the leaf into the air spaces.</p>

<p>The water vapour then diffuses out of the leaf, mainly through the stomata.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Roots absorb water → xylem transports water → water reaches leaf → water evaporates → water vapour diffuses out through stomata.</strong></p>

<h3>Why Is Transpiration Important?</h3>

<p>Transpiration contributes to the movement of water through the plant and helps cool leaves.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant loses water vapour mainly through openings in its leaves.</p>

<p><strong>What is this process called?</strong></p>

<p><strong>Transpiration.</strong></p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Transpiration is not the same as evaporation alone.</p>

<p>It specifically describes water loss from the plant, mainly as water vapour through stomata.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for transpiration', 15),
  (1179, 1179, '
<h2>The Transpiration Stream</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The <strong>transpiration stream</strong> is the continuous movement of water and dissolved mineral ions from the roots through the xylem towards the leaves.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Water Enters the Root</h3>

<p>Water enters root hair cells by osmosis.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Water Enters the Xylem</h3>

<p>Water moves through the root and enters the xylem.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Water Moves Up the Plant</h3>

<p>Water moves through xylem vessels in the stem towards the leaves.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Water Leaves the Leaf</h3>

<p>Water evaporates from leaf surfaces and water vapour diffuses out through stomata.</p>

<p>This loss of water helps maintain a flow of water through the xylem.</p>

<h3>Mineral Ions</h3>

<p>Dissolved mineral ions are carried with the water in the xylem.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is losing water rapidly through its leaves on a hot, dry day.</p>

<p><strong>What is likely to happen to the movement of water through the xylem?</strong></p>

<p>Water movement through the plant can increase because the increased water loss from the leaves strengthens the transpiration-driven flow.</p>

<h3>Complete Pathway</h3>

<p><strong>Soil → roots → xylem → stem → leaves → atmosphere.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for transpiration_stream', 15),
  (1180, 1180, '
<h2>Transpiration Pull</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Transpiration pull</strong> is the pulling force associated with water loss from leaves that helps draw water upward through the xylem.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Water Evaporates</h3>

<p>Water evaporates from moist surfaces inside the leaf.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Water Vapour Leaves</h3>

<p>Water vapour diffuses out through the stomata.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Tension Develops</h3>

<p>The loss of water from the leaf creates tension in the continuous water column within the xylem.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Water Is Pulled Up</h3>

<p>Because water molecules are cohesive, the movement of water at the leaf can transmit a pulling force down the xylem.</p>

<p>Water is therefore drawn from the stem and roots towards the leaves.</p>

<h3>Complete Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Transpiration → water loss from leaf → tension in xylem water column → upward pull on water → continued water movement from roots.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is placed in moving, dry air. Transpiration increases.</p>

<p><strong>What happens to transpiration pull?</strong></p>

<p>In suitable conditions, increased water loss can increase the transpiration-driven pull on the water column.</p>

<h3>Important Point</h3>

<p>Transpiration pull does not mean that leaves physically suck water like a pump. The pull arises from water loss at the leaf and the cohesive properties of the water column.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for transpiration_pull', 15),
  (1181, 1181, '
<h2>Factors Affecting Transpiration Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The rate of transpiration depends on how quickly water vapour can move from the leaf to the surrounding air.</p>

<h3>1. Temperature</h3>

<p>Higher temperature generally increases evaporation and can increase transpiration if other factors are suitable.</p>

<h3>2. Humidity</h3>

<p>High humidity reduces the concentration gradient between the moist leaf air spaces and the surrounding air.</p>

<p>Therefore, transpiration generally decreases.</p>

<h3>3. Wind Speed</h3>

<p>Moving air removes water vapour from around the leaf surface.</p>

<p>This can maintain a steep diffusion gradient and increase transpiration.</p>

<h3>4. Light Intensity</h3>

<p>Higher light intensity can cause stomata to open in many plants, increasing gas exchange and potentially increasing transpiration.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two identical plants are placed under identical light conditions.</p>

<p>Plant A is in humid air.</p>

<p>Plant B is in dry air.</p>

<p><strong>Which plant is likely to have the higher transpiration rate?</strong></p>

<p>Plant B, because the drier surrounding air produces a larger water vapour concentration gradient.</p>

<h3>Summary</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Factor</th>
<th>Typical effect of increase</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Temperature</td>
<td>Transpiration increases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Humidity</td>
<td>Transpiration decreases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Wind speed</td>
<td>Transpiration increases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Light intensity</td>
<td>Often increases transpiration through stomatal opening</td>
</tr>
</table>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for factors_affecting_transpiration', 15),
  (1182, 1182, '
<h2>Investigating Transpiration Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot can be used as an indirect measure of transpiration rate.</p>

<p>A common apparatus for this is a <strong>potometer</strong>.</p>

<h3>How a Potometer Works</h3>

<p>A leafy shoot is connected to a water-filled apparatus containing a capillary tube.</p>

<p>As the shoot loses water by transpiration, water is drawn through the apparatus.</p>

<p>The movement of an air bubble in the capillary tube can be measured.</p>

<h3>Calculating Rate</h3>

<p>If the air bubble moves a known distance in a known time:</p>

<p><strong>Rate of movement = distance moved ÷ time</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Calculation</h3>

<p>An air bubble moves 30 mm in 5 minutes.</p>

<p><strong>Rate = 30 ÷ 5</strong></p>

<p><strong>Rate = 6 mm/min</strong></p>

<h3>Fair Test</h3>

<p>If testing the effect of wind speed, other factors should be controlled.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<ul>
<li>same plant species;</li>
<li>similar leaf area;</li>
<li>same temperature;</li>
<li>same light intensity;</li>
<li>same time period.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Important Limitation</h3>

<p>A potometer measures water uptake, not transpiration directly.</p>

<p>Water uptake is used as an estimate because most of the water taken up by a leafy shoot is eventually lost through transpiration, but not all water absorbed is necessarily transpired immediately.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for transpiration_investigation', 15),
  (1183, 1183, '
<h2>Temperature and Transpiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Temperature affects the rate at which water evaporates from moist surfaces inside a leaf.</p>

<h3>Increasing Temperature</h3>

<p>When temperature increases, water molecules have more kinetic energy and evaporation generally becomes faster.</p>

<p>If stomata remain sufficiently open, this can increase transpiration.</p>

<h3>Decreasing Temperature</h3>

<p>At lower temperatures, evaporation generally occurs more slowly.</p>

<p>Therefore, transpiration can decrease.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is placed in two environments.</p>

<p>Environment A: 20°C.</p>

<p>Environment B: 30°C.</p>

<p>All other conditions are kept constant and the stomata remain open.</p>

<p><strong>Which environment is likely to produce the higher transpiration rate?</strong></p>

<p>Environment B, because the higher temperature generally increases evaporation.</p>

<h3>Exam Reasoning</h3>

<p>Do not simply write:</p>

<p><em>"Temperature increases transpiration."</em></p>

<p>Explain the mechanism:</p>

<p><strong>Higher temperature → faster evaporation → more water vapour available to diffuse out → increased transpiration.</strong></p>

<h3>Important Qualification</h3>

<p>The actual response can depend on other factors, including stomatal behaviour and water availability.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for transpiration_temperature', 15),
  (1184, 1184, '
<h2>Humidity and Transpiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Humidity</strong> describes the amount of water vapour present in the air.</p>

<p>Humidity affects the diffusion gradient between the air spaces inside a leaf and the surrounding atmosphere.</p>

<h3>High Humidity</h3>

<p>When surrounding air is humid, it already contains a relatively large amount of water vapour.</p>

<p>The difference in water vapour concentration between the leaf and atmosphere becomes smaller.</p>

<p>Water vapour therefore diffuses out more slowly.</p>

<p><strong>High humidity → lower transpiration rate.</strong></p>

<h3>Low Humidity</h3>

<p>Dry air contains less water vapour.</p>

<p>This creates a larger gradient between the moist air inside the leaf and the surrounding atmosphere.</p>

<p>Water vapour can diffuse out more rapidly.</p>

<p><strong>Low humidity → higher transpiration rate.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is moved from a humid greenhouse into dry outdoor air.</p>

<p><strong>What happens to the water vapour gradient?</strong></p>

<p>The gradient increases because the surrounding air contains less water vapour.</p>

<p>This can increase the rate of transpiration.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Lower humidity → larger water vapour gradient → faster diffusion → increased transpiration.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for transpiration_humidity', 15),
  (1185, 1185, '
<h2>Wind and Transpiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Wind can affect transpiration by changing the layer of air immediately surrounding the leaf.</p>

<h3>Still Air</h3>

<p>When air is still, water vapour accumulates near the leaf surface.</p>

<p>This creates a relatively humid layer around the leaf.</p>

<p>The diffusion gradient becomes smaller, so transpiration can slow.</p>

<h3>Moving Air</h3>

<p>Wind removes water vapour from around the leaf surface.</p>

<p>This prevents the formation of a thick humid boundary layer.</p>

<p>The water vapour gradient between the leaf and atmosphere remains steep.</p>

<p>Transpiration can therefore increase.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two identical plants are exposed to the same temperature and light.</p>

<p>Plant A is in still air.</p>

<p>Plant B is exposed to moving air.</p>

<p><strong>Which plant is likely to transpire faster?</strong></p>

<p>Plant B.</p>

<p>Moving air removes water vapour surrounding the leaf, helping maintain a steep diffusion gradient.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Wind increases → humid boundary layer removed → water vapour gradient maintained → diffusion increases → transpiration increases.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for wind_and_transpiration', 15),
  (1186, 1186, '
<h2>Light Intensity and Transpiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Light can affect transpiration because it influences stomatal behaviour in many plants.</p>

<h3>Increased Light</h3>

<p>In many plants, increasing light intensity causes guard cells to become more turgid and stomata to open.</p>

<p>Open stomata allow carbon dioxide to enter for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>They also provide a route for water vapour to leave.</p>

<p>Therefore, transpiration can increase.</p>

<h3>Reduced Light</h3>

<p>In lower light conditions, stomata in many plants tend to become less open.</p>

<p>This can reduce water vapour loss.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is moved from darkness into bright light.</p>

<p><strong>Why might its transpiration rate increase?</strong></p>

<p>Light can promote stomatal opening, increasing the pathway available for water vapour to diffuse out.</p>

<h3>Important Qualification</h3>

<p>Light is not the only factor controlling stomata.</p>

<p>Water availability, carbon dioxide concentration and other environmental conditions can also affect stomatal behaviour.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Higher light → stomata tend to open → greater stomatal pore area → increased water vapour loss → potentially higher transpiration.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for light_and_transpiration', 15),
  (1187, 1187, '
<h2>Comparing Factors Affecting Transpiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Several environmental factors can change transpiration rate.</p>

<h3>Temperature</h3>

<p>Higher temperature generally increases evaporation and can increase transpiration.</p>

<h3>Humidity</h3>

<p>Higher humidity reduces the water vapour gradient and generally decreases transpiration.</p>

<h3>Wind</h3>

<p>Higher wind speed removes water vapour around the leaf and can increase transpiration.</p>

<h3>Light</h3>

<p>Higher light can cause stomata to open in many plants, potentially increasing transpiration.</p>

<h3>Worked Comparison</h3>

<p>Consider four situations:</p>

<ul>
<li>A: hot, dry and windy</li>
<li>B: cool, humid and still</li>
<li>C: cool, dry and still</li>
<li>D: hot, humid and still</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Which is most likely to have the highest transpiration rate?</strong></p>

<p><strong>A: hot, dry and windy.</strong></p>

<p>Heat increases evaporation, dry air creates a large water vapour gradient, and wind removes humid air from around the leaf.</p>

<h3>Exam Strategy</h3>

<p>When several factors change at once, explain the effect of each factor before deciding the overall outcome.</p>

<p>Do not simply memorise "hot = high" or "cold = low". Consider the complete set of conditions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for transpiration_factor_comparison', 15),
  (1188, 1188, '
<h2>Transpiration Pull and Root Pressure</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Two processes associated with movement of water in xylem are <strong>transpiration pull</strong> and <strong>root pressure</strong>.</p>

<h3>Transpiration Pull</h3>

<p>Water evaporates from moist surfaces inside leaves and leaves the plant as water vapour through stomata.</p>

<p>This loss of water creates tension in the continuous water column in the xylem.</p>

<p>Because water molecules cohere to one another, the tension can pull water upwards through the xylem.</p>

<h3>Root Pressure</h3>

<p>Root pressure develops when processes in the roots cause water to enter the xylem and generate positive pressure.</p>

<h3>Main Difference</h3>

<p><strong>Transpiration pull is associated with tension generated by water loss at the leaves.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Root pressure is positive pressure generated in the roots.</strong></p>

<h3>Which Is More Important for Tall Plants?</h3>

<p>Transpiration pull provides the major explanation for long-distance upward movement of water in tall terrestrial plants.</p>

<p>Root pressure alone cannot account for this transport.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student says:</p>

<p><em>"Water rises in a tree because the roots push it all the way to the leaves."</em></p>

<p><strong>Why is this incomplete?</strong></p>

<p>Root pressure can contribute to water movement, but transpiration pull associated with water loss from leaves is a major mechanism for long-distance transport.</p>

<h3>Exam Comparison</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Transpiration Pull</th>
<th>Root Pressure</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Origin</td>
<td>Water loss from leaves</td>
<td>Processes in roots</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Type of pressure</td>
<td>Tension / negative pressure</td>
<td>Positive pressure</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Importance</td>
<td>Major role in long-distance transport</td>
<td>Can contribute but is insufficient alone for tall plants</td>
</tr>
</table>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for transpiration_pull_vs_root_pressure', 15),
  (1189, 1189, '
<h2>Stomata</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Stomata</strong> are small pores found mainly in the epidermis of leaves.</p>

<p>They provide a pathway for gases to move between the inside of the leaf and the atmosphere.</p>

<h3>1. Gas Exchange</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide can enter the leaf through open stomata for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Oxygen produced during photosynthesis can leave through the stomata.</p>

<p>Plants also exchange gases associated with respiration.</p>

<h3>2. Water Loss</h3>

<p>Water vapour can diffuse out of the leaf through open stomata.</p>

<p>This is the main route of transpiration.</p>

<h3>3. Stomatal Opening</h3>

<p>Stomata can open or close depending on the conditions and the plant''s needs.</p>

<p>The opening is controlled by specialised cells called <strong>guard cells</strong>.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf needs carbon dioxide for photosynthesis.</p>

<p><strong>Through which structures can carbon dioxide enter?</strong></p>

<p>Through open stomata.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>Stomata have two important effects:</p>

<p><strong>Open stomata → gas exchange increases + water vapour loss can increase.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Closed stomata → gas exchange decreases + water loss decreases.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for stomata', 15),
  (1190, 1190, '
<h2>Opening and Closing of Stomata</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The stomatal pore changes size according to the turgor of the guard cells.</p>

<h3>When Stomata Open</h3>

<p>Guard cells take up water and become more turgid.</p>

<p>Their shape changes, causing the stomatal pore to open.</p>

<p>This allows carbon dioxide to enter and water vapour to leave.</p>

<h3>When Stomata Close</h3>

<p>Guard cells lose water and become less turgid.</p>

<p>The stomatal pore closes.</p>

<p>This reduces water loss and also reduces gas exchange.</p>

<h3>Why Close Stomata?</h3>

<p>During conditions that cause excessive water loss, closing stomata can help conserve water.</p>

<h3>Trade-Off</h3>

<p>Closing stomata reduces water loss, but it also reduces the entry of carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>This can limit photosynthesis if carbon dioxide becomes insufficient.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant experiences severe water shortage.</p>

<p><strong>Why might its stomata close?</strong></p>

<p>Closing the stomata reduces the diffusion of water vapour out of the leaf and therefore helps reduce water loss.</p>

<h3>Exam Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Water shortage → guard cells lose turgor → stomata close → water vapour loss decreases.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for stomatal_opening_and_closing', 15),
  (1191, 1191, '
<h2>Distribution of Stomata on Leaves</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Stomata are not necessarily distributed equally on the upper and lower surfaces of a leaf.</p>

<p>The distribution is related to the plant''s environment and the need to balance gas exchange with water conservation.</p>

<h3>Lower Leaf Surface</h3>

<p>In many terrestrial plants, more stomata occur on the lower epidermis than on the upper epidermis.</p>

<p>The lower surface is often less exposed to direct sunlight and moving air.</p>

<p>This can reduce the rate of water loss compared with having the same number of stomata on the upper surface.</p>

<h3>Exceptions</h3>

<p>Stomatal distribution varies between plant species.</p>

<p>Floating leaves may have stomata mainly on the upper surface because the lower surface is in contact with water.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A terrestrial plant has many more stomata on its lower leaf surface.</p>

<p><strong>Why might this arrangement reduce water loss?</strong></p>

<p>The lower surface is generally less exposed to direct sunlight and moving air, reducing the environmental conditions that promote rapid water loss.</p>

<h3>Important Point</h3>

<p>Do not state that every plant has stomata only on the lower surface. Distribution depends on the plant and its environment.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for stomata_distribution', 15),
  (1192, 1192, '
<h2>Stomatal Adaptations in Xerophytes</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants adapted to dry environments may have stomatal features that reduce water loss.</p>

<h3>Sunken Stomata</h3>

<p>Sunken stomata are located below the general leaf surface.</p>

<p>The depression can trap relatively still, humid air around the stomatal pore.</p>

<p>This reduces the diffusion gradient for water vapour.</p>

<h3>Fewer Stomata</h3>

<p>A lower stomatal density can reduce the total area available for water vapour to escape.</p>

<h3>Stomatal Closure</h3>

<p>Guard cells can close stomata when conditions cause excessive water loss.</p>

<h3>Connecting Structure to Function</h3>

<p><strong>Sunken stomata → still humid air → smaller water vapour gradient → reduced water loss.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Fewer stomata → smaller total pore area → reduced potential water loss.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two plants are exposed to the same dry conditions.</p>

<p>Plant A has exposed stomata. Plant B has sunken stomata.</p>

<p><strong>Which plant is likely to lose water more slowly through its stomata?</strong></p>

<p>Plant B, because the sunken stomata can be sheltered from moving air and maintain more humid conditions around the pore.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for xerophyte_stomatal_adaptations', 15),
  (1193, 1193, '
<h2>Phloem Sieve Tube</h2>

<h3>What is a phloem sieve tube?</h3>

<p>
A <b>phloem sieve tube</b> is part of the plant transport system.
It transports dissolved products of photosynthesis, mainly
<b>sucrose</b>, from sources to parts of the plant that need or store them.
</p>

<h3>What is transported?</h3>

<p>
The main transported substance is sucrose made during photosynthesis.
It can be transported to growing regions, roots, fruits and storage organs.
</p>

<h3>How is the structure specialised?</h3>

<p>
Sieve tube elements are arranged end to end, forming long tubes.
Their end walls contain pores through which phloem sap can pass.
</p>

<p>
Sieve tube elements are associated with <b>companion cells</b>, which
help maintain their functioning.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A plant produces sucrose in its leaves. Which tissue
transports the sucrose to the roots?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Sucrose is a product of photosynthesis.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Products of photosynthesis are transported in phloem.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Phloem contains sieve tubes.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Phloem transports the sucrose from the leaves to the roots.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Phloem Sieve Tube', 15),
  (1194, 1194, '
<h2>Structure of Phloem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Phloem</strong> is a vascular tissue specialised for transporting organic assimilates around a plant.</p>

<h3>1. Sieve Tube Elements</h3>

<p>Sieve tube elements are living cells arranged end to end to form long sieve tubes.</p>

<p>Their end walls contain perforations called <strong>sieve plates</strong>.</p>

<p>These openings allow phloem sap to move between adjacent sieve tube elements.</p>

<h3>2. Companion Cells</h3>

<p>Each sieve tube element is closely associated with a companion cell.</p>

<p>Companion cells contain a nucleus and many mitochondria.</p>

<p>They provide metabolic support for the sieve tube element and are involved in loading and unloading substances.</p>

<h3>3. Phloem Is Living Tissue</h3>

<p>Unlike mature xylem vessel elements, phloem conducting cells are living.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant tissue contains living conducting cells arranged end to end with sieve plates between them.</p>

<p><strong>Which tissue is this?</strong></p>

<p><strong>Phloem.</strong></p>

<h3>Key Structure → Function Links</h3>

<p><strong>Sieve plates → pathway between sieve tube elements.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Companion cells → metabolic support and transport processes.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Living cells → active transport processes can occur.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phloem_structure', 15),
  (1195, 1195, '
<h2>Function of Phloem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The main function of phloem is to transport <strong>organic assimilates</strong> around the plant.</p>

<p>An important assimilate transported by phloem is <strong>sucrose</strong>.</p>

<h3>What Is an Assimilate?</h3>

<p>An assimilate is an organic substance produced by the plant as a result of metabolism, especially photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Sucrose is a major transport sugar in plants.</p>

<h3>Why Must Sucrose Be Transported?</h3>

<p>Leaves are major sites of photosynthesis, but many other parts of the plant need sugars.</p>

<p>For example, sugars may be transported to:</p>

<ul>
<li>growing roots;</li>
<li>developing fruits;</li>
<li>seeds;</li>
<li>growing shoots;</li>
<li>storage organs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Direction of Transport</h3>

<p>Unlike the usual upward movement of water in xylem, phloem transport can occur in different directions in different sieve tubes.</p>

<p>The direction depends on where assimilates are produced and where they are needed.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A mature leaf produces sucrose by photosynthesis. A developing fruit needs sucrose for growth.</p>

<p><strong>Which tissue transports the sucrose?</strong></p>

<p><strong>Phloem.</strong></p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not write that phloem transports only "food".</p>

<p>A stronger scientific answer is:</p>

<p><strong>Phloem transports organic assimilates, especially sucrose, between sources and sinks.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phloem_function', 15),
  (1196, 1196, '
<h2>Sources and Sinks in Phloem Transport</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Understanding <strong>sources</strong> and <strong>sinks</strong> explains why phloem transport can occur in different directions.</p>

<h3>Source</h3>

<p>A <strong>source</strong> is a plant organ or tissue that is supplying assimilates to the phloem.</p>

<p>A mature photosynthesising leaf can act as a source because it produces sucrose.</p>

<h3>Sink</h3>

<p>A <strong>sink</strong> is a plant organ or tissue that receives and uses or stores assimilates.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<ul>
<li>growing roots;</li>
<li>young leaves;</li>
<li>developing fruits;</li>
<li>seeds;</li>
<li>storage organs.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Source → Sink</h3>

<p>Assimilates are transported from sources to sinks.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p><strong>Mature leaf → phloem → developing fruit.</strong></p>

<h3>Why Direction Changes</h3>

<p>Different parts of the plant can become sources or sinks at different times.</p>

<p>A young leaf may initially receive assimilates and therefore act as a sink. Once it becomes photosynthetically active enough to produce more assimilates than it needs, it can become a source.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A mature leaf is producing more sucrose than it currently needs, while a developing fruit is using sucrose for growth.</p>

<p><strong>Which is the source and which is the sink?</strong></p>

<p>The mature leaf is the <strong>source</strong>.</p>

<p>The developing fruit is the <strong>sink</strong>.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Do not define a source simply as "a leaf".</p>

<p>A source is defined by its role in supplying assimilates.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for source_and_sink', 15),
  (1197, 1197, '
<h2>Translocation in Phloem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Translocation</strong> is the transport of organic assimilates, especially sucrose, through the phloem from sources to sinks.</p>

<h3>Source</h3>

<p>The source supplies assimilates.</p>

<p>A mature leaf producing sucrose by photosynthesis is a common source.</p>

<h3>Phloem</h3>

<p>Sucrose is loaded into the phloem and transported through sieve tubes.</p>

<h3>Sink</h3>

<p>The sink receives the assimilates.</p>

<p>The sucrose may then be used in respiration, growth or converted into storage substances.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis → sucrose produced → source → phloem loading → translocation → sink → use or storage.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A potato plant produces sugars in its leaves. Some of the assimilates are transported to underground tubers.</p>

<p><strong>Identify the source and sink.</strong></p>

<p>The leaves are the <strong>source</strong>.</p>

<p>The developing tubers are the <strong>sinks</strong>.</p>

<p>The movement of sucrose through phloem is <strong>translocation</strong>.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Translocation is not the same as transpiration.</p>

<p><strong>Transpiration → water vapour loss.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Translocation → movement of assimilates through phloem.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for translocation', 15),
  (1198, 1198, '
<h2>Loading Sucrose into Phloem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Before sucrose can be transported through phloem, it must be loaded into the sieve tube system.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Sucrose Is Produced</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis in a source leaf produces carbohydrates.</p>

<p>These carbohydrates are converted into transportable sugars, especially sucrose.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Sucrose Reaches the Phloem</h3>

<p>Sucrose moves from photosynthetic cells towards the phloem.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Loading</h3>

<p>Sucrose is loaded into the phloem.</p>

<p>In many plants, active transport involving companion cells is important in this process.</p>

<p>Energy supplied by respiration supports active transport where movement occurs against a concentration gradient.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Phloem Sap Becomes More Concentrated</h3>

<p>Loading sucrose into the sieve tube increases the concentration of dissolved substances in the phloem.</p>

<p>Water can enter the phloem from nearby xylem by osmosis.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A source leaf is producing sucrose rapidly.</p>

<p><strong>Why might companion cells require energy?</strong></p>

<p>They may use energy from respiration to actively transport sucrose during phloem loading.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>Do not state that every step of phloem transport directly uses ATP.</p>

<p>Active loading and unloading processes can require energy, while pressure-driven bulk flow through the sieve tubes is a different part of the process.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phloem_loading', 15),
  (1199, 1199, '
<h2>Pressure Flow in Phloem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>One model used to explain movement of phloem sap is the <strong>pressure-flow hypothesis</strong>.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Sucrose Is Loaded at the Source</h3>

<p>Sucrose is loaded into sieve tubes at the source.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Water Enters the Phloem</h3>

<p>The increased solute concentration in the phloem lowers its water potential.</p>

<p>Water enters the phloem from nearby xylem by osmosis.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Pressure Increases</h3>

<p>Water entering the sieve tube increases hydrostatic pressure at the source.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Sap Moves Towards the Sink</h3>

<p>The pressure difference causes phloem sap to move through the sieve tube towards a region where sucrose is being removed.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Sucrose Is Unloaded</h3>

<p>At the sink, sucrose is removed from the phloem and used or stored.</p>

<p>Water potential in the phloem changes, and water can move away.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Sucrose loading → water enters → pressure rises → phloem sap flows → sucrose unloaded at sink.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>At a source, sucrose is actively loaded into the phloem.</p>

<p><strong>Why does water enter the phloem?</strong></p>

<p>Loading sucrose increases the solute concentration and lowers water potential, causing water to enter by osmosis.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>Pressure flow explains bulk movement through the phloem. It is different from simple diffusion of individual sucrose molecules over short distances.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for pressure_flow_phloem', 15),
  (1200, 1200, '
<h2>Unloading Sucrose at the Sink</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>When phloem sap reaches a sink, sucrose must be removed from the phloem so that it can be used or stored.</p>

<h3>1. Arrival at the Sink</h3>

<p>Phloem sap containing sucrose reaches the sink tissue.</p>

<h3>2. Sucrose Is Unloaded</h3>

<p>Sucrose moves out of the sieve tube and into cells of the sink.</p>

<p>Depending on the tissue and concentration gradient, unloading can involve facilitated diffusion or active transport.</p>

<h3>3. What Happens to the Sucrose?</h3>

<p>The sucrose may be:</p>

<ul>
<li>used in respiration;</li>
<li>used to make new biomass;</li>
<li>converted into starch for storage;</li>
<li>used in developing fruits or seeds.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A developing root receives sucrose through the phloem.</p>

<p><strong>What happens to the sucrose after unloading?</strong></p>

<p>It can be used in respiration and growth, or converted into storage compounds.</p>

<h3>Why Unloading Matters</h3>

<p>Removing sucrose at the sink helps maintain the concentration and pressure difference needed for continued transport from source to sink.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Phloem reaches sink → sucrose unloaded → sucrose used or stored → transport can continue.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phloem_unloading', 15),
  (1201, 1201, '
<h2>Direction of Phloem Transport</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Phloem transport does not always move from the roots to the leaves or from the leaves to the roots.</p>

<p>It moves from a <strong>source</strong> to a <strong>sink</strong>.</p>

<h3>Example 1: Leaf to Root</h3>

<p>A mature leaf produces sucrose.</p>

<p>A growing root requires sucrose.</p>

<p>The direction is:</p>

<p><strong>Leaf → root.</strong></p>

<h3>Example 2: Leaf to Fruit</h3>

<p>A mature leaf produces sucrose.</p>

<p>A developing fruit requires sucrose.</p>

<p>The direction is:</p>

<p><strong>Leaf → fruit.</strong></p>

<h3>Example 3: Storage Organ to Growing Shoot</h3>

<p>A storage organ may release stored carbohydrates when the plant begins new growth.</p>

<p>The storage organ acts as a source and the growing shoot acts as a sink.</p>

<p>The direction can therefore be:</p>

<p><strong>Storage organ → growing shoot.</strong></p>

<h3>Why This Matters</h3>

<p>The direction of phloem transport depends on which tissues are producing or releasing assimilates and which tissues are using or storing them.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is growing rapidly in spring. Stored carbohydrates in an underground storage organ are being transported to new shoots.</p>

<p><strong>Which is the source?</strong></p>

<p>The underground storage organ.</p>

<p><strong>Which is the sink?</strong></p>

<p>The growing shoot.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Never define phloem transport simply as "upward" or "downward".</p>

<p>Use:</p>

<p><strong>Source → sink.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phloem_transport_direction', 15),
  (1202, 1202, '
<h2>Xylem and Phloem Compared</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Xylem and phloem are both vascular tissues, but they transport different substances and have different structures.</p>

<h3>Xylem</h3>

<ul>
<li>Transports water and dissolved mineral ions.</li>
<li>Mature vessel elements are dead.</li>
<li>Vessels have lignified walls.</li>
<li>Transport is mainly from roots towards shoots.</li>
<li>Forms a strong conducting pathway.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Phloem</h3>

<ul>
<li>Transports organic assimilates such as sucrose.</li>
<li>Conducting cells are living.</li>
<li>Contains sieve tube elements and companion cells.</li>
<li>Transport is from sources to sinks.</li>
<li>Direction can vary depending on source and sink positions.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Comparison</h3>

<p>A student says:</p>

<p><em>"Both xylem and phloem transport substances from the roots to the leaves."</em></p>

<p><strong>Is this correct?</strong></p>

<p>Not completely.</p>

<p>Xylem normally transports water and mineral ions from roots towards shoots, while phloem transports assimilates from sources to sinks, which can be in different directions.</p>

<h3>Exam Table</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Xylem</th>
<th>Phloem</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Main transported substances</td>
<td>Water and mineral ions</td>
<td>Organic assimilates</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Main conducting cells</td>
<td>Xylem vessel elements</td>
<td>Sieve tube elements</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cell status</td>
<td>Mature vessels dead</td>
<td>Conducting cells living</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Direction</td>
<td>Mainly roots → shoots</td>
<td>Source → sink</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Key Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Xylem = water + mineral ions.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Phloem = assimilates.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for xylem_and_phloem_comparison', 15),
  (1203, 1203, '
<h2>Ringing Experiment and Phloem Transport</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A ringing experiment can provide evidence that phloem transports organic assimilates.</p>

<h3>What Is Ringing?</h3>

<p>A ring of bark is carefully removed from around a stem.</p>

<p>The removed region includes the tissues containing phloem.</p>

<p>The xylem is left intact.</p>

<h3>What Happens Above the Ring?</h3>

<p>After some time, assimilates can accumulate above the ring.</p>

<p>This happens because the phloem pathway has been interrupted.</p>

<p>Assimilates produced in leaves cannot move normally through the removed phloem to tissues below the ring.</p>

<h3>What Happens Below the Ring?</h3>

<p>Tissues below the ring may receive fewer assimilates from the leaves.</p>

<p>If the interruption continues, growth and storage below the ring can be affected.</p>

<h3>Why Is This Evidence for Phloem?</h3>

<p>The experiment links the removal of phloem-containing tissue with disruption of assimilate transport.</p>

<p>This supports the conclusion that phloem is involved in transporting assimilates.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A ring of bark is removed from a stem. Several days later, swelling is observed above the ring.</p>

<p><strong>What does this suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Assimilates are accumulating above the interruption because their normal phloem pathway has been disrupted.</p>

<h3>Important Limitation</h3>

<p>Ringing damages the plant and removes more than a single isolated cell type.</p>

<p>Therefore, the experiment should be interpreted carefully rather than claiming that it proves every detail of phloem transport.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for ringing_experiment_phloem', 15),
  (1204, 1204, '
<h2>Designing an Investigation of Phloem Transport</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An investigation of phloem transport should begin with a clear question and measurable evidence.</p>

<h3>Example Question</h3>

<p><strong>Does removing a ring of bark affect the accumulation of assimilates above the ring?</strong></p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>The treatment applied to the stem, such as whether a ring of bark is removed.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>A measurable response, such as change in stem diameter, mass, sugar concentration or another suitable indicator of assimilate accumulation.</p>

<h3>Control</h3>

<p>A similar plant or stem section that is not ringed can provide a comparison.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>Where possible, keep factors such as plant species, plant age, growing conditions, water supply and duration of the investigation similar.</p>

<h3>Repeats</h3>

<p>Using several plants or repeated measurements reduces the influence of unusual individual results.</p>

<h3>Conclusion</h3>

<p>The conclusion must be based on the evidence collected.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three ringed stems show greater sugar accumulation above the ring than three unringed control stems.</p>

<p><strong>What conclusion is reasonable?</strong></p>

<p>The results support the idea that interrupting phloem disrupts the normal movement of assimilates.</p>

<h3>Important Scientific Rule</h3>

<p>A good investigation changes one main factor while controlling other relevant factors.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phloem_transport_experiment_design', 15),
  (1205, 1205, '
  <h2>Chloroplasts</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Chloroplasts</b> are structures found in many plant cells and some
    other photosynthetic organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
    They contain the green pigment <b>chlorophyll</b>, which absorbs light
    energy used during photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Are Chloroplasts Important?</h3>

  <p>
    Plants use photosynthesis to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water
    using light energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    Chloroplasts provide the structures in which the light-dependent parts of
    this process occur and contain chlorophyll needed to absorb light.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Are Chloroplasts Common in Leaf Cells?</h3>

  <p>
    Leaves are major organs of photosynthesis.
    Their cells are therefore adapted to capture light and exchange gases
    needed for photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
    Palisade cells, for example, contain many chloroplasts because they are
    positioned where they can receive substantial amounts of light.
  </p>

  <h3>Do All Plant Cells Have Chloroplasts?</h3>

  <p>
    No.
    Different plant cells have different functions.
    A root hair cell, for example, normally does not contain chloroplasts
    because it is underground and is specialised mainly for absorbing water
    and mineral ions.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why would a leaf cell usually contain many chloroplasts?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Leaves are major sites of photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and are the structures
    associated with photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Leaf cells need chloroplasts to capture light energy
    and carry out photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and are the main sites of
    photosynthesis in plant cells.</b>
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Chloroplasts', 15),
  (1206, 1206, '
<h2>Palisade Mesophyll Cell</h2>

<h3>What is a palisade mesophyll cell?</h3>

<p>
A <b>palisade mesophyll cell</b> is a specialised plant cell found mainly
near the upper surface of a leaf. Its main function is to carry out
<b>photosynthesis</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why is it specialised for photosynthesis?</h3>

<p>
The cell contains many <b>chloroplasts</b>. Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll,
which absorbs light energy needed for photosynthesis.
</p>

<h3>How does its structure help its function?</h3>

<p>
The palisade cell is positioned close to the upper surface of the leaf,
where it receives plenty of light.
</p>

<p>
It also has many chloroplasts, increasing its ability to absorb light.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why does a palisade mesophyll cell contain many chloroplasts?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The function of the cell is mainly photosynthesis.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Photosynthesis requires light energy.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, which absorbs light.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> It contains many chloroplasts so that it can absorb more
light energy and carry out photosynthesis efficiently.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Palisade Mesophyll Cell', 15),
  (1207, 1207, '
<h2>Palisade Cell Adaptation</h2>

<h3>What makes a palisade cell efficient at photosynthesis?</h3>

<p>
A palisade mesophyll cell is specialised for photosynthesis.
Its structure helps it capture light and obtain the substances needed
for photosynthesis.
</p>

<h3>Important adaptations</h3>

<p>
The cell contains many <b>chloroplasts</b>, allowing it to absorb
light energy.
</p>

<p>
Palisade cells are positioned close to the upper surface of the leaf,
where light intensity is generally greater.
</p>

<p>
They also have a relatively elongated shape and are arranged closely
together in the leaf.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> A plant is moved from bright light into very dim light.
Why would photosynthesis in its palisade cells decrease?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Photosynthesis requires light energy.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Dim light provides less light energy.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Less light energy is available for photosynthesis.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> The rate of photosynthesis decreases because less light
energy is available.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Palisade Cell Adaptation', 15),
  (1208, 1208, '
  <h2>Plant Cell Walls and Chloroplasts in Classification</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Two important characteristics associated with typical green plant cells are
  the presence of a <b>cellulose cell wall</b> and <b>chloroplasts</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Cellulose cell wall</h3>

  <p>
  The cell wall lies outside the cell membrane and provides mechanical
  support.
  </p>

  <p>
  The main structural substance in typical plant cell walls is cellulose.
  </p>

  <h3>Chloroplasts</h3>

  <p>
  Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and are the organelles where photosynthesis
  takes place in green plant cells.
  </p>

  <p>
  Chlorophyll absorbs light energy used in photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>Using these characteristics</h3>

  <p>
  When identifying a typical green plant cell, the combination of a cellulose
  cell wall, chloroplasts and other plant-cell features provides strong
  evidence that the cell is from a plant.
  </p>

  <p>
  Classification should still use multiple characteristics rather than
  relying on one structure alone.
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Plant Cell Walls and Chloroplasts in Classification', 15),
  (1209, 1209, '
<h2>Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis</strong> is the process by which green plants use light energy to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water.</p>

<h3>Word Equation</h3>

<p><strong>Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen</strong></p>

<p>Light energy and chlorophyll are required.</p>

<h3>Balanced Symbol Equation</h3>

<p><strong>6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</strong></p>

<p>The light energy is absorbed by chlorophyll and used to drive the reactions.</p>

<h3>Where Does It Occur?</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis occurs mainly in the chloroplasts of green plant cells.</p>

<p>Leaves contain many chloroplasts, especially in the palisade mesophyll.</p>

<h3>Why Is Glucose Produced?</h3>

<p>Glucose is an energy-rich organic compound that can be used in respiration or converted into other substances needed by the plant.</p>

<h3>What Happens to the Glucose?</h3>

<p>Glucose can be:</p>

<ul>
<li>used in respiration;</li>
<li>converted to starch for storage;</li>
<li>used to make cellulose;</li>
<li>combined with nitrogen-containing compounds to make amino acids and proteins.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is placed in darkness for several hours and then tested for starch.</p>

<p><strong>Why might little or no starch be detected in a leaf that was previously photosynthesising?</strong></p>

<p>Without light, photosynthesis cannot continue normally, so new glucose is not produced and stored as starch.</p>

<h3>Key Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Light energy → chlorophyll absorbs energy → carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis', 15),
  (1210, 1210, '
<h2>Chlorophyll and Light Energy</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Chlorophyll</strong> is a green pigment found in chloroplasts.</p>

<p>Its important role in photosynthesis is to absorb light energy.</p>

<h3>Why Is Chlorophyll Important?</h3>

<p>The chemical reactions of photosynthesis require an energy input.</p>

<p>Light provides this energy.</p>

<p>Chlorophyll absorbs some of the light energy and makes it available for photosynthetic reactions.</p>

<h3>Why Are Leaves Green?</h3>

<p>Leaves appear green because chlorophyll absorbs some wavelengths of visible light more strongly than others, while green light is relatively less absorbed and more reflected or transmitted.</p>

<h3>Chlorophyll and Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>A green leaf usually contains chlorophyll-containing chloroplasts.</p>

<p>Therefore, green leaves are commonly major sites of photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf has a region that contains no chlorophyll.</p>

<p>The leaf is exposed to light under suitable conditions.</p>

<p><strong>Would you expect that region to produce starch through photosynthesis?</strong></p>

<p>No. Without chlorophyll, that region cannot absorb light energy in the normal photosynthetic way, so starch production through photosynthesis would not be expected there.</p>

<h3>Exam Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Chlorophyll is not the energy itself.</strong></p>

<p>It is a pigment that absorbs light energy.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for chlorophyll_and_light_energy', 15),
  (1211, 1211, '
<h2>Chloroplasts and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A <strong>chloroplast</strong> is a specialised organelle where photosynthesis occurs.</p>

<h3>Chlorophyll</h3>

<p>Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll pigments that absorb light energy.</p>

<h3>Why Are Chloroplasts Abundant in Palisade Cells?</h3>

<p>Palisade mesophyll cells are located close to the upper surface of many leaves.</p>

<p>They receive substantial amounts of light and therefore commonly contain many chloroplasts.</p>

<h3>Photosynthesis and Chloroplasts</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide enters leaves mainly through stomata.</p>

<p>Water is delivered to leaves through xylem.</p>

<p>Inside chloroplast-containing cells, light energy is used in photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Complete Connection</h3>

<p><strong>Roots absorb water → xylem transports water → carbon dioxide enters through stomata → chloroplasts absorb light → photosynthesis produces glucose and oxygen.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has healthy roots and plenty of water, but its leaves contain very few functional chloroplasts.</p>

<p><strong>Why may photosynthesis be reduced?</strong></p>

<p>There is less chlorophyll-containing tissue available to absorb light energy and carry out photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The chloroplast is the organelle where photosynthesis occurs.</p>

<p>Chlorophyll is the pigment within chloroplasts that absorbs light energy.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for chloroplast_structure_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1212, 1212, '
<h2>Carbon Dioxide Entry into a Leaf</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide is a raw material required for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>How Does It Enter?</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide from the surrounding air enters the leaf mainly through openings called <strong>stomata</strong>.</p>

<p>The carbon dioxide then diffuses through internal air spaces towards photosynthesising cells.</p>

<h3>Why Does Diffusion Occur?</h3>

<p>When photosynthesising cells use carbon dioxide, the concentration of carbon dioxide inside the leaf can become lower than that outside.</p>

<p>This concentration difference allows carbon dioxide to diffuse into the leaf.</p>

<h3>Role of Stomata</h3>

<p>Stomata provide pathways for gas exchange.</p>

<p>Guard cells control whether the stomatal pore is open or closed.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has stomata that remain closed for a prolonged period during daylight.</p>

<p><strong>How could this affect photosynthesis?</strong></p>

<p>Less carbon dioxide can enter the leaf, reducing the carbon dioxide available for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Important Connection</h3>

<p>Stomata help the plant obtain carbon dioxide, but opening them also increases the opportunity for water vapour to escape.</p>

<p>This is why plants must regulate stomatal opening.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for carbon_dioxide_entry_leaf', 15),
  (1213, 1213, '
<h2>Light Is Required for Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis requires light energy.</p>

<p>Therefore, a suitable experiment can compare a leaf region exposed to light with a region kept in darkness.</p>

<h3>Experimental Setup</h3>

<p>A plant is first destarched.</p>

<p>Part of a leaf is covered with opaque material so that light cannot reach that region.</p>

<p>The plant is then exposed to light for a suitable period.</p>

<p>The leaf is tested for starch.</p>

<h3>Expected Result</h3>

<p>The exposed region is expected to contain starch if other necessary conditions are available.</p>

<p>The covered region should contain less or no newly formed starch.</p>

<h3>Why Is the Experiment Useful?</h3>

<p>The two regions are on the same leaf, so many factors are naturally similar.</p>

<p>The major difference is access to light.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf is destarched. Half is covered with opaque paper and the plant is placed in light. After testing with iodine, the exposed half turns blue-black while the covered half remains yellow-brown.</p>

<p><strong>What conclusion is supported?</strong></p>

<p>Light was necessary for starch formation under the conditions of the experiment.</p>

<h3>Important Scientific Wording</h3>

<p>Do not conclude that light is the only factor required for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Carbon dioxide, water, suitable temperature and chlorophyll are also important.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for light_required_for_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1214, 1214, '
<h2>Carbon Dioxide Is Required for Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide is a raw material for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>An experiment can test whether carbon dioxide is necessary for starch formation.</p>

<h3>Experimental Idea</h3>

<p>A destarched plant is placed in controlled conditions.</p>

<p>One leaf or part of a leaf is exposed to carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>Another comparable region is kept without available carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>After exposure to light, the leaf is tested for starch.</p>

<h3>Using Potassium Hydroxide</h3>

<p>Potassium hydroxide can absorb carbon dioxide from enclosed air.</p>

<p>It can therefore be used in a suitable experimental setup to create a carbon-dioxide-deficient condition.</p>

<h3>Expected Result</h3>

<p>The region supplied with carbon dioxide can form starch if other conditions are suitable.</p>

<p>The region deprived of carbon dioxide should form much less or no new starch.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A destarched plant is placed in light. Carbon dioxide is removed from the air surrounding one leaf using a suitable carbon dioxide absorber. The leaf region without carbon dioxide does not turn blue-black with iodine.</p>

<p><strong>What does this support?</strong></p>

<p>Carbon dioxide is required for starch formation through photosynthesis under the conditions tested.</p>

<h3>Important Control</h3>

<p>The experimental groups should receive similar light, temperature, water supply and other relevant conditions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for carbon_dioxide_required_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1215, 1215, '
<h2>Light Intensity and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Light intensity</strong> is the amount of light reaching a given area.</p>

<p>Because photosynthesis requires light energy, changing light intensity can change the rate of photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>At Low Light Intensity</h3>

<p>There is relatively little light energy available.</p>

<p>Increasing light intensity can increase the rate of photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>At Higher Light Intensity</h3>

<p>The rate of photosynthesis may eventually stop increasing.</p>

<p>This happens because another factor, such as carbon dioxide concentration or temperature, becomes limiting.</p>

<h3>Typical Graph</h3>

<p>When light intensity is plotted against the rate of photosynthesis:</p>

<p><strong>Low light → rate increases.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Higher light → rate approaches a plateau.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has the following results:</p>

<p>Light intensity = 10 units → photosynthesis rate = 2 units</p>

<p>Light intensity = 20 units → photosynthesis rate = 4 units</p>

<p>Light intensity = 40 units → photosynthesis rate = 6 units</p>

<p>Light intensity = 80 units → photosynthesis rate = 6 units</p>

<p><strong>What does the final result suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Increasing light from 40 to 80 units no longer increases the rate. Therefore, light is no longer the limiting factor.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Do not say that more light always means faster photosynthesis.</p>

<p>The effect depends on whether light is currently the limiting factor.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for light_intensity_and_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1216, 1216, '
<h2>Carbon Dioxide Concentration and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide is a raw material used during photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Therefore, its concentration can affect the rate of photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Low Carbon Dioxide Concentration</h3>

<p>If carbon dioxide concentration is low, carbon dioxide may limit photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Increasing carbon dioxide concentration can increase the rate of photosynthesis when carbon dioxide is the limiting factor.</p>

<h3>Higher Concentration</h3>

<p>Eventually, increasing carbon dioxide further may have little or no additional effect.</p>

<p>This occurs when another factor becomes limiting.</p>

<h3>Typical Pattern</h3>

<p><strong>Low CO₂ → rate increases as CO₂ increases.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Higher CO₂ → rate reaches a plateau.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is tested at different carbon dioxide concentrations.</p>

<p>At 0.02% CO₂, the rate is 3 units.</p>

<p>At 0.04% CO₂, the rate is 6 units.</p>

<p>At 0.08% CO₂, the rate is 9 units.</p>

<p>At 0.12% CO₂, the rate is 9 units.</p>

<p><strong>What is the likely limiting factor at 0.12% CO₂?</strong></p>

<p>Carbon dioxide is no longer limiting. Another factor is limiting the rate.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Increasing carbon dioxide does not guarantee an unlimited increase in photosynthesis.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for carbon_dioxide_concentration_and_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1217, 1217, '
<h2>Measuring the Rate of Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The rate of photosynthesis can be estimated by measuring a product formed or a reactant used over a known period.</p>

<h3>Method 1: Oxygen Production</h3>

<p>Oxygen produced by an aquatic plant can be collected and its volume measured.</p>

<p>The rate can then be calculated:</p>

<p><strong>Rate = volume of oxygen produced ÷ time</strong></p>

<h3>Method 2: Carbon Dioxide Uptake</h3>

<p>The decrease in carbon dioxide concentration can be measured using suitable equipment.</p>

<h3>Why Time Matters</h3>

<p>A rate describes how much change occurs per unit time.</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>An aquatic plant produces 36 cm³ of oxygen in 9 minutes.</p>

<p>Rate = volume ÷ time</p>

<p>Rate = 36 ÷ 9</p>

<p><strong>Rate = 4 cm³/min</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>A plant produces 50 cm³ of oxygen in 10 minutes.</p>

<p>Rate = 50 ÷ 10</p>

<p><strong>Rate = 5 cm³/min</strong></p>

<h3>Comparing the Results</h3>

<p>The second plant or condition has the greater measured rate because 5 cm³/min is greater than 4 cm³/min.</p>

<h3>Important Limitation</h3>

<p>Oxygen production is an estimate of net photosynthetic activity because the plant also uses oxygen in respiration.</p>

<p>Therefore, measured oxygen accumulation may not represent the total amount of oxygen produced by photosynthesis.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for measuring_photosynthesis_rate', 15),
  (1218, 1218, '
<h2>Interpreting Photosynthesis Graphs</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis experiments often produce numerical data that can be represented using graphs.</p>

<h3>What to Look For</h3>

<p>When interpreting a graph, identify:</p>

<ul>
<li>the variables on the axes;</li>
<li>the units;</li>
<li>the overall trend;</li>
<li>any plateau;</li>
<li>any maximum or optimum;</li>
<li>unusual results.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example: Light Intensity</h3>

<p>If the rate increases as light intensity increases, light may be limiting in that region.</p>

<p>If the graph becomes flat, increasing light no longer increases the rate.</p>

<h3>Example: Temperature</h3>

<p>If the rate rises and then falls, the graph suggests an optimum temperature.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A graph gives:</p>

<p>10 units light → 2 units rate</p>

<p>20 units light → 4 units rate</p>

<p>30 units light → 6 units rate</p>

<p>40 units light → 6 units rate</p>

<p><strong>What happens between 30 and 40 units?</strong></p>

<p>The rate remains constant.</p>

<p><strong>What does this suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Light is no longer limiting in that range.</p>

<h3>Evidence-Based Language</h3>

<p>Use phrases such as:</p>

<p><strong>"The data show..."</strong></p>

<p><strong>"The rate increases..."</strong></p>

<p><strong>"The rate reaches a plateau..."</strong></p>

<p><strong>"This suggests..."</strong></p>

<p>Avoid claiming more than the data demonstrate.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis_graphs', 15),
  (1219, 1219, '
<h2>Variables in a Photosynthesis Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A good photosynthesis investigation changes one main factor while keeping other relevant conditions controlled.</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>The <strong>independent variable</strong> is the factor deliberately changed.</p>

<p>For example, if investigating light intensity, light intensity is the independent variable.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>The <strong>dependent variable</strong> is what is measured.</p>

<p>For example, oxygen production per minute can be used as a measure of photosynthetic rate.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>Other relevant factors should be kept as constant as possible.</p>

<p>These may include:</p>

<ul>
<li>temperature;</li>
<li>carbon dioxide concentration;</li>
<li>size or amount of plant material;</li>
<li>time allowed for each measurement.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates the effect of light intensity on photosynthesis.</p>

<p><strong>Independent variable?</strong></p>

<p>Light intensity.</p>

<p><strong>Dependent variable?</strong></p>

<p>Rate of oxygen production.</p>

<p><strong>One control variable?</strong></p>

<p>Temperature.</p>

<h3>Why Control Variables?</h3>

<p>If temperature changes at the same time as light intensity, any change in photosynthesis could be caused by either factor.</p>

<p>That makes the investigation less valid.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Change one main variable → measure the response → control other important variables.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis_practical_variables', 15),
  (1220, 1220, '
<h2>Improving a Photosynthesis Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A strong investigation should produce results that are reliable and allow a valid conclusion.</p>

<h3>1. Repeat Measurements</h3>

<p>Repeating each measurement helps identify unusual results and allows a mean to be calculated.</p>

<h3>2. Calculate a Mean</h3>

<p>If repeated measurements are reasonably consistent, their mean gives a useful estimate of the typical result.</p>

<h3>3. Control Temperature</h3>

<p>Temperature affects photosynthesis, so it should be kept constant when it is not the variable being tested.</p>

<h3>4. Keep Plant Material Consistent</h3>

<p>Using similar amounts and types of plant material makes comparisons fairer.</p>

<h3>5. Allow Sufficient Time</h3>

<p>Measurements should be taken over a suitable, consistent period.</p>

<h3>6. Use a Suitable Measurement</h3>

<p>Measuring gas volume can be more informative than simply counting bubbles because bubbles may differ in size.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student counts bubbles produced by an aquatic plant at different light intensities.</p>

<p>The student obtains very different results from repeated trials.</p>

<p><strong>Give two improvements.</strong></p>

<p>Use more repeats and measure the volume of gas produced rather than relying only on bubble count.</p>

<h3>Conclusion</h3>

<p>A conclusion should describe the relationship supported by the evidence rather than claiming that the experiment proves more than it tested.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for improving_photosynthesis_experiment', 15),
  (1221, 1221, '
<h2>Photosynthesis: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis depends on several conditions working together.</p>

<p>A strong exam answer connects the condition, the biological mechanism and the effect on the rate.</p>

<h3>Example Situation</h3>

<p>A crop is growing in a greenhouse.</p>

<p>The farmer increases light intensity, but after a certain point the photosynthesis rate does not increase.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify the Observation</h3>

<p>The photosynthesis rate has reached a plateau.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Interpret the Plateau</h3>

<p>Light is no longer the main limiting factor under those conditions.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Identify Another Possible Limiting Factor</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide concentration or temperature could now be limiting.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Predict the Effect</h3>

<p>If carbon dioxide is the limiting factor, increasing carbon dioxide concentration may increase the photosynthesis rate.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Recognise the New Plateau</h3>

<p>Eventually another factor may become limiting again.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has a photosynthesis rate of 5 units at moderate light.</p>

<p>Increasing light raises the rate to 8 units.</p>

<p>Further increases in light keep the rate at 8 units.</p>

<p>When carbon dioxide concentration is increased, the rate rises to 11 units.</p>

<p><strong>What was probably limiting after the light increase?</strong></p>

<p>Carbon dioxide concentration.</p>

<h3>Full Exam Reasoning</h3>

<p><strong>Light increased → photosynthesis increased → another factor became limiting → carbon dioxide increased → photosynthesis increased again.</strong></p>

<h3>Core Principle</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis does not depend on one factor in isolation.</p>

<p>The rate at any moment is influenced by whichever required condition is most limiting.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis_complete_reasoning', 15),
  (1222, 1222, '
<h2>The Leaf as a Photosynthetic Organ</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A leaf is specialised for photosynthesis. Its structure allows it to obtain the materials and energy needed for the process.</p>

<h3>1. Large Surface Area</h3>

<p>Most leaves have a broad surface area.</p>

<p>This allows the leaf to intercept a large amount of light.</p>

<h3>2. Thin Structure</h3>

<p>A relatively thin leaf reduces the distance that carbon dioxide must diffuse through the internal tissues.</p>

<h3>3. Chloroplasts</h3>

<p>Mesophyll cells contain chloroplasts with chlorophyll.</p>

<p>These chloroplasts absorb light energy for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>4. Stomata</h3>

<p>Stomata provide pathways for carbon dioxide to enter the leaf and for oxygen and water vapour to leave.</p>

<h3>5. Air Spaces</h3>

<p>Internal air spaces allow gases to diffuse through the leaf towards and away from photosynthesising cells.</p>

<h3>6. Veins</h3>

<p>Xylem brings water and dissolved mineral ions to the leaf.</p>

<p>Phloem transports assimilates away from or around the leaf according to source-sink relationships.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf has a large surface area, many chloroplasts, stomata and a network of veins.</p>

<p><strong>How do these features support photosynthesis?</strong></p>

<p>They help the leaf capture light, obtain carbon dioxide and water, and transport materials and products.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Do not simply list adaptations. Always connect each structure to its function.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for leaf_as_photosynthetic_organ', 15),
  (1223, 1223, '
<h2>Upper Epidermis of a Leaf</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The <strong>upper epidermis</strong> is the outer cell layer on the upper surface of many leaves.</p>

<h3>Protection</h3>

<p>The epidermis protects the internal tissues from physical damage and excessive water loss.</p>

<h3>Transparent Cells</h3>

<p>Upper epidermal cells usually contain few or no chloroplasts, allowing light to pass through towards the photosynthetic mesophyll beneath.</p>

<h3>Waxy Cuticle</h3>

<p>The outer surface is commonly covered by a waxy cuticle.</p>

<p>The cuticle reduces uncontrolled evaporation of water from the leaf surface.</p>

<h3>Why Does This Matter?</h3>

<p>The leaf needs enough water for normal cellular processes and photosynthesis while still allowing carbon dioxide to enter through stomata.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has a damaged waxy cuticle.</p>

<p><strong>What is likely to increase?</strong></p>

<p>Uncontrolled water loss from the leaf surface can increase.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The cuticle reduces water loss, but it is not the main pathway through which carbon dioxide enters the leaf.</p>

<p>Carbon dioxide mainly enters through stomata.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for upper_epidermis_leaf', 15),
  (1224, 1224, '
<h2>Palisade Mesophyll and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The <strong>palisade mesophyll</strong> is a layer of cells located beneath the upper epidermis in many leaves.</p>

<h3>Many Chloroplasts</h3>

<p>Palisade cells commonly contain many chloroplasts.</p>

<p>This allows them to absorb substantial amounts of light energy.</p>

<h3>Position</h3>

<p>They are positioned relatively close to the upper surface of the leaf, where light intensity is often relatively high.</p>

<h3>Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>The palisade mesophyll is therefore a major site of photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Carbon Dioxide</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide entering through stomata diffuses through internal air spaces before reaching photosynthesising cells.</p>

<h3>Water</h3>

<p>Water is delivered to the leaf through xylem in the veins.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A palisade cell contains many chloroplasts and is located near the upper surface of a leaf.</p>

<p><strong>How does each feature help photosynthesis?</strong></p>

<p>Its position gives it good access to light, while its many chloroplasts provide substantial chlorophyll-containing structures for absorbing light energy.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Structure marks should be linked directly to function.</p>

<p><strong>Many chloroplasts → greater capacity to absorb light.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for palisade_mesophyll_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1225, 1225, '
<h2>Spongy Mesophyll and Gas Diffusion</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The <strong>spongy mesophyll</strong> is located below the palisade mesophyll in many leaves.</p>

<h3>Loosely Packed Cells</h3>

<p>Spongy mesophyll cells are arranged relatively loosely, leaving many internal air spaces.</p>

<h3>Air Spaces</h3>

<p>The air spaces provide pathways through which carbon dioxide can diffuse towards photosynthesising cells.</p>

<p>Oxygen produced during photosynthesis can also diffuse through these spaces towards stomata.</p>

<h3>Gas Exchange</h3>

<p>The internal air spaces increase the area of leaf cells exposed to internal air.</p>

<p>This supports efficient diffusion of gases within the leaf.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf has very small internal air spaces.</p>

<p><strong>How could this affect gas movement?</strong></p>

<p>It could increase resistance to diffusion through the leaf and make movement of gases to and from photosynthetic cells less efficient.</p>

<h3>Key Structure → Function</h3>

<p><strong>Loosely arranged cells → large internal air spaces → shorter pathways for gas movement within the leaf.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for spongy_mesophyll', 15),
  (1226, 1226, '
<h2>Leaf Veins and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Leaf veins contain vascular tissues that connect the leaf to the rest of the plant.</p>

<h3>Xylem in the Veins</h3>

<p>Xylem transports water and dissolved mineral ions into the leaf.</p>

<p>Water is needed as a raw material for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Phloem in the Veins</h3>

<p>Phloem transports organic assimilates such as sucrose away from or around the leaf according to source-sink relationships.</p>

<h3>Why Are Veins Spread Through the Leaf?</h3>

<p>A network of veins helps bring water relatively close to photosynthesising cells throughout the leaf.</p>

<p>It also provides pathways for distributing assimilates.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf has damaged veins and begins to wilt while photosynthesis decreases.</p>

<p><strong>Give one reason photosynthesis may decrease.</strong></p>

<p>Damaged xylem can reduce water delivery to the leaf, which can limit photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Xylem and phloem are both found in veins, but they transport different materials.</p>

<p><strong>Xylem → water and mineral ions.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Phloem → organic assimilates.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for leaf_veins_and_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1227, 1227, '
<h2>Lower Epidermis and Stomata</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The <strong>lower epidermis</strong> forms the outer cell layer on the lower surface of many leaves.</p>

<h3>Stomata</h3>

<p>Many terrestrial leaves have a greater number of stomata on the lower surface.</p>

<p>This can reduce direct exposure of stomata to strong sunlight and moving air, helping reduce water loss.</p>

<h3>Gas Exchange</h3>

<p>Stomata allow carbon dioxide to enter the leaf.</p>

<p>Oxygen and water vapour can also move out through the stomatal pores.</p>

<h3>Guard Cells</h3>

<p>Each stoma is controlled by guard cells.</p>

<p>Changes in guard-cell turgor alter the size of the stomatal pore.</p>

<h3>Why Is This Arrangement Useful?</h3>

<p>The plant needs carbon dioxide for photosynthesis but must also limit excessive water loss.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A terrestrial plant has most of its stomata on the lower surface of its leaves.</p>

<p><strong>Why can this arrangement help conserve water?</strong></p>

<p>The lower surface is often less exposed to direct sunlight and air movement, which can reduce the driving force for water loss compared with a fully exposed upper surface.</p>

<h3>Caution</h3>

<p>Stomatal distribution varies among plant species and according to habitat.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for lower_epidermis_and_stomata', 15),
  (1228, 1228, '
<h2>Interpreting a Leaf Cross-Section</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A leaf cross-section shows how different tissues are arranged to support photosynthesis and gas exchange.</p>

<h3>From Top to Bottom</h3>

<p>A typical terrestrial leaf can contain:</p>

<p><strong>Waxy cuticle → upper epidermis → palisade mesophyll → spongy mesophyll → vascular bundle → lower epidermis with stomata.</strong></p>

<h3>Upper Region</h3>

<p>The upper surface receives light.</p>

<p>The palisade mesophyll beneath it contains many chloroplasts.</p>

<h3>Middle Region</h3>

<p>The spongy mesophyll contains internal air spaces that facilitate gas diffusion.</p>

<h3>Vascular Bundle</h3>

<p>The vascular bundle contains xylem and phloem.</p>

<p>Xylem supplies water and mineral ions.</p>

<p>Phloem transports assimilates.</p>

<h3>Lower Region</h3>

<p>Stomata provide pathways for gas exchange and water vapour loss.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student is shown a leaf cross-section and identifies a layer of tightly packed cells containing many chloroplasts.</p>

<p><strong>Which tissue is it most likely to be?</strong></p>

<p>Palisade mesophyll.</p>

<p><strong>Why?</strong></p>

<p>Palisade cells are commonly tightly packed beneath the upper epidermis and contain many chloroplasts for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Exam Skill</h3>

<p>When identifying a leaf tissue, use both its <strong>position</strong> and its <strong>structure</strong>.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for leaf_cross_section', 15),
  (1229, 1229, '
<h2>Leaf Adaptations for Efficient Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The structure of a leaf is closely related to the requirements of photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Adaptation 1: Large Surface Area</h3>

<p>A broad surface allows the leaf to capture more light.</p>

<h3>Adaptation 2: Thin Leaf</h3>

<p>A short diffusion distance helps gases move through the leaf.</p>

<h3>Adaptation 3: Many Chloroplasts</h3>

<p>Palisade cells contain many chloroplasts for absorbing light energy.</p>

<h3>Adaptation 4: Stomata</h3>

<p>Stomata allow carbon dioxide to enter and allow oxygen and water vapour to move out.</p>

<h3>Adaptation 5: Internal Air Spaces</h3>

<p>Spongy mesophyll air spaces facilitate diffusion of gases.</p>

<h3>Adaptation 6: Veins</h3>

<p>Xylem supplies water and mineral ions, while phloem transports assimilates.</p>

<h3>Adaptation 7: Waxy Cuticle</h3>

<p>The cuticle reduces excessive water loss from exposed leaf surfaces.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student is asked:</p>

<p><em>"Explain three ways a leaf is adapted for photosynthesis."</em></p>

<p>A strong answer could state:</p>

<p><strong>Large surface area → captures more light.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Many chloroplasts in palisade cells → absorb more light energy.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Stomata → allow carbon dioxide to enter.</strong></p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>For adaptation questions, use:</p>

<p><strong>Feature → function → advantage for photosynthesis.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for leaf_adaptations_summary', 15),
  (1230, 1230, '
<h2>Photosynthesis and Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis and respiration are different processes, although the substances involved in one process are related to those involved in the other.</p>

<h3>Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis uses light energy to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water.</p>

<p><strong>Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen</strong></p>

<p>Photosynthesis stores energy in the chemical bonds of glucose.</p>

<h3>Respiration</h3>

<p>Respiration releases chemical energy from glucose.</p>

<p>In aerobic respiration, oxygen is used and carbon dioxide and water are produced.</p>

<p><strong>Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + energy</strong></p>

<h3>The Important Difference</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis is an energy-storing process.</p>

<p>Aerobic respiration is an energy-releasing process.</p>

<p>Photosynthesis requires light energy, while aerobic respiration does not require light.</p>

<h3>Do Plants Respire?</h3>

<p>Yes.</p>

<p>Plant cells respire continuously because they require released energy for processes such as active transport, growth and synthesis of substances.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is placed in darkness.</p>

<p><strong>Does respiration stop?</strong></p>

<p>No. Respiration continues because plant cells still require energy.</p>

<p>However, photosynthesis cannot continue without a suitable source of light.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis → stores energy in glucose.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Respiration → releases energy from glucose.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis_and_respiration', 15),
  (1231, 1231, '
<h2>Photosynthesis and Mineral Nutrition</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants need mineral ions from the soil for healthy growth and for making important substances.</p>

<p>Mineral ions are not all raw materials of the photosynthesis equation, but some are essential for the structures and molecules that allow photosynthesis to occur effectively.</p>

<h3>Magnesium</h3>

<p>Magnesium is needed for chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>Chlorophyll absorbs light energy used in photosynthesis.</p>

<p>A magnesium deficiency can therefore reduce chlorophyll formation and cause leaves to become pale or yellow.</p>

<h3>Nitrate Ions</h3>

<p>Nitrate ions provide nitrogen.</p>

<p>Nitrogen is needed to make amino acids and proteins.</p>

<h3>Why Does This Affect Photosynthesis?</h3>

<p>A plant with severe mineral deficiencies may have poor growth and reduced photosynthetic capacity.</p>

<p>For example, inadequate magnesium can reduce chlorophyll production.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two plants are grown under the same light intensity. One receives enough magnesium while the other receives very little.</p>

<p>The magnesium-deficient plant develops pale leaves.</p>

<p><strong>Explain why its photosynthetic performance may be reduced.</strong></p>

<p>Magnesium is needed for chlorophyll production. Less chlorophyll can reduce the plant''s ability to absorb light energy.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Do not say magnesium is a raw material in the balanced photosynthesis equation.</p>

<p>It is a mineral nutrient required for plant processes, including chlorophyll production.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis_and_mineral_nutrition', 15),
  (1232, 1232, '
<h2>Controls in a Photosynthesis Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A control helps a scientist determine whether the tested factor caused the observed result.</p>

<h3>Example: Testing Whether Light Is Required</h3>

<p>A destarched plant has part of one leaf covered with opaque material while the rest remains exposed to light.</p>

<p>The leaf is then tested for starch.</p>

<h3>Why Is This a Useful Control?</h3>

<p>The covered and uncovered regions are on the same leaf and experience many of the same conditions.</p>

<p>The main deliberately changed factor is whether that region receives light.</p>

<h3>Expected Result</h3>

<p>The exposed region is expected to test positive for starch if photosynthesis occurs.</p>

<p>The covered region is expected to contain less newly formed starch if light is required under the experimental conditions.</p>

<h3>Other Variables</h3>

<p>The investigation should keep other important conditions as similar as possible, such as temperature, water availability and the plant material used.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student covers one leaf with opaque paper but changes the temperature of the covered region as well.</p>

<p><strong>Why is this a problem?</strong></p>

<p>Temperature has also changed, so the student cannot confidently attribute the result only to light.</p>

<h3>Key Principle</h3>

<p><strong>Change one independent variable while keeping important control variables constant.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis_experiment_control', 15),
  (1233, 1233, '
<h2>Photosynthesis in the Carbon Cycle</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Photosynthesis is one of the major processes that transfers carbon from the
atmosphere into living organisms.
</p>

<p>
Green plants absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere through their leaves.
The carbon dioxide is then used during photosynthesis to make glucose.
</p>

<h3>THE EQUATION</h3>

<p>
<strong>Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen</strong>
</p>

<p>
Light energy and chlorophyll are required for the process.
</p>

<p>
The carbon atoms originally present in carbon dioxide become part of the
glucose molecule.
</p>

<h3>FOLLOWING THE CARBON</h3>

<p>
Suppose atmospheric carbon dioxide enters a leaf.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon dioxide enters through the stomata.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> The carbon dioxide reaches photosynthetic cells.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Carbon dioxide is used to produce glucose.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> The glucose may be used immediately in respiration or
converted into substances such as starch, cellulose or other organic compounds.
</p>

<h3>WHY THIS MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Without photosynthesis, atmospheric carbon dioxide would not be continually
incorporated into new plant biomass in this way.
</p>

<p>
Plants therefore form an important entry point for carbon into many food chains.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why photosynthesis is important in the carbon cycle.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon dioxide is present in the atmosphere.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Plants absorb the carbon dioxide.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Photosynthesis converts the carbon into organic compounds.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Animals obtain some of this carbon by feeding on plants.</p>

<p>
Therefore, photosynthesis transfers atmospheric carbon into living organisms.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Photosynthesis in the Carbon Cycle', 15),
  (1234, 1234, '
<h2>Water as a Raw Material for Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Water is one of the raw materials required for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Where Does the Water Come From?</h3>

<p>Water is absorbed from the soil by the roots.</p>

<p>It is transported to the leaves through the xylem.</p>

<h3>Pathway</h3>

<p><strong>Soil → root hair cells → root tissues → xylem → leaf.</strong></p>

<h3>Why Is Water Important?</h3>

<p>Water participates in the reactions of photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Therefore, severe water shortage can limit photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Water Shortage and Stomata</h3>

<p>When a plant experiences water stress, guard cells can lose turgor and stomata may close.</p>

<p>This reduces water loss but also restricts carbon dioxide entry.</p>

<h3>Two Effects of Water Stress</h3>

<p><strong>1. Less water is available as a raw material.</strong></p>

<p><strong>2. Stomatal closure can reduce carbon dioxide entry.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant experiences severe drought.</p>

<p>Its stomata close for long periods.</p>

<p><strong>Why can photosynthesis decrease?</strong></p>

<p>Stomatal closure reduces carbon dioxide entry, while severe water shortage can also limit the plant''s ability to maintain normal photosynthetic activity.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Low water availability → stomatal closure → reduced CO₂ entry → photosynthesis may decrease.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for water_in_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1235, 1235, '
<h2>What Happens to Glucose After Photosynthesis?</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis produces glucose, but the plant does not simply leave all of the glucose as free glucose inside the leaf.</p>

<p>Glucose can be converted into different substances depending on the plant''s needs.</p>

<h3>1. Respiration</h3>

<p>Glucose can be broken down during respiration to release usable energy.</p>

<h3>2. Starch Storage</h3>

<p>Glucose can be converted into starch for storage.</p>

<p>Starch is useful because it is relatively insoluble and can be stored without greatly affecting water potential.</p>

<h3>3. Cellulose</h3>

<p>Glucose can be used to make cellulose for plant cell walls.</p>

<h3>4. Amino Acids and Proteins</h3>

<p>Plants can use carbon compounds from glucose together with nitrogen-containing compounds to produce amino acids.</p>

<p>Amino acids can then be used to make proteins.</p>

<h3>5. Other Organic Compounds</h3>

<p>Glucose-derived carbon skeletons can contribute to the synthesis of other organic substances needed by the plant.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf produces glucose during the day but does not immediately use all of it in respiration.</p>

<p><strong>What can happen to some of the remaining glucose?</strong></p>

<p>It can be converted into starch and stored.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis → glucose → respiration / starch / cellulose / other organic substances.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for glucose_fates_after_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1236, 1236, '
<h2>Oxygen Production During Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Oxygen is one of the products of photosynthesis.</p>

<p>When photosynthesis is occurring, oxygen can be released from green plant tissues.</p>

<h3>Where Does the Oxygen Go?</h3>

<p>Some oxygen is used by the plant in respiration.</p>

<p>Some oxygen can diffuse out of the leaf into the surrounding air.</p>

<h3>Simple Aquatic Plant Investigation</h3>

<p>An aquatic plant can be placed in water under light.</p>

<p>Gas bubbles may be observed emerging from the plant.</p>

<p>These bubbles can contain oxygen produced during photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Important Limitation</h3>

<p>Counting bubbles is only an approximate method because bubbles can differ in size.</p>

<p>A better investigation would measure gas volume over a known period.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>An aquatic plant produces 24 cm³ of gas in 6 minutes.</p>

<p><strong>Calculate the rate of gas production.</strong></p>

<p>Rate = volume ÷ time</p>

<p>Rate = 24 ÷ 6</p>

<p><strong>Rate = 4 cm³/min</strong></p>

<h3>Exam Connection</h3>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis → oxygen production → some oxygen may diffuse out of the plant.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis_and_oxygen', 15),
  (1237, 1237, '
<h2>What Plants Do With Glucose</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Glucose produced during photosynthesis does not simply remain inside the chloroplast.</p>

<p>The plant can use glucose in several ways.</p>

<h3>1. Respiration</h3>

<p>Glucose can be broken down during respiration to release energy for cellular activities.</p>

<h3>2. Starch Storage</h3>

<p>Glucose can be converted into starch for storage.</p>

<p>Starch is less soluble than glucose, making it suitable for storage.</p>

<h3>3. Cellulose</h3>

<p>Glucose is used to make cellulose.</p>

<p>Cellulose forms much of the plant cell wall.</p>

<h3>4. Amino Acids and Proteins</h3>

<p>Carbon compounds produced from glucose can be combined with nitrogen to form amino acids.</p>

<p>Amino acids are then used to make proteins.</p>

<h3>5. Other Organic Compounds</h3>

<p>Glucose and its products can also contribute to the synthesis of other substances required by the plant.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant produces glucose faster than it immediately needs it for respiration.</p>

<p><strong>What can happen to some of the excess glucose?</strong></p>

<p>It can be converted into starch and stored.</p>

<h3>Exam Connection</h3>

<p>When asked about the <strong>fate of glucose</strong>, do not give only one use.</p>

<p>Common answers include respiration, starch storage, cellulose formation and formation of amino acids/proteins when nitrogen is available.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis_and_glucose', 15),
  (1238, 1238, '
<h2>Photosynthesis: Complete Process</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis can be understood as a sequence in which raw materials reach photosynthetic cells and light energy is used to make glucose.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Light Reaches the Leaf</h3>

<p>Light passes through the relatively transparent upper tissues and reaches chloroplasts containing chlorophyll.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Carbon Dioxide Enters</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide from the surrounding air enters mainly through stomata.</p>

<p>It then diffuses through internal air spaces towards photosynthetic cells.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Water Reaches the Leaf</h3>

<p>Roots absorb water and xylem transports it to the leaves.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Light Energy Is Absorbed</h3>

<p>Chlorophyll absorbs light energy within chloroplasts.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Glucose Is Produced</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide and water are used in photosynthesis to form glucose, with oxygen released.</p>

<p><strong>6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</strong></p>

<h3>Step 6: Products Are Used</h3>

<p>Glucose can be used in respiration, converted to starch, used to form cellulose, or contribute to the production of amino acids and other compounds.</p>

<p>Oxygen can diffuse out of the leaf through stomata, although some oxygen is also used by plant cells in respiration.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant receives enough light but its roots cannot supply sufficient water.</p>

<p><strong>Why can photosynthesis be affected?</strong></p>

<p>Water is a raw material for photosynthesis, and severe water shortage can also cause stomata to close, restricting carbon dioxide entry.</p>

<h3>Complete Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Light → chlorophyll → energy input</strong></p>

<p><strong>Carbon dioxide → stomata → photosynthetic cells</strong></p>

<p><strong>Water → roots → xylem → leaf</strong></p>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis → glucose + oxygen</strong></p>

<p><strong>Glucose → respiration / starch / cellulose / other compounds</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis_complete_process', 15),
  (1239, 1239, '
<h2>Testing a Leaf for Starch</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A starch test can be used to investigate whether photosynthesis has produced stored carbohydrate in a leaf.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Remove the Leaf</h3>

<p>A leaf is removed from the plant.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Kill the Leaf</h3>

<p>The leaf is placed in boiling water briefly.</p>

<p>This kills the cells and stops chemical reactions.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Remove Chlorophyll</h3>

<p>The leaf is heated in ethanol using a suitable water bath.</p>

<p>The ethanol removes much of the chlorophyll.</p>

<p>This makes the colour change easier to observe.</p>

<h3>Safety</h3>

<p>Ethanol is flammable, so it should not be heated directly over a naked flame.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Soften the Leaf</h3>

<p>The decolourised leaf is rinsed in warm water to soften it.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Add Iodine Solution</h3>

<p>Iodine solution is added to the leaf.</p>

<p><strong>Blue-black colour → starch is present.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Yellow-brown colour → starch has not been detected.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf exposed to light turns blue-black when iodine is added.</p>

<p><strong>What does this show?</strong></p>

<p>Starch is present in the tested region of the leaf.</p>

<h3>Important Limitation</h3>

<p>The starch test shows that starch is present. It does not directly show the exact rate of photosynthesis.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for starch_test_leaf', 15),
  (1240, 1240, '
<h2>Destarching a Plant</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Destarching</strong> is the process of removing or reducing stored starch from a plant before a photosynthesis experiment.</p>

<h3>Why Destarch a Plant?</h3>

<p>If a leaf already contains stored starch, a positive iodine test might not have resulted from photosynthesis occurring during the experiment.</p>

<p>Destarching helps make the starting conditions more controlled.</p>

<h3>How Is a Plant Destarched?</h3>

<p>The plant is kept in darkness for a sufficient period.</p>

<p>During darkness, photosynthesis stops.</p>

<p>The plant continues respiration and uses stored carbohydrates.</p>

<p>This reduces stored starch in the leaves.</p>

<h3>Then What?</h3>

<p>After destarching, the plant can be exposed to controlled experimental conditions.</p>

<p>Any starch formed during the investigation provides stronger evidence that photosynthesis occurred under those conditions.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student wants to test whether light is necessary for starch formation.</p>

<p><strong>Why should the plant first be destarched?</strong></p>

<p>To reduce starch already present in the leaves so that the final starch result is more closely related to photosynthesis during the experiment.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Darkness → photosynthesis stops → respiration continues → stored carbohydrate is used → starch reduced.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for destarching_plants', 15),
  (1241, 1241, '
<h2>Starch Storage in Plants</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants often store carbohydrate in the form of starch.</p>

<h3>Why Store Starch?</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis depends on conditions such as light availability.</p>

<p>A plant therefore needs a way to store carbohydrate produced when photosynthesis supplies more glucose than is immediately required.</p>

<h3>Glucose to Starch</h3>

<p>Glucose molecules can be joined together to form starch.</p>

<p>Starch can accumulate in storage organs such as roots, stems, seeds and other plant tissues.</p>

<h3>Stored Starch Can Be Used Later</h3>

<p>When energy or carbohydrate is required, stored starch can be broken down into smaller soluble sugars.</p>

<p>These sugars can then be transported or used in respiration and other metabolic processes.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has been exposed to strong light for several hours.</p>

<p>Later, it is placed in darkness.</p>

<p><strong>Why can the plant still have carbohydrate available?</strong></p>

<p>Some glucose produced earlier may have been converted to starch and stored.</p>

<h3>Connection to the Starch Test</h3>

<p>The iodine test is used as evidence that starch is present in a leaf.</p>

<p>A blue-black colour indicates starch has been detected.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for starch_storage_in_plants', 15),
  (1242, 1242, '
<h2>Why the Starch Test Provides Evidence of Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis produces glucose. Some of this glucose is converted into starch.</p>

<p>Because starch can accumulate in photosynthesising tissues, detecting starch can provide evidence that photosynthesis has occurred.</p>

<h3>The Logic</h3>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis produces glucose.</strong></p>

<p>↓</p>

<p><strong>Some glucose is converted into starch.</strong></p>

<p>↓</p>

<p><strong>Starch can be detected using iodine.</strong></p>

<h3>Why Destarching Is Important</h3>

<p>A plant may already contain stored starch before an experiment begins.</p>

<p>If this existing starch remains, it could make the results difficult to interpret.</p>

<p>Keeping the plant in darkness before the experiment allows stored carbohydrate to be used in respiration, reducing pre-existing starch.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student tests a leaf without first destarching the plant.</p>

<p>The leaf turns blue-black with iodine.</p>

<p><strong>Can the student conclude that the experimental light condition caused the starch?</strong></p>

<p>No.</p>

<p>The starch may have been present before the experiment.</p>

<h3>Exam Reasoning</h3>

<p>Destarching does not prove that photosynthesis never occurred before the experiment.</p>

<p>It is used to reduce the amount of pre-existing starch so that changes during the experiment can be interpreted more reliably.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for photosynthesis_starch_evidence', 15),
  (1243, 1243, '
<h2>Light Intensity and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Light provides the energy required for photosynthesis.
Changing light intensity can therefore affect the rate at which photosynthesis
takes place.
</p>

<h3>WHEN LIGHT INTENSITY INCREASES</h3>

<p>
At low light intensity, increasing light usually increases the rate of
photosynthesis because more light energy becomes available.
</p>

<p>
Eventually, another factor becomes limiting.
At this point, increasing light intensity further produces little or no
additional increase in the rate.
</p>

<h3>LIMITING FACTOR</h3>

<p>
A <strong>limiting factor</strong> is a factor that prevents a process from
occurring at a higher rate.
</p>

<p>
For example, if light is abundant but carbon dioxide is scarce, carbon dioxide
may limit photosynthesis.
</p>

<h3>GRAPH REASONING</h3>

<p>
Imagine a graph with light intensity on the horizontal axis and rate of
photosynthesis on the vertical axis.
</p>

<p>
At first, the graph rises as light intensity increases.
Later, the graph becomes almost horizontal.
</p>

<p>
The rising section shows that light is limiting.
The plateau shows that another factor has become limiting.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why does photosynthesis eventually stop increasing
when light intensity continues to rise?</p>

<p>
At first, light is limiting. After sufficient light is available, another
factor such as carbon dioxide concentration or temperature limits the rate.
Increasing light beyond this point therefore has little effect.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Light Intensity and Photosynthesis', 15),
  (1244, 1244, '
<h2>Carbon Dioxide and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Carbon dioxide is a raw material required for photosynthesis.
Its concentration can therefore affect the rate of photosynthesis.
</p>

<h3>LOW CARBON DIOXIDE</h3>

<p>
If carbon dioxide concentration is low, carbon dioxide can limit the rate of
photosynthesis.
</p>

<p>
Increasing carbon dioxide concentration can increase the rate while carbon
dioxide remains the limiting factor.
</p>

<h3>WHY THE RATE EVENTUALLY LEVELS OFF</h3>

<p>
A plant needs more than carbon dioxide to photosynthesise.
It also requires suitable light conditions, water and temperature.
</p>

<p>
Once carbon dioxide is no longer limiting, another factor can restrict the
rate.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Suppose a plant is exposed to sufficient light but has a low carbon dioxide
concentration.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbon dioxide is scarce.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Increasing carbon dioxide provides more raw material.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> The photosynthetic rate increases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Eventually another factor becomes limiting.</p>

<p>
Therefore, adding carbon dioxide does not increase photosynthesis without
limit.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Carbon Dioxide and Photosynthesis', 15),
  (1245, 1245, '
<h2>Temperature and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Photosynthesis involves enzyme-controlled reactions.
Temperature therefore affects the rate at which these reactions occur.
</p>

<h3>LOW TEMPERATURE</h3>

<p>
At low temperatures, molecules have less kinetic energy and enzyme-controlled
reactions generally occur more slowly.
The rate of photosynthesis may therefore be low.
</p>

<h3>INCREASING TEMPERATURE</h3>

<p>
As temperature increases within a suitable range, enzyme activity increases
and the rate of photosynthesis can rise.
</p>

<h3>TOO HIGH A TEMPERATURE</h3>

<p>
At excessively high temperatures, enzymes can lose their functional shape.
This is called <strong>denaturation</strong>.
</p>

<p>
High temperatures can also increase water loss, causing stomata to close.
This reduces carbon dioxide entry into the leaf.
</p>

<h3>THE GENERAL PATTERN</h3>

<p>
Low temperature → slow enzyme-controlled reactions
</p>

<p>
Suitable temperature → faster reactions
</p>

<p>
Excessively high temperature → enzyme damage and other stresses
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can very high temperature reduce photosynthesis?</p>

<p>
High temperature can cause enzymes involved in photosynthesis to denature.
It can also increase water loss, causing stomata to close and reducing carbon
dioxide entry. Therefore, the rate of photosynthesis can fall.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Temperature and Photosynthesis', 15),
  (1246, 1246, '
<h2>Limiting Factors in Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Photosynthesis depends on several environmental conditions.
The factor in shortest supply relative to the plant''s needs can limit the
rate of photosynthesis.
</p>

<h3>THREE IMPORTANT FACTORS</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Light intensity</li>
  <li>Carbon dioxide concentration</li>
  <li>Temperature</li>
</ul>

<p>
Water and mineral nutrient availability can also affect plant growth and
photosynthetic performance.
</p>

<h3>HOW LIMITATION CHANGES</h3>

<p>
Imagine a plant receiving very little light.
Light is the limiting factor.
</p>

<p>
If light is increased sufficiently, carbon dioxide might then become the
limiting factor.
</p>

<p>
If carbon dioxide is increased too, temperature might become limiting.
</p>

<p>
The limiting factor can therefore change as conditions change.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> A plant receives high light intensity but its
carbon dioxide concentration is low. Which factor is likely limiting?</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Light is already abundant.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Carbon dioxide is scarce.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Carbon dioxide is therefore more likely to restrict
the rate of photosynthesis.</p>

<p>
The limiting factor is <strong>carbon dioxide concentration</strong>.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Increasing a non-limiting factor has little effect.
The greatest increase occurs when the limiting factor is increased.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Limiting Factors in Photosynthesis', 15),
  (1247, 1247, '
<h2>Temperature and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis involves enzyme-controlled reactions.</p>

<p>Temperature therefore affects the rate of photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>At Low Temperature</h3>

<p>Enzyme-controlled reactions occur more slowly.</p>

<p>As temperature increases, the rate of photosynthesis can increase.</p>

<h3>At an Optimum</h3>

<p>There is a temperature range at which photosynthesis occurs at a relatively high rate.</p>

<h3>At Excessively High Temperature</h3>

<p>Very high temperatures can damage or denature enzymes.</p>

<p>They can also cause excessive water loss and stomatal closure.</p>

<p>As a result, the rate of photosynthesis can decrease.</p>

<h3>Typical Graph</h3>

<p>Temperature against photosynthesis rate commonly produces a curve:</p>

<p><strong>Low temperature → rate increases.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Optimum temperature → highest rate.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Very high temperature → rate decreases.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has photosynthesis rates of:</p>

<p>10°C → 2 units</p>

<p>20°C → 5 units</p>

<p>30°C → 8 units</p>

<p>40°C → 3 units</p>

<p><strong>At which tested temperature is the rate highest?</strong></p>

<p>30°C.</p>

<p><strong>Why does the rate fall at 40°C?</strong></p>

<p>High temperature can damage enzymes and may also cause water stress and stomatal closure.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for temperature_and_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1248, 1248, '
<h2>Limiting Factors in Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A <strong>limiting factor</strong> is a factor that restricts the rate of a process when it is present at an insufficient level.</p>

<h3>Major Limiting Factors</h3>

<p>For photosynthesis, important limiting factors include:</p>

<ul>
<li>light intensity;</li>
<li>carbon dioxide concentration;</li>
<li>temperature;</li>
<li>water availability.</li>
</ul>

<h3>One Factor at a Time</h3>

<p>Suppose light intensity is low.</p>

<p>Increasing light can increase photosynthesis.</p>

<p>But after a certain point, increasing light further may have little effect because another factor has become limiting.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>A plant is receiving enough light but has very little carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>Increasing light will not necessarily increase photosynthesis significantly.</p>

<p>Carbon dioxide is the limiting factor.</p>

<h3>Why the Concept Matters</h3>

<p>Plants need several conditions simultaneously.</p>

<p>Improving one condition does not remove the effect of another condition that is still limiting.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is tested at two light intensities:</p>

<p>Low light → rate = 3 units</p>

<p>High light → rate = 6 units</p>

<p>The carbon dioxide concentration is then increased while high light is maintained, and the rate rises to 9 units.</p>

<p><strong>What does this suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Light was limiting at the lower intensity, while carbon dioxide was limiting under the later high-light condition.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Increasing a factor only increases the rate if that factor is limiting.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for limiting_factor', 15),
  (1249, 1249, '
<h2>Why Plants Need Mineral Ions</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants obtain water and dissolved mineral ions from the soil.</p>

<p>Mineral ions are required for making important substances and for maintaining normal growth and metabolism.</p>

<h3>Mineral Ions Are Not All Used in the Same Way</h3>

<p>Different mineral ions have different functions.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p><strong>Nitrate ions → provide nitrogen.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Magnesium ions → required for chlorophyll production.</strong></p>

<h3>How Roots Obtain Them</h3>

<p>Mineral ions are absorbed by root cells from the soil solution.</p>

<p>When ions must move into root cells against their concentration gradient, active transport can be used.</p>

<h3>Why Deficiency Matters</h3>

<p>If a plant does not obtain enough of an essential mineral ion, a particular process may be affected.</p>

<p>This can lead to characteristic deficiency symptoms such as poor growth or yellowing of leaves.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has adequate water and light but grows poorly because the soil contains insufficient nitrate ions.</p>

<p><strong>Why can growth be reduced?</strong></p>

<p>Nitrate provides nitrogen needed to make amino acids and proteins. Insufficient nitrogen therefore limits protein production and normal growth.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>Mineral ions are required in relatively small quantities compared with water, but their absence can seriously affect plant growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for why_plants_need_mineral_ions', 15),
  (1250, 1250, '
<h2>Nitrate Ions and Plant Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Nitrate ions provide plants with nitrogen.</p>

<p>Nitrogen is an essential element used to make amino acids.</p>

<p>Amino acids are joined together to form proteins.</p>

<h3>Why Are Proteins Important?</h3>

<p>Proteins are required for many functions in plant cells.</p>

<p>They include structural proteins and enzymes that control biochemical reactions.</p>

<h3>What Happens During Nitrate Deficiency?</h3>

<p>If insufficient nitrate is available, the plant cannot obtain enough nitrogen for normal production of nitrogen-containing compounds.</p>

<p>This can result in:</p>

<p>• reduced growth</p>

<p>• poor development</p>

<p>• yellowing or pale leaves, especially in older leaves in many cases</p>

<h3>Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Low nitrate → low nitrogen supply → reduced amino acid/protein production → impaired growth.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Plant A receives sufficient nitrate. Plant B receives very little nitrate. Both plants receive the same amount of light and water.</p>

<p>After several weeks, Plant B is smaller.</p>

<p><strong>Explain the difference.</strong></p>

<p>Plant B has insufficient nitrate to supply enough nitrogen for normal amino acid and protein production, so its growth is reduced.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Nitrate is not used to provide the carbon atoms of glucose in photosynthesis.</p>

<p>It provides nitrogen needed for nitrogen-containing compounds such as amino acids and proteins.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for nitrate_ions_in_plants', 15);
INSERT INTO public.lessons (id, topic_id, content, summary, estimated_minutes) VALUES
  (1251, 1251, '
<h2>Magnesium Ions and Chlorophyll</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Magnesium ions are required for the production of chlorophyll.</p>

<p>Chlorophyll is the green pigment that absorbs light energy used during photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Why Is Magnesium Important?</h3>

<p>Without enough magnesium, the plant''s ability to produce normal amounts of chlorophyll can be affected.</p>

<p>Reduced chlorophyll can reduce the plant''s ability to absorb light energy for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Magnesium Is Different From Nitrate</h3>

<p><strong>Nitrate → supplies nitrogen for amino acids and proteins.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Magnesium → required for chlorophyll production.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant receives enough water, carbon dioxide and light but has very little magnesium.</p>

<p>The leaves become pale.</p>

<p><strong>Explain why photosynthesis may decrease.</strong></p>

<p>Magnesium deficiency can reduce chlorophyll production. With less chlorophyll, the plant may absorb less light energy, reducing photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Key Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Low magnesium → reduced chlorophyll production → reduced light absorption capacity → possible reduction in photosynthesis.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for magnesium_ions_in_plants', 15),
  (1252, 1252, '
<h2>Active Transport of Mineral Ions</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Active transport</strong> is the movement of substances across a cell membrane using energy, often against a concentration gradient.</p>

<h3>Mineral Ion Uptake</h3>

<p>Root cells may contain a higher concentration of certain mineral ions than the surrounding soil solution.</p>

<p>Mineral ions can still be absorbed because membrane transport proteins use energy to move them into the cells.</p>

<h3>Where Does the Energy Come From?</h3>

<p>Energy for active transport is supplied by respiration in the root cells.</p>

<h3>Why Oxygen Availability Can Matter</h3>

<p>Roots require oxygen for aerobic respiration.</p>

<p>If soil becomes waterlogged, air spaces can fill with water and oxygen availability around roots can decrease.</p>

<p>This can reduce aerobic respiration and therefore reduce the energy available for active transport.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two identical plants are grown in the same nutrient solution. Plant A has well-aerated soil. Plant B is grown in severely waterlogged soil.</p>

<p><strong>Why might Plant B absorb mineral ions less effectively?</strong></p>

<p>Waterlogging can reduce oxygen availability to roots. This can reduce aerobic respiration and therefore reduce the energy available for active transport.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Active transport is not the same as diffusion.</p>

<p><strong>Diffusion → passive movement down a concentration gradient.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Active transport → energy-dependent movement, often against a concentration gradient.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for active_transport_mineral_ions', 15),
  (1253, 1253, '
<h2>Evaluating a Mineral Nutrition Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A good mineral nutrition experiment must produce evidence that can be interpreted reliably.</p>

<h3>Use Similar Plants</h3>

<p>Plants should be similar in species, age and starting size.</p>

<p>This reduces differences caused by factors other than mineral availability.</p>

<h3>Use Replicates</h3>

<p>Several plants should be used in each treatment.</p>

<p>Repeats help identify unusual results and allow means to be calculated.</p>

<h3>Control Other Variables</h3>

<p>Important variables such as light, temperature, water supply and other mineral nutrients should be controlled.</p>

<h3>Measure Growth Quantitatively</h3>

<p>Instead of simply writing "healthy" or "unhealthy," measure something such as increase in height or biomass.</p>

<h3>Include a Control Group</h3>

<p>A control group receiving adequate mineral nutrition provides a comparison.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student tests magnesium deficiency using one plant without magnesium and one plant with magnesium.</p>

<p>The plant without magnesium becomes yellow.</p>

<p><strong>Give one weakness of this investigation.</strong></p>

<p>Only one plant was used in each group, so individual variation could have influenced the result.</p>

<h3>Improvement</h3>

<p>Use several plants in each treatment and calculate the mean response.</p>

<h3>Key Principle</h3>

<p>A stronger experiment does not simply produce a result. It makes alternative explanations less likely.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_nutrition_practical_evaluation', 15),
  (1254, 1254, '
<h2>Plant Mineral Nutrition: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student grows two groups of plants.</p>

<p>Both groups receive the same light, water, temperature and carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>Group A receives a complete mineral nutrient solution.</p>

<p>Group B receives the same solution without magnesium.</p>

<p>After several weeks, Group B has pale leaves and a lower mean growth.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify the Changed Variable</h3>

<p>The deliberate difference is the presence of magnesium.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify the Evidence</h3>

<p>The magnesium-deficient plants developed pale leaves and showed reduced growth.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Connect Magnesium to Chlorophyll</h3>

<p>Magnesium is required for chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>Reduced magnesium availability can therefore reduce chlorophyll production.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Connect Chlorophyll to Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>Chlorophyll absorbs light energy used in photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Less chlorophyll can reduce the plant''s capacity to absorb light energy.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Explain the Growth Difference</h3>

<p>Reduced photosynthetic performance can reduce carbohydrate production available for growth.</p>

<p>Magnesium deficiency can therefore contribute to reduced growth under these conditions.</p>

<h3>Complete Chain</h3>

<p><strong>No magnesium → reduced chlorophyll production → reduced light absorption capacity → reduced photosynthetic performance → possible reduction in growth.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Exam Question</h3>

<p><strong>Explain why plants grown without magnesium may show reduced growth.</strong></p>

<p>Magnesium is required for chlorophyll production. Magnesium deficiency can reduce chlorophyll, lowering the plant''s capacity to absorb light for photosynthesis. This can reduce photosynthetic carbohydrate production available for growth.</p>

<h3>Important Scientific Qualification</h3>

<p>The conclusion applies to the conditions of the experiment. A complete scientific explanation should not assume that magnesium is the only factor affecting growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_nutrition_complete_reasoning', 15),
  (1255, 1255, '
<h2>Mineral Concentration and Plant Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The effect of a mineral nutrient on plant growth depends on its concentration and on the plant''s requirements.</p>

<h3>Too Little</h3>

<p>If the concentration is too low, the mineral may become a limiting factor.</p>

<p>Growth can be reduced because the plant cannot obtain enough of the nutrient required for normal processes.</p>

<h3>Adequate Supply</h3>

<p>Increasing the concentration may increase growth until the mineral is no longer limiting.</p>

<h3>Plateau</h3>

<p>Once another factor becomes limiting, adding more of the original mineral may produce little or no further increase in growth.</p>

<h3>Very High Concentrations</h3>

<p>Excessive concentrations of some mineral salts can damage plants or interfere with water uptake and normal cell function.</p>

<p>Therefore, "more mineral" does not automatically mean "more growth."</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Plant growth increases from 10 to 30 mg/L nitrate, then remains almost unchanged from 30 to 60 mg/L.</p>

<p><strong>What does the plateau suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Nitrate may no longer be the main limiting factor above about 30 mg/L under the conditions tested.</p>

<h3>Graph Reasoning</h3>

<p><strong>Low concentration → nutrient limiting → growth increases.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Adequate concentration → another factor becomes limiting → growth plateaus.</strong></p>

<h3>Exam Caution</h3>

<p>Do not claim an exact "best concentration" unless the experiment actually tests enough concentrations to establish it.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_nutrition_dose_response', 15),
  (1256, 1256, '
<h2>Reading a Mineral Nutrition Graph</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A graph of mineral concentration against plant growth can reveal how a nutrient affects growth.</p>

<h3>Axes</h3>

<p>The horizontal axis normally represents the mineral concentration supplied.</p>

<p>The vertical axis represents the measured growth response.</p>

<h3>Increasing Section</h3>

<p>If growth rises as mineral concentration increases, the mineral may be limiting growth in that range.</p>

<h3>Plateau</h3>

<p>If the graph becomes horizontal, additional mineral is producing little or no additional growth.</p>

<p>Another factor may now be limiting.</p>

<h3>Possible Decline</h3>

<p>If growth falls at very high concentrations, the concentration may be harmful under those conditions.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A graph shows:</p>

<p>0 mg/L → 3 cm growth</p>

<p>20 mg/L → 8 cm growth</p>

<p>40 mg/L → 12 cm growth</p>

<p>60 mg/L → 12 cm growth</p>

<p>80 mg/L → 9 cm growth</p>

<p><strong>What happens between 40 and 60 mg/L?</strong></p>

<p>Growth remains at approximately 12 cm.</p>

<p><strong>What happens at 80 mg/L?</strong></p>

<p>Growth decreases to 9 cm.</p>

<h3>Correct Conclusion</h3>

<p>Increasing the nutrient increased growth up to the range around 40 mg/L, after which growth plateaued and then decreased at the highest concentration tested.</p>

<h3>Important Rule</h3>

<p>Describe the pattern first. Explain the biological cause second.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_nutrition_graph_reasoning', 15),
  (1257, 1257, '
<h2>Plant Mineral Nutrition: Complete Exam Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student grows two groups of identical seedlings.</p>

<p>Both groups receive the same amount of water, light and carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>Group A receives a complete nutrient solution.</p>

<p>Group B receives a solution lacking nitrate ions.</p>

<p>After four weeks, Group B is smaller and has pale leaves.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify the Deficiency</h3>

<p>Group B lacks nitrate ions.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify What Nitrate Provides</h3>

<p>Nitrate provides nitrogen.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Identify the Biological Use</h3>

<p>Nitrogen is required to make amino acids and proteins.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Explain Reduced Growth</h3>

<p>Insufficient nitrogen limits production of proteins required for normal growth and development.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Explain the Pale Leaves</h3>

<p>Nitrate deficiency can cause leaves to become pale or yellow because nitrogen deficiency affects normal leaf development and chlorophyll-related processes.</p>

<h3>Step 6: Evaluate the Experiment</h3>

<p>The conclusion is stronger because light, water and carbon dioxide were kept the same between the groups.</p>

<p>However, the experiment would be more reliable if several seedlings were used in each group and their mean growth was calculated.</p>

<h3>Model Answer</h3>

<p>Without nitrate, the plants receive insufficient nitrogen. Nitrogen is needed to make amino acids and proteins, so protein production and normal growth are restricted. Nitrate deficiency can also cause pale or yellow leaves. Because other major growth conditions were controlled, the differences provide evidence that nitrate deficiency contributed to the observed effects.</p>

<h3>Exam Pattern</h3>

<p><strong>Identify nutrient → state its role → connect role to symptom → evaluate evidence.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_mineral_nutrition_complete_problem', 15),
  (1258, 1258, '
<h2>Mineral Ion Deficiency</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Plants require several mineral ions from the soil.
If an essential mineral ion is absent or present in insufficient quantity,
the plant may show deficiency symptoms and reduced growth.
</p>

<h3>NITRATE DEFICIENCY</h3>

<p>
Nitrate supplies nitrogen.
Nitrogen is needed to make amino acids, proteins and nucleic acids.
</p>

<p>
A plant suffering from nitrate deficiency may show:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>poor growth</li>
  <li>yellowing of older leaves</li>
</ul>

<p>
The yellowing is called <strong>chlorosis</strong>.
</p>

<h3>MAGNESIUM DEFICIENCY</h3>

<p>
Magnesium is required for chlorophyll production.
A shortage of magnesium can therefore reduce chlorophyll formation.
Leaves may become yellow while the veins remain relatively greener.
</p>

<h3>HOW TO REASON FROM A SYMPTOM</h3>

<p>
Do not simply memorise the symptom.
Connect the mineral to its biological function.
</p>

<p>
For nitrate:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Nitrate → nitrogen → amino acids/proteins → normal growth</strong>
</p>

<p>
For magnesium:
</p>

<p>
<strong>Magnesium → chlorophyll production → photosynthesis</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can nitrate deficiency cause poor plant growth?</p>

<p>
Nitrate supplies nitrogen. Nitrogen is required to make amino acids and
proteins. Proteins are needed for many cell processes and growth. Therefore,
insufficient nitrate can reduce protein production and limit growth.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Mineral Ion Deficiency', 15),
  (1259, 1259, '
<h2>Nitrate Deficiency</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A plant suffering from nitrate deficiency receives insufficient nitrogen for normal growth.</p>

<h3>What Does the Plant Need Nitrogen For?</h3>

<p>Nitrogen is required to make amino acids.</p>

<p>Amino acids are required to make proteins.</p>

<p>Proteins are needed for cell structure, enzymes and many other cellular functions.</p>

<h3>Symptoms</h3>

<p>Common symptoms include:</p>

<p><strong>1. Reduced growth</strong></p>

<p>The plant may remain smaller because protein production and other nitrogen-dependent processes are limited.</p>

<p><strong>2. Yellowing or pale leaves</strong></p>

<p>Chlorophyll formation and normal leaf development can be affected.</p>

<h3>Why Can Older Leaves Be Affected First?</h3>

<p>Nitrogen can be redistributed within a plant.</p>

<p>When nitrogen is limited, the plant can move some nitrogen-containing compounds from older tissues towards younger growing tissues.</p>

<p>Therefore, deficiency symptoms may become noticeable first in older leaves.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has pale older leaves and reduced growth while other growth conditions are adequate.</p>

<p><strong>Suggest a possible mineral deficiency.</strong></p>

<p>Nitrate deficiency is a possible explanation.</p>

<p><strong>Why?</strong></p>

<p>Nitrate provides nitrogen required for amino acids and proteins, and deficiency can cause reduced growth and leaf yellowing.</p>

<h3>Exam Caution</h3>

<p>A symptom alone does not always prove the exact deficiency.</p>

<p>Other factors can also affect plant colour and growth, so controlled comparison is needed to identify the cause confidently.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for nitrate_deficiency', 15),
  (1260, 1260, '
<h2>Magnesium Deficiency</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Magnesium deficiency occurs when a plant cannot obtain enough magnesium for normal chlorophyll production.</p>

<h3>Main Effect</h3>

<p>Reduced chlorophyll production can cause leaves to become pale or yellow.</p>

<p>This condition is called <strong>chlorosis</strong>.</p>

<h3>Effect on Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>Chlorophyll absorbs light energy.</p>

<p>If chlorophyll levels are reduced, the leaf may absorb less light energy and photosynthesis may decrease.</p>

<h3>Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Magnesium deficiency</strong></p>

<p>↓</p>

<p><strong>Less chlorophyll produced</strong></p>

<p>↓</p>

<p><strong>Leaves become pale/yellow</strong></p>

<p>↓</p>

<p><strong>Reduced capacity to absorb light</strong></p>

<p>↓</p>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis may decrease</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Plant A has sufficient magnesium. Plant B has a severe magnesium deficiency.</p>

<p>Both plants receive the same light intensity.</p>

<p>Plant B develops yellow leaves.</p>

<p><strong>Which plant is likely to have the greater capacity to absorb light for photosynthesis?</strong></p>

<p>Plant A, because sufficient magnesium supports normal chlorophyll production.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Do not say magnesium is the pigment that absorbs light.</p>

<p><strong>Chlorophyll</strong> is the pigment. Magnesium is required for its production.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for magnesium_deficiency', 15),
  (1261, 1261, '
<h2>Nitrate Deficiency Compared with Magnesium Deficiency</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Nitrate and magnesium are both essential mineral nutrients, but their main roles are different.</p>

<h3>Nitrate</h3>

<p>Nitrate provides nitrogen.</p>

<p>Nitrogen is required for amino acids and proteins.</p>

<p>Deficiency can cause reduced growth and yellowing or pale leaves.</p>

<h3>Magnesium</h3>

<p>Magnesium is required for chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>Deficiency can cause chlorosis and can reduce the plant''s capacity for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Do Not Confuse Them</h3>

<p>Both deficiencies can cause leaves to become yellow or pale.</p>

<p>Therefore, colour alone is not always enough to identify the deficiency.</p>

<p>The function of the mineral must also be considered.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Plant A has poor growth and pale leaves because it receives insufficient nitrate.</p>

<p>Plant B has pale leaves because it receives insufficient magnesium.</p>

<p><strong>What is the major difference in the underlying cause?</strong></p>

<p>Plant A lacks sufficient nitrogen for processes such as amino acid and protein production.</p>

<p>Plant B cannot produce chlorophyll normally because magnesium is required for chlorophyll production.</p>

<h3>Comparison</h3>

<p><strong>Nitrate → nitrogen → amino acids → proteins → growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Magnesium → chlorophyll production → light absorption → photosynthesis.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for comparing_nitrate_and_magnesium_deficiency', 15),
  (1262, 1262, '
<h2>Investigating Mineral Deficiency</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A controlled experiment can be used to investigate the effect of a mineral ion on plant growth.</p>

<h3>Example Question</h3>

<p><strong>Does nitrate availability affect plant growth?</strong></p>

<h3>Experimental Design</h3>

<p>Use plants of the same species and similar starting size.</p>

<p>Provide them with the same light, temperature, water and growing conditions.</p>

<p>Give the experimental groups different nitrate concentrations while keeping other mineral nutrients adequate.</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>The independent variable is the nitrate concentration supplied.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>The dependent variable could be plant height, increase in biomass or another measurable indicator of growth.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>• plant species</p>

<p>• starting size</p>

<p>• light intensity</p>

<p>• temperature</p>

<p>• water supply</p>

<p>• other mineral nutrients</p>

<p>• duration of experiment</p>

<h3>Repeats</h3>

<p>Several plants should be used in each treatment.</p>

<p>This reduces the influence of unusual individual plants.</p>

<p>The mean can then be calculated.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Five plants receiving sufficient nitrate grow an average of 12 cm. Five plants receiving very little nitrate grow an average of 5 cm.</p>

<p><strong>What can the data suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Under the conditions tested, low nitrate availability was associated with reduced growth.</p>

<h3>Important Scientific Rule</h3>

<p>The experiment supports a conclusion only for the conditions tested. Do not claim that nitrate is the only factor controlling plant growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_deficiency_controlled_investigation', 15),
  (1263, 1263, '
<h2>Identifying Mineral Deficiency from Evidence</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Mineral deficiencies can produce symptoms that help scientists identify what a plant may be lacking.</p>

<p>However, symptoms should be interpreted carefully.</p>

<h3>Nitrate Deficiency</h3>

<p>Nitrate provides nitrogen.</p>

<p>Insufficient nitrogen can lead to reduced growth and pale or yellow leaves.</p>

<h3>Magnesium Deficiency</h3>

<p>Magnesium is required for chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>Insufficient magnesium can cause chlorosis and may reduce photosynthetic capacity.</p>

<h3>Why Symptoms Alone Can Be Misleading</h3>

<p>Different deficiencies or environmental stresses can produce similar symptoms.</p>

<p>For example, yellowing leaves do not automatically prove that a plant lacks one particular mineral.</p>

<h3>Better Evidence</h3>

<p>A stronger investigation compares plants grown under controlled conditions while changing only the mineral being tested.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant develops yellow leaves.</p>

<p>A student immediately concludes that the plant has magnesium deficiency.</p>

<p><strong>Is this conclusion justified?</strong></p>

<p>Not by leaf colour alone.</p>

<p>The student needs further evidence or a controlled investigation because several factors can cause abnormal leaf colour.</p>

<h3>Scientific Reasoning</h3>

<p><strong>Observation ≠ automatic diagnosis.</strong></p>

<p>The symptom provides a clue. Controlled evidence provides stronger support for the conclusion.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_deficiency_identification', 15),
  (1264, 1264, '
<h2>Interpreting Data from a Mineral Deficiency Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Experimental data can be used to determine how mineral availability affects plant growth.</p>

<h3>Example Data</h3>

<p>Four groups of plants are supplied with different nitrate concentrations.</p>

<p><strong>Group A: 0 mg/L nitrate → mean growth = 2 cm</strong></p>

<p><strong>Group B: 25 mg/L nitrate → mean growth = 7 cm</strong></p>

<p><strong>Group C: 50 mg/L nitrate → mean growth = 11 cm</strong></p>

<p><strong>Group D: 100 mg/L nitrate → mean growth = 11 cm</strong></p>

<h3>What Does the Pattern Show?</h3>

<p>Growth increases as nitrate availability rises from 0 to 50 mg/L.</p>

<p>Increasing nitrate from 50 to 100 mg/L produces no further increase in mean growth in this experiment.</p>

<h3>Possible Explanation</h3>

<p>Nitrate was limiting growth at lower concentrations.</p>

<p>At 50 mg/L, another factor may have become limiting, so additional nitrate no longer increased growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Question</h3>

<p><strong>Which nitrate concentration produced the greatest mean growth?</strong></p>

<p>Both 50 mg/L and 100 mg/L produced 11 cm mean growth.</p>

<h3>Another Question</h3>

<p><strong>Does the data prove that 100 mg/L nitrate is better than 50 mg/L?</strong></p>

<p>No.</p>

<p>Both treatments produced the same mean growth in this experiment.</p>

<h3>Exam Skill</h3>

<p>Describe what the data actually shows before explaining why it may have happened.</p>

<p><strong>Data → pattern → biological explanation.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_deficiency_experiment_data', 15),
  (1265, 1265, '
<h2>How Mineral Deficiency Affects Plant Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth depends on the production of new cells and the synthesis of substances required by those cells.</p>

<p>Mineral ions support many of these processes.</p>

<h3>Nitrate Deficiency</h3>

<p>Low nitrate availability reduces the nitrogen available for amino acid and protein production.</p>

<p>This can restrict cell growth and development.</p>

<h3>Magnesium Deficiency</h3>

<p>Low magnesium availability can reduce chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>This can reduce the plant''s capacity to absorb light energy for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Why Growth Can Decrease</h3>

<p>When photosynthesis is reduced, less carbohydrate may be produced.</p>

<p>When protein synthesis is restricted, cells may also have difficulty carrying out normal growth and development.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant receiving adequate light, water and carbon dioxide grows slowly because nitrate is severely deficient.</p>

<p><strong>Which explanation is strongest?</strong></p>

<p>Low nitrate reduces nitrogen availability. This limits amino acid and protein production, interfering with normal growth.</p>

<h3>Do Not Overgeneralise</h3>

<p>Mineral deficiency is one possible cause of poor growth. Temperature, water availability, light, disease and other factors can also affect growth.</p>

<h3>Exam Structure</h3>

<p><strong>Mineral deficiency → specific biological role affected → cellular consequence → plant-level symptom.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_ion_deficiency_and_growth', 15),
  (1266, 1266, '
<h2>Plant Uptake of Nitrate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Plants need nitrogen to manufacture important nitrogen-containing substances,
including amino acids, proteins and nucleic acids.
</p>

<p>
Plants cannot normally take nitrogen directly from atmospheric nitrogen gas.
Instead, they obtain usable nitrogen compounds from the soil, especially
<strong>nitrate ions</strong>.
</p>

<h3>HOW NITRATE ENTERS A PLANT</h3>

<p>
Nitrate ions are dissolved in the soil water surrounding the roots.
Root cells absorb nitrate ions through transport proteins in their cell
membranes.
</p>

<p>
When nitrate concentration is lower in the soil than inside the root cells,
the plant may use <strong>active transport</strong> to absorb nitrate against
its concentration gradient.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Nitrates are present in the soil solution.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Nitrate ions reach the root surface.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Root cells absorb nitrate ions using membrane transport proteins.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The nitrogen is transported within the plant.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Nitrogen is incorporated into organic compounds such as amino acids.</p>

<h3>WHY PLANTS NEED NITROGEN</h3>

<p>
Nitrogen is needed to make amino acids. Amino acids are joined to form
proteins, which are required for growth, enzymes and cell structures.
</p>

<p>
Nitrogen is also present in nucleic acids such as DNA and RNA.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can a plant grow poorly when there is too little nitrate in the soil?</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> The plant needs nitrogen to make amino acids.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Amino acids are required to make proteins.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Proteins are needed for many cell processes and growth.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> If nitrate supply is too low, nitrogen availability can
limit protein production and therefore plant growth.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Plant Uptake of Nitrate', 15),
  (1267, 1267, '
<h2>Absorption of Mineral Ions by Roots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants obtain mineral ions mainly from the soil solution through their roots.</p>

<h3>Root Hair Cells</h3>

<p>Root hair cells are specialised cells on young roots.</p>

<p>Each root hair extends into the soil and provides a large surface area for absorption.</p>

<h3>Concentration Gradient</h3>

<p>Sometimes the concentration of a particular mineral ion is higher in the soil solution than inside the root cell.</p>

<p>The ion can then enter down its concentration gradient.</p>

<p>However, the concentration of an ion inside the root may sometimes already be higher than in the soil.</p>

<h3>Active Transport</h3>

<p>When mineral ions need to be moved against their concentration gradient, cells can use <strong>active transport</strong>.</p>

<p>Active transport requires energy released by respiration.</p>

<h3>Why Respiration Matters</h3>

<p>Root cells need energy to transport ions when passive movement is insufficient.</p>

<p>This means healthy root absorption depends partly on cellular respiration.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>The concentration of nitrate ions inside a root hair cell is greater than the concentration in the surrounding soil solution.</p>

<p><strong>Can nitrate still enter the root cell?</strong></p>

<p>Yes.</p>

<p>Active transport can move nitrate ions against the concentration gradient using energy from respiration.</p>

<h3>Key Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Mineral ion in soil → root hair cell → active transport when necessary → plant tissues.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_absorption_of_mineral_ions', 15),
  (1268, 1268, '
<h2>Movement of Mineral Ions Through a Plant</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Mineral ions absorbed by roots must be transported to the tissues where they are needed.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Absorption</h3>

<p>Root cells absorb mineral ions from the soil solution.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Movement Through Root Tissues</h3>

<p>Mineral ions move through root tissues towards the xylem.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Entry Into Xylem</h3>

<p>Mineral ions enter the xylem and become part of the water and mineral solution transported through the plant.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Transport to Shoots</h3>

<p>Xylem transports water and dissolved mineral ions from the roots towards stems and leaves.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Use by Cells</h3>

<p>Cells take up mineral ions and use them in processes such as protein production, chlorophyll production and other metabolic activities.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has healthy roots but damaged xylem.</p>

<p><strong>Why might mineral delivery to leaves decrease?</strong></p>

<p>Damage to xylem can interfere with transport of water and dissolved mineral ions from roots towards the leaves.</p>

<h3>Key Pathway</h3>

<p><strong>Soil solution → roots → xylem → stem → leaves and other tissues.</strong></p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Xylem transports mineral ions in solution, but mineral ions are not the same thing as organic assimilates transported by phloem.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_ion_movement_in_plant', 15),
  (1269, 1269, '
<h2>Mineral Ions and Concentration Gradients</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The direction in which a mineral ion moves depends partly on the concentration of that ion inside and outside the root cell.</p>

<h3>Down a Concentration Gradient</h3>

<p>If there is a higher concentration of an ion outside the root cell than inside, the ion may move into the cell down its concentration gradient.</p>

<p>This does not require energy from the cell.</p>

<h3>Against a Concentration Gradient</h3>

<p>If there is a lower concentration of an ion outside the root cell than inside, passive movement into the cell is not favoured.</p>

<p>The root cell can still absorb the ion using active transport.</p>

<h3>Active Transport</h3>

<p>Active transport uses energy released during respiration to move ions across cell membranes.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>The concentration of nitrate ions in the soil solution is 2 units. The concentration inside a root cell is 10 units.</p>

<p><strong>Can the root cell still absorb nitrate?</strong></p>

<p>Yes.</p>

<p>Because the concentration inside is already higher, active transport can move nitrate into the cell against its concentration gradient.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Whenever a question says that the concentration inside the root is higher than outside, think:</p>

<p><strong>Against gradient → active transport → energy required.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for mineral_ion_concentration_gradient', 15),
  (1270, 1270, '
<h2>Root Hair Cells and Mineral Absorption</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Root hair cells are specialised for absorbing water and mineral ions from the soil.</p>

<h3>Long Root Hair</h3>

<p>The root hair extends into the soil.</p>

<p>This increases the surface area available for contact with the soil solution.</p>

<h3>Thin Surface</h3>

<p>The thin outer surface provides a short pathway for substances entering the cell.</p>

<h3>Cell Membrane</h3>

<p>The cell membrane controls movement of substances into and out of the cell.</p>

<h3>Respiration</h3>

<p>Root hair cells contain mitochondria that release energy during respiration.</p>

<p>This energy can support active transport of mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant has very few healthy root hairs.</p>

<p><strong>How could this affect mineral uptake?</strong></p>

<p>The total surface area available for absorption may decrease, reducing the plant''s ability to obtain mineral ions from the soil.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Root hairs do not absorb mineral ions simply because they are "small."</p>

<p>Their large surface area and specialised membrane transport mechanisms are important.</p>

<h3>Structure → Function</h3>

<p><strong>Long extension → large surface area → greater contact with soil solution.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Mitochondria → respiration → energy for active transport.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_hair_cell_adaptation_mineral_absorption', 15),
  (1271, 1271, '
<h2>Waterlogging and Mineral Absorption</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Roots need oxygen for aerobic respiration.</p>

<p>Respiration releases energy needed for active transport of mineral ions.</p>

<h3>What Happens in Waterlogged Soil?</h3>

<p>When soil becomes saturated with water, many of the air spaces between soil particles become filled with water.</p>

<p>This can reduce the movement and availability of oxygen around the roots.</p>

<h3>Effect on Respiration</h3>

<p>Reduced oxygen availability can reduce aerobic respiration in root cells.</p>

<h3>Effect on Active Transport</h3>

<p>Less respiration can mean less energy is available for active transport.</p>

<p>Mineral ion uptake may therefore be reduced.</p>

<h3>Effect on the Plant</h3>

<p>Reduced mineral uptake can contribute to poor growth and deficiency symptoms if the condition persists.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A farmer notices that plants growing in waterlogged soil grow poorly even though the soil contains sufficient mineral ions.</p>

<p><strong>Explain one possible biological reason.</strong></p>

<p>Waterlogging can reduce oxygen availability to roots. This can reduce aerobic respiration and the energy available for active transport of mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Complete Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Waterlogging → less oxygen around roots → less aerobic respiration → less energy for active transport → reduced mineral ion uptake.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for waterlogging_and_mineral_absorption', 15),
  (1272, 1272, '
<h2>Investigating Mineral Requirements Using Hydroponics</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Hydroponics</strong> is a method of growing plants without ordinary soil, using a nutrient solution that supplies water and required mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Why Is This Useful?</h3>

<p>Soil contains many substances that can make it difficult to control exactly which mineral ions are available.</p>

<p>A nutrient solution allows the researcher to control the mineral composition more precisely.</p>

<h3>Testing a Mineral</h3>

<p>To investigate whether a particular mineral is required, plants can be supplied with complete nutrient solutions or solutions lacking the mineral being tested.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>One group receives a complete nutrient solution.</p>

<p>A second group receives the same solution but without magnesium.</p>

<p>If the magnesium-deficient plants develop chlorosis while the control plants remain healthy, this provides evidence that magnesium is required for normal plant development and chlorophyll production.</p>

<h3>Fair Test</h3>

<p>The groups should receive the same:</p>

<p>• plant species</p>

<p>• light conditions</p>

<p>• temperature</p>

<p>• water availability</p>

<p>• growing period</p>

<p>• other essential nutrients</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student wants to test whether magnesium is required for healthy leaves.</p>

<p><strong>What should differ between the experimental and control nutrient solutions?</strong></p>

<p>The presence or absence of magnesium should be the deliberate difference while the other essential nutrients are kept adequate.</p>

<h3>Why a Control Is Essential</h3>

<p>Without a control group receiving adequate magnesium, it would be harder to determine whether leaf changes were caused by magnesium deficiency or another factor.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for hydroponics_and_mineral_nutrition', 15),
  (1273, 1273, '
<h2>Plant Growth and Development</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth and plant development are related but different processes.</p>

<h3>Growth</h3>

<p>Growth is a permanent increase in the size and dry mass of an organism or one of its parts.</p>

<p>In plants, growth occurs mainly through cell division followed by cell enlargement and differentiation.</p>

<h3>Cell Division</h3>

<p>Cells produced by mitosis increase the number of cells in a growing region.</p>

<h3>Cell Enlargement</h3>

<p>Young plant cells can increase in size as they take in water and develop larger vacuoles.</p>

<h3>Cell Differentiation</h3>

<p>Cells can become specialised for particular functions.</p>

<p>For example, cells in developing roots can become specialised into root hair cells.</p>

<h3>Development</h3>

<p>Development refers to the changes that occur as a plant becomes more mature and its structures and functions become increasingly specialised.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling becomes taller and develops larger leaves.</p>

<p><strong>Which process is being observed?</strong></p>

<p>Growth is occurring because the plant is increasing in size.</p>

<p>If cells are also becoming specialised into different tissues, development is occurring as well.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Growth = permanent increase in size or dry mass.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Development = changes in structure, function and organisation as the plant matures.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_and_development', 15),
  (1274, 1274, '
<h2>Primary Growth in Plants</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Primary growth is an increase in the length of roots and shoots.</p>

<p>It allows a young plant to extend its roots through the soil and its shoots towards light.</p>

<h3>Where Primary Growth Occurs</h3>

<p>Primary growth occurs at regions containing actively dividing cells called meristems.</p>

<p>In roots, the root apical meristem is near the root tip.</p>

<p>In shoots, the shoot apical meristem is found at the growing tip.</p>

<h3>Cell Division</h3>

<p>Cells near the meristem divide by mitosis.</p>

<p>This increases cell number.</p>

<h3>Cell Elongation</h3>

<p>New cells can then enlarge, increasing the length of the growing organ.</p>

<h3>Cell Differentiation</h3>

<p>Some cells become specialised as they mature.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A young root increases from 5 cm to 8 cm.</p>

<p><strong>What type of growth has occurred?</strong></p>

<p>Primary growth, because the root has increased in length.</p>

<h3>Complete Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Meristematic cell division → more cells → cell elongation → increased length → primary growth.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for primary_growth_in_plants', 15),
  (1275, 1275, '
<h2>Interpreting Plant Growth Hormone Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant hormone experiments often produce numerical data that must be interpreted carefully.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>A researcher measures the mean root growth at different auxin concentrations.</p>

<p>0 mg/L → 5 mm</p>

<p>1 mg/L → 8 mm</p>

<p>2 mg/L → 11 mm</p>

<p>4 mg/L → 7 mm</p>

<p>8 mg/L → 3 mm</p>

<h3>Step 1: Describe the Data</h3>

<p>Root growth increases from 0 to 2 mg/L.</p>

<p>It reaches the greatest measured value at 2 mg/L.</p>

<p>Growth then decreases as concentration increases further.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify the Pattern</h3>

<p>The response is concentration-dependent.</p>

<p>Low-to-moderate concentration increases growth under these conditions, while higher concentrations inhibit growth.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Compare With the Control</h3>

<p>The control is 0 mg/L, where mean growth is 5 mm.</p>

<p>At 2 mg/L, mean growth is 11 mm.</p>

<p>Therefore, growth is greater at 2 mg/L than in the untreated group.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Avoid Overclaiming</h3>

<p>The data does not prove that 2 mg/L is the universal optimum concentration for all plants.</p>

<p>It is the concentration giving the greatest measured response in this experiment.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_data_interpretation', 15),
  (1276, 1276, '
<h2>Root Cap and Protection of the Growing Root</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The root tip is protected by a structure called the root cap.</p>

<p>The root cap covers the delicate growing tissues at the tip of the root.</p>

<h3>Why Protection Is Necessary</h3>

<p>Roots push through soil.</p>

<p>Soil particles can create mechanical resistance and damage delicate cells.</p>

<p>The root cap helps protect the actively growing region as the root advances through the soil.</p>

<h3>Mucilage</h3>

<p>Root cap cells can release mucilage, which helps reduce friction between the root and surrounding soil particles.</p>

<h3>Root Cap Cells</h3>

<p>Root cap cells are continually replaced because cells at the outer surface can become damaged or shed as the root grows through soil.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root is growing through compact soil.</p>

<p><strong>Why is the root cap important?</strong></p>

<p>It helps protect the delicate growing tissues at the root tip from mechanical damage as the root moves through the soil.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The root cap protects the growing tip.</p>

<p>It is not the main region responsible for producing new root cells.</p>

<p>The root apical meristem performs that role.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_cap_and_root_growth', 15),
  (1277, 1277, '
<h2>Measuring Primary Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Primary growth can be measured by recording changes in the length of a root or shoot over time.</p>

<h3>Growth Rate</h3>

<p>A simple growth rate can be calculated using:</p>

<p><strong>Growth rate = change in length ÷ time</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root is 4 cm long at the beginning of an investigation.</p>

<p>After 5 days, it is 9 cm long.</p>

<p>Change in length:</p>

<p>9 − 4 = 5 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>5 ÷ 5 = 1 cm per day</p>

<h3>Units</h3>

<p>If length is measured in centimetres and time in days, growth rate is expressed in cm/day.</p>

<h3>Why Repeated Measurements Help</h3>

<p>Measuring the same plant repeatedly allows the researcher to see how growth changes over time.</p>

<p>Several plants can also be measured to calculate a mean growth rate.</p>

<h3>Worked Comparison</h3>

<p>Plant A grows 6 cm in 3 days.</p>

<p>Plant B grows 8 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>Plant A:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day</p>

<p>Plant B:</p>

<p>8 ÷ 4 = 2 cm/day</p>

<p>Therefore, the two plants have the same average growth rate even though their total growth differs.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for measuring_primary_growth', 15),
  (1278, 1278, '
<h2>Investigating Plant Growth Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An investigation can test how a factor affects the growth rate of a plant.</p>

<h3>Example Question</h3>

<p>Does temperature affect the growth rate of seedlings?</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>Temperature.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>Growth rate, measured from changes in root or shoot length over time.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>Keep the plant species, starting size, water supply, light conditions, nutrient supply and measurement period the same where appropriate.</p>

<h3>Method</h3>

<p>Place similar seedlings at different controlled temperatures.</p>

<p>Measure their length at regular intervals.</p>

<p>Calculate the growth rate.</p>

<p>Repeat using several seedlings at each temperature.</p>

<h3>Why Starting Size Matters</h3>

<p>If one group begins with much larger plants, differences in final length may be caused partly by the starting size rather than temperature.</p>

<h3>Why Repeats Matter</h3>

<p>Individual plants naturally vary.</p>

<p>Repeating measurements allows a mean response to be calculated.</p>

<h3>Conclusion</h3>

<p>Compare the measured growth rates rather than simply comparing final lengths.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for investigating_plant_growth_rate', 15),
  (1279, 1279, '
<h2>Interpreting a Plant Growth Curve</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth can change in rate as a plant develops.</p>

<p>A graph of length or mass against time can reveal different phases of growth.</p>

<h3>Early Stage</h3>

<p>Growth may initially be slow while the seedling establishes its roots and shoots.</p>

<h3>Rapid Growth</h3>

<p>During a period of rapid growth, the gradient of the graph becomes steeper.</p>

<p>A steeper gradient means a greater growth rate.</p>

<h3>Slowing Growth</h3>

<p>Later, growth may slow.</p>

<p>The graph becomes less steep.</p>

<h3>Important Graph Rule</h3>

<p>Do not judge growth rate only by the height of the graph.</p>

<p>Growth rate is represented by the <strong>gradient</strong>.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Plant A is 20 cm tall after 10 days.</p>

<p>Plant B is 15 cm tall after 10 days.</p>

<p>Can you conclude that Plant A had the greater growth rate?</p>

<p>Not necessarily.</p>

<p>You need information about their starting lengths or the gradients of their growth curves.</p>

<h3>Exam Skill</h3>

<p><strong>Height/length → amount of growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Gradient → rate of growth.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_curve', 15),
  (1280, 1280, '
<h2>Evaluating a Plant Growth Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A good biological experiment should produce evidence that allows a fair conclusion.</p>

<h3>Reliability</h3>

<p>Reliability can be improved by using repeats, several organisms and calculating means.</p>

<h3>Validity</h3>

<p>An investigation is valid when its design actually tests the question being asked.</p>

<p>For example, if the question is about temperature, other major growth conditions should not be changed systematically between groups.</p>

<h3>Control Group</h3>

<p>A control group provides a comparison against the experimental treatment.</p>

<h3>Standardisation</h3>

<p>Plants should be similar in species, age and starting size where possible.</p>

<h3>Measurement</h3>

<p>Use a consistent measurement method.</p>

<p>For example, measure root length from the same reference point each time.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student tests fertiliser concentration using one small plant at each concentration.</p>

<p>The plants are different species and receive different amounts of water.</p>

<p><strong>Give two weaknesses.</strong></p>

<p>First, using different species introduces biological variation.</p>

<p>Second, changing water supply creates another variable that could affect growth.</p>

<h3>Improvement</h3>

<p>Use several similar plants and keep water supply constant.</p>

<h3>Core Rule</h3>

<p>A good conclusion must be supported by a fair comparison.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_experiment_evaluation', 15),
  (1281, 1281, '
<h2>Plant Growth: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student investigates two groups of seedlings.</p>

<p>Group A is placed under suitable growing conditions.</p>

<p>Group B receives the same conditions except that the shoot tips are removed at the beginning of the experiment.</p>

<p>After two weeks, Group A has longer main shoots than Group B.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify the Relevant Structure</h3>

<p>The shoot tip contains the shoot apical meristem and young growing tissues.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify the Growth Process</h3>

<p>The shoot apical meristem produces new cells by mitosis.</p>

<p>These cells can later elongate and differentiate.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Explain the Difference</h3>

<p>Removing the shoot tip reduces the actively growing tissue available for normal primary shoot growth.</p>

<p>Therefore, the main shoot may show reduced extension.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Consider the Experiment</h3>

<p>If the two groups are otherwise treated identically, the difference in main shoot growth provides evidence that removing the shoot tip affected growth.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Avoid Overclaiming</h3>

<p>The experiment does not prove that the shoot tip is the only region capable of growth.</p>

<p>Other tissues and lateral meristems can also contribute to plant growth.</p>

<h3>Complete Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Shoot tip contains apical meristem → mitosis produces new cells → cells elongate and differentiate → primary shoot growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Remove shoot tip → reduced production of new cells in that growing region → reduced main shoot extension.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_complete_reasoning', 15),
  (1282, 1282, '
<h2>Comparing Plant Growth Using Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth experiments can measure either the amount of growth or the rate of growth.</p>

<h3>Amount of Growth</h3>

<p><strong>Change in length = final length − initial length.</strong></p>

<h3>Growth Rate</h3>

<p><strong>Growth rate = change in length ÷ time.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Plant A starts at 5 cm and ends at 15 cm after 5 days.</p>

<p>Change in length:</p>

<p>15 − 5 = 10 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>10 ÷ 5 = 2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Comparison</h3>

<p>Plant B starts at 10 cm and reaches 18 cm after 4 days.</p>

<p>Change in length:</p>

<p>18 − 10 = 8 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>8 ÷ 4 = 2 cm/day</p>

<p>Plant A has greater total growth, but both plants have the same average growth rate.</p>

<h3>Why This Matters</h3>

<p>A question may ask for total growth or growth rate.</p>

<p>Do not answer one when the question asks for the other.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_measurement_comparison', 15),
  (1283, 1283, '
<h2>Plant Growth Graph Analysis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Graphs allow plant growth to be compared across time or between treatments.</p>

<h3>Reading the Axes</h3>

<p>First identify what is plotted on each axis.</p>

<p>For a typical growth curve, time is placed on the horizontal axis and plant length or mass on the vertical axis.</p>

<h3>Gradient</h3>

<p>The gradient represents growth rate when growth is plotted against time.</p>

<p>A steep gradient means rapid growth.</p>

<p>A shallow gradient means slower growth.</p>

<h3>Plateau</h3>

<p>A horizontal section indicates little or no increase in the measured quantity during that period.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant''s length changes as follows:</p>

<p>Day 0 → 4 cm</p>

<p>Day 2 → 8 cm</p>

<p>Day 4 → 14 cm</p>

<p>Day 6 → 20 cm</p>

<p>Day 8 → 22 cm</p>

<p>Growth was faster between days 2 and 6 than between days 6 and 8.</p>

<p>The later section has a smaller gradient.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>When describing a graph, give the data pattern before giving a biological explanation.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<p><strong>"Growth increased rapidly between days 2 and 6, then slowed between days 6 and 8."</strong></p>

<p>Only after that should you discuss possible biological reasons.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_graph_analysis', 15),
  (1284, 1284, '
<h2>Growth by Cell Division</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth requires an increase in the number of cells as well as an increase in the size of existing cells.</p>

<p>Cell division is especially important in meristematic regions because these regions contain cells that can divide repeatedly by mitosis.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Cell Division</h3>

<p>A meristematic cell divides by mitosis.</p>

<p>One parent cell produces two genetically similar daughter cells.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Increase in Cell Number</h3>

<p>Repeated mitotic divisions increase the number of cells in the growing region.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Further Growth</h3>

<p>The newly produced cells can later increase in size and become specialised.</p>

<p>Therefore, cell division is only one part of plant growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root tip contains actively dividing cells.</p>

<p>If one cell divides once:</p>

<p>1 cell → 2 cells</p>

<p>If both daughter cells then divide:</p>

<p>2 cells → 4 cells</p>

<p>After another round:</p>

<p>4 cells → 8 cells</p>

<p>Repeated mitosis therefore rapidly increases cell number.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p><strong>Mitosis increases cell number.</strong></p>

<p>Growth also requires enlargement and differentiation of cells.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for growth_by_cell_division', 15),
  (1285, 1285, '
<h2>Cell Elongation in Plant Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>New cells produced by mitosis do not immediately produce the full length of a growing root or shoot.</p>

<p>Many newly formed cells increase in size through cell elongation.</p>

<h3>What Is Cell Elongation?</h3>

<p>Cell elongation is an increase in the length of a plant cell.</p>

<p>Water enters the cell, increasing its volume and contributing to expansion.</p>

<p>The cell wall provides support while allowing controlled expansion.</p>

<h3>Why Elongation Matters</h3>

<p>When many cells elongate, the tissue becomes longer.</p>

<p>This contributes directly to primary growth of roots and shoots.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Suppose a growing region contains 100 newly formed cells.</p>

<p>If those cells become longer, the total length of the tissue can increase even if no additional cell division occurs during that particular stage.</p>

<h3>Growth Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Cell division → cell elongation → cell differentiation.</strong></p>

<p>These processes are related, but they are not the same process.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not say that plant growth is caused only by mitosis.</p>

<p>Mitosis increases cell number, while cell enlargement contributes to an increase in size.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for cell_elongation_in_plant_growth', 15),
  (1286, 1286, '
<h2>Cell Differentiation During Plant Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Cells produced by cell division can become specialised for different functions.</p>

<p>This process is called differentiation.</p>

<h3>What Happens During Differentiation?</h3>

<p>A newly produced cell develops structures and characteristics that allow it to perform a particular function.</p>

<p>For example, some cells become specialised for transport, absorption or protection.</p>

<h3>Examples in Plants</h3>

<p>A cell may differentiate into a root hair cell, which is specialised for absorption.</p>

<p>Another cell may become part of xylem tissue, which is specialised for water and mineral transport.</p>

<h3>Why Differentiation Is Important</h3>

<p>A plant cannot function efficiently if every cell performs exactly the same role.</p>

<p>Specialised cells allow different parts of the plant to perform different functions.</p>

<h3>Growth Sequence</h3>

<p>Cells produced by meristematic division can:</p>

<p>1. Increase in number through mitosis.</p>

<p>2. Increase in size through enlargement.</p>

<p>3. Become specialised through differentiation.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Division → more cells.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Elongation → larger cells.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Differentiation → specialised cells.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for cell_differentiation_in_plant_growth', 15),
  (1287, 1287, '
<h2>Root Hair Cells and Differentiation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Root hair cells are specialised cells found near the growing regions of roots.</p>

<p>They develop through differentiation.</p>

<h3>Why Root Hair Cells Are Specialised</h3>

<p>Root hair cells absorb water and mineral ions from the soil.</p>

<p>Their long extension greatly increases the surface area available for absorption.</p>

<h3>How Their Structure Supports Function</h3>

<p><strong>Long hair-like extension:</strong> increases surface area in contact with soil.</p>

<p><strong>Thin surface:</strong> provides a short pathway for water movement.</p>

<p><strong>Cell membrane:</strong> controls movement of substances into the cell.</p>

<p><strong>Mitochondria:</strong> provide energy needed for active transport of mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Link to Differentiation</h3>

<p>The root hair cell does not simply become larger.</p>

<p>It develops a specialised structure that allows it to perform a particular function.</p>

<p>That is differentiation.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>If a question asks why a cell has a specialised structure, connect the structure directly to its function.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_hairs_and_differentiation', 15),
  (1288, 1288, '
<h2>Plant Growth Processes: Complete Exam Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student examines cells at different positions behind a root tip.</p>

<p>Region A contains many cells undergoing mitosis.</p>

<p>Region B contains cells that are increasing rapidly in length.</p>

<p>Region C contains cells developing specialised structures, including root hairs.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify Region A</h3>

<p>The main process is cell division.</p>

<p>Therefore, Region A is the zone of cell division.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify Region B</h3>

<p>The cells are increasing in length.</p>

<p>Therefore, Region B is the zone of elongation.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Identify Region C</h3>

<p>The cells are developing specialised structures.</p>

<p>Therefore, Region C is the zone of differentiation.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Explain How They Work Together</h3>

<p>Cell division supplies new cells.</p>

<p>Cell elongation increases the size of those cells.</p>

<p>Cell differentiation produces specialised cells that perform particular functions.</p>

<h3>Model Answer</h3>

<p>Cells near the root tip divide by mitosis, increasing cell number. Further from the tip, newly produced cells elongate, increasing their size and contributing to root extension. Further still, cells differentiate and become specialised for functions such as absorption. These processes together allow the root to grow and develop specialised tissues.</p>

<h3>High-Value Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Division = more cells.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Elongation = larger cells.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Differentiation = specialised cells.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_processes_exam_problem', 15),
  (1289, 1289, '
<h2>From Stored Seed Reserves to Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A germinating seed initially depends on food reserves stored in the seed.</p>

<p>These reserves provide substrates for respiration and materials needed for early growth.</p>

<h3>Before Leaves Become Fully Photosynthetic</h3>

<p>The embryo cannot yet depend mainly on photosynthesis because the developing shoot may not have functional green leaves exposed to light.</p>

<p>Stored carbohydrates, proteins and lipids can therefore be mobilised and used by the developing seedling.</p>

<h3>Respiration Uses the Reserves</h3>

<p>Stored organic substances are broken down during respiration.</p>

<p>The released energy supports processes such as cell division, cell enlargement and active transport.</p>

<h3>After Leaves Develop</h3>

<p>Green leaves exposed to light can carry out photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Photosynthesis produces glucose from carbon dioxide and water.</p>

<p>The glucose can be used in respiration or converted into substances needed for growth and storage.</p>

<h3>The Transition</h3>

<p><strong>Stored reserves → respiration and early growth → leaves develop → photosynthesis increases → seedling becomes increasingly self-sufficient.</strong></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis does not replace respiration.</p>

<p>A growing plant continues to respire even after it begins photosynthesising.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for stored_reserves_to_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1290, 1290, '
<h2>Root Cap and Protection of the Growing Root</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The root cap is a protective structure covering the very tip of a growing root.</p>

<h3>Why Protection Is Necessary</h3>

<p>The root tip pushes through soil as the root grows.</p>

<p>This can expose the delicate growing tissues to abrasion and mechanical damage.</p>

<h3>Function of the Root Cap</h3>

<p>The root cap helps protect the growing root tip.</p>

<p>It also produces substances that can help reduce friction as the root moves through soil.</p>

<h3>Relationship to the Meristem</h3>

<p>The root apical meristem lies behind the root cap.</p>

<p>The meristem contains actively dividing cells that contribute to root growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>If the protective root cap is damaged, the delicate tissues behind it can become more exposed to mechanical damage as the root grows through soil.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Root cap → protection.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Root apical meristem → production of new cells.</strong></p>

<p>Do not identify the root cap as the main cell-producing region.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_cap_function', 15),
  (1291, 1291, '
<h2>Plant Growth Processes: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth involves several linked cellular processes rather than one single event.</p>

<h3>Stage 1: Cell Division</h3>

<p>Meristematic cells divide by mitosis.</p>

<p>This increases cell number.</p>

<h3>Stage 2: Cell Elongation</h3>

<p>Many newly produced cells increase in size and length.</p>

<p>Water uptake and expansion of the cell and vacuole contribute to this process.</p>

<h3>Stage 3: Cell Differentiation</h3>

<p>Cells develop specialised structures and functions.</p>

<p>Examples include root hair cells, xylem cells and phloem cells.</p>

<h3>Worked Exam Problem</h3>

<p>A student observes that the root of a seedling becomes longer over several days.</p>

<p>The student asks how cells contribute to this increase in length.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Cells in the root apical meristem divide by mitosis.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> New cells are produced.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Many of these cells elongate.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Cells differentiate into specialised tissues.</p>

<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Root growth results from coordinated cell division, cell elongation and differentiation.</p>

<h3>Critical Distinction</h3>

<p>Cell division increases the number of cells.</p>

<p>Cell elongation increases cell size.</p>

<p>Differentiation produces specialised cells.</p>

<p>These processes work together to produce a functional growing plant.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_processes_complete', 15),
  (1292, 1292, '
<h2>Calculating Plant Growth Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth can be measured by recording a change in length over a known period.</p>

<h3>Formula</h3>

<p><strong>Growth rate = change in length ÷ time taken</strong></p>

<p>First calculate the change:</p>

<p><strong>Change in length = final length − initial length</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>A root is initially 2 cm long and becomes 8 cm long after 3 days.</p>

<p>Change in length:</p>

<p>8 − 2 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>A shoot increases from 5 cm to 11 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>Change:</p>

<p>11 − 5 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 cm/day</p>

<h3>Why Rate Is Useful</h3>

<p>Two plants may increase by the same amount but take different amounts of time.</p>

<p>The faster plant has the greater growth rate.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Always subtract the initial measurement before dividing by time.</p>
  
<hr>

