<h2>Using the Gradient of a Growth Graph</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A graph can show how plant length changes with time.</p>

<p>The gradient of a length-time graph represents the rate of growth.</p>

<h3>Formula</h3>

<p><strong>Gradient = change in length ÷ change in time</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling is 2 cm long at Day 1 and 8 cm long at Day 4.</p>

<p>Change in length:</p>

<p>8 − 2 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Change in time:</p>

<p>4 − 1 = 3 days</p>

<p>Gradient:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Interpreting the Graph</h3>

<p>A steep positive gradient means the plant is growing rapidly.</p>

<p>A smaller positive gradient means the plant is still growing but more slowly.</p>

<p>A horizontal section means there is no increase in the measured length during that interval.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The height of a point tells you the plant''s measured length at that time.</p>

<p>The gradient tells you how quickly the length is changing.</p>
  
<hr>

