<h2>Variables in a Plant Growth Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A well-designed plant growth experiment requires clear identification of variables.</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>The independent variable is the factor deliberately changed by the investigator.</p>

<p>Example: light intensity.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>The dependent variable is what is measured.</p>

<p>Example: growth rate in cm/day.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>These are factors kept constant so that they do not provide alternative explanations for the result.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>• plant species</p>

<p>• starting size</p>

<p>• water supply</p>

<p>• mineral supply</p>

<p>• temperature</p>

<p>• duration</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates how temperature affects seedling growth.</p>

<p><strong>Independent:</strong> temperature.</p>

<p><strong>Dependent:</strong> growth rate.</p>

<p><strong>Controls:</strong> plant species, starting size, water, light, minerals and duration.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>"Growth rate" is not the independent variable if the student is measuring it.</p>

<p>It is the dependent variable.</p>
  
<hr>

