  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_anomalous_result', 15),
  (1299, 1299, '
<h2>Drawing a Conclusion from Plant Growth Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A scientific conclusion should be based directly on the evidence collected.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates temperature and seedling growth.</p>

<p>Mean growth rates are:</p>

<p>15°C → 0.8 cm/day</p>

<p>25°C → 1.6 cm/day</p>

<p>35°C → 1.2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify the Pattern</h3>

<p>Growth rate increases from 15°C to 25°C.</p>

<p>Growth rate then decreases at 35°C.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify the Highest Tested Value</h3>

<p>25°C gives the highest measured growth rate.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Write the Conclusion</h3>

<p>Under the conditions tested, seedling growth rate was highest at 25°C.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Avoid Overclaiming</h3>

<p>It would be too strong to say that 25°C is the optimum temperature for all plants.</p>

<p>The evidence only supports the tested species and experimental conditions.</p>

<h3>Exam Structure</h3>

<p><strong>State the pattern → quote relevant data → give a limited conclusion.</strong></p>
  
<hr>

