  (1301, 1301, '
<h2>Annual Growth Rings</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>In many woody plants, changes in growth conditions during different parts of the year can produce visible differences in the wood.</p>

<p>These differences can form growth rings.</p>

<h3>How Do Rings Form?</h3>

<p>When conditions favour rapid growth, the vascular cambium can produce wood with characteristics different from wood produced during slower growth.</p>

<p>Repeated changes in growth rate can therefore produce alternating bands in the wood.</p>

<h3>Reading a Growth Ring</h3>

<p>A sequence of rings can provide evidence about past patterns of growth.</p>

<p>However, a ring should not automatically be interpreted as proof of exactly one calendar year because growth patterns depend on the plant species and environmental conditions.</p>

<h3>Environmental Conditions</h3>

<p>Growth can be affected by factors such as:</p>

<p>• water availability</p>

<p>• temperature</p>

<p>• nutrient availability</p>

<p>• damage</p>

<h3>Exam Reasoning</h3>

<p>If one period produced a narrow ring while another produced a wider ring, the difference may indicate different growth conditions.</p>

<p>The ring width alone does not identify the exact cause.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for annual_growth_rings', 15),
  (1302, 1302, '
<h2>Conditions Necessary for Seed Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination is the process by which a seed resumes growth and the embryo develops into a seedling.</p>

<p>Three major conditions are required for successful germination of most seeds:</p>

<p><strong>Water, oxygen and a suitable temperature.</strong></p>

<h3>Water</h3>

<p>The seed absorbs water.</p>

<p>This causes the tissues of the seed to rehydrate and activates metabolic processes needed for growth.</p>

<h3>Oxygen</h3>

<p>The embryo needs energy for growth.</p>

<p>Oxygen allows aerobic respiration to release energy from stored food.</p>

<h3>Suitable Temperature</h3>

<p>Germination depends on enzyme-controlled metabolic reactions.</p>

<p>At a suitable temperature, these reactions occur at an appropriate rate.</p>

<h3>Why Too Much Water Can Be a Problem</h3>

<p>Water itself is necessary, but waterlogged conditions can reduce the availability of oxygen around the seed.</p>

<p>Without enough oxygen, aerobic respiration can be restricted.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three groups of seeds are placed under different conditions.</p>

<p>Group A receives water, oxygen and suitable warmth.</p>

<p>Group B receives water but is kept in oxygen-poor conditions.</p>

<p>Group C receives water and oxygen but is kept at a very low temperature.</p>

<p><strong>Which group should show the best germination?</strong></p>

<p>Group A, because all three essential conditions are provided.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Water → rehydration and metabolic activation.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Oxygen → aerobic respiration → energy.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Suitable temperature → appropriate enzyme activity.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for conditions_for_seed_germination', 15),
  (1303, 1303, '
<h2>Investigating Conditions Required for Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A germination experiment should change one condition while keeping other relevant conditions the same.</p>

<h3>Testing Water</h3>

<p>Place seeds in suitable conditions but prevent one group from receiving water.</p>

<p>Keep oxygen and temperature suitable.</p>

<p>Compare germination with a watered control group.</p>

<h3>Testing Oxygen</h3>

<p>Provide water and suitable temperature but reduce oxygen availability in the experimental group.</p>

<h3>Testing Temperature</h3>

<p>Provide water and oxygen but place groups at different temperatures.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>The type and number of seeds, amount of water, oxygen availability, temperature and observation period should be controlled where appropriate.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>The dependent variable could be the percentage of seeds that germinate after a fixed period.</p>

<h3>Percentage Germination</h3>

<p><strong>Percentage germination = number of seeds germinated ÷ total number of seeds × 100</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>20 seeds are tested. After five days, 15 have germinated.</p>

<p>Percentage germination:</p>

<p>15 ÷ 20 × 100 = 75%</p>

<h3>Improving Reliability</h3>

<p>Use several seeds and repeat the experiment.</p>

<p>Calculate a mean percentage germination where appropriate.</p>

<h3>Fair Test</h3>

<p>If testing temperature, do not simultaneously change the amount of water or oxygen.</p>

<p>Otherwise, a difference in germination could have more than one possible cause.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for investigating_seed_germination', 15),
  (1304, 1304, '
<h2>Seed Dormancy</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Seed dormancy is a period during which a viable seed does not germinate even when some apparently suitable conditions are present.</p>

<p>Dormancy can help seeds survive periods when immediate germination would be disadvantageous.</p>

<h3>Why Dormancy Matters</h3>

<p>If seeds germinated immediately after formation, seedlings could emerge during unsuitable environmental conditions.</p>

<p>Dormancy can delay germination until conditions become more favourable.</p>

<h3>Causes</h3>

<p>Different species have different dormancy mechanisms.</p>

<p>These can include a hard or impermeable seed coat, chemical inhibitors, or an embryo that requires further development before germination.</p>

<h3>Breaking Dormancy</h3>

<p>Different seeds require different treatments.</p>

<p>Some hard-coated seeds may require scarification, which damages or weakens the seed coat enough to allow water to enter.</p>

<p>Some seeds require specific temperature conditions before they germinate.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A batch of viable seeds receives water, oxygen and a suitable temperature but does not germinate.</p>

<p><strong>Can you immediately conclude that water, oxygen or temperature is absent?</strong></p>

<p>No.</p>

<p>The seeds may be dormant.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Failure to germinate does not automatically mean the seed is dead.</strong></p>

<p>A viable dormant seed can remain alive while delaying germination.</p>
  
<hr>

<h2>Seed Dormancy</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Seed dormancy is a period during which a viable seed does not germinate even though it may appear to have the potential to grow.</p>

<h3>Why Dormancy Occurs</h3>

<p>Dormancy can prevent a seed from germinating when environmental conditions are temporarily unsuitable.</p>

<p>This can help ensure that germination occurs when conditions are more favourable for survival.</p>

<h3>Dormancy Is Not the Same as Death</h3>

<p>A dormant seed is alive but metabolically less active than a growing seedling.</p>

<p>A dead seed cannot germinate.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>A viable seed remains inactive during an unfavourable period.</p>

<p>When suitable conditions are restored and dormancy is broken, the seed may begin germination.</p>

<h3>Breaking Dormancy</h3>

<p>Different plant species have different dormancy mechanisms.</p>

<p>Some seeds require particular environmental changes before germination can occur.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Do not state that every seed immediately germinates whenever water, oxygen and a suitable temperature are available.</p>

<p>Some viable seeds have additional dormancy requirements.</p>
  
<hr>

<h2>Seed Dormancy</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Seed dormancy is a temporary state in which a viable seed does not germinate even when some favourable conditions are present.</p>

<h3>Why Dormancy Exists</h3>

<p>Dormancy can prevent a seed from germinating during a period that would be unsuitable for successful establishment.</p>

<p>For example, delaying germination can prevent seedlings from emerging during unfavourable environmental conditions.</p>

<h3>Dormancy Is Not the Same as Death</h3>

<p>A dormant seed is still alive.</p>

<p>A non-viable seed cannot successfully germinate because it is no longer capable of normal germination.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>Seeds are supplied with water, oxygen and a suitable temperature, but none germinate immediately.</p>

<p>This does not prove that the seeds are dead.</p>

<p>If they later germinate after an appropriate dormancy-breaking treatment, the original failure was associated with dormancy.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Different plant species can have different dormancy mechanisms and requirements.</p>

<p>Therefore, do not assume that every dormant seed requires the same treatment.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seed_dormancy', 15),
  (1305, 1305, '
<h2>Epigeal Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>In epigeal germination, the hypocotyl elongates and lifts the cotyledons above the soil surface.</p>

<p>The cotyledons therefore become exposed above the ground during early seedling development.</p>

<h3>What Happens</h3>

<p>The seed absorbs water and germination begins.</p>

<p>The radicle emerges and develops into the primary root.</p>

<p>The hypocotyl elongates and pushes the cotyledons upwards.</p>

<p>The plumule develops into the shoot.</p>

<h3>Why the Cotyledons Rise</h3>

<p>The key feature is elongation of the hypocotyl.</p>

<p>This raises the cotyledons above the soil.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seed germinates and its cotyledons are lifted above the soil surface.</p>

<p><strong>What type of germination is occurring?</strong></p>

<p>Epigeal germination.</p>

<p>The hypocotyl has elongated sufficiently to raise the cotyledons above the soil.</p>

<h3>Identification Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Cotyledons above soil → epigeal germination.</strong></p>
  
<hr>

<h2>Epigeal Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Epigeal germination is a pattern of germination in which the cotyledons are lifted above the soil surface as the seedling develops.</p>

<h3>What Causes the Movement?</h3>

<p>Growth of the embryonic stem region below the cotyledons pushes the cotyledons upward.</p>

<p>The cotyledons therefore become exposed above the soil.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>In a typical epigeal seedling, the young root develops downward while the cotyledons are carried upward above the soil.</p>

<h3>Why the Name Helps</h3>

<p>"Epigeal" refers to the cotyledons being above the ground.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling emerges from the soil and its two cotyledons are clearly visible above the soil surface.</p>

<p>This is evidence of epigeal germination.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Identify epigeal germination from the <strong>position of the cotyledons</strong>, not simply from whether the seedling has emerged.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for epigeal_germination', 15),
  (1306, 1306, '
<h2>Hypogeal Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>In hypogeal germination, the cotyledons remain below the soil surface while the shoot develops above the ground.</p>

<p>The epicotyl elongates and raises the plumule above the soil.</p>

<h3>What Happens</h3>

<p>The radicle emerges and develops into the primary root.</p>

<p>The epicotyl elongates.</p>

<p>The plumule is carried upward.</p>

<p>The cotyledons remain below the soil.</p>

<h3>Key Difference</h3>

<p>In epigeal germination, the hypocotyl raises the cotyledons.</p>

<p>In hypogeal germination, the epicotyl raises the shoot while the cotyledons remain below the soil.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A germinating seed produces a shoot above the soil, but its cotyledons remain underground.</p>

<p><strong>What type of germination is this?</strong></p>

<p>Hypogeal germination.</p>

<h3>Identification Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Cotyledons below soil → hypogeal germination.</strong></p>
  
<hr>

<h2>Hypogeal Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Hypogeal germination is a pattern of germination in which the cotyledons remain below the soil surface as the shoot develops.</p>

<h3>What Causes This?</h3>

<p>The epicotyl elongates and pushes the developing shoot upward while the cotyledons remain underground.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>The root grows downward.</p>

<p>The shoot grows upward.</p>

<p>The cotyledons remain below the soil surface.</p>

<h3>Why the Distinction Matters</h3>

<p>Both epigeal and hypogeal seedlings develop roots and shoots.</p>

<p>The important difference is the position of the cotyledons during development.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling emerges above the soil, but the cotyledons remain underground.</p>

<p>This indicates hypogeal germination.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Do not identify hypogeal germination simply because the shoot grows upward.</p>

<p>The key evidence is that the cotyledons remain below the soil.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for hypogeal_germination', 15),
  (1307, 1307, '
<h2>Starting Seed Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination is the process by which a viable seed resumes growth and develops into a seedling.</p>

<h3>What Happens First?</h3>

<p>When a suitable seed absorbs water, its tissues become hydrated.</p>

<p>Metabolic activity increases.</p>

<p>Enzymes become active and stored food can be mobilised to provide materials and energy for growth.</p>

<h3>Why Water Is Important</h3>

<p>Water is required for metabolic reactions and helps activate the processes needed for growth.</p>

<h3>What Happens Next?</h3>

<p>The embryo resumes growth.</p>

<p>The radicle, which develops into the primary root, emerges first in many seeds.</p>

<p>The young root can then absorb water and mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Water absorption by a seed is an early event in germination.</p>

<p>It does not mean that the seed has completed germination.</p>

<h3>Exam Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Water uptake → metabolic activity increases → stored food is mobilised → embryo grows → radicle emerges.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seed_germination_start', 15),
  (1308, 1308, '
<h2>Stored Food During Seed Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A germinating seedling initially has little or no ability to make enough food by photosynthesis.</p>

<p>It therefore depends on food reserves stored in the seed.</p>

<h3>Why Stored Food Is Needed</h3>

<p>Growth requires energy and raw materials.</p>

<p>Stored carbohydrates, fats and proteins can be broken down and used by the developing embryo.</p>

<h3>Respiration</h3>

<p>Stored organic compounds can be used in respiration to release energy.</p>

<p>This energy supports processes such as cell division, active transport and growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seed germinates underground before its first leaves are exposed to enough light for substantial photosynthesis.</p>

<p>The young seedling can still grow because it uses stored food reserves.</p>

<h3>Transition to Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>Once leaves develop and contain functional chlorophyll, the seedling can begin making its own organic food through photosynthesis.</p>

<p>The seed reserve is therefore particularly important during the early stages of development.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not say that germinating seeds obtain their energy directly from sunlight.</p>

<p>Before substantial photosynthesis begins, energy comes mainly from respiration of stored food.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for stored_food_during_germination', 15),
  (1309, 1309, '
<h2>Germination: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student places viable seeds in three different conditions.</p>

<p><strong>Condition A:</strong> adequate water, oxygen and suitable temperature.</p>

<p><strong>Condition B:</strong> no water but adequate oxygen and suitable temperature.</p>

<p><strong>Condition C:</strong> water present but very little oxygen.</p>

<h3>Condition A</h3>

<p>The seeds have the main environmental conditions needed for germination.</p>

<p>Water hydrates the seed and activates metabolic processes.</p>

<p>Oxygen supports aerobic respiration.</p>

<p>A suitable temperature allows enzyme-controlled reactions to proceed effectively.</p>

<h3>Condition B</h3>

<p>Without sufficient water, normal germination cannot begin effectively.</p>

<p>The seed cannot properly activate the metabolic processes required for growth.</p>

<h3>Condition C</h3>

<p>Water is available, but very low oxygen can limit aerobic respiration.</p>

<p>This reduces the energy available for the growing embryo.</p>

<h3>Complete Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Suitable conditions → seed becomes metabolically active → stored food is mobilised → respiration supplies energy → embryo grows → seedling develops.</strong></p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>When explaining germination, do not merely list "water, oxygen and warmth."</p>

<p>Explain what each condition allows the seed to do.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_complete_reasoning', 15),
  (1310, 1310, '
<h2>Epigeal and Hypogeal Germination Compared</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Epigeal and hypogeal germination describe different patterns of seedling development.</p>

<h3>Epigeal</h3>

<p>The cotyledons are lifted above the soil surface.</p>

<p>This commonly occurs because the hypocotyl elongates.</p>

<h3>Hypogeal</h3>

<p>The cotyledons remain below the soil surface.</p>

<p>This commonly occurs because the epicotyl elongates and raises the shoot.</p>

<h3>Comparison</h3>

<p><strong>Epigeal → cotyledons above soil → hypocotyl elongates.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Hypogeal → cotyledons below soil → epicotyl elongates.</strong></p>

<h3>What Both Have in Common</h3>

<p>Both types involve germination of a viable seed.</p>

<p>Both develop a root and shoot.</p>

<p>Both require coordinated growth of embryonic tissues.</p>

<h3>Worked Identification</h3>

<p>Seedling A has cotyledons above the soil.</p>

<p>Seedling B has cotyledons below the soil while its shoot is above the soil.</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p>A → epigeal.</p>

<p>B → hypogeal.</p>

<h3>Exam Strategy</h3>

<p>Ignore the general direction of the shoot.</p>

<p>Look specifically at the position of the cotyledons.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for epigeal_vs_hypogeal_comparison', 15),
  (1311, 1311, '
<h2>Germination Sequence: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student observes a viable seed changing into a young seedling.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Water Uptake</h3>

<p>The seed absorbs water and becomes hydrated.</p>

<p>Metabolic activity increases.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Stored Food Is Mobilised</h3>

<p>Stored organic compounds are broken down and made available to the developing embryo.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Respiration</h3>

<p>The embryo respires and releases energy from organic compounds.</p>

<p>This energy supports growth processes.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Cell Division and Enlargement</h3>

<p>Cells divide by mitosis and newly formed cells enlarge.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Radicle Emergence</h3>

<p>The radicle emerges and develops into the primary root.</p>

<h3>Step 6: Shoot Development</h3>

<p>The plumule develops into the shoot system.</p>

<h3>Step 7: Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>As functional green leaves develop, photosynthesis increasingly supplies the seedling with organic food.</p>

<h3>Complete Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Water uptake → metabolic activation → stored food mobilisation → respiration → cell division and enlargement → radicle and shoot development → photosynthesis.</strong></p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Do not treat germination as one single event.</p>

<p>It is a sequence of coordinated biological processes that transform the embryo into a growing seedling.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_sequence_problem', 15),
  (1312, 1312, '
<h2>Investigating the Need for Water During Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Water is one of the conditions required for normal germination.</p>

<h3>Experimental Design</h3>

<p>Use two groups of similar viable seeds.</p>

<p><strong>Group A:</strong> receives enough water.</p>

<p><strong>Group B:</strong> is kept dry.</p>

<p>Both groups receive suitable oxygen and temperature.</p>

<h3>What Is Being Tested?</h3>

<p>The independent variable is water availability.</p>

<p>The dependent variable could be the number or percentage of seeds that germinate after a fixed period.</p>

<h3>Expected Result</h3>

<p>The watered group should show greater germination than the dry group if water is required under the conditions used.</p>

<h3>Why the Experiment Works</h3>

<p>Water hydrates the seed and allows metabolic processes involved in germination to become active.</p>

<h3>Fair Test</h3>

<p>The seed species, number of seeds, temperature, oxygen availability and duration should be kept constant.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The experiment tests water availability. It should not be used to conclude that water alone is sufficient for every seed to germinate.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for investigating_water_requirement_germination', 15),
  (1313, 1313, '
<h2>Investigating the Need for Oxygen During Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germinating seeds respire and require energy for growth.</p>

<p>When aerobic respiration occurs, oxygen is required.</p>

<h3>Experimental Design</h3>

<p>Use two groups of similar viable seeds.</p>

<p><strong>Group A:</strong> has access to sufficient oxygen.</p>

<p><strong>Group B:</strong> has very limited oxygen availability.</p>

<p>Water and temperature are kept suitable and similar for both groups.</p>

<h3>Measurement</h3>

<p>Record the number or percentage of seeds that germinate within a fixed period.</p>

<h3>Expected Pattern</h3>

<p>If oxygen is sufficiently limited, germination and early seedling growth may be reduced compared with the well-aerated group.</p>

<h3>Biological Explanation</h3>

<p>Germinating cells require energy for processes such as cell division, enlargement and active transport.</p>

<p>Low oxygen can limit aerobic respiration and therefore reduce the energy available.</p>

<h3>Fair Test</h3>

<p>Keep seed type, number of seeds, water supply, temperature and duration constant.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Do not say oxygen is "food" for the seed.</p>

<p>Oxygen is used in aerobic respiration, which releases energy from organic compounds.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for investigating_oxygen_requirement_germination', 15),
  (1314, 1314, '
<h2>Investigating Temperature and Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Temperature affects the rate of enzyme-controlled reactions involved in germination.</p>

<h3>Experimental Design</h3>

<p>Place equal numbers of similar viable seeds under different temperature conditions.</p>

<p>Keep water and oxygen availability suitable.</p>

<p>Record germination over the same period.</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>Temperature.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>The number or percentage of seeds that germinate within a specified time.</p>

<h3>Why Temperature Matters</h3>

<p>At a low temperature, enzyme-controlled reactions may occur slowly.</p>

<p>As temperature increases within a suitable range, metabolic reactions can become faster.</p>

<p>At excessively high temperatures, proteins including enzymes can be damaged or denatured, reducing normal metabolic activity.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Seeds are tested at 10°C, 20°C, 30°C and 50°C.</p>

<p>If 30°C gives the highest percentage germination while 10°C and 50°C give lower values, the data suggest that the seeds have a more favourable temperature range around 30°C under those experimental conditions.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Do not assume one temperature is the optimum for every plant species.</p>

<p>The optimum must be determined from evidence for the species being tested.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for investigating_temperature_germination', 15),
  (1315, 1315, '
<h2>Calculating Germination Percentage</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination percentage allows different groups of seeds to be compared even when the groups contain different numbers of seeds.</p>

<h3>Formula</h3>

<p><strong>Germination percentage = (number of seeds that germinated ÷ total number of viable seeds) × 100</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>20 viable seeds are planted.</p>

<p>16 germinate.</p>

<p>Germination percentage:</p>

<p>(16 ÷ 20) × 100 = 80%</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>50 viable seeds are planted.</p>

<p>35 germinate.</p>

<p>(35 ÷ 50) × 100 = 70%</p>

<h3>Comparing Groups</h3>

<p>Group A has 16 germinated seeds out of 20.</p>

<p>Group B has 35 germinated seeds out of 50.</p>

<p>Although Group B has more germinated seeds in total, Group A has the higher germination percentage.</p>

<p>This is why percentage is useful when group sizes differ.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_percentage', 15),
  (1316, 1316, '
<h2>Interpreting Germination Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination data should be described accurately before a biological explanation is given.</p>

<h3>Example Data</h3>

<p>Temperature | Germination percentage</p>

<p>10°C → 25%</p>

<p>20°C → 60%</p>

<p>30°C → 85%</p>

<p>40°C → 50%</p>

<h3>Step 1: Describe the Pattern</h3>

<p>Germination increases from 10°C to 30°C.</p>

<p>It then decreases at 40°C.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify the Highest Value</h3>

<p>The highest measured germination percentage is 85% at 30°C.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Give a Possible Explanation</h3>

<p>Increasing temperature within a suitable range can increase the rate of enzyme-controlled metabolic reactions.</p>

<p>At higher temperatures, enzyme activity can be disrupted and proteins can become damaged.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The data support what happened in the experiment.</p>

<p>They do not automatically prove the exact molecular mechanism responsible.</p>

<h3>Exam Method</h3>

<p><strong>Describe → compare → identify maximum/minimum → explain using biology.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_data_interpretation', 15),
  (1317, 1317, '
<h2>Reliability in Germination Experiments</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Reliability refers to the consistency of results.</p>

<h3>Why One Seed Is Not Enough</h3>

<p>Individual seeds can differ in viability and development.</p>

<p>If only one seed is tested, an unusual seed could strongly influence the conclusion.</p>

<h3>Using Replicates</h3>

<p>Use several seeds in each treatment group.</p>

<p>Repeat the experiment where possible.</p>

<p>This provides more observations and reduces the influence of individual variation.</p>

<h3>Using a Mean</h3>

<p>When repeated numerical measurements are collected, calculate a mean to summarise the results.</p>

<p><strong>Mean = total of measurements ÷ number of measurements.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three repeated germination percentages are 70%, 80% and 75%.</p>

<p>Mean:</p>

<p>(70 + 80 + 75) ÷ 3 = 225 ÷ 3 = 75%</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Repeating an experiment improves reliability.</p>

<p>It does not automatically make the experiment valid.</p>

<p>The experiment must also test the intended variable fairly.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_experiment_reliability', 15),
  (1318, 1318, '
<h2>Validity in a Germination Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An investigation is valid when its design allows it to answer the question being investigated.</p>

<h3>Example Question</h3>

<p>A student wants to investigate the effect of temperature on germination.</p>

<h3>What Must Change?</h3>

<p>Temperature should be deliberately changed between treatment groups.</p>

<h3>What Should Stay Constant?</h3>

<p>Important factors such as seed species, number of seeds, water availability, oxygen availability and duration should be controlled.</p>

<h3>Why?</h3>

<p>Suppose the warm group receives more water than the cool group.</p>

<p>If it germinates better, the student cannot determine whether temperature or water caused the difference.</p>

<p>The investigation would therefore have poor control of confounding variables.</p>

<h3>Validity vs Reliability</h3>

<p><strong>Validity → are we testing the intended factor?</strong></p>

<p><strong>Reliability → do we obtain consistent results?</strong></p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Repeating a poorly controlled experiment can produce very consistent results that still do not answer the intended question.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_experiment_validity', 15),
  (1319, 1319, '
<h2>Germination Investigation: Complete Exam Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student investigates the effect of temperature on seed germination.</p>

<p>Four groups of 20 viable seeds are placed at different temperatures.</p>

<p>After seven days, the results are:</p>

<p>10°C → 5 seeds germinated</p>

<p>20°C → 12 seeds germinated</p>

<p>30°C → 18 seeds germinated</p>

<p>40°C → 10 seeds germinated</p>

<h3>Step 1: Calculate Germination Percentages</h3>

<p>10°C:</p>

<p>(5 ÷ 20) × 100 = 25%</p>

<p>20°C:</p>

<p>(12 ÷ 20) × 100 = 60%</p>

<p>30°C:</p>

<p>(18 ÷ 20) × 100 = 90%</p>

<p>40°C:</p>

<p>(10 ÷ 20) × 100 = 50%</p>

<h3>Step 2: Describe the Pattern</h3>

<p>Germination increases from 10°C to 30°C.</p>

<p>It decreases at 40°C.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Identify the Highest Tested Value</h3>

<p>The highest germination percentage is 90% at 30°C.</p>

<p>Therefore, 30°C was the most favourable temperature among those tested.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Explain the Pattern</h3>

<p>Temperature affects enzyme-controlled metabolic reactions involved in germination.</p>

<p>At low temperatures, these reactions can proceed slowly.</p>

<p>At excessively high temperatures, enzymes can be damaged or denatured.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Evaluate the Investigation</h3>

<p>The investigation should use the same seed species, similar viable seeds, equal water supply, adequate oxygen and the same duration.</p>

<p>Several seeds are already used per group, which is better than testing one seed.</p>

<p>Repeating the experiment would provide stronger evidence of reliability.</p>

<h3>Model Conclusion</h3>

<p>Under the conditions tested, germination increased as temperature rose from 10°C to 30°C, reaching 90% at 30°C, but decreased to 50% at 40°C. Therefore, 30°C was the most favourable temperature among those tested. The pattern is consistent with temperature affecting enzyme-controlled processes involved in germination.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_complete_exam_problem', 15),
  (1320, 1320, '
<h2>Analysing Germination Failure</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>When seeds fail to germinate, the correct explanation must be based on evidence.</p>

<h3>Case 1: Seeds Are Completely Dry</h3>

<p>Without sufficient water, the seed cannot properly hydrate and activate the processes required for germination.</p>

<h3>Case 2: Seeds Have Water but Very Little Oxygen</h3>

<p>Respiration may be affected because aerobic respiration requires oxygen.</p>

<p>This can reduce the energy available for early growth.</p>

<h3>Case 3: Temperature Is Too Low</h3>

<p>Enzyme-controlled reactions may occur slowly, reducing the rate of metabolic processes involved in germination.</p>

<h3>Case 4: Temperature Is Excessively High</h3>

<p>High temperatures can damage enzymes and disrupt normal cellular processes.</p>

<h3>Case 5: The Seeds Are Not Viable</h3>

<p>A non-viable seed may fail to germinate even when water, oxygen and suitable temperature are provided.</p>

<h3>Case 6: The Seed Is Dormant</h3>

<p>Some viable seeds do not germinate immediately because dormancy prevents germination until particular conditions or internal changes occur.</p>

<h3>Exam Method</h3>

<p>Do not simply state "the seeds lacked food."</p>

<p>Identify the condition, connect it to a biological process, and then explain how that process affects germination.</p>

<p><strong>Condition → biological effect → effect on germination.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_failure_analysis', 15),
  (1321, 1321, '
<h2>Germination Over Time</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination can be studied by recording how many seeds have germinated at different times.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>Day 0 → 0 seeds</p>

<p>Day 2 → 4 seeds</p>

<p>Day 4 → 12 seeds</p>

<p>Day 6 → 18 seeds</p>

<p>Day 8 → 20 seeds</p>

<h3>What Does the Data Show?</h3>

<p>Germination increases with time during the period measured.</p>

<p>Between Day 0 and Day 6, more seeds become germinated.</p>

<p>By Day 8, all 20 seeds have germinated.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The number of seeds germinated measures cumulative germination.</p>

<p>It is not the same as the rate of germination.</p>

<h3>Calculating Percentage</h3>

<p>On Day 6:</p>

<p>(18 ÷ 20) × 100 = 90%</p>

<h3>Interpreting a Plateau</h3>

<p>If the number of germinated seeds stops increasing, it may mean that no additional seeds germinated during the remaining observation period.</p>

<p>This does not necessarily mean that all seeds were healthy or that germination can never occur later.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Describe exactly what the data show before suggesting a biological explanation.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_time_course', 15),
  (1322, 1322, '
<h2>Calculating Germination Rate from Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination rate describes how quickly germination occurs over a specified time interval.</p>

<h3>Basic Calculation</h3>

<p><strong>Germination rate = change in number of germinated seeds ÷ change in time</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>After 2 days, 4 seeds have germinated.</p>

<p>After 6 days, 16 seeds have germinated.</p>

<p>Change in germinated seeds:</p>

<p>16 − 4 = 12 seeds</p>

<p>Change in time:</p>

<p>6 − 2 = 4 days</p>

<p>Rate:</p>

<p>12 ÷ 4 = 3 seeds per day</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>A group increases from 10 germinated seeds on Day 3 to 18 on Day 7.</p>

<p>Change = 18 − 10 = 8 seeds.</p>

<p>Time = 7 − 3 = 4 days.</p>

<p>Rate = 8 ÷ 4 = 2 seeds per day.</p>

<h3>Why the Time Interval Matters</h3>

<p>Using only the final number of germinated seeds tells you how many germinated, not how rapidly they germinated.</p>

<p>To calculate a rate, both a change in measurement and a change in time are required.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_rate_from_data', 15),
  (1323, 1323, '
<h2>Seed Viability</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A seed is <strong>viable</strong> if it is alive and capable of germinating when suitable conditions are provided.</p>

<h3>Why Viability Matters</h3>

<p>Germination depends not only on external conditions such as water, oxygen and suitable temperature.</p>

<p>The seed itself must also be capable of carrying out the processes required for germination.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two groups of seeds receive enough water, oxygen and a suitable temperature.</p>

<p>Group A has 90% germination.</p>

<p>Group B has 20% germination.</p>

<p>If the conditions were properly controlled, one possible explanation is that the groups differed in viability.</p>

<h3>How Viability Can Be Investigated</h3>

<p>A large sample of seeds can be placed under suitable germination conditions.</p>

<p>The percentage that successfully germinates gives evidence about the viability of the sample.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>A seed that does not germinate is not automatically non-viable.</p>

<p>It may be dormant or may not have received the conditions required for germination.</p>

<h3>Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p>Viable seed + suitable conditions → germination is possible.</p>

<p>Non-viable seed → cannot successfully germinate even when suitable conditions are provided.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seed_viability', 15),
  (1324, 1324, '
<h2>Seed Storage and Viability</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Seeds are living structures. Their ability to germinate can decline during storage if conditions cause damage or ageing.</p>

<h3>Why Storage Matters</h3>

<p>Unsuitable storage conditions can reduce the proportion of seeds that remain viable.</p>

<p>Factors such as excessive moisture and unsuitable temperature can contribute to deterioration.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two batches contain 100 seeds each.</p>

<p>Batch A is stored appropriately and later produces 92 germinated seeds.</p>

<p>Batch B is stored under poor conditions and later produces 55 germinated seeds.</p>

<p>Batch A:</p>

<p>(92 ÷ 100) × 100 = 92%</p>

<p>Batch B:</p>

<p>(55 ÷ 100) × 100 = 55%</p>

<p>The lower germination percentage in Batch B indicates lower viability or another difference affecting germination.</p>

<h3>Important Experimental Point</h3>

<p>To conclude that storage conditions caused the difference, other important factors must be controlled.</p>

<p>The seed species, initial seed quality, storage duration and germination conditions should be comparable.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>Good storage helps preserve the ability of seeds to germinate, but storage alone does not guarantee germination.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seed_storage_and_viability', 15),
  (1325, 1325, '
<h2>Water Uptake at the Start of Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The first major physical event in germination is the uptake of water by the dry seed.</p>

<h3>What Happens When Water Enters?</h3>

<p>The seed tissues become hydrated.</p>

<p>Cells that were metabolically inactive become more active.</p>

<p>Enzymes involved in mobilisation of stored food become active.</p>

<p>Respiration increases as the embryo requires more energy.</p>

<h3>Why Water Is Not Simply "Food"</h3>

<p>Water does not provide the organic food reserves needed for growth.</p>

<p>Instead, it provides the conditions needed for biochemical processes to occur and allows stored substances to be mobilised.</p>

<h3>Worked Sequence</h3>

<p>Dry seed</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Water enters</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Seed tissues hydrate</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Metabolic activity increases</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Stored food is mobilised</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Respiration supplies energy</p>

<p>↓</p>

<p>Embryo grows</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Water uptake begins the process, but successful germination also requires other suitable conditions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seed_germination_water_uptake', 15),
  (1326, 1326, '
<h2>Enzymes During Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germinating seeds need usable nutrients and energy. Enzymes help break stored food substances into smaller substances that can be used by the developing embryo.</p>

<h3>Stored Starch</h3>

<p>Starch is a large carbohydrate that cannot simply be transported into cells in its original form for all the processes required.</p>

<p>Enzymes such as amylase break starch down into smaller sugars.</p>

<h3>Why Sugars Matter</h3>

<p>The sugars released can be used as substrates in respiration.</p>

<p>Respiration releases energy that supports cellular activities involved in growth.</p>

<h3>Stored Proteins</h3>

<p>Proteins can also be broken down into amino acids.</p>

<p>Amino acids can be used by the growing seedling to make new proteins.</p>

<h3>Worked Chain</h3>

<p>Stored starch</p>

<p>→ enzyme action</p>

<p>→ smaller sugars</p>

<p>→ respiration</p>

<p>→ energy released</p>

<p>→ growth processes</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Enzymes do not provide energy themselves.</p>

<p>They catalyse reactions that make the mobilisation and use of stored substances possible at suitable rates.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for enzymes_during_germination', 15),
  (1327, 1327, '
<h2>Conditions for Germination: Complete Reasoning</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Successful germination depends on the seed being viable and receiving suitable environmental conditions.</p>

<h3>The Three Core Conditions</h3>

<p><strong>Water:</strong> hydrates the seed and enables metabolic processes to become active.</p>

<p><strong>Oxygen:</strong> is required for aerobic respiration.</p>

<p><strong>Suitable temperature:</strong> allows enzyme-controlled reactions to occur at appropriate rates.</p>

<h3>Complete Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A student places viable seeds in three conditions:</p>

<p><strong>A:</strong> water + oxygen + suitable temperature.</p>

<p><strong>B:</strong> no water + oxygen + suitable temperature.</p>

<p><strong>C:</strong> water + very little oxygen + suitable temperature.</p>

<p>Group A is expected to show the strongest normal germination.</p>

<p>Group B cannot begin normal germination effectively because insufficient water prevents the required hydration and activation.</p>

<p>Group C may show reduced germination or early growth because limited oxygen restricts aerobic respiration.</p>

<h3>Exam Structure</h3>

<p>When explaining a germination result, identify the missing condition first.</p>

<p>Then state the biological process affected.</p>

<p>Finally explain how this affects germination or growth.</p>

<p><strong>Missing condition → affected process → effect on germination.</strong></p>

<h3>Final Reminder</h3>

<p>Suitable conditions do not guarantee that every seed germinates. Seed viability and dormancy can also affect the result.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_conditions_complete', 15),
  (1328, 1328, '
<h2>Radicle Emergence During Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The radicle is the embryonic structure that develops into the primary root.</p>

<p>During germination, the radicle commonly emerges from the seed before the shoot becomes fully established.</p>

<h3>Why the Radicle Emerges Early</h3>

<p>The developing seedling needs a way to obtain water and mineral ions from the surrounding environment.</p>

<p>Once the root system begins developing, the seedling can establish itself in the soil.</p>

<h3>What Happens After Emergence?</h3>

<p>The radicle grows downward and develops into the primary root.</p>

<p>Root hairs can later develop on suitable regions of the root.</p>

<p>These increase the surface area available for absorption.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student observes a seed after several days.</p>

<p>A small structure has emerged from the seed and is growing downward.</p>

<p>This structure is the radicle.</p>

<p>It will develop into the primary root.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not call the radicle the first leaf.</p>

<p>The radicle is part of the embryonic root system.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for germination_radicle_emergence', 15),
  (1329, 1329, '
<h2>Plumule Emergence During Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The plumule is the embryonic shoot of a seed.</p>

<p>As germination progresses, the plumule develops into the shoot system.</p>

<h3>What the Plumule Produces</h3>

<p>The developing shoot produces leaves and stem tissues.</p>

<p>Once suitable leaves become photosynthetically active, the seedling can increasingly produce its own organic food.</p>

<h3>Relationship with the Radicle</h3>

<p>The radicle develops into the root system.</p>

<p>The plumule develops into the shoot system.</p>

<p>These two embryonic structures therefore establish the two major parts of the young plant.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A germinating seed has produced a structure that grows upward and later develops leaves.</p>

<p>This structure is associated with the plumule and developing shoot.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The plumule is not the same as the mature shoot.</p>

<p>It is the embryonic shoot region that develops into the shoot.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plumule_emergence', 15),
  (1330, 1330, '
<h2>Cotyledons and Early Seedling Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Cotyledons are seed leaves associated with the embryo inside a seed.</p>

<p>In many plants, cotyledons contain or provide access to stored food reserves that support early seedling growth.</p>

<h3>Why They Matter</h3>

<p>The young seedling initially depends heavily on stored nutrients because its photosynthetic leaves may not yet be fully developed.</p>

<p>These stored reserves provide materials and energy for early growth.</p>

<h3>After Germination</h3>

<p>As true leaves develop and photosynthesis becomes effective, the seedling increasingly obtains organic food through photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A germinating seedling grows before its first fully developed leaves can photosynthesise effectively.</p>

<p>The seedling can still grow because stored food reserves associated with the seed provide resources.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Cotyledons are not always simply "food."</p>

<p>They are embryonic seed leaves and may have different roles depending on the plant.</p>

<p>For school-level reasoning, their important early role is associated with supplying stored reserves to the developing seedling.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for cotyledon_function', 15),
  (1331, 1331, '
<h2>Seedling Establishment After Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination does not end when the radicle emerges. The young seedling must establish a root and shoot system that can obtain water, mineral ions and light.</p>

<h3>Step 1: The Radicle Develops</h3>

<p>The radicle develops into the primary root.</p>

<p>The root grows into the soil and begins absorbing water and mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Root Hairs Develop</h3>

<p>Root hair cells develop behind the growing root tip.</p>

<p>They increase the surface area available for absorption.</p>

<h3>Step 3: The Plumule Develops</h3>

<p>The plumule develops into the shoot.</p>

<p>The shoot grows towards the soil surface and eventually produces leaves.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Leaves Become Photosynthetic</h3>

<p>Once leaves are exposed to suitable light, chloroplast-containing cells can carry out photosynthesis.</p>

<p>The seedling increasingly produces its own organic food rather than depending entirely on stored seed reserves.</p>

<h3>Why Establishment Matters</h3>

<p>The seedling must connect two essential resources:</p>

<p><strong>Roots → water and mineral ions</strong></p>

<p><strong>Leaves → light and carbon dioxide</strong></p>

<p>Successful establishment allows the plant to continue growing after the seed''s stored reserves become insufficient.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seedling_establishment_after_germination', 15),
  (1332, 1332, '
<h2>Why a Developing Seedling Needs Light</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Light becomes essential when a young plant begins relying on photosynthesis to manufacture organic food.</p>

<h3>What Light Provides</h3>

<p>Light supplies the energy required for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>In green plant cells, chlorophyll absorbs some of this light energy.</p>

<h3>Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide and water are used to produce glucose, with oxygen released.</p>

<p><strong>6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</strong></p>

<h3>Why This Matters to a Seedling</h3>

<p>Glucose produced by photosynthesis can be used:</p>

<p>• in respiration to release energy</p>

<p>• to form cellulose for cell walls</p>

<p>• to form starch for storage</p>

<p>• to form other organic substances needed for growth</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>A seed can begin germination in darkness because early growth can use stored reserves.</p>

<p>However, prolonged darkness prevents normal dependence on photosynthesis because there is no light energy for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p>Light → chlorophyll absorbs light energy → photosynthesis → glucose production → continued growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seedling_light_requirement', 15),
  (1333, 1333, '
<h2>Root and Shoot Development in a Seedling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A developing seedling must establish both a root system and a shoot system.</p>

<h3>Root System</h3>

<p>The root develops from the radicle.</p>

<p>It anchors the plant and absorbs water and mineral ions from the soil.</p>

<h3>Shoot System</h3>

<p>The shoot develops from the plumule.</p>

<p>It produces stems and leaves.</p>

<p>Leaves provide the main photosynthetic surface of the young plant.</p>

<h3>Why Both Systems Are Needed</h3>

<p>The root supplies water and mineral ions required for growth.</p>

<p>The shoot exposes leaves to light and carbon dioxide for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Water absorbed by roots is transported through xylem to the shoot.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A seedling has healthy leaves but damaged roots.</p>

<p>Its photosynthesis may initially continue, but reduced root function limits water and mineral ion uptake.</p>

<p>Therefore, continued growth can eventually be affected.</p>

<h3>Another Example</h3>

<p>A seedling has healthy roots but its leaves receive insufficient light.</p>

<p>Water and mineral ions may still be absorbed, but photosynthesis is limited.</p>

<p>Therefore, production of new organic food is reduced.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>Successful seedling growth depends on coordinated development of both root and shoot systems.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seedling_root_shoot_balance', 15),
  (1334, 1334, '
<h2>Root Growth Direction in a Developing Seedling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>After the radicle emerges, the young root normally grows into the soil.</p>

<p>This growth direction helps the developing plant establish contact with soil resources.</p>

<h3>Why the Root Grows into the Soil</h3>

<p>The root needs to reach regions where it can obtain water and mineral ions.</p>

<p>It also anchors the developing plant.</p>

<h3>Connection to Gravitropism</h3>

<p>Roots commonly show <strong>positive gravitropism</strong>.</p>

<p>This means the root grows in the direction of gravitational pull.</p>

<p>Auxin contributes to the control of differential growth in roots.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A germinating seed is placed horizontally.</p>

<p>The emerging root initially grows sideways but later curves downward.</p>

<p>The change in direction is evidence that the root responds to gravity.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Gravitropism describes a growth response to gravity.</p>

<p>It is different from simply saying that roots "need soil."</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p>Gravity detected → unequal auxin distribution → unequal growth → root curves downward.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seedling_root_growth_direction', 15),
  (1335, 1335, '
<h2>Shoot Growth Direction in a Developing Seedling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The young shoot normally grows upward and produces leaves above the soil.</p>

<p>This allows the developing leaves to reach light and carbon dioxide required for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Connection to Tropisms</h3>

<p>Shoots commonly show:</p>

<p><strong>Positive phototropism</strong> — growth towards light.</p>

<p><strong>Negative gravitropism</strong> — growth away from gravity.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling is placed horizontally near a light source.</p>

<p>After several days, the shoot curves towards the light.</p>

<p>The bending is a growth response rather than the stem simply being physically pushed sideways.</p>

<h3>Why This Helps the Plant</h3>

<p>Positioning leaves towards available light can improve access to light energy for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The shoot and root respond differently to gravity.</p>

<p><strong>Root → positive gravitropism.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Shoot → negative gravitropism.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seedling_shoot_growth_direction', 15),
  (1336, 1336, '
<h2>Root Hair Development in Young Seedlings</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>As the young root develops, some epidermal cells differentiate into root hair cells.</p>

<p>Root hairs are extensions of individual root epidermal cells.</p>

<h3>Why Root Hairs Develop</h3>

<p>Their main function is to increase the surface area available for absorption of water and mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Water Absorption</h3>

<p>Water enters root hair cells by osmosis when conditions provide a suitable water potential gradient.</p>

<h3>Mineral Ion Absorption</h3>

<p>Mineral ions may enter by diffusion when the gradient allows it.</p>

<p>When ions must be moved against a concentration gradient, active transport can be involved.</p>

<h3>Adaptations</h3>

<p>Root hair cells have:</p>

<p>• a long extension that increases surface area</p>

<p>• a thin cell surface that provides a short diffusion pathway</p>

<p>• transport proteins in the cell membrane</p>

<p>• mitochondria that provide energy for active transport</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning</h3>

<p>If root hairs are removed, the total absorbing surface area decreases.</p>

<p>This can reduce the plant''s ability to absorb water and mineral ions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_hair_development_in_seedlings', 15),
  (1337, 1337, '
<h2>First Leaves and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>As the shoot develops, the first leaves expand and become important photosynthetic organs.</p>

<h3>What the Leaves Need</h3>

<p>For photosynthesis, leaves require:</p>

<p>• light energy</p>

<p>• carbon dioxide</p>

<p>• water</p>

<p>• chlorophyll-containing cells</p>

<h3>Where the Materials Come From</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide enters the leaf through stomata.</p>

<p>Water is transported to the leaf through xylem.</p>

<p>Light is absorbed by chlorophyll.</p>

<h3>Why This Changes the Seedling</h3>

<p>Early development depends heavily on stored food reserves.</p>

<p>Once functional leaves develop, photosynthesis provides a continuing source of glucose.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A seedling has healthy roots and leaves but is placed in complete darkness.</p>

<p>Its roots can still absorb water, but photosynthesis is prevented because there is no light energy.</p>

<p>Therefore, production of glucose by photosynthesis is severely limited.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis and respiration continue to be different processes.</p>

<p>The seedling uses respiration to release energy from organic substances, including glucose produced by photosynthesis.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for first_leaves_seedling_photosynthesis', 15),
  (1338, 1338, '
<h2>Dependence on Seed Reserves During Early Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Before the seedling develops enough photosynthetic leaf area, much of its growth depends on food stored in the seed.</p>

<h3>How Stored Food Is Used</h3>

<p>Stored carbohydrates, lipids and proteins can be mobilised into smaller substances.</p>

<p>These substances can support respiration and the synthesis of new materials.</p>

<h3>Energy for Growth</h3>

<p>Respiration releases energy from suitable organic substrates.</p>

<p>The energy supports:</p>

<p>• cell division</p>

<p>• cell enlargement</p>

<p>• active transport</p>

<p>• synthesis of new cellular materials</p>

<h3>As the Seedling Develops</h3>

<p>The cotyledons or storage tissues may become depleted as their reserves are used.</p>

<p>Meanwhile, leaves develop and photosynthesis becomes increasingly important.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A seedling has exhausted most of its stored reserves but has not developed functional leaves.</p>

<p>Its ability to continue obtaining organic food is limited because photosynthesis is not yet supplying enough glucose.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>Stored reserves support early development; photosynthesis becomes increasingly important as the seedling establishes functional leaves.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seedling_dependence_on_seed_reserves', 15),
  (1339, 1339, '
<h2>Cotyledon Depletion During Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Cotyledons often contain or are associated with stored food reserves that support early seedling development.</p>

<h3>What Happens During Germination?</h3>

<p>Stored nutrients are mobilised and transferred to growing embryonic tissues.</p>

<p>As these reserves are used, the cotyledons can decrease in stored nutrient content.</p>

<h3>Why the Reserves Are Used</h3>

<p>The developing embryo needs materials and energy for growth.</p>

<p>Respiration uses suitable organic substrates to release energy.</p>

<h3>What Happens Later?</h3>

<p>As the first leaves become photosynthetically active, the seedling becomes less dependent on the stored reserves.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A seedling''s cotyledons become smaller and eventually wither while new green leaves expand.</p>

<p>This is consistent with stored reserves being used during early development while the developing leaves increasingly take over food production through photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Cotyledons do not simply "turn into food."</p>

<p>They contain or are associated with reserves that are mobilised and used by the developing plant.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for cotyledon_depletion_during_germination', 15),
  (1340, 1340, '
<h2>Measuring Growth in a Young Seedling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth can be measured quantitatively rather than described only by appearance.</p>

<h3>Measuring Length</h3>

<p>A ruler can be used to measure the length of a shoot or root.</p>

<p>Measurements should be taken using the same method each time.</p>

<h3>Calculating Growth</h3>

<p><strong>Growth = final length − initial length</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>A shoot is 2.0 cm long at the beginning and 7.5 cm after five days.</p>

<p>Growth:</p>

<p>7.5 − 2.0 = 5.5 cm</p>

<h3>Calculating Growth Rate</h3>

<p><strong>Growth rate = change in length ÷ time</strong></p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>5.5 ÷ 5 = 1.1 cm/day</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>A root increases from 1.5 cm to 5.5 cm in four days.</p>

<p>Growth = 5.5 − 1.5 = 4.0 cm.</p>

<p>Growth rate = 4.0 ÷ 4 = 1.0 cm/day.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Always include the correct units.</p>

<p>Length is measured in units such as cm.</p>

<p>Growth rate is measured in units such as cm/day.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seedling_growth_measurement', 15),
  (1341, 1341, '
<h2>Comparing Seedling Growth Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Seedling growth should be compared using measurements rather than visual impressions alone.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>Seedling A:</p>

<p>Initial height = 4 cm</p>

<p>Final height = 10 cm</p>

<p>Time = 3 days</p>

<p>Seedling B:</p>

<p>Initial height = 6 cm</p>

<p>Final height = 11 cm</p>

<p>Time = 3 days</p>

<h3>Step 1: Calculate Growth</h3>

<p>A: 10 − 4 = 6 cm</p>

<p>B: 11 − 6 = 5 cm</p>

<h3>Step 2: Calculate Growth Rate</h3>

<p>A: 6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day</p>

<p>B: 5 ÷ 3 ≈ 1.67 cm/day</p>

<h3>Step 3: Compare</h3>

<p>Seedling A had the greater growth rate over the three-day period.</p>

<h3>Why Final Height Alone Can Mislead</h3>

<p>Seedling B ended taller, but it started taller.</p>

<p>Comparing only final heights would therefore give an incomplete picture of growth.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>When asked which seedling grew faster, calculate change in length over the same time interval.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for comparing_seedling_growth_data', 15),
  (1342, 1342, '
<h2>Cell Division in a Growing Seedling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A seedling increases in size partly because cells divide to produce new cells.</p>

<h3>Where Does Cell Division Occur?</h3>

<p>Rapid cell division occurs in meristematic regions, such as the apical meristems near the tips of roots and shoots.</p>

<h3>What Happens During Mitosis?</h3>

<p>A parent cell divides its nucleus through mitosis to produce two genetically similar daughter nuclei.</p>

<p>The cytoplasm then divides, producing separate cells.</p>

<h3>Why This Increases Growth</h3>

<p>One cell can produce two cells.</p>

<p>Repeated cell division therefore increases the number of cells available to form new tissues.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A meristematic region contains 100 actively dividing cells.</p>

<p>If a group of cells divides and produces additional daughter cells, the total number of cells increases.</p>

<p>Those cells can later elongate and differentiate to form specialised tissues.</p>

<h3>Growth Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Cell division → cell number increases → cells elongate → cells differentiate → tissues develop.</strong></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Cell division alone does not explain all plant growth. Cells must also increase in size and become specialised.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for seedling_growth_by_cell_division', 15),
  (1343, 1343, '
<h2>Cell Elongation in a Growing Seedling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Cell division increases cell number, but cells must also increase in size for substantial growth to occur.</p>

<h3>What Is Cell Elongation?</h3>

<p>Cell elongation is an increase in the length or size of a plant cell.</p>

<p>It is especially important in regions just behind actively dividing cells in growing roots and shoots.</p>

<h3>How Does Elongation Occur?</h3>

<p>Water enters plant cells and contributes to cell expansion.</p>

<p>The cell wall can loosen sufficiently to allow expansion while the cell remains supported.</p>

<p>The vacuole can enlarge and occupy a large proportion of the cell.</p>

<h3>Why This Increases Plant Size</h3>

<p>When many cells elongate, the tissue becomes longer.</p>

<p>This contributes to the increase in root or shoot length.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A group of cells each increases from 0.2 mm to 0.8 mm in length.</p>

<p>Each cell has increased by:</p>

<p>0.8 − 0.2 = 0.6 mm</p>

<p>If many cells undergo similar elongation, the total length of the growing tissue increases substantially.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Cell division → more cells.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Cell elongation → larger cells.</strong></p>

<p>Both contribute to plant growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for cell_elongation_seedling_growth', 15),
  (1344, 1344, '
<h2>Cell Differentiation During Seedling Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>New plant cells do not all remain identical. Many become specialised for particular functions through cell differentiation.</p>

<h3>What Is Differentiation?</h3>

<p>Differentiation is the process by which a cell develops specialised structures and functions.</p>

<h3>Examples in a Young Plant</h3>

<p>Some cells differentiate into:</p>

<p>• root hair cells for absorption</p>

<p>• xylem cells for water and mineral transport</p>

<p>• phloem cells for transport of assimilates</p>

<p>• specialised cells in leaves for photosynthesis and gas exchange</p>

<h3>Why Differentiation Matters</h3>

<p>A growing plant requires different tissues to perform different jobs.</p>

<p>For example, a root hair cell needs adaptations for absorption, while a xylem vessel needs adaptations for water transport.</p>

<h3>Growth Sequence</h3>

<p>Cells produced by division can undergo elongation and then differentiation.</p>

<p><strong>Division → elongation → differentiation → specialised tissues.</strong></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Differentiation does not normally mean that the cell receives completely different DNA.</p>

<p>Different genes are expressed in different cells, producing different structures and functions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for cell_differentiation_in_seedling_growth', 15),
  (1345, 1345, '
<h2>Root Apical Meristem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The root apical meristem is a region of actively dividing cells near the tip of a growing root.</p>

<p>It is important for primary growth because it produces new cells that contribute to an increase in root length.</p>

<h3>Cell Division</h3>

<p>Cells in the meristem divide by mitosis.</p>

<p>This increases the number of cells available for growth and development.</p>

<h3>What Happens to the New Cells?</h3>

<p>Cells produced by the meristem do not all remain permanently identical.</p>

<p>As they move away from the actively dividing region, they can elongate and differentiate.</p>

<h3>Root Growth Is Therefore a Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Cell division → cell elongation → cell differentiation.</strong></p>

<p>These processes work together to produce a growing and functioning root.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A chemical prevents cells in the root apical meristem from undergoing mitosis.</p>

<p><strong>What would you expect?</strong></p>

<p>Fewer new cells would be produced, so normal primary root growth would be reduced.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The root tip is not simply one mass of identical dividing cells.</p>

<p>Different regions behind the meristem perform different roles as cells mature.</p>
  
<hr>

<h2>Root Apical Meristem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The root apical meristem is a region of actively dividing cells near the tip of a growing root.</p>

<h3>Function</h3>

<p>Cells divide by mitosis, increasing the number of cells available for root growth.</p>

<p>New cells then move away from the meristem and can elongate and differentiate.</p>

<h3>Connection to Root Growth</h3>

<p>The root grows longer as new cells are produced and existing cells elongate.</p>

<p>Further from the tip, cells differentiate into specialised types including root hair cells and conducting tissues.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>If mitosis in the root apical meristem increases, more new cells can be produced.</p>

<p>If those cells subsequently elongate, the root can increase in length.</p>

<h3>Root Tip Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Root cap → cell division region → elongation region → differentiation region.</strong></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The root apical meristem should not be confused with the root cap.</p>

<p>The root cap protects the tip; the meristem produces new cells.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_apical_meristem', 15),
  (1346, 1346, '
<h2>Shoot Apical Meristem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The shoot apical meristem is a region of actively dividing cells at or near the growing tip of a shoot.</p>

<p>It contributes to primary growth by producing new cells that allow the shoot to increase in length.</p>

<h3>Cell Division</h3>

<p>Cells in the shoot apical meristem divide by mitosis.</p>

<p>The newly produced cells can later elongate and differentiate.</p>

<h3>What Can Develop?</h3>

<p>Growth from the shoot apex contributes to the formation of new shoot tissues, leaves and other structures associated with the developing shoot.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A young plant has its shoot tip severely damaged.</p>

<p><strong>Why might shoot growth be affected?</strong></p>

<p>The shoot tip contains the shoot apical meristem and other growing tissues. Damage can therefore reduce the production of new cells needed for normal primary growth.</p>

<h3>Comparison With Root Apical Meristem</h3>

<p><strong>Root apical meristem → primary growth of roots.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Shoot apical meristem → primary growth of shoots.</strong></p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>Meristems contain cells capable of repeated division, making them essential regions for plant growth.</p>
  
<hr>

<h2>Shoot Apical Meristem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The shoot apical meristem is a region of actively dividing cells near the tip of a growing shoot.</p>

<h3>What Does It Do?</h3>

<p>Cells produced by the shoot apical meristem contribute to primary growth of the shoot.</p>

<p>New cells can later elongate and differentiate into specialised tissues.</p>

<h3>Primary Growth</h3>

<p>Primary growth increases the length of a plant.</p>

<p>In shoots, the shoot apical meristem contributes to this increase in length.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>If cells in the shoot apical meristem divide and produce new cells, these cells can later enlarge and become specialised.</p>

<p>As this process continues, the shoot becomes longer and develops new tissues and leaves.</p>

<h3>Comparison with the Root</h3>

<p><strong>Root apical meristem → primary growth of the root.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Shoot apical meristem → primary growth of the shoot.</strong></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The meristem produces new cells; it does not mean that every cell at the shoot tip remains permanently undifferentiated.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for shoot_apical_meristem', 15),
  (1347, 1347, '
<h2>Zones of Growth in a Root</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A growing root contains regions where different stages of growth occur.</p>

<h3>Zone of Cell Division</h3>

<p>This region is close to the root tip and contains actively dividing meristematic cells.</p>

<p>Mitosis increases the number of cells.</p>

<h3>Zone of Elongation</h3>

<p>Behind the division region, newly formed cells increase in length.</p>

<p>This elongation contributes strongly to the increase in root length.</p>

<h3>Zone of Differentiation</h3>

<p>Further behind the elongation region, cells become more specialised.</p>

<p>For example, some cells develop into root hair cells.</p>

<h3>Why the Zones Matter</h3>

<p>Root growth is not caused by every cell dividing and enlarging at the same time.</p>

<p>Different regions have different dominant activities.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A scientist examines three regions of a root.</p>

<p>Region A contains many small cells undergoing mitosis.</p>

<p>Region B contains cells that are becoming much longer.</p>

<p>Region C contains specialised cells such as root hair cells.</p>

<p><strong>Identify the regions.</strong></p>

<p>Region A → division zone.</p>

<p>Region B → elongation zone.</p>

<p>Region C → differentiation zone.</p>

<h3>Memory Pattern</h3>

<p><strong>Divide → elongate → differentiate.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for zones_of_root_growth', 15),
  (1348, 1348, '
<h2>Lateral Meristems and Secondary Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Lateral meristems are regions of actively dividing cells that contribute to an increase in the thickness of stems and roots.</p>

<h3>Vascular Cambium</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium is an important lateral meristem.</p>

<p>It produces new vascular tissues and contributes to the increase in stem or root girth.</p>

<h3>Why This Produces Thickness</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium is positioned around existing vascular tissues.</p>

<p>As it produces additional cells, tissues are added around the existing axis.</p>

<p>This contributes to radial growth, meaning growth outward in thickness.</p>

<h3>Primary vs Lateral Meristems</h3>

<p><strong>Apical meristems → mainly length.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Lateral meristems → mainly thickness.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A woody stem increases in diameter each year.</p>

<p><strong>Which type of meristem is mainly responsible?</strong></p>

<p>A lateral meristem, particularly the vascular cambium, contributes to this secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Meristem does not mean "one specific structure."</p>

<p>Meristems are regions containing cells capable of repeated division.</p>

<p>Different meristems contribute to different patterns of plant growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for lateral_meristems', 15),
  (1349, 1349, '
<h2>Growth Zones of a Root: Linking the Processes</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The tip of a growing root contains different regions where different growth processes are prominent.</p>

<h3>1. Root Cap</h3>

<p>The root cap protects the delicate growing tip as the root pushes through soil.</p>

<h3>2. Zone of Cell Division</h3>

<p>Near the root tip, meristematic cells divide repeatedly by mitosis.</p>

<p>This increases the number of cells.</p>

<h3>3. Zone of Elongation</h3>

<p>Cells produced by division increase in length.</p>

<p>This helps push the root farther through the soil.</p>

<h3>4. Zone of Differentiation</h3>

<p>Cells become specialised for particular functions.</p>

<p>Root hair cells develop in this region.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Division → elongation → differentiation.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root cell is found in a region where cells are rapidly dividing.</p>

<p>It is most likely in the zone of cell division.</p>

<p>If the cells are becoming longer, the zone is mainly the elongation zone.</p>

<p>If cells are developing specialised structures such as root hairs, the region is the differentiation zone.</p>

<h3>Exam Strategy</h3>

<p>When a question gives a description of cells, identify the process first.</p>

<p>Rapid mitosis → division zone.</p>

<p>Increase in length → elongation zone.</p>

<p>Specialisation → differentiation zone.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for growth_zones_root_sequence', 15),
  (1350, 1350, '
<h2>Meristematic Cells and Differentiation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Meristematic cells are found in regions where plants are actively growing.</p>

<p>They have the ability to divide repeatedly by mitosis.</p>

<h3>What Happens to Their Daughter Cells?</h3>

<p>After division, some daughter cells remain capable of division while others begin to enlarge and differentiate.</p>

<p>This allows the plant to produce new specialised tissues.</p>

<h3>Why Meristems Are Important</h3>

<p>Meristems provide a continuing supply of new cells.</p>

<p>Without cell production in growing regions, the plant would not be able to maintain continued growth in the same way.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>A cell produced near a root meristem may eventually become part of a specialised root tissue.</p>

<p>The sequence is:</p>

<p><strong>Meristematic cell → mitosis → daughter cell → enlargement → differentiation → specialised cell.</strong></p>

<h3>Important Concept</h3>

<p>Not every daughter cell immediately becomes fully specialised.</p>

<p>Plant development involves coordinated cell division, enlargement and differentiation.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for meristematic_cells_and_differentiation', 15),
  (1351, 1351, '
<h2>Zones of Growth in a Root</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A root tip contains regions with different growth activities.</p>

<h3>1. Zone of Cell Division</h3>

<p>This region is close to the root apical meristem.</p>

<p>Cells divide rapidly by mitosis, increasing cell number.</p>

<h3>2. Zone of Elongation</h3>

<p>Cells behind the division region increase in length.</p>

<p>Cell elongation contributes strongly to an increase in root length.</p>

<h3>3. Zone of Differentiation</h3>

<p>Cells become specialised.</p>

<p>Root hair cells develop in this region, increasing the root''s absorbing surface.</p>

<h3>Why the Zones Work Together</h3>

<p>The regions are not independent processes.</p>

<p>New cells are produced by division, then many enlarge and later become specialised.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Division → elongation → differentiation.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>If mitotic activity at the root tip is reduced, fewer new cells are produced.</p>

<p>This can reduce the supply of cells available for later elongation and differentiation.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>The root cap protects the root tip, but it is not the main region responsible for producing new cells.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for root_growth_zones', 15),
  (1352, 1352, '
<h2>Phototropism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Phototropism is the directional growth response of a plant part to light.</p>

<p>Young shoots commonly show positive phototropism because they grow towards a light source.</p>

<h3>Why Shoots Grow Towards Light</h3>

<p>Light from one direction causes auxin to become unevenly distributed in the shoot.</p>

<p>In a young shoot, auxin promotes cell elongation.</p>

<p>The side away from the light receives more auxin.</p>

<p>Cells on that side elongate more than cells on the illuminated side.</p>

<p>This unequal growth causes the shoot to bend towards the light.</p>

<h3>Complete Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Directional light → uneven auxin distribution → unequal cell elongation → shoot bends → growth towards light.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling is placed beside a window. After several days, its shoot bends towards the window.</p>

<p><strong>Why does this happen?</strong></p>

<p>Light comes mainly from one direction. Auxin becomes unevenly distributed, causing greater elongation on the shaded side. The shoot therefore bends towards the light.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The plant is not moving the whole shoot toward the light.</p>

<p>The bending occurs because the two sides of the growing region elongate at different rates.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism', 15),
  (1353, 1353, '
<h2>Positive and Negative Tropism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A tropism can be described as positive or negative depending on the direction of growth relative to the stimulus.</p>

<h3>Positive Tropism</h3>

<p>Positive tropism means growth towards the stimulus.</p>

<p>For example, a young shoot growing towards light shows positive phototropism.</p>

<h3>Negative Tropism</h3>

<p>Negative tropism means growth away from the stimulus.</p>

<p>For example, roots commonly show negative phototropism because they tend to grow away from light.</p>

<h3>Do Not Confuse Direction of Growth</h3>

<p>The words positive and negative do not mean "good" and "bad."</p>

<p>They describe the direction of growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root grows away from a light source.</p>

<p><strong>What type of response is this?</strong></p>

<p>It is negative phototropism.</p>

<p>The stimulus is light, and the root grows away from it.</p>

<h3>Another Example</h3>

<p>A shoot grows towards light.</p>

<p>This is positive phototropism.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Towards stimulus → positive.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Away from stimulus → negative.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for positive_and_negative_tropism', 15),
  (1354, 1354, '
<h2>Gravitropism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Gravitropism, also called geotropism, is the directional growth response of a plant part to gravity.</p>

<h3>Roots</h3>

<p>Roots generally grow in the direction of gravity.</p>

<p>This is called positive gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Shoots</h3>

<p>Shoots generally grow against the direction of gravity.</p>

<p>This is called negative gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Why This Matters</h3>

<p>Root growth towards gravity helps roots extend into the soil.</p>

<p>Shoot growth against gravity helps shoots emerge from the soil and grow upward.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seed germinates while lying on its side.</p>

<p>After several days, the root curves downward and the shoot curves upward.</p>

<p><strong>What explains this response?</strong></p>

<p>The root shows positive gravitropism, while the shoot shows negative gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Important Point</h3>

<p>Gravitropism is a growth response, not a rapid movement of the whole plant.</p>

<p>The curvature develops because growth is unequal on different sides of the organ.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for gravitropism', 15),
  (1355, 1355, '
<h2>Investigating Phototropism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Phototropism can be investigated by exposing young seedlings to light from controlled directions.</p>

<h3>Basic Experiment</h3>

<p>Grow several young seedlings under similar conditions.</p>

<p>Place them in a box with an opening on one side so that light enters mainly from one direction.</p>

<p>Observe the direction in which the shoots grow.</p>

<h3>Control</h3>

<p>A useful comparison group can receive light more evenly from all directions.</p>

<p>This helps determine whether directional light is responsible for the bending response.</p>

<h3>Variables</h3>

<p><strong>Independent variable:</strong> direction of light.</p>

<p><strong>Dependent variable:</strong> direction or degree of shoot curvature.</p>

<p><strong>Control variables:</strong> seedling species, age, water supply, temperature, growing medium and observation period.</p>

<h3>Measuring Curvature</h3>

<p>The response can be made more quantitative by measuring the angle through which the shoot bends.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Seedlings exposed to light from one side bend towards the opening. Seedlings receiving uniform light remain approximately upright.</p>

<p><strong>What conclusion is supported?</strong></p>

<p>Directional light is associated with directional growth of the shoots.</p>

<h3>Evaluation</h3>

<p>Use several seedlings rather than one.</p>

<p>Repeat the experiment.</p>

<p>Measure curvature rather than relying only on words such as "bent a lot."</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism_experiment', 15),
  (1356, 1356, '
<h2>Interpreting Evidence from a Phototropism Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Experimental observations should be used to distinguish between possible explanations for plant growth.</p>

<h3>Example Data</h3>

<p>Four groups of seedlings are exposed to different conditions.</p>

<p><strong>Group A:</strong> light from all directions → shoot remains approximately upright.</p>

<p><strong>Group B:</strong> light from one side → shoot bends towards light.</p>

<p><strong>Group C:</strong> one-sided light but shoot tip removed → little or no normal phototropic response.</p>

<p><strong>Group D:</strong> one-sided light with the shoot tip covered by an opaque cap → reduced phototropic response.</p>

<h3>What Does This Suggest?</h3>

<p>Groups B, C and D suggest that the shoot tip plays an important role in detecting or coordinating the response to directional light.</p>

<h3>Why the Control Matters</h3>

<p>Group A shows what happens when there is no strong directional light.</p>

<p>Without this comparison, it would be harder to determine whether the bending was caused by directional light.</p>

<h3>Evidence vs Explanation</h3>

<p><strong>Evidence:</strong> the shoot bent towards the light.</p>

<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> directional light affects auxin distribution and therefore causes unequal growth.</p>

<p>The explanation should be consistent with the experimental evidence.</p>

<h3>Exam Skill</h3>

<p>Do not claim that an experiment proves every detail of the mechanism unless the experiment actually measured that mechanism.</p>

<p>An experiment showing bending provides evidence for a growth response. A separate investigation may be needed to directly measure auxin distribution.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism_experimental_evidence', 15),
  (1357, 1357, '
<h2>Phototropism and Gravitropism Compared</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Phototropism and gravitropism are both directional growth responses, but they respond to different stimuli.</p>

<h3>Phototropism</h3>

<p><strong>Stimulus:</strong> light.</p>

<p>A young shoot commonly grows towards light.</p>

<p>This is positive phototropism.</p>

<h3>Gravitropism</h3>

<p><strong>Stimulus:</strong> gravity.</p>

<p>Roots commonly grow in the direction of gravity.</p>

<p>This is positive gravitropism.</p>

<p>Shoots commonly grow against gravity.</p>

<p>This is negative gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Both Are Growth Responses</h3>

<p>Neither response requires the plant to move its entire body.</p>

<p>Growth rates differ between sides of the growing organ, causing curvature.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling lying horizontally develops a root that curves downward and a shoot that curves upward towards a light source.</p>

<p>The root is showing positive gravitropism.</p>

<p>The shoot is showing negative gravitropism in response to gravity and may also show positive phototropism in response to light.</p>

<h3>Exam Strategy</h3>

<p>When you see a tropism question, identify:</p>

<p><strong>1. The stimulus.</strong></p>

<p><strong>2. The plant organ.</strong></p>

<p><strong>3. The direction of growth.</strong></p>

<p>Then determine whether the response is positive or negative.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism_vs_gravitropism', 15),
  (1358, 1358, '
<h2>Phototropism in Shoots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Phototropism is directional growth in response to light.</p>

<p>Shoots generally show positive phototropism, meaning they grow toward a light source.</p>

<h3>Step-by-Step Explanation</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Light comes mainly from one side.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Auxin becomes distributed unevenly in the shoot.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> More auxin accumulates on the shaded side of the shoot.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Cells on the shaded side elongate more.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> The shoot bends toward the light.</p>

<h3>Why the Shoot Bends</h3>

<p>The shoot does not bend because the light physically pushes it.</p>

<p>It bends because the two sides grow at different rates.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling is placed beside a window.</p>

<p>Light enters mainly from the right.</p>

<p>The shaded left side receives more auxin.</p>

<p>The cells on the left elongate more.</p>

<p>The shoot curves toward the right, where the light is coming from.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Always explain phototropism as a difference in growth between two sides of the shoot.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism_shoots', 15),
  (1359, 1359, '
<h2>Investigating Phototropism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Phototropism can be investigated by growing seedlings under controlled lighting conditions.</p>

<h3>Basic Investigation</h3>

<p>Place similar seedlings in controlled conditions.</p>

<p>Expose one group to light from one direction.</p>

<p>Keep other important conditions constant.</p>

<p>Observe the direction of shoot growth over time.</p>

<h3>Variables</h3>

<p><strong>Independent variable:</strong> direction or pattern of light exposure.</p>

<p><strong>Dependent variable:</strong> direction or degree of shoot bending.</p>

<p><strong>Control variables:</strong> plant species, age, water supply, temperature, growing medium and duration.</p>

<h3>Control Group</h3>

<p>A useful control may receive light from a different arrangement, such as light distributed more evenly around the plant.</p>

<p>This helps determine whether directional light is responsible for the bending.</p>

<h3>Improving Reliability</h3>

<p>Use several seedlings rather than one.</p>

<p>Repeat the experiment.</p>

<p>Calculate a mean where appropriate.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>A good experiment changes one main factor while keeping other relevant factors controlled.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism_experiment_setup', 15),
  (1360, 1360, '
<h2>Interpreting Evidence from a Phototropism Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Experimental observations must be used as evidence rather than simply described without interpretation.</p>

<h3>Example Results</h3>

<p>Five seedlings are exposed to light from one side.</p>

<p>After several days, all five shoots bend toward the light.</p>

<p>A control group exposed to approximately equal light from all directions remains relatively upright.</p>

<h3>What Does the Evidence Show?</h3>

<p>The treatment group consistently bends toward the directional light.</p>

<p>The control group does not show the same directional bending.</p>

<p>This supports the conclusion that directional light is associated with phototropic bending.</p>

<h3>What the Evidence Does Not Prove</h3>

<p>The observation alone does not show every step of the mechanism.</p>

<p>To explain the mechanism, additional biological knowledge about auxin and differential cell elongation is required.</p>

<h3>Strong Conclusion</h3>

<p><strong>Seedlings exposed to directional light grew toward the light, whereas the control seedlings did not show the same directional response. This supports the conclusion that shoots exhibit positive phototropism.</strong></p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Do not claim more than the experiment actually measured.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism_evidence', 15),
  (1361, 1361, '
<h2>Gravitropism in Roots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Gravitropism is directional growth in response to gravity.</p>

<p>Roots generally show positive gravitropism because they grow in the direction of gravity.</p>

<h3>Step-by-Step</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Gravity provides a directional stimulus.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Auxin becomes distributed unevenly in the root.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> The response to auxin in roots differs from the response in shoots.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Unequal growth occurs between the two sides.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> The root bends in the direction of gravity.</p>

<h3>Important Difference</h3>

<p>Auxin does not have exactly the same effect in every plant tissue.</p>

<p>In roots, relatively high auxin concentrations can inhibit elongation.</p>

<p>This difference is important when comparing root and shoot tropisms.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A germinating seed is placed horizontally.</p>

<p>After several hours, the root curves downward.</p>

<p>The response is positive gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p><strong>Root growth direction → gravity.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Unequal growth → bending.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for gravitropism_roots', 15),
  (1362, 1362, '
<h2>Gravitropism in Shoots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Shoots generally show negative gravitropism.</p>

<p>Negative gravitropism means growth away from the direction of gravity.</p>

<h3>What Happens?</h3>

<p>When a shoot is placed horizontally, gravity causes an unequal distribution of auxin.</p>

<p>The resulting difference in cell elongation causes the shoot to curve upward.</p>

<h3>Why This Is Called Negative</h3>

<p>The shoot grows away from the direction in which gravity acts.</p>

<p>The term "negative" describes the direction of the growth response, not whether the response is harmful.</p>

<h3>Compare Root and Shoot Responses</h3>

<p><strong>Roots → positive gravitropism.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Shoots → negative gravitropism.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling is placed horizontally.</p>

<p>After several hours, the root curves downward while the shoot curves upward.</p>

<p>The root is showing positive gravitropism.</p>

<p>The shoot is showing negative gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>"Negative gravitropism" does not mean the shoot stops growing.</p>

<p>It means the direction of growth is away from gravity.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for gravitropism_shoots', 15),
  (1363, 1363, '
<h2>Phototropism and Gravitropism Compared</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Phototropism and gravitropism are both examples of tropisms.</p>

<p>A tropism is a directional growth response to a stimulus.</p>

<h3>Phototropism</h3>

<p>The stimulus is light.</p>

<p>Shoots generally show positive phototropism.</p>

<p>The shoot grows toward light.</p>

<h3>Gravitropism</h3>

<p>The stimulus is gravity.</p>

<p>Roots generally show positive gravitropism.</p>

<p>Shoots generally show negative gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Comparison Table</h3>

<p><strong>Phototropism → light → shoot toward light.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Gravitropism → gravity → root toward gravity.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Gravitropism → gravity → shoot away from gravity.</strong></p>

<h3>Common Mechanism</h3>

<p>Both responses can involve unequal auxin distribution and unequal cell elongation.</p>

<p>The difference is the stimulus and the resulting direction of growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling grows beside a window while its root grows downward.</p>

<p>The shoot is showing positive phototropism.</p>

<p>The root is showing positive gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>First identify the stimulus.</p>

<p>Then identify the direction of growth.</p>

<p>Finally explain how unequal growth causes bending.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism_gravitropism_comparison', 15),
  (1364, 1364, '
<h2>Control Experiments in Phototropism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A control experiment provides a comparison that helps determine whether a particular factor caused the observed response.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>Two groups of similar seedlings are used.</p>

<p><strong>Group A:</strong> receives light mainly from one side.</p>

<p><strong>Group B:</strong> receives light approximately equally from all directions.</p>

<p>Both groups receive the same water, temperature, growing medium and duration.</p>

<h3>Expected Observation</h3>

<p>Group A is expected to bend toward the light.</p>

<p>Group B should show much less directional bending.</p>

<h3>Why Group B Matters</h3>

<p>If both groups were exposed to the same general light intensity but only Group A received directional light, the comparison helps isolate the effect of light direction.</p>

<h3>Fair Test</h3>

<p>The seedlings should be as similar as possible.</p>

<p>Other conditions should remain constant.</p>

<p>The experiment should use several seedlings and preferably be repeated.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>A control does not mean "a plant that receives no treatment."</p>

<p>A control is a comparison condition that helps test the effect of the variable being investigated.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism_control_experiment', 15),
  (1365, 1365, '
<h2>Variables in a Tropism Investigation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Correctly identifying variables is essential when designing a plant-growth experiment.</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>The independent variable is the factor deliberately changed by the researcher.</p>

<p>For a phototropism investigation, this could be the direction from which light reaches the plant.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>The dependent variable is the response measured.</p>

<p>This could be the angle through which the shoot bends.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>These are factors kept constant so that the experiment tests the intended variable fairly.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>plant species, starting size, temperature, water supply, growing medium and duration.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student investigates whether light direction affects shoot bending.</p>

<p><strong>Independent variable:</strong> direction of light.</p>

<p><strong>Dependent variable:</strong> angle of shoot bending.</p>

<p><strong>Control variables:</strong> water, temperature, plant species and duration.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>The direction of bending is not the independent variable if it is what the plant does in response to the treatment.</p>

<p>It is the dependent variable.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism_experiment_variables', 15),
  (1366, 1366, '
<h2>Improving a Phototropism Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A basic experiment can be improved to produce stronger and more reliable evidence.</p>

<h3>Use More Plants</h3>

<p>Using several seedlings reduces the influence of unusual individual plants.</p>

<h3>Repeat the Investigation</h3>

<p>Repeating the experiment allows consistent patterns to be identified.</p>

<h3>Calculate a Mean</h3>

<p>If several measurements are collected, a mean can provide a useful summary.</p>

<p><strong>Mean = total of measurements ÷ number of measurements.</strong></p>

<h3>Standardise the Starting Conditions</h3>

<p>Seedlings should have similar age and starting size.</p>

<p>Otherwise, differences in growth may be caused by the starting condition rather than the treatment.</p>

<h3>Measure Quantitatively</h3>

<p>Instead of simply recording "bent" or "did not bend," measure the angle of bending.</p>

<p>Quantitative measurements provide more detailed evidence.</p>

<h3>Control Other Conditions</h3>

<p>Temperature, water, nutrients and duration should be controlled.</p>

<h3>Strong Experimental Design</h3>

<p><strong>Change one factor → control other important factors → use repeats → collect measurements → calculate a mean → compare groups.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for phototropism_experiment_improvements', 15),
  (1367, 1367, '
<h2>Growth Hormones in Plants</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth is controlled partly by chemical substances called plant growth hormones or plant growth regulators.</p>

<p>These substances can influence processes such as cell division, cell elongation, root and shoot growth, and responses to environmental stimuli.</p>

<h3>Auxin</h3>

<p>Auxin is an important plant hormone involved in growth, particularly cell elongation in shoots.</p>

<p>Auxin is produced in growing regions such as shoot tips.</p>

<h3>Unequal Distribution</h3>

<p>When light comes mainly from one direction, auxin becomes distributed unevenly in the shoot.</p>

<p>This contributes to unequal cell elongation and causes the shoot to bend.</p>

<h3>Why Hormones Matter</h3>

<p>Hormones coordinate growth rather than simply providing the material from which the plant is built.</p>

<p>They act as chemical signals that influence how cells behave.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A young shoot is exposed to light from one side and bends towards the light.</p>

<p><strong>What role does auxin play?</strong></p>

<p>Unequal distribution of auxin causes unequal growth of cells on the two sides of the shoot, producing bending.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p><strong>Hormone signal → altered cell behaviour → unequal growth → visible plant response.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_hormones', 15),
  (1368, 1368, '
<h2>Auxin Distribution During Phototropism</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The direction of light affects how auxin is distributed in a young shoot.</p>

<p>When light shines from one side, auxin moves towards the shaded side of the shoot.</p>

<p>The shaded side therefore has a greater concentration of auxin than the illuminated side.</p>

<h3>Effect on Shoot Cells</h3>

<p>In shoots, auxin promotes cell elongation.</p>

<p>The higher auxin concentration on the shaded side causes cells there to elongate more.</p>

<p>The shaded side becomes longer than the illuminated side.</p>

<p>This difference in length causes the shoot to curve towards the light.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Light shines from the left side of a young shoot.</p>

<p>Auxin becomes more concentrated on the right side.</p>

<p>The right side elongates more.</p>

<p>The right side therefore becomes longer than the left side.</p>

<p>The shoot bends towards the left, where the light is coming from.</p>

<h3>Reasoning Rule</h3>

<p>Do not stop at "auxin causes bending."</p>

<p>The complete explanation is:</p>

<p><strong>Unequal auxin distribution → unequal elongation → unequal side lengths → bending.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for auxin_distribution_in_phototropism', 15),
  (1369, 1369, '
<h2>Auxin and Gravitropism in Roots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Auxin also contributes to the gravitropic response of roots.</p>

<p>When a root is placed horizontally, gravity causes auxin to become redistributed so that more auxin accumulates on the lower side of the root.</p>

<h3>Important Difference Between Root and Shoot</h3>

<p>Auxin does not have exactly the same growth effect in roots as it does in shoots.</p>

<p>In roots, a relatively high auxin concentration inhibits cell elongation.</p>

<p>The lower side therefore elongates less than the upper side.</p>

<h3>How Bending Occurs</h3>

<p>The upper side elongates more.</p>

<p>The lower side elongates less.</p>

<p>This unequal growth causes the root to curve downward.</p>

<h3>Complete Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Gravity → auxin redistribution → more auxin on lower side → reduced elongation there → greater elongation on upper side → root curves downward.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A horizontal root begins to curve downward.</p>

<p><strong>Why does unequal auxin distribution cause this?</strong></p>

<p>More auxin accumulates on the lower side, where it inhibits elongation. The upper side elongates more, causing the root to bend downward.</p>

<h3>Critical Exam Point</h3>

<p>Do not apply the shoot rule directly to roots.</p>

<p>In shoots, auxin generally promotes elongation.</p>

<p>In roots, high auxin concentration can inhibit elongation.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for auxin_and_gravitropism_in_roots', 15),
  (1370, 1370, '
<h2>Auxin and Cell Elongation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Auxin is a plant growth hormone that can influence the elongation of cells, especially in young shoots.</p>

<h3>How Cell Elongation Produces Growth</h3>

<p>A young plant cell can increase in length.</p>

<p>Water enters the cell, increasing cell volume and helping the cell expand.</p>

<p>Auxin helps regulate this process.</p>

<p>As many cells elongate, the plant organ becomes longer.</p>

<h3>Growth Is Not Just Cell Division</h3>

<p>Cell division increases the number of cells.</p>

<p>Cell elongation increases the size of existing cells.</p>

<p>Both processes can contribute to plant growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A growing shoot contains 1,000 cells. Over time, the number of cells changes only slightly, but many existing cells become much longer.</p>

<p><strong>Can the shoot still increase in length?</strong></p>

<p>Yes.</p>

<p>Cell elongation can increase the length of the shoot even when cell number does not increase greatly.</p>

<h3>Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Auxin influence → cell elongation → cells become longer → plant organ increases in length.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for auxin_and_cell_elongation', 15),
  (1371, 1371, '
<h2>Auxin and Root Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Auxin affects roots differently from shoots.</p>

<p>Roots are highly sensitive to auxin.</p>

<p>A relatively high concentration of auxin can inhibit root cell elongation.</p>

<p>A lower concentration can promote root growth within an appropriate range.</p>

<h3>Why This Matters</h3>

<p>The response of a root depends on auxin concentration.</p>

<p>This means it is incorrect to say simply that "auxin always increases growth."</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two root regions receive different auxin concentrations.</p>

<p>Region A receives a low concentration within its growth-promoting range.</p>

<p>Region B receives a much higher concentration.</p>

<p>Region B shows less elongation.</p>

<p><strong>Why?</strong></p>

<p>Roots are sensitive to auxin, and a high auxin concentration can inhibit root cell elongation.</p>

<h3>Important Comparison</h3>

<p><strong>Shoots:</strong> auxin generally promotes elongation within the normal physiological range.</p>

<p><strong>Roots:</strong> high auxin concentrations can inhibit elongation.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Never write "more auxin always means more growth."</p>

<p>The effect depends on the organ and the concentration.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for auxin_and_root_growth', 15),
  (1372, 1372, '
<h2>Auxin Concentration and Growth Response</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The relationship between auxin concentration and growth is not simply a straight line.</p>

<p>Different concentrations can produce different growth responses.</p>

<h3>Low Concentration</h3>

<p>A low auxin concentration may promote growth in a particular plant organ.</p>

<h3>Optimum Range</h3>

<p>There may be a concentration range at which growth is greatest under the conditions tested.</p>

<h3>High Concentration</h3>

<p>Beyond an appropriate concentration, auxin can inhibit growth.</p>

<h3>Why Graphs Matter</h3>

<p>An experiment can measure growth at several auxin concentrations.</p>

<p>The results can then be plotted to determine the relationship.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root experiment produces the following pattern:</p>

<p>0 units auxin → 4 mm growth</p>

<p>1 unit → 7 mm growth</p>

<p>2 units → 9 mm growth</p>

<p>5 units → 5 mm growth</p>

<p>10 units → 1 mm growth</p>

<p><strong>What does the data show?</strong></p>

<p>Growth increased up to 2 units, then decreased as auxin concentration increased further.</p>

<h3>Correct Conclusion</h3>

<p>Under the conditions tested, 2 units produced the greatest measured root growth.</p>

<p>Do not conclude that 2 units is universally optimal for every root or every condition.</p>

<h3>Scientific Language</h3>

<p>Use phrases such as <strong>"under the conditions tested"</strong> when the experiment has limited conditions.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for auxin_concentration_and_growth', 15),
  (1373, 1373, '
<h2>Auxin and Apical Dominance</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Apical dominance is the tendency of the main shoot tip to suppress the growth of some lateral buds.</p>

<h3>Role of the Shoot Tip</h3>

<p>The shoot tip produces auxin.</p>

<p>Auxin contributes to the maintenance of apical dominance.</p>

<p>As a result, the main shoot can continue growing while some lateral buds remain less active.</p>

<h3>Removing the Shoot Tip</h3>

<p>If the shoot tip is removed, the major source of auxin from that region is also removed.</p>

<p>Lateral buds may then begin to grow more strongly.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A gardener cuts off the tip of a young shoot.</p>

<p>Several side branches subsequently develop.</p>

<p><strong>What biological explanation fits this observation?</strong></p>

<p>Removing the shoot tip reduces the auxin signal associated with apical dominance, allowing lateral buds to grow more strongly.</p>

<h3>Important Point</h3>

<p>Auxin is not simply a "growth on" chemical.</p>

<p>Its effect depends on where it is produced, its concentration, the tissue involved and interactions with other plant regulators.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for auxin_and_apical_dominance', 15),
  (1374, 1374, '
<h2>Auxin and Root Formation in Cuttings</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Auxin can promote the formation of adventitious roots in suitable plant cuttings.</p>

<p>This is one reason auxin is useful in vegetative propagation.</p>

<h3>What Is a Cutting?</h3>

<p>A cutting is a piece of plant tissue removed from a parent plant and encouraged to form a new plant.</p>

<h3>Role of Auxin</h3>

<p>Applying an appropriate auxin treatment to the base of a cutting can encourage root formation.</p>

<p>Once roots develop, the cutting can absorb water and mineral ions and become established as an independent plant.</p>

<h3>Concentration Matters</h3>

<p>The amount of auxin applied must be appropriate.</p>

<p>More auxin does not automatically produce more roots.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three groups of identical cuttings receive different auxin treatments.</p>

<p>Group A produces few roots.</p>

<p>Group B produces many healthy roots.</p>

<p>Group C produces fewer healthy roots than Group B.</p>

<p><strong>What does this suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Group B received a more suitable auxin treatment under the conditions tested.</p>

<h3>Experimental Control</h3>

<p>A group receiving no added auxin can be used as a control.</p>

<p>The number or mass of roots formed can then be compared between treatments.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for auxin_and_plant_cuttings', 15),
  (1375, 1375, '
<h2>Investigating the Effect of a Growth Hormone</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The effect of a plant growth hormone can be investigated by applying different concentrations and measuring plant growth.</p>

<h3>Experimental Design</h3>

<p>Use similar plants or plant cuttings.</p>

<p>Apply different concentrations of the hormone.</p>

<p>Include a control group receiving no added hormone.</p>

<p>Keep other conditions as constant as possible.</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>The concentration of the hormone.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>A measurable growth response, such as increase in length or number of roots formed.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>Use the same plant species, similar starting size, water supply, temperature, light conditions and experimental duration.</p>

<h3>Repeats</h3>

<p>Several plants should be used at each concentration.</p>

<p>The mean response can then be calculated.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Five cuttings are tested at each auxin concentration.</p>

<p>After 14 days, the number of roots on each cutting is recorded.</p>

<p>The mean number of roots is calculated for each concentration.</p>

<p>The means are then compared.</p>

<h3>Why Mean Is Useful</h3>

<p>Individual cuttings can naturally differ.</p>

<p>Using several cuttings reduces the influence of unusual individual results.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_hormone_experiment', 15),
  (1376, 1376, '
<h2>Plant Growth Hormones: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student places identical young shoots in three conditions.</p>

<p><strong>Plant A:</strong> receives light from all directions.</p>

<p><strong>Plant B:</strong> receives light from one side.</p>

<p><strong>Plant C:</strong> receives light from one side, but the shoot tip is removed.</p>

<p>After several days:</p>

<p>Plant A remains approximately upright.</p>

<p>Plant B bends towards the light.</p>

<p>Plant C shows a greatly reduced bending response.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify the Response</h3>

<p>Plant B shows positive phototropism.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify the Hormone</h3>

<p>Auxin is involved in the growth response.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Explain Plant B</h3>

<p>Directional light causes unequal auxin distribution.</p>

<p>More auxin accumulates on the shaded side.</p>

<p>Cells on that side elongate more.</p>

<p>The unequal elongation causes the shoot to bend towards the light.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Explain Plant C</h3>

<p>Removing the shoot tip removes an important region involved in detecting and coordinating the response.</p>

<p>The normal phototropic response is therefore greatly reduced.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Explain Plant A</h3>

<p>Light comes from all directions rather than strongly from one side.</p>

<p>There is therefore no strong directional growth signal producing the same bending response.</p>

<h3>Complete Answer</h3>

<p>The young shoot responds to directional light by changing its growth pattern. Auxin becomes unevenly distributed, causing greater elongation on the shaded side. This makes the shoot bend towards the light. Removing the shoot tip reduces the normal response because the shoot tip is important in coordinating phototropic growth.</p>

<h3>Key Tixar Rule</h3>

<p>Do not memorise "auxin causes phototropism."</p>

<p>Understand the chain:</p>

<p><strong>Stimulus → hormone distribution → cell response → unequal growth → visible response.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for plant_growth_hormones_complete_reasoning', 15),
  (1377, 1377, '
<h2>Auxin Production in Plants</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Auxin is a plant growth hormone involved in regulating growth and development.</p>

<p>Auxin is produced in growing regions of plants, including shoot tips.</p>

<h3>Why the Shoot Tip Matters</h3>

<p>The shoot tip is an actively growing region.</p>

<p>Auxin produced there can move to other parts of the shoot and influence cell growth.</p>

<h3>What Auxin Does</h3>

<p>Auxin can stimulate cell elongation in shoots at suitable concentrations.</p>

<p>It therefore helps regulate the direction and pattern of shoot growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A shoot tip is producing auxin.</p>

<p>The auxin moves down the shoot and affects the elongation of cells below the tip.</p>

<p>The result is controlled growth of the shoot.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Auxin is not a nutrient.</p>

<p>It is a chemical messenger that regulates growth processes.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>When explaining auxin, connect it to <strong>growth regulation</strong>, especially cell elongation and directional growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for auxin_production', 15),
  (1378, 1378, '
<h2>Movement of Auxin in Shoots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Auxin produced in a shoot tip can move away from the tip into lower regions of the shoot.</p>

<p>This movement allows the hormone produced in one region to influence growth in another region.</p>

<h3>Why Movement Matters</h3>

<p>If auxin remained only at the point where it was produced, it could not regulate growth throughout the developing shoot in the same way.</p>

<p>Movement allows differences in auxin concentration to develop between different sides of a shoot.</p>

<h3>Connection to Phototropism</h3>

<p>When light comes from one side, auxin becomes distributed unevenly.</p>

<p>This unequal distribution causes unequal cell elongation on the two sides of the shoot.</p>

<p>The difference in growth causes the shoot to bend.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A shoot is illuminated from one side.</p>

<p>Auxin becomes more concentrated on the shaded side.</p>

<p>Cells on the shaded side elongate more.</p>

<p>The shoot bends toward the light.</p>

<h3>Key Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Light from one side → unequal auxin distribution → unequal elongation → bending.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for auxin_movement_in_shoots', 15),
  (1379, 1379, '
<h2>Secondary Growth in Plants</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Secondary growth is an increase in the thickness or girth of stems and roots.</p>

<p>It is particularly important in woody plants.</p>

<h3>Why Secondary Growth Matters</h3>

<p>Increasing stem thickness provides structural support as a plant becomes larger.</p>

<h3>Vascular Cambium</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium is a lateral meristem that produces new vascular tissues.</p>

<p>It contributes to an increase in the thickness of the stem or root.</p>

<h3>Primary vs Secondary Growth</h3>

<p><strong>Primary growth → length.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Secondary growth → thickness or girth.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A tree trunk becomes wider over several years.</p>

<p><strong>Which type of growth is mainly responsible?</strong></p>

<p>Secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not confuse secondary growth with an increase in height.</p>

<p>A stem becoming taller is primarily associated with primary growth.</p>

<p>A stem becoming thicker is associated with secondary growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for secondary_growth_in_plants', 15),
  (1380, 1380, '
<h2>Primary Growth Compared with Secondary Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants can increase in both length and thickness.</p>

<h3>Primary Growth</h3>

<p>Primary growth is an increase in the length of roots and shoots.</p>

<p>It occurs mainly at apical meristems.</p>

<p>It allows roots to extend through the soil and shoots to extend upward.</p>

<h3>Secondary Growth</h3>

<p>Secondary growth is an increase in the thickness or girth of roots and stems.</p>

<p>It occurs mainly through the activity of lateral meristems such as the vascular cambium.</p>

<h3>Direct Comparison</h3>

<p><strong>Primary growth → length.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Secondary growth → thickness.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A tree grows from 2 m to 4 m tall while its trunk also becomes wider.</p>

<p>The increase in height is mainly primary growth.</p>

<p>The increase in trunk thickness is secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not identify growth simply from the age of the plant.</p>

<p>A young plant can undergo primary growth, while an established woody plant can undergo both primary and secondary growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for primary_vs_secondary_growth', 15),
  (1381, 1381, '
<h2>Secondary Growth in Stems</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Secondary growth causes the stem of many woody plants to increase in diameter.</p>

<p>The vascular cambium produces new vascular tissues as the stem increases in girth.</p>

<h3>Why Woody Stems Become Thicker</h3>

<p>As the plant continues growing, additional tissues are produced around the existing stem.</p>

<p>This increases the diameter of the stem.</p>

<h3>Support</h3>

<p>A larger stem can provide structural support for a larger plant.</p>

<p>This is especially important in trees and other long-lived woody plants.</p>

<h3>Transport</h3>

<p>Secondary growth also results in the production of additional vascular tissue.</p>

<p>This supports the plant''s increasing transport requirements as the plant becomes larger.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A young tree is initially thin. Several years later, its trunk has a much larger diameter.</p>

<p><strong>What biological process explains this change?</strong></p>

<p>Secondary growth has increased the girth of the trunk.</p>

<h3>Important Point</h3>

<p>Secondary growth is not simply "the stem growing older."</p>

<p>It is a biological growth process involving lateral meristems and production of additional tissues.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for secondary_growth_in_stems', 15),
  (1382, 1382, '
<h2>Secondary Growth in Roots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Secondary growth can also increase the thickness of roots in plants capable of secondary growth.</p>

<p>This is particularly important in many woody plants.</p>

<h3>What Changes?</h3>

<p>Lateral meristems become active and produce additional tissues.</p>

<p>The root therefore increases in diameter.</p>

<h3>Why Root Thickness Matters</h3>

<p>A thicker root can provide greater structural support and contributes to the development of a larger transport system.</p>

<h3>Primary and Secondary Root Growth</h3>

<p>A young root can first extend in length through primary growth.</p>

<p>As the plant develops, some roots can also increase in thickness through secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A woody tree has roots that extend deeper into the soil and also become thicker.</p>

<p><strong>Which growth processes explain the two changes?</strong></p>

<p>Extension in length is primary growth.</p>

<p>Increase in thickness is secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Key Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Longer root → primary growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Thicker root → secondary growth.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for secondary_growth_in_roots', 15),
  (1383, 1383, '
<h2>Primary and Secondary Growth: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A young tree is measured over several years.</p>

<p>Its height increases from 40 cm to 250 cm.</p>

<p>At the same time, the diameter of its stem increases from 1.5 cm to 8 cm.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Analyse Height</h3>

<p>The tree has increased substantially in length.</p>

<p>This is primary growth.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Analyse Stem Diameter</h3>

<p>The stem has become thicker.</p>

<p>This is secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Identify the Growth Regions</h3>

<p>Primary growth is associated mainly with apical meristems.</p>

<p>Secondary growth is associated mainly with lateral meristems such as the vascular cambium.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Explain Why Both Can Occur</h3>

<p>A plant does not have to choose between primary and secondary growth.</p>

<p>A developing woody plant can extend its roots and shoots while also increasing the thickness of stems and roots.</p>

<h3>Model Answer</h3>

<p>The increase in height represents primary growth because the shoot has increased in length. The increase in stem diameter represents secondary growth because the stem has increased in thickness. Primary growth occurs mainly at apical meristems, while secondary growth involves lateral meristems such as the vascular cambium.</p>

<h3>High-Value Exam Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Length → primary growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Thickness → secondary growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Apical meristem → primary growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Lateral meristem → secondary growth.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for primary_secondary_growth_exam_problem', 15),
  (1384, 1384, '
<h2>Secondary Growth and Lateral Meristems</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Secondary growth is the increase in the thickness or girth of a plant stem or root.</p>

<p>It differs from primary growth, which mainly increases the length of the plant.</p>

<h3>Where Does Secondary Growth Occur?</h3>

<p>Secondary growth occurs through the activity of <strong>lateral meristems</strong>.</p>

<p>Lateral meristems contain cells that can divide by mitosis and produce new cells.</p>

<p>The main lateral meristems involved are the <strong>vascular cambium</strong> and <strong>cork cambium</strong>.</p>

<h3>Vascular Cambium</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium produces new vascular tissues.</p>

<p>It produces secondary xylem towards the inside of the stem and secondary phloem towards the outside.</p>

<h3>Why Does This Increase Girth?</h3>

<p>As new cells are produced and added to the stem, the stem becomes wider.</p>

<h3>Primary vs Secondary Growth</h3>

<p><strong>Primary growth:</strong> increase in length.</p>

<p><strong>Secondary growth:</strong> increase in thickness or girth.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>If a question asks how a woody stem becomes thicker, think of <strong>lateral meristems and secondary growth</strong>, not the shoot apical meristem.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for secondary_growth_lateral_meristems', 15),
  (1385, 1385, '
<h2>Vascular Cambium and the Production of Vascular Tissue</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The <strong>vascular cambium</strong> is a layer of dividing cells involved in secondary growth.</p>

<p>It produces new vascular tissue on both sides of the cambium.</p>

<h3>Towards the Inside</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium produces <strong>secondary xylem</strong> towards the centre of the stem.</p>

<p>Secondary xylem contributes to the woody part of the stem and helps transport water and mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Towards the Outside</h3>

<p>The vascular cambium produces <strong>secondary phloem</strong> towards the outside of the stem.</p>

<p>Phloem transports assimilates such as sucrose.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning</h3>

<p>A woody stem becomes wider as the vascular cambium continues producing new cells.</p>

<p>More secondary xylem is added inside the cambium and secondary phloem is added outside it.</p>

<p>Repeated production of these tissues contributes to an increase in stem girth.</p>

<h3>Key Direction</h3>

<p><strong>Vascular cambium → secondary xylem inward + secondary phloem outward.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for vascular_cambium_secondary_xylem_phloem', 15),
  (1386, 1386, '
<h2>Secondary Growth in Woody Stems</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Woody plants can continue increasing in stem thickness after primary growth has increased the length of the stem.</p>

<p>This thickening is mainly associated with secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Step-by-Step Process</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Lateral meristems remain capable of cell division.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The vascular cambium produces new secondary xylem and secondary phloem.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Repeated production adds tissue to the stem.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The diameter or girth of the stem increases.</p>

<h3>Why Is This Important?</h3>

<p>A thicker woody stem provides structural support as the plant becomes larger.</p>

<p>The additional vascular tissue also contributes to the plant''s transport capacity.</p>

<h3>Exam Application</h3>

<p>If an older tree has a much thicker trunk than a young stem, this difference is not explained simply by elongation.</p>

<p>The increase in trunk diameter results largely from secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The shoot apical meristem is mainly responsible for primary growth.</p>

<p>Lateral meristems are responsible for secondary growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for secondary_growth_woody_stems', 15),
  (1387, 1387, '
<h2>Secondary Growth in Roots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Some roots, particularly roots of woody plants, also undergo secondary growth.</p>

<p>This increases the thickness or girth of the root.</p>

<h3>Primary Root Growth</h3>

<p>The root apical meristem contributes mainly to increase in root length.</p>

<h3>Secondary Root Growth</h3>

<p>Lateral meristems contribute to increase in root thickness.</p>

<p>The vascular cambium produces additional vascular tissue as secondary growth continues.</p>

<h3>Worked Comparison</h3>

<p>A young root extending deeper into soil is showing primary growth.</p>

<p>An older woody root becoming thicker is showing secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Why Thickness Matters</h3>

<p>As a plant becomes larger, thicker roots can provide stronger support and contain additional vascular tissue.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Root apical meristem → mainly length.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Lateral meristems → thickness.</strong></p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for secondary_growth_roots', 15),
  (1388, 1388, '
<h2>Interpreting Primary and Secondary Growth Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Growth measurements can be used to distinguish changes in length from changes in thickness.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>A young stem has:</p>

<p>Initial length = 12 cm</p>

<p>Final length = 20 cm</p>

<p>Initial diameter = 0.5 cm</p>

<p>Final diameter = 0.7 cm</p>

<h3>Change in Length</h3>

<p>20 − 12 = 8 cm</p>

<p>The increase in length is evidence of primary growth.</p>

<h3>Change in Diameter</h3>

<p>0.7 − 0.5 = 0.2 cm</p>

<p>The increase in diameter is evidence of thickening and is associated with secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>A plant can undergo primary and secondary growth at the same time in different regions.</p>

<p>Therefore, primary and secondary growth are not necessarily mutually exclusive.</p>

<h3>Exam Method</h3>

<p>When analysing data, first identify <strong>what was measured</strong>.</p>

<p>Length → primary growth.</p>

<p>Thickness or diameter → secondary growth.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for primary_secondary_growth_data', 15),
  (1389, 1389, '
<h2>Environmental Factors and Secondary Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Secondary growth depends on active cell division and the production of new tissues.</p>

<p>Environmental conditions can therefore affect the rate at which secondary growth occurs.</p>

<h3>Water Availability</h3>

<p>Water is required for normal plant metabolism and cell expansion.</p>

<p>Severe water shortage can reduce plant growth.</p>

<h3>Mineral Availability</h3>

<p>Plants require mineral ions to make important substances and maintain normal growth.</p>

<p>Severe mineral deficiency can therefore limit growth.</p>

<h3>Temperature</h3>

<p>Temperature affects enzyme-controlled metabolic processes.</p>

<p>Very low temperatures can slow metabolic reactions, while excessively high temperatures can damage cells and enzymes.</p>

<h3>Interpreting Evidence</h3>

<p>If two groups of woody plants show different increases in stem diameter, the difference may be related to environmental conditions if those conditions were experimentally controlled and compared.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not claim that one environmental factor caused the difference unless the investigation controlled other important variables.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for secondary_growth_environmental_effects', 15),
  (1390, 1390, '
<h2>Primary and Secondary Growth: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student measures a young woody plant over several months.</p>

<p>At the beginning:</p>

<p>Stem length = 15 cm</p>

<p>Stem diameter = 0.6 cm</p>

<p>At the end:</p>

<p>Stem length = 27 cm</p>

<p>Stem diameter = 1.0 cm</p>

<h3>Step 1: Calculate Change in Length</h3>

<p>27 − 15 = 12 cm</p>

<p>The stem increased in length by 12 cm.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Calculate Change in Diameter</h3>

<p>1.0 − 0.6 = 0.4 cm</p>

<p>The stem increased in diameter by 0.4 cm.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Identify the Growth Types</h3>

<p>The increase in length represents primary growth.</p>

<p>The increase in thickness represents secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Link the Growth to Meristems</h3>

<p>Primary growth is associated mainly with apical meristems.</p>

<p>Secondary growth is associated with lateral meristems such as the vascular cambium.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Explain the Overall Result</h3>

<p>The plant became both longer and thicker because primary and secondary growth can occur during the development of a woody plant.</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for primary_secondary_growth_complete_problem', 15),
  (1391, 1391, '<h2>What Is a Noun?</h2>

<h3>1. UNDERSTANDING NOUNS</h3>
<p>A <b>noun</b> is a word that names a person, place, animal, thing, substance, quality, feeling, activity or idea.</p>
<p>Nouns help us identify what we are talking about. Without them, it would be difficult to communicate who did something, where it happened, or what we are describing.</p>

<h3>2. EXAMPLES OF NOUNS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>People:</b> teacher, learner, doctor, Amina</li>
<li><b>Places:</b> school, village, Kenya, Nairobi</li>
<li><b>Animals:</b> goat, lion, eagle</li>
<li><b>Things:</b> book, phone, chair</li>
<li><b>Substances:</b> water, salt, gold</li>
<li><b>Qualities:</b> honesty, kindness, courage</li>
<li><b>Feelings:</b> fear, joy, sadness</li>
<li><b>Ideas:</b> freedom, justice, peace</li>
<li><b>Activities:</b> swimming, reading, dancing</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. NOUNS IN SENTENCES</h3>
<pre>
The learner opened the book.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>learner</b> names the person performing the action.</li>
<li><b>book</b> names the thing receiving the action.</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Honesty builds trust.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>Honesty</b> names a quality.</li>
<li><b>trust</b> names a feeling or state of confidence.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. HOW TO IDENTIFY A NOUN</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the whole sentence to understand its meaning.</li>
<li>Ask what person, place, animal, thing, substance, quality, feeling, activity or idea is being named.</li>
<li>Check how the word functions in the sentence. A word''s role can change depending on its use.</li>
</ol>

<h3>5. WHY CONTEXT MATTERS</h3>
<p>The same word can perform different grammatical functions.</p>
<pre>
I drink water every morning.
The water is cold.
</pre>
<p>In both sentences, <b>water</b> is a noun because it names a substance.</p>
<pre>
We water the plants.
</pre>
<p>Here, <b>water</b> is a verb because it describes an action. Do not classify a word by its spelling alone; examine how it is used.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking that nouns only name physical objects. Abstract ideas such as justice are also nouns.</li>
<li>Assuming that every word after an article is a noun. In “a beautiful house”, “beautiful” is an adjective and “house” is a noun.</li>
<li>Classifying a word without reading the whole sentence.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
A noun names something. Its role in a sentence depends on how it is used.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Nouns', 15),
  (1392, 1392, '<h2>Personal Pronouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT IS A PERSONAL PRONOUN?</h3>
<p>A <b>personal pronoun</b> is a pronoun that refers to a speaker, the person or people being addressed, or someone or something being discussed.</p>
<p>Personal pronouns help avoid unnecessary repetition.</p>
<pre>
Amina is reading. Amina enjoys novels.
Amina is reading. She enjoys novels.
</pre>
<p><b>She</b> replaces the repeated name. The reader can identify who is meant from the previous sentence.</p>

<h3>2. PERSON AND NUMBER</h3>
<p>Personal pronouns are grouped according to person and number.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>First person:</b> the speaker. Singular: I; plural: we.</li>
<li><b>Second person:</b> the person or people addressed. Singular and plural: you.</li>
<li><b>Third person:</b> the person, animal or thing discussed. Singular: he, she, it; plural: they.</li>
</ul>
<p>English uses <i>you</i> for both one person and several people. The context shows which meaning is intended.</p>

<h3>3. SUBJECT PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>Subject pronouns commonly perform the subject role in a clause.</p>
<pre>
I study every evening.
You answered correctly.
He plays football.
She reads carefully.
It looks broken.
We are ready.
They arrived early.
</pre>
<p>In standard English, subject pronouns normally appear in the subject position. For example, write ''She and I went home'' rather than ''Her and me went home'' in a formal sentence where the pronouns are the subject.</p>

<h3>4. OBJECT PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>Object pronouns are used when the pronoun functions as an object of a verb or preposition.</p>
<pre>
The teacher called me.
I helped him.
We invited her.
The coach spoke to us.
The principal thanked them.
</pre>
<p>Common subject–object pairs include:</p>
<pre>
I → me
he → him
she → her
we → us
they → them
</pre>
<p><i>You</i> and <i>it</i> have the same form as subject and object pronouns.</p>

<h3>5. CHOOSING THE CORRECT FORM</h3>
<p>Ask what job the pronoun performs in the sentence.</p>
<pre>
She called him.
He called her.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, <i>she</i> performs the action and is the subject; <i>him</i> receives the action and is the object. The second sentence reverses their roles.</p>
<pre>
Between you and me, the plan may fail.
</pre>
<p>After the preposition <i>between</i>, use the object pronoun <i>me</i>, not the subject pronoun <i>I</i>.</p>

<h3>6. PRONOUNS MUST HAVE CLEAR REFERENCES</h3>
<pre>
Brian told Peter that he had won.
</pre>
<p>The pronoun <i>he</i> is ambiguous because it could refer to Brian or Peter. A clearer sentence identifies the intended person:</p>
<pre>
Brian told Peter, ''I have won.''
Brian told Peter that Peter had won.
</pre>
<p>Choose a pronoun only when the reader can reasonably identify whom or what it refers to.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''Me and Amina went home'' in formal standard English instead of ''Amina and I went home''.</li>
<li>Writing ''The teacher helped she'' instead of ''The teacher helped her''.</li>
<li>Using ''I'' after a preposition, as in ''between you and I'', in formal standard English.</li>
<li>Using a pronoun when two possible references make the sentence unclear.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Subject pronouns: I, you, he, she, it, we, they.
Object pronouns: me, you, him, her, it, us, them.
Choose the form according to its grammatical role and keep its reference clear.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pronouns', 15),
  (1393, 1393, '<h2>Main and Auxiliary Verbs</h2>
<p>A <strong>main verb</strong> expresses the central action, event or state in a clause. An <strong>auxiliary verb</strong>, also called a helping verb, works with a main verb to express features such as tense, aspect, voice, questions, negation or modality.</p>

<h3>Main verbs</h3>
<p>Main verbs carry the central meaning of the clause:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The learners <strong>study</strong> every evening.</em></li>
  <li><em>The baby <strong>slept</strong> peacefully.</em></li>
  <li><em>The room <strong>seems</strong> quiet.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Main verbs can describe actions (<em>write</em>, <em>run</em>), events (<em>happen</em>, <em>break</em>) or states (<em>know</em>, <em>belong</em>, <em>seem</em>).</p>

<h3>The primary auxiliary verbs</h3>
<p>The three primary auxiliaries are <strong>be</strong>, <strong>have</strong> and <strong>do</strong>. Their forms change according to tense and subject.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Be:</strong> am, is, are, was, were, be, being, been. It helps form continuous tenses and the passive voice.</li>
  <li><strong>Have:</strong> have, has, had. It helps form perfect tenses.</li>
  <li><strong>Do:</strong> do, does, did. It helps form many questions, negatives and emphatic statements in the simple present and simple past.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How auxiliaries work</h3>
<p><strong>Continuous aspect:</strong> <em>She is reading.</em> Is is the auxiliary; reading is the main verb in its -ing form.</p>
<p><strong>Perfect aspect:</strong> <em>They have finished.</em> Have is the auxiliary; finished is the main verb in its past participle form.</p>
<p><strong>Passive voice:</strong> <em>The letter was written by Ali.</em> Was is the auxiliary; written is the main verb in its past participle form.</p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> <em>Do you understand?</em> Do helps form the question; understand is the main verb.</p>
<p><strong>Negative:</strong> <em>He does not agree.</em> Does helps form the negative; agree is the main verb.</p>

<h3>Modal auxiliary verbs</h3>
<p>Modals include <em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will</em> and <em>would</em>. They express meanings such as ability, possibility, obligation, advice or willingness.</p>
<p>Examples: <em>She can swim.</em> <em>You should revise.</em> <em>They might arrive later.</em></p>
<p>A modal is followed by the base form of the main verb: <em>She can swim</em>, not <em>She can swims</em> or <em>She can to swim</em>.</p>

<h3>One verb can serve different roles</h3>
<p><em>They have a car.</em> Here, have is the main verb because it expresses possession.</p>
<p><em>They have bought a car.</em> Here, have is an auxiliary because it helps form the present perfect; bought is the main verb.</p>
<p><em>She is happy.</em> Here, is links the subject to a description and functions as the main linking verb.</p>
<p><em>She is singing.</em> Here, is is an auxiliary helping the main verb singing.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>Does she likes tea?</em> Write <em>Does she like tea?</em> The auxiliary does carries the tense, so the main verb uses its base form.</li>
  <li><em>He can plays.</em> Write <em>He can play.</em></li>
  <li>Calling every form of be an auxiliary. It can also function as the main linking verb.</li>
  <li>Confusing the main verb''s meaning with the auxiliary''s grammatical job.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Verbs', 15),
  (1394, 1394, '<h2>Types of Adjectives</h2>
<p>An <strong>adjective</strong> describes a noun or pronoun, or helps identify which person or thing is meant. Adjectives can communicate qualities, quantities, ownership and other information.</p>

<h3>1. Descriptive adjectives</h3>
<p>Descriptive adjectives tell us what a person, place or thing is like.</p>
<ul>
  <li>a <em>careful</em> driver</li>
  <li>a <em>narrow</em> road</li>
  <li>a <em>useful</em> explanation</li>
  <li>a <em>bright</em> classroom</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>a careful driver</em>, the adjective <em>careful</em> describes the noun <em>driver</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Quantitative adjectives</h3>
<p>Quantitative adjectives indicate an amount or quantity.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>some</em> water</li>
  <li><em>little</em> time</li>
  <li><em>enough</em> food</li>
  <li><em>much</em> effort</li>
</ul>
<p>Traditional school grammar often calls these quantitative adjectives. In modern grammar, many words in this group are classified as determiners when they introduce a noun. Their function in the sentence is what matters.</p>

<h3>3. Numeral adjectives</h3>
<p>Numeral adjectives express number or order.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>three</em> books — a cardinal number.</li>
  <li><em>five</em> learners — a cardinal number.</li>
  <li>the <em>first</em> lesson — an ordinal number.</li>
  <li>the <em>third</em> attempt — an ordinal number.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cardinal numbers answer ''how many?'' while ordinal numbers indicate position or order.</p>

<h3>4. Demonstrative adjectives</h3>
<p>Demonstrative words identify which noun is meant.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>This</em> book is useful.</li>
  <li><em>That</em> building is old.</li>
  <li><em>These</em> questions are difficult.</li>
  <li><em>Those</em> trees need water.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use <em>this</em> and <em>that</em> with singular nouns, and <em>these</em> and <em>those</em> with plural nouns.</p>
<p>Compare: <em>This is useful.</em> Here, <em>this</em> stands alone as a pronoun. In <em>This book is useful</em>, it introduces the noun <em>book</em> and functions as a determiner in modern grammar.</p>

<h3>5. Possessive adjectives</h3>
<p>Possessive adjectives, also called possessive determiners in modern grammar, show whose noun is being referred to.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>my</em> notebook</li>
  <li><em>your</em> pencil</li>
  <li><em>his</em> uniform</li>
  <li><em>her</em> bag</li>
  <li><em>our</em> classroom</li>
  <li><em>their</em> teacher</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not confuse possessive determiners with possessive pronouns.</p>
<p>Determiner: <em>This is my book.</em></p>
<p>Pronoun: <em>This book is mine.</em></p>

<h3>6. Interrogative adjectives</h3>
<p>In traditional grammar, <em>which</em> and <em>what</em> are called interrogative adjectives when they introduce a noun in a question.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Which subject</em> do you prefer?</li>
  <li><em>What time</em> does the bus arrive?</li>
</ul>
<p>Compare: <em>Which do you prefer?</em> In this sentence, <em>which</em> stands alone and functions as a pronoun.</p>

<h3>7. Participial adjectives</h3>
<p>Some adjectives are formed from verbs and end in <em>-ing</em> or <em>-ed</em>, among other forms.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The lesson was <em>interesting</em>.</li>
  <li>The learners were <em>interested</em>.</li>
  <li>The <em>broken</em> window needs repair.</li>
</ul>
<p>In many contexts, <em>-ing</em> describes what causes a feeling, while <em>-ed</em> describes the person or thing experiencing it.</p>

<h3>8. Identify an adjective by its function</h3>
<p>Ask what word the adjective describes or helps identify. In <em>The exhausted players entered the field</em>, <em>exhausted</em> describes <em>players</em>. In <em>The players were exhausted</em>, it describes the players after the linking verb <em>were</em>.</p>
<p>Not every word before a noun is necessarily an adjective. Words such as <em>the</em>, <em>my</em> and <em>those</em> are generally classified as determiners in modern grammar, even though older school grammar may group some of them under adjectives.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adjectives', 15),
  (1395, 1395, '<h2>Types of Adverbs</h2>
<p>An <strong>adverb</strong> can modify a verb, an adjective, another adverb or a whole clause. It often tells us how, when, where or how often something happens, or to what degree something is true.</p>

<h3>1. Adverbs of manner</h3>
<p>Adverbs of manner describe how an action is performed.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The learner answered <em>correctly</em>.</li>
  <li>The driver moved <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The baby slept <em>peacefully</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many adverbs of manner end in <em>-ly</em>, but not all do. In <em>She runs fast</em>, <em>fast</em> is an adverb, while <em>fast</em> can also be an adjective in <em>a fast runner</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Adverbs of time</h3>
<p>Adverbs of time tell us when something happens or how long it continues.</p>
<ul>
  <li>We arrived <em>yesterday</em>.</li>
  <li>The lesson will begin <em>soon</em>.</li>
  <li>She waited <em>briefly</em>.</li>
  <li>They have <em>already</em> left.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Adverbs of place and direction</h3>
<p>These adverbs tell us where an action happens or where something moves.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Please wait <em>outside</em>.</li>
  <li>The children looked <em>everywhere</em>.</li>
  <li>He moved <em>forward</em>.</li>
  <li>The bird flew <em>away</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Adverbs of frequency</h3>
<p>Adverbs of frequency indicate how often something happens.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>always</em></li>
  <li><em>usually</em></li>
  <li><em>often</em></li>
  <li><em>sometimes</em></li>
  <li><em>rarely</em></li>
  <li><em>never</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Frequency adverbs usually come before a main verb:</p>
<p><em>She often reads in the evening.</em></p>
<p>They usually come after the verb <em>be</em> when it is the main verb:</p>
<p><em>She is often early.</em></p>
<p>With an auxiliary verb, they commonly appear after the first auxiliary:</p>
<p><em>She has never missed a lesson.</em></p>

<h3>5. Adverbs of degree</h3>
<p>Adverbs of degree indicate intensity or extent. They often modify adjectives, other adverbs or verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The water is <em>very</em> cold. — modifies an adjective.</li>
  <li>She answered <em>extremely</em> quickly. — modifies an adverb.</li>
  <li>I <em>almost</em> fell. — modifies the verb phrase.</li>
  <li>The work is <em>quite</em> difficult. — modifies an adjective.</li>
</ul>
<p>Words such as <em>very</em>, <em>too</em>, <em>almost</em>, <em>enough</em> and <em>extremely</em> can express degree, but they do not all work in exactly the same way.</p>

<h3>6. Adverbs of certainty</h3>
<p>These adverbs show how certain the speaker is about a statement.</p>
<ul>
  <li>She will <em>probably</em> arrive early.</li>
  <li>He has <em>certainly</em> improved.</li>
  <li>They will <em>possibly</em> attend.</li>
</ul>
<p>The choice of adverb changes the speaker''s level of confidence.</p>

<h3>7. Adverbs do not always end in -ly</h3>
<p>Common adverbs without <em>-ly</em> include <em>fast</em>, <em>hard</em>, <em>late</em>, <em>well</em> and <em>often</em>.</p>
<p>Be careful with pairs that have different meanings:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Hard</em> means with effort: She worked hard.</li>
  <li><em>Hardly</em> means almost not: She hardly worked.</li>
  <li><em>Late</em> means after the expected time: He arrived late.</li>
  <li><em>Lately</em> means recently: Have you seen him lately?</li>
  <li><em>Good</em> is usually an adjective: She is a good singer.</li>
  <li><em>Well</em> is often an adverb of manner: She sings well.</li>
</ul>

<h3>8. Identify what the adverb modifies</h3>
<p>Do not classify an adverb by its spelling alone. Ask what it modifies and what meaning it adds.</p>
<p>In <em>The extremely careful driver stopped suddenly</em>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Extremely</em> modifies the adjective <em>careful</em>.</li>
  <li><em>Suddenly</em> modifies the verb <em>stopped</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both are adverbs, but they perform different functions.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adverbs', 15),
  (1396, 1396, '<h2>Articles: A, An and The</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT ARE ARTICLES?</h3>
<p><b>A</b>, <b>an</b> and <b>the</b> are articles. Articles come before nouns or noun phrases and help show whether we mean something generally or a particular thing.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>A</b> and <b>an</b> are indefinite articles. They commonly refer to one singular countable thing that is not identified as a particular known item.</li>
<li><b>The</b> is the definite article. It identifies a particular person or thing that the speaker expects the listener or reader to recognise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. WHEN TO USE A</h3>
<p>Use <b>a</b> before a singular countable noun beginning with a consonant sound.</p>
<pre>
a book
a teacher
a university
a European country
</pre>
<p>The rule depends on sound, not merely on the first written letter. <i>University</i> begins with a /juː/ sound, similar to ''you'', so it takes <i>a</i>.</p>

<h3>3. WHEN TO USE AN</h3>
<p>Use <b>an</b> before a singular countable noun beginning with a vowel sound.</p>
<pre>
an apple
an orange
an umbrella
an hour
an honest person
</pre>
<p>In <i>hour</i> and <i>honest</i>, the initial h is silent, so the words begin with a vowel sound. That is why they take <i>an</i>.</p>
<p>Compare:</p>
<pre>
a university
an umbrella
a useful tool
an unusual event
</pre>
<p>Always listen for the first sound of the word that follows the article.</p>

<h3>4. WHEN TO USE THE</h3>
<p>Use <b>the</b> when the person or thing is identifiable from the context, has already been mentioned, or is understood to be specific.</p>
<pre>
I saw a dog. The dog was barking.
Please close the door.
The sun provides light.
</pre>
<ul>
<li>In the first sentence, <i>a dog</i> introduces a dog that has not yet been identified.</li>
<li>In the next sentence, <i>the dog</i> refers to the same dog, now known from the conversation.</li>
<li>In ''Please close the door'', the speaker expects the listener to know which door is meant.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. WHEN NO ARTICLE IS NEEDED</h3>
<p>English often uses no article when speaking generally about plural countable nouns or uncountable nouns.</p>
<pre>
Books can broaden knowledge.
Water is necessary for life.
Learners need practice.
</pre>
<p>Here, the nouns refer to things in general rather than particular identified items.</p>
<p>However, use <i>the</i> when the meaning is specific:</p>
<pre>
The books on the table are mine.
The water in this bottle is cold.
</pre>

<h3>6. ARTICLES AND COUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>In ordinary use, a singular countable noun usually needs a determiner, such as an article or a possessive word.</p>
<pre>
I bought a pen.
I bought the pen.
I bought my pen.
</pre>
<p>All three are grammatical, but they communicate different information. <i>A pen</i> introduces a non-specific pen, <i>the pen</i> identifies a particular pen, and <i>my pen</i> identifies one associated with the speaker.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''an university'' because the word begins with the letter u. The initial sound is /j/, so use ''a university''.</li>
<li>Writing ''a hour'' instead of ''an hour''. The h is silent.</li>
<li>Using ''the'' for every general statement, such as ''The water is essential for life'' when speaking about water generally.</li>
<li>Leaving a singular countable noun without a determiner in an ordinary sentence, such as ''I bought book''. Write ''I bought a book''.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
A = before a consonant sound.
An = before a vowel sound.
The = for an identifiable or specific noun.
No article = often used for general plural or uncountable nouns.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Determiners', 15),
  (1397, 1397, '<h2>Prepositions of Time</h2>
<p>Prepositions of time show the relationship between an event and a point or period in time. The most common choices include <strong>at, on</strong> and <strong>in</strong>, but other prepositions communicate duration, starting points, deadlines and sequence.</p>

<h3>1. At: precise times and points</h3>
<p>Use <em>at</em> for a specific time on the clock and certain established time expressions.</p>
<ul>
  <li>at 7:30 a.m.</li>
  <li>at noon</li>
  <li>at midnight</li>
  <li>at sunrise</li>
  <li>at night</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The lesson begins at eight o''clock.</em></p>
<p><em>We arrived at midnight.</em></p>

<h3>2. On: days and dates</h3>
<p>Use <em>on</em> with days, dates and particular days or parts of days.</p>
<ul>
  <li>on Monday</li>
  <li>on 12 June</li>
  <li>on my birthday</li>
  <li>on Monday morning</li>
  <li>on the first day of the term</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The examination begins on Friday.</em></p>
<p><em>We travelled on 12 June.</em></p>
<p>Do not normally use <em>on</em> before a day name when using a general expression such as <em>every Monday</em>.</p>

<h3>3. In: months, years and longer periods</h3>
<p>Use <em>in</em> for months, years, seasons and many longer periods.</p>
<ul>
  <li>in October</li>
  <li>in 2026</li>
  <li>in the dry season</li>
  <li>in the twentieth century</li>
  <li>in the morning</li>
  <li>in the afternoon</li>
  <li>in the evening</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The school opened in January.</em></p>
<p><em>We usually revise in the evening.</em></p>

<h3>4. Important exceptions and expressions</h3>
<p>English uses established expressions that must be learned as complete phrases.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>at night</em>, but <em>in the evening</em>.</li>
  <li><em>at the weekend</em> is common in British English, including Kenyan English usage; <em>on the weekend</em> is common in American English.</li>
  <li><em>in time</em> means early enough for something; <em>on time</em> means at the scheduled or expected time.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>We arrived on time for the examination.</em></p>
<p><em>We arrived in time to revise before the examination began.</em></p>

<h3>5. Since and for</h3>
<p><strong>Since</strong> identifies the starting point of an activity or state continuing to a later reference point.</p>
<p><em>She has lived here since 2022.</em></p>
<p><strong>For</strong> identifies the length of time.</p>
<p><em>She has lived here for four years.</em></p>
<p>Compare:</p>
<ul>
  <li>since Monday — a starting point.</li>
  <li>for three days — a duration.</li>
  <li>since last year — a starting point.</li>
  <li>for several months — a duration.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. By and until</h3>
<p><strong>By</strong> means no later than a stated time or deadline.</p>
<p><em>Submit your assignment by Friday.</em></p>
<p>The assignment must be submitted on Friday or earlier.</p>
<p><strong>Until</strong> indicates that an activity or state continues up to a point in time.</p>
<p><em>The library will remain open until six o''clock.</em></p>
<p>The library closes at six, and the period of opening continues up to that time.</p>

<h3>7. During, before and after</h3>
<p><strong>During</strong> identifies a period in which something happens:</p>
<p><em>Do not use your phone during the examination.</em></p>
<p><strong>Before</strong> indicates an earlier time:</p>
<p><em>Wash your hands before eating.</em></p>
<p><strong>After</strong> indicates a later time:</p>
<p><em>We discussed the results after the meeting.</em></p>
<p>Use a noun phrase after these prepositions: <em>before lunch</em>, <em>after the lesson</em>, <em>during the holiday</em>.</p>

<h3>8. Avoid common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>in Monday</em>. Correct: <em>on Monday</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>at 2026</em>. Correct: <em>in 2026</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>since three years</em>. Correct: <em>for three years</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>for 2022</em> when identifying the starting point of an ongoing state. Correct: <em>since 2022</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>Submit it until Friday</em> when Friday is a deadline. Correct: <em>Submit it by Friday</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The correct preposition depends on the time relationship being expressed, not simply on the noun that follows it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prepositions', 15),
  (1398, 1398, '<h2>Linking Words and Logical Relationships</h2>
<p>Linking words connect ideas and show how those ideas relate. They help a reader follow the reasoning in a paragraph and help a writer express relationships clearly.</p>

<h3>1. Addition</h3>
<p>Use <strong>and, also, furthermore, moreover</strong> and <strong>in addition</strong> to add information.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The library has reference books. In addition, it provides access to digital resources.</p>

<h3>2. Contrast</h3>
<p>Use <strong>but, however, although, whereas</strong> and <strong>nevertheless</strong> to show a difference or an unexpected result.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Although the road was muddy, the bus arrived on time.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The first plan was expensive. However, the second plan was affordable.</p>

<h3>3. Cause and result</h3>
<p>Use <strong>because, since</strong> and <strong>as</strong> to introduce a reason. Use <strong>so, therefore, consequently</strong> and <strong>as a result</strong> to introduce a result.</p>
<p><em>Reason:</em> The match was postponed because the field was flooded.</p>
<p><em>Result:</em> The field was flooded; therefore, the match was postponed.</p>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> Do not use <em>because</em> and <em>therefore</em> together to connect the same two clauses in one ordinary sentence: “Because the field was flooded, the match was postponed” is correct. So is “The field was flooded; therefore, the match was postponed.”</p>

<h3>4. Sequence and time</h3>
<p>Use <strong>first, next, then, afterwards, finally, before</strong> and <strong>meanwhile</strong> to show order or timing.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> First, collect the materials. Next, measure the liquid. Finally, record the result.</p>

<h3>5. Examples and clarification</h3>
<p>Use <strong>for example, for instance, such as</strong> and <strong>in other words</strong> to illustrate or clarify an idea.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Some renewable energy sources, such as wind and solar power, produce electricity without burning fossil fuels during generation.</p>

<h3>6. Purpose and condition</h3>
<p>Use <strong>so that</strong> to express purpose and <strong>if, unless</strong> or <strong>provided that</strong> to express a condition.</p>
<p><em>Purpose:</em> She labelled the containers so that everyone could identify their contents.</p>
<p><em>Condition:</em> You will understand the procedure if you follow each step carefully.</p>

<h3>How to choose the right linker</h3>
<p>Do not choose a linking word merely because it sounds formal. Identify the relationship between the ideas first. Ask: Am I adding information, contrasting ideas, giving a reason, showing a result, ordering events, providing an example, expressing purpose or setting a condition?</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using <em>however</em> as though it were an ordinary coordinating conjunction: “The road was flooded, however we continued” is incorrectly punctuated. Write “The road was flooded; however, we continued.”</li>
<li>Using a result linker when the second idea is actually a reason.</li>
<li>Adding linkers that do not express a genuine relationship, making writing awkward.</li>
<li>Repeating the same linker in every sentence instead of choosing one that fits the meaning.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conjunctions', 15),
  (1399, 1399, '<h2>Interjections</h2>
<h3>1. UNDERSTANDING INTERJECTIONS</h3>
<p>An <b>interjection</b> is a word or short phrase used to express sudden emotion, feeling, surprise, or exclamation. It usually stands outside the main grammatical structure of a sentence.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Joy/Delight:</b> Hurray! Wow! Yay!</li>
<li><b>Pain/Discomfort:</b> Ouch! Ah! Oh!</li>
<li><b>Surprise/Astonishment:</b> Gosh! Heavens! Oh no!</li>
<li><b>Hesitation/Attention:</b> Um, er, hey, shh, look!</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. PUNCTUATION RULES</h3>
<p>Use an <b>exclamation mark (!)</b> for strong emotions (<i>Ouch! That hurt.</i>) and a <b>comma (,)</b> for mild expressions (<i>Well, let us start.</i>).</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interjections', 15),
  (1400, 1400, '<h2>Common and Proper Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. THE BASIC DIFFERENCE</h3>
<p>Nouns can name members of a general class or identify a particular person, place, organisation or other named entity.</p>
<ul>
<li>A <b>common noun</b> names a general person, place, animal, thing or category.</li>
<li>A <b>proper noun</b> gives the specific name of a particular person, place, organisation, institution or other named entity.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. COMPARE THE EXAMPLES</h3>
<pre>
Common noun       Proper noun
country           Kenya
city              Nairobi
river             Tana River
learner           Wanjiku
school            Kenyatta High School
month             October
language          Kiswahili
</pre>
<p>''Country'' refers to a general category. ''Kenya'' identifies one particular country. The distinction is about general naming versus specific naming.</p>

<h3>3. CAPITAL LETTERS</h3>
<p>Proper nouns normally begin with capital letters. Common nouns normally begin with lowercase letters unless they start a sentence or form part of a title or name.</p>
<pre>
We visited Mombasa.
Mombasa is a city.
</pre>
<p><b>Mombasa</b> is capitalised because it is a proper noun. <b>city</b> is lowercase because it is a common noun in this sentence.</p>

<h3>4. NAMES WITH MORE THAN ONE WORD</h3>
<p>A proper noun can contain several words. Capitalisation depends on the name and its accepted spelling.</p>
<pre>
Mount Kenya
Lake Victoria
Kenyatta University
East African Community
</pre>
<p>Do not assume that a proper noun must be a single word. Recognise the complete name.</p>

<h3>5. CONTEXT CAN CHANGE THE CLASSIFICATION</h3>
<pre>
My mother is a teacher.
Mother Teresa helped many people.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, <b>mother</b> describes a family relationship and is used as a common noun. In the second, <b>Mother Teresa</b> is a person''s name, so the title forms part of a proper noun.</p>
<p>Similarly, ''president'' is usually a common noun, but capitalisation can vary when it is used as a formal title before a person''s name or according to a publication''s style.</p>

<h3>6. A SIMPLE TEST</h3>
<ul>
<li>Does the word identify a particular named entity? It is likely a proper noun.</li>
<li>Does it name a general type or category? It is likely a common noun.</li>
<li>Check the whole phrase, not just the first word.</li>
</ul>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''kenya'' instead of ''Kenya''.</li>
<li>Capitalising every common noun, such as ''Teacher'' in the middle of a sentence.</li>
<li>Assuming every important person or place must be written as a proper noun. The word ''doctor'' remains a common noun when used generally.</li>
<li>Confusing a title with a person''s full name.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Common noun = a general category.
Proper noun = the specific name of an entity.
Proper nouns normally begin with capital letters.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Common and Proper Nouns', 15),
  (1401, 1401, '<h2>Concrete and Abstract Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. CONCRETE NOUNS</h3>
<p>A <b>concrete noun</b> names something that can be experienced through one or more of the physical senses: sight, hearing, smell, taste or touch.</p>
<p>Examples include:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Sight:</b> tree, rainbow, building</li>
<li><b>Hearing:</b> thunder, music, voice</li>
<li><b>Smell:</b> perfume, smoke</li>
<li><b>Taste:</b> sugar, lemon</li>
<li><b>Touch:</b> stone, cloth, sand</li>
</ul>
<p>A concrete noun does not have to be something you can hold. Thunder and music are concrete because they can be heard.</p>

<h3>2. ABSTRACT NOUNS</h3>
<p>An <b>abstract noun</b> names an idea, quality, feeling, condition or state that is not experienced as a physical object through the senses.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Qualities:</b> honesty, patience, bravery</li>
<li><b>Feelings:</b> love, anger, happiness</li>
<li><b>Ideas:</b> freedom, justice, equality</li>
<li><b>States:</b> childhood, poverty, peace</li>
</ul>
<p>You may observe the effects of an abstract quality, but the quality itself is not a physical object. For example, you can observe an honest action, but you cannot hold honesty in your hand.</p>

<h3>3. COMPARE THE MEANINGS</h3>
<pre>
The child held a stone.
The child showed courage.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>Stone:</b> concrete noun; it can be seen and touched.</li>
<li><b>Courage:</b> abstract noun; it names a quality.</li>
</ul>
<pre>
We heard the music.
She felt happiness.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>Music:</b> concrete noun because it can be heard.</li>
<li><b>Happiness:</b> abstract noun because it names a feeling.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. ABSTRACT NOUNS FORMED FROM OTHER WORDS</h3>
<p>Many abstract nouns are formed from adjectives or verbs. Recognising these word relationships can help you expand your vocabulary.</p>
<pre>
Adjective       Abstract noun
kind            kindness
brave           bravery
honest          honesty

Verb            Abstract noun
decide          decision
arrive          arrival
educate         education
</pre>
<p>These patterns are useful, but spelling changes are not always predictable. Learn the correct form of unfamiliar words in context.</p>

<h3>5. HOW TO CLASSIFY A NOUN</h3>
<ol>
<li>Determine what the word means in the sentence.</li>
<li>Ask whether it names something experienced through the physical senses.</li>
<li>If it names a physical or sensory experience, it is usually concrete. If it names a quality, idea, feeling or state, it is usually abstract.</li>
</ol>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Calling a noun abstract just because it cannot be held. Music and thunder are concrete because they can be heard.</li>
<li>Assuming abstract nouns are unimportant because they are not physical objects.</li>
<li>Classifying words without checking their meanings in context.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Concrete nouns name things experienced through the senses.
Abstract nouns name ideas, qualities, feelings and states.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Concrete and Abstract Nouns', 15),
  (1402, 1402, '<h2>Countable and Uncountable Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. COUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>A <b>countable noun</b> names something that can be counted as individual units. It has singular and plural forms in ordinary use.</p>
<pre>
one book → two books
one learner → thirty learners
one mango → five mangoes
</pre>
<p>Singular countable nouns can use <b>a</b> or <b>an</b> when the meaning is appropriate: ''a book'', ''an orange''. Plural countable nouns can be used with numbers: ''three books''.</p>

<h3>2. UNCOUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>An <b>uncountable noun</b> names a substance, material, quality or general mass that is not normally counted as separate units in that meaning.</p>
<pre>
water
rice
information
advice
furniture
equipment
</pre>
<p>In standard English, we normally say:</p>
<ul>
<li>some water, not ''a water'' when referring to water generally</li>
<li>a piece of information, not ''an information''</li>
<li>a piece of advice, not ''an advice''</li>
<li>some furniture, not ''furnitures''</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. HOW TO COUNT UNCOUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>Use a suitable unit or container when you need to count an uncountable substance or item of information.</p>
<pre>
a glass of water
two bags of rice
three pieces of advice
four items of furniture
a piece of equipment
</pre>
<p>The quantity word or unit is counted, while the uncountable noun normally keeps its uncountable form.</p>

<h3>4. QUANTIFIERS</h3>
<p>Quantifiers show quantity or amount. Choose one that agrees with the noun''s use.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Many / few:</b> normally used with plural countable nouns. Example: many books; few learners.</li>
<li><b>Much / little:</b> normally used with uncountable nouns. Example: much water; little information.</li>
<li><b>Some / a lot of:</b> can be used with both. Example: some books; some water; a lot of furniture.</li>
<li><b>Any:</b> often used in questions and negative statements with both types. Example: any questions; any sugar.</li>
</ul>
<pre>
How many books did you buy?
How much water do we need?
</pre>
<p>Use <b>how many</b> for countable units and <b>how much</b> for uncountable amounts.</p>

<h3>5. SOME NOUNS CAN BE BOTH</h3>
<p>A noun''s countability can change with its meaning.</p>
<pre>
There is chicken in the stew.
Two chickens are in the coop.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, <b>chicken</b> refers to meat and is uncountable. In the second, <b>chickens</b> refers to individual animals and is countable.</p>
<pre>
I need some paper.
I wrote two papers.
</pre>
<p><b>Paper</b> can refer to material in general or to individual documents, depending on context.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using a number directly with an uncountable noun: ''three informations''. Say ''three pieces of information''.</li>
<li>Using ''much'' with ordinary plural countable nouns when ''many'' is required.</li>
<li>Assuming a noun must always be countable or always uncountable in every context.</li>
<li>Adding a plural ending to uncountable nouns used in their ordinary mass meaning, such as ''furnitures''.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Countable: one book, two books, many books.
Uncountable: some water, much water, two litres of water.
Choose words according to the noun''s meaning in context.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Countable and Uncountable Nouns', 15),
  (1403, 1403, '<h2>Collective Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT IS A COLLECTIVE NOUN?</h3>
<p>A <b>collective noun</b> names a group of people, animals or things considered as a unit.</p>
<p>Examples include:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>People:</b> team, committee, audience, class, family</li>
<li><b>Animals:</b> herd, flock, swarm, pack</li>
<li><b>Things:</b> bunch, collection, bundle, set</li>
</ul>
<p>A collective noun allows us to refer to a group without naming every member individually.</p>

<h3>2. COLLECTIVE NOUNS IN SENTENCES</h3>
<pre>
The team practises every afternoon.
A flock of birds crossed the sky.
The committee discussed the proposal.
A herd of cattle entered the field.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>team</b> names a group of players.</li>
<li><b>flock</b> names a group of birds.</li>
<li><b>committee</b> names a group of people appointed to work together.</li>
<li><b>herd</b> names a group of animals.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. THE GROUP AND ITS MEMBERS</h3>
<p>A collective noun is grammatically singular in many constructions when the group is treated as one unit.</p>
<pre>
The team is ready.
The committee has reached a decision.
</pre>
<p>The verb agrees with the collective noun when the group is presented as a single unit.</p>
<p>In British English, including standard usage common in Kenya, a plural verb can also be used when attention is on the individual members acting separately.</p>
<pre>
The team are arguing among themselves.
The committee are divided in their opinions.
</pre>
<p>These plural constructions emphasise the members rather than the group as one unit. The singular construction is also possible when the group is treated as one whole. Keep the choice consistent with the intended meaning and the variety of English being used.</p>

<h3>4. COLLECTIVE NOUNS ARE NOT ALWAYS THE SAME</h3>
<p>Choose a collective noun that suits the group and context.</p>
<pre>
a flock of sheep
a herd of cattle
a swarm of bees
a pack of wolves
a bunch of bananas
a class of learners
</pre>
<p>These expressions are common combinations. A group may have other descriptions depending on context, but the noun should make sense for the group being described.</p>

<h3>5. COLLECTIVE NOUN OR PLURAL NOUN?</h3>
<pre>
The players are warming up.
The team is warming up.
</pre>
<p><b>Players</b> is a plural noun referring directly to individual people. <b>Team</b> is a collective noun referring to the group as a unit.</p>
<p>A collective noun is not simply any plural noun. ''Books'' names multiple items, while ''collection'' can name the group formed by those items.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking that every collective noun must always take a singular verb. Meaning and British English usage can affect agreement.</li>
<li>Confusing a collective noun with a plural noun such as ''players''.</li>
<li>Choosing an unsuitable group word, such as ''a swarm of cattle''.</li>
<li>Changing between singular and plural agreement without a clear reason.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
A collective noun names a group.
Use singular agreement when the group acts as one unit.
Plural agreement may be used in British English when the members are emphasised.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Collective Nouns', 15),
  (1404, 1404, '<h2>Singular and Plural Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. SINGULAR AND PLURAL MEANING</h3>
<p>A <b>singular noun</b> names one person, animal, place, thing or unit. A <b>plural noun</b> names more than one.</p>
<pre>
Singular                 Plural
learner                  learners
book                     books
tree                     trees
</pre>
<p>In English, the form of a noun often changes to show number. This helps readers and listeners understand whether we mean one item or several.</p>

<h3>2. REGULAR PLURAL NOUNS</h3>
<p>Most countable nouns form their plural by adding <b>-s</b>.</p>
<pre>
pen → pens
desk → desks
river → rivers
</pre>
<p>Nouns ending in <b>-s, -ss, -sh, -ch, -x</b> or commonly <b>-z</b> usually add <b>-es</b>.</p>
<pre>
bus → buses
glass → glasses
dish → dishes
watch → watches
box → boxes
buzz → buzzes
</pre>
<p>The extra syllable in words such as <i>buses</i> and <i>dishes</i> makes the plural easier to pronounce.</p>

<h3>3. NOUNS ENDING IN -Y</h3>
<p>For a noun ending in a consonant followed by <b>-y</b>, change <b>-y</b> to <b>-ies</b>.</p>
<pre>
city → cities
baby → babies
country → countries
</pre>
<p>When a vowel comes before the final <b>-y</b>, usually add <b>-s</b> instead.</p>
<pre>
boy → boys
key → keys
day → days
</pre>
<p>Check the letter immediately before the <i>y</i>. In <i>city</i>, it is a consonant; in <i>boy</i>, it is a vowel.</p>

<h3>4. NOUNS ENDING IN -F OR -FE</h3>
<p>Some nouns ending in <b>-f</b> or <b>-fe</b> change to <b>-ves</b>.</p>
<pre>
leaf → leaves
wife → wives
knife → knives
</pre>
<p>However, this is not a universal rule. Some nouns simply add <b>-s</b>.</p>
<pre>
roof → roofs
chief → chiefs
belief → beliefs
</pre>
<p>Learn the plural form of unfamiliar words rather than assuming every noun follows the same pattern.</p>

<h3>5. USING NUMBER CORRECTLY</h3>
<pre>
One mango is ripe.
Three mangoes are ripe.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, the singular noun <i>mango</i> agrees with <i>is</i>. In the second, the plural noun <i>mangoes</i> agrees with <i>are</i>.</p>
<p>Plural spelling and verb agreement are related but different skills. Changing the noun alone does not automatically correct every other part of the sentence.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''citys'' instead of ''cities''.</li>
<li>Writing ''boxs'' instead of ''boxes''.</li>
<li>Assuming every noun ending in -f changes to -ves.</li>
<li>Using a singular verb with a plural subject, such as ''The learners is ready''.</li>
<li>Adding a plural ending to an uncountable noun when it is being used in its ordinary uncountable meaning.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Singular = one.
Plural = more than one.
Apply the correct spelling pattern, then check agreement in the sentence.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Singular and Plural Nouns', 15),
  (1405, 1405, '<h2>Possessive Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT DOES POSSESSION MEAN?</h3>
<p>A <b>possessive noun</b> shows ownership, belonging, association or a relationship. English commonly uses an apostrophe ('') with s to mark possession.</p>
<pre>
Amina''s book
the teacher''s desk
the dog''s collar
</pre>
<p>These expressions tell us whose book, desk or collar is being discussed. Possession can also express relationships and association, not only legal ownership.</p>

<h3>2. SINGULAR NOUNS</h3>
<p>For most singular nouns, add <b>''s</b>.</p>
<pre>
the girl''s bag
the boy''s bicycle
the child''s uniform
James''s notebook
</pre>
<p>For a singular name ending in s, both <i>James''s</i> and <i>James''</i> appear in published styles. Follow the required style consistently; <i>James''s</i> is a widely accepted form.</p>

<h3>3. REGULAR PLURAL NOUNS ENDING IN -S</h3>
<p>For a regular plural noun that already ends in s, add an apostrophe after the s.</p>
<pre>
the girls'' classroom
the teachers'' meeting
the players'' uniforms
</pre>
<p>Compare:</p>
<pre>
the girl''s uniform   = uniform belonging to one girl
the girls'' uniforms  = uniforms belonging to several girls
</pre>
<p>The position of the apostrophe helps distinguish singular possession from plural possession.</p>

<h3>4. IRREGULAR PLURALS</h3>
<p>For irregular plural nouns that do not end in s, usually add <b>''s</b>.</p>
<pre>
children''s books
men''s shoes
women''s voices
people''s opinions
</pre>
<p>The rule depends on the noun''s actual plural form, not simply on whether it refers to more than one person.</p>

<h3>5. JOINT AND SEPARATE POSSESSION</h3>
<p>When two people share the same item, the apostrophe usually goes on the last name.</p>
<pre>
Amina and Brian''s project
</pre>
<p>This normally means one project shared by Amina and Brian.</p>
<p>When each person has a separate item, both names take the possessive form.</p>
<pre>
Amina''s and Brian''s projects
</pre>
<p>This indicates that Amina and Brian have separate projects. Context can help clarify the intended meaning.</p>

<h3>6. POSSESSIVE NOUNS AND CONTRACTIONS</h3>
<p>An apostrophe can mark possession or missing letters in a contraction. Do not confuse the two.</p>
<pre>
The learner''s book is missing.
The learner''s finished the work.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, <i>learner''s</i> means the book belongs to or is associated with the learner. In the second, <i>learner''s</i> is a contraction of <i>learner has</i> in a suitable context, though the sentence is more naturally written as ''The learner has finished the work'' in formal school writing.</p>
<p>For ordinary school grammar, remember that a possessive noun shows a relationship, while a contraction joins words by omitting letters.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''the girls uniform'' when possession is intended: write ''the girl''s uniform'' for one girl.</li>
<li>Writing ''the girl''s'' when referring to several girls: write ''the girls'''' only if the apostrophe follows the plural s, as in ''the girls'' classroom''.</li>
<li>Adding an apostrophe to ordinary plurals unnecessarily, such as ''apple''s'' when you simply mean several apples.</li>
<li>Confusing a possessive apostrophe with the apostrophe in a contraction.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Singular noun: girl''s
Regular plural ending in s: girls''
Irregular plural not ending in s: children''s
The apostrophe helps show the relationship or possession.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Possessive Nouns', 15),
  (1406, 1406, '<h2>Articles: A, An and The</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT ARE ARTICLES?</h3>
<p><b>A</b>, <b>an</b> and <b>the</b> are articles. Articles come before nouns or noun phrases and help show whether we mean something generally or a particular thing.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>A</b> and <b>an</b> are indefinite articles. They commonly refer to one singular countable thing that is not identified as a particular known item.</li>
<li><b>The</b> is the definite article. It identifies a particular person or thing that the speaker expects the listener or reader to recognise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. WHEN TO USE A</h3>
<p>Use <b>a</b> before a singular countable noun beginning with a consonant sound.</p>
<pre>
a book
a teacher
a university
a European country
</pre>
<p>The rule depends on sound, not merely on the first written letter. <i>University</i> begins with a /juː/ sound, similar to ''you'', so it takes <i>a</i>.</p>

<h3>3. WHEN TO USE AN</h3>
<p>Use <b>an</b> before a singular countable noun beginning with a vowel sound.</p>
<pre>
an apple
an orange
an umbrella
an hour
an honest person
</pre>
<p>In <i>hour</i> and <i>honest</i>, the initial h is silent, so the words begin with a vowel sound. That is why they take <i>an</i>.</p>
<p>Compare:</p>
<pre>
a university
an umbrella
a useful tool
an unusual event
</pre>
<p>Always listen for the first sound of the word that follows the article.</p>

<h3>4. WHEN TO USE THE</h3>
<p>Use <b>the</b> when the person or thing is identifiable from the context, has already been mentioned, or is understood to be specific.</p>
<pre>
I saw a dog. The dog was barking.
Please close the door.
The sun provides light.
</pre>
<ul>
<li>In the first sentence, <i>a dog</i> introduces a dog that has not yet been identified.</li>
<li>In the next sentence, <i>the dog</i> refers to the same dog, now known from the conversation.</li>
<li>In ''Please close the door'', the speaker expects the listener to know which door is meant.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. WHEN NO ARTICLE IS NEEDED</h3>
<p>English often uses no article when speaking generally about plural countable nouns or uncountable nouns.</p>
<pre>
Books can broaden knowledge.
Water is necessary for life.
Learners need practice.
</pre>
<p>Here, the nouns refer to things in general rather than particular identified items.</p>
<p>However, use <i>the</i> when the meaning is specific:</p>
<pre>
The books on the table are mine.
The water in this bottle is cold.
</pre>

<h3>6. ARTICLES AND COUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>In ordinary use, a singular countable noun usually needs a determiner, such as an article or a possessive word.</p>
<pre>
I bought a pen.
I bought the pen.
I bought my pen.
</pre>
<p>All three are grammatical, but they communicate different information. <i>A pen</i> introduces a non-specific pen, <i>the pen</i> identifies a particular pen, and <i>my pen</i> identifies one associated with the speaker.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''an university'' because the word begins with the letter u. The initial sound is /j/, so use ''a university''.</li>
<li>Writing ''a hour'' instead of ''an hour''. The h is silent.</li>
<li>Using ''the'' for every general statement, such as ''The water is essential for life'' when speaking about water generally.</li>
<li>Leaving a singular countable noun without a determiner in an ordinary sentence, such as ''I bought book''. Write ''I bought a book''.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
A = before a consonant sound.
An = before a vowel sound.
The = for an identifiable or specific noun.
No article = often used for general plural or uncountable nouns.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Articles: A, An and The', 15),
  (1407, 1407, '<h2>Quantifiers</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT IS A QUANTIFIER?</h3>
<p>A <b>quantifier</b> is a word or expression that tells us how much or how many of something is being referred to. Quantifiers usually come before nouns or noun phrases.</p>
<pre>
many learners
much water
a few mistakes
a little patience
enough food
</pre>
<p>Choosing the correct quantifier depends on whether the noun is countable or uncountable and on the meaning you want to express.</p>

<h3>2. QUANTIFIERS WITH COUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>Use these quantifiers with plural countable nouns:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Many:</b> a large number. Example: Many learners attended the lesson.</li>
<li><b>A few:</b> some, enough to be meaningful. Example: A few learners asked questions.</li>
<li><b>Few:</b> not many; often suggests that the number is insufficient. Example: Few learners understood the difficult explanation.</li>
<li><b>Several:</b> more than two, but not a large or exact number. Example: Several books were missing.</li>
<li><b>Each / every:</b> refer to members individually and normally take a singular countable noun. Example: Each learner has a book.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. QUANTIFIERS WITH UNCOUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Much:</b> a large amount. Example: We do not have much time.</li>
<li><b>A little:</b> some amount, often enough for a purpose. Example: We have a little sugar left.</li>
<li><b>Little:</b> not much; often suggests an insufficient amount. Example: There is little hope of finishing today.</li>
<li><b>A great deal of:</b> a large amount. Example: The task requires a great deal of patience.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. A FEW VERSUS FEW; A LITTLE VERSUS LITTLE</h3>
<p>The article <b>a</b> changes the usual implication of these expressions.</p>
<pre>
We have a few books.
We have few books.

There is a little milk.
There is little milk.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>A few books</b> means some books are available.</li>
<li><b>Few books</b> emphasises that the number is small, possibly too small.</li>
<li><b>A little milk</b> means some milk is available.</li>
<li><b>Little milk</b> emphasises scarcity.</li>
</ul>
<p>The difference is not simply the quantity. It is also the speaker''s attitude toward whether the amount is sufficient.</p>

<h3>5. QUANTIFIERS USED WITH BOTH TYPES</h3>
<p>Some quantifiers work with both countable and uncountable nouns.</p>
<pre>
some books / some water
a lot of learners / a lot of time
enough chairs / enough space
all the students / all the information
plenty of opportunities / plenty of food
</pre>
<p>Use <b>how many</b> with countable nouns and <b>how much</b> with uncountable nouns.</p>
<pre>
How many chairs are needed?
How much space is available?
</pre>

<h3>6. SOME AND ANY</h3>
<p><b>Some</b> is common in affirmative statements. <b>Any</b> is common in questions and negative statements.</p>
<pre>
We have some food.
We do not have any food.
Do you have any questions?
</pre>
<p>However, this is not an absolute rule. <i>Some</i> is often used in offers and requests when a positive response is expected or when something is being offered.</p>
<pre>
Would you like some tea?
Could I have some water?
</pre>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''much students'' instead of ''many students''.</li>
<li>Writing ''many information'' instead of ''much information'' or ''a lot of information''.</li>
<li>Confusing ''few'' with ''a few'' and ''little'' with ''a little''.</li>
<li>Using ''each learners'' instead of ''each learner''.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Many, few, a few → countable nouns.
Much, little, a little → uncountable nouns.
Some, a lot of, enough → can work with both.
Meaning and context determine the best choice.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Quantifiers', 15),
  (1408, 1408, '<h2>Formation of Noun Phrases</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT IS A NOUN PHRASE?</h3>
<p>A <b>noun phrase</b> is a group of words organised around a noun or pronoun as its central element. It functions as a unit in a sentence.</p>
<pre>
books
the books
the old books
the old books on the shelf
</pre>
<p>Each expression is a noun phrase. The central noun is <b>books</b>; the other words add information about which books, their qualities or their location.</p>

<h3>2. THE HEAD NOUN</h3>
<p>The main noun in a noun phrase is called the <b>head noun</b>. Other words in the phrase depend on or describe this central noun.</p>
<pre>
the diligent learner
those three large mangoes
a very interesting story
</pre>
<ul>
<li>In ''the diligent learner'', the head noun is <b>learner</b>.</li>
<li>In ''those three large mangoes'', the head noun is <b>mangoes</b>.</li>
<li>In ''a very interesting story'', the head noun is <b>story</b>.</li>
</ul>
<p>To find the head noun, identify the main person, place, thing or idea the phrase refers to.</p>

<h3>3. DETERMINERS BEFORE THE NOUN</h3>
<p>Determiners help identify or quantify the noun. They can include articles, possessives, demonstratives and quantifiers.</p>
<pre>
a learner
the learner
my learner
this learner
several learners
</pre>
<p>The determiner usually appears before adjectives and the head noun. A noun phrase does not need to contain every possible element.</p>

<h3>4. ADJECTIVES AND OTHER DESCRIPTIVE WORDS</h3>
<p>Adjectives add information about the head noun.</p>
<pre>
a book
a useful book
a very useful reference book
</pre>
<p>Here, <i>useful</i> describes the book, while <i>very</i> modifies the adjective <i>useful</i>. The head noun remains <i>book</i>.</p>
<p>Several descriptive words may occur together, but they should help identify or describe the noun clearly.</p>

<h3>5. ADDING INFORMATION AFTER THE NOUN</h3>
<p>A noun phrase can include a prepositional phrase or a relative clause after the head noun.</p>
<pre>
the books on the table
the learner who won the prize
the house near the river
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>on the table</b> tells us which books are meant.</li>
<li><b>who won the prize</b> identifies the learner.</li>
<li><b>near the river</b> adds information about the house.</li>
</ul>
<p>These additional elements make the noun phrase more specific. The entire phrase can still function as one unit in the sentence.</p>

<h3>6. NOUN PHRASES IN SENTENCES</h3>
<pre>
The young learner answered the question.
</pre>
<p><b>The young learner</b> is a noun phrase functioning as the subject. <b>The question</b> is another noun phrase functioning as the object.</p>
<pre>
The girl with the red bag arrived.
</pre>
<p><b>The girl with the red bag</b> is the subject noun phrase. The phrase <i>with the red bag</i> adds identifying information about the girl.</p>

<h3>7. BUILDING A CLEAR NOUN PHRASE</h3>
<ol>
<li>Choose the person, place, thing or idea you want to name.</li>
<li>Add a suitable determiner if required.</li>
<li>Add descriptive words only when they improve meaning.</li>
<li>Add identifying information after the noun when necessary.</li>
<li>Check that the whole phrase makes sense in its sentence.</li>
</ol>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming a noun phrase must contain several words. ''Books'' alone can be a noun phrase.</li>
<li>Identifying an adjective as the head noun in ''the clever learner''. The head noun is ''learner''.</li>
<li>Confusing a noun phrase with a complete sentence. A noun phrase does not necessarily express a complete thought.</li>
<li>Adding too many descriptive words until the main meaning becomes unclear.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
A noun phrase is built around a head noun.
Determiners, adjectives and additional phrases can identify or describe it.
The complete phrase functions as one unit in a sentence.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formation of Noun Phrases', 15),
  (1409, 1409, '<h2>Personal Pronouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT IS A PERSONAL PRONOUN?</h3>
<p>A <b>personal pronoun</b> is a pronoun that refers to a speaker, the person or people being addressed, or someone or something being discussed.</p>
<p>Personal pronouns help avoid unnecessary repetition.</p>
<pre>
Amina is reading. Amina enjoys novels.
Amina is reading. She enjoys novels.
</pre>
<p><b>She</b> replaces the repeated name. The reader can identify who is meant from the previous sentence.</p>

<h3>2. PERSON AND NUMBER</h3>
<p>Personal pronouns are grouped according to person and number.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>First person:</b> the speaker. Singular: I; plural: we.</li>
<li><b>Second person:</b> the person or people addressed. Singular and plural: you.</li>
<li><b>Third person:</b> the person, animal or thing discussed. Singular: he, she, it; plural: they.</li>
</ul>
<p>English uses <i>you</i> for both one person and several people. The context shows which meaning is intended.</p>

<h3>3. SUBJECT PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>Subject pronouns commonly perform the subject role in a clause.</p>
<pre>
I study every evening.
You answered correctly.
He plays football.
She reads carefully.
It looks broken.
We are ready.
They arrived early.
</pre>
<p>In standard English, subject pronouns normally appear in the subject position. For example, write ''She and I went home'' rather than ''Her and me went home'' in a formal sentence where the pronouns are the subject.</p>

<h3>4. OBJECT PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>Object pronouns are used when the pronoun functions as an object of a verb or preposition.</p>
<pre>
The teacher called me.
I helped him.
We invited her.
The coach spoke to us.
The principal thanked them.
</pre>
<p>Common subject–object pairs include:</p>
<pre>
I → me
he → him
she → her
we → us
they → them
</pre>
<p><i>You</i> and <i>it</i> have the same form as subject and object pronouns.</p>

<h3>5. CHOOSING THE CORRECT FORM</h3>
<p>Ask what job the pronoun performs in the sentence.</p>
<pre>
She called him.
He called her.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, <i>she</i> performs the action and is the subject; <i>him</i> receives the action and is the object. The second sentence reverses their roles.</p>
<pre>
Between you and me, the plan may fail.
</pre>
<p>After the preposition <i>between</i>, use the object pronoun <i>me</i>, not the subject pronoun <i>I</i>.</p>

<h3>6. PRONOUNS MUST HAVE CLEAR REFERENCES</h3>
<pre>
Brian told Peter that he had won.
</pre>
<p>The pronoun <i>he</i> is ambiguous because it could refer to Brian or Peter. A clearer sentence identifies the intended person:</p>
<pre>
Brian told Peter, ''I have won.''
Brian told Peter that Peter had won.
</pre>
<p>Choose a pronoun only when the reader can reasonably identify whom or what it refers to.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''Me and Amina went home'' in formal standard English instead of ''Amina and I went home''.</li>
<li>Writing ''The teacher helped she'' instead of ''The teacher helped her''.</li>
<li>Using ''I'' after a preposition, as in ''between you and I'', in formal standard English.</li>
<li>Using a pronoun when two possible references make the sentence unclear.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Subject pronouns: I, you, he, she, it, we, they.
Object pronouns: me, you, him, her, it, us, them.
Choose the form according to its grammatical role and keep its reference clear.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Personal Pronouns', 15),
  (1410, 1410, '<h2>Possessive Pronouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT DO POSSESSIVE PRONOUNS DO?</h3>
<p><b>Possessive pronouns</b> show that something belongs to, or is associated with, a person or group. They can stand in place of a noun phrase to avoid repetition.</p>
<pre>
This book is mine.
That bag is yours.
The blue bicycle is hers.
The final decision is theirs.
</pre>
<p>In each example, the possessive pronoun identifies who the item or decision belongs to.</p>

<h3>2. THE MAIN POSSESSIVE PRONOUNS</h3>
<pre>
Personal pronoun       Possessive pronoun
I                      mine
you                    yours
he                     his
she                    hers
it                     (no commonly used independent form)
we                     ours
they                   theirs
</pre>
<p>There is no apostrophe in <i>yours</i>, <i>hers</i>, <i>ours</i> or <i>theirs</i>. The forms <i>its</i> and <i>it''s</i> have different uses: <i>its</i> is a possessive determiner, while <i>it''s</i> usually means <i>it is</i> or <i>it has</i>.</p>

<h3>3. POSSESSIVE PRONOUNS AND POSSESSIVE DETERMINERS</h3>
<p>These forms are related but they do not function in the same way.</p>
<pre>
This is my book.
This book is mine.

That is her bag.
That bag is hers.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>My</b> and <b>her</b> are possessive determiners: they come before a noun.</li>
<li><b>Mine</b> and <b>hers</b> are possessive pronouns: they stand independently without a following noun.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not write ''This book is my'' or ''That bag is her'' when you mean that the items belong to the speaker or another person.</p>

<h3>4. AVOIDING REPETITION</h3>
<pre>
Your answer is correct, and my answer is correct.
Your answer is correct, and mine is correct.
</pre>
<p><b>Mine</b> replaces the noun phrase <i>my answer</i>. The meaning remains clear without repeating the noun.</p>
<pre>
Their classroom is large. Our classroom is small.
Their classroom is large. Ours is small.
</pre>
<p><b>Ours</b> replaces <i>our classroom</i>. This is one reason possessive pronouns make writing more concise.</p>

<h3>5. POSSESSIVE PRONOUNS DO NOT USE APOSTROPHES</h3>
<pre>
Correct: The victory was theirs.
Correct: That seat is hers.
Incorrect: The victory was their''s.
Incorrect: That seat is her''s.
</pre>
<p>Unlike many possessive nouns, possessive pronouns are not written with an apostrophe.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using a possessive determiner without a noun: ''The bag is my'' instead of ''The bag is mine''.</li>
<li>Adding an apostrophe to ''yours'', ''hers'', ''ours'' or ''theirs''.</li>
<li>Confusing ''its'' with ''it''s''.</li>
<li>Repeating a noun phrase when a clear possessive pronoun would be more concise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Possessive determiner + noun: my book.
Possessive pronoun standing alone: the book is mine.
Possessive pronouns do not take apostrophes.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Possessive Pronouns', 15),
  (1411, 1411, '<h2>Reflexive and Emphatic Pronouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT ARE THESE PRONOUNS?</h3>
<p>Reflexive and emphatic pronouns use forms ending in <b>-self</b> or <b>-selves</b>. The same form can serve different purposes depending on the sentence.</p>
<pre>
myself
yourself
himself
herself
itself
ourselves
yourselves
themselves
</pre>
<p>Use the singular forms with singular references and the plural forms with plural references. <i>Yourself</i> normally refers to one person; <i>yourselves</i> refers to more than one person addressed.</p>

<h3>2. REFLEXIVE PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>A <b>reflexive pronoun</b> is used when the object refers back to the subject of the same clause.</p>
<pre>
Amina blamed herself.
The boy injured himself.
I taught myself to swim.
They prepared themselves for the exam.
</pre>
<p>In ''Amina blamed herself'', <i>Amina</i> is both the person performing the action and the person receiving the blame. The pronoun refers back to the subject.</p>

<h3>3. WHY THE REFLEXIVE FORM MATTERS</h3>
<pre>
He washed him.
He washed himself.
</pre>
<p>The first sentence normally means that he washed another male person. The second means that he washed his own body.</p>
<p>The reflexive pronoun removes the distinction that would otherwise be unclear. Use it when the subject and object refer to the same person or thing and the sentence requires that form.</p>

<h3>4. EMPHATIC PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>An <b>emphatic pronoun</b>, also called an intensive pronoun, adds emphasis to a noun or pronoun. It stresses who performed an action or who was involved.</p>
<pre>
The principal herself welcomed the visitors.
I myself completed the assignment.
The players themselves organised the event.
</pre>
<p>Here, the pronouns emphasise the principal, the speaker and the players. They do not function as objects referring back to the subject.</p>

<h3>5. THE REMOVAL TEST</h3>
<p>A useful way to distinguish the two uses is to remove the pronoun and see what happens to the sentence''s basic meaning and structure.</p>
<pre>
She introduced herself.
She introduced.

The principal herself opened the meeting.
The principal opened the meeting.
</pre>
<ul>
<li>In the first pair, removing <i>herself</i> leaves the sentence incomplete or changes the intended meaning because the reflexive object is needed in this use of <i>introduce</i>.</li>
<li>In the second pair, removing <i>herself</i> leaves a complete sentence. The pronoun only adds emphasis.</li>
</ul>
<p>This test is useful, but always consider the meaning and grammar of the full sentence.</p>

<h3>6. REFLEXIVE PRONOUNS ARE NOT A SUBSTITUTE FOR OBJECT PRONOUNS</h3>
<pre>
Correct: The teacher spoke to me.
Incorrect in standard usage: The teacher spoke to myself.
Correct: Please contact me.
Incorrect in standard usage: Please contact myself.
</pre>
<p>Use a reflexive pronoun when it refers back to the subject or when it adds emphasis to a suitable noun phrase. Do not use it merely to sound formal.</p>

<h3>7. SINGULAR AND PLURAL FORMS</h3>
<pre>
I → myself
you (one person) → yourself
he → himself
she → herself
it → itself
we → ourselves
you (several people) → yourselves
they → themselves
</pre>
<p>For example, ''The learners prepared themselves'' refers to several learners. ''The learner prepared himself'' refers to one learner when the masculine pronoun is appropriate to the context.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using ''myself'' instead of ''me'' after a verb or preposition when no reflexive or emphatic meaning is intended.</li>
<li>Using an emphatic pronoun without identifying the person or group being emphasised.</li>
<li>Confusing ''He saw him'' with ''He saw himself''.</li>
<li>Using ''theirself'' in place of the standard plural form ''themselves'' in formal school writing.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Reflexive: the pronoun refers back to the subject.
Emphatic: the pronoun adds emphasis and can usually be removed.
The same -self or -selves form can serve either function.
</pre>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reflexive and Emphatic Pronouns', 15),
  (1412, 1412, '<h2>Demonstrative Pronouns</h2>
<p>A <strong>demonstrative pronoun</strong> points to a particular person, object, place, or idea. It helps the reader or listener identify exactly what is being referred to.</p>

<h3>The four demonstrative pronouns</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>This</strong> — refers to one person or thing that is near.</li>
  <li><strong>That</strong> — refers to one person or thing that is farther away.</li>
  <li><strong>These</strong> — refers to more than one person or thing that is near.</li>
  <li><strong>Those</strong> — refers to more than one person or thing that is farther away.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Choosing the correct pronoun</h3>
<p>Two questions help you choose correctly:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Is the noun singular or plural?</li>
  <li>Is it being presented as near or farther away?</li>
</ol>
<p>For example, if you are holding one book, you might say, <em>This is my book.</em> If you are pointing to several books across the room, you might say, <em>Those are my books.</em></p>

<h3>Demonstrative pronouns versus demonstrative determiners</h3>
<p>A demonstrative pronoun stands in place of a noun phrase: <em>These are expensive.</em></p>
<p>A demonstrative determiner comes before a noun and identifies it: <em>These shoes are expensive.</em></p>
<p>The word <em>these</em> is the pronoun in the first sentence because it stands alone. In the second, it determines which shoes are being discussed.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>This are my notes.</em> Incorrect: <em>this</em> is singular. Write <em>These are my notes.</em></li>
  <li><em>Those is my bag.</em> Incorrect: <em>those</em> is plural. Write <em>That is my bag.</em></li>
  <li>Choosing a pronoun based only on distance while ignoring number. Both number and intended reference matter.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Apply the skill</h3>
<p>Imagine a learner pointing to one pen on a nearby desk and several pens on a distant desk. The learner can say, <em>This is my pen</em> and <em>Those are my pens.</em> The pronouns make the intended references clear without repeating the nouns.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Demonstrative Pronouns', 15),
  (1413, 1413, '<h2>Relative Pronouns</h2>
<p>A <strong>relative pronoun</strong> introduces a clause that gives information about a noun or pronoun already mentioned. It connects that information to the noun being described.</p>

<h3>Common relative pronouns</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Who</strong> — refers to people and commonly functions as the subject of the relative clause.</li>
  <li><strong>Whom</strong> — refers to people when they function as an object in formal English.</li>
  <li><strong>Whose</strong> — expresses possession or association.</li>
  <li><strong>Which</strong> — refers to animals or things.</li>
  <li><strong>That</strong> — can refer to people, animals or things in many identifying relative clauses.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How a relative pronoun works</h3>
<p>Consider these two sentences:</p>
<p><em>The girl won the race. She trained every day.</em></p>
<p>They can be combined as:</p>
<p><em>The girl who trained every day won the race.</em></p>
<p>The clause <em>who trained every day</em> identifies or describes the girl. The relative pronoun who connects that clause to the noun girl.</p>

<h3>Choosing the correct pronoun</h3>
<p><strong>Who:</strong> <em>The student who answered correctly received a mark.</em></p>
<p><strong>Whom:</strong> <em>The student whom we congratulated smiled.</em> In the relative clause, we congratulated the student; the student is the object of congratulated.</p>
<p><strong>Whose:</strong> <em>The learner whose book was missing reported it.</em></p>
<p><strong>Which:</strong> <em>The machine which stopped working was repaired.</em></p>
<p><strong>That:</strong> <em>The book that I borrowed is interesting.</em></p>

<h3>Identifying and additional information</h3>
<p>A defining relative clause identifies which person or thing is meant: <em>The player who scored the goal is my cousin.</em> Without the clause, the reader may not know which player is meant.</p>
<p>A non-defining relative clause adds extra information: <em>My cousin, who lives in Embu, is visiting.</em> The clause is separated by commas. In standard usage, <em>that</em> is not used to introduce a non-defining relative clause.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using <em>whose</em> when the sentence requires a subject relative pronoun: <em>The boy whose won</em> is incorrect; write <em>The boy who won</em>.</li>
  <li>Using <em>which</em> to refer to a person in a context where <em>who</em> is expected.</li>
  <li>Confusing <em>who</em> and <em>whom</em>. In formal English, use who for a subject and whom for an object.</li>
  <li>Leaving out commas around non-defining clauses.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relative Pronouns', 15),
  (1414, 1414, '<h2>Indefinite Pronouns</h2>
<p>An <strong>indefinite pronoun</strong> refers to a person, thing or amount without identifying it precisely. It is useful when the identity or quantity is unknown, unimportant or deliberately left unspecified.</p>

<h3>Common indefinite pronouns</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>People:</strong> someone, somebody, anyone, anybody, everyone, everybody, no one, nobody.</li>
  <li><strong>Things:</strong> something, anything, everything, nothing.</li>
  <li><strong>Amounts or choices:</strong> all, some, any, none, many, few, several, each, either, neither.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pronoun meaning matters</h3>
<p><em>Someone has left a notebook.</em> The speaker knows that a person left it but does not identify the person.</p>
<p><em>Nothing was damaged.</em> The pronoun refers to no thing being damaged.</p>
<p><em>Several arrived early.</em> Several stands in for a plural group of people or things understood from context.</p>

<h3>Subject–verb agreement</h3>
<p>Indefinite pronouns do not all take the same kind of verb.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Usually singular:</strong> everyone, everybody, someone, somebody, anyone, anybody, no one, nobody, each, either and neither.</li>
  <li><strong>Usually plural:</strong> both, few, many and several.</li>
  <li><strong>Depends on meaning or context:</strong> all, some, any and none.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples: <em>Everyone is ready.</em> <em>Several are absent.</em> <em>Some of the water is dirty.</em> <em>Some of the books are missing.</em></p>
<p>With <em>all</em>, <em>some</em> and similar words, look at the noun or amount being referred to. An uncountable amount such as water takes a singular verb; a plural countable noun such as books takes a plural verb.</p>

<h3>Singular meaning and inclusive reference</h3>
<p>Words such as <em>everyone</em> and <em>someone</em> are grammatically singular, even though they refer to people. In contemporary English, singular <em>they</em> is widely used when a person''s gender is unknown or irrelevant: <em>Everyone should bring their book.</em></p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>Everyone are ready.</em> Use <em>Everyone is ready.</em></li>
  <li><em>Several is missing.</em> Use <em>Several are missing.</em></li>
  <li>Assuming that <em>some</em> always takes a singular or always takes a plural verb. The noun or amount being referred to determines agreement.</li>
  <li>Using a pronoun without enough context for the reader to understand what it refers to.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Indefinite Pronouns', 15),
  (1415, 1415, '<h2>Pronoun–Antecedent Agreement</h2>
<p>An <strong>antecedent</strong> is the word or phrase a pronoun refers to. Pronoun–antecedent agreement means that the pronoun must fit its antecedent in number and, where relevant, person and meaning.</p>

<h3>Find the antecedent first</h3>
<p>In <em>Amina forgot her notebook</em>, the antecedent of <em>her</em> is Amina. In <em>The players collected their equipment</em>, the antecedent of <em>their</em> is the players.</p>
<p>Before choosing a pronoun, ask: who or what does it refer to? Then check whether the reference is clear and consistent.</p>

<h3>Agreement in number</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Singular antecedent: <em>The boy said he was ready.</em></li>
  <li>Plural antecedent: <em>The boys said they were ready.</em></li>
  <li>Singular indefinite pronoun: <em>Everyone must bring their book.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Singular <em>they</em> is standard in contemporary English when referring to one person whose gender is unknown, irrelevant or expressed as they/them. Do not assume that every pronoun referring to a grammatically singular word must be he or she.</p>

<h3>Compound antecedents</h3>
<p>When two people or things are joined by <em>and</em>, the antecedent is usually plural: <em>Brian and Wanjiku said they would attend.</em></p>
<p>When alternatives are joined by <em>or</em> or <em>nor</em>, agreement depends on the structure and meaning. A clear rewrite is often better than a confusing pronoun: instead of <em>Either the teacher or the learners must bring his book</em>, write <em>The teacher must bring the teacher''s book, or the learners must bring their books.</em></p>

<h3>Clear reference</h3>
<p>A pronoun can agree grammatically but still be unclear.</p>
<p><em>When Kamau spoke to Otieno, he was angry.</em> The reader cannot be certain whether he refers to Kamau or Otieno.</p>
<p>Improve it by naming the person: <em>Kamau was angry when he spoke to Otieno.</em> Or, if Otieno was angry: <em>When Kamau spoke to Otieno, Otieno was angry.</em></p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using a plural pronoun for a single, specific antecedent without a reason.</li>
  <li>Using a singular verb or pronoun with a plural antecedent when the sentence requires plural agreement.</li>
  <li>Allowing a pronoun to refer to two possible nouns.</li>
  <li>Changing the intended reference midway through a sentence.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Editing method</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Underline the pronoun.</li>
  <li>Identify the exact antecedent.</li>
  <li>Check number and intended reference.</li>
  <li>Rewrite the sentence if the relationship remains unclear.</li>
</ol>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Pronoun–Antecedent Agreement', 15),
  (1416, 1416, '<h2>Main and Auxiliary Verbs</h2>
<p>A <strong>main verb</strong> expresses the central action, event or state in a clause. An <strong>auxiliary verb</strong>, also called a helping verb, works with a main verb to express features such as tense, aspect, voice, questions, negation or modality.</p>

<h3>Main verbs</h3>
<p>Main verbs carry the central meaning of the clause:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The learners <strong>study</strong> every evening.</em></li>
  <li><em>The baby <strong>slept</strong> peacefully.</em></li>
  <li><em>The room <strong>seems</strong> quiet.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Main verbs can describe actions (<em>write</em>, <em>run</em>), events (<em>happen</em>, <em>break</em>) or states (<em>know</em>, <em>belong</em>, <em>seem</em>).</p>

<h3>The primary auxiliary verbs</h3>
<p>The three primary auxiliaries are <strong>be</strong>, <strong>have</strong> and <strong>do</strong>. Their forms change according to tense and subject.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Be:</strong> am, is, are, was, were, be, being, been. It helps form continuous tenses and the passive voice.</li>
  <li><strong>Have:</strong> have, has, had. It helps form perfect tenses.</li>
  <li><strong>Do:</strong> do, does, did. It helps form many questions, negatives and emphatic statements in the simple present and simple past.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How auxiliaries work</h3>
<p><strong>Continuous aspect:</strong> <em>She is reading.</em> Is is the auxiliary; reading is the main verb in its -ing form.</p>
<p><strong>Perfect aspect:</strong> <em>They have finished.</em> Have is the auxiliary; finished is the main verb in its past participle form.</p>
<p><strong>Passive voice:</strong> <em>The letter was written by Ali.</em> Was is the auxiliary; written is the main verb in its past participle form.</p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> <em>Do you understand?</em> Do helps form the question; understand is the main verb.</p>
<p><strong>Negative:</strong> <em>He does not agree.</em> Does helps form the negative; agree is the main verb.</p>

<h3>Modal auxiliary verbs</h3>
<p>Modals include <em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will</em> and <em>would</em>. They express meanings such as ability, possibility, obligation, advice or willingness.</p>
<p>Examples: <em>She can swim.</em> <em>You should revise.</em> <em>They might arrive later.</em></p>
<p>A modal is followed by the base form of the main verb: <em>She can swim</em>, not <em>She can swims</em> or <em>She can to swim</em>.</p>

<h3>One verb can serve different roles</h3>
<p><em>They have a car.</em> Here, have is the main verb because it expresses possession.</p>
<p><em>They have bought a car.</em> Here, have is an auxiliary because it helps form the present perfect; bought is the main verb.</p>
<p><em>She is happy.</em> Here, is links the subject to a description and functions as the main linking verb.</p>
<p><em>She is singing.</em> Here, is is an auxiliary helping the main verb singing.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>Does she likes tea?</em> Write <em>Does she like tea?</em> The auxiliary does carries the tense, so the main verb uses its base form.</li>
  <li><em>He can plays.</em> Write <em>He can play.</em></li>
  <li>Calling every form of be an auxiliary. It can also function as the main linking verb.</li>
  <li>Confusing the main verb''s meaning with the auxiliary''s grammatical job.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Main and Auxiliary Verbs', 15),
  (1417, 1417, '<h2>Action and Linking Verbs</h2>
<p>A verb can express an action or connect a subject to information about that subject. Understanding this difference helps you interpret sentences accurately and construct them correctly.</p>

<h3>Action verbs</h3>
<p>An <strong>action verb</strong> expresses something a person, animal or thing does, or an event that takes place. The action may be physical or mental.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Physical action: <em>The farmer planted maize.</em></li>
  <li>Mental action: <em>Achieng remembered the answer.</em></li>
  <li>Event: <em>The glass broke.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Action verbs do not always describe movements that can be seen. Words such as <em>think, understand, believe</em> and <em>remember</em> can express mental activity.</p>

<h3>Linking verbs</h3>
<p>A <strong>linking verb</strong> connects the subject to a word or phrase that identifies or describes it. It does not express an action performed on an object in that use.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The soup tastes salty.</em> Salty describes the soup.</li>
  <li><em>Wanjiku became a nurse.</em> A nurse identifies what Wanjiku became.</li>
  <li><em>The learners seem tired.</em> Tired describes the learners.</li>
</ul>
<p>Common linking verbs include forms of <em>be</em> (am, is, are, was, were), and verbs such as <em>become, seem, appear, remain, feel, look, smell, sound, taste</em> and <em>grow</em> when they connect a subject to a description or identity.</p>

<h3>The same verb can perform different jobs</h3>
<p>Do not classify a verb by memorising the word alone. Examine how it is used.</p>
<p><em>The soup smells delicious.</em> Smells is linking because delicious describes the soup.</p>
<p><em>The cook smells the soup.</em> Smells expresses an action performed by the cook.</p>
<p><em>She looked tired.</em> Looked is linking because tired describes she.</p>
<p><em>She looked at the timetable.</em> Looked expresses an action directed towards the timetable.</p>

<h3>Subject complements</h3>
<p>The description or identity after a linking verb is called a <strong>subject complement</strong>. It completes the meaning by referring back to the subject.</p>
<p>In <em>The road became slippery</em>, slippery describes the road. It is not an object receiving an action.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming that every verb involving the senses is linking. In <em>He tasted the stew</em>, tasted expresses an action.</li>
  <li>Calling the adjective after a linking verb an object. In <em>The sky became dark</em>, dark describes the subject.</li>
  <li>Assuming that action verbs must be physical. <em>She knows the answer</em> expresses a mental state.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Action and Linking Verbs', 15),
  (1418, 1418, '<h2>Regular and Irregular Verbs</h2>
<p>Verbs change form to express grammatical information such as tense. Regular and irregular verbs differ in how they form their past simple and past participle forms.</p>

<h3>Regular verbs</h3>
<p>A <strong>regular verb</strong> normally forms both the past simple and the past participle by adding <em>-ed</em> or <em>-d</em> to its base form.</p>
<ul>
  <li>work → worked → worked</li>
  <li>clean → cleaned → cleaned</li>
  <li>live → lived → lived</li>
</ul>
<p>The three forms shown are the base form, past simple and past participle. The past participle is used in constructions such as <em>has worked</em> and <em>was cleaned</em>.</p>

<h3>Spelling changes in regular verbs</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Most verbs add <em>-ed</em>: <em>help → helped</em>.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in silent <em>-e</em> usually add <em>-d</em>: <em>arrive → arrived</em>.</li>
  <li>Many consonant + <em>y</em> endings change <em>y</em> to <em>i</em> before <em>-ed</em>: <em>study → studied</em>.</li>
  <li>When a verb ends in vowel + <em>y</em>, retain the <em>y</em>: <em>play → played</em>.</li>
  <li>Some short verbs double the final consonant: <em>stop → stopped</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Consonant doubling depends on spelling and stress patterns; it does not happen with every verb ending in a consonant.</p>

<h3>Irregular verbs</h3>
<p>An <strong>irregular verb</strong> does not follow the usual pattern for forming its past simple and past participle. Its forms must be learnt through use.</p>
<ul>
  <li>go → went → gone</li>
  <li>write → wrote → written</li>
  <li>eat → ate → eaten</li>
  <li>see → saw → seen</li>
  <li>buy → bought → bought</li>
  <li>cut → cut → cut</li>
</ul>
<p>Some irregular verbs change both forms; some change only one; others have the same form in all three positions.</p>

<h3>Past simple versus past participle</h3>
<p>Use the <strong>past simple</strong> to express a past event: <em>She wrote a letter yesterday.</em></p>
<p>Use the <strong>past participle</strong> with an appropriate auxiliary in constructions such as the perfect aspect or passive voice: <em>She has written a letter.</em> <em>The letter was written yesterday.</em></p>
<p>Do not confuse these forms. In standard English, <em>She has went</em> is incorrect; write <em>She has gone</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using the past simple after <em>has</em> or <em>have</em>: <em>has ate</em> should be <em>has eaten</em>.</li>
  <li>Adding <em>-ed</em> to an irregular verb: <em>goed</em> should be <em>went</em>.</li>
  <li>Assuming all verbs with identical past and participle forms are regular. <em>Buy → bought → bought</em> is irregular.</li>
  <li>Ignoring spelling changes in regular verbs such as <em>study → studied</em>.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Regular and Irregular Verbs', 15),
  (1419, 1419, '<h2>Transitive and Intransitive Verbs</h2>
<p>A verb''s use can be classified by whether it takes a direct object. A <strong>transitive verb</strong> has a direct object in that use. An <strong>intransitive verb</strong> does not take a direct object in that use.</p>

<h3>Direct objects</h3>
<p>A direct object receives or is directly affected by the action expressed by the verb. It answers questions such as <em>what?</em> or <em>whom?</em> after the verb.</p>
<p><em>The learner answered the question.</em></p>
<p>Answered what? <em>The question.</em> Therefore, answered is transitive in this sentence.</p>
<p><em>The teacher praised Amina.</em></p>
<p>Praised whom? <em>Amina.</em> Praised is transitive in this sentence.</p>

<h3>Intransitive verbs</h3>
<p>An intransitive verb does not take a direct object in the sentence.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The baby cried.</em></li>
  <li><em>The visitors arrived late.</em></li>
  <li><em>The sun disappeared.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Words such as <em>late</em> and phrases such as <em>at noon</em> can provide additional information without becoming direct objects.</p>

<h3>Do not confuse an object with an adverbial</h3>
<p>In <em>The child slept on the sofa</em>, slept has no direct object. On the sofa tells where the child slept.</p>
<p>In <em>The child kicked the ball across the field</em>, the ball is the direct object; across the field tells where the action took place.</p>
<p>Do not assume that every noun after a verb is an object. Its grammatical role matters.</p>

<h3>Some verbs can be both</h3>
<p>Many verbs are transitive in one sentence and intransitive in another.</p>
<p><em>She opened the door.</em> Opened is transitive because it has the object the door.</p>
<p><em>The door opened.</em> Opened is intransitive because the sentence has no direct object.</p>
<p><em>They are eating lunch.</em> Eating is transitive because lunch is the object.</p>
<p><em>They are eating.</em> Eating is intransitive in this sentence because no direct object is expressed.</p>

<h3>Direct and indirect objects</h3>
<p>Some transitive verbs can take both an indirect object and a direct object.</p>
<p><em>The teacher gave the learner a book.</em></p>
<p>The teacher gave what? <em>A book</em> is the direct object. Gave it to whom? <em>The learner</em> is the indirect object.</p>
<p>The same meaning can be expressed as <em>The teacher gave a book to the learner.</em></p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Calling a verb intransitive simply because it is followed by extra words. Extra words may be adverbials, not objects.</li>
  <li>Assuming a verb has the same transitivity in every sentence.</li>
  <li>Confusing the direct object with the subject. In <em>The ball broke the window</em>, ball is the subject and window is the direct object.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transitive and Intransitive Verbs', 15),
  (1420, 1420, '<h2>Finite and Non-finite Verbs</h2>
<p>Verb forms differ in whether they are marked for tense and participate directly in the clause''s subject–verb relationship. A <strong>finite verb</strong> functions as the verb of a clause and is associated with tense and, where relevant, subject agreement. A <strong>non-finite verb form</strong> does not independently express tense in that way.</p>

<h3>Finite verbs</h3>
<p>Finite verbs anchor a clause in time or grammatical structure.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>She walks to school.</em> Walks is finite and agrees with she in the simple present.</li>
  <li><em>They walked to school.</em> Walked is finite and expresses past time.</li>
  <li><em>The children are playing.</em> Are is finite; it agrees with the plural subject.</li>
  <li><em>He has finished.</em> Has is the finite auxiliary; finished is a non-finite past participle.</li>
</ul>
<p>A finite verb may consist of more than one word. In a verb group such as <em>has been working</em>, has is finite, while been and working are non-finite forms.</p>

<h3>Non-finite verb forms</h3>
<p>Three common non-finite forms are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Infinitive:</strong> <em>to read</em>, or the bare form <em>read</em> after some verbs and modals.</li>
  <li><strong>Gerund-participle (-ing form):</strong> <em>reading</em>, as in <em>Reading improves vocabulary</em> or <em>She is reading</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Past participle:</strong> <em>written</em>, <em>broken</em>, <em>finished</em>, as in <em>the broken window</em> or <em>has finished</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The exact grammatical role depends on the construction. For example, reading is non-finite in <em>Reading is useful</em>, where the -ing form functions as the subject. In <em>She is reading</em>, reading is also non-finite, while is carries tense.</p>

<h3>One sentence can contain both</h3>
<p><em>After finishing her homework, Wanjiku went outside.</em></p>
<p>Finishing is non-finite; it does not independently locate the action in tense. Went is finite and anchors the main clause in the past.</p>
<p><em>The work completed yesterday has been checked.</em></p>
<p>Completed is a past participle in a non-finite modifier. Has is finite and been checked forms the passive perfect verb group.</p>

<h3>How to identify the finite verb</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Find the clause''s verb group.</li>
  <li>Look for the verb form carrying tense or the agreement with the subject.</li>
  <li>If the group contains auxiliaries, identify the finite auxiliary rather than assuming every verb form is finite.</li>
  <li>Check whether other verb forms are infinitives, -ing forms or past participles dependent on the clause structure.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Calling every verb in a sentence finite.</li>
  <li>Assuming that every word ending in -ed is a finite past-tense verb. In <em>has finished</em>, finished is a past participle.</li>
  <li>Assuming an -ing form always forms a continuous tense. In <em>Swimming is enjoyable</em>, swimming functions as the subject.</li>
  <li>Confusing the number of verb words with the number of finite verbs.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Finite and Non-finite Verbs', 15),
  (1421, 1421, '<h2>Infinitives</h2>
<p>An <strong>infinitive</strong> is a non-finite verb form. English commonly uses the <strong>to-infinitive</strong> (to + base verb), as in <em>to learn</em>, and the <strong>bare infinitive</strong>, which is the base verb without to, as in <em>learn</em>.</p>

<h3>To-infinitives</h3>
<p>The to-infinitive can perform several roles in a sentence.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>After certain verbs:</strong> <em>She hopes to pass.</em></li>
  <li><strong>To express purpose:</strong> <em>He went to the library to study.</em></li>
  <li><strong>After adjectives:</strong> <em>I am ready to begin.</em></li>
  <li><strong>As a subject or subject-like construction:</strong> <em>To read widely is useful.</em></li>
  <li><strong>After question words:</strong> <em>She knows how to solve the problem.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>Bare infinitives</h3>
<p>Some words and constructions require the base verb without to.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>After modal auxiliaries:</strong> <em>can swim</em>, <em>should revise</em>, <em>might arrive</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>After do-support:</strong> <em>Does he know?</em>, <em>They did not leave.</em></li>
  <li><strong>After make and let in common active constructions:</strong> <em>The teacher made us rewrite the paragraph.</em> <em>Let them speak.</em></li>
  <li><strong>After verbs of perception in certain constructions:</strong> <em>I saw him cross the road.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Some constructions change in the passive: <em>We were made to rewrite the paragraph.</em> Here, to is required.</p>

<h3>Infinitives after verbs</h3>
<p>Some verbs are commonly followed by a to-infinitive:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>agree to help</em></li>
  <li><em>decide to leave</em></li>
  <li><em>hope to succeed</em></li>
  <li><em>plan to study</em></li>
  <li><em>promise to return</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Other verbs commonly take an -ing form, such as <em>enjoy reading</em> and <em>avoid wasting</em>. Some verbs can take either form with little change in meaning; others change meaning. Learn the pattern used by the particular verb rather than applying one rule to all verbs.</p>

<h3>Purpose and result</h3>
<p>Compare:</p>
<p><em>She went to the shop to buy bread.</em> The infinitive explains her purpose.</p>
<p><em>He was too tired to continue.</em> The infinitive completes a construction expressing that he was excessively tired for the action to be possible.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She can to swim.</em> Write <em>She can swim.</em></li>
  <li><em>He decided going home.</em> In this meaning, write <em>He decided to go home.</em></li>
  <li><em>The teacher made us to repeat it.</em> In the active construction, write <em>The teacher made us repeat it.</em></li>
  <li>Assuming every verb must be followed by an infinitive. The required pattern depends on the verb and construction.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Infinitives', 15),
  (1422, 1422, '<h2>Gerunds</h2>
<p>A <strong>gerund</strong> is an -ing form of a verb used in a position normally occupied by a noun phrase. It can name an activity, express an idea about an action, or act as the complement of certain verbs and prepositions.</p>

<h3>Gerunds as subjects</h3>
<p>A gerund can name the activity being discussed.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Reading improves vocabulary.</em></li>
  <li><em>Swimming strengthens the body.</em></li>
  <li><em>Cheating damages trust.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>In each sentence, the -ing form identifies an activity that the sentence makes a statement about.</p>

<h3>Gerunds as objects</h3>
<p>Some verbs are commonly followed by a gerund:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>enjoy reading</em></li>
  <li><em>avoid wasting</em></li>
  <li><em>finish writing</em></li>
  <li><em>suggest revising</em></li>
  <li><em>consider changing</em></li>
  <li><em>practise speaking</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Example: <em>She enjoys solving mathematical problems.</em> Solving is the gerund form functioning as the complement of enjoys.</p>

<h3>Gerunds after prepositions</h3>
<p>When a verb follows a preposition, the -ing form is normally used.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>He succeeded by working consistently.</em></li>
  <li><em>She left without saying goodbye.</em></li>
  <li><em>They are interested in learning English.</em></li>
  <li><em>Before answering, read the question carefully.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Be careful with expressions such as <em>look forward to</em>. Here, to is a preposition, so it is followed by a gerund: <em>I look forward to meeting you.</em></p>

<h3>Gerunds and infinitives</h3>
<p>Some verbs are normally followed by a gerund, some by an infinitive, and some by either form.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>She enjoys drawing.</em> Enjoy is followed by a gerund.</li>
  <li><em>She hopes to draw.</em> Hope is followed by a to-infinitive.</li>
  <li><em>He started reading.</em> <em>He started to read.</em> Both are possible with little difference in meaning in this context.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some verbs change meaning depending on the form that follows them. For example, <em>stop smoking</em> means cease the activity of smoking, while <em>stop to smoke</em> means pause another activity in order to smoke.</p>

<h3>Gerunds versus continuous verb forms</h3>
<p>Both forms end in -ing, but their grammatical roles differ.</p>
<p><em>Swimming is enjoyable.</em> Swimming names an activity and functions as the subject.</p>
<p><em>She is swimming.</em> Swimming is part of a continuous verb construction, with is as the finite auxiliary.</p>
<p>The spelling alone does not determine whether an -ing form is a gerund. Examine how it functions in the sentence.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>He enjoys to read.</em> In standard usage, write <em>He enjoys reading.</em></li>
  <li><em>She is interested in to learn.</em> Write <em>She is interested in learning.</em></li>
  <li><em>I look forward to meet you.</em> Write <em>I look forward to meeting you.</em></li>
  <li>Calling every -ing form a gerund, even when it forms part of a continuous verb construction.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Gerunds', 15),
  (1423, 1423, '<h2>Participles</h2>
<p>A <strong>participle</strong> is a verb form that can help construct verb phrases or function in descriptions and clauses. Two important forms are the present participle, which ends in -ing, and the past participle, such as written, broken or finished.</p>

<h3>Present participles</h3>
<p>The present participle is the -ing form used in constructions such as the continuous aspect.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The children are playing.</em></li>
  <li><em>She was preparing supper.</em></li>
  <li><em>They have been waiting.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>In these examples, the participle works with an auxiliary verb. It does not independently express the tense of the whole verb group.</p>

<h3>Past participles</h3>
<p>Regular verbs usually form the past participle with -ed or -d, while irregular verbs may have different forms.</p>
<ul>
  <li>work → worked</li>
  <li>write → written</li>
  <li>break → broken</li>
  <li>choose → chosen</li>
  <li>take → taken</li>
</ul>
<p>Past participles appear in perfect constructions and passive constructions.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Perfect: <em>She has written the report.</em></li>
  <li>Passive: <em>The report was written yesterday.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>Participles as modifiers</h3>
<p>A participle can describe a noun.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The crying baby needs attention.</em> Crying describes the baby.</li>
  <li><em>The broken window was replaced.</em> Broken describes the window.</li>
  <li><em>The students waiting outside will enter next.</em> Waiting describes which students are meant.</li>
</ul>
<p>Present participles often suggest an ongoing activity or characteristic. Past participles often suggest a completed action, a resulting state or a passive relationship, depending on the verb and context.</p>

<h3>Participial phrases</h3>
<p>A participial phrase contains a participle and the words that belong with it.</p>
<p><em>Carrying a heavy bag, the learner climbed the stairs.</em></p>
<p><em>Carrying a heavy bag</em> describes the learner and gives additional information about the action.</p>
<p>The phrase must clearly refer to the noun it is intended to describe.</p>

<h3>Avoid dangling participles</h3>
<p>Compare:</p>
<p><em>Walking to school, the rain began to fall.</em></p>
<p>This sentence mistakenly suggests that the rain was walking to school.</p>
<p>Write instead: <em>Walking to school, I noticed that the rain had begun to fall.</em></p>
<p>Now the participial phrase clearly describes I, the person walking.</p>

<h3>Participles versus gerunds</h3>
<p>Both gerunds and present participles end in -ing. A gerund functions like a noun phrase: <em>Reading helps me learn.</em> A participle may form part of a verb group or describe a noun: <em>I am reading</em>; <em>the reading child</em>.</p>
<p>Some grammar frameworks use the broader term <em>-ing form</em> for both gerunds and present participles. The important skill is identifying the form''s role in context.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She has wrote a letter.</em> Use the past participle: <em>She has written a letter.</em></li>
  <li>Using a participial phrase that appears to describe the wrong noun.</li>
  <li>Assuming every past participle expresses past time by itself. In <em>a broken cup</em>, broken describes the cup rather than anchoring a finite clause in past time.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Participles', 15),
  (1424, 1424, '<h2>Present Simple</h2>
<p>The <strong>present simple</strong> is used to describe habits, repeated actions, general truths, stable situations and certain scheduled events. It does not automatically mean that an action is happening at this exact moment.</p>

<h3>Forming the present simple</h3>
<p>With <strong>I, you, we</strong> and <strong>they</strong>, use the base form:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>I study every evening.</em></li>
  <li><em>They walk to school.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>With <strong>he, she</strong> and <strong>it</strong>, the verb normally takes -s or -es:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>She studies every evening.</em></li>
  <li><em>He watches the news.</em></li>
  <li><em>The bus arrives at six.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>Spelling rules for the third-person singular</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Most verbs add -s: <em>read → reads</em>.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in -s, -sh, -ch, -x or -o commonly add -es: <em>wash → washes</em>, <em>go → goes</em>.</li>
  <li>Many verbs ending in consonant + y change y to i and add -es: <em>study → studies</em>.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in vowel + y usually add -s: <em>play → plays</em>.</li>
  <li>Be and have have special forms: <em>she is</em>, <em>she has</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>When to use it</h3>
<p><strong>Habits:</strong> <em>My brother exercises every morning.</em></p>
<p><strong>General truths:</strong> <em>Water freezes at 0°C under standard atmospheric pressure.</em></p>
<p><strong>Stable situations:</strong> <em>She lives in Embu.</em></p>
<p><strong>Schedules:</strong> <em>The lesson starts at eight tomorrow.</em></p>
<p><strong>Instructions or commentary:</strong> <em>First, you add the flour. Then you mix the ingredients.</em></p>

<h3>Negative sentences</h3>
<p>Use do not or don''t with I, you, we and they. Use does not or doesn''t with he, she and it.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>I do not understand the question.</em></li>
  <li><em>She does not understand the question.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>After does or doesn''t, use the base form: <em>She doesn''t understand</em>, not <em>She doesn''t understands</em>.</p>

<h3>Questions</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>Do you attend the club?</em></li>
  <li><em>Does he attend the club?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Use the base form after do or does. The auxiliary carries the grammatical marking.</p>
<p>With the verb be, form questions without do: <em>Is she ready?</em> not <em>Does she be ready?</em></p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>He walk to school.</em> Write <em>He walks to school.</em></li>
  <li><em>Does Amina studies?</em> Write <em>Does Amina study?</em></li>
  <li><em>She don''t know.</em> Write <em>She doesn''t know.</em></li>
  <li>Using the present simple for an action in progress when the context calls for the present continuous.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Present Simple', 15),
  (1425, 1425, '<h2>Present Continuous</h2>
<p>The <strong>present continuous</strong>, also called the present progressive, describes an action in progress around the present time, a temporary situation, a developing change or a future arrangement.</p>

<h3>Forming the present continuous</h3>
<p>Use the present form of be + the -ing form of the main verb.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I <strong>am studying</strong>.</li>
  <li>He <strong>is reading</strong>.</li>
  <li>They <strong>are working</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The auxiliary must agree with the subject: am with I, is with he/she/it and are with you/we/they.</p>

<h3>Actions happening around now</h3>
<p><em>The learners are writing their answers.</em></p>
<p>The action is in progress at the time being discussed.</p>
<p>The action does not have to be happening at the exact second of speaking:</p>
<p><em>I am reading a novel this week.</em></p>
<p>The reading is a temporary activity around the present period, even if the learner is not holding the novel at that exact moment.</p>

<h3>Temporary situations and changes</h3>
<p><strong>Temporary situation:</strong> <em>She is staying with her aunt this month.</em></p>
<p><strong>Developing change:</strong> <em>The town is growing rapidly.</em></p>
<p><strong>Future arrangement:</strong> <em>We are meeting the principal on Friday.</em></p>
<p>For a future arrangement, the context normally indicates that plans have already been made.</p>

<h3>Spelling the -ing form</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Most verbs add -ing: <em>read → reading</em>.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in silent -e usually drop the e: <em>write → writing</em>.</li>
  <li>Some short verbs double the final consonant: <em>run → running</em>.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in -ie change ie to y: <em>lie → lying</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Consonant doubling depends on the spelling and stress pattern; it is not required for every verb.</p>

<h3>Negative sentences and questions</h3>
<p>Negative: <em>She is not listening.</em></p>
<p>Question: <em>Are they listening?</em></p>
<p>Use the appropriate form of be. Do not add do or does to form present-continuous questions or negatives.</p>

<h3>Stative verbs</h3>
<p>Many verbs describing states, knowledge, possession or feelings are not normally used in the continuous form when they keep their usual stative meaning.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>I understand the explanation.</em> Not normally: <em>I am understanding the explanation.</em></li>
  <li><em>She owns a bicycle.</em> Not normally: <em>She is owning a bicycle.</em></li>
  <li><em>They believe the story.</em> Not normally: <em>They are believing the story.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Some verbs can be used in either form when their meaning changes: <em>I think you are right</em> expresses an opinion, while <em>I am thinking about the problem</em> describes a process of thought.</p>

<h3>Present simple versus present continuous</h3>
<p><em>She works at a clinic</em> presents her work as a general or stable situation.</p>
<p><em>She is working at a clinic this month</em> presents the situation as temporary.</p>
<p><em>They play football every Saturday</em> describes a habit.</p>
<p><em>They are playing football now</em> describes an action in progress.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>They is waiting.</em> Write <em>They are waiting.</em></li>
  <li><em>She is write a letter.</em> Write <em>She is writing a letter.</em></li>
  <li><em>Do you studying?</em> Write <em>Are you studying?</em></li>
  <li>Using the continuous form automatically for every verb, including stative uses that normally take the simple form.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Present Continuous', 15),
  (1426, 1426, '<h2>Past Simple</h2>
<p>The <strong>past simple</strong> describes events, actions or situations viewed as completed in the past. It is commonly used when the time is stated or understood from context.</p>

<h3>Regular past forms</h3>
<p>Most regular verbs form the past simple with -ed or -d.</p>
<ul>
  <li>walk → walked</li>
  <li>clean → cleaned</li>
  <li>arrive → arrived</li>
  <li>study → studied</li>
  <li>stop → stopped</li>
</ul>
<p>Apply the spelling rules carefully: verbs ending in silent -e add -d; many consonant + y endings change y to i and add -ed; some short verbs double the final consonant.</p>

<h3>Irregular past forms</h3>
<p>Irregular verbs do not follow the regular -ed pattern.</p>
<ul>
  <li>go → went</li>
  <li>see → saw</li>
  <li>take → took</li>
  <li>write → wrote</li>
  <li>buy → bought</li>
  <li>come → came</li>
</ul>
<p>Learn the past simple form separately from the past participle. For example, the past simple of write is wrote, while the past participle is written.</p>

<h3>When to use the past simple</h3>
<p><strong>Completed event:</strong> <em>The learners submitted their assignments yesterday.</em></p>
<p><strong>Past state:</strong> <em>My grandfather lived in Nyeri when he was young.</em></p>
<p><strong>Sequence of events:</strong> <em>She entered the room, greeted the teacher and sat down.</em></p>
<p><strong>Past habit or repeated event:</strong> <em>We visited our grandparents every December.</em></p>

<h3>Negative sentences</h3>
<p>Use did not or didn''t + the base form of the main verb.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>She did not attend the meeting.</em></li>
  <li><em>They didn''t finish the exercise.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Did carries the past-tense marking, so the main verb uses its base form. Write <em>did not go</em>, not <em>did not went</em>.</p>

<h3>Questions</h3>
<p>Use did + subject + base form:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Did you complete the assignment?</em></li>
  <li><em>Did the visitors arrive early?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Again, use the base form after did. With be, form questions directly: <em>Was she ready?</em> <em>Were they late?</em></p>

<h3>Past simple versus present perfect</h3>
<p>The past simple often places an event in a finished past period: <em>I lost my keys yesterday.</em></p>
<p>The present perfect connects a past event to the present or to an experience without specifying a finished past time: <em>I have lost my keys</em> can imply that I still cannot find them.</p>
<p>Do not normally combine the present perfect with a finished past-time expression such as <em>yesterday</em>. Write <em>I saw him yesterday</em>, not <em>I have seen him yesterday</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She goed home.</em> Write <em>She went home.</em></li>
  <li><em>Did he wrote the letter?</em> Write <em>Did he write the letter?</em></li>
  <li><em>They didn''t arrived.</em> Write <em>They didn''t arrive.</em></li>
  <li>Using the past simple without checking whether the sentence requires a different time relationship.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Past Simple', 15),
  (1427, 1427, '<h2>Past Continuous</h2>
<p>The <strong>past continuous</strong>, also called the past progressive, describes an action or situation that was in progress at a particular time in the past.</p>

<h3>Form</h3>
<p>Use <strong>was/were + the -ing form</strong> of the main verb.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I was reading.</li>
  <li>She was preparing supper.</li>
  <li>They were playing football.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use was with I, he, she and it. Use were with you, we and they.</p>

<h3>An action in progress at a past time</h3>
<p><em>At eight o''clock last night, I was revising English.</em></p>
<p>The sentence presents the revision as an activity in progress at eight o''clock. It does not focus on when the activity began or ended.</p>
<p><em>When the teacher entered, the learners were writing.</em></p>
<p>The writing was already in progress when the teacher entered.</p>

<h3>An action interrupted by another action</h3>
<p>The past continuous often describes the background activity, while the past simple describes an event that occurred during it.</p>
<p><em>While I was walking home, it started to rain.</em></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Was walking:</strong> the activity in progress.</li>
  <li><strong>Started:</strong> the event that occurred during that activity.</li>
</ul>
<p>The continuous action does not have to stop permanently. The sentence simply places the second event within its time frame.</p>

<h3>Two actions happening at the same time</h3>
<p><em>While Amina was cooking, her brother was setting the table.</em></p>
<p>Both activities were in progress during the same period. The past continuous helps show that overlap.</p>

<h3>Background description</h3>
<p>The past continuous can establish the situation in a narrative:</p>
<p><em>The wind was blowing, people were hurrying home, and dark clouds were gathering.</em></p>
<p>These ongoing details establish the scene before a new event is introduced.</p>

<h3>Negative sentences and questions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Negative: <em>He was not sleeping.</em></li>
  <li>Question: <em>Were they listening?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Use was or were to form negatives and questions. Do not add did: <em>Was he sleeping?</em> is correct, not <em>Did he was sleeping?</em></p>

<h3>Past simple versus past continuous</h3>
<p><em>She cooked supper at six.</em> This presents the cooking as a past event.</p>
<p><em>She was cooking supper at six.</em> This presents the cooking as in progress at that time.</p>
<p>The difference is not simply that one action is long and the other short. The key distinction is whether the sentence views the event as a whole or as an activity in progress at a past reference time.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>They was playing.</em> Write <em>They were playing.</em></li>
  <li><em>She was cook supper.</em> Write <em>She was cooking supper.</em></li>
  <li><em>Did you were reading?</em> Write <em>Were you reading?</em></li>
  <li>Using the continuous form automatically with stative verbs such as know when the intended meaning is a state rather than an ongoing activity.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Past Continuous', 15),
  (1428, 1428, '<h2>Present Perfect</h2>
<p>The <strong>present perfect</strong> connects a past event or situation with the present. It is formed using <strong>has/have + the past participle</strong>.</p>

<h3>Form</h3>
<ul>
  <li>I have finished.</li>
  <li>She has arrived.</li>
  <li>They have written the report.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use has with he, she and it. Use have with I, you, we and they.</p>
<p>Remember to use the past participle, not automatically the past-simple form: <em>has gone</em>, not <em>has went</em>.</p>

<h3>Past actions with a present result</h3>
<p><em>I have lost my key.</em></p>
<p>The loss happened in the past, but it matters now because the key may still be missing.</p>
<p><em>The rain has damaged the road.</em></p>
<p>The damage occurred before now and has a present consequence.</p>

<h3>Life experiences</h3>
<p>The present perfect can describe whether someone has experienced something at any time up to now, without specifying a finished past time.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>I have visited Mombasa.</em></li>
  <li><em>She has never ridden a horse.</em></li>
  <li><em>Have you ever seen a solar eclipse?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Words such as <em>ever</em>, <em>never</em> and <em>before</em> commonly appear in experience statements.</p>

<h3>An unfinished period or situation</h3>
<p><em>We have had three tests this term.</em></p>
<p>If the term is still in progress, the statement counts events within a period that remains open.</p>
<p><em>She has lived here for five years.</em></p>
<p>This normally means she began living here five years ago and still lives here.</p>
<p><em>They have known each other since childhood.</em></p>
<p>Since identifies the starting point; for expresses a duration.</p>

<h3>Already, just and yet</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Already:</strong> <em>She has already submitted her work.</em> The action happened earlier than expected or by now.</li>
  <li><strong>Just:</strong> <em>They have just arrived.</em> The event happened very recently.</li>
  <li><strong>Yet:</strong> <em>Have you finished yet?</em> or <em>I have not finished yet.</em> It is common in questions and negatives about an expected event.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Negative sentences and questions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Negative: <em>He has not completed the task.</em></li>
  <li>Question: <em>Have they arrived?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Use has or have directly. Do not use did to form present-perfect questions.</p>

<h3>Present perfect versus past simple</h3>
<p><em>I saw the film yesterday.</em> Yesterday is a finished past-time expression, so the past simple is appropriate.</p>
<p><em>I have seen the film.</em> This describes experience without stating when the viewing happened.</p>
<p>In standard usage, avoid combining the present perfect with a finished past-time expression such as <em>last week</em> or <em>in 2022</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She have finished.</em> Write <em>She has finished.</em></li>
  <li><em>He has wrote.</em> Write <em>He has written.</em></li>
  <li><em>I have seen her yesterday.</em> Write <em>I saw her yesterday.</em></li>
  <li>Using the present perfect when the context requires a clearly finished past time.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Present Perfect', 15),
  (1429, 1429, '<h2>Present Perfect Continuous</h2>
<p>The <strong>present perfect continuous</strong> connects an activity that began in the past with the present. It often emphasises duration, repeated activity or the ongoing process rather than simply the completed result.</p>

<h3>Form</h3>
<p>Use <strong>has/have + been + the -ing form</strong>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I have been studying.</li>
  <li>She has been working.</li>
  <li>They have been waiting.</li>
</ul>
<p>Has or have agrees with the subject. Been is the past participle of be, and the final verb takes the -ing form.</p>

<h3>An activity continuing until now</h3>
<p><em>I have been reading for two hours.</em></p>
<p>The sentence emphasises the activity and its duration. It commonly suggests that the reading is still in progress or has only recently stopped.</p>
<p><em>It has been raining since dawn.</em></p>
<p>Since dawn identifies when the activity began; the present perfect continuous connects that activity to the present.</p>

<h3>Recent activity with present evidence</h3>
<p><em>Her hands are dirty because she has been gardening.</em></p>
<p>The gardening may have stopped, but its effects are visible now.</p>
<p><em>You look tired. Have you been working all night?</em></p>
<p>The question connects recent activity to the person''s present condition.</p>

<h3>For and since</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>For</strong> introduces a duration: <em>for twenty minutes</em>, <em>for three weeks</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Since</strong> introduces a starting point: <em>since Monday</em>, <em>since 2024</em>, <em>since noon</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples: <em>We have been waiting for an hour.</em> <em>We have been waiting since nine o''clock.</em></p>

<h3>Present perfect continuous versus present perfect simple</h3>
<p><em>She has written three pages.</em> The present perfect simple emphasises the completed result or quantity.</p>
<p><em>She has been writing for an hour.</em> The present perfect continuous emphasises the activity and duration; it does not state how many pages she completed.</p>
<p>Both forms may be possible in some contexts, but the emphasis differs.</p>

<h3>Stative verbs</h3>
<p>Stative verbs such as <em>know, believe, own</em> and <em>belong</em> are not normally used in the continuous form when they express their usual states.</p>
<p>Write <em>I have known her for years</em>, rather than normally writing <em>I have been knowing her for years</em>.</p>
<p>Some verbs can be continuous when they describe an activity rather than a state. For example, <em>think</em> can express an opinion in <em>I think it is correct</em> or an ongoing process in <em>I have been thinking about the problem</em>.</p>

<h3>Negative sentences and questions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Negative: <em>He has not been sleeping well.</em></li>
  <li>Question: <em>Have they been practising?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Put not after has or have. In questions, move has or have before the subject.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She has been study for hours.</em> Write <em>She has been studying for hours.</em></li>
  <li><em>They has been waiting.</em> Write <em>They have been waiting.</em></li>
  <li><em>I have been knowing him since 2020.</em> Normally write <em>I have known him since 2020.</em></li>
  <li>Confusing an activity''s duration with a completed result or quantity.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Present Perfect Continuous', 15),
  (1430, 1430, '<h2>Past Perfect</h2>
<p>The <strong>past perfect</strong> describes an event or situation that occurred before a particular point or event in the past. It helps establish the order of past events when the sequence matters.</p>

<h3>Form</h3>
<p>Use <strong>had + the past participle</strong> for all subjects.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I had finished.</li>
  <li>She had left.</li>
  <li>They had written the report.</li>
</ul>
<p>Had does not change according to the subject. Use the past participle, such as finished, gone, written or eaten.</p>

<h3>An earlier event before a later past event</h3>
<p><em>When we reached the station, the train had left.</em></p>
<p>There are two past events:</p>
<ol>
  <li>The train left first.</li>
  <li>We reached the station later.</li>
</ol>
<p>The past perfect marks the earlier event. The past simple marks the later event.</p>

<h3>Explaining a past condition</h3>
<p><em>She was tired because she had slept badly.</em></p>
<p>Sleeping badly occurred before the past condition of being tired. The past perfect helps explain the cause of that condition.</p>
<p><em>He could not enter because he had forgotten his key.</em></p>
<p>Forgetting the key happened before the failed attempt to enter.</p>

<h3>Reported situations and experiences</h3>
<p>The past perfect is often used when reporting something from a past point of view.</p>
<p><em>By the time the meeting began, the secretary had prepared the documents.</em></p>
<p>The preparation was complete before the meeting began.</p>

<h3>Past perfect versus past simple</h3>
<p>Use the past simple when the sequence is clear or when the sentence simply reports past events:</p>
<p><em>She opened the door and entered the room.</em></p>
<p>The order is clear from the sequence of verbs, so the past perfect is not necessary.</p>
<p>Use the past perfect when you need to show that an event was already complete before another past reference point:</p>
<p><em>She had opened the door before the visitors arrived.</em></p>
<p>The past perfect is not simply a way to make every earlier action sound more formal. Use it when the past-before-past relationship is meaningful.</p>

<h3>Negative sentences and questions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Negative: <em>They had not eaten before the journey.</em></li>
  <li>Question: <em>Had she completed the task before noon?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Use had directly. The past participle remains unchanged.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She had went home.</em> Write <em>She had gone home.</em></li>
  <li>Using the past perfect for every action in a story even when no earlier-than-past relationship needs to be shown.</li>
  <li>Failing to distinguish the earlier event from the later past reference point.</li>
  <li>Assuming that the word before always requires the past perfect. The tense depends on meaning and context.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Past Perfect', 15),
  (1431, 1431, '<h2>Past Perfect Continuous</h2>
<p>The <strong>past perfect continuous</strong> describes an activity that continued over a period before a particular point or event in the past. It emphasises duration, ongoing process or repeated activity leading up to that past reference point.</p>

<h3>Form</h3>
<p>Use <strong>had + been + the -ing form</strong>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I had been reading.</li>
  <li>She had been working.</li>
  <li>They had been practising.</li>
</ul>
<p>Had remains the same for all subjects. Been is followed by the -ing form of the main verb.</p>

<h3>Duration before a past event</h3>
<p><em>They had been waiting for two hours when the bus arrived.</em></p>
<p>The waiting began before the bus arrived and continued up to that point. The phrase for two hours emphasises the duration.</p>
<p><em>She had been studying since dawn when the electricity went off.</em></p>
<p>The studying was already in progress before the power failure.</p>

<h3>Explaining a past result or condition</h3>
<p><em>The ground was wet because it had been raining.</em></p>
<p>The rain occurred before the ground was observed to be wet. The past perfect continuous emphasises the activity that caused the condition.</p>
<p><em>He was exhausted because he had been carrying heavy boxes.</em></p>
<p>The earlier activity explains the later state of exhaustion.</p>

<h3>Past perfect continuous versus past perfect simple</h3>
<p><em>She had written five pages before lunch.</em></p>
<p>The past perfect simple emphasises the completed result or quantity: five pages.</p>
<p><em>She had been writing for two hours before lunch.</em></p>
<p>The past perfect continuous emphasises the duration of the activity, not the amount completed.</p>
<p>Both can describe earlier events, but they focus on different aspects of those events.</p>

<h3>For and since</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>For:</strong> duration, such as <em>for an hour</em> or <em>for several months</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Since:</strong> starting point, such as <em>since morning</em> or <em>since January</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>By noon, the learners had been revising for three hours.</em></p>
<p><em>By noon, the learners had been revising since nine o''clock.</em></p>
<p>Both sentences connect the revision to a past reference point.</p>

<h3>When not to use it</h3>
<p>Stative verbs such as <em>know, own, believe</em> and <em>belong</em> are not normally used in the continuous form when they express their usual state.</p>
<p>Write <em>She had known him for years before they worked together</em>, rather than normally writing <em>She had been knowing him for years</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>They had been wait for hours.</em> Write <em>They had been waiting for hours.</em></li>
  <li><em>She has been working before the meeting began.</em> If the activity is viewed from a past reference point, write <em>She had been working before the meeting began.</em></li>
  <li>Using the continuous form when the sentence''s main focus is a completed quantity or result.</li>
  <li>Forgetting that the activity is related to a point in the past, not necessarily to the present.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Past Perfect Continuous', 15),
  (1432, 1432, '<h2>Future Forms</h2>
<p>English does not use one single form for every future event. The form you choose tells the reader whether something is a prediction, a plan, an arrangement, or an action that will be completed before a future time.</p>

<h3>1. Will + base verb</h3>
<p>Use <strong>will + base verb</strong> for predictions, decisions made at the moment of speaking, promises, and offers.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Prediction:</strong> The rain will stop soon.</li>
  <li><strong>Immediate decision:</strong> The phone is ringing. I will answer it.</li>
  <li><strong>Promise:</strong> I will return your book tomorrow.</li>
  <li><strong>Offer:</strong> I will help you carry the box.</li>
</ul>
<p>After <em>will</em>, use the base form of the verb. Say <em>will go</em>, not <em>will goes</em> or <em>will went</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Be going to + base verb</h3>
<p>Use <strong>am/is/are going to + base verb</strong> for intentions already formed and predictions based on present evidence.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Intention:</strong> She is going to study Chemistry tonight.</li>
  <li><strong>Present evidence:</strong> Those dark clouds are going to bring rain.</li>
</ul>
<p>The verb <em>be</em> must agree with the subject: <em>I am going to</em>, <em>he is going to</em>, and <em>they are going to</em>.</p>

<h3>3. Present continuous for arrangements</h3>
<p>Use the present continuous for a future arrangement that has already been organised, often with a stated time or place.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> We are meeting the head teacher at 10 a.m. tomorrow.</p>
<p>This suggests that the meeting has been arranged, not merely imagined.</p>

<h3>4. Present simple for timetables</h3>
<p>Use the present simple for fixed schedules, programmes, and timetables.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The bus leaves at 6:30 a.m. tomorrow.</p>
<p>The bus is following a schedule. The sentence is not describing a personal intention.</p>

<h3>5. Future perfect</h3>
<p>Use <strong>will have + past participle</strong> for an action that will be completed before a particular future time.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> By Friday, Amina will have completed the assignment.</p>
<p>The reference point is Friday. The assignment will be complete by then.</p>

<h3>Compare the meanings</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>I will visit my aunt.</em> — A prediction, promise, or decision, depending on context.</li>
  <li><em>I am going to visit my aunt.</em> — An intention or evidence-based prediction.</li>
  <li><em>I am visiting my aunt on Saturday.</em> — An arranged visit.</li>
  <li><em>The train arrives at noon.</em> — A scheduled event.</li>
  <li><em>By noon, I will have arrived.</em> — Arrival completed before noon.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: She will goes home. Correct: She will go home.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: They is going to travel. Correct: They are going to travel.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: By tomorrow, I will have finish. Correct: By tomorrow, I will have finished.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Choose a future form according to the meaning and context, not simply because the event has not happened yet.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Future Forms', 15),
  (1433, 1433, '<h2>Choosing Tense</h2>
<p>Choosing a tense means selecting a verb form that accurately expresses when an event happens and how it relates to another time or event. The correct tense depends on the meaning of the whole sentence, not on a time word alone.</p>

<h3>1. Start with the time of the event</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Present:</strong> Facts, habits, or actions happening now.</li>
  <li><strong>Past:</strong> Events that happened before the present.</li>
  <li><strong>Future:</strong> Events expected or planned to happen later.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Water boils at normal atmospheric pressure. — A general fact.</li>
  <li>We visited the museum yesterday. — A completed past event.</li>
  <li>We will visit the museum tomorrow. — A future event.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Ask whether the action is complete or in progress</h3>
<p>Compare these sentences:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>When I arrived, they cooked.</em> — This can describe a completed past action, depending on context.</li>
  <li><em>When I arrived, they were cooking.</em> — Their cooking was already in progress at the time I arrived.</li>
</ul>
<p>The past continuous often provides background for a shorter event expressed in the past simple.</p>

<h3>3. Ask whether the action connects to another time</h3>
<p>The present perfect connects a past event to the present. It can describe an experience, a recent event, or a past action with a present result.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>I have lost my key.</em> — The loss matters now because I do not have the key.</li>
  <li><em>I lost my key yesterday.</em> — The sentence places the event at a specific, finished past time.</li>
</ul>
<p>In standard usage, avoid the present perfect with a finished-time expression such as <em>yesterday</em> when the sentence locates the event entirely in that past period.</p>

<h3>4. Recognise actions completed before other past events</h3>
<p>Use the past perfect when you need to show that one past action happened before another past event.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The lesson had started before we entered the classroom.</p>
<p>The lesson started first; our entry happened later. The past perfect makes that order clear.</p>

<h3>5. Use time expressions as clues, not automatic answers</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>Every day</em> often accompanies the present simple for habits.</li>
  <li><em>At the moment</em> often accompanies the present continuous.</li>
  <li><em>Last week</em> often accompanies the past simple.</li>
  <li><em>Since Monday</em> may accompany a perfect form, depending on meaning.</li>
  <li><em>By next month</em> may signal the future perfect when completion is emphasised.</li>
</ul>
<p>These expressions are clues. Always check what the speaker intends to communicate.</p>

<h3>6. A reliable decision process</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Locate the event in present, past, or future time.</li>
  <li>Decide whether the action is habitual, in progress, completed, or connected to another time.</li>
  <li>Check whether another event establishes a reference point.</li>
  <li>Choose the tense that expresses that relationship.</li>
  <li>Read the complete sentence to check that the meaning is logical.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: I have seen him yesterday. Correct: I saw him yesterday.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: She is knowing the answer. Correct: She knows the answer.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: When the bell rang, we eat. Correct: When the bell rang, we were eating, if the eating was already in progress.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Tense communicates both time and the way an action relates to that time.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Choosing the Correct Tense', 15),
  (1434, 1434, '<h2>Tense Consistency</h2>
<p>Tense consistency means choosing verb tenses that make the time relationships in a sentence or passage clear. It does not mean that every verb must have the same tense. Tenses can change when the time, meaning, or relationship between events changes.</p>

<h3>1. Keep the same time frame when it has not changed</h3>
<p>When describing a sequence of events that all happened in the past, keep the verbs in an appropriate past-time frame.</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Yesterday, Njeri walked to school and meets her friend.</p>
<p><strong>Correct:</strong> Yesterday, Njeri walked to school and met her friend.</p>
<p>Both actions occurred yesterday, so the past simple is appropriate for both.</p>

<h3>2. Change tense when the meaning requires it</h3>
<p>A tense change is correct when it reflects a real difference in time.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Kamau missed the bus yesterday, so he is walking to school today.</p>
<p><em>Missed</em> refers to yesterday; <em>is walking</em> refers to what is happening today. The change is meaningful, not an error.</p>

<h3>3. Keep past-tense narration coherent</h3>
<p>When telling a story in the past, use the past simple for completed events and the past continuous for actions in progress when relevant.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners were writing when the bell rang. They packed their books and left the room.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Were writing</em> describes an action already in progress.</li>
  <li><em>Rang</em> marks the event that occurred during it.</li>
  <li><em>Packed</em> and <em>left</em> describe later completed events.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Distinguish general truths from past events</h3>
<p>A general truth may remain in the present tense even when the surrounding passage uses the past tense.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher explained that the Earth revolves around the Sun.</p>
<p><em>Explained</em> refers to a past event. <em>Revolves</em> expresses a general scientific fact, so the present tense is suitable.</p>

<h3>5. Maintain the correct sequence of events</h3>
<p>Use perfect tenses when they help make the order of events clear.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> By the time the match began, the spectators had taken their seats.</p>
<p>The spectators sat down first; the match began afterwards. The past perfect marks the earlier action.</p>

<h3>6. How to correct tense shifts</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Identify the time frame of the passage.</li>
  <li>Underline the main verbs.</li>
  <li>Determine when each action occurs.</li>
  <li>Correct any tense that conflicts with the intended time.</li>
  <li>Keep deliberate changes that express a different time or a general truth.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: She opened the window and looks outside. Correct: She opened the window and looked outside.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: I was walking home when I see the accident. Correct: I was walking home when I saw the accident.</li>
  <li>Not necessarily incorrect: He said that water boils at 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure. The reporting verb is past, but the general truth remains in the present.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Keep tenses consistent with the intended timeline, not mechanically identical.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tense Consistency', 15),
  (1435, 1435, '<h2>Identifying the Subject</h2>
<p>The subject is the word or group of words that tells us who or what a clause is about. In many sentences, it identifies the person or thing performing an action, experiencing a state, or being described.</p>

<h3>1. Find the verb first</h3>
<p>A reliable way to find the subject is to identify the main verb, then ask who or what performs the action or is described.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The young learner answered the question.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Verb: <em>answered</em>.</li>
  <li>Ask: Who answered?</li>
  <li>Subject: <em>The young learner</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The complete subject includes the main noun and the words that describe or determine it. The head noun is <em>learner</em>, but the complete subject is <em>The young learner</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Do not confuse the subject with the object</h3>
<p>The subject and object have different roles.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The librarian helped the new student.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Subject: <em>The librarian</em> — the person who performed the action.</li>
  <li>Verb: <em>helped</em>.</li>
  <li>Object: <em>the new student</em> — the person who received the help.</li>
</ul>
<p>To identify the subject, do not simply choose the noun nearest the verb.</p>

<h3>3. Watch for extra information between subject and verb</h3>
<p>A phrase between the subject and the verb may distract you, but it does not automatically become part of the subject.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The basket of mangoes is on the table.</p>
<p>The subject is <em>The basket of mangoes</em>, whose head noun is <em>basket</em>. The phrase <em>of mangoes</em> describes the basket. It does not make the subject plural.</p>

<h3>4. Find the subject in questions</h3>
<p>Questions often place an auxiliary verb before the subject.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Have the players arrived?</p>
<ul>
  <li>Auxiliary verb: <em>Have</em>.</li>
  <li>Subject: <em>the players</em>.</li>
  <li>Main verb: <em>arrived</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not mistake the first word in a question for the subject.</p>

<h3>5. Recognise subjects in commands</h3>
<p>In a command, the subject is usually understood to be <em>you</em>, even when it is not written.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Close the door.</p>
<p>The understood subject is <em>you</em>: (You) close the door.</p>

<h3>6. Recognise subjects in sentences beginning with ''there''</h3>
<p>In sentences such as <em>There are three books on the desk</em>, the word <em>there</em> introduces the statement; it is not the subject in the usual grammatical analysis.</p>
<p>The grammatical subject is <em>three books</em>. The verb agrees with that subject.</p>

<h3>7. A practical method</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Locate the verb or verb phrase.</li>
  <li>Ask who or what performs the action or is described.</li>
  <li>Check whether the sentence is a question or command.</li>
  <li>Distinguish the subject from objects and descriptive phrases.</li>
  <li>Identify the complete subject and, when needed, its head noun.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>In ''The box of pencils is missing'', the subject is ''The box of pencils'', not just ''pencils''.</li>
  <li>In ''Did the child finish?'', the subject is ''the child'', not ''did''.</li>
  <li>In ''Open your books'', the understood subject is ''you''.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Find the subject by its grammatical role, not merely by its position in the sentence.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying the Subject', 15),
  (1436, 1436, '<h2>Singular Subject–Verb Agreement</h2>
<p>Subject–verb agreement means matching a verb to its subject in number and person. In the present simple, a third-person singular subject usually takes a verb ending in <em>-s</em> or <em>-es</em>. This is one of the most common places where English learners make errors.</p>

<h3>1. The basic present-simple rule</h3>
<p>With <em>he</em>, <em>she</em>, <em>it</em>, or a singular noun, the present-simple verb usually ends in <em>-s</em> or <em>-es</em>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The learner <strong>reads</strong> every evening.</li>
  <li>Mary <strong>watches</strong> the news.</li>
  <li>The machine <strong>works</strong> well.</li>
</ul>
<p>With <em>I</em>, <em>you</em>, <em>we</em>, and <em>they</em>, use the base form for most present-simple verbs: <em>I read</em>, <em>you read</em>, <em>we read</em>, <em>they read</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Forming the singular verb</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Most verbs add <em>-s</em>: work → works; read → reads.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in many sibilant sounds add <em>-es</em>: watch → watches; pass → passes; wash → washes.</li>
  <li>For a consonant followed by <em>y</em>, change <em>y</em> to <em>ies</em>: study → studies; carry → carries.</li>
  <li>For a vowel followed by <em>y</em>, add <em>-s</em>: play → plays; enjoy → enjoys.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. The special verb ''be''</h3>
<p>In the present tense, <em>be</em> changes form according to the subject:</p>
<ul>
  <li>I am ready.</li>
  <li>You are ready.</li>
  <li>He, she, or it is ready.</li>
  <li>We are ready.</li>
  <li>They are ready.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not use <em>is</em> with every subject or replace <em>are</em> with <em>is</em> just because a noun appears near the verb.</p>

<h3>4. The special verbs ''have'' and ''do''</h3>
<p>With a third-person singular subject:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>have</em> becomes <em>has</em>: She has a notebook.</li>
  <li><em>do</em> becomes <em>does</em>: He does his homework.</li>
</ul>
<p>When <em>does</em> is the auxiliary in a question or negative sentence, the main verb returns to its base form:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Correct: Does she <strong>play</strong> tennis?</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Does she <strong>plays</strong> tennis?</li>
  <li>Correct: He does not <strong>like</strong> noise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Do not let nearby nouns mislead you</h3>
<p>The verb agrees with the subject, not with a noun inside a phrase that describes the subject.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The list of names is on the desk.</p>
<p>The head noun is <em>list</em>, which is singular. The noun <em>names</em> is part of the phrase <em>of names</em> and does not control the verb.</p>

<h3>6. Indefinite pronouns that are singular</h3>
<p>In standard English, pronouns such as <em>everyone</em>, <em>everybody</em>, <em>someone</em>, <em>anyone</em>, <em>nobody</em>, and <em>each</em> generally take singular verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Everyone <strong>is</strong> present.</li>
  <li>Each of the learners <strong>has</strong> a book.</li>
  <li>Nobody <strong>knows</strong> the answer.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: The girl walk to school. Correct: The girl walks to school.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Each of the players have a uniform. Correct: Each of the players has a uniform.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Does he writes neatly? Correct: Does he write neatly.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Find the true subject first. Then choose the verb form that agrees with it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Agreement with Singular Subjects', 15),
  (1437, 1437, '<h2>Plural Subject–Verb Agreement</h2>
<p>A plural subject refers to more than one person, animal, place, or thing. In the present simple, plural subjects generally use the base form of the verb, without the <em>-s</em> ending used with third-person singular subjects.</p>

<h3>1. The basic rule</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Singular: The learner <strong>writes</strong> neatly.</li>
  <li>Plural: The learners <strong>write</strong> neatly.</li>
  <li>Singular: The dog <strong>barks</strong> loudly.</li>
  <li>Plural: The dogs <strong>bark</strong> loudly.</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice the difference: in the present simple, adding more subjects usually removes the <em>-s</em> from the main verb.</p>

<h3>2. Plural nouns ending in -s</h3>
<p>Many plural nouns end in <em>-s</em>, but the verb does not automatically take <em>-s</em> because the subject is plural.</p>
<p><strong>Correct:</strong> The teachers prepare the examinations.</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The teachers prepares the examinations.</p>
<p>The noun ending and verb ending follow different rules. Do not match them by spelling; match the verb to the subject''s grammatical number.</p>

<h3>3. The verb ''be''</h3>
<p>The present-tense forms of <em>be</em> are different from most other verbs:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Singular: The learner <strong>is</strong> ready.</li>
  <li>Plural: The learners <strong>are</strong> ready.</li>
  <li>Singular past: The learner <strong>was</strong> ready.</li>
  <li>Plural past: The learners <strong>were</strong> ready.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. The verbs ''have'' and ''do''</h3>
<p>These verbs also change form:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Singular: She <strong>has</strong> a dictionary. Plural: They <strong>have</strong> dictionaries.</li>
  <li>Singular: He <strong>does</strong> the work. Plural: They <strong>do</strong> the work.</li>
</ul>
<p>In questions and negatives, use <em>do</em> with plural subjects:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Do the players understand the rules?</li>
  <li>The players do not understand the rules.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Plural subjects separated from verbs</h3>
<p>Extra information may appear between the subject and the verb. The verb must still agree with the subject.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The books on the top shelf belong to the librarian.</p>
<p>The subject is <em>The books</em>, not <em>the top shelf</em>. Therefore, use <em>belong</em>, not <em>belongs</em>.</p>

<h3>6. Irregular plural nouns</h3>
<p>Some plural nouns do not end in <em>-s</em>. They still take plural verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The children <strong>play</strong> outside.</li>
  <li>The men <strong>are</strong> working.</li>
  <li>The people <strong>have</strong> arrived.</li>
  <li>The mice <strong>run</strong> away.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: The girls sings well. Correct: The girls sing well.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: The children is tired. Correct: The children are tired.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: The files in the folder belongs to me. Correct: The files in the folder belong to me.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Identify the plural subject first, then choose the matching verb form.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Agreement with Plural Subjects', 15),
  (1438, 1438, '<h2>Compound Subjects</h2>
<p>A compound subject consists of two or more subjects joined together. The conjunction connecting them helps determine whether the verb should be singular or plural.</p>

<h3>1. Subjects joined by ''and''</h3>
<p>Two distinct subjects joined by <em>and</em> usually form a plural subject.</p>
<ul>
  <li>John and Peter <strong>are</strong> classmates.</li>
  <li>The teacher and the librarian <strong>organise</strong> the event.</li>
  <li>Reading and writing <strong>require</strong> practice.</li>
</ul>
<p>Think of the subjects together: John plus Peter means two people, so the plural verb is appropriate.</p>

<h3>2. When ''and'' refers to one combined thing</h3>
<p>Sometimes two nouns joined by <em>and</em> refer to one unit, one dish, or one idea. In that case, a singular verb may be appropriate.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Bread and butter is my usual breakfast.</p>
<p>Here, the phrase is treated as one combined breakfast. Meaning determines the agreement.</p>

<h3>3. Subjects joined by ''or'' or ''nor''</h3>
<p>When subjects are joined by <em>or</em> or <em>nor</em>, the verb commonly agrees with the subject nearest to it. This is often called proximity agreement.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Either the teacher or the learners <strong>are</strong> presenting first.</li>
  <li>Either the learners or the teacher <strong>is</strong> presenting first.</li>
  <li>Neither the head teacher nor the deputies <strong>are</strong> attending.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the subjects differ in number, the sentence can sound awkward. Rewriting it can improve clarity: <em>The teacher will present first, or the learners will.</em></p>

<h3>4. ''As well as'', ''along with'', and ''together with''</h3>
<p>These expressions add information about the subject, but they do not normally create a compound subject in the same way as <em>and</em>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The head teacher, together with the deputies, <strong>is</strong> attending.</li>
  <li>The captain, as well as the players, <strong>was</strong> interviewed.</li>
</ul>
<p>The main subject remains <em>head teacher</em> or <em>captain</em>. The additional phrase does not determine the verb.</p>

<h3>5. Indefinite expressions such as ''each'' and ''every''</h3>
<p>When <em>each</em> or <em>every</em> comes before a series of singular nouns joined by <em>and</em>, the subject is often treated as singular because the individuals are considered separately.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Every desk and every chair is numbered.</p>
<p>The emphasis is on each item individually.</p>

<h3>6. How to analyse a compound subject</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Identify all the nouns or pronouns that form the subject.</li>
  <li>Identify the conjunction joining them.</li>
  <li>Ask whether the subject represents separate people or things, one combined unit, or alternatives.</li>
  <li>Apply the relevant agreement rule.</li>
  <li>Rewrite the sentence if the result sounds confusing.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: Amina and Wanjiku is present. Correct: Amina and Wanjiku are present.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: The principal, together with the teachers, are attending. Correct: The principal, together with the teachers, is attending.</li>
  <li>With ''either...or'', check the subject nearest the verb when the subjects differ in number.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> The conjunction and the intended meaning determine how a compound subject agrees with its verb.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Agreement with Compound Subjects', 15),
  (1439, 1439, '<h2>Indefinite Pronouns and Agreement</h2>
<p>An indefinite pronoun refers to a person or thing without identifying exactly who or what it is. Examples include <em>everyone</em>, <em>somebody</em>, <em>anything</em>, and <em>several</em>. These pronouns do not all take the same verb form.</p>

<h3>1. Indefinite pronouns that are singular</h3>
<p>In standard English, these commonly take singular verbs:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Everyone, everybody, someone, somebody, anyone, anybody, no one, nobody</em>.</li>
  <li><em>Everything, something, anything, nothing</em>.</li>
  <li><em>Each</em> and <em>either</em> when used as pronouns referring to one of two alternatives.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Everyone <strong>is</strong> ready.</li>
  <li>Someone <strong>has</strong> left a bag behind.</li>
  <li>Nothing <strong>seems</strong> unusual.</li>
  <li>Each <strong>has</strong> a turn.</li>
</ul>
<p>Words such as <em>everyone</em> refer to multiple people in meaning, but grammatically they are singular in standard agreement.</p>

<h3>2. Indefinite pronouns that are plural</h3>
<p><em>Both</em>, <em>few</em>, <em>many</em>, and <em>several</em> normally take plural verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Both <strong>are</strong> correct.</li>
  <li>Few <strong>understand</strong> the instructions.</li>
  <li>Several <strong>have</strong> applied.</li>
  <li>Many <strong>were</strong> absent.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Pronouns whose number depends on context</h3>
<p><em>Some</em>, <em>all</em>, <em>any</em>, <em>most</em>, and <em>none</em> can take singular or plural verbs depending on what they refer to.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Some of the <strong>water is</strong> missing. — Water is uncountable here.</li>
  <li>Some of the <strong>books are</strong> missing. — Books is plural.</li>
  <li>Most of the <strong>work is</strong> complete.</li>
  <li>Most of the <strong>learners are</strong> present.</li>
</ul>
<p>Look at the noun or quantity being referred to, rather than memorising one verb for every use of these pronouns.</p>

<h3>4. ''Each of'' and ''one of''</h3>
<p>When the subject is <em>each</em> or <em>one</em>, the verb is singular even if a plural noun follows <em>of</em>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Each of the learners <strong>has</strong> a textbook.</li>
  <li>One of the doors <strong>is</strong> open.</li>
  <li>Every one of the answers <strong>is</strong> marked.</li>
</ul>
<p>The plural nouns <em>learners</em>, <em>doors</em>, and <em>answers</em> belong to phrases introduced by <em>of</em>; they do not control the main verb.</p>

<h3>5. ''None'' and meaning</h3>
<p><em>None</em> can be used with singular or plural agreement depending on the intended meaning and the noun being referred to. Plural agreement is natural when referring to multiple countable items.</p>
<ul>
  <li>None of the water <strong>is</strong> clean.</li>
  <li>None of the students <strong>are</strong> ready. — Common and natural when referring to students individually.</li>
</ul>
<p>In formal writing, choose a form that fits the noun and the meaning, and keep the sentence clear.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: Everybody have a responsibility. Correct: Everybody has a responsibility.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Several is missing. Correct: Several are missing.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Some of the books is damaged. Correct: Some of the books are damaged.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Identify the indefinite pronoun, determine what it refers to, and then select the appropriate verb.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Agreement with Indefinite Pronouns', 15),
  (1440, 1440, '<h2>Collective Nouns and Agreement</h2>
<p>A collective noun names a group considered as a unit or as a collection of individuals. Examples include <em>team</em>, <em>family</em>, <em>committee</em>, <em>audience</em>, <em>class</em>, and <em>staff</em>.</p>

<h3>1. Treating the group as one unit</h3>
<p>When the group acts together as a single unit, a singular verb is generally appropriate in standard British English, which is commonly used in Kenyan formal writing.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The team <strong>is</strong> preparing for the final.</li>
  <li>The committee <strong>has</strong> reached a decision.</li>
  <li>The class <strong>was</strong> quiet during the examination.</li>
</ul>
<p>The focus is on the group as one organised body.</p>

<h3>2. Treating members as individuals</h3>
<p>When the sentence emphasises the separate actions or opinions of the members, plural agreement may be used in British English.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The team <strong>are</strong> arguing among themselves.</li>
  <li>The committee <strong>are</strong> divided in their opinions.</li>
  <li>The family <strong>are</strong> packing their own bags.</li>
</ul>
<p>The focus is on the members acting separately rather than on the group as one unit. In other varieties of English, especially American English, singular agreement is more common with collective nouns.</p>

<h3>3. Let the meaning guide the choice</h3>
<p>Compare:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The jury has reached its verdict.</em> — The jury is treated as one body making one decision.</li>
  <li><em>The jury are returning to their seats.</em> — The members are considered individually.</li>
</ul>
<p>In formal writing, avoid switching between singular and plural agreement without a reason. Pronouns such as <em>it</em> and <em>its</em> usually accompany singular treatment; <em>they</em> and <em>their</em> accompany plural treatment.</p>

<h3>4. A collective noun is not automatically plural</h3>
<p>The word names many people, but that does not mean the verb must always be plural.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The audience was silent.</p>
<p>The audience consists of many people, but the sentence describes the audience as one quiet group.</p>

<h3>5. Distinguish collective nouns from plural nouns</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>The team is training.</em> — <em>Team</em> is a singular collective noun.</li>
  <li><em>The players are training.</em> — <em>Players</em> is a plural noun.</li>
  <li><em>The police are investigating.</em> — <em>Police</em> normally takes plural agreement in standard usage.</li>
  <li><em>The people are waiting.</em> — <em>People</em> is plural.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. Making the sentence clear</h3>
<p>If the intended agreement feels awkward or could confuse the reader, rewrite the sentence to name the individuals.</p>
<p><strong>Instead of:</strong> The committee are divided in its opinion.</p>
<p><strong>Write:</strong> The committee members are divided in their opinions.</p>
<p>The revised version makes the plural meaning explicit and keeps the pronoun consistent.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect for a group acting as one unit: The team are winning the trophy as a single unit. More consistent: The team is winning the trophy.</li>
  <li>Inconsistent: The family are packing its bags. Better: The family is packing its bags, or The family members are packing their bags.</li>
  <li>Do not assume every word naming a group follows the same agreement rule; usage depends on the noun and meaning.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Ask whether the collective noun represents one unit or individual members, then maintain consistent verb and pronoun agreement.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Agreement with Collective Nouns', 15),
  (1441, 1441, '<h2>Common Subject–Verb Agreement Errors</h2>
<p>Subject–verb agreement errors often happen because a sentence contains extra phrases, unusual subjects, or a verb form that looks familiar but does not match the subject. The solution is to identify the true subject before choosing the verb.</p>

<h3>1. Letting the nearest noun control the verb</h3>
<p>The verb agrees with the grammatical subject, not necessarily the noun closest to it.</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The quality of the answers are impressive.</p>
<p><strong>Correct:</strong> The quality of the answers is impressive.</p>
<p>The head noun is <em>quality</em>, which is singular. <em>Of the answers</em> is a phrase describing that quality.</p>

<h3>2. Errors with ''each'', ''every'', and ''everyone''</h3>
<p>These words generally take singular verbs in standard English.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Each student <strong>has</strong> a textbook.</li>
  <li>Every chair <strong>is</strong> clean.</li>
  <li>Everyone <strong>knows</strong> the rule.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not choose a plural verb just because the sentence refers to many people or things.</p>

<h3>3. Errors with compound subjects</h3>
<p>Two distinct subjects joined by <em>and</em> generally take a plural verb.</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The farmer and the driver was late.</p>
<p><strong>Correct:</strong> The farmer and the driver were late.</p>
<p>However, phrases such as <em>together with</em> and <em>as well as</em> do not usually create a plural subject on their own.</p>

<h3>4. Errors in questions and negatives</h3>
<p>When <em>does</em> or <em>do</em> acts as an auxiliary, the main verb stays in its base form.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Correct: Does she <strong>know</strong> the answer?</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Does she <strong>knows</strong> the answer?</li>
  <li>Correct: He does not <strong>like</strong> noise.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: He does not <strong>likes</strong> noise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Errors with amounts, distances, and periods of time</h3>
<p>When an amount of time, money, distance, or measurement is treated as one total, it often takes a singular verb.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ten kilometres <strong>is</strong> a long distance to walk.</li>
  <li>Five thousand shillings <strong>is</strong> enough for the purchase.</li>
  <li>Two hours <strong>is</strong> sufficient for the examination.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the sentence emphasises separate units, plural agreement may be appropriate. Let the intended meaning guide the choice.</p>

<h3>6. Errors with ''there is'' and ''there are''</h3>
<p>In these sentences, the noun that follows the verb determines whether the verb should be singular or plural.</p>
<ul>
  <li>There <strong>is</strong> a book on the desk.</li>
  <li>There <strong>are</strong> three books on the desk.</li>
</ul>
<p>In careful formal writing, avoid allowing <em>there</em> to hide the real subject.</p>

<h3>7. Errors with titles and names</h3>
<p>A title of a book, film, or other single work generally takes a singular verb, even if the title contains a plural noun.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>Things Fall Apart</em> is a well-known novel.</p>
<p>The title refers to one work, so singular agreement is appropriate.</p>

<h3>8. A five-step correction method</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Locate the main verb.</li>
  <li>Identify the subject that controls it.</li>
  <li>Find the head noun or pronoun in that subject.</li>
  <li>Determine whether the subject is singular, plural, or a collective noun whose meaning affects agreement.</li>
  <li>Check the verb form and read the corrected sentence aloud or silently for clarity.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Final check</h3>
<p>Do not correct a sentence merely because a verb looks different from a nearby noun. First establish the grammatical relationship between the subject and verb. A sentence can contain many nouns but still have one singular subject.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Common Agreement Errors', 15),
  (1442, 1442, '<h2>Subjects and Predicates</h2>
<p>A sentence usually communicates a complete thought by identifying what the sentence is about and saying something about it. These two main parts are the <strong>subject</strong> and the <strong>predicate</strong>.</p>

<h3>1. The subject</h3>
<p>The subject identifies the person, thing, place, or idea that the sentence is about. It may be a single word or a group of words.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Birds</em> sing.</li>
  <li><em>The young boy</em> opened the gate.</li>
  <li><em>Several students from our school</em> won the competition.</li>
</ul>
<p>The complete subject includes its main word and the words that describe or determine it. In the last example, the complete subject is <em>Several students from our school</em>, while the head noun is <em>students</em>.</p>

<h3>2. The predicate</h3>
<p>The predicate tells us something about the subject. It contains a verb and may include objects, complements, and other information.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Birds <em>sing</em>.</li>
  <li>The young boy <em>opened the gate</em>.</li>
  <li>Several students from our school <em>won the competition yesterday</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The complete predicate includes the verb and everything in the predicate that belongs with it.</p>

<h3>3. Finding both parts</h3>
<p>Consider this sentence:</p>
<p><strong>The new science teacher explained the experiment carefully.</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Complete subject: <em>The new science teacher</em>.</li>
  <li>Head noun of the subject: <em>teacher</em>.</li>
  <li>Complete predicate: <em>explained the experiment carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>Main verb: <em>explained</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Do not confuse the subject with the first word</h3>
<p>Subjects can appear after verbs in questions, and introductory expressions may come before the subject.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Are the players ready?</em> — Subject: <em>the players</em>.</li>
  <li><em>After the long journey, the travellers rested.</em> — Subject: <em>the travellers</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Find the grammatical subject, not simply the first noun you see.</p>

<h3>5. Commands have an understood subject</h3>
<p>In a command such as <em>Close the window</em>, the subject is usually the understood pronoun <em>you</em>.</p>
<p>The complete meaning is: <em>(You) close the window.</em></p>

<h3>6. Sentence completeness</h3>
<p>A complete sentence generally needs an independent clause that expresses a complete thought.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Incomplete: <em>Because the road was flooded.</em></li>
  <li>Complete: <em>Because the road was flooded, we arrived late.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>The first example leaves the reader waiting for the result of the flooding. The second completes the thought.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Do not label only the head noun as the complete subject when describing the whole sentence.</li>
  <li>Do not assume everything after the subject is only a verb; the predicate can contain objects and complements.</li>
  <li>Do not treat every group of words beginning with a capital letter as a complete sentence.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> The subject tells what the sentence is about; the predicate tells what happens or what is true about it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Subjects and Predicates', 15),
  (1443, 1443, '<h2>Objects and Complements</h2>
<p>Objects and complements both add information to a sentence, but they do different jobs. An object is commonly affected by the action of a verb. A complement completes the meaning of a verb or describes or identifies a subject or object.</p>

<h3>1. Direct objects</h3>
<p>A direct object receives the action expressed by a transitive verb. To find it, identify the verb and ask <em>what?</em> or <em>whom?</em> after the verb.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The student answered the question.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Verb: <em>answered</em>.</li>
  <li>Answered what? <em>the question</em>.</li>
  <li>Direct object: <em>the question</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Indirect objects</h3>
<p>An indirect object commonly identifies the person who receives something or benefits from an action. It often appears before the direct object.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher gave Amina a certificate.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Verb: <em>gave</em>.</li>
  <li>Indirect object: <em>Amina</em>.</li>
  <li>Direct object: <em>a certificate</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The teacher gave what? A certificate. The teacher gave it to whom? Amina.</p>

<h3>3. Subject complements</h3>
<p>A subject complement follows a linking verb, such as <em>be</em>, <em>seem</em>, or <em>become</em>, and identifies or describes the subject.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The room is quiet.</em> — <em>quiet</em> describes the subject.</li>
  <li><em>My aunt became a doctor.</em> — <em>a doctor</em> identifies the subject''s role.</li>
  <li><em>The food smells delicious.</em> — <em>delicious</em> describes the food.</li>
</ul>
<p>These words are not direct objects because they do not receive an action from the linking verb.</p>

<h3>4. Object complements</h3>
<p>An object complement identifies or describes the direct object.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The class elected Brian captain.</em> — <em>captain</em> identifies Brian''s role.</li>
  <li><em>The news made everyone happy.</em> — <em>happy</em> describes everyone.</li>
</ul>
<p>In the first example, the class elected Brian, and the word <em>captain</em> tells us what Brian was elected to be.</p>

<h3>5. Not every sentence has an object</h3>
<p>Some verbs do not require an object.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The baby slept.</em> — No object is needed.</li>
  <li><em>The birds flew away.</em> — The verb has no direct object here.</li>
  <li><em>The baby drank milk.</em> — <em>milk</em> is the direct object.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not label every word after a verb as an object. It may be an adverbial phrase, a complement, or another part of the sentence.</p>

<h3>6. A reliable method</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Find the verb.</li>
  <li>Decide whether it expresses an action or links the subject to a description or identity.</li>
  <li>For an action verb, ask whether a direct object receives the action.</li>
  <li>Check whether a person receives something as an indirect object.</li>
  <li>Check whether a word identifies or describes the subject or object instead.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>In ''The soup tastes salty'', <em>salty</em> is a subject complement, not an object.</li>
  <li>In ''She sent her brother a message'', <em>her brother</em> is the indirect object and <em>a message</em> is the direct object.</li>
  <li>In ''The child slept peacefully'', <em>peacefully</em> describes how the child slept; it is not an object.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Objects receive or are involved in an action; complements complete or describe an identity or state.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Objects and Complements', 15),
  (1444, 1444, '<h2>Phrases</h2>
<p>A phrase is a group of words that functions as a unit in a sentence but does not contain a complete subject–predicate relationship of its own. A phrase can name something, describe it, show a relationship, or add information about an action.</p>

<h3>1. Noun phrases</h3>
<p>A noun phrase has a noun or pronoun as its central word, called the head. Other words may determine, describe, or add information about it.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>the book</em></li>
  <li><em>three very difficult questions</em></li>
  <li><em>the girl with the blue bag</em></li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>three very difficult questions</em>, the head noun is <em>questions</em>. The whole phrase can function as a subject or object.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>The girl with the blue bag</em> won the prize.</p>

<h3>2. Verb phrases</h3>
<p>A verb phrase contains a main verb and may include one or more auxiliary verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>is reading</em></li>
  <li><em>has finished</em></li>
  <li><em>will have completed</em></li>
  <li><em>might have been waiting</em></li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>has finished</em>, <em>has</em> is an auxiliary and <em>finished</em> is the main verb.</p>

<h3>3. Prepositional phrases</h3>
<p>A prepositional phrase begins with a preposition and usually includes a noun phrase as its object.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>under the table</em></li>
  <li><em>after the lesson</em></li>
  <li><em>with great care</em></li>
  <li><em>near the main gate</em></li>
</ul>
<p>These phrases can express place, time, direction, manner, or other relationships.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners waited <em>near the main gate</em>.</p>

<h3>4. Adjective phrases</h3>
<p>An adjective phrase has an adjective as its head and describes a noun or pronoun.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>very proud</em></li>
  <li><em>extremely useful</em></li>
  <li><em>full of confidence</em></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The team was <em>extremely proud</em> of its performance.</p>

<h3>5. Adverb phrases</h3>
<p>An adverb phrase functions like an adverb by modifying a verb, adjective, or another adverb.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>very quickly</em></li>
  <li><em>quite carefully</em></li>
  <li><em>rather slowly</em></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> She completed the work <em>quite carefully</em>.</p>

<h3>6. Phrases can have different functions</h3>
<p>The same type of phrase can serve different roles in different sentences.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The new student</em> arrived. — Noun phrase functioning as subject.</li>
  <li>I greeted <em>the new student</em>. — Noun phrase functioning as object.</li>
  <li>The bag <em>on the chair</em> is mine. — Prepositional phrase describing which bag.</li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Phrase or clause?</h3>
<p>A phrase does not have its own complete subject–predicate structure. A clause contains a subject and a verb, though a dependent clause cannot stand alone as a complete sentence.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Phrase: <em>after the match</em>.</li>
  <li>Clause: <em>after the match ended</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The second example contains a subject, <em>the match</em>, and a verb, <em>ended</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>A long phrase is not automatically a clause.</li>
  <li>A phrase can contain a noun and a verb form without expressing a complete clause.</li>
  <li>Identify a phrase by how its words function together, not simply by counting words.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> A phrase is a functional group of words that operates as a unit within a larger sentence.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Phrases', 15),
  (1445, 1445, '<h2>Main and Subordinate Clauses</h2>
<p>A clause is a group of words containing a subject and a verb. Clauses differ in whether they can express a complete thought independently or depend on another clause.</p>

<h3>1. Main clauses</h3>
<p>A main clause, also called an independent clause, can stand alone as a complete sentence.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The learners completed the exercise.</li>
  <li>We arrived before noon.</li>
  <li>The rain stopped.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each clause communicates a complete thought without needing another clause to finish its meaning.</p>

<h3>2. Subordinate clauses</h3>
<p>A subordinate clause, also called a dependent clause, contains a subject and a verb but does not normally express a complete thought on its own in the way it is used.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>because the road was flooded</em></li>
  <li><em>although she was tired</em></li>
  <li><em>when the bell rang</em></li>
</ul>
<p>These groups contain subjects and verbs, but they leave the reader expecting a main idea.</p>
<p><strong>Complete example:</strong> We arrived late <em>because the road was flooded</em>.</p>

<h3>3. Recognising subordinating conjunctions</h3>
<p>Subordinate clauses often begin with words such as:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Time: <em>when, while, before, after, until</em>.</li>
  <li>Reason: <em>because, since, as</em>.</li>
  <li>Condition: <em>if, unless</em>.</li>
  <li>Contrast or concession: <em>although, though, even though, whereas</em>.</li>
  <li>Purpose: <em>so that, in order that</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>These words help explain how one idea relates to another, but the presence of one of them does not by itself tell you whether the whole sentence is complete.</p>

<h3>4. Relative clauses</h3>
<p>A relative clause is a subordinate clause that describes a noun or pronoun. It often begins with <em>who</em>, <em>which</em>, or <em>that</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The girl <em>who won the race</em> is my cousin.</p>
<p>The main clause is <em>The girl is my cousin</em>. The relative clause <em>who won the race</em> describes the girl.</p>

<h3>5. Noun clauses</h3>
<p>A noun clause functions like a noun within a larger clause.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>What he said</em> surprised everyone.</p>
<p>The clause <em>What he said</em> functions as the subject of the main verb <em>surprised</em>.</p>

<h3>6. How clauses combine</h3>
<p>Clauses can be combined to show relationships between ideas.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Reason:</strong> She stayed home because she was ill.</li>
  <li><strong>Time:</strong> We began when the teacher arrived.</li>
  <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> Although it was late, they continued working.</li>
  <li><strong>Condition:</strong> If you revise regularly, you will improve.</li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Avoiding sentence fragments</h3>
<p>A subordinate clause used alone is usually a fragment when it is intended to be a complete sentence.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Fragment: <em>Although the shop was open.</em></li>
  <li>Complete: <em>Although the shop was open, we did not enter.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>A subordinate clause can be part of a complete sentence, but it needs an independent clause to complete the thought in these examples.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Do not confuse a phrase with a clause: a clause has a subject and a verb.</li>
  <li>Do not assume a clause is independent just because it contains a subject and a verb.</li>
  <li>Do not leave a subordinate clause as a fragment when a complete sentence is intended.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> A main clause can stand alone; a subordinate clause performs a role within a larger sentence and depends on it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Main and Subordinate Clauses', 15),
  (1446, 1446, '<h2>Simple Sentences</h2>
<p>A simple sentence contains one independent clause. It expresses a complete thought and can have a short or long structure. The number of words does not determine whether a sentence is simple; the number and type of clauses do.</p>

<h3>1. The basic structure</h3>
<p>A simple sentence can contain a subject and a predicate.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The baby cried.</li>
  <li>The learners revised.</li>
  <li>Our neighbours planted trees.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each example contains one independent clause.</p>

<h3>2. Simple sentences can be longer</h3>
<p>A simple sentence can include adjectives, adverbs, objects, and phrases without becoming a compound or complex sentence.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The tired learners in the library revised their notes quietly after lunch.</p>
<p>This is still a simple sentence because it has one independent clause. The additional words expand the information but do not create another clause.</p>

<h3>3. Compound subject, simple sentence</h3>
<p>A sentence may have two or more subjects but still contain only one independent clause.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Amina and Brian prepared the presentation.</p>
<p>The compound subject is <em>Amina and Brian</em>, but the sentence has one predicate and one independent clause.</p>

<h3>4. Compound predicate, simple sentence</h3>
<p>A subject can perform two or more actions within one independent clause.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher entered the room, greeted the learners, and began the lesson.</p>
<p>The teacher performs several actions, but the sentence contains one independent clause with a compound predicate.</p>

<h3>5. Simple sentence versus a compound sentence</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Simple:</strong> The rain stopped, and the sun appeared. — This has two independent clauses, so it is compound, despite the comma and conjunction making it one written sentence.</li>
  <li><strong>Simple:</strong> After the rain, the sun appeared. — This has one independent clause and a phrase giving time.</li>
  <li><strong>Complex:</strong> After the rain stopped, the sun appeared. — This has an independent clause and a subordinate clause.</li>
</ul>
<p>Count clauses, not commas, conjunctions, or verb words alone.</p>

<h3>6. Avoiding fragments</h3>
<p>A simple sentence must express a complete thought.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Fragment: <em>Because the bus was late.</em></li>
  <li>Simple sentence: <em>The bus was late.</em></li>
  <li>Complex sentence: <em>Because the bus was late, we missed the opening speech.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>7. A test for simple sentences</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Find the subject and verb of each clause.</li>
  <li>Identify any subordinate clauses introduced by words such as <em>because</em>, <em>although</em>, or <em>when</em>.</li>
  <li>Determine how many independent clauses the sentence contains.</li>
  <li>If it contains one independent clause and no subordinate clause, it is simple.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>A long sentence can still be simple if it contains one independent clause.</li>
  <li>A sentence with two subjects joined by ''and'' is not automatically compound.</li>
  <li>A sentence with two independent clauses joined by ''and'' is compound, not simple.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> A simple sentence contains one independent clause, regardless of its length or the number of actions expressed.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Simple Sentences', 15),
  (1447, 1447, '<h2>What Is a Compound Sentence?</h2>
<p>A <strong>compound sentence</strong> contains two or more independent clauses joined correctly. An independent clause has a subject and a verb and expresses a complete thought.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the two parts</h3>
<p>Consider these sentences:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The rain stopped.</li>
  <li>The players returned to the field.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each sentence expresses a complete thought. They can be joined to form a compound sentence:</p>
<p><em>The rain stopped, and the players returned to the field.</em></p>
<p>The sentence contains two independent clauses:</p>
<ol>
  <li>The rain stopped.</li>
  <li>The players returned to the field.</li>
</ol>

<h3>2. Joining independent clauses</h3>
<p>Independent clauses can be joined using coordinating conjunctions. The common conjunctions are <strong>for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so</strong>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>And</strong> adds information: Amina revised her notes, and Brian completed the assignment.</li>
  <li><strong>But</strong> shows contrast: He studied hard, but he did not finish the paper.</li>
  <li><strong>Or</strong> presents a choice: We can walk to school, or we can take a bus.</li>
  <li><strong>So</strong> shows a result: The road was flooded, so we used another route.</li>
  <li><strong>Yet</strong> shows an unexpected contrast: She was nervous, yet she spoke confidently.</li>
  <li><strong>For</strong> gives a reason: We left early, for the weather was worsening.</li>
  <li><strong>Nor</strong> adds a negative alternative: He did not apologise, nor did he explain his behaviour.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Punctuation</h3>
<p>When two independent clauses are joined by a coordinating conjunction, place a comma before the conjunction.</p>
<p>Correct: <em>The bell rang, and the learners entered the classroom.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The bell rang and, the learners entered the classroom.</em></p>
<p>A semicolon can also join closely related independent clauses without a coordinating conjunction.</p>
<p><em>The bell rang; the learners entered the classroom.</em></p>

<h3>4. Avoid comma splices</h3>
<p>A comma splice occurs when two independent clauses are joined using only a comma.</p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The match ended, the spectators went home.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>The match ended, so the spectators went home.</em></p>
<p>Also correct: <em>The match ended; the spectators went home.</em></p>

<h3>5. Do not confuse a long sentence with a compound sentence</h3>
<p>A sentence is not compound simply because it is long or contains the word <em>and</em>. The clauses must each express a complete thought.</p>
<p><em>Mary washed the clothes and cleaned the house.</em></p>
<p>This is a simple sentence with a compound predicate: Mary performed two actions, but there is only one subject and one independent clause.</p>
<p><em>Mary washed the clothes, and Peter cleaned the house.</em></p>
<p>This is a compound sentence because each clause has its own subject and expresses a complete thought.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Compound Sentences', 15),
  (1448, 1448, '<h2>What Is a Complex Sentence?</h2>
<p>A <strong>complex sentence</strong> contains one independent clause and at least one subordinate (dependent) clause.</p>
<p>An independent clause expresses a complete thought. A subordinate clause has a subject and a verb but depends on another clause to complete its meaning or function within the sentence.</p>

<h3>1. Recognise the difference</h3>
<p><strong>Independent clause:</strong> We stayed indoors.</p>
<p><strong>Subordinate clause:</strong> because it was raining.</p>
<p>The subordinate clause begins with <em>because</em> and gives a reason, but it does not normally express a complete sentence on its own in this use.</p>
<p>Combined: <em>We stayed indoors because it was raining.</em></p>
<p>This is a complex sentence because it contains one independent clause and one subordinate clause.</p>

<h3>2. Common subordinating conjunctions</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Time:</strong> when, while, before, after, until, as soon as.</li>
  <li><strong>Reason:</strong> because, since, as.</li>
  <li><strong>Condition:</strong> if, unless, provided that.</li>
  <li><strong>Contrast or concession:</strong> although, though, even though, whereas.</li>
  <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> so that, in order that.</li>
  <li><strong>Result or comparison:</strong> than, as ... as, so ... that, where these introduce subordinate clauses.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>When the bell rang, the learners left the classroom.</em> (time)</li>
  <li><em>She passed because she prepared carefully.</em> (reason)</li>
  <li><em>If you practise regularly, your accuracy will improve.</em> (condition)</li>
  <li><em>Although he was tired, he completed the assignment.</em> (concession)</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Subordinate clauses can appear in different positions</h3>
<p>A subordinate clause may come before or after the independent clause.</p>
<p><strong>Subordinate clause first:</strong> <em>Although the road was muddy, we continued walking.</em></p>
<p>Use a comma after the introductory subordinate clause.</p>
<p><strong>Independent clause first:</strong> <em>We continued walking although the road was muddy.</em></p>
<p>A comma is usually unnecessary when a subordinate clause follows the independent clause and is essential to the sentence''s meaning.</p>

<h3>4. Relative clauses</h3>
<p>A subordinate clause can describe a noun. Such a clause is often introduced by <em>who, whom, whose, which</em> or <em>that</em>.</p>
<p><em>The learner who won the competition received a certificate.</em></p>
<p>The clause <em>who won the competition</em> describes which learner is meant.</p>
<p><em>The book that you borrowed is on the table.</em></p>
<p>The clause <em>that you borrowed</em> identifies the book.</p>

<h3>5. Avoid incomplete sentences</h3>
<p>A subordinate clause used alone is usually a sentence fragment.</p>
<p>Incomplete: <em>Although the teacher explained the rule.</em></p>
<p>Complete: <em>Although the teacher explained the rule, some learners remained confused.</em></p>
<p>The first clause creates an expectation that is completed by the independent clause.</p>

<h3>6. Do not confuse a complex sentence with a compound sentence</h3>
<p><strong>Compound:</strong> <em>The sun set, and the workers went home.</em> Both clauses are independent.</p>
<p><strong>Complex:</strong> <em>When the sun set, the workers went home.</em> One clause depends on the other.</p>
<p>The important distinction is not the sentence''s length; it is the relationship between its clauses.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Complex Sentences', 15),
  (1449, 1449, '<h2>Understanding Compound-Complex Sentences</h2>
<p>A <strong>compound-complex sentence</strong> contains at least two independent clauses and at least one subordinate clause.</p>
<p>It combines the structure of a compound sentence with the structure of a complex sentence.</p>

<h3>1. Count the clauses, not the words</h3>
<p>Consider this example:</p>
<p><em>Although it was raining, the players continued the match, and the spectators remained in their seats.</em></p>
<p>There are three clauses:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Although it was raining</strong> — subordinate clause.</li>
  <li><strong>The players continued the match</strong> — independent clause.</li>
  <li><strong>The spectators remained in their seats</strong> — independent clause.</li>
</ol>
<p>Because the sentence has two independent clauses and one subordinate clause, it is compound-complex.</p>

<h3>2. Understand how the clauses connect</h3>
<p>The word <em>although</em> introduces the subordinate clause and shows concession. The conjunction <em>and</em> joins the two independent clauses.</p>
<p>These words perform different jobs. A subordinating conjunction makes one clause dependent on another, while a coordinating conjunction can join independent clauses.</p>

<h3>3. Another example</h3>
<p><em>When the examination ended, the candidates handed in their papers, and the invigilator collected them.</em></p>
<ul>
  <li><em>When the examination ended</em> — subordinate clause showing time.</li>
  <li><em>The candidates handed in their papers</em> — independent clause.</li>
  <li><em>The invigilator collected them</em> — independent clause.</li>
</ul>
<p>The introductory subordinate clause is followed by a comma. The two independent clauses are joined with a comma before <em>and</em>.</p>

<h3>4. Build one systematically</h3>
<p>Start with two independent clauses:</p>
<p><em>The farmer planted maize. The rain arrived.</em></p>
<p>Join them with a coordinating conjunction:</p>
<p><em>The farmer planted maize, and the rain arrived.</em></p>
<p>Now add a subordinate clause:</p>
<p><em>Because the soil was ready, the farmer planted maize, and the rain arrived.</em></p>
<p>The result is compound-complex: two independent clauses and one subordinate clause.</p>

<h3>5. Avoid common classification errors</h3>
<p><strong>Compound:</strong> <em>The farmer planted maize, and the rain arrived.</em> Two independent clauses; no subordinate clause.</p>
<p><strong>Complex:</strong> <em>Because the soil was ready, the farmer planted maize.</em> One independent clause and one subordinate clause.</p>
<p><strong>Compound-complex:</strong> <em>Because the soil was ready, the farmer planted maize, and the rain arrived.</em> Two independent clauses and one subordinate clause.</p>
<p>A sentence does not become compound-complex merely because it has several verbs. Identify the clauses and determine whether each expresses a complete thought.</p>

<h3>6. Punctuation</h3>
<p>Use punctuation according to the relationships between clauses:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Place a comma after an introductory subordinate clause.</li>
  <li>Place a comma before a coordinating conjunction that joins two independent clauses.</li>
  <li>Do not place a comma automatically before every conjunction. Check what the conjunction joins.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Compound-Complex Sentences', 15),
  (1450, 1450, '<h2>Statements and Questions</h2>
<p>Sentences can be classified by their communicative purpose. A <strong>statement</strong> gives information, while a <strong>question</strong> asks for information or confirmation.</p>

<h3>1. Statements</h3>
<p>A statement normally follows the usual subject–verb order and ends with a full stop.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The learners are preparing for their examinations.</em></li>
  <li><em>Our school opened a new library.</em></li>
  <li><em>She has completed the assignment.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Statements can be affirmative or negative.</p>
<p>Affirmative: <em>He understands the instructions.</em></p>
<p>Negative: <em>He does not understand the instructions.</em></p>
<p>A negative statement still gives information; it simply denies or rejects something.</p>

<h3>2. Questions that use auxiliary verbs</h3>
<p>In many English questions, an auxiliary verb comes before the subject.</p>
<p>Statement: <em>You are ready.</em></p>
<p>Question: <em>Are you ready?</em></p>
<p>Statement: <em>She has finished.</em></p>
<p>Question: <em>Has she finished?</em></p>
<p>Statement: <em>They will attend.</em></p>
<p>Question: <em>Will they attend?</em></p>

<h3>3. Questions with do, does and did</h3>
<p>When a statement has no auxiliary verb, use <em>do, does</em> or <em>did</em> to form many present and past simple questions.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Statement: <em>You play football.</em> Question: <em>Do you play football?</em></li>
  <li>Statement: <em>She plays football.</em> Question: <em>Does she play football?</em></li>
  <li>Statement: <em>They visited Nairobi.</em> Question: <em>Did they visit Nairobi?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>After <em>does</em> or <em>did</em>, use the base form of the main verb.</p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>Does she plays football?</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>Does she play football?</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>Did they visited Nairobi?</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>Did they visit Nairobi?</em></p>

<h3>4. Wh-questions</h3>
<p>Wh-questions ask for specific information rather than a simple yes or no.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Who</strong> asks about a person: Who called you?</li>
  <li><strong>What</strong> asks about a thing or action: What happened?</li>
  <li><strong>Where</strong> asks about a place: Where do you live?</li>
  <li><strong>When</strong> asks about time: When will the lesson begin?</li>
  <li><strong>Why</strong> asks about a reason: Why did you leave early?</li>
  <li><strong>Which</strong> asks someone to select from alternatives: Which book did you borrow?</li>
  <li><strong>How</strong> asks about manner, condition or degree: How did you solve the problem?</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Word order in questions</h3>
<p>Compare:</p>
<p>Statement: <em>She can solve the problem.</em></p>
<p>Question: <em>Can she solve the problem?</em></p>
<p>Wh-question: <em>How can she solve the problem?</em></p>
<p>When an auxiliary verb is present, it usually comes before the subject in a direct question. A wh-word usually comes first.</p>

<h3>6. Questions where the question word is the subject</h3>
<p>Not every question reverses the subject and auxiliary verb.</p>
<p><em>Who broke the window?</em></p>
<p>Here, <em>who</em> is the subject. We do not normally say <em>Who did break the window?</em> in a neutral question. The form with <em>did</em> can occur for emphasis, but it has a different use.</p>

<h3>7. Punctuation and indirect questions</h3>
<p>Direct questions end with a question mark: <em>Where is the office?</em></p>
<p>An indirect question embedded in a statement normally does not use question word order:</p>
<p>Direct: <em>Where is the office?</em></p>
<p>Indirect: <em>Could you tell me where the office is?</em></p>
<p>The indirect question is part of a larger question, so the whole sentence ends with a question mark. In a statement such as <em>I know where the office is.</em>, use a full stop.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Statements and Questions', 15),
  (1451, 1451, '<h2>Commands and Exclamations</h2>
<p>Commands and exclamations serve different purposes. A <strong>command</strong> directs someone to do or not do something. An <strong>exclamation</strong> expresses a strong feeling or reaction.</p>

<h3>1. Commands and instructions</h3>
<p>Commands usually use the base form of the verb. The subject is generally the understood word <em>you</em>, even though it is not written.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Close the door.</em></li>
  <li><em>Read the instructions carefully.</em></li>
  <li><em>Turn left at the junction.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>Close the door</em>, the implied subject is <em>you</em>: you close the door.</p>
<p>Commands are common in instructions, recipes, notices, rules and procedures because they tell the reader what action to take.</p>

<h3>2. Negative commands</h3>
<p>Use <em>do not</em> or its contraction <em>don''t</em> before the base form of the verb to tell someone not to do something.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Positive: <em>Touch the switch.</em></li>
  <li>Negative: <em>Do not touch the switch.</em></li>
  <li>Positive: <em>Enter the room.</em></li>
  <li>Negative: <em>Don''t enter the room.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Incorrect: <em>Do not touches the switch.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>Do not touch the switch.</em></p>

<h3>3. Polite commands and requests</h3>
<p>Commands do not have to sound harsh. Adding <em>please</em> or using a polite question can make a request more courteous.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Direct command: <em>Pass me the book.</em></li>
  <li>Polite command: <em>Please pass me the book.</em></li>
  <li>Polite request: <em>Could you pass me the book, please?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>The intended action may be the same, but the wording changes the tone and the level of politeness.</p>

<h3>4. Exclamations</h3>
<p>An exclamation expresses a strong emotion such as surprise, joy, anger, fear or admiration.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>What a beautiful performance!</em></li>
  <li><em>How quickly the time has passed!</em></li>
  <li><em>That was an incredible result!</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Exclamations often end with an exclamation mark, but the mark alone does not make a sentence an exclamation. The meaning and structure also matter.</p>

<h3>5. What and how exclamations</h3>
<p><strong>What</strong> can introduce an exclamation focusing on a noun phrase:</p>
<p><em>What a difficult question that was!</em></p>
<p><strong>How</strong> can emphasise an adjective or adverb:</p>
<p><em>How difficult that question was!</em></p>
<p><em>How beautifully she sings!</em></p>
<p>Notice that the exclamatory word order differs from the usual direct-question structure. An exclamation such as <em>How beautifully she sings!</em> is not asking how she sings; it expresses admiration.</p>

<h3>6. Do not confuse a command with a statement</h3>
<p><em>You must close the gate.</em> is a statement that expresses obligation.</p>
<p><em>Close the gate.</em> is a direct command.</p>
<p>Both may encourage the same action, but they have different grammatical structures.</p>

<h3>7. Choose punctuation by purpose</h3>
<ul>
  <li>A routine command can end with a full stop: <em>Write your name on the paper.</em></li>
  <li>An urgent or forceful command may end with an exclamation mark: <em>Stop!</em></li>
  <li>An exclamation expressing strong feeling often ends with an exclamation mark: <em>What wonderful news!</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Use exclamation marks selectively. A sentence does not need one simply because it is an instruction.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Commands and Exclamations', 15),
  (1452, 1452, '<h2>Sentence Fragments and Run-on Sentences</h2>
<p>A complete sentence expresses a complete thought and normally contains an independent clause. Two common errors are <strong>sentence fragments</strong> and <strong>run-on sentences</strong>.</p>

<h3>1. Sentence fragments</h3>
<p>A sentence fragment is a group of words punctuated as a sentence even though it does not form a complete independent clause in the intended context.</p>
<p><strong>Fragment:</strong> Because the road was flooded.</p>
<p>The word <em>because</em> introduces a reason, but the reader is left waiting for the result.</p>
<p><strong>Complete sentence:</strong> Because the road was flooded, we used another route.</p>

<h3>2. Common causes of fragments</h3>
<p><strong>A. A subordinate clause standing alone</strong></p>
<p>Fragment: <em>Although she studied throughout the weekend.</em></p>
<p>Complete: <em>Although she studied throughout the weekend, she still needed more practice.</em></p>
<p><strong>B. A phrase mistaken for a complete sentence</strong></p>
<p>Fragment: <em>After the final examination.</em></p>
<p>Complete: <em>We went home after the final examination.</em></p>
<p><strong>C. A missing main verb</strong></p>
<p>Fragment: <em>The learners in the science laboratory.</em></p>
<p>Complete: <em>The learners are in the science laboratory.</em></p>
<p>To correct a fragment, identify what is missing and add the necessary information without changing the intended meaning.</p>

<h3>3. Run-on sentences</h3>
<p>A run-on sentence occurs when two or more independent clauses are joined incorrectly. A run-on can happen without any punctuation or when a comma is used alone.</p>
<p><strong>Fused sentence:</strong> The rain stopped the players returned to the field.</p>
<p><strong>Comma splice:</strong> The rain stopped, the players returned to the field.</p>
<p>Both versions incorrectly join two independent clauses.</p>

<h3>4. Correcting run-on sentences</h3>
<p><strong>Method 1: Use a full stop.</strong></p>
<p>The rain stopped. The players returned to the field.</p>
<p><strong>Method 2: Use a comma and a coordinating conjunction.</strong></p>
<p>The rain stopped, and the players returned to the field.</p>
<p><strong>Method 3: Use a semicolon.</strong></p>
<p>The rain stopped; the players returned to the field.</p>
<p><strong>Method 4: Use a subordinate clause if the meaning supports it.</strong></p>
<p>When the rain stopped, the players returned to the field.</p>

<h3>5. Diagnose before correcting</h3>
<p>Do not correct every long sentence by splitting it. First identify the clauses and their intended relationship.</p>
<ul>
  <li>If a group of words cannot stand as a complete sentence, check for a fragment.</li>
  <li>If two complete thoughts are joined incorrectly, check for a run-on.</li>
  <li>If one idea explains a reason, condition or time, a subordinate clause may communicate the relationship more accurately.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. Common mistake</h3>
<p>Incorrect: <em>Although the match was difficult. The team won.</em></p>
<p>Better: <em>Although the match was difficult, the team won.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The team won, the supporters celebrated.</em></p>
<p>Better: <em>The team won, so the supporters celebrated.</em></p>
<p>The first error separates a subordinate clause from the thought that completes it. The second joins two independent clauses with only a comma.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sentence Fragments and Run-on Sentences', 15),
  (1453, 1453, '<h2>Modal Verbs</h2>
<h3>1. WHAT ARE MODAL AUXILIARY VERBS?</h3>
<p><b>Modal verbs</b> are auxiliary (helping) verbs that express the speaker''s attitude towards the action: ability, permission, obligation, advice, possibility, or necessity.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Core modals:</b> can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would, ought to.</li>
<li><b>Key properties:</b> They do NOT take ''-s'' in the third person (<i>he can</i>, not <i>he cans</i>), and are followed by a <b>bare infinitive</b> (base verb without ''to'').</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Modal Verbs', 15),
  (1454, 1454, '<h2>Ability and Permission</h2>
<h3>1. EXPRESSING ABILITY</h3>
<p>We use <b>can</b> for present general ability (<i>I can swim</i>) and <b>could</b> for past general ability (<i>She could read at age four</i>). For specific past achievements, use <b>was/were able to</b>.</p>
<h3>2. EXPRESSING PERMISSION</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Can:</b> Informal permission (<i>Can I borrow your pen?</i>)</li>
<li><b>May:</b> Formal or polite permission (<i>May I leave the room, Sir?</i>)</li>
<li><b>Could:</b> Polite request for permission (<i>Could I ask a question?</i>)</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ability and Permission', 15),
  (1455, 1455, '<h2>Obligation and Advice</h2>
<h3>1. EXPRESSING OBLIGATION AND NECESSITY</h3>
<p><b>Must</b> expresses strong internal obligation or strict necessity imposed by a speaker or law (<i>You must wear a seatbelt</i>). <b>Have to</b> expresses external obligation due to rules or circumstances (<i>Students have to submit assignments by Friday</i>).</p>
<h3>2. EXPRESSING ADVICE AND RECOMMENDATION</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Should / Ought to:</b> Used to give friendly advice or moral obligation (<i>You should get more sleep; you ought to apologize</i>).</li>
<li><b>Had better:</b> Used for urgent advice with a warning of negative consequences (<i>You had better hurry or you will miss the bus</i>).</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Obligation and Advice', 15),
  (1456, 1456, '<h2>Possibility and Probability</h2>
<h3>1. DEGREES OF CERTAINTY</h3>
<p>We use modal verbs to express how certain we are about a fact or prediction:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Must:</b> Near 100% certainty based on logical deduction (<i>The lights are on; they must be home</i>).</li>
<li><b>May / Might / Could:</b> Around 30%–50% possibility (<i>It might rain later today</i>).</li>
<li><b>Cannot (Can''t):</b> Negative certainty / impossibility (<i>He only just left London; he can''t be in Nairobi already</i>).</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Possibility and Probability', 15),
  (1457, 1457, '<h2>Active Voice</h2>
<h3>1. STRUCTURE OF ACTIVE VOICE</h3>
<p>In the <b>active voice</b>, the grammatical subject of the sentence performs the action expressed by the verb.</p>
<p><b>Formula:</b> <code>Subject (Doer) + Verb (Action) + Object (Receiver)</code></p>
<ul>
<li><i>Example:</i> <b>The chef</b> (Subject) <b>cooked</b> (Verb) <b>a delicious meal</b> (Object).</li>
<li><b>Why use Active Voice?</b> It makes writing direct, vigorous, clear, and concise.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Active Voice', 15),
  (1458, 1458, '<h2>Passive Voice</h2>
<h3>1. STRUCTURE OF PASSIVE VOICE</h3>
<p>In the <b>passive voice</b>, the subject receives the action rather than performing it. The doer may be introduced with ''by'' or omitted entirely.</p>
<p><b>Formula:</b> <code>Object become Subject + Appropriate Form of ''to be'' + Past Participle (V3) + (by Agent)</code></p>
<ul>
<li><i>Active:</i> The teacher marked the scripts.</li>
<li><i>Passive:</i> The scripts <b>were marked</b> by the teacher.</li>
<li><b>When to use Passive:</b> When the doer is unknown, unimportant, or when the focus is on the result (e.g. scientific reports: <i>Water was heated to 100°C</i>).</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Passive Voice', 15),
  (1459, 1459, '<h2>Direct Speech</h2>
<h3>1. WHAT IS DIRECT SPEECH?</h3>
<p><b>Direct speech</b> quotes the exact words spoken by a person, enclosed in <b>quotation marks (“ ” or '' '')</b>.</p>
<h3>2. PUNCTUATION RULES FOR DIRECT SPEECH</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>comma</b> introduces the spoken words if the reporting clause comes first (<i>Amina said, “We must leave now.”</i>).</li>
<li>The first spoken word inside quotation marks begins with a <b>capital letter</b>.</li>
<li>Punctuation marks (full stop, question mark, exclamation mark) go <b>inside</b> the closing quotation marks.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Direct Speech', 15),
  (1460, 1460, '<h2>Reported Speech (Indirect Speech)</h2>
<h3>1. WHAT IS REPORTED SPEECH?</h3>
<p><b>Reported speech</b> reports what someone said without using their exact words and without quotation marks.</p>
<h3>2. KEY TRANSFORMATION RULES (BACKSHIFTING)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Present Simple → Past Simple:</b> <i>“I like tea” → He said that he liked tea.</i></li>
<li><b>Present Continuous → Past Continuous:</b> <i>“I am reading” → She said she was reading.</i></li>
<li><b>Past Simple → Past Perfect:</b> <i>“I saw him” → He said he had seen him.</i></li>
<li><b>Will → Would / Can → Could / May → Might</b></li>
<li><b>Time & Place shifts:</b> now → then, today → that day, tomorrow → the next day, here → there.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reported Speech', 15),
  (1461, 1461, '<h2>Conditional Sentences</h2>
<h3>1. FOUR MAIN TYPES OF CONDITIONALS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Zero Conditional (Universal Truths / Facts):</b> <code>If + Present Simple, Present Simple</code> (<i>If you heat ice, it melts.</i>)</li>
<li><b>First Conditional (Real / Likely Future):</b> <code>If + Present Simple, will + base verb</code> (<i>If it rains, we will stay indoors.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Second Conditional (Unreal / Hypothetical Present):</b> <code>If + Past Simple, would + base verb</code> (<i>If I won the lottery, I would travel the world.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Third Conditional (Unreal Past / Regret):</b> <code>If + Past Perfect, would have + V3</code> (<i>If we had left earlier, we would have caught the train.</i>)</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Conditional Sentences', 15),
  (1462, 1462, '<h2>Types of Adjectives</h2>
<p>An <strong>adjective</strong> describes a noun or pronoun, or helps identify which person or thing is meant. Adjectives can communicate qualities, quantities, ownership and other information.</p>

<h3>1. Descriptive adjectives</h3>
<p>Descriptive adjectives tell us what a person, place or thing is like.</p>
<ul>
  <li>a <em>careful</em> driver</li>
  <li>a <em>narrow</em> road</li>
  <li>a <em>useful</em> explanation</li>
  <li>a <em>bright</em> classroom</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>a careful driver</em>, the adjective <em>careful</em> describes the noun <em>driver</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Quantitative adjectives</h3>
<p>Quantitative adjectives indicate an amount or quantity.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>some</em> water</li>
  <li><em>little</em> time</li>
  <li><em>enough</em> food</li>
  <li><em>much</em> effort</li>
</ul>
<p>Traditional school grammar often calls these quantitative adjectives. In modern grammar, many words in this group are classified as determiners when they introduce a noun. Their function in the sentence is what matters.</p>

<h3>3. Numeral adjectives</h3>
<p>Numeral adjectives express number or order.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>three</em> books — a cardinal number.</li>
  <li><em>five</em> learners — a cardinal number.</li>
  <li>the <em>first</em> lesson — an ordinal number.</li>
  <li>the <em>third</em> attempt — an ordinal number.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cardinal numbers answer ''how many?'' while ordinal numbers indicate position or order.</p>

<h3>4. Demonstrative adjectives</h3>
<p>Demonstrative words identify which noun is meant.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>This</em> book is useful.</li>
  <li><em>That</em> building is old.</li>
  <li><em>These</em> questions are difficult.</li>
  <li><em>Those</em> trees need water.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use <em>this</em> and <em>that</em> with singular nouns, and <em>these</em> and <em>those</em> with plural nouns.</p>
<p>Compare: <em>This is useful.</em> Here, <em>this</em> stands alone as a pronoun. In <em>This book is useful</em>, it introduces the noun <em>book</em> and functions as a determiner in modern grammar.</p>

<h3>5. Possessive adjectives</h3>
<p>Possessive adjectives, also called possessive determiners in modern grammar, show whose noun is being referred to.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>my</em> notebook</li>
  <li><em>your</em> pencil</li>
  <li><em>his</em> uniform</li>
  <li><em>her</em> bag</li>
  <li><em>our</em> classroom</li>
  <li><em>their</em> teacher</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not confuse possessive determiners with possessive pronouns.</p>
<p>Determiner: <em>This is my book.</em></p>
<p>Pronoun: <em>This book is mine.</em></p>

<h3>6. Interrogative adjectives</h3>
<p>In traditional grammar, <em>which</em> and <em>what</em> are called interrogative adjectives when they introduce a noun in a question.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Which subject</em> do you prefer?</li>
  <li><em>What time</em> does the bus arrive?</li>
</ul>
<p>Compare: <em>Which do you prefer?</em> In this sentence, <em>which</em> stands alone and functions as a pronoun.</p>

<h3>7. Participial adjectives</h3>
<p>Some adjectives are formed from verbs and end in <em>-ing</em> or <em>-ed</em>, among other forms.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The lesson was <em>interesting</em>.</li>
  <li>The learners were <em>interested</em>.</li>
  <li>The <em>broken</em> window needs repair.</li>
</ul>
<p>In many contexts, <em>-ing</em> describes what causes a feeling, while <em>-ed</em> describes the person or thing experiencing it.</p>

<h3>8. Identify an adjective by its function</h3>
<p>Ask what word the adjective describes or helps identify. In <em>The exhausted players entered the field</em>, <em>exhausted</em> describes <em>players</em>. In <em>The players were exhausted</em>, it describes the players after the linking verb <em>were</em>.</p>
<p>Not every word before a noun is necessarily an adjective. Words such as <em>the</em>, <em>my</em> and <em>those</em> are generally classified as determiners in modern grammar, even though older school grammar may group some of them under adjectives.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Types of Adjectives', 15),
  (1463, 1463, '<h2>Order of Adjectives</h2>
<p>When several adjectives describe one noun, English usually places them in a conventional order. The order helps the description sound natural and makes the noun easier to identify.</p>

<h3>1. The usual order</h3>
<p>A useful guide is:</p>
<p><strong>Determiner → quantity/number → opinion → size → age → shape → colour → origin → material → purpose → noun</strong></p>
<p>Example: <em>those three beautiful small old round brown Kenyan wooden dining tables</em></p>
<p>This example contains many descriptive details. In ordinary writing, using fewer adjectives is often clearer.</p>

<h3>2. Understand each position</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Determiner:</strong> a, the, this, those, my.</li>
  <li><strong>Quantity or number:</strong> three, several, many.</li>
  <li><strong>Opinion:</strong> beautiful, useful, unpleasant.</li>
  <li><strong>Size:</strong> small, large, tall.</li>
  <li><strong>Age:</strong> old, young, new.</li>
  <li><strong>Shape:</strong> round, square, rectangular.</li>
  <li><strong>Colour:</strong> red, black, yellow.</li>
  <li><strong>Origin:</strong> Kenyan, Japanese, African.</li>
  <li><strong>Material:</strong> wooden, plastic, metal.</li>
  <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> cooking, sleeping, dining, sports.</li>
</ul>
<p>Purpose words often form a compound description close to the noun: <em>a small metal cooking pot</em>.</p>

<h3>3. Apply the order</h3>
<p>Suppose you want to describe a bag using <em>leather</em>, <em>beautiful</em> and <em>small</em>.</p>
<p>Identify each type:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>beautiful</em> — opinion.</li>
  <li><em>small</em> — size.</li>
  <li><em>leather</em> — material.</li>
</ul>
<p>Arrange them as opinion → size → material → noun:</p>
<p><em>a beautiful small leather bag</em></p>
<p><em>A leather small beautiful bag</em> sounds unnatural because the adjectives are not in the usual order.</p>

<h3>4. Natural order is a guide, not a mechanical rule</h3>
<p>Adjective order can change when a writer wants emphasis, when adjectives belong to different phrases, or when a phrase has become established in common use.</p>
<p>For example, <em>a big bad wolf</em> is an established expression. Do not force every sentence into an artificial sequence if the result sounds unnatural.</p>
<p>Some adjectives are closely connected to the noun and are not freely interchangeable. In <em>medical equipment</em>, <em>medical</em> identifies the type of equipment; it is not merely a decorative description.</p>

<h3>5. Coordinate adjectives</h3>
<p>Coordinate adjectives independently describe a noun and can often be joined with <em>and</em> or reversed in order.</p>
<p><em>It was a cold, windy evening.</em></p>
<p>You can say <em>a windy, cold evening</em> and retain much of the meaning. A comma is appropriate between coordinate adjectives.</p>
<p>Cumulative adjectives build a description in stages and are not usually separated by commas.</p>
<p><em>a small wooden box</em></p>
<p>Here, <em>small</em> describes the box and <em>wooden</em> identifies its material. A comma is not normally used between them.</p>

<h3>6. Avoid adjective overload</h3>
<p>Correct order does not automatically make a description effective. Compare:</p>
<p><em>She carried a beautiful small new black leather school bag.</em></p>
<p><em>She carried a new school bag.</em></p>
<p>Choose the details that help the reader understand the intended meaning. Unnecessary adjectives make sentences slower and less precise.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Order of Adjectives', 15),
  (1464, 1464, '<h2>Degrees of Comparison</h2>
<p>Adjectives can compare the qualities of people, places or things. English commonly uses three degrees: <strong>positive</strong>, <strong>comparative</strong> and <strong>superlative</strong>.</p>

<h3>1. Positive degree</h3>
<p>The positive form describes a quality without making a comparison.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The road is <em>wide</em>.</li>
  <li>The learner is <em>careful</em>.</li>
  <li>This exercise is <em>difficult</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Comparative degree</h3>
<p>The comparative form compares two people, things or situations. Short adjectives often take <em>-er</em>, while many longer adjectives use <em>more</em>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>tall → taller: Amina is taller than Wanjiku.</li>
  <li>fast → faster: This bus is faster than the other one.</li>
  <li>careful → more careful: He is more careful than before.</li>
  <li>interesting → more interesting: This book is more interesting than the film.</li>
</ul>
<p>The word <em>than</em> commonly introduces the second part of a comparison.</p>

<h3>3. Superlative degree</h3>
<p>The superlative form identifies the highest or lowest degree within a group of three or more, or within a defined group.</p>
<ul>
  <li>tall → tallest: Amina is the tallest learner in the group.</li>
  <li>fast → fastest: This is the fastest bus on the route.</li>
  <li>careful → most careful: She is the most careful driver in the team.</li>
  <li>interesting → most interesting: That was the most interesting chapter in the book.</li>
</ul>
<p>Superlatives commonly take <em>the</em> before them when identifying one member of a group, although context and determiner choice can vary.</p>

<h3>4. Spelling changes</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Most short adjectives:</strong> add <em>-er</em> or <em>-est</em>: tall → taller → tallest.</li>
  <li><strong>Adjectives ending in -e:</strong> add <em>-r</em> or <em>-st</em>: large → larger → largest.</li>
  <li><strong>Consonant + y:</strong> change <em>y</em> to <em>i</em>: happy → happier → happiest.</li>
  <li><strong>Some short vowel-consonant forms:</strong> double the final consonant: big → bigger → biggest.</li>
  <li><strong>Many longer adjectives:</strong> use <em>more</em> and <em>most</em>: careful → more careful → most careful.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are useful patterns, not a guarantee for every adjective. Some two-syllable adjectives allow more than one form depending on usage.</p>

<h3>5. Irregular comparisons</h3>
<p>Some common adjectives have irregular forms that must be learned.</p>
<ul>
  <li>good → better → best</li>
  <li>bad → worse → worst</li>
  <li>far → farther/further → farthest/furthest, depending on context and variety of English</li>
  <li>little → less → least, when referring to amount</li>
  <li>many/much → more → most</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not form these comparisons by adding <em>-er</em> or <em>-est</em> to the positive form.</p>

<h3>6. Avoid double comparison</h3>
<p>Incorrect: <em>more taller</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>taller</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>most fastest</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>fastest</em></p>
<p>Do not normally use <em>more</em> with an adjective that already has the comparative ending, or <em>most</em> with one that already has the superlative ending.</p>

<h3>7. Make comparisons logically</h3>
<p>Incorrect: <em>My bag is heavier than Mary.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>My bag is heavier than Mary''s.</em></p>
<p>The first sentence compares a bag with a person. The corrected version compares one bag with Mary''s bag.</p>
<p>Use <em>as ... as</em> to express equality:</p>
<p><em>This road is as wide as the other road.</em></p>
<p>Use <em>not as ... as</em> to express a lack of equality:</p>
<p><em>This road is not as wide as the other road.</em></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Positive, Comparative and Superlative Forms', 15),
  (1465, 1465, '<h2>Types of Adverbs</h2>
<p>An <strong>adverb</strong> can modify a verb, an adjective, another adverb or a whole clause. It often tells us how, when, where or how often something happens, or to what degree something is true.</p>

<h3>1. Adverbs of manner</h3>
<p>Adverbs of manner describe how an action is performed.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The learner answered <em>correctly</em>.</li>
  <li>The driver moved <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The baby slept <em>peacefully</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many adverbs of manner end in <em>-ly</em>, but not all do. In <em>She runs fast</em>, <em>fast</em> is an adverb, while <em>fast</em> can also be an adjective in <em>a fast runner</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Adverbs of time</h3>
<p>Adverbs of time tell us when something happens or how long it continues.</p>
<ul>
  <li>We arrived <em>yesterday</em>.</li>
  <li>The lesson will begin <em>soon</em>.</li>
  <li>She waited <em>briefly</em>.</li>
  <li>They have <em>already</em> left.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Adverbs of place and direction</h3>
<p>These adverbs tell us where an action happens or where something moves.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Please wait <em>outside</em>.</li>
  <li>The children looked <em>everywhere</em>.</li>
  <li>He moved <em>forward</em>.</li>
  <li>The bird flew <em>away</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Adverbs of frequency</h3>
<p>Adverbs of frequency indicate how often something happens.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>always</em></li>
  <li><em>usually</em></li>
  <li><em>often</em></li>
  <li><em>sometimes</em></li>
  <li><em>rarely</em></li>
  <li><em>never</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Frequency adverbs usually come before a main verb:</p>
<p><em>She often reads in the evening.</em></p>
<p>They usually come after the verb <em>be</em> when it is the main verb:</p>
<p><em>She is often early.</em></p>
<p>With an auxiliary verb, they commonly appear after the first auxiliary:</p>
<p><em>She has never missed a lesson.</em></p>

<h3>5. Adverbs of degree</h3>
<p>Adverbs of degree indicate intensity or extent. They often modify adjectives, other adverbs or verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The water is <em>very</em> cold. — modifies an adjective.</li>
  <li>She answered <em>extremely</em> quickly. — modifies an adverb.</li>
  <li>I <em>almost</em> fell. — modifies the verb phrase.</li>
  <li>The work is <em>quite</em> difficult. — modifies an adjective.</li>
</ul>
<p>Words such as <em>very</em>, <em>too</em>, <em>almost</em>, <em>enough</em> and <em>extremely</em> can express degree, but they do not all work in exactly the same way.</p>

<h3>6. Adverbs of certainty</h3>
<p>These adverbs show how certain the speaker is about a statement.</p>
<ul>
  <li>She will <em>probably</em> arrive early.</li>
  <li>He has <em>certainly</em> improved.</li>
  <li>They will <em>possibly</em> attend.</li>
</ul>
<p>The choice of adverb changes the speaker''s level of confidence.</p>

<h3>7. Adverbs do not always end in -ly</h3>
<p>Common adverbs without <em>-ly</em> include <em>fast</em>, <em>hard</em>, <em>late</em>, <em>well</em> and <em>often</em>.</p>
<p>Be careful with pairs that have different meanings:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Hard</em> means with effort: She worked hard.</li>
  <li><em>Hardly</em> means almost not: She hardly worked.</li>
  <li><em>Late</em> means after the expected time: He arrived late.</li>
  <li><em>Lately</em> means recently: Have you seen him lately?</li>
  <li><em>Good</em> is usually an adjective: She is a good singer.</li>
  <li><em>Well</em> is often an adverb of manner: She sings well.</li>
</ul>

<h3>8. Identify what the adverb modifies</h3>
<p>Do not classify an adverb by its spelling alone. Ask what it modifies and what meaning it adds.</p>
<p>In <em>The extremely careful driver stopped suddenly</em>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Extremely</em> modifies the adjective <em>careful</em>.</li>
  <li><em>Suddenly</em> modifies the verb <em>stopped</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both are adverbs, but they perform different functions.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Types of Adverbs', 15),
  (1466, 1466, '<h2>Adverbs of Time, Place, Manner and Frequency</h2>
<p>Adverbs can add information about when an action happens, where it happens, how it happens and how often it happens. Identifying the question an adverb answers helps you understand its function.</p>

<h3>1. Adverbs of time: When?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of time tell us when an event happens, has happened or will happen. Some also communicate how long an event lasts or how soon it occurs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>We arrived <em>yesterday</em>.</li>
  <li>The visitors will arrive <em>soon</em>.</li>
  <li>She has <em>already</em> completed the assignment.</li>
  <li>They are leaving <em>now</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask <em>when?</em> to identify the time information. In the sentence <em>We arrived yesterday</em>, the word <em>yesterday</em> tells us when the arrival occurred.</p>

<h3>2. Adverbs of place: Where?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of place tell us where an action occurs or where someone or something moves.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The children are playing <em>outside</em>.</li>
  <li>Look <em>everywhere</em> for the missing book.</li>
  <li>The visitors went <em>upstairs</em>.</li>
  <li>Please come <em>here</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask <em>where?</em> or <em>in which direction?</em> to identify the information. Do not confuse an adverb of place with a prepositional phrase: <em>outside</em> is an adverb in <em>Wait outside</em>, while <em>outside the classroom</em> is a prepositional phrase.</p>

<h3>3. Adverbs of manner: How?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of manner explain how an action is performed.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The nurse spoke <em>gently</em>.</li>
  <li>The learner answered <em>correctly</em>.</li>
  <li>The mechanic worked <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The athlete ran <em>fast</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many adverbs of manner end in <em>-ly</em>, but this is not a universal rule. <em>Fast</em>, <em>hard</em> and <em>well</em> can all function as adverbs of manner.</p>
<p>Be careful with adjectives after linking verbs:</p>
<p><em>The soup smells good.</em> Here, <em>good</em> describes the soup.</p>
<p><em>The cook works well.</em> Here, <em>well</em> describes how the cook works.</p>

<h3>4. Adverbs of frequency: How often?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of frequency indicate how regularly something happens.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Always</em>: every time or on every relevant occasion.</li>
  <li><em>Usually</em>: on most occasions.</li>
  <li><em>Often</em>: many times.</li>
  <li><em>Sometimes</em>: on some occasions.</li>
  <li><em>Rarely</em>: not often.</li>
  <li><em>Never</em>: not at any time.</li>
</ul>
<p>These words express different frequencies; they are not exact numerical measurements.</p>

<h3>5. Position of frequency adverbs</h3>
<p>Frequency adverbs usually appear before the main verb:</p>
<p><em>She often visits her grandmother.</em></p>
<p>They usually appear after the verb <em>be</em>:</p>
<p><em>She is often early.</em></p>
<p>With an auxiliary verb, they commonly appear after the first auxiliary:</p>
<p><em>She has never missed a lesson.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect in ordinary neutral word order: <em>She visits often her grandmother.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>She often visits her grandmother.</em></p>

<h3>6. One sentence can contain several types</h3>
<p>Consider: <em>The team practised carefully outside yesterday.</em></p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Carefully</em> — manner: how did the team practise?</li>
  <li><em>Outside</em> — place: where did the team practise?</li>
  <li><em>Yesterday</em> — time: when did the team practise?</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>The team often practises outside</em>, <em>often</em> indicates frequency and <em>outside</em> indicates place.</p>

<h3>7. Avoid confusing adverbs with time and place phrases</h3>
<p><em>We met yesterday.</em> — <em>Yesterday</em> is an adverb of time.</p>
<p><em>We met after the lesson.</em> — <em>After the lesson</em> is a prepositional phrase expressing time.</p>
<p><em>They waited outside.</em> — <em>Outside</em> functions as an adverb of place.</p>
<p><em>They waited outside the office.</em> — <em>Outside the office</em> functions as a prepositional phrase.</p>
<p>Classify the whole expression according to its grammatical function, not merely the meaning it communicates.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adverbs of Time and Place', 15),
  (1467, 1467, '<h2>Adverbs of Time, Place, Manner and Frequency</h2>
<p>Adverbs can add information about when an action happens, where it happens, how it happens and how often it happens. Identifying the question an adverb answers helps you understand its function.</p>

<h3>1. Adverbs of time: When?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of time tell us when an event happens, has happened or will happen. Some also communicate how long an event lasts or how soon it occurs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>We arrived <em>yesterday</em>.</li>
  <li>The visitors will arrive <em>soon</em>.</li>
  <li>She has <em>already</em> completed the assignment.</li>
  <li>They are leaving <em>now</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask <em>when?</em> to identify the time information. In the sentence <em>We arrived yesterday</em>, the word <em>yesterday</em> tells us when the arrival occurred.</p>

<h3>2. Adverbs of place: Where?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of place tell us where an action occurs or where someone or something moves.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The children are playing <em>outside</em>.</li>
  <li>Look <em>everywhere</em> for the missing book.</li>
  <li>The visitors went <em>upstairs</em>.</li>
  <li>Please come <em>here</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask <em>where?</em> or <em>in which direction?</em> to identify the information. Do not confuse an adverb of place with a prepositional phrase: <em>outside</em> is an adverb in <em>Wait outside</em>, while <em>outside the classroom</em> is a prepositional phrase.</p>

<h3>3. Adverbs of manner: How?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of manner explain how an action is performed.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The nurse spoke <em>gently</em>.</li>
  <li>The learner answered <em>correctly</em>.</li>
  <li>The mechanic worked <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The athlete ran <em>fast</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many adverbs of manner end in <em>-ly</em>, but this is not a universal rule. <em>Fast</em>, <em>hard</em> and <em>well</em> can all function as adverbs of manner.</p>
<p>Be careful with adjectives after linking verbs:</p>
<p><em>The soup smells good.</em> Here, <em>good</em> describes the soup.</p>
<p><em>The cook works well.</em> Here, <em>well</em> describes how the cook works.</p>

<h3>4. Adverbs of frequency: How often?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of frequency indicate how regularly something happens.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Always</em>: every time or on every relevant occasion.</li>
  <li><em>Usually</em>: on most occasions.</li>
  <li><em>Often</em>: many times.</li>
  <li><em>Sometimes</em>: on some occasions.</li>
  <li><em>Rarely</em>: not often.</li>
  <li><em>Never</em>: not at any time.</li>
</ul>
<p>These words express different frequencies; they are not exact numerical measurements.</p>

<h3>5. Position of frequency adverbs</h3>
<p>Frequency adverbs usually appear before the main verb:</p>
<p><em>She often visits her grandmother.</em></p>
<p>They usually appear after the verb <em>be</em>:</p>
<p><em>She is often early.</em></p>
<p>With an auxiliary verb, they commonly appear after the first auxiliary:</p>
<p><em>She has never missed a lesson.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect in ordinary neutral word order: <em>She visits often her grandmother.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>She often visits her grandmother.</em></p>

<h3>6. One sentence can contain several types</h3>
<p>Consider: <em>The team practised carefully outside yesterday.</em></p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Carefully</em> — manner: how did the team practise?</li>
  <li><em>Outside</em> — place: where did the team practise?</li>
  <li><em>Yesterday</em> — time: when did the team practise?</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>The team often practises outside</em>, <em>often</em> indicates frequency and <em>outside</em> indicates place.</p>

<h3>7. Avoid confusing adverbs with time and place phrases</h3>
<p><em>We met yesterday.</em> — <em>Yesterday</em> is an adverb of time.</p>
<p><em>We met after the lesson.</em> — <em>After the lesson</em> is a prepositional phrase expressing time.</p>
<p><em>They waited outside.</em> — <em>Outside</em> functions as an adverb of place.</p>
<p><em>They waited outside the office.</em> — <em>Outside the office</em> functions as a prepositional phrase.</p>
<p>Classify the whole expression according to its grammatical function, not merely the meaning it communicates.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adverbs of Manner and Frequency', 15),
  (1468, 1468, '<h2>Adverbs of Degree</h2>
<p>An <strong>adverb of degree</strong> tells us how much, how strongly or to what extent something is true or happens. It can modify an adjective, another adverb or a verb phrase.</p>

<h3>1. Degree modifying adjectives</h3>
<p>Adverbs of degree can strengthen or limit an adjective.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The water is <em>very</em> cold.</li>
  <li>The examination was <em>extremely</em> difficult.</li>
  <li>The room is <em>fairly</em> large.</li>
  <li>The explanation was <em>slightly</em> confusing.</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>very cold</em>, the word <em>very</em> intensifies the adjective <em>cold</em>. In <em>slightly confusing</em>, <em>slightly</em> reduces the strength of the description.</p>

<h3>2. Degree modifying adverbs</h3>
<p>An adverb of degree can also modify another adverb.</p>
<ul>
  <li>She answered <em>very quickly</em>.</li>
  <li>The driver reacted <em>surprisingly slowly</em>.</li>
  <li>The learners worked <em>quite efficiently</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>very quickly</em>, <em>quickly</em> describes how she answered, while <em>very</em> indicates the degree of speed.</p>

<h3>3. Degree modifying verbs or verb phrases</h3>
<p>Some adverbs of degree modify the action or the whole verb phrase.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I <em>almost</em> fell.</li>
  <li>She <em>completely</em> forgot the appointment.</li>
  <li>They <em>hardly</em> spoke during the meeting.</li>
  <li>We <em>nearly</em> missed the bus.</li>
</ul>
<p>Placement can change the intended meaning. Compare:</p>
<p><em>Almost everyone passed.</em> — Nearly everyone passed, though not necessarily everyone.</p>
<p><em>Everyone almost passed.</em> — Each person came close to passing but did not necessarily pass.</p>

<h3>4. Very, too and enough</h3>
<p>These words do not mean the same thing.</p>
<p><strong>Very</strong> increases intensity:</p>
<p><em>The bag is very heavy.</em></p>
<p><strong>Too</strong> indicates excess, often beyond what is acceptable or possible:</p>
<p><em>The bag is too heavy for the child to carry.</em></p>
<p><strong>Enough</strong> indicates a sufficient amount or degree:</p>
<p><em>The bag is light enough for the child to carry.</em></p>
<p>Notice the positions:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Very heavy</em> — degree word before the adjective.</li>
  <li><em>Too heavy</em> — degree word before the adjective.</li>
  <li><em>Heavy enough</em> — <em>enough</em> after the adjective.</li>
  <li><em>Enough water</em> — <em>enough</em> before the noun.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. So and such</h3>
<p><strong>So</strong> usually modifies an adjective or adverb:</p>
<p><em>The road was so narrow that the bus could not pass.</em></p>
<p><em>She ran so quickly that nobody caught up with her.</em></p>
<p><strong>Such</strong> usually introduces a noun phrase:</p>
<p><em>It was such a narrow road that the bus could not pass.</em></p>
<p><em>They were such difficult questions that many learners needed extra time.</em></p>
<p>A useful contrast is <em>so difficult</em> but <em>such a difficult question</em>.</p>

<h3>6. Avoid incorrect combinations</h3>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The task was enough easy.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>The task was easy enough.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>It was so a difficult question.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>It was such a difficult question.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The room was too very hot.</em></p>
<p>Better: <em>The room was very hot.</em> or <em>The room was too hot to work in comfortably.</em></p>

<h3>7. Choose degree words carefully</h3>
<p>Some adjectives have meanings that already express an extreme degree. In formal writing, expressions such as <em>absolutely impossible</em> and <em>completely exhausted</em> are often more natural than combinations such as <em>very impossible</em>. The best choice depends on the adjective and the intended meaning.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adverbs of Degree', 15),
  (1469, 1469, '<h2>Adjective–Adverb Distinction</h2>
<p>Adjectives and adverbs can describe related ideas, but they perform different grammatical jobs. An <strong>adjective</strong> typically describes a noun or pronoun. An <strong>adverb</strong> can modify a verb, adjective, another adverb or a whole clause.</p>

<h3>1. Adjectives describe nouns</h3>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
  <li>She is a <em>careful</em> driver.</li>
  <li>That is a <em>beautiful</em> painting.</li>
  <li>The road is <em>narrow</em>.</li>
  <li>The learners seem <em>tired</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>a careful driver</em>, the adjective describes the noun <em>driver</em>. In <em>The road is narrow</em>, the adjective describes the subject <em>road</em> through the linking verb <em>is</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Adverbs describe actions or modify descriptions</h3>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
  <li>She drives <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The painting was <em>beautifully</em> displayed.</li>
  <li>The bus moved <em>slowly</em>.</li>
  <li>The learners answered <em>correctly</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>She drives carefully</em>, the adverb describes how she drives. The adverb does not describe the noun <em>she</em>.</p>

<h3>3. Linking verbs usually take adjectives as complements</h3>
<p>Linking verbs connect a subject to a description or state. Common examples include <em>be, seem, become, appear, look, feel, smell, taste</em> and <em>sound</em> when they function as linking verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The soup tastes <em>good</em>.</li>
  <li>The child looks <em>tired</em>.</li>
  <li>The music sounds <em>beautiful</em>.</li>
  <li>The fabric feels <em>soft</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The words after the linking verbs describe the soup, child, music and fabric. Adjectives are appropriate.</p>
<p>Compare with action verbs:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The chef tasted the soup <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The child looked <em>carefully</em> at the drawing.</li>
  <li>The musician played <em>beautifully</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Here, the adverbs describe actions rather than states or qualities.</p>

<h3>4. Do not assume every -ly word is an adverb</h3>
<p>Some adjectives end in <em>-ly</em>:</p>
<ul>
  <li>a <em>friendly</em> neighbour</li>
  <li>a <em>lovely</em> afternoon</li>
  <li>a <em>lively</em> discussion</li>
  <li>an <em>elderly</em> person</li>
</ul>
<p>These words describe nouns and are adjectives. English has no universal rule that every adjective becomes an adverb simply by adding <em>-ly</em>.</p>

<h3>5. Common adjective–adverb pairs</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Good / well:</strong> She is a good singer. She sings well.</li>
  <li><strong>Bad / badly:</strong> It was a bad performance. He performed badly.</li>
  <li><strong>Careful / carefully:</strong> She is careful. She checks her work carefully.</li>
  <li><strong>Quick / quickly:</strong> He is a quick learner. He learns quickly.</li>
  <li><strong>Hard / hardly:</strong> They worked hard. They hardly worked.</li>
  <li><strong>Late / lately:</strong> He arrived late. Have you seen him lately?</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Hardly</em> means almost not, and <em>lately</em> means recently. They are not simply interchangeable with <em>hard</em> and <em>late</em>.</p>

<h3>6. Avoid changing the word form blindly</h3>
<p>Incorrect: <em>She sings beautiful.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>She sings beautifully.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The soup tastes well.</em> (when describing its flavour)</p>
<p>Correct: <em>The soup tastes good.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>He completed the work careful.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>He completed the work carefully.</em></p>

<h3>7. A reliable test</h3>
<p>Ask what is being described:</p>
<ul>
  <li>If the word describes a noun or pronoun, an adjective is often needed.</li>
  <li>If it describes how an action happens, an adverb is often needed.</li>
  <li>If it follows a linking verb and describes the subject, an adjective is usually appropriate.</li>
  <li>If it modifies another adjective or adverb, use an adverb of degree where appropriate.</li>
</ul>
<p>Always examine the function in the sentence. Do not decide based only on the word''s ending.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Adjective–Adverb Distinction', 15),
  (1470, 1470, '<h2>Prepositions of Time</h2>
<p>Prepositions of time show the relationship between an event and a point or period in time. The most common choices include <strong>at, on</strong> and <strong>in</strong>, but other prepositions communicate duration, starting points, deadlines and sequence.</p>

<h3>1. At: precise times and points</h3>
<p>Use <em>at</em> for a specific time on the clock and certain established time expressions.</p>
<ul>
  <li>at 7:30 a.m.</li>
  <li>at noon</li>
  <li>at midnight</li>
  <li>at sunrise</li>
  <li>at night</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The lesson begins at eight o''clock.</em></p>
<p><em>We arrived at midnight.</em></p>

<h3>2. On: days and dates</h3>
<p>Use <em>on</em> with days, dates and particular days or parts of days.</p>
<ul>
  <li>on Monday</li>
  <li>on 12 June</li>
  <li>on my birthday</li>
  <li>on Monday morning</li>
  <li>on the first day of the term</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The examination begins on Friday.</em></p>
<p><em>We travelled on 12 June.</em></p>
<p>Do not normally use <em>on</em> before a day name when using a general expression such as <em>every Monday</em>.</p>

<h3>3. In: months, years and longer periods</h3>
<p>Use <em>in</em> for months, years, seasons and many longer periods.</p>
<ul>
  <li>in October</li>
  <li>in 2026</li>
  <li>in the dry season</li>
  <li>in the twentieth century</li>
  <li>in the morning</li>
  <li>in the afternoon</li>
  <li>in the evening</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The school opened in January.</em></p>
<p><em>We usually revise in the evening.</em></p>

<h3>4. Important exceptions and expressions</h3>
<p>English uses established expressions that must be learned as complete phrases.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>at night</em>, but <em>in the evening</em>.</li>
  <li><em>at the weekend</em> is common in British English, including Kenyan English usage; <em>on the weekend</em> is common in American English.</li>
  <li><em>in time</em> means early enough for something; <em>on time</em> means at the scheduled or expected time.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>We arrived on time for the examination.</em></p>
<p><em>We arrived in time to revise before the examination began.</em></p>

<h3>5. Since and for</h3>
<p><strong>Since</strong> identifies the starting point of an activity or state continuing to a later reference point.</p>
<p><em>She has lived here since 2022.</em></p>
<p><strong>For</strong> identifies the length of time.</p>
<p><em>She has lived here for four years.</em></p>
<p>Compare:</p>
<ul>
  <li>since Monday — a starting point.</li>
  <li>for three days — a duration.</li>
  <li>since last year — a starting point.</li>
  <li>for several months — a duration.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. By and until</h3>
<p><strong>By</strong> means no later than a stated time or deadline.</p>
<p><em>Submit your assignment by Friday.</em></p>
<p>The assignment must be submitted on Friday or earlier.</p>
<p><strong>Until</strong> indicates that an activity or state continues up to a point in time.</p>
<p><em>The library will remain open until six o''clock.</em></p>
<p>The library closes at six, and the period of opening continues up to that time.</p>

<h3>7. During, before and after</h3>
<p><strong>During</strong> identifies a period in which something happens:</p>
<p><em>Do not use your phone during the examination.</em></p>
<p><strong>Before</strong> indicates an earlier time:</p>
<p><em>Wash your hands before eating.</em></p>
<p><strong>After</strong> indicates a later time:</p>
<p><em>We discussed the results after the meeting.</em></p>
<p>Use a noun phrase after these prepositions: <em>before lunch</em>, <em>after the lesson</em>, <em>during the holiday</em>.</p>

<h3>8. Avoid common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>in Monday</em>. Correct: <em>on Monday</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>at 2026</em>. Correct: <em>in 2026</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>since three years</em>. Correct: <em>for three years</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>for 2022</em> when identifying the starting point of an ongoing state. Correct: <em>since 2022</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>Submit it until Friday</em> when Friday is a deadline. Correct: <em>Submit it by Friday</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The correct preposition depends on the time relationship being expressed, not simply on the noun that follows it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prepositions of Time', 15),
  (1471, 1471, '<h2>Prepositions of Place</h2>
<p>Prepositions of place describe the position of a person or thing in relation to another person, thing or location. The correct choice depends on the relationship being expressed.</p>

<h3>1. In: inside an area or space</h3>
<p>Use <em>in</em> when something is inside a space, enclosed area or geographical region.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The books are <em>in</em> the cupboard.</li>
  <li>The learners are <em>in</em> the classroom.</li>
  <li>Nairobi is <em>in</em> Kenya.</li>
  <li>She lives <em>in</em> Mombasa.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>In</em> presents the place as a space or area containing the person or thing.</p>

<h3>2. On: a surface or line</h3>
<p>Use <em>on</em> when something rests on a surface or is positioned along a line or route.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The book is <em>on</em> the desk.</li>
  <li>There is a picture <em>on</em> the wall.</li>
  <li>The village is <em>on</em> the main road.</li>
  <li>The notice is <em>on</em> the board.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not assume that <em>on</em> always means touching a horizontal surface. English also uses it for walls, roads and other established spatial relationships.</p>

<h3>3. At: a point or specific location</h3>
<p>Use <em>at</em> when treating a place as a point, meeting location or activity location.</p>
<ul>
  <li>We met <em>at</em> the gate.</li>
  <li>She is waiting <em>at</em> the bus stop.</li>
  <li>He is <em>at</em> school.</li>
  <li>They arrived <em>at</em> the station.</li>
</ul>
<p>Compare:</p>
<p><em>She is at the school.</em> — The school is treated as a location.</p>
<p><em>She is in the school.</em> — She is inside the school building or grounds, depending on context.</p>
<p>The distinction is about how the speaker conceptualises the location, not just its size.</p>

<h3>4. Above, over, below and under</h3>
<p>These prepositions describe vertical relationships.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Above:</strong> at a higher level than something, without necessarily being directly over it. <em>The clock is above the door.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Over:</strong> higher than, often directly above, or covering something. <em>The roof is over the room.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Below:</strong> at a lower level than something. <em>The valley lies below the hill.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Under:</strong> lower than, often directly beneath or covered by something. <em>The shoes are under the bed.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>The meanings overlap in some contexts, so choose the word that best communicates the spatial relationship.</p>

<h3>5. Between and among</h3>
<p><strong>Between</strong> commonly identifies the position separating two people or things, and it can also be used for clearly distinct individual items in a larger group.</p>
<p><em>The clinic is between the bank and the market.</em></p>
<p><em>The agreement was made between the three schools.</em></p>
<p><strong>Among</strong> usually indicates a position within or surrounded by a group.</p>
<p><em>The teacher walked among the learners.</em></p>
<p>Avoid the oversimplification that <em>between</em> can only ever refer to two items. The intended relationship determines the choice.</p>

<h3>6. Behind, in front of, beside and next to</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Behind:</strong> at the back of something. <em>The garden is behind the house.</em></li>
  <li><strong>In front of:</strong> before or ahead of something in position. <em>The bus stopped in front of the gate.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Beside:</strong> at the side of something. <em>She sat beside her sister.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Next to:</strong> immediately beside something. <em>The clinic is next to the pharmacy.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Near, opposite and inside</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Near:</strong> a short distance from something. <em>The shop is near the school.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Opposite:</strong> facing something or located across from it. <em>The bank is opposite the market.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Inside:</strong> within the interior of a place or object. <em>The keys are inside the drawer.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Outside:</strong> not inside a place or object. <em>The learners are waiting outside the hall.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>8. Distinguish prepositions from adverbs</h3>
<p>A preposition normally introduces a complement, often a noun phrase or pronoun.</p>
<p><em>The children waited outside the classroom.</em> — <em>Outside the classroom</em> is a prepositional phrase.</p>
<p><em>The children waited outside.</em> — <em>Outside</em> functions as an adverb because no complement follows it.</p>
<p>This distinction matters because the same word can perform different grammatical functions in different sentences.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prepositions of Place', 15),
  (1472, 1472, '<h2>Prepositions of Direction and Movement</h2>
<h3>1. UNDERSTANDING MOVEMENT PREPOSITIONS</h3>
<p>Prepositions of direction and movement describe how someone or something moves from one place to another.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Into / Out of:</b> Entering or leaving an enclosed space (<i>She walked into the classroom; He stepped out of the car</i>).</li>
<li><b>Onto / Off:</b> Moving to or from a surface (<i>The cat jumped onto the roof; The cup fell off the table</i>).</li>
<li><b>Across / Through:</b> Across a 2D surface (<i>swim across the river</i>) vs through a 3D volume (<i>walk through the forest</i>).</li>
<li><b>Towards / Away from:</b> In the direction of or moving further from an object (<i>She ran towards the bus</i>).</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prepositions of Direction and Movement', 15),
  (1473, 1473, '<h2>Prepositional Phrases</h2>
<h3>1. STRUCTURE OF PREPOSITIONAL PHRASES</h3>
<p>A <b>prepositional phrase</b> consists of a preposition followed by its object (a noun, pronoun, or noun phrase) and any modifying words.</p>
<p><b>Formula:</b> <code>Preposition + Modifier(s) + Object of Preposition</code></p>
<ul>
<li><i>Adjectival function:</i> The book <b>on the wooden desk</b> belongs to Grace. (Modifies ''book'')</li>
<li><i>Adverbial function:</i> We studied <b>during the evening</b>. (Modifies ''studied'' — tells when)</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prepositional Phrases', 15),
  (1474, 1474, '<h2>Coordinating Conjunctions</h2>
<h3>1. THE FANBOYS CONJUNCTIONS</h3>
<p><b>Coordinating conjunctions</b> connect words, phrases, or independent clauses of equal grammatical rank. Remember them using the acronym <b>FANBOYS</b>:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>F</b>or (Reason/Cause)</li>
<li><b>A</b>nd (Addition)</li>
<li><b>N</b>or (Negative alternative)</li>
<li><b>B</b>ut (Contrast)</li>
<li><b>O</b>r (Alternative)</li>
<li><b>Y</b>et (Surprising contrast)</li>
<li><b>S</b>o (Result/Consequence)</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Coordinating Conjunctions', 15),
  (1475, 1475, '<h2>Subordinating Conjunctions</h2>
<h3>1. JOINING MAIN AND DEPENDENT CLAUSES</h3>
<p>A <b>subordinating conjunction</b> joins an independent (main) clause to a dependent (subordinate) clause, establishing a relationship of time, reason, condition, concession, or purpose.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Time:</b> before, after, while, since, until, when, as soon as</li>
<li><b>Cause/Reason:</b> because, since, as</li>
<li><b>Condition:</b> if, unless, provided that</li>
<li><b>Concession/Contrast:</b> although, even though, whereas, while</li>
<li><b>Purpose:</b> so that, in order that</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Subordinating Conjunctions', 15),
  (1476, 1476, '<h2>Correlative Conjunctions</h2>
<h3>1. PAIRED CONJUNCTIONS</h3>
<p><b>Correlative conjunctions</b> always work in pairs to connect grammatically parallel words, phrases, or clauses.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Either ... or:</b> Choice between two alternatives (<i>Either stay or leave.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Neither ... nor:</b> Negative pairing (<i>Neither John nor his brother attended.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Both ... and:</b> Inclusive addition of two items (<i>She is both talented and humble.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Not only ... but also:</b> Emphatic addition (<i>He is not only a scholar but also an athlete.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Whether ... or:</b> Alternative possibilities (<i>Whether you agree or disagree, the decision is final.</i>)</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Correlative Conjunctions', 15),
  (1477, 1477, '<h2>Linking Words and Logical Relationships</h2>
<p>Linking words connect ideas and show how those ideas relate. They help a reader follow the reasoning in a paragraph and help a writer express relationships clearly.</p>

<h3>1. Addition</h3>
<p>Use <strong>and, also, furthermore, moreover</strong> and <strong>in addition</strong> to add information.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The library has reference books. In addition, it provides access to digital resources.</p>

<h3>2. Contrast</h3>
<p>Use <strong>but, however, although, whereas</strong> and <strong>nevertheless</strong> to show a difference or an unexpected result.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Although the road was muddy, the bus arrived on time.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The first plan was expensive. However, the second plan was affordable.</p>

<h3>3. Cause and result</h3>
<p>Use <strong>because, since</strong> and <strong>as</strong> to introduce a reason. Use <strong>so, therefore, consequently</strong> and <strong>as a result</strong> to introduce a result.</p>
<p><em>Reason:</em> The match was postponed because the field was flooded.</p>
<p><em>Result:</em> The field was flooded; therefore, the match was postponed.</p>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> Do not use <em>because</em> and <em>therefore</em> together to connect the same two clauses in one ordinary sentence: “Because the field was flooded, the match was postponed” is correct. So is “The field was flooded; therefore, the match was postponed.”</p>

<h3>4. Sequence and time</h3>
<p>Use <strong>first, next, then, afterwards, finally, before</strong> and <strong>meanwhile</strong> to show order or timing.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> First, collect the materials. Next, measure the liquid. Finally, record the result.</p>

<h3>5. Examples and clarification</h3>
<p>Use <strong>for example, for instance, such as</strong> and <strong>in other words</strong> to illustrate or clarify an idea.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Some renewable energy sources, such as wind and solar power, produce electricity without burning fossil fuels during generation.</p>

<h3>6. Purpose and condition</h3>
<p>Use <strong>so that</strong> to express purpose and <strong>if, unless</strong> or <strong>provided that</strong> to express a condition.</p>
<p><em>Purpose:</em> She labelled the containers so that everyone could identify their contents.</p>
<p><em>Condition:</em> You will understand the procedure if you follow each step carefully.</p>

<h3>How to choose the right linker</h3>
<p>Do not choose a linking word merely because it sounds formal. Identify the relationship between the ideas first. Ask: Am I adding information, contrasting ideas, giving a reason, showing a result, ordering events, providing an example, expressing purpose or setting a condition?</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using <em>however</em> as though it were an ordinary coordinating conjunction: “The road was flooded, however we continued” is incorrectly punctuated. Write “The road was flooded; however, we continued.”</li>
<li>Using a result linker when the second idea is actually a reason.</li>
<li>Adding linkers that do not express a genuine relationship, making writing awkward.</li>
<li>Repeating the same linker in every sentence instead of choosing one that fits the meaning.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Linking Words and Logical Relationships', 15),
  (1478, 1478, '<h2>Synonyms</h2>
<p><strong>Synonyms</strong> are words that have the same or nearly the same meaning in a particular context. They allow speakers and writers to express ideas precisely and avoid unnecessary repetition.</p>

<h3>1. Exact meaning depends on context</h3>
<p>Many synonyms are similar rather than completely interchangeable.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> <strong>Big</strong> and <strong>large</strong> have similar meanings: “a big house” and “a large house.”</p>
<p>However, natural usage differs: we commonly say “a big mistake,” not “a large mistake,” and “a large amount,” not usually “a big amount” in formal writing.</p>

<h3>2. Synonyms can differ in strength</h3>
<p>Words may express different degrees of the same idea.</p>
<p><strong>Annoyed → angry → furious</strong></p>
<p>These words describe increasing intensity. Replacing one with another can change the force of a sentence.</p>

<h3>3. Synonyms can differ in formality</h3>
<p><strong>Ask</strong> is neutral, <strong>request</strong> is more formal, and <strong>inquire</strong> often means to ask for information formally.</p>
<p><em>Informal:</em> Can I ask you a question?</p>
<p><em>Formal:</em> May I request further information?</p>

<h3>4. Synonyms depend on the intended meaning</h3>
<p>A word can have more than one meaning, and each meaning may require different synonyms.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “Bright light” can mean strong light. “A bright learner” means an intelligent learner. “Strong” fits the first meaning in some contexts, while “intelligent” fits the second.</p>

<h3>5. Choosing an appropriate synonym</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the whole sentence.</li>
<li>Identify the meaning the original word expresses there.</li>
<li>Consider tone, intensity and formality.</li>
<li>Replace the word and reread the sentence to check that its meaning remains appropriate.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming words listed as synonyms in a dictionary can always replace one another.</li>
<li>Choosing a word that is too strong or too weak for the situation.</li>
<li>Ignoring common word partnerships, such as “make a decision” and “heavy rain”.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Synonyms', 15),
  (1479, 1479, '<h2>Antonyms</h2>
<p><strong>Antonyms</strong> are words with opposite or contrasting meanings. They help express differences, comparisons and changes.</p>

<h3>1. Gradable opposites</h3>
<p>These describe opposite ends of a scale and allow degrees between them.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>hot/cold</strong>, <strong>easy/difficult</strong>, <strong>wide/narrow</strong>.</p>
<p>A road can be neither especially wide nor especially narrow. The opposites do not have to describe every possible state.</p>

<h3>2. Complementary opposites</h3>
<p>These are opposites in which one term generally excludes the other in the relevant context.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>alive/dead</strong>, <strong>present/absent</strong>, <strong>pass/fail</strong> in a particular examination result system.</p>
<p>Be careful: real-world usage can depend on context. For example, a person who is neither fully present nor completely absent may be described as partly attending.</p>

<h3>3. Relational opposites</h3>
<p>These describe the same relationship from opposite viewpoints.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>buy/sell</strong>, <strong>teacher/student</strong> in a teaching relationship, and <strong>lend/borrow</strong>.</p>
<p>If Kendi lends a book to Musa, Musa borrows the book from Kendi. The actions describe opposite perspectives on one transaction.</p>

<h3>4. Forming antonyms with prefixes</h3>
<p>Some words form opposites by adding prefixes such as <strong>un-</strong>, <strong>in-</strong>, <strong>im-</strong>, <strong>il-</strong>, <strong>ir-</strong> or <strong>dis-</strong>.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>kind/unkind</strong>, <strong>possible/impossible</strong>, <strong>legal/illegal</strong>, <strong>regular/irregular</strong>, <strong>agree/disagree</strong>.</p>
<p>There is no single prefix that works for every word. Learn the conventional form rather than inventing one.</p>

<h3>5. Antonyms in context</h3>
<p>Choose an opposite that matches the particular meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The opposite of <strong>generous</strong> is commonly <strong>stingy</strong> or <strong>mean</strong> when describing willingness to give. The opposite of <strong>generous</strong> in a different context may require a more precise explanation.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming every word has one exact opposite.</li>
<li>Adding a negative prefix that does not form a standard word.</li>
<li>Choosing a word that contrasts with the wrong meaning of the original word.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Antonyms', 15),
  (1480, 1480, '<h2>Homophones</h2>
<p><strong>Homophones</strong> are words that sound alike but have different meanings and, often, different spellings. Correct writing depends on recognising which word fits the sentence.</p>

<h3>1. Common homophone pairs</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>there</strong>: refers to a place or introduces a statement — “Put the bag there.”</li>
<li><strong>their</strong>: shows possession — “Their bags are outside.”</li>
<li><strong>they''re</strong>: short for “they are” — “They''re waiting outside.”</li>
<li><strong>your</strong>: shows possession — “Your book is here.”</li>
<li><strong>you''re</strong>: short for “you are” — “You''re ready.”</li>
<li><strong>to</strong>: a preposition or part of an infinitive — “Go to school”; “to read”.</li>
<li><strong>too</strong>: means “also” or “excessively” — “I came too”; “too hot”.</li>
<li><strong>two</strong>: the number 2 — “Two pupils arrived.”</li>
<li><strong>here</strong>: this place — “Come here.”</li>
<li><strong>hear</strong>: perceive sound — “I can hear music.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Use a meaning test</h3>
<p>When unsure, replace the word with its full meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “They''re late” means “They are late.” The sentence works, so <strong>they''re</strong> is correct.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “Their late” does not mean “They are late”. In that sentence, <strong>they''re</strong> is required.</p>

<h3>3. Possession is not the same as contraction</h3>
<p><strong>Your</strong> and <strong>their</strong> show possession. <strong>You''re</strong> and <strong>they''re</strong> are contractions.</p>
<p>An apostrophe in a contraction represents omitted letters. It does not automatically indicate ownership. Compare <strong>its</strong> (possession) with <strong>it''s</strong> (it is or it has).</p>

<h3>4. Proofreading homophones</h3>
<p>Spell-checkers may not detect a wrong homophone because the mistaken word is still a correctly spelt English word. Read for meaning, not spelling alone.</p>
<p>For every suspicious word, ask: Does its meaning fit this sentence? Can I replace it with its full form or definition?</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing “your welcome” instead of “you''re welcome”.</li>
<li>Writing “their coming” instead of “they''re coming”.</li>
<li>Confusing “hear” with “here”.</li>
<li>Assuming a spell-checker will detect every incorrect word choice.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homophones', 15),
  (1481, 1481, '<h2>Homonyms and Homographs</h2>
<p>Some English words share a spelling or pronunciation but have different meanings. Recognising the intended meaning requires attention to context.</p>

<h3>1. Homonyms</h3>
<p>The term <strong>homonym</strong> is often used for words that have the same spelling and pronunciation but different meanings. Terminology can vary between dictionaries and language courses, so focus on identifying the meanings accurately.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> A <strong>bat</strong> can be an animal or an object used to hit a ball.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> A <strong>bank</strong> can be a financial institution or the land beside a river.</p>

<h3>2. Homographs</h3>
<p><strong>Homographs</strong> are words written the same way but having different meanings. Some are pronounced differently.</p>
<p><strong>Lead</strong> can be pronounced /liːd/ when it means to guide, as in “lead the team”. It can be pronounced /lɛd/ when it refers to the metal, as in “a lead pipe”.</p>
<p><strong>Wind</strong> can refer to moving air, pronounced /wɪnd/, or the action of turning or twisting something, pronounced /waɪnd/.</p>

<h3>3. Context identifies the meaning</h3>
<p>Read the words around the ambiguous word. They often provide clues to the intended meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “She deposited money at the bank” points to a financial institution.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “They sat on the bank and watched the river” points to the river''s edge.</p>

<h3>4. Use the meaning in your own sentence</h3>
<p>To show that you understand a word with several meanings, write a sentence that makes the intended meaning unmistakable.</p>
<p>Weak: “I saw a bat.”</p>
<p>Clear: “A bat flew out of the cave at dusk.”</p>
<p>Clear: “The batter picked up the bat before facing the bowler.”</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming a word has only one meaning because its spelling is familiar.</li>
<li>Using the surrounding sentence incorrectly when interpreting an ambiguous word.</li>
<li>Confusing words with shared spelling with words that merely sound alike.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Homonyms and Homographs', 15),
  (1482, 1482, '<h2>Prefixes and Suffixes</h2>
<p>A <strong>prefix</strong> is a group of letters added to the beginning of a word. A <strong>suffix</strong> is a group of letters added to the end. Both can change a word''s meaning, and suffixes often change its grammatical role.</p>

<h3>1. Prefixes that change meaning</h3>
<p>Prefixes usually modify the meaning of a base word.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>un-</strong>: not or the opposite — unhappy, unfair.</li>
<li><strong>dis-</strong>: opposite, removal or reversal — disagree, disconnect.</li>
<li><strong>re-</strong>: again — rewrite, rebuild.</li>
<li><strong>mis-</strong>: wrongly or badly — misunderstand, mislead.</li>
<li><strong>pre-</strong>: before — preview, preheat.</li>
<li><strong>over-</strong>: too much or above — overcook, overconfident.</li>
<li><strong>under-</strong>: insufficiently or below — underpay, underestimate.</li>
<li><strong>im-, in-, il-, ir-</strong>: negative meaning in words such as impossible, inactive, illegal and irregular.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> To <strong>rewrite</strong> means to write again. The prefix <strong>re-</strong> adds the idea of repetition.</p>

<h3>2. Suffixes that form nouns</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>-ness</strong>: kind → kindness; dark → darkness.</li>
<li><strong>-ment</strong>: develop → development; agree → agreement.</li>
<li><strong>-tion / -sion</strong>: educate → education; decide → decision.</li>
<li><strong>-er / -or</strong>: teach → teacher; act → actor.</li>
</ul>
<p>These suffixes can form nouns that name qualities, processes, results or people.</p>

<h3>3. Suffixes that form adjectives</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>-ful</strong>: care → careful; hope → hopeful.</li>
<li><strong>-less</strong>: care → careless; hope → hopeless.</li>
<li><strong>-able / -ible</strong>: read → readable; access → accessible.</li>
<li><strong>-ous</strong>: danger → dangerous.</li>
<li><strong>-ive</strong>: attract → attractive.</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice that <strong>-ful</strong> often means having or showing something, while <strong>-less</strong> often means without it. A careful person takes care; a careless person does not take enough care.</p>

<h3>4. Suffixes that form verbs and adverbs</h3>
<p><strong>-ise / -ize</strong> can form verbs, as in modernise and organise. British English commonly uses <em>-ise</em> in many words, while both spellings are accepted for many such words depending on the chosen style.</p>
<p><strong>-en</strong> can form verbs such as widen and strengthen. <strong>-ly</strong> commonly forms adverbs from adjectives: quick → quickly; careful → carefully.</p>
<p>Not every word ending in <em>-ly</em> is an adverb: friendly and lovely are adjectives.</p>

<h3>5. Spelling changes</h3>
<p>Adding an affix may require a spelling change.</p>
<ul>
<li>happy → happiness: change <em>y</em> to <em>i</em> before <em>-ness</em>.</li>
<li>easy → easier: change <em>y</em> to <em>i</em> before <em>-er</em>.</li>
<li>hope → hopeful: retain the final <em>e</em> here.</li>
<li>use → useful: the final <em>e</em> is retained.</li>
</ul>
<p>Spelling rules depend on the word and the suffix. Check the resulting word rather than applying one rule blindly.</p>

<h3>6. Choosing the correct affix</h3>
<p>First identify the meaning you want. Then choose a recognised word form that expresses it. For example, <em>disagree</em> is a standard word, but adding any negative prefix to any verb does not automatically create a valid word.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Adding an affix that does not form a standard English word.</li>
<li>Confusing a prefix that changes meaning with a suffix that changes grammatical role.</li>
<li>Assuming every word ending in <em>-ly</em> is an adverb.</li>
<li>Ignoring spelling changes when adding a suffix.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Prefixes and Suffixes', 15),
  (1483, 1483, '<h2>Roots and Word Families</h2>
<p>A <strong>root</strong> is the central meaningful element of a word. A <strong>word family</strong> is a group of related words built from the same root or base. Understanding these relationships helps learners expand vocabulary and recognise unfamiliar words.</p>

<h3>1. Base words and related forms</h3>
<p>Consider the word family built around <strong>act</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>act</strong> — to do something or perform a role.</li>
<li><strong>action</strong> — something done; an act or process.</li>
<li><strong>active</strong> — doing things or participating.</li>
<li><strong>activity</strong> — an action or organised task.</li>
<li><strong>actor</strong> — a person who performs a role.</li>
<li><strong>react</strong> — to respond to something.</li>
</ul>
<p>The words are related, but they do not all mean the same thing. Prefixes and suffixes may change both meaning and grammatical role.</p>

<h3>2. Recognising word families</h3>
<p>A word family may include nouns, verbs, adjectives and adverbs.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>success</strong> — noun.</li>
<li><strong>succeed</strong> — verb.</li>
<li><strong>successful</strong> — adjective.</li>
<li><strong>successfully</strong> — adverb.</li>
</ul>
<p>These forms are related, but each fits different positions in a sentence.</p>

<h3>3. Roots that appear in many words</h3>
<p>Some roots appear across a large number of English words. For example, the Latin root <strong>spect</strong> relates to looking or seeing:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>inspect</strong> — examine carefully.</li>
<li><strong>spectator</strong> — a person who watches.</li>
<li><strong>respect</strong> — esteem or regard; its history is related to the root, although its modern meaning is not simply ''look again''.</li>
</ul>
<p>Roots can provide clues, but a root does not always reveal the full modern meaning. Confirm unfamiliar words using context or a dictionary.</p>

<h3>4. Use context with word structure</h3>
<p>When you meet an unfamiliar word:</p>
<ol>
<li>Look for a familiar root or base.</li>
<li>Identify any prefix or suffix.</li>
<li>Use the sentence to infer a possible meaning.</li>
<li>Check whether that meaning makes sense in the passage.</li>
<li>Verify it in a dictionary when precision matters.</li>
</ol>

<h3>5. Watch for misleading similarities</h3>
<p>Words that look similar are not necessarily members of the same word family. Similar spelling alone does not prove a shared origin or meaning. Likewise, a word may change its spelling when related forms are created: <em>decide</em>, <em>decision</em> and <em>decisive</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming every word with similar letters has the same root.</li>
<li>Assuming related words have identical meanings.</li>
<li>Using a noun where a verb or adjective is required.</li>
<li>Guessing a word''s full meaning from its root without considering context.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Roots and Word Families', 15),
  (1484, 1484, '<h2>Compound Words</h2>
<p>A <strong>compound word</strong> is formed when two or more words combine to express a particular meaning. The meaning may be closely related to the parts, or it may be more specialised.</p>

<h3>1. Closed compounds</h3>
<p>Closed compounds are written as one word.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>classroom</strong>, <strong>football</strong>, <strong>toothbrush</strong>, <strong>sunlight</strong>.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The learners left their books in the classroom.</p>

<h3>2. Open compounds</h3>
<p>Open compounds are written as separate words but function together as a unit.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>bus stop</strong>, <strong>post office</strong>, <strong>swimming pool</strong>, <strong>high school</strong>.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The bus stopped at the bus stop near the market.</p>

<h3>3. Hyphenated compounds</h3>
<p>Hyphenated compounds use a hyphen between their parts.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>mother-in-law</strong>, <strong>well-known</strong>, <strong>part-time</strong>.</p>
<p>Some compounds are hyphenated in one grammatical position but written differently in another. Follow accepted spelling and consistent style.</p>

<h3>4. Compound meaning</h3>
<p>The meaning of a compound is not always a simple sum of its parts.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> A <strong>greenhouse</strong> is a structure used for growing plants. It is not simply any house that is green.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> A <strong>blackboard</strong> is a board for writing on, although it does not have to be black.</p>

<h3>5. Compound nouns, adjectives and verbs</h3>
<p>Compounds can belong to different word classes.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Noun:</strong> classroom, bus stop, toothbrush.</li>
<li><strong>Adjective:</strong> well-known, part-time.</li>
<li><strong>Verb:</strong> to babysit, to proofread.</li>
</ul>
<p>The grammatical role depends on how the compound is used in the sentence.</p>

<h3>6. Plurals and spelling</h3>
<p>Most simple compound nouns form their plural by adding <em>-s</em> or <em>-es</em> in the usual way: classroom → classrooms; toothbrush → toothbrushes. Some compounds form the plural on the main noun within the compound, as in <strong>mothers-in-law</strong>.</p>
<p>Compound spelling is not completely predictable. When uncertain, check a reliable dictionary rather than deciding based only on pronunciation.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming every compound must be written as one word.</li>
<li>Changing the accepted spelling of an open or hyphenated compound.</li>
<li>Interpreting a compound only from the literal meanings of its parts.</li>
<li>Forming irregular compound plurals incorrectly.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Compound Words', 15),
  (1485, 1485, '<h2>Word Classes and Word Forms</h2>
<p>A word''s <strong>word class</strong> describes its grammatical role, such as noun, verb, adjective or adverb. A <strong>word form</strong> is the particular form of a word used in a sentence. The same base can appear in different classes or forms.</p>

<h3>1. Nouns</h3>
<p>Nouns name people, places, things, qualities or ideas.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The <strong>decision</strong> surprised the class.</p>
<p>Here, <em>decision</em> names an outcome or act of deciding, so it functions as a noun.</p>

<h3>2. Verbs</h3>
<p>Verbs express actions, events or states.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The committee will <strong>decide</strong> tomorrow.</p>
<p><em>Decide</em> is a verb because it expresses the action the committee will take.</p>

<h3>3. Adjectives</h3>
<p>Adjectives commonly describe nouns or pronouns.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> It was a <strong>decisive</strong> victory.</p>
<p><em>Decisive</em> describes the victory, so it functions as an adjective.</p>

<h3>4. Adverbs</h3>
<p>Adverbs can modify verbs, adjectives, other adverbs or whole clauses.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The team acted <strong>decisively</strong>.</p>
<p><em>Decisively</em> explains how the team acted, so it functions as an adverb.</p>

<h3>5. Recognising the form a sentence requires</h3>
<p>Look at the words surrounding the blank and identify the grammatical job required.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Article or determiner + noun:</strong> a decision.</li>
<li><strong>Modal verb + base verb:</strong> will decide.</li>
<li><strong>Adjective + noun:</strong> decisive action.</li>
<li><strong>Verb + adverb:</strong> act decisively.</li>
</ul>
<p>These patterns are useful clues, but grammar depends on the whole sentence. An adjective can also follow a linking verb: “The result was decisive.”</p>

<h3>6. The same word can have different classes</h3>
<p>Some words change class depending on how they are used.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “We <strong>clean</strong> the room” uses <em>clean</em> as a verb.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The room is <strong>clean</strong>” uses <em>clean</em> as an adjective.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “We need a <strong>clean</strong>” is not the usual way to express the action or condition; the sentence structure must support the intended class and meaning.</p>

<h3>7. Do not rely on endings alone</h3>
<p>Many adverbs end in <em>-ly</em>, but not all words with that ending are adverbs. <strong>Friendly</strong> and <strong>lonely</strong> are usually adjectives. Similarly, a word ending in <em>-ing</em> may be part of a verb phrase, a noun or an adjective, depending on its use.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Choosing a word because its meaning is correct but its grammatical form is wrong.</li>
<li>Assuming every word ending in -ly is an adverb.</li>
<li>Assuming a word always belongs to one word class.</li>
<li>Ignoring sentence structure when selecting a form from a word family.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Word Classes and Word Forms', 15),
  (1486, 1486, '<h2>Collocations</h2>
<p><strong>Collocations</strong> are words that commonly occur together in natural English. A sentence may be grammatically possible but sound unnatural if it uses an unusual combination of words.</p>

<h3>1. Verb + noun</h3>
<p>Some verbs naturally combine with particular nouns.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>make</strong> a decision, a mistake, an effort.</li>
<li><strong>do</strong> homework, work, research.</li>
<li><strong>take</strong> a break, a photograph, responsibility.</li>
<li><strong>pay</strong> attention, a fine, a visit.</li>
<li><strong>keep</strong> a promise, a record, a secret.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> We made a decision after discussing the evidence.</p>
<p>“Made a decision” is the conventional collocation. “Did a decision” is not standard in this context.</p>

<h3>2. Adjective + noun</h3>
<p>Some adjectives are conventionally paired with particular nouns.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>heavy rain</strong></li>
<li><strong>strong tea</strong></li>
<li><strong>bright future</strong></li>
<li><strong>serious problem</strong></li>
<li><strong>deep sleep</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> Heavy rain flooded several roads.</p>
<p>English speakers do not freely substitute every adjective with a similar meaning. “Strong rain” may be understood, but “heavy rain” is the usual collocation.</p>

<h3>3. Adverb + adjective</h3>
<p>Some adverbs naturally intensify particular adjectives.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>deeply concerned</strong></li>
<li><strong>highly successful</strong></li>
<li><strong>perfectly clear</strong></li>
<li><strong>closely related</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> The two ideas are closely related.</p>

<h3>4. Verb + adverb</h3>
<p>Some combinations are also conventional: <strong>apologise sincerely</strong>, <strong>whisper softly</strong>, <strong>strongly recommend</strong>.</p>
<p>Choose combinations that express the intended meaning and sound natural in the context.</p>

<h3>5. Collocations and grammar</h3>
<p>Collocations do not remove the need for correct grammar. The verb must still agree with its subject, and its tense must suit the sentence.</p>
<p><em>Present:</em> She makes a decision.</p>
<p><em>Past:</em> She made a decision.</p>
<p><em>Future:</em> She will make a decision.</p>

<h3>6. How to learn collocations</h3>
<ol>
<li>Learn a useful phrase rather than an isolated word.</li>
<li>Notice which words occur together in reliable reading and listening materials.</li>
<li>Write your own sentence using the phrase.</li>
<li>Check whether the phrase fits the meaning and level of formality.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Translating word-for-word from another language when English uses a different combination.</li>
<li>Replacing one word in a familiar phrase with a near-synonym that does not collocate naturally.</li>
<li>Assuming that every possible grammatical combination is equally idiomatic.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Collocations', 15),
  (1487, 1487, '<h2>Phrasal Verbs</h2>
<p>A <strong>phrasal verb</strong> combines a verb with one or more particles, usually an adverb or preposition. The combination may have a meaning that differs from the meaning of the verb alone.</p>

<h3>1. Common phrasal verbs</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>give up</strong> — stop trying or quit: “She refused to give up.”</li>
<li><strong>find out</strong> — discover information: “We found out the cause.”</li>
<li><strong>carry on</strong> — continue: “Carry on with your work.”</li>
<li><strong>look after</strong> — take care of: “He looks after his younger brother.”</li>
<li><strong>put off</strong> — postpone: “They put off the meeting.”</li>
<li><strong>turn down</strong> — reject or reduce the level of something: “She turned down the offer.”</li>
<li><strong>run out of</strong> — use all of a supply: “We ran out of water.”</li>
<li><strong>bring up</strong> — mention a subject or raise a child: “He brought up an important point.”</li>
<li><strong>work out</strong> — solve, calculate or develop successfully: “We worked out the answer.”</li>
<li><strong>set off</strong> — begin a journey or trigger something: “They set off at dawn.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Phrasal verbs can have several meanings</h3>
<p>The surrounding sentence determines which meaning is intended.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “Turn down the radio” means reduce its volume. “Turn down the invitation” means reject it.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “Bring up the issue” means mention it. “Bring up a child” means raise the child.</p>

<h3>3. Separable phrasal verbs</h3>
<p>With some phrasal verbs, the object can appear between the verb and particle or after the particle.</p>
<p><em>Both correct:</em> “She turned down the offer.” / “She turned the offer down.”</p>
<p>When the object is a pronoun, it must normally go between the verb and particle in this pattern.</p>
<p><em>Correct:</em> “She turned it down.”</p>
<p><em>Incorrect:</em> “She turned down it.”</p>

<h3>4. Inseparable phrasal verbs</h3>
<p>Some phrasal verbs do not allow the object to be placed between the verb and particle.</p>
<p><em>Correct:</em> “He looks after his sister.”</p>
<p><em>Incorrect:</em> “He looks his sister after.”</p>
<p>Learn the pattern of each phrasal verb rather than assuming all of them behave alike.</p>

<h3>5. Phrasal verbs with no object</h3>
<p>Some phrasal verbs do not take a direct object.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The plane took off.”</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The children grew up.”</p>

<h3>6. Choosing a phrasal verb</h3>
<p>In many contexts, a phrasal verb has a more formal single-word alternative: <em>find out</em> can mean discover, <em>put off</em> can mean postpone, and <em>carry on</em> can mean continue. Neither form is always better; choose the expression that fits the audience and context.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Guessing the meaning from the verb alone.</li>
<li>Separating an inseparable phrasal verb.</li>
<li>Putting a pronoun after the particle in a separable phrasal verb.</li>
<li>Ignoring context when one phrasal verb has several meanings.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Phrasal Verbs', 15),
  (1488, 1488, '<h2>Idioms</h2>
<p>An <strong>idiom</strong> is an established expression whose intended meaning cannot always be worked out from the ordinary meanings of its individual words. Idioms are common in conversation, stories, speeches and informal writing.</p>

<h3>1. Recognising idiomatic meaning</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Break the ice</strong> — make people feel more comfortable at the beginning of an interaction.</li>
<li><strong>Once in a blue moon</strong> — very rarely.</li>
<li><strong>Spill the beans</strong> — reveal information, especially a secret.</li>
<li><strong>Under the weather</strong> — feeling unwell.</li>
<li><strong>A piece of cake</strong> — something very easy to do.</li>
<li><strong>Hit the nail on the head</strong> — identify or explain something exactly.</li>
<li><strong>Burn the midnight oil</strong> — work or study late into the night.</li>
<li><strong>Cost an arm and a leg</strong> — be very expensive.</li>
<li><strong>Let the cat out of the bag</strong> — reveal a secret, often accidentally.</li>
<li><strong>Keep an eye on</strong> — watch or monitor something carefully.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Use context to infer meaning</h3>
<p>Consider the situation and what the speaker is trying to communicate.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The first question was a piece of cake, but the final question was difficult.” The contrast with “difficult” shows that <em>a piece of cake</em> means very easy.</p>

<h3>3. Idioms and literal language</h3>
<p>An expression may have both a literal and an idiomatic interpretation.</p>
<p><em>Literal:</em> The cat escaped from the bag.</p>
<p><em>Idiomatic:</em> She let the cat out of the bag by revealing the surprise party.</p>
<p>In the second sentence, no actual cat is involved; the expression refers to revealing a secret.</p>

<h3>4. Idioms and audience</h3>
<p>Idioms can make speech vivid and natural, but not every audience will understand every expression. In formal reports, technical explanations or instructions, direct wording may be clearer.</p>
<p><em>Idiomatic:</em> We need to get the ball rolling.</p>
<p><em>Direct:</em> We need to begin the activity.</p>

<h3>5. Use idioms accurately</h3>
<p>Learn the complete expression, its meaning and the situations in which it is appropriate. Some idioms allow changes in tense or pronouns, but their wording and grammar still follow conventional patterns.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Interpreting an idiom literally when the context is figurative.</li>
<li>Changing the words of an idiom so much that it no longer sounds natural.</li>
<li>Using an informal idiom in a context that requires precise, formal language.</li>
<li>Assuming every colourful expression is appropriate for every audience.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Idioms and Expressions', 15),
  (1489, 1489, '<h2>Commonly Confused Words</h2>
<p>Some English words are frequently confused because they sound alike, look similar or have related meanings. The best way to distinguish them is to identify the meaning and grammatical role required by the sentence.</p>

<h3>1. Their, there and they''re</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Their</strong> shows possession: “Their classroom is clean.”</li>
<li><strong>There</strong> refers to a place or introduces an expression: “Put the books there.” / “There are three books.”</li>
<li><strong>They''re</strong> means “they are”: “They''re ready.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Your and you''re</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Your</strong> shows possession: “Your answer is correct.”</li>
<li><strong>You''re</strong> means “you are”: “You''re correct.”</li>
</ul>
<p>Use the substitution test: if “you are” fits, write <em>you''re</em>; if the sentence identifies something belonging to the listener, write <em>your</em>.</p>

<h3>3. Its and it''s</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Its</strong> is possessive: “The dog wagged its tail.”</li>
<li><strong>It''s</strong> means “it is” or “it has”: “It''s raining.” / “It''s been a long day.”</li>
</ul>
<p>The apostrophe in <em>it''s</em> marks a contraction, not possession.</p>

<h3>4. Then and than</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Then</strong> refers to time, sequence or a consequence: “We ate, then we left.”</li>
<li><strong>Than</strong> is commonly used in comparisons: “This route is shorter than the other one.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Affect and effect</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Affect</strong> is usually a verb meaning to influence: “Lack of sleep can affect concentration.”</li>
<li><strong>Effect</strong> is usually a noun meaning a result: “The effect was noticeable.”</li>
</ul>
<p>These are common patterns, not absolute rules. <em>Effect</em> can also be a verb meaning to bring about, as in “effect a change”, and <em>affect</em> has specialised uses as a noun.</p>

<h3>6. Advice and advise</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Advice</strong> is a noun: “Her advice was helpful.” It is normally uncountable in standard English.</li>
<li><strong>Advise</strong> is a verb: “I advise you to revise regularly.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Lose and loose</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lose</strong> is a verb meaning to misplace something or fail to keep or win it: “Do not lose your notes.”</li>
<li><strong>Loose</strong> is usually an adjective meaning not firmly attached or not tight: “The screw is loose.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>8. Quiet, quite and quit</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Quiet</strong> means making little noise: “The room is quiet.”</li>
<li><strong>Quite</strong> means fairly, rather or completely, depending on context: “The question was quite difficult.”</li>
<li><strong>Quit</strong> means stop or leave: “He decided to quit the activity.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>9. Principal and principle</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Principal</strong> can mean the head of a school or the main person or thing: “The principal addressed the learners.”</li>
<li><strong>Principle</strong> means a rule, belief or fundamental idea: “Honesty is an important principle.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>How to check a confusing word</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the intended meaning.</li>
<li>Identify the grammatical role required.</li>
<li>Try a substitution test where appropriate.</li>
<li>Read the entire sentence again to ensure the word fits.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Choosing a word because it sounds correct rather than checking its meaning.</li>
<li>Confusing contractions with possessive forms.</li>
<li>Assuming a common pattern has no exceptions.</li>
<li>Correcting spelling without checking whether the word fits the sentence.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Commonly Confused Words', 15),
  (1490, 1490, '<h2>Meaning in Context</h2>
<p><strong>Meaning in context</strong> is the meaning a word or expression has in the particular sentence or passage where it appears. A word may have several meanings, so choosing a definition without reading the surrounding text can lead to errors.</p>

<h3>1. One word, different meanings</h3>
<p>Consider the word <strong>light</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>“The bag is light.” — not heavy.</li>
<li>“Switch on the light.” — a source of illumination.</li>
<li>“They light the stove.” — set something burning or make it produce light.</li>
</ul>
<p>The same spelling has different grammatical roles and meanings. The sentence tells you which one is intended.</p>

<h3>2. Use surrounding clues</h3>
<p>Writers often provide clues through explanations, examples, contrasts or causes and results.</p>
<p><strong>Definition clue:</strong> “Arid, meaning extremely dry, land supports little vegetation.” The phrase after the comma explains <em>arid</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Contrast clue:</strong> “Unlike his talkative brother, Kamau was reserved and said very little.” The contrast helps explain <em>reserved</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Example clue:</strong> “Nocturnal animals, such as owls and some bats, are active at night.” The examples help explain <em>nocturnal</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Cause-and-effect clue:</strong> “The ground was slippery, so the runner moved cautiously.” The result suggests that <em>cautiously</em> means carefully to avoid danger.</p>

<h3>3. Use grammar as a clue</h3>
<p>The word class and sentence structure can narrow the meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The workers <strong>conducted</strong> an experiment.” Here, <em>conducted</em> means carried out, not led electricity or guided someone physically.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The conductor conducted electricity through the wire.” The nearby words indicate a different meaning.</p>

<h3>4. Infer without guessing wildly</h3>
<p>To infer a word''s meaning:</p>
<ol>
<li>Read the sentence containing the word.</li>
<li>Read the sentence before and after it when necessary.</li>
<li>Identify clues such as examples, contrasts, explanations and results.</li>
<li>Suggest a meaning that fits those clues.</li>
<li>Replace the unfamiliar word with your suggested meaning and check whether the passage still makes sense.</li>
</ol>

<h3>5. Distinguish a likely meaning from a proven meaning</h3>
<p>Context may narrow the possibilities without providing a complete definition. If a passage does not supply enough evidence, avoid claiming more than the text supports. A dictionary can help confirm the meaning.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using the first dictionary meaning without checking the passage.</li>
<li>Ignoring nearby contrasts or examples.</li>
<li>Confusing a literal meaning with a specialised or figurative meaning.</li>
<li>Inventing a meaning that the passage does not support.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Meaning in Context', 15),
  (1491, 1491, '<h2>Literal and Figurative Language</h2>
<p><strong>Literal language</strong> communicates the direct, ordinary meaning of words. <strong>Figurative language</strong> uses words imaginatively to create an image, comparison or effect beyond their literal meaning.</p>

<h3>1. Literal meaning</h3>
<p>Literal statements describe what is directly meant by the words.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The child opened the window.”</p>
<p>The sentence reports an action. It does not require the reader to interpret an implied comparison.</p>

<h3>2. Figurative meaning</h3>
<p>Figurative language can make a description more vivid, persuasive or memorable.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The news hit him like a hammer.”</p>
<p>The news did not physically strike him. The comparison suggests that it had a sudden and powerful emotional effect.</p>

<h3>3. Metaphorical expressions</h3>
<p>Many familiar expressions are figurative.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Time is money.</strong> Time is presented as something valuable that should not be wasted.</li>
<li><strong>She has a heart of stone.</strong> The expression suggests that she appears emotionally unfeeling.</li>
<li><strong>The classroom was a zoo.</strong> This suggests disorder or noisy activity, not that the classroom contained actual zoo animals.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Figurative language depends on context</h3>
<p>The same expression may be literal in one situation and figurative in another.</p>
<p><em>Literal:</em> “The river swallowed the small boat” could describe a boat disappearing into a river, though the verb is vivid.</p>
<p><em>Figurative:</em> “The crowd swallowed him up” suggests that the crowd surrounded or obscured him.</p>
<p>Do not label a phrase figurative merely because it sounds dramatic. Ask whether the words are intended literally or to suggest another meaning.</p>

<h3>5. Why writers use figurative language</h3>
<ul>
<li>To create vivid mental pictures.</li>
<li>To communicate an emotion or experience efficiently.</li>
<li>To emphasise an idea.</li>
<li>To make descriptions memorable.</li>
<li>To shape a reader''s attitude towards a person, event or idea.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. Interpreting figurative language</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the expression.</li>
<li>Consider its literal meaning.</li>
<li>Ask whether that literal meaning makes sense in the context.</li>
<li>Infer the idea, feeling or comparison being communicated.</li>
<li>Explain how the expression affects the reader or strengthens the message.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Taking a figurative expression literally when the context indicates otherwise.</li>
<li>Identifying a figure of speech without explaining its meaning.</li>
<li>Giving an interpretation that is not supported by the passage.</li>
<li>Assuming figurative language is always poetic; it also appears in everyday speech and persuasive writing.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Literal and Figurative Meaning', 15),
  (1492, 1492, '<h2>Denotation and Connotation</h2>
<p>Words communicate both information and associations. Understanding the difference between <strong>denotation</strong> and <strong>connotation</strong> helps a learner interpret descriptions, recognise bias and choose words carefully when writing.</p>

<h3>1. Denotation: the direct meaning</h3>
<p>The <strong>denotation</strong> of a word is its basic, conventional meaning — what it refers to or describes.</p>
<p>For example, a <strong>snake</strong> is a legless reptile. This is its basic meaning, regardless of whether the speaker likes or fears snakes.</p>
<p>Similarly, <strong>home</strong> refers to the place where someone lives.</p>

<h3>2. Connotation: the associations</h3>
<p>The <strong>connotation</strong> of a word consists of the feelings, attitudes or associations it suggests beyond its basic meaning. These associations may be positive, negative or neutral, and they can vary between people and cultures.</p>
<p>For example, <strong>home</strong> may suggest comfort, belonging, safety or family. These associations are not part of the word''s basic dictionary meaning, but they influence how a reader feels about it.</p>
<p>Calling someone a <strong>snake</strong> may suggest dishonesty or betrayal. The speaker is using the word''s negative associations rather than merely identifying an animal.</p>

<h3>3. Positive, negative and neutral connotations</h3>
<p>Words with similar denotations can create different impressions.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Confident:</strong> often suggests belief in one''s ability.</li>
<li><strong>Arrogant:</strong> suggests excessive self-importance.</li>
<li><strong>Self-assured:</strong> usually suggests confidence and composure.</li>
</ul>
<p>These words can describe related behaviour, but they do not create the same impression of the person.</p>

<h3>4. Word choice changes a description</h3>
<p>Compare these descriptions of the same person:</p>
<p><em>“The politician is persistent.”</em></p>
<p><em>“The politician is stubborn.”</em></p>
<p>Both may describe someone who refuses to change course. <strong>Persistent</strong> often presents that quality positively, while <strong>stubborn</strong> often presents it negatively.</p>
<p>The writer''s choice can influence the reader''s judgement even when the underlying behaviour is similar.</p>

<h3>5. Connotation depends on context</h3>
<p>Connotations are not fixed in every situation. A word that is insulting in one context may be affectionate or humorous in another. Consider the speaker, audience, situation and surrounding sentences before deciding what a word implies.</p>

<h3>6. How to analyse connotation</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the word and its basic meaning.</li>
<li>Consider the feelings or associations it carries in the passage.</li>
<li>Compare it with a more neutral or alternative word.</li>
<li>Explain how the choice influences the reader''s impression.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing a word''s basic meaning with the feelings associated with it.</li>
<li>Assuming every reader will respond to a word in exactly the same way.</li>
<li>Calling a word positive or negative without explaining the context.</li>
<li>Ignoring how a writer''s vocabulary may influence the reader''s opinion.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Denotation and Connotation', 15),
  (1493, 1493, '<h2>Similes</h2>
<p>A <strong>simile</strong> is a figure of speech that compares two different things, usually using <strong>like</strong> or <strong>as</strong>, to highlight a shared quality.</p>

<h3>1. How a simile works</h3>
<p>Consider: <em>“The runner was as fast as the wind.”</em></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Subject:</strong> the runner.</li>
<li><strong>Comparison:</strong> the wind.</li>
<li><strong>Shared quality:</strong> speed.</li>
</ul>
<p>The sentence does not claim that the runner literally becomes wind. It uses the comparison to emphasise speed.</p>

<h3>2. Common examples</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>As busy as a bee:</strong> very busy or active.</li>
<li><strong>Like a fish out of water:</strong> uncomfortable or out of place in an unfamiliar situation.</li>
<li><strong>As light as a feather:</strong> very light.</li>
<li><strong>Like thunder:</strong> potentially suggests a loud, powerful sound, depending on the sentence.</li>
<li><strong>As cold as ice:</strong> extremely cold, or figuratively emotionally unfeeling.</li>
</ul>
<p>Interpret each comparison using its context. A simile may describe a physical quality, an emotion, a sound or a situation.</p>

<h3>3. Identify the quality being compared</h3>
<p>Finding the word <em>like</em> or <em>as</em> is not enough. Explain what the comparison suggests.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “Her voice was like a gentle stream.”</p>
<p>The comparison may suggest that her voice was calm, smooth or soothing. The surrounding description helps establish the intended effect.</p>

<h3>4. Similes versus literal comparisons</h3>
<p>Not every sentence using <em>like</em> or <em>as</em> is a simile.</p>
<p><em>Literal:</em> “He looks like his father.” This may simply state a resemblance.</p>
<p><em>Simile:</em> “He fought like a lion.” This figuratively compares his courage or fighting spirit with a lion''s.</p>
<p>Look for an imaginative comparison that highlights a quality rather than automatically labelling every use of these words a simile.</p>

<h3>5. Explain the effect of a simile</h3>
<p>A strong interpretation explains both the comparison and its effect on the reader.</p>
<p>For example, saying “as light as a feather” means “light” is incomplete. A better explanation is that the comparison emphasises how little something weighs and helps the reader imagine its lightness.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Identifying the comparison without explaining its meaning.</li>
<li>Assuming every use of ''like'' or ''as'' creates a simile.</li>
<li>Giving an interpretation that the passage does not support.</li>
<li>Explaining the literal objects but missing the quality they share.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Similes', 15),
  (1494, 1494, '<h2>Metaphors</h2>
<p>A <strong>metaphor</strong> describes one thing as another to suggest a shared quality or relationship. Unlike a typical simile, it does not explicitly use <em>like</em> or <em>as</em> to make the comparison.</p>

<h3>1. How a metaphor works</h3>
<p>Consider: <em>“Time is a thief.”</em></p>
<p>Time is not literally a person who steals. The metaphor suggests that time takes away opportunities, youth or moments that cannot be recovered.</p>
<p>The comparison invites the reader to think about time through the idea of stealing.</p>

<h3>2. Common metaphorical expressions</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>She is the backbone of the team.</strong> She is essential to the team''s strength and stability.</li>
<li><strong>He carries a heavy burden.</strong> He has a difficult responsibility or emotional problem.</li>
<li><strong>The classroom was a furnace.</strong> The classroom was extremely hot.</li>
<li><strong>Her words were a knife.</strong> Her words caused sharp emotional pain.</li>
<li><strong>Knowledge is a key.</strong> Knowledge can open up opportunities or provide access to understanding.</li>
</ul>
<p>The context may add further meaning. Do not assume that a familiar metaphor has exactly the same effect in every passage.</p>

<h3>3. Identify the two ideas</h3>
<p>When analysing a metaphor, ask:</p>
<ol>
<li>What is being described?</li>
<li>What is it being presented as?</li>
<li>What quality do the two things share?</li>
<li>What impression or emotion does the metaphor create?</li>
</ol>
<p>For example, in <em>“The child was a ray of sunshine in the home”</em>, the child is presented as sunshine. The comparison suggests happiness, warmth or hope.</p>

<h3>4. Metaphor versus simile</h3>
<p><strong>Simile:</strong> “Her determination was like a rock.”</p>
<p><strong>Metaphor:</strong> “Her determination was a rock.”</p>
<p>Both expressions suggest firmness or stability, but the simile states an explicit comparison while the metaphor presents one thing as another.</p>

<h3>5. Metaphors can shape how readers think</h3>
<p>A metaphor does more than decorate a sentence. Describing a challenge as a <em>mountain</em> presents it as difficult to climb; describing it as a <em>puzzle</em> presents it as something that can be solved by finding the right pieces.</p>
<p>The comparison directs attention towards particular qualities and may influence how a reader interprets the situation.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Explaining only the literal objects without identifying the intended comparison.</li>
<li>Confusing a metaphor with a direct factual statement.</li>
<li>Calling every descriptive phrase a metaphor.</li>
<li>Ignoring how a metaphor shapes the reader''s impression.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Metaphors', 15),
  (1495, 1495, '<h2>Personification</h2>
<p><strong>Personification</strong> is a figure of speech that gives human qualities, feelings, actions or intentions to something non-human, such as an animal, object, natural force or abstract idea.</p>

<h3>1. Recognising personification</h3>
<p>Consider: <em>“The wind whispered through the trees.”</em></p>
<p>Wind can produce sounds, but whispering is a human action. The writer presents the wind as if it were a person speaking quietly.</p>
<p>The expression may create an impression of gentleness, secrecy or calmness.</p>

<h3>2. Common examples</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>The sun smiled down on the village.</strong> The sun is presented as smiling, creating a warm or welcoming impression.</li>
<li><strong>The angry storm attacked the coast.</strong> The storm is presented as an angry attacker, emphasising its destructive force.</li>
<li><strong>Opportunity knocked at her door.</strong> Opportunity is presented as a visitor offering a chance.</li>
<li><strong>The old house groaned in the wind.</strong> The house is described as groaning like a person in discomfort.</li>
<li><strong>Fear gripped him.</strong> Fear is presented as something capable of physically holding a person.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Explain the effect</h3>
<p>When analysing personification, identify the non-human subject, the human quality or action, and the effect created.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The city never sleeps.”</p>
<p>The city is presented as a person who remains awake. The expression suggests constant activity, movement or nightlife.</p>

<h3>4. Personification versus ordinary description</h3>
<p>Not every action involving a non-human subject is personification. A river can flow, a tree can grow, and a clock can tick without being given human qualities.</p>
<p>Compare:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Literal:</em> “The wind blew against the door.”</li>
<li><em>Personification:</em> “The wind angrily pounded on the door.”</li>
</ul>
<p>The second description presents the wind as if it had human anger and intention.</p>

<h3>5. Why writers use personification</h3>
<ul>
<li>To make descriptions vivid and memorable.</li>
<li>To create mood or atmosphere.</li>
<li>To make abstract ideas easier to imagine.</li>
<li>To communicate the strength or character of a natural event.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Identifying an action as personification when it is an ordinary non-human action.</li>
<li>Failing to identify the human quality being attributed.</li>
<li>Explaining the image without describing its effect.</li>
<li>Confusing personification with a comparison that does not give human qualities.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Personification', 15),
  (1496, 1496, '<h2>Hyperbole</h2>
<p><strong>Hyperbole</strong> is deliberate exaggeration used for emphasis, humour, frustration or emotional effect. It is not normally intended to be interpreted as a precise factual statement.</p>

<h3>1. Recognising exaggeration</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>I''ve told you a thousand times.</strong> The speaker means that something has been repeated many times, not necessarily exactly one thousand times.</li>
<li><strong>This bag weighs a ton.</strong> The speaker means the bag feels extremely heavy.</li>
<li><strong>I waited forever.</strong> The speaker means the wait felt very long.</li>
<li><strong>I''m so hungry I could eat a horse.</strong> The speaker is exaggerating to express extreme hunger.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Hyperbole communicates emotion</h3>
<p>Exaggeration often reveals how a speaker feels rather than describing a measurable fact.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The whole world was against me.”</p>
<p>This may express frustration, loneliness or the feeling of receiving no support. It does not prove that every person in the world opposed the speaker.</p>

<h3>3. Hyperbole versus an ordinary strong statement</h3>
<p>A strong statement is not automatically hyperbole.</p>
<p><em>Ordinary:</em> “The journey took five hours.”</p>
<p><em>Hyperbole:</em> “The journey took a hundred years.”</p>
<p>The second expression deliberately presents an impossible or implausible duration to emphasise how long the journey seemed.</p>

<h3>4. Hyperbole and other figures of speech</h3>
<p>Hyperbole is defined by exaggeration. A sentence may contain more than one figure of speech.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The wind was a monster that swallowed the entire town.”</p>
<p>The monster image is metaphorical, while the impossible scale of the wind swallowing an entire town may also be hyperbolic. Explain the specific effect of each feature rather than relying on a label alone.</p>

<h3>5. When hyperbole is appropriate</h3>
<p>Hyperbole can be effective in informal conversation, advertising, humour, creative writing and emotional speech. In scientific reports, official records and factual explanations, exaggerated claims can mislead readers if they are presented as facts.</p>

<h3>6. Interpreting hyperbole</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the statement that is exaggerated.</li>
<li>Explain what would be literally true.</li>
<li>Infer the feeling or idea the speaker intends to emphasise.</li>
<li>Explain the effect on the audience.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Taking an obvious exaggeration as a literal claim.</li>
<li>Calling any large number hyperbole even when it may be accurate.</li>
<li>Identifying exaggeration without explaining its intended meaning.</li>
<li>Using hyperbole in a context where readers need precise factual information.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hyperbole', 15),
  (1497, 1497, '<h2>Tone and Word Choice</h2>

  <h3>1. What is tone?</h3>
  <p><strong>Tone</strong> is the attitude a speaker or writer communicates towards a subject, a person, or an audience. We recognise tone through the words chosen, the details included, and the way ideas are expressed.</p>

  <p>For example, a writer can describe the same event in different ways:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Appreciative:</strong> "The volunteers worked tirelessly to restore the damaged classroom."</li>
    <li><strong>Critical:</strong> "The volunteers carelessly left the classroom unfinished."</li>
    <li><strong>Neutral:</strong> "The volunteers worked on the damaged classroom."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The event is similar, but the words communicate different attitudes.</p>

  <h3>2. Recognising common tones</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Optimistic:</strong> expresses hope or confidence about the future.</li>
    <li><strong>Pessimistic:</strong> expects an unfavourable outcome.</li>
    <li><strong>Critical:</strong> points out faults or weaknesses.</li>
    <li><strong>Appreciative:</strong> expresses gratitude or admiration.</li>
    <li><strong>Sarcastic:</strong> uses words to mock or criticise, often by saying the opposite of what is meant.</li>
    <li><strong>Concerned:</strong> communicates worry about a situation.</li>
    <li><strong>Persuasive:</strong> attempts to influence the audience''s beliefs or actions.</li>
    <li><strong>Neutral:</strong> presents information without clearly expressing approval or disapproval.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A passage may communicate more than one tone. Choose the description best supported by the evidence rather than forcing every passage into one category.</p>

  <h3>3. How word choice changes meaning</h3>
  <p>Words with similar dictionary meanings can carry different emotional associations.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Confident" suggests belief in one''s ability.</li>
    <li>"Arrogant" suggests excessive self-importance.</li>
    <li>"Economical" often suggests avoiding waste.</li>
    <li>"Stingy" suggests an unwillingness to spend or share.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These differences are important because a writer''s choice can influence how readers judge a person or event.</p>

  <h3>4. How to identify tone accurately</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify what the writer is discussing.</li>
    <li>Notice words that express judgement, emotion, praise, blame, or uncertainty.</li>
    <li>Ask what attitude those words communicate in context.</li>
    <li>Support your interpretation with a quotation or specific detail from the passage.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>5. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not identify tone using only the topic. A passage about a disaster may be factual, sympathetic, critical, or hopeful. The topic alone does not determine the tone.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tone and Word Choice', 15),
  (1498, 1498, '<h2>Ambiguity and Multiple Meanings</h2>

  <h3>1. What is ambiguity?</h3>
  <p><strong>Ambiguity</strong> occurs when a word, phrase, or sentence can reasonably be understood in more than one way. The reader may need additional context to determine the intended meaning.</p>

  <p>Consider: <em>"I saw the man with the telescope."</em></p>
  <p>This could mean that I used a telescope to see the man, or that the man had a telescope. The sentence does not make the intended relationship clear.</p>

  <h3>2. Words with multiple meanings</h3>
  <p>Some individual words have several meanings. The surrounding words and situation usually help identify the intended one.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bank:</strong> a financial institution, or the land beside a river.</li>
    <li><strong>Bat:</strong> a flying mammal, or equipment used in a game such as cricket.</li>
    <li><strong>Light:</strong> illumination, or something that is not heavy.</li>
    <li><strong>File:</strong> a collection of documents, or a tool used to smooth a surface.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For example, in "The batter struck the ball with a bat", the sporting context identifies the intended meaning of <em>bat</em>.</p>

  <h3>3. Types of ambiguity</h3>
  <p><strong>Lexical ambiguity</strong> arises when a word has more than one possible meaning.</p>
  <p>Example: "She sat beside the bank." Without further context, the word <em>bank</em> could refer to a financial institution or a riverbank.</p>

  <p><strong>Structural ambiguity</strong> arises when a sentence''s grammatical arrangement allows more than one interpretation.</p>
  <p>Example: "The teacher discussed the project with the learner."</p>
  <p>This normally means the teacher and learner discussed the project together, but in a different context, the phrase may be understood as identifying a project associated with the learner.</p>

  <h3>4. How context resolves meaning</h3>
  <p>Context includes the surrounding words, the topic, the situation, and what the writer has already explained.</p>
  <p>Compare:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"The fisherman sat on the bank and watched the water." Here, <em>bank</em> means the land beside a river.</li>
    <li>"She deposited her salary at the bank." Here, <em>bank</em> means a financial institution.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5. How to remove ambiguity</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the word or phrase that permits multiple interpretations.</li>
    <li>State the different meanings a reader could reasonably infer.</li>
    <li>Use context to determine the intended meaning, if enough information exists.</li>
    <li>If necessary, rewrite the sentence to make the intended meaning explicit.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>For example, replace "I saw the man with the telescope" with either "Using the telescope, I saw the man" or "I saw the man who was carrying a telescope."</p>

  <h3>6. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Not every word with several dictionary meanings makes a sentence ambiguous. If context clearly establishes the intended meaning, the sentence may be perfectly clear. Ambiguity exists when multiple interpretations remain reasonably possible in that context.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Ambiguity and Multiple Meanings', 15),
  (1499, 1499, '<h2>Capitalisation</h2>

  <h3>1. What is capitalisation?</h3>
  <p><strong>Capitalisation</strong> is the use of capital letters where English writing requires them. Correct capitalisation helps readers recognise sentence beginnings, names, titles, and abbreviations.</p>

  <h3>2. Capital letters at the beginning of sentences</h3>
  <p>The first word of a sentence begins with a capital letter.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incorrect: <em>the learners arrived early.</em></li>
    <li>Correct: <em>The learners arrived early.</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>A new sentence also begins with a capital letter after a full stop, question mark, or exclamation mark when the next word starts a new sentence.</p>

  <h3>3. Names of people and places</h3>
  <p>Use capital letters for proper nouns: the specific names of people, places, institutions, and organisations.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>Wanjiku</em>, <em>Kenya</em>, <em>Nairobi</em></li>
    <li><em>Kenyatta University</em></li>
    <li><em>Lake Victoria</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Common nouns are not normally capitalised unless they begin a sentence or form part of an official name.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>We visited a <em>university</em>.</li>
    <li>We visited <em>Kenyatta University</em>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Days, months, languages, and nationalities</h3>
  <p>Capitalise the names of days, months, languages, and nationalities.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>Monday</em>, <em>October</em>, <em>English</em>, <em>Kiswahili</em>, <em>Kenyan</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Seasons such as <em>summer</em> and <em>winter</em> are generally lowercase in ordinary English writing.</p>

  <h3>5. The pronoun ''I''</h3>
  <p>The first-person singular pronoun <em>I</em> is always capitalised, even in the middle of a sentence.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incorrect: <em>My brother and i went home.</em></li>
    <li>Correct: <em>My brother and I went home.</em></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. Titles and abbreviations</h3>
  <p>Capitals are used in personal titles before names, such as <em>Dr Mwangi</em> and <em>Professor Achieng</em>. When a title is used generally, it is usually lowercase: <em>The professor addressed the class.</em></p>
  <p>Many abbreviations and initialisms use capital letters, for example <em>KICD</em> and <em>KCSE</em>. Follow the established spelling of the particular name or abbreviation.</p>

  <h3>7. Editing a sentence</h3>
  <p>Read a sentence and check each capital letter against a rule. Do not capitalise ordinary words merely because they seem important.</p>
  <p>Incorrect: <em>last monday, my Friend and i travelled to nakuru to practise english.</em></p>
  <p>Correct: <em>Last Monday, my friend and I travelled to Nakuru to practise English.</em></p>

  <h3>8. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>In English, not every important-looking noun needs a capital letter. Common nouns such as <em>teacher</em>, <em>school</em>, and <em>river</em> normally remain lowercase unless a specific naming or sentence-position rule applies.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Capitalisation', 15),
  (1500, 1500, '<h2>Common Spelling Patterns</h2>

  <h3>1. Why spelling patterns matter</h3>
  <p>English spelling is not perfectly predictable, but recurring patterns help writers spell many words accurately. A pattern is a useful guide, not a guarantee that every word follows the same rule.</p>

  <h3>2. The silent-e pattern</h3>
  <p>In many words, a final silent <em>e</em> changes the sound of the preceding vowel.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>cap → cape</em></li>
    <li><em>kit → kite</em></li>
    <li><em>hop → hope</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>The final <em>e</em> is not pronounced in these words. However, English has exceptions, so do not apply this pattern blindly.</p>

  <h3>3. Doubling a final consonant</h3>
  <p>When adding a vowel-starting suffix such as <em>-ing</em> or <em>-ed</em>, some short words double their final consonant.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>run → running</em></li>
    <li><em>stop → stopped</em></li>
    <li><em>plan → planning</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>This commonly happens when a one-syllable word ends in a consonant–vowel–consonant pattern, as in <em>run</em> and <em>stop</em>. The final consonant is usually not doubled after two vowels or two consonants, as in <em>rain → raining</em> and <em>help → helping</em>.</p>

  <h3>4. Dropping final ''e''</h3>
  <p>When a word ends in silent <em>e</em> and a suffix beginning with a vowel is added, the <em>e</em> is often dropped.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>hope → hoping</em></li>
    <li><em>write → writing</em></li>
    <li><em>make → making</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>With suffixes beginning with a consonant, the final <em>e</em> is often retained: <em>hopeful</em>, <em>careless</em>. There are exceptions, including <em>true → truly</em>.</p>

  <h3>5. Changing ''y'' to ''i''</h3>
  <p>When a word ends in consonant + <em>y</em>, the <em>y</em> often changes to <em>i</em> before a suffix such as <em>-es</em> or <em>-ed</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>city → cities</em></li>
    <li><em>try → tried</em></li>
    <li><em>happy → happier</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Before <em>-ing</em>, the <em>y</em> is usually retained: <em>try → trying</em>. When a word ends in vowel + <em>y</em>, the <em>y</em> is normally retained: <em>play → played</em>.</p>

  <h3>6. ''ie'' and ''ei''</h3>
  <p>Some words use <em>ie</em>, as in <em>believe</em>, and others use <em>ei</em>, as in <em>receive</em>. The familiar guideline ''i before e except after c'' can help with some words, but it has many exceptions. Learn common spellings and check unfamiliar words rather than treating the guideline as universal.</p>

  <h3>7. British and American spelling</h3>
  <p>English has accepted spelling varieties. Kenyan school English generally follows British English conventions.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>British: <em>colour</em>; American: <em>color</em></li>
    <li>British: <em>centre</em>; American: <em>center</em></li>
    <li>British: <em>travelling</em>; American: <em>traveling</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Choose the required variety and use it consistently within a piece of writing.</p>

  <h3>8. A reliable spelling check</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the base word.</li>
    <li>Identify the suffix or ending being added.</li>
    <li>Apply the relevant pattern.</li>
    <li>Check for exceptions or accepted spelling variants.</li>
    <li>Read the completed word in context.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>9. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not assume that one spelling rule explains every word. Patterns help you make informed choices, but irregular spellings and exceptions still need to be recognised.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Common Spelling Patterns', 15);
INSERT INTO public.lessons (id, topic_id, content, summary, estimated_minutes) VALUES
  (1501, 1501, '<h2>Silent Letters</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a silent letter?</h3>
  <p>A <strong>silent letter</strong> is a letter that appears in a word''s spelling but is not pronounced in that word. Silent letters are part of English spelling history and must often be learned through word patterns and practice.</p>

  <h3>2. Silent ''k''</h3>
  <p>The letter <em>k</em> is silent in many words beginning with <em>kn-</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>know</em></li>
    <li><em>knee</em></li>
    <li><em>knife</em></li>
    <li><em>knock</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>In these words, pronunciation begins with the sound represented by <em>n</em>, not a separate <em>k</em> sound.</p>

  <h3>3. Silent ''w''</h3>
  <p>The letter <em>w</em> is silent in many words beginning with <em>wr-</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>write</em></li>
    <li><em>wrong</em></li>
    <li><em>wrist</em></li>
    <li><em>wrap</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>These words begin with the sound represented by <em>r</em>.</p>

  <h3>4. Silent ''b''</h3>
  <p>The letter <em>b</em> is silent in some words, particularly after <em>m</em> at the end of a word.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>lamb</em></li>
    <li><em>thumb</em></li>
    <li><em>comb</em></li>
    <li><em>climb</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>The final <em>b</em> is not pronounced in these examples. However, <em>b</em> is pronounced in words such as <em>number</em> and <em>habit</em>.</p>

  <h3>5. Silent ''l''</h3>
  <p>The letter <em>l</em> is silent in several common words.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>walk</em></li>
    <li><em>talk</em></li>
    <li><em>half</em></li>
    <li><em>could</em></li>
    <li><em>should</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Notice that a spelling pattern can occur in several words, but pronunciation should still be checked word by word.</p>

  <h3>6. Silent ''gh''</h3>
  <p>The letter combination <em>gh</em> is silent in words such as:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>night</em></li>
    <li><em>light</em></li>
    <li><em>daughter</em></li>
    <li><em>though</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>However, <em>gh</em> is pronounced differently in some words, such as <em>laugh</em>. Do not assume that every occurrence of <em>gh</em> is silent.</p>

  <h3>7. Silent ''t''</h3>
  <p>The letter <em>t</em> is silent in words such as:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>listen</em></li>
    <li><em>castle</em></li>
    <li><em>whistle</em></li>
    <li><em>fasten</em></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>8. How to learn silent letters</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Look at the spelling of the word.</li>
    <li>Say the word naturally rather than pronouncing every letter separately.</li>
    <li>Identify which written letter or letter combination is not heard.</li>
    <li>Compare the word with other examples of the same pattern.</li>
    <li>Practise the word in a sentence so its spelling and pronunciation become familiar.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>9. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse a silent letter with a letter that represents a sound different from its usual sound. A silent letter is not pronounced at all in that word. Also, pronunciation can vary by accent, so use the pronunciation expected in the variety of English you are learning.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Silent Letters', 15),
  (1502, 1502, '<h2>Confused Spellings</h2>

  <h3>1. Why these words cause mistakes</h3>
  <p>Some English words sound alike, look alike, or differ by only one or two letters. Their meanings and grammatical roles may be different. To choose the correct word, examine what the sentence means and how the word functions.</p>

  <h3>2. Their, there and they''re</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Their</strong> shows possession: "The learners collected their books."</li>
    <li><strong>There</strong> refers to a place or introduces a statement: "Put the bag there." "There are three books."</li>
    <li><strong>They''re</strong> means "they are": "They''re ready for the examination."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Test the sentence by replacing <em>they''re</em> with <em>they are</em>. If the sentence no longer makes sense, you probably need another word.</p>

  <h3>3. Your and you''re</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your</strong> shows possession: "Your answer is correct."</li>
    <li><strong>You''re</strong> means "you are": "You''re improving."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Remember that the apostrophe in <em>you''re</em> marks omitted letters. It does not show possession.</p>

  <h3>4. Its and it''s</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Its</strong> shows possession: "The dog wagged its tail."</li>
    <li><strong>It''s</strong> means "it is" or "it has": "It''s raining." "It''s been a long day."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This pair is especially important because possessive <em>its</em> does not use an apostrophe, unlike many possessive nouns.</p>

  <h3>5. Then and than</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Then</strong> refers to time, sequence, or a consequence: "Finish your work, then submit it."</li>
    <li><strong>Than</strong> is used in comparisons: "This exercise is harder than the previous one."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. Affect and effect</h3>
  <p><strong>Affect</strong> is usually a verb meaning to influence. <strong>Effect</strong> is usually a noun meaning a result.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Lack of sleep can affect concentration."</li>
    <li>"One effect of lack of sleep is poor concentration."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are useful general rules, although both words have less common uses in other grammatical roles.</p>

  <h3>7. Lose and loose</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lose</strong> is a verb meaning to misplace something or fail to win: "Do not lose your notes."</li>
    <li><strong>Loose</strong> is an adjective meaning not firmly attached or not tight: "The screw is loose."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>8. Quiet, quite and quit</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Quiet:</strong> making little noise. "The room was quiet."</li>
    <li><strong>Quite:</strong> to a considerable degree, or fairly, depending on context. "The test was quite difficult."</li>
    <li><strong>Quit:</strong> to stop doing something. "She decided to quit the activity."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>9. A reliable correction method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the complete sentence for meaning.</li>
    <li>Identify the grammatical role required.</li>
    <li>Substitute a simple definition or expansion, such as "they are".</li>
    <li>Choose the word that fits both the meaning and grammar.</li>
    <li>Read the corrected sentence again.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>10. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not select a word simply because it sounds right. When words sound alike, meaning and grammatical role are more reliable than pronunciation.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Commonly Confused Spellings', 15),
  (1503, 1503, '<h2>Full Stops and Question Marks</h2>

  <h3>1. What punctuation does</h3>
  <p>Punctuation helps readers recognise sentence boundaries and understand how statements and questions are intended to work. A full stop and a question mark perform different jobs.</p>

  <h3>2. Full stops</h3>
  <p>A <strong>full stop</strong> (.) normally ends a complete statement.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"The lesson begins at eight o''clock."</li>
    <li>"We revised the topic carefully."</li>
    <li>"The learners completed their work."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A full stop also ends many commands written as ordinary instructions: "Close the door." Use an exclamation mark instead when a strong emotion or forceful exclamation is intended.</p>

  <h3>3. Question marks</h3>
  <p>A <strong>question mark</strong> (?) normally ends a direct question.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Where is your exercise book?"</li>
    <li>"Have you completed the assignment?"</li>
    <li>"Why did the experiment fail?"</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Questions can begin with words such as <em>who</em>, <em>what</em>, <em>where</em>, <em>when</em>, <em>why</em>, and <em>how</em>, or with an auxiliary verb such as <em>is</em>, <em>do</em>, or <em>have</em>.</p>

  <h3>4. Direct and indirect questions</h3>
  <p>A direct question asks something directly and normally ends with a question mark.</p>
  <p>Direct: "Where is the library?"</p>
  <p>An indirect question reports or embeds a question within a statement. It does not automatically take a question mark.</p>
  <p>Indirect: "She asked where the library was."</p>
  <p>Compare: "Can you tell me where the library is?" This whole sentence is a direct question, so it ends with a question mark.</p>

  <h3>5. Do not use punctuation to hide an incomplete sentence</h3>
  <p>A full stop does not turn an incomplete thought into a complete sentence.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incomplete: "Because the rain was heavy."</li>
    <li>Complete: "Because the rain was heavy, the match was postponed."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The first example is a subordinate clause without the main clause needed to complete its meaning in ordinary formal writing.</p>

  <h3>6. Avoid repeated punctuation</h3>
  <p>In formal school writing, use one appropriate question mark or full stop rather than strings such as "What happened??" or "The lesson is over...."</p>

  <h3>7. A practical check</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify whether the sentence makes a statement, asks a direct question, or gives an instruction.</li>
    <li>Check that the sentence expresses a complete thought where required.</li>
    <li>Choose the correct ending punctuation.</li>
    <li>Read the sentence aloud to check whether its structure matches its purpose.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>8. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>A sentence that contains a question word is not always a direct question. For example, "I know why she left." is a statement and ends with a full stop.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Full Stops and Question Marks', 15),
  (1504, 1504, '<h2>Commas</h2>

  <h3>1. What a comma does</h3>
  <p>A <strong>comma</strong> (,) separates certain parts of a sentence to make its structure and meaning easier to follow. It marks a smaller boundary than a full stop; it does not normally separate two complete sentences by itself.</p>

  <h3>2. Commas in lists</h3>
  <p>Use commas to separate items in a list of three or more.</p>
  <p>Example: "We packed books, pens, rulers and exercise books."</p>
  <p>A comma before the final item, called the Oxford comma, is optional in many styles. It can be useful when it prevents confusion. Follow the required style consistently.</p>

  <h3>3. Commas after introductory elements</h3>
  <p>A comma often follows an introductory word, phrase, or subordinate clause.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"After the lesson, the learners went home."</li>
    <li>"However, the experiment produced a different result."</li>
    <li>"When the rain stopped, the match resumed."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The comma helps the reader identify where the introductory element ends and the main clause begins.</p>

  <h3>4. Commas in direct address</h3>
  <p>Use commas to separate the name or title of the person being addressed.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Amina, please read the passage."</li>
    <li>"Please read the passage, Amina."</li>
    <li>"Please, Amina, read the passage carefully."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Without commas, direct address can become confusing or change how a sentence is interpreted.</p>

  <h3>5. Commas around extra information</h3>
  <p>Use paired commas around non-essential information that can be removed without destroying the sentence''s basic identification of the person or thing.</p>
  <p>"Kisumu, which lies beside Lake Victoria, attracts many visitors."</p>
  <p>The phrase "which lies beside Lake Victoria" adds information but is not necessary to identify Kisumu in this sentence.</p>
  <p>Do not automatically place commas around information that identifies which person or thing is meant.</p>
  <p>"Learners who revise regularly often improve."</p>
  <p>Here, "who revise regularly" identifies the group being discussed, so commas are not normally used.</p>

  <h3>6. Commas between adjectives</h3>
  <p>Commas can separate coordinate adjectives—adjectives that independently describe the same noun.</p>
  <p>"It was a difficult, exhausting journey."</p>
  <p>A useful test is whether you can insert <em>and</em> between the adjectives or reverse their order naturally. Do not use a comma automatically between every pair of adjectives.</p>

  <h3>7. Commas with quotations and dates</h3>
  <p>In many British English styles, a comma may introduce direct speech or separate a reporting clause from the quotation:</p>
  <p>"The teacher said, ''Check your answers carefully.''"</p>
  <p>Commas can also separate elements of a date when the format requires them. For example, in "On 9 October 2026, the class met again", the comma separates the introductory date phrase from the main clause.</p>

  <h3>8. The comma splice</h3>
  <p>A <strong>comma splice</strong> occurs when two independent clauses are joined using only a comma.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incorrect: "The bell rang, the learners entered the classroom."</li>
    <li>Correct: "The bell rang, and the learners entered the classroom."</li>
    <li>Correct: "The bell rang. The learners entered the classroom."</li>
    <li>Correct: "The bell rang; the learners entered the classroom."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>9. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not put a comma wherever you pause while speaking. Punctuation depends on sentence structure and meaning, not only on breathing or pauses.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Commas', 15),
  (1505, 1505, '<h2>Apostrophes</h2>

  <h3>1. What an apostrophe does</h3>
  <p>An <strong>apostrophe</strong> ('') is mainly used to show possession or to mark omitted letters in contractions. It is not normally used simply to make a word plural.</p>

  <h3>2. Apostrophes in contractions</h3>
  <p>A contraction combines words by omitting one or more letters. The apostrophe marks the omission.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>do not → don''t</em></li>
    <li><em>cannot → can''t</em></li>
    <li><em>they are → they''re</em></li>
    <li><em>it is → it''s</em></li>
    <li><em>we have → we''ve</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Contractions are common in conversation and informal writing. In formal school essays and official letters, the full forms may be more appropriate, depending on the task.</p>

  <h3>3. Singular possession</h3>
  <p>Use an apostrophe followed by <em>s</em> to show possession by a singular noun, including most singular nouns ending in <em>s</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>the learner''s book</em> — the book belonging to one learner</li>
    <li><em>the teacher''s desk</em> — the desk associated with one teacher</li>
    <li><em>James''s pen</em> — the pen belonging to James</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Some style guides use different conventions for names ending in <em>s</em>; follow the required style consistently.</p>

  <h3>4. Plural possession</h3>
  <p>For regular plural nouns ending in <em>s</em>, place the apostrophe after the final <em>s</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>the learners'' books</em> — books belonging to multiple learners</li>
    <li><em>the teachers'' meeting</em> — a meeting for or involving multiple teachers</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For irregular plural nouns that do not end in <em>s</em>, add apostrophe + <em>s</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>the children''s uniforms</em></li>
    <li><em>the men''s room</em></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5. Distinguishing possession from a plural</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><em>One student''s notebook</em> — possession by one student.</li>
    <li><em>Several students'' notebooks</em> — possession by several students.</li>
    <li><em>Several students arrived</em> — plural only; no apostrophe is needed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ask two questions: Is the noun simply plural, or does it show ownership or association? If it shows possession, identify whether the possessor is singular or plural.</p>

  <h3>6. Its and it''s</h3>
  <p><strong>Its</strong> is possessive: "The school changed its timetable."</p>
  <p><strong>It''s</strong> is a contraction of <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em>: "It''s ready." "It''s been repaired."</p>
  <p>Do not add an apostrophe to <em>its</em> merely because it shows possession.</p>

  <h3>7. Apostrophes and dates or numbers</h3>
  <p>Do not normally use apostrophes to make ordinary plurals.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incorrect: <em>book''s for sale</em></li>
    <li>Correct: <em>books for sale</em></li>
    <li>Incorrect: <em>1990''s</em> when simply referring to the decade</li>
    <li>Correct: <em>1990s</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Apostrophes may appear in special cases for clarity, such as pluralising a single letter: "Mind your p''s and q''s." This is an exception, not a general plural rule.</p>

  <h3>8. A reliable editing method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Check whether letters have been omitted in a contraction.</li>
    <li>If ownership is intended, identify the possessor.</li>
    <li>Decide whether the possessor is singular, a regular plural ending in <em>s</em>, or an irregular plural.</li>
    <li>Place the apostrophe accordingly.</li>
    <li>Remove apostrophes that incorrectly mark ordinary plurals.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>9. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>An apostrophe does not mean ''more than one''. Compare <em>teacher''s</em> (one teacher possesses something), <em>teachers''</em> (multiple teachers possess something), and <em>teachers</em> (plural only).</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Apostrophes', 15),
  (1506, 1506, '<h2>Colons and Semicolons</h2>

  <h3>1. The colon and semicolon</h3>
  <p>A <strong>colon</strong> (:) often introduces an explanation, example, or list. A <strong>semicolon</strong> (;) can link closely related independent clauses or separate complex items in a list.</p>
  <p>They are not interchangeable. The punctuation should reflect the relationship between the ideas.</p>

  <h3>2. Colons before lists</h3>
  <p>A colon can introduce a list when the words before it form a complete introductory statement.</p>
  <p>Example: "Bring three items to the lesson: a ruler, a pencil and an exercise book."</p>
  <p>The words before the colon form a complete statement: "Bring three items to the lesson."</p>
  <p>Avoid placing a colon directly between a verb or preposition and the items it introduces.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incorrect: "The items are: pens, books and rulers."</li>
    <li>Better: "The items are pens, books and rulers."</li>
    <li>Also correct: "Bring the following items: pens, books and rulers."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Colons before explanations</h3>
  <p>A colon can introduce an explanation, conclusion, or detail that clarifies the preceding statement.</p>
  <p>"The result was unexpected: the plant grew faster in the shaded area."</p>
  <p>The second part explains what made the result unexpected.</p>

  <h3>4. Colons in formal writing</h3>
  <p>Colons can introduce a quotation or formal statement after a complete introductory clause, depending on the style and context.</p>
  <p>"The notice gave one instruction: ''Submit all assignments by Friday.''"</p>
  <p>They also appear in conventional formats such as times (8:30) and certain headings or labels.</p>

  <h3>5. Semicolons between independent clauses</h3>
  <p>A semicolon can join two independent clauses that are closely related in meaning.</p>
  <p>"The rain had stopped; the match resumed."</p>
  <p>Both parts can stand as complete sentences. The semicolon shows a close relationship without using a coordinating conjunction.</p>

  <h3>6. Semicolons with linking adverbs</h3>
  <p>When a linking adverb connects two independent clauses, a semicolon often comes before it and a comma after it.</p>
  <p>"The first method was unreliable; therefore, we tested another."</p>
  <p>Other linking expressions include <em>however</em>, <em>consequently</em>, and <em>nevertheless</em>.</p>

  <h3>7. Semicolons in complex lists</h3>
  <p>Use semicolons to separate list items that already contain commas, especially when each item contains a location or other internal detail.</p>
  <p>"The group visited Embu, Kenya; Arusha, Tanzania; and Kampala, Uganda."</p>
  <p>The semicolons make the boundaries between the three items clear.</p>

  <h3>8. Choosing between punctuation marks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Use a colon when the second part introduces or explains what came before.</li>
    <li>Use a semicolon to link closely related independent clauses.</li>
    <li>Use a semicolon to separate complex list items containing internal commas.</li>
    <li>Use a full stop when the ideas should be separated into distinct sentences.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>9. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>A semicolon should not normally join an independent clause to a dependent clause that cannot stand alone. A colon should not be inserted after every introductory phrase. First check the grammar of the words before the punctuation mark.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Colons and Semicolons', 15),
  (1507, 1507, '<h2>Quotation Marks</h2>

  <h3>1. What quotation marks do</h3>
  <p>Quotation marks show the exact words spoken or written by someone, or identify certain titles and special uses of words. In British English, single quotation marks are commonly used for direct speech, although double quotation marks are also accepted by many style guides. Be consistent.</p>

  <h3>2. Direct speech</h3>
  <p>Direct speech reproduces a speaker''s exact words.</p>
  <p>Example: Amina said, ''I have completed my assignment.''</p>
  <p>The reporting clause identifies the speaker, while the quoted words reproduce what the speaker said.</p>

  <h3>3. Punctuation with direct speech</h3>
  <p>When a reporting clause comes before a statement, a comma commonly introduces the quotation.</p>
  <p>''The examination begins at nine,'' the teacher said.</p>
  <p>When the reporting clause comes after the quotation, the comma usually appears inside the closing quotation mark in this style.</p>
  <p>''The examination begins at nine,'' the teacher said.</p>
  <p>A direct question keeps its question mark inside the quotation marks when the quoted words are a question.</p>
  <p>The teacher asked, ''Have you revised the topic?''</p>
  <p>If the entire sentence is a question but the quotation itself is a statement, the question mark may belong outside the quotation marks, depending on the sentence''s structure and the style used.</p>

  <h3>4. Breaking direct speech</h3>
  <p>A reporting clause can interrupt a sentence of direct speech.</p>
  <p>''If you practise daily,'' the coach said, ''your performance will improve.''</p>
  <p>The first part is not a complete sentence on its own, so the punctuation reflects that the quotation continues after the reporting clause.</p>

  <h3>5. Direct speech and reported speech</h3>
  <p>Direct speech gives the exact words: Peter said, ''I am tired.''</p>
  <p>Reported speech gives the meaning without quoting the exact words: Peter said that he was tired.</p>
  <p>Reported speech does not normally require quotation marks around the reported statement.</p>

  <h3>6. Quotation marks around titles and words</h3>
  <p>Quotation marks may be used for short works, such as a poem, article, short story, or individual chapter, depending on the required style.</p>
  <p>They may also identify a word being discussed rather than used in its ordinary meaning: The word ''their'' shows possession.</p>

  <h3>7. Quoting accurately</h3>
  <p>When quoting a source, reproduce its wording accurately. Do not silently change the original wording while presenting it as an exact quotation. If you paraphrase, use your own words instead.</p>

  <h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Forgetting the opening or closing quotation mark.</li>
    <li>Changing the words inside a quotation without indicating the change.</li>
    <li>Using quotation marks around reported speech unnecessarily.</li>
    <li>Switching between single and double quotation styles without a reason.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Quotation Marks', 15),
  (1508, 1508, '<h2>Hyphens and Dashes</h2>

  <h3>1. Hyphens and dashes are different</h3>
  <p>A <strong>hyphen</strong> (-) is short and commonly joins parts of a word or compound expression. A <strong>dash</strong> (— or –) is longer and can mark a break, interruption, or additional information. Their functions are different.</p>

  <h3>2. Hyphens in compound modifiers</h3>
  <p>A hyphen can join words that work together as a modifier before a noun.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>a well-known author</li>
    <li>a ten-year-old learner</li>
    <li>a part-time job</li>
    <li>a carefully planned lesson</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Notice that not every group of words needs a hyphen. Adverbs ending in ''-ly'' are generally not hyphenated with the adjective they modify: ''a carefully planned lesson''.</p>

  <h3>3. Hyphens with numbers and fractions</h3>
  <p>Hyphens are often used in written compound numbers from twenty-one to ninety-nine.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>twenty-one</li>
    <li>forty-six</li>
    <li>ninety-nine</li>
  </ul>
  <p>They can also appear in fractions used as compound modifiers: ''a two-thirds majority''. Style and context may affect the exact convention.</p>

  <h3>4. Hyphens can prevent confusion</h3>
  <p>Compare:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>re-sign</em> means to sign again.</li>
    <li><em>resign</em> means to leave a position or job.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A hyphen can help distinguish words that would otherwise look alike.</p>

  <h3>5. Dashes for additional information</h3>
  <p>A dash can set off an explanation or interruption that the writer wants to emphasise.</p>
  <p>''The class had one goal—to understand the method fully.''</p>
  <p>''The final result—after several corrections—was accurate.''</p>
  <p>When a pair of dashes encloses extra information, both dashes are needed unless the sentence ends at that point.</p>

  <h3>6. Dashes for a sudden break</h3>
  <p>A dash can show an interruption or a sudden change in thought.</p>
  <p>''I was about to submit the assignment—but the power went off.''</p>
  <p>Use this effect deliberately. Too many dashes can make formal writing seem disjointed.</p>

  <h3>7. Choosing the correct mark</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Use a hyphen to join parts of a compound word or modifier where required.</li>
    <li>Use a dash to create a stronger break or set off an explanation.</li>
    <li>Use ordinary punctuation instead when a hyphen or dash is unnecessary.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>
  <p>Do not use a hyphen merely because a phrase contains several words. Also avoid replacing every comma or colon with a dash. Choose punctuation based on grammar, meaning, and the effect intended.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hyphens, Dashes and Brackets', 15),
  (1509, 1509, '<h2>Skimming</h2>

  <h3>1. What skimming means</h3>
  <p><strong>Skimming</strong> is reading quickly to understand the general subject, main idea, organisation, and overall direction of a text. You do not try to understand every sentence or remember every detail.</p>

  <h3>2. When skimming is useful</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Previewing a long passage before answering comprehension questions.</li>
    <li>Deciding whether an article contains information you need.</li>
    <li>Understanding the overall argument before reading closely.</li>
    <li>Reviewing a chapter to recall its main sections.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. What to look at first</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Title:</strong> predict the general subject.</li>
    <li><strong>Headings and subheadings:</strong> identify the main sections.</li>
    <li><strong>Opening paragraph:</strong> look for the topic and central claim.</li>
    <li><strong>First sentences of paragraphs:</strong> identify how ideas develop, while remembering that the main idea can appear elsewhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> look for the overall message or final position.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Use these clues to build a general understanding. Do not assume every passage follows the same structure.</p>

  <h3>4. Example of skimming</h3>
  <p>Read this short passage quickly:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>Many schools are establishing gardens to supplement classroom learning. Learners measure plant growth, observe insects, and investigate the conditions plants need to survive. Gardens can also encourage teamwork and responsibility. However, successful projects require reliable water supplies, suitable land, and regular care.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>A useful skimming summary is: <em>School gardens support practical learning and teamwork, but they need resources and maintenance.</em></p>
  <p>You do not need to memorise every example to understand the passage''s main message.</p>

  <h3>5. Skimming versus reading closely</h3>
  <p>Skimming gives a broad overview. Close reading examines details, evidence, vocabulary, and relationships between ideas.</p>
  <p>For example, skimming may reveal that a passage discusses the benefits and challenges of school gardens. Close reading would help you identify each benefit, the exact resources required, and the evidence used to support the claims.</p>

  <h3>6. How to skim effectively</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Set a purpose: decide what general understanding you need.</li>
    <li>Move through the text quickly, focusing on structure and major ideas.</li>
    <li>Notice repeated terms, contrast words such as ''however'', and conclusion signals.</li>
    <li>State the central idea in one sentence.</li>
    <li>Return to the text for close reading if a question requires specific evidence.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>7. Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Reading every word slowly, which defeats the purpose of skimming.</li>
    <li>Looking only at the title and guessing the message without checking the passage.</li>
    <li>Confusing a minor example with the central idea.</li>
    <li>Using skimming to answer a question that requires an exact date, name, or quotation.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Skimming', 15),
  (1510, 1510, '<h2>Scanning</h2>

  <h3>1. What scanning means</h3>
  <p><strong>Scanning</strong> is reading quickly to locate a specific piece of information. Instead of trying to understand the whole passage, you search for a target such as a date, name, number, place, or particular term.</p>

  <h3>2. When scanning is useful</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Finding a date in a notice.</li>
    <li>Locating a person''s name in a report.</li>
    <li>Finding a price in a table or advertisement.</li>
    <li>Locating a particular instruction in a set of procedures.</li>
    <li>Finding the paragraph containing a term needed for a comprehension answer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Decide what you are looking for</h3>
  <p>Before scanning, turn the question into a clear search target.</p>
  <p>Question: ''On what date does registration close?''</p>
  <p>Target: a date associated with registration closing—not any date that happens to appear in the text.</p>

  <h3>4. Use visual clues</h3>
  <p>Move your eyes quickly across the text, looking for the form of information you need.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Dates often contain numbers and month names.</li>
    <li>Names often begin with capital letters.</li>
    <li>Prices may use a currency symbol or a label such as ''KSh''.</li>
    <li>Headings can help narrow the area where an answer is likely to appear.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are search clues, not proof. Always read the surrounding words once you find a possible match.</p>

  <h3>5. Example of scanning</h3>
  <p>Read this notice with a specific question in mind:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p><strong>SCIENCE CLUB REGISTRATION</strong></p>
    <p>Registration opens on 5 October. Learners must submit their forms by 16 October. The first meeting is on 21 October at 3:30 p.m. in the science laboratory. The registration fee is KSh 100.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>Question: What is the registration fee?</p>
  <p>Scan for the fee or currency marker, then read the matching phrase. The answer is <em>KSh 100</em>.</p>

  <h3>6. Scanning versus skimming</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skimming:</strong> find the general message or overall structure.</li>
    <li><strong>Scanning:</strong> locate a specific fact or detail.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you need to know what a passage is mainly about, skim it. If you need the date of a meeting, scan for the date and verify its connection to the meeting.</p>

  <h3>7. How to scan accurately</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the question and identify the exact information required.</li>
    <li>Choose a clue: a keyword, number, name, date, or heading.</li>
    <li>Search the text quickly for that clue.</li>
    <li>Read the surrounding sentence to confirm the information answers the question.</li>
    <li>Give the answer in the requested form and include necessary units or context.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Choosing the first number found without checking what it refers to.</li>
    <li>Confusing the date registration opens with the date it closes.</li>
    <li>Searching for a word without considering synonyms or alternative wording.</li>
    <li>Copying extra information instead of answering the exact question.</li>
  </ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Scanning', 15),
  (1511, 1511, '<h2>Finding the Main Idea</h2>
<p>The <strong>main idea</strong> is the central point a passage communicates. It tells you what the passage is mainly about and what the writer wants you to understand about it.</p>

<h3>1. Topic versus main idea</h3>
<p>The <strong>topic</strong> is the subject being discussed. The <strong>main idea</strong> makes a complete statement about that subject.</p>
<ul>
<li>Topic: School gardens.</li>
<li>Main idea: School gardens help learners develop practical skills while producing fresh vegetables.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. How to identify the main idea</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the whole passage rather than focusing on one sentence.</li>
<li>Identify the subject that appears throughout the passage.</li>
<li>Ask what the writer mainly wants to say about that subject.</li>
<li>Check whether your answer explains the passage as a whole, not just one detail.</li>
</ol>

<h3>3. Where the main idea appears</h3>
<p>The main idea may appear at the beginning, at the end, or across several sentences. Sometimes, the reader must combine the information to express it in one sentence.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>At Kijani School, learners turned an unused patch of land into a vegetable garden. They learnt how to prepare soil, plant seedlings and conserve water. The school kitchen now uses some of the vegetables, while learners sell the surplus to raise money for gardening tools.</em></p>
<p><strong>Topic:</strong> The school garden.</p>
<p><strong>Main idea:</strong> The school garden gives learners practical experience and provides vegetables and income for the school.</p>
<p><strong>Why?</strong> This statement connects the passage''s main benefits instead of mentioning only planting or selling vegetables.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not confuse an interesting detail with the main idea. A single sentence about selling vegetables does not capture the whole passage.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Main Ideas', 15),
  (1512, 1512, '<h2>Understanding Supporting Details</h2>
<p><strong>Supporting details</strong> are the facts, examples, explanations or descriptions that develop a main idea. They help readers understand why the main idea matters or how it is supported.</p>

<h3>1. Types of supporting details</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Facts:</strong> Information that can be checked or verified.</li>
<li><strong>Examples:</strong> Specific cases that illustrate a point.</li>
<li><strong>Explanations:</strong> Information showing how or why something happens.</li>
<li><strong>Descriptions:</strong> Details that help readers picture a person, place or situation.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence:</strong> Information used to support a claim, such as relevant observations or reliable data.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. How details support a main idea</h3>
<p>A main idea makes a broad point. Supporting details explain, demonstrate or provide evidence for that point.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>Reading regularly can improve a learner''s language skills. Encountering new words helps learners expand their vocabulary. Following different sentence patterns exposes them to varied ways of expressing ideas. Discussing what they read can also strengthen comprehension.</em></p>
<p><strong>Main idea:</strong> Regular reading can improve language skills.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting details:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Reading introduces learners to new vocabulary.</li>
<li>Reading exposes learners to different sentence patterns.</li>
<li>Discussing reading can strengthen comprehension.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Selecting relevant details</h3>
<p>A detail is relevant when it directly explains or supports the main idea. An interesting fact that does not contribute to the main point may be irrelevant.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not treat every sentence as equally important. Look at the relationship between each detail and the central point.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Supporting Details', 15),
  (1513, 1513, '<h2>Finding Explicit Information</h2>
<p><strong>Explicit information</strong> is information stated directly in a text. The reader does not need to infer it, although the information may need to be located, compared or put into their own words.</p>

<h3>1. What explicit information looks like</h3>
<p>A passage may directly state a person''s name, a date, a place, a reason, an event or a sequence of actions. Questions may ask who, what, when, where, why or how.</p>

<h3>2. A reliable method</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the question carefully and identify the information requested.</li>
<li>Locate the relevant sentence or section in the passage.</li>
<li>Check that the words you select answer the exact question.</li>
<li>Answer clearly, using your own words when appropriate without changing the meaning.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>The environmental club met at the school hall on Thursday afternoon. Its members collected litter around the playing field and planted twelve seedlings near the library. The deputy head teacher supplied the seedlings.</em></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Where did the club meet?</strong> At the school hall.</li>
<li><strong>When did it meet?</strong> On Thursday afternoon.</li>
<li><strong>How many seedlings were planted?</strong> Twelve.</li>
<li><strong>Who supplied the seedlings?</strong> The deputy head teacher.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Answer only what is asked</h3>
<p>If a question asks for a place, give the place. If it asks for two reasons, identify two reasons that the passage actually states. Avoid adding unsupported information.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not replace what the passage says with what you think probably happened. Explicit-information questions test accurate reading, not guessing.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Explicit Information', 15),
  (1514, 1514, '<h2>Reading Between the Lines</h2>
<p>An <strong>inference</strong> is a conclusion a reader reaches by combining clues in a text with reasonable knowledge. <strong>Implied meaning</strong> is what a writer communicates indirectly rather than stating openly.</p>

<h3>1. Evidence plus reasoning</h3>
<p>A sound inference has two parts:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Textual evidence:</strong> The words, actions or details the writer provides.</li>
<li><strong>Reasoning:</strong> What those details reasonably suggest.</li>
</ul>
<p>An inference should be supported by the passage. It is not a guess based only on personal opinion.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example</h3>
<p><em>When the final bell rang, Njeri remained at her desk, checking each answer on her examination paper. She only packed her bag after the teacher announced that all scripts had been collected.</em></p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> How did Njeri approach her examination?</p>
<p><strong>Evidence:</strong> She checked every answer and stayed until the scripts were collected.</p>
<p><strong>Inference:</strong> She was careful and wanted to review her work before submitting it.</p>
<p>The passage does not directly call Njeri careful; her actions support that conclusion.</p>

<h3>3. Inferring feelings and attitudes</h3>
<p>Writers may suggest feelings through actions, speech, facial expressions or descriptions of behaviour. For example, a character who avoids eye contact, speaks quietly and repeatedly checks the door may be nervous, depending on the surrounding context.</p>

<h3>4. Inferring causes and outcomes</h3>
<p>Use the events described to work out a likely reason or consequence. Keep the conclusion within the limits of the evidence.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not confuse a possible explanation with a proven one. If several explanations fit the clues, acknowledge uncertainty or choose the one best supported by the text.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Inference and Implied Meaning', 15),
  (1515, 1515, '<h2>Using Context to Understand Words</h2>
<p><strong>Context clues</strong> are hints in the surrounding words, sentences or passage that help a reader work out the meaning of an unfamiliar word or expression.</p>

<h3>1. Definition or explanation clues</h3>
<p>The text explains the unfamiliar word directly.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> A <strong>nocturnal</strong> animal, one that is active at night, often rests during the day.</p>
<p>The explanation tells us that <em>nocturnal</em> means active at night.</p>

<h3>2. Synonym or restatement clues</h3>
<p>A nearby word or phrase expresses a similar meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The instructions were <strong>concise</strong>; they were brief and used very few words.</p>

<h3>3. Contrast clues</h3>
<p>A contrasting idea helps reveal the meaning. Words such as <em>but</em>, <em>although</em>, <em>unlike</em> and <em>however</em> may signal contrast.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Unlike his <strong>timid</strong> brother, who rarely spoke in public, Joel readily addressed the crowd. The contrast suggests that <em>timid</em> means shy or lacking confidence.</p>

<h3>4. Example clues</h3>
<p>Examples show the kind of thing a word describes.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Citrus fruits, such as oranges, lemons and limes, contain vitamin C. These examples help explain the category <em>citrus fruits</em>.</p>

<h3>5. Cause-and-effect clues</h3>
<p>An action or result may help reveal a word''s meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The path was <strong>slippery</strong> after the rain, so several walkers moved cautiously to avoid falling. The risk of falling helps explain the meaning of <em>slippery</em>.</p>

<h3>6. A careful method</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the sentence and the sentences around it.</li>
<li>Look for explanations, similar words, contrasts, examples or results.</li>
<li>Suggest a meaning that fits the context.</li>
<li>Replace the unfamiliar word with your suggested meaning and check whether the sentence still makes sense.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Important limitation</h3>
<p>Context clues do not always reveal an exact meaning. If the passage does not provide enough information, use a reliable dictionary or ask for clarification rather than pretending to know.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Context Clues', 15),
  (1516, 1516, '<h2>Understanding Why a Text Was Written</h2>
<p>An author''s <strong>purpose</strong> is the reason a writer creates a text. Understanding the purpose helps you distinguish information from persuasion, entertainment and instructions.</p>

<h3>1. Common purposes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>To inform:</strong> Give facts, explanations or information.</li>
<li><strong>To persuade:</strong> Convince readers to accept an idea or take action.</li>
<li><strong>To entertain:</strong> Engage readers through stories, humour, suspense or interesting experiences.</li>
<li><strong>To instruct:</strong> Explain how to do something through directions or steps.</li>
<li><strong>To describe:</strong> Create a clear picture of a person, place, object or experience.</li>
<li><strong>To express feelings:</strong> Communicate personal emotions, reactions or experiences.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. How to identify purpose</h3>
<p>Do not identify the purpose from the topic alone. Two texts about farming may have different purposes: one may explain how crops grow, while another urges farmers to adopt a particular method.</p>
<ol>
<li>Identify what the writer mainly does.</li>
<li>Examine the language, structure and details used.</li>
<li>Ask what the writer wants readers to know, feel or do.</li>
<li>Support your answer with evidence from the text.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>Every household should plant at least one tree this rainy season. Trees help reduce soil erosion and provide shade. Join Saturday''s community planting exercise and bring a spade.</em></p>
<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To persuade readers to plant trees and participate in the community exercise.</p>
<p><strong>Evidence:</strong> The writer says ''Every household should'' and directly invites readers to participate.</p>

<h3>3. More than one purpose</h3>
<p>A text can have several purposes. A story may entertain while also teaching a lesson. When a question asks for the main purpose, identify the one most strongly supported by the text.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not answer only with the subject. ''Trees'' is a topic, not a purpose. State what the writer is trying to achieve.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Author''s Purpose', 15),
  (1517, 1517, '<h2>Identifying Audience and Tone</h2>
<p>The <strong>audience</strong> is the person or group a text is intended for. The <strong>tone</strong> is the writer''s attitude towards the subject or audience, as revealed through word choice and expression.</p>

<h3>1. Identifying the audience</h3>
<p>Consider the text''s purpose, vocabulary, assumed knowledge, level of formality and format.</p>
<ul>
<li>A school notice may address learners, teachers or parents.</li>
<li>A technical guide may address people who already understand a specialised subject.</li>
<li>A children''s story may use simple language and familiar situations.</li>
<li>A formal letter to a government office normally uses respectful, carefully structured language.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Recognising tone</h3>
<p>Tone can be formal, informal, respectful, enthusiastic, concerned, critical, humorous, sarcastic, sympathetic or reassuring, among others.</p>
<p>Choose a tone that the words actually support. Do not label a text ''angry'' merely because it discusses a serious problem.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Text A:</strong> <em>Kindly submit your completed forms to the office by Friday.</em></p>
<p><strong>Text B:</strong> <em>Get those forms in by Friday, or we will have a problem!</em></p>
<p>Both texts address the submission of forms, but Text A uses a polite, formal tone, while Text B sounds forceful and potentially threatening.</p>

<h3>3. Support your interpretation</h3>
<p>When asked about audience or tone, name the most likely audience or attitude and quote or describe the language that supports your answer. The same text may suit more than one audience, so use the strongest clues.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Audience and tone are different. ''Parents'' may be the audience; ''respectful'' may describe the tone.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Audience and Tone', 15),
  (1518, 1518, '<h2>Distinguishing Facts from Opinions</h2>
<p>A <strong>fact</strong> is a statement that can be checked against evidence. An <strong>opinion</strong> expresses a judgement, belief, preference or evaluation.</p>

<h3>1. Recognising facts</h3>
<p>Facts can often be verified using records, reliable observations, measurements or other evidence.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> ''The school library opens at 8.00 a.m.'' This is verifiable by checking the library''s opening arrangements.</p>

<h3>2. Recognising opinions</h3>
<p>Opinions may contain value judgements or preferences, often expressed through words such as <em>best</em>, <em>worst</em>, <em>beautiful</em>, <em>boring</em> or <em>should</em>.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> ''The school library is the most inspiring place in the county.'' The claim depends on a judgement about what is inspiring.</p>

<h3>3. Important distinctions</h3>
<ul>
<li>A sentence is not automatically an opinion because it contains a number. The number may be verifiable.</li>
<li>A sentence is not automatically a fact because it sounds confident.</li>
<li>Opinions can be supported by facts, but the supporting facts do not turn every judgement into an objective fact.</li>
<li>A statement about what should happen often expresses a recommendation or value judgement.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>The school planted 120 trees last year. Tree planting is the most worthwhile activity a school can organise.</em></p>
<p>The first sentence is a fact if school records can verify the number. The second is an opinion because ''most worthwhile'' expresses a judgement.</p>

<h3>4. Checking a statement</h3>
<p>Ask: Could reliable evidence establish whether this statement is accurate? Does it express a personal judgement or preference? Some statements contain both factual and opinion-based elements, so examine each claim carefully.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not confuse an unsupported claim with an opinion in every case. A claim about an event may be factual in nature but still require evidence before you can accept it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Fact and Opinion', 15),
  (1519, 1519, '<h2>Recognising Bias and Persuasive Language</h2>
<p><strong>Bias</strong> is a tendency to present a person, group, event or issue in a way that favours one side or viewpoint. <strong>Persuasive language</strong> is language chosen to influence what readers think, feel or do.</p>

<h3>1. How bias can appear</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Selective information:</strong> Including evidence that supports one side while leaving out relevant opposing evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Loaded words:</strong> Using emotionally strong terms to make a person or idea seem admirable or terrible.</li>
<li><strong>One-sided descriptions:</strong> Presenting only the advantages or only the disadvantages of an issue.</li>
<li><strong>Unfair generalisations:</strong> Making broad claims about a whole group from limited examples.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Persuasive techniques</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Emotive language:</strong> Appeals to feelings, such as ''innocent families'' or ''a shocking waste''.</li>
<li><strong>Rhetorical questions:</strong> Questions used to make readers consider a point rather than simply request an answer.</li>
<li><strong>Repetition:</strong> Repeating words or ideas for emphasis.</li>
<li><strong>Direct address:</strong> Speaking to readers using ''you'' or ''your''.</li>
<li><strong>Calls to action:</strong> Direct requests such as ''Join us'' or ''Act today''.</li>
<li><strong>Appeals to authority or popularity:</strong> Referring to experts or claiming that many people agree. Such appeals still need to be assessed carefully.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>Only a careless community would ignore this dangerous rubbish problem. Join our clean-up on Saturday and prove that you care about your children.</em></p>
<p>The words ''careless'' and ''dangerous'' create an emotional response. ''Prove that you care'' pressures readers by linking participation to their identity as caring people. The text promotes a clean-up but uses emotionally forceful language.</p>

<h3>3. Analyse rather than merely label</h3>
<p>To explain bias or persuasion, identify the specific words or omitted information, describe their likely effect, and explain how they support the writer''s position.</p>

<h3>4. Keep a critical mind</h3>
<p>Persuasion is not automatically dishonest, and a strong opinion is not automatically unfair. Check the evidence, consider alternative viewpoints and distinguish emotional appeal from proof.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not assume a text is biased merely because it takes a position. Bias is more convincingly identified through unfair selection, loaded presentation or insufficient consideration of relevant evidence.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Bias and Persuasive Language', 15),
  (1520, 1520, '<h2>Testing Whether a Claim Is Well Supported</h2>
<p>A <strong>claim</strong> is a statement that someone asks readers to accept as true or reasonable. <strong>Evidence</strong> is information used to support or challenge that claim. Good readers examine both before deciding what to believe.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the claim</h3>
<p>Find the main assertion being made. For example: ''Providing a quiet study room improves learners'' concentration.'' This is a claim because it makes an assertion that can be investigated.</p>

<h3>2. Examine the evidence</h3>
<p>Evidence may include relevant measurements, records, observations, examples, expert findings or research results. Ask:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Relevance:</strong> Does the evidence address the claim?</li>
<li><strong>Reliability:</strong> Is the source in a position to provide accurate information?</li>
<li><strong>Sufficiency:</strong> Is there enough evidence to justify the conclusion?</li>
<li><strong>Currency:</strong> Is the information recent enough for the issue?</li>
<li><strong>Fairness:</strong> Have important limitations or alternative explanations been considered?</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Correlation and causation</h3>
<p>When two things happen together, one does not necessarily cause the other. If learners who attend a study group achieve higher marks, that alone does not prove that the study group caused the improvement. Other factors, such as prior preparation, may also matter.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>A school claims that its new reading programme improves comprehension. It reports that average comprehension scores increased after the programme began.</em></p>
<p><strong>What the evidence supports:</strong> Scores increased after the programme began.</p>
<p><strong>What remains uncertain:</strong> Whether the programme caused the increase. To assess this, we would need further information, such as how scores were measured, whether the learners changed in other ways and whether a suitable comparison was available.</p>

<h3>4. Reach a proportionate conclusion</h3>
<p>Do not accept a claim merely because it is confidently stated or accompanied by a number. Do not reject it automatically either. Decide whether the available evidence strongly supports it, partly supports it or leaves it uncertain.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>A source can be reliable but still provide evidence that is insufficient for a particular claim. Always assess both the source and the evidence itself.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evaluating Claims and Evidence', 15),
  (1521, 1521, '<h2>Finding the Information That Matters</h2>
<p><strong>Key information</strong> is the information a reader needs to understand the central message, answer a question or complete a task. It is more important than background details that do not affect the purpose.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the purpose first</h3>
<p>The information that matters depends on what you are trying to find out.</p>
<ul>
<li>For an examination date, the date, time and venue may be essential.</li>
<li>For a scientific explanation, the process, conditions and results may matter most.</li>
<li>For a story, the important information may include the main characters, problem, actions and outcome.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. A practical method</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the question or purpose of the reading.</li>
<li>Look for information that directly answers it.</li>
<li>Distinguish essential facts from examples, repetition and background.</li>
<li>Check that the selected information is accurate and complete enough for the task.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>The school science exhibition will take place on Friday, 16 October, in the main hall from 9.00 a.m. to 1.00 p.m. Learners presenting projects must arrive by 8.15 a.m. to arrange their displays. The hall was painted last year, and the exhibition committee meets every Wednesday.</em></p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> When and where will the exhibition take place?</p>
<p><strong>Key information:</strong> Friday, 16 October, in the main hall.</p>
<p>The arrival time matters if you are presenting a project, but it is not necessary to answer this particular question. The painting and committee meeting details are unrelated to the requested information.</p>

<h3>3. Do not confuse key information with short information</h3>
<p>A long explanation may contain the essential reason for an event, while a short date or number may be irrelevant to your question. Importance depends on purpose, not length.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not copy every fact simply because it appears in the passage. Select information that answers the task while preserving any essential conditions or qualifications.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Key Information', 15),
  (1522, 1522, '<h2>Separating Main Points from Details</h2>
<p>A <strong>main point</strong> is an important idea that a passage develops. A <strong>supporting detail</strong> explains, illustrates or provides evidence for that point. A longer passage may contain several main points, each supported by its own details.</p>

<h3>1. How the two relate</h3>
<p>Think of a passage as a structure:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Main point:</strong> The central statement in a section.</li>
<li><strong>Supporting details:</strong> Examples, explanations, facts or reasons that develop it.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Find the main points</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the entire passage to understand its overall subject.</li>
<li>Look for ideas that organise or connect several sentences.</li>
<li>Group details that explain the same idea.</li>
<li>Give each group a short heading or write its main point in a sentence.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>School libraries support learning in several ways. They give learners access to books and other information resources. They also provide quiet spaces for reading and research. In addition, librarians can guide learners towards suitable materials and help them develop research skills.</em></p>
<p><strong>Main point:</strong> School libraries support learning in several ways.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting details:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>They provide access to learning resources.</li>
<li>They offer quiet spaces for reading and research.</li>
<li>Librarians guide learners and help develop research skills.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Passages with several main points</h3>
<p>A passage about school libraries might have separate sections on resources, study spaces and research guidance. Each section can have its own main point, while all three contribute to the overall message that libraries support learning.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not list examples as if they were all equally important ideas. First identify the broader point that connects them, then attach each detail to the point it supports.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Main Points and Supporting Details', 15),
  (1523, 1523, '<h2>Choosing Information That Answers the Task</h2>
<p><strong>Relevant information</strong> directly helps answer a question, explain an idea or complete a task. Irrelevant information may be true and interesting, but it does not help with the purpose at hand.</p>

<h3>1. Relevance depends on the question</h3>
<p>The same fact can be relevant to one question and irrelevant to another. A person''s age may matter in a biography but not in a question asking where an event took place.</p>

<h3>2. Test each detail</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Connection:</strong> Does it address the question or main point?</li>
<li><strong>Usefulness:</strong> Does it help explain, prove or clarify the point?</li>
<li><strong>Necessity:</strong> Would removing it leave an important gap in the answer?</li>
<li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> Is it supported by the text or a reliable source?</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>A community plans to reduce plastic litter near a river. Organisers propose placing labelled bins at busy points, holding clean-up activities and educating residents about proper disposal. The river is also a popular place for birdwatching, and the nearby market sells fresh fruit every morning.</em></p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> What actions are proposed to reduce plastic litter?</p>
<p><strong>Relevant information:</strong> Placing labelled bins, holding clean-up activities and educating residents about proper disposal.</p>
<p>The birdwatching and market details do not answer the question.</p>

<h3>3. Keep important qualifications</h3>
<p>Do not remove words that limit or qualify a claim. For example, ''some filters remove certain impurities'' is not equivalent to ''filters remove all impurities''. Accurate selection preserves the original meaning.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not select a detail merely because it repeats a word from the question. Check whether it actually answers the question being asked.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Selecting Relevant Information', 15),
  (1524, 1524, '<h2>Making Writing More Concise</h2>
<p>Removing unnecessary details means taking out information that does not contribute meaningfully to the purpose, while preserving the central message and all essential facts.</p>

<h3>1. What can be removed?</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Repetition:</strong> Restating the same idea without adding meaning.</li>
<li><strong>Irrelevant background:</strong> Details that do not help answer the question.</li>
<li><strong>Unnecessary description:</strong> Extra adjectives or explanations that do not serve the purpose.</li>
<li><strong>Unneeded examples:</strong> Examples that repeat a point already made clearly.</li>
<li><strong>Wordy expressions:</strong> Phrases that can be replaced with shorter, equally accurate wording.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. What must remain?</h3>
<p>Keep the main idea, essential supporting details, important names or figures, and qualifications that affect meaning. Concise writing should not become vague or inaccurate.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Wordy version:</strong> <em>Due to the fact that the road was flooded with water, the bus was unable to continue moving forward along the road.</em></p>
<p><strong>Concise version:</strong> <em>The bus could not continue because the road was flooded.</em></p>
<p>The shorter version preserves the cause and result while removing redundant phrases such as ''due to the fact that'' and ''moving forward along the road''.</p>

<h3>3. A reliable editing method</h3>
<ol>
<li>State the purpose of the passage in one sentence.</li>
<li>Mark the facts needed to preserve that purpose.</li>
<li>Remove repeated or unrelated material.</li>
<li>Replace wordy expressions with clear alternatives.</li>
<li>Read the shortened version to check accuracy, clarity and completeness.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not remove a detail merely because it is long. A condition, reason or warning may be essential even when it takes several words to express.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Removing Unnecessary Details', 15),
  (1525, 1525, '<h2>Restating Ideas in Your Own Words</h2>
<p><strong>Paraphrasing</strong> means expressing information or an idea from a source using your own wording and sentence structure while preserving its original meaning.</p>

<h3>1. Why paraphrase?</h3>
<p>Paraphrasing helps you demonstrate understanding, make difficult information clearer and use source material in your own writing. It is not simply replacing a few words with synonyms.</p>

<h3>2. A reliable method</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Understand:</strong> Read the original until you understand the whole idea.</li>
<li><strong>Identify:</strong> Note the essential meaning, facts and relationships.</li>
<li><strong>Restate:</strong> Put the idea into your own sentence structure and wording.</li>
<li><strong>Compare:</strong> Check that your version preserves the original meaning and qualifications.</li>
<li><strong>Acknowledge:</strong> When using another person''s ideas in academic work, give the required citation or acknowledgement.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Original:</strong> <em>Regular physical activity can improve mood and help people manage stress.</em></p>
<p><strong>Paraphrase:</strong> <em>Being active consistently may help people feel better and cope with stress.</em></p>
<p>The wording and sentence structure differ, but the main meaning remains. The word ''may'' also preserves the original idea that these are possible benefits, not guaranteed results.</p>

<h3>3. Preserve accuracy</h3>
<p>Do not change a possibility into a certainty, reverse a cause and effect, omit a key condition or add a claim the source does not make.</p>

<h3>4. Paraphrasing versus summarising</h3>
<p>A paraphrase restates a particular idea or passage in different words and may be similar in length to the original. A summary gives only the main points and is usually shorter.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Changing just a few words while keeping the original sentence structure may be too close to the source. First understand the idea, then write it afresh.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Paraphrasing', 15),
  (1526, 1526, '<h2>Combining Related Ideas</h2>
<p>Combining ideas means joining related information to express a complete thought clearly and efficiently. It helps writers avoid repetitive sentences and show relationships between ideas.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the relationship</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Addition:</strong> Both ideas contribute information. Use words such as <em>and</em>, <em>also</em> and <em>furthermore</em>.</li>
<li><strong>Contrast:</strong> The ideas differ. Use <em>but</em>, <em>although</em> or <em>whereas</em>.</li>
<li><strong>Cause and effect:</strong> One idea explains why another happens. Use <em>because</em>, <em>so</em> or <em>therefore</em> appropriately.</li>
<li><strong>Condition:</strong> One event depends on another. Use <em>if</em> or <em>unless</em>.</li>
<li><strong>Sequence:</strong> Events occur in an order. Use <em>before</em>, <em>after</em> or <em>then</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Worked examples</h3>
<p><strong>Addition:</strong> The learners planted trees. They watered the seedlings.</p>
<p><em>Combined:</em> The learners planted trees and watered the seedlings.</p>
<p><strong>Contrast:</strong> The road was narrow. The bus passed safely.</p>
<p><em>Combined:</em> Although the road was narrow, the bus passed safely.</p>
<p><strong>Cause and effect:</strong> The river flooded. The footbridge became inaccessible.</p>
<p><em>Combined:</em> The footbridge became inaccessible because the river flooded.</p>

<h3>3. Preserve meaning</h3>
<p>Choose a joining word that expresses the actual relationship. Do not use <em>because</em> when the sentences merely describe two events that happened together. Keep important conditions and avoid changing who did what.</p>

<h3>4. Avoid comma splices</h3>
<p>Do not join two complete sentences with only a comma.</p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The rain stopped, the learners returned outside.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>The rain stopped, so the learners returned outside.</em></p>
<p>You can also use a full stop or a semicolon where appropriate.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Combining Related Ideas', 15),
  (1527, 1527, '<h2>Writing an Accurate Summary</h2>
<p>A <strong>summary</strong> is a shorter version of a passage that preserves its most important ideas. It leaves out minor details, repeated points and examples that are not necessary for understanding the central message.</p>

<h3>1. What a good summary does</h3>
<ul>
<li>Includes the main points relevant to the task.</li>
<li>Uses clear, concise wording.</li>
<li>Preserves the original meaning and important qualifications.</li>
<li>Uses your own words where appropriate.</li>
<li>Avoids personal opinions and information not supported by the source.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. A reliable method</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the entire passage to understand its message.</li>
<li>Identify the main idea and essential supporting points.</li>
<li>Remove examples, repetition and irrelevant details.</li>
<li>Combine related points into a logical paragraph or list.</li>
<li>Check that the summary is shorter and still accurate.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Original passage:</strong> <em>Many learners travel to school by walking. Walking costs no fare and can provide daily physical activity. However, learners who live far away may become tired, arrive late or face unsafe roads. Communities can improve their journeys by organising safe walking groups and identifying dangerous sections of roads.</em></p>
<p><strong>Summary:</strong> Walking is an affordable way for learners to reach school and get exercise, but long or unsafe journeys can cause difficulties. Communities can improve safety through walking groups and by identifying dangerous road sections.</p>
<p>The summary retains the benefits, problems and suggested responses while removing less essential wording.</p>

<h3>3. Follow the question''s focus</h3>
<p>If asked to summarise the problems, focus on the problems. If asked for solutions, include the proposed solutions. A general summary and a question-focused summary may contain different information.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Copying the entire passage with only a few words changed.</li>
<li>Including minor examples while missing the main point.</li>
<li>Adding personal opinions or invented information.</li>
<li>Making the summary so short that essential meaning disappears.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Writing Concise Summaries', 15),
  (1528, 1528, '<h2>Turning Information into Useful Notes</h2>
<p><strong>Note-making</strong> is the process of selecting and arranging important information from a source so it can be reviewed or used later. Good notes make relationships between ideas easy to see.</p>

<h3>1. Select before you write</h3>
<p>Identify the purpose of your notes. Notes for revising a process may need ordered steps, while notes for comparing two ideas may need similarities and differences.</p>

<h3>2. Common ways to organise notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Headings and subheadings:</strong> Group information under main topics and smaller related points.</li>
<li><strong>Numbered lists:</strong> Show stages or actions that must occur in sequence.</li>
<li><strong>Bullets:</strong> Present related points without implying a fixed order.</li>
<li><strong>Tables:</strong> Compare features across categories.</li>
<li><strong>Flow diagrams:</strong> Show stages, decisions or cause-and-effect relationships.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Use concise language</h3>
<p>Notes can use short phrases, familiar abbreviations and symbols, provided you understand them later. Keep technical terms, essential definitions, important figures and necessary conditions.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Source information:</strong> Plants need suitable light, water and mineral nutrients to grow. Roots absorb water and minerals from the soil. Leaves capture light, which plants use during photosynthesis. Too little water can cause wilting.</p>
<p><strong>Organised notes:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Plant growth requirements</strong>
  <ul>
  <li>Light — energy for photosynthesis.</li>
  <li>Water — absorbed by roots; shortage may cause wilting.</li>
  <li>Mineral nutrients — absorbed from soil through roots.</li>
  </ul>
</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Make relationships clear</h3>
<p>Place related ideas together and use indentation to show which details belong under each main point. Keep a sequence in the correct order when order affects meaning.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not copy an entire paragraph and call it note-making. Notes should be selective and organised, but not so abbreviated that the meaning becomes unclear.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Organising Notes', 15),
  (1529, 1529, '<h2>Planning Before Writing</h2>
<p>A <strong>composition plan</strong> is an organised outline of the ideas, events or arguments you intend to develop in a piece of writing. Planning helps you stay focused, arrange ideas logically and avoid repeating the same point.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the task</h3>
<p>Read the question carefully. Identify the required form, topic, purpose, audience and any limits or instructions.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Narrative:</strong> Plan characters, setting, conflict, key events and resolution.</li>
<li><strong>Descriptive:</strong> Choose the subject and organise sensory details or features.</li>
<li><strong>Expository:</strong> Arrange explanations and facts in a logical order.</li>
<li><strong>Argumentative:</strong> State a position, develop reasons, consider opposing views where appropriate and reach a conclusion.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Build a simple outline</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Introduction:</strong> Introduce the subject, situation or central argument.</li>
<li><strong>Body:</strong> Arrange the main ideas or events in a sensible order.</li>
<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Resolve the story, summarise the explanation or reinforce the argument.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example: Argumentative composition</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Should schools provide time for independent reading?</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Position:</strong> Yes, schools should provide regular independent-reading time.</li>
<li><strong>Reason 1:</strong> It gives learners regular practice with written English.</li>
<li><strong>Reason 2:</strong> It exposes learners to new vocabulary and ideas.</li>
<li><strong>Reason 3:</strong> It allows learners to explore subjects of personal interest.</li>
<li><strong>Possible concern:</strong> Timetables are already crowded.</li>
<li><strong>Response:</strong> Short, planned sessions can fit into the school day.</li>
<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Regular reading time is worthwhile when organised effectively.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Check your plan</h3>
<p>Make sure every main point answers the question, the order is logical and you have enough information to develop each point. A plan is a guide, not a requirement to write every point exactly as drafted.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not start writing immediately if the question requires several different points. A brief plan can prevent irrelevant paragraphs and a conclusion that introduces an entirely new argument.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Planning and Organising Ideas', 15),
  (1530, 1530, '<h2>Writing Strong Topic Sentences</h2>
<p>A <strong>topic sentence</strong> states the main point of a paragraph. It helps readers understand what the paragraph will explain, describe or argue.</p>

<h3>1. What makes a strong topic sentence?</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clear focus:</strong> It identifies the paragraph''s central idea.</li>
<li><strong>Suitable scope:</strong> It is neither too broad nor too narrow for the paragraph.</li>
<li><strong>Development:</strong> It allows supporting sentences to explain, illustrate or justify it.</li>
<li><strong>Relevance:</strong> It directly contributes to the composition''s overall purpose.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Topic versus topic sentence</h3>
<p><strong>Topic:</strong> School gardens.</p>
<p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> School gardens provide learners with practical opportunities to develop agricultural skills.</p>
<p>The topic names a subject. The topic sentence makes a complete statement about that subject.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> Reading regularly helps learners develop stronger language skills.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting sentences:</strong> Reading introduces unfamiliar words in meaningful contexts. It also exposes learners to different sentence structures. Discussing a text can further improve their understanding of what they read.</p>
<p>Each supporting sentence develops the topic sentence. A sentence about the colour of the school library would not belong unless the paragraph connected it to the main point.</p>

<h3>4. Where topic sentences appear</h3>
<p>A topic sentence often appears at the beginning of a paragraph, but it can also appear later. Some paragraphs develop their main idea through a sequence of details, with the central point becoming clear near the end.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing a sentence so broad that the paragraph cannot develop it adequately.</li>
<li>Making a sentence too specific to support several explanatory sentences.</li>
<li>Introducing several unrelated ideas in one topic sentence.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Topic Sentences', 15),
  (1531, 1531, '<h2>Supporting Sentences</h2>
<p>Supporting sentences develop the idea introduced by a topic sentence. They explain, provide evidence, give examples, or describe details that help the reader understand the main point.</p>

<h3>1. Four ways to support a topic sentence</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Explanation:</strong> clarifies why or how the main point is true.</li>
  <li><strong>Example:</strong> gives a specific instance that illustrates the point.</li>
  <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> provides relevant facts, observations, or reliable information.</li>
  <li><strong>Detail:</strong> adds specific information that makes the idea clearer.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> Reading regularly improves a learner''s language skills.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting sentence 1 — explanation:</strong> Regular reading exposes learners to new vocabulary and correct sentence patterns.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting sentence 2 — example:</strong> For instance, a learner who reads different stories may discover unfamiliar words and learn how they are used.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting sentence 3 — result:</strong> With repeated exposure, the learner can use suitable words and construct clearer sentences when writing.</p>

<h3>3. Develop rather than repeat</h3>
<p>A supporting sentence should add something useful. Repeating the topic sentence in different words does not develop the paragraph.</p>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> Reading regularly improves language because reading often makes language better.</p>
<p><strong>Stronger:</strong> Reading regularly introduces learners to varied vocabulary, which helps them express ideas more precisely.</p>

<h3>4. Keep support relevant</h3>
<p>Every supporting sentence should help explain or establish the paragraph''s main point. Interesting information that does not support that point belongs elsewhere.</p>
<p><strong>Check:</strong> Ask, “How does this sentence help the reader understand or believe the topic sentence?” If there is no clear answer, revise or remove it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Supporting Sentences', 15),
  (1532, 1532, '<h2>Unity in a Paragraph</h2>
<p>A paragraph has unity when every sentence contributes to one central idea. The topic sentence introduces that idea, while the supporting sentences explain, illustrate or establish it.</p>

<h3>1. How unity works</h3>
<p>Consider a paragraph about the benefits of school gardens. Relevant sentences might explain how gardens provide practical learning, encourage responsibility and improve the appearance of the school. A sentence about a learner''s favourite musician would break the unity unless the paragraph clearly connected it to the garden topic.</p>

<h3>2. Find the central idea first</h3>
<p>Before judging a sentence, state the paragraph''s main point in one clear sentence. Then compare each supporting sentence with that point.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Keep:</strong> sentences that explain, give evidence for or illustrate the central idea.</li>
  <li><strong>Revise:</strong> sentences that could be relevant but have an unclear connection.</li>
  <li><strong>Remove or relocate:</strong> sentences that introduce a different subject.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Example of a paragraph without unity</h3>
<p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> Preparing for examinations requires a consistent study routine.</p>
<p><strong>Relevant support:</strong> A timetable helps learners allocate time to different subjects. Short revision sessions also make it easier to review difficult concepts regularly.</p>
<p><strong>Unrelated sentence:</strong> The school football team has new uniforms.</p>
<p>The last sentence introduces a different topic and should be removed or placed in a paragraph about school sports.</p>

<h3>4. Unity is not the same as repetition</h3>
<p>A paragraph can remain focused without saying the same thing repeatedly. Each sentence should contribute a new reason, example, explanation or useful detail that supports the central idea.</p>

<h3>5. Final unity check</h3>
<p>Read the topic sentence, then read each supporting sentence. Ask whether the paragraph would become less clear or less convincing if that sentence were removed. If a sentence has no meaningful connection to the central idea, revise it, remove it or move it to a more suitable paragraph.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Paragraph Unity', 15),
  (1533, 1533, '<h2>Coherence and Cohesion</h2>
<p>Coherence and cohesion help readers follow ideas in a paragraph or composition, but they describe different aspects of writing.</p>

<h3>1. Coherence: a logical flow of ideas</h3>
<p>Coherence exists when ideas are organised in a sensible order and the reader can understand how they relate.</p>
<p><strong>Unclear order:</strong> The water boiled. I filled the kettle. I switched it on.</p>
<p><strong>Coherent order:</strong> I filled the kettle with water. I switched it on. When the water boiled, I poured it into a cup.</p>

<h3>2. Cohesion: the links between sentences</h3>
<p>Cohesion is created by language that connects ideas across sentences. Common devices include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Connectives:</strong> however, therefore, because, although, meanwhile.</li>
  <li><strong>Pronoun reference:</strong> ''Amina bought a book. She read it that evening.''</li>
  <li><strong>Repetition of key terms:</strong> repeating an important word when needed to keep the subject clear.</li>
  <li><strong>Related vocabulary:</strong> using words with clear links to the topic, such as ''rain'', ''run-off'' and ''drainage''.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Choose connectives by meaning</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Addition:</strong> furthermore, also, in addition.</li>
  <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> however, although, nevertheless.</li>
  <li><strong>Cause and result:</strong> because, therefore, consequently.</li>
  <li><strong>Sequence:</strong> first, next, finally.</li>
  <li><strong>Example:</strong> for example, for instance.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not choose a connective only because it sounds formal. Its meaning must match the relationship between the ideas.</p>

<h3>4. Avoid weak connections</h3>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> The road was flooded. Therefore, it had rained heavily.</p>
<p>This may be a possible explanation, but ''therefore'' presents the rain as a conclusion established by the flooding. A more careful version is: ''The road was flooded, possibly because it had rained heavily.''</p>
<p><strong>Another weak link:</strong> ''Kendi lost her notebook. This was expensive.'' The word ''this'' has an unclear reference: losing the notebook is not necessarily expensive.</p>
<p><strong>Clearer:</strong> ''Kendi lost her notebook. Replacing it cost her money.''</p>

<h3>5. Revision method</h3>
<p>First check coherence: does each idea follow logically from the previous one? Then check cohesion: are the relationships and references between ideas clear? A paragraph can use many connectives and still lack coherence if its ideas are poorly organised.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Coherence and Cohesion', 15),
  (1534, 1534, '<h2>Introductions and Conclusions</h2>
<p>An introduction prepares the reader for a composition. A conclusion brings the composition to a purposeful close. Both should suit the type of writing and the main idea.</p>

<h3>1. Writing an effective introduction</h3>
<p>An introduction should orient the reader to the subject and establish the direction of the composition. Depending on the task, it may:</p>
<ul>
  <li>introduce the topic directly;</li>
  <li>provide brief background information;</li>
  <li>establish a situation, question or point of view;</li>
  <li>present the main argument in argumentative writing.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Topic:</strong> Why learners should read regularly.</p>
<p><strong>Effective introduction:</strong> Reading is an important habit that helps learners expand their vocabulary and understand different ideas. Making time for reading each day can strengthen both language skills and independent learning.</p>
<p>This introduction introduces the topic and indicates the direction of the composition without trying to explain every point at once.</p>

<h3>2. Avoid weak openings</h3>
<p>Openings such as ''Since the beginning of time...'' or ''I am going to write about...'' often waste words or sound mechanical. Begin with information that is relevant to the task.</p>

<h3>3. Writing an effective conclusion</h3>
<p>A conclusion should bring the main discussion together. It can summarise the central points, reinforce a reasoned position, or show the outcome of a narrative.</p>
<p><strong>Example conclusion:</strong> By reading regularly and choosing suitable material, learners can steadily improve their vocabulary, comprehension and confidence in writing.</p>
<p>A conclusion should not introduce a major new argument that requires its own explanation. In a story, the ending should resolve the main situation or provide a convincing final moment.</p>

<h3>4. Match the ending to the purpose</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Expository:</strong> draw together the explanation''s main points.</li>
  <li><strong>Argumentative:</strong> restate the position and reinforce the strongest reasons.</li>
  <li><strong>Narrative:</strong> resolve the main conflict or reveal the outcome.</li>
  <li><strong>Descriptive:</strong> leave the reader with a fitting final impression of the subject.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Keep the composition connected</h3>
<p>The introduction, body and conclusion should serve the same overall purpose. A conclusion that contradicts the introduction without explaining why makes the composition feel incomplete. Before finishing, check that the opening establishes the right direction and the ending fulfils the reader''s expectations.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Introductions and Conclusions', 15),
  (1535, 1535, '<h2>Narrative Composition</h2>
<p>A narrative composition tells a story about events involving characters in a particular setting. It may describe real or imagined events, but the story should have a clear sequence and a meaningful outcome.</p>

<h3>1. Core elements of a narrative</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Characters:</strong> the people or other beings involved in the events.</li>
  <li><strong>Setting:</strong> where and when the events occur.</li>
  <li><strong>Plot:</strong> the connected sequence of events.</li>
  <li><strong>Conflict or problem:</strong> the difficulty that creates tension or drives the story.</li>
  <li><strong>Resolution:</strong> how the main problem ends or changes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. A practical story structure</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Orientation:</strong> establish the characters, setting and initial situation.</li>
  <li><strong>Complication:</strong> introduce a problem, unexpected event or challenge.</li>
  <li><strong>Development:</strong> show how the characters respond and how the situation changes.</li>
  <li><strong>Climax:</strong> present the turning point or most intense moment.</li>
  <li><strong>Resolution:</strong> show the outcome and provide a satisfying ending.</li>
</ol>
<p>Not every story must follow this exact pattern, but the reader should be able to follow the events and understand how they connect.</p>

<h3>3. Plan before writing</h3>
<p>Before drafting, decide:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Who is the main character?</li>
  <li>Where and when does the story happen?</li>
  <li>What does the character want or need?</li>
  <li>What goes wrong?</li>
  <li>What does the character do, and what happens as a result?</li>
  <li>How does the story end?</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Make events believable and connected</h3>
<p>Each important event should arise naturally from what came before it. If a character suddenly solves a serious problem without any explanation, the ending may feel unconvincing.</p>
<p>Use sequence words such as ''later'', ''meanwhile'', ''eventually'' and ''the following morning'' when they clarify the order of events. Avoid beginning every sentence with ''then''.</p>

<h3>5. Use description and dialogue purposefully</h3>
<p>Specific details help readers imagine the scene. Dialogue can reveal character, show disagreement or move the plot forward. However, long descriptions and conversations should not distract from the central events.</p>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> I was frightened. I ran. I saw a person. I was more frightened.</p>
<p><strong>Stronger:</strong> Footsteps sounded behind me on the empty path. I quickened my pace, but the footsteps grew louder. When I turned, a watchman called out that I had dropped my bag.</p>
<p>The stronger version uses connected actions and relevant details to create tension and then explain the situation.</p>

<h3>6. Check the finished narrative</h3>
<p>Confirm that the story answers the task, has a clear sequence, develops a central problem or experience, and ends convincingly. Check paragraphing, verb tense, dialogue punctuation, spelling and grammar.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Narrative Composition', 15),
  (1536, 1536, '<h2>Descriptive Composition</h2>
<p>A descriptive composition creates a clear impression of a person, place, object, scene or experience through carefully chosen details. Its purpose is to help the reader imagine the subject, not merely to list its features.</p>

<h3>1. Observe before describing</h3>
<p>Before writing, identify the subject and the details that make it distinctive.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sight:</strong> colour, shape, size, movement and appearance.</li>
<li><strong>Sound:</strong> voices, footsteps, birds, machinery or silence.</li>
<li><strong>Smell:</strong> fresh rain, cooking food, flowers or smoke.</li>
<li><strong>Touch:</strong> roughness, smoothness, warmth, coldness or texture.</li>
<li><strong>Taste:</strong> use when it is relevant to the subject.</li>
</ul>
<p>Choose details that matter. A description of a busy market may benefit from sounds, movement and smells, while a description of a person may focus on appearance, behaviour and distinctive habits.</p>

<h3>2. Use precise language</h3>
<p>General words often create weak descriptions. Replace vague expressions with accurate details.</p>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> The room was nice and had many things.</p>
<p><strong>Stronger:</strong> Morning light fell across the wooden desks, and neatly arranged exercise books covered the front table.</p>
<p>The stronger version gives the reader specific details to imagine.</p>

<h3>3. Use figurative language carefully</h3>
<p>Similes, metaphors and personification can make a description vivid when they suit the subject.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Simile:</strong> The clouds hung like grey blankets above the hills.</li>
<li><strong>Metaphor:</strong> The market was a sea of moving bodies.</li>
<li><strong>Personification:</strong> The wind rattled the windows impatiently.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not force figurative language into every sentence. Accurate, simple description is often more effective than a string of comparisons.</p>

<h3>4. Organise details</h3>
<p>Choose a clear order instead of jumping randomly between details. You could describe a place from the entrance to the back, from near to far, or from the general scene to one important detail.</p>
<p>For a person, you might move from general appearance to clothing, expression and behaviour. The order should help the reader build a clear mental picture.</p>

<h3>5. Build a consistent impression</h3>
<p>Details should work together to create the intended mood or impression. If you are describing a peaceful morning, rushing traffic and blaring horns may contradict that mood unless the contrast is deliberate.</p>

<h3>6. Revise the description</h3>
<p>Remove repeated adjectives, vague phrases and irrelevant details. Check that each paragraph develops a distinct part of the description and that the reader can identify the subject clearly.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Descriptive Composition', 15),
  (1537, 1537, '<h2>Expository Composition</h2>
<p>An expository composition explains a topic, process, concept or situation clearly. Its main purpose is to help the reader understand something through organised information and explanations.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the task</h3>
<p>Expository questions often ask you to explain how something works, describe causes and effects, discuss the importance of a subject or show how a process takes place.</p>
<p>Underline the instruction word and the topic. If the question asks for causes, focus on causes. If it asks how a process works, explain the stages in a sensible order.</p>

<h3>2. Plan the explanation</h3>
<p>Before writing, identify the main points needed to answer the question. Arrange them in a logical order, such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>general explanation of the topic;</li>
<li>first main point and its explanation;</li>
<li>additional points supported by examples or evidence;</li>
<li>a conclusion that brings the explanation together.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Explain rather than merely list</h3>
<p><strong>Topic:</strong> Causes of soil erosion.</p>
<p><strong>Weak point:</strong> Cutting down trees causes soil erosion.</p>
<p><strong>Developed explanation:</strong> Cutting down trees exposes the soil because fewer roots hold it together. Rainwater can then carry loose soil away more easily, especially on sloping land.</p>
<p>A useful explanatory paragraph states a point, explains how or why it occurs, and adds an example or relevant detail when necessary.</p>

<h3>4. Use logical connections</h3>
<p>Connect ideas according to their relationship. Words such as ''because'' introduce reasons, ''as a result'' indicates consequences, and ''for example'' introduces an illustration. Use these expressions only when their meanings fit the ideas.</p>

<h3>5. Keep information accurate and relevant</h3>
<p>Include facts and examples that help explain the topic. Avoid unsupported claims, unnecessary stories and repeated points. If the task requires a process, make sure no essential stage is omitted.</p>

<h3>6. Conclude the explanation</h3>
<p>The conclusion should reinforce the main understanding or summarise the key points. It should not introduce an unrelated topic or simply repeat the introduction word for word.</p>

<h3>7. Check your work</h3>
<p>Ask whether a reader unfamiliar with the topic could follow your explanation. Check the order of ideas, clarity of examples, accuracy of statements, paragraph unity, grammar and spelling.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Expository Composition', 15),
  (1538, 1538, '<h2>Argumentative Composition</h2>
<p>An argumentative composition presents a clear position on an issue and supports it with reasons and relevant evidence. It aims to persuade readers through logical reasoning rather than unsupported opinion or personal attacks.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the question</h3>
<p>Identify the issue being debated and determine what the question requires. You may be asked to agree, disagree, discuss both sides or give your own view. Your plan should respond directly to that instruction.</p>

<h3>2. Establish a clear position</h3>
<p>Your position, or central claim, tells the reader what you are arguing.</p>
<p><strong>Issue:</strong> Should schools provide more time for independent reading?</p>
<p><strong>Clear position:</strong> Schools should provide more time for independent reading because it can strengthen comprehension, vocabulary and independent study habits.</p>
<p>The position gives the composition direction and identifies the main reasons to develop.</p>

<h3>3. Build each argument</h3>
<p>A useful argument paragraph contains:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Claim:</strong> the point supporting your position.</li>
<li><strong>Reasoning:</strong> why the claim matters or how it works.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence or example:</strong> relevant information that supports the reasoning.</li>
<li><strong>Link:</strong> how the point strengthens the overall position.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not invent statistics or present an unsupported assumption as an established fact. A relevant, clearly explained example is more useful than an impressive but unreliable figure.</p>

<h3>4. Consider opposing views fairly</h3>
<p>A strong argument recognises reasonable objections and responds to them. For example, some may argue that independent reading takes time away from other lessons. You could acknowledge the concern and explain how a planned reading period can complement rather than replace essential instruction.</p>
<p>Represent the opposing view accurately. Do not distort it just to make your own position appear stronger.</p>

<h3>5. Organise the composition</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Introduction:</strong> introduce the issue and state your position or direction.</li>
<li><strong>Body:</strong> develop one main reason per paragraph, supported by explanation and relevant evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Counterargument and response:</strong> address an important opposing view when appropriate.</li>
<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> reinforce your position and bring the main reasons together.</li>
</ol>

<h3>6. Use persuasive language responsibly</h3>
<p>Words such as ''therefore'', ''however'' and ''consequently'' can make relationships between ideas clear. Avoid insults, exaggerated certainty and emotional language that substitutes for reasoning.</p>

<h3>7. Check the strength of the argument</h3>
<p>Ask whether each claim has support, whether the reasoning follows logically, whether the evidence is relevant, and whether you have addressed the exact question. A confident tone cannot compensate for weak reasoning.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Argumentative Composition', 15),
  (1539, 1539, '<h2>Editing and Proofreading</h2>
<p>Editing and proofreading improve a piece of writing, but they focus on different levels. <strong>Editing</strong> improves meaning, organisation, clarity and sentence construction. <strong>Proofreading</strong> checks for remaining errors in spelling, punctuation, grammar and presentation.</p>

<h3>1. Edit in stages</h3>
<p>Checking everything at once makes errors easier to miss. Use a deliberate order:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Purpose and content:</strong> Does the composition answer the question and include the necessary information?</li>
<li><strong>Organisation:</strong> Do the paragraphs follow a logical order and develop their main ideas?</li>
<li><strong>Clarity:</strong> Are sentences precise, complete and easy to understand?</li>
<li><strong>Grammar:</strong> Are tense, subject–verb agreement, pronouns and sentence structures correct?</li>
<li><strong>Mechanics:</strong> Are spelling, capitalisation and punctuation accurate?</li>
</ol>

<h3>2. Correct the error, not just the appearance</h3>
<p><strong>Original:</strong> The students was late because they misses the bus.</p>
<p><strong>Corrected:</strong> The students were late because they missed the bus.</p>
<p>''Students'' is plural, so it takes ''were''. The past-tense verb ''missed'' is needed to keep the sentence consistent with the past event.</p>

<h3>3. Improve unclear or wordy sentences</h3>
<p><strong>Original:</strong> Due to the fact that it was raining, we decided to remain inside.</p>
<p><strong>Revised:</strong> Because it was raining, we decided to remain inside.</p>
<p>The revised sentence keeps the meaning while removing unnecessary words. However, do not shorten a sentence if doing so removes information needed for accuracy.</p>

<h3>4. Check common problem areas</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sentence fragments:</strong> incomplete sentences presented as complete ones.</li>
<li><strong>Run-on sentences:</strong> independent clauses joined incorrectly.</li>
<li><strong>Tense shifts:</strong> changes in tense without a clear reason.</li>
<li><strong>Agreement errors:</strong> subjects and verbs that do not match.</li>
<li><strong>Pronoun reference:</strong> pronouns whose meaning is unclear.</li>
<li><strong>Punctuation errors:</strong> missing full stops, misplaced commas or incorrect apostrophes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Proofread carefully</h3>
<p>Read slowly, checking one type of error at a time. Reading aloud can reveal awkward sentences and missing words. Reading from the last sentence towards the first can help you focus on individual sentences, although you should still read the whole composition normally to check meaning and flow.</p>

<h3>6. Keep the writer''s meaning</h3>
<p>Editing should improve the writing without changing the intended message accidentally. After making corrections, reread the complete composition to ensure the revised sentences still connect logically and answer the original task.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Editing and Proofreading', 15),
  (1540, 1540, '<h2>Informal Letters</h2>
<p>An informal letter is a personal message written to someone with whom the writer has a familiar relationship, such as a friend, relative or classmate. Its language is usually friendly and natural, but it should still be clear, organised and appropriate to the purpose.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the purpose</h3>
<p>Before writing, identify the recipient, the reason for writing and the information the recipient needs. You might be writing to share news, describe an experience, offer advice, congratulate someone or invite a friend to an event.</p>

<h3>2. Structure of an informal letter</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Writer''s address:</strong> traditionally placed at the top right-hand side.</li>
<li><strong>Date:</strong> written below the address.</li>
<li><strong>Salutation:</strong> for example, Dear Amina,</li>
<li><strong>Opening:</strong> greet the recipient and establish the reason for writing.</li>
<li><strong>Body:</strong> develop the message in organised paragraphs.</li>
<li><strong>Closing remarks:</strong> finish the message naturally.</li>
<li><strong>Subscription and name:</strong> for example, Your friend, followed by the writer''s name.</li>
</ol>

<h3>3. Example</h3>
<p><strong>12 River Road</strong><br>
<strong>Embu</strong><br>
<strong>9 October 2026</strong></p>
<p>Dear Wanjiku,</p>
<p>How have you been? I hope your studies are going well. I am writing to tell you about the science exhibition we attended at school last week.</p>
<p>Our class displayed a model that demonstrated how rainwater can be collected for household use. Preparing it taught us the importance of planning carefully and working together. I wish you had been there to see the other projects as well.</p>
<p>Please write back and tell me how things are going at your school. I look forward to hearing from you.</p>
<p>Your friend,<br>
Kamau</p>

<h3>4. Choose an appropriate tone</h3>
<p>Informal writing can use contractions such as ''I''m'' and ''we''ve'' when they sound natural. Friendly expressions are suitable, but avoid slang that may confuse the recipient or make the message unsuitable for the occasion.</p>

<h3>5. Organise the body</h3>
<p>Give each paragraph a clear purpose. For example, one paragraph can explain why you are writing, another can describe the event or news, and a final paragraph can ask questions or explain what you expect next.</p>

<h3>6. Check before sending</h3>
<p>Confirm that you have answered every part of the task, included the expected letter features, organised the information logically and checked spelling, grammar and punctuation. A friendly tone does not excuse unclear writing.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Informal Letters', 15),
  (1541, 1541, '<h2>Formal Letters</h2>
<p>A formal letter is written for an official, professional or public purpose. It may be addressed to a school principal, organisation, company, public office or person with whom the writer has a formal relationship.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the purpose and recipient</h3>
<p>Common purposes include making a request, raising a concern, providing information, applying for a service or responding to an official matter. The purpose determines the details that belong in the letter.</p>

<h3>2. Structure of a formal letter</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Writer''s address:</strong> placed at the top, commonly on the right.</li>
<li><strong>Date:</strong> written below the writer''s address.</li>
<li><strong>Recipient''s address:</strong> the name or title and address of the person or organisation, usually on the left.</li>
<li><strong>Salutation:</strong> for example, Dear Sir/Madam, or Dear Mr Otieno, where appropriate.</li>
<li><strong>Subject or reference line:</strong> a brief statement of the purpose, when suitable or required.</li>
<li><strong>Body:</strong> a clear opening, developed explanation and appropriate closing paragraph.</li>
<li><strong>Formal subscription:</strong> for example, Yours faithfully or Yours sincerely, followed by the writer''s name.</li>
</ol>
<p>Exact placement and conventions can vary by institution and style. Follow the format required by the task or examination.</p>

<h3>3. Example: a request for information</h3>
<p><strong>12 River Road</strong><br>
<strong>Embu</strong><br>
<strong>9 October 2026</strong></p>
<p>The Principal<br>
Green Valley Secondary School<br>
P.O. Box 120<br>
Embu</p>
<p>Dear Sir/Madam,</p>
<p><strong>RE: REQUEST FOR INFORMATION ABOUT THE SCHOOL LIBRARY</strong></p>
<p>I am writing to enquire about the arrangements for learners who wish to use the school library after lessons.</p>
<p>I would appreciate information about the opening hours, borrowing procedures and any rules learners must observe when using the library.</p>
<p>Thank you for your time and assistance. I look forward to your response.</p>
<p>Yours faithfully,<br>
Kamau Mwangi</p>

<h3>4. Maintain a formal tone</h3>
<p>Use respectful, precise language. Avoid slang, texting abbreviations and unnecessarily emotional expressions. Politeness does not require excessively complicated vocabulary.</p>

<h3>5. Make the purpose clear</h3>
<p>The opening paragraph should tell the recipient why you are writing. The body should provide necessary details in a logical order. The final paragraph should state the action requested, offer thanks where appropriate or explain the next step.</p>

<h3>6. Select the closing correctly</h3>
<p>A common convention is to use ''Yours faithfully'' when the salutation is ''Dear Sir/Madam'' and ''Yours sincerely'' when addressing the recipient by name, such as ''Dear Mr Otieno''. Some organisations use different conventions, so follow the instructions provided.</p>

<h3>7. Proofread carefully</h3>
<p>Check the recipient''s details, date, subject line, paragraph organisation, factual accuracy, tone and punctuation. An incorrectly addressed letter or unclear request may prevent the reader from acting on the message.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formal Letters', 15),
  (1542, 1542, '<h2>Application Letters</h2>
<p>An application letter is a formal letter in which a person applies for a position, opportunity, admission, placement or other specified consideration. It introduces the applicant and explains why they are suitable for the opportunity.</p>

<h3>1. Read the opportunity carefully</h3>
<p>Identify the position or opportunity, the organisation, the required qualifications, the deadline and any documents requested. Your letter should respond to the stated requirements rather than make general claims about yourself.</p>

<h3>2. Structure of an application letter</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Sender''s address and date.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Recipient''s address.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Formal salutation.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Subject line:</strong> name the position or opportunity.</li>
<li><strong>Opening paragraph:</strong> state what you are applying for and where you learnt about it, if relevant.</li>
<li><strong>Supporting paragraph:</strong> connect your qualifications, skills and experience to the requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Closing paragraph:</strong> express interest and refer to any requested supporting documents.</li>
<li><strong>Formal subscription and name.</strong></li>
</ol>

<h3>3. Demonstrate suitability with evidence</h3>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> I am a very hardworking and talented person.</p>
<p><strong>Stronger:</strong> As secretary of our science club, I recorded meeting decisions, prepared notices and helped organise two school exhibitions.</p>
<p>The stronger statement gives specific evidence of skills instead of relying on unsupported praise.</p>

<h3>4. Match your evidence to the opportunity</h3>
<p>If the position requires communication, organisation or teamwork, select truthful examples that demonstrate those qualities. School projects, clubs, volunteering and relevant responsibilities can provide evidence even when you have limited formal work experience.</p>

<h3>5. Be honest and concise</h3>
<p>Do not invent qualifications, experience, references or achievements. Include only information relevant to the opportunity and avoid repeating the same claim in different words.</p>

<h3>6. Mention supporting documents accurately</h3>
<p>If the advert requests a curriculum vitae, certificates or other documents, state that they are enclosed or attached only when you are actually submitting them. Follow the application instructions carefully.</p>

<h3>7. Final check</h3>
<p>Confirm that the position title and organisation are correct, the letter explains your suitability, the tone is professional and the requested details are included. Check names, dates, spelling and any attachment requirements before sending.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Application Letters', 15),
  (1543, 1543, '<h2>Emails and Digital Messages</h2>
<p>Emails and digital messages allow people to exchange information electronically. Their structure and tone depend on the purpose, recipient, urgency and communication platform.</p>

<h3>1. Choose the right channel and tone</h3>
<p>An email to a principal, employer or organisation usually requires a formal tone. A message to a close friend may be more relaxed. Even in informal messages, clarity and respect remain important.</p>

<h3>2. Structure of an email</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recipient:</strong> enter the correct email address.</li>
<li><strong>Subject:</strong> summarise the purpose in a few meaningful words.</li>
<li><strong>Greeting:</strong> use a suitable salutation.</li>
<li><strong>Opening:</strong> state why you are writing.</li>
<li><strong>Body:</strong> provide the necessary information in short, organised paragraphs.</li>
<li><strong>Action or closing:</strong> state what response or action is needed, if any.</li>
<li><strong>Sign-off:</strong> include your name or an appropriate closing.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Write useful subject lines</h3>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> Hello</p>
<p><strong>Clearer:</strong> Request for Permission to Attend the Science Workshop</p>
<p>A useful subject line helps the recipient understand the purpose before opening the message.</p>

<h3>4. Keep the message concise and complete</h3>
<p>Include the context the recipient needs, the relevant facts and any action requested. Avoid unnecessary background, repeated points and ambiguous phrases such as ''please deal with that thing''. Name the item or action clearly.</p>

<h3>5. Use To, Cc and Bcc appropriately</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>To:</strong> the main recipient expected to read or act on the message.</li>
<li><strong>Cc:</strong> people who should receive a visible copy for information.</li>
<li><strong>Bcc:</strong> recipients whose addresses should not be visible to other recipients in that email.</li>
</ul>
<p>Include only appropriate recipients. Do not expose other people''s contact details unnecessarily.</p>

<h3>6. Handle attachments carefully</h3>
<p>Mention an attachment when it is relevant and ensure the correct file is attached before sending. Use a clear filename, check that the file opens, and confirm that it contains the intended document.</p>

<h3>7. Communicate responsibly</h3>
<p>Check recipient addresses before sending. Avoid sharing confidential information with unintended recipients, and be cautious about suspicious links or requests for passwords. Digital messages can be forwarded or saved, so write with care.</p>

<h3>8. Proofread before sending</h3>
<p>Check the recipient, subject, tone, facts, requested action, attachments and spelling. A short message can still be complete when it answers the reader''s likely questions clearly.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Emails and Digital Messages', 15),
  (1544, 1544, '<h2>Curriculum Vitae (CV)</h2>
<p>A curriculum vitae (CV) is a document that presents a person''s education, skills, experience, achievements and relevant activities. It helps an organisation assess whether the applicant meets the requirements of an opportunity.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the purpose</h3>
<p>A CV is not a life story. Select information relevant to the opportunity and present it accurately. A learner applying for a school leadership role may emphasise responsibility and teamwork, while someone applying for a computer-related opportunity may highlight digital skills and relevant projects.</p>

<h3>2. Main sections of a CV</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Personal and contact details:</strong> full name, appropriate telephone number, email address and location where relevant.</li>
<li><strong>Personal profile:</strong> a short statement describing relevant strengths and goals.</li>
<li><strong>Education:</strong> schools or institutions attended and relevant qualifications or current studies.</li>
<li><strong>Skills:</strong> abilities relevant to the opportunity.</li>
<li><strong>Experience:</strong> relevant employment, volunteering, school responsibilities or projects.</li>
<li><strong>Achievements and activities:</strong> genuine accomplishments that demonstrate relevant abilities.</li>
<li><strong>Referees:</strong> people who can provide a reference, if requested or appropriate.</li>
</ol>
<p>The order and sections may vary according to the opportunity. Do not include unnecessary personal details such as national identification numbers or private family information.</p>

<h3>3. Write a focused profile</h3>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> I am a good person who wants any job and can do everything.</p>
<p><strong>Stronger:</strong> A secondary school learner with strong organisational skills, an interest in reading and experience supporting group activities. Seeking an opportunity to contribute responsibly to school library activities.</p>
<p>A good profile is brief, relevant and supported by the rest of the CV.</p>

<h3>4. Present education and experience honestly</h3>
<p>List education in a clear order, usually from the most recent to the earliest. Give accurate dates where available. If you have no paid employment, you may include relevant school projects, volunteering or responsibilities, provided you describe them truthfully.</p>

<h3>5. Show evidence of skills</h3>
<p>Instead of listing ''leadership'' alone, explain how you have demonstrated it. For example: ''Coordinated a four-person group project and ensured that each section was completed before the deadline.''</p>

<h3>6. Make the CV easy to read</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use clear headings and consistent formatting.</li>
<li>Use short bullet points for skills and responsibilities.</li>
<li>Keep descriptions concise and relevant.</li>
<li>Check spelling, grammar, dates and contact details.</li>
<li>Follow any length or format requirements in the advert.</li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Handle referees responsibly</h3>
<p>Provide referees only when requested or appropriate. Ask their permission before listing their contact details, and ensure the details are correct.</p>

<h3>8. Final accuracy check</h3>
<p>Every qualification, achievement and experience listed should be genuine. Tailor the CV to the opportunity without exaggerating your abilities or copying irrelevant details into every application.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Curriculum Vitae', 15),
  (1545, 1545, '<h2>Reports</h2>
<p>A report presents information about an event, investigation, activity or situation in a clear and organised form. Its structure and tone depend on the purpose, the intended reader and any instructions provided.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the purpose</h3>
<p>A report may describe what happened at an event, present findings from an investigation, explain a problem or recommend action. Before writing, establish:</p>
<ul>
<li>What is being reported?</li>
<li>Who will read the report?</li>
<li>What information or findings are required?</li>
<li>Is the writer expected to make recommendations?</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Common report structure</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Title:</strong> identifies the subject of the report.</li>
<li><strong>To/from/date:</strong> included when required by the reporting format.</li>
<li><strong>Introduction or purpose:</strong> explains why the report was prepared.</li>
<li><strong>Findings or main details:</strong> presents relevant facts in a logical order.</li>
<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> summarises what the findings indicate.</li>
<li><strong>Recommendations:</strong> suggests appropriate action when requested or useful.</li>
<li><strong>Name or signature:</strong> included where the format requires it.</li>
</ol>
<p>Not every report requires every section. Follow the task instructions and the conventions of the organisation.</p>

<h3>3. Separate facts from opinions</h3>
<p><strong>Factual:</strong> The meeting began at 2.00 p.m. and was attended by 18 learners.</p>
<p><strong>Opinion:</strong> The meeting was the most exciting event the school has ever held.</p>
<p>A report should distinguish observations and verifiable information from personal judgements. Opinions may be included when relevant, but they should be clearly presented and supported.</p>

<h3>4. Organise findings clearly</h3>
<p>Use headings, paragraphs, numbered points or tables when they help the reader locate information. Group related findings together instead of presenting them in the order they happened to come to mind.</p>

<h3>5. Make recommendations practical</h3>
<p>A recommendation should respond to a finding. If a report finds that rubbish bins are insufficient, a relevant recommendation might be to place additional bins in the affected areas and establish a regular collection schedule.</p>

<h3>6. Use an objective tone</h3>
<p>Prefer clear, neutral language. Avoid insults, exaggerated claims and unsupported accusations. Identify limitations when information is incomplete rather than pretending to know more than the evidence establishes.</p>

<h3>7. Review the report</h3>
<p>Check that the title matches the task, the facts are accurate, the findings answer the purpose and any recommendations follow logically. Proofread names, dates, figures, grammar and headings.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reports', 15),
  (1546, 1546, '<h2>Notices and Announcements</h2>
<p>A notice or announcement communicates important information to a specific audience. It should make the key message easy to find and understand quickly.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the difference</h3>
<p>A <strong>notice</strong> is usually a written message displayed or circulated to inform people about an event, change, rule or opportunity. An <strong>announcement</strong> may be written or spoken and often communicates information that people need to know or act on.</p>

<h3>2. Essential information</h3>
<p>Depending on the purpose, include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Heading:</strong> identifies the subject.</li>
<li><strong>Audience:</strong> states who the message concerns.</li>
<li><strong>Main message:</strong> explains the event, change or information.</li>
<li><strong>Date and time:</strong> where relevant.</li>
<li><strong>Venue or location:</strong> where relevant.</li>
<li><strong>Required action:</strong> what the audience should do.</li>
<li><strong>Contact or issuing authority:</strong> when needed.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Example of a notice</h3>
<p><strong>GREEN VALLEY SECONDARY SCHOOL</strong></p>
<p><strong>NOTICE: SCHOOL CLEAN-UP</strong></p>
<p>All learners are informed that a school clean-up will take place on Saturday, 17 October, from 8.00 a.m. to 11.00 a.m. Learners should report to the assembly ground and bring suitable cleaning materials where possible. Class representatives should submit attendance lists to the teacher in charge.</p>
<p>Issued by: Environmental Club</p>
<p><em>Use a date, time, venue and organiser that match the actual task or event. The example is illustrative.</em></p>

<h3>4. Be brief but complete</h3>
<p>A notice is not an essay. Include only information needed to understand the message and act on it. However, excessive brevity can create confusion if the date, venue, audience or required action is missing.</p>

<h3>5. Use clear and direct language</h3>
<p>Prefer ''Submit your form to the office by Friday'' to ''It would be appreciated if the form could somehow be made available at some point before the end of the week.'' The direct version identifies the action, destination and deadline.</p>

<h3>6. Match the audience and channel</h3>
<p>A notice for learners may use straightforward school-related language. A public announcement may need to explain unfamiliar terms or provide additional directions. Spoken announcements should be paced clearly and repeat essential details when necessary.</p>

<h3>7. Check the details</h3>
<p>Verify dates, times, venue, names, deadlines and instructions before displaying or delivering the message. A polished notice with an incorrect date is still ineffective.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Notices and Announcements', 15),
  (1547, 1547, '<h2>Invitations</h2>
<p>An invitation asks a person or group to attend an event or take part in an activity. An effective invitation makes the occasion clear and gives recipients the information they need to respond or attend.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the occasion and audience</h3>
<p>Consider whether the invitation is formal or informal, who will receive it and what relationship the writer has with them. A wedding invitation, a school event invitation and a message inviting a friend to a birthday gathering require different tones and levels of formality.</p>

<h3>2. Essential invitation details</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Host or organiser:</strong> who is inviting the recipient.</li>
<li><strong>Occasion:</strong> what the event is.</li>
<li><strong>Date and time:</strong> when it will happen.</li>
<li><strong>Venue:</strong> where it will take place.</li>
<li><strong>Recipient or audience:</strong> who is invited, where necessary.</li>
<li><strong>Response instructions:</strong> whether the recipient should confirm attendance.</li>
<li><strong>Additional details:</strong> dress code, items to bring or other requirements, when relevant.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Formal invitations</h3>
<p>Formal invitations use respectful, carefully chosen language and a clear layout. They may be issued by an institution or on behalf of an organiser.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>
<p>The Principal and staff of Green Valley Secondary School request the pleasure of your company at the school''s annual prize-giving ceremony on Friday, 23 October, at 10.00 a.m. in the school hall. Kindly confirm your attendance with the school office by 16 October.</p>
<p><em>This is an illustrative invitation; use the actual event details when completing a task.</em></p>

<h3>4. Informal invitations</h3>
<p>An informal invitation can sound warmer and more personal. For example: ''Hi Amina, I''d love you to join us for my birthday lunch on Saturday at 1.00 p.m. at our home. Please let me know if you can come.''</p>

<h3>5. Make attendance expectations clear</h3>
<p>If recipients must confirm, provide a deadline and a contact method. If the event is limited to a particular group, state that clearly. Avoid ambiguous phrases such as ''come sometime in the afternoon'' when a precise time is needed.</p>

<h3>6. Responding to an invitation</h3>
<p>A response should acknowledge the invitation and clearly accept or decline. When declining, a brief expression of thanks and regret is usually sufficient. Do not imply acceptance if you cannot attend.</p>

<h3>7. Proofread before sending</h3>
<p>Check the spelling of names, event title, date, time, venue, contact details and response deadline. The tone should match the occasion and the relationship with the recipient.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Invitations', 15),
  (1548, 1548, '<h2>Agendas and Minutes</h2>
  <p>An <strong>agenda</strong> is a planned list of items to be discussed during a meeting. <strong>Minutes</strong> are the official written record of what happened, what was decided, and what actions were assigned.</p>

  <h3>1. Writing an agenda</h3>
  <p>An agenda helps a meeting stay organised. It normally includes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Name of the organisation or group.</li>
    <li>Type of meeting, date, time and venue.</li>
    <li>Items in the order they will be discussed.</li>
    <li>Any other business and the closing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Example:</strong></p>
  <p><strong>MWANGA SECONDARY SCHOOL ENVIRONMENT CLUB</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Agenda for the meeting on 15 October 2026 at 3.30 p.m. in Room 4</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Opening and apologies</li>
    <li>Confirmation of the previous minutes</li>
    <li>Review of the school compound clean-up</li>
    <li>Planning the next environmental activity</li>
    <li>Any other business</li>
    <li>Date of the next meeting and adjournment</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>2. Writing minutes</h3>
  <p>Minutes record the meeting rather than predict what will be discussed. A useful format includes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Meeting title, date, time and venue.</li>
    <li>Members present and apologies received.</li>
    <li>Numbered agenda items and a concise record of each discussion.</li>
    <li>Decisions made and actions assigned, including responsible people and deadlines.</li>
    <li>Time of adjournment and, where required, the chairperson''s confirmation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Example entry:</strong> <em>Agenda item 3: Clean-up exercise. Members agreed to organise a clean-up on 22 October. The secretary will prepare the notice by 18 October.</em></p>

  <h3>3. Important distinctions</h3>
  <p>An agenda says what the meeting intends to discuss; minutes say what actually happened. Do not invent decisions, include personal opinions as facts, or write every spoken word. Use clear, neutral language and record agreed actions accurately.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Agendas and Minutes', 15),
  (1549, 1549, '<h2>Instructions and Procedures</h2>
  <p><strong>Instructions</strong> tell someone what to do. A <strong>procedure</strong> presents a sequence of actions for completing a task or achieving a result. You encounter them in experiments, recipes, school rules, equipment guides and practical activities.</p>

  <h3>1. A clear structure</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Title or purpose:</strong> state what the instructions will help the reader do.</li>
    <li><strong>Materials or requirements:</strong> list what is needed, if applicable.</li>
    <li><strong>Steps:</strong> present actions in the order they must happen.</li>
    <li><strong>Warning or final check:</strong> include important precautions or how to confirm completion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Use precise language</h3>
  <p>Instructions commonly use the imperative form of a verb: <em>Place, measure, connect, stir, record</em>. Begin each step with a clear action when possible.</p>
  <p>Vague: <em>Do the thing properly and then continue.</em></p>
  <p>Precise: <em>Measure 100 ml of water and pour it into the clean container.</em></p>

  <h3>3. Sequence and logical order</h3>
  <p>Words such as <em>first, next, after that, before, finally</em> help readers follow a process. However, numbering the steps may be clearer than using a sequence word in every sentence.</p>
  <p><strong>Example: How to submit a written assignment</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write your name, class and the assignment title on the first page.</li>
    <li>Check that every question has been answered.</li>
    <li>Correct spelling and punctuation errors.</li>
    <li>Arrange the pages in the correct order.</li>
    <li>Submit the assignment to the teacher before the stated deadline.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>4. Avoid common errors</h3>
  <p>Do not leave out a necessary step, mix up the order, use unclear pronouns such as <em>it</em> when the object is uncertain, or combine several complicated actions in one sentence. Use warnings where an action could cause harm. Instructions should be complete enough for the intended reader to follow without guessing.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Instructions and Procedures', 15),
  (1550, 1550, '<h2>Speeches and Presentations</h2>
  <p>A speech is spoken communication prepared for an audience. A presentation may combine speaking with visual aids, demonstrations or other supporting material. Both require a clear purpose, an organised message and language suited to the listeners.</p>

  <h3>1. Structure of a speech</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Opening:</strong> greet the audience appropriately, introduce the topic and establish interest.</li>
    <li><strong>Body:</strong> develop the main points in a logical order, using explanations, examples or evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> summarise the central message and finish with a suitable final statement or call to action.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Know the audience and purpose</h3>
  <p>A speech to fellow learners may use a different tone from one delivered to school administrators. Before writing, ask: Who will listen? What do they already know? What should they understand, feel or do after listening?</p>

  <h3>3. Develop and connect ideas</h3>
  <p>Use clear topic sentences and transitions such as <em>firstly, in addition, however, as a result</em> and <em>finally</em>. Support claims with relevant reasons or examples. Avoid repeating the same point in different words merely to make the speech longer.</p>

  <h3>4. Delivery</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Speak audibly and at a pace the audience can follow.</li>
    <li>Use pauses to separate ideas and emphasise important points.</li>
    <li>Maintain appropriate eye contact rather than reading every word from a page.</li>
    <li>Use natural gestures and an expression suited to the message.</li>
    <li>Practise names, difficult words and transitions before presenting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5. Visual aids</h3>
  <p>Slides, charts and posters should support the spoken message, not replace it. Use readable text, accurate information and only the details needed to explain a point. Do not turn slides into full paragraphs that the audience must read while you speak.</p>

  <p><strong>Example opening:</strong> <em>Good morning, fellow learners and teachers. Today, I would like to explain three practical ways our school can reduce the amount of food wasted at lunchtime.</em></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Speeches and Presentations', 15),
  (1551, 1551, '<h2>Listening for Main Ideas</h2>
  <p>The <strong>main idea</strong> is the central message a speaker wants listeners to understand. Supporting details explain, prove or illustrate that message. Listening for the main idea means understanding the overall point rather than trying to remember every word.</p>

  <h3>1. Identify the purpose</h3>
  <p>Before or while listening, ask what the speaker is mainly doing: explaining a process, reporting an event, persuading an audience, giving an opinion or describing a problem.</p>

  <h3>2. Listen for repeated and emphasised ideas</h3>
  <p>Speakers often signal important points through repetition, emphasis, summaries and phrases such as <em>the main reason is, what matters most, in summary</em> or <em>the key point</em>. These clues are useful, but the whole message still matters.</p>

  <h3>3. Separate the main idea from details</h3>
  <p>Consider this short spoken message:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>Our school library is being used less frequently because many learners do not know which resources are available. From next week, the librarian will introduce each class to the catalogue and organise short library visits. The aim is to help learners find useful reading materials more easily.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p><strong>Main idea:</strong> The school is introducing activities to help learners use the library more effectively.</p>
  <p><strong>Supporting details:</strong> Learners will be shown the catalogue, and short class visits will begin next week.</p>

  <h3>4. Summarise without changing the message</h3>
  <p>A useful summary captures the speaker''s central point in one sentence. Do not mistake an interesting example, a minor date or a single statistic for the overall message. If a speaker develops several related points, identify the idea that connects them.</p>

  <h3>5. Check your interpretation</h3>
  <p>Ask whether your summary represents the whole message and whether the details you selected support it. If the speaker gives a reason and a proposed solution, include both when they are central to the message.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Listening for Main Ideas', 15),
  (1552, 1552, '<h2>Listening for Details</h2>
  <p>Listening for details means identifying specific information in a spoken message, such as names, dates, places, quantities, reasons, instructions and conditions. It differs from listening for the main idea, which focuses on the overall message.</p>

  <h3>1. Know which details matter</h3>
  <p>Before listening, consider what information you need. For an announcement, you may need the event, date, time, venue and action required. For an explanation, you may need the reasons, examples or sequence of steps.</p>

  <h3>2. Listen for precise information</h3>
  <p>Pay attention to words that change meaning, especially negatives and restrictions such as <em>not, only, except, before</em> and <em>unless</em>. Distinguish similar numbers and dates, and do not replace what was said with what you expected to hear.</p>

  <h3>3. Example: a school announcement</h3>
  <blockquote>
    <p>All members of the science club should meet in the laboratory at 2.15 p.m. on Thursday. Bring your completed project plans, but do not bring chemicals. The teacher will explain the safety arrangements before the practical session begins.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Who?</strong> Science club members.</li>
    <li><strong>Where?</strong> The laboratory.</li>
    <li><strong>When?</strong> Thursday at 2.15 p.m.</li>
    <li><strong>Bring?</strong> Completed project plans.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not bring?</strong> Chemicals.</li>
    <li><strong>What happens next?</strong> The teacher explains the safety arrangements.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Take brief, accurate notes</h3>
  <p>Record keywords, numbers and relationships rather than attempting to write every word. For example: <em>Science club — Thu, 2.15 p.m. — lab — bring plans — no chemicals — safety briefing.</em> Check that abbreviations remain understandable.</p>

  <h3>5. Verify before answering</h3>
  <p>Compare each answer with the information actually given. Do not confuse the time of a meeting with its duration, the person making an announcement with the person expected to act, or a suggestion with a confirmed decision.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Listening for Specific Details', 15),
  (1553, 1553, '<h2>Listening to Instructions</h2>
  <p>Listening to instructions means understanding what a speaker wants you to do, the order of actions, and any conditions or restrictions that apply. Successful listeners identify both the action and the details needed to perform it correctly.</p>

  <h3>1. Identify the action words</h3>
  <p>Listen for verbs such as <em>write, collect, underline, compare, submit</em> and <em>explain</em>. These tell you what action is required. Pay attention to the object of each verb: ''underline the verbs'' is different from ''underline the nouns''.</p>

  <h3>2. Follow the sequence</h3>
  <p>Words such as <em>first, next, before, after, then</em> and <em>finally</em> show the order of actions. Some instructions depend on earlier steps, so changing the sequence may lead to an incorrect result.</p>

  <h3>3. Notice conditions and restrictions</h3>
  <p>Words such as <em>only, unless, do not, except</em> and <em>until</em> can change what you must do. Do not ignore a restriction simply because you remember the main action.</p>

  <h3>4. Example</h3>
  <blockquote>
    <p>Read the passage silently. Underline two unfamiliar words. Look up their meanings in a dictionary, then write one original sentence using each word. Do not copy the example sentences from the dictionary.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the passage silently.</li>
    <li>Underline two unfamiliar words.</li>
    <li>Look up their meanings.</li>
    <li>Write an original sentence for each word.</li>
    <li>Avoid copying dictionary examples.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>5. Check your understanding</h3>
  <p>If an instruction is unclear, ask a specific question before beginning. You can also repeat the key steps in your own words to confirm that you understood them correctly. Do not assume that an example is compulsory unless the speaker says so.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Following Instructions', 15),
  (1554, 1554, '<h2>Speaker Purpose and Attitude</h2>
  <p>A speaker''s <strong>purpose</strong> is the reason for speaking. A speaker''s <strong>attitude</strong> is the feeling or viewpoint conveyed towards the subject or audience. Identifying both helps listeners understand not only what is said, but why and how it is said.</p>

  <h3>1. Common purposes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Inform:</strong> provide facts or updates.</li>
    <li><strong>Explain:</strong> make a process or idea understandable.</li>
    <li><strong>Persuade:</strong> encourage an audience to accept a view or take action.</li>
    <li><strong>Warn:</strong> draw attention to danger or possible consequences.</li>
    <li><strong>Entertain:</strong> amuse or engage an audience.</li>
    <li><strong>Instruct:</strong> tell an audience how to do something.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Recognising attitude</h3>
  <p>A speaker may sound enthusiastic, doubtful, disappointed, concerned, confident, sarcastic or sympathetic. Attitude can be conveyed through word choice, emphasis, pace, pauses and tone of voice.</p>

  <h3>3. Use evidence, not guesswork</h3>
  <p>Consider this statement: <em>We have received three reminders about keeping the classroom clean, yet litter is still being left under the desks. We must take this responsibility seriously.</em></p>
  <p><strong>Likely purpose:</strong> persuade learners to keep the classroom clean.</p>
  <p><strong>Likely attitude:</strong> concerned or disappointed.</p>
  <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> the repeated reminders, the continued littering and the phrase ''take this responsibility seriously''.</p>

  <h3>4. Separate purpose from attitude</h3>
  <p>Purpose answers, ''What is the speaker trying to achieve?'' Attitude answers, ''How does the speaker feel or what viewpoint do they express?'' A speaker may use a calm tone to warn people, or an enthusiastic tone to explain a topic.</p>

  <h3>5. Avoid unsupported interpretations</h3>
  <p>When explaining your answer, point to words or vocal clues that support it. Do not label a speaker as angry merely because they disagree, or as persuasive simply because they speak loudly. The message and its context matter.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Speaker Purpose and Attitude', 15),
  (1555, 1555, '<h2>Inferring Spoken Meaning</h2>
  <p>To <strong>infer</strong> is to work out a meaning that is suggested rather than directly stated. Listeners combine spoken words with context, tone, emphasis and relevant background knowledge to understand implied messages.</p>

  <h3>1. Use clues from the message</h3>
  <p>Consider what the speaker says, the situation in which it is said, and how the speaker says it. An inference should be supported by clues, not simply by what you imagine might be happening.</p>

  <h3>2. Recognise implied meaning</h3>
  <p>If someone says, <em>The classroom is rather dark; could someone open the curtains?</em>, the speaker is not merely describing the light. The request implies that opening the curtains may improve visibility.</p>

  <h3>3. Interpret tone in context</h3>
  <p>The words <em>That was helpful</em> may express sincere appreciation. Depending on tone and context, they may also be sarcastic. The words alone may not be enough to decide; listen for vocal emphasis and consider what happened immediately before the statement.</p>

  <h3>4. Understand indirect requests</h3>
  <p>Speakers sometimes make requests indirectly to sound polite. <em>Could you move your bag?</em> is usually a request, not a question about physical ability. <em>It is getting late</em> may suggest that a meeting should end, depending on the situation.</p>

  <h3>5. Justify your inference</h3>
  <p>Use the pattern: <strong>inference + clue + explanation</strong>. For example: ''The learner is probably uncertain because they pause repeatedly and ask whether the instructions are correct.'' Avoid presenting an inference as a confirmed fact when the evidence only makes it likely.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Inferring Spoken Meaning', 15),
  (1556, 1556, '<h2>Note-taking from Spoken Information</h2>
  <p>Note-taking from spoken information means recording the most useful ideas while someone speaks. Good notes preserve important meaning without attempting to write every word.</p>

  <h3>1. Decide what to capture</h3>
  <p>Listen for the main idea, key supporting points, names, dates, quantities, definitions, examples and actions required. What counts as important depends on the purpose: instructions require sequence and conditions, while a discussion may require arguments and evidence.</p>

  <h3>2. Use keywords and abbreviations</h3>
  <p>Short notes are often easier to write and review. For example:</p>
  <p><strong>Spoken message:</strong> The school garden project begins on Friday. Each group should bring two watering cans, and the agriculture teacher will demonstrate seed spacing before planting.</p>
  <p><strong>Notes:</strong> Garden project — Friday; each group: 2 watering cans; agriculture teacher: demonstrate seed spacing before planting.</p>
  <p>Use abbreviations only when you can recognise them later. Do not shorten important names, numbers or conditions so much that the meaning becomes unclear.</p>

  <h3>3. Organise information</h3>
  <p>Use headings, numbered steps, bullets or a simple table. Group related ideas together. For a cause-and-effect explanation, use arrows or labels; for a procedure, number the steps in order.</p>

  <h3>4. Listen while writing</h3>
  <p>Do not let one missed word prevent you from following the rest of the message. Leave a space and continue listening. Pay special attention to corrections, contrast words such as <em>however</em>, and restrictions such as <em>only</em> or <em>not</em>.</p>

  <h3>5. Review and complete your notes</h3>
  <p>After listening, expand unclear abbreviations, correct any mistakes and fill gaps using information you actually heard. Separate the speaker''s facts from your own questions or interpretations. Never invent missing details simply to make the notes look complete.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Note-taking from Spoken Information', 15),
  (1557, 1557, '<h2>Summarising Spoken Information</h2>
  <p>A spoken summary presents the most important ideas from a speech, announcement, discussion or explanation in a shorter form. It preserves the original meaning without including every example or repetition.</p>

  <h3>1. Identify the central message</h3>
  <p>First determine what the speaker is mainly communicating. Then identify the supporting points necessary to understand that message. Distinguish essential information from illustrations, repetition and unrelated details.</p>

  <h3>2. Select and combine key points</h3>
  <p>Group related ideas together and express them concisely. Preserve important names, dates, figures, reasons and conditions when they are essential to the message.</p>

  <h3>3. Use your own words</h3>
  <p>A summary should normally restate the information clearly rather than reproduce long sections word for word. Keep technical terms and exact figures where changing them would alter the meaning.</p>

  <h3>4. Example</h3>
  <p><strong>Spoken message:</strong> <em>Our school has recorded a high level of food waste. Some learners take more food than they can eat, while others leave fruit untouched. Starting next week, the dining hall will encourage smaller first servings, with additional portions available on request. Learners will also be reminded to take only what they can finish.</em></p>
  <p><strong>Summary:</strong> The school will reduce food waste by encouraging learners to take manageable portions and request more when needed.</p>

  <h3>5. Check the summary</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Does it capture the speaker''s central message?</li>
    <li>Does it preserve essential facts and conditions?</li>
    <li>Does it avoid adding unsupported opinions?</li>
    <li>Is it shorter and clearer than the original?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A summary is not a personal response. Save your own judgement or recommendations for a separate section if they are requested.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Summarising Spoken Information', 15),
  (1558, 1558, '<h2>Clear and Organised Speaking</h2>
  <p>Effective speaking communicates ideas so that listeners can understand them easily. A speaker needs a clear purpose, logically ordered points, suitable vocabulary and an audible, controlled delivery.</p>

  <h3>1. Organise before speaking</h3>
  <p>Decide what you want listeners to understand. Arrange your ideas in a useful order, such as problem then solution, cause then effect, or first step through to final step.</p>

  <h3>2. Use complete and precise ideas</h3>
  <p>Choose words that express your meaning accurately. Avoid vague expressions such as <em>that thing</em> or <em>do it somehow</em> when the audience needs a specific explanation. Use complete sentences when explaining a complex point.</p>

  <h3>3. Connect ideas</h3>
  <p>Link points using expressions such as <em>first, because, however, for example, therefore</em> and <em>finally</em>. These help listeners follow the relationship between ideas. Do not use linking words mechanically when no logical relationship exists.</p>

  <h3>4. Control your delivery</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Speak loudly enough for the intended audience.</li>
    <li>Use a moderate pace and pause between important ideas.</li>
    <li>Pronounce words as clearly as you can.</li>
    <li>Use natural eye contact and posture.</li>
    <li>Reduce unnecessary fillers such as repeated <em>um</em> or <em>you know</em>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5. Adapt to the listener</h3>
  <p>Explain unfamiliar terms, give relevant examples and check understanding when necessary. A technical explanation for a specialist may require different vocabulary from an explanation for a younger learner.</p>

  <p><strong>Example:</strong> Instead of saying, <em>Things get dirty and then people become sick</em>, say, <em>When drinking water becomes contaminated with harmful microorganisms, people may develop waterborne illnesses.</em> The second version expresses the relationship more precisely.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Clear Expression', 15),
  (1559, 1559, '<h2>Asking and Answering Questions</h2>
  <p>Asking questions helps speakers obtain information, clarify meaning and encourage discussion. Answering questions requires listening carefully, understanding what is being asked and responding directly.</p>

  <h3>1. Recognise question words</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Who:</strong> asks about a person or people.</li>
    <li><strong>What:</strong> asks about an object, action or idea.</li>
    <li><strong>When:</strong> asks about time.</li>
    <li><strong>Where:</strong> asks about place.</li>
    <li><strong>Why:</strong> asks for a reason.</li>
    <li><strong>How:</strong> asks about a method, manner or process.</li>
    <li><strong>Which:</strong> asks someone to identify from a set of choices.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Form clear questions</h3>
  <p>Use a question form that matches the information you need. For example, <em>When does the meeting begin?</em> asks for a time, while <em>Why does the meeting begin early?</em> asks for a reason.</p>

  <h3>3. Listen before answering</h3>
  <p>Identify the exact question and any limits it contains. If asked for two reasons, give two relevant reasons. If asked to compare, explain both sides and the difference between them.</p>

  <h3>4. Answer directly and completely</h3>
  <p>Begin with the answer, then add the necessary explanation or evidence. Avoid unrelated information. If you do not know the answer, say so honestly or ask for clarification rather than inventing facts.</p>

  <h3>5. Ask for clarification politely</h3>
  <p>Useful expressions include <em>Could you please repeat the last point?</em>, <em>Do you mean that we should submit it today?</em> and <em>Could you explain what you mean by that term?</em></p>

  <p><strong>Example:</strong> Question: <em>Why should learners revise their work before submission?</em> Answer: <em>They should revise it to identify errors, improve clarity and ensure that all parts of the task are complete.</em></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Asking and Answering Questions', 15),
  (1560, 1560, '<h2>Giving Instructions and Explanations Orally</h2>
  <p>Oral instructions tell listeners what actions to perform. Oral explanations help listeners understand how something works or why something happens. Both require accurate information, clear language and a logical sequence.</p>

  <h3>1. Giving instructions</h3>
  <p>Use action verbs and place steps in the order they must be performed. Include necessary quantities, locations, deadlines and restrictions. Pause between steps when listeners need time to act.</p>

  <h3>2. Giving explanations</h3>
  <p>State the idea first, then explain it using reasons, stages or examples. For a cause-and-effect explanation, make the relationship clear with words such as <em>because, therefore</em> and <em>as a result</em>.</p>

  <h3>3. Adjust to the audience</h3>
  <p>Use language appropriate to the listeners'' age, experience and familiarity with the task. Explain specialised terms when necessary. Do not assume that everyone already knows a process you find familiar.</p>

  <h3>4. Check understanding</h3>
  <p>Ask a listener to repeat the key steps or invite questions when accuracy matters. If someone misunderstands, explain the unclear part in a different way rather than merely repeating the same sentence more loudly.</p>

  <h3>5. Example: explaining a process</h3>
  <p><strong>Topic:</strong> How to prepare for a group presentation.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>First, agree on the topic and the main message.</li>
    <li>Next, divide the research and speaking tasks among group members.</li>
    <li>Then, combine the information and check its accuracy.</li>
    <li>Finally, rehearse the presentation and confirm who will present each section.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The sequence is clear, each action has a purpose, and the final step checks readiness before presenting.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Giving Instructions and Explanations', 15),
  (1561, 1561, '<h2>Turn-taking in Conversation</h2>
  <p>Turn-taking is the way speakers share opportunities to speak during a conversation or discussion. Effective turn-taking allows participants to contribute, listen, respond and disagree without unnecessarily interrupting one another.</p>

  <h3>1. Recognise when a turn is ending</h3>
  <p>A speaker may signal the end of a turn through a pause, a completed sentence, falling intonation or a question directed at someone else. These clues help another participant enter the conversation at an appropriate moment.</p>

  <h3>2. Enter the conversation politely</h3>
  <p>Useful expressions include <em>May I add something?</em>, <em>Could I respond to that point?</em> and <em>I would like to contribute an idea.</em> In a formal meeting, follow the chairperson''s rules for requesting the floor.</p>

  <h3>3. Show active listening</h3>
  <p>Listen to the speaker''s complete point before responding. Brief acknowledgements such as <em>I see</em> or <em>That is a useful point</em> can show attention, but avoid repeatedly interrupting with comments.</p>

  <h3>4. Disagree without silencing others</h3>
  <p>Challenge the idea rather than attacking the person. For example: <em>I understand your concern, but I interpret the evidence differently because...</em> Allow the other speaker to explain their reasoning.</p>

  <h3>5. Share participation fairly</h3>
  <p>Dominating a discussion prevents others from contributing. If you have spoken several times, make space for quieter participants. If someone has not had a chance to speak, invite their contribution respectfully.</p>

  <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>Could I build on your suggestion? I agree that we need more bins, and I think placing them near the dining hall would make them easier to use.</em></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Turn-taking', 15),
  (1562, 1562, '<h2>Expressing Opinions and Giving Reasons</h2>
  <p>An opinion is a person''s view or judgement about an issue. In effective discussion, a speaker states the opinion clearly and supports it with relevant reasons or evidence.</p>

  <h3>1. State an opinion clearly</h3>
  <p>Useful expressions include <em>I believe that...</em>, <em>In my view...</em>, <em>I would argue that...</em> and <em>From my perspective...</em>. Choose an expression that suits the situation and the strength of your claim.</p>

  <h3>2. Support the opinion with reasons</h3>
  <p>Words such as <em>because, since</em> and <em>as</em> introduce reasons. Expressions such as <em>for example</em> introduce supporting illustrations. A reason should explain why the opinion is reasonable, not merely repeat it.</p>

  <h3>3. Distinguish opinions from facts</h3>
  <p>A fact can be checked against reliable evidence. An opinion expresses a judgement or preference. For example, ''The library opens at eight o''clock'' is a factual claim that can be verified. ''The library should open earlier'' is a recommendation that needs reasons.</p>

  <h3>4. A simple structure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Opinion:</strong> state your position.</li>
    <li><strong>Reason:</strong> explain why you hold it.</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence or example:</strong> support the reason where possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> connect the support to your position.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>In my view, the school should provide more reading time because regular reading builds vocabulary and comprehension. For example, learners who read a variety of texts encounter different sentence structures and ways of expressing ideas.</em></p>

  <h3>5. Be fair and precise</h3>
  <p>Avoid claiming that everyone agrees with you unless you have evidence. Recognise reasonable opposing views and explain why you still hold your position. Strong opinions become more convincing when they are supported by relevant evidence rather than insults or exaggeration.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Expressing Opinions and Giving Reasons', 15),
  (1563, 1563, '<h2>Agreement and Disagreement in Discussion</h2>
  <p>In a discussion, participants may support, partly support or oppose an idea. Effective speakers express their position clearly while remaining respectful of other people.</p>

  <h3>1. Expressing agreement</h3>
  <p>Useful expressions include <em>I agree with that point because...</em>, <em>That is a convincing reason</em> and <em>I would add that...</em>. When possible, explain what you agree with rather than simply saying ''yes''.</p>

  <h3>2. Expressing partial agreement</h3>
  <p>You may accept one part of an argument but disagree with another. For example: <em>I agree that the library needs more books, but I think we should also improve access to the books we already have.</em></p>

  <h3>3. Expressing disagreement</h3>
  <p>Useful expressions include <em>I see the point, but...</em>, <em>I respectfully disagree because...</em> and <em>Could we consider another explanation?</em> Explain the reason for your disagreement and address the idea rather than attacking the speaker.</p>

  <h3>4. Respond to the actual argument</h3>
  <p>Listen carefully before replying. Do not misrepresent the other person''s position or introduce an unrelated issue. If the other speaker provides new evidence, consider it rather than repeating your original point without reflection.</p>

  <h3>5. Reach a constructive outcome</h3>
  <p>When possible, identify common ground, clarify the remaining difference and suggest a practical next step. Agreement is not always necessary, but the discussion should improve understanding.</p>

  <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>I agree that learners need more practice questions. However, I also believe they need explanations of their mistakes so they understand how to improve.</em></p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Agreement and Disagreement', 15),
  (1564, 1564, '<h2>Group Discussions</h2>
  <p>A group discussion is an exchange of ideas in which participants explore an issue, share evidence, ask questions and work towards a conclusion or decision. A productive discussion depends on preparation, listening and balanced participation.</p>

  <h3>1. Prepare for the discussion</h3>
  <p>Understand the topic, identify the question being discussed and gather relevant information. Prepare a few points and examples, but remain willing to consider new evidence.</p>

  <h3>2. Contribute relevant points</h3>
  <p>Make each contribution clear and connected to the topic. Explain your reasons and give examples where useful. Avoid repeating a point already made unless you are adding an important distinction.</p>

  <h3>3. Listen and respond</h3>
  <p>Pay attention to other contributions. You may build on a point, ask for clarification, provide a different example or respectfully challenge the reasoning. Respond to what was actually said.</p>

  <h3>4. Share responsibility</h3>
  <p>Participants should allow others to speak, remain respectful and help keep the conversation focused. A chairperson may guide the order of contributions, manage time and summarise emerging points.</p>

  <h3>5. Reach a conclusion</h3>
  <p>At the end, identify the strongest points, any areas of agreement, unresolved questions and the next action if one is required. A conclusion should reflect the discussion rather than simply repeat one participant''s opinion.</p>

  <p><strong>Example topic:</strong> Should the school introduce a weekly reading period?</p>
  <p>Participants might discuss benefits, timetable constraints, access to books and possible ways to organise the period before agreeing on a recommendation.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Discussions and Debates', 15),
  (1565, 1565, '<h2>Oral Presentations</h2>
  <p>An oral presentation is a prepared spoken explanation delivered to an audience. It may use slides, charts, objects or demonstrations, but the speaker''s main responsibility is to communicate the information clearly.</p>

  <h3>1. Plan the message</h3>
  <p>Identify the purpose, audience and time available. Select the most relevant information and organise it into an opening, a body with connected points, and a conclusion.</p>

  <h3>2. Prepare supporting material</h3>
  <p>Check facts, spellings, figures and sources before presenting. If you use slides or charts, make sure they are readable and directly support the point being explained. Avoid placing entire paragraphs on slides.</p>

  <h3>3. Rehearse the delivery</h3>
  <p>Practise the opening, transitions and conclusion. Time the presentation, pronounce difficult words and rehearse how you will explain any diagram or visual aid. Do not depend entirely on memorising every sentence.</p>

  <h3>4. Engage the audience</h3>
  <p>Speak audibly, use an appropriate pace, make natural eye contact and pause after important points. Use gestures where helpful, but avoid movements that distract from the message.</p>

  <h3>5. Handle questions</h3>
  <p>Listen to the whole question, answer directly and give evidence or clarification when needed. If you do not know an answer, acknowledge the limit honestly rather than inventing information.</p>

  <p><strong>Example structure for a short presentation on saving water:</strong> introduce the problem, explain two causes of water waste, present practical solutions, and conclude with one action the audience can take.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Oral Presentations', 15),
  (1566, 1566, '<h2>Interviews</h2>
<p>An interview is a structured conversation in which one person asks questions to obtain information, assess suitability, or understand another person''s experiences and opinions.</p>

<h3>1. Parts of an interview</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Interviewer:</strong> asks questions and guides the conversation.</li>
  <li><strong>Interviewee:</strong> answers questions and provides relevant information.</li>
  <li><strong>Opening:</strong> greetings, introductions and an explanation of the purpose.</li>
  <li><strong>Main discussion:</strong> questions, answers and follow-up questions.</li>
  <li><strong>Closing:</strong> final questions, appreciation and polite departure.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Preparing for an interview</h3>
<p>Understand the purpose, anticipate likely questions, organise relevant examples and prepare questions to ask. For a school leadership interview, you might prepare examples of responsibility, teamwork and problem-solving.</p>

<h3>3. Answering effectively</h3>
<p>Listen to the complete question, answer directly, explain your point and provide a relevant example when appropriate.</p>
<p><strong>Example question:</strong> Why would you make a good class representative?</p>
<p><strong>Weak answer:</strong> Because I am good.</p>
<p><strong>Improved answer:</strong> I would make a good class representative because I listen to others, communicate their concerns respectfully and help organise class activities.</p>

<h3>4. Interview conduct</h3>
<p>Arrive on time, greet the interviewer politely, listen without interrupting and speak clearly. If you do not understand a question, ask for clarification rather than guessing.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Giving answers that are unrelated to the question.</li>
  <li>Memorising responses so rigidly that they sound unnatural.</li>
  <li>Interrupting, exaggerating achievements or using unnecessarily informal language.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Interviews', 15),
  (1567, 1567, '<h2>Non-verbal Communication</h2>
<p>Non-verbal communication is the sharing of meaning without relying entirely on spoken or written words. It includes facial expressions, gestures, posture, eye contact, physical distance and aspects of the voice.</p>

<h3>1. Facial expressions</h3>
<p>Facial expressions can communicate emotions such as happiness, surprise, confusion or concern. However, an expression does not always reveal exactly what a person feels.</p>

<h3>2. Gestures</h3>
<p>Gestures are movements of the hands, arms or head that communicate meaning.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Raising a hand in class can signal a desire to contribute. Waving may communicate greeting or farewell.</p>
<p>A gesture can have different meanings in different cultural or social contexts, so avoid assuming that every gesture means the same thing everywhere.</p>

<h3>3. Posture and eye contact</h3>
<p>Posture refers to how someone sits, stands or holds their body. An attentive posture may help show engagement. Eye contact can support attention and confidence, but its appropriate amount varies across people and contexts.</p>

<h3>4. Tone, pace and pauses</h3>
<p>Although words carry the main verbal message, vocal features such as tone, volume, speed and pauses influence how the message is understood.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Saying ''That was helpful'' warmly may express genuine appreciation. Saying the same words with exaggerated emphasis may communicate sarcasm.</p>

<h3>5. Reading non-verbal signals carefully</h3>
<p>Interpret signals in context. A person who looks away may be thinking, distracted, uncomfortable or following a cultural convention. One signal alone is not reliable proof of someone''s feelings or intentions.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming that crossed arms always mean anger or rejection.</li>
  <li>Maintaining intense eye contact when it makes someone uncomfortable.</li>
  <li>Using gestures that conflict with the intended message.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Non-verbal Communication', 15),
  (1568, 1568, '<h2>Vowel Sounds</h2>
<p>Vowel sounds are speech sounds produced without a significant obstruction of the airflow through the mouth. In English, vowel sounds can be represented by the letters <strong>a, e, i, o, u</strong> and sometimes <strong>y</strong>, but the relationship between spelling and sound is not one-to-one.</p>

<h3>1. Vowel letters and vowel sounds</h3>
<p>Do not confuse a vowel letter with a vowel sound. The same letter may represent different sounds, and the same sound may be spelt in different ways.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>cat:</strong> the vowel letter a represents the vowel sound in /kæt/.</li>
  <li><strong>cake:</strong> the letter a represents a different vowel sound in /keɪk/.</li>
  <li><strong>see</strong> and <strong>sea:</strong> different spellings represent the same vowel sound in many varieties of English.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Short and long vowel sounds</h3>
<p>In school pronunciation work, vowels are often compared as short and long sounds. These labels describe sound contrasts, not simply how long a letter is written.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>ship</strong> /ɪ/ and <strong>sheep</strong> /iː/ have contrasting vowel sounds.</li>
  <li><strong>full</strong> /ʊ/ and <strong>fool</strong> /uː/ have contrasting vowel sounds.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pronunciation varies between English accents. The International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA) provides symbols for distinguishing sounds more precisely.</p>

<h3>3. Diphthongs</h3>
<p>A diphthong is a vowel sound in which the tongue position changes noticeably within one syllable.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>day</strong> commonly contains /eɪ/.</li>
  <li><strong>boy</strong> commonly contains /ɔɪ/.</li>
  <li><strong>my</strong> commonly contains /aɪ/.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. How to practise vowel contrasts</h3>
<p>Say each word naturally, listen for the difference and repeat the pair. Keep the consonants as similar as possible so that you focus on the vowel.</p>
<p><strong>Practice pair:</strong> ship — sheep. The main contrast is /ɪ/ versus /iː/.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming that each vowel letter always has one sound.</li>
  <li>Pronouncing a word from its spelling without considering its accepted pronunciation.</li>
  <li>Confusing a difference in vowel quality with a difference in spelling alone.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Vowel Sounds', 15),
  (1569, 1569, '<h2>Consonant Sounds</h2>
<p>Consonant sounds are produced when airflow is obstructed or narrowed at some point in the vocal tract. The obstruction may involve the lips, teeth, tongue or other speech organs.</p>

<h3>1. Voiced and voiceless consonants</h3>
<p>Voiced consonants involve vibration of the vocal folds; voiceless consonants do not involve this vibration in the same way.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>/p/ and /b/:</strong> compare ''pat'' and ''bat''.</li>
  <li><strong>/f/ and /v/:</strong> compare ''fan'' and ''van''.</li>
  <li><strong>/s/ and /z/:</strong> compare ''sip'' and ''zip''.</li>
</ul>
<p>To feel the difference, gently place your fingers on your throat as you produce a sustained /s/ and then /z/. The voiced /z/ produces vocal-fold vibration.</p>

<h3>2. Places of articulation</h3>
<p>The place of articulation describes where the airflow is obstructed or narrowed.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bilabial:</strong> both lips, as in /p/, /b/ and /m/.</li>
  <li><strong>Labiodental:</strong> lower lip and upper teeth, as in /f/ and /v/.</li>
  <li><strong>Dental:</strong> tongue near or against the teeth, as in /θ/ in ''thin'' and /ð/ in ''this''.</li>
  <li><strong>Alveolar:</strong> tongue near the ridge behind the upper teeth, as in /t/, /d/, /s/ and /z/.</li>
  <li><strong>Velar:</strong> the back of the tongue approaches the soft palate, as in /k/ and /ɡ/.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Manner of articulation</h3>
<p>The manner of articulation describes how airflow is controlled.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Plosives:</strong> airflow is briefly blocked and released, as in /p/, /b/, /t/, /d/, /k/ and /ɡ/.</li>
  <li><strong>Fricatives:</strong> air passes through a narrow gap, creating friction, as in /f/, /v/, /s/ and /z/.</li>
  <li><strong>Nasals:</strong> air flows through the nose, as in /m/, /n/ and /ŋ/.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Sound versus letter</h3>
<p>A consonant sound is not the same as a consonant letter. For example, ''ship'' begins with the two-letter spelling <strong>sh</strong>, which represents one consonant sound /ʃ/.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming that every written consonant represents a separate sound.</li>
  <li>Confusing voiced and voiceless pairs such as /f/ and /v/.</li>
  <li>Mixing up the place of articulation with the manner of articulation.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Consonant Sounds', 15),
  (1570, 1570, '<h2>Similar and Contrasting Sounds</h2>
<p>English contains words that differ by only one speech sound. Learning to hear and produce these contrasts helps prevent confusion in speaking, listening and spelling.</p>

<h3>1. Minimal pairs</h3>
<p>A minimal pair consists of two words that differ in one sound in the same position and have different meanings.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>pin — bin:</strong> /p/ contrasts with /b/ at the beginning.</li>
  <li><strong>fan — van:</strong> /f/ contrasts with /v/ at the beginning.</li>
  <li><strong>ship — sheep:</strong> /ɪ/ contrasts with /iː/ in the vowel position.</li>
  <li><strong>cap — cup:</strong> the vowel sounds contrast.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Hearing the contrast</h3>
<p>To practise, say each pair slowly, then at normal speed. Pay attention to the exact position of the sound difference. Do not change several sounds at once.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> In ''pin'' and ''bin'', the vowel and final consonant remain the same. Only the initial consonant changes.</p>

<h3>3. Producing the contrast</h3>
<p>For consonant pairs, notice where the tongue or lips move and whether the vocal folds vibrate. For vowel pairs, notice the shape of the mouth and the position of the tongue.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> For /s/ and /z/, keep the tongue position similar but add vocal-fold vibration for /z/.</p>

<h3>4. Sounds that learners may confuse</h3>
<p>Some English contrasts may be unfamiliar to speakers of particular languages. Practise the actual contrast rather than assuming that two sounds are identical because they are written similarly.</p>
<p>Accents vary, and not all speakers maintain every contrast in the same way. Aim for consistent, understandable pronunciation.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Changing both the vowel and consonant when practising a minimal pair.</li>
  <li>Relying only on spelling instead of listening to the sounds.</li>
  <li>Repeating a word quickly before learning how the contrasting sounds are produced.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Similar and Contrasting Sounds', 15),
  (1571, 1571, '<h2>Syllables</h2>
<p>A syllable is a unit of pronunciation organised around a vowel sound. A word may contain one syllable or several syllables. Count the sounds heard in normal pronunciation, not simply the number of vowel letters.</p>

<h3>1. Counting syllables</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>book</strong> — one syllable.</li>
  <li><strong>teacher</strong> — two syllables: teach-er.</li>
  <li><strong>computer</strong> — three syllables: com-pu-ter.</li>
  <li><strong>education</strong> — commonly four syllables: ed-u-ca-tion.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Syllables and vowel sounds</h3>
<p>Each syllable normally has a vowel sound as its centre. Two written vowel letters may represent one vowel sound, as in <strong>rain</strong>. A word may also contain a vowel letter that is not pronounced, as the final e in <strong>cake</strong>.</p>

<h3>3. Open and closed syllables</h3>
<p>An <strong>open syllable</strong> ends in a vowel sound, as in the first syllable of <strong>o-pen</strong>. A <strong>closed syllable</strong> ends in a consonant sound, as in <strong>cat</strong> or the first syllable of <strong>pic-nic</strong>.</p>
<p>These patterns can help with reading and spelling, although English pronunciation has exceptions.</p>

<h3>4. Finding syllables</h3>
<p>Say the word naturally and listen for its vowel-sound units. You may tap once for each syllable, then check your result by pronouncing the word slowly without artificially adding sounds.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''Important'' is commonly pronounced with three syllables: im-por-tant.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Counting vowel letters instead of vowel sounds.</li>
  <li>Giving every written vowel a separate syllable.</li>
  <li>Breaking words into syllables in a way that changes their natural pronunciation.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Syllables', 15),
  (1572, 1572, '<h2>Word Stress</h2>
<p>Word stress is the emphasis placed on one syllable in a word. A stressed syllable is often more prominent because of a combination of loudness, length, pitch and vowel quality.</p>

<h3>1. Identifying stressed syllables</h3>
<p>In pronunciation guides, primary stress is often marked with /ˈ/ immediately before the stressed syllable.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>TAble:</strong> /ˈteɪbəl/ — stress on the first syllable.</li>
  <li><strong>beGIN:</strong> /bɪˈɡɪn/ — stress on the second syllable.</li>
  <li><strong>imPORtant:</strong> /ɪmˈpɔːtənt/ in a common British pronunciation — stress on the second syllable.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pronunciation may vary between English accents, but the stress pattern should remain consistent within the variety being used.</p>

<h3>2. Stress can distinguish words</h3>
<p>Some words have different stress patterns depending on their grammatical use.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>REcord</strong> — a noun meaning a written account.</li>
  <li><strong>reCORD</strong> — a verb meaning to capture or store information.</li>
  <li><strong>PREsent</strong> — a noun meaning a gift, or an adjective meaning current.</li>
  <li><strong>preSENT</strong> — a verb meaning to show or deliver something.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Finding word stress</h3>
<p>Listen to a reliable pronunciation model, repeat the word and identify its most prominent syllable. Use a dictionary''s pronunciation guide when unsure. Do not assume that every long word has stress on the same syllable.</p>

<h3>4. Practising stress</h3>
<p>Say the stressed syllable clearly without turning every syllable into an equally strong one.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> In ''begin'', the second syllable is stressed: be-GIN. Giving equal emphasis to both syllables may sound unnatural.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Putting stress on the wrong syllable.</li>
  <li>Giving every syllable equal prominence.</li>
  <li>Assuming spelling alone reveals the stress pattern.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Word Stress', 15),
  (1573, 1573, '<h2>Sentence Stress</h2>
<p>Sentence stress is the emphasis placed on particular words in a spoken sentence. It helps listeners identify the most important information and can change the meaning or implication of a message.</p>

<h3>1. Content words and function words</h3>
<p>Content words commonly carry the main information. They include nouns, main verbs, adjectives and many adverbs. Function words include articles, auxiliary verbs, pronouns and prepositions; these are often less prominent, although they can be stressed for emphasis.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''The BOY bought a NEW BAG.'' The nouns and adjective carry much of the information.</p>

<h3>2. Stress changes the focus</h3>
<p>Emphasising a different word can correct an assumption or highlight a contrast.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>I</strong> borrowed your book. — I did it, not someone else.</li>
  <li>I <strong>borrowed</strong> your book. — I borrowed it; I did not buy or steal it.</li>
  <li>I borrowed your <strong>book</strong>. — It was your book, not another item.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Stress in questions and corrections</h3>
<p>Speakers often stress words that express contrast or answer a question.</p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Who submitted the report?</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>MARY</strong> submitted the report.</p>
<p>The stress on ''Mary'' highlights the person who performed the action.</p>

<h3>4. Practising sentence stress</h3>
<p>First identify the message you want to communicate. Then choose the word that carries the main information or contrast and make it prominent. Read the sentence aloud and check whether the emphasis matches your intended meaning.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Giving every word equal emphasis.</li>
  <li>Stressing a word that does not match the intended message.</li>
  <li>Assuming a sentence has only one possible stress pattern regardless of context.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Sentence Stress', 15),
  (1574, 1574, '<h2>Rhythm</h2>
<p>Rhythm in spoken English is the pattern created by stressed and unstressed syllables, pauses and the timing of speech. It helps speech sound connected and organised rather than like a sequence of isolated words.</p>

<h3>1. Stressed and unstressed syllables</h3>
<p>In a phrase, some syllables receive more prominence than others. Function words are often reduced in ordinary speech, while important content words are more prominent.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''The students are reading a book.'' In natural speech, ''students'', ''reading'' and ''book'' may carry more prominence than ''the'', ''are'' and ''a''. The exact pattern depends on the intended message.</p>

<h3>2. Rhythm groups</h3>
<p>Speakers organise words into meaningful groups. Pauses and changes in prominence can separate ideas, but excessive pauses may make speech difficult to follow.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''After the lesson, / we went to the library.'' The pause separates the introductory phrase from the main clause.</p>

<h3>3. Linking words in connected speech</h3>
<p>In fluent speech, words often connect smoothly. Sounds may link across word boundaries, and unstressed words may be pronounced more weakly. This is normal connected speech, not careless pronunciation.</p>

<h3>4. Using rhythm effectively</h3>
<p>To practise, read a short sentence aloud, mark its important words, and group related ideas together. Keep a steady, natural flow and pause at meaningful boundaries.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Pausing between every word.</li>
  <li>Speaking so quickly that important words become unclear.</li>
  <li>Using the same rhythm for every sentence regardless of its meaning.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Rhythm', 15),
  (1575, 1575, '<h2>Intonation</h2>
<p>Intonation is the pattern of pitch movement in spoken language. Pitch may rise, fall or move in more complex ways across a phrase or sentence. Intonation helps communicate whether a speaker is asking, stating, checking, contrasting or expressing an attitude.</p>

<h3>1. Falling intonation</h3>
<p>A falling pattern often occurs in statements and many wh-questions, although the pattern depends on context and the speaker''s intention.</p>
<ul>
  <li>''The lesson is over.'' — a falling pattern can communicate a complete statement.</li>
  <li>''Where are you going?'' — a falling pattern is common in a direct wh-question.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Rising intonation</h3>
<p>A rising pattern often occurs in yes/no questions and can indicate that the speaker is checking information or inviting confirmation.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''Are you ready?'' A rise towards the end is common in many varieties of English.</p>

<h3>3. Intonation and attitude</h3>
<p>Intonation can suggest uncertainty, interest, surprise or incompleteness, but pitch alone does not prove a speaker''s feelings. Interpret it together with the words and context.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''Really?'' may express genuine surprise, a request for confirmation or disbelief, depending on its pitch movement and the situation.</p>

<h3>4. Intonation in lists and continuing ideas</h3>
<p>A speaker may use rising or continuing pitch on items in a list and a fall on the final item to signal completion.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''We bought rice, beans and flour.'' The speaker may keep the pitch open across earlier items and use a falling pattern on ''flour''. Patterns vary by context.</p>

<h3>5. Practising intonation</h3>
<p>Listen to a clear spoken model, imitate its pitch movement and consider the speaker''s intention. Practise the same words with different intonation patterns to hear how the implication can change.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming every question must end with rising intonation.</li>
  <li>Confusing pitch movement with loudness.</li>
  <li>Interpreting an intonation pattern without considering context.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Intonation', 15),
  (1576, 1576, '<h2>Formal and Informal Language</h2>
<p>Formal and informal language are different styles of communication. The appropriate style depends on the audience, purpose, relationship and situation.</p>

<h3>1. Formal language</h3>
<p>Formal language is commonly used in official letters, applications, academic writing, reports and communication with people in positions of responsibility.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Uses respectful and precise expressions.</li>
  <li>Avoids slang and unnecessarily casual abbreviations.</li>
  <li>Uses complete sentences and appropriate grammar.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "I am writing to request permission to attend the meeting."</p>

<h3>2. Informal language</h3>
<p>Informal language is commonly used when communicating with friends, relatives and people with whom one has a familiar relationship.</p>
<ul>
  <li>May use contractions, everyday expressions and conversational language.</li>
  <li>May be more relaxed and personal.</li>
  <li>Still requires clarity and respect.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "Can I come to the meeting?"</p>

<h3>3. Choosing the right style</h3>
<p>Consider three questions before speaking or writing:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Audience:</strong> Who will receive the message?</li>
  <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Am I requesting, explaining, reporting or chatting?</li>
  <li><strong>Situation:</strong> Is the communication official or personal?</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Changing the register</h3>
<p>Changing register means adjusting language to suit a new situation without changing the essential message.</p>
<p><strong>Informal:</strong> "Send me the notes now."</p>
<p><strong>Formal:</strong> "Could you please send me the notes at your earliest convenience?"</p>
<p>The formal version is more courteous and suitable for many official situations.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using slang in an official application.</li>
  <li>Assuming formal language must always be complicated.</li>
  <li>Using informal language that sounds rude or demanding.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Formal and Informal Language', 15),
  (1577, 1577, '<h2>Polite Requests and Responses</h2>
<p>A request asks someone to do something. A polite request communicates the need while respecting the other person''s choice, time and circumstances.</p>

<h3>1. Making polite requests</h3>
<p>Common structures include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Could you ...?</strong> "Could you explain this question?"</li>
  <li><strong>Would you mind ...?</strong> "Would you mind closing the window?"</li>
  <li><strong>May I ...?</strong> "May I borrow your dictionary?"</li>
  <li><strong>Could I please ...?</strong> "Could I please use your calculator?"</li>
</ul>
<p>These expressions are generally more courteous than direct commands such as "Give me your book."</p>

<h3>2. Responding positively</h3>
<p>When accepting a request, answer clearly and courteously.</p>
<p><strong>Request:</strong> "Could you help me carry these books?"</p>
<p><strong>Response:</strong> "Of course. I would be happy to help."</p>

<h3>3. Declining politely</h3>
<p>If you cannot fulfil a request, acknowledge it, explain briefly if appropriate and offer an alternative when possible.</p>
<p><strong>Request:</strong> "Could you lend me your notes now?"</p>
<p><strong>Response:</strong> "I''m sorry, I need them for revision at the moment. I can lend them to you this evening."</p>

<h3>4. Responding to ''Would you mind ...?''</h3>
<p>When someone asks, "Would you mind opening the window?", saying "No, not at all" normally means you are willing to do it. Saying "Yes, I would" normally means you do mind. Because the wording can be confusing, a clear response such as "Certainly, I''ll open it" avoids ambiguity.</p>

<h3>5. Tone and context</h3>
<p>Politeness depends on more than a sentence structure. A respectful tone, appropriate timing and acknowledgement of the other person''s response also matter.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using commands when a request is more appropriate.</li>
  <li>Responding to a negative question with an unclear "yes" or "no".</li>
  <li>Making a request sound polite while ignoring the other person''s refusal.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Polite Requests and Responses', 15),
  (1578, 1578, '<h2>Offers and Invitations</h2>
<p>An offer expresses willingness to provide help, an item or a service. An invitation asks someone to attend an event or take part in an activity.</p>

<h3>1. Making offers</h3>
<p>Common expressions include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>"Would you like some water?"</li>
  <li>"Shall I help you carry those books?"</li>
  <li>"Can I get you a chair?"</li>
  <li>"I can help you prepare the presentation if you wish."</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Accepting offers</h3>
<p>Accept clearly and show appreciation when appropriate.</p>
<p><strong>Offer:</strong> "Would you like some tea?"</p>
<p><strong>Response:</strong> "Yes, please. Thank you."</p>

<h3>3. Declining offers</h3>
<p>Decline respectfully when you do not need or want the offer.</p>
<p><strong>Offer:</strong> "Shall I carry your bag?"</p>
<p><strong>Response:</strong> "No, thank you. I can manage."</p>

<h3>4. Making invitations</h3>
<p>Include the activity or event and, where necessary, the date, time and place.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "Would you like to join us for the debate on Friday at 3 p.m. in the school hall?"</p>

<h3>5. Accepting or declining invitations</h3>
<p><strong>Accepting:</strong> "Thank you for inviting me. I would be delighted to attend."</p>
<p><strong>Declining:</strong> "Thank you for the invitation. Unfortunately, I will not be available that afternoon."</p>
<p>If appropriate, you can suggest another time. Do not accept an invitation merely to avoid disappointing the person if you cannot attend.</p>

<h3>6. Distinguishing offers from invitations</h3>
<p>An offer proposes help or something for another person to receive. An invitation proposes participation in an event or activity. The context determines which function a sentence performs.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Leaving out essential details from an invitation.</li>
  <li>Declining abruptly without acknowledging the offer.</li>
  <li>Confusing offering help with inviting someone to an event.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Offers and Invitations', 15),
  (1579, 1579, '<h2>Advice and Suggestions</h2>
<p>Advice recommends what someone should do in a situation. A suggestion proposes an idea or possible action for consideration. Both can help people make decisions or solve problems.</p>

<h3>1. Giving advice</h3>
<p>Common structures include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>should:</strong> "You should revise the difficult topics first."</li>
  <li><strong>ought to:</strong> "You ought to apologise for arriving late."</li>
  <li><strong>had better:</strong> "You had better leave now if you want to catch the bus."</li>
  <li><strong>If I were you ...:</strong> "If I were you, I would ask the teacher for clarification."</li>
</ul>
<p>''Had better'' often communicates a stronger recommendation and may imply a negative consequence if the advice is ignored.</p>

<h3>2. Making suggestions</h3>
<p>Common expressions include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>"Why don''t we study together?"</li>
  <li>"How about forming a revision group?"</li>
  <li>"We could ask the teacher for another example."</li>
  <li>"Let''s review the question step by step."</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Responding to advice</h3>
<p>You can accept advice, ask for clarification or explain respectfully why you will choose another option.</p>
<p><strong>Accepting:</strong> "That is helpful advice. I will try it."</p>
<p><strong>Seeking clarification:</strong> "Could you explain how that would help?"</p>
<p><strong>Declining:</strong> "Thank you for the suggestion, but I think another approach would work better for me."</p>

<h3>4. Making advice useful</h3>
<p>Good advice should be relevant to the problem, practical and specific. Instead of saying "Work harder", identify an action the person can take.</p>
<p><strong>Vague:</strong> "Improve your English."</p>
<p><strong>Specific:</strong> "Read one short article each day, note unfamiliar words and use five of them in your own sentences."</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing ''should'' with ''must'' when an action is recommended rather than compulsory.</li>
  <li>Giving general advice without a practical next step.</li>
  <li>Presenting suggestions in a way that sounds insulting or controlling.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Advice and Suggestions', 15),
  (1580, 1580, '<h2>Apologies and Complaints</h2>
<p>An apology acknowledges a mistake or harm and expresses regret. A complaint communicates dissatisfaction about a problem and may request an explanation, correction or solution.</p>

<h3>1. Making an apology</h3>
<p>A clear apology identifies the problem, accepts appropriate responsibility and explains how the situation will be addressed.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "I apologise for submitting the assignment late. I should have informed you earlier, and I will plan my work more carefully next time."</p>

<h3>2. Responding to apologies</h3>
<p>Depending on the situation, you may accept an apology, explain the effect of the action or discuss how to prevent the problem from happening again.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "Thank you for apologising. Please let me know earlier if you expect another delay."</p>

<h3>3. Making a complaint</h3>
<p>An effective complaint describes the problem accurately, provides relevant details and states what action would resolve it.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "I am writing to report that the library computer has not been working since Monday. Could the fault please be investigated?"</p>

<h3>4. Formal and informal complaints</h3>
<p>A complaint to a close friend may use conversational language. A complaint to a school administrator, service provider or organisation usually requires a more formal and factual style.</p>

<h3>5. Keeping complaints constructive</h3>
<p>Focus on the behaviour, event or problem rather than attacking the person''s character. Avoid insults, threats and unsupported accusations.</p>
<p><strong>Unhelpful:</strong> "Your staff are completely useless."</p>
<p><strong>Constructive:</strong> "I waited for an hour after my appointment time. Could you explain the delay and advise how this can be avoided in future?"</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Apologising without acknowledging what went wrong.</li>
  <li>Making a complaint without explaining the problem.</li>
  <li>Using insulting language that makes a solution less likely.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Apologies and Complaints', 15),
  (1581, 1581, '<h2>Persuasion and Negotiation</h2>
<p>Persuasion is the attempt to influence someone''s beliefs, opinions or actions using reasons, evidence and appropriate language. Negotiation is a discussion in which people with different needs or positions try to reach an acceptable agreement.</p>

<h3>1. Persuasion through reasons and evidence</h3>
<p>A persuasive message should make a clear claim, support it with relevant reasons and provide evidence or examples where possible.</p>
<p><strong>Weak claim:</strong> "Our school should have a reading club because reading is good."</p>
<p><strong>Stronger argument:</strong> "Our school should establish a reading club because regular reading builds vocabulary and comprehension. Members could meet weekly to discuss books and share reading strategies."</p>

<h3>2. Persuasive language</h3>
<p>Useful techniques include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Facts:</strong> information that can be checked.</li>
  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> specific cases that illustrate a point.</li>
  <li><strong>Rhetorical questions:</strong> questions used to make an audience reflect.</li>
  <li><strong>Inclusive language:</strong> words such as ''we'' and ''our'' that create a shared sense of purpose.</li>
  <li><strong>Repetition:</strong> repeating a key phrase for emphasis.</li>
</ul>
<p>Persuasion is strongest when these techniques support a sound argument rather than replace evidence.</p>

<h3>3. Understanding negotiation</h3>
<p>Negotiation involves explaining your needs, listening to the other person, identifying shared interests and considering possible compromises.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> A group wants to meet after school, but one member must catch an early bus. They could agree to meet during lunch or share some tasks online.</p>

<h3>4. Reaching an agreement</h3>
<p>Identify the problem, state each person''s needs, suggest options, compare the options and confirm the final agreement. A fair agreement should be clear and realistic.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using threats or insults instead of reasons.</li>
  <li>Making claims without reliable support.</li>
  <li>Treating negotiation as a contest in which only one person can benefit.</li>
  <li>Agreeing to terms that remain unclear.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Persuasion and Negotiation', 15),
  (1582, 1582, '<h2>Language for Different Audiences</h2>
<p>Effective communication adapts language to the people receiving a message. Age, familiarity, knowledge, role, purpose and situation can influence the vocabulary, detail and tone that are appropriate.</p>

<h3>1. Identifying the audience</h3>
<p>Before communicating, consider who will receive the message and what they already know.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Classmates:</strong> familiar examples and direct explanations may be appropriate.</li>
  <li><strong>School administrators:</strong> respectful, organised and precise language is usually expected.</li>
  <li><strong>Young learners:</strong> simple vocabulary and concrete examples may help explain unfamiliar ideas.</li>
  <li><strong>Specialists:</strong> technical terms may be appropriate when the audience understands them.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Adjusting vocabulary and detail</h3>
<p>Use words that the audience can understand. Explain technical terms when needed, and avoid unnecessary detail when the purpose is to give a brief instruction.</p>
<p><strong>For a younger learner:</strong> "Evaporation happens when liquid water changes into water vapour."</p>
<p><strong>For a science report:</strong> "Evaporation is the process by which molecules at a liquid''s surface enter the gaseous state."</p>
<p>Both statements describe the same process, but they use different levels of technical detail.</p>

<h3>3. Adjusting tone</h3>
<p>Tone may be formal, friendly, reassuring, persuasive or neutral. Choose a tone that suits the audience without becoming dishonest or disrespectful.</p>

<h3>4. Keeping the message accurate</h3>
<p>Adapting a message does not mean changing facts to please an audience. Simplify explanations where useful, but preserve the essential meaning.</p>

<h3>5. Checking effectiveness</h3>
<p>Ask whether the audience can understand the main point, whether the vocabulary is suitable and whether the message achieves its purpose. If confusion remains, clarify the wording or give a relevant example.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using unexplained technical vocabulary with beginners.</li>
  <li>Speaking to adults in a patronising way when simplifying information.</li>
  <li>Changing the facts while adapting the language.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Language for Different Audiences', 15),
  (1583, 1583, '<h2>Resolving Miscommunication</h2>
<p>Miscommunication occurs when a message is misunderstood, interpreted differently from the speaker''s intention or not received as intended. It may result from unclear wording, unfamiliar vocabulary, assumptions, distractions, tone or differences in context.</p>

<h3>1. Identifying the source of misunderstanding</h3>
<p>Before responding, identify what was said, what was understood and where the difference arose.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> A teacher says, "Submit the work by Friday." One learner assumes this means Friday morning, while another thinks the deadline is the end of Friday. The time has not been specified clearly enough.</p>

<h3>2. Asking for clarification</h3>
<p>Useful expressions include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>"Could you explain what you mean by that?"</li>
  <li>"Do you mean that we should submit it before lessons begin on Friday?"</li>
  <li>"Let me check that I have understood correctly."</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Paraphrasing</h3>
<p>Paraphrasing means expressing another person''s message in your own words to check your understanding.</p>
<p><strong>Original:</strong> "Please bring the completed form next week."</p>
<p><strong>Paraphrase:</strong> "Just to confirm, you want me to bring the finished form sometime next week?"</p>
<p>If the timing matters, ask a more specific follow-up question.</p>

<h3>4. Correcting a misunderstanding</h3>
<p>Explain your intended meaning calmly, provide missing information and acknowledge any wording that caused confusion.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "I am sorry I was unclear. I meant that the meeting begins at 2 p.m., not that we should arrive at 2 p.m."</p>

<h3>5. Preventing future misunderstandings</h3>
<p>Use precise wording, specify dates and times, avoid unexplained abbreviations and invite questions. In written communication, reread the message before sending it.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming that a misunderstanding was deliberate.</li>
  <li>Arguing before checking what the other person understood.</li>
  <li>Repeating the same unclear message instead of explaining it differently.</li>
</ul>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Resolving Miscommunication', 15),
  (1584, 1584, '<h2>Reading Digital Texts</h2>
  <p>Digital texts are messages and documents read on electronic devices. Examples include websites, emails, online articles, electronic notices, learning platforms and social media posts.</p>
  <h3>How digital reading differs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hyperlinks:</strong> Connect the reader to another page or resource.</li>
    <li><strong>Headings and menus:</strong> Help readers locate information quickly.</li>
    <li><strong>Search tools:</strong> Help locate names, phrases or topics within a page.</li>
    <li><strong>Multimedia:</strong> Text may be combined with images, audio, video or interactive elements.</li>
    <li><strong>Updates:</strong> Online information can change, so dates and versions may matter.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>A purposeful reading method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the purpose: what do you need to find out?</li>
    <li>Scan the title, headings, date and source.</li>
    <li>Read the relevant sections carefully.</li>
    <li>Follow links only when they help answer your question, and check where they lead.</li>
    <li>Summarise the information and verify important claims.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>You need the deadline for a school application. On an official notice, locate the application heading, identify the deadline, and check the year and any later update. Do not rely on an old screenshot if a current official notice is available.</p>
  <h3>Read links critically</h3>
  <p>A link''s visible wording may not fully describe the page it opens. Check the destination before entering personal information or downloading a file. A professional-looking website is not automatically trustworthy.</p>
  <h3>Common errors</h3>
  <p>Do not assume that the first search result is the most reliable, that a popular post is accurate, or that a date shown in an old article applies today. Separate what the text states from what you infer.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Reading Digital Texts', 15),
  (1585, 1585, '<h2>Visual Information</h2>
  <p>Visual information communicates meaning through images, symbols, diagrams, maps, tables, charts, graphs, posters and infographics. Readers must interpret both what is shown and how it is presented.</p>
  <h3>Common forms</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tables:</strong> Organise information into rows and columns.</li>
    <li><strong>Bar charts:</strong> Compare quantities across categories.</li>
    <li><strong>Line graphs:</strong> Show how values change, often over time.</li>
    <li><strong>Pie charts:</strong> Show parts of a whole.</li>
    <li><strong>Maps:</strong> Represent locations, routes or spatial patterns.</li>
    <li><strong>Posters and infographics:</strong> Combine words and visuals to inform or persuade.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to interpret a visual</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the title to identify the subject.</li>
    <li>Check labels, units, keys, scales and the source.</li>
    <li>Identify the values or symbols relevant to the question.</li>
    <li>Compare the relevant information accurately.</li>
    <li>State a conclusion supported by the visual, not by guesswork.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A bar chart shows books borrowed in one week: Monday 12, Tuesday 18 and Wednesday 15. Tuesday had the highest number. The difference between Tuesday and Monday was 18 − 12 = 6 books.</p>
  <h3>Notice how visuals can mislead</h3>
  <p>A graph with a vertical axis that starts above zero can make small differences look much larger. Images can also distract from missing evidence. Always inspect the scale and labels before drawing a conclusion.</p>
  <h3>Common errors</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse a category''s label with its value, ignore units, or claim that one factor caused another merely because the graph shows a relationship.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Understanding Visual Information', 15),
  (1586, 1586, '<h2>Purpose and Audience</h2>
  <p>Every text is produced for a reason and intended for particular readers or listeners. Identifying the purpose and audience helps you understand why the writer chose specific words, details and structures.</p>
  <h3>Common purposes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>To inform:</strong> Present facts or explain something.</li>
    <li><strong>To persuade:</strong> Influence beliefs, opinions or actions.</li>
    <li><strong>To entertain:</strong> Amuse or engage an audience.</li>
    <li><strong>To instruct:</strong> Explain how to perform a task.</li>
    <li><strong>To warn:</strong> Alert people to a danger or risk.</li>
    <li><strong>To describe:</strong> Give details about a person, place, object or experience.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Identifying the audience</h3>
  <p>Consider who the text addresses, their likely knowledge, their needs and the situation in which they will receive the message. The intended audience may be named directly or implied by the vocabulary and content.</p>
  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <p><strong>Text A:</strong> "To operate the machine, press the green button and wait for the indicator light."</p>
  <p>Purpose: to instruct. Audience: people operating the machine.</p>
  <p><strong>Text B:</strong> "Join our clean-up campaign this Saturday and help make our neighbourhood safer and healthier."</p>
  <p>Purpose: to persuade people to participate. Audience: members of the neighbourhood.</p>
  <h3>Use evidence, not guesses</h3>
  <p>Words such as "must", "join", "discover" or "beware" may suggest a purpose, but one word is not always enough. Look at the whole message, its structure and the action it encourages.</p>
  <h3>Common error</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse the topic with the purpose. A text about road safety might inform readers about accidents, warn them about danger or persuade them to obey traffic rules.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Identifying Purpose and Audience', 15),
  (1587, 1587, '<h2>Evaluating Sources</h2>
  <p>A source is where information comes from, such as a book, website, interview, report, video or social media post. Evaluating a source means deciding how suitable and trustworthy it is for a particular purpose.</p>
  <h3>Four questions to ask</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Authority:</strong> Who produced the information? Do they have relevant knowledge or responsibility?</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> Are claims supported by data, documents, examples or identifiable sources?</li>
    <li><strong>Currency:</strong> When was it published or updated? Does the topic require recent information?</li>
    <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Is the source informing, selling, entertaining, persuading or promoting a particular viewpoint?</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Check claims against other evidence</h3>
  <p>For important claims, compare the source with independent, credible sources. Check whether they support the same facts and whether the original evidence can be traced. Several websites repeating one unverified claim do not necessarily provide independent confirmation.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A social media account claims that a new school examination rule has been introduced. Before accepting the claim, check the relevant examination authority''s official announcement, its publication date and whether the rule actually applies to your examination.</p>
  <h3>Match the source to the task</h3>
  <p>A personal account can be useful for understanding someone''s experience, but it may not establish a national statistic. An official document may be authoritative about a published rule, but it may not explain every person''s experience of that rule.</p>
  <h3>Common errors</h3>
  <p>Do not assume that a source is accurate because it looks professional, has many followers or appears high in search results. Also, do not reject a source solely because it is unfamiliar; examine its evidence and relevance.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Evaluating Sources', 15),
  (1588, 1588, '<h2>Misleading Claims</h2>
  <p>A misleading claim gives an inaccurate impression of the facts. It may contain a false statement, omit important context, exaggerate evidence or present an opinion as if it were an established fact.</p>
  <h3>Common warning signs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Exaggeration:</strong> "This method works perfectly for everyone."</li>
    <li><strong>Missing context:</strong> Reporting a number without explaining what it measures or when it was collected.</li>
    <li><strong>Unsupported certainty:</strong> Presenting a prediction as a guaranteed outcome.</li>
    <li><strong>Misleading comparison:</strong> Comparing two figures measured under different conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Emotional pressure:</strong> Using fear or urgency to discourage careful checking.</li>
    <li><strong>False implication:</strong> Suggesting that one event caused another without evidence.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Fact, opinion and inference</h3>
  <p>A <strong>fact</strong> is a statement that can be checked against evidence. An <strong>opinion</strong> expresses a judgement or belief. An <strong>inference</strong> is a conclusion drawn from available information. Inferences can be reasonable, but they are not automatically proven facts.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p><strong>Claim:</strong> "Our club''s membership doubled this term, so our club is the best in the school."</p>
  <p>The increase in membership may be verifiable if the figures are accurate. However, the conclusion that the club is the best does not follow from membership growth alone. Other evidence would be needed to assess quality.</p>
  <h3>How to examine a claim</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify exactly what is being claimed.</li>
    <li>Find the evidence and its original source.</li>
    <li>Check dates, definitions, comparisons and missing information.</li>
    <li>Decide whether the evidence supports the full claim or only part of it.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Common error</h3>
  <p>A claim is not necessarily false just because it is persuasive or emotional. Evaluate its evidence rather than accepting or rejecting it based only on how it sounds.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recognising Misleading Claims', 15),
  (1589, 1589, '<h2>Comparing Sources</h2>
  <p>Comparing sources means examining two or more accounts of a subject to identify agreement, disagreement, differences in emphasis and gaps in evidence.</p>
  <h3>What to compare</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Main claim:</strong> What does each source say?</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> What facts, examples or data support each claim?</li>
    <li><strong>Date:</strong> Were the sources produced at different times?</li>
    <li><strong>Purpose and viewpoint:</strong> Is each source informing, persuading, reporting or promoting?</li>
    <li><strong>Scope:</strong> Are they discussing the same group, location, period or definition?</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p><strong>Source A:</strong> A school report states that attendance improved from 80% to 90% between two terms.</p>
  <p><strong>Source B:</strong> A learner says that many classmates were absent during the second term.</p>
  <p>The sources appear to disagree, but they may refer to different classes, days or ways of measuring attendance. Check the dates, groups and records before deciding that one source contradicts the other.</p>
  <h3>Use a comparison method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Write each source''s main claim.</li>
    <li>List the evidence each source gives.</li>
    <li>Check whether they use the same definitions and time period.</li>
    <li>Identify agreement, disagreement or information that only one source provides.</li>
    <li>Make a conclusion that reflects the strength and limits of the evidence.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Common error</h3>
  <p>Do not count the number of sources and assume the majority must be correct. Several sources may repeat the same original report. Independence, evidence and relevance matter more than quantity alone.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Comparing Information from Sources', 15),
  (1590, 1590, '<h2>Responsible Online Communication</h2>
  <p>Responsible online communication means sharing messages, images and information in ways that are respectful, accurate, safe and appropriate for the audience and platform.</p>
  <h3>Core practices</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Respect:</strong> Criticise ideas or actions without insulting or harassing people.</li>
    <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> Check important claims before forwarding them.</li>
    <li><strong>Privacy:</strong> Obtain permission before sharing another person''s private messages, photographs or personal details.</li>
    <li><strong>Security:</strong> Avoid sharing passwords, verification codes or sensitive information with untrusted people.</li>
    <li><strong>Context:</strong> Make sure a quotation, image or clip is not presented in a way that changes its meaning.</li>
    <li><strong>Accountability:</strong> Correct errors and acknowledge mistakes when you discover them.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Digital tone</h3>
  <p>Messages lack many cues available in face-to-face conversation, such as facial expression and tone of voice. A brief response can therefore sound rude even when that was not intended. Use clear wording and ask for clarification when necessary.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A classmate shares an unverified message claiming that school will be closed tomorrow. Instead of forwarding it immediately, check an official school announcement. If you already shared the incorrect message, send a correction to the same audience.</p>
  <h3>Before posting or forwarding</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Is the information accurate and in context?</li>
    <li>Is sharing it necessary and appropriate?</li>
    <li>Does it reveal someone else''s private information?</li>
    <li>Could the wording be misunderstood or needlessly hurtful?</li>
    <li>Can you take responsibility for the message if challenged?</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Common error</h3>
  <p>Being able to access a photograph or message does not automatically give you permission to share it. Likewise, deleting a post does not guarantee that nobody has saved or copied it.</p>', 'Canonical lesson notes for Responsible Online Communication', 15),
  (1591, 1591, '<h2>Data vs Information</h2>
<p><b>Data</b> refers to raw, unprocessed facts, while <b>Information</b> is processed data that is meaningful and useful.</p>
<h3>1. Data</h3>
<ul>
<li>Unorganized facts</li>
<li>No meaning on its own</li>
<li>Can be numbers, text, symbols</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
75, 82, 90 → just numbers (data)
</div>
<h3>2. Information</h3>
<ul>
<li>Processed and organized data</li>
<li>Has meaning</li>
<li>Used for decision making</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Average score = 82% → meaningful (information)
</div>
<h3>3. Differences</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Data</th><th>Information</th></tr>
<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Raw</td><td>Processed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Use</td><td>Input</td><td>Decision making</td></tr>
</table>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data vs Information', 15),
  (1592, 1592, '<h2>Characteristics of Computers</h2>
<p>Computers have unique features that make them powerful tools.</p>
<h3>Key Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Speed:</b> Performs millions of operations per second</li>
<li><b>Accuracy:</b> Produces correct results if input is correct</li>
<li><b>Diligence:</b> Does not get tired</li>
<li><b>Storage:</b> Can store large amounts of data</li>
<li><b>Automation:</b> Works automatically once programmed</li>
<li><b>Versatility:</b> Can perform many tasks</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Computer calculates results faster than humans → speed + accuracy
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Computers are powerful because they combine speed, accuracy, and storage.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Characteristics of Computers', 15),
  (1593, 1593, '<h2>Types of Computers</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Computers are classified based on size, power, and purpose. Each type is designed for specific tasks ranging from simple personal use to highly complex scientific calculations.
</p>
<h3> CLASSIFICATION BY SIZE & POWER</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Supercomputers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Most powerful computers</li>
<li>Used in weather forecasting, space research, simulations</li>
<li>Example tasks: climate modeling, nuclear research</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Mainframe Computers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Handle large-scale processing</li>
<li>Used by banks, governments, large organizations</li>
<li>Support many users at once</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Minicomputers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Mid-sized systems (less common today)</li>
<li>Used in small organizations</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Microcomputers (Personal Computers)</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Most common type</li>
<li>Examples: desktops, laptops, tablets</li>
<li>Used for everyday tasks</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Embedded Computers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Built into devices</li>
<li>Examples: washing machines, cars, microwaves</li>
<li>Perform specific functions</li>
</ul>
</ul>
<h3> COMPARISON TABLE</h3>
<pre>
Type           | Power     | Users      | Example Use
------------------------------------------------------
Supercomputer  | Very High | Few        | Weather prediction
Mainframe      | High      | Many       | Banking systems
Microcomputer  | Moderate  | One        | Personal use
Embedded       | Low       | Single use | Appliances
</pre>
<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Differences between supercomputer and mainframe</li>
<li>Examples of embedded systems</li>
<li>Classification based on purpose</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Types of Computers', 15),
  (1594, 1594, '<h2>Computer Generations (1st → 5th)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Computer generations refer to the evolution of computer technology over time.
Each generation is defined by major technological advancements.
</p>
<h3> GENERATIONS OVERVIEW</h3>
<h4>1⃣ First Generation (1940s–1950s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used vacuum tubes</li>
<li>Very large and expensive</li>
<li>Generated a lot of heat</li>
<li>Example: ENIAC</li>
</ul>
<h4>2⃣ Second Generation (1950s–1960s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used transistors</li>
<li>Smaller, faster, more reliable</li>
<li>Less heat produced</li>
</ul>
<h4>3⃣ Third Generation (1960s–1970s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used integrated circuits (ICs)</li>
<li>Even smaller and faster</li>
<li>Introduction of operating systems</li>
</ul>
<h4>4⃣ Fourth Generation (1970s–present)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used microprocessors</li>
<li>Rise of personal computers</li>
<li>Very high speed and efficiency</li>
</ul>
<h4>5⃣ Fifth Generation (present & future)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Artificial Intelligence (AI)</li>
<li>Machine learning and automation</li>
<li>Focus on smart systems</li>
</ul>
<h3> SUMMARY TABLE</h3>
<pre>
Generation | Technology        | Key Feature
------------------------------------------------
1st        | Vacuum tubes      | Large, hot
2nd        | Transistors       | Smaller, faster
3rd        | ICs               | Reliable
4th        | Microprocessors   | Personal computers
5th        | AI                | Intelligent systems
</pre>
<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Key technology in each generation</li>
<li>Differences between generations</li>
<li>Examples (ENIAC, modern AI systems)</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Computer Generations', 15),
  (1595, 1595, '<h2>Number Systems</h2>
<p>
  Computers use different number systems to represent and process data efficiently.
  These include decimal, binary, and hexadecimal systems, which can be converted into one another.
</p>
<h3> 1. Decimal (Base 10)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses digits 0–9</li>
  <li>Used in everyday human calculations</li>
  <li>Each digit has a place value based on powers of 10</li>
</ul>
<h3> 2. Binary (Base 2)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses only 0 and 1</li>
  <li>Each digit is called a bit</li>
  <li>Used internally by computers</li>
</ul>
<h3> 3. Hexadecimal (Base 16)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses digits 0–9 and letters A–F</li>
  <li>A = 10, B = 11, C = 12, D = 13, E = 14, F = 15</li>
  <li>Used to simplify long binary numbers</li>
</ul>
<h3> Why Binary is Used</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>1 = ON</b> (electric signal present)</li>
  <li><b>0 = OFF</b> (no signal)</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Computers use binary because they only understand two states:
ON  → 1
OFF → 0
</pre>
<h3> Number System Conversions</h3>
<h4> Decimal to Binary</h4>
<pre>
Example: 10₁₀ → Binary
10 ÷ 2 = 5 remainder 0
5 ÷ 2  = 2 remainder 1
2 ÷ 2  = 1 remainder 0
1 ÷ 2  = 0 remainder 1
Read from bottom to top:
10₁₀ = 1010₂
</pre>
<h4> Binary to Decimal</h4>
<pre>
Example: 1010₂ → Decimal
(1×2³) + (0×2²) + (1×2¹) + (0×2⁰)
= 8 + 0 + 2 + 0
= 10₁₀
</pre>
<h4> Binary to Hexadecimal</h4>
<pre>
Example: 1010₂ → Hexadecimal
Group into 4 bits:
1010 = A
So, 1010₂ = A₁₆
</pre>
<h4> Hexadecimal to Binary</h4>
<pre>
Example: A₁₆ → Binary
A = 10 in decimal
10 = 1010₂
So, A₁₆ = 1010₂
</pre>
<h3> Exam Focus Areas</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Convert decimal to binary</li>
  <li>Convert binary to decimal</li>
  <li>Convert binary to hexadecimal</li>
  <li>Explain why computers use binary</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Number Systems', 15),
  (1596, 1596, '<h2>Data Representation</h2>
<p>
  Data in computers is stored and processed using binary digits (0s and 1s).
  These binary values form the basis of all data storage and measurement.
</p>
<h3> 1. Units of Data Storage</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Bit:</b> Smallest unit of data (0 or 1)</li>
  <li><b>Nibble:</b> 4 bits</li>
  <li><b>Byte:</b> 8 bits</li>
  <li><b>Kilobyte (KB):</b> 1024 Bytes</li>
  <li><b>Megabyte (MB):</b> 1024 KB</li>
  <li><b>Gigabyte (GB):</b> 1024 MB</li>
  <li><b>Terabyte (TB):</b> 1024 GB</li>
</ul>
<h3> 2. Key Formula: Bits Calculation</h3>
<div class="example-box">
  <b>Formula:</b><br><br>
  Number of Bits = Number of Bytes × 8
</div>
<h3> 3. Examples of Bit Calculation</h3>
<pre>
Example 1:
5 Bytes → 5 × 8 = 40 Bits
Example 2:
10 Bytes → 10 × 8 = 80 Bits
Example 3:
2 KB → 2 × 1024 Bytes × 8 = 16384 Bits
</pre>
<h3> 4. Character Encoding</h3>
<h4> ASCII</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Uses numbers to represent characters</li>
  <li>Example: ''A'' = 65</li>
</ul>
<h4> Unicode</h4><ul><li>Supports many languages worldwide</li></ul>
<div class="example-box">
   Example:<br>
  ''A'' = 65 (ASCII)<br>
  Each character is stored using binary inside the computer
</div>
<h3> 5. Relationship Between Units</h3>
<pre>
1 Byte  = 8 Bits
1 KB     = 1024 Bytes
1 MB     = 1024 KB
1 GB     = 1024 MB
1 TB     = 1024 GB
</pre>
<h3> 6. Why Binary is Used</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Computers use ON (1) and OFF (0) signals</li>
  <li>Binary is easy for electronic circuits</li>
  <li>All data is stored as 0s and 1s</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Important Fact: All data in a computer is stored in binary form, and can be measured in bits and bytes.
</div>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Data storage works like building blocks where everything is made from bits.
  Bytes, KB, MB, and GB are just bigger groups of those bits.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data Representation', 15),
  (1597, 1597, '<h2>CPU Components (ALU, CU, Registers)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Central Processing Unit (CPU) is the main component of a computer system and is often called the “brain” of the computer.
It interprets and executes instructions, processes data, and controls the operations of all other components.
</p>
<h3> MAIN COMPONENTS OF THE CPU</h3>
<h4>1. Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Performs arithmetic operations such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division</li>
  <li>Performs logical operations such as AND, OR, NOT, and comparisons</li>
  <li>Handles decision-making processes inside the computer</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Control Unit (CU)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Directs and coordinates all activities inside the CPU</li>
  <li>Fetches instructions from memory</li>
  <li>Decodes instructions into signals the CPU can understand</li>
  <li>Controls the flow of data between CPU, memory, and input/output devices</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Registers</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very small and fast memory locations inside the CPU</li>
  <li>Store temporary data and instructions during processing</li>
  <li>Examples include:</li>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Accumulator:</b> Stores intermediate results of calculations</li>
    <li><b>Program Counter (PC):</b> Keeps track of the next instruction</li>
    <li><b>Instruction Register (IR):</b> Holds the current instruction being executed</li>
  </ul>
</ul>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF THE CPU</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Controls all computer operations</li>
  <li>Processes data and instructions</li>
  <li>Coordinates all hardware components</li>
  <li>Ensures programs run correctly</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for CPU Components', 15),
  (1598, 1598, '<h2>Instruction Cycle (Fetch → Decode → Execute)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The instruction cycle is the repeated process the CPU follows to execute program instructions.
It runs continuously while the computer is powered on, allowing software to function step by step.
</p>

<h3> MAIN STAGES OF THE INSTRUCTION CYCLE</h3>

<h4>1. Fetch</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The CPU retrieves an instruction from main memory (RAM)</li>
  <li>The instruction is stored in the Instruction Register (IR)</li>
  <li>The Program Counter (PC) points to the next instruction</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Decode</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The Control Unit (CU) interprets the instruction</li>
  <li>It determines what operation is required</li>
  <li>It identifies the data and components needed</li>
</ul>

<h4>3. Execute</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The ALU or other CPU components carry out the instruction</li>
  <li>Results are produced and stored in registers or memory</li>
  <li>Output may be sent to an output device if required</li>
</ul>

<h3> INSTRUCTION CYCLE FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Fetch → Decode → Execute → Fetch → Decode → Execute (repeats continuously)
</pre>

<h3> DETAILED PROCESS INSIDE CPU</h3>
<ul>
  <li>PC sends address to memory</li>
  <li>Instruction is fetched into IR</li>
  <li>CU decodes instruction</li>
  <li>ALU executes operation</li>
  <li>Result stored and PC updates</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANCE OF THE INSTRUCTION CYCLE</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Enables computers to run programs step by step</li>
  <li>Ensures systematic execution of instructions</li>
  <li>Keeps CPU working continuously and efficiently</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Instruction Cycle', 15),
  (1599, 1599, '<h2>Von Neumann Architecture</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Von Neumann architecture is a computer design model where both data and instructions are stored in the same main memory.
It forms the basic structure of most modern computers and follows a sequential processing system.
</p>
<h3> MAIN COMPONENTS</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>CPU:</b> Contains the ALU and Control Unit (executes instructions)</li>
  <li><b>Main Memory (RAM):</b> Stores both data and instructions</li>
  <li><b>Input Devices:</b> Used to enter data into the computer</li>
  <li><b>Output Devices:</b> Display processed results</li>
  <li><b>System Bus:</b> Transfers data and instructions between components</li>
</ul>
<h3> VON NEUMANN STRUCTURE</h3>
<pre>
Input Devices → Memory ↔ CPU → Output Devices
                     ↑
                 System Bus
</pre>

<h3> TYPES OF BUSES AND THEIR PURPOSES</h3>
<h4>1. Data Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers actual data between CPU, memory, and input/output devices</li>
  <li>Usually bidirectional (data can move both ways)</li>
  <li>Width of data bus affects performance (more bits = faster transfer)</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Address Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers memory addresses from CPU to memory</li>
  <li>Usually unidirectional (CPU → Memory)</li>
  <li>Determines how much memory a system can access</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Control Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers control signals between CPU and other components</li>
  <li>Includes signals like read, write, interrupt, and clock signals</li>
  <li>Ensures all parts of the system are synchronized</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Data and instructions share the same memory</li>
  <li>Instructions are processed one at a time (sequential execution)</li>
  <li>CPU follows the fetch–decode–execute cycle</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW IT WORKS</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Input data and instructions are stored in memory</li>
  <li>CPU fetches instruction from memory</li>
  <li>Instruction is decoded by Control Unit</li>
  <li>ALU executes the instruction</li>
  <li>Result is stored back in memory or sent to output</li>
</ol>
<h3> LIMITATION: VON NEUMANN BOTTLENECK</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Data and instructions share the same bus/path</li>
  <li>CPU must wait to access memory</li>
  <li>Slows down processing speed</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Problem:
CPU ↔ SAME BUS ↔ MEMORY (data + instructions)
This creates a bottleneck (traffic jam in data flow)
</pre>
<h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Simple and easy to design</li>
  <li>Cost-effective</li>
  <li>Flexible program storage (data + instructions together)</li>
</ul>
<h3> SIMPLE EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Von Neumann architecture is like a single road used by both instructions and data, which makes it simple but can cause delays when traffic is heavy.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Von Neumann Architecture', 15),
  (1600, 1600, '<h2>Introduction to Computers</h2>
<p>A <strong>computer</strong> is a machine that takes in information (input), processes it, stores it, and produces results (output).</p>
<h2>1. Hardware (Physical Parts)</h2>
<p><strong>Hardware</strong> is anything you can see and touch.</p>
<h3>Main Components</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Input Devices:</strong> Used to enter data into the computer</li>
<li><strong>Processing Unit (CPU):</strong> The "brain" of the computer</li>
<li><strong>Output Devices:</strong> Show results</li>
<li><strong>Storage Devices:</strong> Save data</li>
</ul>
<h3>Examples of Hardware</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keyboard (typing)</li>
<li>Mouse (pointing)</li>
<li>Monitor (screen)</li>
<li>CPU/System Unit (processing)</li>
<li>Hard drive / SSD (storage)</li>
<li>Printer (output)</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Software (Programs)</h2>
<p><strong>Software</strong> tells the hardware what to do.</p>

<h3>Types of Software</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>System Software:</strong> Controls the computer (Operating System)</li>
<li><strong>Application Software:</strong> Programs used by users (Word, Chrome)</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Examples:<br>
Windows (OS)<br>
Google Chrome (browser)<br>
Microsoft Word (document program)
</div>
<h2>3. Operating System (OS)</h2>
<p>The <strong>Operating System</strong> is the most important software. It connects the user to the hardware.</p>
<ul>
<li>Manages memory</li>
<li>Controls devices</li>
<li>Runs applications</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Examples:<br>
Windows<br>
Linux<br>
macOS
</div>
<h2>4. Peripheral Devices</h2>
<p>Peripheral devices are external hardware connected to the computer.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Input:</strong> keyboard, mouse, scanner</li>
<li><strong>Output:</strong> monitor, printer, speakers</li>
<li><strong>Storage:</strong> flash drive, external hard disk</li>
</ul>
<h2>5. Liveware (Human Factor)</h2>
<p><strong>Liveware</strong> refers to the human user of the computer.</p>
<ul>
<li>The person operating the computer</li>
<li>Gives instructions</li>
<li>Interprets results</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Without a user, a computer cannot function meaningfully.
</div>
<h2>How Everything Works Together</h2>
<ul>
<li>User (liveware) gives input</li>
<li>Input devices send data to CPU</li>
<li>CPU processes the data</li>
<li>Results are shown through output devices</li>
<li>Data can be stored for later use</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: A computer system = Hardware + Software + Liveware working together.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Hardware vs software', 15),
  (1601, 1601, '<h2>Input & Output Devices</h2>
<p>Input devices allow users to <strong>send data to the computer</strong>. Output devices allow the computer to <strong>present results to the user</strong>.</p>
<h2>1. Input Devices</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keyboard:</strong> Types letters, numbers, symbols. Used for typing documents, commands. <em>Advantage:</em> Fast for text input. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Requires literacy/knowledge of layout.</li>
<li><strong>Mouse:</strong> Pointing device to select items. Used for GUI navigation. <em>Advantage:</em> Accurate pointer control. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Needs flat surface.</li>
<li><strong>Touchscreen:</strong> Detects finger/stylus input directly on screen. Used in phones, tablets, ATMs. <em>Advantage:</em> Direct interaction, intuitive. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Can get dirty, less precise for long text.</li>
<li><strong>Stylus:</strong> Pen-like tool for touchscreens. Used in tablets for drawing or handwriting. <em>Advantage:</em> More precise than finger. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Can be lost, needs compatible screen.</li>
<li><strong>Joystick:</strong> Game controller, moves objects in multiple directions. Used in gaming, simulations. <em>Advantage:</em> Easy control in games. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Limited general use.</li>
<li><strong>Voice Input:</strong> Converts spoken words to digital commands. Used in smartphones, voice assistants. <em>Advantage:</em> Hands-free, fast. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Background noise may affect accuracy.</li>
<li><strong>Scanner:</strong> Converts physical documents/images to digital. Used in offices, schools. <em>Advantage:</em> Preserves original document. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Slower than direct typing.</li>
<li><strong>Pinball / Specialized Controllers:</strong> Used in entertainment or arcade systems. <em>Advantage:</em> Engaging interaction. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Limited to niche applications.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Typing a report → keyboard input<br>
Drawing a sketch → stylus input<br>
Moving a character in a game → joystick input<br>
Voice commands to phone → voice input
</div>
<h2>2. Output Devices</h2>
<p>Output devices allow the computer to present results to the user.</p>
<h3>Soft Copy Output</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monitor / Screen:</strong> Displays text, images, video. Advantage: Real-time, interactive. Disadvantage: Requires power, cannot be held physically.</li>
<li><strong>Speakers / Headphones:</strong> Produce sound output (music, alerts). Advantage: Hands-free audio feedback. Disadvantage: Affected by noise, not permanent.</li>
<li><strong>Voice Output:</strong> Converts text to speech (TTS). Advantage: Accessibility for visually impaired, hands-free. Disadvantage: Requires quality software, may mispronounce words.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Hard Copy Output</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Printers:</strong> Produces permanent copies on paper.</li>
<li><strong>Dot Matrix / Impact Printers:</strong> Strike ink onto paper via pins. Advantage: Cheap, multi-copy printing. Disadvantage: Noisy, low resolution.</li>
<li><strong>Laser Printers:</strong> Use laser beams to print toner. Advantage: High speed, high quality. Disadvantage: Expensive initial cost, toner cartridges.</li>
<li><strong>Inkjet Printers:</strong> Spray tiny ink droplets onto paper. Advantage: High quality, color printing. Disadvantage: Slower than laser, ink expensive.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Printing an assignment → hard copy (paper)<br>
Watching video → soft copy (monitor)<br>
Listening to music → soft copy (speakers)
</div>
<h2>3. Advantages and Disadvantages</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Device Type</th><th>Advantage</th><th>Disadvantage</th></tr>
<tr><td>Keyboard</td><td>Fast text input</td><td>Needs literacy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mouse</td><td>Accurate pointer control</td><td>Needs flat surface</td></tr>
<tr><td>Touchscreen</td><td>Intuitive, direct interaction</td><td>Less precise for typing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stylus</td><td>Precision drawing</td><td>Can be lost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Joystick</td><td>Easy control in games</td><td>Limited general use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voice Input</td><td>Hands-free</td><td>Background noise affects accuracy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monitor</td><td>Real-time display</td><td>Cannot hold physically</td></tr>
<tr><td>Printer</td><td>Permanent copy</td><td>Costs paper/ink</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speaker</td><td>Audio feedback</td><td>No visual info, affected by noise</td></tr>
</table>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Input & Output Devices', 15),
  (1602, 1602, '<h2>Memory & Storage</h2>
<p>Computers need memory to <strong>store data and instructions</strong>. There are two main types: <strong>Primary memory</strong> (fast, temporary) and <strong>Secondary storage</strong> (slower, permanent).</p>
<h2>1. Primary Memory (Main Memory)</h2>
<p>Used by the CPU to store data that is being actively used.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>RAM (Random Access Memory):</strong> Volatile memory; data lost when power is off. Used for active programs and OS. Types: <em>DRAM (Dynamic), SRAM (Static)</em>. Advantage: Fast access. Disadvantage: Temporary.</li>
<li><strong>ROM (Read-Only Memory):</strong> Non-volatile; stores permanent instructions (e.g., BIOS). Cannot be modified easily. Types: PROM, EPROM, EEPROM. Advantage: Permanent. Disadvantage: Cannot change easily.</li>
<li><strong>Cache Memory:</strong> Very fast memory located close to CPU. Stores frequently used data for quick access. Types: L1 (smallest, fastest), L2 (larger, slower than L1), L3 (largest, shared in multi-core CPUs). Advantage: Improves CPU performance. Disadvantage: Expensive, limited size.</li>
<li><strong>Registers:</strong> Smallest, fastest memory inside CPU. Temporarily stores data, instructions, or addresses being processed. Advantage: Immediate access for CPU. Disadvantage: Very limited storage.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Buses in Memory</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data Bus:</strong> Transfers data between CPU and memory.</li>
<li><strong>Address Bus:</strong> Carries memory addresses to read/write locations.</li>
<li><strong>Control Bus:</strong> Sends control signals (read/write operations).</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
CPU accesses RAM → stores active program<br>
Registers hold intermediate calculation results<br>
Cache holds frequently used data to speed CPU
</div>
<h2>2. Secondary Storage</h2>
<p>Used for permanent storage of programs and data. Slower than primary memory but non-volatile.</p>
<h3>Magnetic Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hard Disk Drive (HDD):</strong> Uses magnetic disks to store data. Advantage: High capacity, cheap per GB. Disadvantage: Slower than SSD, sensitive to shocks and magnets.</li>
<li><strong>Magnetic Tape:</strong> Stores large data sequentially. Advantage: Cheap, good for backups. Disadvantage: Slow, sequential access.</li>
<li><strong>Floppy Disk:</strong> Old magnetic storage. Advantage: Portable. Disadvantage: Very low capacity, obsolete.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Solid-State Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>SSD (Solid-State Drive):</strong> Uses flash memory, no moving parts. Advantage: Fast, durable. Disadvantage: More expensive per GB than HDD.</li>
<li><strong>USB Flash Drive:</strong> Portable flash memory. Advantage: Easy to carry, plug-and-play. Disadvantage: Small capacity, can be lost easily.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Optical Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>CD-ROM / CD-R / CD-RW:</strong> 700 MB. CD-R: write once. CD-RW: rewriteable. Advantage: Cheap, portable. Disadvantage: Low capacity, slower than HDD/SSD.</li>
<li><strong>DVD / DVD-R / DVD-RW:</strong> 4.7–8.5 GB. Advantage: Larger capacity than CD. Disadvantage: Slower than HDD/SSD.</li>
<li><strong>Blu-Ray Disc:</strong> 25–50 GB. Advantage: High capacity, good for HD video. Disadvantage: Expensive, requires Blu-Ray drive.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Handling & Care of Storage Devices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keep HDDs away from strong magnets → data can be erased.</li>
<li>Avoid exposing disks (HDD, CDs/DVDs/BDs) to dust, smoke, heat, or scratches.</li>
<li>Use anti-static precautions for RAM or motherboards.</li>
<li>Do not bend or drop flash drives or floppy disks.</li>
<li>Back up critical data to multiple storage types for safety.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Saving a document → stored on HDD/SSD<br>
Watching a movie → from DVD/Blu-Ray or SSD<br>
Booting computer → BIOS instructions from ROM
</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Key Fact: Primary memory is fast but volatile; secondary storage is slower but permanent. Proper care ensures long lifespan and data safety.</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Memory & Storage', 15),
  (1603, 1603, '<h2>Operating System</h2>
<p>An operating system (OS) is a set of system software that manages computer hardware and software resources, serving as an interface between the user and the computer hardware. It allows applications to run, manages memory, schedules tasks, and controls peripheral devices.</p>
<h2>Key Notes and Concepts</h2>
<h3>1. Primary Roles and Goals</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Interface:</strong> Acts as an intermediary between the user and computer hardware, making the computer convenient to use.</li>
<li><strong>Resource Manager:</strong> Allocates and manages shared hardware resources such as CPU time, memory, and Input/Output (I/O) devices, ensuring efficient utilization.</li>
<li><strong>Abstraction:</strong> Conceals the complexities of hardware from applications, providing a uniform interface.</li>
<li><strong>Kernel:</strong> The core, essential part of the OS that is always running and manages critical functions.</li>
<li><strong>Shell:</strong> The component that interprets user commands and gets them executed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Core Functions of an Operating System</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Process Management:</strong> Manages the execution of applications (processes), including scheduling, creation, termination, and synchronization.</li>
<li><strong>Memory Management:</strong> Tracks and allocates Main Memory (RAM). It decides which processes load into memory and manages virtual memory (using hard disk space as an extension of RAM).</li>
<li><strong>File Management:</strong> Organizes, creates, deletes, and manages files and directories on storage devices.</li>
<li><strong>Device Management:</strong> Uses device drivers to communicate with hardware peripherals (printers, disks, keyboards).</li>
<li><strong>Security and Protection:</strong> Protects data and resources from unauthorized access via authentication (passwords) and isolation of processes.</li>
<li><strong>Error Detection:</strong> Monitors system health and reports errors to prevent system failure.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Key OS Concepts and Techniques</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>System Calls:</strong> The interface between a running program and the OS kernel, allowing user-level programs to request services.</li>
<li><strong>Multitasking/Multiprogramming:</strong> The ability of an OS to switch between tasks quickly, allowing multiple programs to run seemingly at the same time and keeping the CPU busy.</li>
<li><strong>Interrupts:</strong> Signals sent to the CPU by hardware or software to request immediate attention, allowing the OS to react to events.</li>
<li><strong>Deadlock Handling:</strong> Manages situations where two or more processes are stuck waiting for resources held by each other.</li>
<li><strong>Spooling:</strong> Temporarily storing data in a buffer (e.g., for printers) so the CPU can continue other tasks while the slow device works.</li>
<li><strong>Booting:</strong> The process of starting a computer by loading the kernel into memory.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Types of Operating Systems</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Batch OS:</strong> Jobs with similar needs are grouped (batched) and processed together without user interaction (e.g., payroll).</li>
<li><strong>Time-Sharing (Multitasking) OS:</strong> Allows multiple users to share the system simultaneously by switching between tasks quickly (e.g., Unix, Windows).</li>
<li><strong>Distributed OS:</strong> Manages a group of independent computers and makes them appear as a single system.</li>
<li><strong>Real-Time OS (RTOS):</strong> Used when strict time constraints are required (e.g., robotics, airbag controllers).</li>
<li><strong>Mobile OS:</strong> Designed specifically for smartphones and tablets (e.g., Android, iOS).</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Common Examples</h3>
<ul>
<li>Windows: Microsoft’s commercial OS for desktops and servers.</li>
<li>Linux: Open-source, Unix-based OS, popular on servers.</li>
<li>macOS: Apple’s desktop OS.</li>
<li>Android/iOS: Dominant mobile operating systems.</li>
</ul>
<h3>6. OS Structure</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monolithic Kernel:</strong> All OS services run in kernel space, high performance but less stable (e.g., Linux).</li>
<li><strong>Microkernel:</strong> Only essential services run in kernel space; others run in user space, increasing stability (e.g., Minix).</li>
<li><strong>Hybrid Kernel:</strong> Combines aspects of both (e.g., Windows NT, macOS).</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Operating systems', 15),
  (1604, 1604, '<h2>File Management</h2>
<p>File management is a fundamental concept in computing that involves how an operating system (OS) stores, names, organizes, and manipulates files. In a high school curriculum, this typically covers the hierarchy of storage, file attributes, and common operations.</p>
<h3>1. The File Hierarchy (Folders and Directories)</h3>
<p>Computers use a hierarchical structure (often called a "tree structure") to organize data.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Drive:</strong> The top level of storage (e.g., C: drive for a Hard Drive, D: for a USB).</li>
<li><strong>Folders (Directories):</strong> Containers used to group related files together.</li>
<li><strong>Sub-folders:</strong> Folders inside other folders.</li>
<li><strong>Files:</strong> The actual data units (documents, images, programs).</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example Path:<br>
C:\Documents\School\ComputerScience\Notes.docx<br>
"School" is a folder, and "Notes.docx" is the file.
</div>
<h3>2. File Naming and Extensions</h3>
<p>A filename consists of two parts: the name and the extension, separated by a dot.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Filename:</strong> A descriptive name (e.g., History_Essay)</li>
<li><strong>Extension:</strong> Indicates the file type and program used to open it</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Extension</th><th>Program Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Documents</td><td>.docx, .pdf, .txt</td><td>Word, Adobe Reader</td></tr>
<tr><td>Images</td><td>.jpg, .png, .gif</td><td>Photos, Photoshop</td></tr>
<tr><td>Audio</td><td>.mp3, .wav</td><td>Spotify, Media Player</td></tr>
<tr><td>Video</td><td>.mp4, .mov</td><td>VLC, QuickTime</td></tr>
<tr><td>Executables</td><td>.exe, .app</td><td>System Applications</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>3. Basic File Operations</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Create:</strong> Making a new file or folder</li>
<li><strong>Save / Save As:</strong> Writing data to storage ("Save As" changes name/type)</li>
<li><strong>Copy:</strong> Duplicate file in another location</li>
<li><strong>Move (Cut/Paste):</strong> Change file location</li>
<li><strong>Rename:</strong> Change filename</li>
<li><strong>Delete:</strong> Send file to Recycle Bin / Trash</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. File Metadata and Attributes</h3>
<p>Files have hidden information called metadata:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>File Size:</strong> KB, MB, GB</li>
<li><strong>Date Created/Modified</strong></li>
<li><strong>Permissions:</strong> Read, Write, Execute</li>
<li><strong>Status:</strong> Hidden, System, Read-only</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Storage Best Practices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use descriptive names (e.g., Bio_Lab_Report_V2)</li>
<li>Organize files into folders</li>
<li>Backup files regularly (Cloud or external drive)</li>
<li>Avoid deep nesting (too many folders inside folders)</li>
</ul>
<h3>6. Compression and Archiving</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compression (.zip, .rar):</strong> Reduces file size</li>
<li><strong>Extracting:</strong> Restores compressed files to original form</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example:<br>
Large folder → compressed to .zip → sent via email → extracted by receiver
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: Good file management makes it easier to find files, saves storage space, and prevents data loss.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for File management', 15),
  (1605, 1605, '<h2>Introduction to Programming</h2>
<p><strong>Programming</strong> is the process of creating a set of instructions that tells a computer how to perform a specific task.</p>
<div class="example-box">
Think of programming like writing a recipe:<br>
Ingredients → Data<br>
Steps → Code<br>
Final dish → Output
</div>
<h2>1. Essential Programming Terminology</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Algorithm:</strong> A step-by-step procedure for solving a problem.</li>
<li><strong>Source Code:</strong> The actual text written by a programmer.</li>
<li><strong>Syntax:</strong> Rules that define how code must be written.</li>
<li><strong>Bug:</strong> An error in a program.</li>
<li><strong>Debugging:</strong> Finding and fixing errors.</li>
<li><strong>Variable:</strong> A container for storing data.</li>
<li><strong>Compiler/Interpreter:</strong> Tools that translate code into machine language (1s and 0s).</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. The Programming Process (SDLC)</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Problem Definition:</strong> Understand the problem.</li>
<li><strong>Design:</strong> Plan the solution (flowcharts, pseudocode).</li>
<li><strong>Coding:</strong> Write the program.</li>
<li><strong>Testing:</strong> Find and fix bugs.</li>
<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Update and improve the program.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example:<br>
Build a calculator → plan steps → code → test → fix errors
</div>
<h2>3. Categories of Programming Languages</h2>
<h3>Low-Level Languages</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Machine Code:</strong> Binary (0s and 1s), very hard for humans.</li>
<li><strong>Assembly Language:</strong> Uses short codes (MOV, ADD).</li>
</ul>
<h3>High-Level Languages (HLL)</h3>
<p>Designed to be easy for humans.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Language</th><th>Use</th><th>Strength</th></tr>
<tr><td>Python</td><td>AI, Data Science</td><td>Easy for beginners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Java</td><td>Apps, Systems</td><td>Portable</td></tr>
<tr><td>C++</td><td>Games</td><td>Fast</td></tr>
<tr><td>JavaScript</td><td>Web</td><td>Runs in browsers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swift</td><td>iOS apps</td><td>Optimized for Apple</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>4. Fundamental Logic Structures</h2>
<h3>A. Sequence</h3>
<p>Instructions executed step by step.</p>
<h3>B. Selection (Decision)</h3>
<p>Program chooses based on condition.</p>
<div class="example-box">
IF age ≥ 18 → allow voting<br>
ELSE → deny access
</div>
<h3>C. Iteration (Loop)</h3>
<p>Repeats instructions.</p>
<div class="example-box">
Send 100 emails automatically
</div>
<h2>5. Data Types</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Integer:</strong> Whole numbers (5, -42)</li>
<li><strong>Float:</strong> Decimal numbers (3.14)</li>
<li><strong>String:</strong> Text ("Hello")</li>
<li><strong>Boolean:</strong> True/False</li>
</ul>
<h2>6. Interpreted vs Compiled Languages</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compiled:</strong> Entire program translated before running (e.g., C++) → faster</li>
<li><strong>Interpreted:</strong> Runs line-by-line (e.g., Python) → easier debugging</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
C++ → compiled → fast execution<br>
Python → interpreted → easier to debug
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: Every program you use — from games to mobile apps — follows these same core principles.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Introduction to Programming', 15),
  (1606, 1606, '<h2>Loops & Conditionals</h2>
<p><strong>Conditionals:</strong> decision making</p>
<pre>
if x > 5:
  print("Big")
</pre>
<p><strong>Loops:</strong> repetition</p>
<pre>
for i in range(5):
  print(i)
</pre>
<div class="example-box">
Loop prints numbers 0–4
</div>
<h2>Control Structures</h2>
<p>Control structures are the "brain" of any program. They allow the code to make decisions and repeat tasks, moving beyond simple step-by-step execution.</p>
<h2>1. Conditional Statements (Selection)</h2>
<p>Conditionals allow a program to execute specific blocks of code only if a certain condition is met.</p>
<h3>A. The if Statement</h3>
<p>The most basic decision-making statement. If the condition is true, the code runs.</p>
<pre>
age = 20
if age >= 18:
    print("You are an adult.")
</pre>
<h3>B. The if-else Statement</h3>
<p>Provides an alternative path if the condition is false.</p>
<pre>
score = 45
if score >= 50:
    print("Pass")
else:
    print("Fail")
</pre>
<h3>C. The if-elif-else Ladder</h3>
<p>Used when there are more than two possible outcomes.</p>
<pre>
grade = 85
if grade >= 90:
    print("A")
elif grade >= 80:
    print("B")
elif grade >= 70:
    print("C")
else:
    print("D or F")
</pre>
<h3>D. Nested if Statements</h3>
<p>An if statement inside another if for complex conditions.</p>
<pre>
has_ticket = True
has_id = False

if has_ticket:
    if has_id:
        print("Entry allowed.")
    else:
        print("Please show your ID.")
else:
    print("No ticket, no entry.")
</pre>
<h2>2. Loops (Iteration)</h2>
<p>Loops repeat a block of code multiple times.</p>
<h3>A. The for Loop</h3>
<p>Used when you know how many times to repeat.</p>
<pre>
for i in range(5):
    print("Loop number:", i)
</pre>
<h3>B. The while Loop</h3>
<p>Repeats while a condition is true.</p>
<pre>
count = 5
while count > 0:
    print(count)
    count = count - 1
print("Blast off!")
</pre>
<h3>C. Nested Loops</h3>
<p>A loop inside another loop.</p>
<pre>
for row in range(3):
    for col in range(3):
        print("Row", row, "Col", col)
</pre>
<h2>3. Key Loop Controls</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>break:</strong> Stops the loop immediately</li>
<li><strong>continue:</strong> Skips current iteration</li>
</ul>
<h2>Comparison: Loops vs Conditionals</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Conditionals (if)</th><th>Loops (for/while)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Decision making</td><td>Repetition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Execution</td><td>Runs once</td><td>Runs repeatedly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keywords</td><td>if, else, elif</td><td>for, while</td></tr>
<tr><td>Example</td><td>Checking password</td><td>Printing multiple pages</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="keyfact">
 Conditionals decide WHAT to do, loops decide HOW MANY TIMES to do it.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Loops & conditionals', 15),
  (1607, 1607, '<h2>Functions & Methods</h2>
<p>A function is reusable code.</p>
<pre>
def add(a,b):
  return a+b
</pre>
<div class="example-box">
add(2,3) = 5
</div>
<h2>In programming, functions and methods are blocks of reusable code designed to perform a specific action.</h2>
<p>They help break down complex programs into smaller, manageable, and organized pieces.</p>
<h2>1. What is a Function?</h2>
<p>A function is a self-contained block of code that only runs when it is called. You can pass data, known as parameters, into a function, and it can return data as a result.</p>
<h3>Key Components:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Declaration/Definition:</strong> Creating the function and giving it a name.</li>
<li><strong>Parameters:</strong> Variables listed in the function definition (the "inputs").</li>
<li><strong>Arguments:</strong> The actual values sent to the function when it is called.</li>
<li><strong>Return Statement:</strong> The "output" the function sends back to the main program.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example in Python:</h3>
<pre>
# Defining the function
def greet_user(name): 
    return "Hello, " + name
# Calling the function
message = greet_user("Alex")
print(message)
</pre>
<h2>2. What is a Method?</h2>
<p>A method is essentially a function that "belongs" to an object or a class. While functions can be called by themselves, a method must be called on a specific object.</p>
<h3>The Difference:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Function:</strong> calculate_area(5, 10) (Independent)</li>
<li><strong>Method:</strong> my_dog.bark() (Dependent on the "dog" object)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example of a String Method:</h3>
<pre>
text = "hello world"
print(text.upper())
</pre>
<h2>3. Why Use Functions and Methods?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reusability (DRY):</strong> Write code once and reuse it.</li>
<li><strong>Abstraction:</strong> Use code without knowing internal details.</li>
<li><strong>Organization:</strong> Makes code easier to read and debug.</li>
<li><strong>Modularity:</strong> Allows teamwork in programming.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4. Function Scope (Local vs Global)</h2>
<p>Scope determines where a variable can be used.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Local Scope:</strong> Variable inside function.</li>
<li><strong>Global Scope:</strong> Variable outside function.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example:</h3>
<pre>
x = 10 # Global variable
def my_function():
    y = 5 # Local variable
    print(x)
    print(y)

my_function()
print(y) # ERROR
</pre>
<h2>5. Built-in vs User-Defined Functions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Built-in:</strong> Provided by language (print(), len())</li>
<li><strong>User-defined:</strong> Created by programmer</li>
</ul>
<h2>Summary Table</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Function</th><th>Method</th></tr>
<tr><td>Location</td><td>Independent</td><td>Inside class</td></tr>
<tr><td>Call</td><td>function_name()</td><td>object.method()</td></tr>
<tr><td>Relationship</td><td>No object</td><td>Linked to object</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data</td><td>Uses parameters</td><td>Uses object data</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="keyfact">
 Functions are independent blocks of code, while methods belong to objects.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Functions & methods', 15),
  (1608, 1608, '<h2>Arrays & Lists</h2>
<p>In programming, we often need to store a collection of similar items (like a list of student names or temperatures). Instead of creating many separate variables, we use <b>Arrays and Lists</b>.</p>
<h3>1. What is an Array?</h3>
<p>An <b>Array</b> is a collection of items stored at contiguous (neighboring) memory locations. It holds a fixed number of items, usually of the same data type.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Fixed Size:</b> Cannot be changed after creation.</li>
<li><b>Homogeneous:</b> Same data type.</li>
<li><b>Index-Based:</b> Each element has a position.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. The Concept of Indexing</h3>
<p>Indexing starts at <b>0</b>:</p>
<ul>
<li>First element → index 0</li>
<li>Second element → index 1</li>
<li>Last element → index (n-1)</li>
</ul>
<pre>
prices = [10.50, 20.00, 5.75]
prices[0] = 10.50
prices[2] = 5.75
</pre>
<h3>3. Arrays vs Lists</h3>
<table border="1">
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Array</th><th>List</th></tr>
<tr><td>Size</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Dynamic</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data Types</td><td>Same type</td><td>Mixed allowed (Python)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Memory</td><td>Efficient</td><td>More flexible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Languages</td><td>C, C++, Java</td><td>Python, JavaScript</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>4. Common Operations</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Accessing:</b> Get value using index</li>
<li><b>Updating:</b> Change value</li>
<li><b>Appending:</b> Add item (Lists)</li>
<li><b>Traversing:</b> Loop through items</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Code Examples</h3>
<h4>Python (Lists)</h4>
<pre>
fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Cherry"]
fruits.append("Orange")
fruits[1] = "Blueberry"
for x in fruits:
    print(x)
</pre>
<h4>Java (Arrays)</h4>
<pre>
int[] numbers = new int[5];

numbers[0] = 10;
numbers[1] = 20;

System.out.println(numbers[0]);
</pre>
<h3>6. Two-Dimensional (2D) Arrays</h3>
<pre>
grid = [
  [1,2,3],
  [4,5,6],
  [7,8,9]
]

print(grid[1][1])
</pre>
<h3>7. Why Use Arrays/Lists?</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Efficiency:</b> Fast access using index</li>
<li><b>Group Processing:</b> Handle many items with loops</li>
<li><b>Sorting:</b> Organize data easily</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
numbers = [1,2,3]
numbers[0] = 1
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Arrays & lists', 15),
  (1609, 1609, '<h2>Debugging</h2>
<p>
<b>Debugging</b> is the systematic process of identifying, analyzing, and fixing errors (bugs) in a computer program. It is a core skill in programming and software development.
</p>
<h3> DEBUGGING PROCESS</h3>
<ol>
<li><b>Reproduce the error</b>: Make the bug happen consistently</li>
<li><b>Locate the error</b>: Identify the exact part of the code causing the issue</li>
<li><b>Analyze the cause</b>: Understand why the error occurs</li>
<li><b>Fix and test</b>: Correct the code and verify the solution</li>
</ol>
<h3> TYPES OF ERRORS</h3>
<h4>1. Syntax Errors</h4>
<p>
Errors caused by breaking programming language rules. The program will not run.
</p>
<pre>
if age >= 18
    print("Allowed")  # Missing colon causes error

# Fix
if age >= 18:
    print("Allowed")
</pre>
<h4>2. Runtime Errors</h4>
<p>
Errors that occur while the program is running (often causes crash).
</p>
<pre>
x = 10
y = 0
print(x / y)  # Division by zero error

# Fix
if y != 0:
    print(x / y)
</pre>
<h4>3. Logical Errors</h4>
<p>
The program runs but produces incorrect results.
</p>
<pre>
# Wrong
average = num1 + num2 / 2

# Correct
average = (num1 + num2) / 2
</pre>
<h3> COMMON ERROR TYPES</h3>
<table border="1">
<tr><th>Error</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
<tr><td>NameError</td><td>Variable not defined</td><td>Define before use</td></tr>
<tr><td>TypeError</td><td>Wrong data type operation</td><td>Convert types (e.g. str(), int())</td></tr>
<tr><td>IndexError</td><td>Invalid list index</td><td>Check list size</td></tr>
<tr><td>IndentationError</td><td>Wrong spacing</td><td>Fix indentation</td></tr>
</table>
<h3> DEBUGGING TECHNIQUES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Print debugging</b>: Use print() to trace values</li>
<li><b>Debugger tools</b>: Breakpoints, step-by-step execution</li>
<li><b>Rubber duck debugging</b>: Explain code to find logic errors</li>
<li><b>Code isolation</b>: Comment out sections to locate errors</li>
</ul>
<pre>
print("DEBUG:", x)
</pre>
<h3> DEBUGGING CHECKLIST</h3>
<ul>
<li>Read error messages carefully</li>
<li>Check spelling and syntax</li>
<li>Verify variable values and types</li>
<li>Simplify the problem</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Debugging = Systematic process of finding and fixing errors in code
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Debugging', 15),
  (1610, 1610, '<h2>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)</h2>
<p>
<b>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)</b> is a programming paradigm that organizes software around <b>objects</b>, which combine data (attributes) and behavior (methods).
</p>
<h3> REAL-LIFE ANALOGY</h3>
<div class="example-box">
Car → Object<br>
Properties → color, speed<br>
Methods → drive(), brake()
</div>
<h3> CORE PRINCIPLES (PILLARS OF OOP)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Encapsulation</b>: Hiding internal data and controlling access</li>
<li><b>Inheritance</b>: Reusing properties from another class</li>
<li><b>Polymorphism</b>: Same method behaves differently in different classes</li>
<li><b>Abstraction</b>: Hiding complex implementation and showing only essentials</li>
</ul>
<h3> BASIC STRUCTURE (PYTHON)</h3>
<pre>
class Car:
    def __init__(self, brand):
        self.brand = brand

    def drive(self):
        print(self.brand + " is moving")

my_car = Car("Toyota")
my_car.drive()
</pre>
<h3> KEY CONCEPTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Class</b>: Blueprint for objects</li>
<li><b>Object</b>: Instance of a class</li>
<li><b>Attributes</b>: Data inside objects</li>
<li><b>Methods</b>: Functions inside classes</li>
</ul>
<h3> WHY OOP IS IMPORTANT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Improves code reusability</li>
<li>Makes programs modular</li>
<li>Easy to maintain and scale</li>
<li>Closer to real-world modeling</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 OOP = Objects + Data + Behavior working together
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)', 15),
  (1611, 1611, '<h2>Classes & Objects</h2>
<p>
In Object-Oriented Programming (OOP), a <b>class</b> is a blueprint (template), while an <b>object</b> is a real instance created from that blueprint.
</p>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Class</b>: Defines structure (attributes + methods)</li>
<li><b>Object</b>: A real entity created from a class with actual values</li>
</ul>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
class Student:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

student1 = Student("Alex")

print(student1.name)
</pre>
<h3> COMPONENTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Attributes</b>: Variables inside a class (e.g., name)</li>
<li><b>Methods</b>: Functions inside a class</li>
<li><b>Constructor (__init__)</b>: Initializes object values</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD ANALOGY</h3>
<ul>
<li>Class → Blueprint of a house</li>
<li>Object → Actual house built from blueprint</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Class = Template<br>
Object = Real instance created from template
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Classes & Objects', 15),
  (1612, 1612, '<h2>Inheritance</h2>
<p>
<b>Inheritance</b> is an Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concept where a new class (child class) acquires properties and methods from an existing class (parent class).
</p>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<p>
It represents a <b>parent → child relationship</b> and allows code reuse and extension.
</p>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
class Animal:
    def speak(self):
        print("Animal sound")

class Dog(Animal):   # Dog inherits from Animal
    def bark(self):
        print("Woof!")

d = Dog()
d.speak()  # inherited method
d.bark()   # own method
</pre>
<h3> TYPES OF INHERITANCE</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Single Inheritance</b>: One parent → one child</li>
<li><b>Multiple Inheritance</b>: One child → multiple parents</li>
<li><b>Multilevel Inheritance</b>: Grandparent → Parent → Child</li>
<li><b>Hierarchical Inheritance</b>: One parent → multiple children</li>
</ul>
<h3> BENEFITS OF INHERITANCE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Code reusability</li>
<li>Reduces repetition</li>
<li>Improves maintainability</li>
<li>Supports hierarchical classification</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD ANALOGY</h3>
<ul>
<li>Animal → Dog, Cat, Bird</li>
<li>Vehicle → Car, Bus, Truck</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Inheritance = Parent class shares features with child classes
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Inheritance', 15),
  (1613, 1613, '<h2>Encapsulation</h2>
<p>
<b>Encapsulation</b> is an Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concept that involves 
<b>bundling data (variables) and methods (functions)</b> together and restricting direct access to the data.
</p>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<p>
Data is hidden from outside access and can only be modified through controlled methods.
</p>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
class Bank:
    def __init__(self):
        self.__balance = 0   # Private variable

    def deposit(self, amount):
        self.__balance += amount

    def get_balance(self):
        return self.__balance
</pre>
<h3> ACCESS MODIFIERS (IMPORTANT)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Public</b>: Accessible anywhere</li>
<li><b>Protected (_var)</b>: Should not be accessed directly</li>
<li><b>Private (__var)</b>: Strongly restricted access</li>
</ul>
<h3> WHY ENCAPSULATION?</h3>
<ul>
<li>Protects data from unauthorized access</li>
<li>Prevents accidental modification</li>
<li>Improves code security and reliability</li>
<li>Controls how data is used</li>
</ul>
<h3> REAL-WORLD ANALOGY</h3>
<ul>
<li>ATM Machine → You cannot access money directly</li>
<li>You use methods (deposit/withdraw) instead</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Encapsulation = Data hiding + Controlled access
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Encapsulation', 15),
  (1614, 1614, '<h2>Recursion</h2>
<p>
<b>Recursion</b> is a programming technique where a function calls itself to solve a problem by breaking it into smaller sub-problems.
</p>
<h3> KEY COMPONENTS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Base Case</b>: The stopping condition that ends recursion</li>
<li><b>Recursive Case</b>: The part where the function calls itself</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW IT WORKS</h3>
<pre>
factorial(3)
= 3 × factorial(2)
= 3 × 2 × factorial(1)
= 3 × 2 × 1
= 6
</pre>
<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE (FACTORIAL)</h3>
<pre>
def factorial(n):
    if n == 1:   # Base case
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n-1)  # Recursive call
</pre>
<h3> IMPORTANT RULES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Must have a base case (to avoid infinite recursion)</li>
<li>Each call should move toward the base case</li>
<li>Uses stack memory (call stack)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> RECURSION VS ITERATION</h3>
<pre>
Recursion        | Iteration
-----------------------------
Function calls   | Loops (for/while)
Uses call stack  | Uses variables
Cleaner logic    | More memory efficient
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Factorial and Fibonacci calculations</li>
<li>Tree and graph traversal</li>
<li>File system navigation</li>
<li>Divide-and-conquer algorithms</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Recursion = Function solving smaller versions of itself
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Recursion', 15),
  (1615, 1615, '<h2>Stacks</h2>



<p>
A <b>stack</b> is a linear data structure that follows the <b>LIFO (Last In, First Out)</b> principle.
The last element added is the first one to be removed.
</p>

---

<h3> KEY OPERATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Push</b>: Add element to the top</li>
<li><b>Pop</b>: Remove the top element</li>
<li><b>Peek/Top</b>: View the top element without removing it</li>
<li><b>isEmpty</b>: Check if stack has no elements</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> VISUAL FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Push → [1, 2, 3] → Pop → [1, 2]
            ↑
          Top
</pre>

---

<h3> TYPES OF STACKS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Simple Stack</b>: Basic LIFO structure</li>
<li><b>Dynamic Stack</b>: Grows/shrinks in size (using lists/linked lists)</li>
<li><b>Call Stack</b>: Used in function calls (program execution)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
stack = []

# Push
stack.append(1)
stack.append(2)

# Pop
stack.pop()

# Peek
print(stack[-1])
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Undo/Redo operations in apps</li>
<li>Function calls (call stack)</li>
<li>Expression evaluation (e.g., calculators)</li>
<li>Browser history navigation</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Stack = Pile of plates <br>
Last plate added → first removed
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Stacks', 15),
  (1616, 1616, '<h2>Queues</h2>



<p>
A <b>queue</b> is a linear data structure that follows the <b>FIFO (First In, First Out)</b> principle.
The first element added is the first one to be removed.
</p>

---

<h3> KEY OPERATIONS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Enqueue</b>: Add element to the rear (end)</li>
<li><b>Dequeue</b>: Remove element from the front</li>
<li><b>Peek/Front</b>: View the first element without removing it</li>
<li><b>isEmpty</b>: Check if queue has no elements</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> VISUAL FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Enqueue → [1, 2, 3] → Dequeue → [2, 3]
</pre>

---

<h3> TYPES OF QUEUES</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Simple Queue</b>: Basic FIFO structure</li>
<li><b>Circular Queue</b>: End connects to front to reuse space</li>
<li><b>Priority Queue</b>: Elements processed based on priority</li>
<li><b>Deque (Double-ended Queue)</b>: Insert/remove from both ends</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> PYTHON EXAMPLE</h3>
<pre>
from collections import deque

queue = deque()

# Enqueue
queue.append(1)
queue.append(2)

# Dequeue
queue.popleft()

# Peek
print(queue[0])
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Printer queue (documents printed in order)</li>
<li>CPU task scheduling</li>
<li>Call center systems</li>
<li>Data buffering in streaming</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Queue = Line at a shop <br>
First person in line → first served
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Queues', 15),
  (1617, 1617, '<h2>Advanced Databases</h2>

  <p>
    Advanced databases focus on powerful techniques used in real-world systems to manage large and complex data efficiently.
    These include JOIN operations, normalization, indexing, and performance optimization.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Advanced database systems are designed to organize data efficiently, reduce duplication, and speed up data retrieval.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. SQL JOINs</h3>
  <p>
    JOIN operations are used to combine data from two or more tables based on related columns.
  </p>

  <h4>INNER JOIN</h4>
  <p>Returns only records that match in both tables.</p>

  <pre>
SELECT Students.Name, Courses.CourseName
FROM Students
INNER JOIN Enrollments ON Students.ID = Enrollments.StudentID
INNER JOIN Courses ON Enrollments.CourseID = Courses.CourseID;
  </pre>

  <h4>LEFT JOIN</h4>
  <p>Returns all records from the left table and matching records from the right table.</p>

  <pre>
SELECT Students.Name, Courses.CourseName
FROM Students
LEFT JOIN Enrollments ON Students.ID = Enrollments.StudentID
LEFT JOIN Courses ON Enrollments.CourseID = Courses.CourseID;
  </pre>

  <h4>RIGHT JOIN</h4>
  <p>Returns all records from the right table and matching records from the left table.</p>

  <h3> 2. Normalization</h3>
  <p>
    Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database to reduce redundancy and improve data integrity.
  </p>

  <h4> Before Normalization</h4>
  <pre>
Student | Course | Instructor
Alex    | Math   | Mr. John
Alex    | Science| Mr. Mike
  </pre>

  <h4> After Normalization</h4>
  <pre>
Students Table
ID | Name

Courses Table
CourseID | CourseName

Enrollments Table
StudentID | CourseID
  </pre>

  <p>
    This removes duplication and improves efficiency.
  </p>

  <h3> 3. Indexing</h3>
  <p>
    An index is a database structure that improves the speed of data retrieval operations.
  </p>

  <pre>
CREATE INDEX idx_name ON Students(Name);
  </pre>

  <p>
    Without an index, the database scans every record. With an index, it quickly locates data.
  </p>

  <h3> 4. Performance Optimization</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Query Optimization:</b> Writing efficient SQL statements</li>
    <li><b>Indexing:</b> Speeds up searching</li>
    <li><b>Efficient Joins:</b> Reduce processing time</li>
    <li><b>Normalization:</b> Reduces redundancy</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These tables are connected using relationships and JOIN queries to retrieve data efficiently.
  </p>

  <h3> Summary</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    JOIN = Combine tables<br>
    Normalization = Remove duplication<br>
    Index = Speed up searching<br>
    Optimization = Improve performance
  </div>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Advanced databases are like highly organized libraries where books are sorted, indexed, and connected for fast retrieval.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Advanced Databases', 15),
  (1618, 1618, '<h2>Tables & Records</h2>
  <p>
    A database is an organized collection of related data stored electronically and managed by a
    Database Management System (DBMS) such as MySQL or PostgreSQL.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Databases store information in structured formats called tables, making data easy to access, update, and manage.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Components of a Database System</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Hardware:</b> Physical devices such as servers, storage drives, and memory</li>
    <li><b>Software:</b> DBMS like MySQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL</li>
    <li><b>Data:</b> The actual information stored in the system</li>
    <li><b>Procedures:</b> Rules and instructions for managing data</li>
    <li><b>Users:</b> Database administrators and end users</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Data Organization in Tables</h3>
  <p>
    Data in a database is stored in tables similar to spreadsheets.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Table:</b> A collection of related data</li>
    <li><b>Row (Record):</b> A single complete entry (e.g., one student)</li>
    <li><b>Column (Field):</b> A specific type of data (e.g., Name, Age)</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Example Table</h4>
  <table border="1">
    <tr><th>ID</th><th>Name</th><th>Age</th></tr>
    <tr><td>101</td><td>Alex</td><td>20</td></tr>
    <tr><td>102</td><td>Mary</td><td>22</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3> 3. Primary Key</h3>
  <p>
    A primary key is a field that uniquely identifies each record in a table.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Must be unique</li>
    <li>Cannot be empty (NULL)</li>
    <li>Used to avoid duplicate records</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Student ID = Primary Key
  </div>

  <h3> 4. Importance of Databases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Organizes large amounts of data efficiently</li>
    <li>Improves data retrieval and storage</li>
    <li>Reduces duplication of data</li>
    <li>Supports fast decision-making</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A database is like a digital filing cabinet where information is stored in organized tables.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Tables & Records', 15),
  (1619, 1619, '<h2>SQL (Structured Query Language)</h2>
  <p>
    SQL is a standard programming language used to communicate with databases.
    It allows users to create, retrieve, update, and delete data in a database system.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    SQL is the language that allows humans to communicate with databases and control stored data.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Basic SQL Query</h3>
  <p>
    The SELECT statement is used to retrieve data from a database table.
  </p>

  <pre>
SELECT * FROM students;
  </pre>

  <p>
    This command retrieves all records from the "students" table.
  </p>

  <h3> 2. Filtering Data</h3>
  <p>
    The WHERE clause is used to filter records based on conditions.
  </p>

  <pre>
SELECT Name FROM students WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>

  <p>
    This retrieves only the name of the student with ID 101.
  </p>

  <h3> 3. Common SQL Commands</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>SELECT:</b> Retrieves data</li>
    <li><b>INSERT:</b> Adds new data</li>
    <li><b>UPDATE:</b> Modifies existing data</li>
    <li><b>DELETE:</b> Removes data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. SQL Tools</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Direct SQL:</b> Writing commands manually in a query editor</li>
    <li><b>Form-Based Interfaces:</b> Using applications to input data</li>
    <li><b>Database Tools:</b> phpMyAdmin, MySQL Workbench, Microsoft Access</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 5. Example Queries</h3>
  <pre>
-- Select specific columns
SELECT Name, Age FROM students;

-- Filter records
SELECT * FROM students WHERE Age > 18;

-- Insert data
INSERT INTO students (Name, Age) VALUES (''John'', 20);
  </pre>

  <h3> 6. Importance of SQL</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Used in almost all modern applications</li>
    <li>Handles large volumes of data efficiently</li>
    <li>Essential for web development and backend systems</li>
    <li>Supports data analysis and reporting</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    SQL is like giving instructions to a librarian so they can find, add, or remove books from a library database.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for SQL Basics', 15),
  (1620, 1620, '<h2>CRUD Operations</h2>
  <p>
    CRUD represents the four fundamental operations used to manage data in a database system.
    These operations form the basis of all database interactions and backend systems.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    CRUD is the foundation of database management, controlling how data is created, accessed, modified, and removed.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. Meaning of CRUD</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Create:</b> Adds new records into a database</li>
    <li><b>Read:</b> Retrieves existing data</li>
    <li><b>Update:</b> Modifies existing records</li>
    <li><b>Delete:</b> Removes records from a database</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 2. CRUD and SQL Mapping</h3>
  <h4>Create (INSERT)</h4>
  <pre>
INSERT INTO Students (ID, Name) VALUES (101, ''Alex'');
  </pre>
  <h4>Read (SELECT)</h4>
  <pre>
SELECT Name FROM Students WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>
  <h4>Update</h4>
  <pre>
UPDATE Students SET Name = ''Alexander'' WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>
  <h4>Delete</h4>
  <pre>
DELETE FROM Students WHERE ID = 101;
  </pre>
  <h3> 3. Database Integrity Rules</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Data Type Validation:</b> Ensures correct data formats are used</li>
    <li><b>Referential Integrity:</b> Maintains relationships between related tables</li>
    <li><b>Primary Key Constraint:</b> Ensures uniqueness of records</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 4. Importance of CRUD</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Forms the core of all database operations</li>
    <li>Used in every application that stores data</li>
    <li>Ensures structured and controlled data management</li>
    <li>Supports backend system development</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    CRUD is like managing a notebook: you can write new information, read it, edit it, or erase it when no longer needed.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for CRUD Operations', 15),
  (1621, 1621, '<h2>Relational Databases</h2>
  <p>
    A relational database is a type of database that stores data in multiple tables that are linked together using relationships.
    This structure reduces duplication and improves data organization and efficiency.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Relational databases store data in separate tables and connect them using keys to ensure efficiency and avoid duplication.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. Why Relationships Matter</h3>
  <p>
    Instead of storing all data in one large table, relational databases divide data into smaller tables and link them.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Students Table → student details<br>
    Courses Table → course details<br>
    Enrollments Table → links students and courses
  </div>
  <h3> 2. Types of Relationships</h3>
  <h4>One-to-One (1:1)</h4>
  <p>One record in a table is linked to one record in another table.</p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: One student → One ID card
  </div>
  <h4>One-to-Many (1:M)</h4>
  <p>One record in a table is linked to many records in another table.</p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: One teacher → Many students
  </div>
  <h4>Many-to-Many (M∞M)</h4>
  <p>Many records in one table are linked to many records in another table.</p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Students ↔ Courses
  </div>
  <h3> 3. Primary Key</h3>
  <p>
    A primary key is a unique identifier for each record in a table.
  </p>
  <pre>
Students Table
ID | Name
101 | Alex
102 | Jane
  </pre>

  <h3> 4. Foreign Key</h3>
  <p>
    A foreign key is a field in one table that refers to the primary key in another table.
  </p>

  <pre>
Enrollments Table
StudentID | CourseID
101       | 501
  </pre>

  <h3> 5. Example Database Structure</h3>
  <pre>
Students
ID | Name

Courses
CourseID | CourseName

Enrollments
StudentID | CourseID
  </pre>

  <h3> 6. SQL Example (Relationships)</h3>
  <pre>
CREATE TABLE Students (
  ID INT PRIMARY KEY,
  Name VARCHAR(50)
);

CREATE TABLE Courses (
  CourseID INT PRIMARY KEY,
  CourseName VARCHAR(50)
);

CREATE TABLE Enrollments (
  StudentID INT,
  CourseID INT,
  FOREIGN KEY (StudentID) REFERENCES Students(ID),
  FOREIGN KEY (CourseID) REFERENCES Courses(CourseID)
);
  </pre>

  <h3> 7. Referential Integrity</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ensures relationships between tables remain valid</li>
    <li>Prevents invalid foreign key entries</li>
    <li>Stops deletion of linked records</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 8. Importance of Relational Databases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Reduces data duplication</li>
    <li>Improves data accuracy and consistency</li>
    <li>Allows complex queries across multiple tables</li>
    <li>Makes data management scalable</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A relational database is like a network of linked notebooks where each notebook stores a different type of information, but all are connected.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Relational Databases', 15),
  (1622, 1622, '<h2>Transactions & ACID Properties</h2>
  <p>
    A transaction is a sequence of database operations that are executed as a single logical unit of work.
    Either all operations succeed, or none are applied.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Transactions ensure that database operations are reliable, consistent, and safe even when errors occur.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Example of a Transaction</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    Bank Transfer System:<br>
    Step 1: Deduct money from Account A<br>
    Step 2: Add money to Account B
  </div>

  <p>
    If any step fails, the entire transaction is canceled to prevent data errors.
  </p>

  <h3> 2. ACID Properties</h3>

  <h4> Atomicity</h4>
  <p>
    Ensures that all operations in a transaction succeed together or fail together.
  </p>

  <h4> Consistency</h4>
  <p>
    Ensures the database remains in a valid state before and after the transaction.
  </p>

  <h4> Isolation</h4>
  <p>
    Ensures multiple transactions do not interfere with each other when executed at the same time.
  </p>

  <h4> Durability</h4>
  <p>
    Once a transaction is committed, changes are permanent even after system failure.
  </p>

  <h3> 3. Transaction States</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Begin:</b> Transaction starts</li>
    <li><b>Commit:</b> Saves all changes permanently</li>
    <li><b>Rollback:</b> Cancels changes and restores previous state</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. SQL Transaction Example</h3>

  <pre>
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;
  </pre>
  <p>
    If an error occurs before COMMIT, the system can perform a ROLLBACK.
  </p>
  <h3> 5. Importance of Transactions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Prevents data corruption</li>
    <li>Ensures accurate financial systems</li>
    <li>Maintains data integrity</li>
    <li>Supports multi-user systems safely</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A transaction is like a full mission: either everything is completed successfully, or nothing is saved.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transactions & ACID Properties', 15),
  (1623, 1623, '<h2>NoSQL Databases (MongoDB Concept)</h2>
  <p>
    NoSQL databases are non-relational databases designed to store large volumes of unstructured or semi-structured data.
    They are commonly used in modern applications that require flexibility and high scalability.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    NoSQL databases are designed for speed, flexibility, and scalability rather than strict table structures.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. Why NoSQL?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Handles large-scale and big data systems</li>
    <li>Flexible schema (no fixed table structure)</li>
    <li>High performance for real-time applications</li>
    <li>Suitable for modern web and mobile apps</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 2. Types of NoSQL Databases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Document-based:</b> MongoDB (stores JSON-like documents)</li>
    <li><b>Key-value:</b> Redis (stores data as key-value pairs)</li>
    <li><b>Column-based:</b> Cassandra (stores data in columns)</li>
    <li><b>Graph-based:</b> Neo4j (stores relationships between data)</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 3. MongoDB Structure</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Database:</b> Top-level container</li>
    <li><b>Collection:</b> Group of documents (like a table)</li>
    <li><b>Document:</b> Individual record stored in JSON format</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> 4. Example MongoDB Document</h3>
  <pre>
{
  "name": "Alex",
  "age": 20,
  "course": "Computer Science"
}
  </pre>
  <h3> 5. SQL vs NoSQL Comparison</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>SQL</th>
      <th>NoSQL</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Structure</td>
      <td>Fixed tables</td>
      <td>Flexible documents</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Schema</td>
      <td>Rigid</td>
      <td>Dynamic</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Scalability</td>
      <td>Vertical scaling</td>
      <td>Horizontal scaling</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Examples</td>
      <td>MySQL, PostgreSQL</td>
      <td>MongoDB, Redis</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <h3> 6. Importance of NoSQL</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Supports big data applications</li>
    <li>Used in social media platforms</li>
    <li>Handles real-time analytics</li>
    <li>Improves performance in large systems</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    NoSQL databases are like flexible digital storage systems that can store different types of data without strict rules.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for NoSQL Databases (MongoDB Concept)', 15),
  (1624, 1624, '<h2>Backup & Recovery Strategies</h2>

<p><b>Backup</b> is creating copies of data, while <b>recovery</b> is restoring data after loss or failure.</p>
<h3>1. Why Backup is Important</h3>
<ul>
<li>Prevents data loss</li>
<li>Protects against system failure</li>
<li>Helps recover from cyber attacks</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Types of Backups</h3>

<h4>Full Backup</h4>
<p>Copies all data.</p>
<h4>Incremental Backup</h4>
<p>Copies only changes since last backup.</p>
<h4>Differential Backup</h4>
<p>Copies changes since last full backup.</p>
<h3>3. Recovery Methods</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Restore:</b> Recover from backup</li>
<li><b>Point-in-time recovery:</b> Restore to a specific time</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Backup Strategies</h3>
<ul>
<li>Regular backups (daily/weekly)</li>
<li>Store backups offsite/cloud</li>
<li>Use multiple backup copies</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. The 3-2-1 Rule</h3>
<ul>
<li>3 copies of data</li>
<li>2 different storage types</li>
<li>1 offsite copy</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Laptop crash → restore from cloud backup → data recovered
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Backup + Recovery = Data survival strategy.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Backup & Recovery Strategies', 15),
  (1625, 1625, '<h2>Why Networks are Used</h2>
  <p>
    Computer networks are used to connect devices so that they can share resources, communicate,
    and exchange data efficiently.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Networks allow computers to work together, making systems faster, cheaper, and more efficient.
  </p>
  <h3> Reasons for Using Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Resource Sharing:</b> Multiple users can share printers, software, and storage devices</li>
    <li><b>Data Sharing:</b> Files and databases can be accessed by many users</li>
    <li><b>Communication:</b> Enables email, video calls, and instant messaging</li>
    <li><b>Cost Efficiency:</b> Reduces cost by sharing hardware and software</li>
    <li><b>Backup & Reliability:</b> Central storage allows easy data backup and recovery</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of Networking</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improves teamwork and collaboration</li>
    <li>Increases efficiency in organizations</li>
    <li>Reduces duplication of resources</li>
    <li>Supports global communication systems</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Networks are like teamwork systems where many computers share tools and information instead of working alone.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Why Networks are Used', 15),
  (1626, 1626, '<h2>LAN, MAN, WAN, PAN Comparison</h2>
  <p>
    Computer networks are classified based on their geographical coverage and size.
    Each type serves different communication needs depending on distance and scale.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Networks are classified by how far devices are spread—from personal devices (PAN) to global networks (WAN).
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>PAN (Personal Area Network):</b> Very small network for personal devices like Bluetooth connections</li>
    <li><b>LAN (Local Area Network):</b> Covers a small area like a school, office, or home</li>
    <li><b>MAN (Metropolitan Area Network):</b> Covers a city or large town</li>
    <li><b>WAN (Wide Area Network):</b> Covers large geographical areas including countries and continents</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Comparison Summary</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Coverage</th>
      <th>Example</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>PAN</td>
      <td>Very small (personal)</td>
      <td>Bluetooth between phone and headset</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>LAN</td>
      <td>Small area</td>
      <td>School computer lab</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>MAN</td>
      <td>City/town</td>
      <td>City internet network</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>WAN</td>
      <td>Global</td>
      <td>Internet</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <h3> Importance of Network Types</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Helps organize communication systems</li>
    <li>Improves data sharing efficiency</li>
    <li>Determines network cost and design</li>
    <li>Supports different levels of connectivity</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    These networks are like circles of communication: PAN is your personal circle,
    LAN is your building, MAN is your city, and WAN is the whole world.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for LAN, MAN, WAN, PAN Comparison', 15),
  (1627, 1627, '<h2>Network Topologies</h2>
  <p>
    Network topology refers to the physical or logical arrangement of computers and devices in a network.
    It shows how devices are connected and how data flows between them.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Network topology determines how computers are arranged and how they communicate in a network.
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Network Topologies</h3>
  <h3>1. Bus Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>All devices are connected to a single backbone cable</li>
    <li>Data travels in both directions along the cable</li>
    <li>Easy to install but performance reduces with many devices</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>2. Star Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>All devices connect to a central device (hub or switch)</li>
    <li>Most commonly used in schools and offices</li>
    <li>If one device fails, others are not affected</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>3. Ring Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Devices are connected in a circular format</li>
    <li>Data travels in one direction or both directions</li>
    <li>A failure in one device can affect the entire network</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>4. Mesh Topology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Every device is connected to every other device</li>
    <li>Highly reliable and fault-tolerant</li>
    <li>Expensive and complex to install</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Comparison Summary</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Bus:</b> Simple but slow with many users</li>
    <li><b>Star:</b> Reliable and most commonly used</li>
    <li><b>Ring:</b> Moderate performance but less fault-tolerant</li>
    <li><b>Mesh:</b> Most reliable but expensive</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Advantages of Topologies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Help organize network structure</li>
    <li>Improve communication efficiency</li>
    <li>Allow easy troubleshooting depending on type</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Network topology is like the layout of a city showing how roads (connections) link buildings (computers).
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Network Topologies', 15),
  (1628, 1628, '<h2>Transmission Media</h2>
  <p>
    Transmission media refers to the physical or wireless pathways through which data is transmitted
    from one device to another in a network.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Transmission media are the communication channels that allow data to travel between devices either through cables or wireless signals.
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Transmission Media</h3>
  <h3>1. Guided (Wired) Media</h3>
  <p>Data is transmitted through physical cables.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Twisted Pair Cable:</b> Common in telephone and Ethernet networks</li>
    <li><b>Coaxial Cable:</b> Used in cable TV and broadband</li>
    <li><b>Fiber Optic Cable:</b> Fastest transmission, uses light signals</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>2. Unguided (Wireless) Media</h3>
  <p>Data is transmitted through air without cables.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Radio Waves:</b> Used in Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, mobile phones</li>
    <li><b>Microwaves:</b> Require line-of-sight communication</li>
    <li><b>Satellites:</b> Provide global communication coverage</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Comparison</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Wired:</b> More stable, faster, but less flexible</li>
    <li><b>Wireless:</b> Flexible and mobile, but may suffer interference</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Importance of Transmission Media</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Enables data communication between devices</li>
    <li>Supports internet connectivity</li>
    <li>Allows sharing of resources</li>
    <li>Enables global communication systems</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Transmission media are like roads or invisible air paths that allow data to travel between computers.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Transmission Media', 15),
  (1629, 1629, '<h2>IP Addressing</h2>
  <p>
    An IP (Internet Protocol) address is a unique numerical label assigned to each device connected to a network.
    It allows devices to identify and communicate with each other.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    An IP address is like a digital home address that allows data to reach the correct device on a network.
  </p>

  <h3> Structure of IPv4</h3>
  <p>
    IPv4 is a 32-bit address divided into four parts called octets. Each octet is separated by a dot.
  </p>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: 192.168.1.1
  </div>

  <ul>
    <li>Each part is called an <b>octet</b></li>
    <li>Each octet ranges from 0 to 255</li>
    <li>Total = 4 octets = 32 bits</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Binary Representation</h3>
  <p>
    Each octet is made up of 8 binary digits (bits).
  </p>

  <pre>
192 → 11000000
168 → 10101000
1   → 00000001
1   → 00000001
  </pre>

  <h3> Types of IP Addresses</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Private IP:</b> Used inside local networks (e.g., home Wi-Fi, school lab)</li>
    <li><b>Public IP:</b> Used on the internet and assigned by ISP</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Static vs Dynamic IP</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Static IP:</b> Does not change over time</li>
    <li><b>Dynamic IP:</b> Changes automatically using DHCP</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of IP Addressing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Identifies devices on a network</li>
    <li>Ensures correct data delivery</li>
    <li>Enables internet communication</li>
    <li>Supports network organization</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    An IP address is like a house number for a device so that data knows exactly where to go.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for IP Addressing', 15),
  (1630, 1630, '<h2>DNS (Domain Name System)</h2>
  <p>
    DNS (Domain Name System) is a system that translates human-readable domain names
    (like google.com) into machine-readable IP addresses that computers use to identify each other on a network.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    DNS acts like the internet’s phonebook by converting easy-to-remember names into IP addresses that computers understand.
  </p>

  <h3> Why DNS is Needed</h3>
  <p>
    Computers communicate using numbers (IP addresses), but humans prefer names.
    DNS bridges this gap.
  </p>

  <div class="example-box">
    google.com → 142.250.190.78<br>
    facebook.com → 157.240.229.35
  </div>

  <h3> How DNS Works (Step-by-Step)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>User types a website name in the browser</li>
    <li>The request is sent to a DNS server</li>
    <li>The DNS server looks up the matching IP address</li>
    <li>The IP address is sent back to the browser</li>
    <li>The browser connects to the website server</li>
  </ol>

  <h3> Key Components</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Domain Name:</b> Human-friendly website name (e.g., google.com)</li>
    <li><b>IP Address:</b> Numerical address used by computers</li>
    <li><b>DNS Server:</b> Stores and manages domain-to-IP mappings</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of DNS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Makes internet easier to use</li>
    <li>Eliminates need to memorize IP addresses</li>
    <li>Speeds up website access</li>
    <li>Supports global internet communication</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    DNS is like a contact list in your phone: you type a name, and it finds the number automatically.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for DNS (Domain Name System)', 15),
  (1631, 1631, '<h2>Client-Server Model</h2>
  <p>
    The client-server model is a network structure where computers are divided into two roles:
    clients that request services and servers that provide those services.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    In a client-server network, one central system (server) provides resources while other computers (clients)
    request and use those resources.
  </p>

  <h3> Components</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Client:</b> A device or program that requests services (e.g., web browser)</li>
    <li><b>Server:</b> A powerful computer that provides services, data, or resources</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> How It Works</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>The client sends a request (e.g., opening a website)</li>
    <li>The server receives and processes the request</li>
    <li>The server sends back the required response/data</li>
  </ol>

  <h3> Example</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    • A web browser (client) requests a webpage<br>
    • The web server processes the request<br>
    • The server sends the webpage back to the browser
  </div>

  <h3> Advantages of Client-Server Model</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Centralized control of data and resources</li>
    <li>Improved security and data protection</li>
    <li>Easier backup and maintenance</li>
    <li>Efficient resource sharing</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Disadvantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Expensive to set up and maintain</li>
    <li>Depends heavily on the server (if it fails, network stops)</li>
    <li>Requires skilled administration</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    The client-server model is like ordering food in a restaurant:
    you (client) request, and the kitchen (server) prepares and serves your order.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Client-Server Model', 15),
  (1632, 1632, '<h2>Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Networks</h2>
  <p>
    A peer-to-peer (P2P) network is a type of network where all computers are equal.
    Each computer (node) can act as both a client (requesting data) and a server (providing data).
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    In a peer-to-peer network, there is no central server; all computers share resources directly with each other.
  </p>

  <h3> Characteristics of P2P Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>No central server controlling the network</li>
    <li>All computers have equal status</li>
    <li>Each device can share files and resources</li>
    <li>Simple to install and manage</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Example of P2P Network</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    • Sharing files directly between two computers using a USB network connection or LAN without a server
  </div>

  <h3> Advantages of P2P Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Easy to set up</li>
    <li>Low installation cost</li>
    <li>No need for a dedicated server</li>
    <li>Useful for small networks</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Disadvantages of P2P Networks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Less secure compared to client-server networks</li>
    <li>Difficult to manage in large networks</li>
    <li>No central backup system</li>
    <li>Performance may slow down with many users</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Comparison: P2P vs Client-Server</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>P2P Network</th>
      <th>Client-Server Network</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Control</td>
      <td>Decentralized</td>
      <td>Centralized</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Security</td>
      <td>Lower</td>
      <td>Higher</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Cost</td>
      <td>Low</td>
      <td>High</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Setup</td>
      <td>Simple</td>
      <td>Complex</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    A peer-to-peer network is like a group of friends sharing things directly with each other
    without a boss controlling everything.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Peer-to-Peer Networks', 15),
  (1633, 1633, '<h2>Network Troubleshooting Basics</h2>
  <p>
  Troubleshooting is the systematic process of identifying, diagnosing, and fixing problems in a computer network
  to ensure it operates efficiently and reliably.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Network troubleshooting helps users detect, isolate, and resolve issues so that communication,
  devices, and internet services continue to function properly.
</p>
<h3> Common Network Problems</h3>
<ul>
  <li>No internet connection</li>
  <li>Slow network performance</li>
  <li>Device failing to connect to Wi-Fi or LAN</li>
  <li>Incorrect IP configuration</li>
  <li>DNS errors (website not loading)</li>
  <li>Intermittent connection drops</li>
</ul>
<h3> Basic Troubleshooting Steps</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Check physical connections (cables, routers, switches)</li>
  <li>Restart network devices (router, modem, computer)</li>
  <li>Verify network settings and IP configuration</li>
  <li>Test connectivity using tools like ping</li>
  <li>Check firewall or antivirus settings</li>
  <li>Reconnect to the network or Wi-Fi</li>
  <li>Update or reinstall network drivers</li>
</ol>
<h3> Troubleshooting Tools</h3>
<pre>
 Ping        → Tests if a device is reachable on a network
 Traceroute  → Shows the path data takes to reach a destination
 IPConfig     → Displays IP address and network configuration
 DNS Lookup   → Checks domain name resolution
</pre>
<h3> Advanced Troubleshooting Techniques</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Checking bandwidth usage to detect congestion</li>
  <li>Using network analyzers to monitor traffic</li>
  <li>Testing with different devices to isolate faults</li>
  <li>Changing DNS servers to fix browsing issues</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Troubleshooting</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Restores network connectivity quickly</li>
  <li>Reduces downtime in communication and work</li>
  <li>Helps identify faulty hardware or software</li>
  <li>Improves overall network performance</li>
  <li>Saves time and repair costs</li>
</ul>
<h3> Real-Life Example</h3>
<p>
  When a school computer lab loses internet access, technicians may restart the router, check cables,
  and use the ping command to identify where the connection is failing.
</p>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Troubleshooting is like being a network doctor who diagnoses and fixes problems when computers,
  networks, or internet connections stop working properly.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Network Troubleshooting Basics', 15),
  (1634, 1634, '<h2>Data Communication Concepts</h2>
  <p>
  Data communication refers to the exchange of digital data between two or more devices
  through a transmission medium such as cables, fiber optics, or wireless signals.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Data communication is the process of sending and receiving digital information between devices
  using agreed rules (protocols) and communication technologies.
</p>
<h3> Key Components of Data Communication</h3>
<pre>
 Sender          → Device that sends data
 Receiver        → Device that receives data
 Message         → The data being transmitted (text, images, video, etc.)
 Transmission Medium → Path used to carry data (cables or wireless signals)
 Protocol        → Rules that control data communication
</pre>
<h3> Key Concepts</h3>
<pre>
 Bandwidth   → Amount of data transmitted per second (speed of connection)
 Protocols   → Rules that ensure proper communication between devices (e.g., TCP/IP, HTTP)
 Modem       → Converts digital signals for transmission over communication lines
 Router      → Directs data between different networks
</pre>
<h3> Explanation of Concepts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Bandwidth:</b> Determines how much data can be transferred in a given time (higher bandwidth = faster communication)</li>
  <li><b>Protocols:</b> Set rules that ensure devices understand each other during communication</li>
  <li><b>Modem:</b> Converts digital signals to analog and back to enable internet connection</li>
  <li><b>Router:</b> Directs data packets between networks such as home and the internet</li>
</ul>
<h3> Modes of Data Communication</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Simplex:</b> Data flows in one direction only (e.g., TV broadcast)</li>
  <li><b>Half Duplex:</b> Data flows in both directions, but one at a time (e.g., walkie-talkie)</li>
  <li><b>Full Duplex:</b> Data flows in both directions simultaneously (e.g., phone call)</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Data Communication</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Enables internet communication and connectivity</li>
  <li>Allows sharing of files and information globally</li>
  <li>Supports online learning, banking, and business operations</li>
  <li>Improves speed and efficiency of communication</li>
</ul>
<h3> Real-Life Example</h3>
<p>
  When you send a WhatsApp message, your phone acts as the sender, the internet is the transmission medium,
  and the recipient’s phone is the receiver. Protocols ensure the message is delivered correctly.
</p>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Data communication is like a digital conversation between devices where messages are sent,
  received, and understood using rules (protocols) and speed (bandwidth).
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data Communication Concepts', 15),
  (1635, 1635, '<h2>Network Security</h2>
  <p>
  Network security refers to the protection of computer networks, systems, and data
  from unauthorized access, damage, theft, or cyber attacks such as hacking, malware, sniffing, and spoofing.
</p>

<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Network security ensures that only authorized users can access systems and data,
  while preventing, detecting, and responding to cyber threats.
</p>
<h3> Types of Network Security Threats</h3>
<pre>
 Malware       → Harmful software (viruses, worms, ransomware)
 Phishing      → Fake messages that trick users into revealing information
‍ Hacking       → Unauthorized access to systems or data
 Eavesdropping → Interception of network data during transmission
 Sniffing      → Capturing and monitoring network data packets secretly
 Spoofing      → Pretending to be a trusted device or user to gain access
 Denial of Service (DoS) → Overloading a system to make it unavailable
</pre>
<h3> Methods of Network Security</h3>
<pre>
 Firewall        → Blocks unauthorized access to a network
 Encryption      → Converts data into unreadable code
 Authentication  → Verifies user identity (passwords, biometrics, 2FA)
 Antivirus       → Detects and removes malicious software
 Access Control  → Restricts user permissions and privileges
 Monitoring      → Tracks network activity for suspicious behavior
</pre>
<h3> Explanation of Key Methods</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Firewall:</b> Filters incoming and outgoing network traffic to block threats</li>
  <li><b>Encryption:</b> Converts data into a secret code that can only be read with a key</li>
  <li><b>Authentication:</b> Confirms user identity before granting access</li>
  <li><b>Antivirus:</b> Scans and removes malicious programs from a computer</li>
  <li><b>Access Control:</b> Limits what users can access in a network system</li>
</ul>
<h3> Sniffing vs Spoofing</h3>
<ul>  <li><b>Sniffing:</b> Secretly capturing and analyzing data traveling across a network without permission</li>
  <li><b>Spoofing:</b> Pretending to be a trusted device, user, or website to trick systems into granting access</li>
</ul>
<h3> Importance of Network Security</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Protects sensitive and confidential information</li>
  <li>Prevents hacking, sniffing, and spoofing attacks</li>
  <li>Ensures privacy of users and organizations</li>
  <li>Maintains system reliability and trust</li>
  <li>Protects financial and personal data</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Network security is like a smart security system that uses locks, guards, and cameras
  to stop intruders, detect spies, and prevent people from pretending to be someone else.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Network Security', 15),
  (1636, 1636, '<h2>Networking Hardware</h2>
  <p>
  Networking hardware refers to physical devices used to connect computers together
  so that they can communicate and share resources such as files, printers, and internet access.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Networking hardware acts as the communication bridge between computers,
  controlling how data is sent, received, and directed across a network efficiently and securely.
</p>
<h3> Types of Networking Hardware</h3>
<pre>
 NIC (Network Interface Card) → Connects a computer to a network
 Hub                         → Sends data to all connected devices
 Switch                      → Sends data only to the correct device
 Router                      → Connects different networks and provides internet access
 Bridge                      → Connects two similar networks and filters traffic
 Brouter                     → Combines the functions of a bridge and a router
 Access Point               → Allows wireless devices to connect to a network
 Modem                       → Converts signals for internet communication
</pre>
<h3> Functions of Networking Devices</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>NIC:</b> Allows a computer to connect to a network and communicate with other devices</li>
  <li><b>Hub:</b> Broadcasts data to all connected devices (simple but inefficient)</li>
  <li><b>Switch:</b> Sends data only to the intended device using MAC addresses (efficient)</li>
  <li><b>Router:</b> Connects different networks and directs data between them (e.g., LAN to internet)</li>
  <li><b>Bridge:</b> Connects two separate but similar networks and reduces traffic by filtering data</li>
  <li><b>Brouter:</b> Combines the functions of a bridge and a router; routes some data and bridges others</li>
  <li><b>Modem:</b> Converts digital signals to allow internet communication</li>
  <li><b>Access Point:</b> Provides wireless connectivity for Wi-Fi devices</li>
</ul>
<h3> Key Differences</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Hub:</b> Sends data to all devices (no filtering)</li>
  <li><b>Switch:</b> Sends data only to the correct device (uses MAC address)</li>
  <li><b>Bridge:</b> Connects two LANs and filters traffic between them</li>
  <li><b>Router:</b> Connects different networks and routes data using IP addresses</li>
  <li><b>Brouter:</b> Works as both a bridge and a router depending on the data type</li>
</ul>
<h3> Advantages of Networking Hardware</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Enables sharing of files and resources</li>
  <li>Allows multiple devices to access the internet</li>
  <li>Improves communication between computers</li>
  <li>Reduces cost through shared hardware like printers</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Networking hardware is like a system of roads and traffic controllers that helps computers
  send, receive, and manage information across different networks efficiently.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Networking Hardware', 15),
  (1637, 1637, '<h2>Word Processing </h2>
 <p>
  Word processing refers to the use of computer software to create, edit, format, store,
  and print text-based documents such as letters, reports, CVs, essays, and official documents.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Word processing software allows users to produce well-structured and professional documents quickly,
  with the ability to edit, correct, and improve content easily without rewriting everything.
</p>
<h3> Examples of Word Processing Software</h3>
<pre>
 Microsoft Word        → Most widely used word processor
 Google Docs          → Online collaborative document editor
 LibreOffice Writer   → Free offline word processing software
 WPS Writer           → Lightweight and easy-to-use word processor
</pre>
<h3> Key Features of Word Processing</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Typing, editing, and deleting text</li>
  <li>Text formatting (bold, italics, underline, font size, color)</li>
  <li>Page layout control (margins, orientation, spacing)</li>
  <li>Spell check and grammar correction tools</li>
  <li>Insertion of images, tables, shapes, and charts</li>
  <li>Find and replace text</li>
  <li>Saving and exporting files (DOCX, PDF, TXT)</li>
  <li>Printing documents professionally</li>
</ul>
<h3> Keyboard Shortcuts in Word Processing</h3>
<pre>
Ctrl + C   → Copy selected text
Ctrl + V   → Paste copied text
Ctrl + X   → Cut selected text
Ctrl + Z   → Undo last action
Ctrl + Y   → Redo last action
Ctrl + B   → Make text Bold
Ctrl + I   → Make text Italic
Ctrl + U   → Underline text
Ctrl + A   → Select all text in the document
Ctrl + S   → Save the document
Ctrl + P   → Print document
Ctrl + F   → Find a word or phrase in the document
Ctrl + H   → Find and replace text
Ctrl + N   → Create a new document
Ctrl + O   → Open an existing document
Ctrl + L   → Align text to the left
Ctrl + E   → Center align text
Ctrl + R   → Align text to the right
Ctrl + J   → Justify text (align both sides)
</pre>
<h3> Advanced Features</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Track changes for editing review</li>
  <li>Comments for collaboration</li>
  <li>Templates for CVs, letters, and reports</li>
  <li>Auto-save and cloud storage integration</li>
  <li>Mail merge for sending bulk personalized documents</li>
</ul>

<h3> Advantages of Word Processing</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Saves time compared to handwriting</li>
  <li>Allows easy editing without rewriting the whole document</li>
  <li>Produces neat, professional, and readable documents</li>
  <li>Enables easy sharing via email or cloud storage</li>
  <li>Reduces paper usage through digital storage</li>
</ul>

<h3> Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Requires electricity and a computer/device</li>
  <li>Can be affected by software crashes or data loss</li>
  <li>Requires basic computer skills to use effectively</li>
  <li>May lead to over-reliance on auto-correction tools</li>
</ul>

<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Word processing is like a powerful digital typewriter that allows you to write, edit, format,
  and improve documents easily and professionally using a computer.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Word Processing', 15),
  (1638, 1638, '<h2>Spreadsheets</h2>
  <p>
  Spreadsheets are application programs used to organize, store, and analyze data
  in rows and columns. They also allow automatic calculations using formulas and functions.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  A spreadsheet is like a digital worksheet where data is arranged in rows and columns,
  and calculations are performed automatically using formulas.
</p>
<h3> Examples of Spreadsheet Software</h3>
<pre>
 Microsoft Excel   → Most popular spreadsheet program
 Google Sheets     → Online spreadsheet application
 LibreOffice Calc  → Free spreadsheet software
 Apple Numbers     → Spreadsheet for Apple devices
</pre>
<h3> Structure of a Spreadsheet</h3>

<div style="background: linear-gradient(145deg, #131326, #1c1c36); border: 1px solid #323254; border-radius: 14px; padding: 16px; margin: 20px 0; box-shadow: 0 8px 25px rgba(0,0,0,0.45); text-align: center;">
  <img src="/images/spreadsheet_screenshot.png" alt="Real World Spreadsheet Interface Screenshot" style="width: 100%; max-width: 680px; height: auto; border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid #323560; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.6); display: block; margin: 0 auto 10px auto; object-fit: contain;" />
  <p style="font-size:0.85em; color:#a0a0b0; text-align:center; margin-top:10px; margin-bottom:0;"><b>Figure:</b> Real World Spreadsheet Interface Screenshot (showing Formula Bar, Rows, Columns, and Data Cells)</p>
</div>

<ul>
  <li><b>Rows:</b> Horizontal lines identified by numbers (1, 2, 3...)</li>
  <li><b>Columns:</b> Vertical lines identified by letters (A, B, C...)</li>
  <li><b>Cells:</b> Intersection of a row and column (e.g. A1, B2)</li>
</ul>
<h3> Explanation of Spreadsheet Parts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Rows:</b> Horizontal sections identified by numbers</li>
  <li><b>Columns:</b> Vertical sections identified by letters</li>
  <li><b>Cells:</b> Boxes where data is entered</li>
  <li><b>Cell Address:</b> Name of a cell formed using column letter and row number (e.g., B3)</li>
  <li><b>Formula Bar:</b> Area used to enter or edit formulas</li>
  <li><b>Worksheet:</b> One page inside a spreadsheet file</li>
</ul>
<h3> Data Types in Spreadsheets</h3>
<pre>
Text     → Names or words
Numbers  → Values used in calculations
Dates    → Calendar dates
Formulas → Instructions for calculations
</pre>
<h3> Key Features</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Organizing data into tables</li>
  <li>Automatic calculations using formulas</li>
  <li>Sorting data alphabetically or numerically</li>
  <li>Filtering important information</li>
  <li>Creating charts and graphs</li>
  <li>Data analysis and reporting tools</li>
</ul>
<h3> Important Formulas</h3>
<pre>
=SUM(A1:A10)         → Adds values in a range
=AVERAGE(A1:A10)     → Calculates the mean
=MAX(A1:A10)         → Finds highest value
=MIN(A1:A10)         → Finds lowest value
=COUNT(A1:A10)       → Counts numeric entries
=COUNTA(A1:A10)      → Counts all non-empty cells
=COUNTBLANK(A1:A10)  → Counts empty cells
=IF(A1>50,"Pass","Fail") → Logical decision test
=IFS(A1>80,"A",A1>60,"B",A1>40,"C") → Multiple conditions
=SUMIF(A1:A10,">50") → Adds values greater than 50
=COUNTIF(A1:A10,">50") → Counts values greater than 50
=AVERAGEIF(A1:A10,">50") → Average of values above condition
</pre>
<h3> Formula Example</h3>

<div style="background:#131326; border:1px solid #2a2a48; border-radius:12px; padding:16px; margin:20px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);">
  <div style="display:flex; align-items:center; background:#1c1c36; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:12px; border:1px solid #323254; font-family:monospace; font-size:0.9em; color:#00e5ff;">
    <span style="color:#ff007f; font-weight:bold; margin-right:12px;">fx</span>
    <span style="color:#00ffcc; font-weight:bold;">=SUM(B1:B3) &nbsp;➔ &nbsp;Result: 240</span>
  </div>
  <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; text-align:center; font-family:''Segoe UI'', Roboto, sans-serif; font-size:0.9em; color:#e0e0e0;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="background:#22223e; color:#00e5ff;">
          <th style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; width:40px; background:#16162d; color:#888;">#</th>
          <th style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; min-width:100px;">A</th>
          <th style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; min-width:100px;">B</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">1</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px;">Math</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">80</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">2</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px;">English</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">70</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">3</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px;">Science</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">90</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="background:rgba(0, 229, 255, 0.1);">
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; background:#16162d; color:#888; font-weight:bold;">4</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; font-weight:bold; color:#00e5ff;">Total (=SUM)</td>
          <td style="border:1px solid #323254; padding:8px; font-weight:bold; color:#00ffcc; font-size:1.05em; border:2px solid #00ffcc;">240</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="font-size:0.85em; color:#a0a0b0; text-align:center; margin-top:10px; margin-bottom:0;"><b>Figure:</b> Spreadsheet SUM Formula Result Grid</p>
</div>
<h3> Uses of Spreadsheets</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Keeping school marks and records</li>
  <li>Business accounting and budgeting</li>
  <li>Creating timetables and schedules</li>
  <li>Data analysis and reporting</li>
  <li>Inventory and stock management</li>
</ul>
<h3> Advantages</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Saves time by automating calculations</li>
  <li>Reduces human errors in mathematics</li>
  <li>Makes data analysis easier</li>
  <li>Helps in decision making using charts</li>
  <li>Stores large amounts of information neatly</li>
</ul>
<h3> Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect formulas can produce wrong results</li>
  <li>Large spreadsheets may become difficult to manage</li>
  <li>Requires computer knowledge to use advanced features</li>
  <li>Data can be lost if not saved properly</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  A spreadsheet is like a smart digital table that stores information
  and performs calculations automatically when data is entered.
</p>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Spreadsheets', 15),
  (1639, 1639, '<h2>Presentations</h2>
  <p>
    Presentation software is used to display information in an organized way using slides.
    It helps users communicate ideas visually using text, images, audio, and video.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Presentations are used to communicate information clearly to an audience using visual slides
    instead of long written documents.
  </p>
  <h3> Examples of Presentation Software</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    1. Microsoft PowerPoint – most popular slide software<br>
    2. Google Slides – online presentation tool<br>
    3. LibreOffice Impress – free presentation software<br>
    4. Canva – design-based presentation tool
  </div>
  <h3> Key Features of Presentation Software</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Creation of multiple slides</li>
    <li>Insertion of text, images, audio, and video</li>
    <li>Slide transitions and animations</li>
    <li>Pre-designed templates and themes</li>
    <li>Presenter notes and slideshow mode</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Makes information easier to understand</li>
    <li>Improves audience engagement</li>
    <li>Helps organize ideas clearly</li>
    <li>Supports visual learning</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Presentation software is like a digital flipbook where each page (slide) shows a piece of information
    to help explain a topic clearly.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Presentations', 15),
  (1640, 1640, '<h2>Email & Communication</h2>
  <p>
  Email is a method of sending digital messages over the internet.
  It is one of the most common forms of electronic communication used in education,
  business, and personal life.
</p>
<h3> Key Idea</h3>
<p>
  Email allows users to send and receive messages instantly over the internet,
  including text, images, documents, videos, and links.
</p>
<h3> Examples of Email Services</h3>
<pre>
 Gmail       → Google email service
 Outlook     → Microsoft email service
 Yahoo Mail  → Yahoo communication service
 ProtonMail  → Secure encrypted email service
</pre>
<h3> Features of Email</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Send and receive messages instantly</li>
  <li>Attach files such as documents, images, and videos</li>
  <li>Send emails to multiple recipients using CC and BCC</li>
  <li>Spam and junk mail filtering</li>
  <li>Organizing emails into folders and labels</li>
  <li>Access emails from phones, tablets, and computers</li>
</ul>
<h3> Structure of an Email Address</h3>

<div style="background:#131326; border:1px solid #2a2a48; border-radius:12px; padding:18px; margin:20px 0; text-align:center; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);">
  <div style="display:inline-block; background:#1c1c36; border:1px solid #00e5ff; padding:12px 24px; border-radius:30px; font-family:monospace; font-size:1.2em; letter-spacing:1px; margin-bottom:15px;">
    <span style="color:#ff007f; font-weight:bold;">student</span><span style="color:#ffdd57; font-weight:bold;">@</span><span style="color:#00e5ff; font-weight:bold;">gmail</span><span style="color:#00ffcc; font-weight:bold;">.com</span>
  </div>
  <div style="display:flex; justify-content:center; gap:15px; flex-wrap:wrap; font-size:0.85em; margin-top:10px;">
    <div style="background:#241933; border:1px solid #ff007f; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; color:#ff80bf;"><b>Username:</b> student</div>
    <div style="background:#2d291e; border:1px solid #ffdd57; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; color:#ffeb80;"><b>Separator:</b> @</div>
    <div style="background:#1a2838; border:1px solid #00e5ff; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; color:#80f0ff;"><b>Domain Name:</b> gmail</div>
    <div style="background:#19332b; border:1px solid #00ffcc; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; color:#80ffdf;"><b>Extension:</b> .com</div>
  </div>
</div>
<h3> Explanation of Parts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Username:</b> The unique name of the email owner</li>
  <li><b>@ Symbol:</b> Separates the username from the mail service provider</li>
  <li><b>Domain Name:</b> The email service provider such as Gmail or Yahoo</li>
  <li><b>Domain Extension:</b> The ending of the domain such as .com, .org, or .edu</li>
</ul>
<h3> Common Domain Extensions</h3>
<pre>
.com  → Commercial organizations
.org  → Organizations
.edu  → Educational institutions
.gov  → Government institutions
.net  → Network services
</pre>
<h3> Structure of an Email</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>To:</b> Recipient''s email address</li>
  <li><b>CC:</b> Sends a copy to another recipient</li>
  <li><b>BCC:</b> Sends a hidden copy to another recipient</li>
  <li><b>Subject:</b> The topic of the message</li>
  <li><b>Body:</b> The main message content</li>
  <li><b>Attachments:</b> Files added to the email</li>
</ul>
<h3> Advantages of Email</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Fast communication across the world</li>
  <li>Low cost compared to traditional mail</li>
  <li>Allows sending files easily</li>
  <li>Can be accessed anytime and anywhere</li>
  <li>Easy storage and searching of messages</li>
</ul>
<h3> Disadvantages of Email</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Spam or unwanted emails may occur</li>
  <li>Requires internet access</li>
  <li>Viruses can be spread through attachments</li>
  <li>Emails can be ignored or misunderstood</li>
</ul>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Email is like a digital letter sent through the internet instead of paper and post offices.
  It allows people to communicate quickly from anywhere in the world.
</p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Email & Communication', 15),
  (1641, 1641, '<h2>Collaboration Tools</h2>
  <p>
    Collaboration tools are digital software applications that allow two or more people to work together
    on tasks, projects, or communication in real time or asynchronously (not at the same time).
    They are widely used in schools, businesses, and remote teams.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    The main purpose of collaboration tools is to improve teamwork by allowing users to share files,
    communicate instantly, edit documents together, and manage tasks efficiently.
  </p>
  <h3> Examples of Collaboration Tools</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    1. Google Docs – real-time document editing<br>
    2. Google Drive – cloud file storage and sharing<br>
    3. Zoom – video conferencing and meetings<br>
    4. Microsoft Teams – chat, meetings, and teamwork platform<br>
    5. Slack – team messaging and communication
  </div>
  <h3> Features of Collaboration Tools</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Real-time editing of documents</li>
    <li>Video and audio conferencing</li>
    <li>File sharing and cloud storage</li>
    <li>Instant messaging and group chats</li>
    <li>Task assignment and project tracking</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improves teamwork and communication</li>
    <li>Saves time and increases productivity</li>
    <li>Allows remote work from anywhere</li>
    <li>Reduces need for physical meetings</li>
  </ul>
  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Imagine a group of students writing one essay together at the same time even if they are in different homes.
    That is what collaboration tools make possible.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Collaboration Tools (Full Course)', 15),
  (1642, 1642, '<h2>Software Development Methodologies</h2>



<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Software development methodologies are structured approaches used to plan, design, develop, test, and maintain software systems.
They guide how teams organize work, manage time, and deliver reliable software.
</p>

---

<h3> 1. WATERFALL MODEL</h3>
<ul>
<li>Linear and sequential approach</li>
<li>Each phase must be completed before moving to the next</li>
<li>Clear structure and documentation</li>
</ul>

<h4> FLOW</h4>
<pre>
Requirements → Design → Implementation → Testing → Deployment → Maintenance
</pre>

<h4> ADVANTAGES</h4>
<ul>
<li>Easy to understand and manage</li>
<li>Clear stages and documentation</li>
</ul>

<h4> LIMITATIONS</h4>
<ul>
<li>Not flexible to changes</li>
<li>Errors discovered late</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> 2. AGILE MODEL</h3>
<ul>
<li>Iterative and flexible approach</li>
<li>Work divided into small cycles called <b>sprints</b></li>
<li>Continuous feedback and improvement</li>
</ul>

<h4> FLOW</h4>
<pre>
Plan → Develop → Test → Review → Improve (repeat)
</pre>

<h4> ADVANTAGES</h4>
<ul>
<li>Highly flexible</li>
<li>Early detection of issues</li>
<li>Customer involvement</li>
</ul>

<h4> LIMITATIONS</h4>
<ul>
<li>Less predictable timeline</li>
<li>Requires strong teamwork</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> 3. SPIRAL MODEL</h3>
<ul>
<li>Combines iterative development with risk analysis</li>
<li>Focuses on identifying and reducing risks early</li>
<li>Development occurs in repeated cycles (spirals)</li>
</ul>

<h4> FLOW</h4>
<pre>
Plan → Risk Analysis → Develop → Test → Repeat
</pre>

<h4> ADVANTAGES</h4>
<ul>
<li>Good for large, complex projects</li>
<li>Early risk detection</li>
</ul>

<h4> LIMITATIONS</h4>
<ul>
<li>Complex and expensive</li>
<li>Requires expertise</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> COMPARISON</h3>
<pre>
Waterfall        | Agile           | Spiral
----------------------------------------------
Linear           | Iterative       | Iterative + Risk
Rigid            | Flexible        | Flexible
Late testing     | Continuous      | Continuous
Low risk focus   | Moderate        | High risk focus
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD USE CASES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Waterfall → Government systems (fixed requirements)</li>
<li>Agile → Mobile apps, startups</li>
<li>Spiral → Large banking or safety-critical systems</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Waterfall = Step-by-step <br>
Agile = Flexible cycles <br>
Spiral = Risk-focused cycles
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Agile is most used today because it adapts easily to changing requirements.
</div>

---

<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Differences between Agile, Waterfall, and Spiral</li>
<li>Advantages and disadvantages of each</li>
<li>When to use each model</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Software Development Methodologies', 15),
  (1643, 1643, '<h2>Version Control Systems (Git & GitHub)</h2>



<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Version control is a system that tracks and manages changes to files (especially source code) over time.
It allows developers to collaborate, maintain history, and revert to previous versions when errors occur.
</p>

<p>
Without version control, managing large projects and teamwork becomes difficult and error-prone.
</p>

---

<h3> TYPES OF VERSION CONTROL</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Local VCS:</b> Tracks changes on a single computer</li>
<li><b>Centralized VCS:</b> Uses a central server (e.g., SVN)</li>
<li><b>Distributed VCS:</b> Every user has a full copy (e.g., Git)</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> GIT</h3>
<ul>
<li>Distributed version control system</li>
<li>Tracks file changes locally</li>
<li>Supports branching and merging</li>
<li>Works offline and online</li>
</ul>

<h4> BASIC GIT FLOW</h4>
<pre>
Write Code → Stage (git add) → Commit (git commit) → Repository
</pre>

---

<h3> GITHUB</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cloud-based hosting service for Git repositories</li>
<li>Enables collaboration among developers</li>
<li>Stores and shares code online</li>
<li>Supports features like pull requests and issue tracking</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> KEY TERMS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Repository (Repo):</b> Project storage containing code and history</li>
<li><b>Commit:</b> Snapshot of changes saved in the repository</li>
<li><b>Branch:</b> Separate line of development</li>
<li><b>Merge:</b> Combining changes from different branches</li>
<li><b>Clone:</b> Copying a repository to a local machine</li>
<li><b>Pull:</b> Fetching updates from remote repository</li>
<li><b>Push:</b> Sending changes to remote repository</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Developer A works on login feature (branch A)</li>
<li>Developer B works on dashboard (branch B)</li>
<li>Both changes are merged into main project</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> COMMON EXAM CONFUSION</h3>
<ul>
<li>Git = tool/software</li>
<li>GitHub = platform/service</li>
<li>Commit ≠ Save (it’s a tracked snapshot)</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Git = Tracks changes locally <br>
GitHub = Stores and shares code online
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Version control allows safe collaboration without overwriting each other''s work.
</div>

---

<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Difference between Git and GitHub</li>
<li>Meaning of key terms (commit, branch, repo)</li>
<li>Basic Git workflow</li>
<li>Advantages of version control</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Version Control Systems', 15),
  (1644, 1644, '<h2>Software Testing Types</h2>

  


<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Software testing is the process of evaluating a software system to ensure it works correctly, meets requirements, and is free from defects (bugs).
</p>

<p>
Testing is done in stages, starting from small components to the entire system, ensuring reliability at every level.
</p>

---

<h3> TYPES OF TESTING</h3>

<ul>
<li><b>1. Unit Testing</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Tests individual components or functions</li>
<li>Performed by developers</li>
<li>Focus: smallest parts of the system</li>
</ul>

<li><b>2. Integration Testing</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Tests interaction between combined components</li>
<li>Checks data flow between modules</li>
</ul>

<li><b>3. System Testing</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Tests the complete and integrated system</li>
<li>Ensures system meets specified requirements</li>
</ul>

<li><b>4. User Acceptance Testing (UAT)</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Performed by end users or clients</li>
<li>Verifies system meets real-world needs</li>
<li>Final approval before release</li>
</ul>
</ul>

---

<h3> TESTING FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Unit → Integration → System → UAT
</pre>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Check login function → Unit testing</li>
<li>Login + database connection → Integration testing</li>
<li>Full application test → System testing</li>
<li>User tries app → UAT</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> COMMON EXAM TRAPS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Unit = smallest part (NOT full system)</li>
<li>UAT = done by users (NOT developers)</li>
<li>Integration = connection between modules</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Unit = Individual parts <br>
Integration = Parts working together <br>
System = Whole system <br>
UAT = User approval
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Bugs found early (unit stage) are cheaper to fix than bugs found later (system/UAT).
</div>

---

<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Order of testing stages</li>
<li>Purpose of each testing type</li>
<li>Who performs each test</li>
<li>Differences between stages</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Software Testing Types', 15),
  (1645, 1645, '<h2>Documentation and Software Maintenance</h2>

  <h3>

<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Documentation and maintenance are critical phases of the software development lifecycle (SDLC) that occur after or alongside development.
They ensure that software remains understandable, usable, scalable, and functional over time.
</p>

<p>
Without proper documentation and maintenance, software becomes difficult to update, fix, or use effectively.
</p>

---

<h3> DOCUMENTATION</h3>
<p>
Documentation is the written or visual description of how a software system works and how it should be used or maintained.
</p>

<h4> TYPES OF DOCUMENTATION</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>User Documentation:</b> Guides end-users on how to operate the software (manuals, help guides)</li>
<li><b>Technical Documentation:</b> Describes system design, code structure, and architecture for developers</li>
<li><b>System Documentation:</b> Overall system overview including hardware, software, and processes</li>
</ul>

<h4> IMPORTANCE</h4>
<ul>
<li>Helps users understand how to use the system</li>
<li>Assists developers in maintaining and upgrading software</li>
<li>Improves collaboration in development teams</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> SOFTWARE MAINTENANCE</h3>
<p>
Software maintenance is the process of modifying and updating software after it has been deployed to fix issues, improve performance, or adapt to new requirements.
</p>

<h4> TYPES OF MAINTENANCE</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Corrective Maintenance:</b> Fixing errors and bugs discovered after release</li>
<li><b>Adaptive Maintenance:</b> Updating software to work in new environments (e.g., OS updates)</li>
<li><b>Perfective Maintenance:</b> Enhancing features and improving performance</li>
<li><b>Preventive Maintenance:</b> Making changes to prevent future problems</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> REAL-WORLD EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fixing a crash bug → Corrective</li>
<li>Updating app for new Android version → Adaptive</li>
<li>Improving app speed → Perfective</li>
<li>Refactoring code to avoid future bugs → Preventive</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Documentation = Understanding the system <br>
Maintenance = Improving and fixing the system
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Most software cost occurs after release due to maintenance, not development.
</div>

---

<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Types of documentation</li>
<li>Four types of maintenance</li>
<li>Differences between maintenance categories</li>
<li>Importance of documentation</li>
</ul>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Documentation and Maintenance', 15),
  (1646, 1646, '<h2>Malware (Malicious Software)</h2>

<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Malware refers to any software intentionally designed to harm, exploit, or disrupt computer systems, networks, or data.
It can steal information, damage files, spy on users, or take control of systems without permission.
</p>

<p>
Malware spreads through infected files, email attachments, downloads, or network vulnerabilities.
</p>

---

<h3> TYPES OF MALWARE</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Virus</b>: Attaches to files and spreads when the file is executed</li>
<li><b>Worm</b>: Self-replicates and spreads automatically across networks</li>
<li><b>Trojan Horse</b>: Disguised as legitimate software to trick users</li>
<li><b>Ransomware</b>: Locks or encrypts data and demands payment</li>
<li><b>Spyware</b>: Secretly monitors user activity and collects data</li>
<li><b>Adware</b>: Displays unwanted advertisements</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> REAL EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Opening an infected email attachment → Virus spreads</li>
<li>Network vulnerability exploited → Worm spreads automatically</li>
<li>Fake game download → Trojan installs malware</li>
<li>Files encrypted → Ransomware demands payment</li>
<li>Background tracking → Spyware steals information</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> EFFECTS OF MALWARE</h3>
<ul>
<li>Data loss or corruption</li>
<li>Unauthorized access to systems</li>
<li>Slower computer performance</li>
<li>Financial loss (ransomware, fraud)</li>
<li>Privacy breaches</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> PREVENTION METHODS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Install and update antivirus software</li>
<li>Avoid suspicious downloads and links</li>
<li>Keep operating systems updated</li>
<li>Use firewalls</li>
<li>Scan external devices (USBs)</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Virus = Needs user action <br>
Worm = Spreads automatically <br>
Trojan = Disguised as safe software <br>
Ransomware = Locks data for money
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Malware spreads mainly through user mistakes and unsecured systems.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Malware and Its Types', 15),
  (1647, 1647, '<h2>Phishing and Social Engineering</h2>

<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Phishing and social engineering are cyberattack techniques that exploit human psychology rather than technical weaknesses.
Instead of hacking systems directly, attackers trick users into revealing sensitive information such as passwords, bank details, or personal data.
</p>

<p>
These attacks rely on trust, urgency, fear, or curiosity to manipulate victims.
</p>

---

<h3> TYPES OF ATTACKS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Phishing:</b> Fake emails/websites pretending to be legitimate sources</li>
<li><b>Spear Phishing:</b> Targeted phishing aimed at specific individuals</li>
<li><b>Smishing:</b> Phishing via SMS messages</li>
<li><b>Vishing:</b> Voice calls pretending to be trusted organizations</li>
<li><b>Social Engineering:</b> Psychological manipulation to gain access or data</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> REAL EXAMPLES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Email pretending to be a bank asking for login details</li>
<li>Fake website that looks identical to a real login page</li>
<li>Urgent message: "Your account will be locked—click here!"</li>
<li>Phone call pretending to be IT support requesting passwords</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> WARNING SIGNS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Urgent or threatening language</li>
<li>Suspicious links or email addresses</li>
<li>Requests for sensitive information</li>
<li>Poor grammar or unusual formatting</li>
</ul>

---

<h3> PREVENTION METHODS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Verify sender identity before responding</li>
<li>Do not click suspicious links</li>
<li>Use multi-factor authentication (MFA)</li>
<li>Check website URLs carefully</li>
<li>Never share passwords or confidential data</li>
</ul>

---

<div class="example-box">
Phishing = Fake message tricking you <br>
Social Engineering = Manipulating your thinking
</div>

<div class="keyfact">
 Most cyberattacks succeed because of human error, not system weakness.
</div>
', 'Canonical lesson notes for Phishing and Social Engineering', 15),
  (1648, 1648, '<h2>Password Security</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    Passwords are the first line of defense for protecting digital accounts. Weak passwords can be easily guessed,
    cracked, or stolen using automated hacking tools, while strong passwords significantly reduce security risks.
  </p>
  <h3> BEST PRACTICES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Use a mix of uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols</li>
    <li>Use long passwords (at least 8–12 characters or more)</li>
    <li>Avoid personal details like names or birthdates</li>
    <li>Do not reuse passwords across different accounts</li>
    <li>Enable Two-Factor Authentication (2FA)</li>
    <li>Change passwords regularly</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Weak:</b> 123456</li>
    <li><b>Weak:</b> password</li>
    <li><b>Strong:</b> K9!mZ@82xP</li>
    <li><b>Strong:</b> Tr7#bQ91@Lm</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> COMMON PASSWORD ATTACKS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Brute force attacks (trying many combinations)</li>
    <li>Phishing (tricking users into revealing passwords)</li>
    <li>Dictionary attacks (using common words)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> WHAT IS 2FA?</h3>
  <p>
    Two-Factor Authentication (2FA) adds an extra layer of security by requiring a second verification step
    such as an SMS code, email code, or authentication app.
  </p>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    Banks, email providers, and social media platforms require strong passwords and sometimes 2FA to protect user accounts
    and prevent unauthorized access.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Protects personal data</li>
    <li>Reduces risk of hacking</li>
    <li>Improves account security</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Characteristics of strong passwords</li>
    <li>Meaning of 2FA</li>
    <li>Examples of weak vs strong passwords</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Strong Password = Hard to guess combination of letters, numbers, and symbols <br>
    2FA = Extra security step beyond password
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Password Security Practices', 15),
  (1649, 1649, '<h2>Data Privacy and Backup</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    Data privacy refers to the protection of personal and sensitive information from unauthorized access, misuse, or exposure.
    Backup strategies involve creating copies of data so it can be restored in case of loss, corruption, or cyberattacks.
  </p>
  <h3> 3-2-1 BACKUP RULE</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>3 copies of data</b> (original + 2 backups)</li>
    <li><b>2 different storage types</b> (e.g., hard drive + cloud)</li>
    <li><b>1 off-site backup</b> (stored in a different location)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> BACKUP METHODS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Cloud storage (Google Drive, OneDrive)</li>
    <li>External hard drives</li>
    <li>USB flash drives</li>
    <li>Network backups in organizations</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Saving school work on laptop + Google Drive + USB</li>
    <li>Companies backing up databases to cloud servers</li>
    <li>Photographers storing images on multiple drives</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> DATA PRIVACY MEASURES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using strong passwords</li>
    <li>Encryption of sensitive data</li>
    <li>Two-factor authentication (2FA)</li>
    <li>Access control permissions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    Organizations use backup systems and privacy controls to protect customer data, prevent data breaches, and ensure business continuity after system failures or cyberattacks.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Prevents data loss</li>
    <li>Protects sensitive information</li>
    <li>Ensures recovery after attacks or failures</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>3-2-1 backup rule</li>
    <li>Importance of data privacy</li>
    <li>Examples of backup methods</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Backup = Copy of data for recovery <br>
    Privacy = Protection of sensitive data
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data Privacy and Backup Strategies', 15),
  (1650, 1650, '<h2>Internet vs World Wide Web (WWW)</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    The Internet and the World Wide Web are closely related but not the same. The Internet is the global
    system of interconnected computers and networks, while the World Wide Web (WWW) is a service that runs
    on top of the Internet and provides access to web pages and websites.
  </p>
  <h3> DIFFERENCE</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Internet:</b> Global network infrastructure (hardware + connections)</li>
    <li><b>WWW:</b> Collection of web pages accessed through browsers</li>
    <li><b>Internet:</b> Includes email, FTP, VoIP, websites</li>
    <li><b>WWW:</b> Only web-based content (web pages)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Internet:</b> Sending emails, video calls, file sharing</li>
    <li><b>WWW:</b> Visiting websites like Google, Wikipedia, YouTube</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> RELATIONSHIP</h3>
  <p>
    The WWW is a service that operates on the Internet. Without the Internet, the Web cannot function.
  </p>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    When you use a browser to open websites, you are using the WWW, but the data is transmitted through the Internet infrastructure.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Internet enables global communication</li>
    <li>WWW provides easy access to information</li>
    <li>Together they support education, business, and entertainment</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Difference between Internet and WWW</li>
    <li>Definition of each</li>
    <li>Examples of Internet services vs Web services</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Internet = Network infrastructure <br>
    WWW = Websites and web pages
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Internet vs World Wide Web', 15),
  (1651, 1651, '<h2>Web Browsers and Search Engines</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    A web browser is a software application used to access, retrieve, and display web pages on the Internet.
    A search engine is an online system that helps users find information by searching indexed web pages.
  </p>
  <h3> DIFFERENCE</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Web Browser:</b> Used to display web pages (e.g., Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge)</li>
    <li><b>Search Engine:</b> Used to search for information (e.g., Google, Bing, Yahoo)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Using Google Chrome to open YouTube</li>
    <li>Using Google Search to find “photosynthesis notes”</li>
    <li>Using Firefox to browse Wikipedia</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> HOW SEARCH ENGINES WORK</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>User enters a keyword or query</li>
    <li>Search engine scans indexed websites</li>
    <li>It ranks results based on relevance</li>
    <li>Displays a list of links to the user</li>
  </ol>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    Web browsers allow access to online platforms like e-learning systems, while search engines help students and professionals find information quickly for research and learning.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Fast access to information</li>
    <li>Easy navigation of the Internet</li>
    <li>Supports education and research</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Difference between browser and search engine</li>
    <li>Examples of each</li>
    <li>Functions of search engines</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Browser = Tool for viewing websites <br>
    Search Engine = Tool for finding websites
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Web Browsers and Search Engines', 15),
  (1652, 1652, '<h2>URLs and Domain Names</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    A URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is the complete address used to locate resources on the Internet such as web pages,
    images, or files. A domain name is the human-readable name that represents an IP address and makes websites easier to access.
  </p>
  <h3> STRUCTURE OF A URL</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Protocol:</b> http or https (communication rules)</li>
    <li><b>Domain name:</b> Website name (e.g., google.com)</li>
    <li><b>Path:</b> Specific page or resource location</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>https://www.google.com</li>
    <li>https://example.com/page</li>
    <li>https://www.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> BREAKDOWN EXAMPLE</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    https://www.google.com/search<br>
    Protocol → https<br>
    Domain → google.com<br>
    Path → /search
  </div>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    Domain names are used instead of IP addresses because they are easier for humans to remember,
    while computers still use IP addresses internally to locate servers.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES OF DOMAIN NAMES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Easier to remember than IP addresses</li>
    <li>More user-friendly for navigation</li>
    <li>Can represent brands and organizations</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Definition of URL</li>
    <li>Parts of a URL</li>
    <li>Meaning of domain name</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    URL = Full web address <br>
    Domain name = Human-friendly website name
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for URLs and Domain Names', 15),
  (1653, 1653, '<h2>HTTP vs HTTPS</h2>
  <h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
  <p>
    HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is used to transfer web data between a browser and a web server.
    HTTPS is the secure version of HTTP that uses encryption to protect data during transmission.
  </p>
  <h3> DIFFERENCES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>HTTP:</b> Not secure, data sent in plain text</li>
    <li><b>HTTPS:</b> Secure, data is encrypted</li>
    <li><b>HTTP:</b> Uses port 80</li>
    <li><b>HTTPS:</b> Uses port 443</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAMPLES</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>http://example.com → insecure connection</li>
    <li>https://bank.com → secure connection</li>
    <li>https://shopping-site.com → encrypted payment data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> How HTTPS Works</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Browser requests a secure connection</li>
    <li>Server sends SSL/TLS certificate</li>
    <li>Data is encrypted before transmission</li>
    <li>Only intended receiver can decrypt it</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> REAL-WORLD APPLICATION</h3>
  <p>
    HTTPS is used in online banking, e-commerce, email services, and any system that handles sensitive data like passwords and payments.
  </p>

  <h3> ADVANTAGES OF HTTPS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Protects user data</li>
    <li>Prevents hacking and interception</li>
    <li>Builds trust in websites</li>
    <li>Required for secure transactions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Difference between HTTP and HTTPS</li>
    <li>Meaning of encryption</li>
    <li>Importance of secure communication</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    HTTP = Not secure communication <br>
    HTTPS = Secure encrypted communication
  </div>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for HTTP vs HTTPS', 15),
  (1654, 1654, '<h2>Cookies and Tracking</h2>
  <p>
    Cookies are small text files stored on a user''s device by websites. They are used to remember user information
    such as login details, preferences, and browsing activity.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tracking refers to the process of monitoring user activity online, often using cookies or similar technologies.
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Cookies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Session Cookies:</b> Temporary cookies deleted when the browser is closed</li>
    <li><b>Persistent Cookies:</b> Stored on the device for a longer period</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> How Cookies Work</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>User visits a website</li>
    <li>Website sends a cookie to the browser</li>
    <li>Browser stores the cookie</li>
    <li>Website retrieves it on next visit</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Keeping a user logged in on a website</li>
    <li>Saving items in an online shopping cart</li>
    <li>Showing personalized ads based on browsing history</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    E-commerce websites use cookies to remember shopping cart items, while social media platforms use them to personalize content and ads.
  </p>

  <h3> Advantages of Cookies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improve user experience</li>
    <li>Save login information</li>
    <li>Personalize content</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Risks of Cookies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Privacy concerns</li>
    <li>Tracking user behavior without consent</li>
    <li>Possible misuse of personal data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Cookies are like a website’s memory — they help it remember who you are and what you like.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cookies and Tracking', 15),
  (1655, 1655, '<h2>Basic HTML (HyperText Markup Language)</h2>
  <p>
    HTML is the standard markup language used to create and structure web pages.
    It uses tags to define elements such as headings, paragraphs, images, and links.
  </p>

  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    HTML provides the structure of a web page, while other technologies like CSS and JavaScript add style and interactivity.
  </p>

  <h3> Common HTML Tags</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>&lt;html&gt;</b> – Root element of a webpage</li>
    <li><b>&lt;head&gt;</b> – Contains metadata (title, links, etc.)</li>
    <li><b>&lt;body&gt;</b> – Contains visible content</li>
    <li><b>&lt;h1&gt; to &lt;h6&gt;</b> – Headings</li>
    <li><b>&lt;p&gt;</b> – Paragraph text</li>
    <li><b>&lt;a&gt;</b> – Hyperlinks</li>
    <li><b>&lt;img&gt;</b> – Images</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Example</h3>
  <pre>
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;My First Page&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;

  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;This is my first webpage&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
  </pre>

  <h3> Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    HTML is used by web developers to create websites, online forms, blogs, and web applications.
  </p>

  <h3> Exam Focus</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Definition of HTML</li>
    <li>Structure of an HTML document</li>
    <li>Functions of basic tags</li>
    <li>Difference between head and body</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    HTML is like the skeleton of a website — it gives structure but not style.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Basic HTML', 15),
  (1656, 1656, '<h2>Data vs Information</h2>
  <p>
    Data refers to raw, unprocessed facts and figures that have no meaning on their own.
    Information is data that has been processed, organized, and given meaning for decision-making.
  </p>

  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Data becomes information when it is processed, organized, or analyzed to give meaning.
  </p>

  <h3> Differences Between Data and Information</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Data:</b> Raw facts, unorganized, meaningless on its own</li>
    <li><b>Information:</b> Processed data, meaningful and useful</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Examples</h3>

  <h4>Data Example</h4>
  <div class="example-box">
    50, 60, 70, 80, 90
  </div>

  <h4>Information Example</h4>
  <div class="example-box">
    Average score = 70<br>
    Highest score = 90<br>
    Performance: Good
  </div>

  <h3> Conversion Process</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Collect raw data</li>
    <li>Process (calculate, sort, analyze)</li>
    <li>Organize results</li>
    <li>Present meaningful information</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Schools collect student marks (data) and process them into grades and reports (information) used for decision-making.
  </p>

  <h3> Importance</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Helps in decision-making</li>
    <li>Makes raw facts meaningful</li>
    <li>Improves understanding of situations</li>
    <li>Supports analysis in business and education</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Data is raw material, while information is the finished product after processing.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data vs Information', 15),
  (1657, 1657, '<h2>Types of Information Systems</h2>

  <p>
    Information systems are organized systems that collect, process, store, and distribute data to support
    operations, management, and decision-making in an organization.
  </p>
  <h3> Types of Information Systems</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>1. TPS (Transaction Processing System):</b> Handles daily routine transactions</li>
    <li><b>2. MIS (Management Information System):</b> Provides summarized reports for managers</li>
    <li><b>3. DSS (Decision Support System):</b> Helps managers make complex decisions using data analysis</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Detailed Explanation</h3>

  <h4>1. Transaction Processing System (TPS)</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Handles everyday business transactions</li>
    <li>Operates in real-time</li>
    <li>High speed and accuracy required</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Supermarket billing system, ATM withdrawals, online purchases
  </div>

  <h4>2. Management Information System (MIS)</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Converts data into reports for managers</li>
    <li>Used for planning and monitoring performance</li>
    <li>Works on summarized data</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Monthly sales reports, school performance reports
  </div>

  <h4>3. Decision Support System (DSS)</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Helps in complex decision-making</li>
    <li>Uses models, simulations, and data analysis</li>
    <li>Supports strategic planning</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Investment analysis systems, weather forecasting systems
  </div>

  <h3> Comparison</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>TPS:</b> Handles daily operations</li>
    <li><b>MIS:</b> Produces reports for managers</li>
    <li><b>DSS:</b> Supports decision-making</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Banks use TPS for transactions, MIS for financial reports, and DSS for investment and risk analysis.
  </p>

  <h3> Importance of Information Systems</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improves efficiency in organizations</li>
    <li>Supports decision-making</li>
    <li>Reduces human error</li>
    <li>Enhances data management</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Information systems are tools that help organizations collect data, process it, and use it for decisions.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Types of Information Systems', 15),
  (1658, 1658, '<h2>Data Lifecycle</h2>

  <p>
    The data lifecycle refers to the stages that data passes through from its creation to its final disposal.
    It ensures that data is properly managed, protected, and used efficiently throughout its existence.
  </p>
  <h3> Stages of Data Lifecycle</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>1. Collection:</b> Gathering raw data from sources</li>
    <li><b>2. Processing:</b> Organizing and converting data into useful information</li>
    <li><b>3. Storage:</b> Saving data in databases or storage systems</li>
    <li><b>4. Usage:</b> Using data for decision-making and analysis</li>
    <li><b>5. Archiving:</b> Long-term storage of important data</li>
    <li><b>6. Deletion:</b> Removing unnecessary or outdated data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-life Example</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    Student marks → Collected during exams → Processed into grades → Stored in school database → Used for reports → Archived → Old records deleted
  </div>

  <h3> Importance of Data Lifecycle</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ensures efficient data management</li>
    <li>Improves data security and organization</li>
    <li>Reduces storage costs</li>
    <li>Helps in compliance with data regulations</li>
    <li>Improves decision-making accuracy</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    The data lifecycle is like the life of data — it is created, used, stored, and eventually deleted when no longer needed.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Data Lifecycle', 15),
  (1659, 1659, '<h2>Big Data</h2>
  <p>
    Big Data refers to extremely large and complex datasets that cannot be easily stored, processed, or analyzed
    using traditional data processing tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is used in modern systems where data is generated continuously from many sources such as social media,
    sensors, and online transactions.
  </p>
  <h3> Characteristics of Big Data (3Vs)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Volume:</b> Very large amounts of data generated daily</li>
    <li><b>Velocity:</b> High speed at which data is produced and processed</li>
    <li><b>Variety:</b> Different types of data (text, images, videos, sensor data)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Additional Vs (Advanced Concept)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Veracity:</b> Accuracy and reliability of data</li>
    <li><b>Value:</b> Usefulness of data for decision-making</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Examples of Big Data Sources</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Social media platforms (Facebook, Instagram, TikTok)</li>
    <li>E-commerce websites (Amazon, Jumia)</li>
    <li>IoT devices (smart sensors, smartwatches)</li>
    <li>Banking transactions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Predicting customer behavior in marketing</li>
    <li>Fraud detection in banking systems</li>
    <li>Traffic prediction in smart cities</li>
    <li>Healthcare data analysis</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Importance of Big Data</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Helps organizations make better decisions</li>
    <li>Improves efficiency and performance</li>
    <li>Enables personalized services</li>
    <li>Supports innovation and research</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Big Data is like a massive ocean of information that needs powerful tools to analyze and understand it.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Big Data Basics', 15),
  (1660, 1660, '<h2>Computer Ethics</h2>
  <p>
    Computer ethics refers to the moral principles and guidelines that govern the responsible use of computers,
    digital systems, and information technology.
  </p>
  <p>
    It helps users decide what is right and wrong when using technology, ensuring fairness, respect, and safety in the digital world.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Principles of Computer Ethics</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Privacy:</b> Respecting personal and confidential information</li>
    <li><b>Integrity:</b> Not altering or destroying data without permission</li>
    <li><b>Responsibility:</b> Using technology in a safe and lawful way</li>
    <li><b>Respect:</b> Avoiding harassment, cyberbullying, or harmful behavior</li>
    <li><b>Legal use:</b> Using licensed software and legal resources</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Examples of Ethical Behavior</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Protecting passwords and personal accounts</li>
    <li>Respecting others'' digital privacy</li>
    <li>Using licensed software instead of pirated versions</li>
    <li>Citing sources when using online information</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Examples of Unethical Behavior</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Hacking into someone’s account</li>
    <li>Sharing private information without consent</li>
    <li>Cyberbullying or online harassment</li>
    <li>Using pirated software</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Schools, workplaces, and governments use Acceptable Use Policies (AUPs) to guide users on ethical behavior when using computer systems.
  </p>

  <h3> Importance of Computer Ethics</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Promotes safe and responsible technology use</li>
    <li>Protects users from harm and exploitation</li>
    <li>Builds trust in digital systems</li>
    <li>Reduces cybercrime and misuse of technology</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Computer ethics means using technology in a way that is right, fair, and does not harm others.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Computer Ethics', 15),
  (1661, 1661, '<h2>Cyber Laws</h2>
  <p>
    Cyber laws are legal rules and regulations that govern the use of computers, digital systems, and the Internet.
    They are designed to protect users, data, and systems from cybercrime and misuse.
  </p>
  <p>
    These laws define acceptable behavior online and provide punishment for illegal digital activities.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Cyber laws ensure safe, legal, and responsible use of digital technologies and protect individuals and organizations from cybercrime.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Areas Covered by Cyber Laws</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Cybercrime:</b> Illegal activities such as hacking, phishing, and fraud</li>
    <li><b>Data Protection:</b> Safeguarding personal and sensitive information</li>
    <li><b>Online Transactions:</b> Regulating e-commerce and digital payments</li>
    <li><b>Intellectual Property:</b> Protecting digital content and software</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Examples of Cyber Laws in Action</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Punishment for hacking computer systems</li>
    <li>Laws protecting online banking and mobile money transactions</li>
    <li>Regulation of social media use and online communication</li>
    <li>Protection of personal data by organizations</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 3. Examples of Cybercrime</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Hacking into computer systems</li>
    <li>Online fraud and phishing scams</li>
    <li>Identity theft</li>
    <li>Spreading malware or viruses</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Governments and legal authorities enforce cyber laws to prosecute cybercriminals and protect individuals,
    businesses, and national security systems.
  </p>

  <h3> 5. Importance of Cyber Laws</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Protects users from cyber threats</li>
    <li>Ensures secure online transactions</li>
    <li>Prevents misuse of technology</li>
    <li>Maintains trust in digital systems</li>
    <li>Supports law enforcement in digital investigations</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Cyber laws are like traffic rules for the internet — they control behavior and punish misuse.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cyber Laws', 15),
  (1662, 1662, '<h2>Copyright and Piracy</h2>
  <p>
    Copyright is a legal right that protects the original work of creators such as software, music, books, images, and videos.
    It gives the creator exclusive rights to use, distribute, and sell their work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Piracy is the illegal copying, downloading, or distribution of copyrighted material without permission from the owner.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Copyright protects original work, while piracy violates those rights by unauthorized copying or distribution.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Copyright</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Legal protection for original creators</li>
    <li>Gives ownership rights over creative work</li>
    <li>Requires permission for use or distribution</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Piracy</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Illegal copying or sharing of protected content</li>
    <li>Includes software, music, movies, and books</li>
    <li>Violates intellectual property laws</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 3. Examples</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Legal:</b> Buying licensed Microsoft Office software</li>
    <li><b>Illegal:</b> Downloading cracked software or pirated movies</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Companies use digital licensing systems and legal protections to prevent unauthorized copying and protect intellectual property.
  </p>

  <h3> 5. Importance of Copyright</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Protects creators’ rights</li>
    <li>Encourages innovation and creativity</li>
    <li>Ensures fair economic reward for work</li>
    <li>Reduces illegal distribution of content</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Copyright means “this is mine legally,” while piracy means “taking what is not yours without permission.”
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Copyright and Piracy', 15),
  (1663, 1663, '<h2>Plagiarism</h2>
  <p>
    Plagiarism is the act of using another person’s work, ideas, or intellectual property without giving proper credit,
    and presenting it as your own.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is considered academic dishonesty and is punishable in schools, universities, and professional environments.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Plagiarism occurs when someone presents another person’s work or ideas as their own without acknowledgment.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Forms of Plagiarism</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Direct copying:</b> Copying text word-for-word without citation</li>
    <li><b>Paraphrasing without credit:</b> Rewriting someone’s ideas without acknowledgment</li>
    <li><b>Self-plagiarism:</b> Reusing your own previous work without permission</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Examples of Plagiarism</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Copying an assignment from the internet and submitting it as your own</li>
    <li>Using a friend’s project without credit</li>
    <li>Copy-pasting content from books or websites without citation</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 3. How to Avoid Plagiarism</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Always cite sources used in your work</li>
    <li>Use quotation marks for direct quotes</li>
    <li>Paraphrase properly and acknowledge authors</li>
    <li>Use plagiarism detection tools</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Schools and universities use plagiarism detection software (e.g., Turnitin) to check originality of student work.
  </p>

  <h3> 5. Consequences of Plagiarism</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Loss of marks or academic penalties</li>
    <li>Disqualification from exams or courses</li>
    <li>Damage to academic reputation</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Plagiarism is like stealing someone’s intellectual work and pretending it is yours.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Plagiarism', 15),
  (1664, 1664, '<h2>Digital Citizenship</h2>
  <p>
    Digital citizenship refers to the responsible, safe, and appropriate use of technology and digital platforms
    by individuals in an online environment.
  </p>
  <p>
    It involves understanding how to behave ethically, communicate respectfully, and protect oneself and others online.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Digital citizenship is about using technology responsibly while respecting others and protecting personal information.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Elements of Digital Citizenship</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Digital Etiquette:</b> Proper and respectful behavior online</li>
    <li><b>Online Safety:</b> Protecting personal information and privacy</li>
    <li><b>Responsible Communication:</b> Sharing accurate and respectful information</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Examples of Good Digital Citizenship</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Respecting others on social media platforms</li>
    <li>Avoiding cyberbullying or harassment</li>
    <li>Protecting passwords and personal data</li>
    <li>Verifying information before sharing</li>
    <li>Using technology for positive learning purposes</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 3. Bad Digital Behavior (to Avoid)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Cyberbullying or spreading hate</li>
    <li>Sharing false information (fake news)</li>
    <li>Accessing unauthorized systems</li>
    <li>Sharing private information of others</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Application</h3>
  <p>
    Schools and organizations teach digital citizenship to ensure users behave responsibly in online platforms,
    especially social media and learning environments.
  </p>

  <h3> 5. Importance of Digital Citizenship</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Promotes safe internet usage</li>
    <li>Reduces cyberbullying cases</li>
    <li>Protects personal and sensitive data</li>
    <li>Encourages respectful communication</li>
    <li>Builds a positive online community</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Digital citizenship means behaving online the same way you would behave responsibly in real life.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Digital Citizenship', 15),
  (1665, 1665, '<h2>Artificial Intelligence (AI Basics)</h2>
  <p>
    Artificial Intelligence (AI) is the ability of machines or computer systems to simulate human intelligence processes
    such as learning, reasoning, problem-solving, and decision-making.
  </p>
  <p>
    AI systems analyze data, recognize patterns, and make decisions or predictions without being explicitly programmed for every situation.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    AI enables machines to think and act like humans by learning from data and improving over time.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Key Concepts in AI</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Machine Learning:</b> Systems learn from data</li>
    <li><b>Natural Language Processing (NLP):</b> Understanding human language</li>
    <li><b>Computer Vision:</b> Interpreting images and videos</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Types of AI</h3>

  <h4> Narrow AI</h4>
  <p>
    Designed to perform a specific task only.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Chatbots, face recognition systems, recommendation engines
  </div>

  <h4> General AI</h4>
  <p>
    Hypothetical AI that can perform any intellectual task like a human.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Still under research and not yet fully developed
  </div>

  <h3> 3. Examples of AI in Action</h3>
  <pre>
Phone Face Unlock → AI recognizes facial patterns
YouTube/Netflix → AI recommends videos based on behavior
  </pre>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Virtual assistants (Siri, Google Assistant)</li>
    <li>Self-driving cars</li>
    <li>Fraud detection in banking systems</li>
    <li>Medical diagnosis and disease prediction</li>
    <li>Smart recommendation systems</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 5. Advantages of AI</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Automates repetitive tasks</li>
    <li>Improves decision-making</li>
    <li>Works faster than humans in data analysis</li>
    <li>Reduces human error</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    AI is like giving computers a “brain” that allows them to learn, think, and make decisions like humans.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Artificial Intelligence (AI Basics)', 15),
  (1666, 1666, '<h2>Machine Learning (Introduction)</h2>
  <p>
    Machine Learning (ML) is a branch of Artificial Intelligence (AI) that enables systems to learn from data,
    identify patterns, and improve performance without being explicitly programmed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Instead of following fixed rules, ML systems learn from experience and make predictions or decisions based on data.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Machine Learning allows computers to learn patterns from data instead of being manually programmed for every task.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Types of Machine Learning</h3>

  <h4> Supervised Learning</h4>
  <p>
    The system is trained using labeled data (input + correct output).
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Email spam detection (spam / not spam)
  </div>

  <h4> Unsupervised Learning</h4>
  <p>
    The system finds patterns in unlabeled data without given answers.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Customer grouping in marketing
  </div>

  <h4> Reinforcement Learning</h4>
  <p>
    The system learns through rewards and penalties based on its actions.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Game-playing AI improving through trial and error
  </div>

  <h3> 2. Example of Machine Learning</h3>
  <pre>
Email System → Learns spam patterns → Automatically filters unwanted emails
  </pre>

  <h3> 3. Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Recommendation systems (Netflix, YouTube, TikTok)</li>
    <li>Spam email filtering systems</li>
    <li>Stock market prediction tools</li>
    <li>Speech recognition (voice assistants)</li>
    <li>Self-driving cars</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. AI vs Machine Learning</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>AI:</b> Broad field of intelligent machines</li>
    <li><b>ML:</b> Subset of AI that learns from data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Machine Learning is like teaching a computer by giving it examples instead of writing step-by-step instructions.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Machine Learning (Introduction)', 15),
  (1667, 1667, '<h2>Cloud Computing</h2>
  <p>
    Cloud computing is the delivery of computing services such as storage, servers, databases, networking,
    and software over the internet instead of using local computer hardware.
  </p>
  <p>
    It allows users to access data, applications, and services from anywhere using an internet connection.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Cloud computing replaces local storage and processing with internet-based services that can be accessed anytime, anywhere.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Types of Cloud Services</h3>

  <h4> IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service)</h4>
  <p>
    Provides virtual computing resources such as servers and storage.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Amazon Web Services (AWS)
  </div>

  <h4> PaaS (Platform as a Service)</h4>
  <p>
    Provides platforms for developers to build and deploy applications.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Google App Engine
  </div>

  <h4> SaaS (Software as a Service)</h4>
  <p>
    Provides ready-to-use software over the internet.
  </p>
  <div class="example-box">
    Example: Google Drive, Microsoft Office 365
  </div>

  <h3> 2. Example of Cloud Computing</h3>
  <pre>
Google Drive → Stores files online
Dropbox → Allows file access from anywhere
OneDrive → Cloud-based storage system
  </pre>

  <h3> 3. Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Online storage services (Google Drive, Dropbox)</li>
    <li>Video streaming (YouTube, Netflix)</li>
    <li>Online collaboration tools (Google Docs, Teams)</li>
    <li>Business data backup systems</li>
    <li>Mobile app cloud syncing</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Advantages of Cloud Computing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Access data from anywhere</li>
    <li>Reduces cost of hardware</li>
    <li>Automatic updates and maintenance</li>
    <li>Scalable storage and services</li>
    <li>Improved collaboration</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Cloud computing is like renting a powerful computer over the internet instead of owning one physically.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Cloud Computing', 15),
  (1668, 1668, '<h2>Internet of Things (IoT)</h2>
  <p>
    The Internet of Things (IoT) refers to a network of physical devices that are connected to the internet
    and can collect, share, and exchange data automatically.
  </p>
  <p>
    These devices operate with minimal or no human intervention by communicating with each other through the internet.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    IoT connects everyday objects to the internet, allowing them to collect and exchange data automatically.
  </p>

  <h3> 1. Characteristics of IoT</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Connectivity:</b> Devices are connected to the internet</li>
    <li><b>Automation:</b> Devices perform tasks automatically</li>
    <li><b>Real-time data:</b> Information is collected and processed instantly</li>
    <li><b>Interconnectivity:</b> Devices communicate with each other</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 2. Example of IoT System</h3>
  <pre>
Smart Home System:
Phone → Controls lights → Adjusts temperature → Manages security system
  </pre>

  <h3> 3. IoT Devices</h3>
  <div class="example-box">
    • Smart thermostats<br>
    • Smart lights<br>
    • Smart watches<br>
    • Security cameras<br>
    • Smart fridges
  </div>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Smart homes (automated lighting and heating)</li>
    <li>Wearable devices (fitness trackers, smartwatches)</li>
    <li>Smart cities (traffic control systems)</li>
    <li>Agriculture (soil moisture sensors)</li>
    <li>Healthcare monitoring systems</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 5. Benefits of IoT</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Improves efficiency and automation</li>
    <li>Saves time and reduces human effort</li>
    <li>Enables remote monitoring and control</li>
    <li>Improves decision-making using real-time data</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    IoT is like giving everyday objects the ability to “talk” to each other and work automatically using the internet.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Internet of Things (IoT)', 15),
  (1669, 1669, '<h2>Blockchain Basics</h2>
  <p>
    Blockchain is a distributed digital ledger technology used to record transactions securely across multiple computers
    in a way that makes the records difficult to alter or tamper with.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each record (called a block) is linked to the previous block, forming a continuous chain of data known as a blockchain.
  </p>
  <h3> Key Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Blockchain is a secure system where data is stored in linked blocks across a distributed network, making it nearly impossible to alter.
  </p>
  <h3> 1. How Blockchain Works</h3>
  <pre>
Transaction → Verified by network → Added to a block → Block added to chain → Stored permanently
  </pre>

  <h3> 2. Key Features of Blockchain</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Decentralization:</b> No central authority controls the system</li>
    <li><b>Security:</b> Uses cryptography to protect data</li>
    <li><b>Transparency:</b> All transactions are visible to participants</li>
    <li><b>Immutability:</b> Data cannot be changed once recorded</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 3. Structure of Blockchain</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Block:</b> Contains transaction data</li>
    <li><b>Chain:</b> Linked sequence of blocks</li>
    <li><b>Nodes:</b> Computers that maintain copies of the blockchain</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 4. Real-world Applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Cryptocurrencies (Bitcoin, Ethereum)</li>
    <li>Secure financial transactions</li>
    <li>Supply chain tracking (food, goods, medicine)</li>
    <li>Digital identity verification</li>
    <li>Smart contracts</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> 5. Advantages of Blockchain</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>High security and data integrity</li>
    <li>Reduces fraud and corruption</li>
    <li>No need for intermediaries</li>
    <li>Transparent transaction history</li>
  </ul>

  <h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
  <p>
    Blockchain is like a digital notebook shared across many computers where every entry is locked, verified, and cannot be secretly changed.
  </p>
  ', 'Canonical lesson notes for Blockchain Basics', 15);

