<h2>Distance</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Distance is the total length of the path travelled by an object.</p>

<h3>What is distance?</h3>

<p>
When something moves, it follows a path from one position to another.
The <b>distance</b> tells us the total length of that path.
</p>

<p>
Distance answers:
</p>

<p><b>How far did the object travel?</b></p>

<p>
Distance does not describe the direction of movement. It only measures
the total length travelled.
</p>

<h3>Example 1: A straight journey</h3>

<p>
A student walks 800 m from home to school.
Find the distance travelled.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify what the question gives.</p>

<p>
The student walks 800 m.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify what is being asked.</p>

<p>
The question asks for the distance.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Distance = <b>800 m</b></p>

<h3>Example 2: A journey with two sections</h3>

<p>
A student walks 300 m from home to a shop and then 200 m from the shop
to school. Find the total distance travelled.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the first part.</p>

<p>
Home to shop = 300 m
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the second part.</p>

<p>
Shop to school = 200 m
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Add the lengths of the two parts.</p>

<p>
Distance = 300 + 200
</p>

<p>
Distance = <b>500 m</b>
</p>

<p><b>Answer: 500 m</b></p>

<h3>Example 3: A journey with three sections</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 2 km from A to B, 3 km from B to C and 4 km from C
to D. Find the total distance travelled.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> List every section of the path.</p>

<p>
A to B = 2 km
</p>

<p>
B to C = 3 km
</p>

<p>
C to D = 4 km
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Add all sections.</p>

<p>
Distance = 2 + 3 + 4
</p>

<p>
Distance = <b>9 km</b>
</p>

<p><b>Answer: 9 km</b></p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
If an object travels along several sections, the total distance is the
sum of the lengths of all the sections travelled.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not count only the final section of a journey. Distance includes
the <b>whole path travelled</b>.
</p>
