<h2>Speed</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Speed is the distance travelled per unit of time.</p>

<h3>What does speed tell us?</h3>

<p>
Speed tells us how quickly distance is being covered.
</p>

<p>
For example, a speed of 60 km/h means that an object covers
60 kilometres in 1 hour if it maintains that speed.
</p>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<p><b>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</b></p>

<p>
Using symbols:
</p>

<p><b>s = d / t</b></p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels 120 km in 2 hours. Find its speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Write the distance.</p>

<p>
d = 120 km
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Write the time.</p>

<p>
t = 2 h
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Use the formula.</p>

<p>
s = d / t
</p>

<p>
s = 120 / 2
</p>

<p>
s = <b>60 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 45 km in 3 hours. Find the speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>

<p>
d = 45 km
</p>

<p>
t = 3 h
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>

<p>
s = 45 / 3
</p>

<p>
s = <b>15 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bus travels 240 km in 4 hours. Find its speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Distance = 240 km.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Time = 4 h.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>

<p>
s = 240 / 4
</p>

<p>
s = <b>60 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we divide?</h3>

<p>
The total distance tells us how far the object travelled.
The total time tells us how long it took.
Dividing distance by time tells us how much distance was covered
for each unit of time.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not multiply distance by time when finding speed.
The correct relationship is:
</p>

<p><b>Speed = Distance ÷ Time</b></p>
