<h2>Velocity</h2>

<p><b>One concept:</b> Velocity is speed in a specified direction.</p>

<h3>Speed versus velocity</h3>

<p>
Speed tells us how quickly an object moves.
Velocity tells us how quickly it moves <b>and in which direction</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>60 km/h</b> describes speed.
</p>

<p>
<b>60 km/h east</b> describes velocity.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels at 80 km/h east.
State its velocity.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the speed.</p>

<p>
Speed = 80 km/h
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the direction.</p>

<p>
Direction = east
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Velocity = <b>80 km/h east</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A train moves at 100 km/h north.
State its velocity.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Speed = 100 km/h.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Direction = north.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Velocity = <b>100 km/h north</b></p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A cyclist moves at 25 km/h west.
State the velocity.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Speed = 25 km/h.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Direction = west.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> Velocity = <b>25 km/h west</b></p>

<h3>Important idea</h3>

<p>
Velocity requires a direction. A statement such as “50 km/h” gives a
speed, but “50 km/h south” gives a velocity.
</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>

<p>
Do not call a value velocity if its direction has not been specified.
</p>
', 1),
