<h2>Relative Speed in the Same Direction</h2>

<p>
When two objects move in the <b>same direction</b>, the faster object gains on
the slower object only by the difference between their speeds.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = faster speed − slower speed</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we subtract?</h3>

<p>
Suppose a car travels at 80 km/h while another car ahead travels at 60 km/h.
In one hour, the first car travels 80 km while the second travels 60 km.
</p>

<p>
The first car therefore gains:
</p>

<p>
80 − 60 = 20 km
</p>

<p>
So the distance between them decreases by <b>20 km every hour</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A bus travels at 70 km/h. A car travelling in the same direction moves at
50 km/h. Find the relative speed of the bus with respect to the car.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the faster speed.</p>
<p>Faster speed = 70 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the slower speed.</p>
<p>Slower speed = 50 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Subtract.</p>
<p>Relative speed = 70 − 50 = <b>20 km/h</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels at 25 km/h while another cyclist ahead travels at 18 km/h
in the same direction. Find their relative speed.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>25 km/h is faster than 18 km/h.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 25 − 18</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = <b>7 km/h</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A lorry moves at 90 km/h and a van moves at 65 km/h in the same direction.
Find how quickly the lorry is reducing the distance between itself and the van.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = 90 − 65</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Relative speed = <b>25 km/h</b></p>

<p>
The distance between them decreases at 25 km/h.
</p>

<h3>Key Rule</h3>

<p>
<b>Same direction → subtract the speeds.</b>
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Relative Speed in the Same Direction</h2>

<p>
When two objects move in the <b>same direction</b>, the distance between them
changes according to the difference between their speeds.
</p>

<p>
If the faster object has speed v₁ and the slower object has speed v₂:
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = v₁ − v₂</b>
</p>

<p>
The faster object gains on the slower object at this relative speed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels at 80 km/h and a bus travels at 50 km/h in the same direction.
Find the relative speed of the car with respect to the bus.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the faster speed.</p>

<p>Car = 80 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the slower speed.</p>

<p>Bus = 50 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Subtract.</p>

<p>
Relative speed = 80 − 50
</p>

<p><b>Answer = 30 km/h</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist moves at 25 km/h while another cyclist ahead moves at 18 km/h
in the same direction.
</p>

<p>
Relative speed = 25 − 18
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = 7 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A lorry travels at 60 km/h and a car travels at 90 km/h in the same direction.
</p>

<p>
Relative speed = 90 − 60
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = 30 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
For objects moving in the same direction, <b>subtract their speeds</b> to
find how quickly one gains on the other.
</p>
