<h2>Relative Speed in Opposite Directions</h2>

<p>
When two objects move towards each other in <b>opposite directions</b>,
their speeds work together to reduce the distance between them.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Relative speed = speed of first object + speed of second object</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we add?</h3>

<p>
Suppose one car moves at 60 km/h towards another car moving at 40 km/h.
After one hour, the first car has covered 60 km towards the other car and the
second has covered 40 km towards the first.
</p>

<p>
Together they have reduced the distance by:
</p>

<p>
60 + 40 = 100 km
</p>

