<h2>Translating Motion Words into an Equation</h2>

<p>
A motion word problem becomes easier when the words are converted into the
three quantities:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Distance</li>
<li>Speed</li>
<li>Time</li>
</ul>

<p>
The main relationship is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Distance = Speed × Time</b>
</p>

<p>
The important skill on this screen is not solving complicated equations.
It is translating the information in the question into the correct equation.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels at 60 km/h for 4 hours. Find the distance travelled.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify speed.</p>
<p>s = 60 km/h</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify time.</p>
<p>t = 4 h</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Use d = st.</p>

<p>
d = 60 × 4
</p>

<p><b>Answer = 240 km</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist covers 90 km in 3 hours. Find the speed.
</p>

<p>
The unknown is speed, so:
</p>

<p>
<b>s = d/t</b>
</p>

<p>
s = 90/3 = <b>30 km/h</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bus travels at 50 km/h and covers 200 km. Find the time.
</p>

<p>
The unknown is time:
</p>

<p>
<b>t = d/s</b>
</p>

<p>
t = 200/50
</p>

<p><b>Answer = 4 hours</b></p>

<h3>Method</h3>

<p>
Before calculating, ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>What is known? What is unknown? Which form of d = st gives me the
unknown?</b>
</p>
