<h2>Representing a Multi-Stage Journey</h2>

<p>
A journey can consist of several separate stages.
</p>

<p>
For example, a vehicle may travel:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Stage 1: 60 km</li>
<li>Stage 2: 40 km</li>
<li>Stage 3: 30 km</li>
</ul>

<p>
The journey is still one trip, but we calculate each stage separately.
</p>

<p>
For each stage:
</p>

<p>
<b>Distance = Speed × Time</b>
</p>

<p>
Then the required quantities can be combined.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels for 2 hours at 50 km/h, then for 1 hour at 60 km/h.
Find the distance travelled in each stage.
</p>

<p><b>Stage 1:</b></p>
<p>d₁ = 50 × 2 = <b>100 km</b></p>

<p><b>Stage 2:</b></p>
<p>d₂ = 60 × 1 = <b>60 km</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels for 3 hours at 20 km/h, then 2 hours at 15 km/h.
</p>

<p><b>Stage 1:</b></p>
<p>d₁ = 20 × 3 = <b>60 km</b></p>

<p><b>Stage 2:</b></p>
<p>d₂ = 15 × 2 = <b>30 km</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A bus travels for 1.5 hours at 80 km/h and then for 2 hours at 50 km/h.
Find the distance travelled in each stage.
</p>

<p><b>Stage 1:</b></p>
<p>d₁ = 80 × 1.5 = <b>120 km</b></p>

<p><b>Stage 2:</b></p>
<p>d₂ = 50 × 2 = <b>100 km</b></p>

<h3>Important Idea</h3>

<p>
Do not use one speed for the entire journey when the speed changes.
Calculate each stage using its own speed and time.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Representing a Multi-Stage Journey</h2>

<p>
A journey may contain several separate stages.
For example, a vehicle may travel from A to B and then from B to C.
</p>

<p>
Each stage has its own:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Distance</li>
<li>Speed</li>
<li>Time</li>
</ul>

<p>
The important rule is to keep the stages separate before combining them.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A car travels 100 km in the first stage and 80 km in the second stage.
Represent the total distance.
</p>

<p>
Stage 1 = 100 km
</p>

<p>
Stage 2 = 80 km
</p>

<p>
Total distance = 100 + 80
</p>

<p>
<b>Total distance = 180 km</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A bus travels for 2 hours in the first stage and 3 hours in the second stage.
</p>

<p>
Total time = 2 + 3
</p>

<p>
<b>Total time = 5 hours</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A cyclist travels 40 km at 20 km/h and then 30 km at 15 km/h.
Represent the journey using separate stages.
</p>

<p>
Stage 1:
</p>

<p>
Distance = 40 km, Speed = 20 km/h
</p>

<p>
Stage 2:
</p>

<p>
Distance = 30 km, Speed = 15 km/h
</p>

<p>
The stages should not be combined until the required quantity has been
calculated for each stage.
</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>
Treat each stage as its own small motion problem first.
</p>
