<h2>Finding Displacement from Two Positions</h2>

<p>
Displacement tells us the <b>change in position</b>.
</p>

<p>
If an object starts at position x₁ and finishes at position x₂:
</p>

<p>
<b>Displacement = final position − initial position</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Δx = x₂ − x₁</b>
</p>

<p>
The order matters. Always subtract the <b>initial position from the final
position</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
An object moves from position +2 to position +8.
Find its displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Initial position = +2</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Final position = +8</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Displacement = 8 − 2</p>

<p><b>Answer = +6 units</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
An object moves from position +7 to position +3.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Initial position = +7</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Final position = +3</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Displacement = 3 − 7 = <b>−4 units</b></p>

<p>
The negative sign means the displacement is towards the left.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
An object moves from position −5 to position +4.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Displacement = final − initial</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Δx = 4 − (−5)</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b></p>
<p>Δx = 4 + 5</p>

<p><b>Answer = +9 units</b></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Displacement is not simply the final number. It measures how the position
changed.
</p>
', 1),
