<h2>Finding Distance from Positions</h2>

<p>
When an object moves directly from one position to another without turning
back, the distance travelled is the size of the change in position.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Distance = |final position − initial position|</b>
</p>

<p>
The vertical bars mean <b>absolute value</b>, so the distance is never negative.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
An object moves from +2 to +9.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Change in position = 9 − 2 = 7</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Distance = |7| = <b>7 units</b></p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
An object moves from +8 to +3.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Change in position = 3 − 8 = −5</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>Distance = |−5|</p>

<p><b>Answer = 5 units</b></p>

<p>
The displacement is −5 units, but the distance travelled is 5 units.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
An object moves from −4 to +6.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b></p>
<p>Change in position = 6 − (−4)</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b></p>
<p>= 10</p>

<p><b>Answer = 10 units</b></p>

<h3>Key Difference</h3>

<p>
<b>Displacement</b> can be positive or negative.
</p>

<p>
<b>Distance</b> is always positive or zero.
</p>
', 1),
