<h2>Writing a Constraint from a Statement</h2>

<h3>The skill</h3>

<p>
A linear programming problem often gives us a condition in words.
We must translate that condition into a mathematical inequality.
</p>

<p>
The important steps are:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Identify the quantities.</li>
  <li>Combine the quantities according to the statement.</li>
  <li>Identify the restriction word.</li>
  <li>Write the inequality.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A farmer has <b>30 hectares at most</b> for maize and beans.
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = hectares of maize
</p>

<p>
y = hectares of beans
</p>

<p>
The total land used is:
</p>

<p>
x + y
</p>

<p>
"At most 30" means the total cannot be greater than 30.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 30</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A workshop has <b>50 hours available</b> for two activities.
If x and y are the hours used for the activities, the total hours
cannot exceed 50.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 50</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A school has <b>at least 200 students</b> in two groups.
Let x and y represent the numbers of students in the two groups.
</p>

<p>
The total number is:
</p>

<p>
x + y
</p>

<p>
"At least 200" means the total cannot be below 200.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 200</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not choose the inequality symbol before understanding the words.
The wording determines the mathematical relationship.
</p>
