<h2>Understanding ≤</h2>

<h3>What does ≤ mean?</h3>

<p>
The symbol:
</p>

<p>
<b>≤</b>
</p>

<p>
means <b>less than or equal to</b>.
</p>

<p>
It combines two ideas:
</p>

<p>
<b>less than</b> OR <b>equal to</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
x ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
This allows:
</p>

<p>
x = 10
</p>

<p>
because x can be equal to 10.
</p>

<p>
It also allows:
</p>

<p>
x = 7
</p>

<p>
because 7 is less than 10.
</p>

<p>
But it does not allow:
</p>

<p>
x = 12
</p>

<p>
because 12 is greater than 10.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Check whether x = 8 satisfies:
</p>

<p>
x ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
Substitute x = 8:
</p>

<p>
8 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
This is true.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 8 is allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Check x = 10 in:
</p>

<p>
x ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
Substitute:
</p>

<p>
10 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
This is true because equality is allowed.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 10 is allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Check x = 13 in:
</p>

<p>
x ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
Substitute:
</p>

<p>
13 ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
This is false.
</p>

<p>
Therefore x = 13 is not allowed.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
The equality part matters.
A value exactly on the limit is allowed when the condition is ≤.
</p>
