<h2>Non-Negativity Constraints</h2>

<h3>Why do we need x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0?</h3>

<p>
In many linear programming problems, x and y represent quantities
such as:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>number of products;</li>
  <li>kilograms of material;</li>
  <li>hectares of land;</li>
  <li>hours of work.</li>
</ul>

<p>
These quantities cannot normally be negative.
</p>

<p>
You cannot produce -5 shirts or use -3 hectares of land.
</p>

<p>
Therefore we usually write:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
These are called <b>non-negativity constraints</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
x represents the number of chairs produced.
</p>

<p>
Because a number of chairs cannot be negative:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
y represents kilograms of maize produced.
</p>

<p>
The quantity cannot be negative:
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
x represents the number of shirts and y represents the number of
trousers.
</p>

<p>
Both quantities must be non-negative:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
This means neither quantity can be less than zero.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
Non-negativity does not mean x and y must be positive.
</p>

<p>
Zero is allowed.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
x = 0
</p>

<p>
satisfies x ≥ 0.
</p>

<p>
This could mean that none of that product is produced.
</p>
