<h2>Checking Whether an Inequality Matches a Condition</h2>

<h3>Why check?</h3>

<p>
A common mistake in linear programming is choosing the wrong inequality
symbol.
</p>

<p>
We can test whether an inequality matches the original condition by
checking what values it allows.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A farmer has <b>at most 20 hectares</b>.
</p>

<p>
Suppose we write:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≥ 20
</p>

<p>
Does this make sense?
</p>

<p>
No.
</p>

<p>
The inequality allows values greater than 20.
For example:
</p>

<p>
x + y = 25
</p>

<p>
But 25 hectares are more than the available 20 hectares.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the correct constraint is:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 20</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A school requires <b>at least 300 learners</b>.
</p>

<p>
Suppose we write:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 300
</p>

<p>
This would allow only 300 or fewer learners.
</p>

<p>
But the school needs 300 or more.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the correct constraint is:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≥ 300</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A factory can use <b>no more than 50 kg</b> of material.
</p>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 50
</p>

<p>
Test 50:
</p>

<p>
50 ≤ 50
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Test 45:
</p>

<p>
45 ≤ 50
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Test 60:
</p>

<p>
60 ≤ 50
</p>

<p>
False.
</p>

<p>
That is exactly what "no more than 50 kg" requires.
</p>

<h3>Useful check</h3>

<p>
Choose a value that clearly should be allowed and one that clearly
should not be allowed.
Then see whether your inequality behaves correctly.
</p>
