<h2>Drawing a Boundary Line from Its Intercepts</h2>

<h3>The idea</h3>

<p>
Once we know the x-intercept and y-intercept of a boundary equation,
we can use those two points to draw the line.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Draw:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y = 10</b>
</p>

<p>
First find the x-intercept.
</p>

<p>
Set y = 0:
</p>

<p>
x = 10
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>(10, 0)</b>
</p>

<p>
Now find the y-intercept.
</p>

<p>
Set x = 0:
</p>

<p>
y = 10
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>(0, 10)</b>
</p>

<p>
Plot (10,0) and (0,10), then draw a straight line through them.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Draw:
</p>

<p>
<b>2x + y = 8</b>
</p>

<p>
For the x-intercept:
</p>

<p>
y = 0
</p>

<p>
2x = 8
</p>

<p>
x = 4
</p>

<p>
So the first point is:
</p>

<p>
<b>(4,0)</b>
</p>

<p>
For the y-intercept:
</p>

<p>
x = 0
</p>

<p>
y = 8
</p>

<p>
So the second point is:
</p>

<p>
<b>(0,8)</b>
</p>

<p>
Plot both points and join them with a straight line.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Draw:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + 2y = 12</b>
</p>

<p>
x-intercept:
</p>

<p>
3x = 12
</p>

<p>
x = 4
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>(4,0)</b>
</p>

<p>
y-intercept:
</p>

<p>
2y = 12
</p>

<p>
y = 6
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>(0,6)</b>
</p>

<p>
The boundary line passes through (4,0) and (0,6).
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Two different points determine a straight line.
For these problems, the two easiest points are usually the
x-intercept and y-intercept.
