<h2>Solid and Broken Boundary Lines</h2>

<h3>Why does the boundary line sometimes look different?</h3>

<p>
When graphing an inequality, we must decide whether the boundary itself
belongs to the solution.
</p>

<p>
There are two cases.
</p>

<h3>Case 1: ≤ or ≥</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 10</b>
</p>

<p>
The equality case is included.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
x + y = 10
</p>

<p>
is allowed.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the boundary line is drawn as a
<b>solid line</b>.
</p>

<p>
The same applies to:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≥ 10
</p>

<p>
because equality is also included.
</p>

<h3>Case 2: &lt; or &gt;</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
x + y &lt; 10
</p>

<p>
Here equality is NOT included.
</p>

<p>
A point where x + y = 10 is not part of the solution.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the boundary is drawn as a
<b>broken/dashed line</b>.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Graph the boundary for:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 8
</p>

<p>
Because ≤ includes equality:
</p>

<p>
<b>Use a solid line.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Graph the boundary for:
</p>

<p>
2x + y ≥ 12
</p>

<p>
Because ≥ includes equality:
</p>

<p>
<b>Use a solid line.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Graph the boundary for:
</p>

<p>
x + 2y &lt; 10
</p>

<p>
Because &lt; does not include equality:
</p>

<p>
<b>Use a broken/dashed line.</b>
</p>

<h3>Important rule</h3>

<p>
<b>≤ or ≥ → solid boundary</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>&lt; or &gt; → broken boundary</b>
</p>

<p>
The reason is simple:
the line is included only when equality is allowed.
</p>
