<h2>Meaning of the Feasible Region</h2>

<h3>What is a feasible region?</h3>

<p>
A <b>feasible region</b> is the part of the graph containing points
that satisfy <b>all</b> the given constraints at the same time.
</p>

<p>
The word <b>feasible</b> means possible or allowed.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, a feasible point is a point that obeys every restriction.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose the constraints are:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
The first inequality gives a region below the boundary
x + y = 10.
</p>

<p>
The conditions x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0 keep us in the first quadrant.
</p>

<p>
The part satisfying all three conditions is the feasible region.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose a point is inside the region satisfying:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
but it has x = -2.
</p>

<p>
Then:
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
is not satisfied.
</p>

<p>
Therefore the point is not feasible.
</p>

<p>
Being inside one shaded region is not enough.
The point must satisfy every constraint.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider the constraints:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 8
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 2
</p>

<p>
y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
Consider the point (3,4).
</p>

<p>
Check the first constraint:
</p>

<p>
3 + 4 ≤ 8
</p>

<p>
7 ≤ 8
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Check the second:
</p>

<p>
3 ≥ 2
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Check the third:
</p>

<p>
4 ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
True.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (3,4) is a feasible point.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
<b>Feasible region = common region satisfying every constraint.</b>
</p>
', 1),
  (118, 118, '
<h2>Identifying the Feasible Region</h2>

<h3>The skill</h3>

<p>
When several inequalities are graphed, each inequality produces a
solution region.
</p>

<p>
The feasible region is where those solution regions overlap.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 2
</p>

<p>
y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
First, identify the region satisfying x + y ≤ 10.
</p>

<p>
Next, keep only the part where x ≥ 2.
</p>

<p>
Finally, keep only the part where y ≥ 0.
</p>

<p>
The remaining common region is the feasible region.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
2x + y ≤ 12
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
The non-negativity constraints keep us in the first quadrant.
</p>

<p>
The inequality 2x + y ≤ 12 selects the side containing (0,0).
</p>

<p>
The overlap of these conditions is the feasible region.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
x + y ≤ 8
</p>

<p>
x + 2y ≤ 10
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
There are four restrictions.
</p>

<p>
We do not choose the region from only one inequality.
</p>

<p>
We keep the part of the graph that satisfies all four conditions.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
The feasible region is not necessarily the largest shaded region.
It is the <b>common</b> region.
</p>
