<h2>Finding a Corner Point on an Axis</h2>

<h3>Why do axes matter?</h3>

<p>
Because linear programming usually includes:
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
the axes can form part of the boundary of the feasible region.
</p>

<p>
A corner point may therefore occur where a boundary line meets an
axis.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
2x + y = 10
</p>

<p>
To find where the line meets the x-axis, set:
</p>

<p>
y = 0
</p>

<p>
Then:
</p>

<p>
2x = 10
</p>

<p>
x = 5
</p>

<p>
So the point is:
</p>

<p>
<b>(5,0)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find where:
</p>

<p>
2x + y = 10
</p>

<p>
meets the y-axis.
</p>

<p>
Set:
</p>

<p>
x = 0
</p>

<p>
Then:
</p>

<p>
y = 10
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
<b>(0,10)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p>
3x + 2y = 12
</p>

<p>
x-axis:
</p>

<p>
y = 0
</p>

<p>
3x = 12
</p>

<p>
x = 4
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(4,0)</b>
</p>

<p>
y-axis:
</p>

<p>
x = 0
</p>

<p>
2y = 12
</p>

<p>
y = 6
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(0,6)</b>
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
A boundary line meeting an axis gives a candidate boundary point.
Whether that point belongs to the feasible region must still be checked
against all constraints.
</p>
