<h2>Forming a Cost Objective Function</h2>

<p>
A cost objective function represents the total amount spent on the
different quantities being considered.
</p>

<p>
The same idea used for profit applies:
</p>

<p>
<b>Total cost = cost of first item + cost of second item</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
A factory uses:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Material A costing KSh 300 per unit</li>
<li>Material B costing KSh 500 per unit</li>
</ul>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = units of A<br>
y = units of B
</p>

<p>
Cost of A = 300x
</p>

<p>
Cost of B = 500y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 300x + 500y</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A farmer buys fertilizer A at KSh 2,000 per bag and fertilizer B
at KSh 1,500 per bag.
</p>

<p>
Let x and y be the numbers of bags.
</p>

<p>
Cost:
</p>

<p>
C = 2000x + 1500y
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A school buys notebooks at KSh 40 each and textbooks at KSh 250 each.
</p>

<p>
Let:
</p>

<p>
x = notebooks<br>
y = textbooks
</p>

<p>
Notebook cost = 40x
</p>

<p>
Textbook cost = 250y
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 40x + 250y</b>
</p>

<p>
If the problem asks for the lowest total cost, the objective is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Minimize C</b>
</p>
