  (137, 137, '
<h2>Meaning of an Optimal Solution</h2>

<p>
An <b>optimal solution</b> is the feasible solution that gives the
best possible value of the objective function.
</p>

<p>
"Best" depends on the objective:
</p>

<ul>
<li>For maximum profit, the best solution gives the greatest profit.</li>
<li>For minimum cost, the best solution gives the smallest cost.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example 1: Maximum Profit</h3>

<p>
Suppose three feasible production plans give:
</p>

<ul>
<li>A → KSh 4,000 profit</li>
<li>B → KSh 5,500 profit</li>
<li>C → KSh 4,800 profit</li>
</ul>

<p>
Because the objective is to <b>maximize profit</b>, compare the values:
</p>

<p>
4000, 5500, 4800
</p>

<p>
The greatest value is 5500.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>B is the optimal solution.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Minimum Cost</h3>

<p>
Three feasible plans have costs:
</p>

<ul>
<li>A → KSh 7,000</li>
<li>B → KSh 5,200</li>
<li>C → KSh 6,100</li>
</ul>

<p>
The objective is to minimize cost.
</p>

<p>
The smallest value is KSh 5,200.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>B is the optimal solution.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
P = 300x + 500y
</p>

<p>
and the feasible points produce:
</p>

<p>
(2,3) → P = 2,100
</p>

<p>
(4,2) → P = 2,200
</p>

<p>
(1,4) → P = 2,300
</p>

<p>
For maximum profit, the greatest value is 2,300.
</p>

<p>
Therefore (1,4) is the optimal solution among the points considered.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
<b>Feasible</b> means the solution is allowed.
</p>

<p>
<b>Optimal</b> means it is the best among the allowed solutions
according to the objective.
</p>
', 1),
  (138, 138, '
<h2>Why Corner Points Are Tested</h2>

<p>
In a linear programming problem, the feasible region contains many
possible combinations of the variables.
</p>

<p>
For a linear objective function, the best value occurs at a
<b>corner point</b> of the feasible region, when an optimum exists.
</p>

<p>
This gives us a practical method:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Find the feasible region.</li>
<li>Identify its corner points.</li>
<li>Evaluate the objective function at those corner points.</li>
<li>Choose the greatest or smallest value as required.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose the feasible region has corner points:
</p>

<p>
(0,0), (6,0), (4,3), (0,5)
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 500x + 400y</b>
</p>

<p>
We test the corner points because these are the points where the
boundary of the feasible region changes direction.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 300x + 200y</b>
</p>

<p>
and the feasible region has corners:
</p>

<p>
(0,0), (8,0), (5,4), (0,6)
</p>

<p>
We evaluate C at these corner points rather than randomly selecting
points outside the feasible region.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
A graph may contain infinitely many feasible points.
</p>

<p>
We cannot reasonably calculate the objective value at every possible
point one by one.
</p>

<p>
The corner-point method reduces the problem to a finite set of
important points.
</p>

<h3>The important rule</h3>

<p>
<b>For a linear programming problem, once the feasible region is known,
the corner points are the points we test for the optimum.</b>
', 1),
  (139, 139, '
<h2>Evaluating the Objective at Every Corner Point</h2>

<p>
Once the corner points have been found, substitute each one into
the objective function.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 400x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
and the feasible region has corners:
</p>

<p>
A(0,0), B(5,0), C(3,4), D(0,4)
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Evaluate A(0,0)</h3>

<p>
x = 0, y = 0
</p>

<p>
P = 400(0) + 300(0)
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Evaluate B(5,0)</h3>

<p>
P = 400(5) + 300(0)
</p>

<p>
P = 2000
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 2,000</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Evaluate C(3,4)</h3>

<p>
P = 400(3) + 300(4)
</p>

<p>
P = 1200 + 1200
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 2,400</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Evaluate D(0,4)</h3>

<p>
P = 400(0) + 300(4)
</p>

<p>
P = 1200
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 1,200</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore the objective values are:
</p>

<ul>
<li>A → 0</li>
<li>B → 2,000</li>
<li>C → 2,400</li>
<li>D → 1,200</li>
</ul>

<p>
We have not yet chosen the answer.
</p>

<p>
First we calculate all the objective values.
</p>

<p>
The next step is to compare them according to whether we are
maximizing or minimizing.
</p>
