  (141, 141, '
<h2>Finding the Minimum from Corner Values</h2>

<p>
When the objective is to be <b>minimized</b>, choose the smallest
objective value among the feasible corner points.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose the corner values of a cost function are:
</p>

<ul>
<li>A → 2,500</li>
<li>B → 1,800</li>
<li>C → 2,200</li>
<li>D → 3,000</li>
</ul>

<p>
The smallest value is:
</p>

<p>
<b>1,800</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore B gives the minimum cost.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
C = 300x + 500y
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(0,0) → C = 0</li>
<li>(4,0) → C = 1,200</li>
<li>(3,4) → C = 2,900</li>
<li>(0,5) → C = 2,500</li>
</ul>

<p>
The smallest value is 0 at (0,0).
</p>

<p>
However, this answer must only be accepted if (0,0) satisfies
the original requirements of the problem.
</p>

<p>
This is important because a point can be mathematically feasible
under the displayed inequalities but still be unsuitable if the
original problem contains additional requirements.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Three feasible production plans have costs:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(2,3) → KSh 4,500</li>
<li>(4,1) → KSh 3,800</li>
<li>(1,4) → KSh 5,000</li>
</ul>

<p>
The smallest cost is:
</p>

<p>
<b>KSh 3,800</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore (4,1) is the minimum-cost solution.
</p>

<h3>Rule</h3>

<p>
<b>Minimization → choose the smallest objective value.</b>
