<h2>Complete Corner-Point Minimisation</h2>

<p>
The same corner-point method can be used when the objective is to
<b>minimize</b>.
</p>

<p>
The process is:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Find the feasible corner points.</li>
<li>Write the objective function.</li>
<li>Evaluate the objective at every corner.</li>
<li>Compare the values.</li>
<li>Choose the smallest value.</li>
<li>State the optimal point and minimum value.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose the feasible corner points are:
</p>

<p>
(1,1), (5,1), (4,4), (1,5)
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>C = 400x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
We want to minimize C.
</p>

<h3>Corner 1: (1,1)</h3>

<p>
C = 400(1) + 300(1)
</p>

<p>
C = 700
</p>

<h3>Corner 2: (5,1)</h3>

<p>
C = 400(5) + 300(1)
</p>

<p>
C = 2000 + 300
</p>

<p>
C = 2300
</p>

<h3>Corner 3: (4,4)</h3>

<p>
C = 400(4) + 300(4)
</p>

<p>
C = 1600 + 1200
</p>

<p>
C = 2800
</p>

<h3>Corner 4: (1,5)</h3>

<p>
C = 400(1) + 300(5)
</p>

<p>
C = 400 + 1500
</p>

<p>
C = 1900
</p>

<h3>Compare</h3>

<p>
The smallest value is:
</p>

<p>
<b>700</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Optimal point = (1,1)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Minimum C = 700</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
For maximum:
</p>

<p>
<b>Choose the greatest value.</b>
</p>

<p>
For minimum:
</p>

<p>
<b>Choose the smallest value.</b>
</p>
