<h2>Complete Linear Programming Solution</h2>

<p>
We can now put the major skills together.
</p>

<p>
A complete linear programming solution follows this chain:
</p>

<p>
<b>Constraints → Feasible Region → Corner Points → Objective Function → Objective Values → Optimum</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose a problem has constraints whose feasible region has the
following corner points:
</p>

<p>
<b>(0,0), (8,0), (6,4), (0,6)</b>
</p>

<p>
Suppose the objective is to maximize:
</p>

<p>
<b>P = 400x + 500y</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Evaluate (0,0)</h3>

<p>
P = 400(0) + 500(0)
</p>

<p>
P = 0
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Evaluate (8,0)</h3>

<p>
P = 400(8) + 500(0)
</p>

<p>
P = 3200
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Evaluate (6,4)</h3>

<p>
P = 400(6) + 500(4)
</p>

<p>
P = 2400 + 2000
</p>

<p>
P = 4400
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Evaluate (0,6)</h3>

<p>
P = 400(0) + 500(6)
</p>

<p>
P = 3000
</p>

<h3>Step 5: Compare</h3>

<p>
The objective values are:
</p>

<ul>
<li>(0,0) → 0</li>
<li>(8,0) → 3200</li>
<li>(6,4) → 4400</li>
<li>(0,6) → 3000</li>
</ul>

<p>
The greatest value is:
</p>

<p>
<b>4400</b>
</p>

<h3>Final Answer</h3>

<p>
<b>Optimal point = (6,4)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximum P = 4400</b>
</p>

<h3>What the answer means</h3>

<p>
If x and y represent quantities being produced, then:
</p>

<p>
x = 6
</p>

<p>
y = 4
</p>

<p>
is the combination that produces the greatest possible value of P
among the feasible corner points.
</p>

<h3>Complete method to remember</h3>

<ol>
<li>Find the constraints.</li>
<li>Draw or identify the feasible region.</li>
<li>Find the corner points.</li>
<li>Form the objective function.</li>
<li>Substitute every corner point.</li>
<li>Calculate the objective value at each point.</li>
<li>Choose maximum or minimum as required.</li>
<li>State both the optimal point and optimal value.</li>
</ol>
