<h2>Translating Non-Negativity Conditions</h2>

<p>
In a production problem, a quantity normally cannot be negative.
</p>

<p>
You cannot produce -5 tables.
</p>

<p>
Therefore decision variables usually satisfy:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
If x represents the number of shirts:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
This says the number of shirts cannot be negative.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
If x represents tables and y represents chairs:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
Both quantities must be non-negative.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose a problem has:
</p>

<p>
2x + 3y ≤ 60
</p>

<p>
Even though the inequality limits the resource, we also need:
</p>

<p>
x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0
</p>

<p>
because x and y represent quantities being produced.
</p>

<h3>Why this matters</h3>

<p>
Without non-negativity restrictions, a graph could include negative
values that have no meaning for the production problem.
</p>
