<h2>Building a Complete Linear Programming Model</h2>

<p>
A complete model contains three main parts:
</p>

<ol>
<li><b>Decision variables</b></li>
<li><b>Constraints</b></li>
<li><b>Objective function</b></li>
</ol>

<p>
We may also include:
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>
A factory makes tables and chairs.
</p>

<p>
Each table uses 3 hours of labour.
Each chair uses 2 hours.
The factory has at most 60 labour hours.
</p>

<p>
The factory can make at most 20 items altogether.
</p>

<p>
Profit is KSh 500 per table and KSh 300 per chair.
The factory wants maximum profit.
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Define variables</h3>

<p>
x = number of tables
</p>

<p>
y = number of chairs
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Labour constraint</h3>

<p>
Tables use 3x hours.
</p>

<p>
Chairs use 2y hours.
</p>

<p>
Total labour = 3x + 2y.
</p>

<p>
At most 60 hours:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + 2y ≤ 60</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Production constraint</h3>

<p>
Total items = x + y.
</p>

<p>
At most 20:
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 20</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Non-negativity</h3>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<h3>Step 5: Objective</h3>

<p>
Profit from tables = 500x.
</p>

<p>
Profit from chairs = 300y.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Maximize P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<h3>Complete model</h3>

<p>
<b>Maximize P = 500x + 300y</b>
</p>

<p>
subject to:
</p>

<p>
<b>3x + 2y ≤ 60</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>x + y ≤ 20</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0</b>
</p>

<p>
This is the mathematical model of the written problem.
', 1),
