  (198, 198, '
<h2>Total Frequency</h2>

<p>
The <b>total frequency</b> is the total number of observations in a
frequency table.
</p>

<p>
To find it, add all the frequencies.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>6</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>5</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
Total frequency:
</p>

<p>
4 + 6 + 5 = <b>15</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the table represents 15 observations.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>6</td>
<td>7</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>7</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>8</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
Total frequency:
</p>

<p>
3 + 7 + 2 + 4 = <b>16</b>
</p>

<h3>Why total frequency matters</h3>

<p>
The total frequency provides a useful check.
If the original data contained 16 observations, the frequencies in the
table should add to 16.
</p>
