<h2>Adding Probabilities of Mutually Exclusive Events</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
When two events are mutually exclusive, they cannot happen in the same trial.
Therefore, the probability of either event happening is found by <b>adding</b>
their probabilities.
</p>

<p>
For mutually exclusive events A and B:
</p>

<p>
<b>P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we add?</h3>

<p>
Suppose a die is rolled.
</p>

<p>
Event A = getting 1<br>
Event B = getting 2
</p>

<p>
The events cannot happen together.
</p>

<p>
Getting 1 uses one possible outcome.
Getting 2 uses another possible outcome.
</p>

<p>
So the favourable outcomes for "1 or 2" are:
</p>

<p>
{1, 2}
</p>

<p>
There are two favourable outcomes out of six.
</p>

<p>
P(1 or 2) = 2/6 = 1/3
</p>

<p>
The same result comes from:
</p>

<p>
P(1) + P(2)
</p>

<p>
= 1/6 + 1/6
</p>

<p>
= 2/6
</p>

<p>
= 1/3
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Getting 2 or 5</h3>

<p>
For a fair die:
</p>

<p>
P(2) = 1/6
</p>

<p>
P(5) = 1/6
</p>

<p>
Since getting 2 and getting 5 are mutually exclusive:
</p>

<p>
P(2 or 5)
= 1/6 + 1/6
= 2/6
= <b>1/3</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Getting an even or odd number</h3>

<p>
For one die:
</p>

<p>
Even = {2, 4, 6}
</p>

<p>
Odd = {1, 3, 5}
</p>

<p>
The two events are mutually exclusive.
</p>

<p>
P(Even) = 3/6 = 1/2
</p>

<p>
P(Odd) = 3/6 = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(Even or Odd)
= 1/2 + 1/2
= <b>1</b>
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
The addition rule is being used because the events <b>cannot overlap</b>.
</p>
