<h2>Putting Probabilities on Tree Branches</h2>

<p>
A tree diagram can show not only what can happen, but also
<b>how likely each result is</b>.
</p>

<p>
The probability written beside a branch tells us the probability
of taking that branch at that stage.
</p>

<h3>The branch-sum rule</h3>

<p>
All branches leaving the same point represent all possible results
at that stage.
Therefore their probabilities must add to 1.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<pre>
       A (0.3)
      /
Start
      \
       B (0.7)
</pre>

<p>
Check:
</p>

<p>
0.3 + 0.7 = 1
</p>

<h3>Example 1: Fair coin</h3>

<p>
A fair coin has two equally likely outcomes:
</p>

<p>
H and T.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(H) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
P(T) = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Check:
</p>

<p>
1/2 + 1/2 = 1
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Bag of balls</h3>

<p>
A bag contains 3 red balls and 2 blue balls.
</p>

<p>
First find the total:
</p>

<p>
3 + 2 = <b>5 balls</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(R) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
P(B) = 2/5
</p>

<p>
Check:
</p>

<p>
3/5 + 2/5 = 5/5 = 1
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Three colours</h3>

<p>
A box contains:
</p>

<ul>
<li>5 white counters</li>
<li>3 black counters</li>
<li>2 red counters</li>
</ul>

<p>
Total counters:
</p>

<p>
5 + 3 + 2 = <b>10</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(W) = 5/10 = 1/2
</p>

<p>
P(B) = 3/10
</p>

<p>
P(R) = 2/10 = 1/5
</p>

<p>
Check:
</p>

<p>
1/2 + 3/10 + 1/5
</p>

<p>
= 5/10 + 3/10 + 2/10
</p>

<p>
= 10/10
</p>

<p>
= <b>1</b>
</p>

<h3>Why do we check the sum?</h3>

<p>
Because the branches must account for every possible result.
If their probabilities do not add to 1, then something is missing
or incorrectly calculated.
</p>
