<h2>Tree Diagrams Without Replacement</h2>

<p>
<b>Without replacement</b> means that after the first selection,
the selected object is <b>not</b> returned to the collection.
</p>

<p>
This changes the contents of the bag before the second selection.
Therefore the second-stage probabilities may change.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A bag contains:
</p>

<ul>
<li>3 red balls</li>
<li>2 blue balls</li>
</ul>

<p>
Total:
</p>

<p>
3 + 2 = <b>5</b>
</p>

<h3>Suppose the first ball is red</h3>

<p>
First:
</p>

<p>
P(R) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
One red ball is removed.
</p>

<p>
The bag now contains:
</p>

<p>
2 red and 2 blue.
</p>

<p>
Total:
</p>

<p>
2 + 2 = <b>4</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(R on second draw) = 2/4 = 1/2
</p>

<p>
P(B on second draw) = 2/4 = 1/2
</p>

<p>
Notice that the second probabilities are different from the original
probabilities.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1: RR</h3>

<p>
First red:
</p>

<p>
P(R) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
After removing red:
</p>

<p>
2 red remain out of 4.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(R) = 2/4
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
P(RR) = 3/5 × 2/4
</p>

<p>
= 6/20
</p>

<p>
= <b>3/10</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2: RB</h3>

<p>
First red:
</p>

<p>
P(R) = 3/5
</p>

<p>
After removing red:
</p>

<p>
2 red and 2 blue remain.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(B) = 2/4
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
P(RB) = 3/5 × 2/4
</p>

<p>
= 6/20
</p>

<p>
= <b>3/10</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3: BB</h3>

<p>
First blue:
</p>

<p>
P(B) = 2/5
</p>

<p>
After removing blue:
</p>

<p>
3 red and 1 blue remain.
</p>

<p>
There are 4 balls.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
P(B) = 1/4
</p>

<p>
So:
</p>

<p>
P(BB) = 2/5 × 1/4
</p>

<p>
= 2/20
</p>

<p>
= <b>1/10</b>
</p>

<h3>Compare the two situations</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>With replacement</th>
<th>Without replacement</th>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>First object returned?</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Contents restored?</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td>Can second probability change?</td>
<td>No, in this setup</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
The most important question to ask is:
</p>

<p>
<b>“Was the first object put back?”</b>
</p>

<p>
If yes, use the restored contents for the second stage.
If no, update the contents before calculating the second probability.
</p>
