<h2>Vector Notation</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A vector can be named using a letter with an arrow above it,
such as:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗a</b>
</p>

<p>
It can also be named using two points:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b>
</p>

<p>
The notation <b>⃗AB</b> means the vector that starts at A
and ends at B.
</p>

<p>
The order of the letters matters.
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b> means A → B.
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗BA</b> means B → A.
</p>

<p>
These point in opposite directions.
</p>

<h3>WHY THE ORDER MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>A ─────────→ B</b>
</p>

<p>
The correct notation is:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗AB</b>
</p>

<p>
If we write:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗BA</b>
</p>

<p>
we have reversed the direction.
</p>

<h3>LETTER NOTATION</h3>

<p>
Sometimes a vector is given a single letter, for example:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗a</b>
</p>

<p>
This is useful when performing operations such as:
</p>

<p>
<b>⃗a + ⃗b</b>
</p>

<p>
The letter is simply a name for the vector.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
A vector moves from P to Q.
Write its vector notation.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the starting point.</p>
<p>
The vector starts at P.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the ending point.</p>
<p>
The vector ends at Q.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write the letters in start-to-end order.</p>

<p>
<b>Answer: ⃗PQ</b>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
A vector is represented by:
</p>

<p>
<b>R ←────── S</b>
</p>

<p>
Write its vector notation.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The arrow points toward R.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Therefore the movement is from S to R.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Write start point first.</p>

<p>
<b>Answer: ⃗SR</b>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
If a vector is called <b>⃗a</b>, can we tell its direction simply
from the letter a?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The letter a is only a name.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The actual direction must come from its diagram,
description, or other information.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
No. The letter <b>⃗a</b> names the vector but does not by itself
tell us which way it points.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
For two-point notation:
</p>

<p>
<b>First letter = starting point.</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Second letter = ending point.</b>
</p>
