<h2>Scalar vs Vector Quantities</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
The key difference between a scalar and a vector is whether
<b>direction is required</b>.
</p>

<p>
<b>Scalar:</b> magnitude only.
</p>

<p>
<b>Vector:</b> magnitude and direction.
</p>

<table>
<tr>
  <th>Quantity</th>
  <th>Type</th>
  <th>Why?</th>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Mass</td>
  <td>Scalar</td>
  <td>No direction is needed.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Time</td>
  <td>Scalar</td>
  <td>No direction is needed.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Distance</td>
  <td>Scalar</td>
  <td>It tells how much ground was covered.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Speed</td>
  <td>Scalar</td>
  <td>It tells how fast something moves.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Displacement</td>
  <td>Vector</td>
  <td>It requires magnitude and direction.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Velocity</td>
  <td>Vector</td>
  <td>It requires magnitude and direction.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Force</td>
  <td>Vector</td>
  <td>A force acts in a particular direction.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Acceleration</td>
  <td>Vector</td>
  <td>It has magnitude and direction.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>THE MOST IMPORTANT TEST</h3>

<p>
When you are unsure whether a quantity is scalar or vector,
do not guess from the name.
</p>

<p>
Ask:
</p>

<p>
<b>
"If I give only the numerical size, have I completely described
the quantity?"
</b>
</p>

<p>
If yes → scalar.
</p>

<p>
If no, because direction is also needed → vector.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
Classify <b>50 km</b> of road travelled as scalar or vector.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the quantity.</p>
<p>
The statement describes distance travelled.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Ask whether direction is required.</p>
<p>
Distance tells us how much ground was covered.
It does not require a direction.
</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Distance is a <b>scalar</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
Classify <b>50 km east</b> as scalar or vector if it describes
a displacement.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Identify the magnitude.</p>
<p>
Magnitude = 50 km.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Identify the direction.</p>
<p>
Direction = east.
</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Both magnitude and direction are present.</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Displacement is a <b>vector</b>.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A car travels at 80 km/h north. If we are describing its velocity,
is it scalar or vector?
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnitude = 80 km/h.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Direction = north.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Both magnitude and direction are given.</p>

<p><b>Conclusion:</b></p>
<p>
Velocity is a <b>vector quantity</b>.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
<b>Direction is the deciding factor.</b>
</p>
