<h2>Horizontal and Vertical Components</h2>

<h3>ONE CONCEPT</h3>

<p>
A column vector has two <b>components</b>.
Each component describes movement in one direction.
</p>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-4 \\
6
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
the two components are:
</p>

<p>
<b>Horizontal component = -4</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Vertical component = 6</b>
</p>

<p>
The word <b>component</b> simply means one part of the vector
that describes a particular direction.
</p>

<h3>WHY TWO COMPONENTS?</h3>

<p>
Movement on a two-dimensional coordinate plane can change in two
directions:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>left/right</li>
  <li>up/down</li>
</ul>

<p>
Therefore we need two components to describe the movement.
</p>

<p>
The first component handles horizontal movement.
The second handles vertical movement.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
7 \\
3
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
identify the two components.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Read the top entry.</p>
<p>
Horizontal component = 7.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Read the bottom entry.</p>
<p>
Vertical component = 3.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
Horizontal component = 7<br>
Vertical component = 3
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
-8 \\
5
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
identify the horizontal and vertical components.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> The first entry is -8.</p>

<p>
Therefore horizontal component = -8.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> The second entry is 5.</p>

<p>
Therefore vertical component = 5.
</p>

<p>
The vector therefore describes 8 units left and 5 units up.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</h3>

<p>
A vector moves 9 units left and 4 units down.
Identify its horizontal and vertical components.
</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Left means negative horizontal movement.</p>

<p>
Horizontal component = -9.
</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Down means negative vertical movement.</p>

<p>
Vertical component = -4.
</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
Horizontal component = -9<br>
Vertical component = -4
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\text{horizontal component} \\
\text{vertical component}
\end{pmatrix}
\]
</p>

<p>
The first component tells us what happens left/right.
The second tells us what happens up/down.
