<h2>Meaning of Vector Subtraction</h2>

<p>
<b>Vector subtraction</b> means finding the movement that remains when one
vector is taken away from another vector.
</p>

<p>
For vectors, subtraction is closely connected to direction.
</p>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = 7 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
and:
</p>

<p>
<b>b = 3 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
Then:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = 4 units right</b>
</p>

<p>
because 3 units of the 7-unit movement are removed.
</p>

<h3>Why direction matters</h3>

<p>
Vectors are not just numbers.
They have direction.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, when subtracting vectors, we must subtract their corresponding
components while keeping their signs.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
A vector represents 8 units to the right.
Another vector represents 3 units to the right.
</p>

<p>
Subtract the second from the first:
</p>

<p>
8 - 3 = 5
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Result = 5 units right.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A vector represents 9 units upward.
Another represents 4 units upward.
</p>

<p>
9 - 4 = 5
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>Result = 5 units upward.</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider the vectors:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (7, 5)</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>b = (2, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Subtract corresponding components:
</p>

<p>
Horizontal:
</p>

<p>
7 - 2 = 5
</p>

<p>
Vertical:
</p>

<p>
5 - 3 = 2
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>a - b = (5, 2)</b>
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Vector subtraction is performed component by component.
</p>

<p>
The first component is subtracted from the first component,
and the second component from the second component.
</p>
', 1),
