  (343, 343, '
<h2>Recognising a Scalar Multiple</h2>

<h3>What is a scalar multiple?</h3>

<p>
A vector is a <b>scalar multiple</b> of another vector if it can be obtained by multiplying the whole vector by one number.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>a = (2, 3)</b>
</p>

<p>
Multiplying a by 2 gives:
</p>

<p>
<b>2a = (4, 6)</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore <b>(4, 6)</b> is a scalar multiple of <b>(2, 3)</b>.
</p>

<h3>What must happen?</h3>

<p>
The <b>same scalar</b> must multiply every component.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>(2, 3) → (6, 9)</b>
</p>

<p>
Both components were multiplied by 3:
</p>

<p>
<b>2 × 3 = 6</b>
<br>
<b>3 × 3 = 9</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(6, 9) = 3(2, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Is <b>(8, 12)</b> a scalar multiple of <b>(2, 3)</b>?
</p>

<p>
Compare the corresponding components:
</p>

<p>
<b>8 ÷ 2 = 4</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>12 ÷ 3 = 4</b>
</p>

<p>
The same number, 4, was used for both components.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(8, 12) = 4(2, 3)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Is <b>(-6, 10)</b> a scalar multiple of <b>(-3, 5)</b>?
</p>

<p>
<b>-6 ÷ -3 = 2</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>10 ÷ 5 = 2</b>
</p>

<p>
The same scalar is obtained.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
<b>(-6, 10) = 2(-3, 5)</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Is <b>(6, 8)</b> a scalar multiple of <b>(2, 3)</b>?
</p>

<p>
<b>6 ÷ 2 = 3</b>
</p>

<p>
But:
</p>

<p>
<b>8 ÷ 3 ≠ 3</b>
</p>

<p>
The same scalar does not work for both components.
</p>

<p>
Therefore <b>(6, 8)</b> is <b>not</b> a scalar multiple of <b>(2, 3)</b>.
</p>

<h3>Key rule</h3>

<p>
To recognise a scalar multiple, the <b>same number must multiply every component</b>.
</p>
