  <h2>Structures Shared by Plant and Animal Cells</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    Plant and animal cells are different, but they also share several
    important structures.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both types of cells are eukaryotic cells and commonly contain:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>cell membrane</b></li>
    <li><b>cytoplasm</b></li>
    <li><b>nucleus</b></li>
    <li><b>mitochondria</b></li>
    <li><b>ribosomes</b></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Do They Share These Structures?</h3>

  <p>
    Plant and animal cells are both living cells.
    They therefore need structures that perform fundamental cellular
    functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Cell membrane</b>
    → controls movement of substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Cytoplasm</b>
    → provides an environment for many chemical reactions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Nucleus</b>
    → contains genetic material.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Mitochondria</b>
    → carry out important stages of aerobic respiration.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Ribosomes</b>
    → make proteins.
  </p>

  <h3>Important Distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Saying that plant and animal cells share these structures does not mean
    that the cells are identical.
  </p>

  <p>
    Plant cells also have structures such as the cell wall, chloroplasts and
    a large permanent vacuole that are not characteristic of typical animal
    cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why do both plant and animal cells need mitochondria?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Both plant and animal cells require usable energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Mitochondria are sites of important stages of aerobic
    respiration.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Both types of cells can contain mitochondria because
    both require energy for cellular processes.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Plant and animal cells share structures required for fundamental
    cellular functions.</b>
  </p>
