  <h2>What Is Cell Specialisation?</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Cell specialisation</b> is the process by which cells develop
    structures and characteristics that make them suited to particular
    functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a multicellular organism, cells do not all need to perform the same job.
    Different cells become adapted for different roles.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Is Specialisation Necessary?</h3>

  <p>
    A large multicellular organism has many different requirements.
    It needs cells for transport, movement, communication, absorption,
    protection, reproduction and many other functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Specialised cells allow the organism to divide these jobs among different
    groups of cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <p>
    A <b>red blood cell</b> is specialised for transporting oxygen.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <b>root hair cell</b> is specialised for absorbing water and mineral ions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <b>nerve cell</b> is specialised for transmitting electrical signals
    through the nervous system.
  </p>

  <h3>Specialisation Means Adaptation for a Job</h3>

  <p>
    The important question is not simply:
    <b>"What does this cell look like?"</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Ask:
    <b>"How does its structure help it perform its function?"</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why is cell specialisation useful in a multicellular
    organism?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> A multicellular organism needs many different functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Different cells can develop different structures.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 3:</b> These structures make the cells effective at particular jobs.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Specialisation allows division of labour between cells.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Specialised cells have structures adapted to perform particular functions.</b>
  </p>
