<h2>Levels of Organisation</h2>

<h3>How is a multicellular organism organised?</h3>

<p>
Multicellular organisms are organised into levels. Specialised cells
work together to form tissues, tissues form organs, and organs can
work together as organ systems.
</p>

<p>
The sequence is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Cell → Tissue → Organ → Organ System → Organism</b>
</p>

<h3>Why does this organisation matter?</h3>

<p>
Each level builds on the one before it. This allows a large organism
to divide complex tasks among specialised structures.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Arrange these structures from simplest to most
complex: heart, muscle cell, circulatory system, muscle tissue.</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> A muscle cell is one specialised cell.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> Similar specialised cells form muscle tissue.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Different tissues form the heart.</p>

<p><b>Step 4:</b> The heart works with other organs in the circulatory
system.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b></p>

<p>
Muscle cell → Muscle tissue → Heart → Circulatory system
</p>
