<h2>Organ Specialisation</h2>

<h3>What does organ specialisation mean?</h3>

<p>
An organ is specialised to perform a particular major function.
Different tissues within the organ contribute to that function.
</p>

<h3>Example: the leaf</h3>

<p>
A leaf is a plant organ specialised mainly for photosynthesis.
It contains several tissues that contribute to this function.
</p>

<p>
For example, palisade tissue contains many chloroplasts, while
vascular tissue transports substances through the leaf.
</p>

<h3>Why do organs contain different tissues?</h3>

<p>
A complex function usually requires several different tasks.
Different tissues can perform these tasks and work together.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why is a leaf classified as an organ?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> A leaf contains different types of tissue.</p>

<p><b>Step 2:</b> These tissues perform different roles.</p>

<p><b>Step 3:</b> Together they allow the leaf to carry out its main
functions, including photosynthesis and transport.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> A leaf is an organ because different tissues work
together within it to perform its functions.</p>
