<h2>Why Mitosis Produces Genetically Similar Cells</h2>

<h3>What happens to genetic material?</h3>

<p>
Before mitosis, the cell copies its DNA. This creates two copies of each chromosome
so that genetic material can be distributed to the daughter nuclei.
</p>

<p>
During mitosis, sister chromatids are separated so that each new nucleus receives
one copy of each chromosome.
</p>

<h3>Why is this important?</h3>

<p>
The daughter cells therefore normally receive the same chromosome number and
equivalent genetic information as the original cell.
</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>

<p><b>Question:</b> Why must chromosomes be copied before mitosis?</p>

<p><b>Step 1:</b> Each daughter cell needs a complete set of genetic information.</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> The original DNA must therefore be copied.</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> The copied chromosomes can then be separated during mitosis.</p>

<p><b>Answer:</b> DNA is copied so each daughter cell can receive a complete set of
genetic information.</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
“Genetically similar” does not mean that every daughter cell will always remain
identical in appearance or function. Cells can later become specialised through
differential gene expression.
</p>
