  <p>
    Suppose a characteristic is controlled by two alleles:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b> = dominant allele
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b> = recessive allele
  </p>

  <p>
    We could cross:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    This is a monohybrid cross because we are following one gene with two
    alleles.
  </p>

  <h3>What are we trying to find?</h3>

  <p>
    A monohybrid cross can help us determine the possible:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>genotypes of offspring</li>
    <li>phenotypes of offspring</li>
    <li>genotypic ratios</li>
    <li>phenotypic ratios</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Monohybrid cross = inheritance of one characteristic at a time.</b>
  </p>
