  <h2>Why Alleles Separate During Meiosis</h2>

  <h3>Start with a diploid cell</h3>

  <p>
    A diploid individual has two copies of an autosomal gene, with one
    inherited from each parent.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a simple example, consider genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The two alleles are located at corresponding positions on a pair of
    homologous chromosomes.
  </p>

  <h3>What happens in meiosis?</h3>

  <p>
    During meiosis I, homologous chromosomes separate.
  </p>

  <p>
    One homologous chromosome carries <b>A</b> and the other carries <b>a</b>
    in this simplified example.
  </p>

  <p>
    As the homologous chromosomes separate, the alleles are separated into
    different cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Result</h3>

  <p>
    The gametes receive one allele:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    or
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>a</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>

  <p>
    This is why a parent with genotype Aa can pass either A or a to an
    offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    The alleles do not randomly disappear.
  </p>

  <p>
    The homologous chromosomes carrying them are separated during meiosis.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Meiosis reduces the chromosome number and separates homologous
    chromosomes, causing the two alleles of a gene to enter different
    gametes.</b>
  </p>
