  (811, 811, '
  <h2>Test Cross</h2>

  <h3>What problem does a test cross solve?</h3>

  <p>
    An individual showing a dominant phenotype can have either:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    or
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Looking at the phenotype alone cannot distinguish between these two
    genotypes in a simple dominant-recessive model.
  </p>

  <h3>The solution</h3>

  <p>
    The individual with the unknown genotype is crossed with a
    <b>homozygous recessive</b> individual.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is called a <b>test cross</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Why use aa?</h3>

  <p>
    The aa parent can contribute only the recessive allele <b>a</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the offspring results reveal which allele the unknown parent
    contributed.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose the unknown parent has a dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cross it with:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    If the unknown parent is <b>AA</b>:
  </p>

  <p>
    AA × aa → all <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    If the unknown parent is <b>Aa</b>:
  </p>

  <p>
    Aa × aa → <b>Aa</b> and <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    <b>A test cross uses a homozygous recessive individual to help determine
    the genotype of an individual showing a dominant phenotype.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (812, 812, '
  <h2>Interpreting a Test Cross</h2>

  <h3>The unknown parent</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose an organism shows the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its genotype could be:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA</b> or <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    We cross it with:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Possible result 1: all dominant phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose all observed offspring have the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the unknown parent were AA:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>AA × aa → all Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Every offspring would show the dominant phenotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Possible result 2: recessive offspring appear</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose some offspring show the recessive phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    The unknown parent must have contributed a recessive allele.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the unknown parent must be:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Because:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Aa × aa → Aa or aa</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Key inference</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Recessive offspring in a test cross prove that the dominant-phenotype
    parent carries the recessive allele.</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Important caution</h3>

  <p>
    In a real experiment, observing only a small number of offspring does not
    make probability an absolute guarantee. Larger samples give stronger
    evidence.
  </p>
