  (818, 818, '
  <h2>Codominance</h2>

  <p>
    <b>Codominance</b> occurs when two different alleles are both expressed
    clearly in a heterozygous individual.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is different from incomplete dominance.
  </p>

  <h3>Incomplete dominance</h3>

  <p>
    The heterozygote has an intermediate phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example:
    <b>RW = pink</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Codominance</h3>

  <p>
    Both alleles contribute to the phenotype at the same time.
  </p>

  <p>
    A classic human example is the ABO blood group system.
  </p>

  <p>
    The alleles <b>I<sup>A</sup></b> and <b>I<sup>B</sup></b> are codominant.
    When a person has both alleles, both are expressed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup></b> produces blood group AB.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not call AB a blend</h3>

  <p>
    Group AB is not an intermediate between A and B. Both A and B antigens are
    expressed on the red blood cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    In codominance, both alleles are expressed in the heterozygote.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (819, 819, '
  <h2>Solving a Codominance Cross</h2>

  <p>
    Codominance crosses are solved by separating the alleles into gametes,
    combining them, and then interpreting the resulting genotype.
  </p>

  <h3>ABO blood group example</h3>

  <p>
    The three main alleles are:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>I<sup>A</sup></b></li>
    <li><b>I<sup>B</sup></b></li>
    <li><b>i</b></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup></b> and <b>I<sup>B</sup></b> are codominant.
    Both are dominant over <b>i</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Cross</h3>

  <p>
    Consider:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup>i × I<sup>B</sup>i</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    The first parent produces:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>A</sup></b> or <b>i</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The second parent produces:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>I<sup>B</sup></b> or <b>i</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Combine them</h3>

  <p>
    Possible offspring:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup></li>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>i</li>
    <li>I<sup>B</sup>i</li>
    <li>ii</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 3: Determine the blood groups</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup> = AB</li>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup>i = A</li>
    <li>I<sup>B</sup>i = B</li>
    <li>ii = O</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Therefore all four blood groups are possible in this particular cross.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (820, 820, '
  <h2>Incomplete Dominance Compared with Codominance</h2>

  <p>
    Incomplete dominance and codominance are often confused because both differ
    from simple complete dominance.
  </p>

  <h3>Incomplete dominance</h3>

  <p>
    The heterozygote has an intermediate phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example:
  </p>

  <p>
    RR = red
  </p>

  <p>
    WW = white
  </p>

  <p>
    RW = pink
  </p>

  <p>
    The heterozygote does not show either parental phenotype exactly.
  </p>

  <h3>Codominance</h3>

  <p>
    Both alleles are expressed in the heterozygote.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example:
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>A</sup> = A
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>B</sup>I<sup>B</sup> = B
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>A</sup>I<sup>B</sup> = AB
  </p>

  <p>
    In AB blood, both A and B antigens are expressed.
  </p>

  <h3>Quick comparison</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Incomplete dominance:</b> heterozygote shows an intermediate phenotype.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Codominance:</b> heterozygote expresses both allele effects.
  </p>

  <p>
    Neither pattern means that the DNA alleles physically merge into a single
    allele.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (821, 821, '
  <h2>Multiple Alleles</h2>

  <p>
    A gene can sometimes exist in more than two alternative forms in a
    population. These alternative forms are called <b>alleles</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    When a gene has more than two possible alleles in the population, it is
    described as having <b>multiple alleles</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    An individual still receives only two alleles for a particular gene:
    one allele from each parent.
  </p>

  <p>
    Multiple alleles means that the population has more than two possible
    versions of the gene.
  </p>

  <h3>ABO blood group example</h3>

  <p>
    The ABO blood group system has three alleles:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>I<sup>A</sup></li>
    <li>I<sup>B</sup></li>
    <li>i</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A person receives two of these alleles.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <p>
    I<sup>A</sup>i
  </p>

  <p>
    The person has two alleles, not three.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    Multiple alleles describe the possible alleles in the population, not the
    number of alleles carried by one individual.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (822, 822, '
  <h2>The Rhesus Factor</h2>

  <p>
    The <b>Rhesus factor</b>, commonly called the <b>Rh factor</b>, is another
    characteristic of human blood.
  </p>

  <p>
    A person is described as <b>Rhesus positive (Rh+)</b> if the relevant
    Rhesus antigen is present on the surface of their red blood cells.
  </p>

  <p>
    A person is described as <b>Rhesus negative (Rh−)</b> if that antigen is
    absent.
  </p>

  <h3>Simple genetic model</h3>

  <p>
    For basic inheritance problems, we can represent the Rh-positive allele as
    <b>R</b> and the Rh-negative allele as <b>r</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    In this simplified model:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>RR</b> = Rh+</li>
    <li><b>Rr</b> = Rh+</li>
    <li><b>rr</b> = Rh−</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Therefore, Rh+ is dominant over Rh− in this simplified model.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    The ABO blood group and the Rhesus factor are separate blood characteristics.
    A person''s complete blood type can therefore include both.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A positive</b> means ABO group A and Rh positive.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>O negative</b> means ABO group O and Rh negative.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (823, 823, '
  <h2>Rhesus Factor Genotypes</h2>

  <p>
    In the simplified inheritance model, the Rh-positive allele is represented
    by <b>R</b> and the Rh-negative allele by <b>r</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Rh positive</h3>

  <p>
    A person can be Rh positive with either:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>RR</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    or
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Rr</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Both genotypes contain at least one dominant R allele.
  </p>

  <h3>Rh negative</h3>

  <p>
    A person is Rh negative when the genotype is:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>rr</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Both alleles are recessive, so no dominant R allele is present.
  </p>

  <h3>Working backwards</h3>

  <p>
    If a person is known to be Rh negative, their genotype is certain in this
    model: <b>rr</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If a person is Rh positive, their genotype could be <b>RR</b> or <b>Rr</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, knowing the phenotype does not always tell us the exact genotype.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (824, 824, '
  <h2>Rhesus Factor Cross: RR × rr</h2>

  <p>
    Consider a cross between an Rh-positive parent with genotype <b>RR</b>
    and an Rh-negative parent with genotype <b>rr</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Write the parental genotypes</h3>

  <p>
    <b>RR × rr</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Find the gametes</h3>

  <p>
    The RR parent can produce only <b>R</b> gametes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rr parent can produce only <b>r</b> gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Combine the alleles</h3>

  <p>
    Every offspring receives R from the first parent and r from the second.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore every offspring has:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Rr</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Determine the phenotype</h3>

  <p>
    Rr is Rh positive in the simplified model.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>100% of the offspring are predicted to be Rh positive.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (825, 825, '
  <h2>Determining an Unknown Rhesus Genotype</h2>

  <p>
    An Rh-positive person can have genotype <b>RR</b> or <b>Rr</b>.
    Therefore, knowing that someone is Rh positive does not reveal their exact
    genotype.
  </p>

  <h3>Use offspring evidence</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose an Rh-positive parent has an Rh-negative child.
  </p>

  <p>
    An Rh-negative child must have genotype:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>rr</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    The child must therefore have received an <b>r</b> allele from each parent.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the Rh-positive parent contributed r, that parent must carry r.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore the Rh-positive parent must be:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Rr</b>
  </p>

  <h3>Reasoning chain</h3>

  <p>
    Rh-negative child → rr → receives r from both parents → Rh-positive parent
    supplied r → parent is Rr.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is an example of using offspring genotype to infer a parent''s genotype.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (826, 826, '
  <h2>ABO Red Cell Compatibility</h2>

  <p>
    For red-cell transfusion, compatibility depends on the antigens carried by
    the donor''s red blood cells and the antibodies present in the recipient''s
    plasma.
  </p>

  <h3>Group O red cells</h3>

  <p>
    Group O red cells have neither A nor B antigen. Therefore they do not
    introduce A or B antigen to the recipient.
  </p>

  <h3>Group AB red cells</h3>

  <p>
    Group AB red cells carry both A and B antigens.
  </p>

  <p>
    A recipient whose plasma contains antibodies against A or B can react to
    those donor red-cell antigens.
  </p>

  <h3>Basic ABO red-cell rule</h3>

  <p>
    A recipient should not receive donor red cells carrying an ABO antigen
    against which the recipient has the corresponding antibody.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    A group B recipient has anti-A antibodies.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, giving group A red cells would introduce A antigen that can react
    with those anti-A antibodies.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is why blood compatibility must be checked before transfusion.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (827, 827, '
  <h2>How Advantageous Alleles Become More Common</h2>

  <p>
    Natural selection can change the frequency of alleles in a population over
    generations.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose a population contains two alleles for a characteristic:
    <b>A</b> and <b>a</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Suppose individuals with allele A have an inherited characteristic that
    increases survival in a particular environment.
  </p>

  <p>
    These individuals may survive and reproduce more successfully.
  </p>

  <p>
    Their offspring can inherit allele A.
  </p>

  <p>
    Over many generations, allele A may therefore become more common in the
    population.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    The allele does not become more common because individual organisms
    intentionally increase it.
  </p>

  <p>
    It becomes more common because individuals carrying it leave more
    surviving offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Population change</h3>

  <p>
    Natural selection acts on individuals, but its long-term result is a change
    in the genetic composition of the population.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (828, 828, '
  <h2>What Fossils Can Reveal</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Fossils can reveal information about organisms that lived in the past and
  allow scientists to compare organisms from different periods.
  </p>

  <h3>Comparing fossils</h3>

  <p>
  Suppose fossils from an older rock layer have a particular bone structure,
  while fossils from a younger layer have a modified version of the same
  structure.
  </p>

  <p>
  If the organisms are related and the layers have been correctly placed in
  chronological order, the differences can provide evidence of change over
  time.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  Fossil A has a small molar with a broad surface.
  </p>

  <p>
  Fossil B, found in a younger layer, has a larger molar with a different
  surface structure.
  </p>

  <p>
  Fossil C, from an even younger layer, has a still more modified molar.
  </p>

  <p>
  The sequence does not mean that Fossil A suddenly transformed into Fossil B.
  Instead, it provides evidence that characteristics in the lineage changed
  across generations.
  </p>

  <h3>What fossils cannot automatically tell us</h3>

  <p>
  A fossil usually gives only partial information. It may not preserve every
  structure of the organism or every stage between two forms.
  </p>

  <p>
  Therefore, fossils are interpreted together with other evidence.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (829, 829, '
  <h2>Limitations of the Fossil Record</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  The fossil record is valuable evidence for evolutionary change, but it is
  incomplete.
  </p>

  <h3>Why fossils are incomplete</h3>

  <p>
  Most organisms do not become fossils. Soft tissues usually decay, and
  organisms may be destroyed before fossilisation occurs.
  </p>

  <p>
  Fossil formation usually requires particular environmental conditions.
  Hard structures such as bones and shells are more likely to leave fossil
  evidence than soft tissues.
  </p>

  <h3>Missing stages</h3>

  <p>
  Because fossilisation is rare, there may be long gaps between fossils.
  Scientists may therefore not have fossils representing every generation
  in an evolutionary lineage.
  </p>

  <h3>Correct interpretation</h3>

  <p>
  The absence of a particular fossil does <b>not</b> automatically prove that
  the organism or intermediate form never existed.
  </p>

  <p>
  It may simply mean that suitable fossilisation conditions did not occur or
  that the fossil has not yet been discovered.
  </p>

  <h3>Why fossils remain useful</h3>

  <p>
  Even though incomplete, fossil evidence can still reveal major changes in
  organisms and the order in which those changes occurred.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (830, 830, '
  <h2>Homologous Structures</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Homologous structures</b> are structures found in different organisms
  that have a similar underlying structural plan because they were inherited
  from a common ancestor, even though they may perform different functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  The forelimbs of humans, bats and whales contain corresponding bones.
  </p>

  <p>
  A human forelimb is adapted for handling objects.
  A bat forelimb is adapted for flight.
  A whale forelimb is adapted for movement through water.
  </p>

  <p>
  Their functions differ, but their underlying arrangement of bones shows
  important similarities.
  </p>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>

  <p>
  If organisms have structures with similar underlying plans but different
  functions, one explanation is that the structures were inherited from a
  common ancestor and modified for different environments or functions.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
  Homologous structures are identified mainly by their <b>underlying
  structural similarity</b>, not simply because they perform the same
  function.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (831, 831, '
  <h2>Vestigial Structures</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  A <b>vestigial structure</b> is a reduced structure that has little or no
  significant function in its present form compared with the corresponding
  structure in ancestral organisms or related organisms.
  </p>

  <h3>How vestigial structures arise</h3>

  <p>
  An inherited structure may become less useful when the environment or way
  of life changes.
  </p>

  <p>
  If individuals with reduced versions of the structure have no major
  disadvantage, natural selection may not maintain the structure in its
  original form.
  </p>

  <p>
  Over many generations, the structure may become reduced.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  The human appendix is commonly discussed as a reduced structure. It should
  not be described simply as completely useless; it has biological functions,
  but it is greatly reduced compared with the large caecum used in digestion
  by some herbivorous mammals.
  </p>

  <h3>Evolutionary significance</h3>

  <p>
  Vestigial structures can provide evidence of evolutionary history because
  they may represent modified remnants of structures that were more important
  in ancestors.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (832, 832, '
  <h2>Comparing Homologous and Vestigial Structures</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Homologous and vestigial structures can both provide evidence about
  evolutionary relationships, but they are not the same concept.
  </p>

  <h3>Homologous structures</h3>

  <p>
  These have a similar underlying structural plan in different organisms.
  Their functions may differ.
  </p>

  <p>
  Example: the forelimbs of humans, bats and whales.
  </p>

  <h3>Vestigial structures</h3>

  <p>
  These are reduced structures that have little or no significant function
  in their present form compared with the corresponding ancestral structure.
  </p>

  <p>
  Example: the human appendix compared with the much larger caecum of some
  herbivorous mammals.
  </p>

  <h3>The key difference</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Homologous</b> focuses on a shared underlying structural plan among
  different organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Vestigial</b> focuses on reduction of a structure compared with its
  ancestral or corresponding form.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not confuse them</h3>

  <p>
  A structure does not become homologous simply because it is small.
  Likewise, a structure is not vestigial simply because different organisms
  use it for different purposes.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (833, 833, '
<h2>Genetic Diversity</h2>

<p>
<b>Genetic diversity</b> is the variation in inherited characteristics among individuals of the same species.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider a population of maize plants.
</p>

<p>
Some plants may produce larger cobs, while others produce smaller cobs. Some may have greater resistance to a particular disease, while others may be more susceptible.
</p>

<p>
Some of these differences can result from differences in their genes.
</p>

<h3>Why genetic diversity matters</h3>

<p>
A population with genetic variation is more likely to contain individuals with characteristics that may help them survive when environmental conditions change.
</p>

<p>
For example, if a disease affects a crop, individuals with inherited resistance may survive and reproduce more successfully.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
Genetic diversity is different from environmental variation.
</p>

<p>
A difference caused only by the environment is not necessarily an inherited genetic difference.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, when identifying genetic diversity, focus on <b>inherited differences</b>.
</p>
', 1),
  (834, 834, '
  <h2>Variation in Sexual Reproduction</h2>

  <h3>What is variation?</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Variation</b> means differences in characteristics between individuals
    of the same species.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, people can differ in height, blood group, natural eye colour,
    hair characteristics and many other features.
  </p>

  <h3>Why does sexual reproduction produce variation?</h3>

  <p>
    Sexual reproduction involves two parents.
    Each parent contributes a gamete to the offspring.
  </p>

  <p>
    The sperm contributes one set of chromosomes and the egg contributes
    another set.
  </p>

  <p>
    The combination formed at fertilisation is therefore different from the
    combination found in either parent.
  </p>

  <h3>Step-by-step</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Meiosis produces haploid gametes.</li>
    <li>Different gametes can contain different combinations of genetic information.</li>
    <li>A sperm and an egg meet during fertilisation.</li>
    <li>Their genetic information is combined.</li>
    <li>A genetically unique combination is formed in the zygote.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>

  <p>
    Imagine a mother produces egg A and egg B, while a father produces
    sperm X and sperm Y.
  </p>

  <p>
    Possible fertilisations include:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>A + X</b><br>
    <b>A + Y</b><br>
    <b>B + X</b><br>
    <b>B + Y</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    These combinations are not necessarily genetically identical.
    Therefore, different fertilisation events can produce offspring with
    different genetic combinations.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    Variation does not mean that every individual has completely different
    genes. Individuals of the same species share many genes, but they can
    possess different versions of genes and different combinations of genetic
    information.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (835, 835, '
  <h2>Genetic Variation from Fertilisation</h2>

  <h3>What happens during fertilisation?</h3>

  <p>
    Fertilisation occurs when a male gamete and a female gamete fuse.
  </p>

  <p>
    In humans, this means a haploid sperm cell combines with a haploid egg
    cell to form a diploid zygote.
  </p>

  <h3>Why does this create variation?</h3>

  <p>
    A large number of sperm cells can be produced, and they do not all carry
    exactly the same genetic combination.
  </p>

  <p>
    Egg cells can also differ genetically.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the particular sperm that fertilises the particular egg affects
    the genetic combination of the resulting zygote.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose three sperm cells have combinations:
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Sperm 1 = AB</b><br>
    <b>Sperm 2 = Ab</b><br>
    <b>Sperm 3 = aB</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Suppose an egg carries <b>ab</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If Sperm 1 fertilises the egg, the combination is different from the
    combination produced if Sperm 2 fertilises the same egg.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the identity of the sperm involved in fertilisation affects
    the genetic combination of the offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Fertilisation is random with respect to which sperm and egg meet, so it
    creates another source of genetic variation.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (836, 836, '
  <h2>Meiosis and Genetic Variation</h2>

  <h3>How are meiosis and variation connected?</h3>

  <p>
    Meiosis is not simply a process for reducing chromosome number.
    It also contributes to genetic variation.
  </p>

  <p>
    During meiosis, homologous chromosomes are separated into different cells.
    Their different orientations can result in different combinations of
    chromosomes entering gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Step-by-step connection</h3>

  <ol>
    <li>Homologous chromosomes pair during meiosis I.</li>
    <li>The chromosome pairs line up at the equator.</li>
    <li>The orientation of the pairs can differ.</li>
    <li>Homologous chromosomes separate.</li>
    <li>Different chromosome combinations enter the resulting cells.</li>
    <li>Meiosis ultimately produces haploid gametes.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Then fertilisation adds another source</h3>

  <p>
    The genetic variation produced through meiosis is not the end of the
    process.
  </p>

  <p>
    During fertilisation, a sperm combines with an egg.
  </p>

  <p>
    Different sperm and eggs can therefore combine in different ways.
  </p>

  <h3>The complete idea</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Meiosis creates different combinations in gametes.</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Fertilisation combines genetic information from two gametes.</b>
  </p>

  <p>
    Together, these processes contribute strongly to genetic variation in
    sexually reproducing organisms.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (837, 837, '
  <h2>Genetic Variation and Environmental Variation</h2>

  <h3>Not every difference is caused by genes</h3>

  <p>
    Individuals can differ because of their genes, because of environmental
    conditions, or because of a combination of both.
  </p>

  <h3>Genetic variation</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Genetic variation</b> refers to differences in genetic information
    between individuals.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, differences in blood group are genetically determined.
  </p>

  <h3>Environmental variation</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Environmental variation</b> results from differences in the conditions
    in which organisms develop or live.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, two plants with similar genetic potential can grow to
    different sizes if one receives enough water and mineral nutrients while
    the other does not.
  </p>

  <h3>Characteristics affected by both</h3>

  <p>
    Some characteristics are influenced by both genes and the environment.
  </p>

  <p>
    Human height, for example, is influenced by inherited genetic information
    as well as factors such as nutrition and health during development.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked classification</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Blood group:</b> mainly genetic.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Plant height in different nutrient conditions:</b> environmental
    conditions can affect the observed height.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Human height:</b> influenced by both genetic and environmental factors.
  </p>

  <h3>Remember</h3>

  <p>
    <b>A visible difference between two individuals does not automatically
    mean that their genes are different.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (838, 838, '
  <h2>Inherited Variation and Acquired Differences</h2>

  <h3>What is inherited variation?</h3>

  <p>
    An inherited characteristic is one whose genetic information is passed
    from parents to offspring.
  </p>

  <p>
    Such characteristics can therefore be transmitted through reproduction.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Blood group is an inherited characteristic.
  </p>

  <p>
    A child receives genetic information associated with blood group from
    their parents.
  </p>

  <h3>What is an acquired difference?</h3>

  <p>
    An acquired difference develops during an individual''s lifetime because
    of experiences, environmental conditions, training, injury, diet or
    other factors.
  </p>

  <p>
    An acquired difference is not automatically passed genetically to the
    individual''s offspring.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    If a person develops strong muscles through exercise, the increased muscle
    size is an acquired change.
  </p>

  <p>
    The person does not pass the increased muscle size directly to a child
    through their gametes.
  </p>

  <h3>Important distinction</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Inherited:</b> associated with genetic information passed from parents
    to offspring.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Acquired:</b> develops during an individual''s lifetime and is not
    automatically inherited genetically.
  </p>

  <h3>Exam trap</h3>

  <p>
    Do not assume that every characteristic seen in a parent will be inherited
    by a child.
  </p>

  <p>
    A parent may acquire a characteristic during life without changing the
    genetic information passed to the offspring.
  </p>
