  (847, 847, '
  <h2>Comparing Continuous and Discontinuous Variation</h2>

  <p>
    The easiest way to distinguish continuous and discontinuous variation is
    to ask whether the characteristic forms a range or separate categories.
  </p>

  <h3>Continuous variation</h3>

  <p>
    There are many possible intermediate values.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example: human height.
  </p>

  <h3>Discontinuous variation</h3>

  <p>
    Individuals fall into distinct categories.
  </p>

  <p>
    Example: ABO blood group.
  </p>

  <h3>Comparison</h3>

  <p>
    Height:
  </p>

  <p>
    150 cm → 151 cm → 152 cm → 153 cm → many possible values.
  </p>

  <p>
    Blood group:
  </p>

  <p>
    A | B | AB | O
  </p>

  <p>
    There is no ordinary ABO category halfway between A and B.
  </p>

  <h3>Exam clue</h3>

  <p>
    If a question gives a measurement with many possible intermediate values,
    think <b>continuous variation</b>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If it gives clearly separated categories, think <b>discontinuous variation</b>.
  </p>
