  (865, 865, '
  <h2>Evidence for Evolution Must Be Interpreted</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Scientific evidence does not mean simply finding a similarity and declaring
  that evolution has occurred.
  </p>

  <p>
  Scientists examine evidence, compare explanations and look for patterns
  that are consistent with evolutionary relationships.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: fossils</h3>

  <p>
  A fossil by itself shows that an organism or trace existed in the past.
  A sequence of correctly dated fossils can provide evidence of changes in
  characteristics over time.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: homologous structures</h3>

  <p>
  Similar underlying structures in different organisms can support the
  explanation that they inherited the structures from a common ancestor.
  </p>

  <h3>Example: vestigial structures</h3>

  <p>
  Reduced structures can support the idea that present organisms retain
  modified remnants of ancestral structures.
  </p>

  <h3>Strong scientific reasoning</h3>

  <p>
  Evidence → observation → comparison → explanation.
  </p>

  <p>
  The strongest evolutionary explanations are supported by multiple
  independent lines of evidence rather than one isolated observation.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (866, 866, '
  <h2>Evolution: Putting the Evidence Together</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Evolutionary explanations become stronger when different types of evidence
  point toward the same conclusion.
  </p>

  <h3>Evidence 1: Fossils</h3>

  <p>
  Fossils provide evidence about organisms that lived in the past and can show
  changes in characteristics through geological time.
  </p>

  <h3>Evidence 2: Homologous structures</h3>

  <p>
  Similar underlying structures in different organisms can support the idea
  of common ancestry.
  </p>

  <h3>Evidence 3: Vestigial structures</h3>

  <p>
  Reduced structures can provide evidence that present organisms retain
  modified remnants of ancestral structures.
  </p>

  <h3>Connecting the evidence</h3>

  <p>
  Suppose fossils show that a group changed over time.
  </p>

  <p>
  Suppose living members of the group also possess corresponding structures
  that share a common underlying plan.
  </p>

  <p>
  Suppose some structures are reduced compared with ancestral forms.
  </p>

  <p>
  These observations can be interpreted together as evidence for evolutionary
  relationships and change over time.
  </p>

  <h3>Important principle</h3>

  <p>
  Evidence supports an explanation; scientists still have to interpret the
  evidence carefully.
  </p>
