  <h2>Yeast as a Fungus</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Yeast</b> is a fungus. Unlike many familiar fungi such as mushrooms,
  yeast is mainly <b>unicellular</b>.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell structure</h3>

  <p>
  A yeast cell is eukaryotic and therefore contains a membrane-bound nucleus.
  It also has a cell wall.
  </p>

  <h3>Nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Yeast obtains organic nutrients from its surroundings.
  It can respire aerobically when oxygen is available.
  </p>

  <p>
  When oxygen is limited, yeast can carry out anaerobic respiration,
  producing ethanol and carbon dioxide.
  </p>

  <h3>Reproduction</h3>

  <p>
  Yeast commonly reproduces asexually by <b>budding</b>.
  A small outgrowth forms on the parent cell, grows and eventually separates.
  </p>

  <h3>Why yeast belongs to Fungi</h3>

  <p>
  Yeast has the fundamental characteristics of fungi even though it does not
  form the large multicellular structures seen in mushrooms.
