  (872, 872, '
<h2>What Is Biodiversity?</h2>

<p>
<b>Biodiversity</b> is the variety of living organisms found in a particular area or on Earth as a whole.
</p>

<p>
It includes the variety of different organisms such as plants, animals, fungi and microorganisms.
</p>

<h3>Three levels of biodiversity</h3>

<p>
Biodiversity can be considered at three related levels:
</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Genetic diversity</b> — differences in genetic characteristics among individuals of the same species.</li>
<li><b>Species diversity</b> — the variety of species in an area.</li>
<li><b>Ecosystem diversity</b> — the variety of ecosystems in an area.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A forest containing many species of trees, insects, birds and fungi has high species diversity.
</p>

<p>
If individuals of the same species also have different inherited characteristics, the area has genetic diversity as well.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Biodiversity is not simply the number of animals in an area. It describes the <b>variety of living organisms and their biological differences</b>.
</p>
