<h2>Why Biodiversity Is Important</h2>

<p>
Biodiversity is important because living organisms are connected through ecosystems and provide many biological resources and functions.
</p>

<h3>Food</h3>

<p>
Humans obtain food from many organisms, including crops, livestock, fish and other organisms.
</p>

<h3>Medicines</h3>

<p>
Plants, microorganisms and other organisms can provide substances that are useful in medicine.
</p>

<h3>Ecosystem processes</h3>

<p>
Organisms contribute to processes such as decomposition, pollination, nutrient cycling and food-web interactions.
</p>

<h3>Genetic resources</h3>

<p>
Genetic diversity can provide characteristics that may be useful in agriculture, such as disease resistance or tolerance to environmental conditions.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
The importance of biodiversity is not limited to individual species. Organisms interact within ecosystems, so losing species can affect other organisms and ecosystem processes.
</p>
