  <h2>What Is Classification?</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  <b>Classification</b> is the process of grouping organisms according to
  their similarities and differences.
  </p>

  <p>
  Scientists classify organisms because there are millions of different
  organisms. Without an organised system, identifying and studying them
  would be extremely difficult.
  </p>

  <h3>Why classification is necessary</h3>

  <p>
  Imagine trying to organise a library containing thousands of books without
  grouping them in any way. Finding one particular book would be difficult.
  </p>

  <p>
  Classification performs a similar job for living organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  Organisms with important similarities can be placed into the same groups.
  Organisms with major differences are placed into different groups.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
  A lion and a domestic cat have many similarities. Both have hair, produce
  milk for their young and possess similar basic body organisation.
  </p>

  <p>
  A lion and a maize plant have much less in common structurally and
  physiologically.
  </p>

  <p>
  Classification therefore helps scientists organise organisms according to
  biological relationships and characteristics.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (875, 875, '
  <h2>Why Organisms Are Classified</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Classification allows scientists to organise biodiversity into meaningful
  groups.
  </p>

  <h3>Identification</h3>

  <p>
  Classification makes it easier to identify an unknown organism by comparing
  its characteristics with organisms that have already been classified.
  </p>

  <h3>Comparison</h3>

  <p>
  Once organisms are placed into groups, scientists can compare their
  structures, functions and other characteristics more easily.
  </p>

  <h3>Communication</h3>

  <p>
  Scientists in different places need a consistent way of referring to the
  same organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  A standard classification and naming system reduces confusion caused by
  different local names.
  </p>

  <h3>Relationships</h3>

  <p>
  Classification can also help scientists communicate information about
  similarities and evolutionary relationships among organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  Therefore, classification is not simply memorising names. It is a system
  for organising and communicating biological information.
  ', 1),
  (876, 876, '
  <h2>Characteristics Used in Classification</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  Scientists do not classify organisms by appearance alone. They examine
  biological characteristics that can be compared systematically.
  </p>

  <h3>Cell structure</h3>

  <p>
  Scientists can consider whether an organism is made of prokaryotic or
  eukaryotic cells and whether the cells possess particular structures.
  </p>

  <h3>Number of cells</h3>

  <p>
  An organism may be unicellular or multicellular.
  </p>

  <h3>Mode of nutrition</h3>

  <p>
  Organisms differ in how they obtain nutrients. Some produce organic food
  through photosynthesis while others obtain nutrients from other organisms
  or organic material.
  </p>

  <h3>Body organisation</h3>

  <p>
  Scientists can compare structures such as tissues, organs and specialised
  systems.
  </p>

  <h3>Reproduction</h3>

  <p>
  Reproductive characteristics can also help distinguish groups of organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
  Good classification therefore uses multiple biological characteristics
  rather than relying on one superficial feature.
  ', 1),
  (877, 877, '
  <h2>Prokaryotic and Eukaryotic Cells in Classification</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  One important characteristic used when comparing organisms is their cell
  type.
  </p>

  <h3>Prokaryotic cells</h3>

  <p>
  Prokaryotic cells do not have a membrane-bound nucleus.
  Their genetic material is located in a region of the cytoplasm rather than
  inside a nucleus.
  </p>

  <p>
  Bacteria are examples of organisms made of prokaryotic cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Eukaryotic cells</h3>

  <p>
  Eukaryotic cells contain a nucleus surrounded by a nuclear membrane.
  They also contain membrane-bound organelles such as mitochondria.
  </p>

  <p>
  Plants, animals, fungi and many protists are made of eukaryotic cells.
  </p>

  <h3>Why this matters in classification</h3>

  <p>
  The difference between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cell organisation is a
  major biological distinction.
  </p>

  <p>
  It provides a much stronger classification characteristic than simply
  comparing an organism''s colour or size.
  </p>
  ', 1),
  (878, 878, '
  <h2>Five-Kingdom Classification</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
  A traditional biological classification system groups organisms into five
  kingdoms:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Monera</li>
    <li>Protoctista</li>
    <li>Fungi</li>
    <li>Plantae</li>
    <li>Animalia</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Why these kingdoms are separated</h3>

  <p>
  The groups differ in important characteristics such as cell organisation,
  number of cells, mode of nutrition and body organisation.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Monera</b> contains prokaryotic organisms such as bacteria.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Protoctista</b> traditionally includes diverse mostly unicellular
  eukaryotic organisms, although this group is not considered a natural
  evolutionary group in modern classification.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Fungi</b> includes organisms such as mushrooms, moulds and yeasts.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Plantae</b> includes plants.
  </p>

  <p>
  <b>Animalia</b> includes animals.
  </p>

  <h3>Important note</h3>

  <p>
  Classification systems have changed as scientific evidence has improved.
  Therefore, students should distinguish between a traditional school
  classification framework and modern evolutionary classification.
