<h2>Species Distribution Patterns</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The individuals of a species are not always spread evenly across a habitat.
Their positions can form different distribution patterns.
</p>

<h3>THREE COMMON PATTERNS</h3>

<p>
<strong>Clumped:</strong> individuals occur in groups or patches.</p>

<p>
<strong>Uniform:</strong> individuals are spaced at relatively similar
distances from one another.</p>

<p>
<strong>Random:</strong> there is no obvious regular spacing or grouping pattern.
</p>

<h3>CLUMPED DISTRIBUTION</h3>

<p>
This is common when resources occur in patches.
For example, plants may grow in groups where water or nutrients are available.
Animals may also cluster around food or shelter.
</p>

<h3>UNIFORM DISTRIBUTION</h3>

<p>
Uniform spacing can result from strong competition or territorial behaviour.
Individuals may maintain a distance from one another.
</p>

<h3>RANDOM DISTRIBUTION</h3>

<p>
Random distribution occurs when the position of one individual does not strongly
determine where another individual occurs.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Distribution pattern describes <strong>where individuals occur</strong>.
It is different from density, which describes <strong>how many individuals
occur per unit area</strong>.
</p>
