<h2>Biomagnification</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<b>Biomagnification</b> is the increase in concentration of certain persistent pollutants in organisms at higher trophic levels of a food chain.
</p>

<p>
It is especially important for pollutants that:
</p>

<ul>
<li>are difficult to break down;</li>
<li>remain in organisms for long periods;</li>
<li>can accumulate in body tissues.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How It Happens</h3>

<p>
A pollutant enters an ecosystem.
</p>

<p>
Small organisms take it up.
</p>

<p>
Larger organisms eat many smaller organisms.
</p>

<p>
The pollutant can therefore become concentrated in organisms higher in the food chain.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p>
<b>water → small aquatic organisms → fish → fish-eating bird</b>
</p>

<p>
If a persistent pollutant enters the water, small organisms may contain small concentrations.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
A fish eats many contaminated organisms.
</p>

<p>
The pollutant can accumulate in the fish.
</p>

<p>
A fish-eating bird may consume many contaminated fish.
</p>

<p>
The pollutant concentration in the bird can therefore become higher.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Organisms at higher trophic levels can therefore be exposed to greater concentrations than organisms lower in the food chain.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Biomagnification is different from simple exposure to a pollutant. The key idea is that concentration increases at higher trophic levels.
</p>
', 1),
  (936, 936, '
<h2>Nitrification</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Nitrification</strong> is the bacterial conversion of ammonia or
ammonium compounds into nitrite and then nitrate.
</p>

<h3>THE TWO MAIN STAGES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Stage 1:</strong> Ammonia/ammonium compounds are converted into nitrites.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Stage 2:</strong> Nitrites are converted into nitrates.
</p>

<p>
Both stages are carried out by specialised soil bacteria.
</p>

<h3>WHY NITRATE MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Nitrate ions are an important source of nitrogen for plants.
Plants absorb nitrate ions from the soil through their roots.
</p>

<p>
The nitrogen can then be incorporated into amino acids, proteins and other
nitrogen-containing compounds.
</p>

<h3>FOLLOWING THE NITROGEN</h3>

<p>
Consider nitrogen-containing waste in the soil.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Organic nitrogen compounds are broken down and nitrogen
can become available as ammonia/ammonium.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Nitrifying bacteria convert ammonia/ammonium into nitrite.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Other nitrifying bacteria convert nitrite into nitrate.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Plant roots absorb nitrate.</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT DISTINCTION</h3>

<p>
Nitrification is not the same as nitrogen fixation.
Nitrogen fixation introduces atmospheric nitrogen into usable nitrogen compounds,
while nitrification converts one soil nitrogen form into another.
</p>
', 1),
  (937, 937, '
<h2>Denitrification</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Denitrification</strong> is the bacterial conversion of nitrates in the
soil into nitrogen-containing gases, eventually returning nitrogen to the
atmosphere.
</p>

<p>
This process is an important part of completing the nitrogen cycle.
</p>

<h3>WHERE IT OCCURS</h3>

<p>
Denitrification is particularly associated with oxygen-poor or waterlogged
conditions where certain bacteria use nitrate during their metabolism.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Nitrogen exists in nitrate compounds in the soil.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Denitrifying bacteria use nitrate under suitable
low-oxygen conditions.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Nitrogen-containing gases are produced.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Nitrogen returns to the atmosphere.</p>

<h3>WHY IT MATTERS</h3>

<p>
If nitrogen only moved from the atmosphere into soil and organisms, atmospheric
nitrogen would continually decrease.
</p>

<p>
Denitrification provides a pathway that returns nitrogen to the atmosphere.
</p>

<h3>COMPLETE CONNECTION</h3>

<p>
Atmospheric nitrogen → fixation → soil nitrogen compounds → nitrification →
nitrate → plant uptake → animals → decomposition → soil nitrogen → 
denitrification → atmospheric nitrogen.
</p>

<p>
The exact chemical forms change during the cycle, but nitrogen itself is being
recycled through the environment.
</p>
', 1),
  (938, 938, '
<h2>Fertiliser Runoff</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Fertilisers can improve crop growth when used appropriately.
However, excess fertiliser can be carried from farmland into rivers and lakes
by rainwater.
</p>

<p>
This movement of dissolved nutrients from land into water is called
<strong>nutrient runoff</strong>.
</p>

<h3>WHY RUNOFF IS A PROBLEM</h3>

<p>
Excess nitrates and phosphates entering water bodies can provide unusually
large amounts of nutrients for algae and aquatic plants.
</p>

<p>
This can contribute to <strong>eutrophication</strong>.
</p>

<h3>THE CHAIN OF EVENTS</h3>

<p>
<strong>Excess fertiliser</strong>
→ nutrients enter water
→ rapid algal growth
→ algae die
→ decomposers break them down
→ decomposer respiration uses oxygen
→ dissolved oxygen decreases
→ aquatic organisms may die.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT CONNECTION</h3>

<p>
The fertiliser itself does not directly remove oxygen from the water.
The major oxygen depletion occurs because microorganisms decompose the
increased amount of dead organic material.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain how excessive fertiliser use can cause fish deaths.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Excess nutrients enter a water body.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Algae grow rapidly.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Large amounts of algae die.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Decomposers break down the dead algae.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Their respiration uses dissolved oxygen.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> Oxygen concentration falls.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 7:</strong> Fish and other aquatic organisms may die because there is
not enough oxygen for aerobic respiration.
</p>
', 1),
  (939, 939, '
<h2>Water Availability and Plant Distribution</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Water is an essential resource for plants.
It acts as a reactant in photosynthesis, is needed for transport and helps
maintain cell turgidity.
</p>

<p>
The amount of available water therefore influences where different plants
can survive and grow.
</p>

<h3>WATER AVAILABILITY AS AN ABIOTIC FACTOR</h3>

<p>
Water availability is an <strong>abiotic factor</strong> because it is a
non-living part of the environment.
</p>

<p>
Areas with high and reliable water availability can support plant species
that require abundant water.
Areas with little available water favour plants with adaptations that reduce
water loss or improve water uptake.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE: DRY ENVIRONMENTS</h3>

<p>
A plant living in a dry environment may have features such as:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>reduced leaf surface area</li>
  <li>a thick waxy cuticle</li>
  <li>stomata positioned in protected locations</li>
  <li>deep or widespread roots</li>
</ul>

<p>
These features can reduce water loss or improve access to water.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
Water availability is not simply a description of rainfall.
It refers to how much water is actually accessible to organisms.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why might a plant adapted to a dry environment survive better than a plant without these adaptations?</p>

<p>
The adapted plant may lose water more slowly or obtain water more effectively.
It is therefore more likely to maintain sufficient water for cell function
during dry conditions.
</p>
', 1),
  (940, 940, '
<h2>Photosynthetic Productivity</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Productivity</strong> in an ecosystem refers to the rate at which
producers build organic material from inorganic substances, mainly through
photosynthesis.
</p>

<p>
Plants use light energy to convert carbon dioxide and water into organic
compounds. The chemical energy stored in these compounds becomes available
to organisms that feed directly or indirectly on the producers.
</p>

<h3>WHY PRODUCTIVITY MATTERS</h3>

<p>
A food chain begins with producers because they introduce energy-rich organic
material into the biological part of an ecosystem.
</p>

<p>
If producers photosynthesise rapidly, they can potentially make more organic
material available to the rest of the ecosystem.
</p>

<h3>FACTORS AFFECTING PRODUCTIVITY</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Light intensity</li>
  <li>Carbon dioxide concentration</li>
  <li>Temperature</li>
  <li>Water availability</li>
  <li>Availability of mineral nutrients</li>
</ul>

<p>
The effect of each factor depends on the conditions under which the plant is
growing.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can low light intensity reduce productivity?</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Photosynthesis requires light energy.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Low light provides less energy for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> The rate of photosynthesis may therefore decrease.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Less organic material may be produced per unit time.</p>

<p>
Therefore, low light intensity can limit productivity.
</p>
