<h2>Abiotic Factors and Species Distribution</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The distribution of organisms is affected by both biotic and abiotic factors.
</p>

<p>
Important abiotic factors include:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>temperature</li>
  <li>light intensity</li>
  <li>water availability</li>
  <li>soil pH</li>
  <li>soil mineral content</li>
  <li>salinity</li>
  <li>oxygen concentration</li>
</ul>

<h3>HOW AN ABIOTIC FACTOR AFFECTS DISTRIBUTION</h3>

<p>
Each species has conditions under which it can survive, grow and reproduce
successfully.
</p>

<p>
If a particular environmental condition becomes unsuitable, the species may
become less abundant or disappear from that part of the habitat.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE: WATER</h3>

<p>
A plant that requires abundant water may be more common close to a river than
on dry ground farther away.
</p>

<p>
However, other factors may also differ between the locations.
A proper investigation is therefore needed to determine which factor is
associated with the distribution.
</p>

<h3>WORKED REASONING</h3>

<p><strong>Observation:</strong> A plant occurs mainly in areas with moist soil.</p>

<p>
<strong>Reasoning:</strong> Water availability may influence its distribution
because the plant requires water for photosynthesis, transport and maintenance
of cell turgidity.
</p>

<p>
However, this observation alone does not prove that water is the only factor
responsible.
</p>
', 1),
