<h2>Evaluating an Ecological Conclusion</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
A scientific conclusion should match the evidence collected.
A researcher should avoid making a claim that is stronger than the data allow.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A student measures plant abundance at different distances from a river and
finds fewer plants farther from the river.
</p>

<p>
A reasonable conclusion is:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Plant abundance decreases with increasing distance from the river in the
area studied.
</strong>
</p>

<p>
A much stronger statement would be:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Distance from the river causes plants to decrease.
</strong>
</p>

<p>
The second statement may not be justified because other factors could also
change with distance.
</p>

<h3>WHAT MAKES A CONCLUSION STRONG?</h3>

<ul>
  <li>It refers directly to the data.</li>
  <li>It identifies the observed pattern.</li>
  <li>It avoids unsupported explanations.</li>
  <li>It considers limitations.</li>
  <li>It is based on sufficient evidence.</li>
</ul>

<h3>RELIABILITY</h3>

<p>
If repeated samples produce similar results, confidence in the observed
pattern increases.
</p>

<h3>VALIDITY</h3>

<p>
The investigation should actually measure what it was designed to investigate.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher samples only one quadrat at each location and finds that plants
are more abundant near a stream.
</p>

<p>
The observation may be useful, but the conclusion is limited because one
quadrat at each location may not adequately represent the whole area.
</p>

<p>
More appropriately distributed repeated samples would provide stronger evidence.
</p>
', 1),
