<h2>Range of Ecological Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The <strong>range</strong> describes the spread of a set of measurements.
</p>

<p>
It is calculated by subtracting the smallest value from the largest value.
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Range = highest value − lowest value
</strong>
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
The numbers of plants in five quadrats are:
</p>

<p>
4, 7, 5, 11, 8
</p>

<p>
Highest value = 11
</p>

<p>
Lowest value = 4
</p>

<p>
Range = 11 − 4
</p>

<p>
= <strong>7</strong>
</p>

<h3>WHAT DOES THE RANGE TELL US?</h3>

<p>
A small range means the measurements are relatively close to one another.
A large range means the measurements are more spread out.
</p>

<h3>WORKED COMPARISON</h3>

<p>
Sample A:
</p>

<p>
8, 9, 10, 9, 8
</p>

<p>
Range = 10 − 8 = 2
</p>

<p>
Sample B:
</p>

<p>
3, 12, 7, 15, 5
</p>

<p>
Range = 15 − 3 = 12
</p>

<p>
Sample B has the greater spread.
</p>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

<p>
The range does not tell us the average.
It tells us how far apart the highest and lowest measurements are.
</p>
', 1),
