<h2>Anomalous Results in Ecological Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
An <strong>anomalous result</strong> is a measurement that differs substantially
from the general pattern of the other results.
</p>

<h3>WORKED EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A researcher records plant numbers in six similar quadrats:
</p>

<p>
12, 14, 13, 15, 14, 35
</p>

<p>
Most measurements are between 12 and 15.
The value 35 is very different from the others and may be anomalous.
</p>

<h3>DO NOT AUTOMATICALLY DELETE IT</h3>

<p>
An unusual result should not simply be removed because it does not fit the
expected pattern.
</p>

<p>
The researcher should investigate possible explanations.
</p>

<h3>POSSIBLE CAUSES</h3>

<ul>
  <li>recording error</li>
  <li>counting error</li>
  <li>equipment or measurement problem</li>
  <li>incorrect sampling procedure</li>
  <li>a genuinely unusual part of the habitat</li>
</ul>

<h3>WHAT SHOULD THE RESEARCHER DO?</h3>

<p>
Check the original measurement and sampling conditions.
If an error is confirmed, the result can be corrected or excluded with a
clear justification.
</p>

<p>
If no error is found, the result may represent genuine biological variation
and should not automatically be discarded.
</p>
', 1),
