<h2>Predation</h2>

<p>
<b>Predation</b> is an interaction in which one organism, the <b>predator</b>, captures and feeds on another organism, the <b>prey</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
A lion hunting and eating a zebra is an example of predation.
</p>

<p>
The:
</p>

<ul>
<li>lion is the predator</li>
<li>zebra is the prey</li>
</ul>

<h3>Predation affects populations</h3>

<p>
If the number of predators increases, predation pressure on prey populations may increase.
</p>

<p>
If prey numbers decrease greatly, predators may have less food available.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, predator and prey populations can influence one another.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
Predation is not the same as competition.
</p>

<p>
In competition, organisms require the same limited resource.
</p>

<p>
In predation, one organism captures and feeds on another organism.
</p>
', 1),
  (963, 963, '
<h2>Producers</h2>

<p>
A <b>producer</b> is an organism that makes its own organic food from simpler substances.
</p>

<p>
Most producers in ecosystems are green plants and algae. They use <b>photosynthesis</b> to make glucose.
</p>

<h3>What does a plant need?</h3>

<p>
For photosynthesis, a green plant needs:
</p>

<ul>
<li>light energy</li>
<li>carbon dioxide</li>
<li>water</li>
<li>chlorophyll</li>
</ul>

<p>
The glucose produced can be used in respiration or converted into other substances such as starch.
</p>

<h3>Why are producers important?</h3>

<p>
Producers introduce energy from sunlight into many ecosystems.
</p>

<p>
Animals cannot obtain this energy directly from sunlight. They obtain chemical energy by feeding on organisms or organic material.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
In a grassland:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → zebra → lion</b>
</p>

<p>
The grass is the producer because it makes its own organic food.
', 1),
  (964, 964, '
<h2>Consumers</h2>

<p>
A <b>consumer</b> is an organism that obtains organic nutrients and energy by feeding on other organisms or organic material.
</p>

<p>
Unlike producers, consumers do not make their own organic food from carbon dioxide and water using photosynthesis.
</p>

<h3>Examples</h3>

<ul>
<li>A cow feeds on grass.</li>
<li>A lion feeds on other animals.</li>
<li>A human may feed on plants and animals.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Types of consumers</h3>

<p>
Consumers can be classified according to what they feed on.
</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Herbivores</b> feed mainly on plants.</li>
<li><b>Carnivores</b> feed mainly on animals.</li>
<li><b>Omnivores</b> feed on both plant and animal material.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
In:
</p>

<p>
<b>Grass → grasshopper → frog</b>
</p>

<p>
The grasshopper and frog are consumers because they obtain organic nutrients by feeding on other organisms.
', 1),
