<h2>Energy Transfer in Food Chains</h2>

<p>
Energy enters most ecosystems through producers capturing light energy during photosynthesis.
</p>

<p>
When a consumer feeds on a producer, chemical energy stored in the producer''s organic substances is transferred to the consumer.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
<b>Sunlight → grass → zebra → lion</b>
</p>

<p>
The grass captures light energy.
</p>

<p>
The zebra obtains chemical energy by feeding on the grass.
</p>

<p>
The lion obtains chemical energy by feeding on the zebra.
</p>

<h3>Energy is lost</h3>

<p>
Energy is not transferred completely from one trophic level to the next.
</p>

<p>
Energy is used by organisms for processes such as:
</p>

<ul>
<li>respiration</li>
<li>movement</li>
<li>growth</li>
<li>active transport</li>
<li>maintaining body processes</li>
</ul>

<p>
Some energy is also lost in waste and uneaten material.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, less chemical energy is generally available at higher trophic levels.
