Decomposer organisms obtain nutrients and energy from organic material.
During their respiration, some of the carbon is released as carbon dioxide.
</p>

<p>
Some carbon also remains in the soil as organic matter.
Over long periods, some carbon-containing material can become buried and,
under suitable conditions, contribute to fossil fuel formation.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Imagine a tree leaf falls onto the soil.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> The leaf contains carbon-containing organic compounds.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Fungi and bacteria colonise the dead leaf.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Decomposers break down the organic material.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Decomposer respiration releases some carbon as carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> Some remaining material contributes to soil organic matter.</p>

<h3>WHY DECOMPOSITION MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Without decomposition, dead organic material would accumulate and nutrients
would remain locked in dead material for much longer.
</p>

<p>
Decomposition therefore connects dead organisms back to nutrient cycling.
</p>
