<p>
Population density:
</p>

<p>
50,000 ÷ 100 = <b>500 people/km²</b>
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Area A contains 20,000 people in 200 km².
</p>

<p>
Density:
</p>

<p>
20,000 ÷ 200 = <b>100 people/km²</b>
</p>

<p>
Area B contains 15,000 people in 50 km².
</p>

<p>
Density:
</p>

<p>
15,000 ÷ 50 = <b>300 people/km²</b>
</p>

<p>
Area B has the higher population density even though it has fewer people overall.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
A high population density means many people occupy each unit of area.
</p>

<p>
It does not necessarily mean the total population is larger.
</p>
