<h2>Mitochondria and Aerobic Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Mitochondria</strong> are organelles in which important later stages
of aerobic respiration occur.
</p>

<p>
Cells that require large amounts of energy often contain many mitochondria.
</p>

<h3>STRUCTURE AND FUNCTION</h3>

<p>
A mitochondrion has an outer membrane and an inner membrane.
The inner membrane is highly folded, forming structures called
<strong>cristae</strong>.
</p>

<p>
The folds provide a large surface area for reactions associated with later
stages of aerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>DOES ALL RESPIRATION OCCUR IN MITOCHONDRIA?</h3>

<p>
No.
</p>

<p>
The first stage of glucose breakdown, glycolysis, occurs in the cytoplasm.
Later aerobic stages occur in mitochondria.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
A muscle cell has a high energy demand during repeated contraction.
It therefore contains many mitochondria to support the large amount of aerobic
respiration required.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
It is more accurate to say that mitochondria are major sites of aerobic
respiration rather than saying that all respiration happens there.
</p>
', 1),
  (1037, 1037, '
<h2>Aerobic Respiration and Oxygen Supply</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Aerobic respiration requires oxygen.
Cells therefore need a continuous supply of oxygen when their energy demands
are being met mainly by aerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>PATHWAY OF OXYGEN</h3>

<p>
In humans:
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Air → lungs → blood → tissues → cells
</strong>
</p>

<p>
Oxygen diffuses from the alveoli into the blood and is transported to body
tissues.
</p>

<p>
At the tissues, oxygen moves from the blood into cells and can then be used
in aerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES EXERCISE INCREASE OXYGEN DEMAND?</h3>

<p>
During exercise, muscles contract more frequently and require more energy.
The rate of respiration therefore increases.
</p>

<p>
More oxygen may be required to support the increased rate of aerobic respiration.
</p>

<h3>IF OXYGEN SUPPLY IS NOT ENOUGH</h3>

<p>
If the oxygen supply cannot meet the demand of actively respiring muscle cells,
anaerobic respiration can contribute to energy release.
</p>

<h3>WORKED REASONING</h3>

<p>
A person begins running.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> Muscles contract more rapidly.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> Their energy demand increases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> Respiration rate increases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> Oxygen demand increases for aerobic respiration.</p>

<p>
Therefore breathing and heart rate increase to help deliver oxygen to the
working muscles.
</p>
', 1),
