<h2>Factors Affecting Respiration Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The rate of respiration can change when environmental or physiological
conditions change.
</p>

<h3>TEMPERATURE</h3>

<p>
Respiration involves enzyme-controlled reactions.
As temperature increases within a suitable range, enzyme activity can increase,
so respiration rate may increase.
</p>

<p>
At excessively high temperatures, enzymes can become denatured and respiration
rate can decrease.
</p>

<h3>OXYGEN AVAILABILITY</h3>

<p>
Aerobic respiration requires oxygen.
If oxygen availability becomes insufficient, the contribution of aerobic
respiration can be limited and anaerobic pathways may become more important.
</p>

<h3>GLUCOSE AVAILABILITY</h3>

<p>
Glucose is a respiratory substrate.
Its availability can affect the rate at which respiration can occur, depending
on the cells and conditions being studied.
</p>

<h3>EXAMPLE</h3>

<p>
Yeast cells are supplied with glucose at different temperatures.
The amount of carbon dioxide produced per minute can be measured.
</p>

<p>
A higher carbon dioxide production rate indicates a higher respiration rate,
provided the investigation is designed so that carbon dioxide production is a
valid measure of the respiration being studied.
</p>

<h3>FAIR TEST</h3>

<p>
If temperature is being investigated, other important conditions should be
kept constant, such as the amount of yeast, glucose concentration and volume.
</p>
