<h2>Fermentation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Fermentation</strong> refers to anaerobic metabolic processes in which
organic molecules such as glucose are broken down without oxygen.
</p>

<p>
In yeast, alcoholic fermentation produces ethanol and carbon dioxide.
</p>

<p>
<strong>
Glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide + energy
</strong>
</p>

<h3>FERMENTATION IN BREAD MAKING</h3>

<p>
Yeast is mixed with dough containing sugars.
The yeast respires anaerobically when oxygen becomes limited.
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide produced by the yeast becomes trapped in the dough as bubbles.
These bubbles cause the dough to expand.
</p>

<p>
Ethanol is also produced.
Much of the ethanol evaporates during baking.
</p>

<h3>FERMENTATION IN BREWING</h3>

<p>
Yeast can convert sugars into ethanol and carbon dioxide.
The ethanol is retained in the liquid during alcoholic fermentation.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
Fermentation does not mean that oxygen is being converted into another
substance.
It specifically involves metabolic pathways that allow cells to obtain some
energy without oxygen.
</p>
