<h2>Investigating Respiration in Germinating Seeds</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Germinating seeds are useful for investigating respiration because their cells
are metabolically active as the embryo develops.
</p>

<h3>WHY DO GERMINATING SEEDS RESPire?</h3>

<p>
The growing embryo requires energy for processes such as cell division,
growth and synthesis of new materials.
</p>

<p>
Respiration releases energy from stored food reserves.
</p>

<h3>MEASURING OXYGEN UPTAKE</h3>

<p>
A respirometer can be used to investigate oxygen consumption.
</p>

<p>
If germinating seeds consume oxygen in a sealed system, the volume or pressure
of gas may change.
</p>

<p>
A coloured liquid indicator can be used to detect movement caused by changes
in gas volume or pressure.
</p>

<h3>ROLE OF CARBON DIOXIDE ABSORPTION</h3>

<p>
A substance such as soda lime can absorb carbon dioxide.
If carbon dioxide produced by respiration is removed while oxygen is consumed,
the change in gas volume can be used to estimate oxygen uptake.
</p>

<h3>CONTROL</h3>

<p>
A control containing non-respiring material, such as boiled seeds, can help
show whether changes are due to respiration rather than temperature or
pressure changes alone.
</p>
