<h2>Temperature and Yeast Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Yeast respiration can be used to investigate the effect of temperature on
the rate of respiration.
</p>

<h3>WHY TEMPERATURE MATTERS</h3>

<p>
Respiration involves enzymes.
As temperature increases within a suitable range, enzyme molecules generally
collide more frequently with their substrates and reaction rates can increase.
</p>

<p>
At temperatures above the optimum, enzyme structure can be disrupted and
respiration rate can decrease.
</p>

<h3>EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN</h3>

<p>
A student could place equal amounts of yeast and glucose solution at different
temperatures.
</p>

<p>
Carbon dioxide production can be measured over a fixed time.
</p>

<h3>VARIABLES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Independent variable:</strong> temperature.</p>

<p>
<strong>Dependent variable:</strong> respiration rate, estimated from carbon
dioxide production per unit time.</p>

<p>
<strong>Control variables:</strong> amount of yeast, glucose concentration,
volume of solution and measurement time.</p>

<h3>EXPECTED PATTERN</h3>

<p>
At low temperatures, respiration may be slow.
</p>

<p>
As temperature rises toward the optimum, respiration rate may increase.
</p>

<p>
Above the optimum, respiration rate may decrease because enzymes are damaged
or denatured.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The exact optimum temperature depends on the organism and experimental
conditions.
Do not assume one temperature is the optimum for every organism.
</p>
