<h2>Inhalation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
<strong>Inhalation</strong> is the movement of air into the lungs.
</p>

<h3>STEP-BY-STEP MECHANISM</h3>

<p>
<strong>Step 1:</strong> The diaphragm contracts.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 2:</strong> The diaphragm moves downward and becomes flatter.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 3:</strong> The external intercostal muscles contract.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 4:</strong> The ribs move upward and outward.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 5:</strong> The volume of the thoracic cavity increases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 6:</strong> The pressure inside the lungs decreases.</p>

<p>
<strong>Step 7:</strong> Air moves into the lungs because atmospheric pressure
is now greater than pressure inside the lungs.</p>

<h3>WHY DOES VOLUME AFFECT PRESSURE?</h3>

<p>
When the enclosed space containing air becomes larger, the pressure of the air
inside decreases.
</p>

<p>
This pressure difference causes air to move into the lungs.
</p>

<h3>EXAM CHAIN</h3>

<p>
<strong>
Diaphragm contracts
→ thoracic volume increases
→ lung pressure decreases
→ air enters.
</strong>
</p>
