<h2>Airways and Their Functions</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The airways transport air between the external environment and the alveoli.
Different structures have different roles.
</p>

<h3>NOSE AND NASAL CAVITY</h3>

<p>
The nose and nasal cavity help warm, moisten and filter incoming air.
</p>

<h3>TRACHEA</h3>

<p>
The trachea carries air toward the bronchi.
Cartilage supports its walls and helps keep the airway open.
</p>

<h3>BRONCHI</h3>

<p>
The trachea divides into two main bronchi.
Each bronchus carries air toward one lung.
</p>

<h3>BRONCHIOLES</h3>

<p>
Bronchi branch into progressively smaller bronchioles.
They distribute air throughout the lungs.
</p>

<h3>ALVEOLI</h3>

<p>
The smallest airways lead to alveoli, where gaseous exchange occurs.
</p>

<h3>MUCOUS MEMBRANE</h3>

<p>
Mucus traps dust particles and microorganisms entering with inhaled air.
</p>

<h3>CILIATED CELLS</h3>

<p>
Cilia beat to move mucus toward the throat, where it can be swallowed.
This helps protect the lungs from trapped particles and microorganisms.
</p>
