<h2>Composition of Inspired and Expired Air</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The air entering the lungs is called <strong>inspired air</strong>.
The air leaving the lungs is called <strong>expired air</strong>.
Their composition is different because gaseous exchange occurs in the lungs.
</p>

<h3>INSPIRED AIR</h3>

<p>
Inspired air contains a relatively high proportion of oxygen and a very small
proportion of carbon dioxide.
</p>

<h3>EXPIRED AIR</h3>

<p>
Expired air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than inspired air.
It also contains more water vapour.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES OXYGEN DECREASE?</h3>

<p>
Oxygen diffuses from the alveoli into the blood.
Some of this oxygen is then transported to respiring cells.
Therefore, less oxygen remains in the air that is exhaled.
</p>

<h3>WHY DOES CARBON DIOXIDE INCREASE?</h3>

<p>
Respiring cells produce carbon dioxide.
The blood transports carbon dioxide to the lungs.
It then diffuses into the alveoli and is exhaled.
</p>

<h3>IMPORTANT</h3>

<p>
The change in gas composition is evidence that gases are exchanged between
the air in the lungs and the blood.
</p>
', 1),
