<h2>Cartilage in the Trachea</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The trachea is an airway that carries air toward the bronchi.
Its walls contain rings of <strong>cartilage</strong>.
</p>

<h3>WHY IS CARTILAGE IMPORTANT?</h3>

<p>
Cartilage provides structural support and helps prevent the trachea from
collapsing when pressure inside the airway changes during breathing.
</p>

<h3>WHY MUST THE AIRWAY REMAIN OPEN?</h3>

<p>
Air must travel freely toward the bronchioles and alveoli.
If the trachea collapsed significantly, less air would reach the lungs.
