  <h2>Why Blood Groups Matter in Transfusion</h2>

  <p>
    A blood transfusion involves giving blood or blood components from a donor
    to a recipient.
  </p>

  <p>
    The donor''s red blood cells carry molecules called <b>antigens</b> on their
    surfaces. The recipient''s plasma contains <b>antibodies</b> that can
    recognise particular antigens.
  </p>

  <p>
    If incompatible red blood cells are given, antibodies can bind to the donor
    cells and cause them to <b>agglutinate</b> (clump).
  </p>

  <p>
    This can be dangerous because the clumped cells can interfere with normal
    blood circulation and may be destroyed.
  </p>

  <h3>Why matching matters</h3>

  <p>
    Before a transfusion, blood is tested to help ensure compatibility between
    donor and recipient.
  </p>

  <p>
    The ABO group and Rhesus status are important parts of this compatibility
    testing.
  </p>

  <h3>Important</h3>

  <p>
    Blood transfusion is not simply a matter of matching the letters. Actual
    transfusion decisions require proper blood grouping, crossmatching and
    clinical procedures.
  </p>
  ', 1),
