<h2>Atrial Systole</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Atrial systole is the contraction of the atria.
It helps move blood from the atria into the ventricles.
</p>

<h3>What happens?</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Blood has entered the atria.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The atrial walls contract.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Pressure inside the atria increases.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Blood is pushed through the atrioventricular valves into the ventricles.</p>

<h3>Why are the atrioventricular valves open?</h3>

<p>
The pressure in the atria becomes greater than the pressure in the ventricles.
This pressure difference drives blood into the ventricles.
</p>

<h3>What happens next?</h3>

<p>
The ventricles then contract.
As ventricular pressure rises, the atrioventricular valves close.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> What is the main effect of atrial systole?</p>

<p>
It increases pressure in the atria and pushes blood into the ventricles.
</p>
', 1),
