<h2>Measuring Pulse Rate Before and After Exercise</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Pulse rate can be used as a simple measure of how the cardiovascular system responds to exercise.
</p>

<h3>Basic investigation</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Allow the person to rest quietly.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Measure the pulse for a fixed time, such as 30 seconds.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Multiply the result by 2 to estimate beats per minute.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Perform a standard exercise for a fixed period.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Measure the pulse immediately after exercise.</p>

<p><strong>Step 6:</strong> Continue measuring during recovery if required.</p>

<h3>Example calculation</h3>

<p>
A student records 38 pulse beats in 30 seconds.
</p>

<p>
38 × 2 = 76 beats per minute.
</p>

<p>
The student''s estimated pulse rate is therefore <strong>76 bpm</strong>.
</p>

<h3>Making the investigation fair</h3>

<p>
If several students are being compared, the exercise duration and intensity should be kept as similar as possible.
Measurements should also be taken at the same time point after exercise.
</p>

<h3>Repeats</h3>

<p>
Repeating measurements reduces the effect of random variation.
A mean can then be calculated from repeated results.
</p>

<h3>Expected result</h3>

<p>
Pulse rate should generally increase after exercise and then decrease during recovery.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> A student has a pulse of 36 beats in 30 seconds before exercise and 60 beats in 30 seconds immediately after exercise. Calculate both rates.</p>

<p>
Before exercise:
</p>

<p>
36 × 2 = <strong>72 bpm</strong>
</p>

<p>
After exercise:
</p>

<p>
60 × 2 = <strong>120 bpm</strong>
</p>

<p>
The pulse rate increased by:
</p>

<p>
120 − 72 = <strong>48 bpm</strong>
</p>
