<h2>Arteries</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Arteries are blood vessels that carry blood <strong>away from the heart</strong>.
</p>

<h3>Important definition</h3>

<p>
The direction of blood flow determines whether a vessel is an artery or a vein.
Arteries do not mean "oxygenated blood" in every case.
</p>

<p>
For example, the pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood from the heart to the lungs.
</p>

<h3>Adaptations of arteries</h3>

<ul>
  <li>thick muscular walls</li>
  <li>many elastic fibres</li>
  <li>relatively narrow lumen compared with veins</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why are the walls thick?</h3>

<p>
Blood leaving the heart is under relatively high pressure.
Thick muscular and elastic walls help arteries withstand and maintain this pressure.
</p>

<h3>Why are arteries elastic?</h3>

<p>
The heart pumps blood in pulses.
Elastic fibres allow the artery wall to stretch when pressure rises and recoil as pressure falls.
This helps maintain blood flow between heartbeats.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Explain why an artery has a thick wall.</p>

<p>
Blood is pumped from the heart at high pressure. A thick muscular and elastic wall helps the artery withstand this pressure and maintain the movement of blood.
</p>
', 1),
  (1134, 1134, '
<h2>Veins</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
Veins are blood vessels that carry blood <strong>towards the heart</strong>.
</p>

<h3>Important definition</h3>

<p>
The direction of blood flow determines whether a vessel is a vein.
Veins do not necessarily carry deoxygenated blood.
</p>

<p>
For example, the pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart.
</p>

<h3>Adaptations of veins</h3>

<ul>
  <li>thinner walls than arteries</li>
  <li>larger lumen</li>
  <li>valves</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why are the walls thinner?</h3>

<p>
Blood in veins is generally at lower pressure than blood in arteries, so veins do not require the same thick muscular and elastic walls.
</p>

<h3>Why do veins have valves?</h3>

<p>
Blood returning to the heart can be moving against gravity, especially from the legs.
Valves prevent blood from flowing backwards.
</p>

<h3>How does skeletal muscle help?</h3>

<p>
When muscles around veins contract, they can compress the veins and help push blood towards the heart.
The valves ensure that the blood is pushed in the correct direction.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Why are valves important in veins?</p>

<p>
Blood pressure in veins is relatively low. Valves prevent blood from flowing backwards and help maintain one-way movement towards the heart.
</p>
', 1),
