<h2>Why Capillaries Have a Narrow Lumen</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The lumen is the internal space through which blood flows inside a blood vessel.
Capillaries have very narrow lumens.
</p>

<h3>Why is the narrow lumen useful?</h3>

<p>
A narrow lumen means red blood cells pass close to the capillary wall.
This helps reduce the distance oxygen must travel from the blood to the surrounding tissue.
</p>

<h3>Slower blood flow</h3>

<p>
The extensive capillary network provides a large total cross-sectional area.
Blood moves more slowly through individual capillaries than through the larger arteries.
This gives more opportunity for exchange between blood and tissues.
</p>

<h3>Connection with red blood cells</h3>

<p>
Red blood cells are flexible and can pass through very narrow capillaries.
Their close contact with the capillary wall helps efficient oxygen exchange.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> How does a narrow capillary lumen help oxygen reach body cells?</p>

<p>
It keeps red blood cells close to the capillary wall, reducing the distance oxygen must diffuse before reaching surrounding tissues.
</p>
', 1),
