<h2>The Lymphatic System</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>
The lymphatic system is a network of vessels and organs involved in returning excess tissue fluid to the bloodstream and supporting immune defence.
</p>

<h3>Its transport role</h3>

<p>
Lymphatic vessels collect excess tissue fluid from tissues.
The collected fluid, called lymph, is eventually returned to the blood.
</p>

<h3>Its defence role</h3>

<p>
Lymph nodes contain immune cells and can help detect and respond to pathogens carried in lymph.
</p>

<h3>Why does the lymphatic system matter?</h3>

<p>
It therefore has two important connections to transport and defence:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>maintaining tissue-fluid balance</li>
  <li>supporting immune responses</li>
</ul>

<h3>Blood system vs lymphatic system</h3>

<p>
The blood circulatory system is a closed network driven by the pumping action of the heart.
The lymphatic system collects excess tissue fluid and returns it to the blood.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p><strong>Question:</strong> Give two functions of the lymphatic system.</p>

<p>
It returns excess tissue fluid to the bloodstream and supports immune defence.
</p>
