<h2>How Antibodies Help Destroy Pathogens</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Antibodies do not simply float around randomly attacking everything. They recognise specific antigens.</p>

<h3>Step 1: A Pathogen Enters</h3>

<p>Suppose a bacterium enters the body carrying a particular antigen.</p>

<h3>Step 2: The Antigen Is Recognised</h3>

<p>A lymphocyte capable of responding to that antigen is activated.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Antibodies Are Produced</h3>

<p>The lymphocyte produces antibodies that are complementary to the antigen.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Antibodies Bind</h3>

<p>The antibodies attach to antigens on the pathogen.</p>

<h3>Step 5: The Pathogen Is Targeted</h3>

<p>The bound antibodies can help other parts of the immune system identify and destroy the pathogen.</p>

<p>Some antibodies can also block pathogens or toxins from attaching to cells.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Pathogen → antigen recognised → lymphocyte response → specific antibodies → antibody binds antigen → pathogen is neutralised or targeted for destruction.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A bacterium enters the body. Its surface contains antigen A. A suitable lymphocyte produces antibody A.</p>

<p><strong>Why does antibody A bind to the bacterium?</strong></p>

<p>Because the antibody has a shape complementary to antigen A.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>The specificity of antibodies allows the immune system to distinguish between different foreign substances.</p>
