<h2>Mineral Ion Transport in Plants</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants need mineral ions from the soil for healthy growth and metabolism.</p>

<h3>Nitrate Ions</h3>

<p>Nitrate ions provide nitrogen needed to make amino acids and proteins.</p>

<p>A shortage of nitrate can reduce plant growth.</p>

<h3>Magnesium Ions</h3>

<p>Magnesium is needed for chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>Magnesium deficiency can cause chlorosis, in which leaves become yellow because chlorophyll formation is affected.</p>

<h3>How Mineral Ions Enter Roots</h3>

<p>Mineral ions may enter root cells by active transport when their concentration in the soil is lower than the concentration inside the root cells.</p>

<p>Active transport requires energy from respiration.</p>

<h3>Why Water and Mineral Ions Are Different</h3>

<p>Water commonly enters root hair cells by osmosis.</p>

<p>Mineral ions may require active transport.</p>

<p>Therefore, water and mineral ions do not necessarily enter roots by the same mechanism.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A root cell contains a higher concentration of nitrate ions than the surrounding soil.</p>

<p><strong>How can nitrate ions still enter the root cell?</strong></p>

<p>They can be taken up by active transport using energy from respiration.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Mineral ion needed → concentration gradient may oppose uptake → active transport → energy required.</strong></p>
