<h2>Factors Affecting Transpiration Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The rate of transpiration depends on how quickly water vapour can move from the leaf to the surrounding air.</p>

<h3>1. Temperature</h3>

<p>Higher temperature generally increases evaporation and can increase transpiration if other factors are suitable.</p>

<h3>2. Humidity</h3>

<p>High humidity reduces the concentration gradient between the moist leaf air spaces and the surrounding air.</p>

<p>Therefore, transpiration generally decreases.</p>

<h3>3. Wind Speed</h3>

<p>Moving air removes water vapour from around the leaf surface.</p>

<p>This can maintain a steep diffusion gradient and increase transpiration.</p>

<h3>4. Light Intensity</h3>

<p>Higher light intensity can cause stomata to open in many plants, increasing gas exchange and potentially increasing transpiration.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two identical plants are placed under identical light conditions.</p>

<p>Plant A is in humid air.</p>

<p>Plant B is in dry air.</p>

<p><strong>Which plant is likely to have the higher transpiration rate?</strong></p>

<p>Plant B, because the drier surrounding air produces a larger water vapour concentration gradient.</p>

<h3>Summary</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Factor</th>
<th>Typical effect of increase</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Temperature</td>
<td>Transpiration increases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Humidity</td>
<td>Transpiration decreases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Wind speed</td>
<td>Transpiration increases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Light intensity</td>
<td>Often increases transpiration through stomatal opening</td>
</tr>
</table>
