<h2>Investigating Transpiration Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot can be used as an indirect measure of transpiration rate.</p>

<p>A common apparatus for this is a <strong>potometer</strong>.</p>

<h3>How a Potometer Works</h3>

<p>A leafy shoot is connected to a water-filled apparatus containing a capillary tube.</p>

<p>As the shoot loses water by transpiration, water is drawn through the apparatus.</p>

<p>The movement of an air bubble in the capillary tube can be measured.</p>

<h3>Calculating Rate</h3>

<p>If the air bubble moves a known distance in a known time:</p>

<p><strong>Rate of movement = distance moved ÷ time</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Calculation</h3>

<p>An air bubble moves 30 mm in 5 minutes.</p>

<p><strong>Rate = 30 ÷ 5</strong></p>

<p><strong>Rate = 6 mm/min</strong></p>

<h3>Fair Test</h3>

<p>If testing the effect of wind speed, other factors should be controlled.</p>

<p>For example:</p>

<ul>
<li>same plant species;</li>
<li>similar leaf area;</li>
<li>same temperature;</li>
<li>same light intensity;</li>
<li>same time period.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Important Limitation</h3>

<p>A potometer measures water uptake, not transpiration directly.</p>

<p>Water uptake is used as an estimate because most of the water taken up by a leafy shoot is eventually lost through transpiration, but not all water absorbed is necessarily transpired immediately.</p>
