<h2>Stomata</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Stomata</strong> are small pores found mainly in the epidermis of leaves.</p>

<p>They provide a pathway for gases to move between the inside of the leaf and the atmosphere.</p>

<h3>1. Gas Exchange</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide can enter the leaf through open stomata for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Oxygen produced during photosynthesis can leave through the stomata.</p>

<p>Plants also exchange gases associated with respiration.</p>

<h3>2. Water Loss</h3>

<p>Water vapour can diffuse out of the leaf through open stomata.</p>

<p>This is the main route of transpiration.</p>

<h3>3. Stomatal Opening</h3>

<p>Stomata can open or close depending on the conditions and the plant''s needs.</p>

<p>The opening is controlled by specialised cells called <strong>guard cells</strong>.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf needs carbon dioxide for photosynthesis.</p>

<p><strong>Through which structures can carbon dioxide enter?</strong></p>

<p>Through open stomata.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>Stomata have two important effects:</p>

<p><strong>Open stomata → gas exchange increases + water vapour loss can increase.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Closed stomata → gas exchange decreases + water loss decreases.</strong></p>
