<h2>Translocation in Phloem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p><strong>Translocation</strong> is the transport of organic assimilates, especially sucrose, through the phloem from sources to sinks.</p>

<h3>Source</h3>

<p>The source supplies assimilates.</p>

<p>A mature leaf producing sucrose by photosynthesis is a common source.</p>

<h3>Phloem</h3>

<p>Sucrose is loaded into the phloem and transported through sieve tubes.</p>

<h3>Sink</h3>

<p>The sink receives the assimilates.</p>

<p>The sucrose may then be used in respiration, growth or converted into storage substances.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis → sucrose produced → source → phloem loading → translocation → sink → use or storage.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A potato plant produces sugars in its leaves. Some of the assimilates are transported to underground tubers.</p>

<p><strong>Identify the source and sink.</strong></p>

<p>The leaves are the <strong>source</strong>.</p>

<p>The developing tubers are the <strong>sinks</strong>.</p>

<p>The movement of sucrose through phloem is <strong>translocation</strong>.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Translocation is not the same as transpiration.</p>

<p><strong>Transpiration → water vapour loss.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Translocation → movement of assimilates through phloem.</strong></p>
