<h2>Loading Sucrose into Phloem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Before sucrose can be transported through phloem, it must be loaded into the sieve tube system.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Sucrose Is Produced</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis in a source leaf produces carbohydrates.</p>

<p>These carbohydrates are converted into transportable sugars, especially sucrose.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Sucrose Reaches the Phloem</h3>

<p>Sucrose moves from photosynthetic cells towards the phloem.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Loading</h3>

<p>Sucrose is loaded into the phloem.</p>

<p>In many plants, active transport involving companion cells is important in this process.</p>

<p>Energy supplied by respiration supports active transport where movement occurs against a concentration gradient.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Phloem Sap Becomes More Concentrated</h3>

<p>Loading sucrose into the sieve tube increases the concentration of dissolved substances in the phloem.</p>

<p>Water can enter the phloem from nearby xylem by osmosis.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A source leaf is producing sucrose rapidly.</p>

<p><strong>Why might companion cells require energy?</strong></p>

<p>They may use energy from respiration to actively transport sucrose during phloem loading.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>Do not state that every step of phloem transport directly uses ATP.</p>

<p>Active loading and unloading processes can require energy, while pressure-driven bulk flow through the sieve tubes is a different part of the process.</p>
