<h2>Pressure Flow in Phloem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>One model used to explain movement of phloem sap is the <strong>pressure-flow hypothesis</strong>.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Sucrose Is Loaded at the Source</h3>

<p>Sucrose is loaded into sieve tubes at the source.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Water Enters the Phloem</h3>

<p>The increased solute concentration in the phloem lowers its water potential.</p>

<p>Water enters the phloem from nearby xylem by osmosis.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Pressure Increases</h3>

<p>Water entering the sieve tube increases hydrostatic pressure at the source.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Sap Moves Towards the Sink</h3>

<p>The pressure difference causes phloem sap to move through the sieve tube towards a region where sucrose is being removed.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Sucrose Is Unloaded</h3>

<p>At the sink, sucrose is removed from the phloem and used or stored.</p>

<p>Water potential in the phloem changes, and water can move away.</p>

<h3>Complete Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Sucrose loading → water enters → pressure rises → phloem sap flows → sucrose unloaded at sink.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>At a source, sucrose is actively loaded into the phloem.</p>

<p><strong>Why does water enter the phloem?</strong></p>

<p>Loading sucrose increases the solute concentration and lowers water potential, causing water to enter by osmosis.</p>

<h3>Key Point</h3>

<p>Pressure flow explains bulk movement through the phloem. It is different from simple diffusion of individual sucrose molecules over short distances.</p>
