<h2>Ringing Experiment and Phloem Transport</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A ringing experiment can provide evidence that phloem transports organic assimilates.</p>

<h3>What Is Ringing?</h3>

<p>A ring of bark is carefully removed from around a stem.</p>

<p>The removed region includes the tissues containing phloem.</p>

<p>The xylem is left intact.</p>

<h3>What Happens Above the Ring?</h3>

<p>After some time, assimilates can accumulate above the ring.</p>

<p>This happens because the phloem pathway has been interrupted.</p>

<p>Assimilates produced in leaves cannot move normally through the removed phloem to tissues below the ring.</p>

<h3>What Happens Below the Ring?</h3>

<p>Tissues below the ring may receive fewer assimilates from the leaves.</p>

<p>If the interruption continues, growth and storage below the ring can be affected.</p>

<h3>Why Is This Evidence for Phloem?</h3>

<p>The experiment links the removal of phloem-containing tissue with disruption of assimilate transport.</p>

<p>This supports the conclusion that phloem is involved in transporting assimilates.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A ring of bark is removed from a stem. Several days later, swelling is observed above the ring.</p>

<p><strong>What does this suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Assimilates are accumulating above the interruption because their normal phloem pathway has been disrupted.</p>

<h3>Important Limitation</h3>

<p>Ringing damages the plant and removes more than a single isolated cell type.</p>

<p>Therefore, the experiment should be interpreted carefully rather than claiming that it proves every detail of phloem transport.</p>
