<h2>Designing an Investigation of Phloem Transport</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An investigation of phloem transport should begin with a clear question and measurable evidence.</p>

<h3>Example Question</h3>

<p><strong>Does removing a ring of bark affect the accumulation of assimilates above the ring?</strong></p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>The treatment applied to the stem, such as whether a ring of bark is removed.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>A measurable response, such as change in stem diameter, mass, sugar concentration or another suitable indicator of assimilate accumulation.</p>

<h3>Control</h3>

<p>A similar plant or stem section that is not ringed can provide a comparison.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>Where possible, keep factors such as plant species, plant age, growing conditions, water supply and duration of the investigation similar.</p>

<h3>Repeats</h3>

<p>Using several plants or repeated measurements reduces the influence of unusual individual results.</p>

<h3>Conclusion</h3>

<p>The conclusion must be based on the evidence collected.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three ringed stems show greater sugar accumulation above the ring than three unringed control stems.</p>

<p><strong>What conclusion is reasonable?</strong></p>

<p>The results support the idea that interrupting phloem disrupts the normal movement of assimilates.</p>

<h3>Important Scientific Rule</h3>

<p>A good investigation changes one main factor while controlling other relevant factors.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1205, 1205, '
  <h2>Chloroplasts</h2>

  <h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Chloroplasts</b> are structures found in many plant cells and some
    other photosynthetic organisms.
  </p>

  <p>
    They contain the green pigment <b>chlorophyll</b>, which absorbs light
    energy used during photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Are Chloroplasts Important?</h3>

  <p>
    Plants use photosynthesis to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water
    using light energy.
  </p>

  <p>
    Chloroplasts provide the structures in which the light-dependent parts of
    this process occur and contain chlorophyll needed to absorb light.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Are Chloroplasts Common in Leaf Cells?</h3>

  <p>
    Leaves are major organs of photosynthesis.
    Their cells are therefore adapted to capture light and exchange gases
    needed for photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
    Palisade cells, for example, contain many chloroplasts because they are
    positioned where they can receive substantial amounts of light.
  </p>

  <h3>Do All Plant Cells Have Chloroplasts?</h3>

  <p>
    No.
    Different plant cells have different functions.
    A root hair cell, for example, normally does not contain chloroplasts
    because it is underground and is specialised mainly for absorbing water
    and mineral ions.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Question:</b> Why would a leaf cell usually contain many chloroplasts?
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 1:</b> Leaves are major sites of photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Step 2:</b> Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and are the structures
    associated with photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Conclusion:</b> Leaf cells need chloroplasts to capture light energy
    and carry out photosynthesis.
  </p>

  <h3>KEY IDEA</h3>

  <p>
    <b>Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and are the main sites of
    photosynthesis in plant cells.</b>
  </p>
  ', 1),
