<h2>Carbon Dioxide Is Required for Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide is a raw material for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>An experiment can test whether carbon dioxide is necessary for starch formation.</p>

<h3>Experimental Idea</h3>

<p>A destarched plant is placed in controlled conditions.</p>

<p>One leaf or part of a leaf is exposed to carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>Another comparable region is kept without available carbon dioxide.</p>

<p>After exposure to light, the leaf is tested for starch.</p>

<h3>Using Potassium Hydroxide</h3>

<p>Potassium hydroxide can absorb carbon dioxide from enclosed air.</p>

<p>It can therefore be used in a suitable experimental setup to create a carbon-dioxide-deficient condition.</p>

<h3>Expected Result</h3>

<p>The region supplied with carbon dioxide can form starch if other conditions are suitable.</p>

<p>The region deprived of carbon dioxide should form much less or no new starch.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A destarched plant is placed in light. Carbon dioxide is removed from the air surrounding one leaf using a suitable carbon dioxide absorber. The leaf region without carbon dioxide does not turn blue-black with iodine.</p>

<p><strong>What does this support?</strong></p>

<p>Carbon dioxide is required for starch formation through photosynthesis under the conditions tested.</p>

<h3>Important Control</h3>

<p>The experimental groups should receive similar light, temperature, water supply and other relevant conditions.</p>
