<h2>Photosynthesis and Respiration</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis and respiration are different processes, although the substances involved in one process are related to those involved in the other.</p>

<h3>Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis uses light energy to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water.</p>

<p><strong>Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen</strong></p>

<p>Photosynthesis stores energy in the chemical bonds of glucose.</p>

<h3>Respiration</h3>

<p>Respiration releases chemical energy from glucose.</p>

<p>In aerobic respiration, oxygen is used and carbon dioxide and water are produced.</p>

<p><strong>Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + energy</strong></p>

<h3>The Important Difference</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis is an energy-storing process.</p>

<p>Aerobic respiration is an energy-releasing process.</p>

<p>Photosynthesis requires light energy, while aerobic respiration does not require light.</p>

<h3>Do Plants Respire?</h3>

<p>Yes.</p>

<p>Plant cells respire continuously because they require released energy for processes such as active transport, growth and synthesis of substances.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A plant is placed in darkness.</p>

<p><strong>Does respiration stop?</strong></p>

<p>No. Respiration continues because plant cells still require energy.</p>

<p>However, photosynthesis cannot continue without a suitable source of light.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis → stores energy in glucose.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Respiration → releases energy from glucose.</strong></p>
  ', 1),
  (1231, 1231, '
<h2>Photosynthesis and Mineral Nutrition</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plants need mineral ions from the soil for healthy growth and for making important substances.</p>

<p>Mineral ions are not all raw materials of the photosynthesis equation, but some are essential for the structures and molecules that allow photosynthesis to occur effectively.</p>

<h3>Magnesium</h3>

<p>Magnesium is needed for chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>Chlorophyll absorbs light energy used in photosynthesis.</p>

<p>A magnesium deficiency can therefore reduce chlorophyll formation and cause leaves to become pale or yellow.</p>

<h3>Nitrate Ions</h3>

<p>Nitrate ions provide nitrogen.</p>

<p>Nitrogen is needed to make amino acids and proteins.</p>

<h3>Why Does This Affect Photosynthesis?</h3>

<p>A plant with severe mineral deficiencies may have poor growth and reduced photosynthetic capacity.</p>

<p>For example, inadequate magnesium can reduce chlorophyll production.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Two plants are grown under the same light intensity. One receives enough magnesium while the other receives very little.</p>

<p>The magnesium-deficient plant develops pale leaves.</p>

<p><strong>Explain why its photosynthetic performance may be reduced.</strong></p>

<p>Magnesium is needed for chlorophyll production. Less chlorophyll can reduce the plant''s ability to absorb light energy.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Do not say magnesium is a raw material in the balanced photosynthesis equation.</p>

<p>It is a mineral nutrient required for plant processes, including chlorophyll production.</p>
  ', 1),
