  (1235, 1235, '
<h2>What Happens to Glucose After Photosynthesis?</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis produces glucose, but the plant does not simply leave all of the glucose as free glucose inside the leaf.</p>

<p>Glucose can be converted into different substances depending on the plant''s needs.</p>

<h3>1. Respiration</h3>

<p>Glucose can be broken down during respiration to release usable energy.</p>

<h3>2. Starch Storage</h3>

<p>Glucose can be converted into starch for storage.</p>

<p>Starch is useful because it is relatively insoluble and can be stored without greatly affecting water potential.</p>

<h3>3. Cellulose</h3>

<p>Glucose can be used to make cellulose for plant cell walls.</p>

<h3>4. Amino Acids and Proteins</h3>

<p>Plants can use carbon compounds from glucose together with nitrogen-containing compounds to produce amino acids.</p>

<p>Amino acids can then be used to make proteins.</p>

<h3>5. Other Organic Compounds</h3>

<p>Glucose-derived carbon skeletons can contribute to the synthesis of other organic substances needed by the plant.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A leaf produces glucose during the day but does not immediately use all of it in respiration.</p>

<p><strong>What can happen to some of the remaining glucose?</strong></p>

<p>It can be converted into starch and stored.</p>

<h3>Exam Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Photosynthesis → glucose → respiration / starch / cellulose / other organic substances.</strong></p>
