<h2>Nitrate Deficiency Compared with Magnesium Deficiency</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Nitrate and magnesium are both essential mineral nutrients, but their main roles are different.</p>

<h3>Nitrate</h3>

<p>Nitrate provides nitrogen.</p>

<p>Nitrogen is required for amino acids and proteins.</p>

<p>Deficiency can cause reduced growth and yellowing or pale leaves.</p>

<h3>Magnesium</h3>

<p>Magnesium is required for chlorophyll production.</p>

<p>Deficiency can cause chlorosis and can reduce the plant''s capacity for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Do Not Confuse Them</h3>

<p>Both deficiencies can cause leaves to become yellow or pale.</p>

<p>Therefore, colour alone is not always enough to identify the deficiency.</p>

<p>The function of the mineral must also be considered.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Plant A has poor growth and pale leaves because it receives insufficient nitrate.</p>

<p>Plant B has pale leaves because it receives insufficient magnesium.</p>

<p><strong>What is the major difference in the underlying cause?</strong></p>

<p>Plant A lacks sufficient nitrogen for processes such as amino acid and protein production.</p>

<p>Plant B cannot produce chlorophyll normally because magnesium is required for chlorophyll production.</p>

<h3>Comparison</h3>

<p><strong>Nitrate → nitrogen → amino acids → proteins → growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Magnesium → chlorophyll production → light absorption → photosynthesis.</strong></p>
