<h2>Investigating Plant Growth Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An investigation can test how a factor affects the growth rate of a plant.</p>

<h3>Example Question</h3>

<p>Does temperature affect the growth rate of seedlings?</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>Temperature.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>Growth rate, measured from changes in root or shoot length over time.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>Keep the plant species, starting size, water supply, light conditions, nutrient supply and measurement period the same where appropriate.</p>

<h3>Method</h3>

<p>Place similar seedlings at different controlled temperatures.</p>

<p>Measure their length at regular intervals.</p>

<p>Calculate the growth rate.</p>

<p>Repeat using several seedlings at each temperature.</p>

<h3>Why Starting Size Matters</h3>

<p>If one group begins with much larger plants, differences in final length may be caused partly by the starting size rather than temperature.</p>

<h3>Why Repeats Matter</h3>

<p>Individual plants naturally vary.</p>

<p>Repeating measurements allows a mean response to be calculated.</p>

<h3>Conclusion</h3>

<p>Compare the measured growth rates rather than simply comparing final lengths.</p>
