<h2>Evaluating a Plant Growth Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A good biological experiment should produce evidence that allows a fair conclusion.</p>

<h3>Reliability</h3>

<p>Reliability can be improved by using repeats, several organisms and calculating means.</p>

<h3>Validity</h3>

<p>An investigation is valid when its design actually tests the question being asked.</p>

<p>For example, if the question is about temperature, other major growth conditions should not be changed systematically between groups.</p>

<h3>Control Group</h3>

<p>A control group provides a comparison against the experimental treatment.</p>

<h3>Standardisation</h3>

<p>Plants should be similar in species, age and starting size where possible.</p>

<h3>Measurement</h3>

<p>Use a consistent measurement method.</p>

<p>For example, measure root length from the same reference point each time.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student tests fertiliser concentration using one small plant at each concentration.</p>

<p>The plants are different species and receive different amounts of water.</p>

<p><strong>Give two weaknesses.</strong></p>

<p>First, using different species introduces biological variation.</p>

<p>Second, changing water supply creates another variable that could affect growth.</p>

<h3>Improvement</h3>

<p>Use several similar plants and keep water supply constant.</p>

<h3>Core Rule</h3>

<p>A good conclusion must be supported by a fair comparison.</p>
