<h2>Plant Growth Graph Analysis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Graphs allow plant growth to be compared across time or between treatments.</p>

<h3>Reading the Axes</h3>

<p>First identify what is plotted on each axis.</p>

<p>For a typical growth curve, time is placed on the horizontal axis and plant length or mass on the vertical axis.</p>

<h3>Gradient</h3>

<p>The gradient represents growth rate when growth is plotted against time.</p>

<p>A steep gradient means rapid growth.</p>

<p>A shallow gradient means slower growth.</p>

<h3>Plateau</h3>

<p>A horizontal section indicates little or no increase in the measured quantity during that period.</p>

