<h2>Root Hair Cells and Differentiation</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Root hair cells are specialised cells found near the growing regions of roots.</p>

<p>They develop through differentiation.</p>

<h3>Why Root Hair Cells Are Specialised</h3>

<p>Root hair cells absorb water and mineral ions from the soil.</p>

<p>Their long extension greatly increases the surface area available for absorption.</p>

<h3>How Their Structure Supports Function</h3>

<p><strong>Long hair-like extension:</strong> increases surface area in contact with soil.</p>

<p><strong>Thin surface:</strong> provides a short pathway for water movement.</p>

<p><strong>Cell membrane:</strong> controls movement of substances into the cell.</p>

<p><strong>Mitochondria:</strong> provide energy needed for active transport of mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Link to Differentiation</h3>

<p>The root hair cell does not simply become larger.</p>

<p>It develops a specialised structure that allows it to perform a particular function.</p>

<p>That is differentiation.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>If a question asks why a cell has a specialised structure, connect the structure directly to its function.</p>
