<h2>Plant Growth Processes: Complete Exam Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student examines cells at different positions behind a root tip.</p>

<p>Region A contains many cells undergoing mitosis.</p>

<p>Region B contains cells that are increasing rapidly in length.</p>

<p>Region C contains cells developing specialised structures, including root hairs.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify Region A</h3>

<p>The main process is cell division.</p>

<p>Therefore, Region A is the zone of cell division.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify Region B</h3>

<p>The cells are increasing in length.</p>

<p>Therefore, Region B is the zone of elongation.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Identify Region C</h3>

<p>The cells are developing specialised structures.</p>

<p>Therefore, Region C is the zone of differentiation.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Explain How They Work Together</h3>

<p>Cell division supplies new cells.</p>

<p>Cell elongation increases the size of those cells.</p>

<p>Cell differentiation produces specialised cells that perform particular functions.</p>

<h3>Model Answer</h3>

<p>Cells near the root tip divide by mitosis, increasing cell number. Further from the tip, newly produced cells elongate, increasing their size and contributing to root extension. Further still, cells differentiate and become specialised for functions such as absorption. These processes together allow the root to grow and develop specialised tissues.</p>

<h3>High-Value Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Division = more cells.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Elongation = larger cells.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Differentiation = specialised cells.</strong></p>
