<h2>From Stored Seed Reserves to Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A germinating seed initially depends on food reserves stored in the seed.</p>

<p>These reserves provide substrates for respiration and materials needed for early growth.</p>

<h3>Before Leaves Become Fully Photosynthetic</h3>

<p>The embryo cannot yet depend mainly on photosynthesis because the developing shoot may not have functional green leaves exposed to light.</p>

<p>Stored carbohydrates, proteins and lipids can therefore be mobilised and used by the developing seedling.</p>

<h3>Respiration Uses the Reserves</h3>

<p>Stored organic substances are broken down during respiration.</p>

<p>The released energy supports processes such as cell division, cell enlargement and active transport.</p>

<h3>After Leaves Develop</h3>

<p>Green leaves exposed to light can carry out photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Photosynthesis produces glucose from carbon dioxide and water.</p>

<p>The glucose can be used in respiration or converted into substances needed for growth and storage.</p>

<h3>The Transition</h3>

<p><strong>Stored reserves → respiration and early growth → leaves develop → photosynthesis increases → seedling becomes increasingly self-sufficient.</strong></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis does not replace respiration.</p>

<p>A growing plant continues to respire even after it begins photosynthesising.</p>
