<h2>Calculating Plant Growth Rate</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth can be measured by recording a change in length over a known period.</p>

<h3>Formula</h3>

<p><strong>Growth rate = change in length ÷ time taken</strong></p>

<p>First calculate the change:</p>

<p><strong>Change in length = final length − initial length</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>A root is initially 2 cm long and becomes 8 cm long after 3 days.</p>

<p>Change in length:</p>

<p>8 − 2 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>A shoot increases from 5 cm to 11 cm in 4 days.</p>

<p>Change:</p>

<p>11 − 5 = 6 cm</p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 cm/day</p>

<h3>Why Rate Is Useful</h3>

<p>Two plants may increase by the same amount but take different amounts of time.</p>

<p>The faster plant has the greater growth rate.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Always subtract the initial measurement before dividing by time.</p>
  
