<h2>Repeating a Plant Growth Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth varies naturally between individual plants.</p>

<p>Repeating measurements helps determine whether an observed pattern is consistent.</p>

<h3>Using Replicates</h3>

<p>Instead of using one seedling per treatment, several seedlings can be used.</p>

<p>The growth of each seedling can be measured.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three seedlings under one condition grow:</p>

<p>4 cm, 5 cm and 6 cm.</p>

<p>Mean growth:</p>

<p>(4 + 5 + 6) ÷ 3 = 15 ÷ 3 = 5 cm</p>

<h3>Why the Mean Helps</h3>

<p>The mean gives a summary of the measurements rather than relying on one individual plant.</p>

<h3>Reliability</h3>

<p>If repeated groups produce similar results, confidence in the consistency of the pattern increases.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Repeating an experiment does not remove systematic errors.</p>

<p>A consistently biased method can still produce consistent but inaccurate results.</p>
  
