<h2>Anomalous Results in a Plant Growth Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>An anomalous result is a measurement that does not fit the general pattern of the other results.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Five seedlings under the same conditions grow:</p>

<p>5 cm, 5.2 cm, 4.8 cm, 5.1 cm and 9.5 cm.</p>

<p>The 9.5 cm result is much higher than the others and may be anomalous.</p>

<h3>What Should the Student Do?</h3>

<p>The result should not simply be deleted because it is inconvenient.</p>

<p>The student should investigate possible causes.</p>

<h3>Possible Causes</h3>

<p>• measurement error</p>

<p>• damaged equipment</p>

<p>• a recording mistake</p>

<p>• an unusual biological difference</p>

<p>• an uncontrolled environmental difference</p>

<h3>Repeating the Measurement</h3>

<p>If possible, repeat the measurement or investigate the original plant.</p>

<p>The reason for the unusual result should be considered before deciding how to handle it.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>An anomalous result is not automatically an error.</p>

<p>It may represent genuine biological variation.</p>
  
