<h2>Investigating Temperature and Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Temperature affects the rate of enzyme-controlled reactions involved in germination.</p>

<h3>Experimental Design</h3>

<p>Place equal numbers of similar viable seeds under different temperature conditions.</p>

<p>Keep water and oxygen availability suitable.</p>

<p>Record germination over the same period.</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>Temperature.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>The number or percentage of seeds that germinate within a specified time.</p>

<h3>Why Temperature Matters</h3>

<p>At a low temperature, enzyme-controlled reactions may occur slowly.</p>

<p>As temperature increases within a suitable range, metabolic reactions can become faster.</p>

<p>At excessively high temperatures, proteins including enzymes can be damaged or denatured, reducing normal metabolic activity.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Seeds are tested at 10°C, 20°C, 30°C and 50°C.</p>

<p>If 30°C gives the highest percentage germination while 10°C and 50°C give lower values, the data suggest that the seeds have a more favourable temperature range around 30°C under those experimental conditions.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Do not assume one temperature is the optimum for every plant species.</p>

<p>The optimum must be determined from evidence for the species being tested.</p>
