<h2>Reliability in Germination Experiments</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Reliability refers to the consistency of results.</p>

<h3>Why One Seed Is Not Enough</h3>

<p>Individual seeds can differ in viability and development.</p>

<p>If only one seed is tested, an unusual seed could strongly influence the conclusion.</p>

<h3>Using Replicates</h3>

<p>Use several seeds in each treatment group.</p>

<p>Repeat the experiment where possible.</p>

<p>This provides more observations and reduces the influence of individual variation.</p>

<h3>Using a Mean</h3>

<p>When repeated numerical measurements are collected, calculate a mean to summarise the results.</p>

<p><strong>Mean = total of measurements ÷ number of measurements.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three repeated germination percentages are 70%, 80% and 75%.</p>

<p>Mean:</p>

<p>(70 + 80 + 75) ÷ 3 = 225 ÷ 3 = 75%</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Repeating an experiment improves reliability.</p>

<p>It does not automatically make the experiment valid.</p>

<p>The experiment must also test the intended variable fairly.</p>
