<h2>Germination Investigation: Complete Exam Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student investigates the effect of temperature on seed germination.</p>

<p>Four groups of 20 viable seeds are placed at different temperatures.</p>

<p>After seven days, the results are:</p>

<p>10°C → 5 seeds germinated</p>

<p>20°C → 12 seeds germinated</p>

<p>30°C → 18 seeds germinated</p>

<p>40°C → 10 seeds germinated</p>

<h3>Step 1: Calculate Germination Percentages</h3>

<p>10°C:</p>

<p>(5 ÷ 20) × 100 = 25%</p>

<p>20°C:</p>

<p>(12 ÷ 20) × 100 = 60%</p>

<p>30°C:</p>

<p>(18 ÷ 20) × 100 = 90%</p>

<p>40°C:</p>

<p>(10 ÷ 20) × 100 = 50%</p>

<h3>Step 2: Describe the Pattern</h3>

<p>Germination increases from 10°C to 30°C.</p>

<p>It decreases at 40°C.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Identify the Highest Tested Value</h3>

<p>The highest germination percentage is 90% at 30°C.</p>

<p>Therefore, 30°C was the most favourable temperature among those tested.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Explain the Pattern</h3>

<p>Temperature affects enzyme-controlled metabolic reactions involved in germination.</p>

<p>At low temperatures, these reactions can proceed slowly.</p>

<p>At excessively high temperatures, enzymes can be damaged or denatured.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Evaluate the Investigation</h3>

<p>The investigation should use the same seed species, similar viable seeds, equal water supply, adequate oxygen and the same duration.</p>

<p>Several seeds are already used per group, which is better than testing one seed.</p>

<p>Repeating the experiment would provide stronger evidence of reliability.</p>

<h3>Model Conclusion</h3>

<p>Under the conditions tested, germination increased as temperature rose from 10°C to 30°C, reaching 90% at 30°C, but decreased to 50% at 40°C. Therefore, 30°C was the most favourable temperature among those tested. The pattern is consistent with temperature affecting enzyme-controlled processes involved in germination.</p>
