<h2>Analysing Germination Failure</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>When seeds fail to germinate, the correct explanation must be based on evidence.</p>

<h3>Case 1: Seeds Are Completely Dry</h3>

<p>Without sufficient water, the seed cannot properly hydrate and activate the processes required for germination.</p>

<h3>Case 2: Seeds Have Water but Very Little Oxygen</h3>

<p>Respiration may be affected because aerobic respiration requires oxygen.</p>

<p>This can reduce the energy available for early growth.</p>

<h3>Case 3: Temperature Is Too Low</h3>

<p>Enzyme-controlled reactions may occur slowly, reducing the rate of metabolic processes involved in germination.</p>

<h3>Case 4: Temperature Is Excessively High</h3>

<p>High temperatures can damage enzymes and disrupt normal cellular processes.</p>

<h3>Case 5: The Seeds Are Not Viable</h3>

<p>A non-viable seed may fail to germinate even when water, oxygen and suitable temperature are provided.</p>

<h3>Case 6: The Seed Is Dormant</h3>

<p>Some viable seeds do not germinate immediately because dormancy prevents germination until particular conditions or internal changes occur.</p>

<h3>Exam Method</h3>

<p>Do not simply state "the seeds lacked food."</p>

<p>Identify the condition, connect it to a biological process, and then explain how that process affects germination.</p>

<p><strong>Condition → biological effect → effect on germination.</strong></p>
