<h2>Germination Over Time</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination can be studied by recording how many seeds have germinated at different times.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>Day 0 → 0 seeds</p>

<p>Day 2 → 4 seeds</p>

<p>Day 4 → 12 seeds</p>

<p>Day 6 → 18 seeds</p>

<p>Day 8 → 20 seeds</p>

<h3>What Does the Data Show?</h3>

<p>Germination increases with time during the period measured.</p>

<p>Between Day 0 and Day 6, more seeds become germinated.</p>

<p>By Day 8, all 20 seeds have germinated.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The number of seeds germinated measures cumulative germination.</p>

<p>It is not the same as the rate of germination.</p>

<h3>Calculating Percentage</h3>

<p>On Day 6:</p>

<p>(18 ÷ 20) × 100 = 90%</p>

<h3>Interpreting a Plateau</h3>

<p>If the number of germinated seeds stops increasing, it may mean that no additional seeds germinated during the remaining observation period.</p>

<p>This does not necessarily mean that all seeds were healthy or that germination can never occur later.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>Describe exactly what the data show before suggesting a biological explanation.</p>
