<h2>Calculating Germination Rate from Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination rate describes how quickly germination occurs over a specified time interval.</p>

<h3>Basic Calculation</h3>

<p><strong>Germination rate = change in number of germinated seeds ÷ change in time</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>After 2 days, 4 seeds have germinated.</p>

<p>After 6 days, 16 seeds have germinated.</p>

<p>Change in germinated seeds:</p>

<p>16 − 4 = 12 seeds</p>

<p>Change in time:</p>

<p>6 − 2 = 4 days</p>

<p>Rate:</p>

<p>12 ÷ 4 = 3 seeds per day</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>A group increases from 10 germinated seeds on Day 3 to 18 on Day 7.</p>

<p>Change = 18 − 10 = 8 seeds.</p>

<p>Time = 7 − 3 = 4 days.</p>

<p>Rate = 8 ÷ 4 = 2 seeds per day.</p>

<h3>Why the Time Interval Matters</h3>

<p>Using only the final number of germinated seeds tells you how many germinated, not how rapidly they germinated.</p>

<p>To calculate a rate, both a change in measurement and a change in time are required.</p>
