<h2>Conditions for Germination: Complete Reasoning</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Successful germination depends on the seed being viable and receiving suitable environmental conditions.</p>

<h3>The Three Core Conditions</h3>

<p><strong>Water:</strong> hydrates the seed and enables metabolic processes to become active.</p>

<p><strong>Oxygen:</strong> is required for aerobic respiration.</p>

<p><strong>Suitable temperature:</strong> allows enzyme-controlled reactions to occur at appropriate rates.</p>

<h3>Complete Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A student places viable seeds in three conditions:</p>

<p><strong>A:</strong> water + oxygen + suitable temperature.</p>

<p><strong>B:</strong> no water + oxygen + suitable temperature.</p>

<p><strong>C:</strong> water + very little oxygen + suitable temperature.</p>

<p>Group A is expected to show the strongest normal germination.</p>

<p>Group B cannot begin normal germination effectively because insufficient water prevents the required hydration and activation.</p>

<p>Group C may show reduced germination or early growth because limited oxygen restricts aerobic respiration.</p>

<h3>Exam Structure</h3>

<p>When explaining a germination result, identify the missing condition first.</p>

<p>Then state the biological process affected.</p>

<p>Finally explain how this affects germination or growth.</p>

<p><strong>Missing condition → affected process → effect on germination.</strong></p>

<h3>Final Reminder</h3>

<p>Suitable conditions do not guarantee that every seed germinates. Seed viability and dormancy can also affect the result.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1328, 1328, '
<h2>Radicle Emergence During Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The radicle is the embryonic structure that develops into the primary root.</p>

<p>During germination, the radicle commonly emerges from the seed before the shoot becomes fully established.</p>

<h3>Why the Radicle Emerges Early</h3>

<p>The developing seedling needs a way to obtain water and mineral ions from the surrounding environment.</p>

<p>Once the root system begins developing, the seedling can establish itself in the soil.</p>

<h3>What Happens After Emergence?</h3>

<p>The radicle grows downward and develops into the primary root.</p>

<p>Root hairs can later develop on suitable regions of the root.</p>

<p>These increase the surface area available for absorption.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A student observes a seed after several days.</p>

<p>A small structure has emerged from the seed and is growing downward.</p>

<p>This structure is the radicle.</p>

<p>It will develop into the primary root.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>Do not call the radicle the first leaf.</p>

<p>The radicle is part of the embryonic root system.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1329, 1329, '
<h2>Plumule Emergence During Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The plumule is the embryonic shoot of a seed.</p>

<p>As germination progresses, the plumule develops into the shoot system.</p>

<h3>What the Plumule Produces</h3>

<p>The developing shoot produces leaves and stem tissues.</p>

<p>Once suitable leaves become photosynthetically active, the seedling can increasingly produce its own organic food.</p>

<h3>Relationship with the Radicle</h3>

<p>The radicle develops into the root system.</p>

<p>The plumule develops into the shoot system.</p>

<p>These two embryonic structures therefore establish the two major parts of the young plant.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A germinating seed has produced a structure that grows upward and later develops leaves.</p>

<p>This structure is associated with the plumule and developing shoot.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The plumule is not the same as the mature shoot.</p>

<p>It is the embryonic shoot region that develops into the shoot.</p>
