<h2>Cotyledons and Early Seedling Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Cotyledons are seed leaves associated with the embryo inside a seed.</p>

<p>In many plants, cotyledons contain or provide access to stored food reserves that support early seedling growth.</p>

<h3>Why They Matter</h3>

<p>The young seedling initially depends heavily on stored nutrients because its photosynthetic leaves may not yet be fully developed.</p>

<p>These stored reserves provide materials and energy for early growth.</p>

<h3>After Germination</h3>

<p>As true leaves develop and photosynthesis becomes effective, the seedling increasingly obtains organic food through photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A germinating seedling grows before its first fully developed leaves can photosynthesise effectively.</p>

<p>The seedling can still grow because stored food reserves associated with the seed provide resources.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>Cotyledons are not always simply "food."</p>

<p>They are embryonic seed leaves and may have different roles depending on the plant.</p>

<p>For school-level reasoning, their important early role is associated with supplying stored reserves to the developing seedling.</p>
