  (1331, 1331, '
<h2>Seedling Establishment After Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Germination does not end when the radicle emerges. The young seedling must establish a root and shoot system that can obtain water, mineral ions and light.</p>

<h3>Step 1: The Radicle Develops</h3>

<p>The radicle develops into the primary root.</p>

<p>The root grows into the soil and begins absorbing water and mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Root Hairs Develop</h3>

<p>Root hair cells develop behind the growing root tip.</p>

<p>They increase the surface area available for absorption.</p>

<h3>Step 3: The Plumule Develops</h3>

<p>The plumule develops into the shoot.</p>

<p>The shoot grows towards the soil surface and eventually produces leaves.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Leaves Become Photosynthetic</h3>

<p>Once leaves are exposed to suitable light, chloroplast-containing cells can carry out photosynthesis.</p>

<p>The seedling increasingly produces its own organic food rather than depending entirely on stored seed reserves.</p>

<h3>Why Establishment Matters</h3>

<p>The seedling must connect two essential resources:</p>

<p><strong>Roots → water and mineral ions</strong></p>

<p><strong>Leaves → light and carbon dioxide</strong></p>

<p>Successful establishment allows the plant to continue growing after the seed''s stored reserves become insufficient.</p>
  ', 1),
