<h2>Why a Developing Seedling Needs Light</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Light becomes essential when a young plant begins relying on photosynthesis to manufacture organic food.</p>

<h3>What Light Provides</h3>

<p>Light supplies the energy required for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>In green plant cells, chlorophyll absorbs some of this light energy.</p>

<h3>Photosynthesis</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide and water are used to produce glucose, with oxygen released.</p>

<p><strong>6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</strong></p>

<h3>Why This Matters to a Seedling</h3>

<p>Glucose produced by photosynthesis can be used:</p>

<p>• in respiration to release energy</p>

<p>• to form cellulose for cell walls</p>

<p>• to form starch for storage</p>

<p>• to form other organic substances needed for growth</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>A seed can begin germination in darkness because early growth can use stored reserves.</p>

<p>However, prolonged darkness prevents normal dependence on photosynthesis because there is no light energy for photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Reasoning Chain</h3>

<p>Light → chlorophyll absorbs light energy → photosynthesis → glucose production → continued growth.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1333, 1333, '
<h2>Root and Shoot Development in a Seedling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A developing seedling must establish both a root system and a shoot system.</p>

<h3>Root System</h3>

<p>The root develops from the radicle.</p>

<p>It anchors the plant and absorbs water and mineral ions from the soil.</p>

<h3>Shoot System</h3>

<p>The shoot develops from the plumule.</p>

<p>It produces stems and leaves.</p>

<p>Leaves provide the main photosynthetic surface of the young plant.</p>

<h3>Why Both Systems Are Needed</h3>

<p>The root supplies water and mineral ions required for growth.</p>

<p>The shoot exposes leaves to light and carbon dioxide for photosynthesis.</p>

<p>Water absorbed by roots is transported through xylem to the shoot.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A seedling has healthy leaves but damaged roots.</p>

<p>Its photosynthesis may initially continue, but reduced root function limits water and mineral ion uptake.</p>

<p>Therefore, continued growth can eventually be affected.</p>

<h3>Another Example</h3>

<p>A seedling has healthy roots but its leaves receive insufficient light.</p>

<p>Water and mineral ions may still be absorbed, but photosynthesis is limited.</p>

<p>Therefore, production of new organic food is reduced.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>Successful seedling growth depends on coordinated development of both root and shoot systems.</p>
  ', 1),
