<h2>Root Hair Development in Young Seedlings</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>As the young root develops, some epidermal cells differentiate into root hair cells.</p>

<p>Root hairs are extensions of individual root epidermal cells.</p>

<h3>Why Root Hairs Develop</h3>

<p>Their main function is to increase the surface area available for absorption of water and mineral ions.</p>

<h3>Water Absorption</h3>

<p>Water enters root hair cells by osmosis when conditions provide a suitable water potential gradient.</p>

<h3>Mineral Ion Absorption</h3>

<p>Mineral ions may enter by diffusion when the gradient allows it.</p>

<p>When ions must be moved against a concentration gradient, active transport can be involved.</p>

<h3>Adaptations</h3>

<p>Root hair cells have:</p>

<p>• a long extension that increases surface area</p>

<p>• a thin cell surface that provides a short diffusion pathway</p>

<p>• transport proteins in the cell membrane</p>

<p>• mitochondria that provide energy for active transport</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning</h3>

<p>If root hairs are removed, the total absorbing surface area decreases.</p>

<p>This can reduce the plant''s ability to absorb water and mineral ions.</p>
