  (1337, 1337, '
<h2>First Leaves and Photosynthesis</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>As the shoot develops, the first leaves expand and become important photosynthetic organs.</p>

<h3>What the Leaves Need</h3>

<p>For photosynthesis, leaves require:</p>

<p>• light energy</p>

<p>• carbon dioxide</p>

<p>• water</p>

<p>• chlorophyll-containing cells</p>

<h3>Where the Materials Come From</h3>

<p>Carbon dioxide enters the leaf through stomata.</p>

<p>Water is transported to the leaf through xylem.</p>

<p>Light is absorbed by chlorophyll.</p>

<h3>Why This Changes the Seedling</h3>

<p>Early development depends heavily on stored food reserves.</p>

<p>Once functional leaves develop, photosynthesis provides a continuing source of glucose.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A seedling has healthy roots and leaves but is placed in complete darkness.</p>

<p>Its roots can still absorb water, but photosynthesis is prevented because there is no light energy.</p>

<p>Therefore, production of glucose by photosynthesis is severely limited.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Photosynthesis and respiration continue to be different processes.</p>

<p>The seedling uses respiration to release energy from organic substances, including glucose produced by photosynthesis.</p>
