<h2>Dependence on Seed Reserves During Early Growth</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Before the seedling develops enough photosynthetic leaf area, much of its growth depends on food stored in the seed.</p>

<h3>How Stored Food Is Used</h3>

<p>Stored carbohydrates, lipids and proteins can be mobilised into smaller substances.</p>

<p>These substances can support respiration and the synthesis of new materials.</p>

<h3>Energy for Growth</h3>

<p>Respiration releases energy from suitable organic substrates.</p>

<p>The energy supports:</p>

<p>• cell division</p>

<p>• cell enlargement</p>

<p>• active transport</p>

<p>• synthesis of new cellular materials</p>

<h3>As the Seedling Develops</h3>

<p>The cotyledons or storage tissues may become depleted as their reserves are used.</p>

<p>Meanwhile, leaves develop and photosynthesis becomes increasingly important.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A seedling has exhausted most of its stored reserves but has not developed functional leaves.</p>

<p>Its ability to continue obtaining organic food is limited because photosynthesis is not yet supplying enough glucose.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p>Stored reserves support early development; photosynthesis becomes increasingly important as the seedling establishes functional leaves.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1339, 1339, '
<h2>Cotyledon Depletion During Germination</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Cotyledons often contain or are associated with stored food reserves that support early seedling development.</p>

<h3>What Happens During Germination?</h3>

<p>Stored nutrients are mobilised and transferred to growing embryonic tissues.</p>

<p>As these reserves are used, the cotyledons can decrease in stored nutrient content.</p>

<h3>Why the Reserves Are Used</h3>

<p>The developing embryo needs materials and energy for growth.</p>

<p>Respiration uses suitable organic substrates to release energy.</p>

<h3>What Happens Later?</h3>

<p>As the first leaves become photosynthetically active, the seedling becomes less dependent on the stored reserves.</p>

<h3>Worked Reasoning Example</h3>

<p>A seedling''s cotyledons become smaller and eventually wither while new green leaves expand.</p>

<p>This is consistent with stored reserves being used during early development while the developing leaves increasingly take over food production through photosynthesis.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Cotyledons do not simply "turn into food."</p>

<p>They contain or are associated with reserves that are mobilised and used by the developing plant.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1340, 1340, '
<h2>Measuring Growth in a Young Seedling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth can be measured quantitatively rather than described only by appearance.</p>

<h3>Measuring Length</h3>

<p>A ruler can be used to measure the length of a shoot or root.</p>

<p>Measurements should be taken using the same method each time.</p>

<h3>Calculating Growth</h3>

<p><strong>Growth = final length − initial length</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>A shoot is 2.0 cm long at the beginning and 7.5 cm after five days.</p>

<p>Growth:</p>

<p>7.5 − 2.0 = 5.5 cm</p>

<h3>Calculating Growth Rate</h3>

<p><strong>Growth rate = change in length ÷ time</strong></p>

<p>Growth rate:</p>

<p>5.5 ÷ 5 = 1.1 cm/day</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>A root increases from 1.5 cm to 5.5 cm in four days.</p>

<p>Growth = 5.5 − 1.5 = 4.0 cm.</p>

<p>Growth rate = 4.0 ÷ 4 = 1.0 cm/day.</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Always include the correct units.</p>

<p>Length is measured in units such as cm.</p>

<p>Growth rate is measured in units such as cm/day.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1341, 1341, '
<h2>Comparing Seedling Growth Data</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Seedling growth should be compared using measurements rather than visual impressions alone.</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>Seedling A:</p>

<p>Initial height = 4 cm</p>

<p>Final height = 10 cm</p>

<p>Time = 3 days</p>

<p>Seedling B:</p>

<p>Initial height = 6 cm</p>

<p>Final height = 11 cm</p>

<p>Time = 3 days</p>

<h3>Step 1: Calculate Growth</h3>

<p>A: 10 − 4 = 6 cm</p>

<p>B: 11 − 6 = 5 cm</p>

<h3>Step 2: Calculate Growth Rate</h3>

<p>A: 6 ÷ 3 = 2 cm/day</p>

<p>B: 5 ÷ 3 ≈ 1.67 cm/day</p>

<h3>Step 3: Compare</h3>

<p>Seedling A had the greater growth rate over the three-day period.</p>

<h3>Why Final Height Alone Can Mislead</h3>

<p>Seedling B ended taller, but it started taller.</p>

<p>Comparing only final heights would therefore give an incomplete picture of growth.</p>

<h3>Exam Rule</h3>

<p>When asked which seedling grew faster, calculate change in length over the same time interval.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1342, 1342, '
<h2>Cell Division in a Growing Seedling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A seedling increases in size partly because cells divide to produce new cells.</p>

<h3>Where Does Cell Division Occur?</h3>

<p>Rapid cell division occurs in meristematic regions, such as the apical meristems near the tips of roots and shoots.</p>

<h3>What Happens During Mitosis?</h3>

<p>A parent cell divides its nucleus through mitosis to produce two genetically similar daughter nuclei.</p>

<p>The cytoplasm then divides, producing separate cells.</p>

<h3>Why This Increases Growth</h3>

<p>One cell can produce two cells.</p>

<p>Repeated cell division therefore increases the number of cells available to form new tissues.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A meristematic region contains 100 actively dividing cells.</p>

<p>If a group of cells divides and produces additional daughter cells, the total number of cells increases.</p>

<p>Those cells can later elongate and differentiate to form specialised tissues.</p>

<h3>Growth Sequence</h3>

<p><strong>Cell division → cell number increases → cells elongate → cells differentiate → tissues develop.</strong></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>Cell division alone does not explain all plant growth. Cells must also increase in size and become specialised.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1343, 1343, '
<h2>Cell Elongation in a Growing Seedling</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Cell division increases cell number, but cells must also increase in size for substantial growth to occur.</p>

<h3>What Is Cell Elongation?</h3>

<p>Cell elongation is an increase in the length or size of a plant cell.</p>

<p>It is especially important in regions just behind actively dividing cells in growing roots and shoots.</p>

<h3>How Does Elongation Occur?</h3>

<p>Water enters plant cells and contributes to cell expansion.</p>

<p>The cell wall can loosen sufficiently to allow expansion while the cell remains supported.</p>

<p>The vacuole can enlarge and occupy a large proportion of the cell.</p>

<h3>Why This Increases Plant Size</h3>

<p>When many cells elongate, the tissue becomes longer.</p>

<p>This contributes to the increase in root or shoot length.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A group of cells each increases from 0.2 mm to 0.8 mm in length.</p>

<p>Each cell has increased by:</p>

<p>0.8 − 0.2 = 0.6 mm</p>

<p>If many cells undergo similar elongation, the total length of the growing tissue increases substantially.</p>

<h3>Key Distinction</h3>

<p><strong>Cell division → more cells.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Cell elongation → larger cells.</strong></p>

<p>Both contribute to plant growth.</p>
