<h2>Interpreting Evidence from a Phototropism Experiment</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Experimental observations should be used to distinguish between possible explanations for plant growth.</p>

<h3>Example Data</h3>

<p>Four groups of seedlings are exposed to different conditions.</p>

<p><strong>Group A:</strong> light from all directions → shoot remains approximately upright.</p>

<p><strong>Group B:</strong> light from one side → shoot bends towards light.</p>

<p><strong>Group C:</strong> one-sided light but shoot tip removed → little or no normal phototropic response.</p>

<p><strong>Group D:</strong> one-sided light with the shoot tip covered by an opaque cap → reduced phototropic response.</p>

<h3>What Does This Suggest?</h3>

<p>Groups B, C and D suggest that the shoot tip plays an important role in detecting or coordinating the response to directional light.</p>

<h3>Why the Control Matters</h3>

<p>Group A shows what happens when there is no strong directional light.</p>

<p>Without this comparison, it would be harder to determine whether the bending was caused by directional light.</p>

<h3>Evidence vs Explanation</h3>

<p><strong>Evidence:</strong> the shoot bent towards the light.</p>

<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> directional light affects auxin distribution and therefore causes unequal growth.</p>

<p>The explanation should be consistent with the experimental evidence.</p>

<h3>Exam Skill</h3>

<p>Do not claim that an experiment proves every detail of the mechanism unless the experiment actually measured that mechanism.</p>

<p>An experiment showing bending provides evidence for a growth response. A separate investigation may be needed to directly measure auxin distribution.</p>
