<h2>Gravitropism in Shoots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Shoots generally show negative gravitropism.</p>

<p>Negative gravitropism means growth away from the direction of gravity.</p>

<h3>What Happens?</h3>

<p>When a shoot is placed horizontally, gravity causes an unequal distribution of auxin.</p>

<p>The resulting difference in cell elongation causes the shoot to curve upward.</p>

<h3>Why This Is Called Negative</h3>

<p>The shoot grows away from the direction in which gravity acts.</p>

<p>The term "negative" describes the direction of the growth response, not whether the response is harmful.</p>

<h3>Compare Root and Shoot Responses</h3>

<p><strong>Roots → positive gravitropism.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Shoots → negative gravitropism.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A seedling is placed horizontally.</p>

<p>After several hours, the root curves downward while the shoot curves upward.</p>

<p>The root is showing positive gravitropism.</p>

<p>The shoot is showing negative gravitropism.</p>

<h3>Exam Trap</h3>

<p>"Negative gravitropism" does not mean the shoot stops growing.</p>

<p>It means the direction of growth is away from gravity.</p>
