<h2>Growth Hormones in Plants</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Plant growth is controlled partly by chemical substances called plant growth hormones or plant growth regulators.</p>

<p>These substances can influence processes such as cell division, cell elongation, root and shoot growth, and responses to environmental stimuli.</p>

<h3>Auxin</h3>

<p>Auxin is an important plant hormone involved in growth, particularly cell elongation in shoots.</p>

<p>Auxin is produced in growing regions such as shoot tips.</p>

<h3>Unequal Distribution</h3>

<p>When light comes mainly from one direction, auxin becomes distributed unevenly in the shoot.</p>

<p>This contributes to unequal cell elongation and causes the shoot to bend.</p>

<h3>Why Hormones Matter</h3>

<p>Hormones coordinate growth rather than simply providing the material from which the plant is built.</p>

<p>They act as chemical signals that influence how cells behave.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A young shoot is exposed to light from one side and bends towards the light.</p>

<p><strong>What role does auxin play?</strong></p>

<p>Unequal distribution of auxin causes unequal growth of cells on the two sides of the shoot, producing bending.</p>

<h3>Key Idea</h3>

<p><strong>Hormone signal → altered cell behaviour → unequal growth → visible plant response.</strong></p>
