<h2>Auxin and Gravitropism in Roots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Auxin also contributes to the gravitropic response of roots.</p>

<p>When a root is placed horizontally, gravity causes auxin to become redistributed so that more auxin accumulates on the lower side of the root.</p>

<h3>Important Difference Between Root and Shoot</h3>

<p>Auxin does not have exactly the same growth effect in roots as it does in shoots.</p>

<p>In roots, a relatively high auxin concentration inhibits cell elongation.</p>

<p>The lower side therefore elongates less than the upper side.</p>

<h3>How Bending Occurs</h3>

<p>The upper side elongates more.</p>

<p>The lower side elongates less.</p>

<p>This unequal growth causes the root to curve downward.</p>

<h3>Complete Chain</h3>

<p><strong>Gravity → auxin redistribution → more auxin on lower side → reduced elongation there → greater elongation on upper side → root curves downward.</strong></p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A horizontal root begins to curve downward.</p>

<p><strong>Why does unequal auxin distribution cause this?</strong></p>

<p>More auxin accumulates on the lower side, where it inhibits elongation. The upper side elongates more, causing the root to bend downward.</p>

<h3>Critical Exam Point</h3>

<p>Do not apply the shoot rule directly to roots.</p>

<p>In shoots, auxin generally promotes elongation.</p>

<p>In roots, high auxin concentration can inhibit elongation.</p>
