<h2>Auxin and Apical Dominance</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Apical dominance is the tendency of the main shoot tip to suppress the growth of some lateral buds.</p>

<h3>Role of the Shoot Tip</h3>

<p>The shoot tip produces auxin.</p>

<p>Auxin contributes to the maintenance of apical dominance.</p>

<p>As a result, the main shoot can continue growing while some lateral buds remain less active.</p>

<h3>Removing the Shoot Tip</h3>

<p>If the shoot tip is removed, the major source of auxin from that region is also removed.</p>

<p>Lateral buds may then begin to grow more strongly.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A gardener cuts off the tip of a young shoot.</p>

<p>Several side branches subsequently develop.</p>

<p><strong>What biological explanation fits this observation?</strong></p>

<p>Removing the shoot tip reduces the auxin signal associated with apical dominance, allowing lateral buds to grow more strongly.</p>

<h3>Important Point</h3>

<p>Auxin is not simply a "growth on" chemical.</p>

<p>Its effect depends on where it is produced, its concentration, the tissue involved and interactions with other plant regulators.</p>
