<p>The amount of auxin applied must be appropriate.</p>

<p>More auxin does not automatically produce more roots.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Three groups of identical cuttings receive different auxin treatments.</p>

<p>Group A produces few roots.</p>

<p>Group B produces many healthy roots.</p>

<p>Group C produces fewer healthy roots than Group B.</p>

<p><strong>What does this suggest?</strong></p>

<p>Group B received a more suitable auxin treatment under the conditions tested.</p>

<h3>Experimental Control</h3>

<p>A group receiving no added auxin can be used as a control.</p>

<p>The number or mass of roots formed can then be compared between treatments.</p>
