<h2>Investigating the Effect of a Growth Hormone</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>The effect of a plant growth hormone can be investigated by applying different concentrations and measuring plant growth.</p>

<h3>Experimental Design</h3>

<p>Use similar plants or plant cuttings.</p>

<p>Apply different concentrations of the hormone.</p>

<p>Include a control group receiving no added hormone.</p>

<p>Keep other conditions as constant as possible.</p>

<h3>Independent Variable</h3>

<p>The concentration of the hormone.</p>

<h3>Dependent Variable</h3>

<p>A measurable growth response, such as increase in length or number of roots formed.</p>

<h3>Control Variables</h3>

<p>Use the same plant species, similar starting size, water supply, temperature, light conditions and experimental duration.</p>

<h3>Repeats</h3>

<p>Several plants should be used at each concentration.</p>

<p>The mean response can then be calculated.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>Five cuttings are tested at each auxin concentration.</p>

<p>After 14 days, the number of roots on each cutting is recorded.</p>

<p>The mean number of roots is calculated for each concentration.</p>

<p>The means are then compared.</p>

<h3>Why Mean Is Useful</h3>

<p>Individual cuttings can naturally differ.</p>

<p>Using several cuttings reduces the influence of unusual individual results.</p>
