<h2>Plant Growth Hormones: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student places identical young shoots in three conditions.</p>

<p><strong>Plant A:</strong> receives light from all directions.</p>

<p><strong>Plant B:</strong> receives light from one side.</p>

<p><strong>Plant C:</strong> receives light from one side, but the shoot tip is removed.</p>

<p>After several days:</p>

<p>Plant A remains approximately upright.</p>

<p>Plant B bends towards the light.</p>

<p>Plant C shows a greatly reduced bending response.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Identify the Response</h3>

<p>Plant B shows positive phototropism.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify the Hormone</h3>

<p>Auxin is involved in the growth response.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Explain Plant B</h3>

<p>Directional light causes unequal auxin distribution.</p>

<p>More auxin accumulates on the shaded side.</p>

<p>Cells on that side elongate more.</p>

<p>The unequal elongation causes the shoot to bend towards the light.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Explain Plant C</h3>

<p>Removing the shoot tip removes an important region involved in detecting and coordinating the response.</p>

<p>The normal phototropic response is therefore greatly reduced.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Explain Plant A</h3>

<p>Light comes from all directions rather than strongly from one side.</p>

<p>There is therefore no strong directional growth signal producing the same bending response.</p>

<h3>Complete Answer</h3>

<p>The young shoot responds to directional light by changing its growth pattern. Auxin becomes unevenly distributed, causing greater elongation on the shaded side. This makes the shoot bend towards the light. Removing the shoot tip reduces the normal response because the shoot tip is important in coordinating phototropic growth.</p>

<h3>Key Tixar Rule</h3>

<p>Do not memorise "auxin causes phototropism."</p>

<p>Understand the chain:</p>

<p><strong>Stimulus → hormone distribution → cell response → unequal growth → visible response.</strong></p>
