<h2>Secondary Growth in Roots</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Secondary growth can also increase the thickness of roots in plants capable of secondary growth.</p>

<p>This is particularly important in many woody plants.</p>

<h3>What Changes?</h3>

<p>Lateral meristems become active and produce additional tissues.</p>

<p>The root therefore increases in diameter.</p>

<h3>Why Root Thickness Matters</h3>

<p>A thicker root can provide greater structural support and contributes to the development of a larger transport system.</p>

<h3>Primary and Secondary Root Growth</h3>

<p>A young root can first extend in length through primary growth.</p>

<p>As the plant develops, some roots can also increase in thickness through secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Worked Example</h3>

<p>A woody tree has roots that extend deeper into the soil and also become thicker.</p>

<p><strong>Which growth processes explain the two changes?</strong></p>

<p>Extension in length is primary growth.</p>

<p>Increase in thickness is secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Key Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Longer root → primary growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Thicker root → secondary growth.</strong></p>
