  (1383, 1383, '
<h2>Primary and Secondary Growth: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A young tree is measured over several years.</p>

<p>Its height increases from 40 cm to 250 cm.</p>

<p>At the same time, the diameter of its stem increases from 1.5 cm to 8 cm.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Analyse Height</h3>

<p>The tree has increased substantially in length.</p>

<p>This is primary growth.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Analyse Stem Diameter</h3>

<p>The stem has become thicker.</p>

<p>This is secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Identify the Growth Regions</h3>

<p>Primary growth is associated mainly with apical meristems.</p>

<p>Secondary growth is associated mainly with lateral meristems such as the vascular cambium.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Explain Why Both Can Occur</h3>

<p>A plant does not have to choose between primary and secondary growth.</p>

<p>A developing woody plant can extend its roots and shoots while also increasing the thickness of stems and roots.</p>

<h3>Model Answer</h3>

<p>The increase in height represents primary growth because the shoot has increased in length. The increase in stem diameter represents secondary growth because the stem has increased in thickness. Primary growth occurs mainly at apical meristems, while secondary growth involves lateral meristems such as the vascular cambium.</p>

<h3>High-Value Exam Rule</h3>

<p><strong>Length → primary growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Thickness → secondary growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Apical meristem → primary growth.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Lateral meristem → secondary growth.</strong></p>
