  (1386, 1386, '
<h2>Secondary Growth in Woody Stems</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>Woody plants can continue increasing in stem thickness after primary growth has increased the length of the stem.</p>

<p>This thickening is mainly associated with secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Step-by-Step Process</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Lateral meristems remain capable of cell division.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The vascular cambium produces new secondary xylem and secondary phloem.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Repeated production adds tissue to the stem.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The diameter or girth of the stem increases.</p>

<h3>Why Is This Important?</h3>

<p>A thicker woody stem provides structural support as the plant becomes larger.</p>

<p>The additional vascular tissue also contributes to the plant''s transport capacity.</p>

<h3>Exam Application</h3>

<p>If an older tree has a much thicker trunk than a young stem, this difference is not explained simply by elongation.</p>

<p>The increase in trunk diameter results largely from secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Important Distinction</h3>

<p>The shoot apical meristem is mainly responsible for primary growth.</p>

<p>Lateral meristems are responsible for secondary growth.</p>
