<h2>Primary and Secondary Growth: Complete Reasoning Problem</h2>

<h3>DEEP NOTES</h3>

<p>A student measures a young woody plant over several months.</p>

<p>At the beginning:</p>

<p>Stem length = 15 cm</p>

<p>Stem diameter = 0.6 cm</p>

<p>At the end:</p>

<p>Stem length = 27 cm</p>

<p>Stem diameter = 1.0 cm</p>

<h3>Step 1: Calculate Change in Length</h3>

<p>27 − 15 = 12 cm</p>

<p>The stem increased in length by 12 cm.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Calculate Change in Diameter</h3>

<p>1.0 − 0.6 = 0.4 cm</p>

<p>The stem increased in diameter by 0.4 cm.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Identify the Growth Types</h3>

<p>The increase in length represents primary growth.</p>

<p>The increase in thickness represents secondary growth.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Link the Growth to Meristems</h3>

<p>Primary growth is associated mainly with apical meristems.</p>

<p>Secondary growth is associated with lateral meristems such as the vascular cambium.</p>

<h3>Step 5: Explain the Overall Result</h3>

<p>The plant became both longer and thicker because primary and secondary growth can occur during the development of a woody plant.</p>
  ', 1),
  (1391, 1391, '<h2>What Is a Noun?</h2>

<h3>1. UNDERSTANDING NOUNS</h3>
<p>A <b>noun</b> is a word that names a person, place, animal, thing, substance, quality, feeling, activity or idea.</p>
<p>Nouns help us identify what we are talking about. Without them, it would be difficult to communicate who did something, where it happened, or what we are describing.</p>

<h3>2. EXAMPLES OF NOUNS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>People:</b> teacher, learner, doctor, Amina</li>
<li><b>Places:</b> school, village, Kenya, Nairobi</li>
<li><b>Animals:</b> goat, lion, eagle</li>
<li><b>Things:</b> book, phone, chair</li>
<li><b>Substances:</b> water, salt, gold</li>
<li><b>Qualities:</b> honesty, kindness, courage</li>
<li><b>Feelings:</b> fear, joy, sadness</li>
<li><b>Ideas:</b> freedom, justice, peace</li>
<li><b>Activities:</b> swimming, reading, dancing</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. NOUNS IN SENTENCES</h3>
<pre>
The learner opened the book.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>learner</b> names the person performing the action.</li>
<li><b>book</b> names the thing receiving the action.</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Honesty builds trust.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>Honesty</b> names a quality.</li>
<li><b>trust</b> names a feeling or state of confidence.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. HOW TO IDENTIFY A NOUN</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the whole sentence to understand its meaning.</li>
<li>Ask what person, place, animal, thing, substance, quality, feeling, activity or idea is being named.</li>
<li>Check how the word functions in the sentence. A word''s role can change depending on its use.</li>
</ol>

<h3>5. WHY CONTEXT MATTERS</h3>
<p>The same word can perform different grammatical functions.</p>
<pre>
I drink water every morning.
The water is cold.
</pre>
<p>In both sentences, <b>water</b> is a noun because it names a substance.</p>
<pre>
We water the plants.
</pre>
<p>Here, <b>water</b> is a verb because it describes an action. Do not classify a word by its spelling alone; examine how it is used.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking that nouns only name physical objects. Abstract ideas such as justice are also nouns.</li>
<li>Assuming that every word after an article is a noun. In “a beautiful house”, “beautiful” is an adjective and “house” is a noun.</li>
<li>Classifying a word without reading the whole sentence.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
A noun names something. Its role in a sentence depends on how it is used.
</pre>', 1),
  (1392, 1392, '<h2>Personal Pronouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT IS A PERSONAL PRONOUN?</h3>
<p>A <b>personal pronoun</b> is a pronoun that refers to a speaker, the person or people being addressed, or someone or something being discussed.</p>
<p>Personal pronouns help avoid unnecessary repetition.</p>
<pre>
Amina is reading. Amina enjoys novels.
Amina is reading. She enjoys novels.
</pre>
<p><b>She</b> replaces the repeated name. The reader can identify who is meant from the previous sentence.</p>

<h3>2. PERSON AND NUMBER</h3>
<p>Personal pronouns are grouped according to person and number.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>First person:</b> the speaker. Singular: I; plural: we.</li>
<li><b>Second person:</b> the person or people addressed. Singular and plural: you.</li>
<li><b>Third person:</b> the person, animal or thing discussed. Singular: he, she, it; plural: they.</li>
</ul>
<p>English uses <i>you</i> for both one person and several people. The context shows which meaning is intended.</p>

<h3>3. SUBJECT PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>Subject pronouns commonly perform the subject role in a clause.</p>
<pre>
I study every evening.
You answered correctly.
He plays football.
She reads carefully.
It looks broken.
We are ready.
They arrived early.
</pre>
<p>In standard English, subject pronouns normally appear in the subject position. For example, write ''She and I went home'' rather than ''Her and me went home'' in a formal sentence where the pronouns are the subject.</p>

<h3>4. OBJECT PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>Object pronouns are used when the pronoun functions as an object of a verb or preposition.</p>
<pre>
The teacher called me.
I helped him.
We invited her.
The coach spoke to us.
The principal thanked them.
</pre>
<p>Common subject–object pairs include:</p>
<pre>
I → me
he → him
she → her
we → us
they → them
</pre>
<p><i>You</i> and <i>it</i> have the same form as subject and object pronouns.</p>

<h3>5. CHOOSING THE CORRECT FORM</h3>
<p>Ask what job the pronoun performs in the sentence.</p>
<pre>
She called him.
He called her.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, <i>she</i> performs the action and is the subject; <i>him</i> receives the action and is the object. The second sentence reverses their roles.</p>
<pre>
Between you and me, the plan may fail.
</pre>
<p>After the preposition <i>between</i>, use the object pronoun <i>me</i>, not the subject pronoun <i>I</i>.</p>

<h3>6. PRONOUNS MUST HAVE CLEAR REFERENCES</h3>
<pre>
Brian told Peter that he had won.
</pre>
<p>The pronoun <i>he</i> is ambiguous because it could refer to Brian or Peter. A clearer sentence identifies the intended person:</p>
<pre>
Brian told Peter, ''I have won.''
Brian told Peter that Peter had won.
</pre>
<p>Choose a pronoun only when the reader can reasonably identify whom or what it refers to.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''Me and Amina went home'' in formal standard English instead of ''Amina and I went home''.</li>
<li>Writing ''The teacher helped she'' instead of ''The teacher helped her''.</li>
<li>Using ''I'' after a preposition, as in ''between you and I'', in formal standard English.</li>
<li>Using a pronoun when two possible references make the sentence unclear.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Subject pronouns: I, you, he, she, it, we, they.
Object pronouns: me, you, him, her, it, us, them.
Choose the form according to its grammatical role and keep its reference clear.
</pre>', 1),
  (1393, 1393, '<h2>Main and Auxiliary Verbs</h2>
<p>A <strong>main verb</strong> expresses the central action, event or state in a clause. An <strong>auxiliary verb</strong>, also called a helping verb, works with a main verb to express features such as tense, aspect, voice, questions, negation or modality.</p>

<h3>Main verbs</h3>
<p>Main verbs carry the central meaning of the clause:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The learners <strong>study</strong> every evening.</em></li>
  <li><em>The baby <strong>slept</strong> peacefully.</em></li>
  <li><em>The room <strong>seems</strong> quiet.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Main verbs can describe actions (<em>write</em>, <em>run</em>), events (<em>happen</em>, <em>break</em>) or states (<em>know</em>, <em>belong</em>, <em>seem</em>).</p>

<h3>The primary auxiliary verbs</h3>
<p>The three primary auxiliaries are <strong>be</strong>, <strong>have</strong> and <strong>do</strong>. Their forms change according to tense and subject.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Be:</strong> am, is, are, was, were, be, being, been. It helps form continuous tenses and the passive voice.</li>
  <li><strong>Have:</strong> have, has, had. It helps form perfect tenses.</li>
  <li><strong>Do:</strong> do, does, did. It helps form many questions, negatives and emphatic statements in the simple present and simple past.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How auxiliaries work</h3>
<p><strong>Continuous aspect:</strong> <em>She is reading.</em> Is is the auxiliary; reading is the main verb in its -ing form.</p>
<p><strong>Perfect aspect:</strong> <em>They have finished.</em> Have is the auxiliary; finished is the main verb in its past participle form.</p>
<p><strong>Passive voice:</strong> <em>The letter was written by Ali.</em> Was is the auxiliary; written is the main verb in its past participle form.</p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> <em>Do you understand?</em> Do helps form the question; understand is the main verb.</p>
<p><strong>Negative:</strong> <em>He does not agree.</em> Does helps form the negative; agree is the main verb.</p>

<h3>Modal auxiliary verbs</h3>
<p>Modals include <em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will</em> and <em>would</em>. They express meanings such as ability, possibility, obligation, advice or willingness.</p>
<p>Examples: <em>She can swim.</em> <em>You should revise.</em> <em>They might arrive later.</em></p>
<p>A modal is followed by the base form of the main verb: <em>She can swim</em>, not <em>She can swims</em> or <em>She can to swim</em>.</p>

<h3>One verb can serve different roles</h3>
<p><em>They have a car.</em> Here, have is the main verb because it expresses possession.</p>
<p><em>They have bought a car.</em> Here, have is an auxiliary because it helps form the present perfect; bought is the main verb.</p>
<p><em>She is happy.</em> Here, is links the subject to a description and functions as the main linking verb.</p>
<p><em>She is singing.</em> Here, is is an auxiliary helping the main verb singing.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>Does she likes tea?</em> Write <em>Does she like tea?</em> The auxiliary does carries the tense, so the main verb uses its base form.</li>
  <li><em>He can plays.</em> Write <em>He can play.</em></li>
  <li>Calling every form of be an auxiliary. It can also function as the main linking verb.</li>
  <li>Confusing the main verb''s meaning with the auxiliary''s grammatical job.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1394, 1394, '<h2>Types of Adjectives</h2>
<p>An <strong>adjective</strong> describes a noun or pronoun, or helps identify which person or thing is meant. Adjectives can communicate qualities, quantities, ownership and other information.</p>

<h3>1. Descriptive adjectives</h3>
<p>Descriptive adjectives tell us what a person, place or thing is like.</p>
<ul>
  <li>a <em>careful</em> driver</li>
  <li>a <em>narrow</em> road</li>
  <li>a <em>useful</em> explanation</li>
  <li>a <em>bright</em> classroom</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>a careful driver</em>, the adjective <em>careful</em> describes the noun <em>driver</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Quantitative adjectives</h3>
<p>Quantitative adjectives indicate an amount or quantity.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>some</em> water</li>
  <li><em>little</em> time</li>
  <li><em>enough</em> food</li>
  <li><em>much</em> effort</li>
</ul>
<p>Traditional school grammar often calls these quantitative adjectives. In modern grammar, many words in this group are classified as determiners when they introduce a noun. Their function in the sentence is what matters.</p>

<h3>3. Numeral adjectives</h3>
<p>Numeral adjectives express number or order.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>three</em> books — a cardinal number.</li>
  <li><em>five</em> learners — a cardinal number.</li>
  <li>the <em>first</em> lesson — an ordinal number.</li>
  <li>the <em>third</em> attempt — an ordinal number.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cardinal numbers answer ''how many?'' while ordinal numbers indicate position or order.</p>

<h3>4. Demonstrative adjectives</h3>
<p>Demonstrative words identify which noun is meant.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>This</em> book is useful.</li>
  <li><em>That</em> building is old.</li>
  <li><em>These</em> questions are difficult.</li>
  <li><em>Those</em> trees need water.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use <em>this</em> and <em>that</em> with singular nouns, and <em>these</em> and <em>those</em> with plural nouns.</p>
<p>Compare: <em>This is useful.</em> Here, <em>this</em> stands alone as a pronoun. In <em>This book is useful</em>, it introduces the noun <em>book</em> and functions as a determiner in modern grammar.</p>

<h3>5. Possessive adjectives</h3>
<p>Possessive adjectives, also called possessive determiners in modern grammar, show whose noun is being referred to.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>my</em> notebook</li>
  <li><em>your</em> pencil</li>
  <li><em>his</em> uniform</li>
  <li><em>her</em> bag</li>
  <li><em>our</em> classroom</li>
  <li><em>their</em> teacher</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not confuse possessive determiners with possessive pronouns.</p>
<p>Determiner: <em>This is my book.</em></p>
<p>Pronoun: <em>This book is mine.</em></p>

<h3>6. Interrogative adjectives</h3>
<p>In traditional grammar, <em>which</em> and <em>what</em> are called interrogative adjectives when they introduce a noun in a question.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Which subject</em> do you prefer?</li>
  <li><em>What time</em> does the bus arrive?</li>
</ul>
<p>Compare: <em>Which do you prefer?</em> In this sentence, <em>which</em> stands alone and functions as a pronoun.</p>

<h3>7. Participial adjectives</h3>
<p>Some adjectives are formed from verbs and end in <em>-ing</em> or <em>-ed</em>, among other forms.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The lesson was <em>interesting</em>.</li>
  <li>The learners were <em>interested</em>.</li>
  <li>The <em>broken</em> window needs repair.</li>
</ul>
<p>In many contexts, <em>-ing</em> describes what causes a feeling, while <em>-ed</em> describes the person or thing experiencing it.</p>

<h3>8. Identify an adjective by its function</h3>
<p>Ask what word the adjective describes or helps identify. In <em>The exhausted players entered the field</em>, <em>exhausted</em> describes <em>players</em>. In <em>The players were exhausted</em>, it describes the players after the linking verb <em>were</em>.</p>
<p>Not every word before a noun is necessarily an adjective. Words such as <em>the</em>, <em>my</em> and <em>those</em> are generally classified as determiners in modern grammar, even though older school grammar may group some of them under adjectives.</p>', 1),
  (1395, 1395, '<h2>Types of Adverbs</h2>
<p>An <strong>adverb</strong> can modify a verb, an adjective, another adverb or a whole clause. It often tells us how, when, where or how often something happens, or to what degree something is true.</p>

<h3>1. Adverbs of manner</h3>
<p>Adverbs of manner describe how an action is performed.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The learner answered <em>correctly</em>.</li>
  <li>The driver moved <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The baby slept <em>peacefully</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many adverbs of manner end in <em>-ly</em>, but not all do. In <em>She runs fast</em>, <em>fast</em> is an adverb, while <em>fast</em> can also be an adjective in <em>a fast runner</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Adverbs of time</h3>
<p>Adverbs of time tell us when something happens or how long it continues.</p>
<ul>
  <li>We arrived <em>yesterday</em>.</li>
  <li>The lesson will begin <em>soon</em>.</li>
  <li>She waited <em>briefly</em>.</li>
  <li>They have <em>already</em> left.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Adverbs of place and direction</h3>
<p>These adverbs tell us where an action happens or where something moves.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Please wait <em>outside</em>.</li>
  <li>The children looked <em>everywhere</em>.</li>
  <li>He moved <em>forward</em>.</li>
  <li>The bird flew <em>away</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Adverbs of frequency</h3>
<p>Adverbs of frequency indicate how often something happens.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>always</em></li>
  <li><em>usually</em></li>
  <li><em>often</em></li>
  <li><em>sometimes</em></li>
  <li><em>rarely</em></li>
  <li><em>never</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Frequency adverbs usually come before a main verb:</p>
<p><em>She often reads in the evening.</em></p>
<p>They usually come after the verb <em>be</em> when it is the main verb:</p>
<p><em>She is often early.</em></p>
<p>With an auxiliary verb, they commonly appear after the first auxiliary:</p>
<p><em>She has never missed a lesson.</em></p>

<h3>5. Adverbs of degree</h3>
<p>Adverbs of degree indicate intensity or extent. They often modify adjectives, other adverbs or verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The water is <em>very</em> cold. — modifies an adjective.</li>
  <li>She answered <em>extremely</em> quickly. — modifies an adverb.</li>
  <li>I <em>almost</em> fell. — modifies the verb phrase.</li>
  <li>The work is <em>quite</em> difficult. — modifies an adjective.</li>
</ul>
<p>Words such as <em>very</em>, <em>too</em>, <em>almost</em>, <em>enough</em> and <em>extremely</em> can express degree, but they do not all work in exactly the same way.</p>

<h3>6. Adverbs of certainty</h3>
<p>These adverbs show how certain the speaker is about a statement.</p>
<ul>
  <li>She will <em>probably</em> arrive early.</li>
  <li>He has <em>certainly</em> improved.</li>
  <li>They will <em>possibly</em> attend.</li>
</ul>
<p>The choice of adverb changes the speaker''s level of confidence.</p>

<h3>7. Adverbs do not always end in -ly</h3>
<p>Common adverbs without <em>-ly</em> include <em>fast</em>, <em>hard</em>, <em>late</em>, <em>well</em> and <em>often</em>.</p>
<p>Be careful with pairs that have different meanings:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Hard</em> means with effort: She worked hard.</li>
  <li><em>Hardly</em> means almost not: She hardly worked.</li>
  <li><em>Late</em> means after the expected time: He arrived late.</li>
  <li><em>Lately</em> means recently: Have you seen him lately?</li>
  <li><em>Good</em> is usually an adjective: She is a good singer.</li>
  <li><em>Well</em> is often an adverb of manner: She sings well.</li>
</ul>

<h3>8. Identify what the adverb modifies</h3>
<p>Do not classify an adverb by its spelling alone. Ask what it modifies and what meaning it adds.</p>
<p>In <em>The extremely careful driver stopped suddenly</em>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Extremely</em> modifies the adjective <em>careful</em>.</li>
  <li><em>Suddenly</em> modifies the verb <em>stopped</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both are adverbs, but they perform different functions.</p>', 1),
  (1396, 1396, '<h2>Articles: A, An and The</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT ARE ARTICLES?</h3>
<p><b>A</b>, <b>an</b> and <b>the</b> are articles. Articles come before nouns or noun phrases and help show whether we mean something generally or a particular thing.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>A</b> and <b>an</b> are indefinite articles. They commonly refer to one singular countable thing that is not identified as a particular known item.</li>
<li><b>The</b> is the definite article. It identifies a particular person or thing that the speaker expects the listener or reader to recognise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. WHEN TO USE A</h3>
<p>Use <b>a</b> before a singular countable noun beginning with a consonant sound.</p>
<pre>
a book
a teacher
a university
a European country
</pre>
<p>The rule depends on sound, not merely on the first written letter. <i>University</i> begins with a /juː/ sound, similar to ''you'', so it takes <i>a</i>.</p>

<h3>3. WHEN TO USE AN</h3>
<p>Use <b>an</b> before a singular countable noun beginning with a vowel sound.</p>
<pre>
an apple
an orange
an umbrella
an hour
an honest person
</pre>
<p>In <i>hour</i> and <i>honest</i>, the initial h is silent, so the words begin with a vowel sound. That is why they take <i>an</i>.</p>
<p>Compare:</p>
<pre>
a university
an umbrella
a useful tool
an unusual event
</pre>
<p>Always listen for the first sound of the word that follows the article.</p>

<h3>4. WHEN TO USE THE</h3>
<p>Use <b>the</b> when the person or thing is identifiable from the context, has already been mentioned, or is understood to be specific.</p>
<pre>
I saw a dog. The dog was barking.
Please close the door.
The sun provides light.
</pre>
<ul>
<li>In the first sentence, <i>a dog</i> introduces a dog that has not yet been identified.</li>
<li>In the next sentence, <i>the dog</i> refers to the same dog, now known from the conversation.</li>
<li>In ''Please close the door'', the speaker expects the listener to know which door is meant.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. WHEN NO ARTICLE IS NEEDED</h3>
<p>English often uses no article when speaking generally about plural countable nouns or uncountable nouns.</p>
<pre>
Books can broaden knowledge.
Water is necessary for life.
Learners need practice.
</pre>
<p>Here, the nouns refer to things in general rather than particular identified items.</p>
<p>However, use <i>the</i> when the meaning is specific:</p>
<pre>
The books on the table are mine.
The water in this bottle is cold.
</pre>

<h3>6. ARTICLES AND COUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>In ordinary use, a singular countable noun usually needs a determiner, such as an article or a possessive word.</p>
<pre>
I bought a pen.
I bought the pen.
I bought my pen.
</pre>
<p>All three are grammatical, but they communicate different information. <i>A pen</i> introduces a non-specific pen, <i>the pen</i> identifies a particular pen, and <i>my pen</i> identifies one associated with the speaker.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''an university'' because the word begins with the letter u. The initial sound is /j/, so use ''a university''.</li>
<li>Writing ''a hour'' instead of ''an hour''. The h is silent.</li>
<li>Using ''the'' for every general statement, such as ''The water is essential for life'' when speaking about water generally.</li>
<li>Leaving a singular countable noun without a determiner in an ordinary sentence, such as ''I bought book''. Write ''I bought a book''.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
A = before a consonant sound.
An = before a vowel sound.
The = for an identifiable or specific noun.
No article = often used for general plural or uncountable nouns.
</pre>', 1),
  (1397, 1397, '<h2>Prepositions of Time</h2>
<p>Prepositions of time show the relationship between an event and a point or period in time. The most common choices include <strong>at, on</strong> and <strong>in</strong>, but other prepositions communicate duration, starting points, deadlines and sequence.</p>

<h3>1. At: precise times and points</h3>
<p>Use <em>at</em> for a specific time on the clock and certain established time expressions.</p>
<ul>
  <li>at 7:30 a.m.</li>
  <li>at noon</li>
  <li>at midnight</li>
  <li>at sunrise</li>
  <li>at night</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The lesson begins at eight o''clock.</em></p>
<p><em>We arrived at midnight.</em></p>

<h3>2. On: days and dates</h3>
<p>Use <em>on</em> with days, dates and particular days or parts of days.</p>
<ul>
  <li>on Monday</li>
  <li>on 12 June</li>
  <li>on my birthday</li>
  <li>on Monday morning</li>
  <li>on the first day of the term</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The examination begins on Friday.</em></p>
<p><em>We travelled on 12 June.</em></p>
<p>Do not normally use <em>on</em> before a day name when using a general expression such as <em>every Monday</em>.</p>

<h3>3. In: months, years and longer periods</h3>
<p>Use <em>in</em> for months, years, seasons and many longer periods.</p>
<ul>
  <li>in October</li>
  <li>in 2026</li>
  <li>in the dry season</li>
  <li>in the twentieth century</li>
  <li>in the morning</li>
  <li>in the afternoon</li>
  <li>in the evening</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The school opened in January.</em></p>
<p><em>We usually revise in the evening.</em></p>

<h3>4. Important exceptions and expressions</h3>
<p>English uses established expressions that must be learned as complete phrases.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>at night</em>, but <em>in the evening</em>.</li>
  <li><em>at the weekend</em> is common in British English, including Kenyan English usage; <em>on the weekend</em> is common in American English.</li>
  <li><em>in time</em> means early enough for something; <em>on time</em> means at the scheduled or expected time.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>We arrived on time for the examination.</em></p>
<p><em>We arrived in time to revise before the examination began.</em></p>

<h3>5. Since and for</h3>
<p><strong>Since</strong> identifies the starting point of an activity or state continuing to a later reference point.</p>
<p><em>She has lived here since 2022.</em></p>
<p><strong>For</strong> identifies the length of time.</p>
<p><em>She has lived here for four years.</em></p>
<p>Compare:</p>
<ul>
  <li>since Monday — a starting point.</li>
  <li>for three days — a duration.</li>
  <li>since last year — a starting point.</li>
  <li>for several months — a duration.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. By and until</h3>
<p><strong>By</strong> means no later than a stated time or deadline.</p>
<p><em>Submit your assignment by Friday.</em></p>
<p>The assignment must be submitted on Friday or earlier.</p>
<p><strong>Until</strong> indicates that an activity or state continues up to a point in time.</p>
<p><em>The library will remain open until six o''clock.</em></p>
<p>The library closes at six, and the period of opening continues up to that time.</p>

<h3>7. During, before and after</h3>
<p><strong>During</strong> identifies a period in which something happens:</p>
<p><em>Do not use your phone during the examination.</em></p>
<p><strong>Before</strong> indicates an earlier time:</p>
<p><em>Wash your hands before eating.</em></p>
<p><strong>After</strong> indicates a later time:</p>
<p><em>We discussed the results after the meeting.</em></p>
<p>Use a noun phrase after these prepositions: <em>before lunch</em>, <em>after the lesson</em>, <em>during the holiday</em>.</p>

<h3>8. Avoid common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>in Monday</em>. Correct: <em>on Monday</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>at 2026</em>. Correct: <em>in 2026</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>since three years</em>. Correct: <em>for three years</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>for 2022</em> when identifying the starting point of an ongoing state. Correct: <em>since 2022</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>Submit it until Friday</em> when Friday is a deadline. Correct: <em>Submit it by Friday</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The correct preposition depends on the time relationship being expressed, not simply on the noun that follows it.</p>', 1),
  (1398, 1398, '<h2>Linking Words and Logical Relationships</h2>
<p>Linking words connect ideas and show how those ideas relate. They help a reader follow the reasoning in a paragraph and help a writer express relationships clearly.</p>

<h3>1. Addition</h3>
<p>Use <strong>and, also, furthermore, moreover</strong> and <strong>in addition</strong> to add information.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The library has reference books. In addition, it provides access to digital resources.</p>

<h3>2. Contrast</h3>
<p>Use <strong>but, however, although, whereas</strong> and <strong>nevertheless</strong> to show a difference or an unexpected result.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Although the road was muddy, the bus arrived on time.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The first plan was expensive. However, the second plan was affordable.</p>

<h3>3. Cause and result</h3>
<p>Use <strong>because, since</strong> and <strong>as</strong> to introduce a reason. Use <strong>so, therefore, consequently</strong> and <strong>as a result</strong> to introduce a result.</p>
<p><em>Reason:</em> The match was postponed because the field was flooded.</p>
<p><em>Result:</em> The field was flooded; therefore, the match was postponed.</p>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> Do not use <em>because</em> and <em>therefore</em> together to connect the same two clauses in one ordinary sentence: “Because the field was flooded, the match was postponed” is correct. So is “The field was flooded; therefore, the match was postponed.”</p>

<h3>4. Sequence and time</h3>
<p>Use <strong>first, next, then, afterwards, finally, before</strong> and <strong>meanwhile</strong> to show order or timing.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> First, collect the materials. Next, measure the liquid. Finally, record the result.</p>

<h3>5. Examples and clarification</h3>
<p>Use <strong>for example, for instance, such as</strong> and <strong>in other words</strong> to illustrate or clarify an idea.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Some renewable energy sources, such as wind and solar power, produce electricity without burning fossil fuels during generation.</p>

<h3>6. Purpose and condition</h3>
<p>Use <strong>so that</strong> to express purpose and <strong>if, unless</strong> or <strong>provided that</strong> to express a condition.</p>
<p><em>Purpose:</em> She labelled the containers so that everyone could identify their contents.</p>
<p><em>Condition:</em> You will understand the procedure if you follow each step carefully.</p>

<h3>How to choose the right linker</h3>
<p>Do not choose a linking word merely because it sounds formal. Identify the relationship between the ideas first. Ask: Am I adding information, contrasting ideas, giving a reason, showing a result, ordering events, providing an example, expressing purpose or setting a condition?</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using <em>however</em> as though it were an ordinary coordinating conjunction: “The road was flooded, however we continued” is incorrectly punctuated. Write “The road was flooded; however, we continued.”</li>
<li>Using a result linker when the second idea is actually a reason.</li>
<li>Adding linkers that do not express a genuine relationship, making writing awkward.</li>
<li>Repeating the same linker in every sentence instead of choosing one that fits the meaning.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1399, 1399, '<h2>Interjections</h2>
<h3>1. UNDERSTANDING INTERJECTIONS</h3>
<p>An <b>interjection</b> is a word or short phrase used to express sudden emotion, feeling, surprise, or exclamation. It usually stands outside the main grammatical structure of a sentence.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Joy/Delight:</b> Hurray! Wow! Yay!</li>
<li><b>Pain/Discomfort:</b> Ouch! Ah! Oh!</li>
<li><b>Surprise/Astonishment:</b> Gosh! Heavens! Oh no!</li>
<li><b>Hesitation/Attention:</b> Um, er, hey, shh, look!</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. PUNCTUATION RULES</h3>
<p>Use an <b>exclamation mark (!)</b> for strong emotions (<i>Ouch! That hurt.</i>) and a <b>comma (,)</b> for mild expressions (<i>Well, let us start.</i>).</p>', 1),
  (1400, 1400, '<h2>Common and Proper Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. THE BASIC DIFFERENCE</h3>
<p>Nouns can name members of a general class or identify a particular person, place, organisation or other named entity.</p>
<ul>
<li>A <b>common noun</b> names a general person, place, animal, thing or category.</li>
<li>A <b>proper noun</b> gives the specific name of a particular person, place, organisation, institution or other named entity.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. COMPARE THE EXAMPLES</h3>
<pre>
Common noun       Proper noun
country           Kenya
city              Nairobi
river             Tana River
learner           Wanjiku
school            Kenyatta High School
month             October
language          Kiswahili
</pre>
<p>''Country'' refers to a general category. ''Kenya'' identifies one particular country. The distinction is about general naming versus specific naming.</p>

<h3>3. CAPITAL LETTERS</h3>
<p>Proper nouns normally begin with capital letters. Common nouns normally begin with lowercase letters unless they start a sentence or form part of a title or name.</p>
<pre>
We visited Mombasa.
Mombasa is a city.
</pre>
<p><b>Mombasa</b> is capitalised because it is a proper noun. <b>city</b> is lowercase because it is a common noun in this sentence.</p>

<h3>4. NAMES WITH MORE THAN ONE WORD</h3>
<p>A proper noun can contain several words. Capitalisation depends on the name and its accepted spelling.</p>
<pre>
Mount Kenya
Lake Victoria
Kenyatta University
East African Community
</pre>
<p>Do not assume that a proper noun must be a single word. Recognise the complete name.</p>

<h3>5. CONTEXT CAN CHANGE THE CLASSIFICATION</h3>
<pre>
My mother is a teacher.
Mother Teresa helped many people.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, <b>mother</b> describes a family relationship and is used as a common noun. In the second, <b>Mother Teresa</b> is a person''s name, so the title forms part of a proper noun.</p>
<p>Similarly, ''president'' is usually a common noun, but capitalisation can vary when it is used as a formal title before a person''s name or according to a publication''s style.</p>

<h3>6. A SIMPLE TEST</h3>
<ul>
<li>Does the word identify a particular named entity? It is likely a proper noun.</li>
<li>Does it name a general type or category? It is likely a common noun.</li>
<li>Check the whole phrase, not just the first word.</li>
</ul>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''kenya'' instead of ''Kenya''.</li>
<li>Capitalising every common noun, such as ''Teacher'' in the middle of a sentence.</li>
<li>Assuming every important person or place must be written as a proper noun. The word ''doctor'' remains a common noun when used generally.</li>
<li>Confusing a title with a person''s full name.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Common noun = a general category.
Proper noun = the specific name of an entity.
Proper nouns normally begin with capital letters.
</pre>', 1),
  (1401, 1401, '<h2>Concrete and Abstract Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. CONCRETE NOUNS</h3>
<p>A <b>concrete noun</b> names something that can be experienced through one or more of the physical senses: sight, hearing, smell, taste or touch.</p>
<p>Examples include:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Sight:</b> tree, rainbow, building</li>
<li><b>Hearing:</b> thunder, music, voice</li>
<li><b>Smell:</b> perfume, smoke</li>
<li><b>Taste:</b> sugar, lemon</li>
<li><b>Touch:</b> stone, cloth, sand</li>
</ul>
<p>A concrete noun does not have to be something you can hold. Thunder and music are concrete because they can be heard.</p>

<h3>2. ABSTRACT NOUNS</h3>
<p>An <b>abstract noun</b> names an idea, quality, feeling, condition or state that is not experienced as a physical object through the senses.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Qualities:</b> honesty, patience, bravery</li>
<li><b>Feelings:</b> love, anger, happiness</li>
<li><b>Ideas:</b> freedom, justice, equality</li>
<li><b>States:</b> childhood, poverty, peace</li>
</ul>
<p>You may observe the effects of an abstract quality, but the quality itself is not a physical object. For example, you can observe an honest action, but you cannot hold honesty in your hand.</p>

<h3>3. COMPARE THE MEANINGS</h3>
<pre>
The child held a stone.
The child showed courage.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>Stone:</b> concrete noun; it can be seen and touched.</li>
<li><b>Courage:</b> abstract noun; it names a quality.</li>
</ul>
<pre>
We heard the music.
She felt happiness.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>Music:</b> concrete noun because it can be heard.</li>
<li><b>Happiness:</b> abstract noun because it names a feeling.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. ABSTRACT NOUNS FORMED FROM OTHER WORDS</h3>
<p>Many abstract nouns are formed from adjectives or verbs. Recognising these word relationships can help you expand your vocabulary.</p>
<pre>
Adjective       Abstract noun
kind            kindness
brave           bravery
honest          honesty

Verb            Abstract noun
decide          decision
arrive          arrival
educate         education
</pre>
<p>These patterns are useful, but spelling changes are not always predictable. Learn the correct form of unfamiliar words in context.</p>

<h3>5. HOW TO CLASSIFY A NOUN</h3>
<ol>
<li>Determine what the word means in the sentence.</li>
<li>Ask whether it names something experienced through the physical senses.</li>
<li>If it names a physical or sensory experience, it is usually concrete. If it names a quality, idea, feeling or state, it is usually abstract.</li>
</ol>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Calling a noun abstract just because it cannot be held. Music and thunder are concrete because they can be heard.</li>
<li>Assuming abstract nouns are unimportant because they are not physical objects.</li>
<li>Classifying words without checking their meanings in context.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Concrete nouns name things experienced through the senses.
Abstract nouns name ideas, qualities, feelings and states.
</pre>', 1),
  (1402, 1402, '<h2>Countable and Uncountable Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. COUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>A <b>countable noun</b> names something that can be counted as individual units. It has singular and plural forms in ordinary use.</p>
<pre>
one book → two books
one learner → thirty learners
one mango → five mangoes
</pre>
<p>Singular countable nouns can use <b>a</b> or <b>an</b> when the meaning is appropriate: ''a book'', ''an orange''. Plural countable nouns can be used with numbers: ''three books''.</p>

<h3>2. UNCOUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>An <b>uncountable noun</b> names a substance, material, quality or general mass that is not normally counted as separate units in that meaning.</p>
<pre>
water
rice
information
advice
furniture
equipment
</pre>
<p>In standard English, we normally say:</p>
<ul>
<li>some water, not ''a water'' when referring to water generally</li>
<li>a piece of information, not ''an information''</li>
<li>a piece of advice, not ''an advice''</li>
<li>some furniture, not ''furnitures''</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. HOW TO COUNT UNCOUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>Use a suitable unit or container when you need to count an uncountable substance or item of information.</p>
<pre>
a glass of water
two bags of rice
three pieces of advice
four items of furniture
a piece of equipment
</pre>
<p>The quantity word or unit is counted, while the uncountable noun normally keeps its uncountable form.</p>

<h3>4. QUANTIFIERS</h3>
<p>Quantifiers show quantity or amount. Choose one that agrees with the noun''s use.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Many / few:</b> normally used with plural countable nouns. Example: many books; few learners.</li>
<li><b>Much / little:</b> normally used with uncountable nouns. Example: much water; little information.</li>
<li><b>Some / a lot of:</b> can be used with both. Example: some books; some water; a lot of furniture.</li>
<li><b>Any:</b> often used in questions and negative statements with both types. Example: any questions; any sugar.</li>
</ul>
<pre>
How many books did you buy?
How much water do we need?
</pre>
<p>Use <b>how many</b> for countable units and <b>how much</b> for uncountable amounts.</p>

<h3>5. SOME NOUNS CAN BE BOTH</h3>
<p>A noun''s countability can change with its meaning.</p>
<pre>
There is chicken in the stew.
Two chickens are in the coop.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, <b>chicken</b> refers to meat and is uncountable. In the second, <b>chickens</b> refers to individual animals and is countable.</p>
<pre>
I need some paper.
I wrote two papers.
</pre>
<p><b>Paper</b> can refer to material in general or to individual documents, depending on context.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using a number directly with an uncountable noun: ''three informations''. Say ''three pieces of information''.</li>
<li>Using ''much'' with ordinary plural countable nouns when ''many'' is required.</li>
<li>Assuming a noun must always be countable or always uncountable in every context.</li>
<li>Adding a plural ending to uncountable nouns used in their ordinary mass meaning, such as ''furnitures''.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Countable: one book, two books, many books.
Uncountable: some water, much water, two litres of water.
Choose words according to the noun''s meaning in context.
</pre>', 1),
  (1403, 1403, '<h2>Collective Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT IS A COLLECTIVE NOUN?</h3>
<p>A <b>collective noun</b> names a group of people, animals or things considered as a unit.</p>
<p>Examples include:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>People:</b> team, committee, audience, class, family</li>
<li><b>Animals:</b> herd, flock, swarm, pack</li>
<li><b>Things:</b> bunch, collection, bundle, set</li>
</ul>
<p>A collective noun allows us to refer to a group without naming every member individually.</p>

<h3>2. COLLECTIVE NOUNS IN SENTENCES</h3>
<pre>
The team practises every afternoon.
A flock of birds crossed the sky.
The committee discussed the proposal.
A herd of cattle entered the field.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>team</b> names a group of players.</li>
<li><b>flock</b> names a group of birds.</li>
<li><b>committee</b> names a group of people appointed to work together.</li>
<li><b>herd</b> names a group of animals.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. THE GROUP AND ITS MEMBERS</h3>
<p>A collective noun is grammatically singular in many constructions when the group is treated as one unit.</p>
<pre>
The team is ready.
The committee has reached a decision.
</pre>
<p>The verb agrees with the collective noun when the group is presented as a single unit.</p>
<p>In British English, including standard usage common in Kenya, a plural verb can also be used when attention is on the individual members acting separately.</p>
<pre>
The team are arguing among themselves.
The committee are divided in their opinions.
</pre>
<p>These plural constructions emphasise the members rather than the group as one unit. The singular construction is also possible when the group is treated as one whole. Keep the choice consistent with the intended meaning and the variety of English being used.</p>

<h3>4. COLLECTIVE NOUNS ARE NOT ALWAYS THE SAME</h3>
<p>Choose a collective noun that suits the group and context.</p>
<pre>
a flock of sheep
a herd of cattle
a swarm of bees
a pack of wolves
a bunch of bananas
a class of learners
</pre>
<p>These expressions are common combinations. A group may have other descriptions depending on context, but the noun should make sense for the group being described.</p>

<h3>5. COLLECTIVE NOUN OR PLURAL NOUN?</h3>
<pre>
The players are warming up.
The team is warming up.
</pre>
<p><b>Players</b> is a plural noun referring directly to individual people. <b>Team</b> is a collective noun referring to the group as a unit.</p>
<p>A collective noun is not simply any plural noun. ''Books'' names multiple items, while ''collection'' can name the group formed by those items.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thinking that every collective noun must always take a singular verb. Meaning and British English usage can affect agreement.</li>
<li>Confusing a collective noun with a plural noun such as ''players''.</li>
<li>Choosing an unsuitable group word, such as ''a swarm of cattle''.</li>
<li>Changing between singular and plural agreement without a clear reason.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
A collective noun names a group.
Use singular agreement when the group acts as one unit.
Plural agreement may be used in British English when the members are emphasised.
</pre>', 1),
  (1404, 1404, '<h2>Singular and Plural Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. SINGULAR AND PLURAL MEANING</h3>
<p>A <b>singular noun</b> names one person, animal, place, thing or unit. A <b>plural noun</b> names more than one.</p>
<pre>
Singular                 Plural
learner                  learners
book                     books
tree                     trees
</pre>
<p>In English, the form of a noun often changes to show number. This helps readers and listeners understand whether we mean one item or several.</p>

<h3>2. REGULAR PLURAL NOUNS</h3>
<p>Most countable nouns form their plural by adding <b>-s</b>.</p>
<pre>
pen → pens
desk → desks
river → rivers
</pre>
<p>Nouns ending in <b>-s, -ss, -sh, -ch, -x</b> or commonly <b>-z</b> usually add <b>-es</b>.</p>
<pre>
bus → buses
glass → glasses
dish → dishes
watch → watches
box → boxes
buzz → buzzes
</pre>
<p>The extra syllable in words such as <i>buses</i> and <i>dishes</i> makes the plural easier to pronounce.</p>

<h3>3. NOUNS ENDING IN -Y</h3>
<p>For a noun ending in a consonant followed by <b>-y</b>, change <b>-y</b> to <b>-ies</b>.</p>
<pre>
city → cities
baby → babies
country → countries
</pre>
<p>When a vowel comes before the final <b>-y</b>, usually add <b>-s</b> instead.</p>
<pre>
boy → boys
key → keys
day → days
</pre>
<p>Check the letter immediately before the <i>y</i>. In <i>city</i>, it is a consonant; in <i>boy</i>, it is a vowel.</p>

<h3>4. NOUNS ENDING IN -F OR -FE</h3>
<p>Some nouns ending in <b>-f</b> or <b>-fe</b> change to <b>-ves</b>.</p>
<pre>
leaf → leaves
wife → wives
knife → knives
</pre>
<p>However, this is not a universal rule. Some nouns simply add <b>-s</b>.</p>
<pre>
roof → roofs
chief → chiefs
belief → beliefs
</pre>
<p>Learn the plural form of unfamiliar words rather than assuming every noun follows the same pattern.</p>

<h3>5. USING NUMBER CORRECTLY</h3>
<pre>
One mango is ripe.
Three mangoes are ripe.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, the singular noun <i>mango</i> agrees with <i>is</i>. In the second, the plural noun <i>mangoes</i> agrees with <i>are</i>.</p>
<p>Plural spelling and verb agreement are related but different skills. Changing the noun alone does not automatically correct every other part of the sentence.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''citys'' instead of ''cities''.</li>
<li>Writing ''boxs'' instead of ''boxes''.</li>
<li>Assuming every noun ending in -f changes to -ves.</li>
<li>Using a singular verb with a plural subject, such as ''The learners is ready''.</li>
<li>Adding a plural ending to an uncountable noun when it is being used in its ordinary uncountable meaning.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Singular = one.
Plural = more than one.
Apply the correct spelling pattern, then check agreement in the sentence.
</pre>', 1),
  (1405, 1405, '<h2>Possessive Nouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT DOES POSSESSION MEAN?</h3>
<p>A <b>possessive noun</b> shows ownership, belonging, association or a relationship. English commonly uses an apostrophe ('') with s to mark possession.</p>
<pre>
Amina''s book
the teacher''s desk
the dog''s collar
</pre>
<p>These expressions tell us whose book, desk or collar is being discussed. Possession can also express relationships and association, not only legal ownership.</p>

<h3>2. SINGULAR NOUNS</h3>
<p>For most singular nouns, add <b>''s</b>.</p>
<pre>
the girl''s bag
the boy''s bicycle
the child''s uniform
James''s notebook
</pre>
<p>For a singular name ending in s, both <i>James''s</i> and <i>James''</i> appear in published styles. Follow the required style consistently; <i>James''s</i> is a widely accepted form.</p>

<h3>3. REGULAR PLURAL NOUNS ENDING IN -S</h3>
<p>For a regular plural noun that already ends in s, add an apostrophe after the s.</p>
<pre>
the girls'' classroom
the teachers'' meeting
the players'' uniforms
</pre>
<p>Compare:</p>
<pre>
the girl''s uniform   = uniform belonging to one girl
the girls'' uniforms  = uniforms belonging to several girls
</pre>
<p>The position of the apostrophe helps distinguish singular possession from plural possession.</p>

<h3>4. IRREGULAR PLURALS</h3>
<p>For irregular plural nouns that do not end in s, usually add <b>''s</b>.</p>
<pre>
children''s books
men''s shoes
women''s voices
people''s opinions
</pre>
<p>The rule depends on the noun''s actual plural form, not simply on whether it refers to more than one person.</p>

<h3>5. JOINT AND SEPARATE POSSESSION</h3>
<p>When two people share the same item, the apostrophe usually goes on the last name.</p>
<pre>
Amina and Brian''s project
</pre>
<p>This normally means one project shared by Amina and Brian.</p>
<p>When each person has a separate item, both names take the possessive form.</p>
<pre>
Amina''s and Brian''s projects
</pre>
<p>This indicates that Amina and Brian have separate projects. Context can help clarify the intended meaning.</p>

<h3>6. POSSESSIVE NOUNS AND CONTRACTIONS</h3>
<p>An apostrophe can mark possession or missing letters in a contraction. Do not confuse the two.</p>
<pre>
The learner''s book is missing.
The learner''s finished the work.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, <i>learner''s</i> means the book belongs to or is associated with the learner. In the second, <i>learner''s</i> is a contraction of <i>learner has</i> in a suitable context, though the sentence is more naturally written as ''The learner has finished the work'' in formal school writing.</p>
<p>For ordinary school grammar, remember that a possessive noun shows a relationship, while a contraction joins words by omitting letters.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''the girls uniform'' when possession is intended: write ''the girl''s uniform'' for one girl.</li>
<li>Writing ''the girl''s'' when referring to several girls: write ''the girls'''' only if the apostrophe follows the plural s, as in ''the girls'' classroom''.</li>
<li>Adding an apostrophe to ordinary plurals unnecessarily, such as ''apple''s'' when you simply mean several apples.</li>
<li>Confusing a possessive apostrophe with the apostrophe in a contraction.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Singular noun: girl''s
Regular plural ending in s: girls''
Irregular plural not ending in s: children''s
The apostrophe helps show the relationship or possession.
</pre>', 1),
  (1406, 1406, '<h2>Articles: A, An and The</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT ARE ARTICLES?</h3>
<p><b>A</b>, <b>an</b> and <b>the</b> are articles. Articles come before nouns or noun phrases and help show whether we mean something generally or a particular thing.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>A</b> and <b>an</b> are indefinite articles. They commonly refer to one singular countable thing that is not identified as a particular known item.</li>
<li><b>The</b> is the definite article. It identifies a particular person or thing that the speaker expects the listener or reader to recognise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. WHEN TO USE A</h3>
<p>Use <b>a</b> before a singular countable noun beginning with a consonant sound.</p>
<pre>
a book
a teacher
a university
a European country
</pre>
<p>The rule depends on sound, not merely on the first written letter. <i>University</i> begins with a /juː/ sound, similar to ''you'', so it takes <i>a</i>.</p>

<h3>3. WHEN TO USE AN</h3>
<p>Use <b>an</b> before a singular countable noun beginning with a vowel sound.</p>
<pre>
an apple
an orange
an umbrella
an hour
an honest person
</pre>
<p>In <i>hour</i> and <i>honest</i>, the initial h is silent, so the words begin with a vowel sound. That is why they take <i>an</i>.</p>
<p>Compare:</p>
<pre>
a university
an umbrella
a useful tool
an unusual event
</pre>
<p>Always listen for the first sound of the word that follows the article.</p>

<h3>4. WHEN TO USE THE</h3>
<p>Use <b>the</b> when the person or thing is identifiable from the context, has already been mentioned, or is understood to be specific.</p>
<pre>
I saw a dog. The dog was barking.
Please close the door.
The sun provides light.
</pre>
<ul>
<li>In the first sentence, <i>a dog</i> introduces a dog that has not yet been identified.</li>
<li>In the next sentence, <i>the dog</i> refers to the same dog, now known from the conversation.</li>
<li>In ''Please close the door'', the speaker expects the listener to know which door is meant.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. WHEN NO ARTICLE IS NEEDED</h3>
<p>English often uses no article when speaking generally about plural countable nouns or uncountable nouns.</p>
<pre>
Books can broaden knowledge.
Water is necessary for life.
Learners need practice.
</pre>
<p>Here, the nouns refer to things in general rather than particular identified items.</p>
<p>However, use <i>the</i> when the meaning is specific:</p>
<pre>
The books on the table are mine.
The water in this bottle is cold.
</pre>

<h3>6. ARTICLES AND COUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>In ordinary use, a singular countable noun usually needs a determiner, such as an article or a possessive word.</p>
<pre>
I bought a pen.
I bought the pen.
I bought my pen.
</pre>
<p>All three are grammatical, but they communicate different information. <i>A pen</i> introduces a non-specific pen, <i>the pen</i> identifies a particular pen, and <i>my pen</i> identifies one associated with the speaker.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''an university'' because the word begins with the letter u. The initial sound is /j/, so use ''a university''.</li>
<li>Writing ''a hour'' instead of ''an hour''. The h is silent.</li>
<li>Using ''the'' for every general statement, such as ''The water is essential for life'' when speaking about water generally.</li>
<li>Leaving a singular countable noun without a determiner in an ordinary sentence, such as ''I bought book''. Write ''I bought a book''.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
A = before a consonant sound.
An = before a vowel sound.
The = for an identifiable or specific noun.
No article = often used for general plural or uncountable nouns.
</pre>', 1),
  (1407, 1407, '<h2>Quantifiers</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT IS A QUANTIFIER?</h3>
<p>A <b>quantifier</b> is a word or expression that tells us how much or how many of something is being referred to. Quantifiers usually come before nouns or noun phrases.</p>
<pre>
many learners
much water
a few mistakes
a little patience
enough food
</pre>
<p>Choosing the correct quantifier depends on whether the noun is countable or uncountable and on the meaning you want to express.</p>

<h3>2. QUANTIFIERS WITH COUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<p>Use these quantifiers with plural countable nouns:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Many:</b> a large number. Example: Many learners attended the lesson.</li>
<li><b>A few:</b> some, enough to be meaningful. Example: A few learners asked questions.</li>
<li><b>Few:</b> not many; often suggests that the number is insufficient. Example: Few learners understood the difficult explanation.</li>
<li><b>Several:</b> more than two, but not a large or exact number. Example: Several books were missing.</li>
<li><b>Each / every:</b> refer to members individually and normally take a singular countable noun. Example: Each learner has a book.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. QUANTIFIERS WITH UNCOUNTABLE NOUNS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Much:</b> a large amount. Example: We do not have much time.</li>
<li><b>A little:</b> some amount, often enough for a purpose. Example: We have a little sugar left.</li>
<li><b>Little:</b> not much; often suggests an insufficient amount. Example: There is little hope of finishing today.</li>
<li><b>A great deal of:</b> a large amount. Example: The task requires a great deal of patience.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. A FEW VERSUS FEW; A LITTLE VERSUS LITTLE</h3>
<p>The article <b>a</b> changes the usual implication of these expressions.</p>
<pre>
We have a few books.
We have few books.

There is a little milk.
There is little milk.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>A few books</b> means some books are available.</li>
<li><b>Few books</b> emphasises that the number is small, possibly too small.</li>
<li><b>A little milk</b> means some milk is available.</li>
<li><b>Little milk</b> emphasises scarcity.</li>
</ul>
<p>The difference is not simply the quantity. It is also the speaker''s attitude toward whether the amount is sufficient.</p>

<h3>5. QUANTIFIERS USED WITH BOTH TYPES</h3>
<p>Some quantifiers work with both countable and uncountable nouns.</p>
<pre>
some books / some water
a lot of learners / a lot of time
enough chairs / enough space
all the students / all the information
plenty of opportunities / plenty of food
</pre>
<p>Use <b>how many</b> with countable nouns and <b>how much</b> with uncountable nouns.</p>
<pre>
How many chairs are needed?
How much space is available?
</pre>

<h3>6. SOME AND ANY</h3>
<p><b>Some</b> is common in affirmative statements. <b>Any</b> is common in questions and negative statements.</p>
<pre>
We have some food.
We do not have any food.
Do you have any questions?
</pre>
<p>However, this is not an absolute rule. <i>Some</i> is often used in offers and requests when a positive response is expected or when something is being offered.</p>
<pre>
Would you like some tea?
Could I have some water?
</pre>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''much students'' instead of ''many students''.</li>
<li>Writing ''many information'' instead of ''much information'' or ''a lot of information''.</li>
<li>Confusing ''few'' with ''a few'' and ''little'' with ''a little''.</li>
<li>Using ''each learners'' instead of ''each learner''.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Many, few, a few → countable nouns.
Much, little, a little → uncountable nouns.
Some, a lot of, enough → can work with both.
Meaning and context determine the best choice.
</pre>', 1),
  (1408, 1408, '<h2>Formation of Noun Phrases</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT IS A NOUN PHRASE?</h3>
<p>A <b>noun phrase</b> is a group of words organised around a noun or pronoun as its central element. It functions as a unit in a sentence.</p>
<pre>
books
the books
the old books
the old books on the shelf
</pre>
<p>Each expression is a noun phrase. The central noun is <b>books</b>; the other words add information about which books, their qualities or their location.</p>

<h3>2. THE HEAD NOUN</h3>
<p>The main noun in a noun phrase is called the <b>head noun</b>. Other words in the phrase depend on or describe this central noun.</p>
<pre>
the diligent learner
those three large mangoes
a very interesting story
</pre>
<ul>
<li>In ''the diligent learner'', the head noun is <b>learner</b>.</li>
<li>In ''those three large mangoes'', the head noun is <b>mangoes</b>.</li>
<li>In ''a very interesting story'', the head noun is <b>story</b>.</li>
</ul>
<p>To find the head noun, identify the main person, place, thing or idea the phrase refers to.</p>

<h3>3. DETERMINERS BEFORE THE NOUN</h3>
<p>Determiners help identify or quantify the noun. They can include articles, possessives, demonstratives and quantifiers.</p>
<pre>
a learner
the learner
my learner
this learner
several learners
</pre>
<p>The determiner usually appears before adjectives and the head noun. A noun phrase does not need to contain every possible element.</p>

<h3>4. ADJECTIVES AND OTHER DESCRIPTIVE WORDS</h3>
<p>Adjectives add information about the head noun.</p>
<pre>
a book
a useful book
a very useful reference book
</pre>
<p>Here, <i>useful</i> describes the book, while <i>very</i> modifies the adjective <i>useful</i>. The head noun remains <i>book</i>.</p>
<p>Several descriptive words may occur together, but they should help identify or describe the noun clearly.</p>

<h3>5. ADDING INFORMATION AFTER THE NOUN</h3>
<p>A noun phrase can include a prepositional phrase or a relative clause after the head noun.</p>
<pre>
the books on the table
the learner who won the prize
the house near the river
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>on the table</b> tells us which books are meant.</li>
<li><b>who won the prize</b> identifies the learner.</li>
<li><b>near the river</b> adds information about the house.</li>
</ul>
<p>These additional elements make the noun phrase more specific. The entire phrase can still function as one unit in the sentence.</p>

<h3>6. NOUN PHRASES IN SENTENCES</h3>
<pre>
The young learner answered the question.
</pre>
<p><b>The young learner</b> is a noun phrase functioning as the subject. <b>The question</b> is another noun phrase functioning as the object.</p>
<pre>
The girl with the red bag arrived.
</pre>
<p><b>The girl with the red bag</b> is the subject noun phrase. The phrase <i>with the red bag</i> adds identifying information about the girl.</p>

<h3>7. BUILDING A CLEAR NOUN PHRASE</h3>
<ol>
<li>Choose the person, place, thing or idea you want to name.</li>
<li>Add a suitable determiner if required.</li>
<li>Add descriptive words only when they improve meaning.</li>
<li>Add identifying information after the noun when necessary.</li>
<li>Check that the whole phrase makes sense in its sentence.</li>
</ol>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming a noun phrase must contain several words. ''Books'' alone can be a noun phrase.</li>
<li>Identifying an adjective as the head noun in ''the clever learner''. The head noun is ''learner''.</li>
<li>Confusing a noun phrase with a complete sentence. A noun phrase does not necessarily express a complete thought.</li>
<li>Adding too many descriptive words until the main meaning becomes unclear.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
A noun phrase is built around a head noun.
Determiners, adjectives and additional phrases can identify or describe it.
The complete phrase functions as one unit in a sentence.
</pre>', 1),
  (1409, 1409, '<h2>Personal Pronouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT IS A PERSONAL PRONOUN?</h3>
<p>A <b>personal pronoun</b> is a pronoun that refers to a speaker, the person or people being addressed, or someone or something being discussed.</p>
<p>Personal pronouns help avoid unnecessary repetition.</p>
<pre>
Amina is reading. Amina enjoys novels.
Amina is reading. She enjoys novels.
</pre>
<p><b>She</b> replaces the repeated name. The reader can identify who is meant from the previous sentence.</p>

<h3>2. PERSON AND NUMBER</h3>
<p>Personal pronouns are grouped according to person and number.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>First person:</b> the speaker. Singular: I; plural: we.</li>
<li><b>Second person:</b> the person or people addressed. Singular and plural: you.</li>
<li><b>Third person:</b> the person, animal or thing discussed. Singular: he, she, it; plural: they.</li>
</ul>
<p>English uses <i>you</i> for both one person and several people. The context shows which meaning is intended.</p>

<h3>3. SUBJECT PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>Subject pronouns commonly perform the subject role in a clause.</p>
<pre>
I study every evening.
You answered correctly.
He plays football.
She reads carefully.
It looks broken.
We are ready.
They arrived early.
</pre>
<p>In standard English, subject pronouns normally appear in the subject position. For example, write ''She and I went home'' rather than ''Her and me went home'' in a formal sentence where the pronouns are the subject.</p>

<h3>4. OBJECT PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>Object pronouns are used when the pronoun functions as an object of a verb or preposition.</p>
<pre>
The teacher called me.
I helped him.
We invited her.
The coach spoke to us.
The principal thanked them.
</pre>
<p>Common subject–object pairs include:</p>
<pre>
I → me
he → him
she → her
we → us
they → them
</pre>
<p><i>You</i> and <i>it</i> have the same form as subject and object pronouns.</p>

<h3>5. CHOOSING THE CORRECT FORM</h3>
<p>Ask what job the pronoun performs in the sentence.</p>
<pre>
She called him.
He called her.
</pre>
<p>In the first sentence, <i>she</i> performs the action and is the subject; <i>him</i> receives the action and is the object. The second sentence reverses their roles.</p>
<pre>
Between you and me, the plan may fail.
</pre>
<p>After the preposition <i>between</i>, use the object pronoun <i>me</i>, not the subject pronoun <i>I</i>.</p>

<h3>6. PRONOUNS MUST HAVE CLEAR REFERENCES</h3>
<pre>
Brian told Peter that he had won.
</pre>
<p>The pronoun <i>he</i> is ambiguous because it could refer to Brian or Peter. A clearer sentence identifies the intended person:</p>
<pre>
Brian told Peter, ''I have won.''
Brian told Peter that Peter had won.
</pre>
<p>Choose a pronoun only when the reader can reasonably identify whom or what it refers to.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing ''Me and Amina went home'' in formal standard English instead of ''Amina and I went home''.</li>
<li>Writing ''The teacher helped she'' instead of ''The teacher helped her''.</li>
<li>Using ''I'' after a preposition, as in ''between you and I'', in formal standard English.</li>
<li>Using a pronoun when two possible references make the sentence unclear.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Subject pronouns: I, you, he, she, it, we, they.
Object pronouns: me, you, him, her, it, us, them.
Choose the form according to its grammatical role and keep its reference clear.
</pre>', 1),
  (1410, 1410, '<h2>Possessive Pronouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT DO POSSESSIVE PRONOUNS DO?</h3>
<p><b>Possessive pronouns</b> show that something belongs to, or is associated with, a person or group. They can stand in place of a noun phrase to avoid repetition.</p>
<pre>
This book is mine.
That bag is yours.
The blue bicycle is hers.
The final decision is theirs.
</pre>
<p>In each example, the possessive pronoun identifies who the item or decision belongs to.</p>

<h3>2. THE MAIN POSSESSIVE PRONOUNS</h3>
<pre>
Personal pronoun       Possessive pronoun
I                      mine
you                    yours
he                     his
she                    hers
it                     (no commonly used independent form)
we                     ours
they                   theirs
</pre>
<p>There is no apostrophe in <i>yours</i>, <i>hers</i>, <i>ours</i> or <i>theirs</i>. The forms <i>its</i> and <i>it''s</i> have different uses: <i>its</i> is a possessive determiner, while <i>it''s</i> usually means <i>it is</i> or <i>it has</i>.</p>

<h3>3. POSSESSIVE PRONOUNS AND POSSESSIVE DETERMINERS</h3>
<p>These forms are related but they do not function in the same way.</p>
<pre>
This is my book.
This book is mine.

That is her bag.
That bag is hers.
</pre>
<ul>
<li><b>My</b> and <b>her</b> are possessive determiners: they come before a noun.</li>
<li><b>Mine</b> and <b>hers</b> are possessive pronouns: they stand independently without a following noun.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not write ''This book is my'' or ''That bag is her'' when you mean that the items belong to the speaker or another person.</p>

<h3>4. AVOIDING REPETITION</h3>
<pre>
Your answer is correct, and my answer is correct.
Your answer is correct, and mine is correct.
</pre>
<p><b>Mine</b> replaces the noun phrase <i>my answer</i>. The meaning remains clear without repeating the noun.</p>
<pre>
Their classroom is large. Our classroom is small.
Their classroom is large. Ours is small.
</pre>
<p><b>Ours</b> replaces <i>our classroom</i>. This is one reason possessive pronouns make writing more concise.</p>

<h3>5. POSSESSIVE PRONOUNS DO NOT USE APOSTROPHES</h3>
<pre>
Correct: The victory was theirs.
Correct: That seat is hers.
Incorrect: The victory was their''s.
Incorrect: That seat is her''s.
</pre>
<p>Unlike many possessive nouns, possessive pronouns are not written with an apostrophe.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using a possessive determiner without a noun: ''The bag is my'' instead of ''The bag is mine''.</li>
<li>Adding an apostrophe to ''yours'', ''hers'', ''ours'' or ''theirs''.</li>
<li>Confusing ''its'' with ''it''s''.</li>
<li>Repeating a noun phrase when a clear possessive pronoun would be more concise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Possessive determiner + noun: my book.
Possessive pronoun standing alone: the book is mine.
Possessive pronouns do not take apostrophes.
</pre>', 1),
  (1411, 1411, '<h2>Reflexive and Emphatic Pronouns</h2>

<h3>1. WHAT ARE THESE PRONOUNS?</h3>
<p>Reflexive and emphatic pronouns use forms ending in <b>-self</b> or <b>-selves</b>. The same form can serve different purposes depending on the sentence.</p>
<pre>
myself
yourself
himself
herself
itself
ourselves
yourselves
themselves
</pre>
<p>Use the singular forms with singular references and the plural forms with plural references. <i>Yourself</i> normally refers to one person; <i>yourselves</i> refers to more than one person addressed.</p>

<h3>2. REFLEXIVE PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>A <b>reflexive pronoun</b> is used when the object refers back to the subject of the same clause.</p>
<pre>
Amina blamed herself.
The boy injured himself.
I taught myself to swim.
They prepared themselves for the exam.
</pre>
<p>In ''Amina blamed herself'', <i>Amina</i> is both the person performing the action and the person receiving the blame. The pronoun refers back to the subject.</p>

<h3>3. WHY THE REFLEXIVE FORM MATTERS</h3>
<pre>
He washed him.
He washed himself.
</pre>
<p>The first sentence normally means that he washed another male person. The second means that he washed his own body.</p>
<p>The reflexive pronoun removes the distinction that would otherwise be unclear. Use it when the subject and object refer to the same person or thing and the sentence requires that form.</p>

<h3>4. EMPHATIC PRONOUNS</h3>
<p>An <b>emphatic pronoun</b>, also called an intensive pronoun, adds emphasis to a noun or pronoun. It stresses who performed an action or who was involved.</p>
<pre>
The principal herself welcomed the visitors.
I myself completed the assignment.
The players themselves organised the event.
</pre>
<p>Here, the pronouns emphasise the principal, the speaker and the players. They do not function as objects referring back to the subject.</p>

<h3>5. THE REMOVAL TEST</h3>
<p>A useful way to distinguish the two uses is to remove the pronoun and see what happens to the sentence''s basic meaning and structure.</p>
<pre>
She introduced herself.
She introduced.

The principal herself opened the meeting.
The principal opened the meeting.
</pre>
<ul>
<li>In the first pair, removing <i>herself</i> leaves the sentence incomplete or changes the intended meaning because the reflexive object is needed in this use of <i>introduce</i>.</li>
<li>In the second pair, removing <i>herself</i> leaves a complete sentence. The pronoun only adds emphasis.</li>
</ul>
<p>This test is useful, but always consider the meaning and grammar of the full sentence.</p>

<h3>6. REFLEXIVE PRONOUNS ARE NOT A SUBSTITUTE FOR OBJECT PRONOUNS</h3>
<pre>
Correct: The teacher spoke to me.
Incorrect in standard usage: The teacher spoke to myself.
Correct: Please contact me.
Incorrect in standard usage: Please contact myself.
</pre>
<p>Use a reflexive pronoun when it refers back to the subject or when it adds emphasis to a suitable noun phrase. Do not use it merely to sound formal.</p>

<h3>7. SINGULAR AND PLURAL FORMS</h3>
<pre>
I → myself
you (one person) → yourself
he → himself
she → herself
it → itself
we → ourselves
you (several people) → yourselves
they → themselves
</pre>
<p>For example, ''The learners prepared themselves'' refers to several learners. ''The learner prepared himself'' refers to one learner when the masculine pronoun is appropriate to the context.</p>

<h3>COMMON MISTAKES</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using ''myself'' instead of ''me'' after a verb or preposition when no reflexive or emphatic meaning is intended.</li>
<li>Using an emphatic pronoun without identifying the person or group being emphasised.</li>
<li>Confusing ''He saw him'' with ''He saw himself''.</li>
<li>Using ''theirself'' in place of the standard plural form ''themselves'' in formal school writing.</li>
</ul>

<h3>KEY IDEA</h3>
<pre>
Reflexive: the pronoun refers back to the subject.
Emphatic: the pronoun adds emphasis and can usually be removed.
The same -self or -selves form can serve either function.
</pre>', 1),
  (1412, 1412, '<h2>Demonstrative Pronouns</h2>
<p>A <strong>demonstrative pronoun</strong> points to a particular person, object, place, or idea. It helps the reader or listener identify exactly what is being referred to.</p>

<h3>The four demonstrative pronouns</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>This</strong> — refers to one person or thing that is near.</li>
  <li><strong>That</strong> — refers to one person or thing that is farther away.</li>
  <li><strong>These</strong> — refers to more than one person or thing that is near.</li>
  <li><strong>Those</strong> — refers to more than one person or thing that is farther away.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Choosing the correct pronoun</h3>
<p>Two questions help you choose correctly:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Is the noun singular or plural?</li>
  <li>Is it being presented as near or farther away?</li>
</ol>
<p>For example, if you are holding one book, you might say, <em>This is my book.</em> If you are pointing to several books across the room, you might say, <em>Those are my books.</em></p>

<h3>Demonstrative pronouns versus demonstrative determiners</h3>
<p>A demonstrative pronoun stands in place of a noun phrase: <em>These are expensive.</em></p>
<p>A demonstrative determiner comes before a noun and identifies it: <em>These shoes are expensive.</em></p>
<p>The word <em>these</em> is the pronoun in the first sentence because it stands alone. In the second, it determines which shoes are being discussed.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>This are my notes.</em> Incorrect: <em>this</em> is singular. Write <em>These are my notes.</em></li>
  <li><em>Those is my bag.</em> Incorrect: <em>those</em> is plural. Write <em>That is my bag.</em></li>
  <li>Choosing a pronoun based only on distance while ignoring number. Both number and intended reference matter.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Apply the skill</h3>
<p>Imagine a learner pointing to one pen on a nearby desk and several pens on a distant desk. The learner can say, <em>This is my pen</em> and <em>Those are my pens.</em> The pronouns make the intended references clear without repeating the nouns.</p>', 1),
  (1413, 1413, '<h2>Relative Pronouns</h2>
<p>A <strong>relative pronoun</strong> introduces a clause that gives information about a noun or pronoun already mentioned. It connects that information to the noun being described.</p>

<h3>Common relative pronouns</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Who</strong> — refers to people and commonly functions as the subject of the relative clause.</li>
  <li><strong>Whom</strong> — refers to people when they function as an object in formal English.</li>
  <li><strong>Whose</strong> — expresses possession or association.</li>
  <li><strong>Which</strong> — refers to animals or things.</li>
  <li><strong>That</strong> — can refer to people, animals or things in many identifying relative clauses.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How a relative pronoun works</h3>
<p>Consider these two sentences:</p>
<p><em>The girl won the race. She trained every day.</em></p>
<p>They can be combined as:</p>
<p><em>The girl who trained every day won the race.</em></p>
<p>The clause <em>who trained every day</em> identifies or describes the girl. The relative pronoun who connects that clause to the noun girl.</p>

<h3>Choosing the correct pronoun</h3>
<p><strong>Who:</strong> <em>The student who answered correctly received a mark.</em></p>
<p><strong>Whom:</strong> <em>The student whom we congratulated smiled.</em> In the relative clause, we congratulated the student; the student is the object of congratulated.</p>
<p><strong>Whose:</strong> <em>The learner whose book was missing reported it.</em></p>
<p><strong>Which:</strong> <em>The machine which stopped working was repaired.</em></p>
<p><strong>That:</strong> <em>The book that I borrowed is interesting.</em></p>

<h3>Identifying and additional information</h3>
<p>A defining relative clause identifies which person or thing is meant: <em>The player who scored the goal is my cousin.</em> Without the clause, the reader may not know which player is meant.</p>
<p>A non-defining relative clause adds extra information: <em>My cousin, who lives in Embu, is visiting.</em> The clause is separated by commas. In standard usage, <em>that</em> is not used to introduce a non-defining relative clause.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using <em>whose</em> when the sentence requires a subject relative pronoun: <em>The boy whose won</em> is incorrect; write <em>The boy who won</em>.</li>
  <li>Using <em>which</em> to refer to a person in a context where <em>who</em> is expected.</li>
  <li>Confusing <em>who</em> and <em>whom</em>. In formal English, use who for a subject and whom for an object.</li>
  <li>Leaving out commas around non-defining clauses.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1414, 1414, '<h2>Indefinite Pronouns</h2>
<p>An <strong>indefinite pronoun</strong> refers to a person, thing or amount without identifying it precisely. It is useful when the identity or quantity is unknown, unimportant or deliberately left unspecified.</p>

<h3>Common indefinite pronouns</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>People:</strong> someone, somebody, anyone, anybody, everyone, everybody, no one, nobody.</li>
  <li><strong>Things:</strong> something, anything, everything, nothing.</li>
  <li><strong>Amounts or choices:</strong> all, some, any, none, many, few, several, each, either, neither.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pronoun meaning matters</h3>
<p><em>Someone has left a notebook.</em> The speaker knows that a person left it but does not identify the person.</p>
<p><em>Nothing was damaged.</em> The pronoun refers to no thing being damaged.</p>
<p><em>Several arrived early.</em> Several stands in for a plural group of people or things understood from context.</p>

<h3>Subject–verb agreement</h3>
<p>Indefinite pronouns do not all take the same kind of verb.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Usually singular:</strong> everyone, everybody, someone, somebody, anyone, anybody, no one, nobody, each, either and neither.</li>
  <li><strong>Usually plural:</strong> both, few, many and several.</li>
  <li><strong>Depends on meaning or context:</strong> all, some, any and none.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples: <em>Everyone is ready.</em> <em>Several are absent.</em> <em>Some of the water is dirty.</em> <em>Some of the books are missing.</em></p>
<p>With <em>all</em>, <em>some</em> and similar words, look at the noun or amount being referred to. An uncountable amount such as water takes a singular verb; a plural countable noun such as books takes a plural verb.</p>

<h3>Singular meaning and inclusive reference</h3>
<p>Words such as <em>everyone</em> and <em>someone</em> are grammatically singular, even though they refer to people. In contemporary English, singular <em>they</em> is widely used when a person''s gender is unknown or irrelevant: <em>Everyone should bring their book.</em></p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>Everyone are ready.</em> Use <em>Everyone is ready.</em></li>
  <li><em>Several is missing.</em> Use <em>Several are missing.</em></li>
  <li>Assuming that <em>some</em> always takes a singular or always takes a plural verb. The noun or amount being referred to determines agreement.</li>
  <li>Using a pronoun without enough context for the reader to understand what it refers to.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1415, 1415, '<h2>Pronoun–Antecedent Agreement</h2>
<p>An <strong>antecedent</strong> is the word or phrase a pronoun refers to. Pronoun–antecedent agreement means that the pronoun must fit its antecedent in number and, where relevant, person and meaning.</p>

<h3>Find the antecedent first</h3>
<p>In <em>Amina forgot her notebook</em>, the antecedent of <em>her</em> is Amina. In <em>The players collected their equipment</em>, the antecedent of <em>their</em> is the players.</p>
<p>Before choosing a pronoun, ask: who or what does it refer to? Then check whether the reference is clear and consistent.</p>

<h3>Agreement in number</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Singular antecedent: <em>The boy said he was ready.</em></li>
  <li>Plural antecedent: <em>The boys said they were ready.</em></li>
  <li>Singular indefinite pronoun: <em>Everyone must bring their book.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Singular <em>they</em> is standard in contemporary English when referring to one person whose gender is unknown, irrelevant or expressed as they/them. Do not assume that every pronoun referring to a grammatically singular word must be he or she.</p>

<h3>Compound antecedents</h3>
<p>When two people or things are joined by <em>and</em>, the antecedent is usually plural: <em>Brian and Wanjiku said they would attend.</em></p>
<p>When alternatives are joined by <em>or</em> or <em>nor</em>, agreement depends on the structure and meaning. A clear rewrite is often better than a confusing pronoun: instead of <em>Either the teacher or the learners must bring his book</em>, write <em>The teacher must bring the teacher''s book, or the learners must bring their books.</em></p>

<h3>Clear reference</h3>
<p>A pronoun can agree grammatically but still be unclear.</p>
<p><em>When Kamau spoke to Otieno, he was angry.</em> The reader cannot be certain whether he refers to Kamau or Otieno.</p>
<p>Improve it by naming the person: <em>Kamau was angry when he spoke to Otieno.</em> Or, if Otieno was angry: <em>When Kamau spoke to Otieno, Otieno was angry.</em></p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using a plural pronoun for a single, specific antecedent without a reason.</li>
  <li>Using a singular verb or pronoun with a plural antecedent when the sentence requires plural agreement.</li>
  <li>Allowing a pronoun to refer to two possible nouns.</li>
  <li>Changing the intended reference midway through a sentence.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Editing method</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Underline the pronoun.</li>
  <li>Identify the exact antecedent.</li>
  <li>Check number and intended reference.</li>
  <li>Rewrite the sentence if the relationship remains unclear.</li>
</ol>', 1),
  (1416, 1416, '<h2>Main and Auxiliary Verbs</h2>
<p>A <strong>main verb</strong> expresses the central action, event or state in a clause. An <strong>auxiliary verb</strong>, also called a helping verb, works with a main verb to express features such as tense, aspect, voice, questions, negation or modality.</p>

<h3>Main verbs</h3>
<p>Main verbs carry the central meaning of the clause:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The learners <strong>study</strong> every evening.</em></li>
  <li><em>The baby <strong>slept</strong> peacefully.</em></li>
  <li><em>The room <strong>seems</strong> quiet.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Main verbs can describe actions (<em>write</em>, <em>run</em>), events (<em>happen</em>, <em>break</em>) or states (<em>know</em>, <em>belong</em>, <em>seem</em>).</p>

<h3>The primary auxiliary verbs</h3>
<p>The three primary auxiliaries are <strong>be</strong>, <strong>have</strong> and <strong>do</strong>. Their forms change according to tense and subject.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Be:</strong> am, is, are, was, were, be, being, been. It helps form continuous tenses and the passive voice.</li>
  <li><strong>Have:</strong> have, has, had. It helps form perfect tenses.</li>
  <li><strong>Do:</strong> do, does, did. It helps form many questions, negatives and emphatic statements in the simple present and simple past.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How auxiliaries work</h3>
<p><strong>Continuous aspect:</strong> <em>She is reading.</em> Is is the auxiliary; reading is the main verb in its -ing form.</p>
<p><strong>Perfect aspect:</strong> <em>They have finished.</em> Have is the auxiliary; finished is the main verb in its past participle form.</p>
<p><strong>Passive voice:</strong> <em>The letter was written by Ali.</em> Was is the auxiliary; written is the main verb in its past participle form.</p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> <em>Do you understand?</em> Do helps form the question; understand is the main verb.</p>
<p><strong>Negative:</strong> <em>He does not agree.</em> Does helps form the negative; agree is the main verb.</p>

<h3>Modal auxiliary verbs</h3>
<p>Modals include <em>can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will</em> and <em>would</em>. They express meanings such as ability, possibility, obligation, advice or willingness.</p>
<p>Examples: <em>She can swim.</em> <em>You should revise.</em> <em>They might arrive later.</em></p>
<p>A modal is followed by the base form of the main verb: <em>She can swim</em>, not <em>She can swims</em> or <em>She can to swim</em>.</p>

<h3>One verb can serve different roles</h3>
<p><em>They have a car.</em> Here, have is the main verb because it expresses possession.</p>
<p><em>They have bought a car.</em> Here, have is an auxiliary because it helps form the present perfect; bought is the main verb.</p>
<p><em>She is happy.</em> Here, is links the subject to a description and functions as the main linking verb.</p>
<p><em>She is singing.</em> Here, is is an auxiliary helping the main verb singing.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>Does she likes tea?</em> Write <em>Does she like tea?</em> The auxiliary does carries the tense, so the main verb uses its base form.</li>
  <li><em>He can plays.</em> Write <em>He can play.</em></li>
  <li>Calling every form of be an auxiliary. It can also function as the main linking verb.</li>
  <li>Confusing the main verb''s meaning with the auxiliary''s grammatical job.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1417, 1417, '<h2>Action and Linking Verbs</h2>
<p>A verb can express an action or connect a subject to information about that subject. Understanding this difference helps you interpret sentences accurately and construct them correctly.</p>

<h3>Action verbs</h3>
<p>An <strong>action verb</strong> expresses something a person, animal or thing does, or an event that takes place. The action may be physical or mental.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Physical action: <em>The farmer planted maize.</em></li>
  <li>Mental action: <em>Achieng remembered the answer.</em></li>
  <li>Event: <em>The glass broke.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Action verbs do not always describe movements that can be seen. Words such as <em>think, understand, believe</em> and <em>remember</em> can express mental activity.</p>

<h3>Linking verbs</h3>
<p>A <strong>linking verb</strong> connects the subject to a word or phrase that identifies or describes it. It does not express an action performed on an object in that use.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The soup tastes salty.</em> Salty describes the soup.</li>
  <li><em>Wanjiku became a nurse.</em> A nurse identifies what Wanjiku became.</li>
  <li><em>The learners seem tired.</em> Tired describes the learners.</li>
</ul>
<p>Common linking verbs include forms of <em>be</em> (am, is, are, was, were), and verbs such as <em>become, seem, appear, remain, feel, look, smell, sound, taste</em> and <em>grow</em> when they connect a subject to a description or identity.</p>

<h3>The same verb can perform different jobs</h3>
<p>Do not classify a verb by memorising the word alone. Examine how it is used.</p>
<p><em>The soup smells delicious.</em> Smells is linking because delicious describes the soup.</p>
<p><em>The cook smells the soup.</em> Smells expresses an action performed by the cook.</p>
<p><em>She looked tired.</em> Looked is linking because tired describes she.</p>
<p><em>She looked at the timetable.</em> Looked expresses an action directed towards the timetable.</p>

<h3>Subject complements</h3>
<p>The description or identity after a linking verb is called a <strong>subject complement</strong>. It completes the meaning by referring back to the subject.</p>
<p>In <em>The road became slippery</em>, slippery describes the road. It is not an object receiving an action.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming that every verb involving the senses is linking. In <em>He tasted the stew</em>, tasted expresses an action.</li>
  <li>Calling the adjective after a linking verb an object. In <em>The sky became dark</em>, dark describes the subject.</li>
  <li>Assuming that action verbs must be physical. <em>She knows the answer</em> expresses a mental state.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1418, 1418, '<h2>Regular and Irregular Verbs</h2>
<p>Verbs change form to express grammatical information such as tense. Regular and irregular verbs differ in how they form their past simple and past participle forms.</p>

<h3>Regular verbs</h3>
<p>A <strong>regular verb</strong> normally forms both the past simple and the past participle by adding <em>-ed</em> or <em>-d</em> to its base form.</p>
<ul>
  <li>work → worked → worked</li>
  <li>clean → cleaned → cleaned</li>
  <li>live → lived → lived</li>
</ul>
<p>The three forms shown are the base form, past simple and past participle. The past participle is used in constructions such as <em>has worked</em> and <em>was cleaned</em>.</p>

<h3>Spelling changes in regular verbs</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Most verbs add <em>-ed</em>: <em>help → helped</em>.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in silent <em>-e</em> usually add <em>-d</em>: <em>arrive → arrived</em>.</li>
  <li>Many consonant + <em>y</em> endings change <em>y</em> to <em>i</em> before <em>-ed</em>: <em>study → studied</em>.</li>
  <li>When a verb ends in vowel + <em>y</em>, retain the <em>y</em>: <em>play → played</em>.</li>
  <li>Some short verbs double the final consonant: <em>stop → stopped</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Consonant doubling depends on spelling and stress patterns; it does not happen with every verb ending in a consonant.</p>

<h3>Irregular verbs</h3>
<p>An <strong>irregular verb</strong> does not follow the usual pattern for forming its past simple and past participle. Its forms must be learnt through use.</p>
<ul>
  <li>go → went → gone</li>
  <li>write → wrote → written</li>
  <li>eat → ate → eaten</li>
  <li>see → saw → seen</li>
  <li>buy → bought → bought</li>
  <li>cut → cut → cut</li>
</ul>
<p>Some irregular verbs change both forms; some change only one; others have the same form in all three positions.</p>

<h3>Past simple versus past participle</h3>
<p>Use the <strong>past simple</strong> to express a past event: <em>She wrote a letter yesterday.</em></p>
<p>Use the <strong>past participle</strong> with an appropriate auxiliary in constructions such as the perfect aspect or passive voice: <em>She has written a letter.</em> <em>The letter was written yesterday.</em></p>
<p>Do not confuse these forms. In standard English, <em>She has went</em> is incorrect; write <em>She has gone</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using the past simple after <em>has</em> or <em>have</em>: <em>has ate</em> should be <em>has eaten</em>.</li>
  <li>Adding <em>-ed</em> to an irregular verb: <em>goed</em> should be <em>went</em>.</li>
  <li>Assuming all verbs with identical past and participle forms are regular. <em>Buy → bought → bought</em> is irregular.</li>
  <li>Ignoring spelling changes in regular verbs such as <em>study → studied</em>.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1419, 1419, '<h2>Transitive and Intransitive Verbs</h2>
<p>A verb''s use can be classified by whether it takes a direct object. A <strong>transitive verb</strong> has a direct object in that use. An <strong>intransitive verb</strong> does not take a direct object in that use.</p>

<h3>Direct objects</h3>
<p>A direct object receives or is directly affected by the action expressed by the verb. It answers questions such as <em>what?</em> or <em>whom?</em> after the verb.</p>
<p><em>The learner answered the question.</em></p>
<p>Answered what? <em>The question.</em> Therefore, answered is transitive in this sentence.</p>
<p><em>The teacher praised Amina.</em></p>
<p>Praised whom? <em>Amina.</em> Praised is transitive in this sentence.</p>

<h3>Intransitive verbs</h3>
<p>An intransitive verb does not take a direct object in the sentence.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The baby cried.</em></li>
  <li><em>The visitors arrived late.</em></li>
  <li><em>The sun disappeared.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Words such as <em>late</em> and phrases such as <em>at noon</em> can provide additional information without becoming direct objects.</p>

<h3>Do not confuse an object with an adverbial</h3>
<p>In <em>The child slept on the sofa</em>, slept has no direct object. On the sofa tells where the child slept.</p>
<p>In <em>The child kicked the ball across the field</em>, the ball is the direct object; across the field tells where the action took place.</p>
<p>Do not assume that every noun after a verb is an object. Its grammatical role matters.</p>

<h3>Some verbs can be both</h3>
<p>Many verbs are transitive in one sentence and intransitive in another.</p>
<p><em>She opened the door.</em> Opened is transitive because it has the object the door.</p>
<p><em>The door opened.</em> Opened is intransitive because the sentence has no direct object.</p>
<p><em>They are eating lunch.</em> Eating is transitive because lunch is the object.</p>
<p><em>They are eating.</em> Eating is intransitive in this sentence because no direct object is expressed.</p>

<h3>Direct and indirect objects</h3>
<p>Some transitive verbs can take both an indirect object and a direct object.</p>
<p><em>The teacher gave the learner a book.</em></p>
<p>The teacher gave what? <em>A book</em> is the direct object. Gave it to whom? <em>The learner</em> is the indirect object.</p>
<p>The same meaning can be expressed as <em>The teacher gave a book to the learner.</em></p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Calling a verb intransitive simply because it is followed by extra words. Extra words may be adverbials, not objects.</li>
  <li>Assuming a verb has the same transitivity in every sentence.</li>
  <li>Confusing the direct object with the subject. In <em>The ball broke the window</em>, ball is the subject and window is the direct object.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1420, 1420, '<h2>Finite and Non-finite Verbs</h2>
<p>Verb forms differ in whether they are marked for tense and participate directly in the clause''s subject–verb relationship. A <strong>finite verb</strong> functions as the verb of a clause and is associated with tense and, where relevant, subject agreement. A <strong>non-finite verb form</strong> does not independently express tense in that way.</p>

<h3>Finite verbs</h3>
<p>Finite verbs anchor a clause in time or grammatical structure.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>She walks to school.</em> Walks is finite and agrees with she in the simple present.</li>
  <li><em>They walked to school.</em> Walked is finite and expresses past time.</li>
  <li><em>The children are playing.</em> Are is finite; it agrees with the plural subject.</li>
  <li><em>He has finished.</em> Has is the finite auxiliary; finished is a non-finite past participle.</li>
</ul>
<p>A finite verb may consist of more than one word. In a verb group such as <em>has been working</em>, has is finite, while been and working are non-finite forms.</p>

<h3>Non-finite verb forms</h3>
<p>Three common non-finite forms are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Infinitive:</strong> <em>to read</em>, or the bare form <em>read</em> after some verbs and modals.</li>
  <li><strong>Gerund-participle (-ing form):</strong> <em>reading</em>, as in <em>Reading improves vocabulary</em> or <em>She is reading</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Past participle:</strong> <em>written</em>, <em>broken</em>, <em>finished</em>, as in <em>the broken window</em> or <em>has finished</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The exact grammatical role depends on the construction. For example, reading is non-finite in <em>Reading is useful</em>, where the -ing form functions as the subject. In <em>She is reading</em>, reading is also non-finite, while is carries tense.</p>

<h3>One sentence can contain both</h3>
<p><em>After finishing her homework, Wanjiku went outside.</em></p>
<p>Finishing is non-finite; it does not independently locate the action in tense. Went is finite and anchors the main clause in the past.</p>
<p><em>The work completed yesterday has been checked.</em></p>
<p>Completed is a past participle in a non-finite modifier. Has is finite and been checked forms the passive perfect verb group.</p>

<h3>How to identify the finite verb</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Find the clause''s verb group.</li>
  <li>Look for the verb form carrying tense or the agreement with the subject.</li>
  <li>If the group contains auxiliaries, identify the finite auxiliary rather than assuming every verb form is finite.</li>
  <li>Check whether other verb forms are infinitives, -ing forms or past participles dependent on the clause structure.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Calling every verb in a sentence finite.</li>
  <li>Assuming that every word ending in -ed is a finite past-tense verb. In <em>has finished</em>, finished is a past participle.</li>
  <li>Assuming an -ing form always forms a continuous tense. In <em>Swimming is enjoyable</em>, swimming functions as the subject.</li>
  <li>Confusing the number of verb words with the number of finite verbs.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1421, 1421, '<h2>Infinitives</h2>
<p>An <strong>infinitive</strong> is a non-finite verb form. English commonly uses the <strong>to-infinitive</strong> (to + base verb), as in <em>to learn</em>, and the <strong>bare infinitive</strong>, which is the base verb without to, as in <em>learn</em>.</p>

<h3>To-infinitives</h3>
<p>The to-infinitive can perform several roles in a sentence.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>After certain verbs:</strong> <em>She hopes to pass.</em></li>
  <li><strong>To express purpose:</strong> <em>He went to the library to study.</em></li>
  <li><strong>After adjectives:</strong> <em>I am ready to begin.</em></li>
  <li><strong>As a subject or subject-like construction:</strong> <em>To read widely is useful.</em></li>
  <li><strong>After question words:</strong> <em>She knows how to solve the problem.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>Bare infinitives</h3>
<p>Some words and constructions require the base verb without to.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>After modal auxiliaries:</strong> <em>can swim</em>, <em>should revise</em>, <em>might arrive</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>After do-support:</strong> <em>Does he know?</em>, <em>They did not leave.</em></li>
  <li><strong>After make and let in common active constructions:</strong> <em>The teacher made us rewrite the paragraph.</em> <em>Let them speak.</em></li>
  <li><strong>After verbs of perception in certain constructions:</strong> <em>I saw him cross the road.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Some constructions change in the passive: <em>We were made to rewrite the paragraph.</em> Here, to is required.</p>

<h3>Infinitives after verbs</h3>
<p>Some verbs are commonly followed by a to-infinitive:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>agree to help</em></li>
  <li><em>decide to leave</em></li>
  <li><em>hope to succeed</em></li>
  <li><em>plan to study</em></li>
  <li><em>promise to return</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Other verbs commonly take an -ing form, such as <em>enjoy reading</em> and <em>avoid wasting</em>. Some verbs can take either form with little change in meaning; others change meaning. Learn the pattern used by the particular verb rather than applying one rule to all verbs.</p>

<h3>Purpose and result</h3>
<p>Compare:</p>
<p><em>She went to the shop to buy bread.</em> The infinitive explains her purpose.</p>
<p><em>He was too tired to continue.</em> The infinitive completes a construction expressing that he was excessively tired for the action to be possible.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She can to swim.</em> Write <em>She can swim.</em></li>
  <li><em>He decided going home.</em> In this meaning, write <em>He decided to go home.</em></li>
  <li><em>The teacher made us to repeat it.</em> In the active construction, write <em>The teacher made us repeat it.</em></li>
  <li>Assuming every verb must be followed by an infinitive. The required pattern depends on the verb and construction.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1422, 1422, '<h2>Gerunds</h2>
<p>A <strong>gerund</strong> is an -ing form of a verb used in a position normally occupied by a noun phrase. It can name an activity, express an idea about an action, or act as the complement of certain verbs and prepositions.</p>

<h3>Gerunds as subjects</h3>
<p>A gerund can name the activity being discussed.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Reading improves vocabulary.</em></li>
  <li><em>Swimming strengthens the body.</em></li>
  <li><em>Cheating damages trust.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>In each sentence, the -ing form identifies an activity that the sentence makes a statement about.</p>

<h3>Gerunds as objects</h3>
<p>Some verbs are commonly followed by a gerund:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>enjoy reading</em></li>
  <li><em>avoid wasting</em></li>
  <li><em>finish writing</em></li>
  <li><em>suggest revising</em></li>
  <li><em>consider changing</em></li>
  <li><em>practise speaking</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Example: <em>She enjoys solving mathematical problems.</em> Solving is the gerund form functioning as the complement of enjoys.</p>

<h3>Gerunds after prepositions</h3>
<p>When a verb follows a preposition, the -ing form is normally used.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>He succeeded by working consistently.</em></li>
  <li><em>She left without saying goodbye.</em></li>
  <li><em>They are interested in learning English.</em></li>
  <li><em>Before answering, read the question carefully.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Be careful with expressions such as <em>look forward to</em>. Here, to is a preposition, so it is followed by a gerund: <em>I look forward to meeting you.</em></p>

<h3>Gerunds and infinitives</h3>
<p>Some verbs are normally followed by a gerund, some by an infinitive, and some by either form.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>She enjoys drawing.</em> Enjoy is followed by a gerund.</li>
  <li><em>She hopes to draw.</em> Hope is followed by a to-infinitive.</li>
  <li><em>He started reading.</em> <em>He started to read.</em> Both are possible with little difference in meaning in this context.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some verbs change meaning depending on the form that follows them. For example, <em>stop smoking</em> means cease the activity of smoking, while <em>stop to smoke</em> means pause another activity in order to smoke.</p>

<h3>Gerunds versus continuous verb forms</h3>
<p>Both forms end in -ing, but their grammatical roles differ.</p>
<p><em>Swimming is enjoyable.</em> Swimming names an activity and functions as the subject.</p>
<p><em>She is swimming.</em> Swimming is part of a continuous verb construction, with is as the finite auxiliary.</p>
<p>The spelling alone does not determine whether an -ing form is a gerund. Examine how it functions in the sentence.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>He enjoys to read.</em> In standard usage, write <em>He enjoys reading.</em></li>
  <li><em>She is interested in to learn.</em> Write <em>She is interested in learning.</em></li>
  <li><em>I look forward to meet you.</em> Write <em>I look forward to meeting you.</em></li>
  <li>Calling every -ing form a gerund, even when it forms part of a continuous verb construction.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1423, 1423, '<h2>Participles</h2>
<p>A <strong>participle</strong> is a verb form that can help construct verb phrases or function in descriptions and clauses. Two important forms are the present participle, which ends in -ing, and the past participle, such as written, broken or finished.</p>

<h3>Present participles</h3>
<p>The present participle is the -ing form used in constructions such as the continuous aspect.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The children are playing.</em></li>
  <li><em>She was preparing supper.</em></li>
  <li><em>They have been waiting.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>In these examples, the participle works with an auxiliary verb. It does not independently express the tense of the whole verb group.</p>

<h3>Past participles</h3>
<p>Regular verbs usually form the past participle with -ed or -d, while irregular verbs may have different forms.</p>
<ul>
  <li>work → worked</li>
  <li>write → written</li>
  <li>break → broken</li>
  <li>choose → chosen</li>
  <li>take → taken</li>
</ul>
<p>Past participles appear in perfect constructions and passive constructions.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Perfect: <em>She has written the report.</em></li>
  <li>Passive: <em>The report was written yesterday.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>Participles as modifiers</h3>
<p>A participle can describe a noun.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The crying baby needs attention.</em> Crying describes the baby.</li>
  <li><em>The broken window was replaced.</em> Broken describes the window.</li>
  <li><em>The students waiting outside will enter next.</em> Waiting describes which students are meant.</li>
</ul>
<p>Present participles often suggest an ongoing activity or characteristic. Past participles often suggest a completed action, a resulting state or a passive relationship, depending on the verb and context.</p>

<h3>Participial phrases</h3>
<p>A participial phrase contains a participle and the words that belong with it.</p>
<p><em>Carrying a heavy bag, the learner climbed the stairs.</em></p>
<p><em>Carrying a heavy bag</em> describes the learner and gives additional information about the action.</p>
<p>The phrase must clearly refer to the noun it is intended to describe.</p>

<h3>Avoid dangling participles</h3>
<p>Compare:</p>
<p><em>Walking to school, the rain began to fall.</em></p>
<p>This sentence mistakenly suggests that the rain was walking to school.</p>
<p>Write instead: <em>Walking to school, I noticed that the rain had begun to fall.</em></p>
<p>Now the participial phrase clearly describes I, the person walking.</p>

<h3>Participles versus gerunds</h3>
<p>Both gerunds and present participles end in -ing. A gerund functions like a noun phrase: <em>Reading helps me learn.</em> A participle may form part of a verb group or describe a noun: <em>I am reading</em>; <em>the reading child</em>.</p>
<p>Some grammar frameworks use the broader term <em>-ing form</em> for both gerunds and present participles. The important skill is identifying the form''s role in context.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She has wrote a letter.</em> Use the past participle: <em>She has written a letter.</em></li>
  <li>Using a participial phrase that appears to describe the wrong noun.</li>
  <li>Assuming every past participle expresses past time by itself. In <em>a broken cup</em>, broken describes the cup rather than anchoring a finite clause in past time.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1424, 1424, '<h2>Present Simple</h2>
<p>The <strong>present simple</strong> is used to describe habits, repeated actions, general truths, stable situations and certain scheduled events. It does not automatically mean that an action is happening at this exact moment.</p>

<h3>Forming the present simple</h3>
<p>With <strong>I, you, we</strong> and <strong>they</strong>, use the base form:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>I study every evening.</em></li>
  <li><em>They walk to school.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>With <strong>he, she</strong> and <strong>it</strong>, the verb normally takes -s or -es:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>She studies every evening.</em></li>
  <li><em>He watches the news.</em></li>
  <li><em>The bus arrives at six.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>Spelling rules for the third-person singular</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Most verbs add -s: <em>read → reads</em>.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in -s, -sh, -ch, -x or -o commonly add -es: <em>wash → washes</em>, <em>go → goes</em>.</li>
  <li>Many verbs ending in consonant + y change y to i and add -es: <em>study → studies</em>.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in vowel + y usually add -s: <em>play → plays</em>.</li>
  <li>Be and have have special forms: <em>she is</em>, <em>she has</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>When to use it</h3>
<p><strong>Habits:</strong> <em>My brother exercises every morning.</em></p>
<p><strong>General truths:</strong> <em>Water freezes at 0°C under standard atmospheric pressure.</em></p>
<p><strong>Stable situations:</strong> <em>She lives in Embu.</em></p>
<p><strong>Schedules:</strong> <em>The lesson starts at eight tomorrow.</em></p>
<p><strong>Instructions or commentary:</strong> <em>First, you add the flour. Then you mix the ingredients.</em></p>

<h3>Negative sentences</h3>
<p>Use do not or don''t with I, you, we and they. Use does not or doesn''t with he, she and it.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>I do not understand the question.</em></li>
  <li><em>She does not understand the question.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>After does or doesn''t, use the base form: <em>She doesn''t understand</em>, not <em>She doesn''t understands</em>.</p>

<h3>Questions</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>Do you attend the club?</em></li>
  <li><em>Does he attend the club?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Use the base form after do or does. The auxiliary carries the grammatical marking.</p>
<p>With the verb be, form questions without do: <em>Is she ready?</em> not <em>Does she be ready?</em></p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>He walk to school.</em> Write <em>He walks to school.</em></li>
  <li><em>Does Amina studies?</em> Write <em>Does Amina study?</em></li>
  <li><em>She don''t know.</em> Write <em>She doesn''t know.</em></li>
  <li>Using the present simple for an action in progress when the context calls for the present continuous.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1425, 1425, '<h2>Present Continuous</h2>
<p>The <strong>present continuous</strong>, also called the present progressive, describes an action in progress around the present time, a temporary situation, a developing change or a future arrangement.</p>

<h3>Forming the present continuous</h3>
<p>Use the present form of be + the -ing form of the main verb.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I <strong>am studying</strong>.</li>
  <li>He <strong>is reading</strong>.</li>
  <li>They <strong>are working</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The auxiliary must agree with the subject: am with I, is with he/she/it and are with you/we/they.</p>

<h3>Actions happening around now</h3>
<p><em>The learners are writing their answers.</em></p>
<p>The action is in progress at the time being discussed.</p>
<p>The action does not have to be happening at the exact second of speaking:</p>
<p><em>I am reading a novel this week.</em></p>
<p>The reading is a temporary activity around the present period, even if the learner is not holding the novel at that exact moment.</p>

<h3>Temporary situations and changes</h3>
<p><strong>Temporary situation:</strong> <em>She is staying with her aunt this month.</em></p>
<p><strong>Developing change:</strong> <em>The town is growing rapidly.</em></p>
<p><strong>Future arrangement:</strong> <em>We are meeting the principal on Friday.</em></p>
<p>For a future arrangement, the context normally indicates that plans have already been made.</p>

<h3>Spelling the -ing form</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Most verbs add -ing: <em>read → reading</em>.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in silent -e usually drop the e: <em>write → writing</em>.</li>
  <li>Some short verbs double the final consonant: <em>run → running</em>.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in -ie change ie to y: <em>lie → lying</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Consonant doubling depends on the spelling and stress pattern; it is not required for every verb.</p>

<h3>Negative sentences and questions</h3>
<p>Negative: <em>She is not listening.</em></p>
<p>Question: <em>Are they listening?</em></p>
<p>Use the appropriate form of be. Do not add do or does to form present-continuous questions or negatives.</p>

<h3>Stative verbs</h3>
<p>Many verbs describing states, knowledge, possession or feelings are not normally used in the continuous form when they keep their usual stative meaning.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>I understand the explanation.</em> Not normally: <em>I am understanding the explanation.</em></li>
  <li><em>She owns a bicycle.</em> Not normally: <em>She is owning a bicycle.</em></li>
  <li><em>They believe the story.</em> Not normally: <em>They are believing the story.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Some verbs can be used in either form when their meaning changes: <em>I think you are right</em> expresses an opinion, while <em>I am thinking about the problem</em> describes a process of thought.</p>

<h3>Present simple versus present continuous</h3>
<p><em>She works at a clinic</em> presents her work as a general or stable situation.</p>
<p><em>She is working at a clinic this month</em> presents the situation as temporary.</p>
<p><em>They play football every Saturday</em> describes a habit.</p>
<p><em>They are playing football now</em> describes an action in progress.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>They is waiting.</em> Write <em>They are waiting.</em></li>
  <li><em>She is write a letter.</em> Write <em>She is writing a letter.</em></li>
  <li><em>Do you studying?</em> Write <em>Are you studying?</em></li>
  <li>Using the continuous form automatically for every verb, including stative uses that normally take the simple form.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1426, 1426, '<h2>Past Simple</h2>
<p>The <strong>past simple</strong> describes events, actions or situations viewed as completed in the past. It is commonly used when the time is stated or understood from context.</p>

<h3>Regular past forms</h3>
<p>Most regular verbs form the past simple with -ed or -d.</p>
<ul>
  <li>walk → walked</li>
  <li>clean → cleaned</li>
  <li>arrive → arrived</li>
  <li>study → studied</li>
  <li>stop → stopped</li>
</ul>
<p>Apply the spelling rules carefully: verbs ending in silent -e add -d; many consonant + y endings change y to i and add -ed; some short verbs double the final consonant.</p>

<h3>Irregular past forms</h3>
<p>Irregular verbs do not follow the regular -ed pattern.</p>
<ul>
  <li>go → went</li>
  <li>see → saw</li>
  <li>take → took</li>
  <li>write → wrote</li>
  <li>buy → bought</li>
  <li>come → came</li>
</ul>
<p>Learn the past simple form separately from the past participle. For example, the past simple of write is wrote, while the past participle is written.</p>

<h3>When to use the past simple</h3>
<p><strong>Completed event:</strong> <em>The learners submitted their assignments yesterday.</em></p>
<p><strong>Past state:</strong> <em>My grandfather lived in Nyeri when he was young.</em></p>
<p><strong>Sequence of events:</strong> <em>She entered the room, greeted the teacher and sat down.</em></p>
<p><strong>Past habit or repeated event:</strong> <em>We visited our grandparents every December.</em></p>

<h3>Negative sentences</h3>
<p>Use did not or didn''t + the base form of the main verb.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>She did not attend the meeting.</em></li>
  <li><em>They didn''t finish the exercise.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Did carries the past-tense marking, so the main verb uses its base form. Write <em>did not go</em>, not <em>did not went</em>.</p>

<h3>Questions</h3>
<p>Use did + subject + base form:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Did you complete the assignment?</em></li>
  <li><em>Did the visitors arrive early?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Again, use the base form after did. With be, form questions directly: <em>Was she ready?</em> <em>Were they late?</em></p>

<h3>Past simple versus present perfect</h3>
<p>The past simple often places an event in a finished past period: <em>I lost my keys yesterday.</em></p>
<p>The present perfect connects a past event to the present or to an experience without specifying a finished past time: <em>I have lost my keys</em> can imply that I still cannot find them.</p>
<p>Do not normally combine the present perfect with a finished past-time expression such as <em>yesterday</em>. Write <em>I saw him yesterday</em>, not <em>I have seen him yesterday</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She goed home.</em> Write <em>She went home.</em></li>
  <li><em>Did he wrote the letter?</em> Write <em>Did he write the letter?</em></li>
  <li><em>They didn''t arrived.</em> Write <em>They didn''t arrive.</em></li>
  <li>Using the past simple without checking whether the sentence requires a different time relationship.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1427, 1427, '<h2>Past Continuous</h2>
<p>The <strong>past continuous</strong>, also called the past progressive, describes an action or situation that was in progress at a particular time in the past.</p>

<h3>Form</h3>
<p>Use <strong>was/were + the -ing form</strong> of the main verb.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I was reading.</li>
  <li>She was preparing supper.</li>
  <li>They were playing football.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use was with I, he, she and it. Use were with you, we and they.</p>

<h3>An action in progress at a past time</h3>
<p><em>At eight o''clock last night, I was revising English.</em></p>
<p>The sentence presents the revision as an activity in progress at eight o''clock. It does not focus on when the activity began or ended.</p>
<p><em>When the teacher entered, the learners were writing.</em></p>
<p>The writing was already in progress when the teacher entered.</p>

<h3>An action interrupted by another action</h3>
<p>The past continuous often describes the background activity, while the past simple describes an event that occurred during it.</p>
<p><em>While I was walking home, it started to rain.</em></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Was walking:</strong> the activity in progress.</li>
  <li><strong>Started:</strong> the event that occurred during that activity.</li>
</ul>
<p>The continuous action does not have to stop permanently. The sentence simply places the second event within its time frame.</p>

<h3>Two actions happening at the same time</h3>
<p><em>While Amina was cooking, her brother was setting the table.</em></p>
<p>Both activities were in progress during the same period. The past continuous helps show that overlap.</p>

<h3>Background description</h3>
<p>The past continuous can establish the situation in a narrative:</p>
<p><em>The wind was blowing, people were hurrying home, and dark clouds were gathering.</em></p>
<p>These ongoing details establish the scene before a new event is introduced.</p>

<h3>Negative sentences and questions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Negative: <em>He was not sleeping.</em></li>
  <li>Question: <em>Were they listening?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Use was or were to form negatives and questions. Do not add did: <em>Was he sleeping?</em> is correct, not <em>Did he was sleeping?</em></p>

<h3>Past simple versus past continuous</h3>
<p><em>She cooked supper at six.</em> This presents the cooking as a past event.</p>
<p><em>She was cooking supper at six.</em> This presents the cooking as in progress at that time.</p>
<p>The difference is not simply that one action is long and the other short. The key distinction is whether the sentence views the event as a whole or as an activity in progress at a past reference time.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>They was playing.</em> Write <em>They were playing.</em></li>
  <li><em>She was cook supper.</em> Write <em>She was cooking supper.</em></li>
  <li><em>Did you were reading?</em> Write <em>Were you reading?</em></li>
  <li>Using the continuous form automatically with stative verbs such as know when the intended meaning is a state rather than an ongoing activity.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1428, 1428, '<h2>Present Perfect</h2>
<p>The <strong>present perfect</strong> connects a past event or situation with the present. It is formed using <strong>has/have + the past participle</strong>.</p>

<h3>Form</h3>
<ul>
  <li>I have finished.</li>
  <li>She has arrived.</li>
  <li>They have written the report.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use has with he, she and it. Use have with I, you, we and they.</p>
<p>Remember to use the past participle, not automatically the past-simple form: <em>has gone</em>, not <em>has went</em>.</p>

<h3>Past actions with a present result</h3>
<p><em>I have lost my key.</em></p>
<p>The loss happened in the past, but it matters now because the key may still be missing.</p>
<p><em>The rain has damaged the road.</em></p>
<p>The damage occurred before now and has a present consequence.</p>

<h3>Life experiences</h3>
<p>The present perfect can describe whether someone has experienced something at any time up to now, without specifying a finished past time.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>I have visited Mombasa.</em></li>
  <li><em>She has never ridden a horse.</em></li>
  <li><em>Have you ever seen a solar eclipse?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Words such as <em>ever</em>, <em>never</em> and <em>before</em> commonly appear in experience statements.</p>

<h3>An unfinished period or situation</h3>
<p><em>We have had three tests this term.</em></p>
<p>If the term is still in progress, the statement counts events within a period that remains open.</p>
<p><em>She has lived here for five years.</em></p>
<p>This normally means she began living here five years ago and still lives here.</p>
<p><em>They have known each other since childhood.</em></p>
<p>Since identifies the starting point; for expresses a duration.</p>

<h3>Already, just and yet</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Already:</strong> <em>She has already submitted her work.</em> The action happened earlier than expected or by now.</li>
  <li><strong>Just:</strong> <em>They have just arrived.</em> The event happened very recently.</li>
  <li><strong>Yet:</strong> <em>Have you finished yet?</em> or <em>I have not finished yet.</em> It is common in questions and negatives about an expected event.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Negative sentences and questions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Negative: <em>He has not completed the task.</em></li>
  <li>Question: <em>Have they arrived?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Use has or have directly. Do not use did to form present-perfect questions.</p>

<h3>Present perfect versus past simple</h3>
<p><em>I saw the film yesterday.</em> Yesterday is a finished past-time expression, so the past simple is appropriate.</p>
<p><em>I have seen the film.</em> This describes experience without stating when the viewing happened.</p>
<p>In standard usage, avoid combining the present perfect with a finished past-time expression such as <em>last week</em> or <em>in 2022</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She have finished.</em> Write <em>She has finished.</em></li>
  <li><em>He has wrote.</em> Write <em>He has written.</em></li>
  <li><em>I have seen her yesterday.</em> Write <em>I saw her yesterday.</em></li>
  <li>Using the present perfect when the context requires a clearly finished past time.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1429, 1429, '<h2>Present Perfect Continuous</h2>
<p>The <strong>present perfect continuous</strong> connects an activity that began in the past with the present. It often emphasises duration, repeated activity or the ongoing process rather than simply the completed result.</p>

<h3>Form</h3>
<p>Use <strong>has/have + been + the -ing form</strong>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I have been studying.</li>
  <li>She has been working.</li>
  <li>They have been waiting.</li>
</ul>
<p>Has or have agrees with the subject. Been is the past participle of be, and the final verb takes the -ing form.</p>

<h3>An activity continuing until now</h3>
<p><em>I have been reading for two hours.</em></p>
<p>The sentence emphasises the activity and its duration. It commonly suggests that the reading is still in progress or has only recently stopped.</p>
<p><em>It has been raining since dawn.</em></p>
<p>Since dawn identifies when the activity began; the present perfect continuous connects that activity to the present.</p>

<h3>Recent activity with present evidence</h3>
<p><em>Her hands are dirty because she has been gardening.</em></p>
<p>The gardening may have stopped, but its effects are visible now.</p>
<p><em>You look tired. Have you been working all night?</em></p>
<p>The question connects recent activity to the person''s present condition.</p>

<h3>For and since</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>For</strong> introduces a duration: <em>for twenty minutes</em>, <em>for three weeks</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Since</strong> introduces a starting point: <em>since Monday</em>, <em>since 2024</em>, <em>since noon</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples: <em>We have been waiting for an hour.</em> <em>We have been waiting since nine o''clock.</em></p>

<h3>Present perfect continuous versus present perfect simple</h3>
<p><em>She has written three pages.</em> The present perfect simple emphasises the completed result or quantity.</p>
<p><em>She has been writing for an hour.</em> The present perfect continuous emphasises the activity and duration; it does not state how many pages she completed.</p>
<p>Both forms may be possible in some contexts, but the emphasis differs.</p>

<h3>Stative verbs</h3>
<p>Stative verbs such as <em>know, believe, own</em> and <em>belong</em> are not normally used in the continuous form when they express their usual states.</p>
<p>Write <em>I have known her for years</em>, rather than normally writing <em>I have been knowing her for years</em>.</p>
<p>Some verbs can be continuous when they describe an activity rather than a state. For example, <em>think</em> can express an opinion in <em>I think it is correct</em> or an ongoing process in <em>I have been thinking about the problem</em>.</p>

<h3>Negative sentences and questions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Negative: <em>He has not been sleeping well.</em></li>
  <li>Question: <em>Have they been practising?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Put not after has or have. In questions, move has or have before the subject.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She has been study for hours.</em> Write <em>She has been studying for hours.</em></li>
  <li><em>They has been waiting.</em> Write <em>They have been waiting.</em></li>
  <li><em>I have been knowing him since 2020.</em> Normally write <em>I have known him since 2020.</em></li>
  <li>Confusing an activity''s duration with a completed result or quantity.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1430, 1430, '<h2>Past Perfect</h2>
<p>The <strong>past perfect</strong> describes an event or situation that occurred before a particular point or event in the past. It helps establish the order of past events when the sequence matters.</p>

<h3>Form</h3>
<p>Use <strong>had + the past participle</strong> for all subjects.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I had finished.</li>
  <li>She had left.</li>
  <li>They had written the report.</li>
</ul>
<p>Had does not change according to the subject. Use the past participle, such as finished, gone, written or eaten.</p>

<h3>An earlier event before a later past event</h3>
<p><em>When we reached the station, the train had left.</em></p>
<p>There are two past events:</p>
<ol>
  <li>The train left first.</li>
  <li>We reached the station later.</li>
</ol>
<p>The past perfect marks the earlier event. The past simple marks the later event.</p>

<h3>Explaining a past condition</h3>
<p><em>She was tired because she had slept badly.</em></p>
<p>Sleeping badly occurred before the past condition of being tired. The past perfect helps explain the cause of that condition.</p>
<p><em>He could not enter because he had forgotten his key.</em></p>
<p>Forgetting the key happened before the failed attempt to enter.</p>

<h3>Reported situations and experiences</h3>
<p>The past perfect is often used when reporting something from a past point of view.</p>
<p><em>By the time the meeting began, the secretary had prepared the documents.</em></p>
<p>The preparation was complete before the meeting began.</p>

<h3>Past perfect versus past simple</h3>
<p>Use the past simple when the sequence is clear or when the sentence simply reports past events:</p>
<p><em>She opened the door and entered the room.</em></p>
<p>The order is clear from the sequence of verbs, so the past perfect is not necessary.</p>
<p>Use the past perfect when you need to show that an event was already complete before another past reference point:</p>
<p><em>She had opened the door before the visitors arrived.</em></p>
<p>The past perfect is not simply a way to make every earlier action sound more formal. Use it when the past-before-past relationship is meaningful.</p>

<h3>Negative sentences and questions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Negative: <em>They had not eaten before the journey.</em></li>
  <li>Question: <em>Had she completed the task before noon?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Use had directly. The past participle remains unchanged.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>She had went home.</em> Write <em>She had gone home.</em></li>
  <li>Using the past perfect for every action in a story even when no earlier-than-past relationship needs to be shown.</li>
  <li>Failing to distinguish the earlier event from the later past reference point.</li>
  <li>Assuming that the word before always requires the past perfect. The tense depends on meaning and context.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1431, 1431, '<h2>Past Perfect Continuous</h2>
<p>The <strong>past perfect continuous</strong> describes an activity that continued over a period before a particular point or event in the past. It emphasises duration, ongoing process or repeated activity leading up to that past reference point.</p>

<h3>Form</h3>
<p>Use <strong>had + been + the -ing form</strong>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I had been reading.</li>
  <li>She had been working.</li>
  <li>They had been practising.</li>
</ul>
<p>Had remains the same for all subjects. Been is followed by the -ing form of the main verb.</p>

<h3>Duration before a past event</h3>
<p><em>They had been waiting for two hours when the bus arrived.</em></p>
<p>The waiting began before the bus arrived and continued up to that point. The phrase for two hours emphasises the duration.</p>
<p><em>She had been studying since dawn when the electricity went off.</em></p>
<p>The studying was already in progress before the power failure.</p>

<h3>Explaining a past result or condition</h3>
<p><em>The ground was wet because it had been raining.</em></p>
<p>The rain occurred before the ground was observed to be wet. The past perfect continuous emphasises the activity that caused the condition.</p>
<p><em>He was exhausted because he had been carrying heavy boxes.</em></p>
<p>The earlier activity explains the later state of exhaustion.</p>

<h3>Past perfect continuous versus past perfect simple</h3>
<p><em>She had written five pages before lunch.</em></p>
<p>The past perfect simple emphasises the completed result or quantity: five pages.</p>
<p><em>She had been writing for two hours before lunch.</em></p>
<p>The past perfect continuous emphasises the duration of the activity, not the amount completed.</p>
<p>Both can describe earlier events, but they focus on different aspects of those events.</p>

<h3>For and since</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>For:</strong> duration, such as <em>for an hour</em> or <em>for several months</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Since:</strong> starting point, such as <em>since morning</em> or <em>since January</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>By noon, the learners had been revising for three hours.</em></p>
<p><em>By noon, the learners had been revising since nine o''clock.</em></p>
<p>Both sentences connect the revision to a past reference point.</p>

<h3>When not to use it</h3>
<p>Stative verbs such as <em>know, own, believe</em> and <em>belong</em> are not normally used in the continuous form when they express their usual state.</p>
<p>Write <em>She had known him for years before they worked together</em>, rather than normally writing <em>She had been knowing him for years</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>They had been wait for hours.</em> Write <em>They had been waiting for hours.</em></li>
  <li><em>She has been working before the meeting began.</em> If the activity is viewed from a past reference point, write <em>She had been working before the meeting began.</em></li>
  <li>Using the continuous form when the sentence''s main focus is a completed quantity or result.</li>
  <li>Forgetting that the activity is related to a point in the past, not necessarily to the present.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1432, 1432, '<h2>Future Forms</h2>
<p>English does not use one single form for every future event. The form you choose tells the reader whether something is a prediction, a plan, an arrangement, or an action that will be completed before a future time.</p>

<h3>1. Will + base verb</h3>
<p>Use <strong>will + base verb</strong> for predictions, decisions made at the moment of speaking, promises, and offers.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Prediction:</strong> The rain will stop soon.</li>
  <li><strong>Immediate decision:</strong> The phone is ringing. I will answer it.</li>
  <li><strong>Promise:</strong> I will return your book tomorrow.</li>
  <li><strong>Offer:</strong> I will help you carry the box.</li>
</ul>
<p>After <em>will</em>, use the base form of the verb. Say <em>will go</em>, not <em>will goes</em> or <em>will went</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Be going to + base verb</h3>
<p>Use <strong>am/is/are going to + base verb</strong> for intentions already formed and predictions based on present evidence.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Intention:</strong> She is going to study Chemistry tonight.</li>
  <li><strong>Present evidence:</strong> Those dark clouds are going to bring rain.</li>
</ul>
<p>The verb <em>be</em> must agree with the subject: <em>I am going to</em>, <em>he is going to</em>, and <em>they are going to</em>.</p>

<h3>3. Present continuous for arrangements</h3>
<p>Use the present continuous for a future arrangement that has already been organised, often with a stated time or place.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> We are meeting the head teacher at 10 a.m. tomorrow.</p>
<p>This suggests that the meeting has been arranged, not merely imagined.</p>

<h3>4. Present simple for timetables</h3>
<p>Use the present simple for fixed schedules, programmes, and timetables.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The bus leaves at 6:30 a.m. tomorrow.</p>
<p>The bus is following a schedule. The sentence is not describing a personal intention.</p>

<h3>5. Future perfect</h3>
<p>Use <strong>will have + past participle</strong> for an action that will be completed before a particular future time.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> By Friday, Amina will have completed the assignment.</p>
<p>The reference point is Friday. The assignment will be complete by then.</p>

<h3>Compare the meanings</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>I will visit my aunt.</em> — A prediction, promise, or decision, depending on context.</li>
  <li><em>I am going to visit my aunt.</em> — An intention or evidence-based prediction.</li>
  <li><em>I am visiting my aunt on Saturday.</em> — An arranged visit.</li>
  <li><em>The train arrives at noon.</em> — A scheduled event.</li>
  <li><em>By noon, I will have arrived.</em> — Arrival completed before noon.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: She will goes home. Correct: She will go home.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: They is going to travel. Correct: They are going to travel.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: By tomorrow, I will have finish. Correct: By tomorrow, I will have finished.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Choose a future form according to the meaning and context, not simply because the event has not happened yet.</p>', 1),
  (1433, 1433, '<h2>Choosing Tense</h2>
<p>Choosing a tense means selecting a verb form that accurately expresses when an event happens and how it relates to another time or event. The correct tense depends on the meaning of the whole sentence, not on a time word alone.</p>

<h3>1. Start with the time of the event</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Present:</strong> Facts, habits, or actions happening now.</li>
  <li><strong>Past:</strong> Events that happened before the present.</li>
  <li><strong>Future:</strong> Events expected or planned to happen later.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Water boils at normal atmospheric pressure. — A general fact.</li>
  <li>We visited the museum yesterday. — A completed past event.</li>
  <li>We will visit the museum tomorrow. — A future event.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Ask whether the action is complete or in progress</h3>
<p>Compare these sentences:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>When I arrived, they cooked.</em> — This can describe a completed past action, depending on context.</li>
  <li><em>When I arrived, they were cooking.</em> — Their cooking was already in progress at the time I arrived.</li>
</ul>
<p>The past continuous often provides background for a shorter event expressed in the past simple.</p>

<h3>3. Ask whether the action connects to another time</h3>
<p>The present perfect connects a past event to the present. It can describe an experience, a recent event, or a past action with a present result.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>I have lost my key.</em> — The loss matters now because I do not have the key.</li>
  <li><em>I lost my key yesterday.</em> — The sentence places the event at a specific, finished past time.</li>
</ul>
<p>In standard usage, avoid the present perfect with a finished-time expression such as <em>yesterday</em> when the sentence locates the event entirely in that past period.</p>

<h3>4. Recognise actions completed before other past events</h3>
<p>Use the past perfect when you need to show that one past action happened before another past event.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The lesson had started before we entered the classroom.</p>
<p>The lesson started first; our entry happened later. The past perfect makes that order clear.</p>

<h3>5. Use time expressions as clues, not automatic answers</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>Every day</em> often accompanies the present simple for habits.</li>
  <li><em>At the moment</em> often accompanies the present continuous.</li>
  <li><em>Last week</em> often accompanies the past simple.</li>
  <li><em>Since Monday</em> may accompany a perfect form, depending on meaning.</li>
  <li><em>By next month</em> may signal the future perfect when completion is emphasised.</li>
</ul>
<p>These expressions are clues. Always check what the speaker intends to communicate.</p>

<h3>6. A reliable decision process</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Locate the event in present, past, or future time.</li>
  <li>Decide whether the action is habitual, in progress, completed, or connected to another time.</li>
  <li>Check whether another event establishes a reference point.</li>
  <li>Choose the tense that expresses that relationship.</li>
  <li>Read the complete sentence to check that the meaning is logical.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: I have seen him yesterday. Correct: I saw him yesterday.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: She is knowing the answer. Correct: She knows the answer.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: When the bell rang, we eat. Correct: When the bell rang, we were eating, if the eating was already in progress.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Tense communicates both time and the way an action relates to that time.</p>', 1),
  (1434, 1434, '<h2>Tense Consistency</h2>
<p>Tense consistency means choosing verb tenses that make the time relationships in a sentence or passage clear. It does not mean that every verb must have the same tense. Tenses can change when the time, meaning, or relationship between events changes.</p>

<h3>1. Keep the same time frame when it has not changed</h3>
<p>When describing a sequence of events that all happened in the past, keep the verbs in an appropriate past-time frame.</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Yesterday, Njeri walked to school and meets her friend.</p>
<p><strong>Correct:</strong> Yesterday, Njeri walked to school and met her friend.</p>
<p>Both actions occurred yesterday, so the past simple is appropriate for both.</p>

<h3>2. Change tense when the meaning requires it</h3>
<p>A tense change is correct when it reflects a real difference in time.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Kamau missed the bus yesterday, so he is walking to school today.</p>
<p><em>Missed</em> refers to yesterday; <em>is walking</em> refers to what is happening today. The change is meaningful, not an error.</p>

<h3>3. Keep past-tense narration coherent</h3>
<p>When telling a story in the past, use the past simple for completed events and the past continuous for actions in progress when relevant.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners were writing when the bell rang. They packed their books and left the room.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Were writing</em> describes an action already in progress.</li>
  <li><em>Rang</em> marks the event that occurred during it.</li>
  <li><em>Packed</em> and <em>left</em> describe later completed events.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Distinguish general truths from past events</h3>
<p>A general truth may remain in the present tense even when the surrounding passage uses the past tense.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher explained that the Earth revolves around the Sun.</p>
<p><em>Explained</em> refers to a past event. <em>Revolves</em> expresses a general scientific fact, so the present tense is suitable.</p>

<h3>5. Maintain the correct sequence of events</h3>
<p>Use perfect tenses when they help make the order of events clear.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> By the time the match began, the spectators had taken their seats.</p>
<p>The spectators sat down first; the match began afterwards. The past perfect marks the earlier action.</p>

<h3>6. How to correct tense shifts</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Identify the time frame of the passage.</li>
  <li>Underline the main verbs.</li>
  <li>Determine when each action occurs.</li>
  <li>Correct any tense that conflicts with the intended time.</li>
  <li>Keep deliberate changes that express a different time or a general truth.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: She opened the window and looks outside. Correct: She opened the window and looked outside.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: I was walking home when I see the accident. Correct: I was walking home when I saw the accident.</li>
  <li>Not necessarily incorrect: He said that water boils at 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure. The reporting verb is past, but the general truth remains in the present.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Keep tenses consistent with the intended timeline, not mechanically identical.</p>', 1),
  (1435, 1435, '<h2>Identifying the Subject</h2>
<p>The subject is the word or group of words that tells us who or what a clause is about. In many sentences, it identifies the person or thing performing an action, experiencing a state, or being described.</p>

<h3>1. Find the verb first</h3>
<p>A reliable way to find the subject is to identify the main verb, then ask who or what performs the action or is described.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The young learner answered the question.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Verb: <em>answered</em>.</li>
  <li>Ask: Who answered?</li>
  <li>Subject: <em>The young learner</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The complete subject includes the main noun and the words that describe or determine it. The head noun is <em>learner</em>, but the complete subject is <em>The young learner</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Do not confuse the subject with the object</h3>
<p>The subject and object have different roles.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The librarian helped the new student.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Subject: <em>The librarian</em> — the person who performed the action.</li>
  <li>Verb: <em>helped</em>.</li>
  <li>Object: <em>the new student</em> — the person who received the help.</li>
</ul>
<p>To identify the subject, do not simply choose the noun nearest the verb.</p>

<h3>3. Watch for extra information between subject and verb</h3>
<p>A phrase between the subject and the verb may distract you, but it does not automatically become part of the subject.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The basket of mangoes is on the table.</p>
<p>The subject is <em>The basket of mangoes</em>, whose head noun is <em>basket</em>. The phrase <em>of mangoes</em> describes the basket. It does not make the subject plural.</p>

<h3>4. Find the subject in questions</h3>
<p>Questions often place an auxiliary verb before the subject.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Have the players arrived?</p>
<ul>
  <li>Auxiliary verb: <em>Have</em>.</li>
  <li>Subject: <em>the players</em>.</li>
  <li>Main verb: <em>arrived</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not mistake the first word in a question for the subject.</p>

<h3>5. Recognise subjects in commands</h3>
<p>In a command, the subject is usually understood to be <em>you</em>, even when it is not written.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Close the door.</p>
<p>The understood subject is <em>you</em>: (You) close the door.</p>

<h3>6. Recognise subjects in sentences beginning with ''there''</h3>
<p>In sentences such as <em>There are three books on the desk</em>, the word <em>there</em> introduces the statement; it is not the subject in the usual grammatical analysis.</p>
<p>The grammatical subject is <em>three books</em>. The verb agrees with that subject.</p>

<h3>7. A practical method</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Locate the verb or verb phrase.</li>
  <li>Ask who or what performs the action or is described.</li>
  <li>Check whether the sentence is a question or command.</li>
  <li>Distinguish the subject from objects and descriptive phrases.</li>
  <li>Identify the complete subject and, when needed, its head noun.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>In ''The box of pencils is missing'', the subject is ''The box of pencils'', not just ''pencils''.</li>
  <li>In ''Did the child finish?'', the subject is ''the child'', not ''did''.</li>
  <li>In ''Open your books'', the understood subject is ''you''.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Find the subject by its grammatical role, not merely by its position in the sentence.</p>', 1),
  (1436, 1436, '<h2>Singular Subject–Verb Agreement</h2>
<p>Subject–verb agreement means matching a verb to its subject in number and person. In the present simple, a third-person singular subject usually takes a verb ending in <em>-s</em> or <em>-es</em>. This is one of the most common places where English learners make errors.</p>

<h3>1. The basic present-simple rule</h3>
<p>With <em>he</em>, <em>she</em>, <em>it</em>, or a singular noun, the present-simple verb usually ends in <em>-s</em> or <em>-es</em>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The learner <strong>reads</strong> every evening.</li>
  <li>Mary <strong>watches</strong> the news.</li>
  <li>The machine <strong>works</strong> well.</li>
</ul>
<p>With <em>I</em>, <em>you</em>, <em>we</em>, and <em>they</em>, use the base form for most present-simple verbs: <em>I read</em>, <em>you read</em>, <em>we read</em>, <em>they read</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Forming the singular verb</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Most verbs add <em>-s</em>: work → works; read → reads.</li>
  <li>Verbs ending in many sibilant sounds add <em>-es</em>: watch → watches; pass → passes; wash → washes.</li>
  <li>For a consonant followed by <em>y</em>, change <em>y</em> to <em>ies</em>: study → studies; carry → carries.</li>
  <li>For a vowel followed by <em>y</em>, add <em>-s</em>: play → plays; enjoy → enjoys.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. The special verb ''be''</h3>
<p>In the present tense, <em>be</em> changes form according to the subject:</p>
<ul>
  <li>I am ready.</li>
  <li>You are ready.</li>
  <li>He, she, or it is ready.</li>
  <li>We are ready.</li>
  <li>They are ready.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not use <em>is</em> with every subject or replace <em>are</em> with <em>is</em> just because a noun appears near the verb.</p>

<h3>4. The special verbs ''have'' and ''do''</h3>
<p>With a third-person singular subject:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>have</em> becomes <em>has</em>: She has a notebook.</li>
  <li><em>do</em> becomes <em>does</em>: He does his homework.</li>
</ul>
<p>When <em>does</em> is the auxiliary in a question or negative sentence, the main verb returns to its base form:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Correct: Does she <strong>play</strong> tennis?</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Does she <strong>plays</strong> tennis?</li>
  <li>Correct: He does not <strong>like</strong> noise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Do not let nearby nouns mislead you</h3>
<p>The verb agrees with the subject, not with a noun inside a phrase that describes the subject.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The list of names is on the desk.</p>
<p>The head noun is <em>list</em>, which is singular. The noun <em>names</em> is part of the phrase <em>of names</em> and does not control the verb.</p>

<h3>6. Indefinite pronouns that are singular</h3>
<p>In standard English, pronouns such as <em>everyone</em>, <em>everybody</em>, <em>someone</em>, <em>anyone</em>, <em>nobody</em>, and <em>each</em> generally take singular verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Everyone <strong>is</strong> present.</li>
  <li>Each of the learners <strong>has</strong> a book.</li>
  <li>Nobody <strong>knows</strong> the answer.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: The girl walk to school. Correct: The girl walks to school.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Each of the players have a uniform. Correct: Each of the players has a uniform.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Does he writes neatly? Correct: Does he write neatly.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Find the true subject first. Then choose the verb form that agrees with it.</p>', 1),
  (1437, 1437, '<h2>Plural Subject–Verb Agreement</h2>
<p>A plural subject refers to more than one person, animal, place, or thing. In the present simple, plural subjects generally use the base form of the verb, without the <em>-s</em> ending used with third-person singular subjects.</p>

<h3>1. The basic rule</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Singular: The learner <strong>writes</strong> neatly.</li>
  <li>Plural: The learners <strong>write</strong> neatly.</li>
  <li>Singular: The dog <strong>barks</strong> loudly.</li>
  <li>Plural: The dogs <strong>bark</strong> loudly.</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice the difference: in the present simple, adding more subjects usually removes the <em>-s</em> from the main verb.</p>

<h3>2. Plural nouns ending in -s</h3>
<p>Many plural nouns end in <em>-s</em>, but the verb does not automatically take <em>-s</em> because the subject is plural.</p>
<p><strong>Correct:</strong> The teachers prepare the examinations.</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The teachers prepares the examinations.</p>
<p>The noun ending and verb ending follow different rules. Do not match them by spelling; match the verb to the subject''s grammatical number.</p>

<h3>3. The verb ''be''</h3>
<p>The present-tense forms of <em>be</em> are different from most other verbs:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Singular: The learner <strong>is</strong> ready.</li>
  <li>Plural: The learners <strong>are</strong> ready.</li>
  <li>Singular past: The learner <strong>was</strong> ready.</li>
  <li>Plural past: The learners <strong>were</strong> ready.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. The verbs ''have'' and ''do''</h3>
<p>These verbs also change form:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Singular: She <strong>has</strong> a dictionary. Plural: They <strong>have</strong> dictionaries.</li>
  <li>Singular: He <strong>does</strong> the work. Plural: They <strong>do</strong> the work.</li>
</ul>
<p>In questions and negatives, use <em>do</em> with plural subjects:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Do the players understand the rules?</li>
  <li>The players do not understand the rules.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Plural subjects separated from verbs</h3>
<p>Extra information may appear between the subject and the verb. The verb must still agree with the subject.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The books on the top shelf belong to the librarian.</p>
<p>The subject is <em>The books</em>, not <em>the top shelf</em>. Therefore, use <em>belong</em>, not <em>belongs</em>.</p>

<h3>6. Irregular plural nouns</h3>
<p>Some plural nouns do not end in <em>-s</em>. They still take plural verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The children <strong>play</strong> outside.</li>
  <li>The men <strong>are</strong> working.</li>
  <li>The people <strong>have</strong> arrived.</li>
  <li>The mice <strong>run</strong> away.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: The girls sings well. Correct: The girls sing well.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: The children is tired. Correct: The children are tired.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: The files in the folder belongs to me. Correct: The files in the folder belong to me.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Identify the plural subject first, then choose the matching verb form.</p>', 1),
  (1438, 1438, '<h2>Compound Subjects</h2>
<p>A compound subject consists of two or more subjects joined together. The conjunction connecting them helps determine whether the verb should be singular or plural.</p>

<h3>1. Subjects joined by ''and''</h3>
<p>Two distinct subjects joined by <em>and</em> usually form a plural subject.</p>
<ul>
  <li>John and Peter <strong>are</strong> classmates.</li>
  <li>The teacher and the librarian <strong>organise</strong> the event.</li>
  <li>Reading and writing <strong>require</strong> practice.</li>
</ul>
<p>Think of the subjects together: John plus Peter means two people, so the plural verb is appropriate.</p>

<h3>2. When ''and'' refers to one combined thing</h3>
<p>Sometimes two nouns joined by <em>and</em> refer to one unit, one dish, or one idea. In that case, a singular verb may be appropriate.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Bread and butter is my usual breakfast.</p>
<p>Here, the phrase is treated as one combined breakfast. Meaning determines the agreement.</p>

<h3>3. Subjects joined by ''or'' or ''nor''</h3>
<p>When subjects are joined by <em>or</em> or <em>nor</em>, the verb commonly agrees with the subject nearest to it. This is often called proximity agreement.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Either the teacher or the learners <strong>are</strong> presenting first.</li>
  <li>Either the learners or the teacher <strong>is</strong> presenting first.</li>
  <li>Neither the head teacher nor the deputies <strong>are</strong> attending.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the subjects differ in number, the sentence can sound awkward. Rewriting it can improve clarity: <em>The teacher will present first, or the learners will.</em></p>

<h3>4. ''As well as'', ''along with'', and ''together with''</h3>
<p>These expressions add information about the subject, but they do not normally create a compound subject in the same way as <em>and</em>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The head teacher, together with the deputies, <strong>is</strong> attending.</li>
  <li>The captain, as well as the players, <strong>was</strong> interviewed.</li>
</ul>
<p>The main subject remains <em>head teacher</em> or <em>captain</em>. The additional phrase does not determine the verb.</p>

<h3>5. Indefinite expressions such as ''each'' and ''every''</h3>
<p>When <em>each</em> or <em>every</em> comes before a series of singular nouns joined by <em>and</em>, the subject is often treated as singular because the individuals are considered separately.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Every desk and every chair is numbered.</p>
<p>The emphasis is on each item individually.</p>

<h3>6. How to analyse a compound subject</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Identify all the nouns or pronouns that form the subject.</li>
  <li>Identify the conjunction joining them.</li>
  <li>Ask whether the subject represents separate people or things, one combined unit, or alternatives.</li>
  <li>Apply the relevant agreement rule.</li>
  <li>Rewrite the sentence if the result sounds confusing.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: Amina and Wanjiku is present. Correct: Amina and Wanjiku are present.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: The principal, together with the teachers, are attending. Correct: The principal, together with the teachers, is attending.</li>
  <li>With ''either...or'', check the subject nearest the verb when the subjects differ in number.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> The conjunction and the intended meaning determine how a compound subject agrees with its verb.</p>', 1),
  (1439, 1439, '<h2>Indefinite Pronouns and Agreement</h2>
<p>An indefinite pronoun refers to a person or thing without identifying exactly who or what it is. Examples include <em>everyone</em>, <em>somebody</em>, <em>anything</em>, and <em>several</em>. These pronouns do not all take the same verb form.</p>

<h3>1. Indefinite pronouns that are singular</h3>
<p>In standard English, these commonly take singular verbs:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Everyone, everybody, someone, somebody, anyone, anybody, no one, nobody</em>.</li>
  <li><em>Everything, something, anything, nothing</em>.</li>
  <li><em>Each</em> and <em>either</em> when used as pronouns referring to one of two alternatives.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Everyone <strong>is</strong> ready.</li>
  <li>Someone <strong>has</strong> left a bag behind.</li>
  <li>Nothing <strong>seems</strong> unusual.</li>
  <li>Each <strong>has</strong> a turn.</li>
</ul>
<p>Words such as <em>everyone</em> refer to multiple people in meaning, but grammatically they are singular in standard agreement.</p>

<h3>2. Indefinite pronouns that are plural</h3>
<p><em>Both</em>, <em>few</em>, <em>many</em>, and <em>several</em> normally take plural verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Both <strong>are</strong> correct.</li>
  <li>Few <strong>understand</strong> the instructions.</li>
  <li>Several <strong>have</strong> applied.</li>
  <li>Many <strong>were</strong> absent.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Pronouns whose number depends on context</h3>
<p><em>Some</em>, <em>all</em>, <em>any</em>, <em>most</em>, and <em>none</em> can take singular or plural verbs depending on what they refer to.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Some of the <strong>water is</strong> missing. — Water is uncountable here.</li>
  <li>Some of the <strong>books are</strong> missing. — Books is plural.</li>
  <li>Most of the <strong>work is</strong> complete.</li>
  <li>Most of the <strong>learners are</strong> present.</li>
</ul>
<p>Look at the noun or quantity being referred to, rather than memorising one verb for every use of these pronouns.</p>

<h3>4. ''Each of'' and ''one of''</h3>
<p>When the subject is <em>each</em> or <em>one</em>, the verb is singular even if a plural noun follows <em>of</em>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Each of the learners <strong>has</strong> a textbook.</li>
  <li>One of the doors <strong>is</strong> open.</li>
  <li>Every one of the answers <strong>is</strong> marked.</li>
</ul>
<p>The plural nouns <em>learners</em>, <em>doors</em>, and <em>answers</em> belong to phrases introduced by <em>of</em>; they do not control the main verb.</p>

<h3>5. ''None'' and meaning</h3>
<p><em>None</em> can be used with singular or plural agreement depending on the intended meaning and the noun being referred to. Plural agreement is natural when referring to multiple countable items.</p>
<ul>
  <li>None of the water <strong>is</strong> clean.</li>
  <li>None of the students <strong>are</strong> ready. — Common and natural when referring to students individually.</li>
</ul>
<p>In formal writing, choose a form that fits the noun and the meaning, and keep the sentence clear.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: Everybody have a responsibility. Correct: Everybody has a responsibility.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Several is missing. Correct: Several are missing.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Some of the books is damaged. Correct: Some of the books are damaged.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Identify the indefinite pronoun, determine what it refers to, and then select the appropriate verb.</p>', 1),
  (1440, 1440, '<h2>Collective Nouns and Agreement</h2>
<p>A collective noun names a group considered as a unit or as a collection of individuals. Examples include <em>team</em>, <em>family</em>, <em>committee</em>, <em>audience</em>, <em>class</em>, and <em>staff</em>.</p>

<h3>1. Treating the group as one unit</h3>
<p>When the group acts together as a single unit, a singular verb is generally appropriate in standard British English, which is commonly used in Kenyan formal writing.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The team <strong>is</strong> preparing for the final.</li>
  <li>The committee <strong>has</strong> reached a decision.</li>
  <li>The class <strong>was</strong> quiet during the examination.</li>
</ul>
<p>The focus is on the group as one organised body.</p>

<h3>2. Treating members as individuals</h3>
<p>When the sentence emphasises the separate actions or opinions of the members, plural agreement may be used in British English.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The team <strong>are</strong> arguing among themselves.</li>
  <li>The committee <strong>are</strong> divided in their opinions.</li>
  <li>The family <strong>are</strong> packing their own bags.</li>
</ul>
<p>The focus is on the members acting separately rather than on the group as one unit. In other varieties of English, especially American English, singular agreement is more common with collective nouns.</p>

<h3>3. Let the meaning guide the choice</h3>
<p>Compare:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The jury has reached its verdict.</em> — The jury is treated as one body making one decision.</li>
  <li><em>The jury are returning to their seats.</em> — The members are considered individually.</li>
</ul>
<p>In formal writing, avoid switching between singular and plural agreement without a reason. Pronouns such as <em>it</em> and <em>its</em> usually accompany singular treatment; <em>they</em> and <em>their</em> accompany plural treatment.</p>

<h3>4. A collective noun is not automatically plural</h3>
<p>The word names many people, but that does not mean the verb must always be plural.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The audience was silent.</p>
<p>The audience consists of many people, but the sentence describes the audience as one quiet group.</p>

<h3>5. Distinguish collective nouns from plural nouns</h3>
<ul>
  <li><em>The team is training.</em> — <em>Team</em> is a singular collective noun.</li>
  <li><em>The players are training.</em> — <em>Players</em> is a plural noun.</li>
  <li><em>The police are investigating.</em> — <em>Police</em> normally takes plural agreement in standard usage.</li>
  <li><em>The people are waiting.</em> — <em>People</em> is plural.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. Making the sentence clear</h3>
<p>If the intended agreement feels awkward or could confuse the reader, rewrite the sentence to name the individuals.</p>
<p><strong>Instead of:</strong> The committee are divided in its opinion.</p>
<p><strong>Write:</strong> The committee members are divided in their opinions.</p>
<p>The revised version makes the plural meaning explicit and keeps the pronoun consistent.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect for a group acting as one unit: The team are winning the trophy as a single unit. More consistent: The team is winning the trophy.</li>
  <li>Inconsistent: The family are packing its bags. Better: The family is packing its bags, or The family members are packing their bags.</li>
  <li>Do not assume every word naming a group follows the same agreement rule; usage depends on the noun and meaning.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Ask whether the collective noun represents one unit or individual members, then maintain consistent verb and pronoun agreement.</p>', 1),
  (1441, 1441, '<h2>Common Subject–Verb Agreement Errors</h2>
<p>Subject–verb agreement errors often happen because a sentence contains extra phrases, unusual subjects, or a verb form that looks familiar but does not match the subject. The solution is to identify the true subject before choosing the verb.</p>

<h3>1. Letting the nearest noun control the verb</h3>
<p>The verb agrees with the grammatical subject, not necessarily the noun closest to it.</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The quality of the answers are impressive.</p>
<p><strong>Correct:</strong> The quality of the answers is impressive.</p>
<p>The head noun is <em>quality</em>, which is singular. <em>Of the answers</em> is a phrase describing that quality.</p>

<h3>2. Errors with ''each'', ''every'', and ''everyone''</h3>
<p>These words generally take singular verbs in standard English.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Each student <strong>has</strong> a textbook.</li>
  <li>Every chair <strong>is</strong> clean.</li>
  <li>Everyone <strong>knows</strong> the rule.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not choose a plural verb just because the sentence refers to many people or things.</p>

<h3>3. Errors with compound subjects</h3>
<p>Two distinct subjects joined by <em>and</em> generally take a plural verb.</p>
<p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> The farmer and the driver was late.</p>
<p><strong>Correct:</strong> The farmer and the driver were late.</p>
<p>However, phrases such as <em>together with</em> and <em>as well as</em> do not usually create a plural subject on their own.</p>

<h3>4. Errors in questions and negatives</h3>
<p>When <em>does</em> or <em>do</em> acts as an auxiliary, the main verb stays in its base form.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Correct: Does she <strong>know</strong> the answer?</li>
  <li>Incorrect: Does she <strong>knows</strong> the answer?</li>
  <li>Correct: He does not <strong>like</strong> noise.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: He does not <strong>likes</strong> noise.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Errors with amounts, distances, and periods of time</h3>
<p>When an amount of time, money, distance, or measurement is treated as one total, it often takes a singular verb.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ten kilometres <strong>is</strong> a long distance to walk.</li>
  <li>Five thousand shillings <strong>is</strong> enough for the purchase.</li>
  <li>Two hours <strong>is</strong> sufficient for the examination.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the sentence emphasises separate units, plural agreement may be appropriate. Let the intended meaning guide the choice.</p>

<h3>6. Errors with ''there is'' and ''there are''</h3>
<p>In these sentences, the noun that follows the verb determines whether the verb should be singular or plural.</p>
<ul>
  <li>There <strong>is</strong> a book on the desk.</li>
  <li>There <strong>are</strong> three books on the desk.</li>
</ul>
<p>In careful formal writing, avoid allowing <em>there</em> to hide the real subject.</p>

<h3>7. Errors with titles and names</h3>
<p>A title of a book, film, or other single work generally takes a singular verb, even if the title contains a plural noun.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>Things Fall Apart</em> is a well-known novel.</p>
<p>The title refers to one work, so singular agreement is appropriate.</p>

<h3>8. A five-step correction method</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Locate the main verb.</li>
  <li>Identify the subject that controls it.</li>
  <li>Find the head noun or pronoun in that subject.</li>
  <li>Determine whether the subject is singular, plural, or a collective noun whose meaning affects agreement.</li>
  <li>Check the verb form and read the corrected sentence aloud or silently for clarity.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Final check</h3>
<p>Do not correct a sentence merely because a verb looks different from a nearby noun. First establish the grammatical relationship between the subject and verb. A sentence can contain many nouns but still have one singular subject.</p>', 1),
  (1442, 1442, '<h2>Subjects and Predicates</h2>
<p>A sentence usually communicates a complete thought by identifying what the sentence is about and saying something about it. These two main parts are the <strong>subject</strong> and the <strong>predicate</strong>.</p>

<h3>1. The subject</h3>
<p>The subject identifies the person, thing, place, or idea that the sentence is about. It may be a single word or a group of words.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Birds</em> sing.</li>
  <li><em>The young boy</em> opened the gate.</li>
  <li><em>Several students from our school</em> won the competition.</li>
</ul>
<p>The complete subject includes its main word and the words that describe or determine it. In the last example, the complete subject is <em>Several students from our school</em>, while the head noun is <em>students</em>.</p>

<h3>2. The predicate</h3>
<p>The predicate tells us something about the subject. It contains a verb and may include objects, complements, and other information.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Birds <em>sing</em>.</li>
  <li>The young boy <em>opened the gate</em>.</li>
  <li>Several students from our school <em>won the competition yesterday</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The complete predicate includes the verb and everything in the predicate that belongs with it.</p>

<h3>3. Finding both parts</h3>
<p>Consider this sentence:</p>
<p><strong>The new science teacher explained the experiment carefully.</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Complete subject: <em>The new science teacher</em>.</li>
  <li>Head noun of the subject: <em>teacher</em>.</li>
  <li>Complete predicate: <em>explained the experiment carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>Main verb: <em>explained</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Do not confuse the subject with the first word</h3>
<p>Subjects can appear after verbs in questions, and introductory expressions may come before the subject.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Are the players ready?</em> — Subject: <em>the players</em>.</li>
  <li><em>After the long journey, the travellers rested.</em> — Subject: <em>the travellers</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Find the grammatical subject, not simply the first noun you see.</p>

<h3>5. Commands have an understood subject</h3>
<p>In a command such as <em>Close the window</em>, the subject is usually the understood pronoun <em>you</em>.</p>
<p>The complete meaning is: <em>(You) close the window.</em></p>

<h3>6. Sentence completeness</h3>
<p>A complete sentence generally needs an independent clause that expresses a complete thought.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Incomplete: <em>Because the road was flooded.</em></li>
  <li>Complete: <em>Because the road was flooded, we arrived late.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>The first example leaves the reader waiting for the result of the flooding. The second completes the thought.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Do not label only the head noun as the complete subject when describing the whole sentence.</li>
  <li>Do not assume everything after the subject is only a verb; the predicate can contain objects and complements.</li>
  <li>Do not treat every group of words beginning with a capital letter as a complete sentence.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> The subject tells what the sentence is about; the predicate tells what happens or what is true about it.</p>', 1),
  (1443, 1443, '<h2>Objects and Complements</h2>
<p>Objects and complements both add information to a sentence, but they do different jobs. An object is commonly affected by the action of a verb. A complement completes the meaning of a verb or describes or identifies a subject or object.</p>

<h3>1. Direct objects</h3>
<p>A direct object receives the action expressed by a transitive verb. To find it, identify the verb and ask <em>what?</em> or <em>whom?</em> after the verb.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The student answered the question.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Verb: <em>answered</em>.</li>
  <li>Answered what? <em>the question</em>.</li>
  <li>Direct object: <em>the question</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Indirect objects</h3>
<p>An indirect object commonly identifies the person who receives something or benefits from an action. It often appears before the direct object.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher gave Amina a certificate.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Verb: <em>gave</em>.</li>
  <li>Indirect object: <em>Amina</em>.</li>
  <li>Direct object: <em>a certificate</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The teacher gave what? A certificate. The teacher gave it to whom? Amina.</p>

<h3>3. Subject complements</h3>
<p>A subject complement follows a linking verb, such as <em>be</em>, <em>seem</em>, or <em>become</em>, and identifies or describes the subject.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The room is quiet.</em> — <em>quiet</em> describes the subject.</li>
  <li><em>My aunt became a doctor.</em> — <em>a doctor</em> identifies the subject''s role.</li>
  <li><em>The food smells delicious.</em> — <em>delicious</em> describes the food.</li>
</ul>
<p>These words are not direct objects because they do not receive an action from the linking verb.</p>

<h3>4. Object complements</h3>
<p>An object complement identifies or describes the direct object.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The class elected Brian captain.</em> — <em>captain</em> identifies Brian''s role.</li>
  <li><em>The news made everyone happy.</em> — <em>happy</em> describes everyone.</li>
</ul>
<p>In the first example, the class elected Brian, and the word <em>captain</em> tells us what Brian was elected to be.</p>

<h3>5. Not every sentence has an object</h3>
<p>Some verbs do not require an object.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The baby slept.</em> — No object is needed.</li>
  <li><em>The birds flew away.</em> — The verb has no direct object here.</li>
  <li><em>The baby drank milk.</em> — <em>milk</em> is the direct object.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not label every word after a verb as an object. It may be an adverbial phrase, a complement, or another part of the sentence.</p>

<h3>6. A reliable method</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Find the verb.</li>
  <li>Decide whether it expresses an action or links the subject to a description or identity.</li>
  <li>For an action verb, ask whether a direct object receives the action.</li>
  <li>Check whether a person receives something as an indirect object.</li>
  <li>Check whether a word identifies or describes the subject or object instead.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>In ''The soup tastes salty'', <em>salty</em> is a subject complement, not an object.</li>
  <li>In ''She sent her brother a message'', <em>her brother</em> is the indirect object and <em>a message</em> is the direct object.</li>
  <li>In ''The child slept peacefully'', <em>peacefully</em> describes how the child slept; it is not an object.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> Objects receive or are involved in an action; complements complete or describe an identity or state.</p>', 1),
  (1444, 1444, '<h2>Phrases</h2>
<p>A phrase is a group of words that functions as a unit in a sentence but does not contain a complete subject–predicate relationship of its own. A phrase can name something, describe it, show a relationship, or add information about an action.</p>

<h3>1. Noun phrases</h3>
<p>A noun phrase has a noun or pronoun as its central word, called the head. Other words may determine, describe, or add information about it.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>the book</em></li>
  <li><em>three very difficult questions</em></li>
  <li><em>the girl with the blue bag</em></li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>three very difficult questions</em>, the head noun is <em>questions</em>. The whole phrase can function as a subject or object.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>The girl with the blue bag</em> won the prize.</p>

<h3>2. Verb phrases</h3>
<p>A verb phrase contains a main verb and may include one or more auxiliary verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>is reading</em></li>
  <li><em>has finished</em></li>
  <li><em>will have completed</em></li>
  <li><em>might have been waiting</em></li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>has finished</em>, <em>has</em> is an auxiliary and <em>finished</em> is the main verb.</p>

<h3>3. Prepositional phrases</h3>
<p>A prepositional phrase begins with a preposition and usually includes a noun phrase as its object.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>under the table</em></li>
  <li><em>after the lesson</em></li>
  <li><em>with great care</em></li>
  <li><em>near the main gate</em></li>
</ul>
<p>These phrases can express place, time, direction, manner, or other relationships.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The learners waited <em>near the main gate</em>.</p>

<h3>4. Adjective phrases</h3>
<p>An adjective phrase has an adjective as its head and describes a noun or pronoun.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>very proud</em></li>
  <li><em>extremely useful</em></li>
  <li><em>full of confidence</em></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The team was <em>extremely proud</em> of its performance.</p>

<h3>5. Adverb phrases</h3>
<p>An adverb phrase functions like an adverb by modifying a verb, adjective, or another adverb.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>very quickly</em></li>
  <li><em>quite carefully</em></li>
  <li><em>rather slowly</em></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> She completed the work <em>quite carefully</em>.</p>

<h3>6. Phrases can have different functions</h3>
<p>The same type of phrase can serve different roles in different sentences.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The new student</em> arrived. — Noun phrase functioning as subject.</li>
  <li>I greeted <em>the new student</em>. — Noun phrase functioning as object.</li>
  <li>The bag <em>on the chair</em> is mine. — Prepositional phrase describing which bag.</li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Phrase or clause?</h3>
<p>A phrase does not have its own complete subject–predicate structure. A clause contains a subject and a verb, though a dependent clause cannot stand alone as a complete sentence.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Phrase: <em>after the match</em>.</li>
  <li>Clause: <em>after the match ended</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The second example contains a subject, <em>the match</em>, and a verb, <em>ended</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>A long phrase is not automatically a clause.</li>
  <li>A phrase can contain a noun and a verb form without expressing a complete clause.</li>
  <li>Identify a phrase by how its words function together, not simply by counting words.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> A phrase is a functional group of words that operates as a unit within a larger sentence.</p>', 1),
  (1445, 1445, '<h2>Main and Subordinate Clauses</h2>
<p>A clause is a group of words containing a subject and a verb. Clauses differ in whether they can express a complete thought independently or depend on another clause.</p>

<h3>1. Main clauses</h3>
<p>A main clause, also called an independent clause, can stand alone as a complete sentence.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The learners completed the exercise.</li>
  <li>We arrived before noon.</li>
  <li>The rain stopped.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each clause communicates a complete thought without needing another clause to finish its meaning.</p>

<h3>2. Subordinate clauses</h3>
<p>A subordinate clause, also called a dependent clause, contains a subject and a verb but does not normally express a complete thought on its own in the way it is used.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>because the road was flooded</em></li>
  <li><em>although she was tired</em></li>
  <li><em>when the bell rang</em></li>
</ul>
<p>These groups contain subjects and verbs, but they leave the reader expecting a main idea.</p>
<p><strong>Complete example:</strong> We arrived late <em>because the road was flooded</em>.</p>

<h3>3. Recognising subordinating conjunctions</h3>
<p>Subordinate clauses often begin with words such as:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Time: <em>when, while, before, after, until</em>.</li>
  <li>Reason: <em>because, since, as</em>.</li>
  <li>Condition: <em>if, unless</em>.</li>
  <li>Contrast or concession: <em>although, though, even though, whereas</em>.</li>
  <li>Purpose: <em>so that, in order that</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>These words help explain how one idea relates to another, but the presence of one of them does not by itself tell you whether the whole sentence is complete.</p>

<h3>4. Relative clauses</h3>
<p>A relative clause is a subordinate clause that describes a noun or pronoun. It often begins with <em>who</em>, <em>which</em>, or <em>that</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The girl <em>who won the race</em> is my cousin.</p>
<p>The main clause is <em>The girl is my cousin</em>. The relative clause <em>who won the race</em> describes the girl.</p>

<h3>5. Noun clauses</h3>
<p>A noun clause functions like a noun within a larger clause.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>What he said</em> surprised everyone.</p>
<p>The clause <em>What he said</em> functions as the subject of the main verb <em>surprised</em>.</p>

<h3>6. How clauses combine</h3>
<p>Clauses can be combined to show relationships between ideas.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Reason:</strong> She stayed home because she was ill.</li>
  <li><strong>Time:</strong> We began when the teacher arrived.</li>
  <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> Although it was late, they continued working.</li>
  <li><strong>Condition:</strong> If you revise regularly, you will improve.</li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Avoiding sentence fragments</h3>
<p>A subordinate clause used alone is usually a fragment when it is intended to be a complete sentence.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Fragment: <em>Although the shop was open.</em></li>
  <li>Complete: <em>Although the shop was open, we did not enter.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>A subordinate clause can be part of a complete sentence, but it needs an independent clause to complete the thought in these examples.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Do not confuse a phrase with a clause: a clause has a subject and a verb.</li>
  <li>Do not assume a clause is independent just because it contains a subject and a verb.</li>
  <li>Do not leave a subordinate clause as a fragment when a complete sentence is intended.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> A main clause can stand alone; a subordinate clause performs a role within a larger sentence and depends on it.</p>', 1),
  (1446, 1446, '<h2>Simple Sentences</h2>
<p>A simple sentence contains one independent clause. It expresses a complete thought and can have a short or long structure. The number of words does not determine whether a sentence is simple; the number and type of clauses do.</p>

<h3>1. The basic structure</h3>
<p>A simple sentence can contain a subject and a predicate.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The baby cried.</li>
  <li>The learners revised.</li>
  <li>Our neighbours planted trees.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each example contains one independent clause.</p>

<h3>2. Simple sentences can be longer</h3>
<p>A simple sentence can include adjectives, adverbs, objects, and phrases without becoming a compound or complex sentence.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The tired learners in the library revised their notes quietly after lunch.</p>
<p>This is still a simple sentence because it has one independent clause. The additional words expand the information but do not create another clause.</p>

<h3>3. Compound subject, simple sentence</h3>
<p>A sentence may have two or more subjects but still contain only one independent clause.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Amina and Brian prepared the presentation.</p>
<p>The compound subject is <em>Amina and Brian</em>, but the sentence has one predicate and one independent clause.</p>

<h3>4. Compound predicate, simple sentence</h3>
<p>A subject can perform two or more actions within one independent clause.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> The teacher entered the room, greeted the learners, and began the lesson.</p>
<p>The teacher performs several actions, but the sentence contains one independent clause with a compound predicate.</p>

<h3>5. Simple sentence versus a compound sentence</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Simple:</strong> The rain stopped, and the sun appeared. — This has two independent clauses, so it is compound, despite the comma and conjunction making it one written sentence.</li>
  <li><strong>Simple:</strong> After the rain, the sun appeared. — This has one independent clause and a phrase giving time.</li>
  <li><strong>Complex:</strong> After the rain stopped, the sun appeared. — This has an independent clause and a subordinate clause.</li>
</ul>
<p>Count clauses, not commas, conjunctions, or verb words alone.</p>

<h3>6. Avoiding fragments</h3>
<p>A simple sentence must express a complete thought.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Fragment: <em>Because the bus was late.</em></li>
  <li>Simple sentence: <em>The bus was late.</em></li>
  <li>Complex sentence: <em>Because the bus was late, we missed the opening speech.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>7. A test for simple sentences</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Find the subject and verb of each clause.</li>
  <li>Identify any subordinate clauses introduced by words such as <em>because</em>, <em>although</em>, or <em>when</em>.</li>
  <li>Determine how many independent clauses the sentence contains.</li>
  <li>If it contains one independent clause and no subordinate clause, it is simple.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>A long sentence can still be simple if it contains one independent clause.</li>
  <li>A sentence with two subjects joined by ''and'' is not automatically compound.</li>
  <li>A sentence with two independent clauses joined by ''and'' is compound, not simple.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key principle:</strong> A simple sentence contains one independent clause, regardless of its length or the number of actions expressed.</p>', 1),
  (1447, 1447, '<h2>What Is a Compound Sentence?</h2>
<p>A <strong>compound sentence</strong> contains two or more independent clauses joined correctly. An independent clause has a subject and a verb and expresses a complete thought.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the two parts</h3>
<p>Consider these sentences:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The rain stopped.</li>
  <li>The players returned to the field.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each sentence expresses a complete thought. They can be joined to form a compound sentence:</p>
<p><em>The rain stopped, and the players returned to the field.</em></p>
<p>The sentence contains two independent clauses:</p>
<ol>
  <li>The rain stopped.</li>
  <li>The players returned to the field.</li>
</ol>

<h3>2. Joining independent clauses</h3>
<p>Independent clauses can be joined using coordinating conjunctions. The common conjunctions are <strong>for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so</strong>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>And</strong> adds information: Amina revised her notes, and Brian completed the assignment.</li>
  <li><strong>But</strong> shows contrast: He studied hard, but he did not finish the paper.</li>
  <li><strong>Or</strong> presents a choice: We can walk to school, or we can take a bus.</li>
  <li><strong>So</strong> shows a result: The road was flooded, so we used another route.</li>
  <li><strong>Yet</strong> shows an unexpected contrast: She was nervous, yet she spoke confidently.</li>
  <li><strong>For</strong> gives a reason: We left early, for the weather was worsening.</li>
  <li><strong>Nor</strong> adds a negative alternative: He did not apologise, nor did he explain his behaviour.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Punctuation</h3>
<p>When two independent clauses are joined by a coordinating conjunction, place a comma before the conjunction.</p>
<p>Correct: <em>The bell rang, and the learners entered the classroom.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The bell rang and, the learners entered the classroom.</em></p>
<p>A semicolon can also join closely related independent clauses without a coordinating conjunction.</p>
<p><em>The bell rang; the learners entered the classroom.</em></p>

<h3>4. Avoid comma splices</h3>
<p>A comma splice occurs when two independent clauses are joined using only a comma.</p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The match ended, the spectators went home.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>The match ended, so the spectators went home.</em></p>
<p>Also correct: <em>The match ended; the spectators went home.</em></p>

<h3>5. Do not confuse a long sentence with a compound sentence</h3>
<p>A sentence is not compound simply because it is long or contains the word <em>and</em>. The clauses must each express a complete thought.</p>
<p><em>Mary washed the clothes and cleaned the house.</em></p>
<p>This is a simple sentence with a compound predicate: Mary performed two actions, but there is only one subject and one independent clause.</p>
<p><em>Mary washed the clothes, and Peter cleaned the house.</em></p>
<p>This is a compound sentence because each clause has its own subject and expresses a complete thought.</p>', 1),
  (1448, 1448, '<h2>What Is a Complex Sentence?</h2>
<p>A <strong>complex sentence</strong> contains one independent clause and at least one subordinate (dependent) clause.</p>
<p>An independent clause expresses a complete thought. A subordinate clause has a subject and a verb but depends on another clause to complete its meaning or function within the sentence.</p>

<h3>1. Recognise the difference</h3>
<p><strong>Independent clause:</strong> We stayed indoors.</p>
<p><strong>Subordinate clause:</strong> because it was raining.</p>
<p>The subordinate clause begins with <em>because</em> and gives a reason, but it does not normally express a complete sentence on its own in this use.</p>
<p>Combined: <em>We stayed indoors because it was raining.</em></p>
<p>This is a complex sentence because it contains one independent clause and one subordinate clause.</p>

<h3>2. Common subordinating conjunctions</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Time:</strong> when, while, before, after, until, as soon as.</li>
  <li><strong>Reason:</strong> because, since, as.</li>
  <li><strong>Condition:</strong> if, unless, provided that.</li>
  <li><strong>Contrast or concession:</strong> although, though, even though, whereas.</li>
  <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> so that, in order that.</li>
  <li><strong>Result or comparison:</strong> than, as ... as, so ... that, where these introduce subordinate clauses.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>When the bell rang, the learners left the classroom.</em> (time)</li>
  <li><em>She passed because she prepared carefully.</em> (reason)</li>
  <li><em>If you practise regularly, your accuracy will improve.</em> (condition)</li>
  <li><em>Although he was tired, he completed the assignment.</em> (concession)</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Subordinate clauses can appear in different positions</h3>
<p>A subordinate clause may come before or after the independent clause.</p>
<p><strong>Subordinate clause first:</strong> <em>Although the road was muddy, we continued walking.</em></p>
<p>Use a comma after the introductory subordinate clause.</p>
<p><strong>Independent clause first:</strong> <em>We continued walking although the road was muddy.</em></p>
<p>A comma is usually unnecessary when a subordinate clause follows the independent clause and is essential to the sentence''s meaning.</p>

<h3>4. Relative clauses</h3>
<p>A subordinate clause can describe a noun. Such a clause is often introduced by <em>who, whom, whose, which</em> or <em>that</em>.</p>
<p><em>The learner who won the competition received a certificate.</em></p>
<p>The clause <em>who won the competition</em> describes which learner is meant.</p>
<p><em>The book that you borrowed is on the table.</em></p>
<p>The clause <em>that you borrowed</em> identifies the book.</p>

<h3>5. Avoid incomplete sentences</h3>
<p>A subordinate clause used alone is usually a sentence fragment.</p>
<p>Incomplete: <em>Although the teacher explained the rule.</em></p>
<p>Complete: <em>Although the teacher explained the rule, some learners remained confused.</em></p>
<p>The first clause creates an expectation that is completed by the independent clause.</p>

<h3>6. Do not confuse a complex sentence with a compound sentence</h3>
<p><strong>Compound:</strong> <em>The sun set, and the workers went home.</em> Both clauses are independent.</p>
<p><strong>Complex:</strong> <em>When the sun set, the workers went home.</em> One clause depends on the other.</p>
<p>The important distinction is not the sentence''s length; it is the relationship between its clauses.</p>', 1),
  (1449, 1449, '<h2>Understanding Compound-Complex Sentences</h2>
<p>A <strong>compound-complex sentence</strong> contains at least two independent clauses and at least one subordinate clause.</p>
<p>It combines the structure of a compound sentence with the structure of a complex sentence.</p>

<h3>1. Count the clauses, not the words</h3>
<p>Consider this example:</p>
<p><em>Although it was raining, the players continued the match, and the spectators remained in their seats.</em></p>
<p>There are three clauses:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Although it was raining</strong> — subordinate clause.</li>
  <li><strong>The players continued the match</strong> — independent clause.</li>
  <li><strong>The spectators remained in their seats</strong> — independent clause.</li>
</ol>
<p>Because the sentence has two independent clauses and one subordinate clause, it is compound-complex.</p>

<h3>2. Understand how the clauses connect</h3>
<p>The word <em>although</em> introduces the subordinate clause and shows concession. The conjunction <em>and</em> joins the two independent clauses.</p>
<p>These words perform different jobs. A subordinating conjunction makes one clause dependent on another, while a coordinating conjunction can join independent clauses.</p>

<h3>3. Another example</h3>
<p><em>When the examination ended, the candidates handed in their papers, and the invigilator collected them.</em></p>
<ul>
  <li><em>When the examination ended</em> — subordinate clause showing time.</li>
  <li><em>The candidates handed in their papers</em> — independent clause.</li>
  <li><em>The invigilator collected them</em> — independent clause.</li>
</ul>
<p>The introductory subordinate clause is followed by a comma. The two independent clauses are joined with a comma before <em>and</em>.</p>

<h3>4. Build one systematically</h3>
<p>Start with two independent clauses:</p>
<p><em>The farmer planted maize. The rain arrived.</em></p>
<p>Join them with a coordinating conjunction:</p>
<p><em>The farmer planted maize, and the rain arrived.</em></p>
<p>Now add a subordinate clause:</p>
<p><em>Because the soil was ready, the farmer planted maize, and the rain arrived.</em></p>
<p>The result is compound-complex: two independent clauses and one subordinate clause.</p>

<h3>5. Avoid common classification errors</h3>
<p><strong>Compound:</strong> <em>The farmer planted maize, and the rain arrived.</em> Two independent clauses; no subordinate clause.</p>
<p><strong>Complex:</strong> <em>Because the soil was ready, the farmer planted maize.</em> One independent clause and one subordinate clause.</p>
<p><strong>Compound-complex:</strong> <em>Because the soil was ready, the farmer planted maize, and the rain arrived.</em> Two independent clauses and one subordinate clause.</p>
<p>A sentence does not become compound-complex merely because it has several verbs. Identify the clauses and determine whether each expresses a complete thought.</p>

<h3>6. Punctuation</h3>
<p>Use punctuation according to the relationships between clauses:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Place a comma after an introductory subordinate clause.</li>
  <li>Place a comma before a coordinating conjunction that joins two independent clauses.</li>
  <li>Do not place a comma automatically before every conjunction. Check what the conjunction joins.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1450, 1450, '<h2>Statements and Questions</h2>
<p>Sentences can be classified by their communicative purpose. A <strong>statement</strong> gives information, while a <strong>question</strong> asks for information or confirmation.</p>

<h3>1. Statements</h3>
<p>A statement normally follows the usual subject–verb order and ends with a full stop.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>The learners are preparing for their examinations.</em></li>
  <li><em>Our school opened a new library.</em></li>
  <li><em>She has completed the assignment.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Statements can be affirmative or negative.</p>
<p>Affirmative: <em>He understands the instructions.</em></p>
<p>Negative: <em>He does not understand the instructions.</em></p>
<p>A negative statement still gives information; it simply denies or rejects something.</p>

<h3>2. Questions that use auxiliary verbs</h3>
<p>In many English questions, an auxiliary verb comes before the subject.</p>
<p>Statement: <em>You are ready.</em></p>
<p>Question: <em>Are you ready?</em></p>
<p>Statement: <em>She has finished.</em></p>
<p>Question: <em>Has she finished?</em></p>
<p>Statement: <em>They will attend.</em></p>
<p>Question: <em>Will they attend?</em></p>

<h3>3. Questions with do, does and did</h3>
<p>When a statement has no auxiliary verb, use <em>do, does</em> or <em>did</em> to form many present and past simple questions.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Statement: <em>You play football.</em> Question: <em>Do you play football?</em></li>
  <li>Statement: <em>She plays football.</em> Question: <em>Does she play football?</em></li>
  <li>Statement: <em>They visited Nairobi.</em> Question: <em>Did they visit Nairobi?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>After <em>does</em> or <em>did</em>, use the base form of the main verb.</p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>Does she plays football?</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>Does she play football?</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>Did they visited Nairobi?</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>Did they visit Nairobi?</em></p>

<h3>4. Wh-questions</h3>
<p>Wh-questions ask for specific information rather than a simple yes or no.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Who</strong> asks about a person: Who called you?</li>
  <li><strong>What</strong> asks about a thing or action: What happened?</li>
  <li><strong>Where</strong> asks about a place: Where do you live?</li>
  <li><strong>When</strong> asks about time: When will the lesson begin?</li>
  <li><strong>Why</strong> asks about a reason: Why did you leave early?</li>
  <li><strong>Which</strong> asks someone to select from alternatives: Which book did you borrow?</li>
  <li><strong>How</strong> asks about manner, condition or degree: How did you solve the problem?</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Word order in questions</h3>
<p>Compare:</p>
<p>Statement: <em>She can solve the problem.</em></p>
<p>Question: <em>Can she solve the problem?</em></p>
<p>Wh-question: <em>How can she solve the problem?</em></p>
<p>When an auxiliary verb is present, it usually comes before the subject in a direct question. A wh-word usually comes first.</p>

<h3>6. Questions where the question word is the subject</h3>
<p>Not every question reverses the subject and auxiliary verb.</p>
<p><em>Who broke the window?</em></p>
<p>Here, <em>who</em> is the subject. We do not normally say <em>Who did break the window?</em> in a neutral question. The form with <em>did</em> can occur for emphasis, but it has a different use.</p>

<h3>7. Punctuation and indirect questions</h3>
<p>Direct questions end with a question mark: <em>Where is the office?</em></p>
<p>An indirect question embedded in a statement normally does not use question word order:</p>
<p>Direct: <em>Where is the office?</em></p>
<p>Indirect: <em>Could you tell me where the office is?</em></p>
<p>The indirect question is part of a larger question, so the whole sentence ends with a question mark. In a statement such as <em>I know where the office is.</em>, use a full stop.</p>', 1),
  (1451, 1451, '<h2>Commands and Exclamations</h2>
<p>Commands and exclamations serve different purposes. A <strong>command</strong> directs someone to do or not do something. An <strong>exclamation</strong> expresses a strong feeling or reaction.</p>

<h3>1. Commands and instructions</h3>
<p>Commands usually use the base form of the verb. The subject is generally the understood word <em>you</em>, even though it is not written.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Close the door.</em></li>
  <li><em>Read the instructions carefully.</em></li>
  <li><em>Turn left at the junction.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>Close the door</em>, the implied subject is <em>you</em>: you close the door.</p>
<p>Commands are common in instructions, recipes, notices, rules and procedures because they tell the reader what action to take.</p>

<h3>2. Negative commands</h3>
<p>Use <em>do not</em> or its contraction <em>don''t</em> before the base form of the verb to tell someone not to do something.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Positive: <em>Touch the switch.</em></li>
  <li>Negative: <em>Do not touch the switch.</em></li>
  <li>Positive: <em>Enter the room.</em></li>
  <li>Negative: <em>Don''t enter the room.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Incorrect: <em>Do not touches the switch.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>Do not touch the switch.</em></p>

<h3>3. Polite commands and requests</h3>
<p>Commands do not have to sound harsh. Adding <em>please</em> or using a polite question can make a request more courteous.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Direct command: <em>Pass me the book.</em></li>
  <li>Polite command: <em>Please pass me the book.</em></li>
  <li>Polite request: <em>Could you pass me the book, please?</em></li>
</ul>
<p>The intended action may be the same, but the wording changes the tone and the level of politeness.</p>

<h3>4. Exclamations</h3>
<p>An exclamation expresses a strong emotion such as surprise, joy, anger, fear or admiration.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>What a beautiful performance!</em></li>
  <li><em>How quickly the time has passed!</em></li>
  <li><em>That was an incredible result!</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Exclamations often end with an exclamation mark, but the mark alone does not make a sentence an exclamation. The meaning and structure also matter.</p>

<h3>5. What and how exclamations</h3>
<p><strong>What</strong> can introduce an exclamation focusing on a noun phrase:</p>
<p><em>What a difficult question that was!</em></p>
<p><strong>How</strong> can emphasise an adjective or adverb:</p>
<p><em>How difficult that question was!</em></p>
<p><em>How beautifully she sings!</em></p>
<p>Notice that the exclamatory word order differs from the usual direct-question structure. An exclamation such as <em>How beautifully she sings!</em> is not asking how she sings; it expresses admiration.</p>

<h3>6. Do not confuse a command with a statement</h3>
<p><em>You must close the gate.</em> is a statement that expresses obligation.</p>
<p><em>Close the gate.</em> is a direct command.</p>
<p>Both may encourage the same action, but they have different grammatical structures.</p>

<h3>7. Choose punctuation by purpose</h3>
<ul>
  <li>A routine command can end with a full stop: <em>Write your name on the paper.</em></li>
  <li>An urgent or forceful command may end with an exclamation mark: <em>Stop!</em></li>
  <li>An exclamation expressing strong feeling often ends with an exclamation mark: <em>What wonderful news!</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Use exclamation marks selectively. A sentence does not need one simply because it is an instruction.</p>', 1),
  (1452, 1452, '<h2>Sentence Fragments and Run-on Sentences</h2>
<p>A complete sentence expresses a complete thought and normally contains an independent clause. Two common errors are <strong>sentence fragments</strong> and <strong>run-on sentences</strong>.</p>

<h3>1. Sentence fragments</h3>
<p>A sentence fragment is a group of words punctuated as a sentence even though it does not form a complete independent clause in the intended context.</p>
<p><strong>Fragment:</strong> Because the road was flooded.</p>
<p>The word <em>because</em> introduces a reason, but the reader is left waiting for the result.</p>
<p><strong>Complete sentence:</strong> Because the road was flooded, we used another route.</p>

<h3>2. Common causes of fragments</h3>
<p><strong>A. A subordinate clause standing alone</strong></p>
<p>Fragment: <em>Although she studied throughout the weekend.</em></p>
<p>Complete: <em>Although she studied throughout the weekend, she still needed more practice.</em></p>
<p><strong>B. A phrase mistaken for a complete sentence</strong></p>
<p>Fragment: <em>After the final examination.</em></p>
<p>Complete: <em>We went home after the final examination.</em></p>
<p><strong>C. A missing main verb</strong></p>
<p>Fragment: <em>The learners in the science laboratory.</em></p>
<p>Complete: <em>The learners are in the science laboratory.</em></p>
<p>To correct a fragment, identify what is missing and add the necessary information without changing the intended meaning.</p>

<h3>3. Run-on sentences</h3>
<p>A run-on sentence occurs when two or more independent clauses are joined incorrectly. A run-on can happen without any punctuation or when a comma is used alone.</p>
<p><strong>Fused sentence:</strong> The rain stopped the players returned to the field.</p>
<p><strong>Comma splice:</strong> The rain stopped, the players returned to the field.</p>
<p>Both versions incorrectly join two independent clauses.</p>

<h3>4. Correcting run-on sentences</h3>
<p><strong>Method 1: Use a full stop.</strong></p>
<p>The rain stopped. The players returned to the field.</p>
<p><strong>Method 2: Use a comma and a coordinating conjunction.</strong></p>
<p>The rain stopped, and the players returned to the field.</p>
<p><strong>Method 3: Use a semicolon.</strong></p>
<p>The rain stopped; the players returned to the field.</p>
<p><strong>Method 4: Use a subordinate clause if the meaning supports it.</strong></p>
<p>When the rain stopped, the players returned to the field.</p>

<h3>5. Diagnose before correcting</h3>
<p>Do not correct every long sentence by splitting it. First identify the clauses and their intended relationship.</p>
<ul>
  <li>If a group of words cannot stand as a complete sentence, check for a fragment.</li>
  <li>If two complete thoughts are joined incorrectly, check for a run-on.</li>
  <li>If one idea explains a reason, condition or time, a subordinate clause may communicate the relationship more accurately.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. Common mistake</h3>
<p>Incorrect: <em>Although the match was difficult. The team won.</em></p>
<p>Better: <em>Although the match was difficult, the team won.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The team won, the supporters celebrated.</em></p>
<p>Better: <em>The team won, so the supporters celebrated.</em></p>
<p>The first error separates a subordinate clause from the thought that completes it. The second joins two independent clauses with only a comma.</p>', 1),
  (1453, 1453, '<h2>Modal Verbs</h2>
<h3>1. WHAT ARE MODAL AUXILIARY VERBS?</h3>
<p><b>Modal verbs</b> are auxiliary (helping) verbs that express the speaker''s attitude towards the action: ability, permission, obligation, advice, possibility, or necessity.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Core modals:</b> can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would, ought to.</li>
<li><b>Key properties:</b> They do NOT take ''-s'' in the third person (<i>he can</i>, not <i>he cans</i>), and are followed by a <b>bare infinitive</b> (base verb without ''to'').</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1454, 1454, '<h2>Ability and Permission</h2>
<h3>1. EXPRESSING ABILITY</h3>
<p>We use <b>can</b> for present general ability (<i>I can swim</i>) and <b>could</b> for past general ability (<i>She could read at age four</i>). For specific past achievements, use <b>was/were able to</b>.</p>
<h3>2. EXPRESSING PERMISSION</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Can:</b> Informal permission (<i>Can I borrow your pen?</i>)</li>
<li><b>May:</b> Formal or polite permission (<i>May I leave the room, Sir?</i>)</li>
<li><b>Could:</b> Polite request for permission (<i>Could I ask a question?</i>)</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1455, 1455, '<h2>Obligation and Advice</h2>
<h3>1. EXPRESSING OBLIGATION AND NECESSITY</h3>
<p><b>Must</b> expresses strong internal obligation or strict necessity imposed by a speaker or law (<i>You must wear a seatbelt</i>). <b>Have to</b> expresses external obligation due to rules or circumstances (<i>Students have to submit assignments by Friday</i>).</p>
<h3>2. EXPRESSING ADVICE AND RECOMMENDATION</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Should / Ought to:</b> Used to give friendly advice or moral obligation (<i>You should get more sleep; you ought to apologize</i>).</li>
<li><b>Had better:</b> Used for urgent advice with a warning of negative consequences (<i>You had better hurry or you will miss the bus</i>).</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1456, 1456, '<h2>Possibility and Probability</h2>
<h3>1. DEGREES OF CERTAINTY</h3>
<p>We use modal verbs to express how certain we are about a fact or prediction:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Must:</b> Near 100% certainty based on logical deduction (<i>The lights are on; they must be home</i>).</li>
<li><b>May / Might / Could:</b> Around 30%–50% possibility (<i>It might rain later today</i>).</li>
<li><b>Cannot (Can''t):</b> Negative certainty / impossibility (<i>He only just left London; he can''t be in Nairobi already</i>).</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1457, 1457, '<h2>Active Voice</h2>
<h3>1. STRUCTURE OF ACTIVE VOICE</h3>
<p>In the <b>active voice</b>, the grammatical subject of the sentence performs the action expressed by the verb.</p>
<p><b>Formula:</b> <code>Subject (Doer) + Verb (Action) + Object (Receiver)</code></p>
<ul>
<li><i>Example:</i> <b>The chef</b> (Subject) <b>cooked</b> (Verb) <b>a delicious meal</b> (Object).</li>
<li><b>Why use Active Voice?</b> It makes writing direct, vigorous, clear, and concise.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1458, 1458, '<h2>Passive Voice</h2>
<h3>1. STRUCTURE OF PASSIVE VOICE</h3>
<p>In the <b>passive voice</b>, the subject receives the action rather than performing it. The doer may be introduced with ''by'' or omitted entirely.</p>
<p><b>Formula:</b> <code>Object become Subject + Appropriate Form of ''to be'' + Past Participle (V3) + (by Agent)</code></p>
<ul>
<li><i>Active:</i> The teacher marked the scripts.</li>
<li><i>Passive:</i> The scripts <b>were marked</b> by the teacher.</li>
<li><b>When to use Passive:</b> When the doer is unknown, unimportant, or when the focus is on the result (e.g. scientific reports: <i>Water was heated to 100°C</i>).</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1459, 1459, '<h2>Direct Speech</h2>
<h3>1. WHAT IS DIRECT SPEECH?</h3>
<p><b>Direct speech</b> quotes the exact words spoken by a person, enclosed in <b>quotation marks (“ ” or '' '')</b>.</p>
<h3>2. PUNCTUATION RULES FOR DIRECT SPEECH</h3>
<ul>
<li>A <b>comma</b> introduces the spoken words if the reporting clause comes first (<i>Amina said, “We must leave now.”</i>).</li>
<li>The first spoken word inside quotation marks begins with a <b>capital letter</b>.</li>
<li>Punctuation marks (full stop, question mark, exclamation mark) go <b>inside</b> the closing quotation marks.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1460, 1460, '<h2>Reported Speech (Indirect Speech)</h2>
<h3>1. WHAT IS REPORTED SPEECH?</h3>
<p><b>Reported speech</b> reports what someone said without using their exact words and without quotation marks.</p>
<h3>2. KEY TRANSFORMATION RULES (BACKSHIFTING)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Present Simple → Past Simple:</b> <i>“I like tea” → He said that he liked tea.</i></li>
<li><b>Present Continuous → Past Continuous:</b> <i>“I am reading” → She said she was reading.</i></li>
<li><b>Past Simple → Past Perfect:</b> <i>“I saw him” → He said he had seen him.</i></li>
<li><b>Will → Would / Can → Could / May → Might</b></li>
<li><b>Time & Place shifts:</b> now → then, today → that day, tomorrow → the next day, here → there.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1461, 1461, '<h2>Conditional Sentences</h2>
<h3>1. FOUR MAIN TYPES OF CONDITIONALS</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Zero Conditional (Universal Truths / Facts):</b> <code>If + Present Simple, Present Simple</code> (<i>If you heat ice, it melts.</i>)</li>
<li><b>First Conditional (Real / Likely Future):</b> <code>If + Present Simple, will + base verb</code> (<i>If it rains, we will stay indoors.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Second Conditional (Unreal / Hypothetical Present):</b> <code>If + Past Simple, would + base verb</code> (<i>If I won the lottery, I would travel the world.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Third Conditional (Unreal Past / Regret):</b> <code>If + Past Perfect, would have + V3</code> (<i>If we had left earlier, we would have caught the train.</i>)</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1462, 1462, '<h2>Types of Adjectives</h2>
<p>An <strong>adjective</strong> describes a noun or pronoun, or helps identify which person or thing is meant. Adjectives can communicate qualities, quantities, ownership and other information.</p>

<h3>1. Descriptive adjectives</h3>
<p>Descriptive adjectives tell us what a person, place or thing is like.</p>
<ul>
  <li>a <em>careful</em> driver</li>
  <li>a <em>narrow</em> road</li>
  <li>a <em>useful</em> explanation</li>
  <li>a <em>bright</em> classroom</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>a careful driver</em>, the adjective <em>careful</em> describes the noun <em>driver</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Quantitative adjectives</h3>
<p>Quantitative adjectives indicate an amount or quantity.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>some</em> water</li>
  <li><em>little</em> time</li>
  <li><em>enough</em> food</li>
  <li><em>much</em> effort</li>
</ul>
<p>Traditional school grammar often calls these quantitative adjectives. In modern grammar, many words in this group are classified as determiners when they introduce a noun. Their function in the sentence is what matters.</p>

<h3>3. Numeral adjectives</h3>
<p>Numeral adjectives express number or order.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>three</em> books — a cardinal number.</li>
  <li><em>five</em> learners — a cardinal number.</li>
  <li>the <em>first</em> lesson — an ordinal number.</li>
  <li>the <em>third</em> attempt — an ordinal number.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cardinal numbers answer ''how many?'' while ordinal numbers indicate position or order.</p>

<h3>4. Demonstrative adjectives</h3>
<p>Demonstrative words identify which noun is meant.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>This</em> book is useful.</li>
  <li><em>That</em> building is old.</li>
  <li><em>These</em> questions are difficult.</li>
  <li><em>Those</em> trees need water.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use <em>this</em> and <em>that</em> with singular nouns, and <em>these</em> and <em>those</em> with plural nouns.</p>
<p>Compare: <em>This is useful.</em> Here, <em>this</em> stands alone as a pronoun. In <em>This book is useful</em>, it introduces the noun <em>book</em> and functions as a determiner in modern grammar.</p>

<h3>5. Possessive adjectives</h3>
<p>Possessive adjectives, also called possessive determiners in modern grammar, show whose noun is being referred to.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>my</em> notebook</li>
  <li><em>your</em> pencil</li>
  <li><em>his</em> uniform</li>
  <li><em>her</em> bag</li>
  <li><em>our</em> classroom</li>
  <li><em>their</em> teacher</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not confuse possessive determiners with possessive pronouns.</p>
<p>Determiner: <em>This is my book.</em></p>
<p>Pronoun: <em>This book is mine.</em></p>

<h3>6. Interrogative adjectives</h3>
<p>In traditional grammar, <em>which</em> and <em>what</em> are called interrogative adjectives when they introduce a noun in a question.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Which subject</em> do you prefer?</li>
  <li><em>What time</em> does the bus arrive?</li>
</ul>
<p>Compare: <em>Which do you prefer?</em> In this sentence, <em>which</em> stands alone and functions as a pronoun.</p>

<h3>7. Participial adjectives</h3>
<p>Some adjectives are formed from verbs and end in <em>-ing</em> or <em>-ed</em>, among other forms.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The lesson was <em>interesting</em>.</li>
  <li>The learners were <em>interested</em>.</li>
  <li>The <em>broken</em> window needs repair.</li>
</ul>
<p>In many contexts, <em>-ing</em> describes what causes a feeling, while <em>-ed</em> describes the person or thing experiencing it.</p>

<h3>8. Identify an adjective by its function</h3>
<p>Ask what word the adjective describes or helps identify. In <em>The exhausted players entered the field</em>, <em>exhausted</em> describes <em>players</em>. In <em>The players were exhausted</em>, it describes the players after the linking verb <em>were</em>.</p>
<p>Not every word before a noun is necessarily an adjective. Words such as <em>the</em>, <em>my</em> and <em>those</em> are generally classified as determiners in modern grammar, even though older school grammar may group some of them under adjectives.</p>', 1),
  (1463, 1463, '<h2>Order of Adjectives</h2>
<p>When several adjectives describe one noun, English usually places them in a conventional order. The order helps the description sound natural and makes the noun easier to identify.</p>

<h3>1. The usual order</h3>
<p>A useful guide is:</p>
<p><strong>Determiner → quantity/number → opinion → size → age → shape → colour → origin → material → purpose → noun</strong></p>
<p>Example: <em>those three beautiful small old round brown Kenyan wooden dining tables</em></p>
<p>This example contains many descriptive details. In ordinary writing, using fewer adjectives is often clearer.</p>

<h3>2. Understand each position</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Determiner:</strong> a, the, this, those, my.</li>
  <li><strong>Quantity or number:</strong> three, several, many.</li>
  <li><strong>Opinion:</strong> beautiful, useful, unpleasant.</li>
  <li><strong>Size:</strong> small, large, tall.</li>
  <li><strong>Age:</strong> old, young, new.</li>
  <li><strong>Shape:</strong> round, square, rectangular.</li>
  <li><strong>Colour:</strong> red, black, yellow.</li>
  <li><strong>Origin:</strong> Kenyan, Japanese, African.</li>
  <li><strong>Material:</strong> wooden, plastic, metal.</li>
  <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> cooking, sleeping, dining, sports.</li>
</ul>
<p>Purpose words often form a compound description close to the noun: <em>a small metal cooking pot</em>.</p>

<h3>3. Apply the order</h3>
<p>Suppose you want to describe a bag using <em>leather</em>, <em>beautiful</em> and <em>small</em>.</p>
<p>Identify each type:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>beautiful</em> — opinion.</li>
  <li><em>small</em> — size.</li>
  <li><em>leather</em> — material.</li>
</ul>
<p>Arrange them as opinion → size → material → noun:</p>
<p><em>a beautiful small leather bag</em></p>
<p><em>A leather small beautiful bag</em> sounds unnatural because the adjectives are not in the usual order.</p>

<h3>4. Natural order is a guide, not a mechanical rule</h3>
<p>Adjective order can change when a writer wants emphasis, when adjectives belong to different phrases, or when a phrase has become established in common use.</p>
<p>For example, <em>a big bad wolf</em> is an established expression. Do not force every sentence into an artificial sequence if the result sounds unnatural.</p>
<p>Some adjectives are closely connected to the noun and are not freely interchangeable. In <em>medical equipment</em>, <em>medical</em> identifies the type of equipment; it is not merely a decorative description.</p>

<h3>5. Coordinate adjectives</h3>
<p>Coordinate adjectives independently describe a noun and can often be joined with <em>and</em> or reversed in order.</p>
<p><em>It was a cold, windy evening.</em></p>
<p>You can say <em>a windy, cold evening</em> and retain much of the meaning. A comma is appropriate between coordinate adjectives.</p>
<p>Cumulative adjectives build a description in stages and are not usually separated by commas.</p>
<p><em>a small wooden box</em></p>
<p>Here, <em>small</em> describes the box and <em>wooden</em> identifies its material. A comma is not normally used between them.</p>

<h3>6. Avoid adjective overload</h3>
<p>Correct order does not automatically make a description effective. Compare:</p>
<p><em>She carried a beautiful small new black leather school bag.</em></p>
<p><em>She carried a new school bag.</em></p>
<p>Choose the details that help the reader understand the intended meaning. Unnecessary adjectives make sentences slower and less precise.</p>', 1),
  (1464, 1464, '<h2>Degrees of Comparison</h2>
<p>Adjectives can compare the qualities of people, places or things. English commonly uses three degrees: <strong>positive</strong>, <strong>comparative</strong> and <strong>superlative</strong>.</p>

<h3>1. Positive degree</h3>
<p>The positive form describes a quality without making a comparison.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The road is <em>wide</em>.</li>
  <li>The learner is <em>careful</em>.</li>
  <li>This exercise is <em>difficult</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Comparative degree</h3>
<p>The comparative form compares two people, things or situations. Short adjectives often take <em>-er</em>, while many longer adjectives use <em>more</em>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>tall → taller: Amina is taller than Wanjiku.</li>
  <li>fast → faster: This bus is faster than the other one.</li>
  <li>careful → more careful: He is more careful than before.</li>
  <li>interesting → more interesting: This book is more interesting than the film.</li>
</ul>
<p>The word <em>than</em> commonly introduces the second part of a comparison.</p>

<h3>3. Superlative degree</h3>
<p>The superlative form identifies the highest or lowest degree within a group of three or more, or within a defined group.</p>
<ul>
  <li>tall → tallest: Amina is the tallest learner in the group.</li>
  <li>fast → fastest: This is the fastest bus on the route.</li>
  <li>careful → most careful: She is the most careful driver in the team.</li>
  <li>interesting → most interesting: That was the most interesting chapter in the book.</li>
</ul>
<p>Superlatives commonly take <em>the</em> before them when identifying one member of a group, although context and determiner choice can vary.</p>

<h3>4. Spelling changes</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Most short adjectives:</strong> add <em>-er</em> or <em>-est</em>: tall → taller → tallest.</li>
  <li><strong>Adjectives ending in -e:</strong> add <em>-r</em> or <em>-st</em>: large → larger → largest.</li>
  <li><strong>Consonant + y:</strong> change <em>y</em> to <em>i</em>: happy → happier → happiest.</li>
  <li><strong>Some short vowel-consonant forms:</strong> double the final consonant: big → bigger → biggest.</li>
  <li><strong>Many longer adjectives:</strong> use <em>more</em> and <em>most</em>: careful → more careful → most careful.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are useful patterns, not a guarantee for every adjective. Some two-syllable adjectives allow more than one form depending on usage.</p>

<h3>5. Irregular comparisons</h3>
<p>Some common adjectives have irregular forms that must be learned.</p>
<ul>
  <li>good → better → best</li>
  <li>bad → worse → worst</li>
  <li>far → farther/further → farthest/furthest, depending on context and variety of English</li>
  <li>little → less → least, when referring to amount</li>
  <li>many/much → more → most</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not form these comparisons by adding <em>-er</em> or <em>-est</em> to the positive form.</p>

<h3>6. Avoid double comparison</h3>
<p>Incorrect: <em>more taller</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>taller</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>most fastest</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>fastest</em></p>
<p>Do not normally use <em>more</em> with an adjective that already has the comparative ending, or <em>most</em> with one that already has the superlative ending.</p>

<h3>7. Make comparisons logically</h3>
<p>Incorrect: <em>My bag is heavier than Mary.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>My bag is heavier than Mary''s.</em></p>
<p>The first sentence compares a bag with a person. The corrected version compares one bag with Mary''s bag.</p>
<p>Use <em>as ... as</em> to express equality:</p>
<p><em>This road is as wide as the other road.</em></p>
<p>Use <em>not as ... as</em> to express a lack of equality:</p>
<p><em>This road is not as wide as the other road.</em></p>', 1),
  (1465, 1465, '<h2>Types of Adverbs</h2>
<p>An <strong>adverb</strong> can modify a verb, an adjective, another adverb or a whole clause. It often tells us how, when, where or how often something happens, or to what degree something is true.</p>

<h3>1. Adverbs of manner</h3>
<p>Adverbs of manner describe how an action is performed.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The learner answered <em>correctly</em>.</li>
  <li>The driver moved <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The baby slept <em>peacefully</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many adverbs of manner end in <em>-ly</em>, but not all do. In <em>She runs fast</em>, <em>fast</em> is an adverb, while <em>fast</em> can also be an adjective in <em>a fast runner</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Adverbs of time</h3>
<p>Adverbs of time tell us when something happens or how long it continues.</p>
<ul>
  <li>We arrived <em>yesterday</em>.</li>
  <li>The lesson will begin <em>soon</em>.</li>
  <li>She waited <em>briefly</em>.</li>
  <li>They have <em>already</em> left.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Adverbs of place and direction</h3>
<p>These adverbs tell us where an action happens or where something moves.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Please wait <em>outside</em>.</li>
  <li>The children looked <em>everywhere</em>.</li>
  <li>He moved <em>forward</em>.</li>
  <li>The bird flew <em>away</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Adverbs of frequency</h3>
<p>Adverbs of frequency indicate how often something happens.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>always</em></li>
  <li><em>usually</em></li>
  <li><em>often</em></li>
  <li><em>sometimes</em></li>
  <li><em>rarely</em></li>
  <li><em>never</em></li>
</ul>
<p>Frequency adverbs usually come before a main verb:</p>
<p><em>She often reads in the evening.</em></p>
<p>They usually come after the verb <em>be</em> when it is the main verb:</p>
<p><em>She is often early.</em></p>
<p>With an auxiliary verb, they commonly appear after the first auxiliary:</p>
<p><em>She has never missed a lesson.</em></p>

<h3>5. Adverbs of degree</h3>
<p>Adverbs of degree indicate intensity or extent. They often modify adjectives, other adverbs or verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The water is <em>very</em> cold. — modifies an adjective.</li>
  <li>She answered <em>extremely</em> quickly. — modifies an adverb.</li>
  <li>I <em>almost</em> fell. — modifies the verb phrase.</li>
  <li>The work is <em>quite</em> difficult. — modifies an adjective.</li>
</ul>
<p>Words such as <em>very</em>, <em>too</em>, <em>almost</em>, <em>enough</em> and <em>extremely</em> can express degree, but they do not all work in exactly the same way.</p>

<h3>6. Adverbs of certainty</h3>
<p>These adverbs show how certain the speaker is about a statement.</p>
<ul>
  <li>She will <em>probably</em> arrive early.</li>
  <li>He has <em>certainly</em> improved.</li>
  <li>They will <em>possibly</em> attend.</li>
</ul>
<p>The choice of adverb changes the speaker''s level of confidence.</p>

<h3>7. Adverbs do not always end in -ly</h3>
<p>Common adverbs without <em>-ly</em> include <em>fast</em>, <em>hard</em>, <em>late</em>, <em>well</em> and <em>often</em>.</p>
<p>Be careful with pairs that have different meanings:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Hard</em> means with effort: She worked hard.</li>
  <li><em>Hardly</em> means almost not: She hardly worked.</li>
  <li><em>Late</em> means after the expected time: He arrived late.</li>
  <li><em>Lately</em> means recently: Have you seen him lately?</li>
  <li><em>Good</em> is usually an adjective: She is a good singer.</li>
  <li><em>Well</em> is often an adverb of manner: She sings well.</li>
</ul>

<h3>8. Identify what the adverb modifies</h3>
<p>Do not classify an adverb by its spelling alone. Ask what it modifies and what meaning it adds.</p>
<p>In <em>The extremely careful driver stopped suddenly</em>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Extremely</em> modifies the adjective <em>careful</em>.</li>
  <li><em>Suddenly</em> modifies the verb <em>stopped</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both are adverbs, but they perform different functions.</p>', 1),
  (1466, 1466, '<h2>Adverbs of Time, Place, Manner and Frequency</h2>
<p>Adverbs can add information about when an action happens, where it happens, how it happens and how often it happens. Identifying the question an adverb answers helps you understand its function.</p>

<h3>1. Adverbs of time: When?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of time tell us when an event happens, has happened or will happen. Some also communicate how long an event lasts or how soon it occurs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>We arrived <em>yesterday</em>.</li>
  <li>The visitors will arrive <em>soon</em>.</li>
  <li>She has <em>already</em> completed the assignment.</li>
  <li>They are leaving <em>now</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask <em>when?</em> to identify the time information. In the sentence <em>We arrived yesterday</em>, the word <em>yesterday</em> tells us when the arrival occurred.</p>

<h3>2. Adverbs of place: Where?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of place tell us where an action occurs or where someone or something moves.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The children are playing <em>outside</em>.</li>
  <li>Look <em>everywhere</em> for the missing book.</li>
  <li>The visitors went <em>upstairs</em>.</li>
  <li>Please come <em>here</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask <em>where?</em> or <em>in which direction?</em> to identify the information. Do not confuse an adverb of place with a prepositional phrase: <em>outside</em> is an adverb in <em>Wait outside</em>, while <em>outside the classroom</em> is a prepositional phrase.</p>

<h3>3. Adverbs of manner: How?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of manner explain how an action is performed.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The nurse spoke <em>gently</em>.</li>
  <li>The learner answered <em>correctly</em>.</li>
  <li>The mechanic worked <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The athlete ran <em>fast</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many adverbs of manner end in <em>-ly</em>, but this is not a universal rule. <em>Fast</em>, <em>hard</em> and <em>well</em> can all function as adverbs of manner.</p>
<p>Be careful with adjectives after linking verbs:</p>
<p><em>The soup smells good.</em> Here, <em>good</em> describes the soup.</p>
<p><em>The cook works well.</em> Here, <em>well</em> describes how the cook works.</p>

<h3>4. Adverbs of frequency: How often?</h3>
<p>Adverbs of frequency indicate how regularly something happens.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Always</em>: every time or on every relevant occasion.</li>
  <li><em>Usually</em>: on most occasions.</li>
  <li><em>Often</em>: many times.</li>
  <li><em>Sometimes</em>: on some occasions.</li>
  <li><em>Rarely</em>: not often.</li>
  <li><em>Never</em>: not at any time.</li>
</ul>
<p>These words express different frequencies; they are not exact numerical measurements.</p>

<h3>5. Position of frequency adverbs</h3>
<p>Frequency adverbs usually appear before the main verb:</p>
<p><em>She often visits her grandmother.</em></p>
<p>They usually appear after the verb <em>be</em>:</p>
<p><em>She is often early.</em></p>
<p>With an auxiliary verb, they commonly appear after the first auxiliary:</p>
<p><em>She has never missed a lesson.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect in ordinary neutral word order: <em>She visits often her grandmother.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>She often visits her grandmother.</em></p>

<h3>6. One sentence can contain several types</h3>
<p>Consider: <em>The team practised carefully outside yesterday.</em></p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Carefully</em> — manner: how did the team practise?</li>
  <li><em>Outside</em> — place: where did the team practise?</li>
  <li><em>Yesterday</em> — time: when did the team practise?</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>The team often practises outside</em>, <em>often</em> indicates frequency and <em>outside</em> indicates place.</p>

<h3>7. Avoid confusing adverbs with time and place phrases</h3>
<p><em>We met yesterday.</em> — <em>Yesterday</em> is an adverb of time.</p>
<p><em>We met after the lesson.</em> — <em>After the lesson</em> is a prepositional phrase expressing time.</p>
<p><em>They waited outside.</em> — <em>Outside</em> functions as an adverb of place.</p>
<p><em>They waited outside the office.</em> — <em>Outside the office</em> functions as a prepositional phrase.</p>
<p>Classify the whole expression according to its grammatical function, not merely the meaning it communicates.</p>', 1),
  (1467, 1467, '<h2>Adverbs of Time, Place, Manner and Frequency</h2>
