</ul>
<p>These words express different frequencies; they are not exact numerical measurements.</p>

<h3>5. Position of frequency adverbs</h3>
<p>Frequency adverbs usually appear before the main verb:</p>
<p><em>She often visits her grandmother.</em></p>
<p>They usually appear after the verb <em>be</em>:</p>
<p><em>She is often early.</em></p>
<p>With an auxiliary verb, they commonly appear after the first auxiliary:</p>
<p><em>She has never missed a lesson.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect in ordinary neutral word order: <em>She visits often her grandmother.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>She often visits her grandmother.</em></p>

<h3>6. One sentence can contain several types</h3>
<p>Consider: <em>The team practised carefully outside yesterday.</em></p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Carefully</em> — manner: how did the team practise?</li>
  <li><em>Outside</em> — place: where did the team practise?</li>
  <li><em>Yesterday</em> — time: when did the team practise?</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>The team often practises outside</em>, <em>often</em> indicates frequency and <em>outside</em> indicates place.</p>

<h3>7. Avoid confusing adverbs with time and place phrases</h3>
<p><em>We met yesterday.</em> — <em>Yesterday</em> is an adverb of time.</p>
<p><em>We met after the lesson.</em> — <em>After the lesson</em> is a prepositional phrase expressing time.</p>
<p><em>They waited outside.</em> — <em>Outside</em> functions as an adverb of place.</p>
<p><em>They waited outside the office.</em> — <em>Outside the office</em> functions as a prepositional phrase.</p>
<p>Classify the whole expression according to its grammatical function, not merely the meaning it communicates.</p>', 1),
  (1468, 1468, '<h2>Adverbs of Degree</h2>
<p>An <strong>adverb of degree</strong> tells us how much, how strongly or to what extent something is true or happens. It can modify an adjective, another adverb or a verb phrase.</p>

<h3>1. Degree modifying adjectives</h3>
<p>Adverbs of degree can strengthen or limit an adjective.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The water is <em>very</em> cold.</li>
  <li>The examination was <em>extremely</em> difficult.</li>
  <li>The room is <em>fairly</em> large.</li>
  <li>The explanation was <em>slightly</em> confusing.</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>very cold</em>, the word <em>very</em> intensifies the adjective <em>cold</em>. In <em>slightly confusing</em>, <em>slightly</em> reduces the strength of the description.</p>

<h3>2. Degree modifying adverbs</h3>
<p>An adverb of degree can also modify another adverb.</p>
<ul>
  <li>She answered <em>very quickly</em>.</li>
  <li>The driver reacted <em>surprisingly slowly</em>.</li>
  <li>The learners worked <em>quite efficiently</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>very quickly</em>, <em>quickly</em> describes how she answered, while <em>very</em> indicates the degree of speed.</p>

<h3>3. Degree modifying verbs or verb phrases</h3>
<p>Some adverbs of degree modify the action or the whole verb phrase.</p>
<ul>
  <li>I <em>almost</em> fell.</li>
  <li>She <em>completely</em> forgot the appointment.</li>
  <li>They <em>hardly</em> spoke during the meeting.</li>
  <li>We <em>nearly</em> missed the bus.</li>
</ul>
<p>Placement can change the intended meaning. Compare:</p>
<p><em>Almost everyone passed.</em> — Nearly everyone passed, though not necessarily everyone.</p>
<p><em>Everyone almost passed.</em> — Each person came close to passing but did not necessarily pass.</p>

<h3>4. Very, too and enough</h3>
<p>These words do not mean the same thing.</p>
<p><strong>Very</strong> increases intensity:</p>
<p><em>The bag is very heavy.</em></p>
<p><strong>Too</strong> indicates excess, often beyond what is acceptable or possible:</p>
<p><em>The bag is too heavy for the child to carry.</em></p>
<p><strong>Enough</strong> indicates a sufficient amount or degree:</p>
<p><em>The bag is light enough for the child to carry.</em></p>
<p>Notice the positions:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>Very heavy</em> — degree word before the adjective.</li>
  <li><em>Too heavy</em> — degree word before the adjective.</li>
  <li><em>Heavy enough</em> — <em>enough</em> after the adjective.</li>
  <li><em>Enough water</em> — <em>enough</em> before the noun.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. So and such</h3>
<p><strong>So</strong> usually modifies an adjective or adverb:</p>
<p><em>The road was so narrow that the bus could not pass.</em></p>
<p><em>She ran so quickly that nobody caught up with her.</em></p>
<p><strong>Such</strong> usually introduces a noun phrase:</p>
<p><em>It was such a narrow road that the bus could not pass.</em></p>
<p><em>They were such difficult questions that many learners needed extra time.</em></p>
<p>A useful contrast is <em>so difficult</em> but <em>such a difficult question</em>.</p>

<h3>6. Avoid incorrect combinations</h3>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The task was enough easy.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>The task was easy enough.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>It was so a difficult question.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>It was such a difficult question.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The room was too very hot.</em></p>
<p>Better: <em>The room was very hot.</em> or <em>The room was too hot to work in comfortably.</em></p>

<h3>7. Choose degree words carefully</h3>
<p>Some adjectives have meanings that already express an extreme degree. In formal writing, expressions such as <em>absolutely impossible</em> and <em>completely exhausted</em> are often more natural than combinations such as <em>very impossible</em>. The best choice depends on the adjective and the intended meaning.</p>', 1),
  (1469, 1469, '<h2>Adjective–Adverb Distinction</h2>
<p>Adjectives and adverbs can describe related ideas, but they perform different grammatical jobs. An <strong>adjective</strong> typically describes a noun or pronoun. An <strong>adverb</strong> can modify a verb, adjective, another adverb or a whole clause.</p>

<h3>1. Adjectives describe nouns</h3>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
  <li>She is a <em>careful</em> driver.</li>
  <li>That is a <em>beautiful</em> painting.</li>
  <li>The road is <em>narrow</em>.</li>
  <li>The learners seem <em>tired</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>a careful driver</em>, the adjective describes the noun <em>driver</em>. In <em>The road is narrow</em>, the adjective describes the subject <em>road</em> through the linking verb <em>is</em>.</p>

<h3>2. Adverbs describe actions or modify descriptions</h3>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
  <li>She drives <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The painting was <em>beautifully</em> displayed.</li>
  <li>The bus moved <em>slowly</em>.</li>
  <li>The learners answered <em>correctly</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>In <em>She drives carefully</em>, the adverb describes how she drives. The adverb does not describe the noun <em>she</em>.</p>

<h3>3. Linking verbs usually take adjectives as complements</h3>
<p>Linking verbs connect a subject to a description or state. Common examples include <em>be, seem, become, appear, look, feel, smell, taste</em> and <em>sound</em> when they function as linking verbs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The soup tastes <em>good</em>.</li>
  <li>The child looks <em>tired</em>.</li>
  <li>The music sounds <em>beautiful</em>.</li>
  <li>The fabric feels <em>soft</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The words after the linking verbs describe the soup, child, music and fabric. Adjectives are appropriate.</p>
<p>Compare with action verbs:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The chef tasted the soup <em>carefully</em>.</li>
  <li>The child looked <em>carefully</em> at the drawing.</li>
  <li>The musician played <em>beautifully</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Here, the adverbs describe actions rather than states or qualities.</p>

<h3>4. Do not assume every -ly word is an adverb</h3>
<p>Some adjectives end in <em>-ly</em>:</p>
<ul>
  <li>a <em>friendly</em> neighbour</li>
  <li>a <em>lovely</em> afternoon</li>
  <li>a <em>lively</em> discussion</li>
  <li>an <em>elderly</em> person</li>
</ul>
<p>These words describe nouns and are adjectives. English has no universal rule that every adjective becomes an adverb simply by adding <em>-ly</em>.</p>

<h3>5. Common adjective–adverb pairs</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Good / well:</strong> She is a good singer. She sings well.</li>
  <li><strong>Bad / badly:</strong> It was a bad performance. He performed badly.</li>
  <li><strong>Careful / carefully:</strong> She is careful. She checks her work carefully.</li>
  <li><strong>Quick / quickly:</strong> He is a quick learner. He learns quickly.</li>
  <li><strong>Hard / hardly:</strong> They worked hard. They hardly worked.</li>
  <li><strong>Late / lately:</strong> He arrived late. Have you seen him lately?</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Hardly</em> means almost not, and <em>lately</em> means recently. They are not simply interchangeable with <em>hard</em> and <em>late</em>.</p>

<h3>6. Avoid changing the word form blindly</h3>
<p>Incorrect: <em>She sings beautiful.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>She sings beautifully.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The soup tastes well.</em> (when describing its flavour)</p>
<p>Correct: <em>The soup tastes good.</em></p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>He completed the work careful.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>He completed the work carefully.</em></p>

<h3>7. A reliable test</h3>
<p>Ask what is being described:</p>
<ul>
  <li>If the word describes a noun or pronoun, an adjective is often needed.</li>
  <li>If it describes how an action happens, an adverb is often needed.</li>
  <li>If it follows a linking verb and describes the subject, an adjective is usually appropriate.</li>
  <li>If it modifies another adjective or adverb, use an adverb of degree where appropriate.</li>
</ul>
<p>Always examine the function in the sentence. Do not decide based only on the word''s ending.</p>', 1),
  (1470, 1470, '<h2>Prepositions of Time</h2>
<p>Prepositions of time show the relationship between an event and a point or period in time. The most common choices include <strong>at, on</strong> and <strong>in</strong>, but other prepositions communicate duration, starting points, deadlines and sequence.</p>

<h3>1. At: precise times and points</h3>
<p>Use <em>at</em> for a specific time on the clock and certain established time expressions.</p>
<ul>
  <li>at 7:30 a.m.</li>
  <li>at noon</li>
  <li>at midnight</li>
  <li>at sunrise</li>
  <li>at night</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The lesson begins at eight o''clock.</em></p>
<p><em>We arrived at midnight.</em></p>

<h3>2. On: days and dates</h3>
<p>Use <em>on</em> with days, dates and particular days or parts of days.</p>
<ul>
  <li>on Monday</li>
  <li>on 12 June</li>
  <li>on my birthday</li>
  <li>on Monday morning</li>
  <li>on the first day of the term</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The examination begins on Friday.</em></p>
<p><em>We travelled on 12 June.</em></p>
<p>Do not normally use <em>on</em> before a day name when using a general expression such as <em>every Monday</em>.</p>

<h3>3. In: months, years and longer periods</h3>
<p>Use <em>in</em> for months, years, seasons and many longer periods.</p>
<ul>
  <li>in October</li>
  <li>in 2026</li>
  <li>in the dry season</li>
  <li>in the twentieth century</li>
  <li>in the morning</li>
  <li>in the afternoon</li>
  <li>in the evening</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>The school opened in January.</em></p>
<p><em>We usually revise in the evening.</em></p>

<h3>4. Important exceptions and expressions</h3>
<p>English uses established expressions that must be learned as complete phrases.</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>at night</em>, but <em>in the evening</em>.</li>
  <li><em>at the weekend</em> is common in British English, including Kenyan English usage; <em>on the weekend</em> is common in American English.</li>
  <li><em>in time</em> means early enough for something; <em>on time</em> means at the scheduled or expected time.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p><em>We arrived on time for the examination.</em></p>
<p><em>We arrived in time to revise before the examination began.</em></p>

<h3>5. Since and for</h3>
<p><strong>Since</strong> identifies the starting point of an activity or state continuing to a later reference point.</p>
<p><em>She has lived here since 2022.</em></p>
<p><strong>For</strong> identifies the length of time.</p>
<p><em>She has lived here for four years.</em></p>
<p>Compare:</p>
<ul>
  <li>since Monday — a starting point.</li>
  <li>for three days — a duration.</li>
  <li>since last year — a starting point.</li>
  <li>for several months — a duration.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. By and until</h3>
<p><strong>By</strong> means no later than a stated time or deadline.</p>
<p><em>Submit your assignment by Friday.</em></p>
<p>The assignment must be submitted on Friday or earlier.</p>
<p><strong>Until</strong> indicates that an activity or state continues up to a point in time.</p>
<p><em>The library will remain open until six o''clock.</em></p>
<p>The library closes at six, and the period of opening continues up to that time.</p>

<h3>7. During, before and after</h3>
<p><strong>During</strong> identifies a period in which something happens:</p>
<p><em>Do not use your phone during the examination.</em></p>
<p><strong>Before</strong> indicates an earlier time:</p>
<p><em>Wash your hands before eating.</em></p>
<p><strong>After</strong> indicates a later time:</p>
<p><em>We discussed the results after the meeting.</em></p>
<p>Use a noun phrase after these prepositions: <em>before lunch</em>, <em>after the lesson</em>, <em>during the holiday</em>.</p>

<h3>8. Avoid common errors</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>in Monday</em>. Correct: <em>on Monday</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>at 2026</em>. Correct: <em>in 2026</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>since three years</em>. Correct: <em>for three years</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>for 2022</em> when identifying the starting point of an ongoing state. Correct: <em>since 2022</em>.</li>
  <li>Incorrect: <em>Submit it until Friday</em> when Friday is a deadline. Correct: <em>Submit it by Friday</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The correct preposition depends on the time relationship being expressed, not simply on the noun that follows it.</p>', 1),
  (1471, 1471, '<h2>Prepositions of Place</h2>
<p>Prepositions of place describe the position of a person or thing in relation to another person, thing or location. The correct choice depends on the relationship being expressed.</p>

<h3>1. In: inside an area or space</h3>
<p>Use <em>in</em> when something is inside a space, enclosed area or geographical region.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The books are <em>in</em> the cupboard.</li>
  <li>The learners are <em>in</em> the classroom.</li>
  <li>Nairobi is <em>in</em> Kenya.</li>
  <li>She lives <em>in</em> Mombasa.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>In</em> presents the place as a space or area containing the person or thing.</p>

<h3>2. On: a surface or line</h3>
<p>Use <em>on</em> when something rests on a surface or is positioned along a line or route.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The book is <em>on</em> the desk.</li>
  <li>There is a picture <em>on</em> the wall.</li>
  <li>The village is <em>on</em> the main road.</li>
  <li>The notice is <em>on</em> the board.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not assume that <em>on</em> always means touching a horizontal surface. English also uses it for walls, roads and other established spatial relationships.</p>

<h3>3. At: a point or specific location</h3>
<p>Use <em>at</em> when treating a place as a point, meeting location or activity location.</p>
<ul>
  <li>We met <em>at</em> the gate.</li>
  <li>She is waiting <em>at</em> the bus stop.</li>
  <li>He is <em>at</em> school.</li>
  <li>They arrived <em>at</em> the station.</li>
</ul>
<p>Compare:</p>
<p><em>She is at the school.</em> — The school is treated as a location.</p>
<p><em>She is in the school.</em> — She is inside the school building or grounds, depending on context.</p>
<p>The distinction is about how the speaker conceptualises the location, not just its size.</p>

<h3>4. Above, over, below and under</h3>
<p>These prepositions describe vertical relationships.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Above:</strong> at a higher level than something, without necessarily being directly over it. <em>The clock is above the door.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Over:</strong> higher than, often directly above, or covering something. <em>The roof is over the room.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Below:</strong> at a lower level than something. <em>The valley lies below the hill.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Under:</strong> lower than, often directly beneath or covered by something. <em>The shoes are under the bed.</em></li>
</ul>
<p>The meanings overlap in some contexts, so choose the word that best communicates the spatial relationship.</p>

<h3>5. Between and among</h3>
<p><strong>Between</strong> commonly identifies the position separating two people or things, and it can also be used for clearly distinct individual items in a larger group.</p>
<p><em>The clinic is between the bank and the market.</em></p>
<p><em>The agreement was made between the three schools.</em></p>
<p><strong>Among</strong> usually indicates a position within or surrounded by a group.</p>
<p><em>The teacher walked among the learners.</em></p>
<p>Avoid the oversimplification that <em>between</em> can only ever refer to two items. The intended relationship determines the choice.</p>

<h3>6. Behind, in front of, beside and next to</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Behind:</strong> at the back of something. <em>The garden is behind the house.</em></li>
  <li><strong>In front of:</strong> before or ahead of something in position. <em>The bus stopped in front of the gate.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Beside:</strong> at the side of something. <em>She sat beside her sister.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Next to:</strong> immediately beside something. <em>The clinic is next to the pharmacy.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Near, opposite and inside</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Near:</strong> a short distance from something. <em>The shop is near the school.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Opposite:</strong> facing something or located across from it. <em>The bank is opposite the market.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Inside:</strong> within the interior of a place or object. <em>The keys are inside the drawer.</em></li>
  <li><strong>Outside:</strong> not inside a place or object. <em>The learners are waiting outside the hall.</em></li>
</ul>

<h3>8. Distinguish prepositions from adverbs</h3>
<p>A preposition normally introduces a complement, often a noun phrase or pronoun.</p>
<p><em>The children waited outside the classroom.</em> — <em>Outside the classroom</em> is a prepositional phrase.</p>
<p><em>The children waited outside.</em> — <em>Outside</em> functions as an adverb because no complement follows it.</p>
<p>This distinction matters because the same word can perform different grammatical functions in different sentences.</p>', 1),
  (1472, 1472, '<h2>Prepositions of Direction and Movement</h2>
<h3>1. UNDERSTANDING MOVEMENT PREPOSITIONS</h3>
<p>Prepositions of direction and movement describe how someone or something moves from one place to another.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Into / Out of:</b> Entering or leaving an enclosed space (<i>She walked into the classroom; He stepped out of the car</i>).</li>
<li><b>Onto / Off:</b> Moving to or from a surface (<i>The cat jumped onto the roof; The cup fell off the table</i>).</li>
<li><b>Across / Through:</b> Across a 2D surface (<i>swim across the river</i>) vs through a 3D volume (<i>walk through the forest</i>).</li>
<li><b>Towards / Away from:</b> In the direction of or moving further from an object (<i>She ran towards the bus</i>).</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1473, 1473, '<h2>Prepositional Phrases</h2>
<h3>1. STRUCTURE OF PREPOSITIONAL PHRASES</h3>
<p>A <b>prepositional phrase</b> consists of a preposition followed by its object (a noun, pronoun, or noun phrase) and any modifying words.</p>
<p><b>Formula:</b> <code>Preposition + Modifier(s) + Object of Preposition</code></p>
<ul>
<li><i>Adjectival function:</i> The book <b>on the wooden desk</b> belongs to Grace. (Modifies ''book'')</li>
<li><i>Adverbial function:</i> We studied <b>during the evening</b>. (Modifies ''studied'' — tells when)</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1474, 1474, '<h2>Coordinating Conjunctions</h2>
<h3>1. THE FANBOYS CONJUNCTIONS</h3>
<p><b>Coordinating conjunctions</b> connect words, phrases, or independent clauses of equal grammatical rank. Remember them using the acronym <b>FANBOYS</b>:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>F</b>or (Reason/Cause)</li>
<li><b>A</b>nd (Addition)</li>
<li><b>N</b>or (Negative alternative)</li>
<li><b>B</b>ut (Contrast)</li>
<li><b>O</b>r (Alternative)</li>
<li><b>Y</b>et (Surprising contrast)</li>
<li><b>S</b>o (Result/Consequence)</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1475, 1475, '<h2>Subordinating Conjunctions</h2>
<h3>1. JOINING MAIN AND DEPENDENT CLAUSES</h3>
<p>A <b>subordinating conjunction</b> joins an independent (main) clause to a dependent (subordinate) clause, establishing a relationship of time, reason, condition, concession, or purpose.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Time:</b> before, after, while, since, until, when, as soon as</li>
<li><b>Cause/Reason:</b> because, since, as</li>
<li><b>Condition:</b> if, unless, provided that</li>
<li><b>Concession/Contrast:</b> although, even though, whereas, while</li>
<li><b>Purpose:</b> so that, in order that</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1476, 1476, '<h2>Correlative Conjunctions</h2>
<h3>1. PAIRED CONJUNCTIONS</h3>
<p><b>Correlative conjunctions</b> always work in pairs to connect grammatically parallel words, phrases, or clauses.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Either ... or:</b> Choice between two alternatives (<i>Either stay or leave.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Neither ... nor:</b> Negative pairing (<i>Neither John nor his brother attended.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Both ... and:</b> Inclusive addition of two items (<i>She is both talented and humble.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Not only ... but also:</b> Emphatic addition (<i>He is not only a scholar but also an athlete.</i>)</li>
<li><b>Whether ... or:</b> Alternative possibilities (<i>Whether you agree or disagree, the decision is final.</i>)</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1477, 1477, '<h2>Linking Words and Logical Relationships</h2>
<p>Linking words connect ideas and show how those ideas relate. They help a reader follow the reasoning in a paragraph and help a writer express relationships clearly.</p>

<h3>1. Addition</h3>
<p>Use <strong>and, also, furthermore, moreover</strong> and <strong>in addition</strong> to add information.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The library has reference books. In addition, it provides access to digital resources.</p>

<h3>2. Contrast</h3>
<p>Use <strong>but, however, although, whereas</strong> and <strong>nevertheless</strong> to show a difference or an unexpected result.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Although the road was muddy, the bus arrived on time.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The first plan was expensive. However, the second plan was affordable.</p>

<h3>3. Cause and result</h3>
<p>Use <strong>because, since</strong> and <strong>as</strong> to introduce a reason. Use <strong>so, therefore, consequently</strong> and <strong>as a result</strong> to introduce a result.</p>
<p><em>Reason:</em> The match was postponed because the field was flooded.</p>
<p><em>Result:</em> The field was flooded; therefore, the match was postponed.</p>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> Do not use <em>because</em> and <em>therefore</em> together to connect the same two clauses in one ordinary sentence: “Because the field was flooded, the match was postponed” is correct. So is “The field was flooded; therefore, the match was postponed.”</p>

<h3>4. Sequence and time</h3>
<p>Use <strong>first, next, then, afterwards, finally, before</strong> and <strong>meanwhile</strong> to show order or timing.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> First, collect the materials. Next, measure the liquid. Finally, record the result.</p>

<h3>5. Examples and clarification</h3>
<p>Use <strong>for example, for instance, such as</strong> and <strong>in other words</strong> to illustrate or clarify an idea.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Some renewable energy sources, such as wind and solar power, produce electricity without burning fossil fuels during generation.</p>

<h3>6. Purpose and condition</h3>
<p>Use <strong>so that</strong> to express purpose and <strong>if, unless</strong> or <strong>provided that</strong> to express a condition.</p>
<p><em>Purpose:</em> She labelled the containers so that everyone could identify their contents.</p>
<p><em>Condition:</em> You will understand the procedure if you follow each step carefully.</p>

<h3>How to choose the right linker</h3>
<p>Do not choose a linking word merely because it sounds formal. Identify the relationship between the ideas first. Ask: Am I adding information, contrasting ideas, giving a reason, showing a result, ordering events, providing an example, expressing purpose or setting a condition?</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using <em>however</em> as though it were an ordinary coordinating conjunction: “The road was flooded, however we continued” is incorrectly punctuated. Write “The road was flooded; however, we continued.”</li>
<li>Using a result linker when the second idea is actually a reason.</li>
<li>Adding linkers that do not express a genuine relationship, making writing awkward.</li>
<li>Repeating the same linker in every sentence instead of choosing one that fits the meaning.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1478, 1478, '<h2>Synonyms</h2>
<p><strong>Synonyms</strong> are words that have the same or nearly the same meaning in a particular context. They allow speakers and writers to express ideas precisely and avoid unnecessary repetition.</p>

<h3>1. Exact meaning depends on context</h3>
<p>Many synonyms are similar rather than completely interchangeable.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> <strong>Big</strong> and <strong>large</strong> have similar meanings: “a big house” and “a large house.”</p>
<p>However, natural usage differs: we commonly say “a big mistake,” not “a large mistake,” and “a large amount,” not usually “a big amount” in formal writing.</p>

<h3>2. Synonyms can differ in strength</h3>
<p>Words may express different degrees of the same idea.</p>
<p><strong>Annoyed → angry → furious</strong></p>
<p>These words describe increasing intensity. Replacing one with another can change the force of a sentence.</p>

<h3>3. Synonyms can differ in formality</h3>
<p><strong>Ask</strong> is neutral, <strong>request</strong> is more formal, and <strong>inquire</strong> often means to ask for information formally.</p>
<p><em>Informal:</em> Can I ask you a question?</p>
<p><em>Formal:</em> May I request further information?</p>

<h3>4. Synonyms depend on the intended meaning</h3>
<p>A word can have more than one meaning, and each meaning may require different synonyms.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “Bright light” can mean strong light. “A bright learner” means an intelligent learner. “Strong” fits the first meaning in some contexts, while “intelligent” fits the second.</p>

<h3>5. Choosing an appropriate synonym</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the whole sentence.</li>
<li>Identify the meaning the original word expresses there.</li>
<li>Consider tone, intensity and formality.</li>
<li>Replace the word and reread the sentence to check that its meaning remains appropriate.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming words listed as synonyms in a dictionary can always replace one another.</li>
<li>Choosing a word that is too strong or too weak for the situation.</li>
<li>Ignoring common word partnerships, such as “make a decision” and “heavy rain”.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1479, 1479, '<h2>Antonyms</h2>
<p><strong>Antonyms</strong> are words with opposite or contrasting meanings. They help express differences, comparisons and changes.</p>

<h3>1. Gradable opposites</h3>
<p>These describe opposite ends of a scale and allow degrees between them.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>hot/cold</strong>, <strong>easy/difficult</strong>, <strong>wide/narrow</strong>.</p>
<p>A road can be neither especially wide nor especially narrow. The opposites do not have to describe every possible state.</p>

<h3>2. Complementary opposites</h3>
<p>These are opposites in which one term generally excludes the other in the relevant context.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>alive/dead</strong>, <strong>present/absent</strong>, <strong>pass/fail</strong> in a particular examination result system.</p>
<p>Be careful: real-world usage can depend on context. For example, a person who is neither fully present nor completely absent may be described as partly attending.</p>

<h3>3. Relational opposites</h3>
<p>These describe the same relationship from opposite viewpoints.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>buy/sell</strong>, <strong>teacher/student</strong> in a teaching relationship, and <strong>lend/borrow</strong>.</p>
<p>If Kendi lends a book to Musa, Musa borrows the book from Kendi. The actions describe opposite perspectives on one transaction.</p>

<h3>4. Forming antonyms with prefixes</h3>
<p>Some words form opposites by adding prefixes such as <strong>un-</strong>, <strong>in-</strong>, <strong>im-</strong>, <strong>il-</strong>, <strong>ir-</strong> or <strong>dis-</strong>.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>kind/unkind</strong>, <strong>possible/impossible</strong>, <strong>legal/illegal</strong>, <strong>regular/irregular</strong>, <strong>agree/disagree</strong>.</p>
<p>There is no single prefix that works for every word. Learn the conventional form rather than inventing one.</p>

<h3>5. Antonyms in context</h3>
<p>Choose an opposite that matches the particular meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The opposite of <strong>generous</strong> is commonly <strong>stingy</strong> or <strong>mean</strong> when describing willingness to give. The opposite of <strong>generous</strong> in a different context may require a more precise explanation.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming every word has one exact opposite.</li>
<li>Adding a negative prefix that does not form a standard word.</li>
<li>Choosing a word that contrasts with the wrong meaning of the original word.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1480, 1480, '<h2>Homophones</h2>
<p><strong>Homophones</strong> are words that sound alike but have different meanings and, often, different spellings. Correct writing depends on recognising which word fits the sentence.</p>

<h3>1. Common homophone pairs</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>there</strong>: refers to a place or introduces a statement — “Put the bag there.”</li>
<li><strong>their</strong>: shows possession — “Their bags are outside.”</li>
<li><strong>they''re</strong>: short for “they are” — “They''re waiting outside.”</li>
<li><strong>your</strong>: shows possession — “Your book is here.”</li>
<li><strong>you''re</strong>: short for “you are” — “You''re ready.”</li>
<li><strong>to</strong>: a preposition or part of an infinitive — “Go to school”; “to read”.</li>
<li><strong>too</strong>: means “also” or “excessively” — “I came too”; “too hot”.</li>
<li><strong>two</strong>: the number 2 — “Two pupils arrived.”</li>
<li><strong>here</strong>: this place — “Come here.”</li>
<li><strong>hear</strong>: perceive sound — “I can hear music.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Use a meaning test</h3>
<p>When unsure, replace the word with its full meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “They''re late” means “They are late.” The sentence works, so <strong>they''re</strong> is correct.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “Their late” does not mean “They are late”. In that sentence, <strong>they''re</strong> is required.</p>

<h3>3. Possession is not the same as contraction</h3>
<p><strong>Your</strong> and <strong>their</strong> show possession. <strong>You''re</strong> and <strong>they''re</strong> are contractions.</p>
<p>An apostrophe in a contraction represents omitted letters. It does not automatically indicate ownership. Compare <strong>its</strong> (possession) with <strong>it''s</strong> (it is or it has).</p>

<h3>4. Proofreading homophones</h3>
<p>Spell-checkers may not detect a wrong homophone because the mistaken word is still a correctly spelt English word. Read for meaning, not spelling alone.</p>
<p>For every suspicious word, ask: Does its meaning fit this sentence? Can I replace it with its full form or definition?</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing “your welcome” instead of “you''re welcome”.</li>
<li>Writing “their coming” instead of “they''re coming”.</li>
<li>Confusing “hear” with “here”.</li>
<li>Assuming a spell-checker will detect every incorrect word choice.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1481, 1481, '<h2>Homonyms and Homographs</h2>
<p>Some English words share a spelling or pronunciation but have different meanings. Recognising the intended meaning requires attention to context.</p>

<h3>1. Homonyms</h3>
<p>The term <strong>homonym</strong> is often used for words that have the same spelling and pronunciation but different meanings. Terminology can vary between dictionaries and language courses, so focus on identifying the meanings accurately.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> A <strong>bat</strong> can be an animal or an object used to hit a ball.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> A <strong>bank</strong> can be a financial institution or the land beside a river.</p>

<h3>2. Homographs</h3>
<p><strong>Homographs</strong> are words written the same way but having different meanings. Some are pronounced differently.</p>
<p><strong>Lead</strong> can be pronounced /liːd/ when it means to guide, as in “lead the team”. It can be pronounced /lɛd/ when it refers to the metal, as in “a lead pipe”.</p>
<p><strong>Wind</strong> can refer to moving air, pronounced /wɪnd/, or the action of turning or twisting something, pronounced /waɪnd/.</p>

<h3>3. Context identifies the meaning</h3>
<p>Read the words around the ambiguous word. They often provide clues to the intended meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “She deposited money at the bank” points to a financial institution.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “They sat on the bank and watched the river” points to the river''s edge.</p>

<h3>4. Use the meaning in your own sentence</h3>
<p>To show that you understand a word with several meanings, write a sentence that makes the intended meaning unmistakable.</p>
<p>Weak: “I saw a bat.”</p>
<p>Clear: “A bat flew out of the cave at dusk.”</p>
<p>Clear: “The batter picked up the bat before facing the bowler.”</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming a word has only one meaning because its spelling is familiar.</li>
<li>Using the surrounding sentence incorrectly when interpreting an ambiguous word.</li>
<li>Confusing words with shared spelling with words that merely sound alike.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1482, 1482, '<h2>Prefixes and Suffixes</h2>
<p>A <strong>prefix</strong> is a group of letters added to the beginning of a word. A <strong>suffix</strong> is a group of letters added to the end. Both can change a word''s meaning, and suffixes often change its grammatical role.</p>

<h3>1. Prefixes that change meaning</h3>
<p>Prefixes usually modify the meaning of a base word.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>un-</strong>: not or the opposite — unhappy, unfair.</li>
<li><strong>dis-</strong>: opposite, removal or reversal — disagree, disconnect.</li>
<li><strong>re-</strong>: again — rewrite, rebuild.</li>
<li><strong>mis-</strong>: wrongly or badly — misunderstand, mislead.</li>
<li><strong>pre-</strong>: before — preview, preheat.</li>
<li><strong>over-</strong>: too much or above — overcook, overconfident.</li>
<li><strong>under-</strong>: insufficiently or below — underpay, underestimate.</li>
<li><strong>im-, in-, il-, ir-</strong>: negative meaning in words such as impossible, inactive, illegal and irregular.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> To <strong>rewrite</strong> means to write again. The prefix <strong>re-</strong> adds the idea of repetition.</p>

<h3>2. Suffixes that form nouns</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>-ness</strong>: kind → kindness; dark → darkness.</li>
<li><strong>-ment</strong>: develop → development; agree → agreement.</li>
<li><strong>-tion / -sion</strong>: educate → education; decide → decision.</li>
<li><strong>-er / -or</strong>: teach → teacher; act → actor.</li>
</ul>
<p>These suffixes can form nouns that name qualities, processes, results or people.</p>

<h3>3. Suffixes that form adjectives</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>-ful</strong>: care → careful; hope → hopeful.</li>
<li><strong>-less</strong>: care → careless; hope → hopeless.</li>
<li><strong>-able / -ible</strong>: read → readable; access → accessible.</li>
<li><strong>-ous</strong>: danger → dangerous.</li>
<li><strong>-ive</strong>: attract → attractive.</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice that <strong>-ful</strong> often means having or showing something, while <strong>-less</strong> often means without it. A careful person takes care; a careless person does not take enough care.</p>

<h3>4. Suffixes that form verbs and adverbs</h3>
<p><strong>-ise / -ize</strong> can form verbs, as in modernise and organise. British English commonly uses <em>-ise</em> in many words, while both spellings are accepted for many such words depending on the chosen style.</p>
<p><strong>-en</strong> can form verbs such as widen and strengthen. <strong>-ly</strong> commonly forms adverbs from adjectives: quick → quickly; careful → carefully.</p>
<p>Not every word ending in <em>-ly</em> is an adverb: friendly and lovely are adjectives.</p>

<h3>5. Spelling changes</h3>
<p>Adding an affix may require a spelling change.</p>
<ul>
<li>happy → happiness: change <em>y</em> to <em>i</em> before <em>-ness</em>.</li>
<li>easy → easier: change <em>y</em> to <em>i</em> before <em>-er</em>.</li>
<li>hope → hopeful: retain the final <em>e</em> here.</li>
<li>use → useful: the final <em>e</em> is retained.</li>
</ul>
<p>Spelling rules depend on the word and the suffix. Check the resulting word rather than applying one rule blindly.</p>

<h3>6. Choosing the correct affix</h3>
<p>First identify the meaning you want. Then choose a recognised word form that expresses it. For example, <em>disagree</em> is a standard word, but adding any negative prefix to any verb does not automatically create a valid word.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Adding an affix that does not form a standard English word.</li>
<li>Confusing a prefix that changes meaning with a suffix that changes grammatical role.</li>
<li>Assuming every word ending in <em>-ly</em> is an adverb.</li>
<li>Ignoring spelling changes when adding a suffix.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1483, 1483, '<h2>Roots and Word Families</h2>
<p>A <strong>root</strong> is the central meaningful element of a word. A <strong>word family</strong> is a group of related words built from the same root or base. Understanding these relationships helps learners expand vocabulary and recognise unfamiliar words.</p>

<h3>1. Base words and related forms</h3>
<p>Consider the word family built around <strong>act</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>act</strong> — to do something or perform a role.</li>
<li><strong>action</strong> — something done; an act or process.</li>
<li><strong>active</strong> — doing things or participating.</li>
<li><strong>activity</strong> — an action or organised task.</li>
<li><strong>actor</strong> — a person who performs a role.</li>
<li><strong>react</strong> — to respond to something.</li>
</ul>
<p>The words are related, but they do not all mean the same thing. Prefixes and suffixes may change both meaning and grammatical role.</p>

<h3>2. Recognising word families</h3>
<p>A word family may include nouns, verbs, adjectives and adverbs.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>success</strong> — noun.</li>
<li><strong>succeed</strong> — verb.</li>
<li><strong>successful</strong> — adjective.</li>
<li><strong>successfully</strong> — adverb.</li>
</ul>
<p>These forms are related, but each fits different positions in a sentence.</p>

<h3>3. Roots that appear in many words</h3>
<p>Some roots appear across a large number of English words. For example, the Latin root <strong>spect</strong> relates to looking or seeing:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>inspect</strong> — examine carefully.</li>
<li><strong>spectator</strong> — a person who watches.</li>
<li><strong>respect</strong> — esteem or regard; its history is related to the root, although its modern meaning is not simply ''look again''.</li>
</ul>
<p>Roots can provide clues, but a root does not always reveal the full modern meaning. Confirm unfamiliar words using context or a dictionary.</p>

<h3>4. Use context with word structure</h3>
<p>When you meet an unfamiliar word:</p>
<ol>
<li>Look for a familiar root or base.</li>
<li>Identify any prefix or suffix.</li>
<li>Use the sentence to infer a possible meaning.</li>
<li>Check whether that meaning makes sense in the passage.</li>
<li>Verify it in a dictionary when precision matters.</li>
</ol>

<h3>5. Watch for misleading similarities</h3>
<p>Words that look similar are not necessarily members of the same word family. Similar spelling alone does not prove a shared origin or meaning. Likewise, a word may change its spelling when related forms are created: <em>decide</em>, <em>decision</em> and <em>decisive</em>.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming every word with similar letters has the same root.</li>
<li>Assuming related words have identical meanings.</li>
<li>Using a noun where a verb or adjective is required.</li>
<li>Guessing a word''s full meaning from its root without considering context.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1484, 1484, '<h2>Compound Words</h2>
<p>A <strong>compound word</strong> is formed when two or more words combine to express a particular meaning. The meaning may be closely related to the parts, or it may be more specialised.</p>

<h3>1. Closed compounds</h3>
<p>Closed compounds are written as one word.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>classroom</strong>, <strong>football</strong>, <strong>toothbrush</strong>, <strong>sunlight</strong>.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The learners left their books in the classroom.</p>

<h3>2. Open compounds</h3>
<p>Open compounds are written as separate words but function together as a unit.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>bus stop</strong>, <strong>post office</strong>, <strong>swimming pool</strong>, <strong>high school</strong>.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The bus stopped at the bus stop near the market.</p>

<h3>3. Hyphenated compounds</h3>
<p>Hyphenated compounds use a hyphen between their parts.</p>
<p>Examples: <strong>mother-in-law</strong>, <strong>well-known</strong>, <strong>part-time</strong>.</p>
<p>Some compounds are hyphenated in one grammatical position but written differently in another. Follow accepted spelling and consistent style.</p>

<h3>4. Compound meaning</h3>
<p>The meaning of a compound is not always a simple sum of its parts.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> A <strong>greenhouse</strong> is a structure used for growing plants. It is not simply any house that is green.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> A <strong>blackboard</strong> is a board for writing on, although it does not have to be black.</p>

<h3>5. Compound nouns, adjectives and verbs</h3>
<p>Compounds can belong to different word classes.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Noun:</strong> classroom, bus stop, toothbrush.</li>
<li><strong>Adjective:</strong> well-known, part-time.</li>
<li><strong>Verb:</strong> to babysit, to proofread.</li>
</ul>
<p>The grammatical role depends on how the compound is used in the sentence.</p>

<h3>6. Plurals and spelling</h3>
<p>Most simple compound nouns form their plural by adding <em>-s</em> or <em>-es</em> in the usual way: classroom → classrooms; toothbrush → toothbrushes. Some compounds form the plural on the main noun within the compound, as in <strong>mothers-in-law</strong>.</p>
<p>Compound spelling is not completely predictable. When uncertain, check a reliable dictionary rather than deciding based only on pronunciation.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Assuming every compound must be written as one word.</li>
<li>Changing the accepted spelling of an open or hyphenated compound.</li>
<li>Interpreting a compound only from the literal meanings of its parts.</li>
<li>Forming irregular compound plurals incorrectly.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1485, 1485, '<h2>Word Classes and Word Forms</h2>
<p>A word''s <strong>word class</strong> describes its grammatical role, such as noun, verb, adjective or adverb. A <strong>word form</strong> is the particular form of a word used in a sentence. The same base can appear in different classes or forms.</p>

<h3>1. Nouns</h3>
<p>Nouns name people, places, things, qualities or ideas.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The <strong>decision</strong> surprised the class.</p>
<p>Here, <em>decision</em> names an outcome or act of deciding, so it functions as a noun.</p>

<h3>2. Verbs</h3>
<p>Verbs express actions, events or states.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The committee will <strong>decide</strong> tomorrow.</p>
<p><em>Decide</em> is a verb because it expresses the action the committee will take.</p>

<h3>3. Adjectives</h3>
<p>Adjectives commonly describe nouns or pronouns.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> It was a <strong>decisive</strong> victory.</p>
<p><em>Decisive</em> describes the victory, so it functions as an adjective.</p>

<h3>4. Adverbs</h3>
<p>Adverbs can modify verbs, adjectives, other adverbs or whole clauses.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The team acted <strong>decisively</strong>.</p>
<p><em>Decisively</em> explains how the team acted, so it functions as an adverb.</p>

<h3>5. Recognising the form a sentence requires</h3>
<p>Look at the words surrounding the blank and identify the grammatical job required.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Article or determiner + noun:</strong> a decision.</li>
<li><strong>Modal verb + base verb:</strong> will decide.</li>
<li><strong>Adjective + noun:</strong> decisive action.</li>
<li><strong>Verb + adverb:</strong> act decisively.</li>
</ul>
<p>These patterns are useful clues, but grammar depends on the whole sentence. An adjective can also follow a linking verb: “The result was decisive.”</p>

<h3>6. The same word can have different classes</h3>
<p>Some words change class depending on how they are used.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “We <strong>clean</strong> the room” uses <em>clean</em> as a verb.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The room is <strong>clean</strong>” uses <em>clean</em> as an adjective.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “We need a <strong>clean</strong>” is not the usual way to express the action or condition; the sentence structure must support the intended class and meaning.</p>

<h3>7. Do not rely on endings alone</h3>
<p>Many adverbs end in <em>-ly</em>, but not all words with that ending are adverbs. <strong>Friendly</strong> and <strong>lonely</strong> are usually adjectives. Similarly, a word ending in <em>-ing</em> may be part of a verb phrase, a noun or an adjective, depending on its use.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Choosing a word because its meaning is correct but its grammatical form is wrong.</li>
<li>Assuming every word ending in -ly is an adverb.</li>
<li>Assuming a word always belongs to one word class.</li>
<li>Ignoring sentence structure when selecting a form from a word family.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1486, 1486, '<h2>Collocations</h2>
<p><strong>Collocations</strong> are words that commonly occur together in natural English. A sentence may be grammatically possible but sound unnatural if it uses an unusual combination of words.</p>

<h3>1. Verb + noun</h3>
<p>Some verbs naturally combine with particular nouns.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>make</strong> a decision, a mistake, an effort.</li>
<li><strong>do</strong> homework, work, research.</li>
<li><strong>take</strong> a break, a photograph, responsibility.</li>
<li><strong>pay</strong> attention, a fine, a visit.</li>
<li><strong>keep</strong> a promise, a record, a secret.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> We made a decision after discussing the evidence.</p>
<p>“Made a decision” is the conventional collocation. “Did a decision” is not standard in this context.</p>

<h3>2. Adjective + noun</h3>
<p>Some adjectives are conventionally paired with particular nouns.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>heavy rain</strong></li>
<li><strong>strong tea</strong></li>
<li><strong>bright future</strong></li>
<li><strong>serious problem</strong></li>
<li><strong>deep sleep</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> Heavy rain flooded several roads.</p>
<p>English speakers do not freely substitute every adjective with a similar meaning. “Strong rain” may be understood, but “heavy rain” is the usual collocation.</p>

<h3>3. Adverb + adjective</h3>
<p>Some adverbs naturally intensify particular adjectives.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>deeply concerned</strong></li>
<li><strong>highly successful</strong></li>
<li><strong>perfectly clear</strong></li>
<li><strong>closely related</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> The two ideas are closely related.</p>

<h3>4. Verb + adverb</h3>
<p>Some combinations are also conventional: <strong>apologise sincerely</strong>, <strong>whisper softly</strong>, <strong>strongly recommend</strong>.</p>
<p>Choose combinations that express the intended meaning and sound natural in the context.</p>

<h3>5. Collocations and grammar</h3>
<p>Collocations do not remove the need for correct grammar. The verb must still agree with its subject, and its tense must suit the sentence.</p>
<p><em>Present:</em> She makes a decision.</p>
<p><em>Past:</em> She made a decision.</p>
<p><em>Future:</em> She will make a decision.</p>

<h3>6. How to learn collocations</h3>
<ol>
<li>Learn a useful phrase rather than an isolated word.</li>
<li>Notice which words occur together in reliable reading and listening materials.</li>
<li>Write your own sentence using the phrase.</li>
<li>Check whether the phrase fits the meaning and level of formality.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Translating word-for-word from another language when English uses a different combination.</li>
<li>Replacing one word in a familiar phrase with a near-synonym that does not collocate naturally.</li>
<li>Assuming that every possible grammatical combination is equally idiomatic.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1487, 1487, '<h2>Phrasal Verbs</h2>
<p>A <strong>phrasal verb</strong> combines a verb with one or more particles, usually an adverb or preposition. The combination may have a meaning that differs from the meaning of the verb alone.</p>

<h3>1. Common phrasal verbs</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>give up</strong> — stop trying or quit: “She refused to give up.”</li>
<li><strong>find out</strong> — discover information: “We found out the cause.”</li>
<li><strong>carry on</strong> — continue: “Carry on with your work.”</li>
<li><strong>look after</strong> — take care of: “He looks after his younger brother.”</li>
<li><strong>put off</strong> — postpone: “They put off the meeting.”</li>
<li><strong>turn down</strong> — reject or reduce the level of something: “She turned down the offer.”</li>
<li><strong>run out of</strong> — use all of a supply: “We ran out of water.”</li>
<li><strong>bring up</strong> — mention a subject or raise a child: “He brought up an important point.”</li>
<li><strong>work out</strong> — solve, calculate or develop successfully: “We worked out the answer.”</li>
<li><strong>set off</strong> — begin a journey or trigger something: “They set off at dawn.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Phrasal verbs can have several meanings</h3>
<p>The surrounding sentence determines which meaning is intended.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “Turn down the radio” means reduce its volume. “Turn down the invitation” means reject it.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “Bring up the issue” means mention it. “Bring up a child” means raise the child.</p>

<h3>3. Separable phrasal verbs</h3>
<p>With some phrasal verbs, the object can appear between the verb and particle or after the particle.</p>
<p><em>Both correct:</em> “She turned down the offer.” / “She turned the offer down.”</p>
<p>When the object is a pronoun, it must normally go between the verb and particle in this pattern.</p>
<p><em>Correct:</em> “She turned it down.”</p>
<p><em>Incorrect:</em> “She turned down it.”</p>

<h3>4. Inseparable phrasal verbs</h3>
<p>Some phrasal verbs do not allow the object to be placed between the verb and particle.</p>
<p><em>Correct:</em> “He looks after his sister.”</p>
<p><em>Incorrect:</em> “He looks his sister after.”</p>
<p>Learn the pattern of each phrasal verb rather than assuming all of them behave alike.</p>

<h3>5. Phrasal verbs with no object</h3>
<p>Some phrasal verbs do not take a direct object.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The plane took off.”</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The children grew up.”</p>

<h3>6. Choosing a phrasal verb</h3>
<p>In many contexts, a phrasal verb has a more formal single-word alternative: <em>find out</em> can mean discover, <em>put off</em> can mean postpone, and <em>carry on</em> can mean continue. Neither form is always better; choose the expression that fits the audience and context.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Guessing the meaning from the verb alone.</li>
<li>Separating an inseparable phrasal verb.</li>
<li>Putting a pronoun after the particle in a separable phrasal verb.</li>
<li>Ignoring context when one phrasal verb has several meanings.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1488, 1488, '<h2>Idioms</h2>
<p>An <strong>idiom</strong> is an established expression whose intended meaning cannot always be worked out from the ordinary meanings of its individual words. Idioms are common in conversation, stories, speeches and informal writing.</p>

<h3>1. Recognising idiomatic meaning</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Break the ice</strong> — make people feel more comfortable at the beginning of an interaction.</li>
<li><strong>Once in a blue moon</strong> — very rarely.</li>
<li><strong>Spill the beans</strong> — reveal information, especially a secret.</li>
<li><strong>Under the weather</strong> — feeling unwell.</li>
<li><strong>A piece of cake</strong> — something very easy to do.</li>
<li><strong>Hit the nail on the head</strong> — identify or explain something exactly.</li>
<li><strong>Burn the midnight oil</strong> — work or study late into the night.</li>
<li><strong>Cost an arm and a leg</strong> — be very expensive.</li>
<li><strong>Let the cat out of the bag</strong> — reveal a secret, often accidentally.</li>
<li><strong>Keep an eye on</strong> — watch or monitor something carefully.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Use context to infer meaning</h3>
<p>Consider the situation and what the speaker is trying to communicate.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The first question was a piece of cake, but the final question was difficult.” The contrast with “difficult” shows that <em>a piece of cake</em> means very easy.</p>

<h3>3. Idioms and literal language</h3>
<p>An expression may have both a literal and an idiomatic interpretation.</p>
<p><em>Literal:</em> The cat escaped from the bag.</p>
<p><em>Idiomatic:</em> She let the cat out of the bag by revealing the surprise party.</p>
<p>In the second sentence, no actual cat is involved; the expression refers to revealing a secret.</p>

<h3>4. Idioms and audience</h3>
<p>Idioms can make speech vivid and natural, but not every audience will understand every expression. In formal reports, technical explanations or instructions, direct wording may be clearer.</p>
<p><em>Idiomatic:</em> We need to get the ball rolling.</p>
<p><em>Direct:</em> We need to begin the activity.</p>

<h3>5. Use idioms accurately</h3>
<p>Learn the complete expression, its meaning and the situations in which it is appropriate. Some idioms allow changes in tense or pronouns, but their wording and grammar still follow conventional patterns.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Interpreting an idiom literally when the context is figurative.</li>
<li>Changing the words of an idiom so much that it no longer sounds natural.</li>
<li>Using an informal idiom in a context that requires precise, formal language.</li>
<li>Assuming every colourful expression is appropriate for every audience.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1489, 1489, '<h2>Commonly Confused Words</h2>
<p>Some English words are frequently confused because they sound alike, look similar or have related meanings. The best way to distinguish them is to identify the meaning and grammatical role required by the sentence.</p>

<h3>1. Their, there and they''re</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Their</strong> shows possession: “Their classroom is clean.”</li>
<li><strong>There</strong> refers to a place or introduces an expression: “Put the books there.” / “There are three books.”</li>
<li><strong>They''re</strong> means “they are”: “They''re ready.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Your and you''re</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Your</strong> shows possession: “Your answer is correct.”</li>
<li><strong>You''re</strong> means “you are”: “You''re correct.”</li>
</ul>
<p>Use the substitution test: if “you are” fits, write <em>you''re</em>; if the sentence identifies something belonging to the listener, write <em>your</em>.</p>

<h3>3. Its and it''s</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Its</strong> is possessive: “The dog wagged its tail.”</li>
<li><strong>It''s</strong> means “it is” or “it has”: “It''s raining.” / “It''s been a long day.”</li>
</ul>
<p>The apostrophe in <em>it''s</em> marks a contraction, not possession.</p>

<h3>4. Then and than</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Then</strong> refers to time, sequence or a consequence: “We ate, then we left.”</li>
<li><strong>Than</strong> is commonly used in comparisons: “This route is shorter than the other one.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Affect and effect</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Affect</strong> is usually a verb meaning to influence: “Lack of sleep can affect concentration.”</li>
<li><strong>Effect</strong> is usually a noun meaning a result: “The effect was noticeable.”</li>
</ul>
<p>These are common patterns, not absolute rules. <em>Effect</em> can also be a verb meaning to bring about, as in “effect a change”, and <em>affect</em> has specialised uses as a noun.</p>

<h3>6. Advice and advise</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Advice</strong> is a noun: “Her advice was helpful.” It is normally uncountable in standard English.</li>
<li><strong>Advise</strong> is a verb: “I advise you to revise regularly.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Lose and loose</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lose</strong> is a verb meaning to misplace something or fail to keep or win it: “Do not lose your notes.”</li>
<li><strong>Loose</strong> is usually an adjective meaning not firmly attached or not tight: “The screw is loose.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>8. Quiet, quite and quit</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Quiet</strong> means making little noise: “The room is quiet.”</li>
<li><strong>Quite</strong> means fairly, rather or completely, depending on context: “The question was quite difficult.”</li>
<li><strong>Quit</strong> means stop or leave: “He decided to quit the activity.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>9. Principal and principle</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Principal</strong> can mean the head of a school or the main person or thing: “The principal addressed the learners.”</li>
<li><strong>Principle</strong> means a rule, belief or fundamental idea: “Honesty is an important principle.”</li>
</ul>

<h3>How to check a confusing word</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the intended meaning.</li>
<li>Identify the grammatical role required.</li>
<li>Try a substitution test where appropriate.</li>
<li>Read the entire sentence again to ensure the word fits.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Choosing a word because it sounds correct rather than checking its meaning.</li>
<li>Confusing contractions with possessive forms.</li>
<li>Assuming a common pattern has no exceptions.</li>
<li>Correcting spelling without checking whether the word fits the sentence.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1490, 1490, '<h2>Meaning in Context</h2>
<p><strong>Meaning in context</strong> is the meaning a word or expression has in the particular sentence or passage where it appears. A word may have several meanings, so choosing a definition without reading the surrounding text can lead to errors.</p>

<h3>1. One word, different meanings</h3>
<p>Consider the word <strong>light</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>“The bag is light.” — not heavy.</li>
<li>“Switch on the light.” — a source of illumination.</li>
<li>“They light the stove.” — set something burning or make it produce light.</li>
</ul>
<p>The same spelling has different grammatical roles and meanings. The sentence tells you which one is intended.</p>

<h3>2. Use surrounding clues</h3>
<p>Writers often provide clues through explanations, examples, contrasts or causes and results.</p>
<p><strong>Definition clue:</strong> “Arid, meaning extremely dry, land supports little vegetation.” The phrase after the comma explains <em>arid</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Contrast clue:</strong> “Unlike his talkative brother, Kamau was reserved and said very little.” The contrast helps explain <em>reserved</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Example clue:</strong> “Nocturnal animals, such as owls and some bats, are active at night.” The examples help explain <em>nocturnal</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Cause-and-effect clue:</strong> “The ground was slippery, so the runner moved cautiously.” The result suggests that <em>cautiously</em> means carefully to avoid danger.</p>

<h3>3. Use grammar as a clue</h3>
<p>The word class and sentence structure can narrow the meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The workers <strong>conducted</strong> an experiment.” Here, <em>conducted</em> means carried out, not led electricity or guided someone physically.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The conductor conducted electricity through the wire.” The nearby words indicate a different meaning.</p>

<h3>4. Infer without guessing wildly</h3>
<p>To infer a word''s meaning:</p>
<ol>
<li>Read the sentence containing the word.</li>
<li>Read the sentence before and after it when necessary.</li>
<li>Identify clues such as examples, contrasts, explanations and results.</li>
<li>Suggest a meaning that fits those clues.</li>
<li>Replace the unfamiliar word with your suggested meaning and check whether the passage still makes sense.</li>
</ol>

<h3>5. Distinguish a likely meaning from a proven meaning</h3>
<p>Context may narrow the possibilities without providing a complete definition. If a passage does not supply enough evidence, avoid claiming more than the text supports. A dictionary can help confirm the meaning.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Using the first dictionary meaning without checking the passage.</li>
<li>Ignoring nearby contrasts or examples.</li>
<li>Confusing a literal meaning with a specialised or figurative meaning.</li>
<li>Inventing a meaning that the passage does not support.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1491, 1491, '<h2>Literal and Figurative Language</h2>
<p><strong>Literal language</strong> communicates the direct, ordinary meaning of words. <strong>Figurative language</strong> uses words imaginatively to create an image, comparison or effect beyond their literal meaning.</p>

<h3>1. Literal meaning</h3>
<p>Literal statements describe what is directly meant by the words.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The child opened the window.”</p>
<p>The sentence reports an action. It does not require the reader to interpret an implied comparison.</p>

<h3>2. Figurative meaning</h3>
<p>Figurative language can make a description more vivid, persuasive or memorable.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The news hit him like a hammer.”</p>
<p>The news did not physically strike him. The comparison suggests that it had a sudden and powerful emotional effect.</p>

<h3>3. Metaphorical expressions</h3>
<p>Many familiar expressions are figurative.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Time is money.</strong> Time is presented as something valuable that should not be wasted.</li>
<li><strong>She has a heart of stone.</strong> The expression suggests that she appears emotionally unfeeling.</li>
<li><strong>The classroom was a zoo.</strong> This suggests disorder or noisy activity, not that the classroom contained actual zoo animals.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Figurative language depends on context</h3>
<p>The same expression may be literal in one situation and figurative in another.</p>
<p><em>Literal:</em> “The river swallowed the small boat” could describe a boat disappearing into a river, though the verb is vivid.</p>
<p><em>Figurative:</em> “The crowd swallowed him up” suggests that the crowd surrounded or obscured him.</p>
<p>Do not label a phrase figurative merely because it sounds dramatic. Ask whether the words are intended literally or to suggest another meaning.</p>

<h3>5. Why writers use figurative language</h3>
<ul>
<li>To create vivid mental pictures.</li>
<li>To communicate an emotion or experience efficiently.</li>
<li>To emphasise an idea.</li>
<li>To make descriptions memorable.</li>
<li>To shape a reader''s attitude towards a person, event or idea.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. Interpreting figurative language</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the expression.</li>
<li>Consider its literal meaning.</li>
<li>Ask whether that literal meaning makes sense in the context.</li>
<li>Infer the idea, feeling or comparison being communicated.</li>
<li>Explain how the expression affects the reader or strengthens the message.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Taking a figurative expression literally when the context indicates otherwise.</li>
<li>Identifying a figure of speech without explaining its meaning.</li>
<li>Giving an interpretation that is not supported by the passage.</li>
<li>Assuming figurative language is always poetic; it also appears in everyday speech and persuasive writing.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1492, 1492, '<h2>Denotation and Connotation</h2>
<p>Words communicate both information and associations. Understanding the difference between <strong>denotation</strong> and <strong>connotation</strong> helps a learner interpret descriptions, recognise bias and choose words carefully when writing.</p>

<h3>1. Denotation: the direct meaning</h3>
<p>The <strong>denotation</strong> of a word is its basic, conventional meaning — what it refers to or describes.</p>
<p>For example, a <strong>snake</strong> is a legless reptile. This is its basic meaning, regardless of whether the speaker likes or fears snakes.</p>
<p>Similarly, <strong>home</strong> refers to the place where someone lives.</p>

<h3>2. Connotation: the associations</h3>
<p>The <strong>connotation</strong> of a word consists of the feelings, attitudes or associations it suggests beyond its basic meaning. These associations may be positive, negative or neutral, and they can vary between people and cultures.</p>
<p>For example, <strong>home</strong> may suggest comfort, belonging, safety or family. These associations are not part of the word''s basic dictionary meaning, but they influence how a reader feels about it.</p>
<p>Calling someone a <strong>snake</strong> may suggest dishonesty or betrayal. The speaker is using the word''s negative associations rather than merely identifying an animal.</p>

<h3>3. Positive, negative and neutral connotations</h3>
<p>Words with similar denotations can create different impressions.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Confident:</strong> often suggests belief in one''s ability.</li>
<li><strong>Arrogant:</strong> suggests excessive self-importance.</li>
<li><strong>Self-assured:</strong> usually suggests confidence and composure.</li>
</ul>
<p>These words can describe related behaviour, but they do not create the same impression of the person.</p>

<h3>4. Word choice changes a description</h3>
<p>Compare these descriptions of the same person:</p>
<p><em>“The politician is persistent.”</em></p>
<p><em>“The politician is stubborn.”</em></p>
<p>Both may describe someone who refuses to change course. <strong>Persistent</strong> often presents that quality positively, while <strong>stubborn</strong> often presents it negatively.</p>
<p>The writer''s choice can influence the reader''s judgement even when the underlying behaviour is similar.</p>

<h3>5. Connotation depends on context</h3>
<p>Connotations are not fixed in every situation. A word that is insulting in one context may be affectionate or humorous in another. Consider the speaker, audience, situation and surrounding sentences before deciding what a word implies.</p>

<h3>6. How to analyse connotation</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the word and its basic meaning.</li>
<li>Consider the feelings or associations it carries in the passage.</li>
<li>Compare it with a more neutral or alternative word.</li>
<li>Explain how the choice influences the reader''s impression.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confusing a word''s basic meaning with the feelings associated with it.</li>
<li>Assuming every reader will respond to a word in exactly the same way.</li>
<li>Calling a word positive or negative without explaining the context.</li>
<li>Ignoring how a writer''s vocabulary may influence the reader''s opinion.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1493, 1493, '<h2>Similes</h2>
<p>A <strong>simile</strong> is a figure of speech that compares two different things, usually using <strong>like</strong> or <strong>as</strong>, to highlight a shared quality.</p>

<h3>1. How a simile works</h3>
<p>Consider: <em>“The runner was as fast as the wind.”</em></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Subject:</strong> the runner.</li>
<li><strong>Comparison:</strong> the wind.</li>
<li><strong>Shared quality:</strong> speed.</li>
</ul>
<p>The sentence does not claim that the runner literally becomes wind. It uses the comparison to emphasise speed.</p>

<h3>2. Common examples</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>As busy as a bee:</strong> very busy or active.</li>
<li><strong>Like a fish out of water:</strong> uncomfortable or out of place in an unfamiliar situation.</li>
<li><strong>As light as a feather:</strong> very light.</li>
<li><strong>Like thunder:</strong> potentially suggests a loud, powerful sound, depending on the sentence.</li>
<li><strong>As cold as ice:</strong> extremely cold, or figuratively emotionally unfeeling.</li>
</ul>
<p>Interpret each comparison using its context. A simile may describe a physical quality, an emotion, a sound or a situation.</p>

<h3>3. Identify the quality being compared</h3>
<p>Finding the word <em>like</em> or <em>as</em> is not enough. Explain what the comparison suggests.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “Her voice was like a gentle stream.”</p>
<p>The comparison may suggest that her voice was calm, smooth or soothing. The surrounding description helps establish the intended effect.</p>

<h3>4. Similes versus literal comparisons</h3>
<p>Not every sentence using <em>like</em> or <em>as</em> is a simile.</p>
<p><em>Literal:</em> “He looks like his father.” This may simply state a resemblance.</p>
<p><em>Simile:</em> “He fought like a lion.” This figuratively compares his courage or fighting spirit with a lion''s.</p>
<p>Look for an imaginative comparison that highlights a quality rather than automatically labelling every use of these words a simile.</p>

<h3>5. Explain the effect of a simile</h3>
<p>A strong interpretation explains both the comparison and its effect on the reader.</p>
<p>For example, saying “as light as a feather” means “light” is incomplete. A better explanation is that the comparison emphasises how little something weighs and helps the reader imagine its lightness.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Identifying the comparison without explaining its meaning.</li>
<li>Assuming every use of ''like'' or ''as'' creates a simile.</li>
<li>Giving an interpretation that the passage does not support.</li>
<li>Explaining the literal objects but missing the quality they share.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1494, 1494, '<h2>Metaphors</h2>
<p>A <strong>metaphor</strong> describes one thing as another to suggest a shared quality or relationship. Unlike a typical simile, it does not explicitly use <em>like</em> or <em>as</em> to make the comparison.</p>

<h3>1. How a metaphor works</h3>
<p>Consider: <em>“Time is a thief.”</em></p>
<p>Time is not literally a person who steals. The metaphor suggests that time takes away opportunities, youth or moments that cannot be recovered.</p>
<p>The comparison invites the reader to think about time through the idea of stealing.</p>

<h3>2. Common metaphorical expressions</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>She is the backbone of the team.</strong> She is essential to the team''s strength and stability.</li>
<li><strong>He carries a heavy burden.</strong> He has a difficult responsibility or emotional problem.</li>
<li><strong>The classroom was a furnace.</strong> The classroom was extremely hot.</li>
<li><strong>Her words were a knife.</strong> Her words caused sharp emotional pain.</li>
<li><strong>Knowledge is a key.</strong> Knowledge can open up opportunities or provide access to understanding.</li>
</ul>
<p>The context may add further meaning. Do not assume that a familiar metaphor has exactly the same effect in every passage.</p>

<h3>3. Identify the two ideas</h3>
<p>When analysing a metaphor, ask:</p>
<ol>
<li>What is being described?</li>
<li>What is it being presented as?</li>
<li>What quality do the two things share?</li>
<li>What impression or emotion does the metaphor create?</li>
</ol>
<p>For example, in <em>“The child was a ray of sunshine in the home”</em>, the child is presented as sunshine. The comparison suggests happiness, warmth or hope.</p>

<h3>4. Metaphor versus simile</h3>
<p><strong>Simile:</strong> “Her determination was like a rock.”</p>
<p><strong>Metaphor:</strong> “Her determination was a rock.”</p>
<p>Both expressions suggest firmness or stability, but the simile states an explicit comparison while the metaphor presents one thing as another.</p>

<h3>5. Metaphors can shape how readers think</h3>
<p>A metaphor does more than decorate a sentence. Describing a challenge as a <em>mountain</em> presents it as difficult to climb; describing it as a <em>puzzle</em> presents it as something that can be solved by finding the right pieces.</p>
<p>The comparison directs attention towards particular qualities and may influence how a reader interprets the situation.</p>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Explaining only the literal objects without identifying the intended comparison.</li>
<li>Confusing a metaphor with a direct factual statement.</li>
<li>Calling every descriptive phrase a metaphor.</li>
<li>Ignoring how a metaphor shapes the reader''s impression.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1495, 1495, '<h2>Personification</h2>
<p><strong>Personification</strong> is a figure of speech that gives human qualities, feelings, actions or intentions to something non-human, such as an animal, object, natural force or abstract idea.</p>

<h3>1. Recognising personification</h3>
<p>Consider: <em>“The wind whispered through the trees.”</em></p>
<p>Wind can produce sounds, but whispering is a human action. The writer presents the wind as if it were a person speaking quietly.</p>
<p>The expression may create an impression of gentleness, secrecy or calmness.</p>

<h3>2. Common examples</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>The sun smiled down on the village.</strong> The sun is presented as smiling, creating a warm or welcoming impression.</li>
<li><strong>The angry storm attacked the coast.</strong> The storm is presented as an angry attacker, emphasising its destructive force.</li>
<li><strong>Opportunity knocked at her door.</strong> Opportunity is presented as a visitor offering a chance.</li>
<li><strong>The old house groaned in the wind.</strong> The house is described as groaning like a person in discomfort.</li>
<li><strong>Fear gripped him.</strong> Fear is presented as something capable of physically holding a person.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Explain the effect</h3>
<p>When analysing personification, identify the non-human subject, the human quality or action, and the effect created.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The city never sleeps.”</p>
<p>The city is presented as a person who remains awake. The expression suggests constant activity, movement or nightlife.</p>

<h3>4. Personification versus ordinary description</h3>
<p>Not every action involving a non-human subject is personification. A river can flow, a tree can grow, and a clock can tick without being given human qualities.</p>
<p>Compare:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Literal:</em> “The wind blew against the door.”</li>
<li><em>Personification:</em> “The wind angrily pounded on the door.”</li>
</ul>
<p>The second description presents the wind as if it had human anger and intention.</p>

<h3>5. Why writers use personification</h3>
<ul>
<li>To make descriptions vivid and memorable.</li>
<li>To create mood or atmosphere.</li>
<li>To make abstract ideas easier to imagine.</li>
<li>To communicate the strength or character of a natural event.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Identifying an action as personification when it is an ordinary non-human action.</li>
<li>Failing to identify the human quality being attributed.</li>
<li>Explaining the image without describing its effect.</li>
<li>Confusing personification with a comparison that does not give human qualities.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1496, 1496, '<h2>Hyperbole</h2>
<p><strong>Hyperbole</strong> is deliberate exaggeration used for emphasis, humour, frustration or emotional effect. It is not normally intended to be interpreted as a precise factual statement.</p>

<h3>1. Recognising exaggeration</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>I''ve told you a thousand times.</strong> The speaker means that something has been repeated many times, not necessarily exactly one thousand times.</li>
<li><strong>This bag weighs a ton.</strong> The speaker means the bag feels extremely heavy.</li>
<li><strong>I waited forever.</strong> The speaker means the wait felt very long.</li>
<li><strong>I''m so hungry I could eat a horse.</strong> The speaker is exaggerating to express extreme hunger.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Hyperbole communicates emotion</h3>
<p>Exaggeration often reveals how a speaker feels rather than describing a measurable fact.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The whole world was against me.”</p>
<p>This may express frustration, loneliness or the feeling of receiving no support. It does not prove that every person in the world opposed the speaker.</p>

<h3>3. Hyperbole versus an ordinary strong statement</h3>
<p>A strong statement is not automatically hyperbole.</p>
<p><em>Ordinary:</em> “The journey took five hours.”</p>
<p><em>Hyperbole:</em> “The journey took a hundred years.”</p>
<p>The second expression deliberately presents an impossible or implausible duration to emphasise how long the journey seemed.</p>

<h3>4. Hyperbole and other figures of speech</h3>
<p>Hyperbole is defined by exaggeration. A sentence may contain more than one figure of speech.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> “The wind was a monster that swallowed the entire town.”</p>
<p>The monster image is metaphorical, while the impossible scale of the wind swallowing an entire town may also be hyperbolic. Explain the specific effect of each feature rather than relying on a label alone.</p>

<h3>5. When hyperbole is appropriate</h3>
<p>Hyperbole can be effective in informal conversation, advertising, humour, creative writing and emotional speech. In scientific reports, official records and factual explanations, exaggerated claims can mislead readers if they are presented as facts.</p>

<h3>6. Interpreting hyperbole</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the statement that is exaggerated.</li>
<li>Explain what would be literally true.</li>
<li>Infer the feeling or idea the speaker intends to emphasise.</li>
<li>Explain the effect on the audience.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common errors</h3>
<ul>
<li>Taking an obvious exaggeration as a literal claim.</li>
<li>Calling any large number hyperbole even when it may be accurate.</li>
<li>Identifying exaggeration without explaining its intended meaning.</li>
<li>Using hyperbole in a context where readers need precise factual information.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1497, 1497, '<h2>Tone and Word Choice</h2>

  <h3>1. What is tone?</h3>
  <p><strong>Tone</strong> is the attitude a speaker or writer communicates towards a subject, a person, or an audience. We recognise tone through the words chosen, the details included, and the way ideas are expressed.</p>

  <p>For example, a writer can describe the same event in different ways:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Appreciative:</strong> "The volunteers worked tirelessly to restore the damaged classroom."</li>
    <li><strong>Critical:</strong> "The volunteers carelessly left the classroom unfinished."</li>
    <li><strong>Neutral:</strong> "The volunteers worked on the damaged classroom."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The event is similar, but the words communicate different attitudes.</p>

  <h3>2. Recognising common tones</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Optimistic:</strong> expresses hope or confidence about the future.</li>
    <li><strong>Pessimistic:</strong> expects an unfavourable outcome.</li>
    <li><strong>Critical:</strong> points out faults or weaknesses.</li>
    <li><strong>Appreciative:</strong> expresses gratitude or admiration.</li>
    <li><strong>Sarcastic:</strong> uses words to mock or criticise, often by saying the opposite of what is meant.</li>
    <li><strong>Concerned:</strong> communicates worry about a situation.</li>
    <li><strong>Persuasive:</strong> attempts to influence the audience''s beliefs or actions.</li>
    <li><strong>Neutral:</strong> presents information without clearly expressing approval or disapproval.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A passage may communicate more than one tone. Choose the description best supported by the evidence rather than forcing every passage into one category.</p>

  <h3>3. How word choice changes meaning</h3>
  <p>Words with similar dictionary meanings can carry different emotional associations.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Confident" suggests belief in one''s ability.</li>
    <li>"Arrogant" suggests excessive self-importance.</li>
    <li>"Economical" often suggests avoiding waste.</li>
    <li>"Stingy" suggests an unwillingness to spend or share.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These differences are important because a writer''s choice can influence how readers judge a person or event.</p>

  <h3>4. How to identify tone accurately</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify what the writer is discussing.</li>
    <li>Notice words that express judgement, emotion, praise, blame, or uncertainty.</li>
    <li>Ask what attitude those words communicate in context.</li>
    <li>Support your interpretation with a quotation or specific detail from the passage.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>5. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not identify tone using only the topic. A passage about a disaster may be factual, sympathetic, critical, or hopeful. The topic alone does not determine the tone.</p>', 1),
  (1498, 1498, '<h2>Ambiguity and Multiple Meanings</h2>

  <h3>1. What is ambiguity?</h3>
  <p><strong>Ambiguity</strong> occurs when a word, phrase, or sentence can reasonably be understood in more than one way. The reader may need additional context to determine the intended meaning.</p>

  <p>Consider: <em>"I saw the man with the telescope."</em></p>
  <p>This could mean that I used a telescope to see the man, or that the man had a telescope. The sentence does not make the intended relationship clear.</p>

  <h3>2. Words with multiple meanings</h3>
  <p>Some individual words have several meanings. The surrounding words and situation usually help identify the intended one.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bank:</strong> a financial institution, or the land beside a river.</li>
    <li><strong>Bat:</strong> a flying mammal, or equipment used in a game such as cricket.</li>
    <li><strong>Light:</strong> illumination, or something that is not heavy.</li>
    <li><strong>File:</strong> a collection of documents, or a tool used to smooth a surface.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For example, in "The batter struck the ball with a bat", the sporting context identifies the intended meaning of <em>bat</em>.</p>

  <h3>3. Types of ambiguity</h3>
  <p><strong>Lexical ambiguity</strong> arises when a word has more than one possible meaning.</p>
  <p>Example: "She sat beside the bank." Without further context, the word <em>bank</em> could refer to a financial institution or a riverbank.</p>

  <p><strong>Structural ambiguity</strong> arises when a sentence''s grammatical arrangement allows more than one interpretation.</p>
  <p>Example: "The teacher discussed the project with the learner."</p>
  <p>This normally means the teacher and learner discussed the project together, but in a different context, the phrase may be understood as identifying a project associated with the learner.</p>

  <h3>4. How context resolves meaning</h3>
  <p>Context includes the surrounding words, the topic, the situation, and what the writer has already explained.</p>
  <p>Compare:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"The fisherman sat on the bank and watched the water." Here, <em>bank</em> means the land beside a river.</li>
    <li>"She deposited her salary at the bank." Here, <em>bank</em> means a financial institution.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5. How to remove ambiguity</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the word or phrase that permits multiple interpretations.</li>
    <li>State the different meanings a reader could reasonably infer.</li>
    <li>Use context to determine the intended meaning, if enough information exists.</li>
    <li>If necessary, rewrite the sentence to make the intended meaning explicit.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>For example, replace "I saw the man with the telescope" with either "Using the telescope, I saw the man" or "I saw the man who was carrying a telescope."</p>

  <h3>6. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Not every word with several dictionary meanings makes a sentence ambiguous. If context clearly establishes the intended meaning, the sentence may be perfectly clear. Ambiguity exists when multiple interpretations remain reasonably possible in that context.</p>', 1),
  (1499, 1499, '<h2>Capitalisation</h2>

  <h3>1. What is capitalisation?</h3>
  <p><strong>Capitalisation</strong> is the use of capital letters where English writing requires them. Correct capitalisation helps readers recognise sentence beginnings, names, titles, and abbreviations.</p>

  <h3>2. Capital letters at the beginning of sentences</h3>
  <p>The first word of a sentence begins with a capital letter.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incorrect: <em>the learners arrived early.</em></li>
    <li>Correct: <em>The learners arrived early.</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>A new sentence also begins with a capital letter after a full stop, question mark, or exclamation mark when the next word starts a new sentence.</p>

  <h3>3. Names of people and places</h3>
  <p>Use capital letters for proper nouns: the specific names of people, places, institutions, and organisations.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>Wanjiku</em>, <em>Kenya</em>, <em>Nairobi</em></li>
    <li><em>Kenyatta University</em></li>
    <li><em>Lake Victoria</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Common nouns are not normally capitalised unless they begin a sentence or form part of an official name.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>We visited a <em>university</em>.</li>
    <li>We visited <em>Kenyatta University</em>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Days, months, languages, and nationalities</h3>
  <p>Capitalise the names of days, months, languages, and nationalities.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>Monday</em>, <em>October</em>, <em>English</em>, <em>Kiswahili</em>, <em>Kenyan</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Seasons such as <em>summer</em> and <em>winter</em> are generally lowercase in ordinary English writing.</p>

  <h3>5. The pronoun ''I''</h3>
  <p>The first-person singular pronoun <em>I</em> is always capitalised, even in the middle of a sentence.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incorrect: <em>My brother and i went home.</em></li>
    <li>Correct: <em>My brother and I went home.</em></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. Titles and abbreviations</h3>
  <p>Capitals are used in personal titles before names, such as <em>Dr Mwangi</em> and <em>Professor Achieng</em>. When a title is used generally, it is usually lowercase: <em>The professor addressed the class.</em></p>
  <p>Many abbreviations and initialisms use capital letters, for example <em>KICD</em> and <em>KCSE</em>. Follow the established spelling of the particular name or abbreviation.</p>

  <h3>7. Editing a sentence</h3>
  <p>Read a sentence and check each capital letter against a rule. Do not capitalise ordinary words merely because they seem important.</p>
  <p>Incorrect: <em>last monday, my Friend and i travelled to nakuru to practise english.</em></p>
  <p>Correct: <em>Last Monday, my friend and I travelled to Nakuru to practise English.</em></p>

  <h3>8. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>In English, not every important-looking noun needs a capital letter. Common nouns such as <em>teacher</em>, <em>school</em>, and <em>river</em> normally remain lowercase unless a specific naming or sentence-position rule applies.</p>', 1),
  (1500, 1500, '<h2>Common Spelling Patterns</h2>

  <h3>1. Why spelling patterns matter</h3>
  <p>English spelling is not perfectly predictable, but recurring patterns help writers spell many words accurately. A pattern is a useful guide, not a guarantee that every word follows the same rule.</p>

  <h3>2. The silent-e pattern</h3>
  <p>In many words, a final silent <em>e</em> changes the sound of the preceding vowel.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>cap → cape</em></li>
    <li><em>kit → kite</em></li>
    <li><em>hop → hope</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>The final <em>e</em> is not pronounced in these words. However, English has exceptions, so do not apply this pattern blindly.</p>

  <h3>3. Doubling a final consonant</h3>
  <p>When adding a vowel-starting suffix such as <em>-ing</em> or <em>-ed</em>, some short words double their final consonant.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>run → running</em></li>
    <li><em>stop → stopped</em></li>
    <li><em>plan → planning</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>This commonly happens when a one-syllable word ends in a consonant–vowel–consonant pattern, as in <em>run</em> and <em>stop</em>. The final consonant is usually not doubled after two vowels or two consonants, as in <em>rain → raining</em> and <em>help → helping</em>.</p>

  <h3>4. Dropping final ''e''</h3>
  <p>When a word ends in silent <em>e</em> and a suffix beginning with a vowel is added, the <em>e</em> is often dropped.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>hope → hoping</em></li>
    <li><em>write → writing</em></li>
    <li><em>make → making</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>With suffixes beginning with a consonant, the final <em>e</em> is often retained: <em>hopeful</em>, <em>careless</em>. There are exceptions, including <em>true → truly</em>.</p>

  <h3>5. Changing ''y'' to ''i''</h3>
  <p>When a word ends in consonant + <em>y</em>, the <em>y</em> often changes to <em>i</em> before a suffix such as <em>-es</em> or <em>-ed</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>city → cities</em></li>
    <li><em>try → tried</em></li>
    <li><em>happy → happier</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Before <em>-ing</em>, the <em>y</em> is usually retained: <em>try → trying</em>. When a word ends in vowel + <em>y</em>, the <em>y</em> is normally retained: <em>play → played</em>.</p>

  <h3>6. ''ie'' and ''ei''</h3>
  <p>Some words use <em>ie</em>, as in <em>believe</em>, and others use <em>ei</em>, as in <em>receive</em>. The familiar guideline ''i before e except after c'' can help with some words, but it has many exceptions. Learn common spellings and check unfamiliar words rather than treating the guideline as universal.</p>

  <h3>7. British and American spelling</h3>
  <p>English has accepted spelling varieties. Kenyan school English generally follows British English conventions.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>British: <em>colour</em>; American: <em>color</em></li>
    <li>British: <em>centre</em>; American: <em>center</em></li>
    <li>British: <em>travelling</em>; American: <em>traveling</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Choose the required variety and use it consistently within a piece of writing.</p>

  <h3>8. A reliable spelling check</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the base word.</li>
    <li>Identify the suffix or ending being added.</li>
    <li>Apply the relevant pattern.</li>
    <li>Check for exceptions or accepted spelling variants.</li>
    <li>Read the completed word in context.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>9. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not assume that one spelling rule explains every word. Patterns help you make informed choices, but irregular spellings and exceptions still need to be recognised.</p>', 1);
INSERT INTO public.lesson_versions (id, lesson_id, content, version) VALUES
  (1501, 1501, '<h2>Silent Letters</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a silent letter?</h3>
  <p>A <strong>silent letter</strong> is a letter that appears in a word''s spelling but is not pronounced in that word. Silent letters are part of English spelling history and must often be learned through word patterns and practice.</p>

  <h3>2. Silent ''k''</h3>
  <p>The letter <em>k</em> is silent in many words beginning with <em>kn-</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>know</em></li>
    <li><em>knee</em></li>
    <li><em>knife</em></li>
    <li><em>knock</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>In these words, pronunciation begins with the sound represented by <em>n</em>, not a separate <em>k</em> sound.</p>

  <h3>3. Silent ''w''</h3>
  <p>The letter <em>w</em> is silent in many words beginning with <em>wr-</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>write</em></li>
    <li><em>wrong</em></li>
    <li><em>wrist</em></li>
    <li><em>wrap</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>These words begin with the sound represented by <em>r</em>.</p>

  <h3>4. Silent ''b''</h3>
  <p>The letter <em>b</em> is silent in some words, particularly after <em>m</em> at the end of a word.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>lamb</em></li>
    <li><em>thumb</em></li>
    <li><em>comb</em></li>
    <li><em>climb</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>The final <em>b</em> is not pronounced in these examples. However, <em>b</em> is pronounced in words such as <em>number</em> and <em>habit</em>.</p>

  <h3>5. Silent ''l''</h3>
  <p>The letter <em>l</em> is silent in several common words.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>walk</em></li>
    <li><em>talk</em></li>
    <li><em>half</em></li>
    <li><em>could</em></li>
    <li><em>should</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Notice that a spelling pattern can occur in several words, but pronunciation should still be checked word by word.</p>

  <h3>6. Silent ''gh''</h3>
  <p>The letter combination <em>gh</em> is silent in words such as:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>night</em></li>
    <li><em>light</em></li>
    <li><em>daughter</em></li>
    <li><em>though</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>However, <em>gh</em> is pronounced differently in some words, such as <em>laugh</em>. Do not assume that every occurrence of <em>gh</em> is silent.</p>

  <h3>7. Silent ''t''</h3>
  <p>The letter <em>t</em> is silent in words such as:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>listen</em></li>
    <li><em>castle</em></li>
    <li><em>whistle</em></li>
    <li><em>fasten</em></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>8. How to learn silent letters</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Look at the spelling of the word.</li>
    <li>Say the word naturally rather than pronouncing every letter separately.</li>
    <li>Identify which written letter or letter combination is not heard.</li>
    <li>Compare the word with other examples of the same pattern.</li>
    <li>Practise the word in a sentence so its spelling and pronunciation become familiar.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>9. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse a silent letter with a letter that represents a sound different from its usual sound. A silent letter is not pronounced at all in that word. Also, pronunciation can vary by accent, so use the pronunciation expected in the variety of English you are learning.</p>', 1),
  (1502, 1502, '<h2>Confused Spellings</h2>

  <h3>1. Why these words cause mistakes</h3>
  <p>Some English words sound alike, look alike, or differ by only one or two letters. Their meanings and grammatical roles may be different. To choose the correct word, examine what the sentence means and how the word functions.</p>

  <h3>2. Their, there and they''re</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Their</strong> shows possession: "The learners collected their books."</li>
    <li><strong>There</strong> refers to a place or introduces a statement: "Put the bag there." "There are three books."</li>
    <li><strong>They''re</strong> means "they are": "They''re ready for the examination."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Test the sentence by replacing <em>they''re</em> with <em>they are</em>. If the sentence no longer makes sense, you probably need another word.</p>

  <h3>3. Your and you''re</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your</strong> shows possession: "Your answer is correct."</li>
    <li><strong>You''re</strong> means "you are": "You''re improving."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Remember that the apostrophe in <em>you''re</em> marks omitted letters. It does not show possession.</p>

  <h3>4. Its and it''s</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Its</strong> shows possession: "The dog wagged its tail."</li>
    <li><strong>It''s</strong> means "it is" or "it has": "It''s raining." "It''s been a long day."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This pair is especially important because possessive <em>its</em> does not use an apostrophe, unlike many possessive nouns.</p>

  <h3>5. Then and than</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Then</strong> refers to time, sequence, or a consequence: "Finish your work, then submit it."</li>
    <li><strong>Than</strong> is used in comparisons: "This exercise is harder than the previous one."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. Affect and effect</h3>
  <p><strong>Affect</strong> is usually a verb meaning to influence. <strong>Effect</strong> is usually a noun meaning a result.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Lack of sleep can affect concentration."</li>
    <li>"One effect of lack of sleep is poor concentration."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are useful general rules, although both words have less common uses in other grammatical roles.</p>

  <h3>7. Lose and loose</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lose</strong> is a verb meaning to misplace something or fail to win: "Do not lose your notes."</li>
    <li><strong>Loose</strong> is an adjective meaning not firmly attached or not tight: "The screw is loose."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>8. Quiet, quite and quit</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Quiet:</strong> making little noise. "The room was quiet."</li>
    <li><strong>Quite:</strong> to a considerable degree, or fairly, depending on context. "The test was quite difficult."</li>
    <li><strong>Quit:</strong> to stop doing something. "She decided to quit the activity."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>9. A reliable correction method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the complete sentence for meaning.</li>
    <li>Identify the grammatical role required.</li>
    <li>Substitute a simple definition or expansion, such as "they are".</li>
    <li>Choose the word that fits both the meaning and grammar.</li>
    <li>Read the corrected sentence again.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>10. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not select a word simply because it sounds right. When words sound alike, meaning and grammatical role are more reliable than pronunciation.</p>', 1),
  (1503, 1503, '<h2>Full Stops and Question Marks</h2>

  <h3>1. What punctuation does</h3>
  <p>Punctuation helps readers recognise sentence boundaries and understand how statements and questions are intended to work. A full stop and a question mark perform different jobs.</p>

  <h3>2. Full stops</h3>
  <p>A <strong>full stop</strong> (.) normally ends a complete statement.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"The lesson begins at eight o''clock."</li>
    <li>"We revised the topic carefully."</li>
    <li>"The learners completed their work."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A full stop also ends many commands written as ordinary instructions: "Close the door." Use an exclamation mark instead when a strong emotion or forceful exclamation is intended.</p>

  <h3>3. Question marks</h3>
  <p>A <strong>question mark</strong> (?) normally ends a direct question.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Where is your exercise book?"</li>
    <li>"Have you completed the assignment?"</li>
    <li>"Why did the experiment fail?"</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Questions can begin with words such as <em>who</em>, <em>what</em>, <em>where</em>, <em>when</em>, <em>why</em>, and <em>how</em>, or with an auxiliary verb such as <em>is</em>, <em>do</em>, or <em>have</em>.</p>

  <h3>4. Direct and indirect questions</h3>
  <p>A direct question asks something directly and normally ends with a question mark.</p>
  <p>Direct: "Where is the library?"</p>
  <p>An indirect question reports or embeds a question within a statement. It does not automatically take a question mark.</p>
  <p>Indirect: "She asked where the library was."</p>
  <p>Compare: "Can you tell me where the library is?" This whole sentence is a direct question, so it ends with a question mark.</p>

  <h3>5. Do not use punctuation to hide an incomplete sentence</h3>
  <p>A full stop does not turn an incomplete thought into a complete sentence.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incomplete: "Because the rain was heavy."</li>
    <li>Complete: "Because the rain was heavy, the match was postponed."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The first example is a subordinate clause without the main clause needed to complete its meaning in ordinary formal writing.</p>

  <h3>6. Avoid repeated punctuation</h3>
  <p>In formal school writing, use one appropriate question mark or full stop rather than strings such as "What happened??" or "The lesson is over...."</p>

  <h3>7. A practical check</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify whether the sentence makes a statement, asks a direct question, or gives an instruction.</li>
    <li>Check that the sentence expresses a complete thought where required.</li>
    <li>Choose the correct ending punctuation.</li>
    <li>Read the sentence aloud to check whether its structure matches its purpose.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>8. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>A sentence that contains a question word is not always a direct question. For example, "I know why she left." is a statement and ends with a full stop.</p>', 1),
  (1504, 1504, '<h2>Commas</h2>

  <h3>1. What a comma does</h3>
  <p>A <strong>comma</strong> (,) separates certain parts of a sentence to make its structure and meaning easier to follow. It marks a smaller boundary than a full stop; it does not normally separate two complete sentences by itself.</p>

  <h3>2. Commas in lists</h3>
  <p>Use commas to separate items in a list of three or more.</p>
  <p>Example: "We packed books, pens, rulers and exercise books."</p>
  <p>A comma before the final item, called the Oxford comma, is optional in many styles. It can be useful when it prevents confusion. Follow the required style consistently.</p>

  <h3>3. Commas after introductory elements</h3>
  <p>A comma often follows an introductory word, phrase, or subordinate clause.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"After the lesson, the learners went home."</li>
    <li>"However, the experiment produced a different result."</li>
    <li>"When the rain stopped, the match resumed."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The comma helps the reader identify where the introductory element ends and the main clause begins.</p>

  <h3>4. Commas in direct address</h3>
  <p>Use commas to separate the name or title of the person being addressed.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Amina, please read the passage."</li>
    <li>"Please read the passage, Amina."</li>
    <li>"Please, Amina, read the passage carefully."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Without commas, direct address can become confusing or change how a sentence is interpreted.</p>

  <h3>5. Commas around extra information</h3>
  <p>Use paired commas around non-essential information that can be removed without destroying the sentence''s basic identification of the person or thing.</p>
  <p>"Kisumu, which lies beside Lake Victoria, attracts many visitors."</p>
  <p>The phrase "which lies beside Lake Victoria" adds information but is not necessary to identify Kisumu in this sentence.</p>
  <p>Do not automatically place commas around information that identifies which person or thing is meant.</p>
  <p>"Learners who revise regularly often improve."</p>
  <p>Here, "who revise regularly" identifies the group being discussed, so commas are not normally used.</p>

  <h3>6. Commas between adjectives</h3>
  <p>Commas can separate coordinate adjectives—adjectives that independently describe the same noun.</p>
  <p>"It was a difficult, exhausting journey."</p>
  <p>A useful test is whether you can insert <em>and</em> between the adjectives or reverse their order naturally. Do not use a comma automatically between every pair of adjectives.</p>

  <h3>7. Commas with quotations and dates</h3>
  <p>In many British English styles, a comma may introduce direct speech or separate a reporting clause from the quotation:</p>
  <p>"The teacher said, ''Check your answers carefully.''"</p>
  <p>Commas can also separate elements of a date when the format requires them. For example, in "On 9 October 2026, the class met again", the comma separates the introductory date phrase from the main clause.</p>

  <h3>8. The comma splice</h3>
  <p>A <strong>comma splice</strong> occurs when two independent clauses are joined using only a comma.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incorrect: "The bell rang, the learners entered the classroom."</li>
    <li>Correct: "The bell rang, and the learners entered the classroom."</li>
    <li>Correct: "The bell rang. The learners entered the classroom."</li>
    <li>Correct: "The bell rang; the learners entered the classroom."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>9. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>Do not put a comma wherever you pause while speaking. Punctuation depends on sentence structure and meaning, not only on breathing or pauses.</p>', 1),
  (1505, 1505, '<h2>Apostrophes</h2>

  <h3>1. What an apostrophe does</h3>
  <p>An <strong>apostrophe</strong> ('') is mainly used to show possession or to mark omitted letters in contractions. It is not normally used simply to make a word plural.</p>

  <h3>2. Apostrophes in contractions</h3>
  <p>A contraction combines words by omitting one or more letters. The apostrophe marks the omission.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>do not → don''t</em></li>
    <li><em>cannot → can''t</em></li>
    <li><em>they are → they''re</em></li>
    <li><em>it is → it''s</em></li>
    <li><em>we have → we''ve</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Contractions are common in conversation and informal writing. In formal school essays and official letters, the full forms may be more appropriate, depending on the task.</p>

  <h3>3. Singular possession</h3>
  <p>Use an apostrophe followed by <em>s</em> to show possession by a singular noun, including most singular nouns ending in <em>s</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>the learner''s book</em> — the book belonging to one learner</li>
    <li><em>the teacher''s desk</em> — the desk associated with one teacher</li>
    <li><em>James''s pen</em> — the pen belonging to James</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Some style guides use different conventions for names ending in <em>s</em>; follow the required style consistently.</p>

  <h3>4. Plural possession</h3>
  <p>For regular plural nouns ending in <em>s</em>, place the apostrophe after the final <em>s</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>the learners'' books</em> — books belonging to multiple learners</li>
    <li><em>the teachers'' meeting</em> — a meeting for or involving multiple teachers</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For irregular plural nouns that do not end in <em>s</em>, add apostrophe + <em>s</em>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>the children''s uniforms</em></li>
    <li><em>the men''s room</em></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5. Distinguishing possession from a plural</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><em>One student''s notebook</em> — possession by one student.</li>
    <li><em>Several students'' notebooks</em> — possession by several students.</li>
    <li><em>Several students arrived</em> — plural only; no apostrophe is needed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ask two questions: Is the noun simply plural, or does it show ownership or association? If it shows possession, identify whether the possessor is singular or plural.</p>

  <h3>6. Its and it''s</h3>
  <p><strong>Its</strong> is possessive: "The school changed its timetable."</p>
  <p><strong>It''s</strong> is a contraction of <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em>: "It''s ready." "It''s been repaired."</p>
  <p>Do not add an apostrophe to <em>its</em> merely because it shows possession.</p>

  <h3>7. Apostrophes and dates or numbers</h3>
  <p>Do not normally use apostrophes to make ordinary plurals.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incorrect: <em>book''s for sale</em></li>
    <li>Correct: <em>books for sale</em></li>
    <li>Incorrect: <em>1990''s</em> when simply referring to the decade</li>
    <li>Correct: <em>1990s</em></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Apostrophes may appear in special cases for clarity, such as pluralising a single letter: "Mind your p''s and q''s." This is an exception, not a general plural rule.</p>

  <h3>8. A reliable editing method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Check whether letters have been omitted in a contraction.</li>
    <li>If ownership is intended, identify the possessor.</li>
    <li>Decide whether the possessor is singular, a regular plural ending in <em>s</em>, or an irregular plural.</li>
    <li>Place the apostrophe accordingly.</li>
    <li>Remove apostrophes that incorrectly mark ordinary plurals.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>9. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>An apostrophe does not mean ''more than one''. Compare <em>teacher''s</em> (one teacher possesses something), <em>teachers''</em> (multiple teachers possess something), and <em>teachers</em> (plural only).</p>', 1),
  (1506, 1506, '<h2>Colons and Semicolons</h2>

  <h3>1. The colon and semicolon</h3>
  <p>A <strong>colon</strong> (:) often introduces an explanation, example, or list. A <strong>semicolon</strong> (;) can link closely related independent clauses or separate complex items in a list.</p>
  <p>They are not interchangeable. The punctuation should reflect the relationship between the ideas.</p>

  <h3>2. Colons before lists</h3>
  <p>A colon can introduce a list when the words before it form a complete introductory statement.</p>
  <p>Example: "Bring three items to the lesson: a ruler, a pencil and an exercise book."</p>
  <p>The words before the colon form a complete statement: "Bring three items to the lesson."</p>
  <p>Avoid placing a colon directly between a verb or preposition and the items it introduces.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incorrect: "The items are: pens, books and rulers."</li>
    <li>Better: "The items are pens, books and rulers."</li>
    <li>Also correct: "Bring the following items: pens, books and rulers."</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Colons before explanations</h3>
  <p>A colon can introduce an explanation, conclusion, or detail that clarifies the preceding statement.</p>
  <p>"The result was unexpected: the plant grew faster in the shaded area."</p>
  <p>The second part explains what made the result unexpected.</p>

  <h3>4. Colons in formal writing</h3>
  <p>Colons can introduce a quotation or formal statement after a complete introductory clause, depending on the style and context.</p>
  <p>"The notice gave one instruction: ''Submit all assignments by Friday.''"</p>
  <p>They also appear in conventional formats such as times (8:30) and certain headings or labels.</p>

  <h3>5. Semicolons between independent clauses</h3>
  <p>A semicolon can join two independent clauses that are closely related in meaning.</p>
  <p>"The rain had stopped; the match resumed."</p>
  <p>Both parts can stand as complete sentences. The semicolon shows a close relationship without using a coordinating conjunction.</p>

  <h3>6. Semicolons with linking adverbs</h3>
  <p>When a linking adverb connects two independent clauses, a semicolon often comes before it and a comma after it.</p>
  <p>"The first method was unreliable; therefore, we tested another."</p>
  <p>Other linking expressions include <em>however</em>, <em>consequently</em>, and <em>nevertheless</em>.</p>

  <h3>7. Semicolons in complex lists</h3>
  <p>Use semicolons to separate list items that already contain commas, especially when each item contains a location or other internal detail.</p>
  <p>"The group visited Embu, Kenya; Arusha, Tanzania; and Kampala, Uganda."</p>
  <p>The semicolons make the boundaries between the three items clear.</p>

  <h3>8. Choosing between punctuation marks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Use a colon when the second part introduces or explains what came before.</li>
    <li>Use a semicolon to link closely related independent clauses.</li>
    <li>Use a semicolon to separate complex list items containing internal commas.</li>
    <li>Use a full stop when the ideas should be separated into distinct sentences.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>9. Common mistake</h3>
  <p>A semicolon should not normally join an independent clause to a dependent clause that cannot stand alone. A colon should not be inserted after every introductory phrase. First check the grammar of the words before the punctuation mark.</p>', 1),
  (1507, 1507, '<h2>Quotation Marks</h2>

  <h3>1. What quotation marks do</h3>
  <p>Quotation marks show the exact words spoken or written by someone, or identify certain titles and special uses of words. In British English, single quotation marks are commonly used for direct speech, although double quotation marks are also accepted by many style guides. Be consistent.</p>

  <h3>2. Direct speech</h3>
  <p>Direct speech reproduces a speaker''s exact words.</p>
  <p>Example: Amina said, ''I have completed my assignment.''</p>
  <p>The reporting clause identifies the speaker, while the quoted words reproduce what the speaker said.</p>

  <h3>3. Punctuation with direct speech</h3>
  <p>When a reporting clause comes before a statement, a comma commonly introduces the quotation.</p>
  <p>''The examination begins at nine,'' the teacher said.</p>
  <p>When the reporting clause comes after the quotation, the comma usually appears inside the closing quotation mark in this style.</p>
  <p>''The examination begins at nine,'' the teacher said.</p>
  <p>A direct question keeps its question mark inside the quotation marks when the quoted words are a question.</p>
  <p>The teacher asked, ''Have you revised the topic?''</p>
  <p>If the entire sentence is a question but the quotation itself is a statement, the question mark may belong outside the quotation marks, depending on the sentence''s structure and the style used.</p>

  <h3>4. Breaking direct speech</h3>
  <p>A reporting clause can interrupt a sentence of direct speech.</p>
  <p>''If you practise daily,'' the coach said, ''your performance will improve.''</p>
  <p>The first part is not a complete sentence on its own, so the punctuation reflects that the quotation continues after the reporting clause.</p>

  <h3>5. Direct speech and reported speech</h3>
  <p>Direct speech gives the exact words: Peter said, ''I am tired.''</p>
  <p>Reported speech gives the meaning without quoting the exact words: Peter said that he was tired.</p>
  <p>Reported speech does not normally require quotation marks around the reported statement.</p>

  <h3>6. Quotation marks around titles and words</h3>
  <p>Quotation marks may be used for short works, such as a poem, article, short story, or individual chapter, depending on the required style.</p>
  <p>They may also identify a word being discussed rather than used in its ordinary meaning: The word ''their'' shows possession.</p>

  <h3>7. Quoting accurately</h3>
  <p>When quoting a source, reproduce its wording accurately. Do not silently change the original wording while presenting it as an exact quotation. If you paraphrase, use your own words instead.</p>

  <h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Forgetting the opening or closing quotation mark.</li>
    <li>Changing the words inside a quotation without indicating the change.</li>
    <li>Using quotation marks around reported speech unnecessarily.</li>
    <li>Switching between single and double quotation styles without a reason.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (1508, 1508, '<h2>Hyphens and Dashes</h2>

  <h3>1. Hyphens and dashes are different</h3>
  <p>A <strong>hyphen</strong> (-) is short and commonly joins parts of a word or compound expression. A <strong>dash</strong> (— or –) is longer and can mark a break, interruption, or additional information. Their functions are different.</p>

  <h3>2. Hyphens in compound modifiers</h3>
  <p>A hyphen can join words that work together as a modifier before a noun.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>a well-known author</li>
    <li>a ten-year-old learner</li>
    <li>a part-time job</li>
    <li>a carefully planned lesson</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Notice that not every group of words needs a hyphen. Adverbs ending in ''-ly'' are generally not hyphenated with the adjective they modify: ''a carefully planned lesson''.</p>

  <h3>3. Hyphens with numbers and fractions</h3>
  <p>Hyphens are often used in written compound numbers from twenty-one to ninety-nine.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>twenty-one</li>
    <li>forty-six</li>
    <li>ninety-nine</li>
  </ul>
  <p>They can also appear in fractions used as compound modifiers: ''a two-thirds majority''. Style and context may affect the exact convention.</p>

  <h3>4. Hyphens can prevent confusion</h3>
  <p>Compare:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>re-sign</em> means to sign again.</li>
    <li><em>resign</em> means to leave a position or job.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A hyphen can help distinguish words that would otherwise look alike.</p>

  <h3>5. Dashes for additional information</h3>
  <p>A dash can set off an explanation or interruption that the writer wants to emphasise.</p>
  <p>''The class had one goal—to understand the method fully.''</p>
  <p>''The final result—after several corrections—was accurate.''</p>
  <p>When a pair of dashes encloses extra information, both dashes are needed unless the sentence ends at that point.</p>

  <h3>6. Dashes for a sudden break</h3>
  <p>A dash can show an interruption or a sudden change in thought.</p>
  <p>''I was about to submit the assignment—but the power went off.''</p>
  <p>Use this effect deliberately. Too many dashes can make formal writing seem disjointed.</p>

  <h3>7. Choosing the correct mark</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Use a hyphen to join parts of a compound word or modifier where required.</li>
    <li>Use a dash to create a stronger break or set off an explanation.</li>
    <li>Use ordinary punctuation instead when a hyphen or dash is unnecessary.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>
  <p>Do not use a hyphen merely because a phrase contains several words. Also avoid replacing every comma or colon with a dash. Choose punctuation based on grammar, meaning, and the effect intended.</p>', 1),
  (1509, 1509, '<h2>Skimming</h2>

  <h3>1. What skimming means</h3>
  <p><strong>Skimming</strong> is reading quickly to understand the general subject, main idea, organisation, and overall direction of a text. You do not try to understand every sentence or remember every detail.</p>

  <h3>2. When skimming is useful</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Previewing a long passage before answering comprehension questions.</li>
    <li>Deciding whether an article contains information you need.</li>
    <li>Understanding the overall argument before reading closely.</li>
    <li>Reviewing a chapter to recall its main sections.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. What to look at first</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Title:</strong> predict the general subject.</li>
    <li><strong>Headings and subheadings:</strong> identify the main sections.</li>
    <li><strong>Opening paragraph:</strong> look for the topic and central claim.</li>
    <li><strong>First sentences of paragraphs:</strong> identify how ideas develop, while remembering that the main idea can appear elsewhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> look for the overall message or final position.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Use these clues to build a general understanding. Do not assume every passage follows the same structure.</p>

  <h3>4. Example of skimming</h3>
  <p>Read this short passage quickly:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>Many schools are establishing gardens to supplement classroom learning. Learners measure plant growth, observe insects, and investigate the conditions plants need to survive. Gardens can also encourage teamwork and responsibility. However, successful projects require reliable water supplies, suitable land, and regular care.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>A useful skimming summary is: <em>School gardens support practical learning and teamwork, but they need resources and maintenance.</em></p>
  <p>You do not need to memorise every example to understand the passage''s main message.</p>

  <h3>5. Skimming versus reading closely</h3>
  <p>Skimming gives a broad overview. Close reading examines details, evidence, vocabulary, and relationships between ideas.</p>
  <p>For example, skimming may reveal that a passage discusses the benefits and challenges of school gardens. Close reading would help you identify each benefit, the exact resources required, and the evidence used to support the claims.</p>

  <h3>6. How to skim effectively</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Set a purpose: decide what general understanding you need.</li>
    <li>Move through the text quickly, focusing on structure and major ideas.</li>
    <li>Notice repeated terms, contrast words such as ''however'', and conclusion signals.</li>
    <li>State the central idea in one sentence.</li>
    <li>Return to the text for close reading if a question requires specific evidence.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>7. Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Reading every word slowly, which defeats the purpose of skimming.</li>
    <li>Looking only at the title and guessing the message without checking the passage.</li>
    <li>Confusing a minor example with the central idea.</li>
    <li>Using skimming to answer a question that requires an exact date, name, or quotation.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (1510, 1510, '<h2>Scanning</h2>

  <h3>1. What scanning means</h3>
  <p><strong>Scanning</strong> is reading quickly to locate a specific piece of information. Instead of trying to understand the whole passage, you search for a target such as a date, name, number, place, or particular term.</p>

  <h3>2. When scanning is useful</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Finding a date in a notice.</li>
    <li>Locating a person''s name in a report.</li>
    <li>Finding a price in a table or advertisement.</li>
    <li>Locating a particular instruction in a set of procedures.</li>
    <li>Finding the paragraph containing a term needed for a comprehension answer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Decide what you are looking for</h3>
  <p>Before scanning, turn the question into a clear search target.</p>
  <p>Question: ''On what date does registration close?''</p>
  <p>Target: a date associated with registration closing—not any date that happens to appear in the text.</p>

  <h3>4. Use visual clues</h3>
  <p>Move your eyes quickly across the text, looking for the form of information you need.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Dates often contain numbers and month names.</li>
    <li>Names often begin with capital letters.</li>
    <li>Prices may use a currency symbol or a label such as ''KSh''.</li>
    <li>Headings can help narrow the area where an answer is likely to appear.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are search clues, not proof. Always read the surrounding words once you find a possible match.</p>

  <h3>5. Example of scanning</h3>
  <p>Read this notice with a specific question in mind:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p><strong>SCIENCE CLUB REGISTRATION</strong></p>
    <p>Registration opens on 5 October. Learners must submit their forms by 16 October. The first meeting is on 21 October at 3:30 p.m. in the science laboratory. The registration fee is KSh 100.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>Question: What is the registration fee?</p>
  <p>Scan for the fee or currency marker, then read the matching phrase. The answer is <em>KSh 100</em>.</p>

  <h3>6. Scanning versus skimming</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skimming:</strong> find the general message or overall structure.</li>
    <li><strong>Scanning:</strong> locate a specific fact or detail.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you need to know what a passage is mainly about, skim it. If you need the date of a meeting, scan for the date and verify its connection to the meeting.</p>

  <h3>7. How to scan accurately</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the question and identify the exact information required.</li>
    <li>Choose a clue: a keyword, number, name, date, or heading.</li>
    <li>Search the text quickly for that clue.</li>
    <li>Read the surrounding sentence to confirm the information answers the question.</li>
    <li>Give the answer in the requested form and include necessary units or context.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Choosing the first number found without checking what it refers to.</li>
    <li>Confusing the date registration opens with the date it closes.</li>
    <li>Searching for a word without considering synonyms or alternative wording.</li>
    <li>Copying extra information instead of answering the exact question.</li>
  </ul>', 1),
  (1511, 1511, '<h2>Finding the Main Idea</h2>
<p>The <strong>main idea</strong> is the central point a passage communicates. It tells you what the passage is mainly about and what the writer wants you to understand about it.</p>

<h3>1. Topic versus main idea</h3>
<p>The <strong>topic</strong> is the subject being discussed. The <strong>main idea</strong> makes a complete statement about that subject.</p>
<ul>
<li>Topic: School gardens.</li>
<li>Main idea: School gardens help learners develop practical skills while producing fresh vegetables.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. How to identify the main idea</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the whole passage rather than focusing on one sentence.</li>
<li>Identify the subject that appears throughout the passage.</li>
<li>Ask what the writer mainly wants to say about that subject.</li>
<li>Check whether your answer explains the passage as a whole, not just one detail.</li>
</ol>

<h3>3. Where the main idea appears</h3>
<p>The main idea may appear at the beginning, at the end, or across several sentences. Sometimes, the reader must combine the information to express it in one sentence.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>At Kijani School, learners turned an unused patch of land into a vegetable garden. They learnt how to prepare soil, plant seedlings and conserve water. The school kitchen now uses some of the vegetables, while learners sell the surplus to raise money for gardening tools.</em></p>
<p><strong>Topic:</strong> The school garden.</p>
<p><strong>Main idea:</strong> The school garden gives learners practical experience and provides vegetables and income for the school.</p>
<p><strong>Why?</strong> This statement connects the passage''s main benefits instead of mentioning only planting or selling vegetables.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not confuse an interesting detail with the main idea. A single sentence about selling vegetables does not capture the whole passage.</p>', 1),
  (1512, 1512, '<h2>Understanding Supporting Details</h2>
<p><strong>Supporting details</strong> are the facts, examples, explanations or descriptions that develop a main idea. They help readers understand why the main idea matters or how it is supported.</p>

<h3>1. Types of supporting details</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Facts:</strong> Information that can be checked or verified.</li>
<li><strong>Examples:</strong> Specific cases that illustrate a point.</li>
<li><strong>Explanations:</strong> Information showing how or why something happens.</li>
<li><strong>Descriptions:</strong> Details that help readers picture a person, place or situation.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence:</strong> Information used to support a claim, such as relevant observations or reliable data.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. How details support a main idea</h3>
<p>A main idea makes a broad point. Supporting details explain, demonstrate or provide evidence for that point.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>Reading regularly can improve a learner''s language skills. Encountering new words helps learners expand their vocabulary. Following different sentence patterns exposes them to varied ways of expressing ideas. Discussing what they read can also strengthen comprehension.</em></p>
<p><strong>Main idea:</strong> Regular reading can improve language skills.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting details:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Reading introduces learners to new vocabulary.</li>
<li>Reading exposes learners to different sentence patterns.</li>
<li>Discussing reading can strengthen comprehension.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Selecting relevant details</h3>
<p>A detail is relevant when it directly explains or supports the main idea. An interesting fact that does not contribute to the main point may be irrelevant.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not treat every sentence as equally important. Look at the relationship between each detail and the central point.</p>', 1),
  (1513, 1513, '<h2>Finding Explicit Information</h2>
<p><strong>Explicit information</strong> is information stated directly in a text. The reader does not need to infer it, although the information may need to be located, compared or put into their own words.</p>

<h3>1. What explicit information looks like</h3>
<p>A passage may directly state a person''s name, a date, a place, a reason, an event or a sequence of actions. Questions may ask who, what, when, where, why or how.</p>

<h3>2. A reliable method</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the question carefully and identify the information requested.</li>
<li>Locate the relevant sentence or section in the passage.</li>
<li>Check that the words you select answer the exact question.</li>
<li>Answer clearly, using your own words when appropriate without changing the meaning.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>The environmental club met at the school hall on Thursday afternoon. Its members collected litter around the playing field and planted twelve seedlings near the library. The deputy head teacher supplied the seedlings.</em></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Where did the club meet?</strong> At the school hall.</li>
<li><strong>When did it meet?</strong> On Thursday afternoon.</li>
<li><strong>How many seedlings were planted?</strong> Twelve.</li>
<li><strong>Who supplied the seedlings?</strong> The deputy head teacher.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Answer only what is asked</h3>
<p>If a question asks for a place, give the place. If it asks for two reasons, identify two reasons that the passage actually states. Avoid adding unsupported information.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not replace what the passage says with what you think probably happened. Explicit-information questions test accurate reading, not guessing.</p>', 1),
  (1514, 1514, '<h2>Reading Between the Lines</h2>
<p>An <strong>inference</strong> is a conclusion a reader reaches by combining clues in a text with reasonable knowledge. <strong>Implied meaning</strong> is what a writer communicates indirectly rather than stating openly.</p>

<h3>1. Evidence plus reasoning</h3>
<p>A sound inference has two parts:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Textual evidence:</strong> The words, actions or details the writer provides.</li>
<li><strong>Reasoning:</strong> What those details reasonably suggest.</li>
</ul>
<p>An inference should be supported by the passage. It is not a guess based only on personal opinion.</p>

<h3>2. Worked example</h3>
<p><em>When the final bell rang, Njeri remained at her desk, checking each answer on her examination paper. She only packed her bag after the teacher announced that all scripts had been collected.</em></p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> How did Njeri approach her examination?</p>
<p><strong>Evidence:</strong> She checked every answer and stayed until the scripts were collected.</p>
<p><strong>Inference:</strong> She was careful and wanted to review her work before submitting it.</p>
<p>The passage does not directly call Njeri careful; her actions support that conclusion.</p>

<h3>3. Inferring feelings and attitudes</h3>
<p>Writers may suggest feelings through actions, speech, facial expressions or descriptions of behaviour. For example, a character who avoids eye contact, speaks quietly and repeatedly checks the door may be nervous, depending on the surrounding context.</p>

<h3>4. Inferring causes and outcomes</h3>
<p>Use the events described to work out a likely reason or consequence. Keep the conclusion within the limits of the evidence.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not confuse a possible explanation with a proven one. If several explanations fit the clues, acknowledge uncertainty or choose the one best supported by the text.</p>', 1),
  (1515, 1515, '<h2>Using Context to Understand Words</h2>
<p><strong>Context clues</strong> are hints in the surrounding words, sentences or passage that help a reader work out the meaning of an unfamiliar word or expression.</p>

<h3>1. Definition or explanation clues</h3>
<p>The text explains the unfamiliar word directly.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> A <strong>nocturnal</strong> animal, one that is active at night, often rests during the day.</p>
<p>The explanation tells us that <em>nocturnal</em> means active at night.</p>

<h3>2. Synonym or restatement clues</h3>
<p>A nearby word or phrase expresses a similar meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The instructions were <strong>concise</strong>; they were brief and used very few words.</p>

<h3>3. Contrast clues</h3>
<p>A contrasting idea helps reveal the meaning. Words such as <em>but</em>, <em>although</em>, <em>unlike</em> and <em>however</em> may signal contrast.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Unlike his <strong>timid</strong> brother, who rarely spoke in public, Joel readily addressed the crowd. The contrast suggests that <em>timid</em> means shy or lacking confidence.</p>

<h3>4. Example clues</h3>
<p>Examples show the kind of thing a word describes.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> Citrus fruits, such as oranges, lemons and limes, contain vitamin C. These examples help explain the category <em>citrus fruits</em>.</p>

<h3>5. Cause-and-effect clues</h3>
<p>An action or result may help reveal a word''s meaning.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> The path was <strong>slippery</strong> after the rain, so several walkers moved cautiously to avoid falling. The risk of falling helps explain the meaning of <em>slippery</em>.</p>

<h3>6. A careful method</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the sentence and the sentences around it.</li>
<li>Look for explanations, similar words, contrasts, examples or results.</li>
<li>Suggest a meaning that fits the context.</li>
<li>Replace the unfamiliar word with your suggested meaning and check whether the sentence still makes sense.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Important limitation</h3>
<p>Context clues do not always reveal an exact meaning. If the passage does not provide enough information, use a reliable dictionary or ask for clarification rather than pretending to know.</p>', 1),
  (1516, 1516, '<h2>Understanding Why a Text Was Written</h2>
<p>An author''s <strong>purpose</strong> is the reason a writer creates a text. Understanding the purpose helps you distinguish information from persuasion, entertainment and instructions.</p>

<h3>1. Common purposes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>To inform:</strong> Give facts, explanations or information.</li>
<li><strong>To persuade:</strong> Convince readers to accept an idea or take action.</li>
<li><strong>To entertain:</strong> Engage readers through stories, humour, suspense or interesting experiences.</li>
<li><strong>To instruct:</strong> Explain how to do something through directions or steps.</li>
<li><strong>To describe:</strong> Create a clear picture of a person, place, object or experience.</li>
<li><strong>To express feelings:</strong> Communicate personal emotions, reactions or experiences.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. How to identify purpose</h3>
<p>Do not identify the purpose from the topic alone. Two texts about farming may have different purposes: one may explain how crops grow, while another urges farmers to adopt a particular method.</p>
<ol>
<li>Identify what the writer mainly does.</li>
<li>Examine the language, structure and details used.</li>
<li>Ask what the writer wants readers to know, feel or do.</li>
<li>Support your answer with evidence from the text.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>Every household should plant at least one tree this rainy season. Trees help reduce soil erosion and provide shade. Join Saturday''s community planting exercise and bring a spade.</em></p>
<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To persuade readers to plant trees and participate in the community exercise.</p>
<p><strong>Evidence:</strong> The writer says ''Every household should'' and directly invites readers to participate.</p>

<h3>3. More than one purpose</h3>
<p>A text can have several purposes. A story may entertain while also teaching a lesson. When a question asks for the main purpose, identify the one most strongly supported by the text.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not answer only with the subject. ''Trees'' is a topic, not a purpose. State what the writer is trying to achieve.</p>', 1),
  (1517, 1517, '<h2>Identifying Audience and Tone</h2>
<p>The <strong>audience</strong> is the person or group a text is intended for. The <strong>tone</strong> is the writer''s attitude towards the subject or audience, as revealed through word choice and expression.</p>

<h3>1. Identifying the audience</h3>
<p>Consider the text''s purpose, vocabulary, assumed knowledge, level of formality and format.</p>
<ul>
<li>A school notice may address learners, teachers or parents.</li>
<li>A technical guide may address people who already understand a specialised subject.</li>
<li>A children''s story may use simple language and familiar situations.</li>
<li>A formal letter to a government office normally uses respectful, carefully structured language.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Recognising tone</h3>
<p>Tone can be formal, informal, respectful, enthusiastic, concerned, critical, humorous, sarcastic, sympathetic or reassuring, among others.</p>
<p>Choose a tone that the words actually support. Do not label a text ''angry'' merely because it discusses a serious problem.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Text A:</strong> <em>Kindly submit your completed forms to the office by Friday.</em></p>
<p><strong>Text B:</strong> <em>Get those forms in by Friday, or we will have a problem!</em></p>
<p>Both texts address the submission of forms, but Text A uses a polite, formal tone, while Text B sounds forceful and potentially threatening.</p>

<h3>3. Support your interpretation</h3>
<p>When asked about audience or tone, name the most likely audience or attitude and quote or describe the language that supports your answer. The same text may suit more than one audience, so use the strongest clues.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Audience and tone are different. ''Parents'' may be the audience; ''respectful'' may describe the tone.</p>', 1),
  (1518, 1518, '<h2>Distinguishing Facts from Opinions</h2>
<p>A <strong>fact</strong> is a statement that can be checked against evidence. An <strong>opinion</strong> expresses a judgement, belief, preference or evaluation.</p>

<h3>1. Recognising facts</h3>
<p>Facts can often be verified using records, reliable observations, measurements or other evidence.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> ''The school library opens at 8.00 a.m.'' This is verifiable by checking the library''s opening arrangements.</p>

<h3>2. Recognising opinions</h3>
<p>Opinions may contain value judgements or preferences, often expressed through words such as <em>best</em>, <em>worst</em>, <em>beautiful</em>, <em>boring</em> or <em>should</em>.</p>
<p><em>Example:</em> ''The school library is the most inspiring place in the county.'' The claim depends on a judgement about what is inspiring.</p>

<h3>3. Important distinctions</h3>
<ul>
<li>A sentence is not automatically an opinion because it contains a number. The number may be verifiable.</li>
<li>A sentence is not automatically a fact because it sounds confident.</li>
<li>Opinions can be supported by facts, but the supporting facts do not turn every judgement into an objective fact.</li>
<li>A statement about what should happen often expresses a recommendation or value judgement.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>The school planted 120 trees last year. Tree planting is the most worthwhile activity a school can organise.</em></p>
<p>The first sentence is a fact if school records can verify the number. The second is an opinion because ''most worthwhile'' expresses a judgement.</p>

<h3>4. Checking a statement</h3>
<p>Ask: Could reliable evidence establish whether this statement is accurate? Does it express a personal judgement or preference? Some statements contain both factual and opinion-based elements, so examine each claim carefully.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not confuse an unsupported claim with an opinion in every case. A claim about an event may be factual in nature but still require evidence before you can accept it.</p>', 1),
  (1519, 1519, '<h2>Recognising Bias and Persuasive Language</h2>
<p><strong>Bias</strong> is a tendency to present a person, group, event or issue in a way that favours one side or viewpoint. <strong>Persuasive language</strong> is language chosen to influence what readers think, feel or do.</p>

<h3>1. How bias can appear</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Selective information:</strong> Including evidence that supports one side while leaving out relevant opposing evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Loaded words:</strong> Using emotionally strong terms to make a person or idea seem admirable or terrible.</li>
<li><strong>One-sided descriptions:</strong> Presenting only the advantages or only the disadvantages of an issue.</li>
<li><strong>Unfair generalisations:</strong> Making broad claims about a whole group from limited examples.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Persuasive techniques</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Emotive language:</strong> Appeals to feelings, such as ''innocent families'' or ''a shocking waste''.</li>
<li><strong>Rhetorical questions:</strong> Questions used to make readers consider a point rather than simply request an answer.</li>
<li><strong>Repetition:</strong> Repeating words or ideas for emphasis.</li>
<li><strong>Direct address:</strong> Speaking to readers using ''you'' or ''your''.</li>
<li><strong>Calls to action:</strong> Direct requests such as ''Join us'' or ''Act today''.</li>
<li><strong>Appeals to authority or popularity:</strong> Referring to experts or claiming that many people agree. Such appeals still need to be assessed carefully.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>Only a careless community would ignore this dangerous rubbish problem. Join our clean-up on Saturday and prove that you care about your children.</em></p>
<p>The words ''careless'' and ''dangerous'' create an emotional response. ''Prove that you care'' pressures readers by linking participation to their identity as caring people. The text promotes a clean-up but uses emotionally forceful language.</p>

<h3>3. Analyse rather than merely label</h3>
<p>To explain bias or persuasion, identify the specific words or omitted information, describe their likely effect, and explain how they support the writer''s position.</p>

<h3>4. Keep a critical mind</h3>
<p>Persuasion is not automatically dishonest, and a strong opinion is not automatically unfair. Check the evidence, consider alternative viewpoints and distinguish emotional appeal from proof.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not assume a text is biased merely because it takes a position. Bias is more convincingly identified through unfair selection, loaded presentation or insufficient consideration of relevant evidence.</p>', 1),
  (1520, 1520, '<h2>Testing Whether a Claim Is Well Supported</h2>
<p>A <strong>claim</strong> is a statement that someone asks readers to accept as true or reasonable. <strong>Evidence</strong> is information used to support or challenge that claim. Good readers examine both before deciding what to believe.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the claim</h3>
<p>Find the main assertion being made. For example: ''Providing a quiet study room improves learners'' concentration.'' This is a claim because it makes an assertion that can be investigated.</p>

<h3>2. Examine the evidence</h3>
<p>Evidence may include relevant measurements, records, observations, examples, expert findings or research results. Ask:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Relevance:</strong> Does the evidence address the claim?</li>
<li><strong>Reliability:</strong> Is the source in a position to provide accurate information?</li>
<li><strong>Sufficiency:</strong> Is there enough evidence to justify the conclusion?</li>
<li><strong>Currency:</strong> Is the information recent enough for the issue?</li>
<li><strong>Fairness:</strong> Have important limitations or alternative explanations been considered?</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Correlation and causation</h3>
<p>When two things happen together, one does not necessarily cause the other. If learners who attend a study group achieve higher marks, that alone does not prove that the study group caused the improvement. Other factors, such as prior preparation, may also matter.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>A school claims that its new reading programme improves comprehension. It reports that average comprehension scores increased after the programme began.</em></p>
<p><strong>What the evidence supports:</strong> Scores increased after the programme began.</p>
<p><strong>What remains uncertain:</strong> Whether the programme caused the increase. To assess this, we would need further information, such as how scores were measured, whether the learners changed in other ways and whether a suitable comparison was available.</p>

<h3>4. Reach a proportionate conclusion</h3>
<p>Do not accept a claim merely because it is confidently stated or accompanied by a number. Do not reject it automatically either. Decide whether the available evidence strongly supports it, partly supports it or leaves it uncertain.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>A source can be reliable but still provide evidence that is insufficient for a particular claim. Always assess both the source and the evidence itself.</p>', 1),
  (1521, 1521, '<h2>Finding the Information That Matters</h2>
<p><strong>Key information</strong> is the information a reader needs to understand the central message, answer a question or complete a task. It is more important than background details that do not affect the purpose.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the purpose first</h3>
<p>The information that matters depends on what you are trying to find out.</p>
<ul>
<li>For an examination date, the date, time and venue may be essential.</li>
<li>For a scientific explanation, the process, conditions and results may matter most.</li>
<li>For a story, the important information may include the main characters, problem, actions and outcome.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. A practical method</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify the question or purpose of the reading.</li>
<li>Look for information that directly answers it.</li>
<li>Distinguish essential facts from examples, repetition and background.</li>
<li>Check that the selected information is accurate and complete enough for the task.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>The school science exhibition will take place on Friday, 16 October, in the main hall from 9.00 a.m. to 1.00 p.m. Learners presenting projects must arrive by 8.15 a.m. to arrange their displays. The hall was painted last year, and the exhibition committee meets every Wednesday.</em></p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> When and where will the exhibition take place?</p>
<p><strong>Key information:</strong> Friday, 16 October, in the main hall.</p>
<p>The arrival time matters if you are presenting a project, but it is not necessary to answer this particular question. The painting and committee meeting details are unrelated to the requested information.</p>

<h3>3. Do not confuse key information with short information</h3>
<p>A long explanation may contain the essential reason for an event, while a short date or number may be irrelevant to your question. Importance depends on purpose, not length.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not copy every fact simply because it appears in the passage. Select information that answers the task while preserving any essential conditions or qualifications.</p>', 1),
  (1522, 1522, '<h2>Separating Main Points from Details</h2>
<p>A <strong>main point</strong> is an important idea that a passage develops. A <strong>supporting detail</strong> explains, illustrates or provides evidence for that point. A longer passage may contain several main points, each supported by its own details.</p>

<h3>1. How the two relate</h3>
<p>Think of a passage as a structure:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Main point:</strong> The central statement in a section.</li>
<li><strong>Supporting details:</strong> Examples, explanations, facts or reasons that develop it.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Find the main points</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the entire passage to understand its overall subject.</li>
<li>Look for ideas that organise or connect several sentences.</li>
<li>Group details that explain the same idea.</li>
<li>Give each group a short heading or write its main point in a sentence.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>School libraries support learning in several ways. They give learners access to books and other information resources. They also provide quiet spaces for reading and research. In addition, librarians can guide learners towards suitable materials and help them develop research skills.</em></p>
<p><strong>Main point:</strong> School libraries support learning in several ways.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting details:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>They provide access to learning resources.</li>
<li>They offer quiet spaces for reading and research.</li>
<li>Librarians guide learners and help develop research skills.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Passages with several main points</h3>
<p>A passage about school libraries might have separate sections on resources, study spaces and research guidance. Each section can have its own main point, while all three contribute to the overall message that libraries support learning.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not list examples as if they were all equally important ideas. First identify the broader point that connects them, then attach each detail to the point it supports.</p>', 1),
  (1523, 1523, '<h2>Choosing Information That Answers the Task</h2>
<p><strong>Relevant information</strong> directly helps answer a question, explain an idea or complete a task. Irrelevant information may be true and interesting, but it does not help with the purpose at hand.</p>

<h3>1. Relevance depends on the question</h3>
<p>The same fact can be relevant to one question and irrelevant to another. A person''s age may matter in a biography but not in a question asking where an event took place.</p>

<h3>2. Test each detail</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Connection:</strong> Does it address the question or main point?</li>
<li><strong>Usefulness:</strong> Does it help explain, prove or clarify the point?</li>
<li><strong>Necessity:</strong> Would removing it leave an important gap in the answer?</li>
<li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> Is it supported by the text or a reliable source?</li>
</ul>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><em>A community plans to reduce plastic litter near a river. Organisers propose placing labelled bins at busy points, holding clean-up activities and educating residents about proper disposal. The river is also a popular place for birdwatching, and the nearby market sells fresh fruit every morning.</em></p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> What actions are proposed to reduce plastic litter?</p>
<p><strong>Relevant information:</strong> Placing labelled bins, holding clean-up activities and educating residents about proper disposal.</p>
<p>The birdwatching and market details do not answer the question.</p>

<h3>3. Keep important qualifications</h3>
<p>Do not remove words that limit or qualify a claim. For example, ''some filters remove certain impurities'' is not equivalent to ''filters remove all impurities''. Accurate selection preserves the original meaning.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not select a detail merely because it repeats a word from the question. Check whether it actually answers the question being asked.</p>', 1),
  (1524, 1524, '<h2>Making Writing More Concise</h2>
<p>Removing unnecessary details means taking out information that does not contribute meaningfully to the purpose, while preserving the central message and all essential facts.</p>

<h3>1. What can be removed?</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Repetition:</strong> Restating the same idea without adding meaning.</li>
<li><strong>Irrelevant background:</strong> Details that do not help answer the question.</li>
<li><strong>Unnecessary description:</strong> Extra adjectives or explanations that do not serve the purpose.</li>
<li><strong>Unneeded examples:</strong> Examples that repeat a point already made clearly.</li>
<li><strong>Wordy expressions:</strong> Phrases that can be replaced with shorter, equally accurate wording.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. What must remain?</h3>
<p>Keep the main idea, essential supporting details, important names or figures, and qualifications that affect meaning. Concise writing should not become vague or inaccurate.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Wordy version:</strong> <em>Due to the fact that the road was flooded with water, the bus was unable to continue moving forward along the road.</em></p>
<p><strong>Concise version:</strong> <em>The bus could not continue because the road was flooded.</em></p>
<p>The shorter version preserves the cause and result while removing redundant phrases such as ''due to the fact that'' and ''moving forward along the road''.</p>

<h3>3. A reliable editing method</h3>
<ol>
<li>State the purpose of the passage in one sentence.</li>
<li>Mark the facts needed to preserve that purpose.</li>
<li>Remove repeated or unrelated material.</li>
<li>Replace wordy expressions with clear alternatives.</li>
<li>Read the shortened version to check accuracy, clarity and completeness.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not remove a detail merely because it is long. A condition, reason or warning may be essential even when it takes several words to express.</p>', 1),
  (1525, 1525, '<h2>Restating Ideas in Your Own Words</h2>
<p><strong>Paraphrasing</strong> means expressing information or an idea from a source using your own wording and sentence structure while preserving its original meaning.</p>

<h3>1. Why paraphrase?</h3>
<p>Paraphrasing helps you demonstrate understanding, make difficult information clearer and use source material in your own writing. It is not simply replacing a few words with synonyms.</p>

<h3>2. A reliable method</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Understand:</strong> Read the original until you understand the whole idea.</li>
<li><strong>Identify:</strong> Note the essential meaning, facts and relationships.</li>
<li><strong>Restate:</strong> Put the idea into your own sentence structure and wording.</li>
<li><strong>Compare:</strong> Check that your version preserves the original meaning and qualifications.</li>
<li><strong>Acknowledge:</strong> When using another person''s ideas in academic work, give the required citation or acknowledgement.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Original:</strong> <em>Regular physical activity can improve mood and help people manage stress.</em></p>
<p><strong>Paraphrase:</strong> <em>Being active consistently may help people feel better and cope with stress.</em></p>
<p>The wording and sentence structure differ, but the main meaning remains. The word ''may'' also preserves the original idea that these are possible benefits, not guaranteed results.</p>

<h3>3. Preserve accuracy</h3>
<p>Do not change a possibility into a certainty, reverse a cause and effect, omit a key condition or add a claim the source does not make.</p>

<h3>4. Paraphrasing versus summarising</h3>
<p>A paraphrase restates a particular idea or passage in different words and may be similar in length to the original. A summary gives only the main points and is usually shorter.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Changing just a few words while keeping the original sentence structure may be too close to the source. First understand the idea, then write it afresh.</p>', 1),
  (1526, 1526, '<h2>Combining Related Ideas</h2>
<p>Combining ideas means joining related information to express a complete thought clearly and efficiently. It helps writers avoid repetitive sentences and show relationships between ideas.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the relationship</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Addition:</strong> Both ideas contribute information. Use words such as <em>and</em>, <em>also</em> and <em>furthermore</em>.</li>
<li><strong>Contrast:</strong> The ideas differ. Use <em>but</em>, <em>although</em> or <em>whereas</em>.</li>
<li><strong>Cause and effect:</strong> One idea explains why another happens. Use <em>because</em>, <em>so</em> or <em>therefore</em> appropriately.</li>
<li><strong>Condition:</strong> One event depends on another. Use <em>if</em> or <em>unless</em>.</li>
<li><strong>Sequence:</strong> Events occur in an order. Use <em>before</em>, <em>after</em> or <em>then</em>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Worked examples</h3>
<p><strong>Addition:</strong> The learners planted trees. They watered the seedlings.</p>
<p><em>Combined:</em> The learners planted trees and watered the seedlings.</p>
<p><strong>Contrast:</strong> The road was narrow. The bus passed safely.</p>
<p><em>Combined:</em> Although the road was narrow, the bus passed safely.</p>
<p><strong>Cause and effect:</strong> The river flooded. The footbridge became inaccessible.</p>
<p><em>Combined:</em> The footbridge became inaccessible because the river flooded.</p>

<h3>3. Preserve meaning</h3>
<p>Choose a joining word that expresses the actual relationship. Do not use <em>because</em> when the sentences merely describe two events that happened together. Keep important conditions and avoid changing who did what.</p>

<h3>4. Avoid comma splices</h3>
<p>Do not join two complete sentences with only a comma.</p>
<p>Incorrect: <em>The rain stopped, the learners returned outside.</em></p>
<p>Correct: <em>The rain stopped, so the learners returned outside.</em></p>
<p>You can also use a full stop or a semicolon where appropriate.</p>', 1),
  (1527, 1527, '<h2>Writing an Accurate Summary</h2>
<p>A <strong>summary</strong> is a shorter version of a passage that preserves its most important ideas. It leaves out minor details, repeated points and examples that are not necessary for understanding the central message.</p>

<h3>1. What a good summary does</h3>
<ul>
<li>Includes the main points relevant to the task.</li>
<li>Uses clear, concise wording.</li>
<li>Preserves the original meaning and important qualifications.</li>
<li>Uses your own words where appropriate.</li>
<li>Avoids personal opinions and information not supported by the source.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. A reliable method</h3>
<ol>
<li>Read the entire passage to understand its message.</li>
<li>Identify the main idea and essential supporting points.</li>
<li>Remove examples, repetition and irrelevant details.</li>
<li>Combine related points into a logical paragraph or list.</li>
<li>Check that the summary is shorter and still accurate.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Original passage:</strong> <em>Many learners travel to school by walking. Walking costs no fare and can provide daily physical activity. However, learners who live far away may become tired, arrive late or face unsafe roads. Communities can improve their journeys by organising safe walking groups and identifying dangerous sections of roads.</em></p>
<p><strong>Summary:</strong> Walking is an affordable way for learners to reach school and get exercise, but long or unsafe journeys can cause difficulties. Communities can improve safety through walking groups and by identifying dangerous road sections.</p>
<p>The summary retains the benefits, problems and suggested responses while removing less essential wording.</p>

<h3>3. Follow the question''s focus</h3>
<p>If asked to summarise the problems, focus on the problems. If asked for solutions, include the proposed solutions. A general summary and a question-focused summary may contain different information.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Copying the entire passage with only a few words changed.</li>
<li>Including minor examples while missing the main point.</li>
<li>Adding personal opinions or invented information.</li>
<li>Making the summary so short that essential meaning disappears.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1528, 1528, '<h2>Turning Information into Useful Notes</h2>
<p><strong>Note-making</strong> is the process of selecting and arranging important information from a source so it can be reviewed or used later. Good notes make relationships between ideas easy to see.</p>

<h3>1. Select before you write</h3>
<p>Identify the purpose of your notes. Notes for revising a process may need ordered steps, while notes for comparing two ideas may need similarities and differences.</p>

<h3>2. Common ways to organise notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Headings and subheadings:</strong> Group information under main topics and smaller related points.</li>
<li><strong>Numbered lists:</strong> Show stages or actions that must occur in sequence.</li>
<li><strong>Bullets:</strong> Present related points without implying a fixed order.</li>
<li><strong>Tables:</strong> Compare features across categories.</li>
<li><strong>Flow diagrams:</strong> Show stages, decisions or cause-and-effect relationships.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Use concise language</h3>
<p>Notes can use short phrases, familiar abbreviations and symbols, provided you understand them later. Keep technical terms, essential definitions, important figures and necessary conditions.</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Source information:</strong> Plants need suitable light, water and mineral nutrients to grow. Roots absorb water and minerals from the soil. Leaves capture light, which plants use during photosynthesis. Too little water can cause wilting.</p>
<p><strong>Organised notes:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Plant growth requirements</strong>
  <ul>
  <li>Light — energy for photosynthesis.</li>
  <li>Water — absorbed by roots; shortage may cause wilting.</li>
  <li>Mineral nutrients — absorbed from soil through roots.</li>
  </ul>
</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Make relationships clear</h3>
<p>Place related ideas together and use indentation to show which details belong under each main point. Keep a sequence in the correct order when order affects meaning.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not copy an entire paragraph and call it note-making. Notes should be selective and organised, but not so abbreviated that the meaning becomes unclear.</p>', 1),
  (1529, 1529, '<h2>Planning Before Writing</h2>
<p>A <strong>composition plan</strong> is an organised outline of the ideas, events or arguments you intend to develop in a piece of writing. Planning helps you stay focused, arrange ideas logically and avoid repeating the same point.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the task</h3>
<p>Read the question carefully. Identify the required form, topic, purpose, audience and any limits or instructions.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Narrative:</strong> Plan characters, setting, conflict, key events and resolution.</li>
<li><strong>Descriptive:</strong> Choose the subject and organise sensory details or features.</li>
<li><strong>Expository:</strong> Arrange explanations and facts in a logical order.</li>
<li><strong>Argumentative:</strong> State a position, develop reasons, consider opposing views where appropriate and reach a conclusion.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Build a simple outline</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Introduction:</strong> Introduce the subject, situation or central argument.</li>
<li><strong>Body:</strong> Arrange the main ideas or events in a sensible order.</li>
<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Resolve the story, summarise the explanation or reinforce the argument.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Worked example: Argumentative composition</h3>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Should schools provide time for independent reading?</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Position:</strong> Yes, schools should provide regular independent-reading time.</li>
<li><strong>Reason 1:</strong> It gives learners regular practice with written English.</li>
<li><strong>Reason 2:</strong> It exposes learners to new vocabulary and ideas.</li>
<li><strong>Reason 3:</strong> It allows learners to explore subjects of personal interest.</li>
<li><strong>Possible concern:</strong> Timetables are already crowded.</li>
<li><strong>Response:</strong> Short, planned sessions can fit into the school day.</li>
<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Regular reading time is worthwhile when organised effectively.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Check your plan</h3>
<p>Make sure every main point answers the question, the order is logical and you have enough information to develop each point. A plan is a guide, not a requirement to write every point exactly as drafted.</p>

<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>Do not start writing immediately if the question requires several different points. A brief plan can prevent irrelevant paragraphs and a conclusion that introduces an entirely new argument.</p>', 1),
  (1530, 1530, '<h2>Writing Strong Topic Sentences</h2>
<p>A <strong>topic sentence</strong> states the main point of a paragraph. It helps readers understand what the paragraph will explain, describe or argue.</p>

<h3>1. What makes a strong topic sentence?</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clear focus:</strong> It identifies the paragraph''s central idea.</li>
<li><strong>Suitable scope:</strong> It is neither too broad nor too narrow for the paragraph.</li>
<li><strong>Development:</strong> It allows supporting sentences to explain, illustrate or justify it.</li>
<li><strong>Relevance:</strong> It directly contributes to the composition''s overall purpose.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Topic versus topic sentence</h3>
<p><strong>Topic:</strong> School gardens.</p>
<p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> School gardens provide learners with practical opportunities to develop agricultural skills.</p>
<p>The topic names a subject. The topic sentence makes a complete statement about that subject.</p>

<h3>3. Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> Reading regularly helps learners develop stronger language skills.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting sentences:</strong> Reading introduces unfamiliar words in meaningful contexts. It also exposes learners to different sentence structures. Discussing a text can further improve their understanding of what they read.</p>
<p>Each supporting sentence develops the topic sentence. A sentence about the colour of the school library would not belong unless the paragraph connected it to the main point.</p>

<h3>4. Where topic sentences appear</h3>
<p>A topic sentence often appears at the beginning of a paragraph, but it can also appear later. Some paragraphs develop their main idea through a sequence of details, with the central point becoming clear near the end.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Writing a sentence so broad that the paragraph cannot develop it adequately.</li>
<li>Making a sentence too specific to support several explanatory sentences.</li>
<li>Introducing several unrelated ideas in one topic sentence.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1531, 1531, '<h2>Supporting Sentences</h2>
<p>Supporting sentences develop the idea introduced by a topic sentence. They explain, provide evidence, give examples, or describe details that help the reader understand the main point.</p>

<h3>1. Four ways to support a topic sentence</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Explanation:</strong> clarifies why or how the main point is true.</li>
  <li><strong>Example:</strong> gives a specific instance that illustrates the point.</li>
  <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> provides relevant facts, observations, or reliable information.</li>
  <li><strong>Detail:</strong> adds specific information that makes the idea clearer.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Worked example</h3>
<p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> Reading regularly improves a learner''s language skills.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting sentence 1 — explanation:</strong> Regular reading exposes learners to new vocabulary and correct sentence patterns.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting sentence 2 — example:</strong> For instance, a learner who reads different stories may discover unfamiliar words and learn how they are used.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting sentence 3 — result:</strong> With repeated exposure, the learner can use suitable words and construct clearer sentences when writing.</p>

<h3>3. Develop rather than repeat</h3>
<p>A supporting sentence should add something useful. Repeating the topic sentence in different words does not develop the paragraph.</p>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> Reading regularly improves language because reading often makes language better.</p>
<p><strong>Stronger:</strong> Reading regularly introduces learners to varied vocabulary, which helps them express ideas more precisely.</p>

<h3>4. Keep support relevant</h3>
<p>Every supporting sentence should help explain or establish the paragraph''s main point. Interesting information that does not support that point belongs elsewhere.</p>
<p><strong>Check:</strong> Ask, “How does this sentence help the reader understand or believe the topic sentence?” If there is no clear answer, revise or remove it.</p>', 1),
  (1532, 1532, '<h2>Unity in a Paragraph</h2>
<p>A paragraph has unity when every sentence contributes to one central idea. The topic sentence introduces that idea, while the supporting sentences explain, illustrate or establish it.</p>

<h3>1. How unity works</h3>
<p>Consider a paragraph about the benefits of school gardens. Relevant sentences might explain how gardens provide practical learning, encourage responsibility and improve the appearance of the school. A sentence about a learner''s favourite musician would break the unity unless the paragraph clearly connected it to the garden topic.</p>

<h3>2. Find the central idea first</h3>
<p>Before judging a sentence, state the paragraph''s main point in one clear sentence. Then compare each supporting sentence with that point.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Keep:</strong> sentences that explain, give evidence for or illustrate the central idea.</li>
  <li><strong>Revise:</strong> sentences that could be relevant but have an unclear connection.</li>
  <li><strong>Remove or relocate:</strong> sentences that introduce a different subject.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Example of a paragraph without unity</h3>
<p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> Preparing for examinations requires a consistent study routine.</p>
<p><strong>Relevant support:</strong> A timetable helps learners allocate time to different subjects. Short revision sessions also make it easier to review difficult concepts regularly.</p>
<p><strong>Unrelated sentence:</strong> The school football team has new uniforms.</p>
<p>The last sentence introduces a different topic and should be removed or placed in a paragraph about school sports.</p>

<h3>4. Unity is not the same as repetition</h3>
<p>A paragraph can remain focused without saying the same thing repeatedly. Each sentence should contribute a new reason, example, explanation or useful detail that supports the central idea.</p>

<h3>5. Final unity check</h3>
<p>Read the topic sentence, then read each supporting sentence. Ask whether the paragraph would become less clear or less convincing if that sentence were removed. If a sentence has no meaningful connection to the central idea, revise it, remove it or move it to a more suitable paragraph.</p>', 1),
  (1533, 1533, '<h2>Coherence and Cohesion</h2>
<p>Coherence and cohesion help readers follow ideas in a paragraph or composition, but they describe different aspects of writing.</p>

<h3>1. Coherence: a logical flow of ideas</h3>
<p>Coherence exists when ideas are organised in a sensible order and the reader can understand how they relate.</p>
<p><strong>Unclear order:</strong> The water boiled. I filled the kettle. I switched it on.</p>
<p><strong>Coherent order:</strong> I filled the kettle with water. I switched it on. When the water boiled, I poured it into a cup.</p>

<h3>2. Cohesion: the links between sentences</h3>
<p>Cohesion is created by language that connects ideas across sentences. Common devices include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Connectives:</strong> however, therefore, because, although, meanwhile.</li>
  <li><strong>Pronoun reference:</strong> ''Amina bought a book. She read it that evening.''</li>
  <li><strong>Repetition of key terms:</strong> repeating an important word when needed to keep the subject clear.</li>
  <li><strong>Related vocabulary:</strong> using words with clear links to the topic, such as ''rain'', ''run-off'' and ''drainage''.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Choose connectives by meaning</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Addition:</strong> furthermore, also, in addition.</li>
  <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> however, although, nevertheless.</li>
  <li><strong>Cause and result:</strong> because, therefore, consequently.</li>
  <li><strong>Sequence:</strong> first, next, finally.</li>
  <li><strong>Example:</strong> for example, for instance.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not choose a connective only because it sounds formal. Its meaning must match the relationship between the ideas.</p>

<h3>4. Avoid weak connections</h3>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> The road was flooded. Therefore, it had rained heavily.</p>
<p>This may be a possible explanation, but ''therefore'' presents the rain as a conclusion established by the flooding. A more careful version is: ''The road was flooded, possibly because it had rained heavily.''</p>
<p><strong>Another weak link:</strong> ''Kendi lost her notebook. This was expensive.'' The word ''this'' has an unclear reference: losing the notebook is not necessarily expensive.</p>
<p><strong>Clearer:</strong> ''Kendi lost her notebook. Replacing it cost her money.''</p>

<h3>5. Revision method</h3>
<p>First check coherence: does each idea follow logically from the previous one? Then check cohesion: are the relationships and references between ideas clear? A paragraph can use many connectives and still lack coherence if its ideas are poorly organised.</p>', 1),
  (1534, 1534, '<h2>Introductions and Conclusions</h2>
<p>An introduction prepares the reader for a composition. A conclusion brings the composition to a purposeful close. Both should suit the type of writing and the main idea.</p>

<h3>1. Writing an effective introduction</h3>
<p>An introduction should orient the reader to the subject and establish the direction of the composition. Depending on the task, it may:</p>
<ul>
  <li>introduce the topic directly;</li>
  <li>provide brief background information;</li>
  <li>establish a situation, question or point of view;</li>
  <li>present the main argument in argumentative writing.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Topic:</strong> Why learners should read regularly.</p>
<p><strong>Effective introduction:</strong> Reading is an important habit that helps learners expand their vocabulary and understand different ideas. Making time for reading each day can strengthen both language skills and independent learning.</p>
<p>This introduction introduces the topic and indicates the direction of the composition without trying to explain every point at once.</p>

<h3>2. Avoid weak openings</h3>
<p>Openings such as ''Since the beginning of time...'' or ''I am going to write about...'' often waste words or sound mechanical. Begin with information that is relevant to the task.</p>

<h3>3. Writing an effective conclusion</h3>
<p>A conclusion should bring the main discussion together. It can summarise the central points, reinforce a reasoned position, or show the outcome of a narrative.</p>
<p><strong>Example conclusion:</strong> By reading regularly and choosing suitable material, learners can steadily improve their vocabulary, comprehension and confidence in writing.</p>
<p>A conclusion should not introduce a major new argument that requires its own explanation. In a story, the ending should resolve the main situation or provide a convincing final moment.</p>

<h3>4. Match the ending to the purpose</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Expository:</strong> draw together the explanation''s main points.</li>
  <li><strong>Argumentative:</strong> restate the position and reinforce the strongest reasons.</li>
  <li><strong>Narrative:</strong> resolve the main conflict or reveal the outcome.</li>
  <li><strong>Descriptive:</strong> leave the reader with a fitting final impression of the subject.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Keep the composition connected</h3>
<p>The introduction, body and conclusion should serve the same overall purpose. A conclusion that contradicts the introduction without explaining why makes the composition feel incomplete. Before finishing, check that the opening establishes the right direction and the ending fulfils the reader''s expectations.</p>', 1),
  (1535, 1535, '<h2>Narrative Composition</h2>
<p>A narrative composition tells a story about events involving characters in a particular setting. It may describe real or imagined events, but the story should have a clear sequence and a meaningful outcome.</p>

<h3>1. Core elements of a narrative</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Characters:</strong> the people or other beings involved in the events.</li>
  <li><strong>Setting:</strong> where and when the events occur.</li>
  <li><strong>Plot:</strong> the connected sequence of events.</li>
  <li><strong>Conflict or problem:</strong> the difficulty that creates tension or drives the story.</li>
  <li><strong>Resolution:</strong> how the main problem ends or changes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. A practical story structure</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Orientation:</strong> establish the characters, setting and initial situation.</li>
  <li><strong>Complication:</strong> introduce a problem, unexpected event or challenge.</li>
  <li><strong>Development:</strong> show how the characters respond and how the situation changes.</li>
  <li><strong>Climax:</strong> present the turning point or most intense moment.</li>
  <li><strong>Resolution:</strong> show the outcome and provide a satisfying ending.</li>
</ol>
<p>Not every story must follow this exact pattern, but the reader should be able to follow the events and understand how they connect.</p>

<h3>3. Plan before writing</h3>
<p>Before drafting, decide:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Who is the main character?</li>
  <li>Where and when does the story happen?</li>
  <li>What does the character want or need?</li>
  <li>What goes wrong?</li>
  <li>What does the character do, and what happens as a result?</li>
  <li>How does the story end?</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Make events believable and connected</h3>
<p>Each important event should arise naturally from what came before it. If a character suddenly solves a serious problem without any explanation, the ending may feel unconvincing.</p>
<p>Use sequence words such as ''later'', ''meanwhile'', ''eventually'' and ''the following morning'' when they clarify the order of events. Avoid beginning every sentence with ''then''.</p>

<h3>5. Use description and dialogue purposefully</h3>
<p>Specific details help readers imagine the scene. Dialogue can reveal character, show disagreement or move the plot forward. However, long descriptions and conversations should not distract from the central events.</p>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> I was frightened. I ran. I saw a person. I was more frightened.</p>
<p><strong>Stronger:</strong> Footsteps sounded behind me on the empty path. I quickened my pace, but the footsteps grew louder. When I turned, a watchman called out that I had dropped my bag.</p>
<p>The stronger version uses connected actions and relevant details to create tension and then explain the situation.</p>

<h3>6. Check the finished narrative</h3>
<p>Confirm that the story answers the task, has a clear sequence, develops a central problem or experience, and ends convincingly. Check paragraphing, verb tense, dialogue punctuation, spelling and grammar.</p>', 1),
  (1536, 1536, '<h2>Descriptive Composition</h2>
<p>A descriptive composition creates a clear impression of a person, place, object, scene or experience through carefully chosen details. Its purpose is to help the reader imagine the subject, not merely to list its features.</p>

<h3>1. Observe before describing</h3>
<p>Before writing, identify the subject and the details that make it distinctive.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sight:</strong> colour, shape, size, movement and appearance.</li>
<li><strong>Sound:</strong> voices, footsteps, birds, machinery or silence.</li>
<li><strong>Smell:</strong> fresh rain, cooking food, flowers or smoke.</li>
<li><strong>Touch:</strong> roughness, smoothness, warmth, coldness or texture.</li>
<li><strong>Taste:</strong> use when it is relevant to the subject.</li>
</ul>
<p>Choose details that matter. A description of a busy market may benefit from sounds, movement and smells, while a description of a person may focus on appearance, behaviour and distinctive habits.</p>

<h3>2. Use precise language</h3>
<p>General words often create weak descriptions. Replace vague expressions with accurate details.</p>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> The room was nice and had many things.</p>
<p><strong>Stronger:</strong> Morning light fell across the wooden desks, and neatly arranged exercise books covered the front table.</p>
<p>The stronger version gives the reader specific details to imagine.</p>

<h3>3. Use figurative language carefully</h3>
<p>Similes, metaphors and personification can make a description vivid when they suit the subject.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Simile:</strong> The clouds hung like grey blankets above the hills.</li>
<li><strong>Metaphor:</strong> The market was a sea of moving bodies.</li>
<li><strong>Personification:</strong> The wind rattled the windows impatiently.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not force figurative language into every sentence. Accurate, simple description is often more effective than a string of comparisons.</p>

<h3>4. Organise details</h3>
<p>Choose a clear order instead of jumping randomly between details. You could describe a place from the entrance to the back, from near to far, or from the general scene to one important detail.</p>
<p>For a person, you might move from general appearance to clothing, expression and behaviour. The order should help the reader build a clear mental picture.</p>

<h3>5. Build a consistent impression</h3>
<p>Details should work together to create the intended mood or impression. If you are describing a peaceful morning, rushing traffic and blaring horns may contradict that mood unless the contrast is deliberate.</p>

<h3>6. Revise the description</h3>
<p>Remove repeated adjectives, vague phrases and irrelevant details. Check that each paragraph develops a distinct part of the description and that the reader can identify the subject clearly.</p>', 1),
  (1537, 1537, '<h2>Expository Composition</h2>
<p>An expository composition explains a topic, process, concept or situation clearly. Its main purpose is to help the reader understand something through organised information and explanations.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the task</h3>
<p>Expository questions often ask you to explain how something works, describe causes and effects, discuss the importance of a subject or show how a process takes place.</p>
<p>Underline the instruction word and the topic. If the question asks for causes, focus on causes. If it asks how a process works, explain the stages in a sensible order.</p>

<h3>2. Plan the explanation</h3>
<p>Before writing, identify the main points needed to answer the question. Arrange them in a logical order, such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>general explanation of the topic;</li>
<li>first main point and its explanation;</li>
<li>additional points supported by examples or evidence;</li>
<li>a conclusion that brings the explanation together.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Explain rather than merely list</h3>
<p><strong>Topic:</strong> Causes of soil erosion.</p>
<p><strong>Weak point:</strong> Cutting down trees causes soil erosion.</p>
<p><strong>Developed explanation:</strong> Cutting down trees exposes the soil because fewer roots hold it together. Rainwater can then carry loose soil away more easily, especially on sloping land.</p>
<p>A useful explanatory paragraph states a point, explains how or why it occurs, and adds an example or relevant detail when necessary.</p>

<h3>4. Use logical connections</h3>
<p>Connect ideas according to their relationship. Words such as ''because'' introduce reasons, ''as a result'' indicates consequences, and ''for example'' introduces an illustration. Use these expressions only when their meanings fit the ideas.</p>

<h3>5. Keep information accurate and relevant</h3>
<p>Include facts and examples that help explain the topic. Avoid unsupported claims, unnecessary stories and repeated points. If the task requires a process, make sure no essential stage is omitted.</p>

<h3>6. Conclude the explanation</h3>
<p>The conclusion should reinforce the main understanding or summarise the key points. It should not introduce an unrelated topic or simply repeat the introduction word for word.</p>

<h3>7. Check your work</h3>
<p>Ask whether a reader unfamiliar with the topic could follow your explanation. Check the order of ideas, clarity of examples, accuracy of statements, paragraph unity, grammar and spelling.</p>', 1),
  (1538, 1538, '<h2>Argumentative Composition</h2>
<p>An argumentative composition presents a clear position on an issue and supports it with reasons and relevant evidence. It aims to persuade readers through logical reasoning rather than unsupported opinion or personal attacks.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the question</h3>
<p>Identify the issue being debated and determine what the question requires. You may be asked to agree, disagree, discuss both sides or give your own view. Your plan should respond directly to that instruction.</p>

<h3>2. Establish a clear position</h3>
<p>Your position, or central claim, tells the reader what you are arguing.</p>
<p><strong>Issue:</strong> Should schools provide more time for independent reading?</p>
<p><strong>Clear position:</strong> Schools should provide more time for independent reading because it can strengthen comprehension, vocabulary and independent study habits.</p>
<p>The position gives the composition direction and identifies the main reasons to develop.</p>

<h3>3. Build each argument</h3>
<p>A useful argument paragraph contains:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Claim:</strong> the point supporting your position.</li>
<li><strong>Reasoning:</strong> why the claim matters or how it works.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence or example:</strong> relevant information that supports the reasoning.</li>
<li><strong>Link:</strong> how the point strengthens the overall position.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not invent statistics or present an unsupported assumption as an established fact. A relevant, clearly explained example is more useful than an impressive but unreliable figure.</p>

<h3>4. Consider opposing views fairly</h3>
<p>A strong argument recognises reasonable objections and responds to them. For example, some may argue that independent reading takes time away from other lessons. You could acknowledge the concern and explain how a planned reading period can complement rather than replace essential instruction.</p>
<p>Represent the opposing view accurately. Do not distort it just to make your own position appear stronger.</p>

<h3>5. Organise the composition</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Introduction:</strong> introduce the issue and state your position or direction.</li>
<li><strong>Body:</strong> develop one main reason per paragraph, supported by explanation and relevant evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Counterargument and response:</strong> address an important opposing view when appropriate.</li>
<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> reinforce your position and bring the main reasons together.</li>
</ol>

<h3>6. Use persuasive language responsibly</h3>
<p>Words such as ''therefore'', ''however'' and ''consequently'' can make relationships between ideas clear. Avoid insults, exaggerated certainty and emotional language that substitutes for reasoning.</p>

<h3>7. Check the strength of the argument</h3>
<p>Ask whether each claim has support, whether the reasoning follows logically, whether the evidence is relevant, and whether you have addressed the exact question. A confident tone cannot compensate for weak reasoning.</p>', 1),
  (1539, 1539, '<h2>Editing and Proofreading</h2>
<p>Editing and proofreading improve a piece of writing, but they focus on different levels. <strong>Editing</strong> improves meaning, organisation, clarity and sentence construction. <strong>Proofreading</strong> checks for remaining errors in spelling, punctuation, grammar and presentation.</p>

<h3>1. Edit in stages</h3>
<p>Checking everything at once makes errors easier to miss. Use a deliberate order:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Purpose and content:</strong> Does the composition answer the question and include the necessary information?</li>
<li><strong>Organisation:</strong> Do the paragraphs follow a logical order and develop their main ideas?</li>
<li><strong>Clarity:</strong> Are sentences precise, complete and easy to understand?</li>
<li><strong>Grammar:</strong> Are tense, subject–verb agreement, pronouns and sentence structures correct?</li>
<li><strong>Mechanics:</strong> Are spelling, capitalisation and punctuation accurate?</li>
</ol>

<h3>2. Correct the error, not just the appearance</h3>
<p><strong>Original:</strong> The students was late because they misses the bus.</p>
<p><strong>Corrected:</strong> The students were late because they missed the bus.</p>
<p>''Students'' is plural, so it takes ''were''. The past-tense verb ''missed'' is needed to keep the sentence consistent with the past event.</p>

<h3>3. Improve unclear or wordy sentences</h3>
<p><strong>Original:</strong> Due to the fact that it was raining, we decided to remain inside.</p>
<p><strong>Revised:</strong> Because it was raining, we decided to remain inside.</p>
<p>The revised sentence keeps the meaning while removing unnecessary words. However, do not shorten a sentence if doing so removes information needed for accuracy.</p>

<h3>4. Check common problem areas</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sentence fragments:</strong> incomplete sentences presented as complete ones.</li>
<li><strong>Run-on sentences:</strong> independent clauses joined incorrectly.</li>
<li><strong>Tense shifts:</strong> changes in tense without a clear reason.</li>
<li><strong>Agreement errors:</strong> subjects and verbs that do not match.</li>
<li><strong>Pronoun reference:</strong> pronouns whose meaning is unclear.</li>
<li><strong>Punctuation errors:</strong> missing full stops, misplaced commas or incorrect apostrophes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. Proofread carefully</h3>
<p>Read slowly, checking one type of error at a time. Reading aloud can reveal awkward sentences and missing words. Reading from the last sentence towards the first can help you focus on individual sentences, although you should still read the whole composition normally to check meaning and flow.</p>

<h3>6. Keep the writer''s meaning</h3>
<p>Editing should improve the writing without changing the intended message accidentally. After making corrections, reread the complete composition to ensure the revised sentences still connect logically and answer the original task.</p>', 1),
  (1540, 1540, '<h2>Informal Letters</h2>
<p>An informal letter is a personal message written to someone with whom the writer has a familiar relationship, such as a friend, relative or classmate. Its language is usually friendly and natural, but it should still be clear, organised and appropriate to the purpose.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the purpose</h3>
<p>Before writing, identify the recipient, the reason for writing and the information the recipient needs. You might be writing to share news, describe an experience, offer advice, congratulate someone or invite a friend to an event.</p>

<h3>2. Structure of an informal letter</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Writer''s address:</strong> traditionally placed at the top right-hand side.</li>
<li><strong>Date:</strong> written below the address.</li>
<li><strong>Salutation:</strong> for example, Dear Amina,</li>
<li><strong>Opening:</strong> greet the recipient and establish the reason for writing.</li>
<li><strong>Body:</strong> develop the message in organised paragraphs.</li>
<li><strong>Closing remarks:</strong> finish the message naturally.</li>
<li><strong>Subscription and name:</strong> for example, Your friend, followed by the writer''s name.</li>
</ol>

<h3>3. Example</h3>
<p><strong>12 River Road</strong><br>
<strong>Embu</strong><br>
<strong>9 October 2026</strong></p>
<p>Dear Wanjiku,</p>
<p>How have you been? I hope your studies are going well. I am writing to tell you about the science exhibition we attended at school last week.</p>
<p>Our class displayed a model that demonstrated how rainwater can be collected for household use. Preparing it taught us the importance of planning carefully and working together. I wish you had been there to see the other projects as well.</p>
<p>Please write back and tell me how things are going at your school. I look forward to hearing from you.</p>
<p>Your friend,<br>
Kamau</p>

<h3>4. Choose an appropriate tone</h3>
<p>Informal writing can use contractions such as ''I''m'' and ''we''ve'' when they sound natural. Friendly expressions are suitable, but avoid slang that may confuse the recipient or make the message unsuitable for the occasion.</p>

<h3>5. Organise the body</h3>
<p>Give each paragraph a clear purpose. For example, one paragraph can explain why you are writing, another can describe the event or news, and a final paragraph can ask questions or explain what you expect next.</p>

<h3>6. Check before sending</h3>
<p>Confirm that you have answered every part of the task, included the expected letter features, organised the information logically and checked spelling, grammar and punctuation. A friendly tone does not excuse unclear writing.</p>', 1),
  (1541, 1541, '<h2>Formal Letters</h2>
<p>A formal letter is written for an official, professional or public purpose. It may be addressed to a school principal, organisation, company, public office or person with whom the writer has a formal relationship.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the purpose and recipient</h3>
<p>Common purposes include making a request, raising a concern, providing information, applying for a service or responding to an official matter. The purpose determines the details that belong in the letter.</p>

<h3>2. Structure of a formal letter</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Writer''s address:</strong> placed at the top, commonly on the right.</li>
<li><strong>Date:</strong> written below the writer''s address.</li>
<li><strong>Recipient''s address:</strong> the name or title and address of the person or organisation, usually on the left.</li>
<li><strong>Salutation:</strong> for example, Dear Sir/Madam, or Dear Mr Otieno, where appropriate.</li>
<li><strong>Subject or reference line:</strong> a brief statement of the purpose, when suitable or required.</li>
<li><strong>Body:</strong> a clear opening, developed explanation and appropriate closing paragraph.</li>
<li><strong>Formal subscription:</strong> for example, Yours faithfully or Yours sincerely, followed by the writer''s name.</li>
</ol>
<p>Exact placement and conventions can vary by institution and style. Follow the format required by the task or examination.</p>

<h3>3. Example: a request for information</h3>
<p><strong>12 River Road</strong><br>
<strong>Embu</strong><br>
<strong>9 October 2026</strong></p>
<p>The Principal<br>
Green Valley Secondary School<br>
P.O. Box 120<br>
Embu</p>
<p>Dear Sir/Madam,</p>
<p><strong>RE: REQUEST FOR INFORMATION ABOUT THE SCHOOL LIBRARY</strong></p>
<p>I am writing to enquire about the arrangements for learners who wish to use the school library after lessons.</p>
<p>I would appreciate information about the opening hours, borrowing procedures and any rules learners must observe when using the library.</p>
<p>Thank you for your time and assistance. I look forward to your response.</p>
<p>Yours faithfully,<br>
Kamau Mwangi</p>

<h3>4. Maintain a formal tone</h3>
<p>Use respectful, precise language. Avoid slang, texting abbreviations and unnecessarily emotional expressions. Politeness does not require excessively complicated vocabulary.</p>

<h3>5. Make the purpose clear</h3>
<p>The opening paragraph should tell the recipient why you are writing. The body should provide necessary details in a logical order. The final paragraph should state the action requested, offer thanks where appropriate or explain the next step.</p>

<h3>6. Select the closing correctly</h3>
<p>A common convention is to use ''Yours faithfully'' when the salutation is ''Dear Sir/Madam'' and ''Yours sincerely'' when addressing the recipient by name, such as ''Dear Mr Otieno''. Some organisations use different conventions, so follow the instructions provided.</p>

<h3>7. Proofread carefully</h3>
<p>Check the recipient''s details, date, subject line, paragraph organisation, factual accuracy, tone and punctuation. An incorrectly addressed letter or unclear request may prevent the reader from acting on the message.</p>', 1),
  (1542, 1542, '<h2>Application Letters</h2>
<p>An application letter is a formal letter in which a person applies for a position, opportunity, admission, placement or other specified consideration. It introduces the applicant and explains why they are suitable for the opportunity.</p>

<h3>1. Read the opportunity carefully</h3>
<p>Identify the position or opportunity, the organisation, the required qualifications, the deadline and any documents requested. Your letter should respond to the stated requirements rather than make general claims about yourself.</p>

<h3>2. Structure of an application letter</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Sender''s address and date.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Recipient''s address.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Formal salutation.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Subject line:</strong> name the position or opportunity.</li>
<li><strong>Opening paragraph:</strong> state what you are applying for and where you learnt about it, if relevant.</li>
<li><strong>Supporting paragraph:</strong> connect your qualifications, skills and experience to the requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Closing paragraph:</strong> express interest and refer to any requested supporting documents.</li>
<li><strong>Formal subscription and name.</strong></li>
</ol>

<h3>3. Demonstrate suitability with evidence</h3>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> I am a very hardworking and talented person.</p>
<p><strong>Stronger:</strong> As secretary of our science club, I recorded meeting decisions, prepared notices and helped organise two school exhibitions.</p>
<p>The stronger statement gives specific evidence of skills instead of relying on unsupported praise.</p>

<h3>4. Match your evidence to the opportunity</h3>
<p>If the position requires communication, organisation or teamwork, select truthful examples that demonstrate those qualities. School projects, clubs, volunteering and relevant responsibilities can provide evidence even when you have limited formal work experience.</p>

<h3>5. Be honest and concise</h3>
<p>Do not invent qualifications, experience, references or achievements. Include only information relevant to the opportunity and avoid repeating the same claim in different words.</p>

<h3>6. Mention supporting documents accurately</h3>
<p>If the advert requests a curriculum vitae, certificates or other documents, state that they are enclosed or attached only when you are actually submitting them. Follow the application instructions carefully.</p>

<h3>7. Final check</h3>
<p>Confirm that the position title and organisation are correct, the letter explains your suitability, the tone is professional and the requested details are included. Check names, dates, spelling and any attachment requirements before sending.</p>', 1),
  (1543, 1543, '<h2>Emails and Digital Messages</h2>
<p>Emails and digital messages allow people to exchange information electronically. Their structure and tone depend on the purpose, recipient, urgency and communication platform.</p>

<h3>1. Choose the right channel and tone</h3>
<p>An email to a principal, employer or organisation usually requires a formal tone. A message to a close friend may be more relaxed. Even in informal messages, clarity and respect remain important.</p>

<h3>2. Structure of an email</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recipient:</strong> enter the correct email address.</li>
<li><strong>Subject:</strong> summarise the purpose in a few meaningful words.</li>
<li><strong>Greeting:</strong> use a suitable salutation.</li>
<li><strong>Opening:</strong> state why you are writing.</li>
<li><strong>Body:</strong> provide the necessary information in short, organised paragraphs.</li>
<li><strong>Action or closing:</strong> state what response or action is needed, if any.</li>
<li><strong>Sign-off:</strong> include your name or an appropriate closing.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Write useful subject lines</h3>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> Hello</p>
<p><strong>Clearer:</strong> Request for Permission to Attend the Science Workshop</p>
<p>A useful subject line helps the recipient understand the purpose before opening the message.</p>

<h3>4. Keep the message concise and complete</h3>
<p>Include the context the recipient needs, the relevant facts and any action requested. Avoid unnecessary background, repeated points and ambiguous phrases such as ''please deal with that thing''. Name the item or action clearly.</p>

<h3>5. Use To, Cc and Bcc appropriately</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>To:</strong> the main recipient expected to read or act on the message.</li>
<li><strong>Cc:</strong> people who should receive a visible copy for information.</li>
<li><strong>Bcc:</strong> recipients whose addresses should not be visible to other recipients in that email.</li>
</ul>
<p>Include only appropriate recipients. Do not expose other people''s contact details unnecessarily.</p>

<h3>6. Handle attachments carefully</h3>
<p>Mention an attachment when it is relevant and ensure the correct file is attached before sending. Use a clear filename, check that the file opens, and confirm that it contains the intended document.</p>

<h3>7. Communicate responsibly</h3>
<p>Check recipient addresses before sending. Avoid sharing confidential information with unintended recipients, and be cautious about suspicious links or requests for passwords. Digital messages can be forwarded or saved, so write with care.</p>

<h3>8. Proofread before sending</h3>
<p>Check the recipient, subject, tone, facts, requested action, attachments and spelling. A short message can still be complete when it answers the reader''s likely questions clearly.</p>', 1),
  (1544, 1544, '<h2>Curriculum Vitae (CV)</h2>
<p>A curriculum vitae (CV) is a document that presents a person''s education, skills, experience, achievements and relevant activities. It helps an organisation assess whether the applicant meets the requirements of an opportunity.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the purpose</h3>
<p>A CV is not a life story. Select information relevant to the opportunity and present it accurately. A learner applying for a school leadership role may emphasise responsibility and teamwork, while someone applying for a computer-related opportunity may highlight digital skills and relevant projects.</p>

<h3>2. Main sections of a CV</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Personal and contact details:</strong> full name, appropriate telephone number, email address and location where relevant.</li>
<li><strong>Personal profile:</strong> a short statement describing relevant strengths and goals.</li>
<li><strong>Education:</strong> schools or institutions attended and relevant qualifications or current studies.</li>
<li><strong>Skills:</strong> abilities relevant to the opportunity.</li>
<li><strong>Experience:</strong> relevant employment, volunteering, school responsibilities or projects.</li>
<li><strong>Achievements and activities:</strong> genuine accomplishments that demonstrate relevant abilities.</li>
<li><strong>Referees:</strong> people who can provide a reference, if requested or appropriate.</li>
</ol>
<p>The order and sections may vary according to the opportunity. Do not include unnecessary personal details such as national identification numbers or private family information.</p>

<h3>3. Write a focused profile</h3>
<p><strong>Weak:</strong> I am a good person who wants any job and can do everything.</p>
<p><strong>Stronger:</strong> A secondary school learner with strong organisational skills, an interest in reading and experience supporting group activities. Seeking an opportunity to contribute responsibly to school library activities.</p>
<p>A good profile is brief, relevant and supported by the rest of the CV.</p>

<h3>4. Present education and experience honestly</h3>
<p>List education in a clear order, usually from the most recent to the earliest. Give accurate dates where available. If you have no paid employment, you may include relevant school projects, volunteering or responsibilities, provided you describe them truthfully.</p>

<h3>5. Show evidence of skills</h3>
<p>Instead of listing ''leadership'' alone, explain how you have demonstrated it. For example: ''Coordinated a four-person group project and ensured that each section was completed before the deadline.''</p>

<h3>6. Make the CV easy to read</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use clear headings and consistent formatting.</li>
<li>Use short bullet points for skills and responsibilities.</li>
<li>Keep descriptions concise and relevant.</li>
<li>Check spelling, grammar, dates and contact details.</li>
<li>Follow any length or format requirements in the advert.</li>
</ul>

<h3>7. Handle referees responsibly</h3>
<p>Provide referees only when requested or appropriate. Ask their permission before listing their contact details, and ensure the details are correct.</p>

<h3>8. Final accuracy check</h3>
<p>Every qualification, achievement and experience listed should be genuine. Tailor the CV to the opportunity without exaggerating your abilities or copying irrelevant details into every application.</p>', 1),
  (1545, 1545, '<h2>Reports</h2>
<p>A report presents information about an event, investigation, activity or situation in a clear and organised form. Its structure and tone depend on the purpose, the intended reader and any instructions provided.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the purpose</h3>
<p>A report may describe what happened at an event, present findings from an investigation, explain a problem or recommend action. Before writing, establish:</p>
<ul>
<li>What is being reported?</li>
<li>Who will read the report?</li>
<li>What information or findings are required?</li>
<li>Is the writer expected to make recommendations?</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Common report structure</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Title:</strong> identifies the subject of the report.</li>
<li><strong>To/from/date:</strong> included when required by the reporting format.</li>
<li><strong>Introduction or purpose:</strong> explains why the report was prepared.</li>
<li><strong>Findings or main details:</strong> presents relevant facts in a logical order.</li>
<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> summarises what the findings indicate.</li>
<li><strong>Recommendations:</strong> suggests appropriate action when requested or useful.</li>
<li><strong>Name or signature:</strong> included where the format requires it.</li>
</ol>
<p>Not every report requires every section. Follow the task instructions and the conventions of the organisation.</p>

<h3>3. Separate facts from opinions</h3>
<p><strong>Factual:</strong> The meeting began at 2.00 p.m. and was attended by 18 learners.</p>
<p><strong>Opinion:</strong> The meeting was the most exciting event the school has ever held.</p>
<p>A report should distinguish observations and verifiable information from personal judgements. Opinions may be included when relevant, but they should be clearly presented and supported.</p>

<h3>4. Organise findings clearly</h3>
<p>Use headings, paragraphs, numbered points or tables when they help the reader locate information. Group related findings together instead of presenting them in the order they happened to come to mind.</p>

<h3>5. Make recommendations practical</h3>
<p>A recommendation should respond to a finding. If a report finds that rubbish bins are insufficient, a relevant recommendation might be to place additional bins in the affected areas and establish a regular collection schedule.</p>

<h3>6. Use an objective tone</h3>
<p>Prefer clear, neutral language. Avoid insults, exaggerated claims and unsupported accusations. Identify limitations when information is incomplete rather than pretending to know more than the evidence establishes.</p>

<h3>7. Review the report</h3>
<p>Check that the title matches the task, the facts are accurate, the findings answer the purpose and any recommendations follow logically. Proofread names, dates, figures, grammar and headings.</p>', 1),
  (1546, 1546, '<h2>Notices and Announcements</h2>
<p>A notice or announcement communicates important information to a specific audience. It should make the key message easy to find and understand quickly.</p>

<h3>1. Understand the difference</h3>
<p>A <strong>notice</strong> is usually a written message displayed or circulated to inform people about an event, change, rule or opportunity. An <strong>announcement</strong> may be written or spoken and often communicates information that people need to know or act on.</p>

<h3>2. Essential information</h3>
<p>Depending on the purpose, include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Heading:</strong> identifies the subject.</li>
<li><strong>Audience:</strong> states who the message concerns.</li>
<li><strong>Main message:</strong> explains the event, change or information.</li>
<li><strong>Date and time:</strong> where relevant.</li>
<li><strong>Venue or location:</strong> where relevant.</li>
<li><strong>Required action:</strong> what the audience should do.</li>
<li><strong>Contact or issuing authority:</strong> when needed.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Example of a notice</h3>
<p><strong>GREEN VALLEY SECONDARY SCHOOL</strong></p>
<p><strong>NOTICE: SCHOOL CLEAN-UP</strong></p>
<p>All learners are informed that a school clean-up will take place on Saturday, 17 October, from 8.00 a.m. to 11.00 a.m. Learners should report to the assembly ground and bring suitable cleaning materials where possible. Class representatives should submit attendance lists to the teacher in charge.</p>
<p>Issued by: Environmental Club</p>
<p><em>Use a date, time, venue and organiser that match the actual task or event. The example is illustrative.</em></p>

<h3>4. Be brief but complete</h3>
<p>A notice is not an essay. Include only information needed to understand the message and act on it. However, excessive brevity can create confusion if the date, venue, audience or required action is missing.</p>

<h3>5. Use clear and direct language</h3>
<p>Prefer ''Submit your form to the office by Friday'' to ''It would be appreciated if the form could somehow be made available at some point before the end of the week.'' The direct version identifies the action, destination and deadline.</p>

<h3>6. Match the audience and channel</h3>
<p>A notice for learners may use straightforward school-related language. A public announcement may need to explain unfamiliar terms or provide additional directions. Spoken announcements should be paced clearly and repeat essential details when necessary.</p>

<h3>7. Check the details</h3>
<p>Verify dates, times, venue, names, deadlines and instructions before displaying or delivering the message. A polished notice with an incorrect date is still ineffective.</p>', 1),
  (1547, 1547, '<h2>Invitations</h2>
<p>An invitation asks a person or group to attend an event or take part in an activity. An effective invitation makes the occasion clear and gives recipients the information they need to respond or attend.</p>

<h3>1. Identify the occasion and audience</h3>
<p>Consider whether the invitation is formal or informal, who will receive it and what relationship the writer has with them. A wedding invitation, a school event invitation and a message inviting a friend to a birthday gathering require different tones and levels of formality.</p>

<h3>2. Essential invitation details</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Host or organiser:</strong> who is inviting the recipient.</li>
<li><strong>Occasion:</strong> what the event is.</li>
<li><strong>Date and time:</strong> when it will happen.</li>
<li><strong>Venue:</strong> where it will take place.</li>
<li><strong>Recipient or audience:</strong> who is invited, where necessary.</li>
<li><strong>Response instructions:</strong> whether the recipient should confirm attendance.</li>
<li><strong>Additional details:</strong> dress code, items to bring or other requirements, when relevant.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Formal invitations</h3>
<p>Formal invitations use respectful, carefully chosen language and a clear layout. They may be issued by an institution or on behalf of an organiser.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>
<p>The Principal and staff of Green Valley Secondary School request the pleasure of your company at the school''s annual prize-giving ceremony on Friday, 23 October, at 10.00 a.m. in the school hall. Kindly confirm your attendance with the school office by 16 October.</p>
<p><em>This is an illustrative invitation; use the actual event details when completing a task.</em></p>

<h3>4. Informal invitations</h3>
<p>An informal invitation can sound warmer and more personal. For example: ''Hi Amina, I''d love you to join us for my birthday lunch on Saturday at 1.00 p.m. at our home. Please let me know if you can come.''</p>

<h3>5. Make attendance expectations clear</h3>
<p>If recipients must confirm, provide a deadline and a contact method. If the event is limited to a particular group, state that clearly. Avoid ambiguous phrases such as ''come sometime in the afternoon'' when a precise time is needed.</p>

<h3>6. Responding to an invitation</h3>
<p>A response should acknowledge the invitation and clearly accept or decline. When declining, a brief expression of thanks and regret is usually sufficient. Do not imply acceptance if you cannot attend.</p>

<h3>7. Proofread before sending</h3>
<p>Check the spelling of names, event title, date, time, venue, contact details and response deadline. The tone should match the occasion and the relationship with the recipient.</p>', 1),
  (1548, 1548, '<h2>Agendas and Minutes</h2>
  <p>An <strong>agenda</strong> is a planned list of items to be discussed during a meeting. <strong>Minutes</strong> are the official written record of what happened, what was decided, and what actions were assigned.</p>

  <h3>1. Writing an agenda</h3>
  <p>An agenda helps a meeting stay organised. It normally includes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Name of the organisation or group.</li>
    <li>Type of meeting, date, time and venue.</li>
    <li>Items in the order they will be discussed.</li>
    <li>Any other business and the closing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Example:</strong></p>
  <p><strong>MWANGA SECONDARY SCHOOL ENVIRONMENT CLUB</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Agenda for the meeting on 15 October 2026 at 3.30 p.m. in Room 4</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Opening and apologies</li>
    <li>Confirmation of the previous minutes</li>
    <li>Review of the school compound clean-up</li>
    <li>Planning the next environmental activity</li>
    <li>Any other business</li>
    <li>Date of the next meeting and adjournment</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>2. Writing minutes</h3>
  <p>Minutes record the meeting rather than predict what will be discussed. A useful format includes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Meeting title, date, time and venue.</li>
    <li>Members present and apologies received.</li>
    <li>Numbered agenda items and a concise record of each discussion.</li>
    <li>Decisions made and actions assigned, including responsible people and deadlines.</li>
    <li>Time of adjournment and, where required, the chairperson''s confirmation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Example entry:</strong> <em>Agenda item 3: Clean-up exercise. Members agreed to organise a clean-up on 22 October. The secretary will prepare the notice by 18 October.</em></p>

  <h3>3. Important distinctions</h3>
  <p>An agenda says what the meeting intends to discuss; minutes say what actually happened. Do not invent decisions, include personal opinions as facts, or write every spoken word. Use clear, neutral language and record agreed actions accurately.</p>', 1),
  (1549, 1549, '<h2>Instructions and Procedures</h2>
  <p><strong>Instructions</strong> tell someone what to do. A <strong>procedure</strong> presents a sequence of actions for completing a task or achieving a result. You encounter them in experiments, recipes, school rules, equipment guides and practical activities.</p>

  <h3>1. A clear structure</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Title or purpose:</strong> state what the instructions will help the reader do.</li>
    <li><strong>Materials or requirements:</strong> list what is needed, if applicable.</li>
    <li><strong>Steps:</strong> present actions in the order they must happen.</li>
    <li><strong>Warning or final check:</strong> include important precautions or how to confirm completion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Use precise language</h3>
  <p>Instructions commonly use the imperative form of a verb: <em>Place, measure, connect, stir, record</em>. Begin each step with a clear action when possible.</p>
  <p>Vague: <em>Do the thing properly and then continue.</em></p>
  <p>Precise: <em>Measure 100 ml of water and pour it into the clean container.</em></p>

  <h3>3. Sequence and logical order</h3>
  <p>Words such as <em>first, next, after that, before, finally</em> help readers follow a process. However, numbering the steps may be clearer than using a sequence word in every sentence.</p>
  <p><strong>Example: How to submit a written assignment</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write your name, class and the assignment title on the first page.</li>
    <li>Check that every question has been answered.</li>
    <li>Correct spelling and punctuation errors.</li>
    <li>Arrange the pages in the correct order.</li>
    <li>Submit the assignment to the teacher before the stated deadline.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>4. Avoid common errors</h3>
  <p>Do not leave out a necessary step, mix up the order, use unclear pronouns such as <em>it</em> when the object is uncertain, or combine several complicated actions in one sentence. Use warnings where an action could cause harm. Instructions should be complete enough for the intended reader to follow without guessing.</p>', 1),
  (1550, 1550, '<h2>Speeches and Presentations</h2>
  <p>A speech is spoken communication prepared for an audience. A presentation may combine speaking with visual aids, demonstrations or other supporting material. Both require a clear purpose, an organised message and language suited to the listeners.</p>

  <h3>1. Structure of a speech</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Opening:</strong> greet the audience appropriately, introduce the topic and establish interest.</li>
    <li><strong>Body:</strong> develop the main points in a logical order, using explanations, examples or evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> summarise the central message and finish with a suitable final statement or call to action.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Know the audience and purpose</h3>
  <p>A speech to fellow learners may use a different tone from one delivered to school administrators. Before writing, ask: Who will listen? What do they already know? What should they understand, feel or do after listening?</p>

  <h3>3. Develop and connect ideas</h3>
  <p>Use clear topic sentences and transitions such as <em>firstly, in addition, however, as a result</em> and <em>finally</em>. Support claims with relevant reasons or examples. Avoid repeating the same point in different words merely to make the speech longer.</p>

  <h3>4. Delivery</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Speak audibly and at a pace the audience can follow.</li>
    <li>Use pauses to separate ideas and emphasise important points.</li>
    <li>Maintain appropriate eye contact rather than reading every word from a page.</li>
    <li>Use natural gestures and an expression suited to the message.</li>
    <li>Practise names, difficult words and transitions before presenting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5. Visual aids</h3>
  <p>Slides, charts and posters should support the spoken message, not replace it. Use readable text, accurate information and only the details needed to explain a point. Do not turn slides into full paragraphs that the audience must read while you speak.</p>

  <p><strong>Example opening:</strong> <em>Good morning, fellow learners and teachers. Today, I would like to explain three practical ways our school can reduce the amount of food wasted at lunchtime.</em></p>', 1),
  (1551, 1551, '<h2>Listening for Main Ideas</h2>
  <p>The <strong>main idea</strong> is the central message a speaker wants listeners to understand. Supporting details explain, prove or illustrate that message. Listening for the main idea means understanding the overall point rather than trying to remember every word.</p>

  <h3>1. Identify the purpose</h3>
  <p>Before or while listening, ask what the speaker is mainly doing: explaining a process, reporting an event, persuading an audience, giving an opinion or describing a problem.</p>

  <h3>2. Listen for repeated and emphasised ideas</h3>
  <p>Speakers often signal important points through repetition, emphasis, summaries and phrases such as <em>the main reason is, what matters most, in summary</em> or <em>the key point</em>. These clues are useful, but the whole message still matters.</p>

  <h3>3. Separate the main idea from details</h3>
  <p>Consider this short spoken message:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>Our school library is being used less frequently because many learners do not know which resources are available. From next week, the librarian will introduce each class to the catalogue and organise short library visits. The aim is to help learners find useful reading materials more easily.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p><strong>Main idea:</strong> The school is introducing activities to help learners use the library more effectively.</p>
  <p><strong>Supporting details:</strong> Learners will be shown the catalogue, and short class visits will begin next week.</p>

  <h3>4. Summarise without changing the message</h3>
  <p>A useful summary captures the speaker''s central point in one sentence. Do not mistake an interesting example, a minor date or a single statistic for the overall message. If a speaker develops several related points, identify the idea that connects them.</p>

  <h3>5. Check your interpretation</h3>
  <p>Ask whether your summary represents the whole message and whether the details you selected support it. If the speaker gives a reason and a proposed solution, include both when they are central to the message.</p>', 1),
  (1552, 1552, '<h2>Listening for Details</h2>
  <p>Listening for details means identifying specific information in a spoken message, such as names, dates, places, quantities, reasons, instructions and conditions. It differs from listening for the main idea, which focuses on the overall message.</p>

  <h3>1. Know which details matter</h3>
  <p>Before listening, consider what information you need. For an announcement, you may need the event, date, time, venue and action required. For an explanation, you may need the reasons, examples or sequence of steps.</p>

  <h3>2. Listen for precise information</h3>
  <p>Pay attention to words that change meaning, especially negatives and restrictions such as <em>not, only, except, before</em> and <em>unless</em>. Distinguish similar numbers and dates, and do not replace what was said with what you expected to hear.</p>

  <h3>3. Example: a school announcement</h3>
  <blockquote>
    <p>All members of the science club should meet in the laboratory at 2.15 p.m. on Thursday. Bring your completed project plans, but do not bring chemicals. The teacher will explain the safety arrangements before the practical session begins.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Who?</strong> Science club members.</li>
    <li><strong>Where?</strong> The laboratory.</li>
    <li><strong>When?</strong> Thursday at 2.15 p.m.</li>
    <li><strong>Bring?</strong> Completed project plans.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not bring?</strong> Chemicals.</li>
    <li><strong>What happens next?</strong> The teacher explains the safety arrangements.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Take brief, accurate notes</h3>
  <p>Record keywords, numbers and relationships rather than attempting to write every word. For example: <em>Science club — Thu, 2.15 p.m. — lab — bring plans — no chemicals — safety briefing.</em> Check that abbreviations remain understandable.</p>

  <h3>5. Verify before answering</h3>
  <p>Compare each answer with the information actually given. Do not confuse the time of a meeting with its duration, the person making an announcement with the person expected to act, or a suggestion with a confirmed decision.</p>', 1),
  (1553, 1553, '<h2>Listening to Instructions</h2>
  <p>Listening to instructions means understanding what a speaker wants you to do, the order of actions, and any conditions or restrictions that apply. Successful listeners identify both the action and the details needed to perform it correctly.</p>

  <h3>1. Identify the action words</h3>
  <p>Listen for verbs such as <em>write, collect, underline, compare, submit</em> and <em>explain</em>. These tell you what action is required. Pay attention to the object of each verb: ''underline the verbs'' is different from ''underline the nouns''.</p>

  <h3>2. Follow the sequence</h3>
  <p>Words such as <em>first, next, before, after, then</em> and <em>finally</em> show the order of actions. Some instructions depend on earlier steps, so changing the sequence may lead to an incorrect result.</p>

  <h3>3. Notice conditions and restrictions</h3>
  <p>Words such as <em>only, unless, do not, except</em> and <em>until</em> can change what you must do. Do not ignore a restriction simply because you remember the main action.</p>

  <h3>4. Example</h3>
  <blockquote>
    <p>Read the passage silently. Underline two unfamiliar words. Look up their meanings in a dictionary, then write one original sentence using each word. Do not copy the example sentences from the dictionary.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the passage silently.</li>
    <li>Underline two unfamiliar words.</li>
    <li>Look up their meanings.</li>
    <li>Write an original sentence for each word.</li>
    <li>Avoid copying dictionary examples.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>5. Check your understanding</h3>
  <p>If an instruction is unclear, ask a specific question before beginning. You can also repeat the key steps in your own words to confirm that you understood them correctly. Do not assume that an example is compulsory unless the speaker says so.</p>', 1),
  (1554, 1554, '<h2>Speaker Purpose and Attitude</h2>
  <p>A speaker''s <strong>purpose</strong> is the reason for speaking. A speaker''s <strong>attitude</strong> is the feeling or viewpoint conveyed towards the subject or audience. Identifying both helps listeners understand not only what is said, but why and how it is said.</p>

  <h3>1. Common purposes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Inform:</strong> provide facts or updates.</li>
    <li><strong>Explain:</strong> make a process or idea understandable.</li>
    <li><strong>Persuade:</strong> encourage an audience to accept a view or take action.</li>
    <li><strong>Warn:</strong> draw attention to danger or possible consequences.</li>
    <li><strong>Entertain:</strong> amuse or engage an audience.</li>
    <li><strong>Instruct:</strong> tell an audience how to do something.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Recognising attitude</h3>
  <p>A speaker may sound enthusiastic, doubtful, disappointed, concerned, confident, sarcastic or sympathetic. Attitude can be conveyed through word choice, emphasis, pace, pauses and tone of voice.</p>

  <h3>3. Use evidence, not guesswork</h3>
  <p>Consider this statement: <em>We have received three reminders about keeping the classroom clean, yet litter is still being left under the desks. We must take this responsibility seriously.</em></p>
  <p><strong>Likely purpose:</strong> persuade learners to keep the classroom clean.</p>
  <p><strong>Likely attitude:</strong> concerned or disappointed.</p>
  <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> the repeated reminders, the continued littering and the phrase ''take this responsibility seriously''.</p>

  <h3>4. Separate purpose from attitude</h3>
  <p>Purpose answers, ''What is the speaker trying to achieve?'' Attitude answers, ''How does the speaker feel or what viewpoint do they express?'' A speaker may use a calm tone to warn people, or an enthusiastic tone to explain a topic.</p>

  <h3>5. Avoid unsupported interpretations</h3>
  <p>When explaining your answer, point to words or vocal clues that support it. Do not label a speaker as angry merely because they disagree, or as persuasive simply because they speak loudly. The message and its context matter.</p>', 1),
  (1555, 1555, '<h2>Inferring Spoken Meaning</h2>
  <p>To <strong>infer</strong> is to work out a meaning that is suggested rather than directly stated. Listeners combine spoken words with context, tone, emphasis and relevant background knowledge to understand implied messages.</p>

  <h3>1. Use clues from the message</h3>
  <p>Consider what the speaker says, the situation in which it is said, and how the speaker says it. An inference should be supported by clues, not simply by what you imagine might be happening.</p>

  <h3>2. Recognise implied meaning</h3>
  <p>If someone says, <em>The classroom is rather dark; could someone open the curtains?</em>, the speaker is not merely describing the light. The request implies that opening the curtains may improve visibility.</p>

  <h3>3. Interpret tone in context</h3>
  <p>The words <em>That was helpful</em> may express sincere appreciation. Depending on tone and context, they may also be sarcastic. The words alone may not be enough to decide; listen for vocal emphasis and consider what happened immediately before the statement.</p>

  <h3>4. Understand indirect requests</h3>
  <p>Speakers sometimes make requests indirectly to sound polite. <em>Could you move your bag?</em> is usually a request, not a question about physical ability. <em>It is getting late</em> may suggest that a meeting should end, depending on the situation.</p>

  <h3>5. Justify your inference</h3>
  <p>Use the pattern: <strong>inference + clue + explanation</strong>. For example: ''The learner is probably uncertain because they pause repeatedly and ask whether the instructions are correct.'' Avoid presenting an inference as a confirmed fact when the evidence only makes it likely.</p>', 1),
  (1556, 1556, '<h2>Note-taking from Spoken Information</h2>
  <p>Note-taking from spoken information means recording the most useful ideas while someone speaks. Good notes preserve important meaning without attempting to write every word.</p>

  <h3>1. Decide what to capture</h3>
  <p>Listen for the main idea, key supporting points, names, dates, quantities, definitions, examples and actions required. What counts as important depends on the purpose: instructions require sequence and conditions, while a discussion may require arguments and evidence.</p>

  <h3>2. Use keywords and abbreviations</h3>
  <p>Short notes are often easier to write and review. For example:</p>
  <p><strong>Spoken message:</strong> The school garden project begins on Friday. Each group should bring two watering cans, and the agriculture teacher will demonstrate seed spacing before planting.</p>
  <p><strong>Notes:</strong> Garden project — Friday; each group: 2 watering cans; agriculture teacher: demonstrate seed spacing before planting.</p>
  <p>Use abbreviations only when you can recognise them later. Do not shorten important names, numbers or conditions so much that the meaning becomes unclear.</p>

  <h3>3. Organise information</h3>
  <p>Use headings, numbered steps, bullets or a simple table. Group related ideas together. For a cause-and-effect explanation, use arrows or labels; for a procedure, number the steps in order.</p>

  <h3>4. Listen while writing</h3>
  <p>Do not let one missed word prevent you from following the rest of the message. Leave a space and continue listening. Pay special attention to corrections, contrast words such as <em>however</em>, and restrictions such as <em>only</em> or <em>not</em>.</p>

  <h3>5. Review and complete your notes</h3>
  <p>After listening, expand unclear abbreviations, correct any mistakes and fill gaps using information you actually heard. Separate the speaker''s facts from your own questions or interpretations. Never invent missing details simply to make the notes look complete.</p>', 1),
  (1557, 1557, '<h2>Summarising Spoken Information</h2>
  <p>A spoken summary presents the most important ideas from a speech, announcement, discussion or explanation in a shorter form. It preserves the original meaning without including every example or repetition.</p>

  <h3>1. Identify the central message</h3>
  <p>First determine what the speaker is mainly communicating. Then identify the supporting points necessary to understand that message. Distinguish essential information from illustrations, repetition and unrelated details.</p>

  <h3>2. Select and combine key points</h3>
  <p>Group related ideas together and express them concisely. Preserve important names, dates, figures, reasons and conditions when they are essential to the message.</p>

  <h3>3. Use your own words</h3>
  <p>A summary should normally restate the information clearly rather than reproduce long sections word for word. Keep technical terms and exact figures where changing them would alter the meaning.</p>

  <h3>4. Example</h3>
  <p><strong>Spoken message:</strong> <em>Our school has recorded a high level of food waste. Some learners take more food than they can eat, while others leave fruit untouched. Starting next week, the dining hall will encourage smaller first servings, with additional portions available on request. Learners will also be reminded to take only what they can finish.</em></p>
  <p><strong>Summary:</strong> The school will reduce food waste by encouraging learners to take manageable portions and request more when needed.</p>

  <h3>5. Check the summary</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Does it capture the speaker''s central message?</li>
    <li>Does it preserve essential facts and conditions?</li>
    <li>Does it avoid adding unsupported opinions?</li>
    <li>Is it shorter and clearer than the original?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A summary is not a personal response. Save your own judgement or recommendations for a separate section if they are requested.</p>', 1),
  (1558, 1558, '<h2>Clear and Organised Speaking</h2>
  <p>Effective speaking communicates ideas so that listeners can understand them easily. A speaker needs a clear purpose, logically ordered points, suitable vocabulary and an audible, controlled delivery.</p>

  <h3>1. Organise before speaking</h3>
  <p>Decide what you want listeners to understand. Arrange your ideas in a useful order, such as problem then solution, cause then effect, or first step through to final step.</p>

  <h3>2. Use complete and precise ideas</h3>
  <p>Choose words that express your meaning accurately. Avoid vague expressions such as <em>that thing</em> or <em>do it somehow</em> when the audience needs a specific explanation. Use complete sentences when explaining a complex point.</p>

  <h3>3. Connect ideas</h3>
  <p>Link points using expressions such as <em>first, because, however, for example, therefore</em> and <em>finally</em>. These help listeners follow the relationship between ideas. Do not use linking words mechanically when no logical relationship exists.</p>

  <h3>4. Control your delivery</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Speak loudly enough for the intended audience.</li>
    <li>Use a moderate pace and pause between important ideas.</li>
    <li>Pronounce words as clearly as you can.</li>
    <li>Use natural eye contact and posture.</li>
    <li>Reduce unnecessary fillers such as repeated <em>um</em> or <em>you know</em>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5. Adapt to the listener</h3>
  <p>Explain unfamiliar terms, give relevant examples and check understanding when necessary. A technical explanation for a specialist may require different vocabulary from an explanation for a younger learner.</p>

  <p><strong>Example:</strong> Instead of saying, <em>Things get dirty and then people become sick</em>, say, <em>When drinking water becomes contaminated with harmful microorganisms, people may develop waterborne illnesses.</em> The second version expresses the relationship more precisely.</p>', 1),
  (1559, 1559, '<h2>Asking and Answering Questions</h2>
  <p>Asking questions helps speakers obtain information, clarify meaning and encourage discussion. Answering questions requires listening carefully, understanding what is being asked and responding directly.</p>

  <h3>1. Recognise question words</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Who:</strong> asks about a person or people.</li>
    <li><strong>What:</strong> asks about an object, action or idea.</li>
    <li><strong>When:</strong> asks about time.</li>
    <li><strong>Where:</strong> asks about place.</li>
    <li><strong>Why:</strong> asks for a reason.</li>
    <li><strong>How:</strong> asks about a method, manner or process.</li>
    <li><strong>Which:</strong> asks someone to identify from a set of choices.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Form clear questions</h3>
  <p>Use a question form that matches the information you need. For example, <em>When does the meeting begin?</em> asks for a time, while <em>Why does the meeting begin early?</em> asks for a reason.</p>

  <h3>3. Listen before answering</h3>
  <p>Identify the exact question and any limits it contains. If asked for two reasons, give two relevant reasons. If asked to compare, explain both sides and the difference between them.</p>

  <h3>4. Answer directly and completely</h3>
  <p>Begin with the answer, then add the necessary explanation or evidence. Avoid unrelated information. If you do not know the answer, say so honestly or ask for clarification rather than inventing facts.</p>

  <h3>5. Ask for clarification politely</h3>
  <p>Useful expressions include <em>Could you please repeat the last point?</em>, <em>Do you mean that we should submit it today?</em> and <em>Could you explain what you mean by that term?</em></p>

  <p><strong>Example:</strong> Question: <em>Why should learners revise their work before submission?</em> Answer: <em>They should revise it to identify errors, improve clarity and ensure that all parts of the task are complete.</em></p>', 1),
  (1560, 1560, '<h2>Giving Instructions and Explanations Orally</h2>
  <p>Oral instructions tell listeners what actions to perform. Oral explanations help listeners understand how something works or why something happens. Both require accurate information, clear language and a logical sequence.</p>

  <h3>1. Giving instructions</h3>
  <p>Use action verbs and place steps in the order they must be performed. Include necessary quantities, locations, deadlines and restrictions. Pause between steps when listeners need time to act.</p>

  <h3>2. Giving explanations</h3>
  <p>State the idea first, then explain it using reasons, stages or examples. For a cause-and-effect explanation, make the relationship clear with words such as <em>because, therefore</em> and <em>as a result</em>.</p>

  <h3>3. Adjust to the audience</h3>
  <p>Use language appropriate to the listeners'' age, experience and familiarity with the task. Explain specialised terms when necessary. Do not assume that everyone already knows a process you find familiar.</p>

  <h3>4. Check understanding</h3>
  <p>Ask a listener to repeat the key steps or invite questions when accuracy matters. If someone misunderstands, explain the unclear part in a different way rather than merely repeating the same sentence more loudly.</p>

  <h3>5. Example: explaining a process</h3>
  <p><strong>Topic:</strong> How to prepare for a group presentation.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>First, agree on the topic and the main message.</li>
    <li>Next, divide the research and speaking tasks among group members.</li>
    <li>Then, combine the information and check its accuracy.</li>
    <li>Finally, rehearse the presentation and confirm who will present each section.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The sequence is clear, each action has a purpose, and the final step checks readiness before presenting.</p>', 1),
  (1561, 1561, '<h2>Turn-taking in Conversation</h2>
  <p>Turn-taking is the way speakers share opportunities to speak during a conversation or discussion. Effective turn-taking allows participants to contribute, listen, respond and disagree without unnecessarily interrupting one another.</p>

  <h3>1. Recognise when a turn is ending</h3>
  <p>A speaker may signal the end of a turn through a pause, a completed sentence, falling intonation or a question directed at someone else. These clues help another participant enter the conversation at an appropriate moment.</p>

  <h3>2. Enter the conversation politely</h3>
  <p>Useful expressions include <em>May I add something?</em>, <em>Could I respond to that point?</em> and <em>I would like to contribute an idea.</em> In a formal meeting, follow the chairperson''s rules for requesting the floor.</p>

  <h3>3. Show active listening</h3>
  <p>Listen to the speaker''s complete point before responding. Brief acknowledgements such as <em>I see</em> or <em>That is a useful point</em> can show attention, but avoid repeatedly interrupting with comments.</p>

  <h3>4. Disagree without silencing others</h3>
  <p>Challenge the idea rather than attacking the person. For example: <em>I understand your concern, but I interpret the evidence differently because...</em> Allow the other speaker to explain their reasoning.</p>

  <h3>5. Share participation fairly</h3>
  <p>Dominating a discussion prevents others from contributing. If you have spoken several times, make space for quieter participants. If someone has not had a chance to speak, invite their contribution respectfully.</p>

  <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>Could I build on your suggestion? I agree that we need more bins, and I think placing them near the dining hall would make them easier to use.</em></p>', 1),
  (1562, 1562, '<h2>Expressing Opinions and Giving Reasons</h2>
  <p>An opinion is a person''s view or judgement about an issue. In effective discussion, a speaker states the opinion clearly and supports it with relevant reasons or evidence.</p>

  <h3>1. State an opinion clearly</h3>
  <p>Useful expressions include <em>I believe that...</em>, <em>In my view...</em>, <em>I would argue that...</em> and <em>From my perspective...</em>. Choose an expression that suits the situation and the strength of your claim.</p>

  <h3>2. Support the opinion with reasons</h3>
  <p>Words such as <em>because, since</em> and <em>as</em> introduce reasons. Expressions such as <em>for example</em> introduce supporting illustrations. A reason should explain why the opinion is reasonable, not merely repeat it.</p>

  <h3>3. Distinguish opinions from facts</h3>
  <p>A fact can be checked against reliable evidence. An opinion expresses a judgement or preference. For example, ''The library opens at eight o''clock'' is a factual claim that can be verified. ''The library should open earlier'' is a recommendation that needs reasons.</p>

  <h3>4. A simple structure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Opinion:</strong> state your position.</li>
    <li><strong>Reason:</strong> explain why you hold it.</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence or example:</strong> support the reason where possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> connect the support to your position.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>In my view, the school should provide more reading time because regular reading builds vocabulary and comprehension. For example, learners who read a variety of texts encounter different sentence structures and ways of expressing ideas.</em></p>

  <h3>5. Be fair and precise</h3>
  <p>Avoid claiming that everyone agrees with you unless you have evidence. Recognise reasonable opposing views and explain why you still hold your position. Strong opinions become more convincing when they are supported by relevant evidence rather than insults or exaggeration.</p>', 1),
  (1563, 1563, '<h2>Agreement and Disagreement in Discussion</h2>
  <p>In a discussion, participants may support, partly support or oppose an idea. Effective speakers express their position clearly while remaining respectful of other people.</p>

  <h3>1. Expressing agreement</h3>
  <p>Useful expressions include <em>I agree with that point because...</em>, <em>That is a convincing reason</em> and <em>I would add that...</em>. When possible, explain what you agree with rather than simply saying ''yes''.</p>

  <h3>2. Expressing partial agreement</h3>
  <p>You may accept one part of an argument but disagree with another. For example: <em>I agree that the library needs more books, but I think we should also improve access to the books we already have.</em></p>

  <h3>3. Expressing disagreement</h3>
  <p>Useful expressions include <em>I see the point, but...</em>, <em>I respectfully disagree because...</em> and <em>Could we consider another explanation?</em> Explain the reason for your disagreement and address the idea rather than attacking the speaker.</p>

  <h3>4. Respond to the actual argument</h3>
  <p>Listen carefully before replying. Do not misrepresent the other person''s position or introduce an unrelated issue. If the other speaker provides new evidence, consider it rather than repeating your original point without reflection.</p>

  <h3>5. Reach a constructive outcome</h3>
  <p>When possible, identify common ground, clarify the remaining difference and suggest a practical next step. Agreement is not always necessary, but the discussion should improve understanding.</p>

  <p><strong>Example:</strong> <em>I agree that learners need more practice questions. However, I also believe they need explanations of their mistakes so they understand how to improve.</em></p>', 1),
  (1564, 1564, '<h2>Group Discussions</h2>
  <p>A group discussion is an exchange of ideas in which participants explore an issue, share evidence, ask questions and work towards a conclusion or decision. A productive discussion depends on preparation, listening and balanced participation.</p>

  <h3>1. Prepare for the discussion</h3>
  <p>Understand the topic, identify the question being discussed and gather relevant information. Prepare a few points and examples, but remain willing to consider new evidence.</p>

  <h3>2. Contribute relevant points</h3>
  <p>Make each contribution clear and connected to the topic. Explain your reasons and give examples where useful. Avoid repeating a point already made unless you are adding an important distinction.</p>

  <h3>3. Listen and respond</h3>
  <p>Pay attention to other contributions. You may build on a point, ask for clarification, provide a different example or respectfully challenge the reasoning. Respond to what was actually said.</p>

  <h3>4. Share responsibility</h3>
  <p>Participants should allow others to speak, remain respectful and help keep the conversation focused. A chairperson may guide the order of contributions, manage time and summarise emerging points.</p>

  <h3>5. Reach a conclusion</h3>
  <p>At the end, identify the strongest points, any areas of agreement, unresolved questions and the next action if one is required. A conclusion should reflect the discussion rather than simply repeat one participant''s opinion.</p>

  <p><strong>Example topic:</strong> Should the school introduce a weekly reading period?</p>
  <p>Participants might discuss benefits, timetable constraints, access to books and possible ways to organise the period before agreeing on a recommendation.</p>', 1),
  (1565, 1565, '<h2>Oral Presentations</h2>
  <p>An oral presentation is a prepared spoken explanation delivered to an audience. It may use slides, charts, objects or demonstrations, but the speaker''s main responsibility is to communicate the information clearly.</p>

  <h3>1. Plan the message</h3>
  <p>Identify the purpose, audience and time available. Select the most relevant information and organise it into an opening, a body with connected points, and a conclusion.</p>

  <h3>2. Prepare supporting material</h3>
  <p>Check facts, spellings, figures and sources before presenting. If you use slides or charts, make sure they are readable and directly support the point being explained. Avoid placing entire paragraphs on slides.</p>

  <h3>3. Rehearse the delivery</h3>
  <p>Practise the opening, transitions and conclusion. Time the presentation, pronounce difficult words and rehearse how you will explain any diagram or visual aid. Do not depend entirely on memorising every sentence.</p>

  <h3>4. Engage the audience</h3>
  <p>Speak audibly, use an appropriate pace, make natural eye contact and pause after important points. Use gestures where helpful, but avoid movements that distract from the message.</p>

  <h3>5. Handle questions</h3>
  <p>Listen to the whole question, answer directly and give evidence or clarification when needed. If you do not know an answer, acknowledge the limit honestly rather than inventing information.</p>

  <p><strong>Example structure for a short presentation on saving water:</strong> introduce the problem, explain two causes of water waste, present practical solutions, and conclude with one action the audience can take.</p>', 1),
  (1566, 1566, '<h2>Interviews</h2>
<p>An interview is a structured conversation in which one person asks questions to obtain information, assess suitability, or understand another person''s experiences and opinions.</p>

<h3>1. Parts of an interview</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Interviewer:</strong> asks questions and guides the conversation.</li>
  <li><strong>Interviewee:</strong> answers questions and provides relevant information.</li>
  <li><strong>Opening:</strong> greetings, introductions and an explanation of the purpose.</li>
  <li><strong>Main discussion:</strong> questions, answers and follow-up questions.</li>
  <li><strong>Closing:</strong> final questions, appreciation and polite departure.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Preparing for an interview</h3>
<p>Understand the purpose, anticipate likely questions, organise relevant examples and prepare questions to ask. For a school leadership interview, you might prepare examples of responsibility, teamwork and problem-solving.</p>

<h3>3. Answering effectively</h3>
<p>Listen to the complete question, answer directly, explain your point and provide a relevant example when appropriate.</p>
<p><strong>Example question:</strong> Why would you make a good class representative?</p>
<p><strong>Weak answer:</strong> Because I am good.</p>
<p><strong>Improved answer:</strong> I would make a good class representative because I listen to others, communicate their concerns respectfully and help organise class activities.</p>

<h3>4. Interview conduct</h3>
<p>Arrive on time, greet the interviewer politely, listen without interrupting and speak clearly. If you do not understand a question, ask for clarification rather than guessing.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Giving answers that are unrelated to the question.</li>
  <li>Memorising responses so rigidly that they sound unnatural.</li>
  <li>Interrupting, exaggerating achievements or using unnecessarily informal language.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1567, 1567, '<h2>Non-verbal Communication</h2>
<p>Non-verbal communication is the sharing of meaning without relying entirely on spoken or written words. It includes facial expressions, gestures, posture, eye contact, physical distance and aspects of the voice.</p>

<h3>1. Facial expressions</h3>
<p>Facial expressions can communicate emotions such as happiness, surprise, confusion or concern. However, an expression does not always reveal exactly what a person feels.</p>

<h3>2. Gestures</h3>
<p>Gestures are movements of the hands, arms or head that communicate meaning.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Raising a hand in class can signal a desire to contribute. Waving may communicate greeting or farewell.</p>
<p>A gesture can have different meanings in different cultural or social contexts, so avoid assuming that every gesture means the same thing everywhere.</p>

<h3>3. Posture and eye contact</h3>
<p>Posture refers to how someone sits, stands or holds their body. An attentive posture may help show engagement. Eye contact can support attention and confidence, but its appropriate amount varies across people and contexts.</p>

<h3>4. Tone, pace and pauses</h3>
<p>Although words carry the main verbal message, vocal features such as tone, volume, speed and pauses influence how the message is understood.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Saying ''That was helpful'' warmly may express genuine appreciation. Saying the same words with exaggerated emphasis may communicate sarcasm.</p>

<h3>5. Reading non-verbal signals carefully</h3>
<p>Interpret signals in context. A person who looks away may be thinking, distracted, uncomfortable or following a cultural convention. One signal alone is not reliable proof of someone''s feelings or intentions.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming that crossed arms always mean anger or rejection.</li>
  <li>Maintaining intense eye contact when it makes someone uncomfortable.</li>
  <li>Using gestures that conflict with the intended message.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1568, 1568, '<h2>Vowel Sounds</h2>
<p>Vowel sounds are speech sounds produced without a significant obstruction of the airflow through the mouth. In English, vowel sounds can be represented by the letters <strong>a, e, i, o, u</strong> and sometimes <strong>y</strong>, but the relationship between spelling and sound is not one-to-one.</p>

<h3>1. Vowel letters and vowel sounds</h3>
<p>Do not confuse a vowel letter with a vowel sound. The same letter may represent different sounds, and the same sound may be spelt in different ways.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>cat:</strong> the vowel letter a represents the vowel sound in /kæt/.</li>
  <li><strong>cake:</strong> the letter a represents a different vowel sound in /keɪk/.</li>
  <li><strong>see</strong> and <strong>sea:</strong> different spellings represent the same vowel sound in many varieties of English.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Short and long vowel sounds</h3>
<p>In school pronunciation work, vowels are often compared as short and long sounds. These labels describe sound contrasts, not simply how long a letter is written.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>ship</strong> /ɪ/ and <strong>sheep</strong> /iː/ have contrasting vowel sounds.</li>
  <li><strong>full</strong> /ʊ/ and <strong>fool</strong> /uː/ have contrasting vowel sounds.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pronunciation varies between English accents. The International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA) provides symbols for distinguishing sounds more precisely.</p>

<h3>3. Diphthongs</h3>
<p>A diphthong is a vowel sound in which the tongue position changes noticeably within one syllable.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>day</strong> commonly contains /eɪ/.</li>
  <li><strong>boy</strong> commonly contains /ɔɪ/.</li>
  <li><strong>my</strong> commonly contains /aɪ/.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. How to practise vowel contrasts</h3>
<p>Say each word naturally, listen for the difference and repeat the pair. Keep the consonants as similar as possible so that you focus on the vowel.</p>
<p><strong>Practice pair:</strong> ship — sheep. The main contrast is /ɪ/ versus /iː/.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming that each vowel letter always has one sound.</li>
  <li>Pronouncing a word from its spelling without considering its accepted pronunciation.</li>
  <li>Confusing a difference in vowel quality with a difference in spelling alone.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1569, 1569, '<h2>Consonant Sounds</h2>
<p>Consonant sounds are produced when airflow is obstructed or narrowed at some point in the vocal tract. The obstruction may involve the lips, teeth, tongue or other speech organs.</p>

<h3>1. Voiced and voiceless consonants</h3>
<p>Voiced consonants involve vibration of the vocal folds; voiceless consonants do not involve this vibration in the same way.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>/p/ and /b/:</strong> compare ''pat'' and ''bat''.</li>
  <li><strong>/f/ and /v/:</strong> compare ''fan'' and ''van''.</li>
  <li><strong>/s/ and /z/:</strong> compare ''sip'' and ''zip''.</li>
</ul>
<p>To feel the difference, gently place your fingers on your throat as you produce a sustained /s/ and then /z/. The voiced /z/ produces vocal-fold vibration.</p>

<h3>2. Places of articulation</h3>
<p>The place of articulation describes where the airflow is obstructed or narrowed.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bilabial:</strong> both lips, as in /p/, /b/ and /m/.</li>
  <li><strong>Labiodental:</strong> lower lip and upper teeth, as in /f/ and /v/.</li>
  <li><strong>Dental:</strong> tongue near or against the teeth, as in /θ/ in ''thin'' and /ð/ in ''this''.</li>
  <li><strong>Alveolar:</strong> tongue near the ridge behind the upper teeth, as in /t/, /d/, /s/ and /z/.</li>
  <li><strong>Velar:</strong> the back of the tongue approaches the soft palate, as in /k/ and /ɡ/.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Manner of articulation</h3>
<p>The manner of articulation describes how airflow is controlled.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Plosives:</strong> airflow is briefly blocked and released, as in /p/, /b/, /t/, /d/, /k/ and /ɡ/.</li>
  <li><strong>Fricatives:</strong> air passes through a narrow gap, creating friction, as in /f/, /v/, /s/ and /z/.</li>
  <li><strong>Nasals:</strong> air flows through the nose, as in /m/, /n/ and /ŋ/.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Sound versus letter</h3>
<p>A consonant sound is not the same as a consonant letter. For example, ''ship'' begins with the two-letter spelling <strong>sh</strong>, which represents one consonant sound /ʃ/.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming that every written consonant represents a separate sound.</li>
  <li>Confusing voiced and voiceless pairs such as /f/ and /v/.</li>
  <li>Mixing up the place of articulation with the manner of articulation.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1570, 1570, '<h2>Similar and Contrasting Sounds</h2>
<p>English contains words that differ by only one speech sound. Learning to hear and produce these contrasts helps prevent confusion in speaking, listening and spelling.</p>

<h3>1. Minimal pairs</h3>
<p>A minimal pair consists of two words that differ in one sound in the same position and have different meanings.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>pin — bin:</strong> /p/ contrasts with /b/ at the beginning.</li>
  <li><strong>fan — van:</strong> /f/ contrasts with /v/ at the beginning.</li>
  <li><strong>ship — sheep:</strong> /ɪ/ contrasts with /iː/ in the vowel position.</li>
  <li><strong>cap — cup:</strong> the vowel sounds contrast.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Hearing the contrast</h3>
<p>To practise, say each pair slowly, then at normal speed. Pay attention to the exact position of the sound difference. Do not change several sounds at once.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> In ''pin'' and ''bin'', the vowel and final consonant remain the same. Only the initial consonant changes.</p>

<h3>3. Producing the contrast</h3>
<p>For consonant pairs, notice where the tongue or lips move and whether the vocal folds vibrate. For vowel pairs, notice the shape of the mouth and the position of the tongue.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> For /s/ and /z/, keep the tongue position similar but add vocal-fold vibration for /z/.</p>

<h3>4. Sounds that learners may confuse</h3>
<p>Some English contrasts may be unfamiliar to speakers of particular languages. Practise the actual contrast rather than assuming that two sounds are identical because they are written similarly.</p>
<p>Accents vary, and not all speakers maintain every contrast in the same way. Aim for consistent, understandable pronunciation.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Changing both the vowel and consonant when practising a minimal pair.</li>
  <li>Relying only on spelling instead of listening to the sounds.</li>
  <li>Repeating a word quickly before learning how the contrasting sounds are produced.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1571, 1571, '<h2>Syllables</h2>
<p>A syllable is a unit of pronunciation organised around a vowel sound. A word may contain one syllable or several syllables. Count the sounds heard in normal pronunciation, not simply the number of vowel letters.</p>

<h3>1. Counting syllables</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>book</strong> — one syllable.</li>
  <li><strong>teacher</strong> — two syllables: teach-er.</li>
  <li><strong>computer</strong> — three syllables: com-pu-ter.</li>
  <li><strong>education</strong> — commonly four syllables: ed-u-ca-tion.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Syllables and vowel sounds</h3>
<p>Each syllable normally has a vowel sound as its centre. Two written vowel letters may represent one vowel sound, as in <strong>rain</strong>. A word may also contain a vowel letter that is not pronounced, as the final e in <strong>cake</strong>.</p>

<h3>3. Open and closed syllables</h3>
<p>An <strong>open syllable</strong> ends in a vowel sound, as in the first syllable of <strong>o-pen</strong>. A <strong>closed syllable</strong> ends in a consonant sound, as in <strong>cat</strong> or the first syllable of <strong>pic-nic</strong>.</p>
<p>These patterns can help with reading and spelling, although English pronunciation has exceptions.</p>

<h3>4. Finding syllables</h3>
<p>Say the word naturally and listen for its vowel-sound units. You may tap once for each syllable, then check your result by pronouncing the word slowly without artificially adding sounds.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''Important'' is commonly pronounced with three syllables: im-por-tant.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Counting vowel letters instead of vowel sounds.</li>
  <li>Giving every written vowel a separate syllable.</li>
  <li>Breaking words into syllables in a way that changes their natural pronunciation.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1572, 1572, '<h2>Word Stress</h2>
<p>Word stress is the emphasis placed on one syllable in a word. A stressed syllable is often more prominent because of a combination of loudness, length, pitch and vowel quality.</p>

<h3>1. Identifying stressed syllables</h3>
<p>In pronunciation guides, primary stress is often marked with /ˈ/ immediately before the stressed syllable.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>TAble:</strong> /ˈteɪbəl/ — stress on the first syllable.</li>
  <li><strong>beGIN:</strong> /bɪˈɡɪn/ — stress on the second syllable.</li>
  <li><strong>imPORtant:</strong> /ɪmˈpɔːtənt/ in a common British pronunciation — stress on the second syllable.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pronunciation may vary between English accents, but the stress pattern should remain consistent within the variety being used.</p>

<h3>2. Stress can distinguish words</h3>
<p>Some words have different stress patterns depending on their grammatical use.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>REcord</strong> — a noun meaning a written account.</li>
  <li><strong>reCORD</strong> — a verb meaning to capture or store information.</li>
  <li><strong>PREsent</strong> — a noun meaning a gift, or an adjective meaning current.</li>
  <li><strong>preSENT</strong> — a verb meaning to show or deliver something.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Finding word stress</h3>
<p>Listen to a reliable pronunciation model, repeat the word and identify its most prominent syllable. Use a dictionary''s pronunciation guide when unsure. Do not assume that every long word has stress on the same syllable.</p>

<h3>4. Practising stress</h3>
<p>Say the stressed syllable clearly without turning every syllable into an equally strong one.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> In ''begin'', the second syllable is stressed: be-GIN. Giving equal emphasis to both syllables may sound unnatural.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Putting stress on the wrong syllable.</li>
  <li>Giving every syllable equal prominence.</li>
  <li>Assuming spelling alone reveals the stress pattern.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1573, 1573, '<h2>Sentence Stress</h2>
<p>Sentence stress is the emphasis placed on particular words in a spoken sentence. It helps listeners identify the most important information and can change the meaning or implication of a message.</p>

<h3>1. Content words and function words</h3>
<p>Content words commonly carry the main information. They include nouns, main verbs, adjectives and many adverbs. Function words include articles, auxiliary verbs, pronouns and prepositions; these are often less prominent, although they can be stressed for emphasis.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''The BOY bought a NEW BAG.'' The nouns and adjective carry much of the information.</p>

<h3>2. Stress changes the focus</h3>
<p>Emphasising a different word can correct an assumption or highlight a contrast.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>I</strong> borrowed your book. — I did it, not someone else.</li>
  <li>I <strong>borrowed</strong> your book. — I borrowed it; I did not buy or steal it.</li>
  <li>I borrowed your <strong>book</strong>. — It was your book, not another item.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Stress in questions and corrections</h3>
<p>Speakers often stress words that express contrast or answer a question.</p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong> Who submitted the report?</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>MARY</strong> submitted the report.</p>
<p>The stress on ''Mary'' highlights the person who performed the action.</p>

<h3>4. Practising sentence stress</h3>
<p>First identify the message you want to communicate. Then choose the word that carries the main information or contrast and make it prominent. Read the sentence aloud and check whether the emphasis matches your intended meaning.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Giving every word equal emphasis.</li>
  <li>Stressing a word that does not match the intended message.</li>
  <li>Assuming a sentence has only one possible stress pattern regardless of context.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1574, 1574, '<h2>Rhythm</h2>
<p>Rhythm in spoken English is the pattern created by stressed and unstressed syllables, pauses and the timing of speech. It helps speech sound connected and organised rather than like a sequence of isolated words.</p>

<h3>1. Stressed and unstressed syllables</h3>
<p>In a phrase, some syllables receive more prominence than others. Function words are often reduced in ordinary speech, while important content words are more prominent.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''The students are reading a book.'' In natural speech, ''students'', ''reading'' and ''book'' may carry more prominence than ''the'', ''are'' and ''a''. The exact pattern depends on the intended message.</p>

<h3>2. Rhythm groups</h3>
<p>Speakers organise words into meaningful groups. Pauses and changes in prominence can separate ideas, but excessive pauses may make speech difficult to follow.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''After the lesson, / we went to the library.'' The pause separates the introductory phrase from the main clause.</p>

<h3>3. Linking words in connected speech</h3>
<p>In fluent speech, words often connect smoothly. Sounds may link across word boundaries, and unstressed words may be pronounced more weakly. This is normal connected speech, not careless pronunciation.</p>

<h3>4. Using rhythm effectively</h3>
<p>To practise, read a short sentence aloud, mark its important words, and group related ideas together. Keep a steady, natural flow and pause at meaningful boundaries.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Pausing between every word.</li>
  <li>Speaking so quickly that important words become unclear.</li>
  <li>Using the same rhythm for every sentence regardless of its meaning.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1575, 1575, '<h2>Intonation</h2>
<p>Intonation is the pattern of pitch movement in spoken language. Pitch may rise, fall or move in more complex ways across a phrase or sentence. Intonation helps communicate whether a speaker is asking, stating, checking, contrasting or expressing an attitude.</p>

<h3>1. Falling intonation</h3>
<p>A falling pattern often occurs in statements and many wh-questions, although the pattern depends on context and the speaker''s intention.</p>
<ul>
  <li>''The lesson is over.'' — a falling pattern can communicate a complete statement.</li>
  <li>''Where are you going?'' — a falling pattern is common in a direct wh-question.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Rising intonation</h3>
<p>A rising pattern often occurs in yes/no questions and can indicate that the speaker is checking information or inviting confirmation.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''Are you ready?'' A rise towards the end is common in many varieties of English.</p>

<h3>3. Intonation and attitude</h3>
<p>Intonation can suggest uncertainty, interest, surprise or incompleteness, but pitch alone does not prove a speaker''s feelings. Interpret it together with the words and context.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''Really?'' may express genuine surprise, a request for confirmation or disbelief, depending on its pitch movement and the situation.</p>

<h3>4. Intonation in lists and continuing ideas</h3>
<p>A speaker may use rising or continuing pitch on items in a list and a fall on the final item to signal completion.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> ''We bought rice, beans and flour.'' The speaker may keep the pitch open across earlier items and use a falling pattern on ''flour''. Patterns vary by context.</p>

<h3>5. Practising intonation</h3>
<p>Listen to a clear spoken model, imitate its pitch movement and consider the speaker''s intention. Practise the same words with different intonation patterns to hear how the implication can change.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming every question must end with rising intonation.</li>
  <li>Confusing pitch movement with loudness.</li>
  <li>Interpreting an intonation pattern without considering context.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1576, 1576, '<h2>Formal and Informal Language</h2>
<p>Formal and informal language are different styles of communication. The appropriate style depends on the audience, purpose, relationship and situation.</p>

<h3>1. Formal language</h3>
<p>Formal language is commonly used in official letters, applications, academic writing, reports and communication with people in positions of responsibility.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Uses respectful and precise expressions.</li>
  <li>Avoids slang and unnecessarily casual abbreviations.</li>
  <li>Uses complete sentences and appropriate grammar.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "I am writing to request permission to attend the meeting."</p>

<h3>2. Informal language</h3>
<p>Informal language is commonly used when communicating with friends, relatives and people with whom one has a familiar relationship.</p>
<ul>
  <li>May use contractions, everyday expressions and conversational language.</li>
  <li>May be more relaxed and personal.</li>
  <li>Still requires clarity and respect.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "Can I come to the meeting?"</p>

<h3>3. Choosing the right style</h3>
<p>Consider three questions before speaking or writing:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Audience:</strong> Who will receive the message?</li>
  <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Am I requesting, explaining, reporting or chatting?</li>
  <li><strong>Situation:</strong> Is the communication official or personal?</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Changing the register</h3>
<p>Changing register means adjusting language to suit a new situation without changing the essential message.</p>
<p><strong>Informal:</strong> "Send me the notes now."</p>
<p><strong>Formal:</strong> "Could you please send me the notes at your earliest convenience?"</p>
<p>The formal version is more courteous and suitable for many official situations.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using slang in an official application.</li>
  <li>Assuming formal language must always be complicated.</li>
  <li>Using informal language that sounds rude or demanding.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1577, 1577, '<h2>Polite Requests and Responses</h2>
<p>A request asks someone to do something. A polite request communicates the need while respecting the other person''s choice, time and circumstances.</p>

<h3>1. Making polite requests</h3>
<p>Common structures include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Could you ...?</strong> "Could you explain this question?"</li>
  <li><strong>Would you mind ...?</strong> "Would you mind closing the window?"</li>
  <li><strong>May I ...?</strong> "May I borrow your dictionary?"</li>
  <li><strong>Could I please ...?</strong> "Could I please use your calculator?"</li>
</ul>
<p>These expressions are generally more courteous than direct commands such as "Give me your book."</p>

<h3>2. Responding positively</h3>
<p>When accepting a request, answer clearly and courteously.</p>
<p><strong>Request:</strong> "Could you help me carry these books?"</p>
<p><strong>Response:</strong> "Of course. I would be happy to help."</p>

<h3>3. Declining politely</h3>
<p>If you cannot fulfil a request, acknowledge it, explain briefly if appropriate and offer an alternative when possible.</p>
<p><strong>Request:</strong> "Could you lend me your notes now?"</p>
<p><strong>Response:</strong> "I''m sorry, I need them for revision at the moment. I can lend them to you this evening."</p>

<h3>4. Responding to ''Would you mind ...?''</h3>
<p>When someone asks, "Would you mind opening the window?", saying "No, not at all" normally means you are willing to do it. Saying "Yes, I would" normally means you do mind. Because the wording can be confusing, a clear response such as "Certainly, I''ll open it" avoids ambiguity.</p>

<h3>5. Tone and context</h3>
<p>Politeness depends on more than a sentence structure. A respectful tone, appropriate timing and acknowledgement of the other person''s response also matter.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using commands when a request is more appropriate.</li>
  <li>Responding to a negative question with an unclear "yes" or "no".</li>
  <li>Making a request sound polite while ignoring the other person''s refusal.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1578, 1578, '<h2>Offers and Invitations</h2>
<p>An offer expresses willingness to provide help, an item or a service. An invitation asks someone to attend an event or take part in an activity.</p>

<h3>1. Making offers</h3>
<p>Common expressions include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>"Would you like some water?"</li>
  <li>"Shall I help you carry those books?"</li>
  <li>"Can I get you a chair?"</li>
  <li>"I can help you prepare the presentation if you wish."</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Accepting offers</h3>
<p>Accept clearly and show appreciation when appropriate.</p>
<p><strong>Offer:</strong> "Would you like some tea?"</p>
<p><strong>Response:</strong> "Yes, please. Thank you."</p>

<h3>3. Declining offers</h3>
<p>Decline respectfully when you do not need or want the offer.</p>
<p><strong>Offer:</strong> "Shall I carry your bag?"</p>
<p><strong>Response:</strong> "No, thank you. I can manage."</p>

<h3>4. Making invitations</h3>
<p>Include the activity or event and, where necessary, the date, time and place.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "Would you like to join us for the debate on Friday at 3 p.m. in the school hall?"</p>

<h3>5. Accepting or declining invitations</h3>
<p><strong>Accepting:</strong> "Thank you for inviting me. I would be delighted to attend."</p>
<p><strong>Declining:</strong> "Thank you for the invitation. Unfortunately, I will not be available that afternoon."</p>
<p>If appropriate, you can suggest another time. Do not accept an invitation merely to avoid disappointing the person if you cannot attend.</p>

<h3>6. Distinguishing offers from invitations</h3>
<p>An offer proposes help or something for another person to receive. An invitation proposes participation in an event or activity. The context determines which function a sentence performs.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Leaving out essential details from an invitation.</li>
  <li>Declining abruptly without acknowledging the offer.</li>
  <li>Confusing offering help with inviting someone to an event.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1579, 1579, '<h2>Advice and Suggestions</h2>
<p>Advice recommends what someone should do in a situation. A suggestion proposes an idea or possible action for consideration. Both can help people make decisions or solve problems.</p>

<h3>1. Giving advice</h3>
<p>Common structures include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>should:</strong> "You should revise the difficult topics first."</li>
  <li><strong>ought to:</strong> "You ought to apologise for arriving late."</li>
  <li><strong>had better:</strong> "You had better leave now if you want to catch the bus."</li>
  <li><strong>If I were you ...:</strong> "If I were you, I would ask the teacher for clarification."</li>
</ul>
<p>''Had better'' often communicates a stronger recommendation and may imply a negative consequence if the advice is ignored.</p>

<h3>2. Making suggestions</h3>
<p>Common expressions include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>"Why don''t we study together?"</li>
  <li>"How about forming a revision group?"</li>
  <li>"We could ask the teacher for another example."</li>
  <li>"Let''s review the question step by step."</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Responding to advice</h3>
<p>You can accept advice, ask for clarification or explain respectfully why you will choose another option.</p>
<p><strong>Accepting:</strong> "That is helpful advice. I will try it."</p>
<p><strong>Seeking clarification:</strong> "Could you explain how that would help?"</p>
<p><strong>Declining:</strong> "Thank you for the suggestion, but I think another approach would work better for me."</p>

<h3>4. Making advice useful</h3>
<p>Good advice should be relevant to the problem, practical and specific. Instead of saying "Work harder", identify an action the person can take.</p>
<p><strong>Vague:</strong> "Improve your English."</p>
<p><strong>Specific:</strong> "Read one short article each day, note unfamiliar words and use five of them in your own sentences."</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Confusing ''should'' with ''must'' when an action is recommended rather than compulsory.</li>
  <li>Giving general advice without a practical next step.</li>
  <li>Presenting suggestions in a way that sounds insulting or controlling.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1580, 1580, '<h2>Apologies and Complaints</h2>
<p>An apology acknowledges a mistake or harm and expresses regret. A complaint communicates dissatisfaction about a problem and may request an explanation, correction or solution.</p>

<h3>1. Making an apology</h3>
<p>A clear apology identifies the problem, accepts appropriate responsibility and explains how the situation will be addressed.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "I apologise for submitting the assignment late. I should have informed you earlier, and I will plan my work more carefully next time."</p>

<h3>2. Responding to apologies</h3>
<p>Depending on the situation, you may accept an apology, explain the effect of the action or discuss how to prevent the problem from happening again.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "Thank you for apologising. Please let me know earlier if you expect another delay."</p>

<h3>3. Making a complaint</h3>
<p>An effective complaint describes the problem accurately, provides relevant details and states what action would resolve it.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "I am writing to report that the library computer has not been working since Monday. Could the fault please be investigated?"</p>

<h3>4. Formal and informal complaints</h3>
<p>A complaint to a close friend may use conversational language. A complaint to a school administrator, service provider or organisation usually requires a more formal and factual style.</p>

<h3>5. Keeping complaints constructive</h3>
<p>Focus on the behaviour, event or problem rather than attacking the person''s character. Avoid insults, threats and unsupported accusations.</p>
<p><strong>Unhelpful:</strong> "Your staff are completely useless."</p>
<p><strong>Constructive:</strong> "I waited for an hour after my appointment time. Could you explain the delay and advise how this can be avoided in future?"</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Apologising without acknowledging what went wrong.</li>
  <li>Making a complaint without explaining the problem.</li>
  <li>Using insulting language that makes a solution less likely.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1581, 1581, '<h2>Persuasion and Negotiation</h2>
<p>Persuasion is the attempt to influence someone''s beliefs, opinions or actions using reasons, evidence and appropriate language. Negotiation is a discussion in which people with different needs or positions try to reach an acceptable agreement.</p>

<h3>1. Persuasion through reasons and evidence</h3>
<p>A persuasive message should make a clear claim, support it with relevant reasons and provide evidence or examples where possible.</p>
<p><strong>Weak claim:</strong> "Our school should have a reading club because reading is good."</p>
<p><strong>Stronger argument:</strong> "Our school should establish a reading club because regular reading builds vocabulary and comprehension. Members could meet weekly to discuss books and share reading strategies."</p>

<h3>2. Persuasive language</h3>
<p>Useful techniques include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Facts:</strong> information that can be checked.</li>
  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> specific cases that illustrate a point.</li>
  <li><strong>Rhetorical questions:</strong> questions used to make an audience reflect.</li>
  <li><strong>Inclusive language:</strong> words such as ''we'' and ''our'' that create a shared sense of purpose.</li>
  <li><strong>Repetition:</strong> repeating a key phrase for emphasis.</li>
</ul>
<p>Persuasion is strongest when these techniques support a sound argument rather than replace evidence.</p>

<h3>3. Understanding negotiation</h3>
<p>Negotiation involves explaining your needs, listening to the other person, identifying shared interests and considering possible compromises.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> A group wants to meet after school, but one member must catch an early bus. They could agree to meet during lunch or share some tasks online.</p>

<h3>4. Reaching an agreement</h3>
<p>Identify the problem, state each person''s needs, suggest options, compare the options and confirm the final agreement. A fair agreement should be clear and realistic.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using threats or insults instead of reasons.</li>
  <li>Making claims without reliable support.</li>
  <li>Treating negotiation as a contest in which only one person can benefit.</li>
  <li>Agreeing to terms that remain unclear.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1582, 1582, '<h2>Language for Different Audiences</h2>
<p>Effective communication adapts language to the people receiving a message. Age, familiarity, knowledge, role, purpose and situation can influence the vocabulary, detail and tone that are appropriate.</p>

<h3>1. Identifying the audience</h3>
<p>Before communicating, consider who will receive the message and what they already know.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Classmates:</strong> familiar examples and direct explanations may be appropriate.</li>
  <li><strong>School administrators:</strong> respectful, organised and precise language is usually expected.</li>
  <li><strong>Young learners:</strong> simple vocabulary and concrete examples may help explain unfamiliar ideas.</li>
  <li><strong>Specialists:</strong> technical terms may be appropriate when the audience understands them.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Adjusting vocabulary and detail</h3>
<p>Use words that the audience can understand. Explain technical terms when needed, and avoid unnecessary detail when the purpose is to give a brief instruction.</p>
<p><strong>For a younger learner:</strong> "Evaporation happens when liquid water changes into water vapour."</p>
<p><strong>For a science report:</strong> "Evaporation is the process by which molecules at a liquid''s surface enter the gaseous state."</p>
<p>Both statements describe the same process, but they use different levels of technical detail.</p>

<h3>3. Adjusting tone</h3>
<p>Tone may be formal, friendly, reassuring, persuasive or neutral. Choose a tone that suits the audience without becoming dishonest or disrespectful.</p>

<h3>4. Keeping the message accurate</h3>
<p>Adapting a message does not mean changing facts to please an audience. Simplify explanations where useful, but preserve the essential meaning.</p>

<h3>5. Checking effectiveness</h3>
<p>Ask whether the audience can understand the main point, whether the vocabulary is suitable and whether the message achieves its purpose. If confusion remains, clarify the wording or give a relevant example.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using unexplained technical vocabulary with beginners.</li>
  <li>Speaking to adults in a patronising way when simplifying information.</li>
  <li>Changing the facts while adapting the language.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1583, 1583, '<h2>Resolving Miscommunication</h2>
<p>Miscommunication occurs when a message is misunderstood, interpreted differently from the speaker''s intention or not received as intended. It may result from unclear wording, unfamiliar vocabulary, assumptions, distractions, tone or differences in context.</p>

<h3>1. Identifying the source of misunderstanding</h3>
<p>Before responding, identify what was said, what was understood and where the difference arose.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> A teacher says, "Submit the work by Friday." One learner assumes this means Friday morning, while another thinks the deadline is the end of Friday. The time has not been specified clearly enough.</p>

<h3>2. Asking for clarification</h3>
<p>Useful expressions include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>"Could you explain what you mean by that?"</li>
  <li>"Do you mean that we should submit it before lessons begin on Friday?"</li>
  <li>"Let me check that I have understood correctly."</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Paraphrasing</h3>
<p>Paraphrasing means expressing another person''s message in your own words to check your understanding.</p>
<p><strong>Original:</strong> "Please bring the completed form next week."</p>
<p><strong>Paraphrase:</strong> "Just to confirm, you want me to bring the finished form sometime next week?"</p>
<p>If the timing matters, ask a more specific follow-up question.</p>

<h3>4. Correcting a misunderstanding</h3>
<p>Explain your intended meaning calmly, provide missing information and acknowledge any wording that caused confusion.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "I am sorry I was unclear. I meant that the meeting begins at 2 p.m., not that we should arrive at 2 p.m."</p>

<h3>5. Preventing future misunderstandings</h3>
<p>Use precise wording, specify dates and times, avoid unexplained abbreviations and invite questions. In written communication, reread the message before sending it.</p>

<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assuming that a misunderstanding was deliberate.</li>
  <li>Arguing before checking what the other person understood.</li>
  <li>Repeating the same unclear message instead of explaining it differently.</li>
</ul>', 1),
  (1584, 1584, '<h2>Reading Digital Texts</h2>
  <p>Digital texts are messages and documents read on electronic devices. Examples include websites, emails, online articles, electronic notices, learning platforms and social media posts.</p>
  <h3>How digital reading differs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hyperlinks:</strong> Connect the reader to another page or resource.</li>
    <li><strong>Headings and menus:</strong> Help readers locate information quickly.</li>
    <li><strong>Search tools:</strong> Help locate names, phrases or topics within a page.</li>
    <li><strong>Multimedia:</strong> Text may be combined with images, audio, video or interactive elements.</li>
    <li><strong>Updates:</strong> Online information can change, so dates and versions may matter.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>A purposeful reading method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the purpose: what do you need to find out?</li>
    <li>Scan the title, headings, date and source.</li>
    <li>Read the relevant sections carefully.</li>
    <li>Follow links only when they help answer your question, and check where they lead.</li>
    <li>Summarise the information and verify important claims.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>You need the deadline for a school application. On an official notice, locate the application heading, identify the deadline, and check the year and any later update. Do not rely on an old screenshot if a current official notice is available.</p>
  <h3>Read links critically</h3>
  <p>A link''s visible wording may not fully describe the page it opens. Check the destination before entering personal information or downloading a file. A professional-looking website is not automatically trustworthy.</p>
  <h3>Common errors</h3>
  <p>Do not assume that the first search result is the most reliable, that a popular post is accurate, or that a date shown in an old article applies today. Separate what the text states from what you infer.</p>', 1),
  (1585, 1585, '<h2>Visual Information</h2>
  <p>Visual information communicates meaning through images, symbols, diagrams, maps, tables, charts, graphs, posters and infographics. Readers must interpret both what is shown and how it is presented.</p>
  <h3>Common forms</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tables:</strong> Organise information into rows and columns.</li>
    <li><strong>Bar charts:</strong> Compare quantities across categories.</li>
    <li><strong>Line graphs:</strong> Show how values change, often over time.</li>
    <li><strong>Pie charts:</strong> Show parts of a whole.</li>
    <li><strong>Maps:</strong> Represent locations, routes or spatial patterns.</li>
    <li><strong>Posters and infographics:</strong> Combine words and visuals to inform or persuade.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to interpret a visual</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the title to identify the subject.</li>
    <li>Check labels, units, keys, scales and the source.</li>
    <li>Identify the values or symbols relevant to the question.</li>
    <li>Compare the relevant information accurately.</li>
    <li>State a conclusion supported by the visual, not by guesswork.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A bar chart shows books borrowed in one week: Monday 12, Tuesday 18 and Wednesday 15. Tuesday had the highest number. The difference between Tuesday and Monday was 18 − 12 = 6 books.</p>
  <h3>Notice how visuals can mislead</h3>
  <p>A graph with a vertical axis that starts above zero can make small differences look much larger. Images can also distract from missing evidence. Always inspect the scale and labels before drawing a conclusion.</p>
  <h3>Common errors</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse a category''s label with its value, ignore units, or claim that one factor caused another merely because the graph shows a relationship.</p>', 1),
  (1586, 1586, '<h2>Purpose and Audience</h2>
  <p>Every text is produced for a reason and intended for particular readers or listeners. Identifying the purpose and audience helps you understand why the writer chose specific words, details and structures.</p>
  <h3>Common purposes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>To inform:</strong> Present facts or explain something.</li>
    <li><strong>To persuade:</strong> Influence beliefs, opinions or actions.</li>
    <li><strong>To entertain:</strong> Amuse or engage an audience.</li>
    <li><strong>To instruct:</strong> Explain how to perform a task.</li>
    <li><strong>To warn:</strong> Alert people to a danger or risk.</li>
    <li><strong>To describe:</strong> Give details about a person, place, object or experience.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Identifying the audience</h3>
  <p>Consider who the text addresses, their likely knowledge, their needs and the situation in which they will receive the message. The intended audience may be named directly or implied by the vocabulary and content.</p>
  <h3>Worked examples</h3>
  <p><strong>Text A:</strong> "To operate the machine, press the green button and wait for the indicator light."</p>
  <p>Purpose: to instruct. Audience: people operating the machine.</p>
  <p><strong>Text B:</strong> "Join our clean-up campaign this Saturday and help make our neighbourhood safer and healthier."</p>
  <p>Purpose: to persuade people to participate. Audience: members of the neighbourhood.</p>
  <h3>Use evidence, not guesses</h3>
  <p>Words such as "must", "join", "discover" or "beware" may suggest a purpose, but one word is not always enough. Look at the whole message, its structure and the action it encourages.</p>
  <h3>Common error</h3>
  <p>Do not confuse the topic with the purpose. A text about road safety might inform readers about accidents, warn them about danger or persuade them to obey traffic rules.</p>', 1),
  (1587, 1587, '<h2>Evaluating Sources</h2>
  <p>A source is where information comes from, such as a book, website, interview, report, video or social media post. Evaluating a source means deciding how suitable and trustworthy it is for a particular purpose.</p>
  <h3>Four questions to ask</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Authority:</strong> Who produced the information? Do they have relevant knowledge or responsibility?</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> Are claims supported by data, documents, examples or identifiable sources?</li>
    <li><strong>Currency:</strong> When was it published or updated? Does the topic require recent information?</li>
    <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Is the source informing, selling, entertaining, persuading or promoting a particular viewpoint?</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Check claims against other evidence</h3>
  <p>For important claims, compare the source with independent, credible sources. Check whether they support the same facts and whether the original evidence can be traced. Several websites repeating one unverified claim do not necessarily provide independent confirmation.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A social media account claims that a new school examination rule has been introduced. Before accepting the claim, check the relevant examination authority''s official announcement, its publication date and whether the rule actually applies to your examination.</p>
  <h3>Match the source to the task</h3>
  <p>A personal account can be useful for understanding someone''s experience, but it may not establish a national statistic. An official document may be authoritative about a published rule, but it may not explain every person''s experience of that rule.</p>
  <h3>Common errors</h3>
  <p>Do not assume that a source is accurate because it looks professional, has many followers or appears high in search results. Also, do not reject a source solely because it is unfamiliar; examine its evidence and relevance.</p>', 1),
  (1588, 1588, '<h2>Misleading Claims</h2>
  <p>A misleading claim gives an inaccurate impression of the facts. It may contain a false statement, omit important context, exaggerate evidence or present an opinion as if it were an established fact.</p>
  <h3>Common warning signs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Exaggeration:</strong> "This method works perfectly for everyone."</li>
    <li><strong>Missing context:</strong> Reporting a number without explaining what it measures or when it was collected.</li>
    <li><strong>Unsupported certainty:</strong> Presenting a prediction as a guaranteed outcome.</li>
    <li><strong>Misleading comparison:</strong> Comparing two figures measured under different conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Emotional pressure:</strong> Using fear or urgency to discourage careful checking.</li>
    <li><strong>False implication:</strong> Suggesting that one event caused another without evidence.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Fact, opinion and inference</h3>
  <p>A <strong>fact</strong> is a statement that can be checked against evidence. An <strong>opinion</strong> expresses a judgement or belief. An <strong>inference</strong> is a conclusion drawn from available information. Inferences can be reasonable, but they are not automatically proven facts.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p><strong>Claim:</strong> "Our club''s membership doubled this term, so our club is the best in the school."</p>
  <p>The increase in membership may be verifiable if the figures are accurate. However, the conclusion that the club is the best does not follow from membership growth alone. Other evidence would be needed to assess quality.</p>
  <h3>How to examine a claim</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify exactly what is being claimed.</li>
    <li>Find the evidence and its original source.</li>
    <li>Check dates, definitions, comparisons and missing information.</li>
    <li>Decide whether the evidence supports the full claim or only part of it.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Common error</h3>
  <p>A claim is not necessarily false just because it is persuasive or emotional. Evaluate its evidence rather than accepting or rejecting it based only on how it sounds.</p>', 1),
  (1589, 1589, '<h2>Comparing Sources</h2>
  <p>Comparing sources means examining two or more accounts of a subject to identify agreement, disagreement, differences in emphasis and gaps in evidence.</p>
  <h3>What to compare</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Main claim:</strong> What does each source say?</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> What facts, examples or data support each claim?</li>
    <li><strong>Date:</strong> Were the sources produced at different times?</li>
    <li><strong>Purpose and viewpoint:</strong> Is each source informing, persuading, reporting or promoting?</li>
    <li><strong>Scope:</strong> Are they discussing the same group, location, period or definition?</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p><strong>Source A:</strong> A school report states that attendance improved from 80% to 90% between two terms.</p>
  <p><strong>Source B:</strong> A learner says that many classmates were absent during the second term.</p>
  <p>The sources appear to disagree, but they may refer to different classes, days or ways of measuring attendance. Check the dates, groups and records before deciding that one source contradicts the other.</p>
  <h3>Use a comparison method</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Write each source''s main claim.</li>
    <li>List the evidence each source gives.</li>
    <li>Check whether they use the same definitions and time period.</li>
    <li>Identify agreement, disagreement or information that only one source provides.</li>
    <li>Make a conclusion that reflects the strength and limits of the evidence.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Common error</h3>
  <p>Do not count the number of sources and assume the majority must be correct. Several sources may repeat the same original report. Independence, evidence and relevance matter more than quantity alone.</p>', 1),
  (1590, 1590, '<h2>Responsible Online Communication</h2>
  <p>Responsible online communication means sharing messages, images and information in ways that are respectful, accurate, safe and appropriate for the audience and platform.</p>
  <h3>Core practices</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Respect:</strong> Criticise ideas or actions without insulting or harassing people.</li>
    <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> Check important claims before forwarding them.</li>
    <li><strong>Privacy:</strong> Obtain permission before sharing another person''s private messages, photographs or personal details.</li>
    <li><strong>Security:</strong> Avoid sharing passwords, verification codes or sensitive information with untrusted people.</li>
    <li><strong>Context:</strong> Make sure a quotation, image or clip is not presented in a way that changes its meaning.</li>
    <li><strong>Accountability:</strong> Correct errors and acknowledge mistakes when you discover them.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Digital tone</h3>
  <p>Messages lack many cues available in face-to-face conversation, such as facial expression and tone of voice. A brief response can therefore sound rude even when that was not intended. Use clear wording and ask for clarification when necessary.</p>
  <h3>Worked example</h3>
  <p>A classmate shares an unverified message claiming that school will be closed tomorrow. Instead of forwarding it immediately, check an official school announcement. If you already shared the incorrect message, send a correction to the same audience.</p>
  <h3>Before posting or forwarding</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Is the information accurate and in context?</li>
    <li>Is sharing it necessary and appropriate?</li>
    <li>Does it reveal someone else''s private information?</li>
    <li>Could the wording be misunderstood or needlessly hurtful?</li>
    <li>Can you take responsibility for the message if challenged?</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Common error</h3>
  <p>Being able to access a photograph or message does not automatically give you permission to share it. Likewise, deleting a post does not guarantee that nobody has saved or copied it.</p>', 1),
  (1591, 1591, '<h2>Data vs Information</h2>
<p><b>Data</b> refers to raw, unprocessed facts, while <b>Information</b> is processed data that is meaningful and useful.</p>
<h3>1. Data</h3>
<ul>
<li>Unorganized facts</li>
<li>No meaning on its own</li>
<li>Can be numbers, text, symbols</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
75, 82, 90 → just numbers (data)
</div>
<h3>2. Information</h3>
<ul>
<li>Processed and organized data</li>
<li>Has meaning</li>
<li>Used for decision making</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Average score = 82% → meaningful (information)
</div>
<h3>3. Differences</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Data</th><th>Information</th></tr>
<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Raw</td><td>Processed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Use</td><td>Input</td><td>Decision making</td></tr>
</table>
', 1),
  (1592, 1592, '<h2>Characteristics of Computers</h2>
<p>Computers have unique features that make them powerful tools.</p>
<h3>Key Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Speed:</b> Performs millions of operations per second</li>
<li><b>Accuracy:</b> Produces correct results if input is correct</li>
<li><b>Diligence:</b> Does not get tired</li>
<li><b>Storage:</b> Can store large amounts of data</li>
<li><b>Automation:</b> Works automatically once programmed</li>
<li><b>Versatility:</b> Can perform many tasks</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Computer calculates results faster than humans → speed + accuracy
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Computers are powerful because they combine speed, accuracy, and storage.
</div>
', 1),
  (1593, 1593, '<h2>Types of Computers</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Computers are classified based on size, power, and purpose. Each type is designed for specific tasks ranging from simple personal use to highly complex scientific calculations.
</p>
<h3> CLASSIFICATION BY SIZE & POWER</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Supercomputers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Most powerful computers</li>
<li>Used in weather forecasting, space research, simulations</li>
<li>Example tasks: climate modeling, nuclear research</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Mainframe Computers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Handle large-scale processing</li>
<li>Used by banks, governments, large organizations</li>
<li>Support many users at once</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Minicomputers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Mid-sized systems (less common today)</li>
<li>Used in small organizations</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Microcomputers (Personal Computers)</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Most common type</li>
<li>Examples: desktops, laptops, tablets</li>
<li>Used for everyday tasks</li>
</ul>
<li><b>Embedded Computers</b></li>
<ul>
<li>Built into devices</li>
<li>Examples: washing machines, cars, microwaves</li>
<li>Perform specific functions</li>
</ul>
</ul>
<h3> COMPARISON TABLE</h3>
<pre>
Type           | Power     | Users      | Example Use
------------------------------------------------------
Supercomputer  | Very High | Few        | Weather prediction
Mainframe      | High      | Many       | Banking systems
Microcomputer  | Moderate  | One        | Personal use
Embedded       | Low       | Single use | Appliances
</pre>
<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Differences between supercomputer and mainframe</li>
<li>Examples of embedded systems</li>
<li>Classification based on purpose</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (1594, 1594, '<h2>Computer Generations (1st → 5th)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
Computer generations refer to the evolution of computer technology over time.
Each generation is defined by major technological advancements.
</p>
<h3> GENERATIONS OVERVIEW</h3>
<h4>1⃣ First Generation (1940s–1950s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used vacuum tubes</li>
<li>Very large and expensive</li>
<li>Generated a lot of heat</li>
<li>Example: ENIAC</li>
</ul>
<h4>2⃣ Second Generation (1950s–1960s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used transistors</li>
<li>Smaller, faster, more reliable</li>
<li>Less heat produced</li>
</ul>
<h4>3⃣ Third Generation (1960s–1970s)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used integrated circuits (ICs)</li>
<li>Even smaller and faster</li>
<li>Introduction of operating systems</li>
</ul>
<h4>4⃣ Fourth Generation (1970s–present)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Used microprocessors</li>
<li>Rise of personal computers</li>
<li>Very high speed and efficiency</li>
</ul>
<h4>5⃣ Fifth Generation (present & future)</h4>
<ul>
<li>Artificial Intelligence (AI)</li>
<li>Machine learning and automation</li>
<li>Focus on smart systems</li>
</ul>
<h3> SUMMARY TABLE</h3>
<pre>
Generation | Technology        | Key Feature
------------------------------------------------
1st        | Vacuum tubes      | Large, hot
2nd        | Transistors       | Smaller, faster
3rd        | ICs               | Reliable
4th        | Microprocessors   | Personal computers
5th        | AI                | Intelligent systems
</pre>
<h3> EXAM FOCUS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Key technology in each generation</li>
<li>Differences between generations</li>
<li>Examples (ENIAC, modern AI systems)</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (1595, 1595, '<h2>Number Systems</h2>
<p>
  Computers use different number systems to represent and process data efficiently.
  These include decimal, binary, and hexadecimal systems, which can be converted into one another.
</p>
<h3> 1. Decimal (Base 10)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses digits 0–9</li>
  <li>Used in everyday human calculations</li>
  <li>Each digit has a place value based on powers of 10</li>
</ul>
<h3> 2. Binary (Base 2)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses only 0 and 1</li>
  <li>Each digit is called a bit</li>
  <li>Used internally by computers</li>
</ul>
<h3> 3. Hexadecimal (Base 16)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Uses digits 0–9 and letters A–F</li>
  <li>A = 10, B = 11, C = 12, D = 13, E = 14, F = 15</li>
  <li>Used to simplify long binary numbers</li>
</ul>
<h3> Why Binary is Used</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>1 = ON</b> (electric signal present)</li>
  <li><b>0 = OFF</b> (no signal)</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Computers use binary because they only understand two states:
ON  → 1
OFF → 0
</pre>
<h3> Number System Conversions</h3>
<h4> Decimal to Binary</h4>
<pre>
Example: 10₁₀ → Binary
10 ÷ 2 = 5 remainder 0
5 ÷ 2  = 2 remainder 1
2 ÷ 2  = 1 remainder 0
1 ÷ 2  = 0 remainder 1
Read from bottom to top:
10₁₀ = 1010₂
</pre>
<h4> Binary to Decimal</h4>
<pre>
Example: 1010₂ → Decimal
(1×2³) + (0×2²) + (1×2¹) + (0×2⁰)
= 8 + 0 + 2 + 0
= 10₁₀
</pre>
<h4> Binary to Hexadecimal</h4>
<pre>
Example: 1010₂ → Hexadecimal
Group into 4 bits:
1010 = A
So, 1010₂ = A₁₆
</pre>
<h4> Hexadecimal to Binary</h4>
<pre>
Example: A₁₆ → Binary
A = 10 in decimal
10 = 1010₂
So, A₁₆ = 1010₂
</pre>
<h3> Exam Focus Areas</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Convert decimal to binary</li>
  <li>Convert binary to decimal</li>
  <li>Convert binary to hexadecimal</li>
  <li>Explain why computers use binary</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (1596, 1596, '<h2>Data Representation</h2>
<p>
  Data in computers is stored and processed using binary digits (0s and 1s).
  These binary values form the basis of all data storage and measurement.
</p>
<h3> 1. Units of Data Storage</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Bit:</b> Smallest unit of data (0 or 1)</li>
  <li><b>Nibble:</b> 4 bits</li>
  <li><b>Byte:</b> 8 bits</li>
  <li><b>Kilobyte (KB):</b> 1024 Bytes</li>
  <li><b>Megabyte (MB):</b> 1024 KB</li>
  <li><b>Gigabyte (GB):</b> 1024 MB</li>
  <li><b>Terabyte (TB):</b> 1024 GB</li>
</ul>
<h3> 2. Key Formula: Bits Calculation</h3>
<div class="example-box">
  <b>Formula:</b><br><br>
  Number of Bits = Number of Bytes × 8
</div>
<h3> 3. Examples of Bit Calculation</h3>
<pre>
Example 1:
5 Bytes → 5 × 8 = 40 Bits
Example 2:
10 Bytes → 10 × 8 = 80 Bits
Example 3:
2 KB → 2 × 1024 Bytes × 8 = 16384 Bits
</pre>
<h3> 4. Character Encoding</h3>
<h4> ASCII</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Uses numbers to represent characters</li>
  <li>Example: ''A'' = 65</li>
</ul>
<h4> Unicode</h4><ul><li>Supports many languages worldwide</li></ul>
<div class="example-box">
   Example:<br>
  ''A'' = 65 (ASCII)<br>
  Each character is stored using binary inside the computer
</div>
<h3> 5. Relationship Between Units</h3>
<pre>
1 Byte  = 8 Bits
1 KB     = 1024 Bytes
1 MB     = 1024 KB
1 GB     = 1024 MB
1 TB     = 1024 GB
</pre>
<h3> 6. Why Binary is Used</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Computers use ON (1) and OFF (0) signals</li>
  <li>Binary is easy for electronic circuits</li>
  <li>All data is stored as 0s and 1s</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Important Fact: All data in a computer is stored in binary form, and can be measured in bits and bytes.
</div>
<h3> Simple Explanation</h3>
<p>
  Data storage works like building blocks where everything is made from bits.
  Bytes, KB, MB, and GB are just bigger groups of those bits.
</p>
', 1),
  (1597, 1597, '<h2>CPU Components (ALU, CU, Registers)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Central Processing Unit (CPU) is the main component of a computer system and is often called the “brain” of the computer.
It interprets and executes instructions, processes data, and controls the operations of all other components.
</p>
<h3> MAIN COMPONENTS OF THE CPU</h3>
<h4>1. Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Performs arithmetic operations such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division</li>
  <li>Performs logical operations such as AND, OR, NOT, and comparisons</li>
  <li>Handles decision-making processes inside the computer</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Control Unit (CU)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Directs and coordinates all activities inside the CPU</li>
  <li>Fetches instructions from memory</li>
  <li>Decodes instructions into signals the CPU can understand</li>
  <li>Controls the flow of data between CPU, memory, and input/output devices</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Registers</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Very small and fast memory locations inside the CPU</li>
  <li>Store temporary data and instructions during processing</li>
  <li>Examples include:</li>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Accumulator:</b> Stores intermediate results of calculations</li>
    <li><b>Program Counter (PC):</b> Keeps track of the next instruction</li>
    <li><b>Instruction Register (IR):</b> Holds the current instruction being executed</li>
  </ul>
</ul>
<h3> IMPORTANCE OF THE CPU</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Controls all computer operations</li>
  <li>Processes data and instructions</li>
  <li>Coordinates all hardware components</li>
  <li>Ensures programs run correctly</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (1598, 1598, '<h2>Instruction Cycle (Fetch → Decode → Execute)</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The instruction cycle is the repeated process the CPU follows to execute program instructions.
It runs continuously while the computer is powered on, allowing software to function step by step.
</p>

<h3> MAIN STAGES OF THE INSTRUCTION CYCLE</h3>

<h4>1. Fetch</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The CPU retrieves an instruction from main memory (RAM)</li>
  <li>The instruction is stored in the Instruction Register (IR)</li>
  <li>The Program Counter (PC) points to the next instruction</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Decode</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The Control Unit (CU) interprets the instruction</li>
  <li>It determines what operation is required</li>
  <li>It identifies the data and components needed</li>
</ul>

<h4>3. Execute</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The ALU or other CPU components carry out the instruction</li>
  <li>Results are produced and stored in registers or memory</li>
  <li>Output may be sent to an output device if required</li>
</ul>

<h3> INSTRUCTION CYCLE FLOW</h3>
<pre>
Fetch → Decode → Execute → Fetch → Decode → Execute (repeats continuously)
</pre>

<h3> DETAILED PROCESS INSIDE CPU</h3>
<ul>
  <li>PC sends address to memory</li>
  <li>Instruction is fetched into IR</li>
  <li>CU decodes instruction</li>
  <li>ALU executes operation</li>
  <li>Result stored and PC updates</li>
</ul>

<h3> IMPORTANCE OF THE INSTRUCTION CYCLE</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Enables computers to run programs step by step</li>
  <li>Ensures systematic execution of instructions</li>
  <li>Keeps CPU working continuously and efficiently</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (1599, 1599, '<h2>Von Neumann Architecture</h2>
<h3> DEEP EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Von Neumann architecture is a computer design model where both data and instructions are stored in the same main memory.
It forms the basic structure of most modern computers and follows a sequential processing system.
</p>
<h3> MAIN COMPONENTS</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>CPU:</b> Contains the ALU and Control Unit (executes instructions)</li>
  <li><b>Main Memory (RAM):</b> Stores both data and instructions</li>
  <li><b>Input Devices:</b> Used to enter data into the computer</li>
  <li><b>Output Devices:</b> Display processed results</li>
  <li><b>System Bus:</b> Transfers data and instructions between components</li>
</ul>
<h3> VON NEUMANN STRUCTURE</h3>
<pre>
Input Devices → Memory ↔ CPU → Output Devices
                     ↑
                 System Bus
</pre>

<h3> TYPES OF BUSES AND THEIR PURPOSES</h3>
<h4>1. Data Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers actual data between CPU, memory, and input/output devices</li>
  <li>Usually bidirectional (data can move both ways)</li>
  <li>Width of data bus affects performance (more bits = faster transfer)</li>
</ul>
<h4>2. Address Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers memory addresses from CPU to memory</li>
  <li>Usually unidirectional (CPU → Memory)</li>
  <li>Determines how much memory a system can access</li>
</ul>
<h4>3. Control Bus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Transfers control signals between CPU and other components</li>
  <li>Includes signals like read, write, interrupt, and clock signals</li>
  <li>Ensures all parts of the system are synchronized</li>
</ul>
<h3> KEY IDEA</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Data and instructions share the same memory</li>
  <li>Instructions are processed one at a time (sequential execution)</li>
  <li>CPU follows the fetch–decode–execute cycle</li>
</ul>
<h3> HOW IT WORKS</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Input data and instructions are stored in memory</li>
  <li>CPU fetches instruction from memory</li>
  <li>Instruction is decoded by Control Unit</li>
  <li>ALU executes the instruction</li>
  <li>Result is stored back in memory or sent to output</li>
</ol>
<h3> LIMITATION: VON NEUMANN BOTTLENECK</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Data and instructions share the same bus/path</li>
  <li>CPU must wait to access memory</li>
  <li>Slows down processing speed</li>
</ul>
<pre>
Problem:
CPU ↔ SAME BUS ↔ MEMORY (data + instructions)
This creates a bottleneck (traffic jam in data flow)
</pre>
<h3> ADVANTAGES</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Simple and easy to design</li>
  <li>Cost-effective</li>
  <li>Flexible program storage (data + instructions together)</li>
</ul>
<h3> SIMPLE EXPLANATION</h3>
<p>
The Von Neumann architecture is like a single road used by both instructions and data, which makes it simple but can cause delays when traffic is heavy.
</p>
', 1),
  (1600, 1600, '<h2>Introduction to Computers</h2>
<p>A <strong>computer</strong> is a machine that takes in information (input), processes it, stores it, and produces results (output).</p>
<h2>1. Hardware (Physical Parts)</h2>
<p><strong>Hardware</strong> is anything you can see and touch.</p>
<h3>Main Components</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Input Devices:</strong> Used to enter data into the computer</li>
<li><strong>Processing Unit (CPU):</strong> The "brain" of the computer</li>
<li><strong>Output Devices:</strong> Show results</li>
<li><strong>Storage Devices:</strong> Save data</li>
</ul>
<h3>Examples of Hardware</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keyboard (typing)</li>
<li>Mouse (pointing)</li>
<li>Monitor (screen)</li>
<li>CPU/System Unit (processing)</li>
<li>Hard drive / SSD (storage)</li>
<li>Printer (output)</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Software (Programs)</h2>
<p><strong>Software</strong> tells the hardware what to do.</p>

<h3>Types of Software</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>System Software:</strong> Controls the computer (Operating System)</li>
<li><strong>Application Software:</strong> Programs used by users (Word, Chrome)</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Examples:<br>
Windows (OS)<br>
Google Chrome (browser)<br>
Microsoft Word (document program)
</div>
<h2>3. Operating System (OS)</h2>
<p>The <strong>Operating System</strong> is the most important software. It connects the user to the hardware.</p>
<ul>
<li>Manages memory</li>
<li>Controls devices</li>
<li>Runs applications</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Examples:<br>
Windows<br>
Linux<br>
macOS
</div>
<h2>4. Peripheral Devices</h2>
<p>Peripheral devices are external hardware connected to the computer.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Input:</strong> keyboard, mouse, scanner</li>
<li><strong>Output:</strong> monitor, printer, speakers</li>
<li><strong>Storage:</strong> flash drive, external hard disk</li>
</ul>
<h2>5. Liveware (Human Factor)</h2>
<p><strong>Liveware</strong> refers to the human user of the computer.</p>
<ul>
<li>The person operating the computer</li>
<li>Gives instructions</li>
<li>Interprets results</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Without a user, a computer cannot function meaningfully.
</div>
<h2>How Everything Works Together</h2>
<ul>
<li>User (liveware) gives input</li>
<li>Input devices send data to CPU</li>
<li>CPU processes the data</li>
<li>Results are shown through output devices</li>
<li>Data can be stored for later use</li>
</ul>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: A computer system = Hardware + Software + Liveware working together.
</div>
', 1),
  (1601, 1601, '<h2>Input & Output Devices</h2>
<p>Input devices allow users to <strong>send data to the computer</strong>. Output devices allow the computer to <strong>present results to the user</strong>.</p>
<h2>1. Input Devices</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keyboard:</strong> Types letters, numbers, symbols. Used for typing documents, commands. <em>Advantage:</em> Fast for text input. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Requires literacy/knowledge of layout.</li>
<li><strong>Mouse:</strong> Pointing device to select items. Used for GUI navigation. <em>Advantage:</em> Accurate pointer control. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Needs flat surface.</li>
<li><strong>Touchscreen:</strong> Detects finger/stylus input directly on screen. Used in phones, tablets, ATMs. <em>Advantage:</em> Direct interaction, intuitive. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Can get dirty, less precise for long text.</li>
<li><strong>Stylus:</strong> Pen-like tool for touchscreens. Used in tablets for drawing or handwriting. <em>Advantage:</em> More precise than finger. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Can be lost, needs compatible screen.</li>
<li><strong>Joystick:</strong> Game controller, moves objects in multiple directions. Used in gaming, simulations. <em>Advantage:</em> Easy control in games. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Limited general use.</li>
<li><strong>Voice Input:</strong> Converts spoken words to digital commands. Used in smartphones, voice assistants. <em>Advantage:</em> Hands-free, fast. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Background noise may affect accuracy.</li>
<li><strong>Scanner:</strong> Converts physical documents/images to digital. Used in offices, schools. <em>Advantage:</em> Preserves original document. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Slower than direct typing.</li>
<li><strong>Pinball / Specialized Controllers:</strong> Used in entertainment or arcade systems. <em>Advantage:</em> Engaging interaction. <em>Disadvantage:</em> Limited to niche applications.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Typing a report → keyboard input<br>
Drawing a sketch → stylus input<br>
Moving a character in a game → joystick input<br>
Voice commands to phone → voice input
</div>
<h2>2. Output Devices</h2>
<p>Output devices allow the computer to present results to the user.</p>
<h3>Soft Copy Output</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monitor / Screen:</strong> Displays text, images, video. Advantage: Real-time, interactive. Disadvantage: Requires power, cannot be held physically.</li>
<li><strong>Speakers / Headphones:</strong> Produce sound output (music, alerts). Advantage: Hands-free audio feedback. Disadvantage: Affected by noise, not permanent.</li>
<li><strong>Voice Output:</strong> Converts text to speech (TTS). Advantage: Accessibility for visually impaired, hands-free. Disadvantage: Requires quality software, may mispronounce words.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Hard Copy Output</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Printers:</strong> Produces permanent copies on paper.</li>
<li><strong>Dot Matrix / Impact Printers:</strong> Strike ink onto paper via pins. Advantage: Cheap, multi-copy printing. Disadvantage: Noisy, low resolution.</li>
<li><strong>Laser Printers:</strong> Use laser beams to print toner. Advantage: High speed, high quality. Disadvantage: Expensive initial cost, toner cartridges.</li>
<li><strong>Inkjet Printers:</strong> Spray tiny ink droplets onto paper. Advantage: High quality, color printing. Disadvantage: Slower than laser, ink expensive.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Printing an assignment → hard copy (paper)<br>
Watching video → soft copy (monitor)<br>
Listening to music → soft copy (speakers)
</div>
<h2>3. Advantages and Disadvantages</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Device Type</th><th>Advantage</th><th>Disadvantage</th></tr>
<tr><td>Keyboard</td><td>Fast text input</td><td>Needs literacy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mouse</td><td>Accurate pointer control</td><td>Needs flat surface</td></tr>
<tr><td>Touchscreen</td><td>Intuitive, direct interaction</td><td>Less precise for typing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stylus</td><td>Precision drawing</td><td>Can be lost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Joystick</td><td>Easy control in games</td><td>Limited general use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voice Input</td><td>Hands-free</td><td>Background noise affects accuracy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monitor</td><td>Real-time display</td><td>Cannot hold physically</td></tr>
<tr><td>Printer</td><td>Permanent copy</td><td>Costs paper/ink</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speaker</td><td>Audio feedback</td><td>No visual info, affected by noise</td></tr>
</table>
', 1),
  (1602, 1602, '<h2>Memory & Storage</h2>
<p>Computers need memory to <strong>store data and instructions</strong>. There are two main types: <strong>Primary memory</strong> (fast, temporary) and <strong>Secondary storage</strong> (slower, permanent).</p>
<h2>1. Primary Memory (Main Memory)</h2>
<p>Used by the CPU to store data that is being actively used.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>RAM (Random Access Memory):</strong> Volatile memory; data lost when power is off. Used for active programs and OS. Types: <em>DRAM (Dynamic), SRAM (Static)</em>. Advantage: Fast access. Disadvantage: Temporary.</li>
<li><strong>ROM (Read-Only Memory):</strong> Non-volatile; stores permanent instructions (e.g., BIOS). Cannot be modified easily. Types: PROM, EPROM, EEPROM. Advantage: Permanent. Disadvantage: Cannot change easily.</li>
<li><strong>Cache Memory:</strong> Very fast memory located close to CPU. Stores frequently used data for quick access. Types: L1 (smallest, fastest), L2 (larger, slower than L1), L3 (largest, shared in multi-core CPUs). Advantage: Improves CPU performance. Disadvantage: Expensive, limited size.</li>
<li><strong>Registers:</strong> Smallest, fastest memory inside CPU. Temporarily stores data, instructions, or addresses being processed. Advantage: Immediate access for CPU. Disadvantage: Very limited storage.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Buses in Memory</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data Bus:</strong> Transfers data between CPU and memory.</li>
<li><strong>Address Bus:</strong> Carries memory addresses to read/write locations.</li>
<li><strong>Control Bus:</strong> Sends control signals (read/write operations).</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
CPU accesses RAM → stores active program<br>
Registers hold intermediate calculation results<br>
Cache holds frequently used data to speed CPU
</div>
<h2>2. Secondary Storage</h2>
<p>Used for permanent storage of programs and data. Slower than primary memory but non-volatile.</p>
<h3>Magnetic Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hard Disk Drive (HDD):</strong> Uses magnetic disks to store data. Advantage: High capacity, cheap per GB. Disadvantage: Slower than SSD, sensitive to shocks and magnets.</li>
<li><strong>Magnetic Tape:</strong> Stores large data sequentially. Advantage: Cheap, good for backups. Disadvantage: Slow, sequential access.</li>
<li><strong>Floppy Disk:</strong> Old magnetic storage. Advantage: Portable. Disadvantage: Very low capacity, obsolete.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Solid-State Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>SSD (Solid-State Drive):</strong> Uses flash memory, no moving parts. Advantage: Fast, durable. Disadvantage: More expensive per GB than HDD.</li>
<li><strong>USB Flash Drive:</strong> Portable flash memory. Advantage: Easy to carry, plug-and-play. Disadvantage: Small capacity, can be lost easily.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Optical Storage</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>CD-ROM / CD-R / CD-RW:</strong> 700 MB. CD-R: write once. CD-RW: rewriteable. Advantage: Cheap, portable. Disadvantage: Low capacity, slower than HDD/SSD.</li>
<li><strong>DVD / DVD-R / DVD-RW:</strong> 4.7–8.5 GB. Advantage: Larger capacity than CD. Disadvantage: Slower than HDD/SSD.</li>
<li><strong>Blu-Ray Disc:</strong> 25–50 GB. Advantage: High capacity, good for HD video. Disadvantage: Expensive, requires Blu-Ray drive.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Handling & Care of Storage Devices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keep HDDs away from strong magnets → data can be erased.</li>
<li>Avoid exposing disks (HDD, CDs/DVDs/BDs) to dust, smoke, heat, or scratches.</li>
<li>Use anti-static precautions for RAM or motherboards.</li>
<li>Do not bend or drop flash drives or floppy disks.</li>
<li>Back up critical data to multiple storage types for safety.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Saving a document → stored on HDD/SSD<br>
Watching a movie → from DVD/Blu-Ray or SSD<br>
Booting computer → BIOS instructions from ROM
</div>
<div class="keyfact"> Key Fact: Primary memory is fast but volatile; secondary storage is slower but permanent. Proper care ensures long lifespan and data safety.</div>
', 1),
  (1603, 1603, '<h2>Operating System</h2>
<p>An operating system (OS) is a set of system software that manages computer hardware and software resources, serving as an interface between the user and the computer hardware. It allows applications to run, manages memory, schedules tasks, and controls peripheral devices.</p>
<h2>Key Notes and Concepts</h2>
<h3>1. Primary Roles and Goals</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Interface:</strong> Acts as an intermediary between the user and computer hardware, making the computer convenient to use.</li>
<li><strong>Resource Manager:</strong> Allocates and manages shared hardware resources such as CPU time, memory, and Input/Output (I/O) devices, ensuring efficient utilization.</li>
<li><strong>Abstraction:</strong> Conceals the complexities of hardware from applications, providing a uniform interface.</li>
<li><strong>Kernel:</strong> The core, essential part of the OS that is always running and manages critical functions.</li>
<li><strong>Shell:</strong> The component that interprets user commands and gets them executed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Core Functions of an Operating System</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Process Management:</strong> Manages the execution of applications (processes), including scheduling, creation, termination, and synchronization.</li>
<li><strong>Memory Management:</strong> Tracks and allocates Main Memory (RAM). It decides which processes load into memory and manages virtual memory (using hard disk space as an extension of RAM).</li>
<li><strong>File Management:</strong> Organizes, creates, deletes, and manages files and directories on storage devices.</li>
<li><strong>Device Management:</strong> Uses device drivers to communicate with hardware peripherals (printers, disks, keyboards).</li>
<li><strong>Security and Protection:</strong> Protects data and resources from unauthorized access via authentication (passwords) and isolation of processes.</li>
<li><strong>Error Detection:</strong> Monitors system health and reports errors to prevent system failure.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Key OS Concepts and Techniques</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>System Calls:</strong> The interface between a running program and the OS kernel, allowing user-level programs to request services.</li>
<li><strong>Multitasking/Multiprogramming:</strong> The ability of an OS to switch between tasks quickly, allowing multiple programs to run seemingly at the same time and keeping the CPU busy.</li>
<li><strong>Interrupts:</strong> Signals sent to the CPU by hardware or software to request immediate attention, allowing the OS to react to events.</li>
<li><strong>Deadlock Handling:</strong> Manages situations where two or more processes are stuck waiting for resources held by each other.</li>
<li><strong>Spooling:</strong> Temporarily storing data in a buffer (e.g., for printers) so the CPU can continue other tasks while the slow device works.</li>
<li><strong>Booting:</strong> The process of starting a computer by loading the kernel into memory.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Types of Operating Systems</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Batch OS:</strong> Jobs with similar needs are grouped (batched) and processed together without user interaction (e.g., payroll).</li>
<li><strong>Time-Sharing (Multitasking) OS:</strong> Allows multiple users to share the system simultaneously by switching between tasks quickly (e.g., Unix, Windows).</li>
<li><strong>Distributed OS:</strong> Manages a group of independent computers and makes them appear as a single system.</li>
<li><strong>Real-Time OS (RTOS):</strong> Used when strict time constraints are required (e.g., robotics, airbag controllers).</li>
<li><strong>Mobile OS:</strong> Designed specifically for smartphones and tablets (e.g., Android, iOS).</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Common Examples</h3>
<ul>
<li>Windows: Microsoft’s commercial OS for desktops and servers.</li>
<li>Linux: Open-source, Unix-based OS, popular on servers.</li>
<li>macOS: Apple’s desktop OS.</li>
<li>Android/iOS: Dominant mobile operating systems.</li>
</ul>
<h3>6. OS Structure</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monolithic Kernel:</strong> All OS services run in kernel space, high performance but less stable (e.g., Linux).</li>
<li><strong>Microkernel:</strong> Only essential services run in kernel space; others run in user space, increasing stability (e.g., Minix).</li>
<li><strong>Hybrid Kernel:</strong> Combines aspects of both (e.g., Windows NT, macOS).</li>
</ul>
', 1),
  (1604, 1604, '<h2>File Management</h2>
<p>File management is a fundamental concept in computing that involves how an operating system (OS) stores, names, organizes, and manipulates files. In a high school curriculum, this typically covers the hierarchy of storage, file attributes, and common operations.</p>
<h3>1. The File Hierarchy (Folders and Directories)</h3>
<p>Computers use a hierarchical structure (often called a "tree structure") to organize data.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Drive:</strong> The top level of storage (e.g., C: drive for a Hard Drive, D: for a USB).</li>
<li><strong>Folders (Directories):</strong> Containers used to group related files together.</li>
<li><strong>Sub-folders:</strong> Folders inside other folders.</li>
<li><strong>Files:</strong> The actual data units (documents, images, programs).</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example Path:<br>
C:\Documents\School\ComputerScience\Notes.docx<br>
"School" is a folder, and "Notes.docx" is the file.
</div>
<h3>2. File Naming and Extensions</h3>
<p>A filename consists of two parts: the name and the extension, separated by a dot.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Filename:</strong> A descriptive name (e.g., History_Essay)</li>
<li><strong>Extension:</strong> Indicates the file type and program used to open it</li>
</ul>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Extension</th><th>Program Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Documents</td><td>.docx, .pdf, .txt</td><td>Word, Adobe Reader</td></tr>
<tr><td>Images</td><td>.jpg, .png, .gif</td><td>Photos, Photoshop</td></tr>
<tr><td>Audio</td><td>.mp3, .wav</td><td>Spotify, Media Player</td></tr>
<tr><td>Video</td><td>.mp4, .mov</td><td>VLC, QuickTime</td></tr>
<tr><td>Executables</td><td>.exe, .app</td><td>System Applications</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>3. Basic File Operations</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Create:</strong> Making a new file or folder</li>
<li><strong>Save / Save As:</strong> Writing data to storage ("Save As" changes name/type)</li>
<li><strong>Copy:</strong> Duplicate file in another location</li>
<li><strong>Move (Cut/Paste):</strong> Change file location</li>
<li><strong>Rename:</strong> Change filename</li>
<li><strong>Delete:</strong> Send file to Recycle Bin / Trash</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. File Metadata and Attributes</h3>
<p>Files have hidden information called metadata:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>File Size:</strong> KB, MB, GB</li>
<li><strong>Date Created/Modified</strong></li>
<li><strong>Permissions:</strong> Read, Write, Execute</li>
<li><strong>Status:</strong> Hidden, System, Read-only</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Storage Best Practices</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use descriptive names (e.g., Bio_Lab_Report_V2)</li>
<li>Organize files into folders</li>
<li>Backup files regularly (Cloud or external drive)</li>
<li>Avoid deep nesting (too many folders inside folders)</li>
</ul>
<h3>6. Compression and Archiving</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compression (.zip, .rar):</strong> Reduces file size</li>
<li><strong>Extracting:</strong> Restores compressed files to original form</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example:<br>
Large folder → compressed to .zip → sent via email → extracted by receiver
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: Good file management makes it easier to find files, saves storage space, and prevents data loss.
</div>
', 1),
  (1605, 1605, '<h2>Introduction to Programming</h2>
<p><strong>Programming</strong> is the process of creating a set of instructions that tells a computer how to perform a specific task.</p>
<div class="example-box">
Think of programming like writing a recipe:<br>
Ingredients → Data<br>
Steps → Code<br>
Final dish → Output
</div>
<h2>1. Essential Programming Terminology</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Algorithm:</strong> A step-by-step procedure for solving a problem.</li>
<li><strong>Source Code:</strong> The actual text written by a programmer.</li>
<li><strong>Syntax:</strong> Rules that define how code must be written.</li>
<li><strong>Bug:</strong> An error in a program.</li>
<li><strong>Debugging:</strong> Finding and fixing errors.</li>
<li><strong>Variable:</strong> A container for storing data.</li>
<li><strong>Compiler/Interpreter:</strong> Tools that translate code into machine language (1s and 0s).</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. The Programming Process (SDLC)</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Problem Definition:</strong> Understand the problem.</li>
<li><strong>Design:</strong> Plan the solution (flowcharts, pseudocode).</li>
<li><strong>Coding:</strong> Write the program.</li>
<li><strong>Testing:</strong> Find and fix bugs.</li>
<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Update and improve the program.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
Example:<br>
Build a calculator → plan steps → code → test → fix errors
</div>
<h2>3. Categories of Programming Languages</h2>
<h3>Low-Level Languages</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Machine Code:</strong> Binary (0s and 1s), very hard for humans.</li>
<li><strong>Assembly Language:</strong> Uses short codes (MOV, ADD).</li>
</ul>
<h3>High-Level Languages (HLL)</h3>
<p>Designed to be easy for humans.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Language</th><th>Use</th><th>Strength</th></tr>
<tr><td>Python</td><td>AI, Data Science</td><td>Easy for beginners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Java</td><td>Apps, Systems</td><td>Portable</td></tr>
<tr><td>C++</td><td>Games</td><td>Fast</td></tr>
<tr><td>JavaScript</td><td>Web</td><td>Runs in browsers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swift</td><td>iOS apps</td><td>Optimized for Apple</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>4. Fundamental Logic Structures</h2>
<h3>A. Sequence</h3>
<p>Instructions executed step by step.</p>
<h3>B. Selection (Decision)</h3>
<p>Program chooses based on condition.</p>
<div class="example-box">
IF age ≥ 18 → allow voting<br>
ELSE → deny access
</div>
<h3>C. Iteration (Loop)</h3>
<p>Repeats instructions.</p>
<div class="example-box">
Send 100 emails automatically
</div>
<h2>5. Data Types</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Integer:</strong> Whole numbers (5, -42)</li>
<li><strong>Float:</strong> Decimal numbers (3.14)</li>
<li><strong>String:</strong> Text ("Hello")</li>
<li><strong>Boolean:</strong> True/False</li>
</ul>
<h2>6. Interpreted vs Compiled Languages</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Compiled:</strong> Entire program translated before running (e.g., C++) → faster</li>
<li><strong>Interpreted:</strong> Runs line-by-line (e.g., Python) → easier debugging</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
C++ → compiled → fast execution<br>
Python → interpreted → easier to debug
</div>
<div class="keyfact">
 Key Fact: Every program you use — from games to mobile apps — follows these same core principles.
</div>
', 1),
  (1606, 1606, '<h2>Loops & Conditionals</h2>
<p><strong>Conditionals:</strong> decision making</p>
<pre>
if x > 5:
  print("Big")
</pre>
<p><strong>Loops:</strong> repetition</p>
<pre>
for i in range(5):
  print(i)
</pre>
<div class="example-box">
Loop prints numbers 0–4
</div>
<h2>Control Structures</h2>
<p>Control structures are the "brain" of any program. They allow the code to make decisions and repeat tasks, moving beyond simple step-by-step execution.</p>
<h2>1. Conditional Statements (Selection)</h2>
<p>Conditionals allow a program to execute specific blocks of code only if a certain condition is met.</p>
<h3>A. The if Statement</h3>
<p>The most basic decision-making statement. If the condition is true, the code runs.</p>
<pre>
age = 20
if age >= 18:
    print("You are an adult.")
</pre>
<h3>B. The if-else Statement</h3>
<p>Provides an alternative path if the condition is false.</p>
<pre>
score = 45
if score >= 50:
    print("Pass")
else:
    print("Fail")
</pre>
<h3>C. The if-elif-else Ladder</h3>
<p>Used when there are more than two possible outcomes.</p>
<pre>
grade = 85
if grade >= 90:
    print("A")
elif grade >= 80:
    print("B")
elif grade >= 70:
    print("C")
else:
    print("D or F")
</pre>
<h3>D. Nested if Statements</h3>
<p>An if statement inside another if for complex conditions.</p>
<pre>
has_ticket = True
has_id = False

if has_ticket:
    if has_id:
        print("Entry allowed.")
    else:
        print("Please show your ID.")
else:
    print("No ticket, no entry.")
</pre>
<h2>2. Loops (Iteration)</h2>
<p>Loops repeat a block of code multiple times.</p>
<h3>A. The for Loop</h3>
<p>Used when you know how many times to repeat.</p>
<pre>
for i in range(5):
    print("Loop number:", i)
</pre>
<h3>B. The while Loop</h3>
<p>Repeats while a condition is true.</p>
<pre>
count = 5
while count > 0:
    print(count)
    count = count - 1
print("Blast off!")
</pre>
<h3>C. Nested Loops</h3>
<p>A loop inside another loop.</p>
<pre>
for row in range(3):
    for col in range(3):
        print("Row", row, "Col", col)
</pre>
<h2>3. Key Loop Controls</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>break:</strong> Stops the loop immediately</li>
<li><strong>continue:</strong> Skips current iteration</li>
</ul>
<h2>Comparison: Loops vs Conditionals</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Conditionals (if)</th><th>Loops (for/while)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Decision making</td><td>Repetition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Execution</td><td>Runs once</td><td>Runs repeatedly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keywords</td><td>if, else, elif</td><td>for, while</td></tr>
<tr><td>Example</td><td>Checking password</td><td>Printing multiple pages</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="keyfact">
 Conditionals decide WHAT to do, loops decide HOW MANY TIMES to do it.
</div>
', 1),
  (1607, 1607, '<h2>Functions & Methods</h2>
<p>A function is reusable code.</p>
<pre>
def add(a,b):
  return a+b
</pre>
<div class="example-box">
add(2,3) = 5
</div>
<h2>In programming, functions and methods are blocks of reusable code designed to perform a specific action.</h2>
<p>They help break down complex programs into smaller, manageable, and organized pieces.</p>
<h2>1. What is a Function?</h2>
<p>A function is a self-contained block of code that only runs when it is called. You can pass data, known as parameters, into a function, and it can return data as a result.</p>
<h3>Key Components:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Declaration/Definition:</strong> Creating the function and giving it a name.</li>
<li><strong>Parameters:</strong> Variables listed in the function definition (the "inputs").</li>
<li><strong>Arguments:</strong> The actual values sent to the function when it is called.</li>
<li><strong>Return Statement:</strong> The "output" the function sends back to the main program.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example in Python:</h3>
<pre>
# Defining the function
def greet_user(name): 
    return "Hello, " + name
# Calling the function
message = greet_user("Alex")
print(message)
</pre>
<h2>2. What is a Method?</h2>
<p>A method is essentially a function that "belongs" to an object or a class. While functions can be called by themselves, a method must be called on a specific object.</p>
<h3>The Difference:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Function:</strong> calculate_area(5, 10) (Independent)</li>
<li><strong>Method:</strong> my_dog.bark() (Dependent on the "dog" object)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example of a String Method:</h3>
<pre>
text = "hello world"
print(text.upper())
</pre>
<h2>3. Why Use Functions and Methods?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reusability (DRY):</strong> Write code once and reuse it.</li>
<li><strong>Abstraction:</strong> Use code without knowing internal details.</li>
<li><strong>Organization:</strong> Makes code easier to read and debug.</li>
<li><strong>Modularity:</strong> Allows teamwork in programming.</li>
</ul>
<h2>4. Function Scope (Local vs Global)</h2>
<p>Scope determines where a variable can be used.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Local Scope:</strong> Variable inside function.</li>
<li><strong>Global Scope:</strong> Variable outside function.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example:</h3>
<pre>
x = 10 # Global variable
def my_function():
    y = 5 # Local variable
    print(x)
    print(y)

my_function()
print(y) # ERROR
</pre>
<h2>5. Built-in vs User-Defined Functions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Built-in:</strong> Provided by language (print(), len())</li>
<li><strong>User-defined:</strong> Created by programmer</li>
</ul>
<h2>Summary Table</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Function</th><th>Method</th></tr>
<tr><td>Location</td><td>Independent</td><td>Inside class</td></tr>
<tr><td>Call</td><td>function_name()</td><td>object.method()</td></tr>
<tr><td>Relationship</td><td>No object</td><td>Linked to object</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data</td><td>Uses parameters</td><td>Uses object data</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="keyfact">
 Functions are independent blocks of code, while methods belong to objects.
</div>
', 1),
  (1608, 1608, '<h2>Arrays & Lists</h2>
<p>In programming, we often need to store a collection of similar items (like a list of student names or temperatures). Instead of creating many separate variables, we use <b>Arrays and Lists</b>.</p>
<h3>1. What is an Array?</h3>
<p>An <b>Array</b> is a collection of items stored at contiguous (neighboring) memory locations. It holds a fixed number of items, usually of the same data type.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Fixed Size:</b> Cannot be changed after creation.</li>
<li><b>Homogeneous:</b> Same data type.</li>
<li><b>Index-Based:</b> Each element has a position.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. The Concept of Indexing</h3>
<p>Indexing starts at <b>0</b>:</p>
<ul>
<li>First element → index 0</li>
<li>Second element → index 1</li>
<li>Last element → index (n-1)</li>
</ul>
<pre>
prices = [10.50, 20.00, 5.75]
prices[0] = 10.50
prices[2] = 5.75
</pre>
<h3>3. Arrays vs Lists</h3>
<table border="1">
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Array</th><th>List</th></tr>
<tr><td>Size</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Dynamic</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data Types</td><td>Same type</td><td>Mixed allowed (Python)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Memory</td><td>Efficient</td><td>More flexible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Languages</td><td>C, C++, Java</td><td>Python, JavaScript</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>4. Common Operations</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Accessing:</b> Get value using index</li>
<li><b>Updating:</b> Change value</li>
<li><b>Appending:</b> Add item (Lists)</li>
<li><b>Traversing:</b> Loop through items</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Code Examples</h3>
<h4>Python (Lists)</h4>
<pre>
fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Cherry"]
fruits.append("Orange")
fruits[1] = "Blueberry"
for x in fruits:
    print(x)
</pre>
<h4>Java (Arrays)</h4>
<pre>
int[] numbers = new int[5];

numbers[0] = 10;
numbers[1] = 20;

System.out.println(numbers[0]);
</pre>
<h3>6. Two-Dimensional (2D) Arrays</h3>
<pre>
grid = [
  [1,2,3],
  [4,5,6],
  [7,8,9]
]

print(grid[1][1])
</pre>
<h3>7. Why Use Arrays/Lists?</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Efficiency:</b> Fast access using index</li>
<li><b>Group Processing:</b> Handle many items with loops</li>
<li><b>Sorting:</b> Organize data easily</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
numbers = [1,2,3]
numbers[0] = 1
</div>
