  (8884, 4976, '''Our'' is a possessive determiner used before a noun; ''ours'' is a possessive pronoun that stands independently.', TRUE),
  (8885, 4977, '''Herself'' is emphatic because it emphasises the head teacher and is not required as an object.', TRUE),
  (8886, 4978, 'The children enjoyed themselves at the concert.', TRUE),
  (8887, 4979, '''Him'' refers to another male person; ''himself'' refers back to Musa.', TRUE),
  (8888, 4980, '(a) This is my ruler here. (b) Those are the buildings across the road.', TRUE),
  (8889, 4981, 'In ''These are mine'', these is a demonstrative pronoun. In ''These books are mine'', these is a demonstrative determiner.', TRUE),
  (8890, 4982, 'These are the answers to the questions.', TRUE),
  (8891, 4983, 'The woman who treated my brother is a doctor.', TRUE),
  (8892, 4984, 'The learner whose bag was stolen reported the matter.', TRUE),
  (8893, 4985, 'My aunt, who lives in Kisumu, is visiting.', TRUE),
  (8894, 4986, 'Everybody has submitted their work.', TRUE),
  (8895, 4987, 'Milk takes is because it is treated as a singular amount; oranges takes are because it is plural.', TRUE),
  (8896, 4988, 'Someone called me, but I cannot identify them.', TRUE),
  (8897, 4989, 'The antecedent of both their and they is the players.', TRUE),
  (8898, 4990, 'The girls said they would arrive before noon.', TRUE),
  (8899, 4991, 'One clear revision is: ''When Njeri called Akinyi, Njeri was already at school.'' If Akinyi was at school, write: ''Njeri called Akinyi, who was already at school.''', TRUE),
  (8900, 4992, 'Auxiliary verb: have. Main verb: completed.', TRUE),
  (8901, 4993, 'Does she like reading?', TRUE),
  (8902, 4994, '(a) Have is the main verb. (b) Have is an auxiliary verb and lost is the main verb.', TRUE),
  (8903, 4995, '(a) Linking verb. (b) Action verb.', TRUE),
  (8904, 4996, 'Linking verb: became. Subject complement: restless.', TRUE),
  (8905, 4997, 'In the first sentence, looked is a linking verb. In the second, looked is an action verb.', TRUE),
  (8906, 4998, 'Yesterday, Kamau wrote a letter, and today he has written another.', TRUE),
  (8907, 4999, 'The students have completed the work and went home.', TRUE),
  (8908, 5000, 'Study: study → studied → studied. Buy: buy → bought → bought.', TRUE),
  (8909, 5001, 'Verb: repaired. Direct object: the bicycle. Adverb: carefully.', TRUE),
  (8910, 5002, '(a) Intransitive. (b) Transitive.', TRUE),
  (8911, 5003, 'Direct object: a new book. Indirect object: the pupil.', TRUE),
  (8912, 5004, 'Finite verb: have. Non-finite forms: been and practising.', TRUE),
  (8913, 5005, 'In (a), dances is finite. In (b), wants is finite and to dance is non-finite.', TRUE),
  (8914, 5006, 'Non-finite verb form: walking. Finite verb: found.', TRUE),
  (8915, 5007, 'The learners must finish their work before leaving.', TRUE),
  (8916, 5008, 'To borrow a dictionary is a to-infinitive construction expressing purpose.', TRUE),
  (8917, 5009, 'The coach made the players run, and they hoped to win the match.', TRUE),
  (8918, 5010, 'Learning a new language is a gerund phrase functioning as the subject.', TRUE),
  (8919, 5011, 'The athlete succeeded by training every morning.', TRUE),
  (8920, 5012, '''He stopped talking'' means he ceased talking. ''He stopped to talk'' means he paused what he was doing so that he could talk.', TRUE),
  (8921, 5013, 'Exhausted is a past participle in the phrase ''Exhausted by the journey'', which describes the travellers.', TRUE),
  (8922, 5014, 'After finishing the examination, the learners heard the bell ring.', TRUE),
  (8923, 5015, '(a) Gerund. (b) Present participle in a continuous verb construction. (c) Present participle modifying a noun.', TRUE),
  (8924, 5016, 'Negative: Miriam does not speak English. Question: Does Miriam speak English?', TRUE),
  (8925, 5017, 'My sister studies and goes to college every weekday.', TRUE),
  (8926, 5018, 'The present simple is appropriate because the sentence describes a scheduled event.', TRUE),
  (8927, 5019, 'The children are running in the field.', TRUE),
  (8928, 5020, 'The first sentence describes a habit; the second describes a temporary activity in progress around the present time.', TRUE),
  (8929, 5021, 'Question: Is she preparing for the examination? Negative: She is not preparing for the examination.', TRUE),
  (8930, 5022, 'Negative: The teacher did not explain the rule. Question: Did the teacher explain the rule?', TRUE),
  (8931, 5023, 'Last Friday, we went to the library.', TRUE),
  (8932, 5024, 'Example: The learner woke up early, ate breakfast and walked to school.', TRUE),
  (8933, 5025, 'At 7 p.m. yesterday, I was studying when my friend called.', TRUE),
  (8934, 5026, 'Negative: The children were not playing outside. Question: Were the children playing outside?', TRUE),
  (8935, 5027, 'The first sentence presents eating supper as a completed past event. The second presents supper as an activity in progress when the lights went out.', TRUE),
  (8936, 5028, '(a) She has written three letters. (b) They have not finished the assignment.', TRUE),
  (8937, 5029, 'We travelled to Nakuru last month.', TRUE),
  (8938, 5030, 'The first usually describes a completed period of residence. The second usually describes a period that began in the past and continues to the present.', TRUE),
  (8939, 5031, 'The learners have been revising for three hours, and they are still working.', TRUE),
  (8940, 5032, 'Have painted emphasises the result or completion. Have been painting emphasises the activity and its duration or recent continuation.', TRUE),
  (8941, 5033, 'She has known her neighbour since childhood.', TRUE),
  (8942, 5034, 'By the time the bus arrived, we had been waiting for an hour and the rain had stopped.', TRUE),
  (8943, 5035, 'The secretary had completed the report before the manager arrived.', TRUE),
  (8944, 5036, 'By the time the doctor came, the patient had taken the medicine.', TRUE),
  (8945, 5037, 'The players were tired because they had been training for three hours before the match.', TRUE),
  (8946, 5038, 'Had repaired emphasises the completed result. Had been repairing emphasises the ongoing or preceding activity, without necessarily stating that it was completed.', TRUE),
  (8947, 5039, 'By the time the guests arrived, we had been preparing food for hours.', TRUE),
  (8948, 5040, 'It is going to rain soon.', TRUE),
  (8949, 5041, 'I will help you carry the bag.', TRUE),
  (8950, 5042, 'I will have finished the assignment by Friday.', TRUE),
  (8951, 5043, 'By the time the teacher arrived, the learners had finished their exercise.', TRUE),
  (8952, 5044, 'Musa travelled to Mombasa last week.', TRUE),
  (8953, 5045, 'I was eating supper when the lights went out.', TRUE),
  (8954, 5046, 'Last Saturday, our team trained in the morning, rested at noon and played again in the afternoon.', TRUE),
  (8955, 5047, 'The tenses are correct because ''missed'' refers to yesterday, while ''is taking'' refers to today''s action.', TRUE),
  (8956, 5048, 'The teacher explained that the Earth revolves around the Sun.', TRUE),
  (8957, 5049, 'Complete subject: ''The tall trees beside the river''. Head noun: ''trees''.', TRUE),
  (8958, 5050, 'Subject: ''The class monitor''. Object: ''the exercise books''.', TRUE),
  (8959, 5051, '(a) ''the visitors''. (b) The understood subject is ''you''.', TRUE),
  (8960, 5052, 'The basket of oranges sits beside the door.', TRUE),
  (8961, 5053, 'Does the new student understand the instructions?', TRUE),
  (8962, 5054, '(a) has (b) studies (c) is', TRUE),
  (8963, 5055, 'The students in the classroom discuss the assignment.', TRUE),
  (8964, 5056, '(a) are (b) have (c) arrive', TRUE),
  (8965, 5057, 'The boxes beside the teacher''s desk contain examination papers.', TRUE),
  (8966, 5058, 'The nurse and the doctor are examining the patient.', TRUE),
  (8967, 5059, 'The class captain, together with the prefects, is organising the meeting.', TRUE),
  (8968, 5060, 'Either the players or the coach is responsible for collecting the equipment.', TRUE),
  (8969, 5061, 'Everybody in the two classes has submitted their assignment.', TRUE),
  (8970, 5062, '(a) are (b) has (c) has', TRUE),
  (8971, 5063, 'The verb is singular because the subject is ''One'', not ''learners''.', TRUE),
  (8972, 5064, 'The committee has reached a final decision.', TRUE),
  (8973, 5065, 'The family members are carrying their bags to the bus.', TRUE),
  (8974, 5066, '(a) Treats the team as one unit celebrating together. (b) Emphasises individual members disagreeing with one another.', TRUE),
  (8975, 5067, 'The results of the experiment show that the solution is acidic. The subject is ''The results of the experiment'', and its head noun is ''results''.', TRUE),
  (8976, 5068, '(a) There are many reasons for the delay. (b) Each of the girls has completed the task.', TRUE),
  (8977, 5069, 'Every learner has a role. The teacher, together with two assistants, is checking the work. There are several mistakes in the final paragraph.', TRUE),
  (8978, 5070, 'Complete subject: ''The diligent learners in our class''. Head noun: ''learners''. Complete predicate: ''completed the assignment before lunch''.', TRUE),
  (8979, 5071, 'Subject: ''the farmers''. Complete predicate: ''returned to their fields''. ''After the heavy rain'' is an introductory phrase.', TRUE),
  (8980, 5072, 'It is incomplete because ''although'' introduces a dependent idea. Example: ''Although the team practised every day, it lost the final.''', TRUE),
  (8981, 5073, 'Indirect object: ''the winners''. Direct object: ''certificates''.', TRUE),
  (8982, 5074, '''Confident'' is a subject complement because it describes the subject ''The speaker'' after the linking verb ''sounded''.', TRUE),
  (8983, 5075, 'Direct object: ''the test''. Object complement: ''difficult''.', TRUE),
  (8984, 5076, 'Noun phrase: ''The three exhausted runners near the finish line''. Head noun: ''runners''.', TRUE),
  (8985, 5077, '(a) Verb phrase. (b) Prepositional phrase. (c) Noun phrase.', TRUE),
  (8986, 5078, '''Before sunrise'' is a prepositional phrase. ''Before the sun rises'' is a subordinate adverbial clause.', TRUE),
  (8987, 5079, 'Subordinate clause: ''Although the road was muddy''. Main clause: ''the cyclists completed the journey''.', TRUE),
  (8988, 5080, 'It is a subordinate clause because ''when'' introduces a time relationship. Example: ''When the visitors arrived, the meeting began.''', TRUE),
  (8989, 5081, '(a) Relative clause: ''who repaired the bicycle''. (b) Noun clause: ''What she decided''.', TRUE),
  (8990, 5082, 'It is a simple sentence because it contains one independent clause. Its length comes from descriptive words and phrases, not additional clauses.', TRUE),
  (8991, 5083, '(a) Compound. (b) Complex. (c) Simple.', TRUE),
  (8992, 5084, 'Example: ''The learners celebrated after the final examination.''', TRUE),
  (8993, 5085, 'The electricity went off, but the learners continued studying.', TRUE),
  (8994, 5086, 'The bus broke down, so we arrived late. Alternatively: The bus broke down; we arrived late.', TRUE),
  (8995, 5087, 'No. It is a simple sentence with a compound predicate.', TRUE),
  (8996, 5088, 'Although the road was flooded, the driver continued carefully.', TRUE),
  (8997, 5089, 'Subordinate clause: ''If the rain stops.'' Independent clause: ''we will resume the match.''', TRUE),
  (8998, 5090, 'Because the library was closed, we studied at home.', TRUE),
  (8999, 5091, 'It is a compound-complex sentence. Subordinate clause: ''Although Mary was tired.'' Independent clauses: ''she completed her work'' and ''she submitted it before the deadline.''', TRUE),
  (9000, 5092, 'Because they wanted to remember the procedure, the learners took notes, and the teacher explained the experiment.', TRUE);
INSERT INTO public.answers (id, question_id, answer_text, is_correct) VALUES
  (9001, 5093, 'It is a complex sentence, not a compound-complex sentence.', TRUE),
  (9002, 5094, 'Have the learners completed their assignments?', TRUE),
  (9003, 5095, 'Did Akinyi submit her application?', TRUE),
  (9004, 5096, 'Could you tell me where the head teacher works?', TRUE),
  (9005, 5097, 'Do not bring your phone into the examination room.', TRUE),
  (9006, 5098, 'What a beautiful performance!', TRUE),
  (9007, 5099, '''You should remain seated.'' is a statement giving advice or expressing a recommendation. ''Remain seated.'' is a command with the understood subject ''you''.', TRUE),
  (9008, 5100, 'Although the farmer worked hard, the harvest was poor.', TRUE),
  (9009, 5101, 'The shop was closed. We went to the market. Alternatively: The shop was closed, so we went to the market.', TRUE),
  (9010, 5102, 'The learners arrived late, and the teacher had already started the lesson. Alternatively: The learners arrived late; the teacher had already started the lesson.', TRUE),
  (9011, 5103, 'must', TRUE),
  (9012, 5104, 'cans', TRUE),
  (9013, 5105, 'Bare infinitive', TRUE),
  (9014, 5106, 'May', TRUE),
  (9015, 5107, 'could', TRUE),
  (9016, 5108, 'can', TRUE),
  (9017, 5109, 'should', TRUE),
  (9018, 5110, 'must', TRUE),
  (9019, 5111, 'You ought to respect your elders.', TRUE),
  (9020, 5112, 'must', TRUE),
  (9021, 5113, 'cannot', TRUE),
  (9022, 5114, 'must, could, might', TRUE),
  (9023, 5115, 'Subject: The lightning, Object: the tall pine tree', TRUE),
  (9024, 5116, 'Active voice', TRUE),
  (9025, 5117, 'It is direct and dynamic', TRUE),
  (9026, 5118, 'An inspiring novel was written by the author.', TRUE),
  (9027, 5119, 'was opened', TRUE),
  (9028, 5120, 'To focus on the process and results rather than the experimenter', TRUE),
  (9029, 5121, 'Juma said, “I am ready for the test.”', TRUE),
  (9030, 5122, 'Inside the closing quotation marks', TRUE),
  (9031, 5123, 'requested', TRUE),
  (9032, 5124, 'Mary said that she was studying for her exams that day.', TRUE),
  (9033, 5125, 'the next day', TRUE),
  (9034, 5126, 'Ali said that he had completed the assignment.', TRUE),
  (9035, 5127, 'Zero Conditional', TRUE),
  (9036, 5128, 'were', TRUE),
  (9037, 5129, 'would have won', TRUE),
  (9038, 5130, 'Adjective: ''exhausted''. Noun: ''runner''.', TRUE),
  (9039, 5131, 'This notebook is hers. Alternatively, if preserving the original sentence structure: This is hers.', TRUE),
  (9040, 5132, 'In ''Which book did you borrow?'', ''which'' functions as an interrogative determiner (traditionally an interrogative adjective) because it introduces ''book''. In ''Which did you borrow?'', ''which'' functions as a pronoun because it stands alone.', TRUE),
  (9041, 5133, 'A beautiful small wooden box.', TRUE),
  (9042, 5134, 'He bought a useful new plastic container.', TRUE),
  (9043, 5135, 'A comma is appropriate in ''a quiet, peaceful village'' because ''quiet'' and ''peaceful'' are coordinate adjectives. A comma is not normally used in ''a small wooden table'' because the adjectives build the description cumulatively.', TRUE),
  (9044, 5136, 'Happy → happier → happiest. Large → larger → largest. Careful → more careful → most careful.', TRUE),
  (9045, 5137, 'This question is easier than the previous one.', TRUE),
  (9046, 5138, 'My bag is heavier than Peter''s.', TRUE),
  (9047, 5139, '''Extremely'' is an adverb of degree modifying the adjective ''careful''. ''Suddenly'' is an adverb of manner modifying the verb ''stopped''.', TRUE),
  (9048, 5140, 'She always goes to the library after school.', TRUE),
  (9049, 5141, '''He worked hard'' means he worked with a great deal of effort. ''He hardly worked'' means he did very little work or almost did not work.', TRUE),
  (9050, 5142, '''Yesterday'' is an adverb of time; ''outside'' is an adverb of place; ''hard'' is an adverb of manner.', TRUE),
  (9051, 5143, 'The learners always complete their assignments carefully.', TRUE),
  (9052, 5144, 'In ''The children waited outside'', ''outside'' is an adverb of place. In ''The children waited outside the classroom'', ''outside the classroom'' is a prepositional phrase functioning as an adverbial of place.', TRUE),
  (9053, 5145, '''Yesterday'' is an adverb of time; ''outside'' is an adverb of place; ''hard'' is an adverb of manner.', TRUE),
  (9054, 5146, 'The learners always complete their assignments carefully.', TRUE),
  (9055, 5147, 'In ''The children waited outside'', ''outside'' is an adverb of place. In ''The children waited outside the classroom'', ''outside the classroom'' is a prepositional phrase functioning as an adverbial of place.', TRUE),
  (9056, 5148, '''Very hot'' means the tea has a high temperature. ''Too hot to drink'' means its temperature is excessive for drinking comfortably or safely at that moment.', TRUE),
  (9057, 5149, 'The learner was confident enough. It was such a difficult task.', TRUE),
  (9058, 5150, '''Extremely'' modifies the adverb ''quickly''. ''Almost'' modifies the verb phrase ''fell'', indicating that the athlete came close to falling.', TRUE),
  (9059, 5151, 'The mechanic repaired the engine carefully.', TRUE),
  (9060, 5152, 'The flowers smell sweet. The gardener smelled the flowers carefully.', TRUE),
  (9061, 5153, '''Friendly'' is an adjective describing the noun ''neighbour''. ''Warmly'' is an adverb describing how the neighbour greeted us.', TRUE),
  (9062, 5154, 'The lesson begins at 8:00 a.m. The examination is on Monday. The school opened in January.', TRUE),
  (9063, 5155, '''For three hours'' states the duration of the study. ''Since six o''clock'' states when the study began.', TRUE),
  (9064, 5156, 'The learners must submit their work by Friday.', TRUE),
  (9065, 5157, 'The book is on the desk. The learners are in the classroom. We met at the gate.', TRUE),
  (9066, 5158, '''Between the two desks'' describes the teacher''s position in the space separating the desks. ''Among the learners'' describes the teacher''s position within a group of learners.', TRUE),
  (9067, 5159, 'In ''The cat is outside'', ''outside'' functions as an adverb of place. In ''The cat is outside the house'', ''outside the house'' is a prepositional phrase indicating place.', TRUE),
  (9068, 5160, 'through', TRUE),
  (9069, 5161, 'onto', TRUE),
  (9070, 5162, 'across', TRUE),
  (9071, 5163, 'under the shady mango tree', TRUE),
  (9072, 5164, 'Adverbial phrase of place', TRUE),
  (9073, 5165, 'confidence', TRUE),
  (9074, 5166, 'For, And, Nor, But, Or, Yet, So', TRUE),
  (9075, 5167, 'She was exhausted, but she continued running.', TRUE),
  (9076, 5168, 'so', TRUE),
  (9077, 5169, 'Although', TRUE),
  (9078, 5170, 'unless', TRUE),
  (9079, 5171, 'Dependent clause', TRUE),
  (9080, 5172, 'but', TRUE),
  (9081, 5173, 'Neither...nor', TRUE),
  (9082, 5174, 'Both joined elements must share the exact same grammatical structure', TRUE),
  (9083, 5175, 'Although Amina revised every evening, she still found the final question difficult. Alternatively: Amina revised every evening; however, she still found the final question difficult.', TRUE),
  (9084, 5176, 'The river overflowed, so the nearby road became impassable. Another correct version is: The river overflowed; as a result, the nearby road became impassable.', TRUE),
  (9085, 5177, 'First, I reviewed my notes and identified the topics I did not understand. I practised examination questions so that I could test whether I understood those topics.', TRUE),
  (9086, 5178, 'The coach was annoyed about the late arrival. ''Annoyed'' expresses displeasure but is generally less intense than ''furious''.', TRUE),
  (9087, 5179, '''Intelligent'' fits ''She is a bright learner'' because ''bright'' refers to mental ability. In ''The room is bright'', ''well-lit'' is a suitable replacement because ''bright'' refers to the amount of light.', TRUE),
  (9088, 5180, 'The committee made a decision promptly. ''Promptly'' is a suitable replacement and gives the sentence a slightly more formal tone.', TRUE),
  (9089, 5181, 'The path is wide. ''Wide'' contrasts with ''narrow'' in the dimension of width.', TRUE),
  (9090, 5182, 'Asha lends a pen to Brian. Brian borrows a pen from Asha.', TRUE),
  (9091, 5183, 'Possible → impossible; legal → illegal; regular → irregular. The prefixes vary because English spelling and word formation follow established patterns rather than one universal rule.', TRUE),
  (9092, 5184, 'You''re going to need two pens because your pencil is missing.', TRUE),
  (9093, 5185, 'They''re waiting over there. I can hear the bell.', TRUE),
  (9094, 5186, 'The two friends went to the library because it was too noisy to study at home.', TRUE),
  (9095, 5187, 'In the first sentence, ''bank'' means the land beside a river. In the second, it means a financial institution where money can be deposited.', TRUE),
  (9096, 5188, 'The captain will lead the team to victory. The old pipe was made of lead. In the first sentence, ''lead'' rhymes with ''need''; in the second, it rhymes with ''red''.', TRUE),
  (9097, 5189, 'A bat flew from the cave at dusk. The batter swung the bat and hit the ball.', TRUE),
  (9098, 5190, '''Rewrite'' means write again; ''misread'' means read or interpret incorrectly; ''preheat'' means heat something beforehand.', TRUE),
  (9099, 5191, 'Kindness, development and education. The suffixes form nouns naming a quality, a process or result, and the process or field of educating.', TRUE),
  (9100, 5192, 'Her happiness made the room cheerful.', TRUE),
  (9101, 5193, 'They hope to succeed in the competition. It was a successful project. The team completed the task successfully.', TRUE),
  (9102, 5194, 'Act (verb or noun), action (noun), active (adjective), activity (noun), actor (noun) and react (verb). They share a related base but express different actions, qualities, people or concepts.', TRUE),
  (9103, 5195, '''Spectator'' means a person who watches, while ''inspect'' means to examine carefully. The root provides a clue about seeing, but context and established word meanings are needed for accurate interpretation.', TRUE),
  (9104, 5196, '''Toothbrush'' is a closed compound, ''bus stop'' is an open compound, and ''mother-in-law'' is a hyphenated compound.', TRUE),
  (9105, 5197, 'A greenhouse is a structure used to grow plants under controlled conditions, not merely a house painted green. Example: The farmer grew tomato seedlings in a greenhouse.', TRUE),
  (9106, 5198, 'Classrooms, toothbrushes, mothers-in-law. In ''mother-in-law'', ''mother'' is the main noun, so it takes the plural ending.', TRUE),
  (9107, 5199, 'The committee will decide tomorrow. It was a difficult decision. She answered decisively.', TRUE),
  (9108, 5200, 'In ''They clean the classroom daily'', ''clean'' is a verb describing an action. In ''The classroom is clean'', ''clean'' is an adjective describing the classroom.', TRUE),
  (9109, 5201, 'The careful driver drove carefully through the busy market.', TRUE),
  (9110, 5202, 'I made a decision, did my homework and paid attention to the teacher.', TRUE),
  (9111, 5203, '''Heavy rain'' is the conventional collocation for intense or abundant rainfall. Example: Heavy rain caused flooding along the road.', TRUE),
  (9112, 5204, 'I kept my promise to return the book. The teacher strongly recommended regular reading. The community was deeply concerned about the water shortage.', TRUE),
  (9113, 5205, 'In the first sentence, ''turned down'' means rejected. In the second, it means reduced the radio''s volume.', TRUE),
  (9114, 5206, 'The committee turned it down after a long discussion.', TRUE),
  (9115, 5207, 'The team found out the cause and carried on working.', TRUE),
  (9116, 5208, 'It means Wanjiku studied or worked late into the night while preparing for her examination.', TRUE),
  (9117, 5209, 'The science project was very expensive.', TRUE),
  (9118, 5210, 'The class planned a surprise party for their teacher, but Brian let the cat out of the bag by telling her about it. The expression means that Brian revealed the secret.', TRUE),
  (9119, 5211, 'It''s important to take your advice, because the effect of poor revision can affect your results.', TRUE),
  (9120, 5212, 'Finish your revision, then answer the questions. This question is harder than the previous one.', TRUE),
  (9121, 5213, 'Her advice helped me prepare for the examination. I advise learners to practise explaining their answers.', TRUE),
  (9122, 5214, '''Reserved'' means quiet or restrained in expressing oneself. The contrast with learners who spoke freely and the detail that Njeri rarely volunteered answers support this interpretation.', TRUE),
  (9123, 5215, 'In the first sentence, ''light'' means not heavy. In the second, it means illumination. In the third, it is a verb meaning to set the candle burning.', TRUE),
  (9124, 5216, '''Cautiously'' means carefully and with awareness of possible danger. The damaged bridge, the rail and the testing of each step support this meaning.', TRUE),
  (9125, 5217, 'The expression means that the classroom was extremely noisy or disorderly. The writer compares the classroom to a zoo to emphasise the lack of calm or order.', TRUE),
  (9126, 5218, 'Literally, the phrase describes a heart made of stone. Figuratively, it suggests that someone is unfeeling, harsh or unwilling to show compassion.', TRUE),
  (9127, 5219, 'The news shocked him deeply. This preserves the idea of a powerful emotional impact, but reduces the vivid physical comparison created by ''hit him like a hammer''.', TRUE),
  (9128, 5220, '''Slim'' and ''skinny'' can both describe a thin person. ''Slim'' often has a more favourable association, while ''skinny'' can sound critical or suggest being too thin. The connotations depend on context.', TRUE),
  (9129, 5221, '''Persistent'' presents the learner as determined and willing to continue working. ''Stubborn'' may suggest unreasonable refusal to change. The writer''s choice can therefore make the same general behaviour appear admirable or problematic.', TRUE),
  (9130, 5222, '''Quiet'' neutrally describes someone who speaks little. ''Reserved'' may suggest restraint or privacy, while ''secretive'' can imply an unwillingness to reveal information. The words differ in the attitudes they may convey.', TRUE),
  (9131, 5223, 'The news spread very quickly from one person to another. Comparing it to wildfire emphasises how rapidly and widely the information travelled through the school.', TRUE),
  (9132, 5224, 'The learner remained as calm as a still lake during the examination. The comparison highlights the learner''s composure despite the pressure.', TRUE),
  (9133, 5225, '''The twins look like each other'' can be a literal statement of resemblance. ''The goalkeeper moved like a cat'' is a figurative comparison if it emphasises agility, speed or controlled movement.', TRUE),
  (9134, 5226, 'The examination is presented as a major obstacle that the learner must overcome before graduating. The mountain suggests difficulty, effort and the challenge of reaching a goal.', TRUE),
  (9135, 5227, 'The nurse was an angel to the frightened child. Both versions suggest kindness and comfort, but the metaphor presents the comparison more directly and emphatically.', TRUE),
  (9136, 5228, '''Knowledge is a key'' presents learning as something that opens opportunities or solves problems. It may suit a passage about education creating opportunities. ''Knowledge is a burden'' presents knowing something as difficult or emotionally demanding, such as knowing a painful secret.', TRUE),
  (9137, 5229, 'The village is personified as sleeping, suggesting quietness and stillness. The blanket of stars creates an image of the night sky covering the village, contributing to a peaceful atmosphere.', TRUE),
  (9138, 5230, '''Flowed'' describes an ordinary action of a river. ''Danced'' gives the river a human-like action and may suggest lively, graceful movement over the rocks.', TRUE),
  (9139, 5231, 'The furious storm attacked the rooftops and screamed through the streets. Later, the playful wind danced around the trees. The first sentence presents the storm as hostile and dangerous, while the second gives the wind lively, playful behaviour.', TRUE),
  (9140, 5232, 'The speaker means that there is a very large amount of homework to complete. The mountain exaggeration emphasises how overwhelming the workload feels.', TRUE),
  (9141, 5233, 'I have been waiting for a long time. The revised sentence communicates a lengthy wait more literally, but it loses some of the frustration or impatience expressed by ''forever''.', TRUE),
  (9142, 5234, 'The statement is not automatically hyperbole because it could be a factual claim if every learner failed. Actual examination results would help verify it, while an obviously emotional or exaggerated context might suggest that the speaker means many learners failed.', TRUE),
  (9143, 5235, 'The tone is optimistic. The words ''remained confident'' show that the team expects its effort to succeed despite the setbacks.', TRUE),
  (9144, 5236, 'The original sentence portrays the learner positively as persistent and willing to understand. The revised sentence portrays the learner negatively as difficult and possibly demanding.', TRUE),
  (9145, 5237, 'Example: ''The new school library is a welcoming, valuable space that gives learners an excellent opportunity to explore books and ideas.''', TRUE),
  (9146, 5238, 'Meaning 1: The speaker used binoculars to see the woman. Rewrite: ''Using binoculars, I saw the woman.'' Meaning 2: The woman had the binoculars. Rewrite: ''I saw the woman who was carrying the binoculars.''', TRUE),
  (9147, 5239, 'In ''The bag is light'', light means not heavy. In ''Turn on the light'', it means a source of illumination.', TRUE),
  (9148, 5240, 'Example 1: ''In the office, the parent spoke to the teacher.'' Example 2: ''The parent spoke to the teacher who was in the office.''', TRUE),
  (9149, 5241, 'On Friday, Amina travelled from Mombasa to Nairobi to study English.', TRUE),
  (9150, 5242, '''University'' is lowercase when used as a common noun. In ''Kenyatta University'', it forms part of the institution''s proper name, so both words are capitalised.', TRUE),
  (9151, 5243, 'Yesterday, Peter and I visited Lake Victoria after learning Kiswahili.', TRUE),
  (9152, 5244, 'Running; hoping; cities.', TRUE),
  (9153, 5245, '''Play'' becomes ''playing'' because the ''y'' follows a vowel and is retained before ''-ing''. ''Try'' becomes ''tried'' because the final ''y'' follows a consonant and changes to ''i'' before ''-ed''.', TRUE),
  (9154, 5246, 'The traveller stopped at the centre because he was hoping to find the colourful sign.', TRUE),
  (9155, 5247, 'Knife: silent ''k''; write: silent ''w''; lamb: silent final ''b''.', TRUE),
  (9156, 5248, 'In ''night'', ''gh'' is silent. In ''laugh'', ''gh'' represents the /f/ sound.', TRUE),
  (9157, 5249, 'Example: ''Please knock before you listen at the door.'' In ''knock'', the ''k'' is silent; in ''listen'', the ''t'' is silent.', TRUE),
  (9158, 5250, 'You''re going to lose your notes if you leave them there.', TRUE),
  (9159, 5251, 'Lack of sleep can affect your performance. One effect is reduced concentration. The mathematics test was harder than yesterday''s test.', TRUE),
  (9160, 5252, 'Example: ''The bird cleaned its feathers.'' ''It''s time to leave.'' ''We completed the exercise, then checked our answers.''', TRUE),
  (9161, 5253, 'Where did you put the dictionary? I know where you put the dictionary.', TRUE),
  (9162, 5254, 'Because the bus arrived late, the learners missed the beginning of the lesson. Did the learners miss the beginning of the lesson?', TRUE),
  (9163, 5255, 'Statement: ''The school library closes at five.'' Direct question: ''When does the school library close?'' Indirect question: ''I wonder when the school library closes.''', TRUE),
  (9164, 5256, 'After the examination, the learners packed their books, pens, rulers and calculators.', TRUE),
  (9165, 5257, 'Alternative 1: ''The road was flooded, so the bus could not pass.'' Alternative 2: ''The road was flooded. The bus could not pass.''', TRUE),
  (9166, 5258, 'Without commas: ''My brother who studies in Embu is visiting us tomorrow.'' This suggests the clause identifies which brother. With commas: ''My brother, who studies in Embu, is visiting us tomorrow.'' This presents the information about studying in Embu as extra information about the brother already identified.', TRUE),
  (9167, 5259, 'The girls'' uniforms were wet, and the teachers said it''s time to go home.', TRUE),
  (9168, 5260, '''The child''s bag'' means a bag belonging to one child. ''The children''s bags'' means bags belonging to children. ''The children brought bags'' simply says that the children brought bags; it does not mark possession, so no apostrophe is required.', TRUE),
  (9169, 5261, 'The students are waiting outside, and the school has changed its opening hours.', TRUE),
  (9170, 5262, 'The class needed the following items: pens, pencils and rulers.', TRUE),
  (9171, 5263, 'The first answer appeared correct; however, a later check revealed an error.', TRUE),
  (9172, 5264, 'The learners came from Embu, Kenya; Kisumu, Kenya; and Arusha, Tanzania.', TRUE),
  (9173, 5265, 'The teacher said, ''I will mark your work tomorrow.''', TRUE),
  (9174, 5266, 'Mary said that she was preparing for her examination.', TRUE),
  (9175, 5267, 'Example: The learner asked, ''May I submit the assignment tomorrow?'' The teacher replied, ''Yes, but make sure it is complete.''', TRUE),
  (9176, 5268, 'The ten-year-old learner attended a well-known school and entered a forty-six-page report.', TRUE),
  (9177, 5269, 'Example: ''The clerk asked me to re-sign the form because the first signature was unclear.'' ''After many years of service, she decided to resign from the position.''', TRUE),
  (9178, 5270, 'Only one thing mattered to the team—completing the project on time.', TRUE),
  (9179, 5271, 'A realistic revision timetable can make studying more manageable and help learners identify difficult topics early, provided it allows time for rest.', TRUE),
  (9180, 5272, 'Skimming can quickly reveal whether the article discusses water conservation and which sections are relevant. If its main ideas match the assignment, I can read those sections closely for evidence and details.', TRUE),
  (9181, 5273, 'Example passage: ''The school debate club meets every Thursday. Members research issues and prepare arguments. They practise listening to opposing views and responding respectfully. New members learn to organise evidence before speaking. These activities help learners develop confidence and reasoning skills.'' Summary: ''The school debate club helps learners develop research, reasoning, listening, and speaking skills.''', TRUE),
  (9182, 5274, '5 November.', TRUE),
  (9183, 5275, '7:00 a.m. The clue is the time expression in the sentence stating when the school trip leaves.', TRUE),
  (9184, 5276, 'Example notice: ''The music performance is on 18 November at 2:00 p.m. in the school hall. Entry costs KSh 50.'' Questions and answers: ''When does the performance begin?'' — 2:00 p.m. ''How much is entry?'' — KSh 50.', TRUE),
  (9185, 5277, 'Some learners face difficulties travelling long distances to school, and communities are trying to improve their journeys.', TRUE),
  (9186, 5278, 'Topic: The benefits of trees. Main idea: Trees support the environment by providing shade, reducing soil erosion and offering habitats for birds.', TRUE),
  (9187, 5279, 'It is a supporting detail because it describes one learner''s contribution. The main idea is that learners are preparing for a school performance.', TRUE),
  (9188, 5280, 'Main idea: Clean water and safe water practices are important for health. Supporting details: Treating drinking water can reduce harmful microorganisms; storing water in covered containers helps prevent contamination.', TRUE),
  (9189, 5281, 'Details (a) and (c) support the main idea because walking and swimming are forms of exercise that can improve physical fitness.', TRUE),
  (9190, 5282, 'Main idea: Preparing consistently helps learners perform better in examinations. Supporting details: It helps learners identify topics they do not understand; practising questions improves their ability to apply knowledge; reviewing mistakes helps them correct repeated errors.', TRUE),
  (9191, 5283, 'Date: 14 June. Place: a water-treatment plant in Athi River. Starting time: 9.00 a.m.', TRUE),
  (9192, 5284, 'The deadline is 20 October, and applicants must attach a copy of their latest report form.', TRUE),
  (9193, 5285, 'Brian placed the seedlings beside the fence and watered the soil around them. His sister later brought him a spade.', TRUE),
  (9194, 5286, 'Kamau may feel disappointed, embarrassed or worried about his result. Turning the paper face down and changing the subject suggest that he does not want to discuss it.', TRUE),
  (9195, 5287, 'The session was probably cancelled because rain or stormy weather was expected.', TRUE),
  (9196, 5288, 'Lilian had prepared for the presentation and was making an effort to deliver it confidently. The repeated practice suggests preparation, while standing upright and speaking clearly suggest a controlled delivery.', TRUE),
  (9197, 5289, '''Arid'' means very dry. The phrase ''the air was extremely dry'' provides a direct explanation, supported by the wilting plants.', TRUE),
  (9198, 5290, '''Reluctant'' means unwilling or hesitant. The teammates needed persuasion, while Asha volunteered immediately.', TRUE),
  (9199, 5291, '''Fragile'' means easily broken or damaged. The clue is that Musa wrapped the ornament carefully to prevent it from breaking.', TRUE),
  (9200, 5292, 'The purpose is to instruct readers on how to prepare porridge. The text gives ordered steps and uses action verbs such as ''boil'', ''mix'', ''add'' and ''stir''.', TRUE),
  (9201, 5293, 'Its main purpose is to inform readers about the science fair. The dates, participating classes and venue provide practical event information.', TRUE),
  (9202, 5294, 'The purpose is to persuade the administration to repair the playground fence. The broken sections and learners entering through damaged areas provide reasons for requesting the repair.', TRUE),
  (9203, 5295, 'The audience is parents and guardians. The tone is polite and respectful, as shown by the formal greeting, ''kindly'' and the expression of thanks.', TRUE),
  (9204, 5296, 'The tone is critical and frustrated. ''Once again'' suggests that the problem keeps happening, while ''no clear explanation'' criticises the officials'' response.', TRUE),
  (9205, 5297, 'A likely audience is learners and the wider school community. The tone is enthusiastic and encouraging because ''Imagine'' invites readers to picture a possibility, while ''discover a new world'' presents reading positively.', TRUE),
  (9206, 5298, '(a) Fact: the number of classrooms can be verified. (b) Opinion: ''finest'' is a judgement, and people may disagree about the criteria for deciding which classrooms are best.', TRUE),
  (9207, 5299, 'The second statement expresses an opinion or recommendation because it says what learners should do. The first is a factual claim that can be checked against the library''s lending policy.', TRUE),
  (9208, 5300, '''Thirty learners attended the science exhibition'' is a factual statement that can be checked against attendance records. ''It was the most exciting event of the year'' is an opinion because excitement is an evaluation. The attendance figure alone does not prove that the event was the most exciting.', TRUE),
  (9209, 5301, 'The message uses loaded language, including ''foolish'' and ''brilliant'', to make one choice seem shameful and the other admirable. It also appeals to social identity by suggesting that sensible families have already switched, pressuring readers to follow them.', TRUE),
  (9210, 5302, 'The conclusion may be unfairly one-sided because it judges the entire timetable using only three reported cases. Useful additional information would include how many learners use the buses, how often the problem occurs, whether the timetable has other benefits and what caused the missed buses.', TRUE),
  (9211, 5303, 'The rhetorical question is ''Will we wait until another riverbank collapses before protecting our homes?'' It highlights a possible danger and creates urgency. The call to action is ''Plant grass and trees along the river today.'' It tells readers what action the writer wants them to take.', TRUE),
  (9212, 5304, 'The claim that the book is the best in Kenya is not established by the sales figure alone. The figure may show that 500 learners bought it last month, but it does not prove that it is better than competing books. Relevant evidence could include accurate comparisons of content quality, curriculum alignment, usability and learning outcomes.', TRUE),
  (9213, 5305, 'The conclusion overstates the evidence because a tendency does not guarantee an outcome for every learner. Academic performance depends on many factors. A more careful conclusion is: ''Adequate sleep may be associated with better school performance, but it does not guarantee high marks for every learner.''', TRUE),
  (9214, 5306, 'The reported average rose by 13 percentage points, from 55% to 68%. The figures show an increase after peer study groups were introduced, but they do not by themselves prove that the groups caused the increase. More information about the learners, test comparability, other changes and, where possible, a suitable comparison group would help assess the cause.', TRUE),
  (9215, 5307, 'The key information is that registration must be completed with the English teacher by Friday, and the competition takes place on Tuesday at 10.00 a.m. in Room 4.', TRUE),
  (9216, 5308, 'A balanced diet includes a variety of foods that provide different nutrients needed for energy, growth and health.', TRUE),
  (9217, 5309, 'The materials, safety precautions, procedure and expected observation are most important. They tell the learner what is needed, how to work safely, what to do and what to look for. The scientist''s history is background information unless the task specifically asks about it.', TRUE),
  (9218, 5310, 'Main point: Exercise benefits learners in several ways. Supporting details: It strengthens muscles and supports heart health; it can reduce stress and improve well-being; team sports provide opportunities to practise cooperation.', TRUE),
  (9219, 5311, 'Section 1: Rainwater can be collected from roofs and directed into a storage system. Section 2: Covered tanks help store collected water while reducing contamination risks. Section 3: Filters can remove some impurities from water.', TRUE),
  (9220, 5312, 'Main point: A school library provides resources and spaces that support independent learning. All three details describe ways learners can use the library to study and access information.', TRUE),
  (9221, 5313, 'Composting food waste is the most directly relevant detail because compost can add organic matter and nutrients to the soil. Planting and watering are garden activities, while the fence colour does not explain soil fertility.', TRUE),
  (9222, 5314, 'The relevant details are handwashing with soap, covering coughs and staying home when infectious. Each can help reduce the spread of germs. The uniform colour and school gate location do not directly address disease prevention.', TRUE),
  (9223, 5315, 'The summary is inaccurate because it claims that all filters make all water safe, while the passage says filters remove some particles and may not remove dissolved chemicals or kill all harmful microorganisms. A more faithful version is: ''Some water filters remove suspended particles, but filtering alone may not make water safe to drink.''', TRUE),
  (9224, 5316, 'The meeting is scheduled for 2.00 p.m.', TRUE),
  (9225, 5317, 'The school introduced labelled rubbish bins to help learners separate different types of waste.', TRUE),
  (9226, 5318, 'Because the water tank was empty, the school postponed the cleaning activity until it could be refilled.', TRUE),
  (9227, 5319, 'When strong rain falls on slopes without adequate cover, it can carry the soil away.', TRUE),
  (9228, 5320, 'By examining errors they have made, learners can discover what they misunderstood and answer similar questions more effectively in future. The wording and sentence structure differ, but the main idea remains.', TRUE),
  (9229, 5321, 'The learner changes ''some'' to ''all'' and turns a comparison between partial shade and direct sunlight into a universal claim that shade is best. A faithful paraphrase is: ''Certain plants may grow more successfully in partly shaded conditions than in full sunlight.''', TRUE),
  (9230, 5322, 'Although Miriam studied consistently, she still found the final question difficult.', TRUE),
  (9231, 5323, 'Because the learners left the laboratory taps running, a large amount of water was wasted.', TRUE),
  (9232, 5324, 'Akinyi collected the books and arranged them on the shelf.', TRUE),
  (9233, 5325, 'Plastic bags can persist in the environment, block drainage channels and harm animals. Communities can reduce these problems by encouraging reusable bags, collecting plastic waste and teaching residents to separate waste properly.', TRUE),
  (9234, 5326, 'Trees provide shade, help reduce soil erosion and create habitats for birds and other animals.', TRUE),
  (9235, 5327, 'Regular reading can improve language skills by expanding vocabulary, exposing learners to different sentence structures and strengthening comprehension.', TRUE),
  (9236, 5328, 'Healthy Diet and Nutrients
- Variety: eat different kinds of foods.
- Carbohydrates: provide energy.
- Proteins: support growth and tissue repair.
- Fruits and vegetables: provide vitamins, minerals and fibre.', TRUE),
  (9237, 5329, 'A numbered list or flow diagram would be most suitable because it clearly shows the order of the five stages. An unstructured list may not make the required sequence obvious.', TRUE),
  (9238, 5330, 'Plant growth requirements:
- Water: needed for plant processes and growth.
- Light: provides energy for photosynthesis.
- Soil: can supply water, support and mineral nutrients.
- Result: suitable conditions help the plant grow.', TRUE),
  (9239, 5331, 'Setting: A learner''s home and the journey to school on examination morning. Problem: The learner cannot find the examination admission card. Events: (1) The learner searches the school bag. (2) A sibling remembers seeing the card near the study table. (3) The learner returns to the table and searches carefully. Resolution: The card is found inside a notebook, and the learner reaches school before the examination begins.', TRUE),
  (9240, 5332, 'Position: Learners should help keep their school environment clean. Reasons: (1) Proper disposal reduces litter. (2) Clean surroundings make classrooms and shared spaces more pleasant. (3) Learner participation builds responsibility for shared facilities. Conclusion: Since cleanliness benefits the whole school and develops responsibility, learners should take part in keeping their environment clean.', TRUE),
  (9241, 5333, 'Introduction: Establish the market''s location and the time of day. Body section 1: Describe sights, such as colourful produce and crowded stalls. Body section 2: Describe sounds, such as traders calling out prices and customers bargaining. Body section 3: Describe smells and movement, such as fresh fruit, cooking food and people carrying baskets. Conclusion: Summarise the lively atmosphere and the impression the market creates.', TRUE),
  (9242, 5334, 'Topic sentence: Regular exercise supports learners'' physical and mental well-being. Supporting sentences: Physical activity can strengthen muscles and improve fitness. It can also help learners manage stress and improve their mood.', TRUE),
  (9243, 5335, 'The sentence is too narrow because it focuses on one feature of the library rather than its usefulness. A stronger topic sentence is: ''The school library supports learning by providing resources and a suitable place for study.''', TRUE),
  (9244, 5336, 'The topic sentence is ''Community clean-ups improve shared spaces.'' The sentence about the school football team winning its match is irrelevant because it does not explain or support the benefits of community clean-ups.', TRUE),
  (9245, 5337, 'A clean compound reduces unpleasant smells and makes the school more comfortable. Proper disposal of rubbish also helps prevent blocked drains. For example, placing waste in designated bins keeps paths clear for learners and staff.', TRUE),
  (9246, 5338, 'Regular exercise improves physical fitness and can help learners feel more alert during school activities.', TRUE),
  (9247, 5339, 'Sentence (a) is relevant because it gives a practical way to prevent water waste. Sentence (b) is unrelated to the main point.', TRUE),
  (9248, 5340, 'The sentence ''Our school library has a new dictionary collection'' breaks unity because it discusses library resources rather than the benefits of trees.', TRUE),
  (9249, 5341, 'Regular exercise provides several important health benefits.', TRUE),
  (9250, 5342, 'The sentence may be relevant if the paragraph explains how cyclists should protect pedestrians at crossings. If it discusses only pedestrian behaviour without connecting it to cyclists, it should be revised or moved to a paragraph about pedestrian safety.', TRUE);
INSERT INTO public.answers (id, question_id, answer_text, is_correct) VALUES
  (9251, 5343, 'First, she collected the required materials. She read the instructions. She answered the questions. Finally, she submitted the assignment.', TRUE),
  (9252, 5344, 'The team trained every afternoon; however, it lost the final match.', TRUE),
  (9253, 5345, 'Brian bought a bicycle. The bicycle was expensive.', TRUE),
  (9254, 5346, 'Water is essential for cooking, cleaning and other daily activities, yet it can easily be wasted at home. Simple conservation habits help families use this valuable resource responsibly.', TRUE),
  (9255, 5347, 'School libraries deserve continued support because they give learners access to books, encourage independent study and provide a quiet place to read. Maintaining a useful library is therefore an investment in learning.', TRUE),
  (9256, 5348, 'The conclusion introduces an unrelated issue about a computer laboratory instead of closing the discussion about environmental cleanliness. It should summarise the value of keeping the environment clean or encourage relevant action.', TRUE),
  (9257, 5349, 'Example plan: The main character is a learner who ignores advice to prepare for a school presentation. The setting is the classroom on presentation day. The problem is that the learner forgets important points. The turning point occurs when the learner pauses, organises the remaining ideas and speaks honestly. The resolution is that the presentation improves, and the learner learns the value of preparation.', TRUE),
  (9258, 5350, 'I arrived at school and realised that I had left my project at home. I called my older brother, who had not yet left the house, and asked whether he could bring it. He arrived before the lesson began, allowing me to present my work to the class.', TRUE),
  (9259, 5351, 'The last matatu had left the stage when I reached the roadside at dusk. I checked my pocket and realised that my fare money was missing. Dark clouds gathered above the hills, and the first drops of rain began to fall. Then I heard someone calling my name from across the road.', TRUE),
  (9260, 5352, 'Traders arranged bright tomatoes, green vegetables and ripe bananas along the narrow stalls. Buyers called out prices while metal pans clattered behind the food counters. The smell of roasted maize drifted through the warm air. People moved between the stalls, carrying bags and greeting familiar traders.', TRUE),
  (9261, 5353, 'The old house leaned towards the road, with cracked walls, broken windowpanes and a rusted gate hanging from one hinge.', TRUE),
  (9262, 5354, 'Soft morning light rested on the dew-covered grass as butterflies drifted between the flowers. A gentle breeze stirred the leaves while birds called from the branches overhead.', TRUE),
  (9263, 5355, 'Planting trees can help reduce soil erosion because their roots hold soil particles together. The roots also help stabilise the ground on slopes. In addition, tree canopies can reduce the direct impact of raindrops on the soil. These effects make it harder for rainwater to carry soil away.', TRUE),
  (9264, 5356, 'Vocabulary: Reading exposes learners to unfamiliar words in meaningful contexts, helping them understand and use new expressions. Knowledge: Books and other texts introduce information about people, places and ideas. Writing: Regular reading exposes learners to sentence structures and ways of organising ideas that they can apply in their own writing.', TRUE),
  (9265, 5357, 'Blocked drains can cause flooding because rubbish and other materials prevent rainwater from flowing away freely. As water accumulates faster than it can drain, it may spill onto roads and nearby properties.', TRUE),
  (9266, 5358, 'Learners should receive a reasonable amount of homework on school days because it helps them practise skills taught in class and identify topics they do not yet understand. However, the workload should be manageable so that learners still have time to rest and pursue other responsibilities.', TRUE),
  (9267, 5359, 'Schools should provide learners with access to clean drinking water because it supports hydration throughout the school day. For example, learners who can refill clean drinking bottles during breaks do not have to rely on unsafe water sources. Providing safe water is therefore an important part of maintaining a suitable learning environment.', TRUE),
  (9268, 5360, 'Textbooks are essential for studying required subjects, but they do not provide every opportunity for independent reading. A planned reading period can expose learners to different vocabulary, writing styles and ideas while complementing textbook study rather than replacing it.', TRUE),
  (9269, 5361, 'Yesterday, our class visited the museum. We were excited because the guide explained how ancient tools were used.', TRUE),
  (9270, 5362, 'We are preparing for the upcoming examination.', TRUE),
  (9271, 5363, 'Each of the players has brought their boots, but none of them is ready. ''Each'' is grammatically singular, so ''has'' is used. ''Boots'' is appropriate if each player has brought the pair needed to play. Singular ''their'' is widely accepted in contemporary English when referring to a person without specifying gender.', TRUE),
  (9272, 5364, '12 River Road
Embu
9 October 2026

Dear Amina,

How are you? I hope you are doing well. I am writing to tell you about our school''s cultural day, which took place last Friday.

Learners presented traditional songs, dances and displays from different communities. My favourite part was watching our classmates perform a dance they had practised for several weeks. The event helped us appreciate different cultural traditions and work together.

I wish you could have attended. Tell me about any interesting activities at your school when you write back.

Your friend,
Kamau', TRUE),
  (9273, 5365, 'I''m sorry that you have so much to manage at the moment. You could make a simple timetable that sets aside time for household duties, homework and rest. Try completing the most urgent assignments first, and speak to a trusted adult if your responsibilities leave you with too little time to study. With a realistic plan and some support, things may become easier.', TRUE),
  (9274, 5366, 'An informal letter is usually written to someone familiar, while a formal letter is written for an official or professional purpose. Informal letters allow friendly expressions and contractions, whereas formal letters generally use more restrained and precise language. Informal letters may begin ''Dear Amina'' and close ''Your friend''; formal letters use an appropriate official salutation and a conventional formal closing.', TRUE),
  (9275, 5367, '12 River Road
Embu
9 October 2026

The Principal
Green Valley Secondary School
P.O. Box 120
Embu

Dear Sir/Madam,

RE: REQUEST FOR PERMISSION TO START A READING CLUB

I am writing to request permission to establish a reading club at our school. The club would encourage learners to read regularly, discuss books and develop their vocabulary and comprehension skills.

We propose to meet once a week at a time approved by the school. We would follow school rules and ensure that all books and materials are handled responsibly.

I would be grateful if you considered this request. Thank you for your time and consideration.

Yours faithfully,
Kamau Mwangi', TRUE),
  (9276, 5368, 'I am writing to enquire about the closing date for applications. I would appreciate confirmation of the deadline so that I can submit my application on time.', TRUE),
  (9277, 5369, '''Yours faithfully'' is conventionally used after ''Dear Sir/Madam'' when the recipient''s name is unknown. ''Yours sincerely'' is commonly used when the letter begins with a named salutation, such as ''Dear Ms Wanjiku''.', TRUE),
  (9278, 5370, '12 River Road
Embu
9 October 2026

The Teacher-Librarian
Green Valley Secondary School
P.O. Box 120
Embu

Dear Sir/Madam,

RE: APPLICATION FOR THE POSITION OF SCHOOL LIBRARY ASSISTANT

I am writing to apply for the school library assistant opportunity advertised on the school noticeboard.

I am organised, careful when handling books and willing to assist other learners. Through group assignments and school activities, I have developed skills in arranging materials, following instructions and working cooperatively with others. I would use these skills to help maintain an orderly library and support learners who need assistance locating materials.

I would appreciate the opportunity to be considered for the position. Thank you for your time and consideration.

Yours faithfully,
Kamau Mwangi', TRUE),
  (9279, 5371, 'I am organised and dependable, and I have experience coordinating tasks during school group projects. These skills would help me complete assigned duties carefully and work effectively with others.', TRUE),
  (9280, 5372, 'The applicant should ensure that both the application letter and curriculum vitae are included, verify that the application will be submitted by 20 October using the required method, and check that names, contact details, qualifications and claims are accurate and relevant to the position.', TRUE),
  (9281, 5373, 'To: teacher@example.com
Subject: Clarification on Assignment Submission Deadline

Dear Teacher,

I am writing to confirm the deadline for submitting the English assignment. Could you please let me know the final submission date and whether the work should be submitted in printed or electronic form?

Thank you for your assistance.

Kind regards,
Kamau Mwangi', TRUE),
  (9282, 5374, 'Cc sends a visible copy to additional recipients, and their addresses can generally be seen by the other recipients. Bcc sends a copy without displaying those recipients'' addresses to the other recipients. Bcc may be appropriate when sending an announcement to many people who do not need to see one another''s email addresses.', TRUE),
  (9283, 5375, 'Attach the correct report, open or preview it to ensure it is the intended file, and check the recipient''s address and email text before sending. This prevents an incomplete message and reduces the risk of sharing the wrong document.', TRUE),
  (9284, 5376, 'CURRICULUM VITAE

Name: Kamau Mwangi
Contact: 07XX XXX XXX; kamau@example.com

Profile: A secondary school learner interested in reading, organisation and helping others locate learning materials.

Education: Currently studying at Green Valley Secondary School.

Skills: Organising materials, following instructions, communicating politely and working in a team.

Relevant experience: Helped organise books and learning materials during a class project, if accurate for the applicant.

Activities: Member of the school reading club, if applicable.

Referees: Available on request, where suitable and accepted by the organisation.', TRUE),
  (9285, 5377, 'A dependable applicant with organisational, communication and basic computer skills, seeking an entry-level office assistant opportunity.', TRUE),
  (9286, 5378, 'The learner should ask permission because the teacher may be contacted and asked to comment on the learner''s suitability. Permission respects the teacher''s time and privacy and allows the learner to confirm that the teacher is willing and able to provide a reference.', TRUE),
  (9287, 5379, 'REPORT ON LITTER IN CLASSROOMS

To: The Principal
From: Kamau Mwangi, Class Representative
Date: 9 October 2026

Purpose
This report examines the litter problem observed in classrooms.

Findings
Paper scraps and food wrappers have been found on classroom floors after breaks. Some waste is being left on desks instead of being placed in bins. The observations suggest that waste disposal practices need improvement.

Conclusion
Litter makes classrooms less pleasant and creates additional cleaning work.

Recommendations
Provide clearly marked waste bins where needed, remind learners to dispose of rubbish properly and arrange regular checks after breaks.', TRUE),
  (9288, 5380, 'The writer did not enjoy the music at the event. Further information would be needed to assess the event''s overall success.', TRUE),
  (9289, 5381, 'Finding: Some learners arrive before the school gate opens, causing delays in entering the compound. Recommendation: Review the gate-opening time and, where staffing and safety arrangements permit, open the gate earlier to accommodate arriving learners.', TRUE),
  (9290, 5382, 'GREEN VALLEY SECONDARY SCHOOL

NOTICE: INTER-CLASS DEBATE

All interested learners are invited to attend an inter-class debate on Friday, 16 October, at 2.00 p.m. in the school hall. Class representatives should submit the names of their speakers to the English teacher by Wednesday.

Issued by: English Club

The date and details are illustrative and should be adjusted to the actual event.', TRUE),
  (9291, 5383, 'NOTICE: ENVIRONMENTAL CLUB MEETING

All Environmental Club members will meet on Thursday, 15 October, at 3.30 p.m. in Room 4. Members should bring their activity plans and notebooks.

Issued by: Environmental Club

These details are illustrative.', TRUE),
  (9292, 5384, 'The new venue tells learners where to report, preventing them from going to the wrong place. The effective date tells them which examination or day the change applies to, reducing the risk of confusion about earlier arrangements.', TRUE),
  (9293, 5385, 'GREEN VALLEY SECONDARY SCHOOL

INVITATION TO THE ANNUAL PRIZE-GIVING CEREMONY

The Principal and staff of Green Valley Secondary School cordially invite parents and guardians to the school''s annual prize-giving ceremony on Friday, 23 October, at 10.00 a.m. in the school hall.

Kindly confirm your attendance with the school office by 16 October.

The date and details are illustrative and should be replaced with the actual arrangements.', TRUE),
  (9294, 5386, 'Hi Amina,

I''d love you to come to my birthday gathering at our home on Saturday, 24 October, at 2.00 p.m. We''ll have lunch and spend some time together. Please let me know by Thursday if you can make it.

Hope you can come!
Kamau

The date and details are illustrative.', TRUE),
  (9295, 5387, 'Dear Sir/Madam,

Thank you for inviting me to the ceremony. I regret that I will be unable to attend because of a prior commitment. I appreciate the invitation and wish you a successful event.

Yours faithfully,
Kamau Mwangi', TRUE),
  (9296, 5388, '1. Opening and apologies. 2. Confirmation of the previous minutes. 3. Planning the inter-school debate competition, including teams, venue and budget. 4. Any other business and date of the next meeting.', TRUE),
  (9297, 5389, 'Agenda item: Clean-up exercise. Members agreed that the clean-up would take place on 22 October. The secretary was assigned to prepare the notice by 18 October.', TRUE),
  (9298, 5390, 'Members discussed the cost of materials and agreed to allocate KSh 2,000 for their purchase.', TRUE),
  (9299, 5391, 'Collect the books. Place them on the classroom shelf. Arrange them by subject, keeping each subject''s books together.', TRUE),
  (9300, 5392, '1. Write your name and class on the cover. 2. Check that all assigned exercises are complete. 3. Correct errors and arrange the pages in order. 4. Submit the book to the teacher.', TRUE),
  (9301, 5393, 'The instruction does not identify the chemical, quantity, equipment, heating method or safety precautions. A complete instruction should specify the correct substance and amount, suitable equipment, the required procedure and any relevant safety warning.', TRUE),
  (9302, 5394, 'Good morning, fellow learners and teachers. Today, I would like to discuss how we can keep our school compound clean. I will focus on proper waste disposal, using rubbish bins and taking responsibility for our shared environment.', TRUE),
  (9303, 5395, 'The slide is overcrowded, making the information difficult to read, and reading it word for word weakens audience engagement. The learner should use a short heading, a few key points or a simple visual, then explain the ideas in their own words.', TRUE),
  (9304, 5396, 'In conclusion, regular reading strengthens our vocabulary, understanding and ability to express ideas. Let us begin by setting aside a little time each day to read something useful. A reading habit grows one page at a time. Thank you for listening.', TRUE),
  (9305, 5397, 'The school is considering ways to address lateness caused partly by unreliable transport.', TRUE),
  (9306, 5398, 'Main idea: The class plans to plant trees around the playing field. Supporting detail: Trees help reduce soil erosion.', TRUE),
  (9307, 5399, 'After a market fire damaged several shops, the speaker urged traders to check their electrical wiring to help prevent similar incidents.', TRUE),
  (9308, 5400, 'Venue: Room 12. Time: 4.00 p.m. Item: A notebook.', TRUE),
  (9309, 5401, 'The learner must submit the draft by Monday.', TRUE),
  (9310, 5402, 'Collect three forms and submit two completed forms to the class teacher.', TRUE),
  (9311, 5403, '1. Read the paragraph. 2. Circle three verbs. 3. Use one of the circled verbs in a sentence.', TRUE),
  (9312, 5404, 'Collect five sheets and submit only the two completed pages.', TRUE),
  (9313, 5405, 'Before handing in the composition, check its punctuation and correct any spelling errors.', TRUE),
  (9314, 5406, 'The main purpose is to inform candidates about the examination time and instruct them to be seated by 7.30 a.m.', TRUE),
  (9315, 5407, 'The speaker''s attitude is positive and encouraging. The phrase ''wonderful effort'' expresses praise, while the reference to improvement recognises the team''s progress.', TRUE),
  (9316, 5408, 'The purpose is to warn listeners about water shortages and encourage them to conserve water. The attitude is concerned, as shown by the possible shortage and the request to close taps properly.', TRUE),
  (9317, 5409, 'The learner may be implying that the books are blocking the doorway and should be moved.', TRUE),
  (9318, 5410, 'The learner is probably being sarcastic and means that the classmate is late. The contradiction between the praise for being early and the forty-minute delay supports this inference.', TRUE),
  (9319, 5411, 'The teacher is likely prompting the learner to open the book and begin working.', TRUE),
  (9320, 5412, 'Debate competition — 12 November; venue: main hall; each team: register 4 speakers by 5 November.', TRUE),
  (9321, 5413, 'Leave a space for the second cause and continue listening for the third cause and any further explanation. Review the gap afterward if the speaker repeats or clarifies the information.', TRUE),
  (9322, 5414, 'Correct notes: Bring a ruler and a pencil; do not bring a calculator.', TRUE),
  (9323, 5415, 'To reduce delays, bus passengers must assemble at the gate by 6.45 a.m. from Monday, as the bus will leave at 7.00 a.m. without waiting for late passengers.', TRUE),
  (9324, 5416, 'The speaker encourages learners to plant trees around the school because trees provide shade, protect soil and support wildlife.', TRUE),
  (9325, 5417, 'The speaker explained safe water practices, including boiling water when its safety is uncertain, storing it in clean covered containers and preventing contamination from dirty cups.', TRUE),
  (9326, 5418, 'Take the book and place it on the shelf.', TRUE),
  (9327, 5419, 'I would begin by stating that arriving on time helps learners benefit from the full lesson. I would then explain that punctuality prevents missed instructions and reduces disruption to classmates. I would conclude by encouraging learners to prepare their materials and leave home early enough.', TRUE),
  (9328, 5420, 'The speaker should use a moderate pace, explain unfamiliar technical terms in simpler language, and pause between key ideas or sections.', TRUE),
  (9329, 5421, 'When does the meeting begin? Where does the meeting take place?', TRUE),
  (9330, 5422, 'Checking your work helps you correct errors and ensure that you have completed every part of the task.', TRUE),
  (9331, 5423, 'Could you please explain what you mean by ''resource allocation'' in this discussion?', TRUE),
  (9332, 5424, 'First, write your name and class on the assignment. Next, check that you have answered every question. Then, correct spelling and punctuation errors and arrange the pages in order. Finally, submit the assignment before the deadline.', TRUE),
  (9333, 5425, 'Dividing presentation tasks helps each member know what they are responsible for. It reduces unnecessary repetition and makes it easier to combine the research into one organised presentation.', TRUE),
  (9334, 5426, 'I would politely explain which step must be completed first, restate the steps in order using words such as ''first'', ''next'' and ''finally'', and ask the classmate to repeat the sequence to confirm understanding.', TRUE),
  (9335, 5427, 'They can pause, agree that one person will speak first, and allow the other to respond when the first has finished.', TRUE),
  (9336, 5428, 'I see your point, but I disagree with the suggestion because it may be too expensive. Could we consider a less costly alternative?', TRUE),
  (9337, 5429, 'We have heard a few suggestions so far. Would you like to share your thoughts on this issue?', TRUE),
  (9338, 5430, 'In my view, learners should have a daily reading period because it helps them develop vocabulary and improves their ability to understand different texts.', TRUE),
  (9339, 5431, 'The first is a factual claim that can be verified using school records. The second is an opinion or recommendation that needs reasons, such as enrolment pressure or available classroom space.', TRUE),
  (9340, 5432, 'Our class should have more rubbish bins because the existing bins fill up quickly and learners sometimes leave litter on the floor. Additional bins in convenient places would make proper waste disposal easier.', TRUE),
  (9341, 5433, 'I agree that school gardens can provide vegetables. They can also help learners develop practical skills in planting, caring for crops and understanding how plants grow.', TRUE),
  (9342, 5434, 'I respectfully disagree with the plan because it may cost more than the group can afford. Could we consider a less expensive alternative?', TRUE),
  (9343, 5435, 'I agree that the school may need more classroom space, but repairing unsafe existing classrooms is also important. The school could assess the condition of the current rooms and compare the costs and urgency of repairs with the need for a new classroom.', TRUE),
  (9344, 5436, 'The group could consider whether regular reading improves vocabulary and comprehension, whether the timetable can accommodate the period, and whether enough suitable reading materials are available.', TRUE),
  (9345, 5437, 'The chairperson could give each participant an opportunity to contribute and remind everyone to wait until the current speaker finishes before responding.', TRUE),
  (9346, 5438, 'The group should summarise the points of agreement, record the unresolved concerns and state whether a decision was reached. If no final decision was made, it should recommend a next step, such as gathering more information.', TRUE),
  (9347, 5439, 'Opening: Explain why conserving water at school matters. Body point 1: Identify common sources of waste, such as leaking taps. Body point 2: Explain actions such as reporting leaks and closing taps properly. Conclusion: Encourage every learner to use water responsibly and report leaks promptly.', TRUE),
  (9348, 5440, 'I would replace the paragraphs with short headings, key points and relevant visuals, remove repeated information, and explain the details orally. I would then rehearse and time the presentation.', TRUE),
  (9349, 5441, 'I would say, ''I am not certain of the answer, and I do not want to give you incorrect information. I will check a reliable source and confirm it if possible.''', TRUE),
  (9350, 5442, 'You should select me because I organise tasks well, listen to team members and encourage respectful discussion. I would help the team prepare arguments and ensure everyone contributes.', TRUE),
  (9351, 5443, 'You could say, ''Could you please clarify what you mean by that question?'' Then listen carefully before responding.', TRUE),
  (9352, 5444, 'Interrupting can be corrected by waiting until the interviewer finishes. Giving irrelevant answers can be corrected by addressing the question directly. Arriving late can be avoided by planning the journey and arriving early.', TRUE),
  (9353, 5445, 'The interruptions may make the speaker feel unheard, while divided attention may cause important information to be missed. The learner should put aside distractions, face the speaker comfortably, listen without interrupting and ask relevant questions afterwards.', TRUE),
  (9354, 5446, 'Avoiding eye contact may result from discomfort, concentration or cultural expectations rather than dishonesty. A person''s words, actions and the wider context should be considered before drawing a conclusion.', TRUE),
  (9355, 5447, 'Said warmly with a friendly expression, ''You came early'' may express approval or welcome. Said with exaggerated emphasis and a sarcastic tone, it may suggest criticism or disbelief.', TRUE),
  (9356, 5448, '''Ship'' contains /ɪ/, while ''sheep'' contains /iː/. The difference in vowel sound changes the word''s meaning.', TRUE),
  (9357, 5449, 'The letter ''a'' has different pronunciations in ''cat'' and ''cake''. In ''cat'' it represents /æ/, while in ''cake'' it represents /eɪ/ in many standard pronunciations.', TRUE),
  (9358, 5450, 'A diphthong is a vowel sound that changes in quality within one syllable. In ''boy'', the vowel sound is commonly represented as /ɔɪ/.', TRUE),
  (9359, 5451, '''Fan'' begins with voiceless /f/, while ''van'' begins with voiced /v/. Both are labiodental fricatives, but /v/ involves vocal-fold vibration.', TRUE),
  (9360, 5452, '/m/ is a voiced bilabial nasal. Both lips close, the vocal folds vibrate, and air passes through the nose.', TRUE),
  (9361, 5453, '''Ship'' begins with the single consonant sound /ʃ/, represented by the two letters ''sh''.', TRUE),
  (9362, 5454, '''Pin'' begins with /p/ and ''bin'' begins with /b/. Both contain the same vowel /ɪ/ and final consonant /n/, so they form a minimal pair.', TRUE),
  (9363, 5455, 'The learner should practise the contrast between voiceless /f/ in ''fan'' and voiced /v/ in ''van'', keeping the mouth position similar while changing voicing.', TRUE),
  (9364, 5456, 'Example: ''ship'' /ʃɪp/ and ''sheep'' /ʃiːp/. The vowel changes from /ɪ/ to /iː/, producing two different words.', TRUE),
  (9365, 5457, '''Computer'' has three syllables: com-pu-ter.', TRUE),
  (9366, 5458, '''Rain'' has one syllable because the letters ''ai'' represent one vowel sound in the word.', TRUE),
  (9367, 5459, '''Cat'' is a closed syllable because it ends in the consonant sound /t/. The first syllable of ''open'' is open because it ends in a vowel sound.', TRUE),
  (9368, 5460, 'The second syllable is stressed: be-GIN. Practise making ''gin'' more prominent while keeping the first syllable lighter.', TRUE),
  (9369, 5461, 'Noun: ''The school kept a REcord of attendance.'' Verb: ''Please reCORD the results.''', TRUE),
  (9370, 5462, 'In a common British pronunciation, ''important'' is stressed on the second syllable: im-POR-tant. The learner should listen to a reliable model, make the second syllable prominent and repeat the word naturally.', TRUE),
  (9371, 5463, 'Stress on ''I'' contrasts the speaker with another person. Stress on ''borrowed'' contrasts borrowing with buying or taking. Stress on ''book'' contrasts the book with another item.', TRUE),
  (9372, 5464, 'A natural reading might emphasise ''students'', ''completed'', ''difficult'' and ''task'', while keeping ''the'' and ''the'' less prominent unless the context requires contrast.', TRUE),
  (9373, 5465, '''MARY submitted the report.'' The main stress falls on ''Mary'' because the speaker is correcting who performed the action.', TRUE),
  (9374, 5466, '''Students'', ''reading'' and ''book'' may carry greater prominence, while ''the'', ''are'' and ''a'' are often less prominent. This contrast helps organise the sentence into a natural rhythm.', TRUE),
  (9375, 5467, 'Pausing after every word interrupts the flow and separates words that belong together. The speaker should use natural rhythm, connect related words and pause at meaningful boundaries.', TRUE),
  (9376, 5468, '''After the match, / we returned to school.'' The pause separates the introductory phrase from the main clause and makes the sentence easier to process.', TRUE),
  (9377, 5469, 'A falling intonation pattern is common: the pitch drops towards the end of ''over''. It can make the statement sound complete.', TRUE),
  (9378, 5470, 'The speaker may raise the pitch towards the end of ''ready'' to ask for confirmation or check whether the listener is prepared.', TRUE),
  (9379, 5471, 'A strongly expressive pitch movement may convey surprise, while a more measured rise may ask for confirmation. The surrounding conversation and tone help the listener decide which meaning is intended.', TRUE),
  (9380, 5472, 'Dear Teacher, could you please send me the notes as soon as you are able? Thank you for your assistance.', TRUE),
  (9381, 5473, 'Informal language can feel natural and friendly when messaging a close friend. A job application requires a professional tone, precise wording and respectful language because it is an official communication used to assess suitability for a position.', TRUE),
  (9382, 5474, 'Formal: ''Would it be possible for me to leave the meeting early, please?'' Informal: ''Is it okay if I leave early?'' The formal request is more ceremonious, while the informal version is more conversational.', TRUE),
  (9383, 5475, 'Could I please borrow your dictionary for a moment?', TRUE),
  (9384, 5476, 'Certainly, I would be happy to help you with the exercise.', TRUE),
  (9385, 5477, 'I''m sorry, I need my notes for revision today. I can share them with you after the examination.', TRUE),
  (9386, 5478, 'Would you like to attend our school debate on Friday at 3 p.m. in the school hall? I hope you can join us.', TRUE),
  (9387, 5479, 'No, thank you. I can carry them myself, but I appreciate your offer.', TRUE),
  (9388, 5480, 'An offer proposes help or something to receive: ''Shall I help you arrange the chairs?'' An invitation proposes participation: ''Would you like to attend the school concert on Saturday?''', TRUE),
  (9389, 5481, 'You should test yourself on the topic without looking at your notes. If I were you, I would review the questions I get wrong again after a short interval.', TRUE),
  (9390, 5482, 'Why don''t we practise comprehension questions together? We could then exchange our summaries and check whether each one includes the main points.', TRUE),
  (9391, 5483, 'Thank you for the advice, but I think studying all night may leave me tired. I would rather review the main topics and get enough sleep.', TRUE),
  (9392, 5484, 'I apologise for submitting the assignment late without informing you. I should have managed my time better and communicated the delay. I will plan my deadlines more carefully and inform you promptly if a problem arises.', TRUE),
  (9393, 5485, 'Dear Librarian, I would like to report that one of the library computers has not been working for three days. This has made it difficult for learners to access online study materials. Could the fault please be investigated and repaired? Thank you for your assistance.', TRUE),
  (9394, 5486, 'The statement is insulting and does not identify a specific problem or a remedy. A more effective complaint describes the incident, provides relevant details and requests a reasonable action, making it easier to investigate and resolve the issue.', TRUE),
  (9395, 5487, 'Our school should establish a reading club because it would help learners develop vocabulary and improve comprehension. It would also give learners opportunities to discuss ideas and recommend useful books to one another. The club could meet once a week under the guidance of a teacher.', TRUE),
  (9396, 5488, 'The group could meet during lunch to complete the main discussion and divide any remaining tasks for individual work. This allows the group to collaborate without requiring the member to miss the bus.', TRUE),
  (9397, 5489, 'Emotional language may attract attention, but it does not prove that a claim is true. Relevant evidence gives the audience reasons to trust the argument. A strong speech can use appropriate emotion while relying on accurate facts and sound reasoning.', TRUE),
  (9398, 5490, 'Evaporation happens when liquid water changes into water vapour and mixes with the air. For example, a puddle may slowly disappear when the sun warms it.', TRUE),
  (9399, 5491, 'For learners, use a concise announcement stating the affected lessons, dates and times. For the principal, use a more formal report explaining the change, its reason and any arrangements needed. The actual timetable details must remain accurate in both messages.', TRUE),
  (9400, 5492, 'The speaker may lose the audience because the terminology is unfamiliar. The speaker should use simpler wording where possible, define essential terms with examples and invite questions to check understanding.', TRUE),
  (9401, 5493, 'Could you please confirm the exact deadline? Should we submit the assignment before lessons begin on Friday or by the end of the school day?', TRUE),
  (9402, 5494, 'I''m sorry it seemed that way. I did not see your message, so I was not deliberately ignoring you. Could you send it again so I can respond?', TRUE),
  (9403, 5495, 'Paraphrasing allows the listener to check that the message has been understood correctly. If the instruction is ''Bring your science book and ruler tomorrow'', the listener could ask, ''Just to confirm, I should bring both my science book and a ruler to class tomorrow?'' The speaker can then correct any misunderstanding.', TRUE),
  (9404, 5496, 'I would check which notice comes from an official school source, compare its date and update history, then confirm the examination date through the school''s latest official announcement.', TRUE),
  (9405, 5497, 'Headings reveal where relevant information is likely to appear, while hyperlinks connect the reader to related pages or supporting resources.', TRUE),
  (9406, 5498, 'I would check the date of the post and seek confirmation from the responsible examination authority or an official school source. If no reliable confirmation exists, I would not share the claim as fact.', TRUE),
  (9407, 5499, 'Tuesday had the highest number, with 6 more books borrowed than on Monday.', TRUE),
  (9408, 5500, 'The water level increased by 3 metres. The graph alone cannot prove what caused the increase, such as rainfall, without additional evidence.', TRUE),
  (9409, 5501, 'Sales increased by 4 units. Because the vertical axis begins at 90 instead of zero, the graph may make the increase appear larger than it is. I would inspect the scale and actual values before judging the size of the change.', TRUE),
  (9410, 5502, 'The purpose is to instruct people on how to make water safer to drink. The audience is people who need guidance on treating drinking water.', TRUE),
  (9411, 5503, 'The purpose is to persuade people to plant trees. The direct command encourages immediate action, while ''a greener future'' presents tree planting as beneficial.', TRUE),
  (9412, 5504, 'The purpose is to inform learners and parents about the revised examination dates. The notice should include the new dates and the examinations or classes affected. It should also identify the issuing school and publication date.', TRUE),
  (9413, 5505, 'I would look for an official announcement from the responsible examination or education authority, check its date and confirm that it applies to secondary school learners and the relevant examination. If necessary, I would seek confirmation from the school or another authoritative publication.', TRUE),
  (9414, 5506, 'The claim is questionable because it promises the same outcome for every learner without evidence, and the website provides no research to support it. If the website is selling the method, its promotional purpose should also be considered.', TRUE),
  (9415, 5507, 'A recent post may help explain how one person experienced a school service, but it is insufficient by itself to establish a national statistic or prove an official policy. Those claims need more representative or authoritative evidence.', TRUE),
  (9416, 5508, 'The claim that the team won two matches can be checked against match records. The claim that it is the best team in the country is unsupported because two wins alone do not establish a national ranking.', TRUE),
  (9417, 5509, 'The poster should state how many learners took part, how they were selected, what counted as improvement, how it was measured and over what period. The underlying data or study should also be available for checking.', TRUE),
  (9418, 5510, 'This app can help students practise, identify areas they need to improve and prepare more effectively for examinations.', TRUE),
  (9419, 5511, 'The report may count total visits across the whole term while the learner observed only certain times or days. The sources may also refer to different periods. I would check the report''s data and compare it with observations or records covering the same period.', TRUE),
  (9420, 5512, 'Both websites may be relying on the same report, so their agreement does not independently confirm the statistic. I would examine the original report and look for other sources that verify the data independently.', TRUE),
  (9421, 5513, 'The figures do not necessarily contradict each other because Source A reports June''s total and Source B reports July''s total. Both can be correct if the records support those separate periods.', TRUE),
  (9422, 5514, 'I would verify the closure through an official school announcement or an authorised representative before forwarding the message. If I cannot confirm it, I would not share it as fact.', TRUE),
  (9423, 5515, 'I would not post the photograph without the learner''s permission. I would explain that private images should not be shared without consent and suggest asking the learner first, provided sharing the image is appropriate.', TRUE),
  (9424, 5516, 'I would check the correct date against an official announcement, then send a message such as: ''Correction: I shared the wrong examination date earlier. The official date is 18 October. Sorry for the confusion.''', TRUE),
  (9425, 5517, 'Data is raw facts; information is processed data', TRUE),
  (9426, 5518, 'Raw, unprocessed facts and figures', TRUE),
  (9427, 5519, 'Processed data that is meaningful', TRUE),
